-- Admin study pack for the Exam Room admin page (admins only — students cannot read it).
--   bank           all 3,000 CCN mock questions (Days 1–15) sorted into 18 curriculum areas;
--                  must = true marks the 300 must-know questions
--   keynote_quiz   multiple-choice questions made from the CCN keynote book tables
--   keynote_cards  flashcards made from the keynote book
--   keynote_notes  the keynote book chapters (with diagrams) for reading
-- Safe to run again: it replaces the whole pack.
set lock_timeout = '5s';
begin;
create table if not exists public.admin_study (
  id bigserial primary key,
  collection text not null,
  area text not null,
  must boolean not null default false,
  sort integer not null default 0,
  q text not null,
  opts jsonb,
  ans_idx smallint,
  answer text,
  exp text,
  ref text
);
create index if not exists admin_study_coll_idx on public.admin_study (collection, area, sort);
alter table public.admin_study enable row level security;
drop policy if exists admin_study_select on public.admin_study;
-- Admins only. Uses exam_is_admin() (Exam Room admins); falls back to the profile role if it is missing.
create or replace function public.admin_study_allowed() returns boolean
language plpgsql security definer stable set search_path = public as $f$
begin
  return public.exam_is_admin();
exception when undefined_function then
  return exists (select 1 from public.profiles p where p.id = auth.uid() and p.role = 'admin' and not coalesce(p.disabled, false));
end $f$;
revoke all on function public.admin_study_allowed() from public, anon;
grant execute on function public.admin_study_allowed() to authenticated;
create policy admin_study_select on public.admin_study for select using (public.admin_study_allowed());
revoke all on public.admin_study from anon;
grant select on public.admin_study to authenticated;
delete from public.admin_study;
insert into public.admin_study (collection, area, must, sort, q, opts, ans_idx, answer, exp, ref) values
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, true, 0, $s$The source of pulmonary surfactant is:$s$, $s$["The visceral pleura", "Tracheal glands", "Alveolar capillaries", "Great alveolar (type II) cells"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Great alveolar (type II) cells
Surfactant is a mixture of phospholipids (mainly dipalmitoylphosphatidylcholine) and proteins made by the type II alveolar cells, also called great alveolar cells or type II pneumocytes. It lines the inside of each alveolus and lowers surface tension, so the small alveoli do not collapse at the end of expiration and the lungs are easier to inflate (better compliance). Type I cells, by contrast, are thin, flat cells that form the gas-exchange surface.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The visceral pleura — The visceral pleura is the membrane covering the outside of the lung; it makes pleural fluid for smooth gliding, not surfactant.
• Tracheal glands — Tracheal glands make mucus that traps dust and germs in the airways; they do not make surfactant.
• Alveolar capillaries — Alveolar capillaries carry blood for gas exchange; they do not secrete anything into the alveoli.

⭐ Key point: Type II pneumocytes make surfactant. Preterm babies (before about 34 weeks) lack it, causing respiratory distress syndrome; antenatal steroids speed up its production.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q1$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, true, 1, $s$Antidiuretic hormone (ADH, vasopressin) is synthesized in the hypothalamus and released into the circulation from the:$s$, $s$["Posterior pituitary gland", "Anterior pituitary gland", "Adrenal medulla", "Thyroid gland"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Posterior pituitary gland
ADH (vasopressin) is MADE in the hypothalamus (supraoptic and paraventricular nuclei). It travels down nerve fibres to the POSTERIOR pituitary, where it is stored and released into the blood. ADH makes the collecting ducts keep water, so it raises blood volume and concentrates the urine. Oxytocin is handled the same way.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anterior pituitary gland — The anterior pituitary makes its own hormones (GH, TSH, ACTH, FSH, LH, prolactin), not ADH.
• Adrenal medulla — The adrenal medulla releases adrenaline and noradrenaline.
• Thyroid gland — The thyroid gland releases T3, T4 and calcitonin.

⭐ Key point: Too little ADH = diabetes insipidus (large volumes of dilute urine). Too much ADH = SIADH (low sodium, concentrated urine). Both are common after head injury and brain surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q10$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, true, 2, $s$In adults, the primary site of haematopoiesis is the:$s$, $s$["Liver", "Spleen", "Red bone marrow", "Lymph nodes"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Red bone marrow
HAEMATOPOIESIS means the making of blood cells (red cells, white cells and platelets). In ADULTS it happens mainly in the RED BONE MARROW of the flat bones and the ends of long bones — the sternum, ribs, vertebrae, pelvis (iliac crest), skull and the upper ends of the femur and humerus. All blood cells come from one parent cell, the haematopoietic STEM cell, found in this marrow. That is why bone marrow aspiration/biopsy is taken from the iliac crest or sternum when we want to study blood disorders such as leukaemia or aplastic anaemia. In the unborn baby the order is different: yolk sac first, then liver and spleen, then bone marrow by birth.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Liver — The LIVER is the main site of blood formation in the FOETUS (middle months of pregnancy), not in the healthy adult. It only returns to this job in disease (extramedullary haematopoiesis, e.g. thalassaemia, myelofibrosis).
• Spleen — The SPLEEN filters and destroys old red cells and stores platelets and lymphocytes; it makes blood cells only in the foetus or in marrow failure.
• Lymph nodes — LYMPH NODES filter lymph and help lymphocytes to multiply and mature, but they do not make red cells or platelets.

⭐ Key point: Adult blood cells are made in RED BONE MARROW (sternum, pelvis, vertebrae, ribs, skull). Foetus: yolk sac → liver/spleen → marrow.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q1$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, true, 3, $s$A normal adult's total lung capacity is approximately:$s$, $s$["1500 mL", "2500 mL", "6000 mL", "10000 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 6000 mL
TOTAL LUNG CAPACITY (TLC) is the total air in the lungs after the deepest possible breath in. In a normal adult it is about 6000 mL (6 litres) — a little less in women and smaller people. TLC = vital capacity (about 4600–4800 mL) + residual volume (about 1200 mL, the air that always stays so the alveoli don't collapse). Other volumes to remember: tidal volume about 500 mL (normal quiet breath), inspiratory reserve about 3000 mL, expiratory reserve about 1100–1200 mL. TLC goes DOWN in restrictive disease (pulmonary fibrosis, obesity, chest wall deformity) and goes UP (hyperinflation) in obstructive disease like emphysema/COPD.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1500 mL — 1500 mL is too small; it is close to the residual volume plus a bit, not the whole lung.
• 2500 mL — 2500 mL is roughly the functional residual capacity (air left after a normal breath out), not total capacity.
• 10000 mL — 10000 mL (10 L) is far above normal for any adult human lung.

⭐ Key point: TLC ≈ 6000 mL; tidal volume ≈ 500 mL; residual volume ≈ 1200 mL; vital capacity ≈ 4800 mL.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q3$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, true, 4, $s$The dermis contains all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Blood vessels", "Nerve endings", "Hair follicles", "Keratinocytes"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Keratinocytes
The skin has two main layers. The top layer, the EPIDERMIS, is made mostly of KERATINOCYTES — cells that make keratin, the tough protein that waterproofs and protects the skin. The epidermis has NO blood vessels; it is fed by diffusion from below. Under it is the DERMIS, a thick connective-tissue layer that holds blood vessels, nerve endings (touch, pain, pressure, temperature receptors), hair follicles, sweat glands, sebaceous (oil) glands, collagen and elastin. So the item NOT found as a main dermal component is the keratinocyte — it belongs to the epidermis. Remember: "E for Epidermis, K for Keratin on the outside; D for Dermis, Deep with vessels and nerves."

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Blood vessels — Blood vessels ARE in the dermis; they feed the skin and help control body temperature by dilating or constricting.
• Nerve endings — Nerve endings ARE in the dermis; they let us feel touch, pain, heat and pressure.
• Hair follicles — Hair follicles are rooted in the dermis (and reach into the subcutaneous layer), along with their sebaceous glands.

⭐ Key point: Keratinocytes = EPIDERMIS. Dermis = vessels, nerves, hair follicles, glands, collagen. The epidermis is avascular.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q4$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, true, 5, $s$Which epidermal layer is found only in thick (glabrous) skin, such as the palms and soles?$s$, $s$["Stratum lucidum", "Stratum spinosum", "Stratum granulosum", "Stratum basale"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stratum lucidum
The epidermis has up to five layers (deep to surface): stratum BASALE, SPINOSUM, GRANULOSUM, LUCIDUM and CORNEUM. Memory aid: "Come, Let's Get Sun Burnt" (from top: Corneum, Lucidum, Granulosum, Spinosum, Basale). The STRATUM LUCIDUM is a thin clear layer of dead, flattened cells containing eleidin. It is found ONLY in THICK (glabrous, hairless) skin — the PALMS of the hands and SOLES of the feet — where extra friction and pressure need extra protection. Thin skin over most of the body has only four layers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stratum spinosum — STRATUM SPINOSUM ("prickly layer") is present in both thick and thin skin; it gives strength and contains Langerhans (immune) cells.
• Stratum granulosum — STRATUM GRANULOSUM is present in both; its cells start making keratin and waterproofing lipids and begin to die.
• Stratum basale — STRATUM BASALE is present in all skin; it is the deepest layer, where new cells are made by division and where melanocytes sit.

⭐ Key point: Stratum LUCIDUM = only in thick skin of palms and soles. Order deep→surface: Basale, Spinosum, Granulosum, Lucidum, Corneum.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q7$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, true, 6, $s$Most nutrient absorption occurs in the:$s$, $s$["Stomach", "Small intestine", "Large intestine", "Oesophagus"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Small intestine
Most nutrient absorption takes place in the SMALL INTESTINE (duodenum, jejunum, ileum). It is about 6 metres long and its lining is folded into circular folds, VILLI and MICROVILLI, giving a huge surface (about 200–250 m²) for absorption. Carbohydrates (as glucose), proteins (as amino acids), fats (as fatty acids into lacteals), vitamins and minerals are absorbed here. Special sites: iron and calcium mainly in the duodenum; most nutrients in the jejunum; vitamin B12 and bile salts in the terminal ILEUM. That is why removing the ileum causes B12 deficiency, and diseases like coeliac disease (damaged villi) cause malabsorption.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stomach — The STOMACH mainly stores and mixes food and starts protein digestion with acid and pepsin; it absorbs only a little (water, alcohol, some drugs like aspirin).
• Large intestine — The LARGE INTESTINE mainly absorbs WATER and electrolytes and forms stool; very few nutrients are absorbed there (some vitamins made by gut bacteria, e.g. vitamin K).
• Oesophagus — The OESOPHAGUS simply carries food from the mouth to the stomach by peristalsis; it absorbs nothing.

⭐ Key point: Most absorption = SMALL INTESTINE (villi + microvilli). Iron → duodenum; B12 → terminal ileum; water → large intestine.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q8$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, true, 7, $s$A patient with severe burns loses significant fluid through the damaged skin. This highlights the skin's normal physiological role in:$s$, $s$["Hormone production only", "Fluid/temperature regulation and acting as a protective barrier against fluid loss and infection", "Filtering blood", "Producing red blood cells"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fluid/temperature regulation and acting as a protective barrier against fluid loss and infection
Intact skin is a barrier. It stops body water from evaporating away, keeps out germs, and helps control temperature through sweating and changes in blood flow. In SEVERE BURNS this barrier is destroyed, so the patient loses large amounts of fluid, protein and heat through the wound, and bacteria can enter easily. Capillaries also become leaky, so fluid shifts out of the blood. This explains why burns patients need aggressive IV fluid resuscitation (e.g. Parkland formula 4 mL × kg × %TBSA in 24 h, half in the first 8 h), warming to prevent hypothermia, and strict infection control. The question shows the skin's normal role in FLUID AND TEMPERATURE REGULATION and as a PROTECTIVE BARRIER.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hormone production only — "Hormone production ONLY" is wrong: the skin does help make vitamin D, but that is not what fluid loss in burns shows, and "only" makes it false.
• Filtering blood — FILTERING BLOOD is the job of the kidneys (and the spleen/liver), not the skin.
• Producing red blood cells — PRODUCING RED BLOOD CELLS is the job of the red bone marrow, not the skin.

⭐ Key point: Skin = barrier against fluid loss, infection and heat loss. Burns remove it → fluid loss, hypothermia, infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q9$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, true, 8, $s$The lymphatic system's primary functions include all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Draining excess interstitial fluid back into the bloodstream", "Transporting absorbed dietary fats from the intestine", "Supporting immune function", "Directly pumping oxygenated blood to tissues"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Directly pumping oxygenated blood to tissues
The LYMPHATIC SYSTEM is a network of vessels, nodes and organs (spleen, thymus, tonsils). Its main jobs: (1) DRAIN excess tissue fluid — about 2–3 litres a day leak from capillaries and are returned to the blood via the thoracic duct into the left subclavian vein; (2) ABSORB DIETARY FATS from the gut through lacteals in the villi (as chylomicrons); (3) support IMMUNITY — lymph nodes filter germs and house lymphocytes. The lymphatic system has NO heart pumping it; lymph moves by muscle squeezing, breathing and one-way valves. Pumping oxygenated blood to the tissues is the job of the HEART and arteries (cardiovascular system). Blocked lymph drainage causes lymphoedema (e.g. after axillary node removal in breast surgery, or in filariasis/elephantiasis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Draining excess interstitial fluid back into the bloodstream — Draining excess interstitial fluid back to the blood IS a main lymphatic function; failure causes lymphoedema.
• Transporting absorbed dietary fats from the intestine — Transporting absorbed fats from the intestine IS a lymphatic function (lacteals carry chyle).
• Supporting immune function — Supporting immune function IS a key function (lymph nodes, spleen, lymphocytes).

⭐ Key point: Lymphatics = fluid return, fat absorption, immunity. The HEART pumps oxygenated blood — lymphatics have no pump.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q13$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, true, 9, $s$Which of the following is NOT one of the classic hormones secreted by the anterior pituitary?$s$, $s$["Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH)", "Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH)", "Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)", "Growth hormone (GH)"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Antidiuretic hormone (ADH)
The ANTERIOR PITUITARY gland makes and releases six main hormones: GROWTH HORMONE (GH), THYROID-STIMULATING HORMONE (TSH), ADRENOCORTICOTROPHIC HORMONE (ACTH), FOLLICLE-STIMULATING HORMONE (FSH), LUTEINISING HORMONE (LH) and PROLACTIN. ANTIDIURETIC HORMONE (ADH, vasopressin) is NOT made by the anterior pituitary — it is made in the HYPOTHALAMUS and STORED and RELEASED from the POSTERIOR PITUITARY (along with oxytocin).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) — FSH IS an anterior pituitary hormone.
• Thyroid-stimulating hormone (TSH) — TSH IS an anterior pituitary hormone.
• Growth hormone (GH) — GH IS an anterior pituitary hormone.

⭐ Key point: Damage to the posterior pituitary (e.g. after pituitary surgery or head injury) can cause diabetes insipidus (lack of ADH) with large volumes of dilute urine.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q6$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, true, 10, $s$Residual volume is the amount of air:$s$, $s$["Inhaled during a normal breath", "Exhaled forcefully after normal expiration", "Remaining in the lungs after maximal expiration", "Inhaled during maximal inspiration beyond tidal volume"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Remaining in the lungs after maximal expiration
RESIDUAL VOLUME (RV) is the amount of air that REMAINS in the lungs after a MAXIMAL (forced, complete) EXPIRATION — about 1.2 litres in an average adult. It cannot be breathed out and keeps the alveoli from collapsing completely, allowing continuous gas exchange between breaths.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Inhaled during a normal breath — Air inhaled during a normal breath is the TIDAL VOLUME (about 500 mL).
• Exhaled forcefully after normal expiration — Air exhaled forcefully after a normal expiration is the EXPIRATORY RESERVE VOLUME (about 1.1 L).
• Inhaled during maximal inspiration beyond tidal volume — Air inhaled beyond the tidal volume is the INSPIRATORY RESERVE VOLUME (about 3 L).

⭐ Key point: Residual volume is increased in COPD and asthma (air trapping), and reduced in restrictive lung disease (e.g. fibrosis).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q11 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, true, 11, $s$Arranged from outermost to innermost, the correct order of the three meningeal layers surrounding the brain and spinal cord is$s$, $s$["dura mater, pia mater, arachnoid mater", "arachnoid mater, dura mater, pia mater", "dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater", "pia mater, arachnoid mater, dura mater"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: dura mater, arachnoid mater, pia mater
The three meninges, from OUTSIDE to INSIDE: DURA mater (the tough outer layer), ARACHNOID mater (the middle, web-like layer), PIA mater (the thin inner layer stuck to the brain). Remember "DAP": Dura, Arachnoid, Pia. CSF flows in the subarachnoid space, between the arachnoid and pia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• dura mater, pia mater, arachnoid mater — Dura, pia, arachnoid is the wrong order.
• arachnoid mater, dura mater, pia mater — Arachnoid, dura, pia is the wrong order.
• pia mater, arachnoid mater, dura mater — Pia, arachnoid, dura is the reverse order (inside to outside).

⭐ Key point: Bleeds: extradural (between skull and dura), subdural (between dura and arachnoid), subarachnoid (in the CSF space).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q72$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 12, $s$Intrinsic factor, secreted by gastric parietal cells, is essential for absorption of which nutrient?$s$, $s$["Vitamin B12, absorbed in the terminal ileum", "Iron, absorbed in the duodenum", "Calcium, absorbed in the jejunum", "Vitamin K, absorbed in the colon"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vitamin B12, absorbed in the terminal ileum
Gastric parietal cells make intrinsic factor (as well as hydrochloric acid). Intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12, and the complex is absorbed in the TERMINAL ILEUM. Without intrinsic factor (pernicious anaemia, gastrectomy) or without the terminal ileum (Crohn's disease, resection), B12 is not absorbed. The result is macrocytic anaemia and nerve damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Iron, absorbed in the duodenum — Iron is absorbed mainly in the duodenum, helped by stomach acid and vitamin C, but it does not need intrinsic factor.
• Calcium, absorbed in the jejunum — Calcium is absorbed in the duodenum and jejunum with the help of vitamin D, not intrinsic factor.
• Vitamin K, absorbed in the colon — Vitamin K is partly made by gut bacteria and absorbed with fats; it does not need intrinsic factor.

⭐ Key point: No intrinsic factor = no B12 = pernicious anaemia, which is treated with lifelong IM vitamin B12 (hydroxocobalamin) injections.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q15$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 13, $s$How is TENS distinguished from Stevens-Johnson Syndrome (SJS)?$s$, $s$["Causative organism", "Percentage of body surface area with epidermal detachment (TEN greater than 30%, SJS less than 10%)", "Patient age only", "Presence of fever"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Percentage of body surface area with epidermal detachment (TEN greater than 30%, SJS less than 10%)
SJS and TEN are the same disease at different severities, so the difference is the PERCENTAGE of body surface area where the epidermis peels off. SJS is less than 10%, the SJS/TEN overlap is 10–30%, and TEN is more than 30%. Both are usually drug-induced, with fever, painful skin, mucosal ulcers (mouth, eyes, genitals) and a positive Nikolsky sign. TEN has a much higher death rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Causative organism — The causative agent is usually the same type (drugs such as carbamazepine, phenytoin, allopurinol, sulphonamides, nevirapine), so it doesn't separate them.
• Patient age only — Both can occur at any age.
• Presence of fever — Fever occurs in both, so it doesn't distinguish them.

⭐ Key point: Stop the causing drug immediately. Manage like a major burn: warm room, fluids, non-stick dressings, eye and mouth care, nutrition and pain relief, ideally in a burns unit or ICU.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q99$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 14, $s$How is Toxic Epidermal Necrolysis Syndrome (TENS) best classified?$s$, $s$["A severe, life-threatening mucocutaneous drug reaction with widespread epidermal detachment", "A viral exanthem", "A bacterial skin infection", "A chronic autoimmune skin condition unrelated to medications"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A severe, life-threatening mucocutaneous drug reaction with widespread epidermal detachment
Toxic epidermal necrolysis (TEN) is a severe, life-threatening drug reaction that affects the skin AND mucous membranes. The top layer of skin (epidermis) dies and peels off in sheets over more than 30% of the body, leaving raw areas like a scald burn. The patient loses fluid, protein and heat, and is at high risk of infection and sepsis. The death rate is 25–35% or more.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A viral exanthem — It is not a viral rash (exanthem), although infections can occasionally trigger it.
• A bacterial skin infection — It is not a bacterial skin infection; it is an immune reaction to a drug.
• A chronic autoimmune skin condition unrelated to medications — It is an acute reaction, usually caused by a medication, not a chronic autoimmune disease.

⭐ Key point: Use the SCORTEN score to predict death risk. Care includes barrier nursing, warming, fluids, eye care by an ophthalmologist, and stopping all non-essential drugs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q100$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 15, $s$Hemorrhagic discoloration of the flanks is known as$s$, $s$["Grey-Turner's sign", "Grey-Prumer's sign", "Cullen's sign", "Kullen's sign"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Grey-Turner's sign
Grey-Turner's sign is bruising (bluish discolouration) of the FLANKS. Blood from the retroperitoneum tracks along the tissue planes to the skin of the sides, typically 24–48 hours after the bleed. It is seen in severe (haemorrhagic) acute pancreatitis, ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm, and retroperitoneal bleeding (including ruptured ectopic pregnancy).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Grey-Prumer's sign — "Grey-Prumer's sign" does not exist; it is a made-up distractor.
• Cullen's sign — Cullen's sign is bruising around the UMBILICUS (navel), not the flanks.
• Kullen's sign — "Kullen's sign" is a misspelling distractor of Cullen's sign.

⭐ Key point: Grey-Turner = flanks; Cullen = umbilicus. Both suggest bleeding in the abdomen or retroperitoneum (pancreatitis, ruptured ectopic, AAA).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q125$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 16, $s$According to Maslow's hierarchy, the most basic needs are:$s$, $s$["Safety and security needs", "Self-actualisation needs", "Physiological needs", "Social needs"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Physiological needs
Maslow's hierarchy has five levels, from the bottom up: PHYSIOLOGICAL needs (air, water, food, sleep, elimination), then safety and security, love and belonging, esteem, and self-actualisation at the top. The physiological needs are the most basic and must be met first. In nursing, this is why AIRWAY, BREATHING and CIRCULATION come first when prioritising care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Safety and security needs — Safety and security are the second level.
• Self-actualisation needs — Self-actualisation is the TOP (highest) level.
• Social needs — Social (love and belonging) needs are the third level.

⭐ Key point: In exam questions about priority, choose the answer that meets a physiological need (especially oxygen and airway) before psychological or educational needs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q158$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 17, $s$The hypothalamus links the nervous and endocrine systems and regulates all of the following EXCEPT$s$, $s$["body temperature", "circadian rhythm and sleep-wake cycle", "hunger and thirst", "voluntary skeletal muscle coordination"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: voluntary skeletal muscle coordination
The HYPOTHALAMUS is a small area at the base of the brain that links the nervous system to the endocrine system through the pituitary gland. It is the body's "thermostat and control centre": it regulates body temperature, hunger and satiety, thirst and water balance (makes ADH), the sleep–wake (circadian) rhythm, emotions and stress responses, and controls pituitary hormones. It does NOT coordinate voluntary skeletal muscle movement. Smooth, accurate voluntary movement and balance are the job of the CEREBELLUM, working with the motor cortex and basal ganglia. That is why cerebellar disease causes ataxia, tremor on movement and poor coordination, while hypothalamic damage causes problems like fever, diabetes insipidus or abnormal appetite.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• body temperature — Body temperature IS regulated by the hypothalamus — that is why infection "resets" it and causes fever.
• circadian rhythm and sleep-wake cycle — Circadian rhythm and sleep–wake cycle ARE controlled by the hypothalamus (suprachiasmatic nucleus).
• hunger and thirst — Hunger and thirst ARE controlled by the hypothalamus (feeding/satiety centres; thirst and ADH).

⭐ Key point: Hypothalamus = temperature, hunger, thirst, sleep, hormones. Coordination of voluntary movement = CEREBELLUM.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q5$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 18, $s$Gaseous exchange in the lungs occurs primarily at the level of the:$s$, $s$["Trachea", "Bronchi", "Alveoli", "Larynx"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Alveoli
Gas exchange (oxygen in, carbon dioxide out) happens in the ALVEOLI — tiny air sacs at the end of the airways. There are about 300–500 million of them, giving a surface area of about 70 m² (half a tennis court). Their walls are only one cell thick and are wrapped in capillaries, so the gap between air and blood (the respiratory membrane) is extremely thin (about 0.5 micrometres). Oxygen diffuses from alveolus into blood, CO2 from blood into alveolus. Type I cells form the thin wall; Type II cells make surfactant to stop alveoli collapsing. Diseases that thicken the wall (fibrosis), fill the alveoli (pneumonia, pulmonary oedema) or destroy them (emphysema) all reduce gas exchange.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Trachea — The TRACHEA is a conducting airway; it warms, humidifies and filters air but no gas exchange happens there (it is part of anatomical dead space).
• Bronchi — The BRONCHI are also conducting airways (dead space); their walls are too thick for gas exchange.
• Larynx — The LARYNX is the voice box and protects the airway; it plays no part in gas exchange.

⭐ Key point: Gas exchange = ALVEOLI (huge area, very thin wall). Trachea, bronchi and larynx only conduct air (dead space).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q6$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 19, $s$During subclavian central line insertion, the vein lies:$s$, $s$["Anterior to the subclavian artery, posterior to the clavicle", "Lateral to the carotid artery only", "Superior to the clavicle at all times", "Within the mediastinum only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Anterior to the subclavian artery, posterior to the clavicle
The SUBCLAVIAN VEIN runs under the CLAVICLE (collarbone), over the first rib. It lies IN FRONT OF (anterior to) the subclavian ARTERY, separated from it by the anterior scalene muscle, and BEHIND (posterior to) the clavicle. For a subclavian central line the needle is passed under the clavicle aiming towards the sternal notch. Knowing this anatomy explains the main risks: going too deep can puncture the subclavian ARTERY (bright red pulsatile blood, haematoma that cannot be compressed) or the PLEURA, causing a PNEUMOTHORAX — which is why a chest X-ray is done after insertion to check the tip position (lower SVC/cavo-atrial junction) and exclude pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lateral to the carotid artery only — "Lateral to the carotid artery only" describes the INTERNAL JUGULAR vein in the neck, not the subclavian vein.
• Superior to the clavicle at all times — The subclavian vein is mostly BELOW/behind the clavicle, not above it at all times.
• Within the mediastinum only — It is not "within the mediastinum only"; it is in the root of the neck/upper chest and only joins the internal jugular to form the brachiocephalic vein near the mediastinum.

⭐ Key point: Subclavian vein = ANTERIOR to the subclavian artery, POSTERIOR to the clavicle. Main risks: arterial puncture and pneumothorax → do a chest X-ray.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q14$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 20, $s$The spinal cord's reflex action allows for:$s$, $s$["Conscious decision-making before every movement", "A rapid, automatic response to a stimulus, bypassing the need for the brain to process it first", "Only sensory perception with no motor output", "Hormone regulation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A rapid, automatic response to a stimulus, bypassing the need for the brain to process it first
A REFLEX is a fast, automatic, involuntary response to a stimulus. The signal travels along a REFLEX ARC: receptor → sensory (afferent) neurone → spinal cord (often via an interneurone) → motor (efferent) neurone → effector (muscle). The response happens at the SPINAL CORD level, before the brain has processed the sensation — so you pull your hand off a hot object before you feel pain. Examples: knee jerk (patellar reflex, L3–L4), withdrawal reflex, Babinski sign. Nurses test reflexes in neurological assessment: absent reflexes suggest lower motor neurone or spinal shock; brisk reflexes suggest upper motor neurone lesion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Conscious decision-making before every movement — Reflexes are NOT conscious decisions; conscious control comes from the cerebral cortex and is slower.
• Only sensory perception with no motor output — A reflex has both SENSORY input and MOTOR output; "only sensory perception with no motor output" is not a reflex.
• Hormone regulation — Hormone regulation is done by the endocrine system and hypothalamus, not by spinal reflex arcs.

⭐ Key point: Spinal reflex = rapid, automatic response through the reflex arc, without waiting for the brain.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q15$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 21, $s$In patients with respiratory distress, damage to type II alveolar cells can impair which of the following key functions?$s$, $s$["Immunity", "Capacitation", "Gaseous exchange", "Secretion of surfactant"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Secretion of surfactant
The alveolar wall has two main cell types. TYPE I cells are thin and flat, covering about 95% of the surface and forming the gas exchange barrier. TYPE II cells are rounder and make SURFACTANT — a mixture of phospholipids and proteins that lowers SURFACE TENSION in the alveoli, stopping them from collapsing at the end of expiration and making the lungs easier to inflate. Type II cells also act as stem cells to repair type I cells. Damage to type II cells (as in ARDS, or immaturity in premature babies — neonatal respiratory distress syndrome) means less surfactant, so alveoli collapse (atelectasis), lungs become stiff and hypoxaemia worsens.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immunity — IMMUNITY in the alveoli is mainly provided by alveolar MACROPHAGES, not type II cells.
• Capacitation — CAPACITATION is a process in which SPERM become able to fertilise an egg; it has nothing to do with the lungs.
• Gaseous exchange — Gas exchange happens mainly across TYPE I cells; type II damage affects it indirectly, but the KEY specific type II function is surfactant secretion.

⭐ Key point: Type II alveolar cells = SURFACTANT (prevents collapse). Type I cells = gas exchange surface.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q59$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 22, $s$The juxtaglomerular apparatus regulates blood pressure and renal blood flow chiefly by secreting which substance?$s$, $s$["Antidiuretic hormone", "Aldosterone", "Renin", "Erythropoietin"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Renin
The JUXTAGLOMERULAR APPARATUS (JGA) sits where the afferent arteriole touches the distal tubule of each nephron. Its JUXTAGLOMERULAR cells release RENIN when they sense LOW blood pressure/low renal blood flow, low sodium (detected by the macula densa) or sympathetic stimulation. Renin starts the RENIN–ANGIOTENSIN–ALDOSTERONE SYSTEM (RAAS): renin converts angiotensinogen (from the liver) to angiotensin I; ACE (mainly in the lungs) converts it to ANGIOTENSIN II, which constricts blood vessels and triggers ALDOSTERONE release (sodium and water retention) and ADH and thirst — together raising BP and restoring kidney perfusion. ACE inhibitors and ARBs block this system to treat hypertension and heart failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Antidiuretic hormone — ADH (antidiuretic hormone) is made in the hypothalamus and released from the POSTERIOR PITUITARY, not the JGA.
• Aldosterone — ALDOSTERONE is made in the ADRENAL CORTEX (zona glomerulosa); the JGA starts the chain that leads to it but does not secrete it.
• Erythropoietin — ERYTHROPOIETIN is made by peritubular interstitial cells in the kidney in response to low oxygen; it stimulates red cell production, not BP control.

⭐ Key point: JGA secretes RENIN → angiotensin II → aldosterone → ↑ BP. ACE-I/ARBs block this pathway.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q77$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 23, $s$Hypothalamic damage may cause all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Sweating disorders", "Blood pressure disturbance", "Cardiac rhythm disturbance", "Facial paresis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Facial paresis
The HYPOTHALAMUS controls the AUTONOMIC nervous system and hormones. Damage (trauma, tumour, stroke, surgery) can cause: temperature problems (hyperthermia/hypothermia), SWEATING disorders, BLOOD PRESSURE swings, CARDIAC RHYTHM disturbances (tachycardia, arrhythmias — part of paroxysmal sympathetic hyperactivity after brain injury), diabetes insipidus or SIADH, appetite and sleep problems. FACIAL PARESIS (weakness of facial muscles) comes from damage to the FACIAL NERVE (cranial nerve VII) itself — e.g. Bell's palsy — or to the motor cortex/corticobulbar tract (stroke); it is not caused by hypothalamic damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sweating disorders — SWEATING disorders CAN occur — the hypothalamus controls sweating for temperature regulation.
• Blood pressure disturbance — BLOOD PRESSURE disturbances CAN occur — the hypothalamus controls autonomic tone.
• Cardiac rhythm disturbance — CARDIAC RHYTHM disturbances CAN occur through autonomic dysregulation.

⭐ Key point: Hypothalamic damage → temperature, sweating, BP, heart rhythm, water balance problems. Facial paresis = facial nerve/cortex, not hypothalamus.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q88$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 24, $s$The skin is considered the body's:$s$, $s$["Smallest organ", "Largest organ", "Only avascular organ", "Most vascular organ"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Largest organ
The SKIN is the body's LARGEST ORGAN. In an adult it covers about 1.5–2 square metres and weighs about 3.5–4.5 kg (roughly 15% of body weight). It has three layers — epidermis, dermis and subcutaneous tissue (hypodermis) — and important functions: protection against injury, microorganisms and UV light; prevention of water loss; temperature regulation; sensation (touch, pain, temperature); vitamin D production; and excretion of small amounts of waste in sweat. This is why large burns or skin loss (e.g. toxic epidermal necrolysis) are so dangerous — they cause massive fluid loss, heat loss and infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Smallest organ — The skin is the largest organ, not the smallest.
• Only avascular organ — The skin is not avascular — the dermis has a rich blood supply (only the epidermis has no blood vessels).
• Most vascular organ — Although well supplied with blood, the skin is not the "most vascular" organ (e.g. the kidneys and liver receive more blood per gram).

⭐ Key point: Skin assessment (colour, temperature, turgor, integrity) is a key part of ICU care — pressure ulcers can develop within hours in critically ill patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q1$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 25, $s$During deep tendon reflex testing, the patellar (knee) reflex primarily assesses the integrity of spinal cord segments$s$, $s$["C5-C6", "C7-C8", "L2-L4 (primarily L3-L4)", "S1-S2"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: L2-L4 (primarily L3-L4)
The PATELLAR (knee-jerk) REFLEX is tested by tapping the patellar tendon just below the kneecap, which stretches the quadriceps muscle and causes the leg to kick forward. The reflex travels through the FEMORAL NERVE and spinal cord segments L2–L4, mainly L3–L4. An absent or reduced reflex suggests damage to this pathway (e.g. nerve root compression, peripheral neuropathy, Guillain–Barré syndrome, magnesium toxicity); a brisk reflex suggests an upper motor neurone lesion (e.g. stroke, spinal cord injury above this level).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• C5-C6 — C5–C6 are tested by the biceps and brachioradialis reflexes.
• C7-C8 — C7–C8 are tested by the triceps reflex.
• S1-S2 — S1–S2 are tested by the ankle (Achilles) reflex.

⭐ Key point: In women on magnesium sulphate, loss of the patellar reflex is the first sign of magnesium toxicity.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q3$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 26, $s$Which of the following is NOT a function of the skin?$s$, $s$["Heat regulation", "Protection against microorganisms", "Vitamin D synthesis", "Production of red blood cells"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Production of red blood cells
The SKIN has many functions: HEAT REGULATION (sweating, changing blood flow to the skin), PROTECTION against microorganisms, injury, chemicals and UV radiation, VITAMIN D SYNTHESIS (UV light converts a cholesterol derivative in the skin into vitamin D3), sensation, and preventing water loss. PRODUCTION OF RED BLOOD CELLS happens in the BONE MARROW (haematopoiesis), not in the skin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Heat regulation — Heat regulation IS a skin function.
• Protection against microorganisms — Protection against microorganisms IS a skin function.
• Vitamin D synthesis — Vitamin D synthesis IS a skin function.

⭐ Key point: When the skin is badly damaged (e.g. burns), patients lose heat and fluid rapidly and are at high risk of infection — keep them warm and use strict asepsis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q5$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 27, $s$Thick skin differs from thin skin by having an extra layer called the:$s$, $s$["Stratum basale", "Stratum lucidum", "Stratum corneum", "Stratum granulosum"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stratum lucidum
THICK SKIN (found on the palms of the hands and soles of the feet) has FIVE layers of epidermis. From deep to surface: stratum basale, stratum spinosum, stratum granulosum, STRATUM LUCIDUM (a clear, thin layer of dead cells that adds extra protection against friction), and stratum corneum. THIN SKIN (most of the body) has only four layers — it LACKS the stratum lucidum.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stratum basale — Stratum basale is present in both thick and thin skin (it produces new cells).
• Stratum corneum — Stratum corneum is present in both.
• Stratum granulosum — Stratum granulosum is present in both.

⭐ Key point: Memory aid (deep to surface): "Come Let's Get Sun Burned" — Corneum, Lucidum, Granulosum, Spinosum, Basale (read from top down).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q9$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 28, $s$The following glial cells are located in the central nervous system EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Ependymal cell", "Astrocyte", "Microglia", "Schwann cell"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Schwann cell
GLIAL CELLS support and protect neurons. In the CENTRAL NERVOUS SYSTEM (brain and spinal cord): ASTROCYTES (support, blood–brain barrier), OLIGODENDROCYTES (make myelin in the CNS), MICROGLIA (immune cells), and EPENDYMAL cells (line the ventricles and help produce CSF). In the PERIPHERAL NERVOUS SYSTEM: SCHWANN CELLS (make myelin around peripheral nerves) and satellite cells. So the Schwann cell is the exception.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ependymal cell — Ependymal cells ARE CNS glia.
• Astrocyte — Astrocytes ARE CNS glia.
• Microglia — Microglia ARE CNS glia.

⭐ Key point: Guillain–Barré syndrome damages Schwann cell myelin in peripheral nerves; multiple sclerosis damages oligodendrocyte myelin in the CNS.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q12$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 29, $s$The fluid that passes through the lymphatic vessels$s$, $s$["Flows toward the lungs", "Passes from the lymphatic vessels into the arteries", "Enters the left ventricle of the heart through the right thoracic duct", "Moves in a single direction toward the heart"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Moves in a single direction toward the heart
LYMPH is fluid that leaks out of blood capillaries into the tissues and is collected by LYMPHATIC VESSELS. Lymph flows in ONE DIRECTION ONLY — TOWARDS THE HEART — helped by VALVES inside the vessels (which prevent backflow), muscle contractions and breathing movements. It passes through lymph nodes (which filter it) and finally returns to the blood through the THORACIC DUCT (into the left subclavian vein) and the right lymphatic duct (into the right subclavian vein).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flows toward the lungs — Lymph flows towards the heart and veins, not the lungs.
• Passes from the lymphatic vessels into the arteries — Lymph enters VEINS, not arteries.
• Enters the left ventricle of the heart through the right thoracic duct — Lymph does not enter the left ventricle; it enters the venous system near the heart.

⭐ Key point: Blocked lymphatics cause lymphoedema (e.g. after lymph node removal in breast cancer surgery).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q15$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 30, $s$A patient is inhaling 500 ml of air with 75% Fio2, which of the following term the nurse should document for the amount of air inhaled?$s$, $s$["minute volume", "residual volume", "tidal volume", "total lung capacity"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: tidal volume
TIDAL VOLUME (Vt) is the VOLUME of air INHALED (or exhaled) in ONE NORMAL BREATH — about 500 mL in an average adult (about 6–8 mL/kg). Here, the patient inhales 500 mL per breath, so this is the tidal volume. The FiO2 (75%) describes the OXYGEN CONCENTRATION of that air, not the volume. On a ventilator, tidal volume is set (in volume modes) or monitored (in pressure modes), aiming for 6–8 mL/kg of predicted body weight.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• minute volume — MINUTE VOLUME is tidal volume × respiratory rate (air moved per minute).
• residual volume — RESIDUAL VOLUME is the air left in the lungs after a maximal breath out.
• total lung capacity — TOTAL LUNG CAPACITY is the maximum volume of air the lungs can hold (about 6 L).

⭐ Key point: Low tidal volumes with a fast rate (rapid shallow breathing) are a sign of respiratory fatigue.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q55$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 31, $s$Intrapleural pressure is normally:$s$, $s$["Equal to atmospheric pressure", "Negative relative to atmospheric pressure", "Positive relative to atmospheric pressure", "Highly variable with no consistent pattern"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Negative relative to atmospheric pressure
The PLEURAL SPACE is the thin, fluid-filled gap between the two layers of pleura (visceral pleura covering the lung, parietal pleura lining the chest wall). The lungs naturally want to recoil inwards (elastic recoil), while the chest wall wants to spring outwards. These opposite pulls create a slight SUCTION in the pleural space, so INTRAPLEURAL PRESSURE is normally NEGATIVE relative to atmospheric pressure (about −4 to −5 cmH2O at rest, becoming more negative, about −8, during inspiration). This negative pressure keeps the lungs expanded against the chest wall.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Equal to atmospheric pressure — If intrapleural pressure became EQUAL to atmospheric pressure (e.g. when air enters through a wound), the lung would collapse — this is a pneumothorax.
• Positive relative to atmospheric pressure — POSITIVE intrapleural pressure occurs only during forced expiration (coughing) or in a tension pneumothorax, not normally.
• Highly variable with no consistent pattern — The pressure follows a consistent, predictable pattern with breathing.

⭐ Key point: A chest drain uses an underwater seal to let air out but not in, helping restore the negative pressure so the lung re-expands.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q1$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 32, $s$How many pairs of spinal nerves arise from the human spinal cord?$s$, $s$["12", "24", "31", "43"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 31
There are 31 PAIRS of spinal nerves, one pair leaving each segment of the spinal cord: 8 CERVICAL (C1–C8), 12 THORACIC (T1–T12), 5 LUMBAR (L1–L5), 5 SACRAL (S1–S5) and 1 COCCYGEAL (Co1). Total: 8 + 12 + 5 + 5 + 1 = 31. Each nerve carries both sensory and motor fibres. (Note there are 8 cervical nerves but only 7 cervical vertebrae.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 12 — 12 is the number of pairs of CRANIAL nerves (and also the number of thoracic spinal nerve pairs).
• 24 — 24 is not the correct number.
• 43 — 43 is the total number of nerve pairs (31 spinal + 12 cranial), not spinal nerves alone.

⭐ Key point: Knowing the spinal levels helps nurses assess spinal injuries: e.g. C3–C5 (diaphragm), C5 (biceps), T4 (nipple line sensation), T10 (umbilicus), L4 (knee jerk), S1 (ankle jerk).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q3$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 33, $s$The skin's appendages include all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Hair follicles", "Sweat glands", "Sebaceous glands", "Alveoli"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Alveoli
SKIN APPENDAGES (accessory structures) develop from the epidermis and include: HAIR FOLLICLES and hair, SWEAT GLANDS (eccrine and apocrine — temperature regulation), SEBACEOUS GLANDS (produce sebum to lubricate skin and hair), and NAILS. ALVEOLI are the tiny air sacs in the LUNGS where gas exchange takes place — they are NOT part of the skin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hair follicles — Hair follicles ARE skin appendages.
• Sweat glands — Sweat glands ARE skin appendages.
• Sebaceous glands — Sebaceous glands ARE skin appendages.

⭐ Key point: In burns, deep partial-thickness wounds can heal from surviving appendages (hair follicles, sweat glands), but full-thickness burns destroy them and usually need skin grafting.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q5$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 34, $s$Keratinocytes make up approximately what percentage of the epidermis?$s$, $s$["50%", "75%", "95%", "100%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 95%
KERATINOCYTES are the main cell type of the EPIDERMIS (outer skin layer), making up about 90–95% of its cells. They produce KERATIN, a tough protein that makes the skin waterproof and protects against injury, infection and water loss. Keratinocytes are made in the deepest layer (stratum basale) and move upward, becoming flattened dead cells at the surface (stratum corneum), which are shed. Other epidermal cells (melanocytes, Langerhans cells, Merkel cells) make up the remaining small percentage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50% — 50% underestimates their dominance.
• 75% — 75% underestimates.
• 100% — 100% is incorrect because other cell types are also present.

⭐ Key point: Damage to the epidermis (e.g. burns, pressure injuries, TEN) removes this protective barrier, causing fluid loss and infection risk.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q6$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 35, $s$A rightward shift of the oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve is caused by:$s$, $s$["Decreased temperature", "Increased 2,3-DPG, CO2, and temperature", "Decreased CO2", "Alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increased 2,3-DPG, CO2, and temperature
The oxygen-haemoglobin dissociation curve shows how much oxygen haemoglobin holds at different oxygen levels. A RIGHTWARD SHIFT means haemoglobin holds oxygen LESS tightly and RELEASES MORE OXYGEN to the tissues. It is caused by INCREASED 2,3-DPG, INCREASED CO2, INCREASED TEMPERATURE, and ACIDOSIS (low pH) — conditions found in active tissues that need more oxygen (memory aid: "CADET, face Right" — CO2, Acid, 2,3-DPG, Exercise, Temperature).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Decreased temperature — Decreased temperature shifts the curve to the LEFT.
• Decreased CO2 — Decreased CO2 shifts the curve to the LEFT.
• Alkalosis — Alkalosis shifts the curve to the LEFT.

⭐ Key point: Left shift (e.g. hypothermia, alkalosis, carbon monoxide, fetal haemoglobin) means haemoglobin holds oxygen tightly and releases less to tissues.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q9$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 36, $s$The short period that occurs during the transmission of impulses through the synapses is:$s$, $s$["Synaptic simulation", "Synaptic junction", "Synaptic cleft", "Synaptic delay"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Synaptic delay
SYNAPTIC DELAY is the short time (about 0.5 milliseconds) it takes for a nerve impulse to cross a chemical SYNAPSE. It includes the time for the neurotransmitter to be released from the first neuron, diffuse across the synaptic cleft, and bind to receptors on the next neuron. The more synapses in a pathway, the longer the total delay.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Synaptic simulation — "Synaptic simulation" is not a physiological term.
• Synaptic junction — The synaptic junction is the structure where two neurons meet, not a time period.
• Synaptic cleft — The synaptic cleft is the physical GAP between neurons, not the time taken.

⭐ Key point: Reflex arcs with fewer synapses (e.g. the knee-jerk reflex — one synapse) are very fast.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q12$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 37, $s$Mediastinal change seen in massive pleural effusion on a chest radiograph$s$, $s$["Shift of mediastinum to the site of effusion", "Central mediastinum", "Shift of the mediastinum to the opposite", "Tracheal shift to the site of lesion"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Shift of the mediastinum to the opposite
A MASSIVE PLEURAL EFFUSION fills the chest cavity with fluid, which PUSHES the MEDIASTINUM (heart, trachea) to the OPPOSITE side (away from the effusion).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shift of mediastinum to the site of effusion — Shift towards the effusion occurs in lung collapse (loss of volume), not effusion.
• Central mediastinum — A massive effusion causes shift, not a central mediastinum.
• Tracheal shift to the site of lesion — The trachea shifts AWAY from a massive effusion.

⭐ Key point: Mediastinal shift AWAY: massive effusion, tension pneumothorax. TOWARDS: collapse, pneumonectomy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q43$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 38, $s$When caring for a patient on Pressure Control Ventilation (PCV) for ARDS, the nurse should closely monitor:$s$, $s$["Only respiratory rate", "FiO2 alone", "Only the set pressure, since volume is irrelevant", "Exhaled tidal volume, since it varies with changes in lung compliance and resistance"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Exhaled tidal volume, since it varies with changes in lung compliance and resistance
In PRESSURE CONTROL VENTILATION, the ventilator delivers a set PRESSURE; the TIDAL VOLUME VARIES with lung COMPLIANCE and RESISTANCE. If the lungs become stiffer or the airway narrower, the tidal volume falls. So the nurse must closely monitor EXHALED TIDAL VOLUME.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only respiratory rate — Respiratory rate alone is not enough.
• FiO2 alone — FiO2 alone is not enough.
• Only the set pressure, since volume is irrelevant — Volume is very important.

⭐ Key point: Set appropriate low tidal volume alarms in pressure modes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q127$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 39, $s$A patient's spinal cord injury results in preserved diaphragmatic breathing but loss of intercostal muscle function and all function below the upper chest. This is most consistent with an injury at approximately which level?$s$, $s$["C1-C2", "C6-C8/T1", "T12-L1", "S2-S4"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: C6-C8/T1
The DIAPHRAGM is supplied by the phrenic nerve (C3–C5), so diaphragmatic breathing is PRESERVED in injuries below C5. INTERCOSTAL MUSCLES are supplied by thoracic nerves (T1–T12), so an injury around C6–C8/T1 causes LOSS OF INTERCOSTAL function and function below the upper chest.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• C1-C2 — C1–C2 injuries paralyse the diaphragm.
• T12-L1 — T12–L1 injuries preserve the intercostals.
• S2-S4 — S2–S4 injuries affect the bladder, bowel and sexual function.

⭐ Key point: Patients with lower cervical injuries breathe with the diaphragm alone and have a weak cough — monitor vital capacity.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q169$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 40, $s$A patient with pneumonia develops persistent fever, pleuritic chest pain, and a loculated pleural fluid collection with low pH and low glucose on pleural fluid analysis. This is most consistent with:$s$, $s$["Pulmonary embolism", "A simple transudative effusion from heart failure", "Chronic bronchitis", "Empyema (infected pleural effusion)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Empyema (infected pleural effusion)
An EMPYEMA is an INFECTED pleural effusion (pus in the pleural space), usually complicating PNEUMONIA. Features: PERSISTENT FEVER despite antibiotics, PLEURITIC CHEST PAIN, and a LOCULATED (walled-off, divided into pockets) fluid collection on imaging. Pleural fluid analysis shows LOW pH (below 7.2), LOW GLUCOSE (bacteria and white cells consume it), high LDH, and may show pus or organisms on Gram stain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pulmonary embolism — Pulmonary embolism may cause a small effusion, but not an infected, loculated collection with low pH and glucose.
• A simple transudative effusion from heart failure — Heart failure causes a TRANSUDATE with normal pH and glucose.
• Chronic bronchitis — Chronic bronchitis does not cause pleural collections.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV antibiotics AND drainage (chest tube); loculated empyema may need intrapleural fibrinolytics (tPA/DNase) or surgery (VATS).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q178$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 41, $s$Hypoxaemia caused by true intrapulmonary shunt (blood perfusing completely unventilated alveoli) typically:$s$, $s$["Corrects readily with modest increases in FiO2", "Occurs only with hypoventilation", "Responds poorly to supplemental oxygen because shunted blood never contacts ventilated alveoli", "Is unaffected by PEEP"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Responds poorly to supplemental oxygen because shunted blood never contacts ventilated alveoli
In TRUE SHUNT, blood passes through areas of lung where the alveoli are completely UNVENTILATED (collapsed or filled with fluid). This blood never contacts oxygen, so it returns to the heart deoxygenated. Increasing FiO2 does NOT help much, because the extra oxygen never reaches these alveoli. Treatment is to RE-OPEN the alveoli (PEEP, recruitment manoeuvres, prone positioning).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Corrects readily with modest increases in FiO2 — Hypoxia from low V/Q (partial ventilation) responds to oxygen; true shunt does not.
• Occurs only with hypoventilation — Shunt is not caused by hypoventilation.
• Is unaffected by PEEP — PEEP DOES affect shunt by re-opening alveoli.

⭐ Key point: Shunt is the main cause of hypoxia in ARDS, pneumonia and atelectasis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q136$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 42, $s$A patient with chronic kidney disease receiving erythropoietin therapy for anaemia should also have which nutrient supplemented to ensure the therapy is effective?$s$, $s$["Calcium only", "Vitamin A", "Iron, since adequate iron stores are needed for erythropoietin to be effective", "Vitamin C only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Iron, since adequate iron stores are needed for erythropoietin to be effective
In CKD, the kidneys make too little ERYTHROPOIETIN (EPO) — the hormone that stimulates the bone marrow to produce red blood cells — causing anaemia. EPO therapy (e.g. epoetin) stimulates red cell production, but the marrow needs IRON to make haemoglobin. If iron stores are low, EPO will NOT work well ("EPO resistance"). So IRON is supplemented (often IV iron in dialysis patients), guided by ferritin and transferrin saturation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Calcium only — Calcium is important in CKD bone disease, but it does not make EPO effective.
• Vitamin A — Vitamin A is not needed for EPO therapy.
• Vitamin C only — Vitamin C may help iron absorption, but iron itself is the essential supplement.

⭐ Key point: Other causes of poor EPO response: infection, inflammation, B12 or folate deficiency, hyperparathyroidism, and inadequate dialysis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q148$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 43, $s$A patient with severe insensible fluid losses from high fever who produces a small volume of urine with high specific gravity best demonstrates:$s$, $s$["Diabetes insipidus", "Acute tubular necrosis", "Appropriate physiological water conservation via ADH-mediated concentration of urine", "A state of overhydration"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Appropriate physiological water conservation via ADH-mediated concentration of urine
With HIGH FEVER, the body loses fluid through sweating and breathing (insensible losses). The body responds by releasing ADH, which makes the kidneys CONSERVE WATER, producing a SMALL VOLUME of CONCENTRATED URINE (high specific gravity). This is an APPROPRIATE PHYSIOLOGICAL response.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diabetes insipidus — Diabetes insipidus causes large volumes of dilute urine.
• Acute tubular necrosis — ATN causes dilute urine.
• A state of overhydration — Overhydration causes large volumes of dilute urine.

⭐ Key point: Replace insensible losses in febrile patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q191$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 44, $s$The diaphragm, the main muscle of breathing, is supplied by the:$s$, $s$["Brachial plexus", "Phrenic nerve", "Sciatic nerve", "Vagus nerve"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Phrenic nerve
The DIAPHRAGM, the main muscle of breathing, is supplied by the PHRENIC NERVE, which arises from the cervical spinal nerves C3, C4 and C5. When the diaphragm contracts, it moves down, enlarging the chest and drawing air into the lungs. Injury to the spinal cord at or above C3–C5 can paralyse the diaphragm, requiring long-term ventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Brachial plexus — The brachial plexus (C5–T1) supplies the arm.
• Sciatic nerve — The sciatic nerve supplies the leg.
• Vagus nerve — The vagus nerve supplies the heart, lungs and gut (autonomic), not the diaphragm's motor function.

⭐ Key point: Memory aid: "C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive." Phrenic nerve injury can occur after cardiac surgery or central line insertion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q198$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 45, $s$The volume of air remaining in the lungs after a maximal forced expiration is the:$s$, $s$["Inspiratory reserve volume", "Residual volume", "Tidal volume", "Expiratory reserve volume"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Residual volume
RESIDUAL VOLUME (RV) is the volume of air REMAINING in the lungs after a MAXIMAL FORCED EXPIRATION (about 1.2 L in an average adult). It cannot be breathed out and keeps the alveoli from collapsing completely. It cannot be measured by simple spirometry (needs body plethysmography or gas dilution). In COPD, residual volume is increased (air trapping).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Inspiratory reserve volume — Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV) is the extra air that can be breathed IN after a normal inspiration (about 3 L).
• Tidal volume — Tidal volume (TV) is the volume of a normal breath (about 500 mL).
• Expiratory reserve volume — Expiratory reserve volume (ERV) is the extra air that can be breathed OUT after a normal expiration (about 1.1 L).

⭐ Key point: Functional residual capacity (FRC) = ERV + RV; total lung capacity = IRV + TV + ERV + RV.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q199 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 46, $s$Which of the following is NOT part of the conducting zone of the respiratory system?$s$, $s$["Bronchi", "Terminal bronchioles", "Trachea", "Alveoli"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Alveoli
The respiratory system has two zones. The CONDUCTING ZONE — nose, pharynx, larynx, TRACHEA, BRONCHI, bronchioles and TERMINAL BRONCHIOLES — conducts, warms, humidifies and filters air but does NOT take part in gas exchange (it forms the anatomical DEAD SPACE, about 150 mL). The RESPIRATORY ZONE — respiratory bronchioles, alveolar ducts, alveolar sacs and ALVEOLI — is where GAS EXCHANGE happens. Alveoli are NOT part of the conducting zone.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bronchi — Bronchi are part of the conducting zone.
• Terminal bronchioles — Terminal bronchioles are the last part of the conducting zone.
• Trachea — The trachea is part of the conducting zone.

⭐ Key point: The lungs contain about 300–500 million alveoli, giving a huge surface area (about 70 m²) for gas exchange.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q200$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 47, $s$A ventilated patient has a PaCO₂ of 60 mmHg at a set rate of 14 breaths/min. Keeping the tidal volume the same, what rate is needed to bring PaCO₂ to 40 mmHg?$s$, $s$["18 breaths/min", "21 breaths/min", "28 breaths/min", "9 breaths/min"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 21 breaths/min
PaCO2 is inversely proportional to ALVEOLAR VENTILATION. If tidal volume stays the same, PaCO2 × respiratory rate stays roughly constant. Formula: New rate = (Current PaCO2 × Current rate) ÷ Desired PaCO2. Here: (60 × 14) ÷ 40 = 840 ÷ 40 = 21 breaths/min.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 18 breaths/min — 18 breaths/min is not enough; it would give a PaCO2 of about 47 mmHg (840 ÷ 18).
• 28 breaths/min — 28 breaths/min is too much; it would give a PaCO2 of 30 mmHg (840 ÷ 28) — hyperventilation.
• 9 breaths/min — 9 breaths/min would RAISE the PaCO2 to about 93 mmHg (840 ÷ 9).

⭐ Key point: When increasing the rate, watch for air trapping (auto-PEEP), especially in patients with asthma or COPD.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q15$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 48, $s$After a night of heavy drinking and forceful vomiting, a man has severe retrosternal chest pain. There is a crackling feeling under the skin of the neck, a 'crunching' sound with each heartbeat, and a left pleural effusion. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Perforated duodenal ulcer", "Acute inferior myocardial infarction", "Mallory–Weiss tear (mucosal tear)", "Boerhaave syndrome (oesophageal rupture)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Boerhaave syndrome (oesophageal rupture)
BOERHAAVE SYNDROME is a full-thickness RUPTURE of the oesophagus, usually after FORCEFUL VOMITING (often after heavy drinking). Features: SEVERE RETROSTERNAL CHEST PAIN, SURGICAL EMPHYSEMA (crackling under the skin of the neck — air in the tissues), a "CRUNCHING" sound with each heartbeat (Hamman's sign — air in the mediastinum), and a LEFT PLEURAL EFFUSION.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Perforated duodenal ulcer — A perforated duodenal ulcer causes abdominal pain and air under the diaphragm.
• Acute inferior myocardial infarction — MI doesn't cause surgical emphysema.
• Mallory–Weiss tear (mucosal tear) — A Mallory-Weiss tear is a superficial tear causing vomiting of blood, without surgical emphysema.

⭐ Key point: Boerhaave syndrome is a surgical emergency with high mortality. Nil by mouth, IV antibiotics, and urgent surgical repair.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q129$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 49, $s$The chemical injury in Mendelson's syndrome primarily results from which mechanism?$s$, $s$["Mechanical obstruction of the trachea by food particles alone", "Immune complex deposition in the pulmonary vascular bed", "Acid damage to the alveolar-capillary membrane, causing leaky oedema", "Pulmonary arterial vasospasm triggered by the low pH"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acid damage to the alveolar-capillary membrane, causing leaky oedema
MENDELSON'S SYNDROME is a CHEMICAL PNEUMONITIS caused by inhaling ACIDIC gastric contents (especially pH below 2.5 and volume over 25 mL). The acid DAMAGES THE ALVEOLAR-CAPILLARY MEMBRANE, making it LEAKY. Fluid and protein flood the alveoli (non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema), causing hypoxia, wheeze and bilateral infiltrates — similar to ARDS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mechanical obstruction of the trachea by food particles alone — Solid food particles can block airways, but Mendelson's is mainly a chemical (acid) injury.
• Immune complex deposition in the pulmonary vascular bed — It is not an immune complex disease.
• Pulmonary arterial vasospasm triggered by the low pH — Vasospasm is not the main mechanism.

⭐ Key point: Mendelson's is chemical injury; bacterial aspiration pneumonia may follow later.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q181$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 50, $s$Emphysema on a chest X-ray typically shows:$s$, $s$["Hyperinflation, flat diaphragms and increased lucency", "Small lungs with raised diaphragms on both sides", "Bilateral fluffy alveolar shadowing with large heart", "A single dense lobar consolidation with air bronchogram"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hyperinflation, flat diaphragms and increased lucency
In EMPHYSEMA, the alveolar walls are destroyed, trapping air. The chest X-ray shows HYPERINFLATION (more than 6 anterior ribs visible above the diaphragm), FLAT DIAPHRAGMS, INCREASED LUCENCY (dark lungs with few vessels), a narrow vertical heart, and sometimes bullae (large air spaces).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Small lungs with raised diaphragms on both sides — Small lungs with raised diaphragms suggest fibrosis or reduced lung volume.
• Bilateral fluffy alveolar shadowing with large heart — Bilateral fluffy shadows with a big heart suggest pulmonary oedema.
• A single dense lobar consolidation with air bronchogram — A single dense consolidation suggests pneumonia.

⭐ Key point: Bullae can rupture, causing a pneumothorax in COPD patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q17$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 51, $s$Toxic epidermal necrolysis involves skin detachment of more than:$s$, $s$["1% of body surface area", "5% of body surface area", "10% of body surface area", "30% of body surface area"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 30% of body surface area
In TOXIC EPIDERMAL NECROLYSIS (TEN), skin detachment affects MORE THAN 30% of the body surface area. Stevens–Johnson syndrome (SJS) is less than 10%, and the SJS/TEN overlap is 10–30%.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1% of body surface area — More than 1% is far too low.
• 5% of body surface area — More than 5% is too low.
• 10% of body surface area — More than 10% is the start of the overlap category, not TEN.

⭐ Key point: TEN has a high mortality. Treat it in a burns unit or ICU.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q58$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 52, $s$Manual hyperinflation in ventilated patients aims to:$s$, $s$["Replace the need for any suctioning", "Re-expand collapsed alveoli and move secretions", "Reduce oxygenation to rest the lungs", "Increase the depth of patient sedation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Re-expand collapsed alveoli and move secretions
MANUAL HYPERINFLATION (MHI) uses a manual bag to give slow, deep breaths followed by a quick release. It aims to RE-EXPAND COLLAPSED ALVEOLI (improve lung volume and oxygenation) and MOVE SECRETIONS towards the large airways, so they can be suctioned out.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Replace the need for any suctioning — MHI helps move secretions, which are then removed by suctioning; it does not replace suctioning.
• Reduce oxygenation to rest the lungs — MHI aims to improve oxygenation, not reduce it.
• Increase the depth of patient sedation — MHI does not affect sedation.

⭐ Key point: Avoid MHI in patients with high PEEP requirements (disconnection causes collapse), pneumothorax, severe bronchospasm or haemodynamic instability.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q191$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 53, $s$The layer of skin containing blood vessels, nerves and sweat glands is the:$s$, $s$["Epidermis", "Stratum corneum", "Dermis", "Hypodermis only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dermis
The skin has three layers. The EPIDERMIS is the outer protective layer with no blood vessels. The DERMIS is the middle layer containing blood vessels, nerves, sweat glands, hair follicles and sebaceous glands. The HYPODERMIS (subcutaneous layer) is fat and connective tissue below it. Because the dermis contains the vessels and nerves, deep burns into the dermis are painful and bleed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Epidermis — The epidermis has no blood vessels; it gets nutrients from the dermis.
• Stratum corneum — The stratum corneum is the outermost dead layer of the epidermis.
• Hypodermis only — The hypodermis contains fat and larger vessels, but sweat glands and most nerve endings are in the dermis.

⭐ Key point: Burn depth: superficial (epidermis only), partial thickness (into the dermis), full thickness (through the dermis; painless because the nerves are destroyed).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q90$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 54, $s$A haemothorax on an erect chest X-ray shows:$s$, $s$["Hyperinflation with flat diaphragms", "A meniscus opacity blunting the costophrenic angle", "A visible lung edge with no markings beyond it", "Free air under the right hemidiaphragm"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A meniscus opacity blunting the costophrenic angle
A haemothorax is blood in the pleural space. On an ERECT chest X-ray, the fluid settles at the bottom and appears as a WHITE (opaque) area that BLUNTS the costophrenic angle, with a curved upper edge rising at the sides (MENISCUS sign). If air is also present (haemopneumothorax), there is a straight air–fluid level instead.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperinflation with flat diaphragms — Hyperinflation with flat diaphragms suggests COPD or asthma.
• A visible lung edge with no markings beyond it — A visible lung edge with no markings beyond is a pneumothorax.
• Free air under the right hemidiaphragm — Free air under the diaphragm suggests a perforated bowel.

⭐ Key point: Massive haemothorax (more than 1500 mL) causes shock and needs a chest drain, blood transfusion and possibly thoracotomy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q127$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 55, $s$Free air under the diaphragm on erect chest X-ray suggests:$s$, $s$["Right lower lobe pneumonia", "Perforated hollow viscus", "Amoebic liver abscess", "Right-sided pleural effusion"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Perforated hollow viscus
Free air under the diaphragm (pneumoperitoneum) on an ERECT chest X-ray appears as a dark crescent between the diaphragm and the liver (or stomach). It means air has escaped from a PERFORATED HOLLOW VISCUS: a perforated peptic ulcer, bowel or appendix. It is a surgical emergency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right lower lobe pneumonia — Pneumonia appears in the lung, above the diaphragm.
• Amoebic liver abscess — A liver abscess appears as an area in the liver, not free air.
• Right-sided pleural effusion — Pleural effusion is fluid above the diaphragm.

⭐ Key point: Free air can also be normal for a few days after abdominal surgery (laparotomy or laparoscopy).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q130$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 56, $s$A 'white-out' of one hemithorax with mediastinal shift away suggests:$s$, $s$["Tension pneumothorax", "A large pleural effusion", "Lobar collapse", "Previous pneumonectomy"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A large pleural effusion
A large pleural effusion fills one side of the chest with fluid, making it completely WHITE on X-ray. The large volume PUSHES the mediastinum and trachea AWAY to the opposite side.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tension pneumothorax — Tension pneumothorax shows a BLACK (not white) hemithorax with shift away.
• Lobar collapse — Lobar collapse PULLS the mediastinum TOWARDS the white side.
• Previous pneumonectomy — After pneumonectomy, the mediastinum shifts TOWARDS the operated side.

⭐ Key point: White-out: shift AWAY = effusion. Shift TOWARDS = collapse or pneumonectomy. No shift = consolidation (or effusion with collapse).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q136$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 57, $s$Aldosterone is secreted from which zone of the adrenal cortex?$s$, $s$["Zona glomerulosa", "Zona reticularis", "Adrenal medulla", "Zona fasciculata"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Zona glomerulosa
The adrenal cortex has three layers. The outermost layer, the ZONA GLOMERULOSA, makes mineralocorticoids such as ALDOSTERONE (which keeps sodium and water and excretes potassium). Remember "GFR" from outside in: Glomerulosa = salt (aldosterone), Fasciculata = sugar (cortisol), Reticularis = sex hormones (androgens).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Zona reticularis — The zona reticularis makes androgens.
• Adrenal medulla — The adrenal medulla makes adrenaline and noradrenaline.
• Zona fasciculata — The zona fasciculata makes cortisol.

⭐ Key point: Aldosterone is controlled mainly by the renin-angiotensin system and by potassium levels.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q16$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 58, $s$Fasciculations may develop with disease of the:$s$, $s$["Lateral column of the spinal cord", "Posterior horn of the spinal cord", "Anterior horn of the spinal cord", "Internal capsule"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Anterior horn of the spinal cord
FASCICULATIONS are small, visible twitches of muscle under the skin, caused by spontaneous firing of a motor unit. They are a sign of LOWER MOTOR NEURONE disease, especially disease of the ANTERIOR HORN cells of the spinal cord, where the motor neurones' cell bodies are (for example motor neurone disease or polio).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lateral column of the spinal cord — The lateral column carries upper motor neurone tracts.
• Posterior horn of the spinal cord — The posterior horn is sensory.
• Internal capsule — The internal capsule carries upper motor neurone fibres in the brain.

⭐ Key point: Lower motor neurone signs: weakness, wasting, fasciculations, low tone, and reduced or absent reflexes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q61$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 59, $s$The gag reflex primarily depends on which cranial nerve(s)?$s$, $s$["Cranial nerve VII only", "Cranial nerves I and II", "Cranial nerves IX and X", "Cranial nerve XII only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cranial nerves IX and X
The gag reflex protects the airway. Touching the back of the throat sends a SENSORY signal through the GLOSSOPHARYNGEAL nerve (CN IX) to the brainstem. The MOTOR response (the throat muscles contracting) comes through the VAGUS nerve (CN X). Both are needed, and both come from the medulla.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cranial nerve VII only — CN VII (facial) controls facial expression and taste at the front of the tongue.
• Cranial nerves I and II — CN I (smell) and CN II (sight) aren't involved.
• Cranial nerve XII only — CN XII (hypoglossal) moves the tongue, but isn't the main gag nerve.

⭐ Key point: An absent gag reflex in an unconscious patient means a high risk of aspiration. It is also part of brainstem death testing.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q1$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 60, $s$Which of the following is NOT a hormone?$s$, $s$["Renin", "Atrial natriuretic peptide", "Prolactin", "Thymosin"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Renin
RENIN is an ENZYME, not a hormone. It is released by the kidney's juxtaglomerular cells and converts angiotensinogen into angiotensin I, which then becomes angiotensin II (a hormone). All the other options are hormones.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Atrial natriuretic peptide — Atrial natriuretic peptide IS a hormone, from the heart's atria; it makes the kidneys excrete sodium.
• Prolactin — Prolactin IS a hormone, from the anterior pituitary; it controls milk production.
• Thymosin — Thymosin IS a hormone, from the thymus; it helps develop T-cells.

⭐ Key point: RAAS: renin (enzyme) → angiotensin I → angiotensin II (via ACE) → aldosterone and vasoconstriction.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q6$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 61, $s$Which nerve innervates the diaphragm?$s$, $s$["Median nerve", "Phrenic nerve", "Radial nerve", "Vagus nerve"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Phrenic nerve
The PHRENIC nerve (from spinal roots C3, C4 and C5) supplies the DIAPHRAGM, the main muscle of breathing. "C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive." Spinal cord injury at or above C3–C5 can paralyse the diaphragm and stop breathing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Median nerve — The median nerve supplies the forearm and hand.
• Radial nerve — The radial nerve supplies the back of the arm and hand.
• Vagus nerve — The vagus nerve supplies the heart, lungs and gut (autonomic), not the diaphragm muscle.

⭐ Key point: Phrenic nerve injury (after neck or heart surgery) causes a raised hemidiaphragm on X-ray.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q9$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 62, $s$Concerning pressure-control versus volume-control ventilation:$s$, $s$["Volume control will provide a set tidal volume.", "Lung compliance is irrelevant with pressure control.", "Paediatric ventilation never uses pressure control ventilation.", "Pressure control will provide a set tidal volume."]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Volume control will provide a set tidal volume.
VOLUME-CONTROL ventilation delivers a SET TIDAL VOLUME with each breath. The pressure needed to deliver it changes with lung stiffness (compliance) and airway resistance. In pressure control, the PRESSURE is set and the tidal volume varies with compliance.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lung compliance is irrelevant with pressure control. — Lung compliance matters greatly in pressure control (it determines the tidal volume).
• Paediatric ventilation never uses pressure control ventilation. — Pressure control IS commonly used in paediatric ventilation.
• Pressure control will provide a set tidal volume. — Pressure control does NOT guarantee a set tidal volume.

⭐ Key point: Volume control: watch the peak and plateau pressures. Pressure control: watch the tidal volume and minute volume.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q36$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 63, $s$Which of the following is a meningeal sign?$s$, $s$["Babinski", "Romberg", "Kernig", "Lasègue"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Kernig
KERNIG's sign is a MENINGEAL sign. With the patient lying flat and the hip bent to 90°, straightening the knee causes pain or resistance in the back of the thigh and the back. It is caused by inflamed meninges and nerve roots being stretched. It is seen in meningitis and subarachnoid haemorrhage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Babinski — Babinski is an upper motor neurone sign.
• Romberg — Romberg tests balance (proprioception).
• Lasègue — Lasègue (straight leg raise) tests sciatic nerve or lumbar root irritation.

⭐ Key point: Other meningeal signs: neck stiffness, Brudzinski's sign, photophobia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q65$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 64, $s$Respiratory distress syndrome in a 28-week preterm baby is mainly due to:$s$, $s$["Meconium aspiration", "Congenital heart disease", "Surfactant deficiency", "Excess surfactant"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Surfactant deficiency
In respiratory distress syndrome (RDS), preterm babies (especially below 32–34 weeks) don't make enough SURFACTANT, the substance that keeps the alveoli open. Without it, the alveoli collapse, causing breathing difficulty, grunting, recession and hypoxia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Meconium aspiration — Meconium aspiration affects term or post-term babies.
• Congenital heart disease — Congenital heart disease is a different cause of cyanosis.
• Excess surfactant — RDS is due to too LITTLE surfactant, not too much.

⭐ Key point: Prevent and treat RDS with antenatal steroids, CPAP, and surfactant given via the ETT.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q139$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 65, $s$The Haldane effect describes:$s$, $s$["CO₂ diffusing 20 times faster than oxygen", "Hypoxic vasoconstriction in poorly ventilated lung", "Deoxygenated haemoglobin carrying more CO₂ than oxygenated Hb", "Oxygen release from Hb increasing as pH falls"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Deoxygenated haemoglobin carrying more CO₂ than oxygenated Hb
The HALDANE effect: DEOXYGENATED haemoglobin carries MORE CO₂ than oxygenated haemoglobin. In the tissues, haemoglobin releases oxygen and picks up more CO₂. In the lungs, haemoglobin picks up oxygen and releases CO₂. In COPD patients given too much oxygen, haemoglobin releases its CO₂, which partly explains why their CO₂ can rise.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CO₂ diffusing 20 times faster than oxygen — CO₂ diffusing faster than oxygen (about 20 times) is a fact about diffusion, not the Haldane effect.
• Hypoxic vasoconstriction in poorly ventilated lung — Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction is a different mechanism.
• Oxygen release from Hb increasing as pH falls — Oxygen release from haemoglobin as pH falls is the BOHR effect.

⭐ Key point: Bohr effect = acid helps haemoglobin release O₂. Haldane effect = O₂ helps haemoglobin release CO₂.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q20$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 66, $s$This occurs when air enters the pleura but cannot escape, resulting in one-way valve effect, thus produces shift in the mediastinum.$s$, $s$["Traumatic pneumothorax", "Tension pneumothorax", "Primary spontaneous pneumothorax", "Secondary spontaneous pneumothorax"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tension pneumothorax
A TENSION PNEUMOTHORAX happens when air enters the pleural space but CAN'T ESCAPE (a one-way valve effect). Pressure builds up with each breath, collapsing the lung and SHIFTING the MEDIASTINUM (heart, trachea and great vessels) to the opposite side. This reduces blood return to the heart and causes shock. It is an emergency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Traumatic pneumothorax — A traumatic pneumothorax is caused by injury but isn't necessarily under tension.
• Primary spontaneous pneumothorax — A primary spontaneous pneumothorax occurs in healthy people (often tall, thin young men) and usually isn't tension.
• Secondary spontaneous pneumothorax — A secondary spontaneous pneumothorax occurs with lung disease (such as COPD) and isn't necessarily tension.

⭐ Key point: Signs: distress, absent breath sounds, hyper-resonance, tracheal deviation away, raised JVP, low BP. Decompress immediately.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q33$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 67, $s$In pressure-controlled ventilation, if lung compliance falls the nurse expects:$s$, $s$["The respiratory rate to fall automatically", "Delivered tidal volume to rise automatically", "Delivered tidal volume to fall at the same set pressure", "Peak pressure to rise above the set level"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Delivered tidal volume to fall at the same set pressure
In PRESSURE-CONTROLLED ventilation, the PRESSURE is FIXED. The tidal volume delivered depends on lung compliance. If compliance FALLS (stiffer lungs, such as with ARDS, pneumothorax or abdominal distension), the same pressure delivers LESS VOLUME. Watch for low tidal volume and low minute volume alarms.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The respiratory rate to fall automatically — The respiratory rate doesn't change automatically.
• Delivered tidal volume to rise automatically — Tidal volume FALLS, not rises.
• Peak pressure to rise above the set level — Peak pressure stays at the set level in pressure control.

⭐ Key point: Pressure control: watch the volumes. Volume control: watch the pressures.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q40$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A1 · Applied Anatomy & Physiology$s$, false, 68, $s$The presence of dysphagia suggests damage to cranial nerves:$s$, $s$["V-VII", "III-VI", "IX-X", "VII-XI"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IX-X
DYSPHAGIA (difficulty swallowing) suggests damage to the GLOSSOPHARYNGEAL (CN IX) and VAGUS (CN X) nerves, which supply the throat (pharynx) and larynx muscles and control swallowing and the gag reflex.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• V-VII — CN V–VII control chewing (V) and facial movement (VII), not mainly swallowing.
• III-VI — CN III–VI control eye movements.
• VII-XI — CN VII–XI is a broad group; the key swallowing nerves are IX and X.

⭐ Key point: Patients with dysphagia are at high risk of aspiration. Do a swallow assessment before giving oral food, fluids or drugs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q70$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 69, $s$What is the normal range for HCO3-?$s$, $s$["18-22 mEq/L", "22-26 mEq/L", "26-30 mEq/L", "30-34 mEq/L"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 22-26 mEq/L
Normal serum bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻) is 22–26 mEq/L (mmol/L). Bicarbonate is the main base (alkali) buffer in the blood, and the kidneys control it. Below 22 = metabolic acidosis (or compensation for respiratory alkalosis). Above 26 = metabolic alkalosis (or compensation for respiratory acidosis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 18-22 mEq/L — 18–22 is low (acidosis range).
• 26-30 mEq/L — 26–30 is high (alkalosis or compensation).
• 30-34 mEq/L — 30–34 is clearly raised, as in vomiting or chronic CO₂ retention with compensation.

⭐ Key point: ABG normals: pH 7.35–7.45, PaCO₂ 35–45 mmHg, HCO₃⁻ 22–26 mmol/L. Remember "the lungs control CO₂ (acid), the kidneys control HCO₃⁻ (base)".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q16$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 70, $s$An ABG result of pH 7.51, PaCO2 35 mmHg, and HCO3- 30 mEq/L indicates:$s$, $s$["Metabolic acidosis", "Respiratory alkalosis", "Respiratory acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic alkalosis
Step 1: pH 7.51 is above 7.45, so the patient has alkalosis. Step 2: PaCO₂ 35 is normal, so the lungs are not the cause. Step 3: HCO₃⁻ 30 is above 26, so there is too much base. The bicarbonate moves in the same direction as the pH (both up), which means a METABOLIC cause: uncompensated metabolic alkalosis. ROME: Metabolic = Equal (pH and HCO₃⁻ move the same way).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would need a LOW pH and a LOW HCO₃⁻.
• Respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would need a high pH with a LOW PaCO₂ (below 35, from hyperventilation).
• Respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would need a LOW pH with a HIGH PaCO₂.

⭐ Key point: Causes of metabolic alkalosis: vomiting or NG suction (loss of stomach acid), diuretics, low potassium and too much bicarbonate. Treat the cause and replace chloride and potassium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q17 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 71, $s$In a patient with respiratory failure, the nurse correctly interprets a high PaCO₂ value and a low pH from an ABG. This indicates:$s$, $s$["Metabolic alkalosis", "Respiratory alkalosis", "Metabolic acidosis", "Respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory acidosis
A LOW pH means acidosis. A HIGH PaCO₂ (above 45 mmHg) means CO₂, the respiratory acid, is building up. pH and PaCO₂ move in OPPOSITE directions, so the cause is respiratory. Together they mean respiratory acidosis, which is the typical result of hypoventilation in respiratory failure (type 2): COPD, opioid overdose, exhaustion, neuromuscular weakness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would show a HIGH pH and a HIGH HCO₃⁻.
• Respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would show a HIGH pH and a LOW PaCO₂ (hyperventilation).
• Metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would show a low pH but a LOW HCO₃⁻ as the main change, with a normal or low PaCO₂.

⭐ Key point: Respiratory acidosis is treated by improving ventilation: open the airway, reverse sedatives (naloxone), use NIV (BiPAP), or intubate and ventilate. The kidneys raise HCO₃⁻ to compensate over 2–5 days.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q18$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 72, $s$Winter's formula is used to determine the expected respiratory compensation for:$s$, $s$["Respiratory acidosis", "A primary metabolic acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis", "Respiratory alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A primary metabolic acidosis
WINTER'S FORMULA tells you what the PaCO2 SHOULD be when the lungs are properly compensating for a PRIMARY METABOLIC ACIDOSIS: Expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × HCO3⁻) + 8 ± 2. Example: in DKA with HCO3 of 10 → expected PaCO2 = 15 + 8 = 23 (range 21–25). If the measured PaCO2 matches, compensation is appropriate. If PaCO2 is HIGHER than expected, there is an extra RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (patient tiring, not breathing off enough CO2 — danger sign). If it is LOWER, there is an extra RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS. The body compensates by deep, fast Kussmaul breathing to blow off CO2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory acidosis — In RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS the primary problem is CO2; compensation is by the KIDNEYS (keeping HCO3), so Winter's formula does not apply.
• Metabolic alkalosis — METABOLIC ALKALOSIS uses a different estimate (PaCO2 rises about 0.7 × rise in HCO3), not Winter's formula.
• Respiratory alkalosis — RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS is a primary CO2 problem compensated by the kidneys; Winter's formula is not used.

⭐ Key point: Winter's formula = expected PaCO2 in METABOLIC ACIDOSIS = 1.5 × HCO3 + 8 ± 2.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q17$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 73, $s$The normal pH range for arterial blood is:$s$, $s$["7.0–7.2", "7.35–7.45", "7.5–7.6", "6.8–7.0"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 7.35–7.45
Normal ARTERIAL blood pH is 7.35–7.45. Below 7.35 is ACIDAEMIA (acidosis); above 7.45 is ALKALAEMIA (alkalosis). The body keeps pH in this narrow range because enzymes, heart rhythm and brain function depend on it. Three systems defend pH: chemical buffers (seconds — bicarbonate, proteins, haemoglobin), the LUNGS (minutes — change CO2 by breathing faster or slower), and the KIDNEYS (hours to days — excrete H⁺, keep or lose HCO3⁻). Other normal ABG values: PaCO2 35–45 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 22–26 mmol/L, PaO2 80–100 mmHg, base excess −2 to +2. A pH below 6.8 or above 7.8 is usually not compatible with life.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 7.0–7.2 — 7.0–7.2 is SEVERE ACIDOSIS, seen in conditions like DKA or cardiac arrest — not normal.
• 7.5–7.6 — 7.5–7.6 is ALKALOSIS (e.g. hyperventilation or severe vomiting) — not normal.
• 6.8–7.0 — 6.8–7.0 is life-threatening acidosis, at the edge of survival.

⭐ Key point: Normal arterial pH = 7.35–7.45. PaCO2 35–45; HCO3 22–26.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q19$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 74, $s$The normal anion gap range is:$s$, $s$["0–5mmol/L", "8–18mmol/L", "30–50mmol/L", "100–150mmol/L"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 8–18mmol/L
The ANION GAP (AG) is a calculated value: AG = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻). It represents the "unmeasured" negative ions in the blood (mainly albumin, plus phosphate, sulphate and organic acids). The commonly quoted normal range is about 8–12 (some labs up to 16–18 mmol/L, or 10–20 if potassium is added) — so the best option here is 8–18. A HIGH anion gap metabolic acidosis means extra acid has been added, memory aid "MUDPILES": Methanol, Uraemia, DKA, Propylene glycol/Paraldehyde, Isoniazid/Iron, Lactic acidosis, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates. A NORMAL anion gap acidosis is from bicarbonate loss (diarrhoea, renal tubular acidosis) and is usually hyperchloraemic. Low albumin lowers the AG, so correct for it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0–5mmol/L — 0–5 mmol/L is too LOW; this is seen with very low albumin or lab error, not normal.
• 30–50mmol/L — 30–50 mmol/L is a very HIGH gap, e.g. severe lactic acidosis or ketoacidosis.
• 100–150mmol/L — 100–150 mmol/L is impossible as an anion gap; it is close to the serum sodium value itself.

⭐ Key point: Anion gap = Na − (Cl + HCO3); normal ≈ 8–18 mmol/L. High AG acidosis = "MUDPILES".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q20$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 75, $s$A rise in PaCO2 causing acidosis indicates the disorder is:$s$, $s$["Metabolic", "Respiratory", "Neither respiratory nor metabolic", "Always compensated"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory
To decide whether an acid–base problem is RESPIRATORY or METABOLIC, look at which value moved in the SAME direction as the pH problem. CO2 acts like an ACID in the blood (CO2 + H2O → H2CO3 → H⁺ + HCO3⁻). So if PaCO2 RISES (above 45 mmHg) and the pH FALLS (below 7.35), the cause is the LUNGS not removing enough CO2 — RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS. Causes: hypoventilation from opioid overdose, COPD exacerbation, exhaustion, neuromuscular weakness, head injury. Treatment improves ventilation (stimulate, reverse opioids, NIV/BiPAP, intubation). If the HCO3 had fallen to cause acidosis, it would be metabolic.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic — METABOLIC disorders are caused by changes in HCO3⁻ (bicarbonate), not by a rise in CO2.
• Neither respiratory nor metabolic — "Neither" is wrong — a primary change in PaCO2 is by definition a respiratory disorder.
• Always compensated — It is NOT "always compensated"; acute respiratory acidosis is often uncompensated because the kidneys need days to retain bicarbonate.

⭐ Key point: High PaCO2 + low pH = RESPIRATORY acidosis (hypoventilation). Low HCO3 + low pH = metabolic acidosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q22$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 76, $s$A mother is admitted to the emergency department following complaints of fever and chills. The nurse on duty took her vital signs and noted the following: Temp = 100 °F; apical pulse = 95; respiration = 20 and deep. Measurement of arterial blood gas shows pH 7.37, PaO2 90 mm Hg, PaCO2 40 mm Hg, and HCO3 24 mmol/L. What is your assessment?$s$, $s$["Hypothermia", "Hypothermia and Respiratory Alkalosis", "Hyperthermia", "Hyperthermia and Respiratory Alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hyperthermia
Look at each value. TEMPERATURE 100 °F = about 37.8 °C (°C = (°F − 32) × 5/9). Normal is about 97.7–99.5 °F (36.5–37.5 °C), so 100 °F is a raised temperature — mild HYPERTHERMIA/fever, fitting the "fever and chills" complaint. Pulse 95 and respiration 20 are at the upper end of normal (fever raises both). Now the ABG: pH 7.37 (normal 7.35–7.45), PaCO2 40 (normal 35–45), HCO3 24 (normal 22–26), PaO2 90 (normal 80–100) — ALL NORMAL. So there is no acid–base disorder. The correct assessment is HYPERTHERMIA only. Exam tip: always check every ABG value against its range before naming a disorder; do not guess alkalosis just because breathing is "deep".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypothermia — HYPOTHERMIA means a body temperature below 35 °C (95 °F); 100 °F is raised, not low.
• Hypothermia and Respiratory Alkalosis — "Hypothermia and respiratory alkalosis" is wrong twice: the temperature is high and the pH/PaCO2 are normal.
• Hyperthermia and Respiratory Alkalosis — RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS needs pH above 7.45 and PaCO2 below 35; here pH 7.37 and PaCO2 40 are normal.

⭐ Key point: 100 °F ≈ 37.8 °C = hyperthermia. pH 7.37, PaCO2 40, HCO3 24 = NORMAL ABG. Always check each value against its range.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q24$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 77, $s$The first step in the recommended 5-step ABG interpretation approach is to assess:$s$, $s$["Compensation", "Oxygenation", "Anion gap", "Base excess only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Oxygenation
A common systematic approach to ABG interpretation starts by assessing OXYGENATION (PaO2, SpO2, and the P/F ratio) — because hypoxaemia is the most immediately life-threatening problem — before looking at acid-base balance. The usual steps: (1) Oxygenation; (2) pH (acidosis or alkalosis); (3) PaCO2 (respiratory component); (4) HCO3⁻/base excess (metabolic component); (5) Compensation (and anion gap if metabolic acidosis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Compensation — Compensation is assessed near the end.
• Anion gap — The anion gap is calculated when there is metabolic acidosis.
• Base excess only — Base excess is part of the metabolic assessment, not the first step.

⭐ Key point: Always interpret ABGs together with the patient's clinical condition and the FiO2 they are receiving.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q17$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 78, $s$A normal PaO2 on arterial blood gas analysis is generally considered to be:$s$, $s$["Above 80mmHg", "Below 40mmHg", "Exactly 20mmHg", "Above 200mmHg always required"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Above 80mmHg
PaO2 is the pressure of oxygen dissolved in ARTERIAL blood, measured on an arterial blood gas. On room air at sea level, the normal PaO2 is about 80–100 mmHg (10.5–13.5 kPa). It falls slightly with age. A PaO2 below 80 mmHg indicates HYPOXAEMIA, and below 60 mmHg (8 kPa) defines RESPIRATORY FAILURE — at this point SpO2 is about 90%, and further small falls in PaO2 cause large falls in oxygen saturation (the steep part of the dissociation curve).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Below 40mmHg — A PaO2 below 40 mmHg is severe, life-threatening hypoxaemia (similar to mixed venous blood).
• Exactly 20mmHg — 20 mmHg is incompatible with life for more than a short time.
• Above 200mmHg always required — A PaO2 above 200 mmHg is only seen with high supplemental oxygen; it is not "required" and very high levels may be harmful (oxygen toxicity).

⭐ Key point: Always note the FiO2 when interpreting PaO2 — use the P/F ratio (PaO2 ÷ FiO2) to judge severity of lung injury.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q61$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 79, $s$40 mmol of potassium chloride in 1000 mL normal saline is prescribed over 5 hours. What is the pump rate and how much potassium is given each hour?$s$, $s$["200 mL/hr, giving 4 mmol K⁺ per hour", "200 mL/hr, giving 8 mmol K⁺ per hour", "400 mL/hr, giving 16 mmol K⁺ per hour", "3.3 mL/hr, giving 8 mmol K⁺ per hour"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 200 mL/hr, giving 8 mmol K⁺ per hour
STEP 1 — Pump rate: 1,000 mL ÷ 5 hours = 200 mL/hr. STEP 2 — Potassium per hour: 40 mmol ÷ 5 hours = 8 mmol/hr. Check: 40 mmol/1,000 mL = 0.04 mmol/mL × 200 mL/hr = 8 mmol/hr. ✓ 8 mmol/hr is within the usual safe limit for a peripheral line (no more than 10 mmol/hr).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 200 mL/hr, giving 4 mmol K⁺ per hour — 4 mmol/hr is wrong — half the true amount.
• 400 mL/hr, giving 16 mmol K⁺ per hour — 400 mL/hr would run the bag in 2.5 hours, giving 16 mmol/hr — too fast for a peripheral line.
• 3.3 mL/hr, giving 8 mmol K⁺ per hour — 3.3 mL/hr is far too slow; it would take about 300 hours.

⭐ Key point: Always give IV potassium by pump, never as a push. Check potassium and creatinine before and during replacement; correct magnesium too.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q21 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 80, $s$Over 24 hours a patient received 2,400 mL IV fluid and 300 mL orally. Output was urine 2,200 mL, nasogastric aspirate 0 mL and wound drain 250 mL. What is the 24-hour fluid balance?$s$, $s$["+5,150 mL", "+250 mL", "+500 mL", "-250 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: +250 mL
FLUID BALANCE = TOTAL INPUT − TOTAL OUTPUT. Input: IV 2,400 + oral 300 = 2,700 mL. Output: urine 2,200 + NG 0 + drain 250 = 2,450 mL. Balance: 2,700 − 2,450 = +250 mL (slightly positive).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• +5,150 mL — +5,150 mL adds input and output together.
• +500 mL — +500 mL forgets the drain output (2,700 − 2,200).
• -250 mL — −250 mL reverses the sign.

⭐ Key point: Remember insensible losses (about 500–800 mL/day from skin and breathing, more with fever, sweating or a high respiratory rate), so a balance of +250 mL may actually be slightly negative.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q25 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 81, $s$How many grams of glucose are in 50 mL of 10% dextrose?$s$, $s$["50 g", "2.5 g", "500 g", "5 g"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 5 g
A % (w/v) solution means GRAMS per 100 mL. 10% dextrose = 10 g in 100 mL. So 50 mL contains half of that: 10 ÷ 2 = 5 g of glucose. Check: 50 mL × (10 g ÷ 100 mL) = 5 g. ✓ This is useful in treating hypoglycaemia: e.g. 50 mL of 10% dextrose delivers 5 g glucose (and 50 mL of 50% dextrose delivers 25 g).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50 g — 50 g would be in 500 mL of 10% dextrose.
• 2.5 g — 2.5 g would be in 50 mL of 5% dextrose.
• 500 g — 500 g is a gross error.

⭐ Key point: For hypoglycaemia in adults, 10% dextrose (e.g. 150–200 mL) is preferred over 50% dextrose because it is less damaging to veins.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q30 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 82, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient on high-dose furosemide with a low potassium pH 7.50, PaCO2 54 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 38 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.50: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 38 is HIGH: METABOLIC ALKALOSIS (furosemide causes loss of H⁺, Cl⁻ and K⁺, plus volume contraction). STEP 3 — PaCO2 54 is HIGH: the lungs are compensating by retaining CO2, but the pH is still high. Answer: PARTIALLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a LOW pH and LOW HCO3⁻.
• Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis — Mixed acidosis would have a LOW pH.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Uncompensated would have a NORMAL PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Replace potassium and chloride; consider acetazolamide in persistent diuretic-induced alkalosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q59 · concept asked in 15 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 83, $s$A patient with metabolic acidosis has HCO3⁻ 10 mmol/L and PaCO2 31 mmHg. Using Winter's formula, what does this show?$s$, $s$["Appropriate respiratory compensation — a pure metabolic acidosis", "PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis", "The kidneys are compensating for a respiratory acidosis", "PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis
WINTER'S FORMULA: expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × HCO3⁻) + 8 ± 2 = (1.5 × 10) + 8 = 23 ± 2 = 21–25 mmHg. The ACTUAL PaCO2 is 31 mmHg — HIGHER than expected. The lungs are not blowing off enough CO2, so there is an ADDED RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (e.g. fatigue, sedation, chest problem).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Appropriate respiratory compensation — a pure metabolic acidosis — Appropriate compensation would be 21–25.
• PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis — A lower PaCO2 (below 21) would indicate an added respiratory alkalosis.
• The kidneys are compensating for a respiratory acidosis — The kidneys compensate for respiratory problems, not the reverse here.

⭐ Key point: An inadequately compensating patient with metabolic acidosis may be tiring and need ventilatory support.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q69 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 84, $s$Results: Na⁺ 130, Cl⁻ 97, HCO3⁻ 14 mmol/L. What is the anion gap and its interpretation?$s$, $s$["33 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)", "19 mmol/L — normal", "47 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)", "19 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 19 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)
ANION GAP = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻) = 130 − (97 + 14) = 130 − 111 = 19 mmol/L. Normal is about 8–12 mmol/L (up to 16 if potassium is included). So 19 is RAISED: a HIGH ANION GAP METABOLIC ACIDOSIS — unmeasured acids such as lactate, ketones, uraemic acids or toxins are present.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 33 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 33 mmol/L is a calculation error.
• 19 mmol/L — normal — 19 is correct, but it is RAISED, not normal.
• 47 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 47 mmol/L is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Correct the anion gap for low albumin: add about 2.5 mmol/L for every 10 g/L that albumin is below 40 g/L.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q82 · concept asked in 7 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 85, $s$A mechanically ventilated patient's arterial blood gas shows respiratory alkalosis (low PaCO2, high pH). This most likely indicates:$s$, $s$["Hyperventilation from excessive minute ventilation (rate/tidal volume too high) or patient-driven tachypnea", "Metabolic acidosis compensation", "Underventilation", "Correct ventilator settings requiring no change"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hyperventilation from excessive minute ventilation (rate/tidal volume too high) or patient-driven tachypnea
RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS (low PaCO2, high pH) in a ventilated patient usually means HYPERVENTILATION: either the ventilator settings (rate or tidal volume) are too high, or the patient is breathing fast (pain, anxiety, fever, hypoxia, sepsis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic acidosis compensation — Metabolic acidosis compensation would give a LOW pH.
• Underventilation — Under-ventilation causes respiratory ACIDOSIS.
• Correct ventilator settings requiring no change — The settings need review, not "no change".

⭐ Key point: Reduce minute ventilation if ventilator-driven, or treat the cause of patient tachypnoea.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q121$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 86, $s$Primary hyperaldosteronism (Conn syndrome) classically presents with which combination of findings?$s$, $s$["Hypotension, hyperkalemia, and metabolic acidosis with elevated renin", "Hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis with suppressed plasma renin", "Hypertension with elevated renin and elevated aldosterone", "Normal blood pressure with hyperkalemia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypertension, hypokalemia, and metabolic alkalosis with suppressed plasma renin
PRIMARY HYPERALDOSTERONISM (CONN SYNDROME) is excess ALDOSTERONE production by the adrenal glands (usually an adenoma). Aldosterone makes the kidneys retain SODIUM and water (causing HYPERTENSION) and excrete POTASSIUM and hydrogen ions (causing HYPOKALAEMIA and METABOLIC ALKALOSIS). The high BP and volume SUPPRESS RENIN.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypotension, hyperkalemia, and metabolic acidosis with elevated renin — Hypotension, hyperkalaemia and acidosis describe aldosterone DEFICIENCY (e.g. Addison's).
• Hypertension with elevated renin and elevated aldosterone — Hypertension with high renin and high aldosterone describes SECONDARY hyperaldosteronism (e.g. renal artery stenosis).
• Normal blood pressure with hyperkalemia — Normal BP with hyperkalaemia is not typical.

⭐ Key point: Suspect Conn syndrome in hypertensive patients with low potassium or resistant hypertension.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q160$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 87, $s$A negative base excess (base deficit) on an ABG indicates:$s$, $s$["Respiratory alkalosis specifically", "A relative excess of bicarbonate/buffer base, consistent with alkalosis", "A relative deficit of buffer base, consistent with a metabolic acidosis component", "Normal acid-base status"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A relative deficit of buffer base, consistent with a metabolic acidosis component
BASE EXCESS (BE) shows how much acid or base must be added to return the blood pH to normal, reflecting the METABOLIC part of acid-base status. Normal BE is −2 to +2 mmol/L. A NEGATIVE BE (BASE DEFICIT, e.g. −8) means there is a DEFICIT of BUFFER BASE (bicarbonate) — consistent with METABOLIC ACIDOSIS (e.g. lactic acidosis, DKA, diarrhoea).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory alkalosis specifically — Respiratory alkalosis is shown by low PaCO2, not by base excess.
• A relative excess of bicarbonate/buffer base, consistent with alkalosis — A POSITIVE BE indicates excess base (metabolic alkalosis).
• Normal acid-base status — A significant negative BE is not normal.

⭐ Key point: Base deficit is used in trauma and sepsis as a marker of tissue hypoperfusion and severity.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q65$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 88, $s$A patient with severe hypokalaemia (potassium 2.5mmol/L) is prescribed IV potassium replacement. The nurse's priority safety consideration is to:$s$, $s$["Give it as a rapid IV push for fastest correction", "Ensure it is properly diluted, infused slowly via pump, with continuous cardiac monitoring", "Skip cardiac monitoring since potassium replacement is always safe", "Give it intramuscularly instead"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ensure it is properly diluted, infused slowly via pump, with continuous cardiac monitoring
SEVERE HYPOKALAEMIA (2.5 mmol/L) causes muscle weakness and dangerous ARRHYTHMIAS, so it must be corrected — but IV POTASSIUM is also DANGEROUS if given too fast or too concentrated: it can cause cardiac arrest. Safety rules: always DILUTE potassium properly (use pre-mixed bags where possible); give it SLOWLY by INFUSION PUMP (usually no more than 10 mmol/hour peripherally; up to 20 mmol/hour via a central line with ECG monitoring in critical care); use CONTINUOUS CARDIAC MONITORING for high rates; check potassium levels regularly; and also correct low magnesium.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give it as a rapid IV push for fastest correction — Rapid IV push of potassium can cause fatal cardiac arrest — it is NEVER given as a bolus.
• Skip cardiac monitoring since potassium replacement is always safe — Cardiac monitoring is essential during IV replacement for severe hypokalaemia.
• Give it intramuscularly instead — Intramuscular potassium is not used (painful, unreliable, and can cause tissue damage).

⭐ Key point: Concentrated potassium chloride ampoules should be stored separately and double-checked — they have caused fatal errors.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q152$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 89, $s$A patient with acute kidney injury develops a serum potassium of 6.8mmol/L with peaked T-waves on ECG. The priority nursing action is to:$s$, $s$["Administer a potassium supplement", "Encourage the patient to eat a banana", "Wait for the next scheduled blood draw to confirm", "Alert the physician immediately and prepare for urgent treatment (e.g. calcium gluconate, insulin/dextrose)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Alert the physician immediately and prepare for urgent treatment (e.g. calcium gluconate, insulin/dextrose)
A potassium of 6.8 mmol/L with PEAKED T WAVES is a MEDICAL EMERGENCY — it can lead to fatal arrhythmias. The priority is to ALERT THE PHYSICIAN IMMEDIATELY and PREPARE for URGENT TREATMENT: IV CALCIUM GLUCONATE (stabilises the heart), INSULIN with DEXTROSE and salbutamol (move potassium into cells), and measures to remove potassium (binders, dialysis). Start continuous ECG monitoring.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer a potassium supplement — Potassium supplements would worsen hyperkalaemia.
• Encourage the patient to eat a banana — Bananas are high in potassium.
• Wait for the next scheduled blood draw to confirm — Waiting for the next blood test delays emergency treatment.

⭐ Key point: Stop all potassium-containing fluids and drugs that raise potassium (e.g. ACE inhibitors, spironolactone).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q180$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 90, $s$During continuous renal replacement therapy with regional citrate anticoagulation, the patient's total calcium is rising while ionised calcium falls (total:ionised calcium ratio above 2.5), with a worsening metabolic acidosis. This indicates:$s$, $s$["Hypercalcaemia from excessive calcium replacement", "Citrate accumulation, as the liver cannot clear citrate", "Severe hypoalbuminaemia lowering the total calcium", "Early clotting of the filter and extracorporeal circuit"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Citrate accumulation, as the liver cannot clear citrate
In CRRT with REGIONAL CITRATE ANTICOAGULATION, citrate is added to the circuit to bind calcium and stop clotting. Normally the LIVER converts citrate into bicarbonate. If the liver cannot clear citrate (e.g. liver failure, shock), CITRATE ACCUMULATES. The citrate keeps binding calcium, so the IONISED calcium falls; but the TOTAL calcium rises because citrate-bound calcium is still counted. A TOTAL:IONISED CALCIUM RATIO above 2.5 and a worsening METABOLIC ACIDOSIS (citrate is not being converted to bicarbonate) indicate CITRATE ACCUMULATION.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypercalcaemia from excessive calcium replacement — Hypercalcaemia from excessive replacement would raise BOTH total and ionised calcium.
• Severe hypoalbuminaemia lowering the total calcium — Hypoalbuminaemia lowers TOTAL calcium; it does not cause this pattern.
• Early clotting of the filter and extracorporeal circuit — Clotting of the filter is a different problem; it causes reduced filter life and rising pressures.

⭐ Key point: Treat by reducing or stopping citrate, and switching to another anticoagulant if necessary.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q39$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 91, $s$A woman with metastatic breast cancer has thirst, polyuria, constipation, abdominal pain, confusion and a short QT interval on the ECG. The most likely cause and first treatment are:$s$, $s$["Hypercalcaemia — IV saline, then a bisphosphonate", "Hypokalaemia — IV potassium chloride replacement", "Hyponatraemia from SIADH — strict fluid restriction", "Hypermagnesaemia — IV 10% calcium gluconate"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypercalcaemia — IV saline, then a bisphosphonate
HYPERCALCAEMIA is common in cancer (especially breast cancer with BONE METASTASES). Symptoms: "Bones, stones, groans and moans": THIRST, POLYURIA (excess urine), CONSTIPATION, ABDOMINAL PAIN, CONFUSION, and a SHORT QT interval on ECG. First treatment: rehydration with IV SALINE (high calcium causes dehydration), then a BISPHOSPHONATE (e.g. zoledronic acid) to stop bone breakdown.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypokalaemia — IV potassium chloride replacement — Hypokalaemia causes muscle weakness and U waves, not polyuria with a short QT.
• Hyponatraemia from SIADH — strict fluid restriction — SIADH causes low sodium and fluid retention, not polyuria.
• Hypermagnesaemia — IV 10% calcium gluconate — Hypermagnesaemia is uncommon and doesn't cause this picture.

⭐ Key point: Avoid thiazide diuretics (they raise calcium). Monitor calcium, kidney function and fluid balance.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q104$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, true, 92, $s$ABG: pH 7.20, PaCO₂ 25 mmHg, HCO₃⁻ 10 mmol/L indicates:$s$, $s$["Mixed respiratory and metabolic alkalosis", "Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation", "Acute respiratory acidosis without compensation", "Metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation
Step 1: pH 7.20 is low, so the patient has ACIDOSIS. Step 2: HCO₃⁻ 10 is very low, so the cause is METABOLIC (pH and HCO₃⁻ both down = metabolic). Step 3: PaCO₂ 25 is low; the lungs are blowing off CO₂ to COMPENSATE. Check with Winter's formula: expected PaCO₂ = 1.5 × 10 + 8 = 23 (±2), so 25 fits. This is metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mixed respiratory and metabolic alkalosis — The pH is low (acidosis), so it can't be alkalosis.
• Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation — The pH is low, not high, so it isn't metabolic alkalosis.
• Acute respiratory acidosis without compensation — Respiratory acidosis would have a HIGH PaCO₂; here it is low.

⭐ Key point: Causes of metabolic acidosis: DKA, lactic acidosis, kidney failure, diarrhoea, poisoning. Calculate the anion gap to narrow down the cause.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q88 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 93, $s$In a healthy person, the primary physiological stimulus that drives minute-to-minute ventilation is:$s$, $s$["A rise in arterial carbon dioxide tension (PaCO2)", "A rise in arterial oxygen tension (PaO2)", "A fall in blood glucose", "A rise in serum calcium"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A rise in arterial carbon dioxide tension (PaCO2)
Breathing is controlled mainly by central chemoreceptors in the medulla, which respond to CO₂. CO₂ crosses into the cerebrospinal fluid, forms carbonic acid and lowers the pH there. Even a small rise in PaCO₂ quickly increases the rate and depth of breathing to blow off the extra CO₂. This is the main minute-to-minute drive in a healthy person.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A rise in arterial oxygen tension (PaO2) — A rise in PaO₂ would reduce, not drive, breathing. Low oxygen (below about 60 mmHg) is only a back-up drive, sensed by the carotid and aortic bodies.
• A fall in blood glucose — Blood glucose does not directly control breathing.
• A rise in serum calcium — Serum calcium affects nerves and muscles but is not a breathing stimulus.

⭐ Key point: CO₂ is the main drive to breathe. In chronic CO₂ retainers (some COPD patients), the hypoxic drive matters more, so give controlled oxygen with a target SpO₂ of 88–92%.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q3$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 94, $s$Which of the following causes a leftward shift of the oxyhaemoglobin dissociation curve (increased affinity for oxygen)?$s$, $s$["Increased temperature", "Decreased pH (acidosis)", "Decreased temperature, decreased 2,3-DPG, and alkalosis", "Increased 2,3-DPG"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Decreased temperature, decreased 2,3-DPG, and alkalosis
A LEFT shift means haemoglobin holds oxygen more tightly (higher affinity), so it picks up O₂ easily in the lungs but releases less to the tissues. Causes of a left shift: LOW temperature, LOW 2,3-DPG, ALKALOSIS (high pH), LOW CO₂, and also carbon monoxide and fetal haemoglobin. This option lists three left-shift causes together, so it is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased temperature — Increased temperature shifts the curve to the RIGHT (hot, working tissues need more oxygen released).
• Decreased pH (acidosis) — Acidosis (low pH) shifts the curve to the RIGHT (the Bohr effect).
• Increased 2,3-DPG — Increased 2,3-DPG shifts the curve to the RIGHT (as in anaemia and high altitude).

⭐ Key point: Remember "CADET, face Right": CO₂, Acid, 2,3-DPG, Exercise and Temperature all shift the curve to the RIGHT (more O₂ released). The opposite of each shifts it LEFT.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q4$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 95, $s$What is a cause of metabolic alkalosis?$s$, $s$["Vomiting", "Diarrhea", "Renal failure", "Hypoventilation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vomiting
Vomiting (and NG suction) removes hydrochloric acid (HCl) from the stomach. Losing acid leaves extra bicarbonate behind, which raises the pH. Loss of fluid and chloride makes the kidneys hold on to more bicarbonate, and potassium is lost too. The result is a hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis, as in pyloric stenosis in babies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diarrhea — Diarrhoea causes metabolic ACIDOSIS, because bowel fluid is rich in bicarbonate, which is lost.
• Renal failure — Renal failure causes metabolic ACIDOSIS, because the kidneys cannot excrete acid or make bicarbonate.
• Hypoventilation — Hypoventilation causes RESPIRATORY acidosis (CO₂ builds up).

⭐ Key point: Above the stomach (vomiting) = alkalosis; below the stomach (diarrhoea) = acidosis. Treat with 0.9% saline and potassium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q19$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 96, $s$The modified Allen's test is performed before arterial blood gas sampling primarily to:$s$, $s$["Assess collateral circulation to the hand via the ulnar artery before radial artery puncture", "Measure blood pressure", "Assess for infection at the puncture site", "Determine the patient's pain tolerance"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assess collateral circulation to the hand via the ulnar artery before radial artery puncture
The radial artery is the usual ABG site. Before puncturing it, the modified Allen test checks that the ULNAR artery can supply the hand on its own if the radial artery is damaged or clotted. Method: press both arteries, ask the patient to clench the fist until the hand blanches, then release only the ulnar artery. Colour should return within 5–15 seconds. If it doesn't, choose another site.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Measure blood pressure — Blood pressure is measured with a cuff or arterial line, not the Allen test.
• Assess for infection at the puncture site — Infection at the site is checked by looking for redness, swelling and pus.
• Determine the patient's pain tolerance — Pain tolerance is not what the Allen test measures.

⭐ Key point: A negative (abnormal) Allen test means don't use that radial artery. After an ABG, press firmly for at least 5 minutes (longer if the patient is on anticoagulants).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q20$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 97, $s$A patient is admitted to ICU with diagnosis of vaso-occlusive crisis, which of this management will you start with?$s$, $s$["Transfuse with fresh whole blood", "Order for narcotics and administer", "Rehydrate with normal saline", "Give oral rehydration solution"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Rehydrate with normal saline
A sickle cell vaso-occlusive (pain) crisis happens when sickled cells block small vessels. Dehydration makes the blood thicker and causes more sickling, so the first step is IV REHYDRATION (with normal saline). Rehydration improves blood flow and stops the cycle. Strong pain relief (often opioids) within 30 minutes, oxygen if SpO₂ is low, warmth, and treatment of infection go alongside it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Transfuse with fresh whole blood — Transfusion is not first-line for an uncomplicated pain crisis; it is used for acute chest syndrome, stroke, or severe anaemia (sequestration). Fresh whole blood is rarely used.
• Order for narcotics and administer — Pain relief is essential and urgent, but rehydration is the first treatment this question is testing.
• Give oral rehydration solution — Oral rehydration solution is too slow and not suitable for an ICU patient in crisis.

⭐ Key point: Sickle crisis care: fluids, oxygen, warmth, pain relief within 30 minutes, and antibiotics for infection. Avoid cold, dehydration and hypoxia, because each one triggers more sickling.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q23$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 98, $s$Type II (hypercapnic) respiratory failure is defined by:$s$, $s$["A PaO2 less than 60 mmHg alone", "A PaCO2 greater than 50 mmHg with accompanying respiratory acidosis", "A normal PaCO2 with severe hypoxemia", "Elevated bicarbonate with normal pH"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A PaCO2 greater than 50 mmHg with accompanying respiratory acidosis
Type II (hypercapnic or ventilatory) respiratory failure means the lungs cannot remove CO₂: PaCO₂ above 45–50 mmHg (above 6 kPa), usually with a low PaO₂ and a low pH (respiratory acidosis) when it is acute. The cause is HYPOventilation: COPD, exhausted asthma, opioid overdose, neuromuscular disease (GBS, myasthenia), obesity and chest wall problems.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A PaO2 less than 60 mmHg alone — A PaO₂ below 60 mmHg alone (normal or low CO₂) is TYPE I (hypoxaemic) failure.
• A normal PaCO2 with severe hypoxemia — A normal PaCO₂ with severe hypoxaemia is also type I failure.
• Elevated bicarbonate with normal pH — A raised bicarbonate with normal pH is a compensated or metabolic picture, not the definition.

⭐ Key point: Type 1 = low O₂ only (oxygenation problem: pneumonia, oedema, ARDS). Type 2 = low O₂ + HIGH CO₂ (ventilation problem). Type 2 needs ventilation support (NIV or intubation), not just oxygen.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q59$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 99, $s$A patient's serum sodium is measured at 118mmol/L with confusion and lethargy. The nurse understands that correction of severe hyponatraemia must be done:$s$, $s$["As rapidly as possible regardless of rate", "Gradually and carefully, since overly rapid correction risks osmotic demyelination syndrome", "Only with oral salt tablets, never IV", "With no monitoring required"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Gradually and carefully, since overly rapid correction risks osmotic demyelination syndrome
In chronic low sodium, brain cells adapt by losing solutes to avoid swelling. If sodium is raised too FAST, water rushes out of the brain cells and damages the myelin in the pons, causing osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis). This leads to difficulty speaking and swallowing, quadriplegia, "locked-in" syndrome or death. Correction must therefore be gradual: no more than 8–10 mmol/L in 24 hours, with frequent sodium checks (every 2–4 hours).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• As rapidly as possible regardless of rate — Correcting as fast as possible is exactly what causes osmotic demyelination.
• Only with oral salt tablets, never IV — IV treatment (including 3% saline for severe symptoms such as seizures) is used when needed; oral salt alone is not the rule.
• With no monitoring required — Close monitoring of sodium, fluid balance and neurological status is essential.

⭐ Key point: For severe symptomatic hyponatraemia (seizures, coma), give 3% saline 150 mL boluses to raise sodium by 4–6 mmol/L quickly, then slow down to stay within 8–10 mmol/L per 24 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q63$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 100, $s$A patient with hyperkalemia has peaked T waves. After calcium gluconate, which drug shifts potassium intracellularly?$s$, $s$["Albuterol", "Sodium polystyrene", "Furosemide", "Fludrocortisone"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Albuterol
After calcium gluconate protects the heart, the next step is to SHIFT potassium from the blood into the cells. Albuterol (salbutamol), a beta-2 agonist, does this by stimulating the sodium-potassium pump, lowering K⁺ by about 0.5–1 mmol/L within 30 minutes. It is given by nebuliser (10–20 mg). Insulin with glucose works the same way and is the most reliable shifting drug.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sodium polystyrene — Sodium polystyrene (Kayexalate/calcium resonium) REMOVES potassium through the gut, slowly (hours); it doesn't shift it.
• Furosemide — Furosemide REMOVES potassium in the urine; it doesn't shift it into cells.
• Fludrocortisone — Fludrocortisone increases potassium loss over days; it is not used acutely.

⭐ Key point: Order for high potassium: 1) PROTECT the heart (calcium gluconate 10%), 2) SHIFT K⁺ into cells (insulin and glucose, salbutamol), 3) REMOVE K⁺ (dialysis, resins, diuretics).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q79$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 101, $s$Fluid therapy in a severely dehydrated child typically begins with:$s$, $s$["Oral fluids only, regardless of severity", "A rapid IV fluid bolus for severe dehydration, followed by ongoing maintenance and deficit replacement", "Withholding all fluids until specialist review", "Only subcutaneous fluids"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A rapid IV fluid bolus for severe dehydration, followed by ongoing maintenance and deficit replacement
A severely dehydrated child (lethargic, weak pulse, cold hands, slow capillary refill, sunken eyes) is in or near shock. The first step is a RAPID IV (or IO) fluid bolus of isotonic crystalloid (0.9% saline or Hartmann's), commonly 10–20 mL/kg, repeated after reassessment. After the circulation is restored, the remaining deficit and maintenance fluids are replaced over 24–48 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral fluids only, regardless of severity — Oral fluids alone are for mild to moderate dehydration; severe dehydration needs IV fluids.
• Withholding all fluids until specialist review — Withholding fluid in severe dehydration can be fatal.
• Only subcutaneous fluids — Subcutaneous fluids are too slow for severe dehydration and shock.

⭐ Key point: The WHO plan for severe dehydration (Plan C) is 100 mL/kg of Ringer's lactate in phases. Watch for overload in malnourished children and those with heart disease.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q146$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 102, $s$A patient on TPN is at risk for refeeding syndrome. The nurse should be most vigilant for which of the following electrolyte imbalances?$s$, $s$["Hypernatremia", "Hyperkalemia", "Hypophosphatemia", "Hypocalcemia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypophosphatemia
In refeeding syndrome, starved patients are suddenly given nutrition (enteral or TPN). Insulin rises and drives glucose, PHOSPHATE, potassium and magnesium into the cells. Serum phosphate can fall dangerously, causing muscle weakness (including the diaphragm, leading to breathing failure and failure to wean), heart failure, arrhythmias, confusion, seizures and death. HYPOPHOSPHATAEMIA is the hallmark.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypernatremia — Sodium can shift, but high sodium is not the typical refeeding problem.
• Hyperkalemia — Potassium FALLS (hypokalaemia), it doesn't rise.
• Hypocalcemia — Calcium can be affected but is not the key electrolyte in refeeding.

⭐ Key point: Prevent refeeding: identify at-risk patients (low BMI, little intake for 5 days or more, alcohol misuse), give thiamine before feeding, start slowly (10–20 kcal/kg/day), and check phosphate, potassium and magnesium daily.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q196$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 103, $s$A high anion gap metabolic acidosis, such as in DKA, is caused by:$s$, $s$["Loss of bicarbonate from diarrhoea", "Extra unmeasured acid (e.g. ketones) in the blood", "Excess vomiting", "Hyperventilation alone"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Extra unmeasured acid (e.g. ketones) in the blood
In DIABETIC KETOACIDOSIS (DKA), lack of insulin makes the body burn fat, producing KETOACIDS (beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate). These are EXTRA UNMEASURED ACIDS. They release H⁺, which uses up bicarbonate, and their negative ions are not counted in the anion gap formula, so the anion gap RISES. This is called a HIGH ANION GAP METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. Same process with lactic acidosis (sepsis, shock), uraemia, and poisons like methanol, ethylene glycol and aspirin ("MUDPILES"). Treatment of DKA: IV fluids, IV insulin infusion, potassium replacement and monitoring of ketones, glucose and anion gap until it closes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Loss of bicarbonate from diarrhoea — Loss of bicarbonate from DIARRHOEA causes a NORMAL anion gap (hyperchloraemic) acidosis, because chloride rises to replace the lost bicarbonate.
• Excess vomiting — Excess VOMITING loses stomach acid (HCl), causing METABOLIC ALKALOSIS, not acidosis.
• Hyperventilation alone — HYPERVENTILATION alone blows off CO2 and causes RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS.

⭐ Key point: High anion gap acidosis = ADDED acid (ketones, lactate, toxins). Diarrhoea = normal AG acidosis. Vomiting = alkalosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q23$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 104, $s$The most common anticoagulant used in blood bank donor bags is:$s$, $s$["EDTA", "Heparin", "Tri-sodium citrate", "Double oxalate"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tri-sodium citrate
Blood bank donor bags contain an anticoagulant-preservative such as CPD or CPDA-1 (Citrate, Phosphate, Dextrose, Adenine). The anticoagulant part is TRI-SODIUM CITRATE. Citrate stops clotting by BINDING CALCIUM (calcium is factor IV, needed at many steps of the cascade). The DEXTROSE feeds the red cells, ADENINE helps them make ATP, and PHOSPHATE buffers the pH; CPDA-1 lets whole blood be stored for about 35 days at 2–6 °C. Clinical link: in MASSIVE transfusion, the citrate in many units can bind the patient's own calcium → HYPOCALCAEMIA (tingling, low BP, arrhythmia), so ionised calcium is monitored and calcium is given if low.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• EDTA — EDTA (purple-top tube) is used for full blood count samples; it is not used in donor bags because it is toxic in large amounts.
• Heparin — HEPARIN is used for some samples (green tube) and as a drug, but it does not preserve red cells for weeks of storage.
• Double oxalate — DOUBLE OXALATE is an old laboratory anticoagulant for test samples only; it is toxic and never used for transfusion.

⭐ Key point: Donor bags = CITRATE (CPD/CPDA-1) — binds calcium. Massive transfusion → watch for HYPOCALCAEMIA.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q27$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 105, $s$Assessing dehydration severity in a paediatric patient includes evaluating all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Skin turgor", "Mucous membrane moisture", "Urine output", "The child's favourite toy"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The child's favourite toy
Children dehydrate quickly. Clinical assessment of DEHYDRATION SEVERITY looks at: general condition (alert, irritable, lethargic), SKIN TURGOR (skin pinch goes back slowly in dehydration), MUCOUS MEMBRANES (dry mouth and tongue), eyes (sunken), tears (absent), anterior FONTANELLE in infants (sunken), capillary refill time (over 2 seconds), heart rate (tachycardia), BP (low = late sign), URINE OUTPUT (reduced/no wet nappies), and weight loss (best measure if pre-illness weight is known: mild < 5%, moderate 5–10%, severe > 10%). WHO classifies as no, some, or severe dehydration to guide ORS (Plan A/B) or IV fluids (Plan C). The child's FAVOURITE TOY has no clinical value for hydration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Skin turgor — SKIN TURGOR IS assessed — slow return of a skin pinch shows dehydration.
• Mucous membrane moisture — MUCOUS MEMBRANE moisture IS assessed — a dry mouth shows dehydration.
• Urine output — URINE OUTPUT IS assessed — reduced urine/fewer wet nappies shows dehydration.

⭐ Key point: Paediatric dehydration: turgor, mucous membranes, eyes, tears, fontanelle, capillary refill, HR, urine output, weight loss.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q148$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 106, $s$A fall in HCO3 causing acidosis indicates the disorder is:$s$, $s$["Respiratory", "Metabolic", "Purely a lab error", "Always fatal"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic
Acid–base balance has two parts: the RESPIRATORY part, controlled by the lungs and shown by PaCO2, and the METABOLIC part, controlled mainly by the kidneys and shown by BICARBONATE (HCO3⁻, normal 22–26 mmol/L). Bicarbonate is the body's main BASE (buffer). When HCO3⁻ FALLS and the pH falls (acidosis), the problem is METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. Causes: GAIN of acid (lactic acidosis in shock or sepsis, ketoacidosis in diabetes or starvation, kidney failure, poisons such as methanol or aspirin) or LOSS of bicarbonate (diarrhoea, ileostomy, renal tubular acidosis). The lungs try to compensate by fast, deep breathing to blow off CO2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory — A RESPIRATORY disorder is caused by a change in PaCO2, not bicarbonate.
• Purely a lab error — A low bicarbonate with acidosis is a real, common finding — not a lab error.
• Always fatal — Metabolic acidosis is treatable and not always fatal; outcome depends on the cause.

⭐ Key point: In metabolic acidosis, calculate the ANION GAP (Na⁺ − [Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻]) — a high gap suggests added acids (lactate, ketones, toxins); a normal gap suggests bicarbonate loss.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q23$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 107, $s$Which clinical manifestation would lead the nurse to suspect that a client is experiencing hypermagnesemia?$s$, $s$["Increased respiratory rate and depth", "Soft-tissue calcification and hyperreflexia", "Hot flushed skin and diaphoresis", "Muscle pain and acute rhabdomyolysis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hot flushed skin and diaphoresis
HYPERMAGNESAEMIA (high magnesium) causes VASODILATION and depresses nerves and muscles. Early signs include FLUSHING, a sensation of WARMTH, sweating (DIAPHORESIS), nausea and hypotension. As levels rise: loss of deep tendon reflexes, drowsiness, muscle weakness, respiratory depression, bradycardia and cardiac arrest. Common causes: magnesium sulphate therapy (pre-eclampsia), kidney failure, and magnesium-containing antacids or laxatives.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased respiratory rate and depth — Respiratory rate and depth DECREASE in hypermagnesaemia.
• Soft-tissue calcification and hyperreflexia — Hyperreflexia is a feature of HYPOmagnesaemia; hypermagnesaemia causes HYPOreflexia.
• Muscle pain and acute rhabdomyolysis — Muscle pain and rhabdomyolysis are not typical.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV calcium gluconate (antidote), stop magnesium, give fluids and diuretics, or dialysis in kidney failure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q24$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 108, $s$A patient with hypernatremia (Na⁺ 160 mEq/L) is lethargic. Which fluid should the nurse administer?$s$, $s$["3% saline", "0.9% saline", "D5W", "Lactated Ringer’s"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: D5W
HYPERNATRAEMIA (sodium 160) usually means the body has lost too much WATER relative to sodium. The brain cells shrink, causing LETHARGY, confusion, seizures and coma. Treatment is to replace the FREE WATER deficit with a HYPOTONIC fluid such as 5% DEXTROSE IN WATER (D5W) — after the glucose is metabolised, it provides pure water. Sodium must be lowered SLOWLY (no more than about 10 mmol/L in 24 hours in chronic cases) to avoid cerebral oedema. (If the patient is shocked/hypovolaemic, isotonic saline is given first to restore circulation.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 3% saline — 3% saline is hypertonic — it would raise sodium further.
• 0.9% saline — 0.9% saline (154 mmol/L) is used for volume resuscitation but provides little free water.
• Lactated Ringer’s — Lactated Ringer's is near-isotonic and doesn't correct a free water deficit efficiently.

⭐ Key point: Monitor sodium every 4–6 hours during correction and watch for neurological changes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q27$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 109, $s$Storage lower urinary tract symptoms include the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Frequency", "Urgency", "Hesitancy", "Nocturia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hesitancy
LOWER URINARY TRACT SYMPTOMS (LUTS) are grouped into: STORAGE symptoms (problems holding urine): FREQUENCY, URGENCY, NOCTURIA (waking at night to pass urine), urge incontinence; and VOIDING symptoms (problems emptying): HESITANCY (delay in starting), weak stream, straining, intermittent stream, terminal dribbling and incomplete emptying. HESITANCY is a VOIDING symptom, not a storage symptom.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Frequency — Frequency IS a storage symptom.
• Urgency — Urgency IS a storage symptom.
• Nocturia — Nocturia IS a storage symptom.

⭐ Key point: Voiding symptoms are typical of benign prostatic hyperplasia; storage symptoms are typical of overactive bladder or UTI.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q79$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 110, $s$Post-nephrectomy hospital stay typically ranges from:$s$, $s$["2–7 days", "2–4 weeks", "6 months", "Discharge is always same-day"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2–7 days
NEPHRECTOMY is surgical removal of a kidney (for cancer, trauma, a non-functioning kidney, or living donation). Hospital stay usually ranges from about 2 TO 7 DAYS: about 2–3 days after LAPAROSCOPIC surgery, and about 5–7 days after OPEN surgery, depending on pain control, mobility, bowel function and complications. Nursing care focuses on monitoring for bleeding (BP, pulse, wound, drains, haemoglobin), urine output (function of the remaining kidney), pain control, chest physiotherapy (flank incisions near the diaphragm limit breathing), and early mobilisation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2–4 weeks — 2–4 weeks is unusually long and suggests complications.
• 6 months — 6 months is far too long.
• Discharge is always same-day — Same-day discharge is not standard after nephrectomy.

⭐ Key point: After nephrectomy, avoid nephrotoxic drugs (e.g. NSAIDs) to protect the remaining kidney.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q81$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 111, $s$The kidneys contribute to regulation of acid-base balance mainly by:$s$, $s$["Producing insulin", "Excreting or reabsorbing hydrogen ions and bicarbonate", "Regulating heart rate", "Producing bile"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Excreting or reabsorbing hydrogen ions and bicarbonate
The KIDNEYS regulate ACID-BASE balance by EXCRETING HYDROGEN IONS (acid) into the urine (buffered by phosphate and ammonia) and by REABSORBING and GENERATING BICARBONATE (the body's main buffer). When the blood is too acidic, the kidneys excrete more acid and keep more bicarbonate; when too alkaline, they excrete bicarbonate. This "metabolic" compensation is slow (hours to days), unlike the lungs (minutes).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Producing insulin — Insulin is produced by the pancreas.
• Regulating heart rate — Heart rate is controlled by the nervous system and hormones.
• Producing bile — Bile is produced by the liver.

⭐ Key point: In kidney failure, acid accumulates, causing metabolic acidosis — a common indication for dialysis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q14$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 112, $s$A nurse inadvertently infuses an IV solution containing potassium chloride too rapidly. Which of the following is an appropriate intervention advised by the physician?$s$, $s$["Rapid infusion of IV Ringer\\'s solution", "Rapid infusion of IV NS 0.9%", "Insulin added to 10% dextrose in water solution", "Administration of albumin"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Insulin added to 10% dextrose in water solution
Infusing POTASSIUM CHLORIDE too fast can cause dangerous HYPERKALAEMIA, which can lead to fatal arrhythmias and cardiac arrest. One recommended treatment is INSULIN added to 10% DEXTROSE. Insulin drives POTASSIUM INTO CELLS (by activating the sodium-potassium pump), quickly lowering blood potassium; the dextrose prevents hypoglycaemia. (Other urgent measures: STOP the infusion, give IV calcium gluconate to protect the heart, salbutamol, and remove potassium via binders or dialysis.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rapid infusion of IV Ringer\'s solution — Ringer's solution contains potassium — it would not help.
• Rapid infusion of IV NS 0.9% — Normal saline does not lower potassium significantly.
• Administration of albumin — Albumin does not lower potassium.

⭐ Key point: Always give IV potassium by pump, diluted, and never faster than about 10 mmol/hr peripherally (20 mmol/hr centrally with ECG monitoring).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q16$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 113, $s$Type 1 Respiratory Failure is characterised by:$s$, $s$["Low PaO2 with normal or low PaCO2", "High PaCO2 only", "Normal PaO2 and PaCO2", "High PaO2 with low PaCO2"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Low PaO2 with normal or low PaCO2
TYPE 1 (HYPOXAEMIC) RESPIRATORY FAILURE is defined by a LOW PaO2 (below 60 mmHg / 8 kPa) with a NORMAL or LOW PaCO2. It is a problem with GAS EXCHANGE (oxygenation), caused by V/Q mismatch or shunt — e.g. pneumonia, pulmonary oedema, ARDS, PE. The CO2 is normal or low because the patient often breathes fast.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High PaCO2 only — High PaCO2 is TYPE 2 (hypercapnic) respiratory failure.
• Normal PaO2 and PaCO2 — Normal PaO2 and PaCO2 is not respiratory failure.
• High PaO2 with low PaCO2 — High PaO2 with low PaCO2 is not respiratory failure.

⭐ Key point: Type 1 = oxygenation problem (treat with oxygen, PEEP). Type 2 = ventilation problem (treat with ventilatory support).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q18$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 114, $s$A Modified Allen's Test that shows NO flushing after releasing the ulnar artery indicates:$s$, $s$["Safe to proceed with radial puncture", "Inadequate collateral circulation — avoid that site", "The patient is dehydrated", "A normal, expected finding"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inadequate collateral circulation — avoid that site
The MODIFIED ALLEN'S TEST checks whether the ULNAR ARTERY can supply blood to the hand on its own (collateral circulation), in case the radial artery becomes blocked after puncture. Both arteries are compressed while the patient clenches the fist; the hand goes pale. When the ULNAR artery is released, colour (flushing) should return within about 5–15 seconds. NO FLUSHING means INADEQUATE COLLATERAL CIRCULATION — do NOT use that radial artery; choose another site.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Safe to proceed with radial puncture — It is NOT safe to proceed if there is no flushing.
• The patient is dehydrated — It doesn't indicate dehydration.
• A normal, expected finding — No flushing is an abnormal finding.

⭐ Key point: Document the result of Allen's test before radial arterial puncture or line insertion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q20$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 115, $s$George Kent is a 54-year-old widower with a history of chronic obstructive pulmonary disease and was rushed to the emergency department with increasing shortness of breath, pyrexia, and a productive cough with yellow-green sputum. He has difficulty communicating because of his inability to complete a sentence. One of his sons, Jacob, says he has been unwell for three days. Upon examination, crackles and wheezes can be heard in the lower lobes; he has tachycardia and a bounding pulse. Measurement of arterial blood gas shows pH 7.3, PaCO2 68 mm Hg, HCO3 28 mmol/L, and PaO2 60 mm Hg. How would you interpret this?$s$, $s$["Metabolic Alkalosis, Uncompensated", "Metabolic Acidosis, Partially Compensated", "Respiratory Acidosis, Uncompensated", "Respiratory Acidosis, Partially Compensated"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory Acidosis, Partially Compensated
STEP 1 — pH 7.3: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 68 mmHg is HIGH: RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (COPD exacerbation — tired breathing muscles and airway obstruction cause CO2 retention). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 28 mmol/L is slightly HIGH: the kidneys have started compensating (retaining bicarbonate), but the pH is still below 7.35. Answer: RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS, PARTIALLY COMPENSATED. PaO2 60 mmHg shows hypoxaemia. The infection (yellow-green sputum, fever) has triggered acute-on-chronic respiratory failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic Alkalosis, Uncompensated — Metabolic alkalosis would show a high pH.
• Metabolic Acidosis, Partially Compensated — Metabolic acidosis would show a LOW HCO3⁻.
• Respiratory Acidosis, Uncompensated — Uncompensated would show a NORMAL HCO3⁻ (22–26).

⭐ Key point: Treat with controlled oxygen (target SpO2 88–92%), nebulised bronchodilators, steroids, antibiotics, and NIV if pH below 7.35.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q22$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 116, $s$Radial artery sampling for ABG is typically performed at what needle angle?$s$, $s$["90°", "45°", "10°", "180°"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 45°
For RADIAL ARTERY ABG sampling, the needle is usually inserted at a 30–45 DEGREE angle, with the bevel up, facing against the blood flow. This angle allows the needle to enter the artery smoothly, which lies relatively superficially at the wrist. (For deeper arteries like the femoral, a steeper 60–90° angle is used.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 90° — 90° is used for the femoral artery or intramuscular injections.
• 10° — 10° is too shallow and may miss the artery.
• 180° — 180° is not a possible insertion angle.

⭐ Key point: After sampling, apply firm pressure for at least 5 minutes (longer if anticoagulated) to prevent haematoma.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q23$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 117, $s$Potassium is most abundant in which compartment?$s$, $s$["Intracellular", "Extracellular", "Intravascular", "Interstitial"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intracellular
About 98% of the body's POTASSIUM is INSIDE the CELLS (INTRACELLULAR), at a concentration of about 140–150 mmol/L. Only about 2% is outside the cells (extracellular), where the normal blood level is 3.5–5.0 mmol/L. This large difference is maintained by the SODIUM-POTASSIUM PUMP and is essential for nerve and muscle function, including heart rhythm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Extracellular — Extracellular fluid contains only a small amount.
• Intravascular — Intravascular (blood plasma) contains a small amount.
• Interstitial — Interstitial fluid contains a small amount.

⭐ Key point: Small shifts of potassium between cells and blood (e.g. from insulin, acidosis, cell breakdown) can cause big changes in blood levels.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q26$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 118, $s$The following are causes of respiratory acidosis EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["COPD", "Severe asthma", "Hyperventilation", "Head injury"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hyperventilation
RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS is caused by HYPOVENTILATION — CO2 builds up because not enough is breathed out. Causes include COPD, severe asthma (exhaustion), head injury (depressed breathing centre), opioids, neuromuscular weakness, and chest wall problems. HYPERVENTILATION causes the OPPOSITE — CO2 is blown off, causing RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• COPD — COPD IS a cause (CO2 retention).
• Severe asthma — Severe asthma IS a cause when the patient tires.
• Head injury — Head injury IS a cause (depressed respiratory drive).

⭐ Key point: Respiratory acidosis = high PaCO2, low pH. Respiratory alkalosis = low PaCO2, high pH.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q27$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 119, $s$A patient with a very dry mouth, skin, and mucous membranes is diagnosed with dehydration. Which intervention should the nurse perform when caring for a patient diagnosed with fluid volume deficit?$s$, $s$["Obtaining the clients weight weekly at different times of the day.", "Maintaining I V therapy at the keep vein open rate.", "Monitoring arterial blood gas ABG results", "Assessing urinary intake and output"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assessing urinary intake and output
In FLUID VOLUME DEFICIT (dehydration), the nurse should ASSESS URINARY INTAKE and OUTPUT accurately (and other signs: vital signs, skin turgor, mucous membranes, daily weights, urine colour and specific gravity). Intake and output show whether the patient is receiving enough fluid and whether the kidneys are responding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Obtaining the clients weight weekly at different times of the day. — Weight should be measured DAILY, at the SAME time, with the same scale and clothing — not weekly at different times.
• Maintaining I V therapy at the keep vein open rate. — IV fluid at a "keep vein open" rate is too slow to correct dehydration.
• Monitoring arterial blood gas ABG results — ABGs are not the main monitoring tool for simple dehydration.

⭐ Key point: Signs of dehydration: thirst, dry mucous membranes, low urine output, concentrated urine, tachycardia, hypotension, and weight loss.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q80$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 120, $s$20 mmol of potassium chloride in 1000 mL normal saline is prescribed over 4 hours. What is the pump rate and how much potassium is given each hour?$s$, $s$["250 mL/hr, giving 5 mmol K⁺ per hour", "4.2 mL/hr, giving 5 mmol K⁺ per hour", "500 mL/hr, giving 10 mmol K⁺ per hour", "250 mL/hr, giving 2 mmol K⁺ per hour"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 250 mL/hr, giving 5 mmol K⁺ per hour
STEP 1 — Pump rate: 1,000 mL ÷ 4 hours = 250 mL/hr. STEP 2 — Potassium per hour: 20 mmol ÷ 4 hours = 5 mmol/hr. Check: 20 mmol/1,000 mL = 0.02 mmol/mL × 250 = 5 mmol/hr. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 4.2 mL/hr, giving 5 mmol K⁺ per hour — 4.2 mL/hr is far too slow.
• 500 mL/hr, giving 10 mmol K⁺ per hour — 500 mL/hr would run the bag in 2 hours, giving 10 mmol/hr.
• 250 mL/hr, giving 2 mmol K⁺ per hour — 2 mmol/hr is wrong.

⭐ Key point: A large volume with dilute potassium (20 mmol/L) is suitable for peripheral use, but watch for fluid overload in heart or kidney failure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q23 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 121, $s$Over 24 hours a patient received 2,400 mL IV fluid and 300 mL orally. Output was urine 1,500 mL, nasogastric aspirate 400 mL and wound drain 0 mL. What is the 24-hour fluid balance?$s$, $s$["-800 mL", "+1,200 mL", "+4,600 mL", "+800 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: +800 mL
FLUID BALANCE = INPUT − OUTPUT. Input: IV 2,400 + oral 300 = 2,700 mL. Output: urine 1,500 + NG 400 + drain 0 = 1,900 mL. Balance: 2,700 − 1,900 = +800 mL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• -800 mL — −800 mL reverses the sign.
• +1,200 mL — +1,200 mL forgets the NG losses (2,700 − 1,500).
• +4,600 mL — +4,600 mL adds input and output.

⭐ Key point: NG aspirate is rich in sodium, potassium, chloride and acid — large losses need replacement and can cause metabolic alkalosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q35 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 122, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient who has been at high altitude for several days pH 7.48, PaCO2 26 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 17 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis", "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis
At HIGH ALTITUDE, low oxygen makes people breathe faster, blowing off CO2: RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS. After SEVERAL DAYS, the kidneys excrete bicarbonate to compensate. STEP 1 — pH 7.48: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 26 LOW: RESPIRATORY. STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 17 LOW: kidneys compensating, but pH still above 7.45. Answer: PARTIALLY COMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a HIGH PaCO2.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis needs a HIGH HCO3⁻.
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Uncompensated would have a normal HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Acetazolamide speeds up this renal compensation and is used to prevent altitude sickness.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q60 · concept asked in 15 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 123, $s$Which acid–base disturbance is most likely to result from the following? Over-ventilation on a mechanical ventilator.$s$, $s$["Respiratory acidosis", "Metabolic acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis", "Respiratory alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory alkalosis
A mechanical ventilator controls how much CO2 is removed through MINUTE VENTILATION (rate × tidal volume). OVER-VENTILATION — rate or tidal volume set too high — removes TOO MUCH CO2, so the PaCO2 FALLS and the pH RISES: RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS. Effects include cerebral vasoconstriction (less blood flow to the brain), low potassium, low ionised calcium (tingling, spasm), and arrhythmias.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis results from UNDER-ventilation (CO2 builds up).
• Metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis is caused by acid gain or bicarbonate loss, not ventilator settings.
• Metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis is caused by acid loss (vomiting, NG suction) or bicarbonate gain.

⭐ Key point: Correct by reducing the rate or tidal volume, and check whether the patient is triggering extra breaths because of pain, anxiety or fever.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q61$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 124, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with septic shock who is now exhausted and hypoventilating pH 7.10, PaCO2 56 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 14 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Partially compensated metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.10: severe ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 14 LOW (METABOLIC acidosis — lactic acid from septic shock) AND PaCO2 56 HIGH (RESPIRATORY acidosis — exhausted, hypoventilating). Both push the pH DOWN. Answer: MIXED RESPIRATORY AND METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. (Winter's: expected PaCO2 = 1.5 × 14 + 8 = 29 ± 2; actual 56 is far higher.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Partial compensation would need a LOW PaCO2.
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis has a high pH.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis has a high pH.

⭐ Key point: This patient is tiring and needs urgent ventilatory support (intubation) along with sepsis treatment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q62 · concept asked in 15 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 125, $s$Which condition can cause a high anion gap metabolic acidosis?$s$, $s$["Hyperchloremic acidosis", "Renal tubular acidosis", "Lactic acidosis", "Vomiting"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lactic acidosis
LACTIC ACIDOSIS occurs when tissues lack oxygen (shock, sepsis, cardiac arrest, seizures, severe anaemia) and switch to ANAEROBIC metabolism, producing LACTIC ACID. Lactate is an "UNMEASURED ANION": it uses up bicarbonate without raising chloride, so the ANION GAP RISES — a HIGH ANION GAP METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperchloremic acidosis — Hyperchloraemic acidosis has a NORMAL anion gap (chloride rises to replace lost bicarbonate).
• Renal tubular acidosis — Renal tubular acidosis causes a NORMAL anion gap acidosis.
• Vomiting — Vomiting causes metabolic ALKALOSIS.

⭐ Key point: Lactate above 2 mmol/L is abnormal; above 4 mmol/L indicates severe hypoperfusion. Falling lactate shows successful resuscitation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q63$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 126, $s$A patient's arterial blood gas shows a metabolic acidosis with a raised anion gap in the setting of acute kidney injury. This is best explained by:$s$, $s$["No relationship between AKI and acid-base status", "Loss of bicarbonate through the kidneys alone with no other cause", "Excess bicarbonate production", "Accumulation of unmeasured acids (e.g. sulfates, phosphates) that the failing kidneys cannot excrete"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Accumulation of unmeasured acids (e.g. sulfates, phosphates) that the failing kidneys cannot excrete
In ACUTE KIDNEY INJURY, the kidneys cannot excrete daily acids (sulfates, phosphates, urate and other organic acids) or regenerate bicarbonate. These UNMEASURED ACIDS ACCUMULATE, causing a HIGH ANION GAP METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No relationship between AKI and acid-base status — AKI clearly affects acid-base status.
• Loss of bicarbonate through the kidneys alone with no other cause — Bicarbonate loss alone would give a normal anion gap.
• Excess bicarbonate production — AKI reduces, not increases, bicarbonate production.

⭐ Key point: Severe acidosis (e.g. pH below 7.15) not responding to treatment is an indication for dialysis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q64$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 127, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient on continuous nasogastric suction for 3 days pH 7.53, PaCO2 37 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 38 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis", "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis", "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.53: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 38 HIGH: METABOLIC (NG suction removing acid). STEP 3 — PaCO2 37 NORMAL: no compensation. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — Fully compensated metabolic acidosis would have a normal pH and low HCO3⁻.
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Partial compensation would need a HIGH PaCO2.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a low pH.

⭐ Key point: Replace NG losses with saline and potassium; consider a PPI to reduce acid loss.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q65 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 128, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with acute severe asthma who is now tiring with a quiet chest pH 7.31, PaCO2 65 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 26 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated respiratory acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.31: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 65 HIGH: RESPIRATORY (tiring asthmatic, not breathing enough). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 26 NORMAL: no renal compensation yet (acute). Answer: UNCOMPENSATED (ACUTE) RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS. In asthma, a RISING CO2 and a QUIET CHEST are signs of life-threatening exhaustion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis has a high pH.
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — Partial compensation would need a HIGH HCO3⁻.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis needs a LOW HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Call ICU urgently — this patient may need intubation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q66$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 129, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient admitted minutes ago after a prolonged seizure pH 7.26, PaCO2 40 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 18 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.26: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 18 is LOW: a METABOLIC cause. During a prolonged seizure, the muscles work intensely and produce LACTIC ACID. STEP 3 — PaCO2 40 is NORMAL: the lungs have NOT yet compensated (by breathing faster). Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Fully compensated respiratory acidosis would need a HIGH PaCO2 and HIGH HCO3⁻ with a normal pH.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Uncompensated respiratory acidosis needs a HIGH PaCO2.
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — FULLY compensated metabolic acidosis would have a normal pH with a LOW PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Post-seizure lactic acidosis usually clears within 1–2 hours once the seizure stops — bicarbonate is not needed. Focus on airway, breathing, glucose and stopping seizures.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q67 · concept asked in 6 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 130, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient admitted minutes ago after a prolonged seizure pH 7.24, PaCO2 41 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 13 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.24: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 13 is LOW: METABOLIC (lactic acid from the seizure). STEP 3 — PaCO2 41 is NORMAL: no respiratory compensation yet. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. (Winter's formula would expect a PaCO2 of 1.5 × 13 + 8 = 27.5 ± 2; the patient is not yet compensating, perhaps because they are post-ictal and breathing slowly.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — FULLY compensated would have a normal pH and low PaCO2.
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis has a high pH and high HCO3⁻.
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a high PaCO2 as the primary change.

⭐ Key point: Post-ictal patients may hypoventilate — keep the airway clear (recovery position, suction) and give oxygen.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q68 · concept asked in 6 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 131, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with septic shock who is now exhausted and hypoventilating pH 7.07, PaCO2 68 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 14 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.07: severe ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 14 LOW (METABOLIC — lactic acidosis) and PaCO2 68 HIGH (RESPIRATORY — exhaustion, hypoventilation). Both lower the pH. Answer: MIXED RESPIRATORY AND METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis has a high pH.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Uncompensated respiratory acidosis would have a normal HCO3⁻.
• Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — Uncompensated metabolic acidosis would have a normal PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Mixed acidosis causes very low pH quickly and is life-threatening — urgent airway and ventilatory support, plus treatment of shock.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q70 · concept asked in 15 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 132, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with liver cirrhosis who has been tachypnoeic for days pH 7.47, PaCO2 29 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 19 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis", "Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.47: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 29 mmHg is LOW: the cause is RESPIRATORY (the patient is breathing fast — in liver cirrhosis, toxins such as ammonia and hormonal changes stimulate the breathing centre, causing chronic hyperventilation). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 19 mmol/L is LOW: the kidneys have started compensating by excreting bicarbonate over the past days, but the pH is still above 7.45. Answer: PARTIALLY COMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — UNCOMPENSATED would have a NORMAL HCO3⁻ (22–26).
• Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis — Mixed acidosis would have a LOW pH.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a LOW pH as the primary disturbance.

⭐ Key point: Respiratory alkalosis is common in liver disease, pregnancy, sepsis (early), anxiety, pulmonary embolism and salicylate poisoning.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q72 · concept asked in 15 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 133, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with chronic kidney disease on the renal ward pH 7.36, PaCO2 27 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 16 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fully compensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.36: NORMAL, but on the ACIDIC side of 7.40. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 16 is LOW (metabolic acidosis — the failing kidneys cannot excrete acid or make enough bicarbonate) and PaCO2 27 is LOW (respiratory compensation by breathing more). STEP 3 — Because the pH is back within 7.35–7.45, compensation is FULL. The pH being slightly acidic shows the primary problem is the ACIDOSIS. Answer: FULLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a HIGH PaCO2.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Uncompensated respiratory acidosis needs a high PaCO2 with an abnormal pH.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — PARTIAL compensation would leave the pH below 7.35.

⭐ Key point: In CKD, chronic metabolic acidosis is treated with oral sodium bicarbonate and dialysis when severe.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q73 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 134, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient who has been vomiting for a week pH 7.46, PaCO2 53 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 38 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.46: ALKALOSIS (just above 7.45). STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 38 is HIGH: METABOLIC ALKALOSIS (a week of vomiting has removed stomach acid, H⁺ and Cl⁻). STEP 3 — PaCO2 53 is HIGH: the lungs are retaining CO2 (slower, shallower breathing) to compensate, bringing the pH close to normal but not quite into the normal range. Answer: PARTIALLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would have a LOW pH.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — UNCOMPENSATED would have a NORMAL PaCO2.
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a LOW PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Vomiting also causes dehydration, hypokalaemia and hypochloraemia — treat with IV 0.9% saline plus potassium chloride.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q74 · concept asked in 15 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 135, $s$Renal compensation for chronic respiratory alkalosis involves:$s$, $s$["Increased hydrogen ion secretion", "Increased ammoniagenesis", "Increased bicarbonate reabsorption", "Decreased renal bicarbonate reabsorption, increasing bicarbonate excretion to lower serum bicarbonate"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Decreased renal bicarbonate reabsorption, increasing bicarbonate excretion to lower serum bicarbonate
In CHRONIC RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS (e.g. at altitude, pregnancy, chronic liver disease), the PaCO2 is low and pH is high. The KIDNEYS compensate over 2–3 days by DECREASING BICARBONATE REABSORPTION in the tubules, so more bicarbonate is EXCRETED in the urine. They also secrete fewer hydrogen ions and make less ammonia. This lowers the serum bicarbonate and brings the pH back towards normal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased hydrogen ion secretion — Increased hydrogen ion secretion would raise bicarbonate and worsen alkalosis.
• Increased ammoniagenesis — Increased ammoniagenesis generates new bicarbonate, worsening alkalosis; it is reduced in compensation.
• Increased bicarbonate reabsorption — Increased bicarbonate reabsorption would worsen the alkalosis; this is the compensation for respiratory ACIDOSIS.

⭐ Key point: Expected compensation in chronic respiratory alkalosis: HCO3⁻ falls by about 4–5 mmol/L for every 10 mmHg fall in PaCO2.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q75$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 136, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with septic shock who is now exhausted and hypoventilating pH 7.09, PaCO2 64 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 18 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.09: severe ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 18 is LOW (METABOLIC acidosis — lactic acid from septic shock) AND PaCO2 64 is HIGH (RESPIRATORY acidosis — the patient is exhausted and hypoventilating). STEP 3 — Both changes push the pH DOWN; neither compensates for the other. Answer: MIXED RESPIRATORY AND METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis has a high pH.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Partial compensation of metabolic acidosis would need a LOW PaCO2.
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — Partially compensated respiratory acidosis would need a HIGH HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: This patient is in impending respiratory arrest — needs urgent intubation and ventilation, plus treatment of septic shock (antibiotics, fluids, vasopressors).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q76 · concept asked in 15 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 137, $s$A patient with metabolic acidosis has HCO3⁻ 10 mmol/L and PaCO2 15 mmHg. Using Winter's formula, what does this show?$s$, $s$["Appropriate respiratory compensation — a pure metabolic acidosis", "PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis", "PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis", "The kidneys are compensating for a respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis
WINTER'S FORMULA predicts the expected PaCO2 in metabolic acidosis: expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × HCO3⁻) + 8 ± 2. Here: (1.5 × 10) + 8 = 15 + 8 = 23 ± 2 → 21–25 mmHg. The ACTUAL PaCO2 is 15 mmHg — LOWER than expected. The patient is blowing off MORE CO2 than compensation alone explains, so there is an ADDED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS (e.g. from sepsis, salicylate poisoning, pain, anxiety, or liver failure).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Appropriate respiratory compensation — a pure metabolic acidosis — Appropriate compensation would be a PaCO2 between 21 and 25.
• PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis — An added respiratory acidosis would show a PaCO2 HIGHER than 25.
• The kidneys are compensating for a respiratory acidosis — The kidneys compensate for respiratory problems; here the primary problem is metabolic.

⭐ Key point: Salicylate (aspirin) poisoning classically causes a mixed metabolic acidosis and respiratory alkalosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q77 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 138, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with chronic kidney disease on the renal ward pH 7.36, PaCO2 26 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 18 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis", "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fully compensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.36: NORMAL, on the ACIDIC side. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 18 LOW (metabolic acidosis from CKD) and PaCO2 26 LOW (respiratory compensation). STEP 3 — pH is within normal range: FULL compensation. Answer: FULLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a HIGH PaCO2.
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — Partially compensated respiratory acidosis needs a high PaCO2 and high HCO3⁻.
• Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis — Mixed acidosis would show a HIGH PaCO2 with low HCO3⁻ and a low pH.

⭐ Key point: Metabolic acidosis is rarely fully compensated by the lungs alone in acute illness; full compensation suggests a chronic state like CKD.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q78 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 139, $s$A patient with metabolic acidosis has HCO3⁻ 18 mmol/L and PaCO2 43 mmHg. Using Winter's formula, what does this show?$s$, $s$["Appropriate respiratory compensation — a pure metabolic acidosis", "PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis", "The kidneys are compensating for a respiratory acidosis", "PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis
WINTER'S FORMULA: expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × HCO3⁻) + 8 ± 2 = (1.5 × 18) + 8 = 27 + 8 = 35 ± 2 → 33–37 mmHg. The ACTUAL PaCO2 is 43 mmHg — HIGHER than expected. The lungs are NOT blowing off enough CO2, so there is an ADDED RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (e.g. fatigue, sedation, chest infection, neuromuscular weakness).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Appropriate respiratory compensation — a pure metabolic acidosis — Appropriate compensation would be 33–37 mmHg.
• PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis — A respiratory alkalosis would show a PaCO2 BELOW 33.
• The kidneys are compensating for a respiratory acidosis — The primary problem is metabolic, not a respiratory acidosis being compensated by the kidneys.

⭐ Key point: A "normal-looking" PaCO2 in a patient with metabolic acidosis is actually abnormal — it means the lungs are failing to compensate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q80 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 140, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with severe diarrhoea who has just arrived pH 7.22, PaCO2 43 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 15 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.22: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 15 LOW: METABOLIC (severe diarrhoea loses bicarbonate). STEP 3 — PaCO2 43 NORMAL: the lungs have NOT compensated yet. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — Fully compensated would have a normal pH and low PaCO2.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a high pH and high HCO3⁻.
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a high PaCO2 as the primary change.

⭐ Key point: Diarrhoea causes a normal anion gap (hyperchloraemic) metabolic acidosis; replace fluids and potassium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q81 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 141, $s$A patient with metabolic acidosis has HCO3⁻ 16 mmol/L and PaCO2 24 mmHg. Using Winter's formula, what does this show?$s$, $s$["PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis", "PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis", "The kidneys are compensating for a respiratory acidosis", "Appropriate respiratory compensation — a pure metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis
WINTER'S FORMULA: expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × 16) + 8 ± 2 = 24 + 8 = 32 ± 2 → 30–34 mmHg. The ACTUAL PaCO2 is 24 mmHg — LOWER than expected. The patient is breathing off extra CO2 beyond normal compensation, indicating an ADDED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS (e.g. sepsis, pain, anxiety, salicylates).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis — An added respiratory acidosis would show a PaCO2 above 34.
• The kidneys are compensating for a respiratory acidosis — The kidneys compensate for respiratory problems; here the primary problem is metabolic.
• Appropriate respiratory compensation — a pure metabolic acidosis — Appropriate compensation would be 30–34.

⭐ Key point: Mixed acid–base problems are common in ICU — always compare the actual compensation with the expected value.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q83 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 142, $s$Arterial blood gas for a ventilated patient whose set rate is too high pH 7.57, PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 25 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.57: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 30 mmHg LOW: RESPIRATORY (the ventilator rate is set too high, removing too much CO2). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 25 NORMAL: no renal compensation. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS. The fix is to reduce the rate (or tidal volume) and recheck the ABG.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis needs a HIGH HCO3⁻.
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — Fully compensated metabolic acidosis needs a normal pH and low HCO3⁻.
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — Partial compensation would need a LOW HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Over-ventilation causes cerebral vasoconstriction, hypokalaemia and low ionised calcium (arrhythmias).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q84 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 143, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with chronic kidney disease on the renal ward pH 7.36, PaCO2 26 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 15 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis", "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fully compensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.36: NORMAL, on the ACIDIC side. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 15 LOW (metabolic acidosis from CKD) and PaCO2 26 LOW (respiratory compensation). STEP 3 — pH is in the normal range: FULL compensation. Answer: FULLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis needs a HIGH HCO3⁻.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis needs a high pH and high HCO3⁻.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — PARTIAL compensation would leave the pH below 7.35.

⭐ Key point: In stable CKD, the body adapts over time — ABG values should be compared with the patient's baseline.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q85 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 144, $s$Which acid–base disturbance is most likely to result from the following? Prolonged vomiting with loss of gastric acid.$s$, $s$["Respiratory acidosis", "Respiratory alkalosis", "Metabolic acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic alkalosis
PROLONGED VOMITING removes HYDROCHLORIC ACID (H⁺ and Cl⁻) from the stomach. Losing acid makes the blood more alkaline (bicarbonate rises). Dehydration activates aldosterone, causing the kidneys to retain sodium and excrete H⁺ and K⁺, maintaining the alkalosis. Result: HYPOCHLORAEMIC, HYPOKALAEMIC METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis is caused by hypoventilation.
• Respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis is caused by hyperventilation.
• Metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis is caused by acid gain or bicarbonate loss (e.g. diarrhoea), not vomiting.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV 0.9% saline and potassium chloride; the kidneys then excrete the excess bicarbonate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q86$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 145, $s$A patient in acute respiratory failure has ABG results: pH 7.28, PaCO2 60 mmHg, HCO3 26 mEq/L, PaO2 55 mmHg. This is best interpreted as:$s$, $s$["Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Compensated metabolic alkalosis", "Acute metabolic acidosis", "Acute (uncompensated) respiratory acidosis with hypoxemia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute (uncompensated) respiratory acidosis with hypoxemia
STEP 1 — pH 7.28: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 60 HIGH: RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS. STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 26 NORMAL: the kidneys have not compensated yet (ACUTE). PaO2 55 mmHg is LOW (normal 80–100): HYPOXAEMIA. Answer: ACUTE (UNCOMPENSATED) RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS WITH HYPOXAEMIA — this is acute type II respiratory failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Fully compensated would have a normal pH and high HCO3⁻.
• Compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a high pH and high HCO3⁻.
• Acute metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis needs a LOW HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Treat the cause, give controlled oxygen, and support ventilation (NIV or intubation).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q88$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 146, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with chronic kyphoscoliosis who feels well pH 7.37, PaCO2 65 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 35 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fully compensated respiratory acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.37: NORMAL, slightly on the ACIDIC side. STEP 2 — PaCO2 65 HIGH (respiratory acidosis from chronic hypoventilation due to kyphoscoliosis restricting the chest) and HCO3⁻ 35 HIGH (renal compensation). STEP 3 — pH normal: FULL compensation. Answer: FULLY COMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS. The patient "feels well" because the condition is chronic and stable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis needs a LOW PaCO2.
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a high pH as the primary problem.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Uncompensated would have an abnormal pH and normal HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: These patients may need nocturnal NIV at home; avoid excessive oxygen, which can raise CO2 further.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q89 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 147, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with septic shock and a lactate of 6 mmol/L pH 7.19, PaCO2 26 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 10 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.19: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 10 LOW: METABOLIC (lactic acidosis from septic shock, lactate 6). STEP 3 — PaCO2 26 LOW: the lungs are compensating, but the pH is still low. Answer: PARTIALLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. (Winter's: expected 1.5 × 10 + 8 = 23 ± 2; actual 26 is borderline-high, suggesting the patient may be starting to tire.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — Fully compensated would have a normal pH.
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a high pH.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a high PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Treat the shock (fluids, vasopressors, antibiotics, source control); bicarbonate is not routinely given for lactic acidosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q90 · concept asked in 15 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 148, $s$Results: Na⁺ 130, Cl⁻ 107, HCO3⁻ 12 mmol/L. What is the anion gap and its interpretation?$s$, $s$["35 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)", "11 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)", "23 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)", "11 mmol/L — normal"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 11 mmol/L — normal
ANION GAP = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻) = 130 − (107 + 12) = 130 − 119 = 11 mmol/L. This is within the NORMAL range (about 8–12). With a low bicarbonate (12), this indicates a NORMAL ANION GAP (HYPERCHLORAEMIC) METABOLIC ACIDOSIS — caused by bicarbonate loss (diarrhoea, ileostomy, renal tubular acidosis) or excess chloride (large volumes of 0.9% saline).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 35 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 35 mmol/L is a calculation error.
• 11 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 11 is correct, but it is NORMAL, not raised.
• 23 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 23 mmol/L is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Normal anion gap acidosis: think "HARDUPS" — Hyperalimentation, Acetazolamide, Renal tubular acidosis, Diarrhoea, Ureteric diversion, Pancreatic fistula, Saline.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q91 · concept asked in 7 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 149, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with septic shock who is now exhausted and hypoventilating pH 7.16, PaCO2 62 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 19 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.16: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 19 LOW (METABOLIC acidosis from septic shock) and PaCO2 62 HIGH (RESPIRATORY acidosis from exhaustion). STEP 3 — Both push pH down. Answer: MIXED RESPIRATORY AND METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Fully compensated would have a normal pH.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Uncompensated respiratory acidosis would have a normal HCO3⁻.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Partial compensation of metabolic acidosis would need a LOW PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: A rising CO2 in a septic patient signals exhaustion — prepare for intubation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q92 · concept asked in 15 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 150, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient on high-dose furosemide with a low potassium pH 7.51, PaCO2 53 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 39 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.51: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 39 HIGH: METABOLIC ALKALOSIS (furosemide causes loss of H⁺, Cl⁻ and K⁺, plus volume contraction). STEP 3 — PaCO2 53 HIGH: the lungs are compensating by retaining CO2, but pH remains high. Answer: PARTIALLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would have a low pH.
• Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a low pH and low HCO3⁻.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Uncompensated would have a normal PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Correct potassium and chloride, and review the diuretic dose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q93 · concept asked in 15 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 151, $s$Results: Na⁺ 143, Cl⁻ 95, HCO3⁻ 24 mmol/L. What is the anion gap and its interpretation?$s$, $s$["48 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)", "24 mmol/L — normal", "72 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)", "24 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 24 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)
ANION GAP = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻) = 143 − (95 + 24) = 143 − 119 = 24 mmol/L. This is RAISED (normal 8–12). Interestingly, the bicarbonate is NORMAL (24), so a raised anion gap here suggests a HIDDEN high anion gap metabolic acidosis combined with a metabolic ALKALOSIS (e.g. a patient with DKA who is also vomiting) — the two cancel out in the bicarbonate. The anion gap reveals the hidden acid.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 48 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 48 mmol/L is a calculation error.
• 24 mmol/L — normal — 24 is correct, but it is RAISED, not normal.
• 72 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 72 mmol/L is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Always calculate the anion gap — it can uncover a hidden metabolic acidosis even when bicarbonate looks normal.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q94 · concept asked in 7 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 152, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with chronic kidney disease on the renal ward pH 7.36, PaCO2 26 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 14 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis", "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fully compensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.36: NORMAL, on the ACIDIC side. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 14 LOW (METABOLIC acidosis — CKD) and PaCO2 26 LOW (respiratory compensation). STEP 3 — pH normal: FULL compensation. Answer: FULLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a high pH as the primary problem.
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a high PaCO2.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — PARTIAL compensation would leave the pH below 7.35.

⭐ Key point: Check whether the pH is below or above 7.40 to identify the primary problem in fully compensated states.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q95 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 153, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with chronic kyphoscoliosis who feels well pH 7.38, PaCO2 62 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 34 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fully compensated respiratory acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.38: NORMAL, on the ACIDIC side. STEP 2 — PaCO2 62 HIGH (respiratory acidosis from chronic kyphoscoliosis) and HCO3⁻ 34 HIGH (renal compensation). STEP 3 — pH normal: FULL compensation. Answer: FULLY COMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis needs a LOW HCO3⁻.
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — PARTIAL compensation would leave the pH below 7.35.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a high pH.

⭐ Key point: Chronic CO2 retainers rely partly on hypoxic drive; titrate oxygen to SpO2 88–92%.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q96 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 154, $s$Which ABG indicates uncompensated respiratory alkalosis?$s$, $s$["pH 7.50, PaCO2 50 mmHg, HCO3- 30 mEq/L", "pH 7.30, PaCO2 50 mmHg, HCO3- 26 mEq/L", "pH 7.40, PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3- 18 mEq/L", "pH 7.50, PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3- 24 mEq/L"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: pH 7.50, PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3- 24 mEq/L
UNCOMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS means: HIGH pH (above 7.45), LOW PaCO2 (below 35 — the cause), and NORMAL HCO3⁻ (22–26 — no renal compensation yet). Option D (pH 7.50, PaCO2 30, HCO3⁻ 24) fits exactly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• pH 7.50, PaCO2 50 mmHg, HCO3- 30 mEq/L — pH 7.50, PaCO2 50, HCO3⁻ 30 is partially compensated METABOLIC alkalosis (high HCO3⁻ with compensatory high CO2).
• pH 7.30, PaCO2 50 mmHg, HCO3- 26 mEq/L — pH 7.30, PaCO2 50, HCO3⁻ 26 is uncompensated RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS.
• pH 7.40, PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3- 18 mEq/L — pH 7.40, PaCO2 30, HCO3⁻ 18 has a normal pH — fully compensated (respiratory alkalosis or metabolic acidosis).

⭐ Key point: In uncompensated disorders, only ONE value (PaCO2 or HCO3⁻) is abnormal along with the pH.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q97 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 155, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient on continuous nasogastric suction for 3 days pH 7.48, PaCO2 40 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 33 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Partially compensated metabolic acidosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.48: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 33 is HIGH: METABOLIC ALKALOSIS. Continuous NASOGASTRIC SUCTION removes stomach acid (H⁺ and Cl⁻), so the blood becomes alkaline. STEP 3 — PaCO2 40 is NORMAL: no respiratory compensation. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a low pH and low HCO3⁻.
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a low pH and high PaCO2.
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — PARTIAL compensation would show a HIGH PaCO2 (above 45).

⭐ Key point: Measure NG losses and replace them with 0.9% saline plus potassium chloride; a PPI reduces acid loss.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q98 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 156, $s$A patient with severe acute pancreatitis has hypocalcaemia. This is primarily explained by:$s$, $s$["Calcium being consumed in saponification (fat necrosis) reactions within the abdomen", "A pre-existing unrelated condition", "Excess dietary calcium intake", "Overactive parathyroid glands"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Calcium being consumed in saponification (fat necrosis) reactions within the abdomen
In SEVERE ACUTE PANCREATITIS, pancreatic enzymes digest fat around the pancreas (FAT NECROSIS). The released fatty acids bind CALCIUM to form insoluble "soaps" (SAPONIFICATION). This removes calcium from the blood, causing HYPOCALCAEMIA. It indicates severe disease.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A pre-existing unrelated condition — It is directly related to the pancreatitis.
• Excess dietary calcium intake — Excess dietary calcium does not cause hypocalcaemia.
• Overactive parathyroid glands — Overactive parathyroid glands cause HIGH calcium.

⭐ Key point: Monitor calcium and correct with IV calcium gluconate if symptomatic.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q164$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 157, $s$How many grams of glucose are in 1000 mL of 10% dextrose?$s$, $s$["100 g", "50 g", "10,000 g", "1,000 g"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 100 g
A PERCENTAGE solution (% w/v) means GRAMS per 100 mL. So 10% dextrose = 10 g in every 100 mL. In 1,000 mL: 10 × 10 = 100 g of glucose.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50 g — 50 g is in 1,000 mL of 5% dextrose.
• 10,000 g — 10,000 g is a gross error (ten kilograms).
• 1,000 g — 1,000 g is ten times too much.

⭐ Key point: 1 g of glucose gives about 4 kcal, so 1 L of 10% dextrose gives about 400 kcal; 5% dextrose gives about 200 kcal per litre.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q16 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 158, $s$Over 24 hours a patient received 2,400 mL IV fluid and 0 mL orally. Output was urine 1,500 mL, nasogastric aspirate 0 mL and wound drain 100 mL. What is the 24-hour fluid balance?$s$, $s$["+4,000 mL", "+900 mL", "+800 mL", "-800 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: +800 mL
FLUID BALANCE = TOTAL INPUT − TOTAL OUTPUT. Input: IV 2,400 mL + oral 0 = 2,400 mL. Output: urine 1,500 + NG 0 + drain 100 = 1,600 mL. Balance: 2,400 − 1,600 = +800 mL (POSITIVE balance — more in than out).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• +4,000 mL — +4,000 mL adds input and output together.
• +900 mL — +900 mL forgets the drain output (2,400 − 1,500).
• -800 mL — −800 mL reverses the sign.

⭐ Key point: Remember insensible losses (skin, breathing — about 500–800 mL/day, more with fever) are not measured, so the true balance is less positive.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q24 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 159, $s$Over 24 hours a patient received 2,500 mL IV fluid and 800 mL orally. Output was urine 1,500 mL, nasogastric aspirate 600 mL and wound drain 350 mL. What is the 24-hour fluid balance?$s$, $s$["+1,800 mL", "+850 mL", "+5,750 mL", "-850 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: +850 mL
FLUID BALANCE = INPUT − OUTPUT. Input: IV 2,500 + oral 800 = 3,300 mL. Output: urine 1,500 + NG 600 + drain 350 = 2,450 mL. Balance: 3,300 − 2,450 = +850 mL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• +1,800 mL — +1,800 mL forgets the NG and drain losses (3,300 − 1,500).
• +5,750 mL — +5,750 mL adds input and output.
• -850 mL — −850 mL reverses the sign.

⭐ Key point: Include ALL inputs (IV, oral, NG feeds, drug infusions) and ALL outputs (urine, drains, NG aspirate, vomit, stool) in the fluid chart.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q32 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 160, $s$20 mmol of potassium chloride in 500 mL normal saline is prescribed over 4 hours. What is the pump rate and how much potassium is given each hour?$s$, $s$["125 mL/hr, giving 5 mmol K⁺ per hour", "125 mL/hr, giving 4 mmol K⁺ per hour", "2.1 mL/hr, giving 5 mmol K⁺ per hour", "250 mL/hr, giving 10 mmol K⁺ per hour"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 125 mL/hr, giving 5 mmol K⁺ per hour
STEP 1 — Rate: 500 mL ÷ 4 hours = 125 mL/hr. STEP 2 — Potassium per hour: 20 mmol ÷ 4 hours = 5 mmol/hr. Check: 20 mmol in 500 mL = 0.04 mmol/mL × 125 mL/hr = 5 mmol/hr. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 125 mL/hr, giving 4 mmol K⁺ per hour — 4 mmol/hr is wrong (20 ÷ 5).
• 2.1 mL/hr, giving 5 mmol K⁺ per hour — 2.1 mL/hr is far too slow.
• 250 mL/hr, giving 10 mmol K⁺ per hour — 250 mL/hr would run in 2 hours, giving 10 mmol/hr.

⭐ Key point: Peripheral potassium is usually given at no more than 10 mmol/hr; faster rates need a central line and ECG monitoring. Always use a pump, and never give potassium as an IV bolus.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q33 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 161, $s$40 mmol of potassium chloride in 1000 mL normal saline is prescribed over 4 hours. What is the pump rate and how much potassium is given each hour?$s$, $s$["250 mL/hr, giving 4 mmol K⁺ per hour", "250 mL/hr, giving 10 mmol K⁺ per hour", "4.2 mL/hr, giving 10 mmol K⁺ per hour", "500 mL/hr, giving 20 mmol K⁺ per hour"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 250 mL/hr, giving 10 mmol K⁺ per hour
STEP 1 — Rate: 1,000 mL ÷ 4 hours = 250 mL/hr. STEP 2 — Potassium per hour: 40 mmol ÷ 4 hours = 10 mmol/hr. Check: 40 mmol/1,000 mL = 0.04 mmol/mL × 250 = 10 mmol/hr. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 250 mL/hr, giving 4 mmol K⁺ per hour — 4 mmol/hr is wrong.
• 4.2 mL/hr, giving 10 mmol K⁺ per hour — 4.2 mL/hr is far too slow.
• 500 mL/hr, giving 20 mmol K⁺ per hour — 500 mL/hr would run in 2 hours, giving 20 mmol/hr (dangerous peripherally).

⭐ Key point: 10 mmol/hr is generally the maximum rate via a peripheral line. Check potassium levels and kidney function before and during replacement.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q51 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 162, $s$Which acid–base disturbance is most likely to result from the following? Hypokalaemia from diuretics.$s$, $s$["Respiratory alkalosis", "Metabolic acidosis", "Respiratory acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic alkalosis
Loop and thiazide DIURETICS cause loss of potassium, chloride and hydrogen ions in the urine, and volume contraction. With low potassium, hydrogen ions move into cells and the kidneys excrete more hydrogen, so the blood becomes alkaline: METABOLIC ALKALOSIS (high pH, high bicarbonate). This is called "contraction alkalosis" with hypokalaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis is caused by hyperventilation.
• Metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis is caused by acid gain or bicarbonate loss (e.g. diarrhoea, DKA).
• Respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis is caused by hypoventilation.

⭐ Key point: Treat by replacing potassium and chloride (e.g. KCl) and correcting volume.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q60$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 163, $s$Arterial blood gas for an anxious young woman who is hyperventilating and has tingling around her mouth pH 7.50, PaCO2 22 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 24 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.50 = ALKALOSIS (normal 7.35–7.45). STEP 2 — PaCO2 22 mmHg is LOW (normal 35–45) → RESPIRATORY cause (blowing off CO2 by hyperventilation). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 24 is NORMAL (22–26) → the kidneys have NOT compensated yet. So: UNCOMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS. The tingling around the mouth is from low ionised calcium caused by alkalosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — Fully compensated metabolic acidosis would have a normal pH and low HCO3⁻.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis has a LOW pH and HIGH PaCO2.
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — Partial compensation would show a LOW HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Treat anxiety-related hyperventilation with reassurance and slow breathing; exclude serious causes (PE, asthma, sepsis).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q61 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 164, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with severe diarrhoea who has just arrived pH 7.29, PaCO2 42 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 17 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.29 = ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 17 is LOW → METABOLIC cause (diarrhoea loses bicarbonate). STEP 3 — PaCO2 42 is NORMAL → the lungs have NOT yet compensated. So: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — Full compensation would bring pH into the normal range.
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would show a HIGH PaCO2.
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a HIGH pH and HIGH HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Respiratory compensation for metabolic acidosis (deep, rapid Kussmaul breathing) usually starts within minutes to hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q62 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 165, $s$Sodium bicarbonate during cardiac arrest is most appropriate for:$s$, $s$["Replacing epinephrine when unavailable", "Routine use in every cardiac arrest to correct acidosis", "Ventricular fibrillation as a first-line agent", "Known severe hyperkalemia or tricyclic antidepressant overdose"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Known severe hyperkalemia or tricyclic antidepressant overdose
SODIUM BICARBONATE is NOT given routinely in cardiac arrest — it can worsen intracellular acidosis, cause hypernatraemia and shift the oxygen curve. It is given in specific situations: known severe HYPERKALAEMIA (it moves potassium into cells) and TRICYCLIC ANTIDEPRESSANT OVERDOSE (it overcomes sodium channel blockade). It may also be considered in severe metabolic acidosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Replacing epinephrine when unavailable — Bicarbonate is not a substitute for adrenaline.
• Routine use in every cardiac arrest to correct acidosis — Routine use in every arrest is not recommended.
• Ventricular fibrillation as a first-line agent — In VF, the first-line treatments are defibrillation, adrenaline and amiodarone.

⭐ Key point: Do not give bicarbonate through the same line as adrenaline or calcium without flushing — they precipitate or inactivate each other.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q63$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 166, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient admitted minutes ago after a prolonged seizure pH 7.26, PaCO2 39 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 16 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis", "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.26 = ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 16 is LOW → METABOLIC (lactic acidosis from muscle activity during the seizure). STEP 3 — PaCO2 39 is NORMAL → NOT compensated. So: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis would have a high pH.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Partial compensation would show a LOW PaCO2.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would have a HIGH PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Post-seizure lactic acidosis usually clears within an hour once the seizure stops.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q64 · concept asked in 6 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 167, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with COPD admitted with an infective exacerbation pH 7.34, PaCO2 70 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 34 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated respiratory acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.34 = mildly ACIDOTIC. STEP 2 — PaCO2 70 is HIGH → RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (COPD causes CO2 retention). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 34 is HIGH → the kidneys have retained bicarbonate to compensate, but the pH is still below normal → PARTIALLY COMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a high pH and low PaCO2.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Uncompensated would show a normal HCO3⁻.
• Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis — Mixed acidosis would show a LOW HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: In COPD, target SpO2 88–92%, and consider NIV if pH < 7.35 with high PaCO2.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q66 · concept asked in 15 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 168, $s$Results: Na⁺ 141, Cl⁻ 105, HCO3⁻ 14 mmol/L. What is the anion gap and its interpretation?$s$, $s$["50 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)", "36 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)", "22 mmol/L — normal", "22 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 22 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)
ANION GAP = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻) = 141 − (105 + 14) = 141 − 119 = 22 mmol/L. Normal anion gap is about 8–12 (up to 16 if potassium is included). So 22 is RAISED — a HIGH ANION GAP METABOLIC ACIDOSIS (e.g. lactic acidosis, DKA, renal failure, toxins).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 50 mmol/L is a calculation error.
• 36 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 36 mmol/L is a calculation error.
• 22 mmol/L — normal — 22 is correct, but it is RAISED, not normal.

⭐ Key point: Causes of high anion gap acidosis: "MUDPILES" — Methanol, Uraemia, DKA, Paracetamol/Propylene glycol, Iron/Isoniazid, Lactic acidosis, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q67 · concept asked in 7 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 169, $s$Arterial blood gas for an anxious young woman who is hyperventilating and has tingling around her mouth pH 7.51, PaCO2 22 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 24 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis", "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.51 = ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 22 is LOW → RESPIRATORY (hyperventilation). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 24 is NORMAL → NOT compensated. So: UNCOMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would show a low pH and high PaCO2.
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would show a high HCO3⁻.
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — Partial compensation would show a low HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Perioral tingling and carpopedal spasm are caused by alkalosis lowering ionised calcium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q68 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 170, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient admitted minutes ago after a prolonged seizure pH 7.27, PaCO2 39 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 14 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.27 = ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 14 is LOW → METABOLIC (lactic acidosis after seizure). STEP 3 — PaCO2 39 is NORMAL → NOT compensated. So: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — Full compensation would bring pH to normal and lower the PaCO2.
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a high pH.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a high pH and high HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Check lactate and glucose after prolonged seizures.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q69 · concept asked in 6 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 171, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with chronic kidney disease on the renal ward pH 7.36, PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 14 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis", "Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fully compensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.36 = NORMAL (low-normal, on the acidic side). STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 14 is LOW → METABOLIC ACIDOSIS (CKD — the kidneys can't excrete acid or make bicarbonate). STEP 3 — PaCO2 30 is LOW → the lungs are blowing off CO2 to compensate. Because the pH is back within the normal range, this is FULLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would have a HIGH PaCO2.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a HIGH HCO3⁻.
• Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis — Mixed acidosis would have a HIGH PaCO2 with low HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: In compensated states, the pH tends to lie on the side of the primary problem (7.36 = acidic side → primary acidosis).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q70 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 172, $s$Arterial blood gas for an anxious young woman who is hyperventilating and has tingling around her mouth pH 7.53, PaCO2 22 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 23 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis
Use the three-step method. STEP 1 — pH: 7.53 is above 7.45, so the blood is ALKALOTIC. STEP 2 — Which system caused it? PaCO2 22 mmHg is LOW (normal 35–45). Low CO2 makes the blood alkaline, so the cause is RESPIRATORY (she is breathing fast and "blowing off" CO2 because of anxiety). STEP 3 — Compensation: HCO3⁻ 23 mmol/L is NORMAL (22–26), so the kidneys have NOT yet started to lower bicarbonate (renal compensation takes 2–3 days). Answer: UNCOMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS. The tingling around the mouth happens because alkalosis lowers the IONISED calcium in the blood.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — PARTIALLY compensated would need a LOW HCO3⁻ (below 22) with the pH still abnormal.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — METABOLIC alkalosis needs a HIGH HCO3⁻ (above 26); here it is normal.
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory ACIDOSIS needs a LOW pH and HIGH PaCO2 — the opposite of this gas.

⭐ Key point: Treat panic hyperventilation with calm reassurance and slow breathing, but first exclude serious causes such as pulmonary embolism, asthma, sepsis and DKA.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q71 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 173, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with severe diarrhoea who has just arrived pH 7.26, PaCO2 44 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 14 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Partially compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.26 is below 7.35: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 14 mmol/L is LOW (normal 22–26): a METABOLIC cause. Severe DIARRHOEA loses bicarbonate-rich fluid from the bowel. STEP 3 — PaCO2 44 mmHg is NORMAL (35–45): the lungs have NOT yet compensated by blowing off CO2. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. (Expected compensation by Winter's formula would be a PaCO2 of 1.5 × 14 + 8 = 29 ± 2 mmHg.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would need a HIGH PaCO2 as the main problem.
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — FULLY compensated would bring the pH back into the normal range with a low PaCO2.
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would show a HIGH pH and LOW PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Diarrhoea causes a NORMAL anion gap (hyperchloraemic) acidosis. Treat with fluid and electrolyte replacement (including potassium).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q72 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 174, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient on continuous nasogastric suction for 3 days pH 7.56, PaCO2 43 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 36 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.56 is above 7.45: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 36 mmol/L is HIGH: a METABOLIC cause. Continuous NASOGASTRIC SUCTION removes stomach acid (HCl), so the body loses hydrogen and chloride ions and bicarbonate rises. STEP 3 — PaCO2 43 mmHg is NORMAL: the lungs have NOT compensated (compensation would be slow, shallow breathing to retain CO2). Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis — Mixed acidosis would show a LOW pH.
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — PARTIALLY compensated would need a HIGH PaCO2 (above 45) with the pH still high.
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would show a LOW pH and HIGH PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: NG losses also cause low potassium and chloride. Replace losses with 0.9% saline plus potassium chloride.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q73 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 175, $s$A patient with metabolic acidosis has HCO3⁻ 8 mmol/L and PaCO2 20 mmHg. Using Winter's formula, what does this show?$s$, $s$["PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis", "PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis", "The kidneys are compensating for a respiratory acidosis", "Appropriate respiratory compensation — a pure metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Appropriate respiratory compensation — a pure metabolic acidosis
WINTER'S FORMULA predicts the PaCO2 the lungs SHOULD reach when compensating for a metabolic acidosis: Expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × HCO3⁻) + 8 ± 2. Here: (1.5 × 8) + 8 = 12 + 8 = 20 ± 2 = 18–22 mmHg. The ACTUAL PaCO2 is 20 mmHg — exactly within the expected range. So the lungs are compensating APPROPRIATELY, and this is a PURE (simple) METABOLIC ACIDOSIS with no extra respiratory problem.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• PaCO2 is higher than expected — an added respiratory acidosis — A PaCO2 HIGHER than 22 would mean an added respiratory acidosis (e.g. the patient is tiring).
• PaCO2 is lower than expected — an added respiratory alkalosis — A PaCO2 LOWER than 18 would mean an added respiratory alkalosis.
• The kidneys are compensating for a respiratory acidosis — The kidneys compensate for RESPIRATORY problems; here the primary problem is metabolic.

⭐ Key point: Winter's formula only applies to METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. Use it to detect hidden mixed disorders.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q74 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 176, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with severe pain after a fracture breathing at 32/min pH 7.50, PaCO2 30 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 26 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.50: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 30 mmHg is LOW: a RESPIRATORY cause (severe pain makes the patient breathe fast at 32/min, blowing off CO2). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 26 mmol/L is at the top of NORMAL: the kidneys have NOT compensated. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would show a LOW pH and LOW HCO3⁻.
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would show a LOW pH and HIGH PaCO2.
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — PARTIAL compensation would need a LOW HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Treat the cause — give adequate analgesia. Pain, anxiety, fever, hypoxia, sepsis and PE can all cause hyperventilation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q75$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 177, $s$Arterial blood gas for a post-operative patient still drowsy from anaesthesia with shallow breathing pH 7.29, PaCO2 54 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 25 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated respiratory acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.29: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 54 mmHg is HIGH: a RESPIRATORY cause. The patient is drowsy from anaesthetic drugs (opioids, residual sedatives) and breathing shallowly, so CO2 builds up (HYPOVENTILATION). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 25 mmol/L is NORMAL: the kidneys have not compensated (it takes days). Answer: UNCOMPENSATED (ACUTE) RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis needs a LOW HCO3⁻.
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — FULLY compensated metabolic acidosis would have a normal pH and low HCO3⁻.
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — FULLY compensated respiratory acidosis would have a normal pH and HIGH HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Treat by stimulating and positioning the patient, encouraging deep breathing, reversing opioids (naloxone) if needed, and supporting ventilation if the patient remains drowsy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q76$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 178, $s$Results: Na⁺ 138, Cl⁻ 98, HCO3⁻ 10 mmol/L. What is the anion gap and its interpretation?$s$, $s$["30 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)", "30 mmol/L — normal", "40 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)", "50 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 30 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)
ANION GAP = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻). Here: 138 − (98 + 10) = 138 − 108 = 30 mmol/L. The normal anion gap is about 8–12 mmol/L (up to 16 if potassium is included). 30 is clearly RAISED, showing a HIGH ANION GAP METABOLIC ACIDOSIS — extra unmeasured acids (e.g. lactate, ketones, toxins, uraemic acids) are present.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 30 mmol/L — normal — 30 is correct, but it is NOT normal.
• 40 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 40 mmol/L is a calculation error.
• 50 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 50 mmol/L is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Common causes in ICU: lactic acidosis (shock, sepsis), DKA, kidney failure, and poisoning (methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylates).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q77 · concept asked in 7 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 179, $s$Arterial blood gas for an obese patient with obesity hypoventilation syndrome and a chest infection pH 7.34, PaCO2 63 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 33 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated respiratory acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.34: slightly ACIDOTIC. STEP 2 — PaCO2 63 mmHg is HIGH: a RESPIRATORY cause (obesity hypoventilation — the heavy chest wall and abdomen limit breathing, so CO2 builds up). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 33 mmol/L is HIGH: the kidneys have retained bicarbonate over time to buffer the CO2, but the pH is still below 7.35. Answer: PARTIALLY COMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (likely chronic with an acute worsening from the chest infection).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a HIGH pH.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — UNCOMPENSATED would show a normal HCO3⁻.
• Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a LOW HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: These patients often need non-invasive ventilation (BiPAP) and controlled oxygen therapy (target SpO2 88–92%).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q78 · concept asked in 15 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 180, $s$Arterial blood gases (ABGs) show respiratory alkalosis, and CO2 is very low that is 25 mm of Hg while on ventilator setting respiratory rate is set at 22b/m. What would you do with respiratory rate?$s$, $s$["Do not change the rate", "None of them", "Decrease respiratory rate", "Increase respiratory rate"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Decrease respiratory rate
RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS with a LOW PaCO2 (25 mmHg) in a ventilated patient means the ventilator is "BLOWING OFF" TOO MUCH CO2 — the patient is being OVER-VENTILATED. Minute ventilation = rate × tidal volume, so to raise the CO2 back towards normal (35–45), the nurse/doctor should DECREASE THE RESPIRATORY RATE (or the tidal volume). A rough guide: new rate = current rate × current PaCO2 ÷ target PaCO2 = 22 × 25 ÷ 40 ≈ 14 breaths/min.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Do not change the rate — Not changing the rate leaves the patient alkalotic, which can cause arrhythmias, low potassium and calcium, and reduced brain blood flow.
• None of them — "None of them" is wrong because decreasing the rate is the correct action.
• Increase respiratory rate — Increasing the rate would lower the CO2 even further and worsen the alkalosis.

⭐ Key point: Check whether the patient is triggering extra breaths (pain, anxiety, fever) before changing settings; ventilator changes are made per the doctor's order or unit protocol.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q79$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 181, $s$Arterial blood gas for an anxious young woman who is hyperventilating and has tingling around her mouth pH 7.51, PaCO2 31 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 26 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis", "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis", "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.51: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 31 mmHg is LOW: RESPIRATORY cause (anxious hyperventilation). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 26 mmol/L is NORMAL (upper limit): no renal compensation yet. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would need a HIGH HCO3⁻ as the primary change.
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — PARTIAL compensation would need a LOW HCO3⁻.
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a LOW pH.

⭐ Key point: Any time the pH is abnormal and only ONE value (PaCO2 or HCO3⁻) is abnormal, the disorder is uncompensated.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q80 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 182, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient on continuous nasogastric suction for 3 days pH 7.51, PaCO2 37 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 36 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Partially compensated respiratory acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis", "Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.51: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 36 mmol/L is HIGH: METABOLIC cause (NG suction removing stomach acid). STEP 3 — PaCO2 37 mmHg is NORMAL: the lungs have not compensated. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would have a LOW pH and HIGH PaCO2.
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — PARTIAL compensation would need a HIGH PaCO2 (above 45).
• Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a LOW pH and LOW HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Metabolic alkalosis from vomiting or NG loss is "chloride-responsive" — it improves with IV 0.9% saline and potassium replacement.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q81 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 183, $s$Arterial blood gas for a ventilated patient whose set rate is too high pH 7.55, PaCO2 26 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 23 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.55: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 26 mmHg is LOW: RESPIRATORY cause — the ventilator rate is set too high, so too much CO2 is removed. STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 23 mmol/L is NORMAL: no compensation. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS. The fix is to reduce the ventilator rate or tidal volume.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — PARTIAL compensation would need a LOW HCO3⁻.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would have a LOW pH and HIGH PaCO2.
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a LOW pH.

⭐ Key point: Over-ventilation can cause cerebral vasoconstriction, low potassium, low ionised calcium and arrhythmias.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q82 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 184, $s$Arterial blood gas for a ventilated patient whose set rate is too high pH 7.53, PaCO2 26 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 25 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis", "Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.53: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 26 mmHg is LOW: RESPIRATORY cause (the ventilator rate is too high). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 25 mmol/L is NORMAL: no compensation. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — PARTIAL compensation would show a LOW HCO3⁻.
• Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis — Mixed acidosis would show a LOW pH.
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would show a HIGH HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: Recheck the ABG about 20–30 minutes after a ventilator change.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q83 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 185, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with chronic kyphoscoliosis who feels well pH 7.36, PaCO2 62 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 34 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fully compensated respiratory acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.36: NORMAL (but on the ACIDIC side of 7.40). STEP 2 — PaCO2 62 mmHg is HIGH (respiratory acidosis) and HCO3⁻ 34 mmol/L is HIGH (metabolic alkalosis). Which is the primary problem? The pH is on the acidic side, so the PRIMARY problem is the one that causes acidosis — the HIGH CO2 (chronic hypoventilation from kyphoscoliosis restricting the chest). STEP 3 — The kidneys have raised bicarbonate enough to bring the pH back into the normal range. Answer: FULLY COMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS. The patient "feels well", which fits a chronic, stable state.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — UNCOMPENSATED would have an abnormal pH and normal HCO3⁻.
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would need a LOW HCO3⁻.
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would need a LOW PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Full compensation brings the pH into 7.35–7.45; the side of 7.40 it lies on shows the primary disorder.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q84 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 186, $s$A patient with poorly controlled diabetes presents with fruity breath odor, Kussmaul respirations, and severe hyperglycemia. Kussmaul respirations occur as a compensatory response to which acid-base disturbance?$s$, $s$["Metabolic alkalosis", "Metabolic acidosis, with deep rapid breathing to blow off CO2 and raise pH", "Respiratory alkalosis", "Respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic acidosis, with deep rapid breathing to blow off CO2 and raise pH
In DIABETIC KETOACIDOSIS (DKA), lack of insulin causes the body to burn fat, producing KETONE acids. These acids lower the bicarbonate and pH: METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. The respiratory centre responds by making the patient breathe DEEPLY and RAPIDLY (KUSSMAUL RESPIRATIONS) to BLOW OFF CO2, which helps RAISE the pH towards normal. The fruity (acetone) breath is from ketones.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would cause slow, shallow breathing (to retain CO2), not Kussmaul breathing.
• Respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis is CAUSED by hyperventilation; Kussmaul breathing here is a RESPONSE to acidosis.
• Respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis is caused by HYPOventilation.

⭐ Key point: Treat DKA with IV fluids, fixed-rate insulin, potassium replacement and close monitoring of glucose, ketones, pH and electrolytes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q85$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 187, $s$Results: Na⁺ 140, Cl⁻ 109, HCO3⁻ 10 mmol/L. What is the anion gap and its interpretation?$s$, $s$["31 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)", "21 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)", "21 mmol/L — normal", "41 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 21 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)
ANION GAP = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻) = 140 − (109 + 10) = 140 − 119 = 21 mmol/L. Normal is about 8–12 mmol/L (up to 16 with potassium). 21 is RAISED: a HIGH ANION GAP METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 31 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 31 mmol/L is a calculation error.
• 21 mmol/L — normal — 21 is correct, but it is RAISED, not normal.
• 41 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 41 mmol/L is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: When the anion gap is raised, check lactate, ketones, creatinine/urea, and consider toxins (osmolar gap).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q86 · concept asked in 7 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 188, $s$A stable patient with severe COPD is likely to show which arterial blood gas pattern at baseline?$s$, $s$["Marked hypocapnia with elevated pH", "Normal PaCO2 with severe metabolic acidosis", "Acute respiratory alkalosis with low bicarbonate", "Chronic hypercapnia with compensatory elevation of bicarbonate and near-normal pH"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Chronic hypercapnia with compensatory elevation of bicarbonate and near-normal pH
In STABLE, severe COPD, the damaged lungs cannot remove CO2 efficiently, so the PaCO2 stays HIGH all the time (CHRONIC HYPERCAPNIA). Over days to weeks, the KIDNEYS RETAIN BICARBONATE to buffer the extra acid, raising HCO3⁻ (e.g. 30–36 mmol/L). This brings the pH back to NEAR-NORMAL (often 7.35–7.40). So the baseline gas is a COMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Marked hypocapnia with elevated pH — Marked hypocapnia (low CO2) with high pH is respiratory alkalosis, not typical of COPD.
• Normal PaCO2 with severe metabolic acidosis — COPD causes high CO2, not normal CO2 with severe metabolic acidosis.
• Acute respiratory alkalosis with low bicarbonate — Acute respiratory alkalosis with low bicarbonate is not the usual COPD pattern.

⭐ Key point: Knowing a patient's baseline gas helps: in an exacerbation, the CO2 rises further and the pH falls (acute-on-chronic respiratory acidosis).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q87$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 189, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient on high-dose furosemide with a low potassium pH 7.48, PaCO2 51 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 36 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.48: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 36 mmol/L is HIGH: METABOLIC ALKALOSIS (FUROSEMIDE causes loss of hydrogen, chloride and potassium in urine, and volume contraction). STEP 3 — PaCO2 51 mmHg is HIGH: the lungs are compensating by HYPOVENTILATING to retain CO2, but the pH is still above 7.45. Answer: PARTIALLY COMPENSATED METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis — Mixed acidosis would have a LOW pH.
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — FULLY compensated respiratory acidosis would have a normal pH on the acidic side.
• Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis — UNCOMPENSATED would have a normal PaCO2.

⭐ Key point: Correct the low potassium and chloride; consider reducing the diuretic dose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q88 · concept asked in 15 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 190, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient just resuscitated from a cardiac arrest pH 7.07, PaCO2 61 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 16 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Partially compensated metabolic acidosis", "Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.07: severe ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 61 mmHg is HIGH (RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS — no effective breathing during arrest) AND HCO3⁻ 16 mmol/L is LOW (METABOLIC ACIDOSIS — lactic acid from poor tissue perfusion during arrest). STEP 3 — BOTH values are pushing the pH DOWN in the same direction, so neither is compensating for the other. Answer: MIXED RESPIRATORY AND METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. This explains the very low pH.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — Partial compensation of metabolic acidosis would require a LOW PaCO2.
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a HIGH pH.
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a HIGH pH and HIGH HCO3⁻.

⭐ Key point: After cardiac arrest, ensure adequate ventilation (normal CO2), restore perfusion and oxygenation; the lactic acidosis usually improves as circulation recovers.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q89 · concept asked in 15 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 191, $s$Hypokalemia tends to promote metabolic alkalosis in part because it:$s$, $s$["Promotes an intracellular shift of hydrogen ions and enhances renal hydrogen ion secretion and ammoniagenesis", "Directly raises plasma bicarbonate through increased dietary intake", "Suppresses aldosterone secretion", "Increases respiratory rate, lowering PaCO2 and directly raising pH"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Promotes an intracellular shift of hydrogen ions and enhances renal hydrogen ion secretion and ammoniagenesis
HYPOKALAEMIA promotes METABOLIC ALKALOSIS in two main ways: (1) when blood potassium is low, potassium moves OUT of cells and HYDROGEN IONS move INTO cells to keep the electrical balance — so the blood loses hydrogen ions and becomes alkaline; (2) the KIDNEYS respond by SECRETING MORE HYDROGEN ions into the urine and increasing AMMONIA production (ammoniagenesis), which generates more new bicarbonate. Both effects raise plasma bicarbonate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Directly raises plasma bicarbonate through increased dietary intake — Diet does not directly raise plasma bicarbonate in this way.
• Suppresses aldosterone secretion — Hypokalaemia does not suppress aldosterone; actually, high aldosterone causes potassium loss.
• Increases respiratory rate, lowering PaCO2 and directly raising pH — Hypokalaemia does not increase respiratory rate to cause alkalosis.

⭐ Key point: Metabolic alkalosis is difficult to correct until potassium (and chloride) are replaced.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q90$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 192, $s$Arterial blood gas for a ventilated patient whose set rate is too high pH 7.57, PaCO2 24 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 22 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis", "Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Uncompensated metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.57: ALKALOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 24 mmHg is LOW: RESPIRATORY cause (ventilator rate set too high). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 22 mmol/L is at the low end of NORMAL: no compensation. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — PARTIAL compensation would show a LOW HCO3⁻ (below 22).
• Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis — Mixed acidosis would show a LOW pH.
• Uncompensated metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would show a LOW pH.

⭐ Key point: Reduce minute ventilation (rate or tidal volume) and recheck the ABG.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q91 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 193, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient admitted minutes ago after a prolonged seizure pH 7.25, PaCO2 40 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 17 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Fully compensated metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.25: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 17 mmol/L is LOW: METABOLIC (lactic acidosis from intense muscle activity during the seizure). STEP 3 — PaCO2 40 mmHg is NORMAL: no respiratory compensation yet. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated metabolic acidosis — FULL compensation would bring the pH into normal range with a low PaCO2.
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would have a HIGH PaCO2.
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a HIGH pH.

⭐ Key point: Post-seizure lactic acidosis usually resolves within an hour without bicarbonate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q92 · concept asked in 6 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 194, $s$Arterial blood gas for an obese patient with obesity hypoventilation syndrome and a chest infection pH 7.30, PaCO2 62 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 31 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Partially compensated respiratory acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.30: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — PaCO2 62 mmHg is HIGH: RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (obesity hypoventilation plus chest infection). STEP 3 — HCO3⁻ 31 mmol/L is HIGH: renal compensation has begun, but the pH is still low. Answer: PARTIALLY COMPENSATED RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — FULLY compensated would have a normal pH.
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a HIGH pH.
• Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a HIGH pH.

⭐ Key point: Consider NIV and treat the infection; avoid excessive oxygen.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q93 · concept asked in 15 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 195, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient with severe diarrhoea who has just arrived pH 7.30, PaCO2 41 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 18 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Partially compensated metabolic acidosis", "Uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.30: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 18 mmol/L is LOW: METABOLIC (diarrhoea loses bicarbonate). STEP 3 — PaCO2 41 mmHg is NORMAL: no respiratory compensation yet. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partially compensated metabolic acidosis — PARTIAL compensation would require a LOW PaCO2.
• Uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a HIGH PaCO2.
• Partially compensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a HIGH pH.

⭐ Key point: Compensation for metabolic acidosis is respiratory (fast, deep breathing) and usually begins within hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q94 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 196, $s$The expected respiratory compensation for metabolic alkalosis is:$s$, $s$["Hypoventilation, retaining CO2 to help lower pH toward normal", "Immediate renal correction only", "Hyperventilation to blow off more CO2", "No respiratory change occurs"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypoventilation, retaining CO2 to help lower pH toward normal
In METABOLIC ALKALOSIS, the blood has too much bicarbonate and the pH is high. The lungs compensate by HYPOVENTILATING (slower, shallower breathing), which RETAINS CO2. Extra CO2 forms carbonic acid, which helps LOWER the pH back towards normal. This compensation is limited because hypoventilation also lowers oxygen; PaCO2 rarely rises above about 55 mmHg for compensation alone.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate renal correction only — The kidneys are the main correctors, but respiratory compensation also occurs quickly.
• Hyperventilation to blow off more CO2 — Hyperventilation would lower CO2 and worsen the alkalosis.
• No respiratory change occurs — A respiratory change does occur.

⭐ Key point: Expected PaCO2 in metabolic alkalosis ≈ 0.7 × HCO3⁻ + 20 (± 5).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q95$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 197, $s$Arterial blood gas for a patient admitted minutes ago after a prolonged seizure pH 7.21, PaCO2 43 mmHg, HCO3⁻ 13 mmol/L. What is the correct interpretation?$s$, $s$["Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis", "Partially compensated respiratory acidosis", "Uncompensated metabolic acidosis", "Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uncompensated metabolic acidosis
STEP 1 — pH 7.21: ACIDOSIS. STEP 2 — HCO3⁻ 13 mmol/L is LOW: METABOLIC (lactic acidosis after the seizure). STEP 3 — PaCO2 43 mmHg is NORMAL: not compensated. Answer: UNCOMPENSATED METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. (Using Winter's formula, expected PaCO2 = 1.5 × 13 + 8 = 27.5 ± 2; a PaCO2 of 43 is higher than expected, suggesting the post-ictal patient is not breathing deeply enough — but among the options given, "uncompensated metabolic acidosis" is the best answer.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a HIGH pH.
• Partially compensated respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis needs a HIGH PaCO2 as the primary change.
• Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis — A MIXED acidosis would need a clearly HIGH PaCO2 (above 45).

⭐ Key point: Monitor the airway and breathing in post-ictal patients — drowsiness can cause hypoventilation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q96 · concept asked in 6 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 198, $s$Which condition would result in an elevated anion gap in ABG results?$s$, $s$["Renal tubular acidosis", "Drug-induced metabolic alkalosis", "Hyperkalemia", "Diabetic ketoacidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Diabetic ketoacidosis
DIABETIC KETOACIDOSIS (DKA) produces KETONE acids (beta-hydroxybutyrate and acetoacetate), which are "unmeasured anions". They use up bicarbonate without raising chloride, so the ANION GAP RISES (HIGH ANION GAP METABOLIC ACIDOSIS).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal tubular acidosis — Renal tubular acidosis causes a NORMAL anion gap (hyperchloraemic) acidosis.
• Drug-induced metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis does not cause a raised anion gap acidosis.
• Hyperkalemia — Hyperkalaemia does not itself cause an elevated anion gap.

⭐ Key point: Monitor the anion gap in DKA — its closure is a sign of resolution.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q97$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 199, $s$Which acid–base disturbance is most likely to result from the following? Opioid overdose with respiratory depression.$s$, $s$["Metabolic alkalosis", "Respiratory alkalosis", "Metabolic acidosis", "Respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory acidosis
OPIOIDS (e.g. morphine, heroin, fentanyl) depress the breathing centre in the brainstem, causing slow, shallow breathing (HYPOVENTILATION). CO2 builds up in the blood, causing RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (low pH, high PaCO2). Pinpoint pupils and drowsiness are other signs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis is caused by acid loss (vomiting) or bicarbonate gain.
• Respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis is caused by HYPERventilation.
• Metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis is caused by acid gain or bicarbonate loss (although severe hypoxia can add lactic acidosis).

⭐ Key point: Treat with airway support, bag-mask ventilation and NALOXONE; watch for re-sedation as naloxone wears off.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q98$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 200, $s$Results: Na⁺ 140, Cl⁻ 103, HCO3⁻ 14 mmol/L. What is the anion gap and its interpretation?$s$, $s$["51 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)", "23 mmol/L — normal", "37 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)", "23 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 23 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap)
ANION GAP = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO3⁻) = 140 − (103 + 14) = 140 − 117 = 23 mmol/L. Normal is 8–12 (up to 16 with potassium), so 23 is RAISED: HIGH ANION GAP METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 51 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 51 mmol/L is a calculation error.
• 23 mmol/L — normal — 23 is correct, but it is RAISED, not normal.
• 37 mmol/L — raised (high anion gap) — 37 mmol/L is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: In low albumin states, the anion gap is underestimated; correct by adding about 2.5 for every 10 g/L fall in albumin below 40.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q99 · concept asked in 7 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 201, $s$A patient post-thyroidectomy develops perioral tingling and a positive Chvostek's sign. This suggests:$s$, $s$["A wound infection", "Hypocalcaemia from parathyroid gland disturbance during surgery", "Hyperkalaemia", "Normal recovery with no concern"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypocalcaemia from parathyroid gland disturbance during surgery
During THYROIDECTOMY, the nearby PARATHYROID glands (which control blood calcium) may be bruised, have their blood supply disturbed, or be accidentally removed. This causes HYPOCALCAEMIA, usually 24–48 hours after surgery. Signs: PERIORAL TINGLING (around the mouth), tingling in fingers and toes, muscle cramps, a positive CHVOSTEK'S SIGN (tapping the facial nerve in front of the ear causes twitching of the face), a positive TROUSSEAU'S SIGN (carpal spasm when a BP cuff is inflated), and in severe cases laryngospasm, seizures and arrhythmias (long QT).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A wound infection — Wound infection causes redness, swelling and discharge, not tingling and Chvostek's sign.
• Hyperkalaemia — Hyperkalaemia causes muscle weakness and ECG changes (peaked T waves), not perioral tingling and Chvostek's sign.
• Normal recovery with no concern — This is not normal recovery; it needs prompt treatment.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV calcium gluconate for severe symptoms, then oral calcium and vitamin D. Check calcium levels regularly after thyroid surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q157$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 202, $s$A patient on long-term diuretic therapy develops muscle weakness, cramping, and an irregular pulse. The nurse should suspect an electrolyte disturbance, most likely:$s$, $s$["Hypernatraemia", "Hypokalaemia", "Hypercalcaemia", "Hyperglycaemia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypokalaemia
Long-term DIURETICS such as FUROSEMIDE (loop) and thiazides make the kidneys excrete more sodium and water, and with them, POTASSIUM. This causes HYPOKALAEMIA (potassium below 3.5 mmol/L). Low potassium affects muscles and nerves: MUSCLE WEAKNESS, CRAMPING, fatigue, constipation (gut muscle), and the heart: an IRREGULAR PULSE (ectopic beats, arrhythmias), with ECG changes (flat T waves, U waves, ST depression, long QT). It also increases the risk of digoxin toxicity.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypernatraemia — Hypernatraemia causes thirst, confusion and seizures; diuretics more often cause LOW sodium.
• Hypercalcaemia — Hypercalcaemia causes thirst, polyuria, constipation and confusion (thiazides can raise calcium slightly, but loop diuretics lower it).
• Hyperglycaemia — Hyperglycaemia is not an electrolyte disturbance.

⭐ Key point: Monitor potassium regularly in patients on diuretics; consider oral potassium supplements or a potassium-sparing diuretic (e.g. spironolactone), and correct magnesium too.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q184$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 203, $s$A patient with COPD has pH 7.28, PaCO₂ 70 mmHg and HCO₃⁻ 32 mmol/L. This is best interpreted as:$s$, $s$["Acute uncompensated respiratory acidosis", "Fully compensated respiratory acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation", "Acute-on-chronic respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute-on-chronic respiratory acidosis
Step 1: pH 7.28 = ACIDOSIS. Step 2: PaCO2 70 mmHg (high) = RESPIRATORY cause. Step 3: Check compensation using bicarbonate (HCO3⁻). Expected HCO3⁻ (normal 24): in ACUTE respiratory acidosis, HCO3⁻ rises about 1 mmol/L per 10 mmHg rise in PaCO2 → 24 + 3 = 27. In CHRONIC respiratory acidosis, HCO3⁻ rises about 3.5–4 mmol/L per 10 mmHg → 24 + 10.5–12 = about 35–36. The actual HCO3⁻ is 32 — between the acute and chronic values. And the pH (7.28) is lower than expected for chronic compensation. This means a chronic CO2 retainer (COPD) with an ACUTE worsening: ACUTE-ON-CHRONIC RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute uncompensated respiratory acidosis — Acute uncompensated acidosis would show a nearly normal HCO3⁻ (around 26–27).
• Fully compensated respiratory acidosis — Fully compensated acidosis would show a pH within the normal range (7.35–7.45).
• Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation — Metabolic alkalosis would have a high pH (above 7.45).

⭐ Key point: Acute-on-chronic respiratory acidosis in COPD is often treated with controlled oxygen (target SpO2 88–92%) and non-invasive ventilation (NIV) when pH is below 7.35.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q62$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 204, $s$A patient has K⁺ 7.2 mmol/L with peaked T waves. Which treatment actually REMOVES potassium from the body, rather than moving it into cells or protecting the heart?$s$, $s$["Insulin with 50 mL of 50% dextrose", "IV 10% calcium gluconate", "Calcium resonium (a potassium binder)", "Nebulised salbutamol 10–20 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Calcium resonium (a potassium binder)
Treatments for HYPERKALAEMIA work in three ways: (1) PROTECT THE HEART (IV calcium gluconate — stabilises heart cell membranes); (2) MOVE POTASSIUM INTO CELLS (insulin with dextrose, salbutamol); (3) REMOVE POTASSIUM from the body (potassium BINDERS such as CALCIUM RESONIUM, diuretics, or dialysis). Calcium resonium binds potassium in the gut, exchanging it for calcium, and removes it in the stool.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Insulin with 50 mL of 50% dextrose — Insulin with dextrose moves potassium INTO cells temporarily.
• IV 10% calcium gluconate — Calcium gluconate PROTECTS the heart but doesn't lower potassium.
• Nebulised salbutamol 10–20 mg — Salbutamol moves potassium INTO cells.

⭐ Key point: Effects of insulin and salbutamol last only a few hours — potassium shifts back unless it is removed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q101$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 205, $s$The day after a total thyroidectomy, a patient complains of tingling around the mouth and in the fingertips. Tapping in front of the ear makes the face twitch, and inflating a BP cuff causes carpal spasm. The most likely cause is:$s$, $s$["Hypomagnesaemia from IV fluid dilution", "Respiratory alkalosis from anxiety", "Hypocalcaemia from parathyroid injury", "Thyroid storm from gland handling"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypocalcaemia from parathyroid injury
After THYROIDECTOMY, the PARATHYROID glands (which control calcium) can be damaged or have their blood supply disrupted, causing HYPOCALCAEMIA. Signs: TINGLING around the MOUTH and FINGERTIPS, CHVOSTEK'S SIGN (tapping in front of the ear makes the face twitch), and TROUSSEAU'S SIGN (a BP cuff inflated causes carpal spasm). Severe cases cause tetany, seizures and arrhythmias.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypomagnesaemia from IV fluid dilution — Hypomagnesaemia can cause similar signs, but the classic cause after thyroidectomy is parathyroid injury.
• Respiratory alkalosis from anxiety — Respiratory alkalosis from anxiety may cause tingling, but not these specific signs after thyroidectomy.
• Thyroid storm from gland handling — Thyroid storm causes fever, tachycardia and agitation, not tetany.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV calcium gluconate for severe symptoms, then oral calcium and vitamin D.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q103$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 206, $s$A patient with a head injury has Na⁺ 124 mmol/L, serum osmolality 260 mOsm/kg, urine osmolality 550 mOsm/kg and urine Na⁺ 60 mmol/L. BP, heart rate, CVP and skin turgor are normal and the weight is stable. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Syndrome of inappropriate ADH (SIADH)", "Cranial diabetes insipidus", "Primary (psychogenic) polydipsia", "Cerebral salt wasting syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Syndrome of inappropriate ADH (SIADH)
SIADH (Syndrome of Inappropriate ADH) causes the kidneys to keep too much water, diluting the sodium. Features: LOW SODIUM (124), LOW SERUM OSMOLALITY (260), INAPPROPRIATELY CONCENTRATED URINE (urine osmolality 550, higher than serum), HIGH URINE SODIUM (60), and the patient is EUVOLAEMIC (normal BP, heart rate, CVP, skin turgor, weight). Head injury is a common cause.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cranial diabetes insipidus — Cranial diabetes insipidus causes HIGH sodium and dilute urine.
• Primary (psychogenic) polydipsia — Primary polydipsia causes dilute urine (low urine osmolality).
• Cerebral salt wasting syndrome — Cerebral salt wasting causes HYPOVOLAEMIA (low CVP, tachycardia, weight loss), not euvolaemia.

⭐ Key point: Treat SIADH with fluid restriction. Correct sodium slowly (no more than 8–10 mmol/L in 24 hours).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q105$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 207, $s$After a subarachnoid haemorrhage, a patient has Na⁺ 125 mmol/L and high urine sodium. He is tachycardic, with a low CVP, a postural BP drop, weight loss and a negative fluid balance. The diagnosis and correct treatment are:$s$, $s$["Cerebral salt wasting — give IV saline", "Adrenal failure — fludrocortisone only", "Cranial DI — give desmopressin", "SIADH — strict fluid restriction"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cerebral salt wasting — give IV saline
CEREBRAL SALT WASTING (CSW) occurs after brain injury (e.g. SAH). The kidneys lose excess SODIUM and WATER, causing LOW SODIUM, HIGH URINE SODIUM, and HYPOVOLAEMIA (TACHYCARDIA, LOW CVP, POSTURAL BP DROP, WEIGHT LOSS, NEGATIVE FLUID BALANCE). Treatment: replace salt and water with IV SALINE (sometimes hypertonic saline or fludrocortisone).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adrenal failure — fludrocortisone only — Adrenal failure causes low sodium and high potassium, needs hydrocortisone.
• Cranial DI — give desmopressin — Cranial DI causes high sodium.
• SIADH — strict fluid restriction — SIADH causes euvolaemia; fluid restriction in CSW would worsen hypovolaemia and vasospasm.

⭐ Key point: The key difference between CSW and SIADH is the volume status: hypovolaemic in CSW, euvolaemic in SIADH.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q106$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 208, $s$A patient with chronic hyponatraemia (Na⁺ 108 mmol/L) is being treated. To avoid osmotic demyelination syndrome, sodium should rise by no more than:$s$, $s$["15 mmol/L in the first 12 hours", "8–10 mmol/L in any 24 hours", "1 mmol/L per hour for the first 24 hours", "20 mmol/L in 24 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 8–10 mmol/L in any 24 hours
In CHRONIC hyponatraemia (more than 48 hours), the brain adapts to low sodium. If sodium is corrected TOO FAST, water moves out of brain cells, damaging the protective myelin coating of nerves (OSMOTIC DEMYELINATION SYNDROME — previously called central pontine myelinolysis), causing paralysis, swallowing problems and locked-in syndrome. Sodium should rise by NO MORE THAN 8–10 mmol/L IN ANY 24 HOURS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 15 mmol/L in the first 12 hours — 15 mmol/L in 12 hours is too fast.
• 1 mmol/L per hour for the first 24 hours — 1 mmol/L per hour for 24 hours would give 24 mmol/L in 24 hours — far too fast.
• 20 mmol/L in 24 hours — 20 mmol/L in 24 hours is too fast.

⭐ Key point: Check sodium every 2–4 hours during correction. If correction is too fast, consider re-lowering sodium with water or desmopressin.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q108$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 209, $s$A patient's blood results are Na⁺ 140, Cl⁻ 100, HCO₃⁻ 10 mmol/L, pH 7.15. The anion gap and the type of acidosis are:$s$, $s$["Anion gap 50 — high anion gap metabolic acidosis", "Anion gap 10 — normal anion gap metabolic acidosis", "Anion gap 30 — high anion gap metabolic acidosis", "Anion gap 30 — normal anion gap metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Anion gap 30 — high anion gap metabolic acidosis
ANION GAP = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻). Here: 140 − (100 + 10) = 140 − 110 = 30 mmol/L. A normal anion gap is about 8–12 mmol/L (or up to 16 with potassium included). An anion gap of 30 is HIGH. The low pH and low bicarbonate indicate METABOLIC ACIDOSIS, so this is a HIGH ANION GAP METABOLIC ACIDOSIS (e.g. DKA, lactic acidosis, renal failure, toxins such as methanol).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anion gap 50 — high anion gap metabolic acidosis — An anion gap of 50 is a miscalculation.
• Anion gap 10 — normal anion gap metabolic acidosis — An anion gap of 10 is a miscalculation (it's normal).
• Anion gap 30 — normal anion gap metabolic acidosis — An anion gap of 30 is high, not normal.

⭐ Key point: Causes of high anion gap acidosis: "MUDPILES" — Methanol, Uraemia, DKA, Propylene glycol/Paracetamol, Iron/Isoniazid, Lactic acidosis, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q109$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 210, $s$A patient with profuse cholera-like diarrhoea has pH 7.25, HCO₃⁻ 14, Na⁺ 138 and Cl⁻ 118 mmol/L. This is:$s$, $s$["Normal anion gap (hyperchloraemic) metabolic acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis from chloride loss in stool", "Mixed metabolic and respiratory acidosis", "High anion gap metabolic acidosis from lactate"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Normal anion gap (hyperchloraemic) metabolic acidosis
Anion gap = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻) = 138 − (118 + 14) = 138 − 132 = 6 mmol/L. This is NORMAL. The pH is low (7.25) and bicarbonate is low (14), so this is a NORMAL ANION GAP (HYPERCHLORAEMIC) METABOLIC ACIDOSIS, caused by losing BICARBONATE in DIARRHOEA. The kidneys keep CHLORIDE to maintain electrical balance, so chloride rises.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic alkalosis from chloride loss in stool — Metabolic alkalosis would show a high pH.
• Mixed metabolic and respiratory acidosis — There is no evidence of respiratory acidosis.
• High anion gap metabolic acidosis from lactate — The anion gap is normal, not high.

⭐ Key point: Other causes of normal anion gap acidosis: renal tubular acidosis, large volumes of normal saline, and ureteric diversion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q110$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 211, $s$A patient who has been vomiting repeatedly for 5 days has pH 7.52, HCO₃⁻ 38 mmol/L, Cl⁻ 85 mmol/L and K⁺ 2.8 mmol/L. This is:$s$, $s$["Primary respiratory alkalosis with hypokalaemia", "Hyperchloraemic hyperkalaemic metabolic alkalosis", "Hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis", "Hyperchloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis
Repeated VOMITING causes loss of stomach acid (hydrochloric acid — HCl). This causes: METABOLIC ALKALOSIS (pH 7.52, HCO₃⁻ 38), HYPOCHLORAEMIA (Cl⁻ 85 — chloride lost in vomit), and HYPOKALAEMIA (K⁺ 2.8 — the kidneys exchange potassium to save hydrogen ions and sodium, and aldosterone rises with dehydration). So: HYPOCHLORAEMIC, HYPOKALAEMIC METABOLIC ALKALOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Primary respiratory alkalosis with hypokalaemia — Primary respiratory alkalosis would show a low PaCO2 with a low bicarbonate.
• Hyperchloraemic hyperkalaemic metabolic alkalosis — Chloride is LOW (not high) and potassium is LOW (not high).
• Hyperchloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic acidosis — The pH is HIGH (alkalosis), not acidosis.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV 0.9% saline with potassium chloride.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q111$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 212, $s$A patient who took a large aspirin overdose has tinnitus, pH 7.45, PaCO₂ 25 mmHg and HCO₃⁻ 15 mmol/L. The acid–base disturbance is:$s$, $s$["Respiratory alkalosis with full metabolic compensation", "Mixed respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis", "Metabolic acidosis with full respiratory compensation", "Primary metabolic alkalosis with a compensating acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mixed respiratory alkalosis and metabolic acidosis
In ASPIRIN (salicylate) OVERDOSE, salicylate first STIMULATES the breathing centre, causing HYPERVENTILATION and RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS (low PaCO2). It also interferes with metabolism, causing METABOLIC ACIDOSIS (low HCO₃⁻). These two effects can balance, giving a near-normal pH (7.45), with LOW PaCO2 (25) and LOW HCO₃⁻ (15): a MIXED RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS and METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. Tinnitus is a classic sign of salicylate toxicity.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory alkalosis with full metabolic compensation — Full compensation for respiratory alkalosis would not lower HCO₃⁻ this much this quickly.
• Metabolic acidosis with full respiratory compensation — In metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation, the pH would be below 7.4.
• Primary metabolic alkalosis with a compensating acidosis — This is not metabolic alkalosis; the bicarbonate is LOW.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV sodium bicarbonate (to increase salicylate excretion in urine) and consider haemodialysis in severe cases.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q112$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 213, $s$A patient in DKA has pH 7.05, HCO₃⁻ 8 mmol/L and PaCO₂ 32 mmHg. Using Winter's formula, this indicates:$s$, $s$["Metabolic acidosis with an added respiratory alkalosis", "Metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation", "Primary respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation", "Metabolic acidosis with an added respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic acidosis with an added respiratory acidosis
WINTER'S FORMULA calculates the expected PaCO2 for a metabolic acidosis: Expected PaCO2 = (1.5 × HCO₃⁻) + 8 ± 2 (in mmHg). Here: (1.5 × 8) + 8 = 12 + 8 = 20 ± 2 = 18–22 mmHg. The ACTUAL PaCO2 is 32 mmHg — HIGHER than expected. This means the lungs are not blowing off enough CO2, indicating an ADDED RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (e.g. from exhaustion, reduced consciousness, or chest infection).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic acidosis with an added respiratory alkalosis — A PaCO2 lower than 18 would indicate an added respiratory alkalosis.
• Metabolic acidosis with appropriate respiratory compensation — Appropriate compensation would give a PaCO2 of 18–22.
• Primary respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation — This is a metabolic acidosis (very low HCO₃⁻), not primarily respiratory.

⭐ Key point: A patient with DKA who cannot compensate is tiring — they may need respiratory support. Don't intubate without very careful planning (risk of worsening acidosis).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q113$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 214, $s$After prolonged hypotension, a patient has oliguric AKI with urine Na⁺ 45 mmol/L, fractional excretion of sodium 3%, urine osmolality 300 mOsm/kg, and 'muddy brown' granular casts on urine microscopy. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Pre-renal AKI", "Acute interstitial nephritis", "Post-renal (obstructive) AKI", "Acute tubular necrosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute tubular necrosis
ACUTE TUBULAR NECROSIS (ATN) is damage to the kidney tubules, often after prolonged HYPOTENSION (ischaemia) or toxins. The damaged tubules can't conserve sodium or concentrate urine. Features: URINE SODIUM HIGH (above 40), FRACTIONAL EXCRETION OF SODIUM (FENa) ABOVE 2%, URINE OSMOLALITY LOW (about 300, similar to plasma), and "MUDDY BROWN" GRANULAR CASTS (dead tubular cells).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pre-renal AKI — Pre-renal AKI has LOW urine sodium (below 20), FENa below 1%, high urine osmolality, and bland urine.
• Acute interstitial nephritis — Interstitial nephritis has white cells and white cell casts, with eosinophils.
• Post-renal (obstructive) AKI — Post-renal AKI is caused by obstruction; ultrasound shows hydronephrosis.

⭐ Key point: Treat ATN with supportive care, correct fluids and electrolytes, avoid nephrotoxins, and dialysis if needed. Recovery may take weeks.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q114$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 215, $s$A patient who took staggered paracetamol overdoses over 2 days has ALT 4,000 IU/L, INR 3.2 and arterial pH 7.25 after full fluid resuscitation. The correct next step is:$s$, $s$["Give activated charcoal and repeat the paracetamol level in 4 hours", "Give vitamin K to correct the INR, then plan discharge in 48 hours", "Refer urgently to a liver transplant centre (pH < 7.30 after fluids)", "Stop N-acetylcysteine, because more than 24 hours have now passed"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Refer urgently to a liver transplant centre (pH < 7.30 after fluids)
In PARACETAMOL overdose, liver failure can develop. Criteria for referral to a LIVER TRANSPLANT CENTRE (King's College Criteria) include: arterial pH BELOW 7.30 AFTER FLUID RESUSCITATION (here 7.25), OR all three of: INR above 6.5, creatinine above 300, and grade 3–4 encephalopathy. Here, the patient meets the pH criterion, so URGENT REFERRAL is needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give activated charcoal and repeat the paracetamol level in 4 hours — Activated charcoal is only useful within 1 hour of ingestion.
• Give vitamin K to correct the INR, then plan discharge in 48 hours — Vitamin K doesn't correct liver-failure coagulopathy, and the INR is needed to track liver function.
• Stop N-acetylcysteine, because more than 24 hours have now passed — N-ACETYLCYSTEINE (NAC) should CONTINUE in liver injury, even after 24 hours.

⭐ Key point: Staggered overdoses are high risk because paracetamol levels can't be interpreted on the treatment nomogram — treat with NAC regardless of the level.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q156$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 216, $s$A 6-week-old baby has had projectile, non-bilious vomiting after every feed for a week and is hungry straight after vomiting. A small olive-sized mass is felt in the epigastrium during a test feed. Blood gas shows pH 7.55, Cl⁻ 85 mmol/L and K⁺ 2.9 mmol/L. Before surgery, the priority is:$s$, $s$["Take the baby for pyloromyotomy within the hour, before the alkalosis worsens", "Give IV sodium bicarbonate to correct the acid–base disturbance before surgery", "Restart small frequent oral feeds to replace the losses, then operate tomorrow", "Correct the fluid deficit and alkalosis with IV saline, dextrose and KCl first"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Correct the fluid deficit and alkalosis with IV saline, dextrose and KCl first
PYLORIC STENOSIS causes repeated NON-BILIOUS PROJECTILE vomiting, losing STOMACH ACID (HCl), causing HYPOCHLORAEMIC, HYPOKALAEMIC METABOLIC ALKALOSIS (pH 7.55, Cl⁻ 85, K⁺ 2.9). Surgery is NOT an emergency. The priority is to CORRECT the FLUID DEFICIT and ALKALOSIS with IV SALINE, DEXTROSE and POTASSIUM CHLORIDE first, because anaesthesia in an alkalotic baby can cause post-operative apnoea.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Take the baby for pyloromyotomy within the hour, before the alkalosis worsens — Operating before correction is unsafe.
• Give IV sodium bicarbonate to correct the acid–base disturbance before surgery — Sodium bicarbonate would worsen alkalosis.
• Restart small frequent oral feeds to replace the losses, then operate tomorrow — Oral feeds will be vomited.

⭐ Key point: Proceed to pyloromyotomy when chloride is above about 100 and bicarbonate below about 26.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q180$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 217, $s$During the cooling phase of induced hypothermia, the nurse should monitor closely for:$s$, $s$["Tachycardia, high potassium and low blood glucose", "Bradycardia, low potassium, high glucose, coagulopathy", "Polycythaemia, hypercalcaemia and hypernatraemia", "Hypertension with a shortened bleeding time only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bradycardia, low potassium, high glucose, coagulopathy
During cooling: BRADYCARDIA (the heart slows); LOW POTASSIUM (potassium moves into cells, and cold causes diuresis); HIGH GLUCOSE (insulin resistance and reduced insulin release); and COAGULOPATHY (cold slows clotting enzymes and platelets, increasing bleeding risk). Also watch for shivering, infections, and low magnesium and phosphate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tachycardia, high potassium and low blood glucose — Hypothermia causes bradycardia (not tachycardia), low potassium (not high), and HIGH glucose (not low).
• Polycythaemia, hypercalcaemia and hypernatraemia — These are not typical effects of hypothermia.
• Hypertension with a shortened bleeding time only — Hypothermia prolongs bleeding time (not shortens it).

⭐ Key point: During rewarming, potassium moves back out of the cells, so hyperkalaemia can occur. Avoid giving too much potassium during cooling.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q53$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 218, $s$Hypocalcaemia in severe pancreatitis is caused by:$s$, $s$["Hyperparathyroidism from pancreatic damage", "Saponification of calcium in fat necrosis", "Retention of calcium by the kidneys", "Excess calcium absorption from the gut"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Saponification of calcium in fat necrosis
In severe pancreatitis, enzymes break down fat around the pancreas (FAT NECROSIS). The released fatty acids bind to CALCIUM, forming "soaps" (SAPONIFICATION). This lowers the blood calcium (HYPOCALCAEMIA). A calcium below 2 mmol/L indicates severe disease.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperparathyroidism from pancreatic damage — Pancreatitis does not cause hyperparathyroidism; hyperparathyroidism can cause pancreatitis.
• Retention of calcium by the kidneys — The kidneys do not retain calcium in pancreatitis.
• Excess calcium absorption from the gut — Calcium absorption is not increased.

⭐ Key point: Signs of hypocalcaemia: tingling, muscle cramps, Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs, and a long QT interval.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q73$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 219, $s$A post-TURP patient becomes confused with BP 170/100, HR 50 and sodium 118 mmol/L. This is:$s$, $s$["Cerebral haemorrhage from the hypertension", "Septic shock from a urinary tract infection", "TURP syndrome from absorbed hypotonic irrigation", "Hypoglycaemia from prolonged pre-op fasting"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: TURP syndrome from absorbed hypotonic irrigation
TURP SYNDROME occurs when large volumes of HYPOTONIC irrigation fluid (e.g. glycine) are ABSORBED into the bloodstream through opened prostate veins. This causes FLUID OVERLOAD (hypertension) and DILUTIONAL HYPONATRAEMIA (sodium 118). Low sodium causes brain swelling (confusion, seizures). Reflex BRADYCARDIA can occur.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cerebral haemorrhage from the hypertension — Cerebral haemorrhage would not explain the low sodium.
• Septic shock from a urinary tract infection — Septic shock causes hypotension, not hypertension.
• Hypoglycaemia from prolonged pre-op fasting — Hypoglycaemia doesn't cause hyponatraemia and hypertension.

⭐ Key point: Treat with fluid restriction, diuretics, and hypertonic saline if severe. Correct sodium slowly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q78$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 220, $s$Before surgery for pyloric stenosis, the infant's blood gas usually shows:$s$, $s$["Hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis", "Respiratory acidosis from abdominal distension", "Hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis, normal K⁺", "Hyperkalaemic metabolic acidosis with low Na⁺"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis
In PYLORIC STENOSIS, the thickened pylorus blocks the stomach outlet, causing repeated projectile NON-BILIOUS vomiting. The infant loses STOMACH ACID (hydrochloric acid — hydrogen and chloride ions). This leads to: HYPOCHLORAEMIA (low chloride), METABOLIC ALKALOSIS (loss of acid), and HYPOKALAEMIA (low potassium — the kidneys exchange potassium to save hydrogen ions, and potassium is also lost in vomit). This is called HYPOCHLORAEMIC, HYPOKALAEMIC METABOLIC ALKALOSIS. Surgery (pyloromyotomy) is NOT an emergency — the fluid and electrolyte problems must be corrected FIRST (IV 0.9% saline with dextrose and potassium) because anaesthesia in an alkalotic infant can cause apnoea after surgery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory acidosis from abdominal distension — Respiratory acidosis is not caused by pyloric stenosis.
• Hyperchloraemic metabolic acidosis, normal K⁺ — Hyperchloraemic acidosis is seen with diarrhoea (loss of bicarbonate), not vomiting.
• Hyperkalaemic metabolic acidosis with low Na⁺ — Hyperkalaemic acidosis is seen in kidney failure or adrenal problems, not pyloric stenosis.

⭐ Key point: Pyloric stenosis: usually first-born boys aged 2–8 weeks, projectile non-bilious vomiting after feeds, hungry baby, visible peristalsis, and an "olive-shaped" mass in the upper abdomen.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q106$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 221, $s$In sickle cell crisis, the ICU nurse should avoid:$s$, $s$["IV fluids at the maintenance rate", "Hypothermia, dehydration, hypoxia, acidosis", "Opioid analgesia for severe crisis pain", "Oxygen therapy when the SpO₂ is low"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypothermia, dehydration, hypoxia, acidosis
Sickling of red cells is TRIGGERED by: HYPOTHERMIA (cold causes vasoconstriction, slowing blood flow), DEHYDRATION (thicker blood), HYPOXIA (low oxygen makes HbS polymerise and sickle) and ACIDOSIS (low pH promotes sickling). The ICU nurse must actively AVOID these: keep the patient warm, well hydrated, well oxygenated and treat any acidosis or infection. Pain must be well controlled.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV fluids at the maintenance rate — IV fluids at maintenance rate are HELPFUL; they prevent dehydration.
• Opioid analgesia for severe crisis pain — Opioid analgesia is the standard treatment for severe crisis pain and should NOT be withheld.
• Oxygen therapy when the SpO₂ is low — Oxygen should be GIVEN when SpO2 is low to prevent further sickling.

⭐ Key point: Do not use cold compresses for pain in sickle cell disease — cold triggers sickling.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q110$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 222, $s$Tumour lysis syndrome produces:$s$, $s$["Low K⁺, low phosphate and high Ca²⁺", "High K⁺, high phosphate, high urate, low Ca²⁺", "Low glucose with high Ca²⁺ and low urate", "Low Na⁺ only, with normal potassium"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: High K⁺, high phosphate, high urate, low Ca²⁺
In TUMOUR LYSIS SYNDROME, large numbers of cancer cells break down and release their contents into the blood: POTASSIUM (cells are rich in potassium → HYPERKALAEMIA → arrhythmias), PHOSPHATE (→ HYPERPHOSPHATAEMIA), and nucleic acids that are broken down into URIC ACID (→ HYPERURICAEMIA → crystals block the kidneys). The high phosphate binds calcium, causing HYPOCALCAEMIA (tingling, cramps, seizures, arrhythmias).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low K⁺, low phosphate and high Ca²⁺ — Potassium and phosphate are HIGH, and calcium is LOW, not the reverse.
• Low glucose with high Ca²⁺ and low urate — Glucose is not typically low, calcium is low, and urate is high.
• Low Na⁺ only, with normal potassium — Sodium is not the main problem; potassium is high.

⭐ Key point: Prevention: IV hydration (2–3 L/m²/day), allopurinol or rasburicase, and frequent monitoring of electrolytes, urate, creatinine and urine output.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q112$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 223, $s$Refeeding syndrome after starting feeds in a malnourished ICU patient causes:$s$, $s$["Low phosphate, low potassium and low magnesium", "High calcium, high sodium and high chloride", "High blood glucose with normal electrolytes", "High phosphate, high potassium and high urea"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Low phosphate, low potassium and low magnesium
REFEEDING SYNDROME occurs when feeding starts after starvation. Insulin rises, driving phosphate, potassium and magnesium into cells. This causes LOW PHOSPHATE, LOW POTASSIUM and LOW MAGNESIUM, which can cause heart failure, arrhythmias, breathing problems and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High calcium, high sodium and high chloride — Calcium, sodium and chloride aren't high.
• High blood glucose with normal electrolytes — Electrolytes are abnormal.
• High phosphate, high potassium and high urea — Phosphate, potassium and magnesium are LOW, not high.

⭐ Key point: Start feeds slowly, give thiamine, and monitor electrolytes daily.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q188$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 224, $s$A common electrolyte complication of plasmapheresis, related to the citrate anticoagulant used in the extracorporeal circuit, is:$s$, $s$["Hypernatraemia from the replacement fluid", "Hyperkalaemia from haemolysis in the circuit", "Hyperphosphataemia from cell breakdown", "Hypocalcaemia (citrate toxicity), with perioral tingling"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypocalcaemia (citrate toxicity), with perioral tingling
During PLASMAPHERESIS, blood flows through a machine outside the body (extracorporeal circuit). CITRATE is added as an anticoagulant to stop the blood clotting in the circuit; it works by BINDING CALCIUM. Citrate returns to the patient with the blood, lowering the ionised calcium (HYPOCALCAEMIA, called citrate toxicity), especially when fresh frozen plasma (which also contains citrate) is used as the replacement fluid. Signs: PERIORAL TINGLING (around the mouth), tingling in the fingers, muscle cramps, nausea, and in severe cases tetany, arrhythmias and hypotension.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypernatraemia from the replacement fluid — Hypernatraemia is not a common complication of plasmapheresis.
• Hyperkalaemia from haemolysis in the circuit — Hyperkalaemia from haemolysis is uncommon; the circuit is designed to minimise haemolysis.
• Hyperphosphataemia from cell breakdown — Hyperphosphataemia from cell breakdown is not a typical complication.

⭐ Key point: Monitor ionised calcium; give oral or IV calcium (calcium gluconate) as per protocol. Slow the procedure if symptoms appear.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q193$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 225, $s$A ventilated COPD patient's ABG shows pH 7.50, PaCO₂ 30 mmHg (4 kPa), HCO₃⁻ 34 mmol/L. This indicates:$s$, $s$["Diabetic ketoacidosis needing an insulin infusion", "Over-ventilation with chronic metabolic compensation", "Acute respiratory acidosis needing a higher rate", "A normal blood gas for a patient with severe COPD"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Over-ventilation with chronic metabolic compensation
pH 7.50 is ALKALOSIS. PaCO₂ 30 is LOW (the patient is being over-ventilated). HCO₃⁻ 34 is HIGH. This COPD patient had chronically high CO₂ before, so the kidneys retained bicarbonate to compensate. On the ventilator, CO₂ has been blown down too quickly, but the high bicarbonate remains, causing alkalosis. This is OVER-VENTILATION with chronic metabolic compensation (post-hypercapnic alkalosis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diabetic ketoacidosis needing an insulin infusion — DKA causes ACIDOSIS.
• Acute respiratory acidosis needing a higher rate — Respiratory acidosis has a LOW pH and HIGH PaCO₂.
• A normal blood gas for a patient with severe COPD — This isn't normal; the pH is alkalotic.

⭐ Key point: In chronic CO₂ retainers, aim for their usual PaCO₂ (often higher than normal) and a normal pH, not a "normal" PaCO₂.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q19$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 226, $s$A complication of massive transfusion the ICU nurse should anticipate is:$s$, $s$["Alkalosis from the bicarbonate in stored blood", "Low calcium, high potassium, hypothermia, coagulopathy", "High calcium and low glucose with a fever", "Polycythaemia and hypertension with oedema"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Low calcium, high potassium, hypothermia, coagulopathy
Massive transfusion causes: LOW CALCIUM (citrate in stored blood binds calcium), HIGH POTASSIUM (released from stored red cells), HYPOTHERMIA (cold blood products), and COAGULOPATHY (dilution of clotting factors and platelets). Acidosis can also occur.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Alkalosis from the bicarbonate in stored blood — Alkalosis can occur later as citrate is metabolised, but it isn't the main early complication.
• High calcium and low glucose with a fever — High calcium and low glucose are not typical.
• Polycythaemia and hypertension with oedema — Polycythaemia isn't a complication.

⭐ Key point: Warm all products, check ionised calcium and give calcium, and monitor potassium and clotting.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q33$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 227, $s$Neonatal hypoglycaemia is treated with an IV bolus of:$s$, $s$["1 mL/kg of 5% dextrose", "5 mL/kg of 25% dextrose", "2 mL/kg of 10% dextrose", "10 mL/kg of 50% dextrose"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2 mL/kg of 10% dextrose
Neonatal hypoglycaemia is treated with an IV bolus of 2 mL/kg of 10% DEXTROSE (200 mg/kg), followed by a continuous glucose infusion. Higher concentrations (25%, 50%) are too concentrated (hypertonic) and damage the baby's small veins.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1 mL/kg of 5% dextrose — 1 mL/kg of 5% dextrose is too little glucose.
• 5 mL/kg of 25% dextrose — 5 mL/kg of 25% dextrose is too concentrated and too much.
• 10 mL/kg of 50% dextrose — 10 mL/kg of 50% dextrose is dangerous.

⭐ Key point: Recheck glucose 30 minutes after treatment, and encourage feeding if the baby is well enough.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q97$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 228, $s$The LEAST important part of initial DKA treatment is:$s$, $s$["Lowering blood glucose with insulin", "Giving sodium bicarbonate", "Giving potassium chloride", "Giving fluids"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Giving sodium bicarbonate
The question asks which is LEAST important. Sodium bicarbonate is rarely needed in DKA, and only if the pH is below about 6.9. The acidosis corrects itself once insulin stops ketone production and fluids restore circulation. Bicarbonate can cause harm: low potassium, worsening acidosis inside brain cells, and cerebral oedema in children.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lowering blood glucose with insulin — Insulin IS essential: it stops ketone production and lowers glucose.
• Giving potassium chloride — Potassium IS essential: levels fall dangerously once insulin starts.
• Giving fluids — Fluids ARE the first and most important treatment: patients are often 5–8 litres dry.

⭐ Key point: DKA priority order: 1) fluids, 2) potassium check and replacement, 3) fixed-rate insulin, 4) find and treat the trigger (infection, missed insulin, MI).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q23$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 229, $s$Hyperkalaemia with ECG changes is first treated with:$s$, $s$["Oral potassium binders as the only treatment", "IV calcium gluconate 10% to protect the heart", "IV furosemide alone to excrete potassium", "IV potassium chloride to correct the ECG"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV calcium gluconate 10% to protect the heart
High potassium makes heart muscle cells unstable and can cause fatal arrhythmias (VF, asystole). IV calcium gluconate 10% (30 mL) STABILISES the heart muscle membrane within minutes, protecting the heart while other treatments lower the potassium. It does NOT lower the potassium itself. The ECG should improve; repeat the dose if it doesn't.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral potassium binders as the only treatment — Oral binders work far too slowly (hours to days) for an emergency.
• IV furosemide alone to excrete potassium — Furosemide helps remove potassium only if the kidneys work; it's not the first step.
• IV potassium chloride to correct the ECG — Giving potassium chloride would make hyperkalaemia worse and could cause cardiac arrest.

⭐ Key point: The order is: 1) protect the heart (calcium), 2) shift K⁺ into cells (insulin 10 units with 25 g glucose, salbutamol nebuliser), 3) remove K⁺ (dialysis, binders, diuretics).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q35$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 230, $s$Too-rapid correction of chronic hyponatraemia can cause:$s$, $s$["Severe hyperkalaemia and arrhythmia", "Osmotic demyelination syndrome", "Acute intracerebral haemorrhage", "Pulmonary oedema and hypoxia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Osmotic demyelination syndrome
In long-standing low sodium, brain cells adjust by losing solutes so they don't swell. If sodium is then raised too quickly, water is pulled out of the brain cells and the myelin around nerves in the pons (brainstem) is damaged. This is osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis). It appears 2–6 days later with difficulty speaking and swallowing, weakness of all limbs, "locked-in" syndrome, or death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Severe hyperkalaemia and arrhythmia — Hyperkalaemia is not caused by correcting sodium.
• Acute intracerebral haemorrhage — Brain haemorrhage is not the typical complication.
• Pulmonary oedema and hypoxia — Pulmonary oedema can follow too much IV fluid, but it is not the classic complication of rapid sodium correction.

⭐ Key point: Safe correction: no more than 8–10 mmol/L in 24 hours (6–8 in high-risk patients such as alcohol misuse or malnutrition). If it rises too fast, it can be lowered again with water or desmopressin.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q37$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 231, $s$Oxytocin infusion is given cautiously in patients with heart disease because it can cause:$s$, $s$["Hypotension, tachycardia and fluid overload", "Severe hyperkalaemia", "Hypertension with a slow heart rate", "Profound hypoglycaemia"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypotension, tachycardia and fluid overload
Oxytocin, given as a large IV bolus, relaxes the smooth muscle of blood vessels, causing HYPOTENSION and reflex TACHYCARDIA, which can be dangerous in heart disease. Given in large volumes of fluid over a long time, it has an ADH-like (antidiuretic) effect, causing water retention, FLUID OVERLOAD, low sodium and even pulmonary oedema. In heart disease, give it slowly and in a concentrated form.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Severe hyperkalaemia — Oxytocin doesn't cause severe hyperkalaemia.
• Hypertension with a slow heart rate — High BP with slow pulse is more typical of ergometrine.
• Profound hypoglycaemia — Oxytocin doesn't cause severe hypoglycaemia.

⭐ Key point: In cardiac patients: slow IV oxytocin (not a fast bolus), concentrated infusions (to limit fluid volume), and strict fluid balance.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q128$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 232, $s$Before arterial line insertion, the nurse prepares a transducer set flushed with:$s$, $s$["5% dextrose pressurised to 50 mmHg", "Unpressurised sterile water for injection", "Pressurised normal saline at 300 mmHg", "Heparin 25 000 units in 10 mL only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pressurised normal saline at 300 mmHg
An arterial line transducer set is filled with NORMAL SALINE and placed in a pressure bag inflated to 300 mmHg. Because arterial pressure is high, the bag pressure must be higher than the patient's BP to give a slow continuous flush (about 3 mL/hour) that keeps the line open and stops blood backing up and clotting.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5% dextrose pressurised to 50 mmHg — 5% dextrose can cause wrong glucose results (falsely high) if samples are contaminated, and 50 mmHg is too low.
• Unpressurised sterile water for injection — Unpressurised water can't keep an arterial line open, and water causes haemolysis.
• Heparin 25 000 units in 10 mL only — Heparin 25,000 units in 10 mL is a dangerous concentration; saline alone is usually used.

⭐ Key point: Check the pressure bag stays at 300 mmHg, the flush is running, and there are no air bubbles in the line.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q23$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 233, $s$Indications for urgent dialysis include:$s$, $s$["Hypokalaemia from diuretic therapy", "A creatinine of 120 µmol/L with good output", "Mild oliguria for 2 hours after surgery", "Refractory high K⁺, acidosis, overload, uraemia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Refractory high K⁺, acidosis, overload, uraemia
Urgent dialysis is needed when the kidneys fail and dangerous problems can't be controlled with drugs. Remember AEIOU: ACIDOSIS (severe metabolic acidosis), ELECTROLYTES (refractory high potassium), INTOXICATION (some poisons, such as methanol or lithium), OVERLOAD (fluid overload not responding to diuretics, pulmonary oedema), and URAEMIA (pericarditis, encephalopathy, bleeding).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypokalaemia from diuretic therapy — Low potassium is not an indication for dialysis.
• A creatinine of 120 µmol/L with good output — A creatinine of 120 with good urine output is not an indication.
• Mild oliguria for 2 hours after surgery — Mild oliguria for 2 hours after surgery is not an indication.

⭐ Key point: Dialysis is decided by these complications, not by a creatinine number alone.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q85$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 234, $s$Citrate anticoagulation in CRRT requires monitoring of:$s$, $s$["Ionised calcium in the patient and circuit", "The blood glucose only, every 4 hours", "The platelet count only, checked daily", "The urine sodium only, every 12 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ionised calcium in the patient and circuit
Citrate binds CALCIUM in the blood in the circuit, which stops it from clotting. The citrate–calcium complex returns to the patient, where it is broken down by the liver, releasing calcium. Ionised calcium must be monitored in the PATIENT (to replace calcium) and in the CIRCUIT (post-filter, to check citrate is working). Calcium is replaced by a separate infusion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The blood glucose only, every 4 hours — Glucose isn't the main thing to monitor with citrate.
• The platelet count only, checked daily — Platelets matter more with heparin (HIT risk).
• The urine sodium only, every 12 hours — Urine sodium isn't relevant.

⭐ Key point: Citrate toxicity (citrate building up in liver failure): a low ionised calcium with a high total calcium (total-to-ionised ratio above 2.5), and metabolic acidosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q87$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 235, $s$ABG: pH 7.48, PaCO₂ 48 mmHg, HCO₃⁻ 35 mmol/L indicates:$s$, $s$["Metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation", "Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation", "Respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation", "Acute respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic alkalosis with respiratory compensation
Step 1: pH 7.48 is high, so ALKALOSIS. Step 2: HCO₃⁻ 35 is high (pH and HCO₃⁻ both up), so it is METABOLIC alkalosis. Step 3: PaCO₂ 48 is high; the lungs are breathing less to retain CO₂ (acid), which is respiratory COMPENSATION.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation — Metabolic acidosis would have a low pH and a low HCO₃⁻.
• Respiratory acidosis with metabolic compensation — Respiratory acidosis would have a LOW pH with a high PaCO₂.
• Acute respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation — Respiratory alkalosis would have a LOW PaCO₂.

⭐ Key point: Causes of metabolic alkalosis: vomiting, NG suction, diuretics, low potassium, too much bicarbonate. Treat with saline and potassium chloride.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q89 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 236, $s$A normal anion gap metabolic acidosis is caused by:$s$, $s$["Diarrhoea or large volumes of 0.9% saline", "Methanol poisoning with visual loss", "Diabetic ketoacidosis with high ketones", "Lactic acidosis from septic shock"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Diarrhoea or large volumes of 0.9% saline
The anion gap = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻), normally about 8–12. In NORMAL anion gap (hyperchloraemic) metabolic acidosis, bicarbonate is lost but replaced by CHLORIDE, so the gap stays normal. Causes: DIARRHOEA (loss of bicarbonate-rich gut fluid), large volumes of 0.9% SALINE (high chloride), renal tubular acidosis, and pancreatic fistulae.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Methanol poisoning with visual loss — Methanol causes a HIGH anion gap acidosis.
• Diabetic ketoacidosis with high ketones — DKA causes a HIGH anion gap acidosis (ketones).
• Lactic acidosis from septic shock — Lactic acidosis causes a HIGH anion gap acidosis.

⭐ Key point: High anion gap causes: MUDPILES (Methanol, Uraemia, DKA, Paraldehyde, Iron/Isoniazid, Lactate, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q90$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 237, $s$Hypokalaemia on the ECG shows:$s$, $s$["Flat T waves and prominent U waves", "ST elevation in all of the leads", "Tall, peaked T waves in V2–V4", "Wide QRS complexes and sine waves"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Flat T waves and prominent U waves
Low potassium slows the recovery (repolarisation) of heart muscle cells. The ECG shows FLAT or inverted T WAVES, prominent U WAVES (an extra small wave after the T), ST depression, and a prolonged QU interval. Severe hypokalaemia can cause dangerous arrhythmias (VT, VF, torsades), especially with digoxin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• ST elevation in all of the leads — ST elevation in all leads suggests pericarditis.
• Tall, peaked T waves in V2–V4 — Tall, peaked T waves are a sign of HIGH potassium.
• Wide QRS complexes and sine waves — Wide QRS and sine waves are signs of severe HIGH potassium.

⭐ Key point: Keep K⁺ at 4.0–5.0 mmol/L in cardiac patients. Low magnesium often comes with low potassium; replace both.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q91$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 238, $s$IV potassium on a general ICU line should usually not exceed:$s$, $s$["50 mmol given over 5 minutes in an arrest", "An undiluted bolus through any IV line", "100 mmol/hour through a peripheral cannula", "10–20 mmol/hour via a central line with monitoring"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10–20 mmol/hour via a central line with monitoring
IV potassium is a HIGH-ALERT drug: if given too fast, it can stop the heart. Peripherally, give no more than 10 mmol/hour. Via a CENTRAL line with ECG monitoring, up to 20 mmol/hour (and higher only in very specific ICU protocols). Always use pre-mixed bags or syringes on an infusion pump, never a bolus.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50 mmol given over 5 minutes in an arrest — 50 mmol over 5 minutes would cause cardiac arrest.
• An undiluted bolus through any IV line — An undiluted bolus is NEVER given; it can be fatal.
• 100 mmol/hour through a peripheral cannula — 100 mmol/hour peripherally is extremely dangerous and would damage the vein.

⭐ Key point: Check K⁺ levels regularly during replacement, keep the patient on a cardiac monitor for fast infusions, and store concentrated KCl securely.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q92$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 239, $s$Hypomagnesaemia may cause:$s$, $s$["Hypertension only, with headache", "Bradycardia only, with heart block", "Torsades and refractory hypokalaemia", "Polyuria only, with thirst"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Torsades and refractory hypokalaemia
Magnesium is needed to stabilise heart muscle and nerve cells, and for the kidneys to hold on to potassium. Low magnesium causes muscle weakness, tremor, seizures and ARRHYTHMIAS, including TORSADES de pointes. It also makes LOW POTASSIUM hard to correct (refractory), because the kidneys keep wasting potassium.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension only, with headache — Hypertension alone with headache isn't the typical effect.
• Bradycardia only, with heart block — Bradycardia and heart block are not typical.
• Polyuria only, with thirst — Polyuria and thirst are not typical.

⭐ Key point: If potassium won't come up, check and replace magnesium first. Normal Mg is 0.7–1.0 mmol/L.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q93$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 240, $s$Fluid balance charts are most useful when:$s$, $s$["Left blank during the night shift", "Estimated without any measurement", "Filled in once a week by the manager", "Totalled every 24 hours, with daily weights"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Totalled every 24 hours, with daily weights
Fluid balance charts show all fluid IN (IV, enteral, oral, drugs) and OUT (urine, drains, vomit, NG, stool, insensible losses). They are most useful when totalled EVERY 24 HOURS and added to a CUMULATIVE balance, together with DAILY WEIGHTS. This shows whether the patient is getting overloaded or dry. A large cumulative positive balance in ICU is linked to worse outcomes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Left blank during the night shift — Leaving the chart blank at night gives wrong totals.
• Estimated without any measurement — Estimating without measuring is inaccurate.
• Filled in once a week by the manager — Weekly totals miss daily changes.

⭐ Key point: Record fluids accurately in real time. Daily weight is the best single measure of fluid change (1 kg is about 1 L of fluid).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q95$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 241, $s$Over-hydration (fluid overload) is suggested by:$s$, $s$["Low JVP with tachycardia and cool hands", "Weight loss with a dry mouth and thirst", "Weight gain, raised JVP, crackles, oedema", "Concentrated dark urine with high urea"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Weight gain, raised JVP, crackles, oedema
Fluid overload shows: WEIGHT GAIN (fluid weighs 1 kg per litre), RAISED JVP or CVP, lung CRACKLES (pulmonary oedema), breathlessness, falling SpO₂, peripheral OEDEMA (swollen legs, sacrum), and high BP. In ICU, it can impair healing and organ function.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low JVP with tachycardia and cool hands — A low JVP with tachycardia and cool hands suggests DEHYDRATION.
• Weight loss with a dry mouth and thirst — Weight loss with a dry mouth and thirst suggests DEHYDRATION.
• Concentrated dark urine with high urea — Concentrated dark urine with high urea suggests DEHYDRATION.

⭐ Key point: Treat overload with fluid restriction, diuretics (furosemide) or ultrafiltration (CRRT) if the kidneys fail.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q96$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 242, $s$The kidneys compensate for respiratory acidosis by:$s$, $s$["Retaining bicarbonate and excreting H⁺", "Retaining hydrogen ions in the blood", "Excreting bicarbonate in the urine", "Increasing the rate of breathing"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Retaining bicarbonate and excreting H⁺
In respiratory acidosis, CO₂ (acid) builds up because of poor breathing. The KIDNEYS compensate by RETAINING (reabsorbing and making more) BICARBONATE and EXCRETING more hydrogen ions (acid) in the urine. This raises the blood bicarbonate and brings the pH back toward normal. It takes 3–5 days to work fully.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Retaining hydrogen ions in the blood — Retaining hydrogen ions would make the acidosis worse.
• Excreting bicarbonate in the urine — Excreting bicarbonate would make the acidosis worse.
• Increasing the rate of breathing — Increasing breathing is a lung response, not kidney compensation.

⭐ Key point: In chronic CO₂ retention (COPD), a raised HCO₃⁻ shows the kidneys have compensated over time.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q98$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 243, $s$The lungs compensate for metabolic acidosis by:$s$, $s$["Hypoventilation to raise PaCO₂", "Retaining bicarbonate", "Hyperventilation to lower PaCO₂", "Excreting ammonium"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hyperventilation to lower PaCO₂
In metabolic acidosis, the blood has too much acid (low HCO₃⁻). The LUNGS compensate quickly (within minutes) by HYPERVENTILATING: breathing faster and deeper to blow off CO₂ and lower the PaCO₂. Less CO₂ means less carbonic acid, so the pH rises toward normal. Kussmaul breathing in DKA is a classic example.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypoventilation to raise PaCO₂ — Hypoventilation would RAISE PaCO₂ and worsen the acidosis.
• Retaining bicarbonate — Retaining bicarbonate is a KIDNEY response.
• Excreting ammonium — Excreting ammonium is a KIDNEY response.

⭐ Key point: Winter's formula checks if compensation is appropriate: expected PaCO₂ = 1.5 × HCO₃⁻ + 8 (±2).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q99$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 244, $s$Before giving an NG feed, the nurse confirms tube position by:$s$, $s$["Asking the patient if it feels right", "Listening for injected air (whoosh test)", "Placing the tube end in a cup of water", "Aspirate pH 5.5 or below, or X-ray"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Aspirate pH 5.5 or below, or X-ray
Before giving any NG feed, confirm the tube is in the stomach. The FIRST-line test is to aspirate and test the pH: pH 5.5 OR BELOW means stomach acid (safe to feed). If no aspirate or the pH is above 5.5, get a CHEST X-RAY, interpreted by a trained person.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Asking the patient if it feels right — Asking the patient isn't reliable; a tube in the lung may not cause symptoms.
• Listening for injected air (whoosh test) — The "whoosh" test (listening for air) is UNRELIABLE and must not be used.
• Placing the tube end in a cup of water — Bubbles in a cup of water aren't reliable and risk aspiration.

⭐ Key point: Mark the tube's length at the nostril and recheck its position before each feed and medication.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q147$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 245, $s$A patient with hypokalemia has U waves on ECG. Which drug increases the risk of this finding?$s$, $s$["Metoprolol", "Spironolactone", "Amlodipine", "Furosemide"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Furosemide
Furosemide is a loop diuretic. It blocks the Na-K-2Cl pump in the loop of Henle, so more sodium reaches the collecting duct, where it is swapped for POTASSIUM, which is lost in the urine. This causes HYPOKALAEMIA, and low potassium shows U waves on the ECG.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metoprolol — Metoprolol (a beta-blocker) can slightly RAISE potassium.
• Spironolactone — Spironolactone is a potassium-SPARING diuretic and raises potassium.
• Amlodipine — Amlodipine (a calcium channel blocker) doesn't affect potassium significantly.

⭐ Key point: Monitor potassium in patients on loop or thiazide diuretics, and replace it as needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q6$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 246, $s$A patient with chronic kidney disease commonly develops which acid-base abnormality due to reduced renal excretion of hydrogen ions and reduced bicarbonate regeneration?$s$, $s$["Respiratory acidosis", "Respiratory alkalosis", "Metabolic acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic acidosis
Healthy kidneys remove the daily acid load (hydrogen ions) and make new bicarbonate. In chronic kidney disease, these functions fail, so acid builds up and bicarbonate falls: METABOLIC ACIDOSIS. Phosphate and other acids also accumulate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis is caused by lung problems (CO₂ retention).
• Respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis is caused by over-breathing.
• Metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis is caused by vomiting or diuretics, not CKD.

⭐ Key point: Metabolic acidosis in CKD is treated with oral sodium bicarbonate, and with dialysis if severe.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q9$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 247, $s$A patient with hypochloremia (Cl⁻ 88 mEq/L) and metabolic alkalosis should receive:$s$, $s$["Acetazolamide", "Potassium supplements", "Ammonium chloride", "0.45% saline IV"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 0.45% saline IV
Vomiting or NG drainage causes loss of hydrochloric acid, leading to low chloride (hypochloraemia) and metabolic alkalosis. This "chloride-responsive" alkalosis is corrected by giving CHLORIDE, usually as IV SALINE (sodium chloride). The kidneys can then excrete the extra bicarbonate. The answer key lists 0.45% saline; isotonic 0.9% saline is the usual choice, and both supply chloride.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acetazolamide — Acetazolamide makes the kidneys lose bicarbonate and is used only in special cases (such as fluid-overloaded patients).
• Potassium supplements — Potassium supplements help (low K⁺ often coexists), but chloride replacement is the key.
• Ammonium chloride — Ammonium chloride is an acidifying agent used very rarely for severe alkalosis.

⭐ Key point: Replace potassium as well, because hypokalaemia keeps the alkalosis going.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q10$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 248, $s$In a patient with hypophosphatemia (PO₄²⁻ 1.0 mg/dL), which complication should the nurse anticipate?$s$, $s$["Tetany and positive Trousseau’s sign", "Polyuria and polydipsia", "Muscle weakness and respiratory failure", "Hypertension and edema"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Muscle weakness and respiratory failure
Phosphate is needed to make ATP, the energy for every cell. Severe HYPOPHOSPHATAEMIA (very low phosphate) leaves muscles without energy, causing MUSCLE WEAKNESS, including the diaphragm, which leads to RESPIRATORY FAILURE and failure to wean. It can also cause heart failure, confusion, seizures and red cell breakdown.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tetany and positive Trousseau’s sign — Tetany and Trousseau's sign are signs of LOW CALCIUM.
• Polyuria and polydipsia — Polyuria and polydipsia suggest high glucose or high calcium.
• Hypertension and edema — Hypertension and oedema are not typical of low phosphate.

⭐ Key point: Low phosphate is common in refeeding syndrome, DKA treatment, alcoholism and CRRT. Check and replace it.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q11$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 249, $s$A patient with SIADH has serum Na⁺ 120 mEq/L and urine osmolality 600 mOsm/kg. Which intervention is indicated?$s$, $s$["Free water bolus IV", "Start 0.9% saline at 150 mL/hr", "Administer desmopressin", "Fluid restriction to 800 mL/day"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fluid restriction to 800 mL/day
In SIADH, too much ADH makes the body keep water, diluting the sodium (Na⁺ 120) while the urine is CONCENTRATED (600 mOsm/kg). The first-line treatment is FLUID RESTRICTION (for example 800 mL/day), so the body loses more water than it takes in and the sodium slowly rises.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Free water bolus IV — A free water bolus would make the low sodium WORSE.
• Start 0.9% saline at 150 mL/hr — 0.9% saline can worsen hyponatraemia in SIADH, because the salt is excreted and the water kept.
• Administer desmopressin — Desmopressin acts like ADH and would worsen the problem.

⭐ Key point: For severe symptoms (seizures, coma), give 3% hypertonic saline. Correct no faster than 8–10 mmol/L in 24 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q12$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 250, $s$Which electrolyte would the nurse identify as the major electrolyte responsible for determining the concentration of the extracellular fluid?$s$, $s$["Bicarbonate", "Chloride", "Sodium", "Phosphate"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sodium
SODIUM is the main positively charged ion (cation) outside the cells, in the extracellular fluid. It determines the ECF's concentration (osmolality) and controls where water goes. Serum osmolality is roughly 2 × Na⁺ + glucose + urea.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bicarbonate — Bicarbonate is a buffer, not the main determinant.
• Chloride — Chloride is the main negative ion outside cells but follows sodium.
• Phosphate — Phosphate is mainly inside the cells.

⭐ Key point: Potassium is the main cation INSIDE the cells.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q14$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 251, $s$An ICU patient has pH 7.30, PaCO₂ 30 mmHg, HCO₃⁻ 14 mmol/L, Na⁺ 140 and Cl⁻ 104 mmol/L. The best interpretation is:$s$, $s$["Primary respiratory alkalosis with renal compensation", "High anion gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation", "Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation", "Mixed respiratory acidosis and metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: High anion gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation
Step 1: pH 7.30 shows ACIDOSIS. Step 2: HCO₃⁻ 14 is low, so it is METABOLIC. Step 3: PaCO₂ 30 is low, which is respiratory compensation. Step 4: anion gap = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻) = 140 − (104 + 14) = 22. Normal is about 8–12, so this is a HIGH anion gap metabolic acidosis (such as lactic acidosis or DKA).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Primary respiratory alkalosis with renal compensation — Respiratory alkalosis would have a high pH.
• Normal anion gap metabolic acidosis with respiratory compensation — The anion gap is high (22), not normal.
• Mixed respiratory acidosis and metabolic alkalosis — Mixed respiratory acidosis and metabolic alkalosis would show a high PaCO₂ and high HCO₃⁻.

⭐ Key point: High anion gap causes: MUDPILES (Methanol, Uraemia, DKA, Paraldehyde, Iron/Isoniazid, Lactic acid, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q17$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 252, $s$A septic patient has HCO₃⁻ 12 mmol/L and PaCO₂ 40 mmHg. Using Winter's formula, the nurse recognises:$s$, $s$["A coexisting respiratory alkalosis (hyperventilation)", "Fully appropriate compensation for the acidosis", "A pure metabolic alkalosis with no acidosis", "A coexisting respiratory acidosis (the patient is tiring)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A coexisting respiratory acidosis (the patient is tiring)
Winter's formula predicts the expected PaCO₂ in metabolic acidosis: 1.5 × HCO₃⁻ + 8 (±2). Here: 1.5 × 12 + 8 = 26 (24–28 mmHg). The actual PaCO₂ is 40, much HIGHER than expected. The lungs aren't compensating, so there is ALSO a RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS. The patient is tiring and may need ventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A coexisting respiratory alkalosis (hyperventilation) — A coexisting respiratory alkalosis would give a PaCO₂ LOWER than expected.
• Fully appropriate compensation for the acidosis — Compensation is NOT appropriate (40 is far above 26).
• A pure metabolic alkalosis with no acidosis — There is clearly an acidosis (HCO₃⁻ 12).

⭐ Key point: A septic patient with metabolic acidosis and a "normal" PaCO₂ is in danger. They are tiring, so prepare for respiratory support.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q18$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 253, $s$A patient's anion gap has risen by 12 but HCO₃⁻ has fallen by only 6 (delta ratio about 2). This suggests:$s$, $s$["Pure respiratory acidosis", "A coexisting normal-gap acidosis", "A laboratory error in sodium", "A coexisting metabolic alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A coexisting metabolic alkalosis
The delta ratio compares how much the anion gap has RISEN with how much the bicarbonate has FALLEN. In a pure high anion gap acidosis, they change by about the same amount (ratio 1–2). Here the gap rose by 12 but HCO₃⁻ fell only 6 (ratio 2). The bicarbonate is HIGHER than expected, meaning something is ADDING bicarbonate: a coexisting METABOLIC ALKALOSIS (such as from vomiting).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pure respiratory acidosis — Pure respiratory acidosis doesn't explain an anion gap change.
• A coexisting normal-gap acidosis — A coexisting normal-gap acidosis would make HCO₃⁻ fall MORE (ratio below 1).
• A laboratory error in sodium — A lab error in sodium is possible but not the interpretation here.

⭐ Key point: Delta ratio below 1 = extra normal-gap acidosis; 1–2 = pure high-gap acidosis; above 2 = extra metabolic alkalosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q19$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 254, $s$For hyperkalaemia with ECG changes, after calcium the usual insulin–dextrose dose is:$s$, $s$["1 unit soluble insulin with 500 mL of 50% dextrose", "10 units soluble insulin with 50 mL of 50% dextrose IV", "100 units soluble insulin with 50 mL of 5% dextrose", "10 units long-acting insulin subcutaneously only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10 units soluble insulin with 50 mL of 50% dextrose IV
After calcium, the standard way to SHIFT potassium into cells is 10 UNITS of soluble (short-acting) INSULIN with 25 g of glucose (50 mL of 50% dextrose, or 250 mL of 10%) IV. Insulin lowers K⁺ by 0.6–1.0 mmol/L within 15–30 minutes; the glucose prevents hypoglycaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1 unit soluble insulin with 500 mL of 50% dextrose — 1 unit of insulin is too little, and 500 mL of 50% dextrose is far too much glucose.
• 100 units soluble insulin with 50 mL of 5% dextrose — 100 units of insulin would cause severe hypoglycaemia.
• 10 units long-acting insulin subcutaneously only — Long-acting subcutaneous insulin works too slowly.

⭐ Key point: Check blood glucose regularly for up to 6 hours after insulin and dextrose; hypoglycaemia is common, especially in kidney failure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q20$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 255, $s$In respiratory acidosis, the kidneys compensate by:$s$, $s$["Decreasing HCO3 reabsorption", "Increasing HCO3 reabsorption and H+ excretion", "Having no role in compensation", "Immediately normalising pH within minutes"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increasing HCO3 reabsorption and H+ excretion
In respiratory acidosis (CO₂ retention), the kidneys compensate by INCREASING BICARBONATE reabsorption and making new bicarbonate, while EXCRETING more hydrogen ions (as ammonium and acid phosphate) in the urine. This raises blood bicarbonate, bringing the pH back towards normal. It takes 3–5 days.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Decreasing HCO3 reabsorption — Decreasing bicarbonate would worsen the acidosis.
• Having no role in compensation — The kidneys DO have a role, the main compensation in respiratory acidosis.
• Immediately normalising pH within minutes — Kidney compensation is slow (days), not minutes.

⭐ Key point: Acute respiratory acidosis = low pH, normal HCO₃⁻. Chronic (compensated) = near-normal pH, high HCO₃⁻.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q35$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 256, $s$A diabetic patient on iv normal saline was discovered to have random blood sugar RBS of 2.5mmol/l, which of this action will you take as critical care Nurse?$s$, $s$["Change to ringers' lactate", "Stop the fluid and wait for the Physician", "Send for the physician and wait for his order", "Change the fluid to glucose containing fluid"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Change the fluid to glucose containing fluid
A blood sugar of 2.5 mmol/L is HYPOGLYCAEMIA (below 4 mmol/L), which can cause seizures, brain damage and death. Plain normal saline contains no glucose. The nurse should immediately CHANGE to (or add) a GLUCOSE-containing fluid (such as 10% dextrose 150–200 mL), recheck glucose in 10–15 minutes, and inform the doctor.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Change to ringers' lactate — Ringer's lactate has no glucose and won't correct hypoglycaemia.
• Stop the fluid and wait for the Physician — Stopping the fluid and waiting wastes time.
• Send for the physician and wait for his order — Waiting for an order delays urgent treatment.

⭐ Key point: Treat hypoglycaemia promptly under protocol, then find the cause (insulin dose, missed feeds, sepsis, liver failure).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q89$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 257, $s$The most accurate day-to-day measure of fluid balance in a critically ill patient is:$s$, $s$["Urine colour", "Thirst", "Daily weight", "Skin turgor"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Daily weight
DAILY WEIGHT is the most accurate everyday measure of fluid balance. A change of 1 kg is roughly 1 litre of fluid gained or lost. Weigh at the same time each day, with the same scales and similar clothing or bedding (bed scales in ICU).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Urine colour — Urine colour is a rough guide only.
• Thirst — Thirst is unreliable, especially in sedated or elderly patients.
• Skin turgor — Skin turgor is unreliable, especially in the elderly.

⭐ Key point: Combine daily weight with fluid balance charts, clinical examination and blood tests.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q92$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 258, $s$When hypocalcaemia occurs, the nurse avoids disturbing the client because:$s$, $s$["Anxiety worsens spasms", "Pain relief is priority", "Movement increases bleeding", "Rest prevents infection"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Anxiety worsens spasms
In HYPOCALCAEMIA, nerves and muscles become overexcitable, causing spasms, tetany, cramps and even seizures and laryngospasm. Anxiety, noise and stimulation can WORSEN the spasms (hyperventilation from anxiety also lowers ionised calcium). Keeping the patient calm and in a quiet environment reduces spasms while calcium is replaced.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pain relief is priority — Pain relief isn't the reason here.
• Movement increases bleeding — Movement doesn't cause bleeding in hypocalcaemia.
• Rest prevents infection — Rest doesn't prevent infection.

⭐ Key point: Treat symptomatic hypocalcaemia with IV calcium gluconate 10% (10–20 mL slowly), on a cardiac monitor.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q93$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 259, $s$The antidote for magnesium sulfate toxicity is:$s$, $s$["IV potassium chloride 20 mmol over 10 minutes", "IV sodium bicarbonate 50 mmol over 10 minutes", "IV naloxone 0.4 mg repeated every 2 minutes", "10 mL of 10% calcium gluconate IV over 10 minutes"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10 mL of 10% calcium gluconate IV over 10 minutes
CALCIUM GLUCONATE reverses magnesium toxicity, because calcium competes with magnesium at the nerve and muscle membranes. The dose is 10 mL of 10% calcium gluconate (1 g) IV slowly over 10 minutes. Stop the magnesium infusion and support breathing (oxygen, ventilation if needed).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV potassium chloride 20 mmol over 10 minutes — Potassium chloride doesn't reverse magnesium toxicity.
• IV sodium bicarbonate 50 mmol over 10 minutes — Sodium bicarbonate doesn't reverse it.
• IV naloxone 0.4 mg repeated every 2 minutes — Naloxone reverses opioids, not magnesium.

⭐ Key point: Keep calcium gluconate at the bedside whenever magnesium sulphate is running.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q135 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 260, $s$A patient on furosemide (a loop diuretic) should be monitored closely for which electrolyte disturbance?$s$, $s$["Hypokalaemia", "Hypernatraemia only", "Hyperkalaemia", "Hypercalcaemia only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypokalaemia
Furosemide is a loop diuretic. It makes the kidneys lose sodium, and in exchange the kidneys also lose POTASSIUM in the urine, causing HYPOKALAEMIA. Low potassium can cause muscle weakness, cramps and dangerous arrhythmias (especially with digoxin). Magnesium is also lost.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypernatraemia only — Furosemide doesn't usually cause high sodium.
• Hyperkalaemia — It causes LOW, not high, potassium.
• Hypercalcaemia only — It causes calcium LOSS (it's used to treat high calcium), not hypercalcaemia.

⭐ Key point: Monitor U&Es on loop diuretics. Give potassium supplements or combine with a potassium-sparing diuretic if needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q155$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 261, $s$In a patient undergoing surgery, it was vital to aspirate the contents of the upper gastrointestinal tract. After the operation, the following values were acquired from an arterial blood sample: pH 7.55, PCO2 52 mm Hg and HCO3- 40 mmol/l. What is the underlying disorder?$s$, $s$["Metabolic Acidosis", "Respiratory Acidosis", "Respiratory Alkalosis", "Metabolic Alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic Alkalosis
Step 1: pH 7.55 is HIGH, so ALKALOSIS. Step 2: HCO₃⁻ 40 is HIGH (pH and HCO₃⁻ both up), so it is METABOLIC. Step 3: PCO₂ 52 is high, which is the lungs COMPENSATING by retaining CO₂. Cause: aspiration (suction) of the upper GI tract removed acidic stomach contents (HCl), leaving excess bicarbonate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic Acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a LOW pH and LOW HCO₃⁻.
• Respiratory Acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would have a LOW pH.
• Respiratory Alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a LOW PCO₂.

⭐ Key point: NG suction and vomiting cause hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis. Replace chloride (saline) and potassium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q3$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 262, $s$An elementary student, was rushed to the hospital due to vomiting and a decreased level of consciousness. The patient displays slow and deep (Kussmaul breathing), and he is lethargic and irritable in response to stimulation. He appears to be dehydrated—his eyes are sunken and mucous membranes are dry—and he has a two-week history of polydipsia, polyuria, and weight loss. Measurement of arterial blood gas shows pH 7.0, PaO2 90 mm Hg, PaCO2 23 mm Hg, and HCO3 12 mmol/L; other results are Na+ 126 mmol/L, K+ 5 mmol/L, and Cl- 95 mmol/L. What is your assessment?$s$, $s$["Respiratory Acidosis, Partially Compensated", "Metabolic Alkalosis, Uncompensated", "Respiratory Acidosis, Uncompensated", "Metabolic Acidosis, Partially, Compensated"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic Acidosis, Partially, Compensated
Step 1: pH 7.0 is very LOW, so ACIDOSIS. Step 2: HCO₃⁻ 12 is LOW, so it is METABOLIC (DKA, with polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss and Kussmaul breathing). Step 3: PaCO₂ 23 is low, meaning the lungs are blowing off CO₂ to COMPENSATE. The pH is still abnormal, so it is PARTIALLY compensated metabolic acidosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory Acidosis, Partially Compensated — Respiratory acidosis would have a HIGH PaCO₂.
• Metabolic Alkalosis, Uncompensated — Metabolic alkalosis would have a HIGH pH and HCO₃⁻.
• Respiratory Acidosis, Uncompensated — Respiratory acidosis would have a HIGH PaCO₂ again.

⭐ Key point: Anion gap here = 126 − (95 + 12) = 19, which is high, as expected in DKA.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q8$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 263, $s$The thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle actively reabsorbs sodium, potassium, and chloride via which transporter, the target of loop diuretics?$s$, $s$["Sodium-glucose cotransporter (SGLT2)", "Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC2)", "Epithelial sodium channel (ENaC)", "Na+-Cl- cotransporter (NCC)"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Na+-K+-2Cl- cotransporter (NKCC2)
The thick ascending limb of the loop of Henle reabsorbs sodium, potassium and chloride together using the Na⁺-K⁺-2Cl⁻ COTRANSPORTER (NKCC2). LOOP DIURETICS (furosemide, bumetanide) block NKCC2, so large amounts of salt and water are lost in the urine, making them very powerful diuretics.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sodium-glucose cotransporter (SGLT2) — SGLT2 is in the proximal tubule; SGLT2 inhibitors (gliflozins) block it.
• Epithelial sodium channel (ENaC) — ENaC is in the collecting duct; amiloride blocks it.
• Na+-Cl- cotransporter (NCC) — NCC is in the distal tubule; thiazides block it.

⭐ Key point: Loop diuretic side effects: low potassium, magnesium and calcium, dehydration, and ototoxicity at high doses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q11$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 264, $s$Which pH finding would be consistent with respiratory alkalosis?$s$, $s$["7.49", "7.35", "7.29", "7.45"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 7.49
Respiratory alkalosis is caused by hyperventilation (anxiety, pain, early sepsis, PE, high altitude), which lowers PaCO₂. Less CO₂ means less acid, so the pH RISES above 7.45. A pH of 7.49 is alkalotic.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 7.35 — 7.35 is the lower limit of normal.
• 7.29 — 7.29 is acidotic.
• 7.45 — 7.45 is the upper limit of normal.

⭐ Key point: Normal pH 7.35–7.45. Below 7.35 is acidosis; above 7.45 is alkalosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q12$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 265, $s$A patient with hypokalemia (K⁺ 2.8 mEq/L) is receiving IV potassium. Which nursing action is essential?$s$, $s$["Infuse at 20 mEq/hr via central line", "Administer as an IV bolus", "Dilute in 50 mL D5W over 10 minutes", "Monitor serum K⁺ every 2 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Monitor serum K⁺ every 2 hours
When giving IV potassium, check the serum K⁺ REGULARLY (about every 2–4 hours during rapid replacement) to avoid overcorrection to dangerous HYPERKALAEMIA. Also use an infusion pump, continuous ECG monitoring for faster rates, and watch the IV site (potassium irritates veins).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Infuse at 20 mEq/hr via central line — 20 mmol/hour via a central line is possible with monitoring, but monitoring K⁺ is the essential action.
• Administer as an IV bolus — IV potassium must NEVER be given as a bolus; it can cause cardiac arrest.
• Dilute in 50 mL D5W over 10 minutes — 50 mL over 10 minutes is far too fast.

⭐ Key point: Peripheral K⁺ is usually no more than 10 mmol/hour. Always use pre-diluted solutions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q13$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 266, $s$A patient develops tingling of fingers, muscle cramps, and carpopedal spasm after thyroidectomy. The nurse should suspect:$s$, $s$["Hypocalcaemia", "Hyperkalemia", "Hypercalcaemia", "Hyponatremia"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypocalcaemia
After thyroidectomy, the PARATHYROID glands (behind the thyroid) may be damaged or removed, so PTH falls and blood CALCIUM drops (HYPOCALCAEMIA). Low calcium makes nerves and muscles overexcitable: tingling around the mouth and fingers, muscle cramps, CARPOPEDAL SPASM (Trousseau's sign), facial twitching (Chvostek's sign), and in severe cases laryngospasm and seizures.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperkalemia — High potassium causes weakness and ECG changes, not spasms.
• Hypercalcaemia — High calcium causes weakness, constipation and confusion, not spasms.
• Hyponatremia — Low sodium causes confusion and seizures, not carpopedal spasm.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV calcium gluconate 10%, and keep it available after thyroid and parathyroid surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q15$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 267, $s$A patient has measured Na⁺ 128 mmol/L with blood glucose 40 mmol/L (720 mg/dL). The corrected sodium is about:$s$, $s$["143 mmol/L", "160 mmol/L", "118 mmol/L", "128 mmol/L"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 143 mmol/L
High glucose draws water from the cells into the blood, DILUTING the sodium (pseudo-hyponatraemia, or translocational hyponatraemia). To find the true (corrected) sodium, add about 2.4 mmol/L for every 5.5 mmol/L of glucose above normal. Glucose 40 is about 34.5 above 5.5 → 34.5 ÷ 5.5 ≈ 6.3 × 2.4 ≈ 15. Corrected Na⁺ = 128 + 15 ≈ 143 mmol/L.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 160 mmol/L — 160 mmol/L is too high a correction.
• 118 mmol/L — 118 mmol/L subtracts instead of adding.
• 128 mmol/L — 128 mmol/L ignores the correction.

⭐ Key point: In DKA and HHS, watch the corrected sodium. If it rises as glucose falls, the patient is losing free water.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q17$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 268, $s$In chronic severe hyponatraemia, sodium should not be raised by more than about:$s$, $s$["2 mmol/L in the first week", "15 mmol/L in the first hour", "8–10 mmol/L in the first 24 hours", "20–25 mmol/L in the first 24 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 8–10 mmol/L in the first 24 hours
In chronic severe hyponatraemia, brain cells have adapted. If sodium is corrected too quickly, osmotic demyelination syndrome (central pontine myelinolysis) can cause paralysis, swallowing difficulty or death. Sodium should NOT be raised by more than about 8–10 mmol/L in the first 24 hours (6–8 in high-risk patients).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2 mmol/L in the first week — 2 mmol/L in a week is far too slow.
• 15 mmol/L in the first hour — 15 mmol/L in an hour is dangerously fast.
• 20–25 mmol/L in the first 24 hours — 20–25 mmol/L in 24 hours is far too fast.

⭐ Key point: For severe symptoms (seizures), raise sodium by 4–6 mmol/L quickly with 3% saline, then stay within the 24-hour limit.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q18$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 269, $s$The electrolyte abnormality most typical of refeeding syndrome in a starved ICU patient is:$s$, $s$["Hypercalcaemia", "Hyperkalaemia", "Hypophosphataemia", "Hypernatraemia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypophosphataemia
In REFEEDING syndrome, a starved patient's body switches back to carbohydrate metabolism when feeding starts. Insulin rises, driving glucose, PHOSPHATE, potassium and magnesium into the cells. Blood phosphate drops sharply (HYPOPHOSPHATAEMIA), causing muscle weakness, respiratory failure, heart failure, confusion and seizures.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypercalcaemia — Hypercalcaemia isn't typical of refeeding.
• Hyperkalaemia — Potassium FALLS (hypokalaemia), it doesn't rise.
• Hypernatraemia — Hypernatraemia isn't typical; sodium and water retention can occur, but not high sodium.

⭐ Key point: Prevent refeeding: identify at-risk patients, give thiamine, start slowly (10–20 kcal/kg/day), and check and replace phosphate, potassium and magnesium daily.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q19$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 270, $s$A patient has Hb 10 g/dL, SaO₂ 100% and PaO₂ 100 mmHg. The arterial oxygen content (CaO₂) is about:$s$, $s$["13.7 mL O₂ per dL of blood", "1.34 mL O₂ per dL of blood", "10.0 mL O₂ per dL of blood", "20.1 mL O₂ per dL of blood"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 13.7 mL O₂ per dL of blood
Arterial oxygen content (CaO₂) = (1.34 × Hb × SaO₂) + (0.003 × PaO₂). Here: (1.34 × 10 × 1.0) + (0.003 × 100) = 13.4 + 0.3 = 13.7 mL O₂/dL. Most oxygen is carried by HAEMOGLOBIN; dissolved oxygen (from PaO₂) adds very little.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1.34 mL O₂ per dL of blood — 1.34 is just the oxygen-binding constant.
• 10.0 mL O₂ per dL of blood — 10.0 is the haemoglobin value.
• 20.1 mL O₂ per dL of blood — 20.1 is the normal CaO₂ with Hb 15 g/dL.

⭐ Key point: To improve oxygen delivery, correcting low haemoglobin is often more effective than raising PaO₂ above normal.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q20$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 271, $s$An ABG analysis report shows: pH - 7.30; PCO2 - 35mmHg; HCO3- -18mEq/L. These findings are suggestive of$s$, $s$["Metabolic alkalosis", "Respiratory alkalosis", "Metabolic acidosis", "Respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic acidosis
Step 1: pH 7.30 is LOW, so ACIDOSIS. Step 2: HCO₃⁻ 18 is LOW, so it is METABOLIC. Step 3: PCO₂ 35 is at the lower end of normal, so there is little respiratory compensation yet. This is METABOLIC ACIDOSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a HIGH pH and HCO₃⁻.
• Respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a HIGH pH and LOW PCO₂.
• Respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis would have a HIGH PCO₂.

⭐ Key point: ROME: Respiratory = Opposite (pH and PCO₂ move opposite ways); Metabolic = Equal (pH and HCO₃⁻ move the same way).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q28$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 272, $s$A patient with an NG tube to suction has metabolic alkalosis. The nurse anticipates:$s$, $s$["Hypercalcemia", "Hypermagnesemia", "Hypokalemia", "Hypernatremia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypokalemia
NG suction removes stomach fluid containing hydrochloric acid (H⁺ and Cl⁻), and also some potassium. Losing acid causes METABOLIC ALKALOSIS. In alkalosis, potassium moves into cells, and the kidneys excrete more potassium to save hydrogen ions, causing HYPOKALAEMIA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypercalcemia — Hypercalcaemia isn't typical.
• Hypermagnesemia — Hypermagnesaemia isn't typical.
• Hypernatremia — Hypernatraemia isn't the expected finding.

⭐ Key point: Replace NG losses with saline plus potassium chloride.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q92$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 273, $s$After a chest injury, ABGs show pH 7.26, PaCO2 52 mmHg, HCO3 25 mmol/L. This is:$s$, $s$["Metabolic alkalosis", "Respiratory alkalosis", "Metabolic acidosis", "Respiratory acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory acidosis
Step 1: pH 7.26 is LOW, so ACIDOSIS. Step 2: PaCO₂ 52 is HIGH (pH and PaCO₂ move in opposite directions), so it is RESPIRATORY. Step 3: HCO₃⁻ 25 is normal, so the kidneys haven't compensated yet: an ACUTE (uncompensated) respiratory acidosis. After a chest injury, pain, rib fractures, flail chest or pneumothorax cause hypoventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a HIGH pH and HIGH HCO₃⁻.
• Respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a HIGH pH and LOW PaCO₂.
• Metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a LOW HCO₃⁻.

⭐ Key point: Treat the cause: pain relief (allows deep breathing), drain a pneumothorax, and support ventilation if needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q1$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 274, $s$Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs are clinical indicators of:$s$, $s$["Hypomagnesaemia exclusively", "Hyperkalaemia", "Hypocalcaemia", "Hypernatraemia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypocalcaemia
CHVOSTEK's sign: tapping the facial nerve in front of the ear causes twitching of the face. TROUSSEAU's sign: inflating a BP cuff above systolic for 3 minutes causes spasm of the hand and wrist (carpal spasm). Both show NERVE and MUSCLE overexcitability caused by HYPOCALCAEMIA (low calcium).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypomagnesaemia exclusively — Low magnesium can cause these signs too, but they are classic signs of low calcium; "exclusively magnesium" is wrong.
• Hyperkalaemia — High potassium causes weakness and ECG changes, not these signs.
• Hypernatraemia — High sodium causes confusion and seizures, not these signs.

⭐ Key point: Hypocalcaemia causes tingling, cramps, tetany, laryngospasm, seizures and a long QT. Treat with IV calcium gluconate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q2$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 275, $s$A postoperative patient with hypocalcemia (ionized Ca²⁺ 0.8 mmol/L) exhibits carpopedal spasm and laryngeal stridor. Which action takes priority?$s$, $s$["Apply a nasal cannula at 2 L/min", "Give vitamin D supplements", "Infuse 10% calcium gluconate IV", "Administer oral calcium carbonate"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Infuse 10% calcium gluconate IV
Ionised calcium of 0.8 mmol/L (normal 1.1–1.3) with CARPOPEDAL SPASM and LARYNGEAL STRIDOR is severe, symptomatic hypocalcaemia. Laryngospasm can block the airway. The priority is IV 10% CALCIUM GLUCONATE (10–20 mL slowly over 10 minutes, then an infusion), with cardiac monitoring.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Apply a nasal cannula at 2 L/min — Low-flow oxygen doesn't treat the cause of stridor here.
• Give vitamin D supplements — Vitamin D takes days to work.
• Administer oral calcium carbonate — Oral calcium is too slow for an emergency.

⭐ Key point: Give IV calcium slowly; fast injection can cause arrhythmias. Check magnesium too (low magnesium causes low calcium).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q5$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 276, $s$In a patient with hyperkalemia, which ECG change warrants IMMEDIATE intervention?$s$, $s$["Peaked T waves", "ST-segment depression", "Sinus tachycardia", "First-degree AV block"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Peaked T waves
PEAKED T WAVES are an early ECG sign of hyperkalaemia, but any ECG change in high potassium means the heart is affected and IMMEDIATE treatment is needed (calcium gluconate to protect the heart, then insulin with glucose). Without treatment, changes can quickly progress to a wide QRS, a sine-wave pattern, VF or asystole.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• ST-segment depression — ST depression isn't a typical sign of hyperkalaemia.
• Sinus tachycardia — Sinus tachycardia isn't a sign of hyperkalaemia.
• First-degree AV block — First-degree AV block can occur, but peaked T waves are the classic warning sign.

⭐ Key point: Hyperkalaemia ECG changes in order: peaked T → flat P → long PR → wide QRS → sine wave → VF or asystole.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q6$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 277, $s$A patient with hypermagnesemia (Mg²⁺ 4.5 mg/dL) has hypotension and absent deep tendon reflexes. Which intervention is MOST appropriate?$s$, $s$["Give calcium chloride 10% IV", "Administer magnesium sulfate IV", "Infuse 0.9% normal saline bolus", "Start furosemide 40 mg IV"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Give calcium chloride 10% IV
High magnesium blocks nerve and muscle transmission and relaxes blood vessels, causing ABSENT reflexes, weakness, HYPOTENSION, slow pulse and respiratory depression. CALCIUM (calcium chloride or gluconate IV) directly opposes magnesium's effects on the heart and muscles. It is the first, most important treatment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer magnesium sulfate IV — More magnesium would worsen toxicity.
• Infuse 0.9% normal saline bolus — Saline can help the kidneys excrete magnesium, but isn't the most urgent step.
• Start furosemide 40 mg IV — Furosemide increases magnesium excretion but works too slowly for a symptomatic patient.

⭐ Key point: Stop all magnesium, give IV calcium, and consider dialysis in severe toxicity with kidney failure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q7$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 278, $s$Positive Chvostek's sign indicates:$s$, $s$["Hypernatremia", "Hypercalcaemia", "Hypokalemia", "Hypocalcaemia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypocalcaemia
A positive CHVOSTEK's sign (twitching of the facial muscles when the facial nerve in front of the ear is tapped) shows nerve overexcitability caused by HYPOCALCAEMIA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypernatremia — High sodium doesn't cause Chvostek's sign.
• Hypercalcaemia — HIGH calcium causes weakness, not twitching.
• Hypokalemia — Low potassium causes weakness, not Chvostek's sign.

⭐ Key point: Trousseau's sign (carpal spasm with a BP cuff) is more specific for hypocalcaemia than Chvostek's.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q9$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 279, $s$A completely normal pH on an ABG:$s$, $s$["Only occurs in healthy people", "Always rules out any acid-base disorder", "Does NOT rule out a mixed disorder", "Means PaCO2 and HCO3 must also be normal"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Does NOT rule out a mixed disorder
A NORMAL pH doesn't always mean everything is normal. Two OPPOSING disorders (for example respiratory acidosis plus metabolic alkalosis, or metabolic acidosis plus respiratory alkalosis) can cancel each other out, giving a normal pH. So a normal pH does NOT rule out a MIXED acid–base disorder. Always check the PaCO₂ and HCO₃⁻.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only occurs in healthy people — A normal pH can occur in sick people with mixed disorders.
• Always rules out any acid-base disorder — It doesn't always rule out a disorder.
• Means PaCO2 and HCO3 must also be normal — PaCO₂ and HCO₃⁻ can both be abnormal with a normal pH.

⭐ Key point: If PaCO₂ and HCO₃⁻ are both abnormal but the pH is normal, suspect a mixed disorder.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q11$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 280, $s$Characteristic ECG changes of hypokalaemia include:$s$, $s$["Shortened QT interval", "Flattened T waves and prominent U waves", "Peaked T waves and widened QRS", "Complete absence of P waves in all cases"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Flattened T waves and prominent U waves
Low potassium slows the heart's recovery (repolarisation). The ECG shows FLATTENED or inverted T WAVES, prominent U WAVES (an extra wave after the T), ST depression, and a longer QU interval. Severe hypokalaemia can cause dangerous arrhythmias.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shortened QT interval — A shortened QT is seen in HIGH calcium.
• Peaked T waves and widened QRS — Peaked T waves and a wide QRS are signs of HIGH potassium.
• Complete absence of P waves in all cases — Absent P waves occur in severe hyperkalaemia or AF.

⭐ Key point: Replace potassium carefully (IV no faster than 10 mmol/hour peripherally) and check magnesium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q13$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 281, $s$A patient with hypercalcemia is prescribed calcitonin. The nurse should monitor for:$s$, $s$["Hyperphosphatemia", "Hypoglycemia", "Tachyphylaxis", "Hypocalcemia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tachyphylaxis
CALCITONIN lowers calcium quickly (it reduces bone breakdown and increases kidney calcium excretion), but its effect fades after about 48 hours because the body stops responding: TACHYPHYLAXIS. The nurse should monitor for this loss of effect (calcium rising again). Bisphosphonates are given at the same time for a longer-lasting effect.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperphosphatemia — Calcitonin doesn't cause high phosphate.
• Hypoglycemia — It doesn't usually cause hypoglycaemia.
• Hypocalcemia — Hypocalcaemia is possible but uncommon; tachyphylaxis is the expected issue.

⭐ Key point: Hypercalcaemia treatment: IV saline (first), calcitonin (fast but short-acting), and bisphosphonates (slow but long-acting).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q14$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 282, $s$A potassium chloride infusion of 40 mEq is ordered in 500 mL of NS to run over 4 hours via a peripheral line. What is the resulting rate of potassium delivery in mEq/hr, and is this within the generally accepted peripheral IV limit of 10 mEq/hr?$s$, $s$["20 mEq/hr; exceeds the peripheral limit", "5 mEq/hr; well within the peripheral limit", "40 mEq/hr; grossly exceeds the peripheral limit", "10 mEq/hr; at the upper safe peripheral limit"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10 mEq/hr; at the upper safe peripheral limit
Rate of potassium = total dose ÷ time = 40 mmol ÷ 4 hours = 10 mmol per hour. This is AT the usual maximum safe rate for a PERIPHERAL line (10 mmol/hour), so it is acceptable, but at the upper limit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20 mEq/hr; exceeds the peripheral limit — 20 mmol/hour would need 40 mmol over 2 hours.
• 5 mEq/hr; well within the peripheral limit — 5 mmol/hour would need 40 mmol over 8 hours.
• 40 mEq/hr; grossly exceeds the peripheral limit — 40 mmol/hour would be 40 mmol in 1 hour, which is dangerous.

⭐ Key point: Faster potassium (up to 20 mmol/hour or more) needs a central line and continuous ECG monitoring.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q15$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 283, $s$Hypokalaemia that does not correct despite potassium replacement should prompt the nurse to check:$s$, $s$["Serum urea, as it pushes K⁺ into cells", "Serum magnesium, as low Mg²⁺ causes renal K⁺ loss", "Serum sodium, as high Na⁺ blocks K⁺ uptake", "Serum albumin, as it binds most of the K⁺"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Serum magnesium, as low Mg²⁺ causes renal K⁺ loss
Magnesium is needed for the kidney to hold on to potassium. When magnesium is LOW, the kidneys keep losing potassium, so the hypokalaemia won't correct even with replacement. Check SERUM MAGNESIUM and replace it first (or at the same time); then the potassium will rise.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Serum urea, as it pushes K⁺ into cells — Urea doesn't push potassium into cells.
• Serum sodium, as high Na⁺ blocks K⁺ uptake — Sodium doesn't block potassium uptake in this way.
• Serum albumin, as it binds most of the K⁺ — Albumin doesn't bind most potassium.

⭐ Key point: Low potassium and low magnesium often occur together (diuretics, diarrhoea, alcohol, refeeding).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q18$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 284, $s$A patient with persistent vomiting has metabolic alkalosis and urine chloride below 20 mmol/L. This alkalosis is:$s$, $s$["Saline-responsive; treat with 0.9% saline and potassium", "Saline-resistant; treat with spironolactone only", "Respiratory in origin; treat with rebreathing", "Due to mineralocorticoid excess; treat with surgery"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Saline-responsive; treat with 0.9% saline and potassium
Persistent vomiting causes loss of acid (HCl) and fluid, creating metabolic alkalosis with low chloride. A URINE CHLORIDE BELOW 20 mmol/L shows the kidneys are holding on to chloride (chloride and volume depletion). This is SALINE-RESPONSIVE alkalosis: treat with 0.9% SALINE (replacing chloride and volume) plus POTASSIUM.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Saline-resistant; treat with spironolactone only — Saline-RESISTANT alkalosis has a HIGH urine chloride (such as mineralocorticoid excess).
• Respiratory in origin; treat with rebreathing — The cause is metabolic, not respiratory.
• Due to mineralocorticoid excess; treat with surgery — Mineralocorticoid excess causes saline-resistant alkalosis with high urine chloride.

⭐ Key point: Urine chloride below 20 = saline-responsive (vomiting, NG suction, diuretics after stopping). Above 20 = saline-resistant (Conn's syndrome, Cushing's, severe low potassium).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q19$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 285, $s$20, PCO2 55 mm hg, PO2 65 mm hg, HCO3 22 m mol. What does this mean?$s$, $s$["Respiratory acidosis", "Metabolic acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis", "Respiratory alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory acidosis
Read the values (the pH is shown as ".20", meaning 7.20). pH 7.20 is LOW, so ACIDOSIS. PCO₂ 55 is HIGH, so it is RESPIRATORY. HCO₃⁻ 22 is normal (no kidney compensation yet). PO₂ 65 is low (hypoxaemia). This is acute RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS with hypoxaemia: type 2 respiratory failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic acidosis — Metabolic acidosis would have a LOW HCO₃⁻.
• Metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis would have a HIGH pH and HCO₃⁻.
• Respiratory alkalosis — Respiratory alkalosis would have a HIGH pH and LOW PCO₂.

⭐ Key point: Treat acute respiratory acidosis by improving ventilation (treat the cause, NIV, or intubation).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q21$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 286, $s$In metabolic acidosis, the expected respiratory compensation (fall in PaCO2) is calculated as:$s$, $s$["2 × the pH change", "1.25 × the HCO3 fall", "0.1 × the HCO3 fall", "No compensation ever occurs"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1.25 × the HCO3 fall
In metabolic acidosis, the lungs compensate by breathing faster to lower PaCO₂. A rule of thumb: the PaCO₂ falls by about 1.25 mmHg for every 1 mmol/L fall in HCO₃⁻. Winter's formula is another way: expected PaCO₂ = 1.5 × HCO₃⁻ + 8 (±2).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2 × the pH change — 2 × the pH change isn't the compensation formula.
• 0.1 × the HCO3 fall — 0.1 × the HCO₃⁻ fall is too small.
• No compensation ever occurs — Compensation DOES occur, through the lungs within minutes.

⭐ Key point: If the actual PaCO₂ is higher than expected, there is also respiratory acidosis (the patient is tiring).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q30$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 287, $s$When is the use of spironolactone possibly contraindicated?$s$, $s$["In patients with refractory congestive heart failure", "When also prescribing furosemide", "In hyperkalaemia patients", "In hypernatremia patients"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: In hyperkalaemia patients
Spironolactone is a POTASSIUM-SPARING diuretic: it blocks aldosterone, so the kidneys keep potassium. In a patient who already has HYPERKALAEMIA (high potassium), it can raise potassium to dangerous levels and cause cardiac arrest. It is contraindicated in hyperkalaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• In patients with refractory congestive heart failure — Spironolactone is USED in refractory heart failure (it reduces mortality).
• When also prescribing furosemide — It is often combined with furosemide (which balances potassium loss).
• In hypernatremia patients — High sodium isn't a contraindication.

⭐ Key point: Avoid combining spironolactone with ACE inhibitors and potassium supplements without close monitoring.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q46$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A2 · Acid–Base, ABG, Fluids & Electrolytes$s$, false, 288, $s$The pharmacy needs 500 mL of a 5% dextrose solution but only has 50% dextrose stock solution and sterile water available. How much of the 50% stock solution is needed?$s$, $s$["50 mL of stock, diluted with 450 mL water", "100 mL of stock, diluted with 400 mL water", "250 mL of stock, diluted with 250 mL water", "5 mL of stock, diluted with 495 mL water"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 50 mL of stock, diluted with 450 mL water
Use the dilution formula C1 × V1 = C2 × V2. Stock concentration (C1) = 50%, needed concentration (C2) = 5%, needed volume (V2) = 500 mL. 50 × V1 = 5 × 500 → V1 = 2500 ÷ 50 = 50 mL of stock. Then add 500 − 50 = 450 mL of sterile water to make 500 mL of 5% dextrose.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100 mL of stock, diluted with 400 mL water — 100 mL of stock would give 10% dextrose.
• 250 mL of stock, diluted with 250 mL water — 250 mL of stock would give 25% dextrose.
• 5 mL of stock, diluted with 495 mL water — 5 mL of stock would give 0.5% dextrose.

⭐ Key point: Check: 50 mL of 50% contains 25 g of dextrose; 25 g in 500 mL = 5 g per 100 mL = 5%.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q148$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 289, $s$What are the two key pharmacological antidotes used in the management of organophosphate poisoning?$s$, $s$["Atropine and pralidoxime", "Naloxone and flumazenil", "Atropine and naloxone", "Pralidoxime and N-acetylcysteine"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Atropine and pralidoxime
Organophosphates (insecticides such as Sniper/DDVP) block the enzyme acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine builds up. This causes SLUDGE signs (salivation, lacrimation, urination, diarrhoea, GI cramps, emesis), small pupils, slow pulse, bronchorrhoea (wet chest), and muscle twitching and weakness. ATROPINE blocks the muscarinic effects and dries the chest (give it until the chest is clear). PRALIDOXIME re-activates the enzyme (helps the muscle weakness) if given early.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone and flumazenil — Naloxone and flumazenil reverse opioids and benzodiazepines, not organophosphates.
• Atropine and naloxone — Naloxone has no role here; only atropine is correct in that pair.
• Pralidoxime and N-acetylcysteine — N-acetylcysteine is the antidote for paracetamol; only pralidoxime is correct in that pair.

⭐ Key point: Organophosphate care: remove contaminated clothes, wash the skin (wear PPE yourself), protect the airway, then give large doses of atropine plus pralidoxime.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q28$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 290, $s$Protamine sulphate is antidote of$s$, $s$["Heparin", "Ephedrine", "Pethadine", "Scoline"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Heparin
Protamine sulphate reverses HEPARIN. Protamine is strongly positive (basic) and heparin is strongly negative (acidic), so they bind and neutralise each other. 1 mg of protamine reverses about 100 units of heparin given in the last 2–3 hours. It only partly reverses LMWH (enoxaparin). Give it slowly IV; fast injection causes low BP, slow pulse and anaphylaxis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ephedrine — Ephedrine is a vasopressor for low BP during anaesthesia; it has no protamine antidote.
• Pethadine — Pethidine is an opioid; its antidote is naloxone.
• Scoline — Scoline (suxamethonium) is a muscle relaxant; it has no specific antidote (prolonged block is managed by ventilation).

⭐ Key point: Heparin → protamine. Warfarin → vitamin K plus PCC or FFP. Opioids → naloxone. Benzodiazepines → flumazenil.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q29$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 291, $s$First drug used to treat bradycardia:$s$, $s$["Epinephrine", "Amiodarone", "Deltacortil", "Atropine"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Atropine
For symptomatic bradycardia (low BP, chest pain, shock, syncope, heart failure), the first drug is ATROPINE 500 micrograms IV, repeated up to a total of 3 mg. Atropine blocks the vagus nerve, which speeds up the SA node and AV conduction. If atropine fails, use transcutaneous pacing or adrenaline/isoprenaline infusions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Epinephrine — Epinephrine (adrenaline) is a second-line infusion if atropine fails, or for cardiac arrest.
• Amiodarone — Amiodarone treats FAST rhythms (VT, AF) and can slow the heart further.
• Deltacortil — Deltacortil (prednisolone) is a steroid and has no role in bradycardia.

⭐ Key point: Atropine may not work in a heart transplant patient (no vagal supply) or in complete heart block with wide QRS; prepare for pacing.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q31$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 292, $s$A patient is prescribed dopamine 5 mcg/kg/min. The patient weighs 70 kg and the infusion is mixed as 400 mg in 250 mL D5W. What is the infusion rate in mL/hr?$s$, $s$["13.1 mL/hr", "10.5 mL/hr", "21.9 mL/hr", "26.3 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 13.1 mL/hr
Step 1, dose per minute: 5 mcg × 70 kg = 350 mcg/min. Step 2, dose per hour: 350 × 60 = 21,000 mcg/h = 21 mg/h. Step 3, concentration: 400 mg ÷ 250 mL = 1.6 mg/mL. Step 4, rate: 21 mg/h ÷ 1.6 mg/mL = 13.1 mL/h. Shortcut formula: (dose mcg/kg/min × weight × 60) ÷ concentration in mcg/mL = (5 × 70 × 60) ÷ 1600 = 13.1 mL/h.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10.5 mL/hr — 10.5 mL/h comes from a calculation error, such as using the wrong weight or concentration.
• 21.9 mL/hr — 21.9 mL/h comes from mixing up mg and mL or using the wrong concentration.
• 26.3 mL/hr — 26.3 mL/h is about double the right answer, a common error from using a weaker concentration.

⭐ Key point: Always convert to the SAME units (mcg or mg) before dividing. Have a second nurse check every vasoactive infusion calculation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q38 · concept asked in 10 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 293, $s$The first-line vasopressor of choice in septic shock is:$s$, $s$["Dopamine", "Adrenaline", "Norepinephrine", "Vasopressin"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Norepinephrine
Noradrenaline (norepinephrine) is the first-line vasopressor in septic shock (Surviving Sepsis Campaign). Septic shock is a VASODILATED shock (low SVR). Noradrenaline mainly stimulates alpha-1 receptors, tightening blood vessels and raising MAP, with less effect on heart rate. The target is MAP of 65 mmHg or more after fluids. Ideally it runs through a central line, but it can be started peripherally in a big vein for a short time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dopamine — Dopamine causes more arrhythmias and higher death rates than noradrenaline; it is no longer first-line.
• Adrenaline — Adrenaline is added as a second or third agent (or for low cardiac output), not first-line.
• Vasopressin — Vasopressin is ADDED to noradrenaline (0.03 units/min) to reduce the noradrenaline dose, not used first.

⭐ Key point: Sepsis Hour-1 bundle: lactate, blood cultures, antibiotics, 30 mL/kg crystalloid for low BP, and noradrenaline for MAP below 65.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q74$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 294, $s$What is the standard adult IV/IO dose of epinephrine during cardiac arrest, repeated every 3 to 5 minutes?$s$, $s$["0.1 mg", "10 mg", "1 mg", "100 mcg given only once"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1 mg
In adult cardiac arrest, the dose of adrenaline (epinephrine) is 1 mg IV or IO (10 mL of 1:10,000), repeated every 3–5 minutes (every other 2-minute CPR cycle). In non-shockable rhythms (asystole, PEA) give it as soon as possible. In VF/pulseless VT, give it after the 3rd shock. Flush with 20 mL of saline.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.1 mg — 0.1 mg is too small for an adult; doses of about this size are used for children (10 mcg/kg).
• 10 mg — 10 mg is ten times too high and dangerous.
• 100 mcg given only once — 100 mcg given once is too small and is not repeated as recommended.

⭐ Key point: Adrenaline 1 mg every 3–5 minutes. Amiodarone 300 mg after the 3rd shock, then 150 mg after the 5th. Anaphylaxis is different: 0.5 mg IM of 1:1000.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q112 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 295, $s$The antidote for paracetamol overdose is:$s$, $s$["Flumazenil", "Acetylcysteine", "Naloxone", "Atropine"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acetylcysteine
N-ACETYLCYSTEINE (NAC, acetylcysteine) is the ANTIDOTE for PARACETAMOL (acetaminophen) OVERDOSE. In overdose, the liver's normal safe pathways are overwhelmed, and more paracetamol is turned into a toxic chemical called NAPQI. Normally, GLUTATHIONE in liver cells neutralises NAPQI, but in overdose glutathione runs out and NAPQI destroys liver cells (acute liver failure). NAC REPLENISHES GLUTATHIONE, protecting the liver. It is almost 100% protective if started within 8 hours of ingestion, and still helps when given later. Treatment decisions use the paracetamol level plotted on the treatment nomogram (or treat immediately in staggered or late overdoses).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses BENZODIAZEPINES (e.g. diazepam), not paracetamol.
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses OPIOIDS (e.g. morphine, heroin), not paracetamol.
• Atropine — Atropine treats ORGANOPHOSPHATE poisoning and symptomatic bradycardia.

⭐ Key point: NAC is given IV over 12–21 hours. It can cause anaphylactoid reactions (flushing, rash, wheeze) — slow or pause the infusion and give an antihistamine, then restart.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q40 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 296, $s$The antidote for magnesium sulphate toxicity is:$s$, $s$["Naloxone", "Calcium gluconate", "Vitamin K", "Flumazenil"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Calcium gluconate
MAGNESIUM SULPHATE is used in pre-eclampsia/eclampsia to prevent seizures. In toxicity, high magnesium blocks calcium at nerve endings and in muscles, causing loss of reflexes, respiratory depression and cardiac arrest. The ANTIDOTE is CALCIUM GLUCONATE (10%, 10 mL [1 g] IV over 10 minutes), because calcium directly opposes magnesium's effects at the neuromuscular junction and heart.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids.
• Vitamin K — Vitamin K reverses warfarin.
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines.

⭐ Key point: Keep calcium gluconate at the bedside whenever magnesium sulphate is running, and check reflexes, respiratory rate and urine output hourly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q136 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 297, $s$Using the 4-2-1 rule, what is the hourly maintenance fluid rate for a child weighing 32 kg?$s$, $s$["82 mL/hr", "72 mL/hr", "128 mL/hr", "64 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 72 mL/hr
The 4-2-1 RULE gives HOURLY maintenance fluid: 4 mL/kg/hr for the FIRST 10 kg; 2 mL/kg/hr for the NEXT 10 kg (11–20 kg); 1 mL/kg/hr for EVERY kg ABOVE 20 kg. For 32 kg: first 10 kg = 40 mL/hr; next 10 kg = 20 mL/hr; remaining 12 kg = 12 mL/hr. Total = 40 + 20 + 12 = 72 mL/hr. Shortcut for anyone above 20 kg: 60 + (weight − 20) = 60 + 12 = 72 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 82 mL/hr — 82 mL/hr adds 10 mL/hr too much.
• 128 mL/hr — 128 mL/hr is 4 mL/kg for all 32 kg — nearly double.
• 64 mL/hr — 64 mL/hr is 2 mL/kg for all 32 kg.

⭐ Key point: Maintenance fluid is only for patients who cannot drink enough. Sick children often need less (two-thirds of maintenance) because stress increases ADH, which holds water and risks hyponatraemia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q4 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 298, $s$1,000 mL of Ringer's lactate is prescribed to run over 6 hours using a giving set with a drop factor of 10 drops/mL. What is the correct drip rate?$s$, $s$["28 drops/min", "56 drops/min", "42 drops/min", "167 drops/min"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 28 drops/min
DROPS PER MINUTE = (VOLUME × DROP FACTOR) ÷ TIME IN MINUTES. Here: (1,000 mL × 10 drops/mL) ÷ (6 hours × 60) = 10,000 ÷ 360 = 27.8, which rounds to 28 drops/min. Check: the hourly rate is 1,000 ÷ 6 = 167 mL/hr; with a 10-drop set, drops/min = mL/hr ÷ 6 = 167 ÷ 6 ≈ 28. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 56 drops/min — 56 drops/min is double — the bag would finish in 3 hours.
• 42 drops/min — 42 drops/min would finish in about 4 hours.
• 167 drops/min — 167 drops/min is the rate in mL/HOUR, not drops per minute.

⭐ Key point: Count drops for a full minute when setting a gravity drip, and recheck regularly — gravity rates change with the patient's position and the height of the bag.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q6 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 299, $s$A patient weighs 33 lb. A drug is prescribed at 5 mg/kg. What dose should be given?$s$, $s$["165 mg", "150 mg", "75 mg", "34.1 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 75 mg
STEP 1 — Convert pounds to kilograms: kg = lb ÷ 2.2. 33 lb ÷ 2.2 = 15 kg. STEP 2 — Dose: 5 mg/kg × 15 kg = 75 mg. Check: 15 × 2.2 = 33 lb. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 165 mg — 165 mg uses the weight in POUNDS as if it were kg (5 × 33) — more than double.
• 150 mg — 150 mg is 10 mg/kg — double the dose.
• 34.1 mg — 34.1 mg divides by 2.2 twice — less than half the dose.

⭐ Key point: A weight of 33 lb is a young child (about 3–4 years). Errors with pounds and kilograms are especially dangerous in children; always confirm the weight in kg.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q8 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 300, $s$Cefuroxime 90 mg/kg/day is prescribed in divided doses every 6 hours for an infant weighing 15 kg. How much is each dose?$s$, $s$["22.5 mg", "1,350 mg", "270 mg", "337.5 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 337.5 mg
STEP 1 — Daily dose: 90 mg/kg/day × 15 kg = 1,350 mg per day. STEP 2 — "Every 6 hours" = 4 doses a day (24 ÷ 6 = 4). STEP 3 — Each dose: 1,350 ÷ 4 = 337.5 mg. Check: 337.5 × 4 = 1,350. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 22.5 mg — 22.5 mg is far too little (a decimal or division error).
• 1,350 mg — 1,350 mg is the WHOLE DAY's dose — giving it every 6 hours would be four times the prescribed amount.
• 270 mg — 270 mg would be the dose if it were divided into 5 doses.

⭐ Key point: When a prescription says "mg/kg/DAY in divided doses", calculate the daily total first, then divide by the number of doses. When it says "mg/kg/DOSE", multiply by weight only.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q9 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 301, $s$500 mL of normal saline is running at 150 mL/hr. How long will it take to finish?$s$, $s$["18 h", "3 h 50 min", "3 h 20 min", "4 h 20 min"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3 h 20 min
TIME = VOLUME ÷ RATE. Here: 500 mL ÷ 150 mL/hr = 3.333 hours. Convert the decimal part into minutes: 0.333 × 60 = 20 minutes. So the bag will run for 3 HOURS 20 MINUTES. Check: 150 mL/hr × 3⅓ hr = 500 mL. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 18 h — 18 hours is far too long.
• 3 h 50 min — 3 h 50 min is 30 minutes too long.
• 4 h 20 min — 4 h 20 min is an hour too long.

⭐ Key point: Never write "3.33 hours" as "3 hours 33 minutes" — always multiply the decimal part by 60 to get minutes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q12 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 302, $s$Heparin 20,000 units in 500 mL is ordered at 1,500 units/hour. What rate (mL/hr) should be set on the pump?$s$, $s$["15 mL/hr", "37.5 mL/hr", "40 mL/hr", "75 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 37.5 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 20,000 units ÷ 500 mL = 40 units per mL. STEP 2 — Rate: 1,500 units/hr ÷ 40 units/mL = 37.5 mL/hr. Check: 37.5 × 40 = 1,500 units. ✓ (Note this bag is a different strength from other heparin bags — always calculate from the bag in your hand.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 15 mL/hr — 15 mL/hr would give only 600 units/hr.
• 40 mL/hr — 40 mL/hr confuses the concentration (40 units/mL) with the rate — it gives 1,600 units/hr.
• 75 mL/hr — 75 mL/hr would give 3,000 units/hr — double the dose, with a serious risk of bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Heparin is a high-alert drug: independent double-check by two nurses, label the line clearly, and monitor aPTT/anti-Xa and platelets.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q15 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 303, $s$Ibuprofen is prescribed at 10 mg/kg per dose (maximum single dose 400 mg) for a patient weighing 60 kg. What single dose should be given?$s$, $s$["200 mg", "660 mg", "400 mg", "600 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 400 mg
STEP 1 — Weight-based dose: 10 mg/kg × 60 kg = 600 mg. STEP 2 — Compare with the MAXIMUM SINGLE DOSE: 400 mg. Since 600 mg is MORE than 400 mg, the dose must be CAPPED at 400 mg. Exceeding the maximum increases the risk of stomach bleeding, kidney injury and other side effects without extra benefit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 200 mg — 200 mg is too low (half the maximum).
• 660 mg — 660 mg exceeds the maximum.
• 600 mg — 600 mg is the weight-based dose, but it exceeds the 400 mg maximum.

⭐ Key point: Always check two things in weight-based dosing: the calculation AND the maximum dose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q22 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 304, $s$Soluble insulin 50 units is made up to 50 mL with normal saline. The order is 8 units/hour. What rate should be set?$s$, $s$["32 mL/hr", "16 mL/hr", "8 mL/hr", "4 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 8 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 50 units in 50 mL = 1 unit per mL. STEP 2 — Rate: 8 units/hr ÷ 1 unit/mL = 8 mL/hr. With this standard 1 unit/mL dilution, the pump rate in mL/hr equals the dose in units/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 32 mL/hr — 32 mL/hr would give 32 units/hr — four times the dose; severe hypoglycaemia risk.
• 16 mL/hr — 16 mL/hr would give double.
• 4 mL/hr — 4 mL/hr would give half.

⭐ Key point: Use only an insulin syringe or pre-filled syringe when preparing insulin; write "units" in full (never "U", which can be misread as 0).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q26 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 305, $s$Glyceryl trinitrate (GTN) 50 mg in 500 mL is ordered at 8 mcg/min. What pump rate (mL/hr) will deliver this dose?$s$, $s$["0.1 mL/hr", "2.4 mL/hr", "4.8 mL/hr", "9.6 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 4.8 mL/hr
GTN is prescribed in MICROGRAMS per MINUTE, but pumps run in mL per HOUR. STEP 1 — Concentration: 50 mg in 500 mL = 0.1 mg/mL = 100 micrograms/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per HOUR: 8 micrograms/min × 60 = 480 micrograms/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 480 ÷ 100 = 4.8 mL/hr. Check: 4.8 mL/hr × 100 = 480 mcg/hr ÷ 60 = 8 mcg/min. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.1 mL/hr — 0.1 mL/hr is the CONCENTRATION (mg/mL), not a rate.
• 2.4 mL/hr — 2.4 mL/hr gives only 4 mcg/min — half the dose.
• 9.6 mL/hr — 9.6 mL/hr gives 16 mcg/min — double the dose, risking hypotension.

⭐ Key point: Shortcut for a 100 mcg/mL bag: mL/hr = mcg/min × 0.6. Monitor BP every 5–15 minutes while titrating GTN.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q42 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 306, $s$Digoxin loading (digitalizing) dose is calculated as 10 mcg/kg for a 68 kg patient, with 50% given as the first dose and the remainder split into two further doses. What is the total loading dose, and what is the first dose given?$s$, $s$["Total 340 mcg; first dose 170 mcg", "Total 680 mcg; first dose 680 mcg", "Total 680 mcg; first dose 340 mcg", "Total 680 mcg; first dose 170 mcg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Total 680 mcg; first dose 340 mcg
STEP 1 — Total loading dose: 10 mcg/kg × 68 kg = 680 micrograms. STEP 2 — First dose = 50% of the total: 680 × 0.5 = 340 micrograms. The remaining 340 micrograms is split into two doses of 170 micrograms, usually given 6–8 hours apart.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Total 340 mcg; first dose 170 mcg — Total 340 is half the correct total.
• Total 680 mcg; first dose 680 mcg — A first dose of 680 gives the whole load at once.
• Total 680 mcg; first dose 170 mcg — A first dose of 170 is only a quarter.

⭐ Key point: Check potassium, kidney function and the ECG before digoxin; hypokalaemia increases toxicity.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q51$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 307, $s$A patient with severe theophylline toxicity develops refractory seizures and life-threatening arrhythmias. Beyond supportive care and multiple-dose activated charcoal, which additional treatment may be indicated in severe cases?$s$, $s$["Discontinuation of all monitoring", "Oral rehydration therapy alone", "Induced vomiting", "Haemodialysis or haemoperfusion"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Haemodialysis or haemoperfusion
THEOPHYLLINE (and aminophylline) has a NARROW THERAPEUTIC RANGE. Severe toxicity causes vomiting, tachycardia, life-threatening ARRHYTHMIAS, hypokalaemia, hyperglycaemia and REFRACTORY SEIZURES. Treatment: supportive care, MULTIPLE-DOSE ACTIVATED CHARCOAL (enhances elimination), and in severe cases (seizures, arrhythmias, very high levels), HAEMODIALYSIS or HAEMOPERFUSION, which rapidly remove theophylline from the blood.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Discontinuation of all monitoring — Discontinuing monitoring would be dangerous.
• Oral rehydration therapy alone — Oral rehydration alone is inadequate.
• Induced vomiting — Induced vomiting is not recommended (aspiration risk, seizures).

⭐ Key point: Many drugs (e.g. ciprofloxacin, erythromycin) increase theophylline levels; smoking reduces them. Monitor levels.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q199$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 308, $s$Morphine injection is available as 10 mg in 1 mL. The prescription is 2.5 mg. What volume should be given?$s$, $s$["0.5 mL", "4 mL", "0.25 mL", "0.2 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 0.25 mL
VOLUME = (DOSE WANTED ÷ DOSE IN STOCK) × VOLUME. Here: (2.5 mg ÷ 10 mg) × 1 mL = 0.25 mL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.5 mL — 0.5 mL would give 5 mg (double).
• 4 mL — 4 mL is the stock ÷ wanted (wrong way round).
• 0.2 mL — 0.2 mL would give 2 mg.

⭐ Key point: Use a 1 mL syringe for small volumes to measure accurately. Morphine is a controlled drug — check with a second nurse and record in the controlled drugs register.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q38 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 309, $s$A patient presents with a decreased level of consciousness, and the treating team suspects a mixed sedative overdose. Which initial assessment priority takes precedence over identifying the specific substance ingested?$s$, $s$["Obtaining a detailed history of exactly what was ingested and when", "Sending blood for a full toxicology screen before any intervention", "Airway, breathing, and circulation (ABCs) with immediate stabilisation of any compromise", "Contacting the patient's next of kin for consent"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Airway, breathing, and circulation (ABCs) with immediate stabilisation of any compromise
In any poisoned or overdosed patient with reduced consciousness, the FIRST priority is AIRWAY, BREATHING and CIRCULATION (ABC) — open and protect the airway (recovery position, suction, airway adjuncts, intubation if needed), support breathing (oxygen, bag-mask ventilation), and support circulation (IV access, fluids for hypotension) — plus check blood glucose. Sedative overdoses kill mainly by airway obstruction, aspiration and respiratory depression. Identifying the exact substance comes AFTER the patient is stabilised.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Obtaining a detailed history of exactly what was ingested and when — Taking a detailed history is important, but it must not delay life-saving actions.
• Sending blood for a full toxicology screen before any intervention — A toxicology screen is helpful later; waiting for it before intervening could be fatal.
• Contacting the patient's next of kin for consent — In an emergency, consent is implied; contacting relatives must not delay treatment.

⭐ Key point: After ABCs, check glucose, give specific antidotes when indicated (e.g. naloxone for opioids), consider activated charcoal if the airway is protected, and consult a poisons centre.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q147$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 310, $s$Which of the following is the initial analgesic drug of choice for a patient who is in severe pain, with a score of 9 out of 10, due to a fracture?$s$, $s$["Splint", "IV opiate", "IV paracetamol", "Rectal NSAID"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV opiate
For SEVERE pain (9/10) from a fracture, the first-line analgesic is an IV OPIOID (such as morphine titrated in small doses). It works quickly and strongly. Combine it with non-drug measures (splinting, elevation) and other analgesics (paracetamol).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Splint — A splint is important for comfort but isn't an analgesic drug.
• IV paracetamol — IV paracetamol helps mild to moderate pain but isn't enough alone for 9/10 pain.
• Rectal NSAID — Rectal NSAIDs are slower and may be avoided in trauma (bleeding, kidney risk).

⭐ Key point: Titrate IV morphine in small doses (1–2 mg every few minutes) to effect, and monitor respiratory rate and sedation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q116$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 311, $s$A nurse mistakenly calculates a dopamine infusion using the patient's weight in pounds (80 lb) instead of converting to kilograms (36.4 kg) first. If the ordered dose is 5 mcg/kg/min with a concentration of 1600 mcg/mL, by roughly what factor will the error inflate the calculated rate compared to the correct rate?$s$, $s$["Approximately 2.2 times too high", "Approximately 10 times too high", "No difference, the rate is unaffected", "Approximately half the correct rate"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Approximately 2.2 times too high
The dose depends on weight in KILOGRAMS. 80 lb = 36.4 kg. Using 80 (pounds) instead of 36.4 (kg) makes the weight figure 80 ÷ 36.4 = 2.2 times too big. The infusion rate is directly proportional to weight, so the rate would be about 2.2 TIMES TOO HIGH, a dangerous overdose.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Approximately 10 times too high — 10 times too high is wrong; the factor is 2.2.
• No difference, the rate is unaffected — There IS a difference.
• Approximately half the correct rate — It would be HIGHER, not half.

⭐ Key point: Always weigh in kilograms. 1 kg = 2.2 lb. Double-check weight-based infusions with a second nurse.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q144$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, true, 312, $s$What is the first-line antidote for suspected opioid overdose causing respiratory depression?$s$, $s$["Naloxone", "N-acetylcysteine", "Atropine", "Flumazenil"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Naloxone
NALOXONE is the first-line antidote for opioid overdose. It competitively blocks opioid receptors, reversing respiratory depression, sedation and pinpoint pupils within 1–2 minutes when given IV. The usual dose is 0.4 mg IV (or IM, intranasal), repeated as needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• N-acetylcysteine — N-acetylcysteine is the antidote for paracetamol.
• Atropine — Atropine treats bradycardia and organophosphate poisoning.
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines.

⭐ Key point: Naloxone lasts a shorter time than many opioids (30–90 minutes), so monitor for re-sedation and give repeat doses or an infusion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q151$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 313, $s$To reduce contamination risk, blood cultures should ideally be drawn:$s$, $s$["From a single site only, after antibiotics are given", "From two separate sites, ideally before starting antibiotics", "Only from a central line hub", "Without skin antisepsis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: From two separate sites, ideally before starting antibiotics
Taking blood cultures from TWO separate sites (two sets) helps tell a true infection from skin contamination. If the same germ grows from both sites, it is real; if only one bottle grows a skin germ, it is probably a contaminant. Cultures must be taken BEFORE antibiotics, because even one dose can stop the germs growing in the bottle and give a false negative. Clean the skin with 2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol and let it dry.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• From a single site only, after antibiotics are given — Taking cultures after antibiotics, or from only one site, makes the results unreliable.
• Only from a central line hub — Using only a central line hub increases contamination (unless line infection is suspected, and then a peripheral set is also taken).
• Without skin antisepsis — Skipping skin antisepsis guarantees contamination with skin bacteria.

⭐ Key point: In sepsis: cultures FIRST (two sets), then antibiotics within 1 hour. Never delay antibiotics for long just to get cultures.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q27$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 314, $s$Specific antidote in paracetamol poisoning is$s$, $s$["Desferroxamine", "Naloxone", "Acetyl cysteine", "Activated charcoal"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acetyl cysteine
N-acetylcysteine (NAC, Parvolex) is the specific antidote for paracetamol overdose. In overdose, a toxic metabolite (NAPQI) builds up and destroys liver cells. NAC refills glutathione, which neutralises NAPQI. It works best within 8 hours of ingestion but is still given later. Treatment is guided by the paracetamol blood level at 4 hours or more after ingestion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Desferroxamine — Desferrioxamine is the antidote for iron poisoning.
• Naloxone — Naloxone is the antidote for opioids.
• Activated charcoal — Activated charcoal reduces absorption of many drugs within 1 hour, but it is not a specific antidote.

⭐ Key point: Paracetamol liver damage may not show for 24–72 hours, so start NAC early, check LFTs and INR, and watch for low glucose and liver failure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q30$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 315, $s$A patient on a high-dose, prolonged sodium nitroprusside infusion develops altered mental status and unexplained metabolic acidosis. This should raise suspicion for:$s$, $s$["Cyanide toxicity from accumulation of nitroprusside metabolites", "Serotonin syndrome", "Malignant hyperthermia", "Digoxin toxicity"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cyanide toxicity from accumulation of nitroprusside metabolites
Sodium nitroprusside contains cyanide. When high doses run for a long time, especially with kidney or liver problems, cyanide (and thiocyanate) builds up. Cyanide stops cells using oxygen, causing confusion, metabolic (lactic) acidosis, a rising mixed venous oxygen level (SvO₂), arrhythmias and eventually death. Stop the infusion and give an antidote (hydroxocobalamin or sodium thiosulphate).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Serotonin syndrome — Serotonin syndrome is caused by serotonergic drugs (SSRIs, tramadol, MDMA), not nitroprusside.
• Malignant hyperthermia — Malignant hyperthermia is caused by volatile anaesthetics and suxamethonium.
• Digoxin toxicity — Digoxin toxicity causes nausea, visual changes and arrhythmias, not this picture with nitroprusside.

⭐ Key point: Protect nitroprusside from light (cover the syringe and line), use the lowest dose for the shortest time, and check lactate and acid–base status.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q32$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 316, $s$Which of these drugs is used as an antidote for narcotics$s$, $s$["Black charcoal", "Atropine", "Nalaxone", "Cimetidine"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Nalaxone
Naloxone is the specific antagonist (antidote) for opioids (narcotics such as morphine, heroin, pethidine, fentanyl and tramadol). It blocks opioid receptors and reverses respiratory depression, sedation and small pupils within 1–2 minutes when given IV. Dose: 0.4 mg IV, repeated every 2–3 minutes. Smaller doses (40–100 mcg) are used in post-op patients so pain relief is not completely reversed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Black charcoal — Activated charcoal reduces gut absorption of many oral poisons but does not reverse opioids.
• Atropine — Atropine is the antidote for organophosphates and treats bradycardia.
• Cimetidine — Cimetidine is an H₂ blocker that lowers stomach acid; it is not an antidote.

⭐ Key point: Naloxone works for a SHORTER time (30–90 minutes) than many opioids, so the patient can become sedated again (re-narcotisation). Monitor closely and repeat doses or start an infusion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q34$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 317, $s$A patient sedated with a continuous propofol infusion for several days develops metabolic acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, and cardiac dysfunction. The nurse should suspect:$s$, $s$["Normal, expected sedative effect with no concern", "Propofol Infusion Syndrome, a rare but serious complication of prolonged high-dose propofol use", "A simple allergic reaction to propofol", "Improved sedation with no adverse effect"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Propofol Infusion Syndrome, a rare but serious complication of prolonged high-dose propofol use
Propofol infusion syndrome (PRIS) is a rare but often fatal complication of high-dose (above 4 mg/kg/h) propofol given for more than 48 hours, especially in children, sepsis, head injury and with steroids or catecholamines. It disrupts how the mitochondria burn fat. Signs: unexplained metabolic (lactic) acidosis, rhabdomyolysis (high CK), high potassium, high triglycerides, kidney failure, arrhythmias and heart failure. Stop propofol at once and give supportive care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal, expected sedative effect with no concern — These are dangerous abnormalities, not a normal sedative effect.
• A simple allergic reaction to propofol — An allergic reaction causes rash, wheeze and low BP, not acidosis with rhabdomyolysis.
• Improved sedation with no adverse effect — This picture is harmful; it does not mean better sedation.

⭐ Key point: With long propofol infusions, monitor triglycerides, lactate, CK and ECG, and keep the dose below 4 mg/kg/h where possible.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q36$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 318, $s$A patient with a major burn receives morphine IV. The nurse prioritizes monitoring for:$s$, $s$["Respiratory depression", "Hypertension", "Urinary retention", "Constipation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory depression
Morphine, like all opioids, depresses the respiratory centre in the brainstem, causing slow, shallow breathing, CO₂ retention, low oxygen and apnoea. This is the most dangerous side effect and can be fatal, especially with IV doses, in burns patients who may also have smoke inhalation, and when combined with sedatives. The nurse monitors respiratory rate, depth, SpO₂ and sedation level and keeps naloxone ready.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension — Morphine is more likely to cause LOW blood pressure (vasodilation), not high.
• Urinary retention — Urinary retention is a side effect but not life-threatening.
• Constipation — Constipation is common but not the priority in the acute setting.

⭐ Key point: Opioid danger signs are a respiratory rate below 8–10, increasing sleepiness and pinpoint pupils. Sedation usually comes before respiratory depression, so check the sedation score.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q37$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 319, $s$A litre of Normal Saline is ordered to run over 8 hours via a gravity administration set with a drop factor of 15 gtt/mL. What is the correct flow rate in drops per minute?$s$, $s$["31 gtt/min", "21 gtt/min", "42 gtt/min", "15 gtt/min"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 31 gtt/min
Formula: drops per minute = (volume in mL × drop factor) ÷ time in MINUTES. Volume 1000 mL; drop factor 15 gtt/mL; time 8 hours = 480 minutes. (1000 × 15) ÷ 480 = 15,000 ÷ 480 = 31.25, which rounds to 31 drops per minute.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 21 gtt/min — 21 gtt/min would give too little fluid; it comes from a wrong drop factor or time.
• 42 gtt/min — 42 gtt/min is the answer for a 20 gtt/mL set (1000 × 20 ÷ 480 = 41.7), not 15.
• 15 gtt/min — 15 gtt/min just repeats the drop factor and is not a calculation.

⭐ Key point: Standard giving sets are 20 drops/mL (adult crystalloid), blood sets 15 drops/mL, and paediatric (burette/micro-drip) sets 60 drops/mL. With a 60-drop set, drops per minute = mL per hour.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q39$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 320, $s$Activated charcoal is most effective in acute poisoning management when given:$s$, $s$["More than 24 hours after ingestion", "Early after ingestion (typically within about 1 hour)", "Only intravenously", "Only for iron overdose"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Early after ingestion (typically within about 1 hour)
Activated charcoal binds (adsorbs) many drugs in the gut so they are not absorbed. It works best when given EARLY, ideally within 1 hour of swallowing a poison, because after that much of the drug has already been absorbed. The adult dose is 50 g orally (or via NG tube) and the child dose is 1 g/kg. The patient must have a protected airway or be awake, because aspiration is dangerous.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• More than 24 hours after ingestion — After 24 hours almost all of the drug is absorbed, so charcoal does little (except for some slow-release drugs).
• Only intravenously — Charcoal is given by MOUTH or NG tube, never IV.
• Only for iron overdose — Charcoal does NOT bind iron (or lithium, alcohol, acids or alkalis).

⭐ Key point: Don't use charcoal for iron, lithium, alcohols, corrosives or hydrocarbons such as kerosene, or in a patient who cannot protect their airway.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q40$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 321, $s$Drug therapy for pulmonary oedema includes the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Haematinics", "Diuretics", "Morphine", "Digitalis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Haematinics
The question asks which drug is NOT used. Haematinics (iron, folic acid, vitamin B12) treat anaemia; they do nothing for fluid in the lungs. All the other options can be used: diuretics remove fluid, morphine reduces anxiety and preload, and digitalis strengthens contraction and controls AF rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diuretics — Diuretics (furosemide) ARE used: they remove excess fluid and quickly dilate the veins.
• Morphine — Morphine IS used (now more carefully): it eases breathlessness and anxiety and lowers preload.
• Digitalis — Digitalis (digoxin) IS used in some patients, especially with AF, to slow the rate and improve pumping.

⭐ Key point: Remember "LMNOP" for pulmonary oedema: Lasix (furosemide), Morphine (with caution), Nitrates, Oxygen, Position (sit up).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q52$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 322, $s$What is the first-line pharmacologic treatment for status epilepticus?$s$, $s$["Oral phenytoin", "IV mannitol", "IV benzodiazepine (e.g., lorazepam)", "IV vancomycin"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV benzodiazepine (e.g., lorazepam)
Status epilepticus (a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, or repeated seizures without recovery) is a medical emergency. The FIRST-line drug is an IV BENZODIAZEPINE, for example lorazepam 4 mg IV, which can be repeated once after 5–10 minutes. Alternatives without IV access: buccal midazolam or rectal diazepam. Benzodiazepines boost the calming GABA system and stop most seizures quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral phenytoin — Oral phenytoin is too slow; phenytoin (or levetiracetam or valproate) is given IV as SECOND-line.
• IV mannitol — Mannitol lowers ICP but does not stop seizures.
• IV vancomycin — Vancomycin is an antibiotic; it might be used for meningitis but does not stop seizures.

⭐ Key point: The steps are: ABC, oxygen, check glucose (give dextrose if low), benzodiazepine, then a second-line IV drug, then anaesthesia (propofol or thiopentone) with intubation if seizures continue past 20–30 minutes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q96$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 323, $s$Which sedative agent is preferred over benzodiazepines in mechanically ventilated ICU patients, in part due to its lower associated delirium risk?$s$, $s$["Lorazepam", "Midazolam", "Dexmedetomidine", "Diazepam"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dexmedetomidine
Dexmedetomidine is an alpha-2 agonist that gives light, rousable sedation: the patient is calm but can wake and follow commands. It does not depress breathing. Studies show less delirium and shorter ventilation than with benzodiazepines (MENDS, SEDCOM). Guidelines prefer propofol or dexmedetomidine over benzodiazepines for ventilated ICU patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lorazepam — Lorazepam is a benzodiazepine that increases delirium.
• Midazolam — Midazolam is a benzodiazepine that builds up (especially in kidney failure) and increases delirium.
• Diazepam — Diazepam is a long-acting benzodiazepine that also increases delirium.

⭐ Key point: Dexmedetomidine side effects are bradycardia and hypotension. Benzodiazepines are mainly used for seizures and alcohol withdrawal.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q97$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 324, $s$A critically ill patient in septic shock remains hypotensive despite adequate fluid resuscitation and high-dose vasopressors. Which endocrine abnormality should be considered and evaluated?$s$, $s$["Critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency (relative adrenal insufficiency)", "Primary hyperaldosteronism", "Hyperthyroidism", "Growth hormone excess"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency (relative adrenal insufficiency)
In severe sepsis, the adrenal glands may not produce enough cortisol for the body's needs, or the tissues may not respond to it. This is called critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency (relative adrenal insufficiency). Without enough cortisol, the blood vessels respond poorly to vasopressors and BP stays low. Low-dose hydrocortisone (200 mg/day) is recommended in septic shock needing ongoing vasopressors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Primary hyperaldosteronism — Primary hyperaldosteronism causes HIGH BP and low potassium, not refractory shock.
• Hyperthyroidism — Hyperthyroidism causes fast heart rate and high output, but it is not the classic cause here.
• Growth hormone excess — Growth hormone excess (acromegaly) is a chronic condition, not a cause of shock.

⭐ Key point: Signs that point to adrenal insufficiency: low BP not responding to fluids and vasopressors, low sodium, high potassium, low glucose and a history of steroid use.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q122$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 325, $s$The antidote used to reverse benzodiazepine sedation is:$s$, $s$["Naloxone", "Flumazenil", "Acetylcysteine", "Protamine sulphate"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Flumazenil
FLUMAZENIL is the specific antidote for BENZODIAZEPINES (diazepam, midazolam, lorazepam). It is a competitive ANTAGONIST at the benzodiazepine site of the GABA-A receptor, so it quickly reverses sedation and respiratory depression. Dose: 0.2 mg IV over 15 seconds, repeat 0.1–0.2 mg each minute up to about 1 mg. CAUTIONS: it can cause SEIZURES in patients on long-term benzodiazepines (sudden withdrawal) or after mixed overdose with tricyclic antidepressants, so it is mainly used to reverse procedural sedation, not routine overdose. Its effect is shorter than many benzodiazepines, so RE-SEDATION can occur — keep monitoring.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone — NALOXONE reverses OPIOIDS (morphine, heroin, fentanyl), not benzodiazepines.
• Acetylcysteine — ACETYLCYSTEINE (NAC) is the antidote for PARACETAMOL (acetaminophen) overdose; it replaces glutathione.
• Protamine sulphate — PROTAMINE SULPHATE reverses HEPARIN.

⭐ Key point: Benzodiazepine → FLUMAZENIL; opioid → naloxone; paracetamol → acetylcysteine; heparin → protamine.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q28$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 326, $s$Activated charcoal works by which mechanism to reduce systemic absorption of an ingested poison?$s$, $s$["It adsorbs the toxin onto its large surface area within the gastrointestinal tract, preventing systemic absorption", "It chemically neutralises acids and alkalis", "It induces vomiting to expel the toxin", "It increases intestinal motility to speed transit"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It adsorbs the toxin onto its large surface area within the gastrointestinal tract, preventing systemic absorption
ACTIVATED CHARCOAL is a fine black powder with a huge surface area (about 1000 m² per gram). Many drugs and toxins stick to its surface — this is called ADSORPTION — while still in the stomach and intestine, so they cannot be absorbed into the blood and are passed out in the stool. It works best when given within about 1 HOUR of ingestion, in a dose of about 50 g for adults (1 g/kg for children). The patient MUST have a protected airway (alert, or intubated) because aspiration into the lungs is dangerous. It does NOT bind well to iron, lithium, alcohols, cyanide, acids, alkalis or hydrocarbons ("PHAILS").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It chemically neutralises acids and alkalis — It does NOT neutralise acids or alkalis; corrosives are not bound well and charcoal is contraindicated in corrosive ingestion.
• It induces vomiting to expel the toxin — It does NOT induce vomiting; that was ipecac, which is no longer recommended.
• It increases intestinal motility to speed transit — It does NOT increase gut motility; in fact it can cause constipation. Laxatives/whole-bowel irrigation are separate treatments.

⭐ Key point: Activated charcoal ADSORBS toxins in the gut. Best within 1 hour; airway must be protected; not for iron, lithium, alcohol, corrosives.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q29$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 327, $s$Patients on a continuous insulin infusion in the ICU require blood glucose monitoring at a frequency of approximately:$s$, $s$["Once every 24 hours", "Hourly, or per institutional protocol", "Once weekly", "Only if symptomatic"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hourly, or per institutional protocol
Patients on a CONTINUOUS IV INSULIN INFUSION (e.g. DKA, HHS, post-cardiac surgery, critical illness) need BLOOD GLUCOSE checked about EVERY HOUR (or as per unit protocol, sometimes every 1–2 hours once stable). IV regular insulin acts within minutes and has a short half-life, so glucose can fall quickly; hourly checks let the nurse adjust the rate to stay in the target range (often 6–10 mmol/L, or 140–180 mg/dL in ICU) and catch HYPOGLYCAEMIA early. Hypoglycaemia in sedated patients may be hidden (no symptoms). Also monitor potassium, because insulin moves potassium into cells and can cause HYPOKALAEMIA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Once every 24 hours — Once every 24 hours is far too seldom; dangerous hypo- or hyperglycaemia could develop unnoticed.
• Once weekly — Once weekly makes no sense for an IV insulin infusion; that frequency fits HbA1c or long-term follow-up, not acute care.
• Only if symptomatic — Checking ONLY if symptomatic is unsafe: ICU patients are often sedated and cannot report hypoglycaemia symptoms.

⭐ Key point: IV insulin infusion → glucose check HOURLY (or per protocol). Watch potassium too.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q30$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 328, $s$Bactericidal antibiotics work by:$s$, $s$["Stopping bacterial growth only", "Actively killing bacteria, e.g. by attacking the cell wall", "Only affecting viruses", "Having no mechanism of action"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Actively killing bacteria, e.g. by attacking the cell wall
BACTERICIDAL antibiotics KILL bacteria directly. Many do this by attacking the CELL WALL (beta-lactams such as penicillins, cephalosporins and carbapenems; vancomycin), so the bacterium bursts. Others damage DNA (fluoroquinolones, metronidazole) or cause faulty proteins (aminoglycosides). BACTERIOSTATIC antibiotics only STOP bacteria from growing and multiplying (e.g. tetracyclines, macrolides, clindamycin, sulphonamides), and the immune system then clears them. Bactericidal drugs are preferred in serious infections or weak immunity — e.g. meningitis, endocarditis, neutropenic sepsis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stopping bacterial growth only — "Stopping bacterial growth only" describes BACTERIOSTATIC drugs, not bactericidal ones.
• Only affecting viruses — Antibiotics do NOT work against VIRUSES; viruses need antivirals (e.g. acyclovir, oseltamivir).
• Having no mechanism of action — Every antibiotic has a mechanism of action; "no mechanism" is simply false.

⭐ Key point: BactericiDAL = KILLS (e.g. cell-wall attack by penicillins). BacterioSTATIC = STOPS growth (e.g. tetracyclines, macrolides).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q35$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 329, $s$Dopamine's haemodynamic effect varies by dose, with higher doses primarily producing:$s$, $s$["Renal vasodilation only", "Alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction", "Bronchodilation", "Pure beta-2 effects"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Alpha-adrenergic vasoconstriction
DOPAMINE acts on different receptors at different doses: LOW dose (about 1–5 mcg/kg/min) — dopamine receptors, causing renal and mesenteric vasodilation (the old "renal dose", now not recommended to protect kidneys); MEDIUM dose (about 5–10 mcg/kg/min) — BETA-1 receptors, increasing heart rate and contractility (cardiac output); HIGH dose (above about 10 mcg/kg/min) — ALPHA-1 receptors, causing VASOCONSTRICTION and raised systemic vascular resistance and BP. So the higher-dose effect is ALPHA-ADRENERGIC VASOCONSTRICTION. Nursing: give through a central line if possible (extravasation causes tissue necrosis — antidote phentolamine), monitor BP, heart rate and arrhythmias, and urine output.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal vasodilation only — RENAL VASODILATION occurs at LOW doses, not high doses.
• Bronchodilation — BRONCHODILATION is a beta-2 effect, which is not a main action of dopamine.
• Pure beta-2 effects — Dopamine has no "pure beta-2" effect; its beta effect is mainly beta-1 (heart).

⭐ Key point: Dopamine: low = renal/dopaminergic; medium = beta-1 (heart); HIGH = ALPHA-1 vasoconstriction.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q38$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 330, $s$Before administering any weight-based critical care drug infusion, best practice is to:$s$, $s$["Skip verification if the nurse is experienced", "Have the calculation independently double-checked by a second nurse/pharmacist", "Round the dose up for safety margin", "Administer without checking the infusion pump rate"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Have the calculation independently double-checked by a second nurse/pharmacist
Critical care infusions (e.g. noradrenaline, dopamine, heparin, insulin, potassium, sedatives) are HIGH-ALERT medications: a small calculation error can cause severe harm or death. Weight-based doses (mcg/kg/min) need several steps — weight, concentration, dose, pump rate — and each step can go wrong (decimal errors, wrong units, mg vs mcg). Best practice is an INDEPENDENT DOUBLE CHECK by a second nurse or pharmacist, who does the calculation separately without seeing the first result, then both compare. Also check the right patient, drug, dose, route, time and the pump programming (using smart pumps with drug libraries where available), and document both checkers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Skip verification if the nurse is experienced — Skipping checks because a nurse is "experienced" is unsafe; experienced staff also make errors, especially when busy or tired.
• Round the dose up for safety margin — ROUNDING the dose UP is not safe; it can cause overdose. Doses must be exact or follow approved protocols.
• Administer without checking the infusion pump rate — Giving without checking the PUMP RATE ignores a common source of error (wrong rate programmed).

⭐ Key point: High-alert weight-based infusions need an INDEPENDENT DOUBLE CHECK of the calculation AND the pump rate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q39$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 331, $s$Which class of these drugs is contraindicated in patients with high bleeding tendencies?$s$, $s$["COX 2 selective NSAIDS", "Opioids", "NSAIDS", "Adjuvants"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: NSAIDS
Non-selective NSAIDs (ibuprofen, diclofenac, naproxen, aspirin) block both COX-1 and COX-2 enzymes. Blocking COX-1 stops platelets from making THROMBOXANE A2, so PLATELETS CANNOT CLUMP well — bleeding time increases. COX-1 also protects the stomach lining, so NSAIDs cause gastric ulcers and GI BLEEDING. This combination makes NSAIDs contraindicated (or used with great caution) in patients with bleeding disorders, thrombocytopenia, active bleeding, peptic ulcers or on anticoagulants. Paracetamol or opioids are safer choices for pain in these patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• COX 2 selective NSAIDS — COX-2 SELECTIVE NSAIDs (e.g. celecoxib) spare COX-1, so they have much less effect on platelets and the stomach; they are not the class most linked to bleeding (though they raise cardiovascular risk).
• Opioids — OPIOIDS do not affect platelets or clotting; they are often used for pain when NSAIDs are unsafe.
• Adjuvants — ADJUVANT analgesics (e.g. amitriptyline, gabapentin) do not usually cause bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Non-selective NSAIDs block COX-1 → poor platelet function + GI ulcers → AVOID in bleeding tendency.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q40$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 332, $s$The following are some of the drugs used in MI EXCEPT$s$, $s$["B Blockers", "Statins", "Ca Channel Blockers", "Antibiotics"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Antibiotics
Drug treatment of MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION aims to restore blood flow, reduce heart workload, prevent new clots and protect the heart long term. Memory aid "MONA-BASH" covers the main drugs: Morphine (pain), Oxygen (only if SpO2 < 90–94%), Nitrates (GTN), Aspirin/Antiplatelets (plus clopidogrel/ticagrelor), BETA-BLOCKERS (slow the heart, reduce oxygen demand and arrhythmias), ACE inhibitors (prevent remodelling), STATINS (lower cholesterol and stabilise plaque), Heparin (anticoagulant). Thrombolytics or PCI open the blocked artery. Some CALCIUM CHANNEL BLOCKERS (verapamil, diltiazem) may be used when beta-blockers are contraindicated and to control angina. ANTIBIOTICS have no role, because MI is caused by a blocked coronary artery (thrombus on plaque), not by infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• B Blockers — BETA-BLOCKERS ARE used in MI — they reduce oxygen demand and the risk of arrhythmia and death.
• Statins — STATINS ARE used — high-intensity statin is started in all MI patients unless contraindicated.
• Ca Channel Blockers — CALCIUM CHANNEL BLOCKERS ARE used in some patients for angina/rate control when beta-blockers cannot be used.

⭐ Key point: MI drugs = MONA-BASH (morphine, oxygen, nitrates, aspirin, beta-blocker, ACE-I, statin, heparin). Antibiotics are NOT part of MI treatment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q71$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 333, $s$Which of the following descriptions correctly characterizes the cardiac glycosides (e.g. digoxin)?$s$, $s$["POSITIVE INOTROPE, POSITIVE CHRONOTROPE", "NEGATIVE INOTROPE, NEGATIVE CHRONOTROPE", "POSITIVE INOTROPE, NEGATIVE CHRONOTROPE", "NEGATIVE INOTROPE, POSITIVE CHRONOTROPE"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: POSITIVE INOTROPE, NEGATIVE CHRONOTROPE
DIGOXIN (a cardiac glycoside) has two main heart effects. (1) POSITIVE INOTROPE: it blocks the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase pump in heart muscle; this raises calcium inside the cells, so the heart contracts more STRONGLY — helpful in heart failure. (2) NEGATIVE CHRONOTROPE: it increases vagal (parasympathetic) tone and slows conduction through the AV node, so the HEART RATE FALLS — helpful to control the rate in atrial fibrillation. Nursing: check the APICAL pulse for a full minute before giving and hold if below 60 bpm (adults); monitor potassium (LOW K⁺ increases digoxin toxicity) and digoxin levels (0.5–2 ng/mL). Toxicity signs: nausea, vomiting, anorexia, yellow-green vision (xanthopsia), confusion, bradycardia and arrhythmias; antidote is digoxin-specific antibody (DigiFab).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• POSITIVE INOTROPE, POSITIVE CHRONOTROPE — "Positive inotrope, positive chronotrope" describes drugs like dobutamine/adrenaline — digoxin SLOWS the heart.
• NEGATIVE INOTROPE, NEGATIVE CHRONOTROPE — "Negative inotrope, negative chronotrope" describes beta-blockers or verapamil — digoxin STRENGTHENS contraction.
• NEGATIVE INOTROPE, POSITIVE CHRONOTROPE — "Negative inotrope, positive chronotrope" is the opposite of digoxin on both counts.

⭐ Key point: Digoxin = POSITIVE inotrope (stronger) + NEGATIVE chronotrope (slower). Check apical pulse; hold if < 60; low K⁺ → toxicity.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q75$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 334, $s$Antibiotic choice for treating the underlying Clostridium infection in tetanus typically includes:$s$, $s$["Penicillin or metronidazole", "Aspirin", "Insulin", "No antibiotics are used"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Penicillin or metronidazole
TETANUS is caused by the toxin (tetanospasmin) of CLOSTRIDIUM TETANI, an anaerobic, spore-forming bacterium found in soil and animal dung, entering through wounds. Treatment has several parts: (1) neutralise free toxin — HUMAN TETANUS IMMUNOGLOBULIN (HTIG); (2) kill the bacteria — ANTIBIOTICS: METRONIDAZOLE (preferred, 500 mg IV 6–8 hourly for 7–10 days) or PENICILLIN; (3) clean and debride the wound; (4) control spasms — benzodiazepines (diazepam), magnesium sulphate, muscle relaxants and ventilation if needed; quiet, dark room; (5) manage autonomic instability; (6) give tetanus toxoid vaccine (the disease does not give immunity).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Aspirin — ASPIRIN is an analgesic/antiplatelet; it does not kill Clostridium.
• Insulin — INSULIN controls blood glucose; it has no role against the infection.
• No antibiotics are used — Antibiotics ARE used — saying none are used is wrong.

⭐ Key point: Tetanus: METRONIDAZOLE (or penicillin) + tetanus immunoglobulin + wound debridement + spasm control + vaccine.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q119$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 335, $s$The drug of choice to prevent further convulsions in eclampsia is:$s$, $s$["Diazepam", "Magnesium sulphate", "Phenytoin", "Hydralazine"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Magnesium sulphate
MAGNESIUM SULPHATE is the DRUG OF CHOICE to prevent FURTHER CONVULSIONS in eclampsia. The Collaborative Eclampsia Trial showed MgSO4 is far better than DIAZEPAM and PHENYTOIN: fewer recurrent fits and lower maternal death. It is cheap, available and recommended by WHO. Give a loading dose 4 g IV over 5–15 minutes, then 1 g/hour for 24 hours; a recurrent fit is treated with another 2–4 g IV. During a fit: call for help, left lateral position, protect from injury, airway and oxygen, then MgSO4. Monitor reflexes, respirations and urine output; calcium gluconate is the antidote.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diazepam — DIAZEPAM stops a fit but is less effective at preventing further eclamptic fits and causes sedation in mother and baby (respiratory depression).
• Phenytoin — PHENYTOIN is less effective than MgSO4 in eclampsia.
• Hydralazine — HYDRALAZINE is an ANTIHYPERTENSIVE used to lower BP; it does not prevent convulsions.

⭐ Key point: Eclampsia → MAGNESIUM SULPHATE is the drug of choice (better than diazepam and phenytoin). Hydralazine/labetalol for BP.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q142$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 336, $s$Critical management of a woman with severe bleeding just after delivery includes:$s$, $s$["A continuous IV infusion of high-dose oxytocin", "Setting up dextrose water as the main fluid", "Stopping all uterotonics", "Filling the bladder to stop bleeding"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A continuous IV infusion of high-dose oxytocin
Severe bleeding just after delivery is PRIMARY POSTPARTUM HAEMORRHAGE. The most common cause is UTERINE ATONY (a soft, poorly contracted uterus). Critical management: call for help, ABC, two large-bore IV cannulas, blood for cross-match, warm crystalloid and blood, catheterise to EMPTY the bladder, and RUB UP the uterus (fundal massage). UTEROTONICS are key: oxytocin 10 IU IM/IV, then a CONTINUOUS IV OXYTOCIN INFUSION (e.g. 40 IU in 500 mL over 4 hours) to keep the uterus contracted; ergometrine (not in hypertension), misoprostol, carboprost; TRANEXAMIC ACID 1 g IV within 3 hours of birth. If still bleeding: bimanual compression, balloon tamponade, surgery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Setting up dextrose water as the main fluid — DEXTROSE WATER is not a resuscitation fluid — it leaves the blood vessels quickly; use crystalloids (normal saline, Ringer's) and blood.
• Stopping all uterotonics — STOPPING uterotonics is wrong; they are the main treatment for atony.
• Filling the bladder to stop bleeding — A FULL bladder prevents the uterus from contracting — the bladder must be EMPTIED, not filled.

⭐ Key point: PPH (atony): massage uterus + OXYTOCIN infusion + other uterotonics + tranexamic acid; EMPTY the bladder; crystalloid and blood.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q144$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 337, $s$To provide rapid pain relief in the ICU, which of the following routes of opioid administration is most appropriate?$s$, $s$["Intradermal", "Intraspinal", "Sublingual", "Intravenous"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intravenous
For RAPID PAIN RELIEF in ICU, the INTRAVENOUS (IV) route is most appropriate. IV opioids (e.g. morphine, fentanyl) act within minutes, have predictable absorption (critically ill patients often have poor gut and skin blood flow), and can be titrated easily in small doses or by continuous infusion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intradermal — Intradermal is used for skin tests, not analgesia.
• Intraspinal — Intraspinal (epidural/intrathecal) is effective, but needs specialist insertion — not for rapid general use.
• Sublingual — Sublingual absorption is variable and slower.

⭐ Key point: Assess pain with validated tools (CPOT or BPS in patients who cannot speak) and monitor sedation and breathing.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q28$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 338, $s$A patient develops flushing, pruritus, and a rash on the neck and upper torso shortly after a rapid vancomycin infusion. The most appropriate nursing action is to:$s$, $s$["Slow the infusion rate, as this is histamine-mediated red man syndrome rather than a true allergic reaction", "Stop vancomycin permanently and document a severe anaphylactic allergy", "Administer epinephrine immediately", "Increase the infusion rate since the reaction is self-limited"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Slow the infusion rate, as this is histamine-mediated red man syndrome rather than a true allergic reaction
FLUSHING, ITCHING and a RASH on the face, NECK and UPPER TORSO shortly after a RAPID vancomycin infusion is typical of "RED MAN SYNDROME" (vancomycin infusion reaction). It is caused by direct HISTAMINE release from mast cells due to the fast infusion rate — NOT a true IgE allergy. The appropriate action is to STOP or SLOW THE INFUSION (an antihistamine can help), then continue at a slower rate (at least 60 minutes per gram).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stop vancomycin permanently and document a severe anaphylactic allergy — It is not a true anaphylactic allergy, so vancomycin doesn't need to be permanently stopped.
• Administer epinephrine immediately — Adrenaline is needed only for true anaphylaxis (airway swelling, wheeze, severe hypotension).
• Increase the infusion rate since the reaction is self-limited — Increasing the rate would worsen the reaction.

⭐ Key point: Document as an "infusion reaction" to avoid wrongly labelling the patient as allergic.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q29$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 339, $s$Benzylpenicillin is the chemical name for:$s$, $s$["Penicillin V", "Penicillin F", "Penicillin G", "Phenethicillin"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Penicillin G
BENZYLPENICILLIN is the chemical (generic) name for PENICILLIN G, the original penicillin discovered by Alexander Fleming. It is destroyed by stomach acid, so it is given by INJECTION (IV or IM). It kills many gram-positive bacteria and some others. Uses include: streptococcal infections (e.g. cellulitis, tonsillitis, rheumatic fever prevention), MENINGOCOCCAL disease (meningitis/septicaemia), pneumococcal pneumonia, syphilis (as benzathine penicillin), tetanus, and early neonatal sepsis (with gentamicin).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Penicillin V — Penicillin V is PHENOXYMETHYLPENICILLIN — the acid-stable ORAL form.
• Penicillin F — "Penicillin F" was an early natural penicillin that is not used clinically.
• Phenethicillin — Phenethicillin is a different, semi-synthetic oral penicillin.

⭐ Key point: Always ask about penicillin allergy before giving it. Have adrenaline ready — anaphylaxis can occur within minutes of injection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q32$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 340, $s$A patient who received naloxone for heroin overdose initially improves but becomes drowsy and hypoventilating again 45 minutes later. What is the most likely explanation?$s$, $s$["Naloxone has a shorter half-life than most opioids, so re-sedation occurs as the antidote wears off before the opioid does", "The patient has developed an allergic reaction to naloxone", "The initial dose of naloxone was too high", "This indicates the patient never had an opioid overdose"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Naloxone has a shorter half-life than most opioids, so re-sedation occurs as the antidote wears off before the opioid does
NALOXONE reverses opioid effects quickly, but it has a SHORT HALF-LIFE (about 30–90 minutes). Many opioids (e.g. heroin, morphine, methadone) last longer. When naloxone wears off, the opioid is still present, causing RE-SEDATION and respiratory depression. Patients must be observed for several hours after naloxone, and repeat doses or an infusion may be needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient has developed an allergic reaction to naloxone — This is not an allergic reaction.
• The initial dose of naloxone was too high — A high dose of naloxone doesn't cause re-sedation.
• This indicates the patient never had an opioid overdose — The improvement with naloxone confirms opioid overdose.

⭐ Key point: Observe patients for at least 2–4 hours after naloxone for short-acting opioids, longer for long-acting ones.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q34$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 341, $s$Amiodarone therapy requires monitoring of which organs due to potential toxicity?$s$, $s$["Thyroid, liver, and lungs", "Only the kidneys", "Only the pancreas", "Only the skin"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Thyroid, liver, and lungs
AMIODARONE can damage several organs with long-term use: THYROID (hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism — it contains iodine), LIVER (raised liver enzymes, hepatitis), and LUNGS (pulmonary fibrosis, pneumonitis). Monitoring of thyroid function, liver function and lung function (chest X-ray) is required. It can also affect the eyes (corneal deposits) and skin (grey-blue colour, photosensitivity).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only the kidneys — Kidneys are not the main concern.
• Only the pancreas — The pancreas is not the main concern.
• Only the skin — Skin effects occur but are not the only concern.

⭐ Key point: Amiodarone interacts with warfarin and digoxin — reduce their doses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q37$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 342, $s$Vasopressin is sometimes added in septic shock management primarily to:$s$, $s$["Replace norepinephrine entirely", "Allow a norepinephrine-sparing effect by acting on a different receptor pathway", "Treat bradycardia", "Lower blood pressure"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Allow a norepinephrine-sparing effect by acting on a different receptor pathway
In SEPTIC SHOCK, VASOPRESSIN is added to NORADRENALINE when BP remains low despite moderate doses. It acts on a different receptor pathway (V1 receptors), so it raises BP by a different mechanism, allowing a lower noradrenaline dose ("NORADRENALINE-SPARING effect"). Septic shock may also cause a relative vasopressin deficiency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Replace norepinephrine entirely — It doesn't replace noradrenaline entirely.
• Treat bradycardia — It doesn't treat bradycardia.
• Lower blood pressure — It raises, not lowers, BP.

⭐ Key point: Adding vasopressin is recommended when noradrenaline reaches 0.25–0.5 mcg/kg/min.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q38$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 343, $s$Fentanyl is often preferred over morphine in patients with renal impairment because fentanyl:$s$, $s$["Has active metabolites that accumulate in renal failure", "Has no clinically significant active metabolites that accumulate in renal failure", "Cannot be given intravenously", "Has a much longer half-life"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Has no clinically significant active metabolites that accumulate in renal failure
MORPHINE is broken down into active metabolites (e.g. morphine-6-glucuronide), which are excreted by the kidneys and ACCUMULATE in RENAL FAILURE, causing prolonged sedation and respiratory depression. FENTANYL is broken down in the liver into INACTIVE metabolites, so it does not accumulate in renal failure. This makes fentanyl safer in renal impairment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Has active metabolites that accumulate in renal failure — Fentanyl does NOT have active metabolites that accumulate.
• Cannot be given intravenously — Fentanyl can be given IV.
• Has a much longer half-life — Fentanyl has a shorter half-life after a single dose (though it accumulates in fat with long infusions).

⭐ Key point: Other opioids that are relatively safe in renal failure: alfentanil and remifentanil.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q40$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 344, $s$The dexamethasone dose sometimes used as an alternative to prednisolone in acute asthma is:$s$, $s$["0.06mg/kg/day", "0.6mg/kg/day", "6mg/kg/day", "60mg/kg/day"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 0.6mg/kg/day
In ACUTE ASTHMA, systemic CORTICOSTEROIDS reduce airway inflammation and speed recovery. PREDNISOLONE is commonly used (1–2 mg/kg/day in children, maximum 40–50 mg). DEXAMETHASONE is an alternative, especially in children, at about 0.6 mg/kg/day (usually a single dose, maximum about 16 mg), often given for 1–2 days. Its long action means fewer doses and less vomiting of tablets, improving adherence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.06mg/kg/day — 0.06 mg/kg is ten times TOO LOW — it would not be effective.
• 6mg/kg/day — 6 mg/kg is ten times TOO HIGH — a dangerous overdose.
• 60mg/kg/day — 60 mg/kg is a massive overdose.

⭐ Key point: Steroids take 4–6 hours to start working, so give them early, alongside bronchodilators (salbutamol, ipratropium) and oxygen.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q61$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 345, $s$In the Pritchard regimen of MgSO4, which is correct?$s$, $s$["2 g/hour IV works faster", "5 g is the total loading dose", "The maintenance dose is 5 g IM 4-hourly", "It is the only accepted regimen"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The maintenance dose is 5 g IM 4-hourly
The PRITCHARD REGIMEN of MgSO4: loading dose 4 g IV + 10 g IM (5 g in each buttock), then MAINTENANCE of 5 g IM every 4 HOURS in alternate buttocks.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2 g/hour IV works faster — 2 g/hour IV is not the Pritchard regimen.
• 5 g is the total loading dose — The loading dose is 14 g total, not 5 g.
• It is the only accepted regimen — Other regimens (e.g. Zuspan) are also accepted.

⭐ Key point: Check reflexes, breathing and urine output before each dose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q140$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 346, $s$When starting a patient on opioids, the recommended protocol for preventing constipation is to:$s$, $s$["Add bulk fibre to the diet", "Give enemas as needed", "Increase fluids and exercise only", "Use a bowel stimulant and a stool softener"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Use a bowel stimulant and a stool softener
OPIOIDS slow the movement of the gut (reduced peristalsis) and increase fluid absorption from the stool, causing CONSTIPATION in most patients, and tolerance to this side effect does NOT develop. The recommended prevention is a combination of a BOWEL STIMULANT (e.g. senna, bisacodyl — increases gut movement) and a STOOL SOFTENER (e.g. docusate, or an osmotic laxative such as lactulose or macrogol), started at the same time as the opioid.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Add bulk fibre to the diet — Bulk fibre alone can worsen constipation with opioids (slow gut plus bulk can cause impaction).
• Give enemas as needed — Enemas "as needed" are reactive, not preventive.
• Increase fluids and exercise only — Fluids and exercise help, but are not enough alone.

⭐ Key point: For severe opioid-induced constipation, peripherally acting opioid antagonists (e.g. methylnaltrexone) can be used.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q28$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 347, $s$A patient presents with widened QRS complex, hypotension, and seizures after a tricyclic antidepressant (TCA) overdose. Which treatment is indicated to counteract the cardiotoxic sodium-channel blocking effect?$s$, $s$["IV sodium bicarbonate", "Flumazenil", "Naloxone", "Activated charcoal alone, with no other treatment"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV sodium bicarbonate
TRICYCLIC ANTIDEPRESSANTS (TCAs, e.g. amitriptyline) block SODIUM CHANNELS in the heart, which WIDENS the QRS complex and can cause ventricular arrhythmias, hypotension and seizures. IV SODIUM BICARBONATE (8.4%) is the treatment: the extra sodium overcomes the channel blockade, and the alkalosis (raised pH) reduces TCA binding to the sodium channels. Target blood pH 7.45–7.55.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil can cause seizures in TCA overdose and is contraindicated.
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids, not TCAs.
• Activated charcoal alone, with no other treatment — Activated charcoal may be given early (with a protected airway), but it does not treat established cardiotoxicity.

⭐ Key point: Treat TCA-induced seizures with benzodiazepines; avoid phenytoin and class Ia/Ic antiarrhythmics.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q29$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 348, $s$Bronchodilator nebulisers (e.g. salbutamol) can cause which common side effect that the nurse should monitor for?$s$, $s$["Bradycardia and sedation", "Tachycardia and tremor", "Hypoglycaemia", "Weight gain"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tachycardia and tremor
SALBUTAMOL (a beta-2 agonist) relaxes airway smooth muscle (bronchodilation). It also stimulates beta receptors elsewhere: in the heart, causing TACHYCARDIA and palpitations; in skeletal muscle, causing a fine TREMOR. It can also lower potassium (hypokalaemia) and raise glucose and lactate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bradycardia and sedation — Bradycardia and sedation are not salbutamol effects.
• Hypoglycaemia — Salbutamol can RAISE blood glucose, not lower it.
• Weight gain — Weight gain is not a side effect.

⭐ Key point: Monitor heart rate, potassium and lactate in patients receiving frequent salbutamol nebulisers.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q30$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 349, $s$A patient receiving IV vancomycin develops flushing, a rash on the upper body, and hypotension shortly after the infusion starts. This presentation is most consistent with:$s$, $s$["A true IgE-mediated anaphylactic reaction only", "'Red man syndrome,' related to rapid infusion rate rather than true allergy in many cases", "Normal expected drug effect requiring no action", "Sepsis unrelated to the medication"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 'Red man syndrome,' related to rapid infusion rate rather than true allergy in many cases
VANCOMYCIN infused TOO RAPIDLY can cause direct histamine release from mast cells — the "RED MAN" SYNDROME (now called vancomycin infusion reaction). Features: FLUSHING, itching and a red RASH on the face, neck and upper body, sometimes with HYPOTENSION. In most cases, it is related to the INFUSION RATE, not a true allergy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A true IgE-mediated anaphylactic reaction only — It is not usually a true IgE-mediated anaphylactic reaction (though anaphylaxis is possible and must be excluded if there is wheeze or airway swelling).
• Normal expected drug effect requiring no action — It requires action (slowing or stopping the infusion).
• Sepsis unrelated to the medication — The timing with the infusion points to the drug, not sepsis.

⭐ Key point: Stop or slow the infusion, give an antihistamine, and restart slowly (at least 60 minutes per gram).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q32$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 350, $s$Vasopressor infusions such as norepinephrine and dopamine should preferably be administered via:$s$, $s$["A central venous catheter, to reduce the risk of extravasation injury and tissue necrosis", "A peripheral IV in the hand for at least 24 hours before central access", "Intramuscular injection", "Oral route once the patient is hemodynamically stable"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A central venous catheter, to reduce the risk of extravasation injury and tissue necrosis
VASOPRESSORS such as NORADRENALINE and DOPAMINE are powerful VASOCONSTRICTORS. If they leak out of a vein (EXTRAVASATION), they constrict the blood vessels in the surrounding tissue, causing TISSUE ISCHAEMIA and NECROSIS. A CENTRAL VENOUS CATHETER delivers the drug into a large vein with high blood flow, reducing this risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A peripheral IV in the hand for at least 24 hours before central access — Peripheral infusion for 24 hours increases the risk of extravasation injury; short-term peripheral use may be acceptable in emergencies with a large, well-placed cannula and close monitoring.
• Intramuscular injection — Vasopressors are not given IM.
• Oral route once the patient is hemodynamically stable — Vasopressors are not given orally.

⭐ Key point: If extravasation occurs, stop the infusion, aspirate if possible, and consider phentolamine infiltration.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q33$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 351, $s$10 year boys on admission with diagnosis of leukemia, which of these method/route of drugs administration will you avoid?$s$, $s$["Oral route", "Intravenous route", "Intramuscular route", "Sublingual route"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intramuscular route
Children with LEUKAEMIA often have LOW PLATELETS (thrombocytopenia), so injections into muscle (INTRAMUSCULAR) can cause HAEMATOMAS (bleeding into the muscle). They also have LOW white cells (neutropenia), so IM injections increase the risk of INFECTION and abscesses. The IM route should be AVOIDED.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral route — Oral is safe if the child can swallow.
• Intravenous route — IV (usually via central line) is the main route for chemotherapy and antibiotics.
• Sublingual route — Sublingual is safe.

⭐ Key point: Also avoid rectal temperatures, enemas and suppositories in neutropenic or thrombocytopenic patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q34$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 352, $s$Dobutamine is primarily used in critical care for its:$s$, $s$["Vasoconstrictor effect", "Inotropic effect, improving cardiac contractility and output", "Antiarrhythmic properties", "Diuretic effect"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inotropic effect, improving cardiac contractility and output
DOBUTAMINE is mainly used for its INOTROPIC EFFECT — it stimulates β1 receptors in the heart, INCREASING CONTRACTILITY and CARDIAC OUTPUT. It is used in cardiogenic shock and low-output heart failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vasoconstrictor effect — It is not a vasoconstrictor; it can cause mild vasodilation.
• Antiarrhythmic properties — It is not an antiarrhythmic; it can cause arrhythmias.
• Diuretic effect — It is not a diuretic (though improved output may increase urine).

⭐ Key point: Monitor heart rate and rhythm, BP and urine output during dobutamine infusion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q35$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 353, $s$Which of the following is a thrombolytic agent?$s$, $s$["Streptokinase", "Heparin", "Clexane (enoxaparin)", "Warfarin sodium"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Streptokinase
A THROMBOLYTIC (fibrinolytic) agent DISSOLVES EXISTING CLOTS by activating plasminogen to plasmin, which breaks down fibrin. STREPTOKINASE is a thrombolytic (others: alteplase, tenecteplase, reteplase). Used in STEMI (when PCI is not available), massive PE, and acute ischaemic stroke (alteplase).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Heparin — Heparin is an anticoagulant — it prevents new clots and growth, but doesn't dissolve existing clots.
• Clexane (enoxaparin) — Clexane (enoxaparin) is a low molecular weight heparin — an anticoagulant.
• Warfarin sodium — Warfarin is an oral anticoagulant.

⭐ Key point: Main risk of thrombolysis is bleeding, especially intracranial haemorrhage. Check contraindications carefully.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q37$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 354, $s$Hypnotic drugs, as a pharmacological class, are primarily used to:$s$, $s$["Induce and maintain sleep", "Treat hypertension", "Reverse anticoagulation", "Increase heart rate"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Induce and maintain sleep
HYPNOTIC drugs are used to INDUCE and MAINTAIN SLEEP. Examples: benzodiazepines (e.g. temazepam), "Z-drugs" (zolpidem, zopiclone), and melatonin agonists. In ICU, natural sleep promotion (lights off at night, noise reduction, eye masks, ear plugs) is preferred, and hypnotics are used cautiously because they can cause delirium and respiratory depression.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Treat hypertension — Hypertension is treated with antihypertensives.
• Reverse anticoagulation — Anticoagulation is reversed with specific antidotes.
• Increase heart rate — Increasing heart rate is done with drugs like atropine.

⭐ Key point: Avoid hypnotics in elderly patients where possible (falls, confusion).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q39$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 355, $s$A recognised adverse effect of inotropic infusions (e.g. dobutamine) is:$s$, $s$["Bradycardia", "Reduced myocardial oxygen demand", "Tachyarrhythmias and myocardial ischaemia", "Hypoglycaemia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tachyarrhythmias and myocardial ischaemia
INOTROPES such as DOBUTAMINE (and adrenaline, dopamine, milrinone) make the heart beat faster and harder. This INCREASES the heart's OXYGEN DEMAND. Recognised adverse effects: TACHYCARDIA and TACHYARRHYTHMIAS (e.g. atrial fibrillation, ventricular ectopics, VT) and MYOCARDIAL ISCHAEMIA (chest pain, ST changes), especially in patients with coronary artery disease. Dobutamine can also cause hypotension (from β2 vasodilation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bradycardia — Inotropes usually cause TACHYcardia, not bradycardia.
• Reduced myocardial oxygen demand — Inotropes INCREASE, not reduce, myocardial oxygen demand.
• Hypoglycaemia — Hypoglycaemia is not a typical effect; adrenaline can actually cause HYPERglycaemia.

⭐ Key point: Use the lowest effective dose, monitor ECG continuously, and watch for new arrhythmias or chest pain. Correct potassium and magnesium to reduce arrhythmia risk.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q71$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 356, $s$The two drugs used together to manage the hypermetabolic response in burns are:$s$, $s$["Propranolol and oxandrolone", "Adrenaline and atropine", "Insulin and glucagon", "Furosemide and spironolactone"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Propranolol and oxandrolone
After major BURNS (above about 30–40% TBSA), the body enters a prolonged HYPERMETABOLIC state: very high stress hormones (catecholamines, cortisol), high heart rate, high energy use, MUSCLE WASTING, insulin resistance and poor wound healing, which can last months. Two drugs are used together to reduce this: PROPRANOLOL (a non-selective beta-blocker) — reduces heart rate, cardiac workload, energy expenditure and muscle breakdown; OXANDROLONE (an anabolic steroid) — increases muscle protein synthesis, improves weight gain and wound healing, and shortens hospital stay.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adrenaline and atropine — Adrenaline and atropine are emergency drugs for cardiac arrest and bradycardia.
• Insulin and glucagon — Insulin may be used to control hyperglycaemia, but glucagon is not used for hypermetabolism.
• Furosemide and spironolactone — Furosemide and spironolactone are diuretics, not used for hypermetabolism.

⭐ Key point: Supportive measures also matter: early enteral nutrition with high protein, early wound excision and grafting, warm environment (28–33 °C), and early mobilisation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q122$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 357, $s$A nurse is asked to administer a medication dose that seems unusually high compared to standard practice. The most appropriate action is to:$s$, $s$["Administer it anyway, trusting the prescriber completely without question", "Verify the order, question the prescriber if uncertain, and refuse to administer until the concern is resolved", "Give half the dose without informing anyone", "Ignore the concern to avoid seeming difficult"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Verify the order, question the prescriber if uncertain, and refuse to administer until the concern is resolved
If a medication dose seems UNUSUALLY HIGH, the nurse should VERIFY the order (check references, the patient's weight and kidney function), QUESTION the prescriber, and REFUSE to give it until the concern is resolved.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer it anyway, trusting the prescriber completely without question — Trusting the prescriber blindly is unsafe.
• Give half the dose without informing anyone — Giving half the dose without telling anyone is a medication error.
• Ignore the concern to avoid seeming difficult — Ignoring the concern may harm the patient.

⭐ Key point: Document the conversation and use the chain of command if unresolved.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q189$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 358, $s$Women are how many times more likely than men to develop ICU-AW?$s$, $s$["Equally likely", "About 2 times", "About 4 times", "About 10 times"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: About 4 times
ICU-ACQUIRED WEAKNESS (ICU-AW) is generalised muscle weakness developing during critical illness. Studies (e.g. De Jonghe et al.) found FEMALE SEX to be an independent risk factor, with women about 4 TIMES more likely than men to develop ICU-AW. Other risk factors: sepsis, multi-organ failure, prolonged mechanical ventilation, immobility, hyperglycaemia, corticosteroids and neuromuscular blocking agents.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Equally likely — Women and men are not equally likely according to these studies.
• About 2 times — "About 2 times" underestimates the reported risk.
• About 10 times — "About 10 times" overestimates it.

⭐ Key point: Early mobilisation, good glucose control, and minimising sedation and paralysis help prevent ICU-AW.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q199$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 359, $s$Amoxicillin suspension is available as 250 mg in 5 mL. The prescription is 500 mg. What volume should be given?$s$, $s$["2 mL", "2.5 mL", "10 mL", "20 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10 mL
For oral liquids: VOLUME = (DOSE WANTED ÷ DOSE IN STOCK) × STOCK VOLUME. Here: (500 mg ÷ 250 mg) × 5 mL = 2 × 5 = 10 mL. Check: 5 mL contains 250 mg, so 10 mL contains 500 mg. ✓ A useful "sense check": the dose wanted is double the stock strength, so the volume must be double the stock volume.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2 mL — 2 mL forgets to multiply by the 5 mL stock volume (only 100 mg).
• 2.5 mL — 2.5 mL would give only 125 mg (a quarter of the dose).
• 20 mL — 20 mL would give 1,000 mg — double the dose.

⭐ Key point: Shake suspensions well before measuring (the drug settles at the bottom), and use an oral syringe for accuracy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q3 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 360, $s$1,000 mL of 5% dextrose is prescribed to run over 8 hours using a giving set with a drop factor of 10 drops/mL. What is the correct drip rate?$s$, $s$["21 drops/min", "31 drops/min", "42 drops/min", "125 drops/min"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 21 drops/min
DROPS PER MINUTE = (VOLUME × DROP FACTOR) ÷ TIME IN MINUTES. Here: (1,000 × 10) ÷ (8 × 60) = 10,000 ÷ 480 = 20.8, which rounds to 21 drops/min. Check: 1,000 ÷ 8 = 125 mL/hr; 125 ÷ 6 ≈ 21. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 31 drops/min — 31 drops/min would finish the bag in about 5.4 hours.
• 42 drops/min — 42 drops/min is double — finished in 4 hours.
• 125 drops/min — 125 drops/min is the rate in mL/HOUR.

⭐ Key point: 5% dextrose is mainly "free water" — after the glucose is used, the water spreads to all body compartments, so large volumes can cause hyponatraemia; it is not a resuscitation fluid.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q11 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 361, $s$1,500 mL of 5% dextrose is prescribed to run over 10 hours using a giving set with a drop factor of 20 drops/mL. What is the correct drip rate?$s$, $s$["150 drops/min", "50 drops/min", "38 drops/min", "100 drops/min"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 50 drops/min
DROPS PER MINUTE = (VOLUME × DROP FACTOR) ÷ TIME IN MINUTES. Here: (1,500 × 20) ÷ (10 × 60) = 30,000 ÷ 600 = 50 drops/min. Check: hourly rate = 1,500 ÷ 10 = 150 mL/hr; with a 20-drop set, drops/min = mL/hr ÷ 3 = 50. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 150 drops/min — 150 drops/min is the rate in mL/HOUR.
• 38 drops/min — 38 drops/min would finish in about 13 hours.
• 100 drops/min — 100 drops/min is double the correct rate.

⭐ Key point: Drop factor shortcuts: 10 drops/mL → ÷6; 15 → ÷4; 20 → ÷3; 60 (micro-drip) → drops/min equals mL/hr.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q13 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 362, $s$Adrenaline (epinephrine) 4 mg is diluted to 250 mL and prescribed at 0.05 mcg/kg/min for a patient weighing 55 kg. At what rate (mL/hr) should the infusion pump be set?$s$, $s$["10.3 mL/hr", "20.6 mL/hr", "1.9 mL/hr", "0.2 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10.3 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 4 mg in 250 mL = 0.016 mg/mL = 16 micrograms/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 0.05 mcg/kg/min × 55 kg × 60 min = 165 micrograms/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 165 ÷ 16 = 10.3 mL/hr. Check: 10.3 × 16 = 165 mcg/hr ÷ 60 = 2.75 mcg/min ÷ 55 kg = 0.05 mcg/kg/min. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20.6 mL/hr — 20.6 mL/hr is double the dose (0.1 mcg/kg/min).
• 1.9 mL/hr — 1.9 mL/hr is far too low.
• 0.2 mL/hr — 0.2 mL/hr is a units error.

⭐ Key point: Formula: mL/hr = (mcg/kg/min × kg × 60) ÷ mcg/mL. Adrenaline should be given through a central line where possible and titrated to the target MAP.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q16 · concept asked in 10 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 363, $s$Dopamine is ordered at 10 mcg/kg/min for a 60 kg patient. What is this dose expressed in mg/hr?$s$, $s$["36 mg/hr", "60 mg/hr", "3.6 mg/hr", "6 mg/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 36 mg/hr
To convert micrograms per kg per MINUTE into milligrams per HOUR: STEP 1 — Per minute for the whole patient: 10 mcg/kg/min × 60 kg = 600 micrograms/min. STEP 2 — Per hour: 600 × 60 = 36,000 micrograms/hr. STEP 3 — Convert to milligrams (÷1,000): 36,000 ÷ 1,000 = 36 mg/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 60 mg/hr — 60 mg/hr is a calculation error.
• 3.6 mg/hr — 3.6 mg/hr is ten times too small (a decimal place error).
• 6 mg/hr — 6 mg/hr forgets one multiplication by 60 (it is mg per 10 minutes).

⭐ Key point: Remember the conversions: 1 mg = 1,000 micrograms; 1 hour = 60 minutes. Many drug errors come from mixing up mg and micrograms — write "micrograms" in full, not "µg", to avoid confusion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q17$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 364, $s$Metronidazole infusion is available as 500 mg in 100 mL. The prescription is 400 mg. What volume should be given?$s$, $s$["125 mL", "80 mL", "0.8 mL", "160 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 80 mL
VOLUME = (DOSE WANTED ÷ DOSE IN STOCK) × STOCK VOLUME. Here: (400 mg ÷ 500 mg) × 100 mL = 0.8 × 100 = 80 mL. Check: 500 mg in 100 mL = 5 mg/mL; 400 mg ÷ 5 mg/mL = 80 mL. ✓ So 20 mL of the bag must be removed and discarded (or set the pump to deliver only 80 mL).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 125 mL — 125 mL is stock ÷ wanted × volume — the formula upside down.
• 0.8 mL — 0.8 mL forgets to multiply by 100 mL.
• 160 mL — 160 mL would give 800 mg — double.

⭐ Key point: When giving part of a bag, set the "volume to be infused" (VTBI) on the pump carefully so the pump stops at the right amount.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q18$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 365, $s$1,000 mL of Hartmann's solution is prescribed to run over 8 hours using a giving set with a drop factor of 15 drops/mL. What is the correct drip rate?$s$, $s$["42 drops/min", "125 drops/min", "31 drops/min", "62 drops/min"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 31 drops/min
DROPS PER MINUTE = (VOLUME × DROP FACTOR) ÷ TIME IN MINUTES. Here: (1,000 × 15) ÷ (8 × 60) = 15,000 ÷ 480 = 31.25, which rounds to 31 drops/min. Check: 125 mL/hr ÷ 4 = 31. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 42 drops/min — 42 drops/min would finish in 6 hours.
• 125 drops/min — 125 drops/min is the rate in mL/HOUR.
• 62 drops/min — 62 drops/min is double.

⭐ Key point: Hartmann's (compound sodium lactate) is a balanced crystalloid often preferred over 0.9% saline for large-volume resuscitation (less hyperchloraemic acidosis).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q19 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 366, $s$A nurse is asked to prepare an infusion so that a patient weighing 90 kg receives dopamine at 3 mcg/kg/min. The available bag is 800 mg in 500 mL D5W. What is the required infusion rate in mL/hr?$s$, $s$["10.1 mL/hr", "20.3 mL/hr", "8.4 mL/hr", "16.9 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10.1 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 800 mg in 500 mL = 1.6 mg/mL = 1,600 micrograms/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 3 mcg/kg/min × 90 kg × 60 = 16,200 micrograms/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 16,200 ÷ 1,600 = 10.1 mL/hr. Check: 10.1 × 1,600 = 16,160 ≈ 16,200. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20.3 mL/hr — 20.3 mL/hr is double the dose.
• 8.4 mL/hr — 8.4 mL/hr would give about 2.5 mcg/kg/min.
• 16.9 mL/hr — 16.9 mL/hr would give about 5 mcg/kg/min.

⭐ Key point: Low-dose dopamine (1–5 mcg/kg/min) does NOT protect the kidneys — this old idea has been disproven.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q24$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 367, $s$Ceftazidime 60 mg/kg/day is prescribed in divided doses every 6 hours for an infant weighing 9 kg. How much is each dose?$s$, $s$["540 mg", "135 mg", "15 mg", "108 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 135 mg
STEP 1 — Daily dose: 60 mg/kg/day × 9 kg = 540 mg per day. STEP 2 — Every 6 hours = 4 doses. STEP 3 — Each dose: 540 ÷ 4 = 135 mg.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 540 mg — 540 mg is the whole day's dose — four times too much per dose.
• 15 mg — 15 mg is a calculation error.
• 108 mg — 108 mg would be dividing into 5 doses.

⭐ Key point: Paediatric doses must be calculated carefully and double-checked; small errors cause big relative overdoses in infants.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q27 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 368, $s$500 mL of normal saline is running at 50 mL/hr. How long will it take to finish?$s$, $s$["11 h", "6 h", "10 h", "10 h 30 min"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10 h
To find how long an infusion will last, use: TIME = VOLUME ÷ RATE. Here: 500 mL ÷ 50 mL/hr = 10 hours exactly. Check: 50 mL every hour × 10 hours = 500 mL. ✓ Knowing the finish time lets the nurse plan the next bag, check that the infusion is running as expected (if much more or less fluid has gone in than expected, the pump or line may be faulty), and record accurate fluid balance.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 11 h — 11 hours is one hour too long (it would match a rate of about 45 mL/hr).
• 6 h — 6 hours would only be correct at about 83 mL/hr.
• 10 h 30 min — 10 h 30 min adds 30 minutes that are not there.

⭐ Key point: Write the start time and the expected finish time on the bag label, and check the volume remaining against the fluid chart each hour.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q29 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 369, $s$An infusion is being given via a microdrip set, where the drop factor is 60 gtt/mL, at a pump-set rate of 45 mL/hr. If the pump failed and this had to be run by gravity, what would the drop rate be in gtt/min?$s$, $s$["75 gtt/min", "45 gtt/min", "60 gtt/min", "15 gtt/min"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 45 gtt/min
A MICRODRIP (paediatric) set delivers 60 DROPS per mL. Formula: drops/min = (mL/hr × drop factor) ÷ 60 minutes = (45 × 60) ÷ 60 = 45 drops/min. With a 60-drop set, the drops per minute ALWAYS EQUAL the mL per hour — a handy shortcut.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 75 gtt/min — 75 drops/min is a calculation error.
• 60 gtt/min — 60 drops/min is the drop factor, not the rate.
• 15 gtt/min — 15 drops/min would be the rate for a 20-drop set (45 ÷ 3).

⭐ Key point: Micro-drip sets are used for small, precise volumes (children, drug infusions) when a pump is not available.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q31$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 370, $s$Dopamine 400 mg is diluted to 250 mL and prescribed at 3 mcg/kg/min for a patient weighing 60 kg. At what rate (mL/hr) should the infusion pump be set?$s$, $s$["1.1 mL/hr", "13.5 mL/hr", "0.1 mL/hr", "6.8 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 6.8 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 400 mg in 250 mL = 1.6 mg/mL = 1,600 mcg/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 3 mcg/kg/min × 60 kg × 60 = 10,800 mcg/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 10,800 ÷ 1,600 = 6.75 ≈ 6.8 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1.1 mL/hr — 1.1 mL/hr is far too low.
• 13.5 mL/hr — 13.5 mL/hr is double.
• 0.1 mL/hr — 0.1 mL/hr is a units error.

⭐ Key point: Dopamine can cause tachyarrhythmias and tissue necrosis if it extravasates — use a central line where possible.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q33 · concept asked in 10 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 371, $s$Ampicillin 30 mg/kg/day is prescribed in divided doses every 6 hours for an infant weighing 9 kg. How much is each dose?$s$, $s$["67.5 mg", "270 mg", "7.5 mg", "54 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 67.5 mg
STEP 1 — Daily dose: 30 mg/kg/day × 9 kg = 270 mg per day. STEP 2 — "Every 6 hours" means 4 doses in 24 hours (24 ÷ 6 = 4). STEP 3 — Each dose: 270 ÷ 4 = 67.5 mg. Check: 67.5 × 4 = 270. ✓ In infants, small arithmetic errors make large relative overdoses, so every paediatric calculation should be independently double-checked.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 270 mg — 270 mg is the WHOLE day's dose — giving it every 6 hours would be four times too much.
• 7.5 mg — 7.5 mg is a calculation error — far too little to treat infection.
• 54 mg — 54 mg would be the dose if divided into 5 doses.

⭐ Key point: "mg/kg/DAY" = calculate the daily total, then divide by the number of doses. "mg/kg/DOSE" = multiply by weight only. Read the prescription carefully.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q38 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 372, $s$Aminophylline is ordered as a loading dose of 5 mg/kg over 30 minutes for a 60 kg patient, supplied as 250 mg in 100 mL. What volume should be given, and at what rate over the 30 minutes?$s$, $s$["60 mL total, at a rate of 120 mL/hr", "300 mL total, at a rate of 600 mL/hr", "120 mL total, at a rate of 240 mL/hr", "120 mL total, at a rate of 60 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 120 mL total, at a rate of 240 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Dose: 5 mg/kg × 60 kg = 300 mg. STEP 2 — Concentration: 250 mg in 100 mL = 2.5 mg/mL. STEP 3 — Volume: 300 ÷ 2.5 = 120 mL. STEP 4 — Rate over 30 minutes: 120 mL ÷ 0.5 hr = 240 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 60 mL total, at a rate of 120 mL/hr — 60 mL is only 150 mg.
• 300 mL total, at a rate of 600 mL/hr — 300 mL is the dose in mg confused with mL.
• 120 mL total, at a rate of 60 mL/hr — 120 mL at 60 mL/hr would take 2 hours, not 30 minutes.

⭐ Key point: Aminophylline has a narrow therapeutic range — monitor for nausea, arrhythmias and seizures, and omit the loading dose if the patient already takes theophylline.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q39$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 373, $s$Dobutamine 250 mg is diluted to 250 mL and prescribed at 2.5 mcg/kg/min for a patient weighing 60 kg. At what rate (mL/hr) should the infusion pump be set?$s$, $s$["1.5 mL/hr", "0.1 mL/hr", "9 mL/hr", "18 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 9 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 250 mg in 250 mL = 1 mg/mL = 1,000 mcg/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 2.5 mcg/kg/min × 60 kg × 60 = 9,000 mcg/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 9,000 ÷ 1,000 = 9 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1.5 mL/hr — 1.5 mL/hr is far too low.
• 0.1 mL/hr — 0.1 mL/hr is a units error.
• 18 mL/hr — 18 mL/hr is double.

⭐ Key point: Shortcut for 1 mg/mL: mL/hr = mcg/kg/min × weight × 0.06 (2.5 × 60 × 0.06 = 9).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q41 · concept asked in 10 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 374, $s$750 mL of normal saline is running at 125 mL/hr. How long will it take to finish?$s$, $s$["6 h", "10 h", "6 h 30 min", "7 h"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 6 h
TIME = VOLUME ÷ RATE. Here: 750 mL ÷ 125 mL/hr = 6 hours exactly. Check: 125 × 6 = 750. ✓ The nurse can use this to plan when to prepare the next bag and to check that the correct amount has been given at each hour.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 h — 10 hours would be at 75 mL/hr.
• 6 h 30 min — 6 h 30 min adds 30 minutes that are not there.
• 7 h — 7 hours would be at about 107 mL/hr.

⭐ Key point: Common error: confusing the RATE (mL/hr) with the TIME. Always write the units next to each number.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q43 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 375, $s$Propofol (10 mg/mL) is ordered at 30 mcg/kg/min for an 80 kg patient. What infusion rate in mL/hr should be programmed?$s$, $s$["24 mL/hr", "7.2 mL/hr", "28.8 mL/hr", "14.4 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 14.4 mL/hr
Propofol 1% = 10 mg/mL. STEP 1 — Dose per hour: 30 mcg/kg/min × 80 kg × 60 = 144,000 mcg/hr = 144 mg/hr. STEP 2 — Rate: 144 mg/hr ÷ 10 mg/mL = 14.4 mL/hr. (Equivalent to 1.8 mg/kg/hr.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 24 mL/hr — 24 mL/hr would be 50 mcg/kg/min.
• 7.2 mL/hr — 7.2 mL/hr is half the dose.
• 28.8 mL/hr — 28.8 mL/hr is double.

⭐ Key point: Propofol provides about 1.1 kcal/mL (fat) — count it in nutrition. Watch for hypotension, and for propofol infusion syndrome at high doses for long periods. Change the infusion set every 12 hours (lipid supports bacterial growth).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q44$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 376, $s$Hydrocortisone (reconstituted) is available as 100 mg in 2 mL. The prescription is 50 mg. What volume should be given?$s$, $s$["0.5 mL", "2 mL", "4 mL", "1 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1 mL
VOLUME = (DOSE WANTED ÷ DOSE IN STOCK) × STOCK VOLUME. Here: (50 mg ÷ 100 mg) × 2 mL = 0.5 × 2 = 1 mL. Check: 100 mg is in 2 mL, so 50 mg (half) is in 1 mL (half). ✓ HYDROCORTISONE is used in ICU for refractory septic shock (e.g. 50 mg every 6 hours), adrenal crisis (100 mg IV immediately), and as stress-dose cover in patients on long-term steroids.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.5 mL — 0.5 mL would give only 25 mg.
• 2 mL — 2 mL would give 100 mg — double the dose.
• 4 mL — 4 mL is the formula upside down (stock ÷ wanted).

⭐ Key point: Monitor blood glucose during hydrocortisone therapy (it raises glucose) and watch for infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q48$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 377, $s$Dobutamine is mixed as 250 mg in 250 mL D5W and prescribed at 5 mcg/kg/min for an 80 kg patient. What infusion rate in mL/hr should be set?$s$, $s$["24 mL/hr", "12 mL/hr", "48 mL/hr", "20 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 24 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 250 mg in 250 mL = 1 mg/mL = 1,000 micrograms/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 5 mcg/kg/min × 80 kg × 60 min = 24,000 micrograms/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 24,000 ÷ 1,000 = 24 mL/hr. Check: 24 × 1,000 = 24,000 ÷ 60 = 400 mcg/min ÷ 80 = 5 mcg/kg/min. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 12 mL/hr — 12 mL/hr gives 2.5 mcg/kg/min — half.
• 48 mL/hr — 48 mL/hr gives 10 mcg/kg/min — double.
• 20 mL/hr — 20 mL/hr gives about 4.2 mcg/kg/min.

⭐ Key point: With a 1 mg/mL solution: mL/hr = mcg/kg/min × weight × 0.06 (5 × 80 × 0.06 = 24). Watch for tachycardia and arrhythmias on dobutamine.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q49 · concept asked in 10 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 378, $s$Noradrenaline 8 mg in 250 mL is ordered at 5 mcg/min. What pump rate (mL/hr) will deliver this dose?$s$, $s$["0.2 mL/hr", "4.7 mL/hr", "9.4 mL/hr", "18.8 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 9.4 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 8 mg in 250 mL = 0.032 mg/mL = 32 micrograms/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 5 mcg/min × 60 = 300 micrograms/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 300 ÷ 32 = 9.375 ≈ 9.4 mL/hr. Check: 9.4 × 32 = 300.8 mcg/hr ÷ 60 ≈ 5 mcg/min. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.2 mL/hr — 0.2 mL/hr is a units error.
• 4.7 mL/hr — 4.7 mL/hr is half the dose.
• 18.8 mL/hr — 18.8 mL/hr is double the dose.

⭐ Key point: Noradrenaline should run through a dedicated central line lumen; never flush that lumen, as it would give a bolus.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q50 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 379, $s$Ceftriaxone (reconstituted) is available as 1,000 mg in 10 mL. The prescription is 250 mg. What volume should be given?$s$, $s$["0.2 mL", "2.5 mL", "5 mL", "40 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2.5 mL
VOLUME = (DOSE WANTED ÷ DOSE IN STOCK) × STOCK VOLUME. Here: (250 mg ÷ 1,000 mg) × 10 mL = 0.25 × 10 = 2.5 mL. Check: 1,000 mg in 10 mL = 100 mg/mL; 250 ÷ 100 = 2.5 mL. ✓ Since 250 mg is a quarter of the vial, the volume is a quarter of 10 mL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.2 mL — 0.2 mL would give only 20 mg.
• 5 mL — 5 mL would give 500 mg — double.
• 40 mL — 40 mL is the formula upside down.

⭐ Key point: Ceftriaxone must NOT be mixed with, or given at the same time through the same line as, calcium-containing solutions (e.g. Hartmann's) — especially in newborns, where fatal precipitates have occurred.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q52$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 380, $s$Soluble insulin 100 units is made up to 100 mL with normal saline. The order is 6 units/hour. What rate should be set?$s$, $s$["12 mL/hr", "3 mL/hr", "6 mL/hr", "24 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 6 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 100 units in 100 mL = 1 unit per mL. STEP 2 — Rate: 6 units/hr ÷ 1 unit/mL = 6 mL/hr. With this standard "1 unit per mL" dilution, the pump rate (mL/hr) always equals the dose (units/hr), which reduces calculation errors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 12 mL/hr — 12 mL/hr gives double the dose — risk of hypoglycaemia.
• 3 mL/hr — 3 mL/hr gives half.
• 24 mL/hr — 24 mL/hr gives four times the dose — dangerous hypoglycaemia.

⭐ Key point: Check blood glucose hourly during insulin infusions, and monitor potassium — insulin moves potassium into cells.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q56 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 381, $s$Current ICU sedation guidelines generally favor propofol or dexmedetomidine over benzodiazepine infusions for most mechanically ventilated adults primarily because benzodiazepines are associated with:$s$, $s$["Higher rates of ICU delirium", "Lower cost and easier titration", "Faster awakening times", "Reduced risk of hypotension"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Higher rates of ICU delirium
Current ICU sedation guidelines (e.g. PADIS 2018) recommend PROPOFOL or DEXMEDETOMIDINE rather than BENZODIAZEPINE infusions (e.g. midazolam) for most ventilated adults. Benzodiazepines are associated with HIGHER RATES OF ICU DELIRIUM, deeper and longer sedation (they accumulate, especially in kidney or liver failure), longer ventilation and longer ICU stay. Delirium is linked to higher mortality and long-term cognitive problems.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lower cost and easier titration — Benzodiazepines may be cheaper, but they are harder, not easier, to titrate because they accumulate.
• Faster awakening times — Benzodiazepines cause SLOWER, not faster, awakening.
• Reduced risk of hypotension — Propofol and dexmedetomidine also cause hypotension; reduced hypotension is not the reason.

⭐ Key point: Benzodiazepines are still preferred for alcohol withdrawal and seizures. Use light sedation (RASS −2 to 0) and daily sedation interruption where appropriate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q125$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 382, $s$A patient on long-term corticosteroid therapy for a chronic condition is admitted critically ill. The nurse should anticipate the need for:$s$, $s$["Switching immediately to an unrelated drug class", "No special consideration regarding steroid dosing", "Continued (often increased 'stress-dose') steroid coverage, since abrupt withdrawal risks adrenal crisis", "Abrupt discontinuation of steroids immediately"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Continued (often increased 'stress-dose') steroid coverage, since abrupt withdrawal risks adrenal crisis
Long-term CORTICOSTEROIDS (e.g. prednisolone for asthma, arthritis or autoimmune disease) SUPPRESS the body's own ADRENAL glands. When a patient becomes CRITICALLY ILL (surgery, sepsis, trauma), the body normally needs much more cortisol to cope with stress — but suppressed adrenals cannot produce it. If steroids are stopped suddenly, or not increased, the patient can develop an ADRENAL CRISIS: severe hypotension resistant to fluids and vasopressors, hypoglycaemia, hyponatraemia, hyperkalaemia, and collapse. So the nurse should anticipate CONTINUED, often INCREASED "STRESS-DOSE" STEROIDS (e.g. IV hydrocortisone 50–100 mg every 6–8 hours, or 200 mg/day infusion).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Switching immediately to an unrelated drug class — Switching to an unrelated drug class does not replace cortisol.
• No special consideration regarding steroid dosing — Steroid dosing must be specially considered.
• Abrupt discontinuation of steroids immediately — Abrupt discontinuation risks adrenal crisis.

⭐ Key point: Patients on long-term steroids should carry a steroid card/alert and follow "sick day rules" (double the dose when ill).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q148$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 383, $s$A patient in the PACU has a pulse oximetry reading of 90% on room air, with shallow respirations and drowsiness after opioid analgesia. The nurse's priority action is to:$s$, $s$["Increase the opioid dose to help the patient rest", "Stimulate the patient, apply supplemental oxygen, and consider naloxone per protocol while notifying the anaesthetist", "Discharge the patient to the ward immediately", "Document and reassess in one hour"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stimulate the patient, apply supplemental oxygen, and consider naloxone per protocol while notifying the anaesthetist
After OPIOID analgesia, a DROWSY patient with SHALLOW breathing and SpO2 90% on room air has OPIOID-INDUCED RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION. Priority actions: STIMULATE the patient (call their name, gentle shake), encourage deep breaths, open the airway, APPLY SUPPLEMENTAL OXYGEN, monitor respiratory rate, SpO2 and sedation score, and CONSIDER NALOXONE (per protocol) if breathing does not improve, while NOTIFYING THE ANAESTHETIST.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the opioid dose to help the patient rest — Increasing the opioid would worsen respiratory depression.
• Discharge the patient to the ward immediately — Discharging to the ward is unsafe until breathing and sedation improve.
• Document and reassess in one hour — Waiting an hour could allow respiratory arrest.

⭐ Key point: Naloxone may wear off before the opioid — observe closely after reversal.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q158$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 384, $s$A patient presents with poisoning from ingestion of a substance containing methanol found in illicitly distilled alcohol ('moonshine'). Which clinical feature is a classic warning sign of methanol toxicity?$s$, $s$["Excessive salivation and lacrimation", "Bright red skin discolouration", "Pinpoint pupils", "Visual disturbances or blindness due to optic nerve toxicity from formic acid accumulation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Visual disturbances or blindness due to optic nerve toxicity from formic acid accumulation
METHANOL (in illicitly distilled alcohol) is converted by the liver to FORMIC ACID, which damages the OPTIC NERVE and retina, causing VISUAL DISTURBANCES ("snowstorm vision", blurred vision) and BLINDNESS. It also causes severe high anion gap metabolic acidosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Excessive salivation and lacrimation — Excessive salivation and lacrimation suggest cholinergic poisoning.
• Bright red skin discolouration — Bright red skin suggests cyanide or CO.
• Pinpoint pupils — Pinpoint pupils suggest opioids.

⭐ Key point: Treat with fomepizole or ethanol, bicarbonate, folic acid, and haemodialysis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q165$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 385, $s$A patient with heart failure receiving digoxin has a potassium level of 3.0mmol/L. The nurse should recognise this combination as significant because it:$s$, $s$["Reduces the risk of digoxin toxicity", "Only affects blood pressure, not cardiac rhythm", "Increases the risk of digoxin toxicity and arrhythmia", "Has no bearing on digoxin's effect"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increases the risk of digoxin toxicity and arrhythmia
DIGOXIN competes with potassium for binding to the sodium-potassium pump in heart cells. When POTASSIUM is LOW (3.0 mmol/L), digoxin binds more strongly, INCREASING TOXICITY and the risk of ARRHYTHMIAS (e.g. ventricular ectopics, bigeminy, VT, heart block).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reduces the risk of digoxin toxicity — Low potassium increases, not reduces, toxicity.
• Only affects blood pressure, not cardiac rhythm — It affects heart rhythm.
• Has no bearing on digoxin's effect — Potassium has a major effect on digoxin.

⭐ Key point: Keep potassium above 4.0 mmol/L in patients on digoxin.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q166$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 386, $s$A patient with a suspected benzodiazepine overdose is being considered for flumazenil administration. Which condition makes flumazenil relatively contraindicated?$s$, $s$["Normal renal function", "Mild drowsiness without respiratory compromise", "Chronic benzodiazepine use or co-ingestion with a pro-convulsant drug such as a tricyclic antidepressant, due to seizure risk", "Known allergy to opioids"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Chronic benzodiazepine use or co-ingestion with a pro-convulsant drug such as a tricyclic antidepressant, due to seizure risk
FLUMAZENIL reverses benzodiazepines, but can trigger SEIZURES in patients with CHRONIC BENZODIAZEPINE USE (sudden withdrawal) or co-ingestion of PRO-CONVULSANT drugs such as TRICYCLIC ANTIDEPRESSANTS (the benzodiazepine was suppressing seizures).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal renal function — Normal renal function is not a contraindication.
• Mild drowsiness without respiratory compromise — Mild drowsiness doesn't need flumazenil.
• Known allergy to opioids — Opioid allergy is unrelated.

⭐ Key point: Supportive care (airway, breathing) is usually safer than flumazenil in overdose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q179$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 387, $s$A patient with suspected food poisoning from Clostridium botulinum presents with descending paralysis, diplopia, and dysphagia, with a clear sensorium. What is the priority nursing concern?$s$, $s$["Administering activated charcoal, since this is the priority treatment", "Close monitoring of respiratory function, as respiratory muscle paralysis can develop and require ventilatory support", "Reassuring the patient that no monitoring is required since consciousness is preserved", "Restricting fluids to prevent worsening symptoms"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Close monitoring of respiratory function, as respiratory muscle paralysis can develop and require ventilatory support
BOTULISM causes DESCENDING PARALYSIS (eyes, face, throat, then breathing muscles). The patient remains conscious. The priority is to MONITOR RESPIRATORY FUNCTION (vital capacity, breathing pattern, SpO2), as RESPIRATORY MUSCLE PARALYSIS can develop and require ventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administering activated charcoal, since this is the priority treatment — Activated charcoal isn't the priority.
• Reassuring the patient that no monitoring is required since consciousness is preserved — Monitoring is essential.
• Restricting fluids to prevent worsening symptoms — Fluid restriction isn't indicated.

⭐ Key point: Give antitoxin early and notify public health.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q181$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 388, $s$A patient is admitted 8 hours after a large intentional paracetamol (acetaminophen) overdose. Which investigation is essential to guide treatment decisions?$s$, $s$["A random blood glucose only", "A serum paracetamol level plotted on the treatment nomogram against time since ingestion", "A chest X-ray", "A urine drug screen for opioids"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A serum paracetamol level plotted on the treatment nomogram against time since ingestion
For a paracetamol overdose presenting 8 HOURS after ingestion, a SERUM PARACETAMOL LEVEL (taken at least 4 hours after ingestion) is plotted on the TREATMENT NOMOGRAM against time since ingestion. If above the treatment line, give N-acetylcysteine. (If presenting late or uncertain, start NAC immediately.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A random blood glucose only — Glucose alone doesn't guide treatment.
• A chest X-ray — Chest X-ray doesn't guide treatment.
• A urine drug screen for opioids — Urine drug screen doesn't measure paracetamol risk.

⭐ Key point: NAC is most effective within 8 hours of ingestion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q183$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 389, $s$A patient with beta-blocker overdose presents with severe bradycardia and hypotension refractory to atropine. Which agent is most appropriate as a specific treatment?$s$, $s$["Naloxone", "Flumazenil", "High-dose insulin with glucose (hyperinsulinaemia-euglycaemia therapy) and/or glucagon", "Activated charcoal given IV"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: High-dose insulin with glucose (hyperinsulinaemia-euglycaemia therapy) and/or glucagon
BETA-BLOCKER OVERDOSE blocks the beta receptors in the heart, causing severe BRADYCARDIA, heart block and HYPOTENSION (cardiogenic shock). Atropine often fails. Specific treatments include: GLUCAGON — it raises cyclic AMP in heart cells through its own receptors, BYPASSING the blocked beta receptors, increasing heart rate and contractility; and HIGH-DOSE INSULIN with GLUCOSE (hyperinsulinaemia–euglycaemia therapy, HIET) — it helps the stressed heart muscle use glucose for energy, improving contractility. Other options: calcium, vasopressors, pacing, and ECMO.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids, not beta-blockers.
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines.
• Activated charcoal given IV — Activated charcoal is given ORALLY (not IV), and only early with a protected airway.

⭐ Key point: During HIET, check glucose every 15–30 minutes and potassium frequently (insulin drives potassium into cells).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q186$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 390, $s$A client on propylthiouracil develops a sore throat and fever. What should the nurse suspect?$s$, $s$["Thyroid storm", "Agranulocytosis", "Myxoedema", "Hypocalcaemia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Agranulocytosis
PROPYLTHIOURACIL (PTU) and carbimazole are antithyroid drugs. A rare but serious side effect is AGRANULOCYTOSIS — a severe drop in NEUTROPHILS (white cells that fight bacterial infection). It usually occurs in the first few months of therapy. The first signs are often SORE THROAT, FEVER, mouth ulcers or other infections. The nurse should suspect agranulocytosis, advise stopping the drug, and arrange an URGENT full blood count. If confirmed, the patient needs isolation precautions and possibly antibiotics and G-CSF.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Thyroid storm — Thyroid storm causes very high fever, tachycardia, agitation — not mainly a sore throat.
• Myxoedema — Myxoedema is severe HYPOthyroidism.
• Hypocalcaemia — Hypocalcaemia causes tingling and spasms, not sore throat and fever.

⭐ Key point: Teach all patients starting antithyroid drugs to report sore throat, fever or mouth ulcers immediately. PTU can also cause severe liver injury.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q192$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 391, $s$A patient with methanol or ethylene glycol poisoning is being managed. Which agent competitively inhibits alcohol dehydrogenase and is used as an antidote?$s$, $s$["Atropine", "Naloxone", "Flumazenil", "Fomepizole (or ethanol as an alternative)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fomepizole (or ethanol as an alternative)
METHANOL and ETHYLENE GLYCOL are not very toxic themselves. The enzyme ALCOHOL DEHYDROGENASE in the liver converts them into TOXIC METABOLITES: methanol → formic acid (causes blindness and acidosis); ethylene glycol → glycolic and oxalic acid (causes acidosis and kidney failure). FOMEPIZOLE competitively INHIBITS alcohol dehydrogenase, preventing the formation of these toxic acids. ETHANOL (drinking alcohol) works the same way — the enzyme prefers ethanol — and is used if fomepizole is not available.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Atropine — Atropine treats organophosphate (cholinergic) poisoning.
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioid overdose.
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines.

⭐ Key point: HAEMODIALYSIS removes both the parent alcohols and their toxic acids. Give folic acid (methanol) or thiamine and pyridoxine (ethylene glycol) as co-factors.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q194$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 392, $s$Amiodarone is given as a 150 mg IV bolus over 10 minutes, followed by a maintenance infusion of 1 mg/min for 6 hours, then 0.5 mg/min for the next 18 hours. What is the total dose of amiodarone infused over the full 24-hour period (including the bolus)?$s$, $s$["1050 mg", "660 mg", "900 mg", "1200 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1050 mg
Add up each part of the amiodarone regimen. STEP 1 — Bolus: 150 mg. STEP 2 — First infusion: 1 mg/min for 6 hours = 1 × 60 × 6 = 360 mg. STEP 3 — Second infusion: 0.5 mg/min for the next 18 hours = 0.5 × 60 × 18 = 540 mg. STEP 4 — Total: 150 + 360 + 540 = 1,050 mg in 24 hours. (Strictly, the bolus takes 10 minutes, but the question asks for the total of all parts.) This is the standard IV amiodarone regimen for stable VT or AF with a pulse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 660 mg — 660 mg forgets the 540 mg second infusion part (150 + 360 + 150 by mistake) — it is too low.
• 900 mg — 900 mg leaves out the 150 mg bolus (360 + 540).
• 1200 mg — 1,200 mg would be obtained by wrongly running 1 mg/min for 18 hours or similar miscalculation.

⭐ Key point: Amiodarone is irritant to veins — give through a central line where possible (or a large peripheral vein with an in-line filter) and monitor BP, heart rate and QT.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q1$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 393, $s$Diazepam injection is available as 10 mg in 2 mL. The prescription is 7.5 mg. What volume should be given?$s$, $s$["1.5 mL", "0.8 mL", "2.7 mL", "3 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1.5 mL
Use the formula: VOLUME = (DOSE WANTED ÷ DOSE IN STOCK) × VOLUME OF STOCK. Here: (7.5 mg ÷ 10 mg) × 2 mL = 0.75 × 2 = 1.5 mL. Check: 10 mg is in 2 mL, so 5 mg is in 1 mL; 7.5 mg is one and a half times 5 mg, so 1.5 mL. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.8 mL — 0.8 mL would give only 4 mg.
• 2.7 mL — 2.7 mL would give 13.5 mg — an overdose.
• 3 mL — 3 mL would give 15 mg — double the dose.

⭐ Key point: "What you WANT over what you HAVE, times the volume it's in" (D/H × V). Always do a "common sense" check: is the answer more or less than the stock volume?$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q2 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 394, $s$Soluble insulin 50 units is made up to 50 mL with normal saline. The order is 5 units/hour. What rate should be set?$s$, $s$["20 mL/hr", "2.5 mL/hr", "10 mL/hr", "5 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 5 mL/hr
First work out the CONCENTRATION: 50 units in 50 mL = 1 unit per mL. To give 5 units per hour, you need 5 mL per hour. Formula: Rate (mL/hr) = Dose per hour ÷ Concentration = 5 units/hr ÷ 1 unit/mL = 5 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20 mL/hr — 20 mL/hr would give 20 units/hr — four times the dose; dangerous hypoglycaemia.
• 2.5 mL/hr — 2.5 mL/hr would give only 2.5 units/hr.
• 10 mL/hr — 10 mL/hr would give 10 units/hr — double the dose.

⭐ Key point: The standard "1 unit per mL" insulin infusion makes the pump rate equal to the units per hour. Check blood glucose hourly and watch potassium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q3 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 395, $s$Vancomycin is prescribed at 15 mg/kg per dose for a patient weighing 80 kg. What single dose should be given?$s$, $s$["600 mg", "1,200 mg", "1,350 mg", "1,320 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1,200 mg
Many ICU drugs are prescribed PER KILOGRAM of body weight, so the dose matches the patient's size. Formula: DOSE = mg/kg × WEIGHT (kg). Here: 15 mg/kg × 80 kg = 1,200 mg for each dose. Step-by-step check: 15 × 80 = (10 × 80) + (5 × 80) = 800 + 400 = 1,200 mg. ✓ Use the patient's ACTUAL, MEASURED weight (not a guess), because a wrong weight gives a wrong dose. Vancomycin is a "high-alert" antibiotic: too little fails to treat serious infections like MRSA; too much damages the kidneys and ears.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 600 mg — 600 mg is only 7.5 mg/kg — half the dose; the infection may not be treated.
• 1,350 mg — 1,350 mg is about 16.9 mg/kg — slightly too much (it matches a weight of 90 kg).
• 1,320 mg — 1,320 mg is 16.5 mg/kg (or 15 mg/kg for 88 kg) — the wrong number was used.

⭐ Key point: Give vancomycin slowly (no faster than 1 g per hour) to avoid the "red man" infusion reaction, and check TROUGH levels and kidney function to adjust later doses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q4$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 396, $s$A patient weighs 55 lb. A drug is prescribed at 10 mg/kg. What dose should be given?$s$, $s$["113.6 mg", "250 mg", "500 mg", "550 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 250 mg
Convert pounds (lb) to kilograms: 1 kg = 2.2 lb, so kg = lb ÷ 2.2. 55 lb ÷ 2.2 = 25 kg. Dose = 10 mg/kg × 25 kg = 250 mg.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 113.6 mg — 113.6 mg comes from wrongly dividing 250 by 2.2 again (double conversion).
• 500 mg — 500 mg would be 20 mg/kg.
• 550 mg — 550 mg comes from using pounds as if they were kg (10 × 55).

⭐ Key point: Always use weight in KILOGRAMS for drug calculations. Forgetting to convert pounds to kg causes a dose more than double the correct amount.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q6 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 397, $s$500 mL of Hartmann's solution is prescribed to run over 8 hours using a giving set with a drop factor of 15 drops/mL. What is the correct drip rate?$s$, $s$["32 drops/min", "16 drops/min", "62 drops/min", "21 drops/min"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 16 drops/min
DROPS PER MINUTE = (VOLUME in mL × DROP FACTOR) ÷ TIME in MINUTES. Here: (500 × 15) ÷ (8 × 60) = 7,500 ÷ 480 = 15.6 ≈ 16 drops/min.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 32 drops/min — 32 drops/min would finish the bag in about 4 hours.
• 62 drops/min — 62 drops/min (500 ÷ 8 = 62.5 mL/hr) is the mL/hr rate, not drops/min.
• 21 drops/min — 21 drops/min would finish in about 6 hours.

⭐ Key point: Round to the nearest whole drop. Check the drop factor on the giving-set packet (10, 15 or 20 for adult sets; 60 for paediatric/micro-drip sets).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q7 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 398, $s$Dopamine 400 mg is diluted to 250 mL and prescribed at 12 mcg/kg/min for a patient weighing 75 kg. At what rate (mL/hr) should the infusion pump be set?$s$, $s$["4.5 mL/hr", "0.6 mL/hr", "67.5 mL/hr", "33.8 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 33.8 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 400 mg in 250 mL = 1.6 mg/mL = 1,600 mcg/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 12 mcg/kg/min × 75 kg × 60 min = 54,000 mcg/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 54,000 ÷ 1,600 = 33.75 ≈ 33.8 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 4.5 mL/hr — 4.5 mL/hr would give only about 1.6 mcg/kg/min.
• 0.6 mL/hr — 0.6 mL/hr is far too low (a units error).
• 67.5 mL/hr — 67.5 mL/hr is double the correct rate (24 mcg/kg/min).

⭐ Key point: Formula: Rate (mL/hr) = Dose (mcg/kg/min) × Weight (kg) × 60 ÷ Concentration (mcg/mL).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q8 · concept asked in 10 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 399, $s$Ceftazidime 40 mg/kg/day is prescribed in divided doses every 8 hours for an infant weighing 10 kg. How much is each dose?$s$, $s$["100 mg", "133.3 mg", "400 mg", "13.3 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 133.3 mg
STEP 1 — Daily dose: 40 mg/kg/day × 10 kg = 400 mg per day. STEP 2 — Divide into doses: every 8 hours = 3 doses per day (24 ÷ 8 = 3). STEP 3 — Each dose: 400 ÷ 3 = 133.3 mg.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100 mg — 100 mg would be four doses a day (6-hourly).
• 400 mg — 400 mg is the whole day's dose, not each dose — a triple overdose.
• 13.3 mg — 13.3 mg is a decimal place error (ten times too little).

⭐ Key point: When a drug is prescribed "per day in divided doses", first calculate the daily total, then divide by the number of doses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q9 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 400, $s$Heparin 25,000 units in 500 mL is ordered at 800 units/hour. What rate (mL/hr) should be set on the pump?$s$, $s$["50 mL/hr", "32 mL/hr", "16 mL/hr", "8 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 16 mL/hr
Heparin infusions are prescribed in UNITS PER HOUR. STEP 1 — Find the CONCENTRATION: 25,000 units ÷ 500 mL = 50 units in every 1 mL. STEP 2 — Divide the dose by the concentration: 800 units/hr ÷ 50 units/mL = 16 mL/hr. Check: 16 mL × 50 units = 800 units. ✓ Heparin is a HIGH-ALERT medicine: a pump error can cause fatal bleeding (too much) or new clots (too little).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50 mL/hr — 50 mL/hr would give 2,500 units/hr — more than three times the dose, with a high risk of bleeding.
• 32 mL/hr — 32 mL/hr would give 1,600 units/hr — double the dose.
• 8 mL/hr — 8 mL/hr would give 400 units/hr — half the dose, so the clot may grow.

⭐ Key point: Always double-check heparin calculations and pump settings with a second nurse, follow the unit's heparin nomogram, and check the aPTT (or anti-Xa) 6 hours after any rate change.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q11 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 401, $s$500 mL of 5% dextrose is prescribed to run over 24 hours using a giving set with a drop factor of 10 drops/mL. What is the correct drip rate?$s$, $s$["21 drops/min", "6 drops/min", "5 drops/min", "3 drops/min"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3 drops/min
DROPS PER MINUTE = (VOLUME × DROP FACTOR) ÷ TIME (minutes). Here: (500 × 10) ÷ (24 × 60) = 5,000 ÷ 1,440 = 3.47 ≈ 3 drops/min.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 21 drops/min — 21 drops/min (500 ÷ 24 = 20.8 mL/hr) is the mL/hr rate, not drops/min.
• 6 drops/min — 6 drops/min would finish the bag in about 14 hours.
• 5 drops/min — 5 drops/min would finish in about 17 hours.

⭐ Key point: Very slow drip rates like this are hard to control by gravity — an infusion pump is safer.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q12 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 402, $s$Dobutamine 500 mg is diluted to 250 mL and prescribed at 5 mcg/kg/min for a patient weighing 60 kg. At what rate (mL/hr) should the infusion pump be set?$s$, $s$["18 mL/hr", "1.5 mL/hr", "9 mL/hr", "0.1 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 9 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 500 mg in 250 mL = 2 mg/mL = 2,000 mcg/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 5 mcg/kg/min × 60 kg × 60 min = 18,000 mcg/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 18,000 ÷ 2,000 = 9 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 18 mL/hr — 18 mL/hr is double the correct rate (10 mcg/kg/min).
• 1.5 mL/hr — 1.5 mL/hr is far too low.
• 0.1 mL/hr — 0.1 mL/hr is a units error.

⭐ Key point: Dobutamine usual range: 2.5–20 mcg/kg/min. Watch for tachycardia, arrhythmias and hypotension.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q15 · concept asked in 10 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 403, $s$Glyceryl trinitrate (nitroglycerin) is mixed as 50 mg in 250 mL D5W and ordered to infuse at 20 mcg/min. What rate should the pump deliver, in mL/hr?$s$, $s$["3 mL/hr", "24 mL/hr", "6 mL/hr", "12 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 6 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 50 mg in 250 mL = 0.2 mg/mL = 200 mcg/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 20 mcg/min × 60 = 1,200 mcg/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 1,200 ÷ 200 = 6 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 3 mL/hr — 3 mL/hr gives only 10 mcg/min.
• 24 mL/hr — 24 mL/hr gives 80 mcg/min.
• 12 mL/hr — 12 mL/hr gives 40 mcg/min.

⭐ Key point: GTN (not weight-based here) is titrated to chest pain and BP. Watch for hypotension and headache; avoid with sildenafil.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q20$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 404, $s$A loading dose of phenytoin at 18 mg/kg is ordered for a 70 kg patient. What is the total loading dose in milligrams?$s$, $s$["1400 mg", "700 mg", "980 mg", "1260 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1260 mg
A LOADING DOSE is a larger first dose that quickly brings the drug level in the blood up to the effective (therapeutic) range. It is calculated by weight: DOSE = mg/kg × WEIGHT. Here: 18 mg/kg × 70 kg = 1,260 mg. Check: (18 × 70) = (20 × 70) − (2 × 70) = 1,400 − 140 = 1,260 mg. ✓ Phenytoin loading is used in status epilepticus (after benzodiazepines) to stop seizures and prevent recurrence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1400 mg — 1,400 mg is 20 mg/kg — slightly too much.
• 700 mg — 700 mg is only 10 mg/kg — not enough to reach a therapeutic level.
• 980 mg — 980 mg is 14 mg/kg — not enough.

⭐ Key point: Give IV phenytoin no faster than 50 mg/min in adults (25 mg/min in elderly or cardiac patients) with continuous ECG and BP monitoring — fast infusion can cause hypotension, bradycardia and cardiac arrest. Mix only with 0.9% saline (it precipitates in dextrose) and use a filter. Watch the IV site — extravasation can cause severe tissue damage ("purple glove syndrome").$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q22$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 405, $s$Using the 4-2-1 rule, what is the hourly maintenance fluid rate for a child weighing 12 kg?$s$, $s$["48 mL/hr", "24 mL/hr", "44 mL/hr", "54 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 44 mL/hr
The 4-2-1 RULE (hourly maintenance): 4 mL/kg/hr for the FIRST 10 kg; 2 mL/kg/hr for the NEXT 10 kg; 1 mL/kg/hr for each kg ABOVE 20 kg. For 12 kg: first 10 kg = 40 mL/hr; next 2 kg = 2 × 2 = 4 mL/hr. Total: 44 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 48 mL/hr — 48 mL/hr is 4 mL/kg for all 12 kg.
• 24 mL/hr — 24 mL/hr is 2 mL/kg for all 12 kg.
• 54 mL/hr — 54 mL/hr adds incorrectly.

⭐ Key point: Many sick children need restricted fluids (e.g. two-thirds of maintenance) because of increased ADH secretion; follow local guidelines.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q25 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 406, $s$1,500 mL of Hartmann's solution is prescribed to run over 12 hours using a giving set with a drop factor of 10 drops/mL. What is the correct drip rate?$s$, $s$["21 drops/min", "125 drops/min", "31 drops/min", "42 drops/min"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 21 drops/min
DROPS PER MINUTE = (VOLUME × DROP FACTOR) ÷ TIME (minutes). Here: (1,500 × 10) ÷ (12 × 60) = 15,000 ÷ 720 = 20.8 ≈ 21 drops/min.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 125 drops/min — 125 drops/min (1,500 ÷ 12 = 125) is the mL/hr rate, not drops/min.
• 31 drops/min — 31 drops/min would finish in about 8 hours.
• 42 drops/min — 42 drops/min would finish in about 6 hours.

⭐ Key point: With a drop factor of 10, drops/min = mL/hr ÷ 6. With 15, divide by 4. With 20, divide by 3. With 60, drops/min = mL/hr.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q26 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 407, $s$Noradrenaline (norepinephrine) 8 mg is diluted to 250 mL and prescribed at 0.15 mcg/kg/min for a patient weighing 60 kg. At what rate (mL/hr) should the infusion pump be set?$s$, $s$["0.3 mL/hr", "16.9 mL/hr", "33.8 mL/hr", "2.8 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 16.9 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 8 mg in 250 mL = 0.032 mg/mL = 32 mcg/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 0.15 mcg/kg/min × 60 kg × 60 min = 540 mcg/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 540 ÷ 32 = 16.875 ≈ 16.9 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.3 mL/hr — 0.3 mL/hr is a units error.
• 33.8 mL/hr — 33.8 mL/hr is double (0.3 mcg/kg/min).
• 2.8 mL/hr — 2.8 mL/hr is far too low.

⭐ Key point: Noradrenaline should be given through a central line where possible; extravasation can cause tissue necrosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q27 · concept asked in 10 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 408, $s$An adrenaline (epinephrine) infusion is mixed as 4 mg in 500 mL D5W and ordered to run at 0.1 mcg/kg/min for a 75 kg patient. What is the correct infusion rate in mL/hr?$s$, $s$["56.25 mL/hr", "7.5 mL/hr", "112.5 mL/hr", "28.1 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 56.25 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 4 mg in 500 mL = 0.008 mg/mL = 8 mcg/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 0.1 mcg/kg/min × 75 kg × 60 = 450 mcg/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 450 ÷ 8 = 56.25 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 7.5 mL/hr — 7.5 mL/hr is the dose in mcg/min (0.1 × 75), not mL/hr.
• 112.5 mL/hr — 112.5 mL/hr is double.
• 28.1 mL/hr — 28.1 mL/hr is half.

⭐ Key point: Adrenaline at this dilution needs a high volume; more concentrated solutions are often used in ICU to reduce fluid load.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q28$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 409, $s$Dobutamine 500 mg is diluted to 250 mL and prescribed at 15 mcg/kg/min for a patient weighing 60 kg. At what rate (mL/hr) should the infusion pump be set?$s$, $s$["4.5 mL/hr", "27 mL/hr", "54 mL/hr", "0.5 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 27 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 500 mg in 250 mL = 2,000 mcg/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 15 mcg/kg/min × 60 kg × 60 = 54,000 mcg/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 54,000 ÷ 2,000 = 27 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 4.5 mL/hr — 4.5 mL/hr is far too low.
• 54 mL/hr — 54 mL/hr is double (30 mcg/kg/min).
• 0.5 mL/hr — 0.5 mL/hr is a units error.

⭐ Key point: Compare with question 15: at the same concentration and weight, 5 mcg/kg/min = 9 mL/hr, so 15 mcg/kg/min = 27 mL/hr (three times).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q29 · concept asked in 10 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 410, $s$Glyceryl trinitrate (GTN) 50 mg in 500 mL is ordered at 10 mcg/min. What pump rate (mL/hr) will deliver this dose?$s$, $s$["12 mL/hr", "3 mL/hr", "0.1 mL/hr", "6 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 6 mL/hr
Nitrates such as GTN are prescribed in MICROGRAMS per MINUTE, but pumps run in mL per HOUR, so units must be converted. STEP 1 — Concentration: 50 mg in 500 mL = 0.1 mg/mL = 100 micrograms/mL (1 mg = 1,000 micrograms). STEP 2 — Dose per HOUR: 10 micrograms/min × 60 minutes = 600 micrograms/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 600 ÷ 100 = 6 mL/hr. Check: 6 mL/hr × 100 mcg/mL = 600 mcg/hr ÷ 60 = 10 mcg/min. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 12 mL/hr — 12 mL/hr would give 20 mcg/min — double the dose; risk of hypotension.
• 3 mL/hr — 3 mL/hr would give 5 mcg/min — half the dose.
• 0.1 mL/hr — 0.1 mL/hr is the concentration in mg/mL, not a pump rate.

⭐ Key point: GTN is titrated to symptom relief (chest pain, breathlessness) and BP — check BP every 5–15 minutes when changing the rate. Stop or reduce if systolic BP falls below about 90 mmHg. Never give with sildenafil or tadalafil (severe hypotension).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q30 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 411, $s$Dobutamine 250 mg is diluted to 250 mL and prescribed at 10 mcg/kg/min for a patient weighing 60 kg. At what rate (mL/hr) should the infusion pump be set?$s$, $s$["72 mL/hr", "36 mL/hr", "0.6 mL/hr", "6 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 36 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 250 mg in 250 mL = 1 mg/mL = 1,000 mcg/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 10 mcg/kg/min × 60 kg × 60 = 36,000 mcg/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 36,000 ÷ 1,000 = 36 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 72 mL/hr — 72 mL/hr is double.
• 0.6 mL/hr — 0.6 mL/hr is the dose in mg/min (10 × 60 ÷ 1000), not mL/hr.
• 6 mL/hr — 6 mL/hr is too low.

⭐ Key point: Shortcut for a 1 mg/mL solution: Rate (mL/hr) = mcg/kg/min × weight × 0.06.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q31 · concept asked in 10 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 412, $s$One litre of IV fluid is to infuse over 10 hours using a macrodrip set with a drop factor of 10 gtt/mL. What is the correct rate in drops per minute?$s$, $s$["100 gtt/min", "17 gtt/min", "25 gtt/min", "10 gtt/min"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 17 gtt/min
For a gravity drip (no pump), count DROPS per minute. Formula: DROPS/MIN = (VOLUME in mL × DROP FACTOR) ÷ TIME in MINUTES. Here: volume = 1,000 mL; drop factor = 10 drops (gtt) per mL (a "macrodrip" set); time = 10 hours × 60 = 600 minutes. Calculation: (1,000 × 10) ÷ 600 = 10,000 ÷ 600 = 16.7, which rounds to 17 gtt/min. Check: 17 drops/min ÷ 10 drops/mL = 1.7 mL/min × 600 min ≈ 1,020 mL. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100 gtt/min — 100 gtt/min is the rate in mL/HOUR (1,000 ÷ 10 = 100), not drops per minute — it would empty the bag in about 1.7 hours.
• 25 gtt/min — 25 gtt/min would finish the bag in about 6.7 hours.
• 10 gtt/min — 10 gtt/min would take about 16.7 hours.

⭐ Key point: With a 10 gtt/mL set, a quick shortcut is drops/min = mL/hr ÷ 6. Count the drops for a full minute, and recheck the rate regularly because gravity drips change when the patient moves.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q34$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 413, $s$250 mL of normal saline is running at 63 mL/hr. How long will it take to finish?$s$, $s$["15 h", "4 h 58 min", "3 h 58 min", "3 h 28 min"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3 h 58 min
To find how long an infusion will last: TIME = VOLUME ÷ RATE. Here: 250 mL ÷ 63 mL/hr = 3.968 hours. Convert the decimal part to minutes by multiplying by 60: 0.968 × 60 = 58 minutes. So the bag will run for about 3 HOURS 58 MINUTES. Check: 63 mL/hr × 3.97 hr ≈ 250 mL. ✓ Knowing when a bag will finish helps the nurse prepare the next infusion in time, so important therapy is not interrupted.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 15 h — 15 hours is far too long (it would mean a rate of about 17 mL/hr).
• 4 h 58 min — 4 hours 58 minutes is one hour too long.
• 3 h 28 min — 3 hours 28 minutes is 30 minutes too short (it confuses 0.47 with 0.968).

⭐ Key point: A common mistake is reading "3.97 hours" as "3 hours 97 minutes" — always multiply the decimal part by 60.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q37 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 414, $s$Amoxicillin suspension is available as 250 mg in 5 mL. The prescription is 750 mg. What volume should be given?$s$, $s$["30 mL", "3 mL", "1.7 mL", "15 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 15 mL
For oral liquid medicines, use: VOLUME = (DOSE WANTED ÷ DOSE IN STOCK) × VOLUME OF STOCK. Here: (750 mg ÷ 250 mg) × 5 mL = 3 × 5 = 15 mL. Check: each 5 mL contains 250 mg, so 15 mL contains 3 × 250 = 750 mg. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 30 mL — 30 mL would give 1,500 mg — double the dose.
• 3 mL — 3 mL forgets to multiply by the 5 mL stock volume — only 150 mg.
• 1.7 mL — 1.7 mL is a calculation error (only about 85 mg).

⭐ Key point: Measure liquid medicines with an ORAL syringe or a marked medicine cup — never with a household spoon (sizes vary). Oral syringes should be clearly labelled "oral" so they are not confused with IV syringes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q39 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 415, $s$Norepinephrine is mixed as 4 mg in 250 mL D5W and ordered at 8 mcg/min. What infusion rate in mL/hr should be programmed on the pump?$s$, $s$["30 mL/hr", "60 mL/hr", "8 mL/hr", "15 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 30 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 4 mg in 250 mL = 0.016 mg/mL = 16 mcg/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 8 mcg/min × 60 = 480 mcg/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 480 ÷ 16 = 30 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 60 mL/hr — 60 mL/hr gives 16 mcg/min (double).
• 8 mL/hr — 8 mL/hr is the dose in mcg/min, not mL/hr.
• 15 mL/hr — 15 mL/hr gives 4 mcg/min (half).

⭐ Key point: Non-weight-based noradrenaline doses (mcg/min) are common in some units; always check which units the prescription uses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q41$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 416, $s$Phenobarbital injection is available as 200 mg in 1 mL. The prescription is 150 mg. What volume should be given?$s$, $s$["1.5 mL", "0.8 mL", "1.3 mL", "0.75 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 0.75 mL
VOLUME = (DOSE WANTED ÷ DOSE IN STOCK) × VOLUME. Here: (150 mg ÷ 200 mg) × 1 mL = 0.75 × 1 = 0.75 mL. Check: 200 mg is in 1 mL, so 100 mg is in 0.5 mL and 50 mg is in 0.25 mL; 150 mg = 0.5 + 0.25 = 0.75 mL. ✓ Since the dose wanted is LESS than the stock strength, the volume must be LESS than 1 mL — a quick "common sense" check.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1.5 mL — 1.5 mL would give 300 mg — double the dose.
• 0.8 mL — 0.8 mL would give 160 mg — slightly too much.
• 1.3 mL — 1.3 mL is stock ÷ wanted (200 ÷ 150) — the formula upside down.

⭐ Key point: Use a 1 mL syringe for small volumes. Phenobarbital can cause sedation, respiratory depression and hypotension — monitor breathing, SpO2 and BP, especially if benzodiazepines have also been given.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q42 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 417, $s$1,000 mL of 5% dextrose is prescribed to run over 12 hours using a giving set with a drop factor of 15 drops/mL. What is the correct drip rate?$s$, $s$["42 drops/min", "21 drops/min", "28 drops/min", "83 drops/min"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 21 drops/min
DROPS PER MINUTE = (VOLUME × DROP FACTOR) ÷ TIME IN MINUTES. Here: (1,000 mL × 15 drops/mL) ÷ (12 hours × 60) = 15,000 ÷ 720 = 20.8, which rounds to 21 drops/min. Check: 21 drops/min ÷ 15 = 1.4 mL/min × 720 min ≈ 1,008 mL. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 42 drops/min — 42 drops/min is double — the bag would finish in about 6 hours.
• 28 drops/min — 28 drops/min would finish in about 9 hours.
• 83 drops/min — 83 drops/min is the rate in mL/HOUR (1,000 ÷ 12 = 83.3), not drops per minute.

⭐ Key point: With a 15 drops/mL set, drops/min = mL/hr ÷ 4 (83 ÷ 4 ≈ 21). Always read the drop factor on the giving-set packet.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q46 · concept asked in 8 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 418, $s$Glyceryl trinitrate (GTN) 50 mg in 500 mL is ordered at 15 mcg/min. What pump rate (mL/hr) will deliver this dose?$s$, $s$["4.5 mL/hr", "9 mL/hr", "18 mL/hr", "0.1 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 9 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 50 mg in 500 mL = 0.1 mg/mL = 100 micrograms/mL. STEP 2 — Dose per hour: 15 micrograms/min × 60 = 900 micrograms/hr. STEP 3 — Rate: 900 ÷ 100 = 9 mL/hr. Check: 9 mL/hr × 100 mcg/mL = 900 mcg/hr ÷ 60 = 15 mcg/min. ✓ Compare with Q30: 10 mcg/min = 6 mL/hr, so 15 mcg/min = 9 mL/hr (one and a half times).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 4.5 mL/hr — 4.5 mL/hr gives only 7.5 mcg/min.
• 18 mL/hr — 18 mL/hr gives 30 mcg/min — double.
• 0.1 mL/hr — 0.1 mL/hr is the concentration in mg/mL, not a pump rate.

⭐ Key point: Shortcut for a 100 mcg/mL solution: mL/hr = mcg/min × 0.6. Monitor BP closely during GTN titration.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q50 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 419, $s$Using the 4-2-1 rule, what is the hourly maintenance fluid rate for a child weighing 25 kg?$s$, $s$["100 mL/hr", "50 mL/hr", "75 mL/hr", "65 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 65 mL/hr
4-2-1 RULE for a 25 kg child: first 10 kg = 4 × 10 = 40 mL/hr; next 10 kg = 2 × 10 = 20 mL/hr; remaining 5 kg = 1 × 5 = 5 mL/hr. Total: 40 + 20 + 5 = 65 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100 mL/hr — 100 mL/hr is 4 mL/kg for all 25 kg.
• 50 mL/hr — 50 mL/hr is 2 mL/kg for all 25 kg.
• 75 mL/hr — 75 mL/hr is incorrect.

⭐ Key point: For any child over 20 kg: 60 mL/hr + 1 mL/hr per kg above 20.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q53 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 420, $s$A patient weighs 154 lb. A drug is prescribed at 10 mg/kg. What dose should be given?$s$, $s$["318.2 mg", "1,400 mg", "700 mg", "1,540 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 700 mg
Drug doses are calculated in KILOGRAMS. STEP 1 — Convert pounds to kilograms: 1 kg = 2.2 lb, so 154 lb ÷ 2.2 = 70 kg. STEP 2 — Dose: 10 mg/kg × 70 kg = 700 mg. Check: 70 × 2.2 = 154 lb. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 318.2 mg — 318.2 mg results from dividing by 2.2 TWICE (700 ÷ 2.2) — a double conversion error, giving less than half the dose.
• 1,400 mg — 1,400 mg is 20 mg/kg — double the dose.
• 1,540 mg — 1,540 mg uses the weight in POUNDS as if it were kg (10 × 154) — more than double the dose.

⭐ Key point: Always record weight in kg on the drug chart. Confusing pounds and kg is a well-known cause of serious overdoses, especially in children.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q54 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 421, $s$Using the 4-2-1 rule, what is the hourly maintenance fluid rate for a child weighing 22 kg?$s$, $s$["44 mL/hr", "72 mL/hr", "62 mL/hr", "88 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 62 mL/hr
The 4-2-1 RULE gives HOURLY maintenance fluids: 4 mL/kg/hr for the FIRST 10 kg; 2 mL/kg/hr for the NEXT 10 kg (11–20 kg); 1 mL/kg/hr for each kg ABOVE 20 kg. For 22 kg: first 10 kg = 4 × 10 = 40 mL/hr; next 10 kg = 2 × 10 = 20 mL/hr; last 2 kg = 1 × 2 = 2 mL/hr. Total = 40 + 20 + 2 = 62 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 44 mL/hr — 44 mL/hr is 2 mL/kg for all 22 kg.
• 72 mL/hr — 72 mL/hr is a calculation error.
• 88 mL/hr — 88 mL/hr is 4 mL/kg for all 22 kg — too much fluid.

⭐ Key point: For any child above 20 kg, a shortcut is: 60 mL/hr + 1 mL/hr for each kg above 20 (here 60 + 2 = 62). Critically ill children often need fluid restriction (e.g. two-thirds of maintenance) because of increased ADH.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q56 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 422, $s$Ibuprofen is prescribed at 10 mg/kg per dose (maximum single dose 400 mg) for a patient weighing 70 kg. What single dose should be given?$s$, $s$["700 mg", "233.3 mg", "400 mg", "770 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 400 mg
STEP 1 — Weight-based dose: 10 mg/kg × 70 kg = 700 mg. STEP 2 — Check against the MAXIMUM SINGLE DOSE: 400 mg. Because 700 mg is MORE than the maximum, the dose must be CAPPED at 400 mg. Giving 700 mg would exceed the safe limit and increase the risk of side effects (stomach bleeding, kidney injury, worsening asthma).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 700 mg — 700 mg exceeds the maximum single dose.
• 233.3 mg — 233.3 mg is a calculation error.
• 770 mg — 770 mg exceeds the maximum single dose.

⭐ Key point: Always check the maximum dose — weight-based calculations in adults or large children can exceed safe limits. Ibuprofen should be avoided in kidney injury, active GI bleeding, and late pregnancy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q57 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 423, $s$Magnesium sulfate is available as 40 g in 1000 mL. A loading dose of 4 g is ordered to be infused over 20 minutes. How many millilitres are needed, and at what pump rate (mL/hr)?$s$, $s$["100 mL needed, at a rate of 300 mL/hr", "40 mL needed, at a rate of 120 mL/hr", "400 mL needed, at a rate of 1200 mL/hr", "100 mL needed, at a rate of 200 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 100 mL needed, at a rate of 300 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Concentration: 40 g in 1,000 mL = 0.04 g/mL (40 mg/mL). STEP 2 — Volume for 4 g: 4 ÷ 0.04 = 100 mL. STEP 3 — Rate: 100 mL over 20 minutes = 100 ÷ (20/60) = 100 × 3 = 300 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 40 mL needed, at a rate of 120 mL/hr — 40 mL at 120 mL/hr contains only 1.6 g.
• 400 mL needed, at a rate of 1200 mL/hr — 400 mL contains 16 g — a dangerous overdose.
• 100 mL needed, at a rate of 200 mL/hr — 100 mL at 200 mL/hr would take 30 minutes, not 20.

⭐ Key point: Monitor reflexes, breathing rate and urine output during magnesium therapy; have calcium gluconate ready.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q59$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 424, $s$In a mechanically ventilated ICU patient, delivering salbutamol via a metered-dose inhaler with an inline spacer/adapter is generally preferred over a small-volume nebulizer because it:$s$, $s$["Requires disconnecting the ventilator circuit for each dose", "Delivers a substantially higher total dose of medication", "Is equally effective when administered correctly, with less risk of ventilator circuit contamination", "Cannot be used safely in ventilated patients"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Is equally effective when administered correctly, with less risk of ventilator circuit contamination
In ventilated patients, bronchodilators can be given by METERED-DOSE INHALER (MDI) with an IN-LINE SPACER/adapter, or by a small-volume nebuliser. When given correctly (actuated at the start of inspiration, multiple puffs), the MDI is EQUALLY EFFECTIVE. It has advantages: it does NOT require opening the circuit (LESS RISK OF CONTAMINATION and loss of PEEP), it is quicker, and dose delivery is more consistent.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Requires disconnecting the ventilator circuit for each dose — An MDI with an in-line spacer does NOT require disconnecting the circuit.
• Delivers a substantially higher total dose of medication — It does not deliver a substantially higher dose; ventilated patients often need higher numbers of puffs.
• Cannot be used safely in ventilated patients — It CAN be used safely in ventilated patients.

⭐ Key point: Remove HMEs from between the MDI/nebuliser and the patient during delivery (or place the device correctly), as they trap the medication.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q102$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 425, $s$A patient receiving unfractionated heparin for PE has an aPTT of 110 seconds. What is the priority nursing action?$s$, $s$["Continue the infusion unchanged, as this is therapeutic", "Administer protamine sulfate immediately without an order", "Increase the heparin infusion rate", "Hold or slow the infusion per protocol and notify the physician"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hold or slow the infusion per protocol and notify the physician
An aPTT of 110 seconds is well ABOVE the usual therapeutic range for heparin (typically about 1.5–2.5 times control, e.g. 60–90 seconds depending on the lab). This means the patient is OVER-ANTICOAGULATED and at high RISK OF BLEEDING. The nurse should HOLD or SLOW the infusion according to the heparin PROTOCOL, assess for bleeding (wounds, urine, stools, gums, neuro status), and NOTIFY THE PHYSICIAN. Repeat the aPTT as per protocol.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Continue the infusion unchanged, as this is therapeutic — Continuing unchanged is unsafe; 110 seconds is too high.
• Administer protamine sulfate immediately without an order — Protamine sulphate reverses heparin, but it is given for significant bleeding and needs a prescription.
• Increase the heparin infusion rate — Increasing the rate would worsen the risk of bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Always check the lab's therapeutic range and the unit's heparin nomogram, and double-check pump settings.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q156$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 426, $s$A patient presents with poisoning after ingesting rodenticide containing a long-acting anticoagulant (superwarfarin). What is the appropriate treatment if bleeding or a significantly elevated INR develops?$s$, $s$["A single dose of naloxone", "Immediate whole blood transfusion regardless of INR", "Activated charcoal alone, with no other treatment", "Vitamin K1, with prolonged (potentially weeks-long) therapy due to the long half-life of superwarfarins"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vitamin K1, with prolonged (potentially weeks-long) therapy due to the long half-life of superwarfarins
SUPERWARFARINS (e.g. brodifacoum) in rodent poisons are long-acting anticoagulants that block vitamin K recycling, preventing the liver from making clotting factors II, VII, IX and X. They last MUCH LONGER than warfarin (weeks to months). If bleeding occurs or the INR is significantly raised, treat with VITAMIN K1 (phytomenadione), often in HIGH DOSES and for a PROLONGED period (weeks to months), with regular INR monitoring. For active, severe bleeding, give prothrombin complex concentrate or fresh frozen plasma as well.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A single dose of naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids, not anticoagulants.
• Immediate whole blood transfusion regardless of INR — Whole blood regardless of INR is not appropriate.
• Activated charcoal alone, with no other treatment — Activated charcoal alone does not treat the anticoagulant effect.

⭐ Key point: Superwarfarin poisoning may be intentional or accidental (e.g. children); suspect it in unexplained bleeding with a high INR.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q164$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 427, $s$A patient with significant renal impairment receiving morphine is at increased risk of prolonged sedation and respiratory depression due to:$s$, $s$["Reduced protein binding of morphine", "Enhanced renal clearance of morphine", "Increased hepatic first-pass metabolism", "Accumulation of the active metabolite morphine-6-glucuronide"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Accumulation of the active metabolite morphine-6-glucuronide
MORPHINE is broken down in the liver into metabolites, including MORPHINE-6-GLUCURONIDE (M6G), which is ACTIVE (more potent than morphine itself). M6G is cleared by the KIDNEYS. In RENAL IMPAIRMENT, M6G ACCUMULATES, causing PROLONGED SEDATION and RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reduced protein binding of morphine — Reduced protein binding is not the main reason.
• Enhanced renal clearance of morphine — Renal clearance is REDUCED, not enhanced.
• Increased hepatic first-pass metabolism — Increased hepatic first-pass metabolism is not the cause.

⭐ Key point: In renal impairment, use reduced doses or alternative opioids (e.g. fentanyl, alfentanil), and monitor sedation and breathing.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q172$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 428, $s$A patient with sedative-hypnotic (e.g., barbiturate or benzodiazepine) overdose typically presents with which combination of findings?$s$, $s$["Hypertension and tachyarrhythmia", "Excessive salivation and muscle fasciculations", "Agitation, hyperthermia, and dilated pupils", "CNS depression, respiratory depression, and hypotension, often with normal or slightly constricted pupils"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: CNS depression, respiratory depression, and hypotension, often with normal or slightly constricted pupils
SEDATIVE-HYPNOTIC overdose (e.g. barbiturates, benzodiazepines) depresses the brain, causing: CNS DEPRESSION (drowsiness to coma), RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION (slow, shallow breathing), HYPOTENSION, and hypothermia. Pupils are usually NORMAL or slightly CONSTRICTED.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension and tachyarrhythmia — Hypertension and tachyarrhythmia suggest stimulant toxicity.
• Excessive salivation and muscle fasciculations — Excess salivation and fasciculations suggest cholinergic toxicity (organophosphates).
• Agitation, hyperthermia, and dilated pupils — Agitation, hyperthermia and dilated pupils suggest sympathomimetic toxicity.

⭐ Key point: Flumazenil can reverse benzodiazepines, but may cause seizures — use with caution.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q182$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 429, $s$A patient receiving a noradrenaline infusion via a peripheral IV develops swelling, pain, and blanching at the insertion site. The nurse should suspect:$s$, $s$["Normal, expected finding requiring no action", "Extravasation, requiring the infusion to be stopped, the physician notified, and antidote/local treatment considered per protocol", "An allergic reaction requiring more noradrenaline", "Simply that the IV needs a faster flow rate"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Extravasation, requiring the infusion to be stopped, the physician notified, and antidote/local treatment considered per protocol
NORADRENALINE is a powerful vasoconstrictor. If it EXTRAVASATES (leaks into tissue), it causes SWELLING, PAIN, and BLANCHING, and can lead to tissue necrosis. STOP the infusion, notify the physician, and consider the antidote (PHENTOLAMINE infiltration) and local treatment per protocol.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal, expected finding requiring no action — It's not a normal finding.
• An allergic reaction requiring more noradrenaline — It's not an allergic reaction; more noradrenaline would worsen it.
• Simply that the IV needs a faster flow rate — A faster rate would worsen extravasation.

⭐ Key point: Give noradrenaline through a central line where possible.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q193$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 430, $s$A patient with chronic lithium therapy presents with coarse tremor, ataxia, confusion, and hyperreflexia. Serum lithium is significantly elevated. What is the definitive treatment for severe lithium toxicity?$s$, $s$["Flumazenil", "Haemodialysis", "N-acetylcysteine", "Activated charcoal"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Haemodialysis
LITHIUM is used for bipolar disorder and has a NARROW therapeutic range (about 0.4–1.0 mmol/L). Toxicity (often above 1.5–2.0 mmol/L) is often triggered by dehydration, kidney impairment, or drugs that reduce lithium excretion (NSAIDs, ACE inhibitors, thiazide diuretics). Features: coarse TREMOR, ATAXIA, CONFUSION, HYPERREFLEXIA, seizures, and arrhythmias. Lithium is a small, water-soluble ion that is not bound to proteins, so it is very efficiently removed by HAEMODIALYSIS — the DEFINITIVE treatment for severe toxicity (e.g. very high levels, neurological signs, or kidney failure).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines, not lithium.
• N-acetylcysteine — N-acetylcysteine treats paracetamol overdose.
• Activated charcoal — Activated charcoal does NOT bind lithium (or other metals and ions).

⭐ Key point: In milder toxicity, stop lithium and give IV fluids (0.9% saline) to help the kidneys excrete it. Levels may rebound after dialysis, so recheck.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q195$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 431, $s$A nurse writes: "Nurse will teach the client about insulin administration." What is wrong with this outcome statement?$s$, $s$["It describes a nursing intervention/action, not a measurable client outcome", "Nothing; it is a correctly written outcome", "It is too specific", "It lacks a time frame only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It describes a nursing intervention/action, not a measurable client outcome
An OUTCOME (goal) must describe what the CLIENT will achieve, in measurable terms. "NURSE WILL TEACH the client about insulin administration" describes a NURSING INTERVENTION (the nurse's action), NOT a CLIENT OUTCOME. A correct outcome would be: "The client will correctly demonstrate insulin self-injection by the day of discharge."

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nothing; it is a correctly written outcome — It is not correct as an outcome statement.
• It is too specific — It is not too specific.
• It lacks a time frame only — The main problem is not just the missing time frame; it is not a client outcome at all.

⭐ Key point: Outcomes start with "The client will…", and describe observable behaviour.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q155$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 432, $s$A nurse administers a prescribed antibiotic according to the physician's order. This is an example of which type of nursing intervention?$s$, $s$["Unauthorized intervention", "Interdependent (collaborative) intervention", "Dependent intervention", "Independent intervention"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dependent intervention
A DEPENDENT nursing intervention is carried out based on a PHYSICIAN'S (or other prescriber's) ORDER. Administering a PRESCRIBED ANTIBIOTIC is dependent, because the nurse needs a prescription. However, the nurse still uses judgement (e.g. checking allergies, dose, and observing for side effects).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Unauthorized intervention — Unauthorised interventions are actions outside the nurse's scope.
• Interdependent (collaborative) intervention — Interdependent (collaborative) interventions are done in collaboration with other team members (e.g. physiotherapists, dieticians).
• Independent intervention — Independent interventions are initiated by the nurse without an order (e.g. repositioning, mouth care, teaching).

⭐ Key point: Types of nursing interventions: independent, dependent and interdependent (collaborative).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q157$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 433, $s$Which practice is a key principle of antimicrobial stewardship?$s$, $s$["De-escalating to a narrower-spectrum antibiotic once culture results are known", "Using broad-spectrum antibiotics for as long as possible", "Avoiding cultures before starting antibiotics", "Prolonging every course of antibiotics"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: De-escalating to a narrower-spectrum antibiotic once culture results are known
ANTIMICROBIAL STEWARDSHIP is a set of actions to use antibiotics wisely, to improve patient outcomes and reduce resistance. A key principle is DE-ESCALATION: start broad-spectrum antibiotics in serious infection (after taking cultures), then, when CULTURE and SENSITIVITY results are known (usually 48–72 hours), SWITCH to a NARROWER-SPECTRUM antibiotic that targets the identified organism, or stop antibiotics if no infection is found. Other principles: correct dose, route and duration; IV-to-oral switch; and daily review.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Using broad-spectrum antibiotics for as long as possible — Prolonged broad-spectrum use promotes resistance and C. difficile infection.
• Avoiding cultures before starting antibiotics — Cultures should be taken BEFORE starting antibiotics to guide treatment.
• Prolonging every course of antibiotics — Courses should be as short as effective; prolonged courses increase harm.

⭐ Key point: Nurses contribute by ensuring cultures are taken before antibiotics, giving doses on time, and prompting daily antibiotic review.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q193$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 434, $s$Which sedative causes the LEAST respiratory depression?$s$, $s$["Fentanyl", "Midazolam", "Dexmedetomidine", "Propofol"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dexmedetomidine
DEXMEDETOMIDINE is an alpha-2 agonist sedative. It causes "cooperative sedation": patients are calm but ROUSABLE, and it causes MINIMAL RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION, so it can be used in non-intubated patients and during weaning and extubation. It may also reduce delirium compared with benzodiazepines.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fentanyl — Fentanyl (an opioid) causes significant respiratory depression.
• Midazolam — Midazolam (a benzodiazepine) causes respiratory depression and increases delirium.
• Propofol — Propofol causes respiratory depression and apnoea at higher doses.

⭐ Key point: Side effects of dexmedetomidine: bradycardia and hypotension. Monitor heart rate and BP closely.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q196$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 435, $s$Which group of drugs should generally be avoided when managing ICU delirium?$s$, $s$["Dexmedetomidine", "Benzodiazepines", "Antipsychotics such as haloperidol", "Atypical antipsychotics such as quetiapine"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Benzodiazepines
BENZODIAZEPINES (e.g. midazolam, lorazepam) are associated with an INCREASED RISK of DELIRIUM in ICU patients and should generally be AVOIDED for sedation or delirium management. The exception is delirium due to ALCOHOL or benzodiazepine WITHDRAWAL, where benzodiazepines are the treatment of choice.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dexmedetomidine — Dexmedetomidine may reduce delirium and can help agitated patients.
• Antipsychotics such as haloperidol — Haloperidol may be used short-term for distressing agitation or hallucinations (though it does not shorten delirium).
• Atypical antipsychotics such as quetiapine — Quetiapine (an atypical antipsychotic) may be used for agitation in delirium.

⭐ Key point: Prevent delirium with the ABCDEF bundle: Assess pain, Both SAT and SBT, Choice of sedation, Delirium monitoring (CAM-ICU), Early mobility, Family engagement — plus sleep hygiene and reorientation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q197$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 436, $s$A syringe pump running noradrenaline is mounted high above the patient's heart, and the syringe plunger is not secured in the plunger clamp. The main danger is:$s$, $s$["Siphoning: free flow of drug by gravity, giving a bolus", "Repeated occlusion alarms with no drug being delivered", "Reverse flow of blood up the line into the syringe", "Air embolism from air drawn back into the syringe"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Siphoning: free flow of drug by gravity, giving a bolus
SIPHONING occurs when a syringe pump is mounted HIGH above the patient and the syringe PLUNGER is NOT secured in the plunger clamp. Gravity can pull the drug out of the syringe (free flow), giving an uncontrolled BOLUS of noradrenaline. A bolus of a powerful vasoconstrictor can cause a sudden dangerous rise in BP, arrhythmias and stroke.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Repeated occlusion alarms with no drug being delivered — Repeated occlusion alarms happen when the line is blocked, not when the plunger is loose.
• Reverse flow of blood up the line into the syringe — Blood backflow can happen if the pump is placed BELOW the patient, not above.
• Air embolism from air drawn back into the syringe — Air embolism is not the main danger here.

⭐ Key point: Always secure the plunger and barrel in the pump clamps, use anti-siphon valves, and mount pumps at or near the level of the patient's heart.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q21$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 437, $s$Which is the safest way to change an almost-empty syringe of noradrenaline for a patient who is pressor-dependent?$s$, $s$["Stop the infusion, flush the line with saline, then connect the new syringe", "Run the new syringe through the lumen used for intermittent drug boluses", "Wait for the 'syringe empty' alarm, then draw up and start the next syringe", "Prime a new syringe in a second pump early and switch over, watching the BP"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Prime a new syringe in a second pump early and switch over, watching the BP
A patient who is PRESSOR-DEPENDENT can drop their BP dangerously if the noradrenaline stops even for a short time (noradrenaline has a half-life of about 2 minutes). The safest method is to PRIME A NEW SYRINGE in a SECOND PUMP EARLY (before the first syringe runs out) and SWITCH OVER (e.g. "double pumping" — start the new pump and stop the old one as the BP is watched), WATCHING THE BP closely.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stop the infusion, flush the line with saline, then connect the new syringe — Stopping the infusion and flushing the line would cause a gap in noradrenaline and give a bolus of the drug left in the line.
• Run the new syringe through the lumen used for intermittent drug boluses — Running noradrenaline through a lumen used for boluses risks accidental boluses of noradrenaline when other drugs are given.
• Wait for the 'syringe empty' alarm, then draw up and start the next syringe — Waiting for the "syringe empty" alarm leaves a gap while the new syringe is prepared, causing hypotension.

⭐ Key point: Prepare the next syringe in advance, label clearly, and use a dedicated lumen for vasoactive drugs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q23$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 438, $s$Noradrenaline 4 mg is diluted to 50 mL. What rate (mL/h) delivers 0.1 microgram/kg/min to a 70 kg patient?$s$, $s$["5.25 mL/h", "8.4 mL/h", "0.53 mL/h", "52.5 mL/h"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 5.25 mL/h
Step 1: Concentration = 4 mg in 50 mL = 4000 micrograms ÷ 50 mL = 80 micrograms/mL. Step 2: Dose needed per minute = 0.1 microgram/kg/min × 70 kg = 7 micrograms/min. Step 3: Dose per hour = 7 × 60 = 420 micrograms/hour. Step 4: Rate = 420 ÷ 80 = 5.25 mL/hour.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 8.4 mL/h — 8.4 mL/h would give about 0.16 microgram/kg/min.
• 0.53 mL/h — 0.53 mL/h is ten times too little (decimal place error).
• 52.5 mL/h — 52.5 mL/h is ten times too much — a dangerous overdose (decimal place error).

⭐ Key point: Formula: Rate (mL/h) = Dose (mcg/kg/min) × Weight (kg) × 60 ÷ Concentration (mcg/mL). Always double-check calculations with another nurse.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q24$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 439, $s$Dopamine 200 mg in 50 mL is running at 6 mL/h in an 80 kg patient. What dose is the patient receiving?$s$, $s$["3 micrograms/kg/min", "50 micrograms/kg/min", "0.5 micrograms/kg/min", "5 micrograms/kg/min"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 5 micrograms/kg/min
Step 1: Concentration = 200 mg in 50 mL = 4 mg/mL = 4000 micrograms/mL. Step 2: Amount per hour = 6 mL/h × 4000 = 24,000 micrograms/hour. Step 3: Per minute = 24,000 ÷ 60 = 400 micrograms/min. Step 4: Per kg = 400 ÷ 80 kg = 5 micrograms/kg/min.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 3 micrograms/kg/min — 3 micrograms/kg/min is too low; the calculation was done incorrectly.
• 50 micrograms/kg/min — 50 micrograms/kg/min is ten times too high (decimal place error).
• 0.5 micrograms/kg/min — 0.5 micrograms/kg/min is ten times too low (decimal place error).

⭐ Key point: Formula: Dose (mcg/kg/min) = Rate (mL/h) × Concentration (mcg/mL) ÷ (Weight × 60).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q25$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 440, $s$One litre of normal saline is to run over 8 hours through a giving set that delivers 20 drops/mL. The drip rate is:$s$, $s$["42 drops/min", "125 drops/min", "21 drops/min", "63 drops/min"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 42 drops/min
Formula: Drops per minute = (Volume in mL × Drop factor) ÷ (Time in minutes). Here: Volume = 1000 mL, Drop factor = 20 drops/mL, Time = 8 hours × 60 = 480 minutes. Calculation: (1000 × 20) ÷ 480 = 20,000 ÷ 480 = 41.7 ≈ 42 drops/min.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 125 drops/min — 125 drops/min is the hourly rate in mL (1000 ÷ 8 = 125 mL/h), not drops per minute.
• 21 drops/min — 21 drops/min would deliver only about half the volume in 8 hours.
• 63 drops/min — 63 drops/min would deliver the litre in about 5.3 hours (too fast).

⭐ Key point: Standard giving sets deliver 20 drops/mL (some 15); paediatric (micro-drip) sets deliver 60 drops/mL. Always check the packaging.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q26$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 441, $s$An elderly patient on digoxin and furosemide has nausea, vomiting, confusion, yellow-green tinted vision, a slow irregular pulse and frequent ventricular ectopics (bigeminy). Potassium is 2.9 mmol/L. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Digoxin toxicity, made worse by hypokalaemia", "Beta-blocker overdose", "Hyperkalaemia from renal failure", "Hypercalcaemia"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Digoxin toxicity, made worse by hypokalaemia
DIGOXIN TOXICITY causes: GI symptoms (nausea, vomiting), CONFUSION, VISUAL DISTURBANCES (yellow-green tinted vision — xanthopsia), and ARRHYTHMIAS (slow irregular pulse, ventricular ectopics, bigeminy, heart block). HYPOKALAEMIA (potassium 2.9 mmol/L — here caused by FUROSEMIDE) MAKES DIGOXIN TOXICITY WORSE, because low potassium allows digoxin to bind more strongly to heart muscle.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Beta-blocker overdose — Beta-blocker overdose causes bradycardia and hypotension, but not yellow-green vision.
• Hyperkalaemia from renal failure — Hyperkalaemia doesn't fit; the potassium here is LOW.
• Hypercalcaemia — Hypercalcaemia can worsen digoxin toxicity, but the main clue here is hypokalaemia and digoxin features.

⭐ Key point: Stop digoxin, correct potassium and magnesium, and give digoxin-specific antibody fragments (Digibind/DigiFab) for life-threatening toxicity.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q59$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 442, $s$A patient is still having a generalised seizure 10 minutes after two adequate IV doses of lorazepam. The recommended next step is:$s$, $s$["A second-line IV drug, e.g. levetiracetam 60 mg/kg", "IV haloperidol to control the associated agitation", "Oral carbamazepine given down a nasogastric tube", "A third IV dose of lorazepam 4 mg straight away"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A second-line IV drug, e.g. levetiracetam 60 mg/kg
STATUS EPILEPTICUS is a seizure lasting more than 5 minutes, or repeated seizures without recovery. First-line treatment is BENZODIAZEPINES (e.g. lorazepam, up to 2 doses). If the seizure continues after 2 adequate doses, give a SECOND-LINE IV antiseizure drug: LEVETIRACETAM (60 mg/kg, max 4.5 g), phenytoin/fosphenytoin, or sodium valproate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV haloperidol to control the associated agitation — Haloperidol can lower the seizure threshold and doesn't stop seizures.
• Oral carbamazepine given down a nasogastric tube — Oral carbamazepine acts too slowly and can't be given safely during a seizure.
• A third IV dose of lorazepam 4 mg straight away — More than 2 doses of benzodiazepines increases the risk of respiratory depression without improving seizure control.

⭐ Key point: If seizures continue after second-line drugs (refractory status), proceed to general anaesthesia (e.g. propofol, midazolam, thiopental) with intubation and EEG monitoring.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q94$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 443, $s$A student has fever, headache, neck stiffness, a purpuric rash that does not blanch under a glass, and BP 85/50 mmHg. The priority action is:$s$, $s$["Do a lumbar puncture first to identify the organism", "Wait for blood culture results to choose the antibiotic", "Do a CT head first, then give antibiotics if it is clear", "Give IV ceftriaxone immediately, before any LP or CT"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Give IV ceftriaxone immediately, before any LP or CT
Fever, headache, neck stiffness, and a NON-BLANCHING PURPURIC RASH with HYPOTENSION strongly suggest MENINGOCOCCAL SEPSIS and MENINGITIS. This can kill within hours. The priority is to give IV CEFTRIAXONE (or benzylpenicillin) IMMEDIATELY — before any lumbar puncture or CT — and start resuscitation (fluids) for shock. Take blood cultures if this doesn't delay antibiotics.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Do a lumbar puncture first to identify the organism — Lumbar puncture is CONTRAINDICATED in shock and coagulopathy, and delays treatment.
• Wait for blood culture results to choose the antibiotic — Waiting for culture results is fatal.
• Do a CT head first, then give antibiotics if it is clear — CT before antibiotics delays treatment.

⭐ Key point: Give close contacts antibiotic prophylaxis (ciprofloxacin or rifampicin) and notify public health.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q95$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 444, $s$Ten days after starting a penicillin and an NSAID, a patient develops fever, a maculopapular rash, rising creatinine, blood eosinophilia and white cells (eosinophils) in the urine, with no casts of red cells. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Acute tubular necrosis", "Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis", "Pre-renal AKI from dehydration", "Acute interstitial nephritis (drug-induced)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute interstitial nephritis (drug-induced)
ACUTE INTERSTITIAL NEPHRITIS (AIN) is inflammation of the kidney tissue, usually an ALLERGIC reaction to DRUGS (e.g. PENICILLINS, NSAIDs, PPIs). It typically occurs 1–3 weeks after starting the drug. Features: FEVER, RASH, rising creatinine, BLOOD EOSINOPHILIA, and WHITE CELLS (including EOSINOPHILS) in the urine.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute tubular necrosis — ATN shows muddy brown casts, without fever, rash and eosinophilia.
• Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis — Post-streptococcal GN causes red cell casts, haematuria and hypertension.
• Pre-renal AKI from dehydration — Pre-renal AKI from dehydration shows no fever, rash or eosinophils.

⭐ Key point: Stop the causative drug. Steroids may help recovery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q115$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 445, $s$A 40-year-old has episodes of pounding headache, sweating and palpitations with BP up to 240/130 mmHg. A tumour is found in the adrenal gland. Before surgery, the correct drug order is:$s$, $s$["Alpha-blocker first, then add a beta-blocker", "Beta-blocker first, then add an alpha-blocker", "An ACE inhibitor alone at the maximum dose", "A loop diuretic to reduce circulating volume"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Alpha-blocker first, then add a beta-blocker
PHAEOCHROMOCYTOMA is a tumour of the adrenal gland that releases ADRENALINE and NORADRENALINE, causing episodes of HEADACHE, SWEATING, PALPITATIONS and severe HYPERTENSION. Before surgery, an ALPHA-BLOCKER (e.g. phenoxybenzamine) must be started FIRST to control BP. A BETA-BLOCKER is then added (to control heart rate). Giving a beta-blocker first leaves alpha receptors unopposed, causing severe vasoconstriction and a HYPERTENSIVE CRISIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Beta-blocker first, then add an alpha-blocker — Beta-blocker first can cause a dangerous rise in BP.
• An ACE inhibitor alone at the maximum dose — An ACE inhibitor alone doesn't block catecholamine effects.
• A loop diuretic to reduce circulating volume — A diuretic would worsen the low blood volume.

⭐ Key point: Remember: "Alpha before Beta".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q127$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 446, $s$On day 7 of a heparin infusion, a patient's platelets fall from 250 to 90 × 10⁹/L and a new DVT develops. The most likely diagnosis and correct action are:$s$, $s$["ITP — give IV immunoglobulin and steroids, continue heparin", "DIC — give platelets and fresh frozen plasma, continue heparin", "HIT — stop all heparin and start a non-heparin anticoagulant", "Dilutional thrombocytopenia — transfuse platelets, continue heparin"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: HIT — stop all heparin and start a non-heparin anticoagulant
HEPARIN-INDUCED THROMBOCYTOPENIA (HIT) is an immune reaction where antibodies form against heparin bound to platelet factor 4. These antibodies ACTIVATE PLATELETS, causing them to clump and form clots. Features: a fall in platelets by more than 50% (here from 250 to 90), typically 5–10 DAYS after starting heparin, with new BLOOD CLOTS (e.g. DVT). Action: STOP ALL HEPARIN immediately (including heparin flushes and LMWH) and START a NON-HEPARIN ANTICOAGULANT (e.g. argatroban, fondaparinux, or a DOAC).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• ITP — give IV immunoglobulin and steroids, continue heparin — ITP is not linked to heparin and doesn't cause clots.
• DIC — give platelets and fresh frozen plasma, continue heparin — DIC causes low platelets with abnormal clotting tests and bleeding; continuing heparin in HIT is dangerous.
• Dilutional thrombocytopenia — transfuse platelets, continue heparin — Dilutional thrombocytopenia follows massive transfusion; platelet transfusion in HIT can worsen clotting.

⭐ Key point: Use the 4Ts score (Thrombocytopenia, Timing, Thrombosis, oTher causes) to assess HIT likelihood. Document heparin allergy in the patient's notes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q148$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 447, $s$A 15 kg, 4-year-old child has severe malaria with convulsions and coma. The correct dose of IV artesunate is:$s$, $s$["60 mg (4 mg/kg)", "45 mg (3 mg/kg)", "36 mg (2.4 mg/kg)", "30 mg (2 mg/kg)"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 45 mg (3 mg/kg)
For SEVERE MALARIA, the dose of IV ARTESUNATE in children weighing LESS THAN 20 KG is 3 mg/kg (higher than the adult dose of 2.4 mg/kg, because children clear the drug faster). For a 15 kg child: 3 × 15 = 45 mg. Given at 0, 12 and 24 hours, then daily until oral treatment can be taken.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 60 mg (4 mg/kg) — 60 mg (4 mg/kg) is too high.
• 36 mg (2.4 mg/kg) — 36 mg (2.4 mg/kg) is the dose for children over 20 kg and adults.
• 30 mg (2 mg/kg) — 30 mg (2 mg/kg) is too low.

⭐ Key point: After at least 24 hours of IV artesunate and when the child can take oral medication, complete treatment with a full course of oral ACT.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q178$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 448, $s$A young man with head injury has been sedated with high-dose propofol (above 5 mg/kg/h) for 4 days. He develops a worsening metabolic acidosis, rising CK and potassium, new bradyarrhythmias, rising triglycerides and cloudy (lipaemic) blood. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Malignant hyperthermia", "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome", "Refeeding syndrome", "Propofol infusion syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Propofol infusion syndrome
PROPOFOL INFUSION SYNDROME (PRIS) is a rare but often FATAL complication, usually with HIGH-DOSE PROPOFOL (above 4–5 mg/kg/hour) for MORE THAN 48 HOURS. It is more common in young patients with head injury, sepsis, or those receiving catecholamines or steroids. Propofol interferes with the mitochondria (cell energy production) and fat metabolism. Features: worsening METABOLIC (lactic) ACIDOSIS, RHABDOMYOLYSIS (rising CK), HYPERKALAEMIA, new BRADYARRHYTHMIAS (which may progress to asystole) or Brugada-like ECG changes, RISING TRIGLYCERIDES and LIPAEMIC (cloudy, milky) blood, kidney failure and heart failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Malignant hyperthermia — Malignant hyperthermia occurs DURING anaesthesia with volatile agents or suxamethonium, with rising CO2 and temperature, not after days of propofol.
• Neuroleptic malignant syndrome — Neuroleptic malignant syndrome follows antipsychotic drugs, with rigidity and fever.
• Refeeding syndrome — Refeeding syndrome causes LOW phosphate, potassium and magnesium after starting feeds, not hyperkalaemia and lipaemia.

⭐ Key point: If PRIS is suspected: STOP propofol immediately and switch to another sedative (e.g. midazolam), give supportive care (correct acidosis, treat arrhythmias, consider pacing, haemofiltration). Prevent it by keeping propofol below 4 mg/kg/h and monitoring triglycerides, CK and lactate during long infusions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q184$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 449, $s$A patient remains in septic shock (MAP 60 mmHg) on noradrenaline 0.4 micrograms/kg/min after adequate fluids. The Surviving Sepsis Campaign (2021) recommends next:$s$, $s$["Adding vasopressin rather than increasing noradrenaline further", "Adding phenylephrine as the second agent", "Switching from noradrenaline to dopamine", "Giving a further 60 mL/kg of fluid regardless of response"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Adding vasopressin rather than increasing noradrenaline further
When a patient remains in SEPTIC SHOCK (MAP below 65) despite adequate fluids and a moderate-to-high dose of NORADRENALINE (0.25–0.5 micrograms/kg/min), the SURVIVING SEPSIS CAMPAIGN (2021) suggests ADDING VASOPRESSIN (up to 0.03 units/min) rather than increasing noradrenaline further. Septic shock often causes a relative deficiency of vasopressin, and vasopressin works through a different receptor (V1), so it can raise BP and reduce the noradrenaline dose needed ("noradrenaline-sparing"). If the BP is still low, adrenaline can be added next.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adding phenylephrine as the second agent — Phenylephrine is not recommended as a second-line agent in septic shock.
• Switching from noradrenaline to dopamine — Dopamine causes more arrhythmias than noradrenaline and is not recommended (except in very selected cases).
• Giving a further 60 mL/kg of fluid regardless of response — Fluids should be given based on assessment of fluid responsiveness (e.g. passive leg raising), not a fixed large volume; excess fluid causes oedema and organ dysfunction.

⭐ Key point: Also consider IV hydrocortisone (200 mg/day) for patients needing ongoing vasopressors, and assess heart function (echo) — add dobutamine if the heart is weak. Always look for and control the source of infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q187$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 450, $s$Which of the following is NOT a recognised adverse effect of long-term amiodarone?$s$, $s$["Hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism", "Hyperkalaemia", "Slate-grey skin discolouration and photosensitivity", "Pulmonary fibrosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hyperkalaemia
AMIODARONE is an antiarrhythmic drug that contains a lot of IODINE and builds up in body tissues (very long half-life — weeks to months). Recognised long-term side effects include: THYROID problems — both HYPOthyroidism and HYPERthyroidism (from the iodine load); SLATE-GREY / BLUE-GREY SKIN discolouration and PHOTOSENSITIVITY (sunburn easily); PULMONARY FIBROSIS (lung scarring — can be fatal); liver toxicity (raised enzymes, hepatitis); CORNEAL DEPOSITS (rarely affect vision); peripheral neuropathy; and bradycardia and QT prolongation. HYPERKALAEMIA is NOT a recognised side effect of amiodarone.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypothyroidism or hyperthyroidism — Hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism are well-known side effects (check thyroid function before and every 6 months).
• Slate-grey skin discolouration and photosensitivity — Skin discolouration and photosensitivity are well-known; advise sunscreen and covering up.
• Pulmonary fibrosis — Pulmonary fibrosis is a serious recognised side effect; watch for new cough and breathlessness.

⭐ Key point: Amiodarone interacts with many drugs — it increases warfarin and digoxin levels (reduce their doses). Monitor thyroid, liver and lung function and the ECG.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q189$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 451, $s$A patient on dabigatran has a life-threatening intracranial bleed. The specific reversal agent is:$s$, $s$["Idarucizumab", "Andexanet alfa", "Vitamin K with prothrombin complex concentrate", "Protamine sulphate"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Idarucizumab
DABIGATRAN is a direct THROMBIN inhibitor (a DOAC — direct oral anticoagulant). IDARUCIZUMAB (Praxbind) is a humanised antibody fragment that BINDS DABIGATRAN very tightly (about 350 times more strongly than dabigatran binds thrombin), neutralising its effect within MINUTES. The dose is 5 g IV (two 2.5 g vials). It is used for life-threatening bleeding (e.g. intracranial bleeding) or urgent surgery in patients on dabigatran.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Andexanet alfa — Andexanet alfa reverses FACTOR Xa INHIBITORS (apixaban, rivaroxaban, edoxaban), not dabigatran.
• Vitamin K with prothrombin complex concentrate — Vitamin K with prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC) reverses WARFARIN (vitamin K antagonist).
• Protamine sulphate — Protamine sulphate reverses HEPARIN (unfractionated heparin, and partially LMWH).

⭐ Key point: Dabigatran can also be removed by haemodialysis (it is mainly excreted by the kidneys). Know each anticoagulant and its reversal agent; check the time of the last dose and kidney function.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q190$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 452, $s$A patient given IV naloxone for a methadone overdose wakes up fully. The main nursing concern over the next few hours is that:$s$, $s$["Once fully awake after naloxone, the patient needs no further observation", "Naloxone itself causes a long-lasting respiratory depression after waking", "Naloxone must never be repeated because further doses cause toxicity", "Naloxone wears off before methadone, so respiratory depression can return"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Naloxone wears off before methadone, so respiratory depression can return
NALOXONE is an opioid antagonist that rapidly reverses opioid effects, but its action lasts only about 30–90 MINUTES. METHADONE is a LONG-ACTING opioid (half-life up to 24–36 hours or more). So after naloxone wears off, the methadone is still in the body and the patient can become drowsy again, with RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION returning ("re-narcotisation"), which can be fatal if the patient is not being watched. The nurse must keep the patient under close observation (respiratory rate, SpO2, conscious level), have more naloxone ready, and often start a NALOXONE INFUSION.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Once fully awake after naloxone, the patient needs no further observation — The patient needs continued observation even when fully awake, because methadone outlasts naloxone.
• Naloxone itself causes a long-lasting respiratory depression after waking — Naloxone does not cause respiratory depression; it reverses it. (It can cause acute withdrawal in dependent patients.)
• Naloxone must never be repeated because further doses cause toxicity — Naloxone can and should be repeated if respiratory depression recurs; it is very safe.

⭐ Key point: Observe patients for at least 6 hours after the last naloxone dose for short-acting opioids, and much longer (often 24 hours or more) for methadone or sustained-release opioids. Patients may try to leave once awake — explain the risk.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q191$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 453, $s$To reduce nephrotoxicity and ototoxicity with once-daily gentamicin, the dosing interval is adjusted mainly according to:$s$, $s$["The urine volume passed in the last hour", "The peak level 1 hour after the dose", "The trough (pre-dose) or nomogram level", "The patient's daily temperature chart"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The trough (pre-dose) or nomogram level
GENTAMICIN (an aminoglycoside antibiotic) can damage the KIDNEYS (nephrotoxicity) and the INNER EAR (ototoxicity — deafness and balance problems). Toxicity is linked to high drug levels remaining in the blood for long periods (the TROUGH level — the lowest level just before the next dose). With ONCE-DAILY dosing, a high peak kills bacteria effectively, and a long dose interval allows the level to fall to near zero, reducing toxicity. The DOSING INTERVAL is adjusted according to the TROUGH (pre-dose) level, or a level taken at a set time after the dose and plotted on a NOMOGRAM (e.g. the Hartford nomogram). If the level is high, the interval is extended (e.g. to 36 or 48 hours).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The urine volume passed in the last hour — Urine volume in the last hour is not a reliable guide to gentamicin dosing; kidney function is assessed by creatinine clearance.
• The peak level 1 hour after the dose — Peak levels relate to the drug's effectiveness against bacteria, not mainly to toxicity in once-daily regimens.
• The patient's daily temperature chart — Temperature does not guide gentamicin dosing.

⭐ Key point: Check baseline kidney function, monitor creatinine and drug levels, avoid other nephrotoxic drugs (e.g. NSAIDs, vancomycin, furosemide), and report hearing loss, tinnitus or dizziness.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q192$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 454, $s$A 70 kg man after a crash has pulse 125, BP 95/70, RR 32, urine 10 mL/hour and is anxious and confused. Using the ATLS classes, his estimated blood loss is:$s$, $s$["Class IV, over 2000 mL (over 40%)", "Class I, under 750 mL (under 15%)", "Class II, 750–1500 mL (15–30%)", "Class III, 1500–2000 mL (30–40%)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Class III, 1500–2000 mL (30–40%)
The ATLS haemorrhage classes estimate blood loss from the vital signs. This patient has: pulse 125 (120–140 = Class III), BP 95/70 (systolic decreased), respiratory rate 32 (30–40), urine 10 mL/hr (5–15 mL/hr), and is anxious and CONFUSED. All of these fit CLASS III — blood loss of 1500–2000 mL (30–40% of blood volume). For a 70 kg man, total blood volume is about 5 L (70 mL/kg).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Class IV, over 2000 mL (over 40%) — Class IV (over 2000 mL) shows heart rate over 140, very low BP, negligible urine output and lethargy/unconsciousness.
• Class I, under 750 mL (under 15%) — Class I (under 750 mL) has normal vital signs.
• Class II, 750–1500 mL (15–30%) — Class II (750–1500 mL) has a heart rate of 100–120, normal systolic BP and urine 20–30 mL/hr.

⭐ Key point: Class III and IV need blood transfusion. Do not rely on BP alone — the body hides blood loss until over 30% is lost.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q21$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 455, $s$The definitive treatment of necrotising fasciitis is:$s$, $s$["IV antibiotics alone for at least two weeks", "Hyperbaric oxygen therapy alone, daily", "Warm compresses, elevation and analgesia", "Urgent radical debridement with IV antibiotics"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Urgent radical debridement with IV antibiotics
NECROTISING FASCIITIS is a rapidly spreading infection that kills the fascia and the tissues around it. The dead tissue has no blood supply, so antibiotics given IV CANNOT REACH the infection there. The bacteria keep multiplying in the dead tissue and release toxins that cause septic shock. The DEFINITIVE treatment is therefore URGENT RADICAL SURGICAL DEBRIDEMENT — cutting away all dead and infected tissue until healthy, bleeding tissue is reached — together with high-dose broad-spectrum IV ANTIBIOTICS (often including clindamycin to switch off toxin production). Patients often return to theatre every 24–48 hours for further debridement. Each hour of delay increases the death rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV antibiotics alone for at least two weeks — Antibiotics alone fail, because they cannot penetrate dead tissue. Without surgery the death rate is very high.
• Hyperbaric oxygen therapy alone, daily — Hyperbaric oxygen may be used as an extra treatment in some centres, but it never replaces surgery.
• Warm compresses, elevation and analgesia — Warm compresses, elevation and painkillers are treatments for simple cellulitis. They would allow necrotising fasciitis to spread and kill.

⭐ Key point: ICU care after debridement: fluid resuscitation, vasopressors for septic shock, pain relief, wound care (often negative-pressure dressings), nutrition, and monitoring for kidney failure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q76$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 456, $s$Neonatal adrenaline dose via umbilical venous catheter is:$s$, $s$["10–30 micrograms/kg (0.1–0.3 mL/kg of 1:10 000)", "100 micrograms/kg (0.1 mL/kg of 1:1000)", "0.5 mg/kg (0.5 mL/kg of 1:1000)", "1 mg (10 mL of 1:10 000) for any weight"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10–30 micrograms/kg (0.1–0.3 mL/kg of 1:10 000)
ADRENALINE (epinephrine) is given in newborn resuscitation when the heart rate stays BELOW 60/min despite 30 seconds of effective ventilation and then 60 seconds of coordinated chest compressions with ventilation. The preferred route is IV through an UMBILICAL VENOUS CATHETER (UVC). The dose is 10–30 MICROGRAMS/kg, which is 0.1–0.3 mL/kg of the 1:10,000 solution (0.1 mg/mL). Example: for a 3 kg baby, give 0.3–0.9 mL of 1:10,000 adrenaline, followed by a small saline flush. It can be repeated every 3–5 minutes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100 micrograms/kg (0.1 mL/kg of 1:1000) — 100 micrograms/kg (0.1 mL/kg of 1:1000 — i.e. 1 mL/kg of 1:10,000 diluted) is the higher ENDOTRACHEAL dose, used only when IV access is not available. Giving it IV would be a dangerous overdose.
• 0.5 mg/kg (0.5 mL/kg of 1:1000) — 0.5 mg/kg is a massive overdose (about 20 times or more the correct IV dose) that could cause severe hypertension, brain bleeding and arrhythmias.
• 1 mg (10 mL of 1:10 000) for any weight — 1 mg is the ADULT cardiac arrest dose. It would be a fatal overdose for a newborn.

⭐ Key point: Always use the 1:10,000 strength in newborns, calculate by weight, and double-check the dose with another person.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q90$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 457, $s$Initial empiric antibiotics for early-onset neonatal sepsis are usually:$s$, $s$["Ceftriaxone plus metronidazole in all cases", "Vancomycin alone", "Benzylpenicillin (or ampicillin) plus gentamicin", "Oral amoxicillin alone"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Benzylpenicillin (or ampicillin) plus gentamicin
For EARLY-ONSET neonatal sepsis, the first-line antibiotics are BENZYLPENICILLIN (or AMPICILLIN) PLUS GENTAMICIN, given IV. This combination covers the main early-onset organisms: Group B Streptococcus (penicillin), Listeria (ampicillin), and E. coli and other gram-negative bacteria (gentamicin). The two drugs also work together (synergy) against some organisms. Antibiotics are adjusted once culture results are available, usually at 36–48 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ceftriaxone plus metronidazole in all cases — Ceftriaxone is generally avoided in newborns because it displaces bilirubin from albumin (risk of kernicterus) and can form calcium precipitates with IV calcium. Metronidazole is for anaerobic infections like NEC, not routine sepsis.
• Vancomycin alone — Vancomycin alone is used for resistant gram-positive infections (e.g. MRSA, Staph. epidermidis in late-onset sepsis); it does not cover E. coli.
• Oral amoxicillin alone — Oral amoxicillin is not reliably absorbed in a sick newborn and is not adequate for sepsis.

⭐ Key point: Gentamicin can damage the kidneys and hearing. Check trough levels and adjust dosing intervals, especially in preterm babies.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q94$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 458, $s$Bacterial meningitis in a child: the priority after suspecting it is:$s$, $s$["CT scan before any antibiotics in all cases", "Lumbar puncture before any treatment, even if shocked", "Oral antibiotics and observation", "Blood cultures, then IV antibiotics without delay"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Blood cultures, then IV antibiotics without delay
Bacterial meningitis can kill within hours. After suspecting it, the priority is to take BLOOD CULTURES (quickly) and then give IV ANTIBIOTICS (e.g. ceftriaxone) WITHOUT DELAY. Antibiotics should not be delayed for a CT scan or lumbar puncture.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CT scan before any antibiotics in all cases — CT is only needed before LP if signs of raised ICP; it should not delay antibiotics.
• Lumbar puncture before any treatment, even if shocked — LP should be delayed if the patient is shocked or has signs of raised ICP.
• Oral antibiotics and observation — Oral antibiotics are inadequate for meningitis.

⭐ Key point: Give antibiotics within 1 hour of arrival.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q119$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 459, $s$In bacterial meningitis, dexamethasone is given before or with the first antibiotic dose to reduce:$s$, $s$["The need for maintenance IV fluids", "The total duration of the antibiotic course", "Fever only, with no effect on outcome", "Hearing loss and neurological complications"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hearing loss and neurological complications
DEXAMETHASONE given before or with the first antibiotic dose REDUCES the inflammatory response in the brain caused by dying bacteria. This decreases HEARING LOSS and other NEUROLOGICAL COMPLICATIONS (especially in Haemophilus influenzae and pneumococcal meningitis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The need for maintenance IV fluids — Steroids don't affect fluid needs.
• The total duration of the antibiotic course — Steroids don't shorten the antibiotic course.
• Fever only, with no effect on outcome — Steroids are given for their effect on outcomes, not just fever.

⭐ Key point: Dexamethasone is not given in neonates or if antibiotics have already been given for several hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q120$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 460, $s$A conscious, competent ICU patient asks the nurse to give a lethal dose of morphine. The nurse should:$s$, $s$["Give the dose, because the patient's autonomy must be respected", "Ask the relatives to decide whether the dose should be given", "Refuse, explore the suffering and get the team to improve symptom control", "Give a smaller dose secretly so that the patient feels comforted"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Refuse, explore the suffering and get the team to improve symptom control
Giving a lethal dose is illegal and unethical. The nurse should REFUSE, but also EXPLORE THE SUFFERING (pain, fear, depression) and work with the team to IMPROVE SYMPTOM CONTROL and support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give the dose, because the patient's autonomy must be respected — Autonomy does not extend to requesting illegal acts.
• Ask the relatives to decide whether the dose should be given — Relatives cannot authorise illegal acts.
• Give a smaller dose secretly so that the patient feels comforted — Secretly giving drugs is deceptive.

⭐ Key point: Requests to die often reflect unrelieved suffering.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q142$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 461, $s$The 'principle of double effect' supports giving high-dose opioids to a dying patient when:$s$, $s$["The intention is to relieve suffering, even if death is hastened", "The ICU bed is urgently needed for another patient", "The intention is to shorten the patient's life", "The family has asked for a quicker death"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The intention is to relieve suffering, even if death is hastened
The DOCTRINE OF DOUBLE EFFECT supports giving high-dose opioids when the INTENTION is to RELIEVE SUFFERING, even if death may be hastened as a side effect.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The ICU bed is urgently needed for another patient — Freeing a bed is not an ethical reason.
• The intention is to shorten the patient's life — Intending to shorten life is euthanasia.
• The family has asked for a quicker death — Family wishes don't justify hastening death.

⭐ Key point: Document the intention and doses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q145$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 462, $s$For an ICU patient with acute RV failure from pulmonary embolism, the nurse anticipates:$s$, $s$["Noradrenaline for coronary perfusion; avoid large fluid boluses", "IV furosemide as the first-line treatment of RV failure", "High PEEP with large tidal volumes to recruit the lungs", "A rapid 2-litre fluid bolus to increase the RV preload"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Noradrenaline for coronary perfusion; avoid large fluid boluses
In acute RV failure from PE, the RV is overloaded and dilated. NORADRENALINE raises systemic BP, which improves blood flow to the RV's own coronary arteries. Large fluid boluses OVERDILATE the RV, pushing the septum into the left ventricle (septal shift) and reducing LV filling and output. Give only small, cautious fluid boluses.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV furosemide as the first-line treatment of RV failure — Furosemide isn't first-line in acute shocked RV failure; it may drop BP.
• High PEEP with large tidal volumes to recruit the lungs — High PEEP and large tidal volumes raise lung pressure and RV afterload.
• A rapid 2-litre fluid bolus to increase the RV preload — A 2-litre bolus would overdistend the RV and worsen shock.

⭐ Key point: Massive PE with shock: thrombolysis (or embolectomy), noradrenaline, oxygen, and avoiding intubation if possible (positive pressure can cause collapse).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q4$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 463, $s$In myasthenic crisis, the ICU nurse should avoid giving:$s$, $s$["Pyridostigmine at the prescribed times as ordered", "IV immunoglobulin over five days by infusion", "Aminoglycosides and magnesium, which worsen weakness", "Oxygen therapy by face mask at 4 L/min"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Aminoglycosides and magnesium, which worsen weakness
In MYASTHENIC CRISIS, avoid drugs that further block the neuromuscular junction and worsen weakness: AMINOGLYCOSIDES (gentamicin), MAGNESIUM, fluoroquinolones, some beta-blockers, and non-depolarising muscle relaxants (use them with great caution).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pyridostigmine at the prescribed times as ordered — Pyridostigmine is the TREATMENT (though it may be paused if intubated).
• IV immunoglobulin over five days by infusion — IVIG is a TREATMENT for myasthenic crisis.
• Oxygen therapy by face mask at 4 L/min — Oxygen therapy is appropriate.

⭐ Key point: Always check drug interactions in myasthenia gravis patients before giving new medicines.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q53$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 464, $s$The most common trigger of toxic epidermal necrolysis is:$s$, $s$["Exposure to cold, damp weather for several days", "A viral upper respiratory infection, with no drug", "Food allergy to peanuts, shellfish or eggs", "Drugs, e.g. sulphonamides, anticonvulsants, nevirapine"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Drugs, e.g. sulphonamides, anticonvulsants, nevirapine
TEN is most often caused by DRUGS: sulphonamides (co-trimoxazole), anticonvulsants (carbamazepine, phenytoin, lamotrigine), NEVIRAPINE (an HIV drug), allopurinol and NSAIDs. In Nigeria, nevirapine and co-trimoxazole are common causes. It usually starts 1–4 weeks after starting the drug.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Exposure to cold, damp weather for several days — Cold, damp weather doesn't cause TEN.
• A viral upper respiratory infection, with no drug — Viral infections rarely cause TEN; drugs are the main cause.
• Food allergy to peanuts, shellfish or eggs — Food allergies cause urticaria or anaphylaxis, not TEN.

⭐ Key point: STOP the suspected drug immediately. Record the allergy clearly and tell the patient never to take it again.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q59$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 465, $s$Obese ICU patients should have drug doses for some agents (e.g. propofol infusion) calculated on:$s$, $s$["Height only", "Age only", "Total body weight in every case", "Ideal or adjusted body weight"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ideal or adjusted body weight
In obese patients, some drugs (such as propofol infusions, muscle relaxants and many antibiotics) should be dosed on IDEAL or ADJUSTED BODY WEIGHT, not total body weight. Dosing on total weight can cause overdose, because fat tissue doesn't take up or need the drug in the same way as lean tissue.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Height only — Height alone isn't used directly.
• Age only — Age alone isn't used.
• Total body weight in every case — Total body weight for every drug can cause overdose.

⭐ Key point: Check with pharmacy. Some drugs (such as suxamethonium, LMWH) are dosed on total body weight.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q77$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 466, $s$A patient with meningitis develops photophobia, neck stiffness, and a non-blanching purpuric rash. The nurse should recognise the rash as a red flag suggesting:$s$, $s$["Possible meningococcal sepsis needing urgent antibiotics", "A simple allergic reaction to the antibiotics given", "A normal viral rash with no clinical urgency", "A rash unrelated to the meningitis diagnosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Possible meningococcal sepsis needing urgent antibiotics
A NON-BLANCHING PURPURIC RASH (spots or blotches that do not fade when you press a glass on them) together with fever, neck stiffness and photophobia strongly suggests MENINGOCOCCAL disease (Neisseria meningitidis). The bacteria release toxins that damage blood vessels and cause bleeding into the skin, and can rapidly cause septic shock (meningococcal septicaemia) and death within hours. This is a medical emergency: give IV antibiotics (ceftriaxone or benzylpenicillin) IMMEDIATELY — even before tests if there is any delay — start resuscitation, and alert the team.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A simple allergic reaction to the antibiotics given — Allergic drug rashes are usually itchy, raised and blanching (they fade on pressure). A non-blanching purpuric rash with meningitis signs must never be dismissed as an allergy.
• A normal viral rash with no clinical urgency — Viral rashes usually blanch. A non-blanching rash with fever is treated as meningococcal disease until proven otherwise — it is very urgent.
• A rash unrelated to the meningitis diagnosis — The rash is directly linked to the meningitis; it is a sign of meningococcal infection in the blood.

⭐ Key point: Do the "glass test": a rash that does not fade under a glass + fever = emergency. Nigeria lies in the African "meningitis belt", so epidemics occur in the dry season (December–June). Close contacts need prophylaxis (e.g. ciprofloxacin or rifampicin), and staff use droplet precautions for the first 24 hours of antibiotics.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q102$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 467, $s$A febrile convulsion lasting more than 5 minutes is treated with:$s$, $s$["Restraining the child firmly during the fit", "Buccal midazolam or rectal diazepam, then IV lorazepam", "Oral paracetamol only once the child wakes", "Cold water immersion until the fit stops"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Buccal midazolam or rectal diazepam, then IV lorazepam
Any seizure lasting MORE THAN 5 MINUTES needs emergency drug treatment, because longer seizures are less likely to stop on their own and can damage the brain (status epilepticus). First-line drug: a BENZODIAZEPINE — BUCCAL MIDAZOLAM or RECTAL DIAZEPAM if there is no IV access, or IV LORAZEPAM if IV access is available. If the seizure continues, a second dose of benzodiazepine is given, then second-line drugs (e.g. phenytoin, levetiracetam or phenobarbital). Also protect the airway, give oxygen, and check the blood glucose.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Restraining the child firmly during the fit — Never restrain a fitting child; it can cause injury (fractures, dislocations) and does not stop the seizure.
• Oral paracetamol only once the child wakes — Paracetamol lowers fever and comfort but does NOT stop a seizure and does not prevent febrile convulsions.
• Cold water immersion until the fit stops — Cold water immersion is dangerous: it can cause shivering, hypothermia and drowning risk. It does not stop the fit.

⭐ Key point: During any fit: time it, keep the child safe (move objects away), place in the recovery position after the fit, nothing in the mouth, give oxygen, and check glucose ("DEFG – Don't Ever Forget Glucose").$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q108$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 468, $s$Eclamptic seizures are treated first with:$s$, $s$["Diazepam 10 mg IV, repeated as needed", "Paracetamol IV and cooling only", "Magnesium sulphate 4 g IV loading dose", "Phenytoin infusion over 30 minutes"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Magnesium sulphate 4 g IV loading dose
MAGNESIUM SULPHATE is the drug of choice to TREAT and PREVENT eclamptic seizures. The loading dose is 4 g IV over 5–15 minutes (Zuspan regimen), followed by a maintenance infusion of 1 g/hour for 24 hours. (The Pritchard regimen also gives 10 g IM.) Large trials (the Collaborative Eclampsia Trial and Magpie Trial) showed magnesium is better than diazepam and phenytoin at preventing further fits and reducing maternal death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diazepam 10 mg IV, repeated as needed — Diazepam is less effective than magnesium in eclampsia and causes more sedation and breathing problems for mother and baby.
• Paracetamol IV and cooling only — Paracetamol and cooling do not stop eclamptic seizures.
• Phenytoin infusion over 30 minutes — Phenytoin is less effective than magnesium in eclampsia.

⭐ Key point: Monitor for magnesium toxicity: loss of knee jerks (first sign), respiratory rate below 12/min, urine output below 30 mL/hour. Antidote: calcium gluconate 1 g IV (10 mL of 10%).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q135$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 469, $s$Culture and sensitivity results are used to:$s$, $s$["Diagnose viral infections such as flu", "Start antibiotics only after seven days", "Avoid giving any antibiotics at all", "Narrow antibiotics to the most effective agent"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Narrow antibiotics to the most effective agent
CULTURE AND SENSITIVITY (C&S) tests grow the bacteria causing an infection and test which antibiotics kill them. Results allow the team to NARROW (de-escalate) treatment from broad-spectrum antibiotics to the MOST EFFECTIVE, narrowest-spectrum agent. This is part of ANTIMICROBIAL STEWARDSHIP, which reduces antibiotic resistance, side effects and C. difficile infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diagnose viral infections such as flu — C&S is used for bacterial (and fungal) infections; viruses are diagnosed by other tests (e.g. PCR).
• Start antibiotics only after seven days — In sepsis, antibiotics are started immediately after cultures are taken, not after 7 days.
• Avoid giving any antibiotics at all — C&S guides the choice of antibiotics; it is not to avoid treatment.

⭐ Key point: Always take cultures BEFORE starting antibiotics (but do not delay antibiotics in sepsis). Review antibiotics daily.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q195$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 470, $s$Torsades de pointes is treated first with:$s$, $s$["IV amiodarone 300 mg", "IV digoxin 500 micrograms", "IV adenosine 6 mg", "IV magnesium sulphate 2 g"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV magnesium sulphate 2 g
IV magnesium sulphate 2 g (8 mmol) over 10 minutes is the first-line treatment for torsades de pointes, even if the magnesium level is normal. Magnesium stabilises the heart muscle cell membrane and stops the abnormal triggered beats. Also stop all QT-prolonging drugs and correct potassium to 4.5–5.0. Pulseless torsades is defibrillated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV amiodarone 300 mg — Amiodarone PROLONGS the QT interval and can make torsades worse.
• IV digoxin 500 micrograms — Digoxin doesn't treat torsades and can cause other arrhythmias.
• IV adenosine 6 mg — Adenosine is for narrow-complex SVT, not polymorphic VT.

⭐ Key point: If torsades keeps recurring, overdrive pacing or isoprenaline (which shortens the QT by raising the heart rate) can be used.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q18$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 471, $s$The first drug for anaphylaxis with stridor and hypotension is:$s$, $s$["Nebulised salbutamol 5 mg via mask", "IV hydrocortisone 200 mg bolus", "IM adrenaline 0.5 mg (1:1000)", "IV chlorphenamine 10 mg slowly"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IM adrenaline 0.5 mg (1:1000)
Adrenaline is the most important drug in anaphylaxis. It narrows the blood vessels (raises BP, reduces swelling), opens the airways (relieves wheeze and stridor) and stops further release of chemicals from mast cells. The dose is 0.5 mg IM (0.5 mL of 1:1000) into the outer thigh, repeated every 5 minutes if needed. IM works fast and is safe.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nebulised salbutamol 5 mg via mask — Nebulised salbutamol helps wheeze but does not treat swelling or shock.
• IV hydrocortisone 200 mg bolus — Hydrocortisone works too slowly (hours) to save life in the first minutes.
• IV chlorphenamine 10 mg slowly — Chlorphenamine only helps itching and hives; it doesn't treat airway swelling or shock.

⭐ Key point: IM adrenaline doses: adult 0.5 mg, child 6–12 years 0.3 mg, child under 6 years 0.15 mg. Also give high-flow oxygen and a fluid bolus.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q32$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 472, $s$Paracetamol overdose is treated with:$s$, $s$["IV flumazenil", "IV N-acetylcysteine according to the nomogram", "Oral activated charcoal only after 24 hours", "IV naloxone"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV N-acetylcysteine according to the nomogram
N-acetylcysteine (NAC) replaces glutathione, which mops up the toxic paracetamol breakdown product (NAPQI) that destroys liver cells. Treatment is guided by the paracetamol blood level taken 4 hours or more after ingestion, plotted on the treatment nomogram. If the level is above the line, NAC is given IV (usually as a 2- or 3-bag regimen). It works best within 8 hours but is still given later.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines, not paracetamol.
• Oral activated charcoal only after 24 hours — Activated charcoal helps only within about 1 hour of ingestion, not after 24 hours.
• IV naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids, not paracetamol.

⭐ Key point: Give NAC immediately without waiting for the level if the time of ingestion is unknown, the overdose was staggered, or the patient presents more than 8 hours after a large overdose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q39$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 473, $s$Organophosphate poisoning is treated with:$s$, $s$["Naloxone and flumazenil given together", "Neostigmine infusion to reverse weakness", "Adrenaline and hydrocortisone IV", "Atropine until the chest is dry, plus pralidoxime"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Atropine until the chest is dry, plus pralidoxime
Organophosphates block the enzyme acetylcholinesterase, so acetylcholine builds up and overstimulates nerves. ATROPINE blocks the muscarinic effects: it dries the "wet" chest (bronchorrhoea), raises a slow heart rate and stops vomiting and diarrhoea. Double the dose every 5 minutes until the chest is dry, the heart rate is above 80 and the systolic BP is above 80. PRALIDOXIME (an oxime) reactivates the enzyme and helps muscle weakness if given early.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone and flumazenil given together — Naloxone and flumazenil reverse opioids and benzodiazepines, not organophosphates.
• Neostigmine infusion to reverse weakness — Neostigmine blocks the same enzyme and would make the poisoning WORSE.
• Adrenaline and hydrocortisone IV — Adrenaline and hydrocortisone treat anaphylaxis, not organophosphate poisoning.

⭐ Key point: Organophosphate signs: SLUDGE (salivation, lacrimation, urination, defecation, GI cramps, emesis), "killer Bs" (bradycardia, bronchorrhoea, bronchospasm), small pupils and twitching. Decontaminate the patient and protect staff with PPE.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q40$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 474, $s$An infusion pump with a drug library ('smart pump') improves safety by:$s$, $s$["Alerting the nurse to doses outside safe limits", "Removing the need to check the prescription", "Calculating the diagnosis from the drug list", "Choosing the correct drug for the patient"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Alerting the nurse to doses outside safe limits
A smart pump has a built-in drug library with safe dose limits for each drug and concentration. If the nurse programmes a dose outside the limits (for example a 10-fold error), the pump ALERTS. A soft limit can be overridden after checking; a hard limit cannot be overridden. This reduces programming errors, a major cause of drug harm in ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Removing the need to check the prescription — The prescription must STILL be checked; the pump does not replace this.
• Calculating the diagnosis from the drug list — Pumps do not make diagnoses.
• Choosing the correct drug for the patient — The pump does not choose the drug; the prescriber and nurse do.

⭐ Key point: Smart pumps only work if the drug library is used and kept updated. Always double-check high-risk infusions with a second nurse.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q54$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 475, $s$A nurse ordered by a doctor to give a clearly wrong dose should:$s$, $s$["Give it because the doctor wrote the order", "Question the order and withhold until clarified", "Give half the dose as a compromise", "Ask the patient to decide what to do"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Question the order and withhold until clarified
A nurse is legally and professionally ACCOUNTABLE for every drug they give, even if a doctor prescribed it. If an order is clearly wrong or unsafe (wrong dose, drug or route), the nurse must NOT give it. Withhold the drug, question the order, contact the prescriber to clarify, and document what was done. If the doctor insists on an unsafe order, escalate to senior staff.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give it because the doctor wrote the order — "The doctor ordered it" is not a legal defence if the nurse gives a clearly wrong dose.
• Give half the dose as a compromise — Giving half the dose is still not correct and could cause harm.
• Ask the patient to decide what to do — The patient isn't responsible for checking prescriptions.

⭐ Key point: Before giving any drug, check the rights: right patient, right drug, right dose, right route, right time, right documentation, and right reason.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q160$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 476, $s$Antimicrobial stewardship in ICU aims to:$s$, $s$["Broad-spectrum antibiotics given to every ICU patient", "The right antibiotic, dose and duration, limiting resistance", "Avoiding all antibiotics, even in proven sepsis", "Giving antibiotics for viral infections to be safe"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The right antibiotic, dose and duration, limiting resistance
Antimicrobial stewardship means using antibiotics wisely: the RIGHT drug, the RIGHT dose, the RIGHT route and the RIGHT DURATION, and stopping them when not needed. It includes taking cultures before antibiotics, reviewing at 48–72 hours, DE-ESCALATING to a narrower drug once results are known, and short courses. This limits resistance and C. difficile, and saves money.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Broad-spectrum antibiotics given to every ICU patient — Giving broad-spectrum antibiotics to everyone causes resistance.
• Avoiding all antibiotics, even in proven sepsis — Avoiding antibiotics in proven sepsis would cost lives.
• Giving antibiotics for viral infections to be safe — Antibiotics don't work on viruses.

⭐ Key point: "Start smart, then focus": prompt treatment in sepsis, then review and narrow down.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q19$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 477, $s$Nephrotoxic drugs to avoid in AKI include:$s$, $s$["Insulin given by continuous infusion", "Aminoglycosides, NSAIDs, IV contrast", "Oxygen given by face mask or cannula", "Paracetamol in normal therapeutic doses"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Aminoglycosides, NSAIDs, IV contrast
In AKI, avoid drugs that damage the kidneys further: AMINOGLYCOSIDES (gentamicin, amikacin, which are toxic to the tubules), NSAIDs (reduce kidney blood flow), IV CONTRAST (tubular injury and vasoconstriction), and also vancomycin (monitor levels), ACE inhibitors and ARBs in dehydration, and amphotericin. Doses of drugs cleared by the kidneys must be adjusted.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Insulin given by continuous infusion — Insulin isn't nephrotoxic (though doses may need reducing in kidney failure).
• Oxygen given by face mask or cannula — Oxygen isn't nephrotoxic.
• Paracetamol in normal therapeutic doses — Paracetamol in normal doses is safe for the kidneys.

⭐ Key point: Review all drugs daily in AKI: stop nephrotoxins and adjust the doses of renally cleared drugs (such as antibiotics, LMWH, opioids).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q100$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 478, $s$Percussion as an airway clearance technique is contraindicated in a patient with:$s$, $s$["Cystic fibrosis", "Platelet count <20,000", "Post-operative atelectasis", "Retained secretions"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Platelet count <20,000
Percussion means rhythmically clapping the chest with cupped hands to loosen secretions. In a patient with a very LOW PLATELET count (below 20,000/mm³, or 20 × 10⁹/L), the repeated impacts can cause bruising, bleeding into the chest wall or lungs, or haemoptysis. It is therefore contraindicated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cystic fibrosis — Cystic fibrosis is an INDICATION for chest physiotherapy (thick secretions).
• Post-operative atelectasis — Post-operative atelectasis is an INDICATION.
• Retained secretions — Retained secretions are an INDICATION.

⭐ Key point: Other contraindications to percussion: rib fractures, severe osteoporosis, chest wall wounds or burns, pneumothorax (undrained), haemoptysis and unstable BP.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q103$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 479, $s$Transfusion-associated circulatory overload (TACO) is treated with:$s$, $s$["Give more blood quickly to finish", "Stop, sit up, give oxygen and diuretics", "Start broad-spectrum antibiotics", "Lie the patient completely flat"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stop, sit up, give oxygen and diuretics
TACO (transfusion-associated circulatory overload) happens when blood is given too FAST or in too large a volume for the patient's heart to cope, especially in the elderly, heart failure or kidney failure. It causes breathlessness, high BP, raised JVP and pulmonary oedema. STOP or slow the transfusion, SIT the patient UP, give OXYGEN and DIURETICS (furosemide).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give more blood quickly to finish — More blood quickly would make the overload worse.
• Start broad-spectrum antibiotics — Antibiotics don't treat fluid overload.
• Lie the patient completely flat — Lying flat makes breathing harder in pulmonary oedema.

⭐ Key point: Prevent TACO: transfuse one unit at a time, slowly (over 3–4 hours) in at-risk patients, sometimes with furosemide between units.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q106$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 480, $s$Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) typically occurs:$s$, $s$["Only after switching to oral warfarin", "Within minutes of the first heparin dose", "Only with LMWH given to young children", "5–10 days after starting heparin, with thrombosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 5–10 days after starting heparin, with thrombosis
Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia (HIT) is an immune reaction in which antibodies form against heparin bound to platelet factor 4. It usually starts 5–10 DAYS after starting heparin (sooner if exposed in the last 100 days). Platelets fall by more than 50%, and paradoxically the patient develops new CLOTS (DVT, PE, arterial thrombosis), not bleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only after switching to oral warfarin — Switching to warfarin isn't the trigger.
• Within minutes of the first heparin dose — It doesn't occur within minutes of the first dose (except in re-exposure).
• Only with LMWH given to young children — It can occur with unfractionated heparin and LMWH in all ages.

⭐ Key point: In HIT, STOP ALL heparin (including flushes) and start a non-heparin anticoagulant (argatroban, fondaparinux). Don't give platelets.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q110$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 481, $s$Blood cultures should be taken:$s$, $s$["Before antibiotics, from two sites, aseptically", "Without skin preparation, to save time", "After antibiotics, from a single site only", "From the arterial line only, after flushing"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Before antibiotics, from two sites, aseptically
Blood cultures should be taken BEFORE antibiotics are given (antibiotics can stop bacteria growing in the bottles), from TWO separate SITES (to tell a true infection from skin contamination), using strict ASEPTIC technique (clean the skin with 2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol and let it dry). Take at least 10 mL per bottle in adults.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Without skin preparation, to save time — Skipping skin preparation causes contamination with skin bacteria.
• After antibiotics, from a single site only — Cultures after antibiotics may give false negatives.
• From the arterial line only, after flushing — Taking cultures only from an arterial line after flushing increases contamination.

⭐ Key point: In sepsis, take cultures quickly, but never delay antibiotics for more than about 45 minutes to get them.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q115$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 482, $s$A drug error audit in ICU is used to:$s$, $s$["Identify and punish the nurses", "Increase the number of errors", "Reduce the reporting of errors", "Identify patterns and improve systems"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Identify patterns and improve systems
A drug error audit reviews medication errors and near misses to IDENTIFY PATTERNS (common drugs, times, steps where errors happen) and IMPROVE SYSTEMS: better labelling, double-checking, smart pumps, training, safer storage. The aim is learning, not blame.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Identify and punish the nurses — Punishing individuals discourages reporting and doesn't fix system problems.
• Increase the number of errors — The aim is to reduce errors, not increase them.
• Reduce the reporting of errors — The aim is to ENCOURAGE reporting.

⭐ Key point: A "just culture" encourages honest reporting while still addressing reckless behaviour.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q172$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 483, $s$A child with a severe asthma attack not responding to salbutamol may need:$s$, $s$["IV magnesium sulphate and oxygen", "Oral antibiotics and nothing else", "Strict fluid restriction for 24 hours", "Oral sedation and discharge home"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV magnesium sulphate and oxygen
In severe acute asthma that doesn't respond to salbutamol (and ipratropium) nebulisers, IV MAGNESIUM SULPHATE relaxes airway smooth muscle and helps bronchodilation. Give OXYGEN to keep SpO₂ 94–98%. Steroids are also given. If the child still doesn't improve, consider IV salbutamol or aminophylline and ventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral antibiotics and nothing else — Antibiotics aren't routine in asthma (most attacks are viral).
• Strict fluid restriction for 24 hours — Fluid restriction isn't a treatment.
• Oral sedation and discharge home — Sedation is dangerous in asthma (it reduces breathing), and discharge would be unsafe.

⭐ Key point: Signs of life-threatening asthma: silent chest, exhaustion, cyanosis, SpO₂ below 92%, confusion, a normal or rising PaCO₂.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q195$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 484, $s$A child with HIV and severe pneumonia should receive:$s$, $s$["Oral paracetamol only, given at home", "Oral fluids only, with close observation", "Oxygen, antibiotics and high-dose cotrimoxazole", "Steroids alone, without any antibiotics"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Oxygen, antibiotics and high-dose cotrimoxazole
In a child with HIV and severe pneumonia, PNEUMOCYSTIS pneumonia (PCP) must be considered, as well as bacterial pneumonia. Treatment includes OXYGEN, ANTIBIOTICS for bacterial pneumonia (such as ampicillin and gentamicin, or ceftriaxone), and HIGH-DOSE COTRIMOXAZOLE for PCP. Steroids (prednisolone) are added for moderate to severe PCP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral paracetamol only, given at home — Paracetamol alone doesn't treat severe pneumonia.
• Oral fluids only, with close observation — Oral fluids alone don't treat pneumonia.
• Steroids alone, without any antibiotics — Steroids alone without antibiotics would be dangerous.

⭐ Key point: HIV-exposed infants should receive cotrimoxazole prophylaxis to prevent PCP.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q199$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 485, $s$The maintenance infusion rate of magnesium sulphate after the loading dose is:$s$, $s$["1–2g/hour", "0.1g/hour", "10g/hour", "No maintenance is needed"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1–2g/hour
In severe pre-eclampsia and eclampsia, magnesium sulphate is given as a loading dose (4 g IV over 5–15 minutes) and then a MAINTENANCE infusion of 1–2 g per HOUR (usually 1 g/hour) for 24 hours after delivery or the last fit. This keeps the blood magnesium in the therapeutic range (2–4 mmol/L) to prevent further seizures.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.1g/hour — 0.1 g/hour is far too little to keep a therapeutic level.
• 10g/hour — 10 g/hour would cause dangerous toxicity (respiratory arrest).
• No maintenance is needed — Maintenance IS needed; magnesium falls quickly after the loading dose.

⭐ Key point: Monitor hourly on magnesium: knee reflexes, respiratory rate above 12, urine output above 30 mL/hour, and SpO₂. Antidote: calcium gluconate 10%, 10 mL IV.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q1$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 486, $s$Insulin is infusing via a standard concentration of 1 unit/mL (100 units in 100 mL NS), and the order is for 6 units/hr. What rate should the pump be set to, in mL/hr?$s$, $s$["16.7 mL/hr", "6 mL/hr", "0.6 mL/hr", "60 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 6 mL/hr
At a concentration of 1 unit/mL (100 units in 100 mL), each mL contains 1 unit. So the rate in mL/hour EQUALS the dose in units/hour. For 6 units/hour, set the pump to 6 mL/hour.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 16.7 mL/hr — 16.7 mL/hour comes from a calculation error (100 ÷ 6).
• 0.6 mL/hr — 0.6 mL/hour is ten times too little.
• 60 mL/hr — 60 mL/hour is ten times too much.

⭐ Key point: Formula: rate (mL/hour) = dose (units/hour) ÷ concentration (units/mL). Always double-check insulin infusions with a second nurse.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q83$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 487, $s$The Surviving Sepsis hour-1 bundle includes:$s$, $s$["IV steroids for all patients before blood cultures", "A CT scan before any fluids or antibiotics are given", "Lactate, blood cultures before antibiotics, then broad-spectrum antibiotics", "Antibiotics only after the culture results come back"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lactate, blood cultures before antibiotics, then broad-spectrum antibiotics
The Surviving Sepsis "Hour-1 bundle" includes: 1) measure LACTATE, 2) take BLOOD CULTURES BEFORE ANTIBIOTICS, 3) give BROAD-SPECTRUM ANTIBIOTICS, 4) give 30 mL/kg crystalloid for low BP or lactate of 4 or more, 5) start vasopressors if MAP stays below 65. All should start within the first hour.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV steroids for all patients before blood cultures — Steroids are not given to all sepsis patients before cultures.
• A CT scan before any fluids or antibiotics are given — A CT scan before fluids and antibiotics delays life-saving treatment.
• Antibiotics only after the culture results come back — Waiting for culture results (48–72 hours) delays antibiotics dangerously.

⭐ Key point: Every hour's delay in antibiotics for septic shock increases death. Never delay antibiotics for tests.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q117$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 488, $s$Corticosteroids are given to a mother whose delivery is planned before 37 weeks to:$s$, $s$["Help the baby's lungs mature", "Prepare the cervix for delivery", "Prevent maternal fits", "Keep the mother calm"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Help the baby's lungs mature
Antenatal corticosteroids (dexamethasone or betamethasone, two doses 12–24 hours apart) are given to mothers likely to deliver preterm (usually before 34–35 weeks, and in some cases up to 37 weeks). They cross the placenta and speed up the maturation of the baby's LUNGS (surfactant production), reducing respiratory distress syndrome, brain bleeds and deaths.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prepare the cervix for delivery — They don't prepare the cervix (prostaglandins do).
• Prevent maternal fits — Magnesium sulphate prevents fits.
• Keep the mother calm — Steroids don't calm the mother.

⭐ Key point: Steroids work best when given 24 hours to 7 days before delivery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q127$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 489, $s$The first clinical sign of magnesium toxicity is usually:$s$, $s$["Loss of patellar (knee) reflexes", "Excess urine output", "Hypertension and tachycardia", "Dilated reactive pupils"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Loss of patellar (knee) reflexes
As magnesium levels rise, signs of toxicity appear in order: LOSS of the PATELLAR (knee) REFLEX is usually first (at about 4–5 mmol/L), then respiratory depression (about 6–7.5 mmol/L), then cardiac arrest (above 12 mmol/L). Checking knee reflexes hourly detects toxicity early.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Excess urine output — Toxicity reduces urine output; magnesium itself doesn't cause excess urine.
• Hypertension and tachycardia — Toxicity causes LOW BP and SLOW heart rate.
• Dilated reactive pupils — Pupils aren't typically dilated as the first sign.

⭐ Key point: If knee reflexes are absent, stop the magnesium infusion and check the magnesium level. (Use biceps reflexes if an epidural blocks the legs.)$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q134$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 490, $s$A woman on MgSO₄ has RR 10/min and urine output 15 mL/h. The nurse should:$s$, $s$["Increase the infusion rate", "Give oral fluids and continue", "Record only and recheck tomorrow", "Stop the infusion and inform the doctor"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stop the infusion and inform the doctor
A respiratory rate of 10/min (below 12) shows RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION from magnesium toxicity. Urine output of 15 mL/hour (below 25–30) shows that magnesium (cleared by the kidneys) will build up further. The nurse should STOP the infusion immediately, INFORM the doctor, check reflexes and the magnesium level, and be ready to give calcium gluconate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the infusion rate — Increasing the infusion would worsen toxicity.
• Give oral fluids and continue — Oral fluids and continuing are unsafe.
• Record only and recheck tomorrow — Recording and rechecking tomorrow could be fatal.

⭐ Key point: Hold magnesium if: RR below 12, urine output below 25–30 mL/hour, or reflexes are absent.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q136$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 491, $s$A drug is available as a 1:10,000 solution, commonly used for cardiac arrest adrenaline. How many milligrams are in a 10 mL prefilled syringe of this solution?$s$, $s$["10 mg", "1 mg", "0.1 mg", "100 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1 mg
"1:10,000" means 1 g in 10,000 mL. 1 g = 1000 mg, so 1000 mg ÷ 10,000 mL = 0.1 mg/mL. A 10 mL syringe contains 10 × 0.1 = 1 mg. This is the standard adult cardiac arrest dose of adrenaline.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 mg — 10 mg is ten times too much.
• 0.1 mg — 0.1 mg is the amount in only 1 mL.
• 100 mg — 100 mg is far too much.

⭐ Key point: Adrenaline: 1:1000 = 1 mg/mL (used IM for anaphylaxis, 0.5 mL). 1:10,000 = 0.1 mg/mL (used IV in cardiac arrest, 10 mL).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q141$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 492, $s$A patient is receiving a sedative with a continuous infusion. The nurse notes that the patient's respiratory rate is 8 breaths per minute. The most appropriate action is to:$s$, $s$["Increase the sedative infusion rate", "Stop the sedative infusion and stimulate the patient", "Continue to monitor the patient closely", "Stop the sedative infusion and administer Naloxone"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stop the sedative infusion and stimulate the patient
A respiratory rate of 8 breaths/min in a sedated patient shows RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION, which can lead to hypoxia and respiratory arrest. The most appropriate action is to STOP the SEDATIVE INFUSION and STIMULATE the patient (call their name, gentle shaking). Assess airway, oxygen and SpO₂, give oxygen, and support ventilation if needed. Call for help.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the sedative infusion rate — Increasing the sedation would worsen respiratory depression.
• Continue to monitor the patient closely — Continuing to monitor without action is dangerous.
• Stop the sedative infusion and administer Naloxone — Naloxone only reverses OPIOIDS; the question says "sedative", so stop and stimulate first.

⭐ Key point: Sedation scores (RASS) help prevent over-sedation. Keep a bag-valve-mask and reversal agents ready.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q148$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 493, $s$The dose of suxthamethonium for intubation is$s$, $s$["0.2mg/kg", "1gm for pediatrics", "2mg/kg", "100mg for adult"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2mg/kg
Suxamethonium (succinylcholine) is a fast-acting depolarising muscle relaxant used for rapid sequence intubation. The usual intubating dose is 1–1.5 mg/kg IV, and up to 2 mg/kg is cited, which is the keyed answer here. It works within 30–60 seconds and lasts about 5–10 minutes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.2mg/kg — 0.2 mg/kg is far too little.
• 1gm for pediatrics — 1 g for children is a massive overdose.
• 100mg for adult — 100 mg is a common adult dose (about 1.5 mg/kg for 70 kg), but the question looks for the weight-based dose.

⭐ Key point: Suxamethonium side effects: high potassium (avoid after burns over 24 hours old, crush injury, spinal injury), malignant hyperthermia, bradycardia, muscle pain.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q149$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 494, $s$The second-line drug of choice for status epilepticus, given as an IV loading dose, is:$s$, $s$["Lorazepam", "Salbutamol", "Adrenaline", "Phenytoin"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Phenytoin
Status epilepticus is treated in steps. FIRST-LINE: a benzodiazepine (lorazepam, diazepam or midazolam). If seizures continue after two doses, give a SECOND-LINE IV loading dose: PHENYTOIN (or fosphenytoin), levetiracetam, or valproate. If seizures still continue, the third line is general anaesthesia (propofol or thiopentone) with intubation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lorazepam — Lorazepam is the FIRST-line drug, not second.
• Salbutamol — Salbutamol is a bronchodilator.
• Adrenaline — Adrenaline is used for anaphylaxis and cardiac arrest.

⭐ Key point: Phenytoin loading: 20 mg/kg IV, no faster than 50 mg/minute, with ECG and BP monitoring (it can cause arrhythmias and hypotension).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q152$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 495, $s$Propofol infusion syndrome is most associated with:$s$, $s$["Low doses used for short day-case procedures", "Diluting propofol with 0.9% normal saline", "High doses for more than 48 hours, causing acidosis and rhabdomyolysis", "A single induction dose given to a healthy adult"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: High doses for more than 48 hours, causing acidosis and rhabdomyolysis
Propofol infusion syndrome (PRIS) is rare but often fatal. It is associated with HIGH doses (above 4–5 mg/kg/hour) given for MORE THAN 48 HOURS, especially in children, sepsis, head injury, and with catecholamines or steroids. It causes metabolic ACIDOSIS, RHABDOMYOLYSIS (raised CK), high potassium, high triglycerides, kidney failure, arrhythmias and heart failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low doses used for short day-case procedures — Low doses for short procedures are not associated with PRIS.
• Diluting propofol with 0.9% normal saline — Diluting with saline is not a cause.
• A single induction dose given to a healthy adult — A single induction dose doesn't cause PRIS.

⭐ Key point: On long propofol infusions, monitor triglycerides, CK, lactate and the ECG, and keep the dose below 4 mg/kg/hour.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q157$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 496, $s$In acute severe asthma, steroids should ideally be administered within:$s$, $s$["24 hours", "6 hours", "1 hour", "10 minutes"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1 hour
In acute severe asthma, steroids (oral prednisolone 40–50 mg, or IV hydrocortisone 100 mg) reduce airway inflammation, but they take several hours to work. They should be given EARLY, ideally within 1 HOUR of arrival, so their effect starts as soon as possible.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 24 hours — 24 hours is too late.
• 6 hours — 6 hours is too late.
• 10 minutes — 10 minutes is ideal but not the standard target.

⭐ Key point: Asthma emergency treatment: oxygen, salbutamol, ipratropium, steroids within 1 hour, and IV magnesium if severe.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q27$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 497, $s$Which client is at greater risk for respiratory depression while receiving opioids for analgesia?$s$, $s$["A young female client with advanced multiple myeloma", "A client with a heroin addiction and back pain", "An elderly chronic pain client with a hip fracture", "A child with an arm fracture and cystic fibrosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A child with an arm fracture and cystic fibrosis
A child with CYSTIC FIBROSIS already has damaged lungs, thick secretions and reduced respiratory reserve. Opioids depress the breathing centre and the cough reflex, so this child is at the greatest risk of respiratory depression, CO₂ retention and retained secretions. Children are also more sensitive to opioids per kilogram.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A young female client with advanced multiple myeloma — A young woman with myeloma may be opioid-tolerant from long-term use.
• A client with a heroin addiction and back pain — A person with heroin addiction is usually TOLERANT to opioids.
• An elderly chronic pain client with a hip fracture — An elderly person with chronic pain is at risk, but long-term users may be tolerant; the child with lung disease is highest risk.

⭐ Key point: Monitor respiratory rate, sedation level and SpO₂ in high-risk patients on opioids, and keep naloxone available.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q29$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 498, $s$Which of the following is not a common potential side effect of Class II Antiarrhythmic drugs (e.g. propranolol, atenolol, carvedilol)?$s$, $s$["Hepatotoxicity", "Hypotension", "Negative inotropes", "Bradycardia"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hepatotoxicity
Class II antiarrhythmics are BETA-BLOCKERS (propranolol, atenolol, carvedilol, metoprolol). They block the heart's beta-receptors, causing BRADYCARDIA (slow heart), HYPOTENSION (low BP) and NEGATIVE INOTROPY (weaker contraction). They can also cause bronchospasm, tiredness and hide hypoglycaemia. HEPATOTOXICITY (liver damage) is NOT a typical side effect.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypotension — Hypotension IS a common side effect.
• Negative inotropes — Negative inotropy IS a common effect.
• Bradycardia — Bradycardia IS a common side effect.

⭐ Key point: Avoid beta-blockers in severe asthma, acute heart failure with shock, and high-degree heart block.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q44$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 499, $s$The action to be taken by critical care nurse before administering morphine 4.0 mg intravenously to a patient complaining of incisional pain is$s$, $s$["Monitor the patient's heart rate.", "Clarify the order with the prescriber.", "Assess the patient's respiratory status.", "Assess the patient's incision."]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assess the patient's respiratory status.
IV MORPHINE can cause RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION (slow, shallow breathing), especially in the elderly, the opioid-naive, and patients with lung disease or on other sedatives. Before giving it, ASSESS RESPIRATORY STATUS: rate, depth, SpO₂ and sedation level. Don't give it if the respiratory rate is low or the patient is very drowsy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Monitor the patient's heart rate. — Heart rate is important, but respiratory status is the priority for opioids.
• Clarify the order with the prescriber. — Clarifying the order isn't needed unless the order is unclear.
• Assess the patient's incision. — Assessing the incision is useful but isn't the safety priority before giving morphine.

⭐ Key point: After IV morphine, monitor respiratory rate, sedation and SpO₂, and keep naloxone available.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q47$s$);
insert into public.admin_study (collection, area, must, sort, q, opts, ans_idx, answer, exp, ref) values
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 500, $s$Inj. Digoxin ampoules available contains 500 micrograms in 2ml. What volume is needed when 350 microgram is prescribed?$s$, $s$["0.7ml", "1.4ml", "1.4µg", "0.7µg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1.4ml
The stock is 500 micrograms in 2 mL, so the concentration is 500 ÷ 2 = 250 micrograms per mL. Volume needed = dose wanted ÷ concentration = 350 ÷ 250 = 1.4 mL. Using the formula (want ÷ have) × volume: (350 ÷ 500) × 2 = 0.7 × 2 = 1.4 mL. (The old answer key said 0.7 mL, which is the wrong answer; it has been corrected to 1.4 mL.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.7ml — 0.7 mL forgets to multiply by the 2 mL stock volume (it would give only 175 micrograms).
• 1.4µg — "1.4 µg" uses the wrong unit; the answer is a volume in mL.
• 0.7µg — "0.7 µg" is the wrong number and the wrong unit.

⭐ Key point: Formula: (Dose wanted ÷ Stock strength) × Stock volume. Always check the answer makes sense: 350 is less than 500, so the volume must be less than 2 mL.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q55 · answer key corrected$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 501, $s$Which of these drugs is used as an antidote for paralytic agents$s$, $s$["Atropine", "Adrenaline", "Neostigmin", "Atracurium"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Neostigmin
NEOSTIGMINE is an anticholinesterase. It stops the breakdown of acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction, so more acetylcholine is available to compete with NON-DEPOLARISING muscle relaxants (paralytic agents like rocuronium, vecuronium, atracurium), REVERSING their effect. It is given with glycopyrrolate or atropine to block its effects on the heart and gut.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Atropine — Atropine is given WITH neostigmine to prevent bradycardia, but it doesn't reverse paralysis.
• Adrenaline — Adrenaline isn't a reversal agent.
• Atracurium — Atracurium IS a paralytic agent, not an antidote.

⭐ Key point: Sugammadex directly binds rocuronium and vecuronium and reverses them faster, even from deep block.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q74$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 502, $s$The recommended dose of neomycin in the management of PSE is:$s$, $s$["100mg every 6 hours", "10g once daily", "1g every 6 hours", "500mg once weekly"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1g every 6 hours
Neomycin is an antibiotic poorly absorbed from the gut. It reduces the bacteria that produce ammonia, lowering blood ammonia in hepatic (portosystemic) encephalopathy. The traditional dose is 1 g orally every 6 hours (4 g/day), for short courses.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100mg every 6 hours — 100 mg every 6 hours is too little.
• 10g once daily — 10 g once daily is too much.
• 500mg once weekly — 500 mg once weekly is far too little.

⭐ Key point: Neomycin can cause kidney damage and deafness. Rifaximin is now preferred, as it is safer.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q88$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 503, $s$To inhibit pancreatic secretions, which pharmacologic agent would you anticipate administering to a patient with acute pancreatitis?$s$, $s$["Pancrelipase", "Pepcid", "Somatostatin", "Nitroglycerin"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Somatostatin
SOMATOSTATIN (or its analogue octreotide) inhibits the release of pancreatic enzymes. In acute pancreatitis, reducing pancreatic secretion may lessen autodigestion of the pancreas. (Evidence for routine use is mixed; mainstays are fluids, pain relief and nutrition.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pancrelipase — Pancrelipase REPLACES pancreatic enzymes; it doesn't inhibit secretion.
• Pepcid — Pepcid (famotidine) reduces stomach acid, not pancreatic secretion directly.
• Nitroglycerin — Nitroglycerin is a vasodilator, not used for pancreatitis.

⭐ Key point: Acute pancreatitis care: aggressive IV fluids, strong analgesia, early enteral feeding, and monitoring for complications.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q89$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 504, $s$Which patient would require the least amount of, or no, heparin anticoagulation during hemodialysis?$s$, $s$["A patient who is actively bleeding or recently had surgery", "A patient with a well-functioning arteriovenous fistula", "A patient with no bleeding risk undergoing routine outpatient dialysis", "A patient with a normal platelet count and coagulation studies"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A patient who is actively bleeding or recently had surgery
During haemodialysis, heparin is normally used to stop the blood clotting in the circuit. In patients with ACTIVE BLEEDING or RECENT SURGERY (high bleeding risk), heparin would be dangerous, so dialysis is done with LITTLE OR NO HEPARIN (heparin-free dialysis with saline flushes, or regional citrate anticoagulation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A patient with a well-functioning arteriovenous fistula — A good fistula doesn't reduce the need for anticoagulation.
• A patient with no bleeding risk undergoing routine outpatient dialysis — Patients with no bleeding risk can have standard heparin.
• A patient with a normal platelet count and coagulation studies — Normal clotting means standard heparin is appropriate.

⭐ Key point: Watch the circuit closely for clotting in heparin-free dialysis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q93$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 505, $s$Which of the following is the most widely used method of gut decontamination in modern poisoning management, when indicated?$s$, $s$["Gastric lavage as routine for all poisonings", "Single-dose activated charcoal", "Whole bowel irrigation for all ingestions", "Induced vomiting with ipecac"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Single-dose activated charcoal
SINGLE-DOSE ACTIVATED CHARCOAL (50 g in adults, 1 g/kg in children) is the most widely used gut decontamination method. It binds many drugs in the gut and stops absorption. It works best within 1 hour of ingestion and in patients with a protected airway.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Gastric lavage as routine for all poisonings — Gastric lavage is no longer routine; it has more risks (aspiration) and limited benefit.
• Whole bowel irrigation for all ingestions — Whole bowel irrigation is only for special cases (iron, lithium, body packers, slow-release drugs).
• Induced vomiting with ipecac — Ipecac-induced vomiting is NOT recommended (aspiration risk, no benefit).

⭐ Key point: Charcoal doesn't bind iron, lithium, alcohols, acids, alkalis or hydrocarbons.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q105$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 506, $s$The first-line vasopressor recommended in septic shock, according to major guidelines, is:$s$, $s$["Vasopressin as first choice", "Phenylephrine as first choice", "Dopamine", "Norepinephrine"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Norepinephrine
NOREPINEPHRINE (noradrenaline) is the FIRST-LINE vasopressor in septic shock in all major guidelines (Surviving Sepsis Campaign). It mainly constricts blood vessels (alpha-1) to raise BP, with little rise in heart rate, and is linked to fewer arrhythmias and better outcomes than dopamine.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vasopressin as first choice — Vasopressin is ADDED second, to reduce the noradrenaline dose.
• Phenylephrine as first choice — Phenylephrine isn't first-line (it can lower cardiac output).
• Dopamine — Dopamine causes more arrhythmias and is no longer first-line.

⭐ Key point: Target MAP of 65 mmHg or more. Ideally give through a central line, but it can be started peripherally in a large vein briefly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q113$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 507, $s$The specific antidote for benzodiazepine overdose is:$s$, $s$["Naloxone", "Flumazenil", "Protamine sulfate", "Dantrolene"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Flumazenil
FLUMAZENIL is a competitive antagonist at the benzodiazepine receptor (GABA-A). It reverses the sedation and respiratory depression caused by benzodiazepines (midazolam, diazepam). The dose is 200 micrograms IV, then 100 micrograms every minute as needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses OPIOIDS.
• Protamine sulfate — Protamine reverses HEPARIN.
• Dantrolene — Dantrolene treats malignant hyperthermia.

⭐ Key point: Use flumazenil cautiously: it can trigger SEIZURES in long-term benzodiazepine users or in mixed overdoses (such as with tricyclics).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q141$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 508, $s$The nurse is caring for a patient who is on a continuous infusion of Propofol. The nurse should be aware that the infusion should be changed every:$s$, $s$["48 hours", "24 hours", "72 hours", "12 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 12 hours
Propofol is a lipid emulsion that supports rapid bacterial growth once opened. Propofol infusions (the bottle or syringe and the tubing) should be changed every 12 HOURS (or as per the manufacturer's instructions) to prevent bloodstream infection. Use strict aseptic technique.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 48 hours — 48 hours is too long.
• 24 hours — 24 hours is too long for propofol (it's the time for TPN with lipids).
• 72 hours — 72 hours is far too long.

⭐ Key point: Discard any unused propofol from an opened vial or syringe after 6–12 hours, depending on local policy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q142$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 509, $s$A patient receiving cyclosporine post-transplant has hypertension. Which drug is CONTRAINDICATED?$s$, $s$["Verapamil", "Lisinopril", "Amlodipine", "Nifedipine"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Verapamil
Ciclosporin is broken down by the liver enzyme CYP3A4. VERAPAMIL (a non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blocker) INHIBITS CYP3A4, so ciclosporin levels RISE, risking toxicity (kidney damage, high BP, tremor). Verapamil is the drug to avoid in this question.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lisinopril — Lisinopril (an ACE inhibitor) can be used with caution (watch potassium and kidney function).
• Amlodipine — Amlodipine is commonly used in transplant patients for BP.
• Nifedipine — Nifedipine is also a dihydropyridine used for BP.

⭐ Key point: Many drugs interact with ciclosporin and tacrolimus. Always check interactions and monitor drug levels.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q143$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 510, $s$The nurse is caring for a patient on a continuous sedative infusion. The nurse should be aware that which of the following is a major complication of prolonged sedation?$s$, $s$["A decreased need for mechanical ventilation", "Delirium and an increased length of stay in the ICU", "Increased mobility and decreased risk of pressure ulcers", "An increased level of consciousness"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Delirium and an increased length of stay in the ICU
Prolonged deep sedation causes DELIRIUM, longer ventilation, ICU-acquired weakness, pressure ulcers, VAP, and a LONGER ICU STAY. Guidelines recommend light sedation, daily sedation holds, and using propofol or dexmedetomidine rather than benzodiazepines.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A decreased need for mechanical ventilation — Prolonged sedation INCREASES the need for ventilation.
• Increased mobility and decreased risk of pressure ulcers — It DECREASES mobility and increases pressure ulcers.
• An increased level of consciousness — It decreases, not increases, the level of consciousness.

⭐ Key point: Target light sedation (RASS 0 to −1) unless there is a reason for deeper sedation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q145$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 511, $s$What is the antidote for a cholinergic crisis caused by anticholinesterase medication overdose?$s$, $s$["Flumazenil", "Naloxone", "Atropine", "Protamine sulfate"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Atropine
A CHOLINERGIC crisis is too much acetylcholine (from an overdose of anticholinesterase drugs like neostigmine or pyridostigmine, or from organophosphates). It causes SLUDGE (salivation, lacrimation, urination, diarrhoea, GI cramps, emesis), slow pulse, bronchospasm and weakness. ATROPINE blocks the muscarinic effects.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines.
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids.
• Protamine sulfate — Protamine reverses heparin.

⭐ Key point: Cholinergic crisis can look like myasthenic crisis; both cause weakness, but cholinergic crisis has SLUDGE signs and small pupils.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q148$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 512, $s$During adult cardiac arrest, IV adrenaline (epinephrine) is administered at what dose and interval?$s$, $s$["0.1mg every 1 minute", "1mg every 3–5 minutes", "1mg every 15 minutes", "10mg once only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1mg every 3–5 minutes
In adult cardiac arrest, ADRENALINE 1 mg IV (or IO) is given every 3–5 MINUTES (every second CPR cycle). In non-shockable rhythms (asystole, PEA), give it as soon as possible. In shockable rhythms (VF, pulseless VT), give it after the 3rd shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.1mg every 1 minute — 0.1 mg every minute is the wrong dose and interval.
• 1mg every 15 minutes — Every 15 minutes is too infrequent.
• 10mg once only — 10 mg once only is a dangerous overdose.

⭐ Key point: Flush each dose with 20 mL of saline and raise the arm (for peripheral access).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q156 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 513, $s$Noradrenaline has leaked from a peripheral IV into the tissues. The specific treatment is:$s$, $s$["Applying heat and massaging the area", "Giving IV furosemide", "Local infiltration with phentolamine", "Local injection of more noradrenaline"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Local infiltration with phentolamine
Noradrenaline is a strong vasoconstrictor. If it leaks into the tissues (extravasation), it narrows the local blood vessels, causing tissue ischaemia and necrosis (skin death). The specific treatment is LOCAL INFILTRATION with PHENTOLAMINE (an alpha-blocker), which reverses the vasoconstriction. Stop the infusion and leave the cannula in place to aspirate first.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Applying heat and massaging the area — Applying heat and massage can spread the drug.
• Giving IV furosemide — Furosemide doesn't treat extravasation.
• Local injection of more noradrenaline — More noradrenaline would worsen the damage.

⭐ Key point: Prevent extravasation: give vasopressors through a central line where possible, and check peripheral sites frequently.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q157$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 514, $s$Flushing and rash on the upper body during IV vancomycin is best managed by:$s$, $s$["Giving it as an IV push", "Doubling the dose", "Slowing the infusion rate", "Stopping all antibiotics permanently"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Slowing the infusion rate
"Red man syndrome" (flushing and a rash on the face, neck and upper body, sometimes with itching and low BP) happens when vancomycin is infused too FAST. It is caused by histamine release, not a true allergy. Treat by SLOWING the infusion (over at least 60 minutes, or longer for large doses) and giving antihistamines.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Giving it as an IV push — An IV push would make it worse.
• Doubling the dose — Doubling the dose would make it worse.
• Stopping all antibiotics permanently — Stopping all antibiotics permanently isn't necessary; it's an infusion-rate reaction.

⭐ Key point: Monitor vancomycin trough levels (or AUC) to avoid kidney damage and ototoxicity.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q158$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 515, $s$A 2-year old was admitted for digoxin toxicity. BP is 94/60 mmhg, capillary refill time is 2 seconds and the ECG reveals 1st degree AV block with a heart rate of 60. The critical care nurse should:$s$, $s$["Continue to monitor.", "Prepare for cardioversion.", "Administer atropine.", "Administer vasovagal maneuvers."]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Continue to monitor.
This child with digoxin toxicity has a STABLE BP (94/60), normal capillary refill (2 seconds), and only FIRST-DEGREE AV block with a heart rate of 60. First-degree block alone is usually benign, and the child is well perfused. The right action is to CONTINUE MONITORING closely (ECG, potassium, digoxin level, signs of deterioration).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prepare for cardioversion. — Cardioversion is dangerous in digoxin toxicity (it can trigger VF) and isn't indicated.
• Administer atropine. — Atropine isn't needed; the child is stable.
• Administer vasovagal maneuvers. — Vagal manoeuvres would slow the heart further.

⭐ Key point: Signs of serious digoxin toxicity: severe bradycardia, high-degree heart block, ventricular arrhythmias, high potassium. Treat with Digibind (digoxin-specific antibody).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q43$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 516, $s$The first-line drug for torsades de pointes with a pulse is:$s$, $s$["IV adenosine 6 mg", "IV magnesium sulfate 2 g", "Oral digoxin 250 mcg", "IV amiodarone 900 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV magnesium sulfate 2 g
IV MAGNESIUM SULPHATE (2 g over 10–15 minutes) is the first-line treatment for torsades de pointes with a pulse, even if the magnesium level is normal. It stabilises the heart muscle membrane. Also STOP QT-prolonging drugs and CORRECT potassium. Pulseless torsades is defibrillated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV adenosine 6 mg — Adenosine is for SVT, not torsades.
• Oral digoxin 250 mcg — Digoxin doesn't treat torsades.
• IV amiodarone 900 mg — Amiodarone prolongs QT and can worsen torsades.

⭐ Key point: Torsades is associated with long QT (drugs, low K, low Mg, congenital). Overdrive pacing or isoprenaline may help recurrent episodes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q59$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 517, $s$If a patient continues seizing despite first- and second-line drugs, the next step generally involves:$s$, $s$["Discharging the patient home", "Escalating to general anaesthesia/ICU-level care", "Simply waiting for the seizure to stop on its own", "No further treatment is available"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Escalating to general anaesthesia/ICU-level care
If seizures continue despite first-line (benzodiazepines) and second-line drugs (phenytoin, levetiracetam, valproate), this is REFRACTORY status epilepticus. The next step is GENERAL ANAESTHESIA (propofol, midazolam infusion, or thiopentone) with intubation and ventilation in ICU, and continuous EEG monitoring if available.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Discharging the patient home — Discharging the patient is dangerous.
• Simply waiting for the seizure to stop on its own — Waiting risks brain damage and death.
• No further treatment is available — Further treatment IS available.

⭐ Key point: Prolonged seizures cause brain injury, hyperthermia, acidosis and rhabdomyolysis. Treat aggressively.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q66$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 518, $s$Which class of antibiotics is well known for nephrotoxic potential requiring renal dose adjustment and trough level monitoring in ICU patients?$s$, $s$["Aminoglycosides", "First-generation cephalosporins", "Macrolides", "Penicillins"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Aminoglycosides
AMINOGLYCOSIDES (gentamicin, amikacin) build up in kidney tubule cells, causing NEPHROTOXICITY (acute tubular necrosis). They are also toxic to the ears (OTOTOXICITY: hearing loss, balance problems). Doses are adjusted for kidney function, and TROUGH (pre-dose) levels are monitored.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• First-generation cephalosporins — First-generation cephalosporins are rarely nephrotoxic.
• Macrolides — Macrolides (erythromycin) aren't typically nephrotoxic.
• Penicillins — Penicillins are rarely nephrotoxic (occasional interstitial nephritis).

⭐ Key point: Aminoglycosides are often given once daily to reduce toxicity. Avoid them with other nephrotoxins.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q81$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 519, $s$The antidote for paracetamol (acetaminophen) overdose is:$s$, $s$["IV flumazenil", "IV N-acetylcysteine", "IV naloxone", "IV protamine sulfate"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV N-acetylcysteine
N-ACETYLCYSTEINE (NAC) is the antidote for paracetamol overdose. It replenishes glutathione, which detoxifies the harmful paracetamol metabolite (NAPQI) and protects the liver. It is most effective within 8 hours of ingestion, but is given even later. The treatment nomogram guides its use.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines.
• IV naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids.
• IV protamine sulfate — Protamine reverses heparin.

⭐ Key point: Monitor LFTs, INR, glucose and kidney function. Paracetamol liver damage may not appear for 24–72 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q97 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 520, $s$For septic shock that stays refractory despite fluids and vasopressors, guidelines suggest:$s$, $s$["IV dexamethasone 40 mg as a single bolus", "Oral prednisolone 5 mg daily", "IV hydrocortisone about 200 mg per day", "IV methylprednisolone 1 g daily for 3 days"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV hydrocortisone about 200 mg per day
In septic shock that remains REFRACTORY despite fluids and vasopressors, guidelines suggest IV HYDROCORTISONE about 200 mg per DAY (50 mg every 6 hours, or a continuous infusion). It helps restore vascular responsiveness to vasopressors and shortens the duration of shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV dexamethasone 40 mg as a single bolus — A single 40 mg dexamethasone bolus isn't the recommended regimen.
• Oral prednisolone 5 mg daily — Oral prednisolone 5 mg is too little and too slow.
• IV methylprednisolone 1 g daily for 3 days — Methylprednisolone 1 g daily is far too high (it is a pulse dose for other conditions).

⭐ Key point: Monitor glucose closely with steroids, and stop them when vasopressors are no longer needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q98$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 521, $s$A 24 year old male is presented to ER with head injury. The patient has Tracheostomy intact. Heart Rate is below 50/min.Physician advised to administer Atropine 1 mg immediately. The only route of administering medication is Trachea now. Along with Atropine what other medications can be administered through Trachea;$s$, $s$["Adrenaline", "Calcium", "NaHCO3", "Amiodarone,"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Adrenaline
When there is NO IV or IO access, some drugs can be given down the ETT or tracheostomy and absorbed through the lungs. Remember NAVEL: Naloxone, Atropine, Vasopressin, Epinephrine (ADRENALINE), and Lidocaine. So adrenaline can also be given through the trachea (at a higher dose, about 2–2.5 times the IV dose, diluted in saline).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Calcium — Calcium is irritant and is NOT given by the tracheal route.
• NaHCO3 — Sodium bicarbonate damages the lungs and is NOT given by this route.
• Amiodarone, — Amiodarone is NOT given by the tracheal route.

⭐ Key point: The tracheal route is a last resort. Intraosseous (IO) access is preferred when IV access fails.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q105$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 522, $s$Adult anaphylaxis is first treated with IM adrenaline at a dose of:$s$, $s$["5 mg (5 mL of 1:1000) IV push", "1 mg (10 mL of 1:10,000) intramuscularly in the deltoid", "0.05 mg (0.5 mL of 1:10,000) subcutaneously", "0.5 mg (0.5 mL of 1:1000) into the anterolateral thigh"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 0.5 mg (0.5 mL of 1:1000) into the anterolateral thigh
Adult anaphylaxis is treated FIRST with IM ADRENALINE 0.5 mg (0.5 mL of 1:1000) into the ANTEROLATERAL THIGH (outer middle thigh). The thigh muscle absorbs it quickly. Repeat after 5 minutes if there's no improvement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5 mg (5 mL of 1:1000) IV push — 5 mg IV push is a massive, dangerous overdose.
• 1 mg (10 mL of 1:10,000) intramuscularly in the deltoid — 1 mg of 1:10,000 is the cardiac arrest dose and concentration, given IV, not IM.
• 0.05 mg (0.5 mL of 1:10,000) subcutaneously — 0.05 mg subcutaneously is too little and absorbed too slowly.

⭐ Key point: Then give high-flow oxygen, IV fluids, and lie the patient flat with the legs raised (or sitting if breathless).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q118$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 523, $s$A child weighing 18 kg is prescribed an antibiotic at 10 mg/kg/day divided every 8 hours. How many milligrams should be given per dose?$s$, $s$["60 mg", "180 mg", "30 mg", "90 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 60 mg
Step 1: total daily dose = 18 kg × 10 mg/kg/day = 180 mg/day. Step 2: "divided every 8 hours" = 3 doses a day (24 ÷ 8 = 3). Step 3: dose per administration = 180 ÷ 3 = 60 mg.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 180 mg — 180 mg is the total daily dose, not each dose.
• 30 mg — 30 mg would be 6 doses a day (every 4 hours).
• 90 mg — 90 mg would be 2 doses a day (every 12 hours).

⭐ Key point: Always check whether the order is "per dose" or "per day divided".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q130$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 524, $s$The IV adrenaline dose in paediatric cardiac arrest is:$s$, $s$["1 mg/kg (10 mL/kg of 1:10,000)", "0.1 mcg/kg (0.001 mL/kg of 1:1000)", "10 mcg/kg (0.1 mL/kg of 1:10,000)", "100 mcg/kg (1 mL/kg of 1:1000)"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10 mcg/kg (0.1 mL/kg of 1:10,000)
In paediatric cardiac arrest, the IV or IO adrenaline dose is 10 MICROGRAMS per KILOGRAM, which is 0.1 mL/kg of 1:10,000 adrenaline. Repeat every 3–5 minutes. Example: a 20 kg child gets 200 micrograms (2 mL of 1:10,000).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1 mg/kg (10 mL/kg of 1:10,000) — 1 mg/kg is a massive overdose.
• 0.1 mcg/kg (0.001 mL/kg of 1:1000) — 0.1 mcg/kg is far too little.
• 100 mcg/kg (1 mL/kg of 1:1000) — 100 mcg/kg is ten times too much (an old high-dose regimen no longer used).

⭐ Key point: Paediatric cardiac arrest doses: adrenaline 10 mcg/kg, amiodarone 5 mg/kg, defibrillation 4 J/kg.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q135$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 525, $s$The specific antidote for opioid overdose (e.g. morphine, fentanyl) is:$s$, $s$["N-acetylcysteine", "Naloxone", "Activated charcoal", "Flumazenil"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Naloxone
NALOXONE is the specific antidote for OPIOID overdose (morphine, heroin, fentanyl, tramadol). It blocks opioid receptors, reversing respiratory depression and sedation within minutes. Typical dose: 0.4 mg IV (or IM or intranasal), repeated as needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• N-acetylcysteine — N-acetylcysteine is for paracetamol overdose.
• Activated charcoal — Activated charcoal reduces gut absorption of some drugs; it isn't an antidote.
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines.

⭐ Key point: Naloxone wears off before many opioids, so watch for re-sedation and repeat doses or start an infusion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q141$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 526, $s$Zopiclone, a cyclopyrrolone-class Z-drug similar to zolpidem, is particularly associated with which distinctive adverse effect?$s$, $s$["Serotonin syndrome", "A metallic or bitter taste after use", "Severe hepatotoxicity in most patients", "Malignant hyperthermia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A metallic or bitter taste after use
ZOPICLONE (a "Z-drug" sleeping tablet) is well known for causing a distinctive METALLIC or BITTER TASTE in the mouth, which can last into the next day. Other side effects: drowsiness, dizziness, and the risk of dependence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Serotonin syndrome — Serotonin syndrome is linked to serotonergic drugs (SSRIs, tramadol).
• Severe hepatotoxicity in most patients — Severe liver damage in most patients isn't typical.
• Malignant hyperthermia — Malignant hyperthermia is linked to volatile anaesthetics and suxamethonium.

⭐ Key point: Z-drugs can cause falls and confusion in the elderly. Avoid long-term use.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q142$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 527, $s$The concentration of epinephrine used for endoscopic injection in bleeding ulcers is:$s$, $s$["1:1,000,000", "1:100,000", "1:10,000", "1:1,000"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1:10,000
During endoscopy for a bleeding peptic ulcer, adrenaline (epinephrine) diluted to 1:10,000 (0.1 mg/mL) is injected around the bleeding point. It narrows the vessels and compresses the tissue, slowing the bleeding. It is combined with another method (heat or clips) for lasting haemostasis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1:1,000,000 — 1:1,000,000 is too dilute to be effective.
• 1:100,000 — 1:100,000 is weaker than the standard concentration.
• 1:1,000 — 1:1,000 is too concentrated and could cause dangerous systemic effects.

⭐ Key point: After endoscopic treatment, give high-dose IV PPI to reduce the risk of rebleeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q144$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 528, $s$A patient on fentanyl PCA has a respiratory rate of 8/min and is somnolent. Which antidote should the nurse administer?$s$, $s$["Romazicon", "Flumazenil", "Naloxone", "Physostigmine"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Naloxone
A respiratory rate of 8 and sleepiness on fentanyl PCA show OPIOID-INDUCED respiratory depression. The antidote is NALOXONE (given in small doses, such as 40–100 micrograms IV, repeated, to reverse breathing problems without completely removing pain relief). Stop the PCA, stimulate the patient, give oxygen and support breathing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Romazicon — Romazicon is flumazenil, a benzodiazepine antagonist.
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines, not opioids.
• Physostigmine — Physostigmine reverses anticholinergic toxicity.

⭐ Key point: Monitor patients on PCA: respiratory rate, sedation score and SpO₂, especially in the first 24 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q147$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 529, $s$A nurse must give a 40 mg IV push dose over 4 minutes. The drug is supplied as 40 mg in 4 mL. At what rate, in mL/min, should the nurse push the medication?$s$, $s$["10 mL/min", "1 mL/min", "0.5 mL/min", "4 mL/min"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1 mL/min
The concentration is 40 mg in 4 mL = 10 mg/mL. To give the whole 40 mg (4 mL) over 4 minutes: 4 mL ÷ 4 minutes = 1 mL per minute.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 mL/min — 10 mL/min would give 4 mL in 24 seconds, too fast.
• 0.5 mL/min — 0.5 mL/min would take 8 minutes.
• 4 mL/min — 4 mL/min would give the whole dose in 1 minute, too fast.

⭐ Key point: Use a timer or count seconds when giving slow IV push drugs. Some drugs are dangerous if pushed too fast.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q149$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 530, $s$A patient on dopamine at 15 mcg/kg/min is likely to experience:$s$, $s$["Vasoconstriction", "Bronchospasm", "Renal artery dilation", "Hyperglycemia"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vasoconstriction
Dopamine's effects depend on the dose. Low dose (1–5 mcg/kg/min): dopamine receptors. Medium (5–10): beta-1 receptors (increased contractility and heart rate). HIGH dose (above 10 mcg/kg/min, such as 15): ALPHA receptors, causing VASOCONSTRICTION (raised SVR and BP), which can reduce blood flow to the limbs and organs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bronchospasm — Bronchospasm isn't a typical dopamine effect.
• Renal artery dilation — Renal artery dilation is the old idea of a LOW-dose effect (not proven to protect the kidneys).
• Hyperglycemia — Hyperglycaemia isn't the main dose-related effect.

⭐ Key point: Noradrenaline is now preferred over dopamine for septic shock (fewer arrhythmias).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q156$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 531, $s$A patient on heparin has platelets falling by more than 50% with new thrombosis. The nurse expects to:$s$, $s$["Give a platelet transfusion and keep the heparin", "Switch to low molecular weight heparin injections", "Increase the heparin dose to treat the new clot", "Stop all heparin, including flushes, and start a non-heparin anticoagulant"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stop all heparin, including flushes, and start a non-heparin anticoagulant
A platelet fall of more than 50% with NEW THROMBOSIS while on heparin suggests HEPARIN-INDUCED THROMBOCYTOPENIA (HIT). STOP ALL HEPARIN immediately, including heparin flushes and heparin-coated lines, and start a NON-HEPARIN anticoagulant (argatroban, fondaparinux, bivalirudin) to treat and prevent clots.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give a platelet transfusion and keep the heparin — Platelet transfusion can worsen clotting in HIT, and continuing heparin is dangerous.
• Switch to low molecular weight heparin injections — LMWH cross-reacts with HIT antibodies and must also be avoided.
• Increase the heparin dose to treat the new clot — More heparin would make HIT worse.

⭐ Key point: HIT causes CLOTS, not bleeding. It typically appears 5–10 days after starting heparin.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q158$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 532, $s$Once-daily dosing of gentamicin is used because aminoglycosides:$s$, $s$["Are not toxic to the kidney or ear", "Kill bacteria in a concentration-dependent way", "Are best given as a continuous infusion", "Kill bacteria in a time-dependent way only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Kill bacteria in a concentration-dependent way
Aminoglycosides (gentamicin) kill bacteria in a CONCENTRATION-DEPENDENT way: higher peak levels kill more bacteria. They also have a "post-antibiotic effect" (bacteria stay suppressed after levels fall). Once-daily dosing gives a high peak (better killing) and a low trough (less kidney and ear toxicity, because the drug has time to clear).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Are not toxic to the kidney or ear — Aminoglycosides ARE toxic to the kidneys and ears.
• Are best given as a continuous infusion — Continuous infusion isn't used; it would keep levels constantly raised and increase toxicity.
• Kill bacteria in a time-dependent way only — Time-dependent killing applies to beta-lactams (penicillins, cephalosporins), not aminoglycosides.

⭐ Key point: Check gentamicin levels and kidney function, and adjust the dosing interval in kidney impairment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q160$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A3 · Pharmacology, Drug Calculations & Toxicology$s$, false, 533, $s$Procalcitonin is used in ICU mainly to help:$s$, $s$["Guide when to stop antibiotics in bacterial infection", "Measure kidney function in AKI", "Monitor anticoagulation with heparin", "Diagnose an acute myocardial infarction"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Guide when to stop antibiotics in bacterial infection
PROCALCITONIN is a blood marker that rises in BACTERIAL infections (much less in viral infections). In ICU, it is mainly used to help decide WHEN TO STOP antibiotics: falling levels (for example by 80% or more, or below 0.5 ng/mL) support stopping earlier, which reduces antibiotic use and resistance.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Measure kidney function in AKI — It doesn't measure kidney function.
• Monitor anticoagulation with heparin — It doesn't monitor heparin.
• Diagnose an acute myocardial infarction — Troponin, not procalcitonin, is used for MI.

⭐ Key point: Procalcitonin supports, but doesn't replace, clinical judgement in sepsis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q200$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, true, 534, $s$A patient develops fever with chills and rigors shortly after a blood transfusion is started post-operatively. What is the priority nursing action?$s$, $s$["No action is needed if the temperature is below 38.5 degrees C", "Increase the transfusion rate to finish it quickly", "Give an antipyretic and continue the transfusion at the same rate", "Stop the transfusion immediately, keep the IV line open with normal saline, notify the physician, and send the blood/tubing for investigation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stop the transfusion immediately, keep the IV line open with normal saline, notify the physician, and send the blood/tubing for investigation
Fever, chills and rigors soon after starting blood may be the first sign of a serious reaction: an acute haemolytic reaction (wrong blood), bacterial contamination, or a febrile reaction. The nurse cannot tell which at the bedside, so the SAFEST first action is to STOP the transfusion at once. Keep the vein open with a new giving set and 0.9% saline, check vital signs, inform the doctor, recheck the patient ID against the bag, and return the bag and tubing with blood and urine samples to the lab.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No action is needed if the temperature is below 38.5 degrees C — Waiting for the temperature to reach a certain level could let a fatal haemolytic reaction continue.
• Increase the transfusion rate to finish it quickly — Speeding up the transfusion gives MORE of the possibly harmful blood.
• Give an antipyretic and continue the transfusion at the same rate — An antipyretic can hide the fever, and continuing the transfusion is unsafe until the cause is known.

⭐ Key point: Any reaction means STOP the blood, keep the line open with saline, call the doctor, check the ID, and send the bag back. Most acute haemolytic reactions are caused by giving blood to the wrong patient.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q21$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, true, 535, $s$Massive blood transfusion is defined as:$s$, $s$["350 mL in 5 minutes", "500 mL in 5 minutes", "1 litre in 5 minutes", "Replacement of the patient's total blood volume within 24 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Replacement of the patient's total blood volume within 24 hours
Massive transfusion is usually defined as replacing the patient's whole blood volume (about 70 mL/kg; roughly 10 units of red cells in an adult) within 24 hours. Other definitions include 50% of blood volume within 3 hours, or more than 4 units within 1 hour. It is used in major trauma, PPH and GI bleeding, with a balanced ratio of red cells, FFP and platelets.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 350 mL in 5 minutes — 350 mL in 5 minutes is a rapid infusion, but it is not the definition.
• 500 mL in 5 minutes — 500 mL in 5 minutes is not a recognised definition.
• 1 litre in 5 minutes — 1 litre in 5 minutes is very fast, but the definition is based on blood volume over time.

⭐ Key point: Watch for the complications of massive transfusion: hypothermia, LOW calcium (citrate), HIGH potassium, acidosis and clotting problems. Use a blood warmer and give calcium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q22$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, true, 536, $s$A classic chest X-ray sign of pleural effusion is:$s$, $s$["Hyperinflated lung fields", "Blunting of the costophrenic angle", "A widened mediastinum", "Free air under the diaphragm"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Blunting of the costophrenic angle
Fluid in the pleural space collects at the lowest point first. On an erect chest X-ray, the sharp costophrenic angle (where the diaphragm meets the ribs) becomes blunted, often with a curved upper edge (meniscus sign). At least about 200–300 mL is needed to show on a PA film. A large effusion makes the whole lower zone white and can push the trachea and mediastinum to the opposite side.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperinflated lung fields — Hyperinflated (black, enlarged) lung fields suggest COPD or asthma, not effusion.
• A widened mediastinum — A widened mediastinum suggests aortic injury or dissection.
• Free air under the diaphragm — Free air under the diaphragm means a perforated bowel or stomach.

⭐ Key point: An effusion is dull to percussion, with reduced breath sounds and reduced vocal resonance. Large ones are drained by thoracentesis or a chest drain.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q41$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, true, 537, $s$Microcytic anaemia (low MCV) is classically associated with:$s$, $s$["Iron deficiency", "Vitamin B12 deficiency", "Folate deficiency", "Acute haemorrhage only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Iron deficiency
MCV (mean cell volume) tells us the SIZE of red cells. MICROCYTIC means small cells (MCV under about 80 fL), usually also pale (hypochromic). The commonest cause of microcytic anaemia worldwide is IRON DEFICIENCY — from blood loss (heavy menstruation, hookworm, GI bleeding, ulcers, colon cancer), poor diet, pregnancy or poor absorption. Without iron the cell cannot make enough haemoglobin, so it divides extra times and ends up small. Other microcytic causes: thalassaemia, anaemia of chronic disease (sometimes), sideroblastic anaemia, lead poisoning ("TAILS"). Tests: low ferritin, low serum iron, high TIBC. Clinical signs: koilonychia (spoon nails), angular stomatitis, pica.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vitamin B12 deficiency — VITAMIN B12 deficiency gives MACROCYTIC (large cell, megaloblastic) anaemia with MCV over 100 fL, and may cause neurological signs.
• Folate deficiency — FOLATE deficiency also gives MACROCYTIC megaloblastic anaemia, not microcytic.
• Acute haemorrhage only — ACUTE HAEMORRHAGE gives a NORMOCYTIC anaemia at first, because the cells lost are normal-sized; microcytosis only appears later after iron stores run out.

⭐ Key point: Microcytic (MCV < 80) = IRON DEFICIENCY first ("TAILS"). Macrocytic = B12/folate. Acute bleed = normocytic.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q21$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, true, 538, $s$The mnemonic IPPR, used to check chest X-ray quality before interpretation, stands for:$s$, $s$["Inspiration, Position, Penetration, Rotation", "Identity, Projection, Positioning, Report", "Inflation, Pressure, Perfusion, Rate", "Inspiration, Perfusion, Position, Result"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inspiration, Position, Penetration, Rotation
Before reading a chest X-ray, first check if the film is technically adequate, using IPPR: (1) INSPIRATION — about 5–6 anterior ribs or 9–10 posterior ribs should be seen above the diaphragm; poor inspiration makes the heart look big and the lung bases look hazy. (2) POSITION/PROJECTION — PA (standard) or AP (portable/bedside, which magnifies the heart). (3) PENETRATION — the vertebrae should be just visible behind the heart; over-penetrated films look too dark, under-penetrated too white. (4) ROTATION — the spinous processes should be midway between the medial ends of the clavicles; rotation distorts the mediastinum. Many teachers add identity, date and side markers first.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Identity, Projection, Positioning, Report — "Identity, Projection, Positioning, Report" is not the standard meaning of IPPR (identity is checked separately).
• Inflation, Pressure, Perfusion, Rate — "Inflation, Pressure, Perfusion, Rate" relates to ventilation/haemodynamics, not X-ray quality.
• Inspiration, Perfusion, Position, Result — "Inspiration, Perfusion, Position, Result" wrongly includes perfusion, which an X-ray cannot assess.

⭐ Key point: CXR quality = IPPR: Inspiration, Position, Penetration, Rotation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q43$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, true, 539, $s$The normal haemoglobin range for an adult male is approximately:$s$, $s$["6–8 g/dL", "13.5–17.5 g/dL", "20–24 g/dL", "2–4 g/dL"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 13.5–17.5 g/dL
The NORMAL HAEMOGLOBIN (Hb) range for an ADULT MALE is about 13.5–17.5 g/dL (135–175 g/L). For adult females it is about 12.0–15.5 g/dL. Haemoglobin carries oxygen in red blood cells. Low Hb (anaemia) reduces oxygen delivery to tissues.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 6–8 g/dL — 6–8 g/dL is severe anaemia.
• 20–24 g/dL — 20–24 g/dL is severe polycythaemia.
• 2–4 g/dL — 2–4 g/dL is life-threatening anaemia.

⭐ Key point: In most stable ICU patients, a restrictive transfusion threshold of Hb 7 g/dL (70 g/L) is used; higher in active bleeding or acute coronary syndrome.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q21$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, true, 540, $s$A patient's coagulation panel shows a markedly prolonged PT/INR with normal aPTT. This pattern most specifically suggests an abnormality in:$s$, $s$["The intrinsic coagulation pathway only", "Platelet function only", "No coagulation abnormality", "The extrinsic coagulation pathway (e.g. Factor VII deficiency or warfarin effect)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The extrinsic coagulation pathway (e.g. Factor VII deficiency or warfarin effect)
The PT/INR (prothrombin time) tests the EXTRINSIC pathway (Factor VII) and the common pathway (Factors X, V, II, fibrinogen). The aPTT tests the INTRINSIC pathway (Factors XII, XI, IX, VIII) and the common pathway. A PROLONGED PT/INR with NORMAL aPTT points to a problem in the EXTRINSIC pathway — e.g. FACTOR VII deficiency, WARFARIN effect (Factor VII has the shortest half-life, so it falls first), early VITAMIN K deficiency, or early liver disease.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The intrinsic coagulation pathway only — Intrinsic pathway problems prolong the aPTT (e.g. heparin, haemophilia).
• Platelet function only — Platelet function problems do not prolong the PT/INR.
• No coagulation abnormality — A markedly prolonged PT/INR IS a coagulation abnormality.

⭐ Key point: Warfarin is reversed with vitamin K and prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q155$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, true, 541, $s$A pleural tap shows pleural protein 40 g/L (serum 60 g/L) and pleural LDH 300 IU/L (serum 400 IU/L; upper limit of normal 220). By Light's criteria the fluid is:$s$, $s$["A transudate — e.g. cirrhosis", "A transudate — e.g. heart failure", "An exudate — e.g. parapneumonic effusion, malignancy or TB", "An empyema, because the protein is above 30 g/L"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An exudate — e.g. parapneumonic effusion, malignancy or TB
LIGHT'S CRITERIA classify a pleural effusion as an EXUDATE if ANY of these are met: (1) pleural protein ÷ serum protein MORE THAN 0.5; (2) pleural LDH ÷ serum LDH MORE THAN 0.6; (3) pleural LDH MORE THAN TWO-THIRDS of the upper limit of normal serum LDH. Here: protein ratio = 40 ÷ 60 = 0.67 (above 0.5 ✓); LDH ratio = 300 ÷ 400 = 0.75 (above 0.6 ✓); pleural LDH 300 > 2/3 × 220 = 147 ✓. All three criteria are met, so it's an EXUDATE. Exudates are caused by inflammation, infection or cancer (leaky capillaries or blocked lymphatics), e.g. PARAPNEUMONIC effusion, MALIGNANCY or TUBERCULOSIS (very common in Nigeria).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A transudate — e.g. cirrhosis — Cirrhosis causes a TRANSUDATE (low protein, low LDH).
• A transudate — e.g. heart failure — Heart failure causes a TRANSUDATE (pressure-related).
• An empyema, because the protein is above 30 g/L — An empyema is pus in the pleural space (thick, cloudy, with low pH and glucose, and organisms). A protein above 30 g/L alone does not define empyema.

⭐ Key point: Further tests on pleural fluid: cell count, Gram stain and culture, AFB and TB tests, cytology (cancer cells), pH and glucose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q77$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, true, 542, $s$A 3-year-old has bloody diarrhoea, and a week later becomes pale and oliguric. Bloods show Hb 6 g/dL with fragmented red cells on the film, platelets 40 × 10⁹/L, and a NORMAL prothrombin time. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Disseminated intravascular coagulation", "Haemolytic uraemic syndrome (e.g. E. coli O157)", "Immune thrombocytopenic purpura", "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Haemolytic uraemic syndrome (e.g. E. coli O157)
HAEMOLYTIC URAEMIC SYNDROME (HUS) usually follows BLOODY DIARRHOEA caused by Shiga toxin-producing E. COLI (e.g. O157:H7). Triad: (1) MICROANGIOPATHIC HAEMOLYTIC ANAEMIA (low Hb, FRAGMENTED red cells — schistocytes), (2) THROMBOCYTOPENIA (low platelets), (3) ACUTE KIDNEY INJURY (oliguria). Clotting tests (PT) are NORMAL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Disseminated intravascular coagulation — DIC causes prolonged clotting times (PT and APTT).
• Immune thrombocytopenic purpura — ITP causes low platelets only, without anaemia or kidney failure.
• Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura — TTP mainly affects adults, with neurological symptoms and fever.

⭐ Key point: Antibiotics are usually avoided in E. coli O157 diarrhoea (may increase HUS risk). Care is supportive: fluids, transfusion, and dialysis if needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q118$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, true, 543, $s$Two hours into a blood transfusion, a patient develops severe breathlessness, fever, hypotension and bilateral infiltrates on chest X-ray. JVP is not raised, BNP is normal, and furosemide does not help. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Transfusion-associated circulatory overload (TACO)", "Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction", "Anaphylactic transfusion reaction", "Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI)
TRANSFUSION-RELATED ACUTE LUNG INJURY (TRALI) occurs within 6 hours of transfusion. Donor antibodies attack the recipient's white cells, which damage lung capillaries, causing them to LEAK (non-cardiogenic pulmonary oedema). Features: SEVERE BREATHLESSNESS, FEVER, HYPOTENSION, BILATERAL INFILTRATES. Key differences from fluid overload: JVP is NOT raised, BNP is NORMAL, and FUROSEMIDE does NOT help.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Transfusion-associated circulatory overload (TACO) — TACO (fluid overload) causes HYPERTENSION, raised JVP, high BNP, and improves with furosemide.
• Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction — Acute haemolytic reaction causes fever, loin pain, dark urine and hypotension, but not bilateral lung infiltrates.
• Anaphylactic transfusion reaction — Anaphylaxis causes urticaria, wheeze and hypotension, usually within minutes.

⭐ Key point: Stop the transfusion, give oxygen and respiratory support (may need ventilation). Report to the blood bank; the donor may be excluded from future donations.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q149$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, true, 544, $s$Ten minutes into a blood transfusion, a patient develops fever, rigors, loin pain, hypotension and dark red urine, and starts oozing from the cannula site. The FIRST action is to:$s$, $s$["Give IV chlorphenamine and continue the transfusion at the same rate", "Flush the rest of the unit in quickly with saline, then send samples", "Stop the transfusion and keep the vein open with saline via a new set", "Slow the transfusion and give paracetamol for the fever and rigors"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stop the transfusion and keep the vein open with saline via a new set
An ACUTE HAEMOLYTIC TRANSFUSION REACTION usually happens because the WRONG BLOOD (ABO-incompatible) was given. The recipient's antibodies destroy the donor red cells. Features within minutes: FEVER, RIGORS, LOIN PAIN, HYPOTENSION, DARK RED URINE (haemoglobinuria), and OOZING (DIC). The FIRST action is to STOP THE TRANSFUSION immediately, and KEEP THE VEIN OPEN with 0.9% SALINE through a NEW giving set (to avoid giving more incompatible blood that remains in the old line). Then call the doctor, check patient ID and the blood unit, and start resuscitation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give IV chlorphenamine and continue the transfusion at the same rate — Antihistamines don't treat haemolysis, and continuing the transfusion could be fatal.
• Flush the rest of the unit in quickly with saline, then send samples — Flushing the remaining blood would give MORE incompatible blood.
• Slow the transfusion and give paracetamol for the fever and rigors — Slowing the transfusion still gives incompatible blood.

⭐ Key point: Send the blood bag and giving set back to the lab, with blood samples (FBC, group and crossmatch, DAT, U&Es, clotting) and a urine sample. Report the incident.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q150$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, true, 545, $s$A sickle cell crisis transfusion target is usually HbS below:$s$, $s$["70%", "50%", "90%", "30%"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 30%
In severe sickle cell complications (acute chest syndrome, stroke, multi-organ failure), the aim of transfusion is to reduce the proportion of sickle haemoglobin (HbS) to BELOW 30%. This means most red cells carry normal haemoglobin (HbA), reducing sickling, improving blood flow and oxygen delivery. EXCHANGE transfusion removes sickle cells while giving normal cells.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 70% — 70% HbS would still allow lots of sickling.
• 50% — 50% is not low enough for severe crises.
• 90% — 90% is close to untreated levels.

⭐ Key point: Avoid raising the haemoglobin above about 100 g/L with simple transfusion, because thicker blood increases sickling and stroke risk.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q111$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 546, $s$Haemophilia A results from a deficiency of which clotting factor?$s$, $s$["Factor VIII", "Factor IX", "Factor XI", "Factor XIII"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Factor VIII
Haemophilia A is an X-linked inherited bleeding disorder caused by a lack of clotting factor VIII. It mostly affects males; females are usually carriers. Patients bleed into joints (haemarthrosis) and muscles. The blood test shows a prolonged APTT with a normal PT and platelet count. Treatment is factor VIII concentrate (or desmopressin in mild cases).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Factor IX — Factor IX deficiency is haemophilia B (Christmas disease).
• Factor XI — Factor XI deficiency is haemophilia C, a milder form.
• Factor XIII — Factor XIII deficiency is a rare disorder of clot stabilisation, not haemophilia A.

⭐ Key point: Haemophilia A = factor VIII; B = factor IX. Avoid IM injections, aspirin and NSAIDs, and give the factor BEFORE procedures.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q7$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 547, $s$Laboratory findings consistent with disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC) include:$s$, $s$["Shortened PT/aPTT and elevated platelets", "Prolonged PT/aPTT, thrombocytopenia, low fibrinogen, and elevated D-dimer", "Normal fibrinogen and normal D-dimer", "Isolated elevated hemoglobin"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Prolonged PT/aPTT, thrombocytopenia, low fibrinogen, and elevated D-dimer
In DIC, small clots form throughout the body. These use up platelets and clotting factors, so the patient then BLEEDS from everywhere: line sites, gums, wounds. Laboratory findings: PT and APTT PROLONGED (factors used up), platelets LOW, fibrinogen LOW, and D-dimer HIGH (clot breakdown). Causes include sepsis, trauma, placental abruption, amniotic fluid embolism and HELLP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shortened PT/aPTT and elevated platelets — Shortened clotting times with high platelets do not fit DIC, which consumes clotting factors.
• Normal fibrinogen and normal D-dimer — Normal fibrinogen and D-dimer rule DIC out.
• Isolated elevated hemoglobin — Haemoglobin usually FALLS in DIC (bleeding and haemolysis); a raised haemoglobin alone is unrelated.

⭐ Key point: Treat the cause. Replace with platelets, FFP and cryoprecipitate (for low fibrinogen) if the patient is bleeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q24$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 548, $s$A key chest X-ray sign of pneumothorax is:$s$, $s$["Increased lung markings throughout", "Absence of lung markings peripheral to a visible pleural line", "Bilateral diffuse infiltrates", "Widened mediastinum"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Absence of lung markings peripheral to a visible pleural line
In a pneumothorax, air enters the pleural space and the lung collapses inwards. On X-ray you see a thin white line (the visceral pleural edge), and OUTSIDE it there are NO lung markings (black space with no blood vessels). On a supine ICU film, look for the deep sulcus sign (a deep, dark costophrenic angle).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased lung markings throughout — Increased lung markings suggest fluid, infection or fibrosis, not pneumothorax.
• Bilateral diffuse infiltrates — Bilateral diffuse infiltrates suggest ARDS, pulmonary oedema or pneumonia.
• Widened mediastinum — A widened mediastinum suggests aortic injury.

⭐ Key point: TENSION pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis (distress, low BP, trachea deviated away, absent breath sounds). Decompress with a needle immediately; don't wait for an X-ray.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q42$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 549, $s$CT pulmonary angiography is the imaging investigation of choice to diagnose:$s$, $s$["Pulmonary embolism", "Simple rib fracture", "Peptic ulcer disease", "Osteoarthritis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pulmonary embolism
CT pulmonary angiography (CTPA) injects contrast into a vein and scans the chest as the contrast passes through the lung arteries. A clot shows as a dark "filling defect" in a bright vessel. It is fast, widely available and accurate, so it is the first-choice test for PE. A V/Q scan is used when contrast cannot be given (kidney failure, contrast allergy, pregnancy in some centres).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple rib fracture — A rib fracture is seen on a plain X-ray or a normal CT; contrast angiography is not needed.
• Peptic ulcer disease — Peptic ulcer is diagnosed by endoscopy (OGD).
• Osteoarthritis — Osteoarthritis is diagnosed clinically and with plain X-rays.

⭐ Key point: Use the Wells score and D-dimer first in stable patients. A low score with a normal D-dimer makes PE unlikely.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q54$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 550, $s$Spontaneous bleeding risk significantly increases when the platelet count falls below approximately:$s$, $s$["150,000/microL", "100,000/microL", "10,000–20,000/microL", "250,000/microL"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10,000–20,000/microL
The normal platelet count is 150,000–400,000/microL (150–400 × 10⁹/L). Below 150,000 is THROMBOCYTOPENIA, but the bleeding risk depends on how low it goes: below about 50,000 there is bleeding with surgery or trauma; below 20,000 there is risk of SPONTANEOUS bleeding (petechiae, gum bleeding, nosebleeds); below 10,000 there is high risk of serious internal bleeding, including into the brain. Prophylactic platelet transfusion is usually given below 10,000 (or below 20,000 with fever/sepsis). Nursing care: bleeding precautions — soft toothbrush, no IM injections, avoid aspirin/NSAIDs, electric razor, prevent falls, check stool/urine for blood.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 150,000/microL — 150,000/microL is the LOWER LIMIT OF NORMAL; spontaneous bleeding does not occur here.
• 100,000/microL — 100,000/microL is mild thrombocytopenia; bleeding is unlikely without trauma or surgery.
• 250,000/microL — 250,000/microL is a completely normal count.

⭐ Key point: Spontaneous bleeding risk rises below 10,000–20,000/microL. Normal platelets 150,000–400,000.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q16$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 551, $s$Culture and sensitivity testing of a clinical specimen is primarily performed to:$s$, $s$["Identify the causative organism and determine which antibiotics it is sensitive to, guiding targeted therapy", "Measure the patient's haemoglobin level", "Assess kidney function", "Determine blood type"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Identify the causative organism and determine which antibiotics it is sensitive to, guiding targeted therapy
CULTURE AND SENSITIVITY (C&S) has two parts. CULTURE: the specimen (blood, urine, sputum, wound swab, CSF) is grown on special media to find out WHICH organism is causing the infection. SENSITIVITY: the organism is tested against different antibiotics to see which ones kill or stop it (sensitive) and which ones it resists (resistant). This guides TARGETED therapy — narrowing from broad-spectrum to the right drug, which improves outcomes and reduces antimicrobial resistance. Nursing points: collect specimens with sterile technique and BEFORE starting antibiotics where possible (without delaying antibiotics in sepsis), label correctly, and send quickly. Results usually take 48–72 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Measure the patient's haemoglobin level — Haemoglobin is measured by a full blood count (FBC), not by culture.
• Assess kidney function — Kidney function is assessed by urea, creatinine, eGFR and electrolytes, not culture.
• Determine blood type — Blood type is found by blood grouping and cross-matching, not culture.

⭐ Key point: C&S = identify the ORGANISM + find which ANTIBIOTICS work. Take specimens before antibiotics if it causes no delay.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q18$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 552, $s$Fibrinogen plays its key role in coagulation by:$s$, $s$["Inhibiting platelet aggregation", "Being converted to fibrin to form the structural clot mesh", "Breaking down existing clots", "Regulating serum calcium"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Being converted to fibrin to form the structural clot mesh
FIBRINOGEN (clotting factor I) is a soluble protein made by the liver. At the end of the clotting cascade, THROMBIN cuts fibrinogen into FIBRIN. Fibrin strands join together and are cross-linked by factor XIII to form a strong MESH that traps red cells and platelets, turning the soft platelet plug into a firm, stable clot. Normal fibrinogen is about 2–4 g/L. Low fibrinogen (e.g. in DIC, massive haemorrhage, postpartum haemorrhage, liver failure) causes uncontrolled bleeding and is treated with cryoprecipitate or fibrinogen concentrate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Inhibiting platelet aggregation — Fibrinogen does not INHIBIT platelet aggregation; it actually helps platelets stick together through the GP IIb/IIIa receptor. Drugs like aspirin and clopidogrel inhibit aggregation.
• Breaking down existing clots — BREAKING DOWN clots (fibrinolysis) is done by PLASMIN (activated by tPA), and produces D-dimer from fibrin.
• Regulating serum calcium — Serum calcium is regulated by parathyroid hormone, vitamin D and calcitonin, not fibrinogen (though calcium is factor IV in clotting).

⭐ Key point: Fibrinogen → (thrombin) → FIBRIN mesh = stable clot. Low fibrinogen → give cryoprecipitate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q25$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 553, $s$Severe/major reactions to contrast media can include all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Laryngeal oedema", "Cardiac arrhythmia", "Respiratory arrest", "Improved renal perfusion"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Improved renal perfusion
Contrast media (iodinated contrast for CT, angiography) can cause reactions. MILD: warmth, nausea, itching, mild urticaria. MODERATE: widespread hives, vomiting, bronchospasm, facial swelling. SEVERE (anaphylactoid): LARYNGEAL OEDEMA (airway closure), severe bronchospasm, RESPIRATORY ARREST, shock, CARDIAC ARRHYTHMIA and cardiac arrest. Treatment of severe reactions: stop contrast, call for help, airway, oxygen, IM adrenaline 0.5 mg (1:1000), IV fluids, antihistamine and steroid. Contrast can also cause CONTRAST-INDUCED NEPHROPATHY (worsened kidney function, a rise in creatinine 48–72 h later) — it does NOT improve renal perfusion. Risk is reduced by checking creatinine, hydrating, and holding metformin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Laryngeal oedema — LARYNGEAL OEDEMA IS a severe reaction — it can block the airway.
• Cardiac arrhythmia — CARDIAC ARRHYTHMIA IS a severe reaction, especially in anaphylactoid shock.
• Respiratory arrest — RESPIRATORY ARREST IS a severe reaction — the end point of airway/bronchospasm problems.

⭐ Key point: Severe contrast reaction = laryngeal oedema, bronchospasm, arrhythmia, arrest → IM adrenaline. Contrast can HARM the kidneys, never improve them.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q41$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 554, $s$Positive contrast agents (e.g. iodine, barium) appear on X-ray as:$s$, $s$["Dark/lucent", "Opaque/white", "Invisible", "Same density as air"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Opaque/white
On X-ray, the WHITER an area, the MORE X-rays it has blocked. POSITIVE contrast agents like IODINE and BARIUM have HIGH atomic numbers, so they absorb many X-rays and look OPAQUE/WHITE (radio-OPAQUE), similar to bone or metal. This lets us outline the inside of hollow structures: barium swallow or enema for the gut, iodine for blood vessels (angiography), urinary tract (IVU) or CT enhancement. NEGATIVE contrast agents (air, CO2) absorb few X-rays and look DARK. Nursing: ask about iodine allergy and previous reactions, check kidney function before iodinated contrast, and give fluids/laxatives after barium to prevent constipation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dark/lucent — DARK/LUCENT appearance is seen with AIR or negative contrast, not iodine/barium.
• Invisible — They are certainly not invisible — they are designed to be clearly seen.
• Same density as air — Air density is BLACK on X-ray; positive contrast is the opposite (white).

⭐ Key point: Positive contrast (iodine, barium) = WHITE/radio-opaque. Air/negative contrast = black.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q44$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 555, $s$The three core principles for minimising radiation exposure during a radiological incident are:$s$, $s$["Time, distance, and shielding", "Speed, volume, and pressure", "Triage, treatment, and transport", "Isolation, ventilation, and hydration"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Time, distance, and shielding
There are three key principles to reduce RADIATION EXPOSURE (ALARA — As Low As Reasonably Achievable): (1) TIME — spend as LITTLE time as possible near the radiation source; dose increases with time. (2) DISTANCE — stay as FAR away as possible; radiation falls with the SQUARE of the distance (inverse square law), so doubling the distance cuts exposure to one quarter. (3) SHIELDING — put a barrier between you and the source, e.g. lead aprons, thyroid collars, lead screens, concrete walls. Nurses use these when patients have portable X-rays (step back about 2 metres or behind a screen) or have received radioactive implants.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Speed, volume, and pressure — Speed, volume and pressure are not radiation protection principles.
• Triage, treatment, and transport — Triage, treatment and transport are steps in managing a mass casualty incident, not principles of reducing radiation dose.
• Isolation, ventilation, and hydration — Isolation, ventilation and hydration are not the three core principles for radiation protection.

⭐ Key point: Radiation protection = TIME (less), DISTANCE (more — inverse square law), SHIELDING (lead).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q45$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 556, $s$CK-MB (creatine kinase-MB) is used clinically to help assess:$s$, $s$["Recent or ongoing myocardial injury, particularly reinfarction", "Kidney function", "Coagulation status", "Blood glucose control"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Recent or ongoing myocardial injury, particularly reinfarction
CK-MB is the form of creatine kinase found mainly in HEART MUSCLE. When heart muscle is damaged (myocardial infarction), CK-MB leaks into the blood: it RISES 3–6 hours after injury, PEAKS at 12–24 hours, and RETURNS TO NORMAL within 48–72 hours. Because it clears quickly, it is especially useful to detect REINFARCTION (a new heart attack a few days after the first), when troponin is still high from the first event (troponin stays raised for 7–14 days). Troponin (I or T) is now the preferred, most sensitive and specific marker for diagnosing MI.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Kidney function — KIDNEY function is assessed with urea, creatinine and eGFR, not CK-MB.
• Coagulation status — COAGULATION status is assessed with PT/INR, aPTT, platelets and fibrinogen.
• Blood glucose control — BLOOD GLUCOSE control is assessed with glucose and HbA1c.

⭐ Key point: CK-MB = heart muscle damage; rises 3–6 h, normal by 48–72 h → useful for REINFARCTION. Troponin stays up 7–14 days.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q70$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 557, $s$Trending serial lactate levels during shock resuscitation is useful because:$s$, $s$["Lactate clearance correlates with improving tissue perfusion", "Lactate has no relationship to perfusion status", "It replaces the need for vital sign monitoring", "It only reflects liver function"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lactate clearance correlates with improving tissue perfusion
LACTATE is made when tissues don't get enough oxygen and switch to ANAEROBIC metabolism. In shock, a raised lactate (above 2 mmol/L; above 4 is severe) shows tissue HYPOPERFUSION. Measuring it SERIALLY (e.g. every 2–4 hours) shows whether resuscitation is working: a FALLING lactate (LACTATE CLEARANCE, e.g. a drop of 10–20% or more) means perfusion is improving and is linked to better survival; a static or rising lactate means the patient still needs more treatment (fluids, vasopressors, source control). This is why the Surviving Sepsis Campaign recommends re-measuring lactate if the first value is raised.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lactate has no relationship to perfusion status — Lactate DOES relate to perfusion — that is exactly why it is measured in shock.
• It replaces the need for vital sign monitoring — It does NOT replace vital signs monitoring; it is used together with BP, heart rate, urine output, capillary refill and mental state.
• It only reflects liver function — The liver clears lactate, so liver failure can raise it, but it mainly reflects tissue perfusion/oxygen delivery, not "only" liver function.

⭐ Key point: Falling lactate (lactate clearance) = improving tissue perfusion. Rising lactate = ongoing hypoperfusion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q73$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 558, $s$Both clotting and bleeding occur in DIC because:$s$, $s$["Bleeding uses up clotting factors faster than they are replaced", "The intrinsic pathway releases excess clotting factors", "Only clotting occurs in DIC", "Excess thrombin uses up clotting factors faster than they can be replaced"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Excess thrombin uses up clotting factors faster than they can be replaced
DIC (Disseminated Intravascular Coagulation) is triggered by severe illness — sepsis, major trauma, obstetric emergencies (placental abruption, amniotic fluid embolism), malignancy, snake bite. The trigger causes widespread activation of clotting and EXCESS THROMBIN production. Thrombin forms tiny clots (microthrombi) all through small blood vessels → organ damage (kidneys, lungs, brain, skin). This uses up (CONSUMES) clotting factors, fibrinogen and platelets FASTER than the liver and marrow can replace them — so-called "consumptive coagulopathy" — and the clots are broken down (fibrinolysis), producing D-dimer. With no factors left, the patient BLEEDS from everywhere (IV sites, gums, wounds, GI tract). Labs: low platelets, long PT/aPTT, LOW fibrinogen, HIGH D-dimer. Treat the cause; replace with platelets, FFP and cryoprecipitate if bleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bleeding uses up clotting factors faster than they are replaced — Bleeding is a RESULT of factor consumption by clotting — not the cause of the consumption.
• The intrinsic pathway releases excess clotting factors — The intrinsic pathway does not "release excess clotting factors"; factors are used up, not overproduced.
• Only clotting occurs in DIC — DIC is not "only clotting" — the hallmark is BOTH clotting and bleeding.

⭐ Key point: DIC: excess THROMBIN → widespread micro-clots → clotting factors and platelets CONSUMED → bleeding. Low fibrinogen, high D-dimer.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q128$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 559, $s$Other than transfusion, when is it necessary to take the Rh Factor into consideration?$s$, $s$["Cathertization", "Spleen Rupture", "Pregnancy", "Blood Donation"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pregnancy
The Rh (D) factor is a protein on red blood cells. People are Rh-POSITIVE (have it) or Rh-NEGATIVE (do not). Apart from blood transfusion, it is most important in PREGNANCY. If an Rh-NEGATIVE mother carries an Rh-POSITIVE baby, foetal blood can enter her circulation (during delivery, miscarriage, abortion, ectopic pregnancy, abdominal trauma, amniocentesis, bleeding). She makes anti-D antibodies (SENSITISATION). In a LATER pregnancy, these antibodies cross the placenta and destroy the red cells of an Rh-positive baby — HAEMOLYTIC DISEASE OF THE NEWBORN (anaemia, jaundice, hydrops foetalis). Prevention: give ANTI-D IMMUNOGLOBULIN to Rh-negative mothers at about 28 weeks, within 72 hours after delivery of an Rh-positive baby, and after any sensitising event.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cathertization — CATHETERISATION does not involve the Rh factor.
• Spleen Rupture — SPLEEN RUPTURE may need transfusion (already covered by "other than transfusion"), but the Rh factor is not otherwise relevant.
• Blood Donation — BLOOD DONATION is part of transfusion services — the question asks for situations other than transfusion.

⭐ Key point: Rh factor matters in PREGNANCY: Rh-negative mother + Rh-positive baby → sensitisation → give ANTI-D within 72 h.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q140$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 560, $s$Fluid movement out of capillaries into the interstitium is favoured by:$s$, $s$["High capillary hydrostatic pressure and low plasma oncotic pressure", "Low capillary hydrostatic pressure only", "High plasma oncotic pressure only", "Equal hydrostatic and oncotic pressures"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: High capillary hydrostatic pressure and low plasma oncotic pressure
Fluid movement across capillary walls depends on STARLING FORCES: CAPILLARY HYDROSTATIC PRESSURE (the blood pressure inside capillaries) PUSHES fluid OUT; PLASMA ONCOTIC PRESSURE (from proteins, mainly albumin) PULLS fluid back IN. So fluid moves OUT into the tissues (causing oedema) when HYDROSTATIC pressure is HIGH (e.g. heart failure, fluid overload) and/or ONCOTIC pressure is LOW (e.g. low albumin in liver disease, nephrotic syndrome, malnutrition). Leaky capillaries (sepsis, burns) also increase outflow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low capillary hydrostatic pressure only — Low hydrostatic pressure alone would reduce fluid leaving the capillaries.
• High plasma oncotic pressure only — High plasma oncotic pressure pulls fluid INTO capillaries.
• Equal hydrostatic and oncotic pressures — Equal pressures would produce little net movement.

⭐ Key point: Oedema mechanisms: high hydrostatic pressure, low oncotic pressure, increased capillary permeability, and lymphatic blockage.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q13$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 561, $s$During a blood transfusion, the patient's RR is 24 and BP 90/60. The nurse's priority is to:$s$, $s$["Slow the infusion rate", "Flush the line with normal saline through the same giving set", "Stop the transfusion immediately", "Notify the doctor and continue"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stop the transfusion immediately
A rising RESPIRATORY RATE (24/min) and FALLING BP (90/60) during a blood transfusion suggest a possible SERIOUS TRANSFUSION REACTION — e.g. acute haemolytic reaction, anaphylaxis, sepsis from contaminated blood, or TRALI. The PRIORITY is to STOP THE TRANSFUSION IMMEDIATELY to prevent more of the reacting blood from entering. Then keep the vein open with 0.9% saline through a NEW giving set, check vital signs and patient ID, and call the doctor.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Slow the infusion rate — Slowing the infusion still allows more harmful blood to enter.
• Flush the line with normal saline through the same giving set — Flushing through the SAME set pushes more donor blood into the patient.
• Notify the doctor and continue — Continuing while notifying the doctor delays stopping the reaction.

⭐ Key point: Return the blood bag and giving set to the blood bank, and send blood and urine samples as per protocol.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q16$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 562, $s$A commonly used massive transfusion protocol ratio for packed red cells, plasma, and platelets is approximately:$s$, $s$["1:1:1", "10:1:1", "1:10:1", "5:5:1"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1:1:1
In MASSIVE HAEMORRHAGE (e.g. major trauma), giving only red cells and saline dilutes clotting factors and platelets, worsening bleeding. MASSIVE TRANSFUSION PROTOCOLS give PACKED RED CELLS, PLASMA (FFP) and PLATELETS in a ratio of about 1:1:1 (balanced resuscitation), which more closely replaces whole blood and improves survival (PROPPR trial). Cryoprecipitate (fibrinogen), calcium and tranexamic acid are also given.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10:1:1 — 10:1:1 gives far too little plasma and platelets.
• 1:10:1 — 1:10:1 gives far too much plasma.
• 5:5:1 — 5:5:1 gives too few platelets.

⭐ Key point: Watch for complications of massive transfusion: hypothermia, hypocalcaemia (citrate), hyperkalaemia, acidosis, and TRALI/TACO.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q18$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 563, $s$A patient develops urticaria and itching during a blood transfusion. The nurse should:$s$, $s$["Stop the transfusion", "Administer diphenhydramine", "Slow the infusion rate", "A and B"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A and B
URTICARIA (hives) and ITCHING during a transfusion indicate a MILD ALLERGIC reaction (to plasma proteins in the donor blood). The nurse should STOP (pause) THE TRANSFUSION and ADMINISTER an ANTIHISTAMINE such as DIPHENHYDRAMINE (or chlorphenamine), as prescribed. If symptoms resolve completely and there are no other signs (no fever, breathing difficulty or hypotension), the doctor may allow the transfusion to restart slowly. So "A and B" is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stop the transfusion — Stopping alone is incomplete; the symptoms also need treatment.
• Administer diphenhydramine — An antihistamine alone without pausing the transfusion is unsafe.
• Slow the infusion rate — Slowing the rate is not enough.

⭐ Key point: If wheeze, swelling or hypotension develop, treat as anaphylaxis (IM adrenaline) and do not restart.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q20$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 564, $s$A key early clinical sign that should raise suspicion of DIC in a critically ill patient is:$s$, $s$["Unexplained oozing from IV/puncture sites alongside signs of organ dysfunction", "Isolated mild headache with no other findings", "Normal coagulation results with no bleeding", "Bradycardia alone"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Unexplained oozing from IV/puncture sites alongside signs of organ dysfunction
DISSEMINATED INTRAVASCULAR COAGULATION (DIC) is widespread activation of clotting in critical illness (sepsis, trauma, obstetric emergencies, cancer). Clotting factors and platelets are used up, causing BLEEDING. A KEY EARLY SIGN is UNEXPLAINED OOZING from IV sites, puncture sites, wounds or gums, ALONGSIDE ORGAN DYSFUNCTION (from tiny clots blocking small vessels — e.g. kidney injury, confusion, hypoxia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Isolated mild headache with no other findings — An isolated mild headache is not suggestive.
• Normal coagulation results with no bleeding — Normal clotting with no bleeding does not suggest DIC.
• Bradycardia alone — Bradycardia alone is not suggestive.

⭐ Key point: Investigate with platelets, PT/APTT, fibrinogen and D-dimer; treat the underlying cause.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q21$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 565, $s$The extrinsic coagulation pathway is activated by:$s$, $s$["Contact with collagen", "Tissue factor released from damaged tissue", "Platelet aggregation alone", "Fibrinolysis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tissue factor released from damaged tissue
The EXTRINSIC coagulation pathway is activated by TISSUE FACTOR (TF, thromboplastin), released from DAMAGED TISSUE and blood vessel walls. Tissue factor combines with FACTOR VII, which then activates factor X, leading to thrombin and a clot. This pathway acts quickly after injury. It is measured by the PT/INR.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Contact with collagen — Contact with collagen (exposed surfaces) activates the INTRINSIC pathway (factor XII).
• Platelet aggregation alone — Platelet aggregation is part of primary haemostasis.
• Fibrinolysis — Fibrinolysis breaks down clots.

⭐ Key point: Warfarin mainly affects the extrinsic pathway (factor VII), so it is monitored with INR.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q22$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 566, $s$The direct Coombs test is used to detect:$s$, $s$["Antibodies bound to the surface of red blood cells", "Platelet aggregation", "White cell count", "Clotting factor deficiency"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Antibodies bound to the surface of red blood cells
The DIRECT COOMBS TEST (direct antiglobulin test, DAT) detects ANTIBODIES (or complement) already BOUND to the SURFACE of the patient's RED BLOOD CELLS. A positive result indicates IMMUNE-MEDIATED HAEMOLYSIS — e.g. haemolytic transfusion reaction, haemolytic disease of the newborn (Rh or ABO incompatibility), or autoimmune haemolytic anaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Platelet aggregation — Platelet aggregation is tested differently.
• White cell count — White cell count is part of the full blood count.
• Clotting factor deficiency — Clotting factor deficiency is tested with clotting factor assays.

⭐ Key point: The INDIRECT Coombs test detects free antibodies in the plasma (used in pregnancy screening and crossmatching).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q26$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 567, $s$Which of the following is classified as a positive (radio-opaque/white-appearing) contrast agent?$s$, $s$["Room air", "Carbon dioxide", "Barium", "Vacuum/no contrast"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Barium
CONTRAST AGENTS make structures easier to see on X-ray or CT. POSITIVE (radio-opaque) contrast agents contain heavy atoms that ABSORB X-RAYS strongly, so they appear WHITE on images. Examples: BARIUM sulphate (swallowed or given as an enema to outline the GI tract) and IODINE-based contrast (injected IV for CT scans, angiography, IVU). NEGATIVE (radiolucent) contrast agents such as AIR or CARBON DIOXIDE absorb few X-rays and appear DARK (black); they are sometimes used together with barium in "double-contrast" studies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Room air — Room air is a NEGATIVE contrast agent (appears dark).
• Carbon dioxide — Carbon dioxide is a NEGATIVE contrast agent (appears dark).
• Vacuum/no contrast — "No contrast" means no agent is used.

⭐ Key point: Before iodine contrast: check kidney function, ask about previous contrast reactions and allergies, and ensure hydration. Barium should not be used when bowel perforation is suspected.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q41$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 568, $s$A patient with a suspected small bowel obstruction has a CT scan with oral contrast ordered. The nurse understands the choice of a water-soluble (rather than barium) contrast agent in this context is because:$s$, $s$["Water-soluble contrast is always cheaper", "If perforation is present, water-soluble contrast is safer than barium, which causes severe chemical peritonitis if it leaks into the peritoneum", "Barium provides better image quality in every situation", "There is no difference in safety between the two agents"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: If perforation is present, water-soluble contrast is safer than barium, which causes severe chemical peritonitis if it leaks into the peritoneum
If there is a PERFORATION, BARIUM leaking into the peritoneal cavity causes severe CHEMICAL PERITONITIS and adhesions (it is not absorbed). WATER-SOLUBLE contrast (e.g. Gastrografin) is absorbed and causes much less harm if it leaks, so it is SAFER when perforation is possible.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Water-soluble contrast is always cheaper — Cost is not the reason.
• Barium provides better image quality in every situation — Barium does not always give better images.
• There is no difference in safety between the two agents — There is a significant difference in safety.

⭐ Key point: Water-soluble contrast can also help resolve adhesive small bowel obstruction (diagnostic and therapeutic).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q43$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 569, $s$Blood transfusion reactions can be classified broadly as:$s$, $s$["Only immediate reactions exist; delayed reactions are not possible", "Immediate (e.g. acute haemolytic, allergic) and delayed (e.g. delayed haemolytic, iron overload) reactions", "Only allergic reactions occur", "Reactions cannot be classified in any meaningful way"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Immediate (e.g. acute haemolytic, allergic) and delayed (e.g. delayed haemolytic, iron overload) reactions
BLOOD TRANSFUSION REACTIONS are broadly classified by timing: IMMEDIATE (ACUTE) reactions occur within 24 hours — acute haemolytic reaction (ABO incompatibility), allergic reactions (urticaria to anaphylaxis), febrile non-haemolytic reaction, TRALI (lung injury), TACO (fluid overload), and bacterial contamination (sepsis); DELAYED reactions occur days to weeks later — delayed haemolytic reaction, transfusion-associated graft-versus-host disease, post-transfusion purpura, and IRON OVERLOAD (with repeated transfusions).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only immediate reactions exist; delayed reactions are not possible — Delayed reactions do occur.
• Only allergic reactions occur — Many reaction types exist, not just allergic.
• Reactions cannot be classified in any meaningful way — Reactions are classified meaningfully, which guides management.

⭐ Key point: For any suspected reaction: stop the transfusion, keep the vein open with saline, check vital signs and patient ID, and inform the doctor and blood bank.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q17$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 570, $s$Haematological investigations typically assess:$s$, $s$["Blood cell counts, haemoglobin, and coagulation parameters", "Only urine composition", "Only stool consistency", "Only skin pigmentation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Blood cell counts, haemoglobin, and coagulation parameters
HAEMATOLOGICAL investigations study the BLOOD and blood-forming tissues. They include: FULL BLOOD COUNT (haemoglobin, red cells, white cells and differential, platelets, haematocrit), blood film, COAGULATION tests (PT/INR, APTT, fibrinogen, D-dimer), reticulocyte count, iron studies, B12 and folate, blood group and crossmatch, and bone marrow examination.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only urine composition — Urine composition is assessed by urinalysis (renal/biochemistry).
• Only stool consistency — Stool consistency is a gastrointestinal assessment.
• Only skin pigmentation — Skin pigmentation is a dermatological assessment.

⭐ Key point: Daily FBC and clotting tests are common in ICU to monitor bleeding, infection, and drug effects (e.g. heparin).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q24$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 571, $s$Doppler ultrasound is particularly useful for assessing:$s$, $s$["Bone density", "Blood flow and vascular patency (e.g. DVT, graft/fistula flow)", "Lung consolidation", "Brain electrical activity"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Blood flow and vascular patency (e.g. DVT, graft/fistula flow)
DOPPLER ULTRASOUND measures the movement of blood using the Doppler effect (sound waves change frequency when bouncing off moving blood cells). It is useful for assessing BLOOD FLOW and VASCULAR PATENCY: detecting DVT, arterial stenosis or occlusion, checking AV fistula and graft flow, and assessing fetal and placental blood flow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bone density — Bone density is measured by DEXA scan.
• Lung consolidation — Lung consolidation is assessed by X-ray, CT, or lung ultrasound.
• Brain electrical activity — Brain electrical activity is measured by EEG.

⭐ Key point: Bedside Doppler is used to check peripheral pulses when they can't be felt.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q44$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 572, $s$The minimum amount of pleural fluid usually needed to be visible on a standard erect chest X-ray is:$s$, $s$["10–50ml", "150–300ml", "500–750ml", "1000–1500ml"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 150–300ml
On a standard ERECT (PA) chest X-ray, about 150–300 mL of pleural fluid is needed to blunt the COSTOPHRENIC ANGLE and be visible. Smaller amounts can be detected by lateral X-ray (about 50 mL), ultrasound (as little as 5–20 mL), or CT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10–50ml — 10–50 mL is too little to see on a standard PA film.
• 500–750ml — 500–750 mL is far more than the minimum.
• 1000–1500ml — 1,000–1,500 mL would cause obvious large opacity.

⭐ Key point: Bedside ultrasound is the most sensitive and practical tool for detecting and draining pleural fluid in ICU.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q45$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 573, $s$Concerning the following investigation in DIC, which is not correct?$s$, $s$["Prothrombin time is prolonged", "Platelet count is reduced", "Clotting factors are reduced", "Fibrinogen is increased"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fibrinogen is increased
In DISSEMINATED INTRAVASCULAR COAGULATION (DIC), a serious illness (sepsis, trauma, obstetric emergencies, cancer) triggers widespread clotting in small vessels. This CONSUMES clotting factors and platelets, so the patient both clots and BLEEDS. Typical lab results: PROLONGED PT/INR and APTT (clotting factors used up), LOW PLATELET COUNT, LOW CLOTTING FACTORS, LOW FIBRINOGEN (used up), and HIGH D-DIMER/FDPs (clot breakdown). The statement "FIBRINOGEN is INCREASED" is NOT correct — fibrinogen is usually DECREASED (although it can be normal early because it rises in inflammation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prothrombin time is prolonged — Prothrombin time IS prolonged in DIC.
• Platelet count is reduced — Platelet count IS reduced.
• Clotting factors are reduced — Clotting factors ARE reduced (consumed).

⭐ Key point: Treat the underlying cause; give platelets, FFP and cryoprecipitate if bleeding or before procedures. Use the ISTH DIC score to help diagnosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q124$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 574, $s$A patient's chest X-ray shows a 'ground glass' appearance bilaterally with worsening hypoxaemia following a recent large-volume blood transfusion. The nurse should consider:$s$, $s$["No further evaluation necessary", "Simple volume overload with no other cause needed", "Transfusion-Related Acute Lung Injury (TRALI), a serious transfusion complication", "Normal expected finding after transfusion"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Transfusion-Related Acute Lung Injury (TRALI), a serious transfusion complication
TRANSFUSION-RELATED ACUTE LUNG INJURY (TRALI) occurs within 6 hours of transfusion. Donor antibodies trigger inflammation in the lung capillaries, causing them to leak. Features: BILATERAL "GROUND-GLASS" infiltrates, WORSENING HYPOXAEMIA, fever, and hypotension. It is not caused by volume overload.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No further evaluation necessary — It requires further evaluation.
• Simple volume overload with no other cause needed — Volume overload (TACO) causes hypertension and raised JVP and responds to diuretics.
• Normal expected finding after transfusion — It is not a normal finding.

⭐ Key point: Stop the transfusion, provide oxygen and respiratory support, and report to the blood bank.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q183$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 575, $s$A patient with suspected PE has a low Wells score. Which test is the appropriate next step?$s$, $s$["D-dimer testing to help rule out PE", "Pulmonary angiography", "V/Q scan only", "Immediate CT pulmonary angiography regardless of score"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: D-dimer testing to help rule out PE
For suspected PE with a LOW WELLS SCORE (low probability), the next step is a D-DIMER test. A NEGATIVE D-dimer effectively RULES OUT PE without imaging. If positive, CTPA is done.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pulmonary angiography — Pulmonary angiography is invasive and rarely used.
• V/Q scan only — V/Q scan is used when CTPA is contraindicated.
• Immediate CT pulmonary angiography regardless of score — Immediate CTPA for all low-risk patients exposes many to unnecessary radiation and contrast.

⭐ Key point: D-dimer is sensitive but not specific — it can be raised in many conditions (surgery, infection, pregnancy).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q185$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 576, $s$A patient develops signs of sepsis with an unknown source. Blood cultures are drawn from two different sites before starting antibiotics. This practice is done primarily to:$s$, $s$["Save laboratory costs", "Reduce patient discomfort", "Delay treatment unnecessarily", "Improve the diagnostic yield and help distinguish true bacteraemia from contamination"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Improve the diagnostic yield and help distinguish true bacteraemia from contamination
Taking BLOOD CULTURES from TWO DIFFERENT SITES before antibiotics IMPROVES the chance of DETECTING the organism (higher yield) and helps DISTINGUISH TRUE BACTERAEMIA (organism grows in both sets) from CONTAMINATION (skin organism grows in only one set).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Save laboratory costs — It doesn't save costs.
• Reduce patient discomfort — It doesn't reduce discomfort.
• Delay treatment unnecessarily — It shouldn't delay antibiotics significantly.

⭐ Key point: Use aseptic technique and fill bottles with the correct blood volume.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q188$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 577, $s$A patient with acute hepatic failure requires monitoring for coagulopathy primarily because:$s$, $s$["The liver has no role in clotting factor production", "Coagulopathy never occurs in liver disease", "Only platelets are affected, never clotting factors", "The liver synthesises most clotting factors, so failure impairs coagulation and increases bleeding risk"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The liver synthesises most clotting factors, so failure impairs coagulation and increases bleeding risk
The LIVER makes most CLOTTING FACTORS (including II, V, VII, IX, X, fibrinogen). In acute hepatic failure, production falls, causing COAGULOPATHY (raised INR) and BLEEDING RISK.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The liver has no role in clotting factor production — The liver has a major role in clotting.
• Coagulopathy never occurs in liver disease — Coagulopathy is common in liver disease.
• Only platelets are affected, never clotting factors — Both clotting factors and platelets can be affected.

⭐ Key point: INR is a key marker of liver synthetic function.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q192$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 578, $s$Which of the following is NOT a therapeutic (physiotherapy) modality?$s$, $s$["Cryotherapy", "X-ray machine", "Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulator (TENS)", "Therapeutic ultrasound"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: X-ray machine
THERAPEUTIC (physiotherapy) MODALITIES are physical agents used to TREAT patients — to relieve pain, reduce swelling and promote healing. Examples: CRYOTHERAPY (cold packs/ice — reduces pain, swelling and inflammation after acute injury); TENS (electrical stimulation for pain relief); THERAPEUTIC ULTRASOUND (deep heating, tissue healing); heat therapy (hot packs, infrared); electrical muscle stimulation; and hydrotherapy. An X-RAY MACHINE is a DIAGNOSTIC imaging device used to visualise bones and organs; it is NOT a therapeutic modality.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cryotherapy — Cryotherapy is a therapeutic modality.
• Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulator (TENS) — TENS is a therapeutic modality.
• Therapeutic ultrasound — Therapeutic ultrasound is a therapeutic modality.

⭐ Key point: Each modality has contraindications — e.g. avoid heat over acute inflammation and avoid TENS near pacemakers.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q169$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 579, $s$A malnourished alcoholic has high fever and cough with thick, dark 'redcurrant jelly' sputum. Chest X-ray shows right upper lobe consolidation with cavitation and a bulging fissure. The most likely organism is:$s$, $s$["Mycobacterium tuberculosis", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa", "Staphylococcus aureus", "Klebsiella pneumoniae"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Klebsiella pneumoniae
KLEBSIELLA PNEUMONIAE pneumonia typically affects ALCOHOLICS, diabetics and the malnourished (often after aspiration). Features: HIGH FEVER, cough with thick, dark "REDCURRANT JELLY" sputum, and chest X-ray showing UPPER LOBE CONSOLIDATION with CAVITATION and a BULGING FISSURE (the infected lobe is swollen and heavy).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mycobacterium tuberculosis — TB causes upper lobe cavitation, but has a slower onset (weeks to months), with night sweats and weight loss.
• Pseudomonas aeruginosa — Pseudomonas usually affects patients in hospital, on ventilators, or with cystic fibrosis.
• Staphylococcus aureus — Staphylococcus aureus causes cavitating pneumonia, often after influenza.

⭐ Key point: Klebsiella can be multidrug-resistant; check sensitivities. Aspiration pneumonia in alcoholics often involves anaerobes too.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q69$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 580, $s$A patient admitted with 2 months of cough, haemoptysis, night sweats and weight loss has upper-lobe cavitation on chest X-ray. Until sputum results are back, the correct precaution is:$s$, $s$["Droplet: single room and surgical masks within 1 metre", "Standard precautions only until a sputum smear is positive", "Airborne: negative-pressure room and N95/FFP3 respirators", "Contact: gloves and gown for any contact with the patient"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Airborne: negative-pressure room and N95/FFP3 respirators
Two months of cough, HAEMOPTYSIS, NIGHT SWEATS, WEIGHT LOSS and UPPER-LOBE CAVITATION strongly suggest PULMONARY TUBERCULOSIS (TB). TB spreads through the AIR in tiny droplet nuclei that stay suspended for a long time. Until sputum tests are back, use AIRBORNE PRECAUTIONS: a NEGATIVE-PRESSURE isolation room (if available) and N95/FFP3 RESPIRATORS for staff.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Droplet: single room and surgical masks within 1 metre — Droplet precautions (surgical masks within 1 metre) are not enough for airborne TB.
• Standard precautions only until a sputum smear is positive — Waiting for a positive smear before isolation risks spreading TB.
• Contact: gloves and gown for any contact with the patient — Contact precautions alone don't prevent airborne spread.

⭐ Key point: Continue isolation until three negative sputum smears or after at least 2 weeks of effective treatment with clinical improvement. Give the patient a surgical mask when outside the room.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q70$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 581, $s$A 40-year-old has a sudden 'worst ever' headache that reached maximum intensity within a minute, with neck stiffness and photophobia. A CT head 14 hours after onset is normal. The next investigation is:$s$, $s$["MRI brain with gadolinium contrast", "Electroencephalogram to exclude seizure", "Lumbar puncture for xanthochromia", "Repeat non-contrast CT after 48 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lumbar puncture for xanthochromia
A sudden, severe "THUNDERCLAP" headache (maximum within a minute) with NECK STIFFNESS and PHOTOPHOBIA suggests SUBARACHNOID HAEMORRHAGE (SAH). CT is very sensitive within the first 6 hours, but sensitivity FALLS over time. If CT is normal more than 6 hours after onset (here, 14 hours), the next step is LUMBAR PUNCTURE (at least 12 hours after onset) to look for XANTHOCHROMIA (yellow CSF from broken-down red cells), which confirms SAH.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• MRI brain with gadolinium contrast — MRI may detect SAH, but LP for xanthochromia is the standard next investigation.
• Electroencephalogram to exclude seizure — EEG is for seizures.
• Repeat non-contrast CT after 48 hours — Waiting 48 hours risks a fatal rebleed.

⭐ Key point: If SAH is confirmed, arrange CT angiography to find the aneurysm, and give nimodipine.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q89$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 582, $s$A 75-year-old with atrial fibrillation (not anticoagulated) has sudden, severe abdominal pain that seems out of proportion to the examination — the abdomen is soft with little tenderness. Lactate is 6 mmol/L, and bloody diarrhoea appears later. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Perforated peptic ulcer", "Acute haemorrhagic pancreatitis", "Perforated sigmoid diverticulitis", "Acute mesenteric ischaemia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute mesenteric ischaemia
ACUTE MESENTERIC ISCHAEMIA occurs when blood flow to the bowel is blocked, often by an EMBOLUS from the heart in ATRIAL FIBRILLATION (especially if not anticoagulated). Classic feature: SEVERE PAIN OUT OF PROPORTION to EXAMINATION (the abdomen is soft with little tenderness early on). HIGH LACTATE shows bowel ischaemia. BLOODY DIARRHOEA appears later as the bowel dies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Perforated peptic ulcer — Perforated ulcer causes a rigid abdomen.
• Acute haemorrhagic pancreatitis — Pancreatitis causes pain radiating to the back, with raised lipase.
• Perforated sigmoid diverticulitis — Perforated diverticulitis causes left lower quadrant pain and peritonitis.

⭐ Key point: Diagnose with CT angiography. Treat with urgent surgery or interventional radiology, plus anticoagulation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q132$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 583, $s$A patient with septic shock oozes from cannula sites and the gums and has new petechiae. Platelets are 35 × 10⁹/L, PT and APTT are prolonged, fibrinogen is 0.8 g/L and D-dimer is very high. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Immune thrombocytopenic purpura", "Disseminated intravascular coagulation", "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura", "Vitamin K deficiency"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Disseminated intravascular coagulation
DISSEMINATED INTRAVASCULAR COAGULATION (DIC) occurs when a serious condition (like SEPTIC SHOCK) triggers widespread activation of clotting throughout the body. This USES UP platelets and clotting factors, leading to BLEEDING (oozing from cannula sites and gums, petechiae). Lab findings: LOW PLATELETS (35), PROLONGED PT and APTT, LOW FIBRINOGEN (0.8), and very HIGH D-DIMER (clot breakdown products).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immune thrombocytopenic purpura — ITP causes low platelets only, with normal clotting times and fibrinogen.
• Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura — TTP causes low platelets and haemolysis, but clotting times are usually NORMAL.
• Vitamin K deficiency — Vitamin K deficiency prolongs PT, but platelets and fibrinogen are normal and D-dimer is not raised.

⭐ Key point: Treat the cause (e.g. sepsis). Give blood products (platelets, FFP, cryoprecipitate) if the patient is bleeding or needs a procedure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q147$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 584, $s$A baby is visibly jaundiced at 18 hours of age, with a falling haemoglobin. The mother is blood group O Rh-positive and the baby is A Rh-positive. The most likely cause is:$s$, $s$["ABO haemolytic disease", "Breast-milk jaundice", "Biliary atresia", "Physiological jaundice"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ABO haemolytic disease
Jaundice in the FIRST 24 HOURS of life is ALWAYS ABNORMAL (pathological), usually from HAEMOLYSIS. ABO HAEMOLYTIC DISEASE occurs when the MOTHER is BLOOD GROUP O and the BABY is A or B. The mother's anti-A or anti-B antibodies cross the placenta and destroy the baby's red cells, causing JAUNDICE and a FALLING HAEMOGLOBIN.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Breast-milk jaundice — Breast-milk jaundice starts after the first week.
• Biliary atresia — Biliary atresia causes prolonged jaundice with pale stools and dark urine, usually after 2 weeks.
• Physiological jaundice — Physiological jaundice appears after 24 hours.

⭐ Key point: Check bilirubin and the direct antiglobulin test (DAT). Treat with phototherapy, and exchange transfusion if severe.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q173$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 585, $s$A 2-year-old with sickle cell anaemia suddenly becomes pale and lethargic, with HR 170 and BP 70/40 mmHg. The spleen, previously not palpable, is now enlarged to the umbilicus. Hb has fallen from 8 to 4 g/dL, and the reticulocyte count is HIGH. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Acute chest syndrome", "Vaso-occlusive (painful) crisis", "Aplastic crisis from parvovirus B19", "Acute splenic sequestration crisis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute splenic sequestration crisis
ACUTE SPLENIC SEQUESTRATION CRISIS occurs when sickled red cells get trapped in the spleen. The spleen ENLARGES RAPIDLY and Hb FALLS SUDDENLY (8 to 4). The child becomes PALE, LETHARGIC, and SHOCKED (tachycardia, hypotension). RETICULOCYTES are HIGH (the marrow is still producing cells).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute chest syndrome — Acute chest syndrome causes chest pain, fever and hypoxia.
• Vaso-occlusive (painful) crisis — Vaso-occlusive crisis causes pain without sudden severe anaemia and a large spleen.
• Aplastic crisis from parvovirus B19 — Aplastic crisis causes anaemia with LOW reticulocytes.

⭐ Key point: Treat with urgent blood transfusion and fluid resuscitation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q179$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 586, $s$About the x ray features of haemothorax$s$, $s$["An erect film is less sensitive than a supine film", "It is seen only when more than 1500 mL of blood is present", "A supine film shows a diffuse haze over the affected hemithorax", "Costophrenic blunting appears with as little as 20 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A supine film shows a diffuse haze over the affected hemithorax
A patient lying flat (SUPINE) — as trauma patients usually are — has blood that spreads out along the BACK of the chest rather than collecting at the base. On a supine X-ray, a haemothorax therefore appears as a DIFFUSE HAZE (an increased greyness) over the whole affected side, through which lung markings can still be seen. There may be no clear fluid level.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An erect film is less sensitive than a supine film — The opposite is true: an ERECT film is MORE sensitive, because blood falls to the base and blunts the costophrenic angle.
• It is seen only when more than 1500 mL of blood is present — Haemothorax can be seen on X-ray with much smaller amounts than 1500 mL (about 200–300 mL on an erect film).
• Costophrenic blunting appears with as little as 20 mL — About 200–300 mL is needed to blunt the costophrenic angle on an erect PA film, not 20 mL.

⭐ Key point: Ultrasound (eFAST) can detect even small haemothoraces quickly at the bedside, and CT is the most sensitive.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q7$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 587, $s$A haemopneumothorax on an erect chest X-ray typically shows:$s$, $s$["Diffuse ground-glass shadowing", "A deep sulcus sign only", "A curved meniscus without any air", "A straight horizontal air-fluid level"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A straight horizontal air-fluid level
In a HAEMOPNEUMOTHORAX there is both AIR and BLOOD in the pleural space. On an ERECT chest X-ray, the blood settles at the bottom and the air rises above it. Because air is present, the surface of the fluid forms a STRAIGHT HORIZONTAL AIR-FLUID LEVEL (like water in a glass).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diffuse ground-glass shadowing — Ground-glass shadowing suggests lung inflammation (e.g. pneumonia or ARDS).
• A deep sulcus sign only — The deep sulcus sign suggests a pneumothorax on a SUPINE film.
• A curved meniscus without any air — A curved meniscus (the fluid curving up the chest wall) is seen with pleural fluid WITHOUT air. Air changes it into a straight line.

⭐ Key point: A straight air-fluid level means air AND fluid; a curved meniscus means fluid only.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q16$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 588, $s$Thalassaemia major patients on regular transfusion are at risk of:$s$, $s$["Iron deficiency anaemia", "Iron overload needing chelation", "Vitamin K excess and bleeding", "Secondary polycythaemia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Iron overload needing chelation
In THALASSAEMIA MAJOR, the body cannot make enough normal haemoglobin, so the child needs REGULAR BLOOD TRANSFUSIONS (every 3–4 weeks). Each unit of blood contains about 200–250 mg of iron, and the body has NO natural way to remove excess iron. Over time, iron builds up (IRON OVERLOAD, haemosiderosis) in the heart (heart failure, arrhythmias — the main cause of death), liver (cirrhosis), and endocrine glands (diabetes, delayed growth and puberty, hypothyroidism). IRON CHELATION therapy (desferrioxamine, deferasirox, deferiprone) removes the excess iron.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Iron deficiency anaemia — Transfused patients have too MUCH iron, not too little. Iron tablets must NOT be given.
• Vitamin K excess and bleeding — Vitamin K excess is not a complication of transfusion.
• Secondary polycythaemia — Polycythaemia (too many red cells) does not occur; the underlying problem is anaemia.

⭐ Key point: Monitor ferritin and cardiac MRI (T2*) to check iron levels. Chelation is started after about 10–20 transfusions or when ferritin is above 1000 ng/mL.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q107$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 589, $s$Which condition is a well-established indication for therapeutic plasma exchange?$s$, $s$["Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)", "Simple dehydration", "Uncomplicated urinary tract infection", "Mild hyperkalemia"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP)
THERAPEUTIC PLASMA EXCHANGE (TPE, plasmapheresis) removes the patient's plasma (which contains harmful substances like antibodies or abnormal proteins) and replaces it with donor plasma or albumin. In THROMBOTIC THROMBOCYTOPENIC PURPURA (TTP), antibodies block an enzyme (ADAMTS13) that normally breaks down large von Willebrand factor molecules; without it, platelets clump in small vessels, causing low platelets, haemolytic anaemia, kidney problems, fever and neurological symptoms. TPE removes the antibodies AND replaces the missing ADAMTS13 enzyme from donor plasma. It has reduced TTP mortality from about 90% to about 10–20%. Other established indications: Guillain-Barré syndrome, myasthenia crisis, and some autoimmune kidney diseases.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple dehydration — Simple dehydration is treated with oral or IV fluids.
• Uncomplicated urinary tract infection — An uncomplicated UTI is treated with antibiotics.
• Mild hyperkalemia — Mild hyperkalaemia is treated with diet and drug changes, or medications if needed.

⭐ Key point: TTP is a medical emergency; start plasma exchange urgently. Avoid platelet transfusion in TTP unless there is life-threatening bleeding, as it can worsen clotting.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q192$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 590, $s$Therapeutic plasma exchange, or plasmapheresis, works by:$s$, $s$["Removing plasma containing harmful antibodies and replacing it", "Adding concentrated red blood cells to the circulation", "Diffusing small solutes only, exactly like haemodialysis", "Removing only white blood cells from the circulation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Removing plasma containing harmful antibodies and replacing it
In THERAPEUTIC PLASMA EXCHANGE (plasmapheresis), blood is taken out, the PLASMA (containing HARMFUL ANTIBODIES, immune complexes or toxins) is separated and REMOVED, and REPLACED with albumin or FFP, then the blood cells are returned to the patient.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adding concentrated red blood cells to the circulation — Adding red cells is a transfusion, not plasma exchange.
• Diffusing small solutes only, exactly like haemodialysis — Haemodialysis removes small solutes by diffusion; plasma exchange removes large molecules (antibodies).
• Removing only white blood cells from the circulation — Removing only white cells is leukapheresis.

⭐ Key point: Uses: GBS, myasthenic crisis, TTP, Goodpasture's syndrome, and severe autoimmune conditions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q12$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 591, $s$Before therapeutic plasma exchange, the nurse checks that the patient has:$s$, $s$["A full stomach to prevent hypoglycaemia during the exchange", "Had all IV access removed to reduce the risk of infection", "Good vascular access, and baseline calcium, clotting and vital signs", "Had their usual antihypertensive doses doubled that morning"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Good vascular access, and baseline calcium, clotting and vital signs
Before plasma exchange, the patient needs GOOD VASCULAR ACCESS (a large-bore dialysis-type catheter, because high blood flow is needed) and BASELINE CALCIUM, CLOTTING tests and VITAL SIGNS. Exchange removes clotting factors (bleeding risk), and the citrate anticoagulant binds calcium (causing hypocalcaemia, tingling and cramps).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A full stomach to prevent hypoglycaemia during the exchange — A full stomach isn't needed.
• Had all IV access removed to reduce the risk of infection — IV access is ESSENTIAL; it shouldn't be removed.
• Had their usual antihypertensive doses doubled that morning — Antihypertensives are often HELD (plasma exchange can cause hypotension).

⭐ Key point: During exchange, watch for low calcium (tingling around the mouth), low BP, allergic reactions (to FFP), and bleeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q13$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 592, $s$Plasma exchange is a recognised treatment for:$s$, $s$["Uncomplicated essential hypertension", "Simple urinary tract infection with fever", "Iron-deficiency anaemia in pregnancy", "Myasthenic crisis and Guillain-Barré syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Myasthenic crisis and Guillain-Barré syndrome
Plasma exchange removes harmful ANTIBODIES. It is a recognised treatment for MYASTHENIC CRISIS (antibodies against acetylcholine receptors) and GUILLAIN–BARRÉ SYNDROME (antibodies attacking nerves). Other uses include TTP, Goodpasture's syndrome, and some vasculitides.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncomplicated essential hypertension — Essential hypertension isn't treated with plasma exchange.
• Simple urinary tract infection with fever — A simple UTI is treated with antibiotics.
• Iron-deficiency anaemia in pregnancy — Iron deficiency anaemia is treated with iron.

⭐ Key point: In GBS, plasma exchange and IVIG work equally well; they aren't usually combined.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q14$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 593, $s$Highly protein-bound drugs given just before plasma exchange are likely to:$s$, $s$["Be removed with the plasma, reducing their effect", "Become more potent after the exchange is complete", "Have no change at all in their plasma levels", "Cause toxicity from accumulation in the tissues"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Be removed with the plasma, reducing their effect
Drugs that are highly PROTEIN-BOUND travel in the plasma attached to albumin. When the plasma is removed during exchange, these drugs are REMOVED too, reducing their effect. Give such drugs (some antibiotics, anticonvulsants) AFTER the exchange, not just before.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Become more potent after the exchange is complete — They become less effective, not more potent.
• Have no change at all in their plasma levels — Their levels DO change.
• Cause toxicity from accumulation in the tissues — They are removed, not accumulated.

⭐ Key point: Coordinate drug timing with the apheresis team and pharmacy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q15$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 594, $s$A child with sickle cell disease and sudden severe anaemia with a rapidly enlarging spleen has:$s$, $s$["Aplastic crisis from parvovirus", "Acute splenic sequestration crisis", "Vaso-occlusive bone crisis", "Haemolytic crisis from malaria"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute splenic sequestration crisis
ACUTE SPLENIC SEQUESTRATION CRISIS happens when sickled red cells get trapped (sequestered) in the spleen. Large amounts of blood pool there, so the spleen ENLARGES RAPIDLY and the haemoglobin DROPS SUDDENLY. The child becomes pale, weak and may go into hypovolaemic shock — this can be fatal within hours. Treatment: urgent fluid resuscitation and BLOOD TRANSFUSION. It mostly affects young children (before the spleen shrinks from repeated infarction, usually by about age 5–6).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Aplastic crisis from parvovirus — Aplastic crisis also causes severe anaemia, but the spleen does NOT enlarge suddenly. It is caused by parvovirus stopping red cell production, so reticulocytes are very low.
• Vaso-occlusive bone crisis — Vaso-occlusive (painful) crises cause bone pain, but not sudden severe anaemia with a big spleen.
• Haemolytic crisis from malaria — Haemolytic crisis causes anaemia with jaundice, but a rapidly enlarging spleen with shock is the hallmark of sequestration.

⭐ Key point: Teach parents to feel their child's spleen and check for pallor; seek urgent care if the spleen gets bigger. Repeated sequestration may require splenectomy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q112$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 595, $s$Parvovirus B19 infection in sickle cell disease causes:$s$, $s$["Polycythaemia with a high haematocrit", "Aplastic crisis with very low reticulocytes", "Acute chest syndrome only, with hypoxia", "Splenic sequestration with a big spleen"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Aplastic crisis with very low reticulocytes
PARVOVIRUS B19 attacks red blood cell precursors in the bone marrow and temporarily STOPS red cell production. In normal people this is harmless because red cells live about 120 days. In sickle cell disease, red cells live only about 10–20 days, so stopping production for a few days causes a sharp fall in haemoglobin: an APLASTIC CRISIS. The key lab finding is a very LOW RETICULOCYTE count (few new red cells are being made). Transfusion may be needed until the marrow recovers (usually within 7–10 days).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Polycythaemia with a high haematocrit — Parvovirus causes anaemia, not polycythaemia (too many red cells) or a high haematocrit.
• Acute chest syndrome only, with hypoxia — Acute chest syndrome can be triggered by infections, but it is not the typical effect of parvovirus. It presents with chest pain, fever and new infiltrates on X-ray.
• Splenic sequestration with a big spleen — Splenic sequestration is red cells pooling in an enlarging spleen. In aplastic crisis, the spleen does not suddenly enlarge.

⭐ Key point: Low Hb + LOW reticulocytes = aplastic crisis. Low Hb + HIGH reticulocytes + big spleen = sequestration. Parvovirus is contagious: isolate from pregnant staff and other immunocompromised patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q113$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 596, $s$Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia treatment in ICU may cause:$s$, $s$["Neutropenic sepsis and tumour lysis syndrome", "Immediate iron overload needing chelation", "Hypercoagulability with deep vein thrombosis", "Polycythaemia with hyperviscosity and stroke"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Neutropenic sepsis and tumour lysis syndrome
Treatment of acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (ALL) with chemotherapy destroys cancer cells AND normal white cells. This causes NEUTROPENIA, which can lead to life-threatening NEUTROPENIC SEPSIS. When many cancer cells are killed quickly, their contents flood the blood: TUMOUR LYSIS SYNDROME (high potassium, high phosphate, high uric acid, low calcium), which can cause kidney failure and arrhythmias.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate iron overload needing chelation — Iron overload builds up over years of transfusions, not immediately.
• Hypercoagulability with deep vein thrombosis — Clots can occur (for example with asparaginase), but this is less typical than infection and TLS.
• Polycythaemia with hyperviscosity and stroke — Leukaemia causes ANAEMIA, not polycythaemia.

⭐ Key point: Neutropenic sepsis: fever with a low neutrophil count. Give antibiotics within 1 hour. Prevent TLS with hydration and allopurinol or rasburicase.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q110$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 597, $s$Platelet transfusion in a leukaemic child is usually given when the count falls below:$s$, $s$["150 × 10⁹/L in every child", "10 × 10⁹/L, or higher if bleeding", "100 × 10⁹/L, even if not bleeding", "400 × 10⁹/L before any procedure"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10 × 10⁹/L, or higher if bleeding
Platelets are needed to stop bleeding. In leukaemia, chemotherapy and the disease itself reduce platelet production. A platelet transfusion is given when the count falls below 10 × 10⁹/L (to prevent spontaneous bleeding), or at a higher threshold if the child is bleeding, has a fever or infection, or needs a procedure (such as a lumbar puncture or line insertion).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 150 × 10⁹/L in every child — 150 × 10⁹/L is the lower limit of NORMAL; transfusing at this level is unnecessary.
• 100 × 10⁹/L, even if not bleeding — 100 × 10⁹/L is too high a threshold without bleeding (used only for brain or eye surgery).
• 400 × 10⁹/L before any procedure — 400 × 10⁹/L is above normal; no transfusion is needed.

⭐ Key point: Thresholds: 10 for prevention, 20 with fever or infection, 50 for most procedures and bleeding, 100 for brain or eye surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q111$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 598, $s$Maternal hypothermia after massive transfusion worsens:$s$, $s$["The blood pressure only", "Uterine contraction", "Coagulopathy and bleeding", "The urine output only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Coagulopathy and bleeding
Clotting factors are enzymes that work best at normal body temperature (37 °C). HYPOTHERMIA slows them down and stops platelets working properly, so blood doesn't clot well (COAGULOPATHY) and bleeding continues. Cold blood products from the fridge (red cells are stored at 4 °C) given in large amounts can quickly make the patient cold.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The blood pressure only — Hypothermia affects more than blood pressure; its main effect here is clotting.
• Uterine contraction — Hypothermia makes uterine contraction WORSE, not better.
• The urine output only — Hypothermia affects more than urine output.

⭐ Key point: The "lethal triad" in bleeding: hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy. Use blood warmers and forced-air warming blankets.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q140$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 599, $s$A sickle cell disease patient's haemoglobin genotype is:$s$, $s$["HbAS", "HbAC", "HbSS", "HbAA"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: HbSS
Haemoglobin genotype shows which haemoglobin genes a person has. Sickle cell DISEASE (sickle cell anaemia) is HbSS: two sickle genes, one from each parent. The red cells contain mainly HbS, which sickles when short of oxygen, causing crises, anaemia and organ damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• HbAS — HbAS is sickle cell TRAIT (a carrier): one normal and one sickle gene, usually without symptoms.
• HbAC — HbAC is a carrier of haemoglobin C; it is not sickle cell disease.
• HbAA — HbAA is normal haemoglobin.

⭐ Key point: If both parents are AS, each child has a 25% chance of SS, 50% chance of AS and 25% chance of AA. Genotype testing before marriage helps prevent sickle cell disease.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q200$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 600, $s$Before abdominal ultrasound for FAST, the patient should be:$s$, $s$["In the lithotomy position", "Sitting upright at 90 degrees", "Supine, with the abdomen exposed", "Prone, with a pillow under the hips"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Supine, with the abdomen exposed
FAST (Focused Assessment with Sonography for Trauma) is a quick bedside ultrasound scan done during resuscitation. The patient lies SUPINE (flat on the back) with the ABDOMEN and lower chest exposed, so the operator can scan four areas: around the liver (Morison's pouch), around the spleen, the pelvis, and around the heart (pericardium).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• In the lithotomy position — The lithotomy position is for pelvic examinations.
• Sitting upright at 90 degrees — Sitting upright isn't practical for an injured patient and can move the spine.
• Prone, with a pillow under the hips — Prone (face down) doesn't allow scanning of the abdomen.

⭐ Key point: A positive FAST (free fluid) in an unstable trauma patient means urgent surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q32$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 601, $s$Coagulopathy or low platelet count in a patient with GI bleeding:$s$, $s$["Increases the risk of rebleeding", "Has no bearing on bleeding risk", "Reduces the risk of rebleeding", "Only matters in upper GI bleeds, not lower"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increases the risk of rebleeding
Clotting (coagulation factors and platelets) is what seals a bleeding blood vessel. In a GI bleed, if the patient has a COAGULOPATHY (high INR, liver disease, anticoagulants) or LOW PLATELETS, the bleeding point can't form a stable clot, so bleeding is more likely to continue or START AGAIN after it stops (rebleeding).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Has no bearing on bleeding risk — Clotting problems clearly affect bleeding risk.
• Reduces the risk of rebleeding — They INCREASE, not reduce, the risk of rebleeding.
• Only matters in upper GI bleeds, not lower — They matter in both upper and lower GI bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Correct clotting in GI bleeding: vitamin K, PCC or FFP for high INR, platelets if below 50, and review anticoagulants and antiplatelets.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q101$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 602, $s$A client is receiving a blood transfusion. The critical care nurse observes that the client is experiencing diarrhoea, abdominal pain, and chills. Which of the following actions should the nurse take FIRST?$s$, $s$["Administer meperidine.", "Stop the transfusion.", "Assist the client to the bathroom.", "Get a warming blanket."]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stop the transfusion.
Diarrhoea, abdominal pain and chills during a transfusion are signs of a TRANSFUSION REACTION (possibly haemolytic or septic). The FIRST action is to STOP the transfusion immediately, keep the IV line open with 0.9% saline through a new giving set, check vital signs, and call the doctor. Then return the blood bag and giving set to the lab.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer meperidine. — Meperidine (pethidine) may treat rigors but doesn't address the cause and isn't first.
• Assist the client to the bathroom. — Helping to the bathroom doesn't stop the reaction.
• Get a warming blanket. — A warming blanket may comfort the patient but doesn't stop the reaction.

⭐ Key point: Any new symptom during transfusion: STOP first, then assess. Check the patient's ID against the blood bag again.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q102$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 603, $s$A febrile non-haemolytic transfusion reaction is managed by:$s$, $s$["Ignore it and continue at the same rate", "Speed up the transfusion to finish it", "Slow or stop the transfusion; give paracetamol", "Give IV potassium to correct the fever"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Slow or stop the transfusion; give paracetamol
A febrile non-haemolytic transfusion reaction (FNHTR) is caused by white cells or cytokines in the donor blood. It causes a temperature rise (usually less than 2 °C) and chills, but the patient is otherwise stable. SLOW or STOP the transfusion, give PARACETAMOL, and check the patient. Serious causes (haemolysis, bacterial contamination) must be excluded before continuing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignore it and continue at the same rate — Ignoring the fever could miss a serious reaction.
• Speed up the transfusion to finish it — Speeding up the transfusion could make a reaction worse.
• Give IV potassium to correct the fever — Potassium has no role in treating fever.

⭐ Key point: If the fever is high (above 39 °C), or comes with low BP, back pain or breathlessness, STOP and treat as a serious reaction.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q104$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 604, $s$Transfusion-related acute lung injury (TRALI) presents within 6 hours with:$s$, $s$["Hypertension with a raised JVP and oedema", "Hyperkalaemia only, with peaked T waves", "Hypoxia and bilateral infiltrates, no fluid overload", "A localised rash only, with normal oxygen"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypoxia and bilateral infiltrates, no fluid overload
TRALI (transfusion-related acute lung injury) is caused by antibodies in donor plasma that attack the recipient's white cells in the lungs, making the lung capillaries leak. Within 6 HOURS of transfusion, the patient develops breathlessness, HYPOXIA and BILATERAL lung INFILTRATES on X-ray, WITHOUT fluid overload (the JVP is normal, and BP is often low).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension with a raised JVP and oedema — High BP with raised JVP and oedema describes TACO (fluid overload).
• Hyperkalaemia only, with peaked T waves — High potassium is a separate complication of massive transfusion.
• A localised rash only, with normal oxygen — A localised rash with normal oxygen is a mild allergic reaction.

⭐ Key point: TRALI: low or normal BP, normal JVP, no response to diuretics. Treat with oxygen and ventilation support. Report to the blood bank.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q105$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 605, $s$Platelets should not be given through:$s$, $s$["A central line", "A platelet or new standard giving set", "A peripheral cannula", "A used blood giving set"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A used blood giving set
Platelets are sticky cells. If they are given through a giving set already used for red cells, many platelets stick to the debris and clots in the filter, reducing the dose that reaches the patient. Platelets should be given through a NEW standard blood giving set or a dedicated platelet set, never a USED blood giving set.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A central line — Platelets can be given through a central line.
• A platelet or new standard giving set — A new or platelet giving set is the RIGHT choice.
• A peripheral cannula — Platelets can be given through a peripheral cannula.

⭐ Key point: Platelets are stored at room temperature (20–24 °C) with constant gentle agitation. NEVER refrigerate them; give over about 30 minutes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q108$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 606, $s$DIC laboratory findings include:$s$, $s$["Normal clotting times with a low D-dimer level", "High platelets and high fibrinogen with short clotting times", "Isolated high haemoglobin with normal platelets", "Low platelets and fibrinogen, long PT/APTT, high D-dimer"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Low platelets and fibrinogen, long PT/APTT, high D-dimer
In disseminated intravascular coagulation (DIC), widespread small clots use up (consume) platelets and clotting factors. Lab results show: LOW PLATELETS, LOW FIBRINOGEN, PROLONGED PT and APTT (factors used up), and HIGH D-DIMER (from clot breakdown). Patients may bleed from many sites and also have organ damage from clots.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal clotting times with a low D-dimer level — Normal clotting times with low D-dimer rule out DIC.
• High platelets and high fibrinogen with short clotting times — High platelets and fibrinogen with short clotting times are the opposite of DIC.
• Isolated high haemoglobin with normal platelets — A high haemoglobin with normal platelets doesn't fit DIC.

⭐ Key point: DIC causes: sepsis, trauma, obstetric emergencies (abruption, AFE), malignancy. Treat the cause; give platelets, FFP and cryoprecipitate if bleeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q109$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 607, $s$A low reticulocyte count in anaemia indicates:$s$, $s$["Acute bleeding with good marrow", "Increased red cell destruction", "Haemolysis", "Reduced bone marrow production"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reduced bone marrow production
Reticulocytes are young red blood cells newly released from the bone marrow. Their count shows how hard the marrow is working. A LOW reticulocyte count in anaemia means the BONE MARROW is NOT making enough red cells: iron, B12 or folate deficiency, marrow failure, kidney disease (low erythropoietin), or chronic illness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute bleeding with good marrow — Acute bleeding with a healthy marrow gives a HIGH reticulocyte count (after a few days).
• Increased red cell destruction — Increased red cell destruction (haemolysis) gives a HIGH reticulocyte count.
• Haemolysis — Haemolysis gives a HIGH reticulocyte count.

⭐ Key point: High reticulocytes = marrow responding (haemolysis, bleeding). Low reticulocytes = production problem.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q112$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 608, $s$A blood film showing fragmented red cells (schistocytes) suggests:$s$, $s$["Microangiopathic haemolysis (DIC, TTP)", "Vitamin B12 deficiency with large cells", "Iron deficiency from chronic bleeding", "A normal blood film in a healthy adult"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Microangiopathic haemolysis (DIC, TTP)
Schistocytes are fragmented red cells, broken as they are forced through small vessels partly blocked by tiny clots or damaged endothelium. This is MICROANGIOPATHIC HAEMOLYTIC ANAEMIA, seen in DIC, TTP (thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura), HUS (haemolytic uraemic syndrome), HELLP syndrome, and malignant hypertension.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vitamin B12 deficiency with large cells — B12 deficiency shows LARGE red cells (macrocytes) and hypersegmented neutrophils.
• Iron deficiency from chronic bleeding — Iron deficiency shows SMALL, pale red cells (microcytic, hypochromic).
• A normal blood film in a healthy adult — A normal film doesn't have fragmented cells.

⭐ Key point: TTP is an emergency: low platelets, haemolysis, fever, confusion, kidney problems. Treat with urgent plasma exchange; don't give platelets.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q113$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 609, $s$Culture and sensitivity results guide:$s$, $s$["Sedation dosing in ventilated patients", "Antibiotic choice and de-escalation", "Blood group selection for transfusion", "The choice of IV maintenance fluid"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Antibiotic choice and de-escalation
Culture and sensitivity (C&S) results show WHICH germ is causing the infection and WHICH ANTIBIOTICS it is sensitive or resistant to. This guides the choice of antibiotic and allows DE-ESCALATION: switching from a broad-spectrum empirical antibiotic to a narrower, targeted one.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sedation dosing in ventilated patients — Sedation doses aren't guided by culture results.
• Blood group selection for transfusion — Blood group selection uses group and cross-match tests.
• The choice of IV maintenance fluid — IV fluid choice isn't guided by cultures.

⭐ Key point: Review antibiotics at 48–72 hours, when culture results are usually available: stop, narrow, or continue.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q116$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 610, $s$Raised serum lactate after cardiac arrest mainly reflects:$s$, $s$["Hyperventilation by the team during CPR", "Excess dietary lactate given in the enteral feed", "Liver overproduction of glucose after adrenaline", "Tissue hypoperfusion and anaerobic metabolism"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tissue hypoperfusion and anaerobic metabolism
During cardiac arrest, blood flow to the tissues stops or is very poor (even with good CPR). Without oxygen, cells use ANAEROBIC metabolism, which produces LACTATE. A high lactate after cardiac arrest shows how long and how severely the tissues lacked perfusion. If the lactate falls after return of circulation (lactate clearance), perfusion is improving.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperventilation by the team during CPR — Hyperventilation lowers CO₂, but it isn't the main cause of high lactate.
• Excess dietary lactate given in the enteral feed — Enteral feeds don't cause high lactate.
• Liver overproduction of glucose after adrenaline — Adrenaline can raise lactate a little, but the main cause is hypoperfusion.

⭐ Key point: Persistently high lactate after resuscitation suggests ongoing poor perfusion and a worse prognosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q118$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 611, $s$Patient had head trauma and you suspect that he has intracranial haemorrhage, Which of following modalities you will choose?$s$, $s$["CT", "Conventional x-ray", "MRI", "X-ray with contrast"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: CT
CT (computed tomography) of the head WITHOUT contrast is the first-choice imaging for suspected bleeding inside the skull after head trauma. It is FAST, widely available, shows fresh blood clearly (bright white), shows skull fractures, swelling and midline shift, and can be done in unstable patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Conventional x-ray — A plain skull X-ray shows fractures but not bleeding inside the brain.
• MRI — MRI is slower, harder to do in unstable or ventilated patients, and less sensitive for very fresh blood and fractures.
• X-ray with contrast — Contrast X-ray isn't used for head trauma.

⭐ Key point: CT head is urgent with GCS below 13 (or below 15 after 2 hours), focal neurological signs, seizures, repeated vomiting, signs of skull base fracture, or anticoagulant use.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q122$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 612, $s$On a chest X-ray, the tip of a central venous catheter should lie in the:$s$, $s$["High in the internal jugular vein", "Inside the right ventricle cavity", "In the left subclavian vein", "Lower SVC, just above the right atrium"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lower SVC, just above the right atrium
The tip of a central venous catheter (from the jugular or subclavian vein) should sit in the LOWER SUPERIOR VENA CAVA, just above the junction with the RIGHT ATRIUM (around the level of the carina on X-ray). Here, blood flow is high, which dilutes the drugs, and pressure readings are accurate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High in the internal jugular vein — High in the internal jugular vein is too short and the line may come out.
• Inside the right ventricle cavity — Inside the right ventricle risks arrhythmias and heart perforation.
• In the left subclavian vein — In the left subclavian vein is malpositioned and may cause thrombosis.

⭐ Key point: A tip in the right atrium may cause arrhythmias or, rarely, cardiac tamponade from perforation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q123$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 613, $s$A nasogastric tube on chest X-ray is correctly placed when it:$s$, $s$["Down the midline, below the diaphragm on the left", "Coiled in the pharynx behind the tongue", "Ending above the clavicle in the neck", "Following the line of the right main bronchus"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Down the midline, below the diaphragm on the left
A correctly placed NG tube on chest X-ray goes straight DOWN the MIDLINE (following the oesophagus), passes the carina, crosses the DIAPHRAGM, and its tip is seen in the stomach BELOW the LEFT diaphragm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Coiled in the pharynx behind the tongue — Coiled in the pharynx means it hasn't gone down.
• Ending above the clavicle in the neck — Ending above the clavicle means it is too short.
• Following the line of the right main bronchus — Following the right main bronchus means it is in the LUNG, which is dangerous; remove it before any feed.

⭐ Key point: Feeding through a tube in the lung can kill. Always confirm position (pH 5.5 or below, or X-ray) before the first feed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q124$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 614, $s$A pneumothorax on chest X-ray shows:$s$, $s$["White-out of the whole of one lung", "A pleural line with no lung markings beyond", "Kerley B lines at both lung bases", "Air bronchograms within a lobe"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A pleural line with no lung markings beyond
A pneumothorax shows a thin white PLEURAL LINE (the edge of the collapsed lung) with NO LUNG MARKINGS (black, no blood vessels) beyond it, towards the chest wall. On a supine X-ray, it may show as a deep sulcus sign (a deep, dark costophrenic angle).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• White-out of the whole of one lung — White-out of one lung suggests effusion, collapse or consolidation.
• Kerley B lines at both lung bases — Kerley B lines suggest pulmonary oedema.
• Air bronchograms within a lobe — Air bronchograms suggest consolidation.

⭐ Key point: In a tension pneumothorax, the mediastinum and trachea are also shifted to the opposite side. Treat clinically without waiting for an X-ray.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q128$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 615, $s$Radiation protection for the ICU nurse during portable X-ray includes:$s$, $s$["Taking no precautions for portable films", "Standing directly behind the X-ray tube", "Standing 2 metres away or wearing a lead apron", "Holding the patient still within the beam"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Standing 2 metres away or wearing a lead apron
X-ray radiation decreases with distance (the inverse square law: doubling the distance cuts the dose to a quarter). During a portable X-ray, the nurse should STAND at least 2 METRES away from the X-ray tube, or wear a LEAD APRON if they must stay close. Time, distance and shielding are the three principles of radiation protection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Taking no precautions for portable films — Precautions ARE needed for portable films.
• Standing directly behind the X-ray tube — Standing behind the tube exposes you to scattered radiation.
• Holding the patient still within the beam — Holding the patient in the beam exposes the nurse to direct radiation.

⭐ Key point: Pregnant staff should not be near the X-ray. Use positioning aids instead of holding the patient.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q134$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 616, $s$An endotracheal tube on X-ray should be:$s$, $s$["In the right bronchus", "Below the carina", "3–5 cm above the carina", "At the vocal cords"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3–5 cm above the carina
The ETT tip should lie 3–5 cm ABOVE the CARINA (about at the level of T2–T4, or between the clavicle heads). This allows both lungs to be ventilated, while leaving room for the tube to move a little when the head moves.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• In the right bronchus — In the right bronchus, only the right lung is ventilated.
• Below the carina — Below the carina means in a main bronchus (usually the right).
• At the vocal cords — At the vocal cords, it is too high and may slip out.

⭐ Key point: Neck flexion pushes the tube DOWN; neck extension pulls it UP ("the tube follows the chin").$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q135$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 617, $s$Ultrasound is useful in ICU to:$s$, $s$["Guide line insertion; assess fluid, lungs, heart", "Measure the ICP directly at the bedside", "Replace every CT scan in the ICU", "Diagnose all fractures in trauma patients"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Guide line insertion; assess fluid, lungs, heart
Point-of-care ULTRASOUND in ICU is versatile: it guides LINE INSERTION (central and arterial lines), assesses FLUID status (IVC size and collapse), the LUNGS (B-lines in oedema, effusions, pneumothorax), and the HEART (contraction, tamponade, RV size). It is safe (no radiation), fast and done at the bedside.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Measure the ICP directly at the bedside — ICP isn't measured directly by ultrasound (though optic nerve sheath diameter can suggest raised ICP).
• Replace every CT scan in the ICU — It doesn't replace every CT.
• Diagnose all fractures in trauma patients — It doesn't diagnose all fractures.

⭐ Key point: Ultrasound-guided central line insertion reduces complications like pneumothorax and arterial puncture.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q139$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 618, $s$In DIC, fibrinogen levels are typically:$s$, $s$["Not measurable", "Increased", "Unchanged", "Decreased"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Decreased
In DIC, widespread clotting uses up clotting factors, including FIBRINOGEN, faster than the liver can make them. So fibrinogen levels FALL (below 1.5 g/L is significant). Low fibrinogen, with low platelets, long PT and APTT, and high D-dimer, confirms DIC.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Not measurable — Fibrinogen is measurable.
• Increased — In DIC fibrinogen falls (it rises in inflammation and pregnancy).
• Unchanged — It changes in DIC; it falls.

⭐ Key point: Replace fibrinogen with CRYOPRECIPITATE (or fibrinogen concentrate) if it's below 1.5 g/L with bleeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q122$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 619, $s$On the periodic table, which element used in contrast media has the highest atomic number of the three commonly referenced in radiology (iodine, barium, gadolinium)?$s$, $s$["They are all equal", "Iodine (53)", "Barium (56)", "Gadolinium (64)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Gadolinium (64)
Atomic numbers: IODINE = 53, BARIUM = 56, GADOLINIUM = 64. GADOLINIUM has the HIGHEST atomic number. Iodine is used in CT and X-ray contrast, barium in GI studies (swallow, meal, enema), and gadolinium in MRI contrast (it is paramagnetic, which changes the magnetic signal).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• They are all equal — They aren't equal; each element has a unique atomic number.
• Iodine (53) — Iodine (53) is the lowest of the three.
• Barium (56) — Barium (56) is in the middle.

⭐ Key point: Gadolinium contrast is avoided in severe kidney failure (risk of nephrogenic systemic fibrosis) and in pregnancy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q188$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 620, $s$A patient with blood group O negative is often called the:$s$, $s$["Universal recipient", "Least compatible donor", "Universal donor", "Rh-positive donor"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Universal donor
O-negative red cells have NO A or B antigens and NO Rh(D) antigen, so they can be given to almost any patient without causing a major ABO or Rh reaction. O-negative is therefore called the UNIVERSAL (red cell) DONOR, and it is used in emergencies before the patient's blood group is known.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Universal recipient — The universal recipient is AB-positive.
• Least compatible donor — O-negative is the MOST compatible donor, not the least.
• Rh-positive donor — O-negative is Rh-NEGATIVE, not positive.

⭐ Key point: For plasma it is reversed: AB plasma is the universal plasma donor.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q4$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 621, $s$Which of the following is an orthopaedic-specific indication for ICU admission?$s$, $s$["Fat embolism syndrome", "Minor wrist fracture", "Isolated ankle sprain", "Uncomplicated cast application"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fat embolism syndrome
FAT EMBOLISM SYNDROME is a serious, systemic, potentially life-threatening complication of long bone and pelvic fractures. It can cause respiratory failure (ARDS-like), confusion and coma, needing ventilation and organ support. It is an orthopaedic-specific reason for ICU admission.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Minor wrist fracture — A minor wrist fracture is managed in clinic or on the ward.
• Isolated ankle sprain — An ankle sprain is managed as an outpatient.
• Uncomplicated cast application — An uncomplicated cast doesn't need ICU care.

⭐ Key point: Other orthopaedic reasons for ICU: major polytrauma, massive bleeding (pelvic fractures), compartment syndrome with rhabdomyolysis, and spinal cord injury.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q110$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 622, $s$A pleural effusion typically becomes visible on a standard chest X-ray only once fluid volume reaches approximately:$s$, $s$["2–3 litres", "150–300ml", "5–10ml", "There is no minimum volume — any amount is visible"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 150–300ml
A pleural effusion usually needs about 150–300 mL of fluid before it can be seen on a standard upright (PA) chest X-ray, as blunting of the costophrenic angle. Smaller amounts may be seen on a lateral X-ray (about 50 mL) or ultrasound (very small volumes).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2–3 litres — 2–3 litres would cause a large white-out, far above the detection threshold.
• 5–10ml — 5–10 mL is too small to see on X-ray.
• There is no minimum volume — any amount is visible — Small amounts are NOT visible on X-ray.

⭐ Key point: Ultrasound is the most sensitive bedside test for effusions and guides safe drainage.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q190$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 623, $s$Clotting factors are primarily synthesised by the:$s$, $s$["Liver", "Spleen", "Kidneys", "Pancreas"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Liver
The LIVER makes most clotting factors: fibrinogen (I), prothrombin (II), V, VII, IX, X, XI and others. Factors II, VII, IX and X also need VITAMIN K. That's why liver disease causes bleeding and a raised INR.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Spleen — The spleen stores platelets and removes old blood cells.
• Kidneys — The kidneys make erythropoietin and renin, not clotting factors.
• Pancreas — The pancreas makes digestive enzymes and insulin.

⭐ Key point: In liver failure, a high INR doesn't always correct with vitamin K, because the liver can't make the factors. FFP or PCC may be needed for bleeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q3$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$A4 · Laboratory, Haematology, Transfusion & Radiology$s$, false, 624, $s$Rh incompatibility is of particular clinical concern in pregnancy when:$s$, $s$["The mother is blood type O", "An Rh-positive mother carries an Rh-negative fetus", "An Rh-negative mother carries an Rh-positive fetus", "Both parents are Rh-negative"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An Rh-negative mother carries an Rh-positive fetus
Rh incompatibility matters when an Rh-NEGATIVE mother carries an Rh-POSITIVE baby. Fetal red cells entering her blood can make her produce anti-D antibodies. In a later pregnancy with an Rh-positive baby, these antibodies cross the placenta and destroy the baby's red cells (haemolytic disease of the newborn).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The mother is blood type O — Blood group O alone isn't the Rh issue (ABO incompatibility is a different, milder problem).
• An Rh-positive mother carries an Rh-negative fetus — An Rh-positive mother with an Rh-negative baby isn't a problem.
• Both parents are Rh-negative — If both parents are Rh-negative, the baby is Rh-negative, so there's no problem.

⭐ Key point: Give anti-D immunoglobulin to Rh-negative mothers at 28 weeks, after delivery (if the baby is Rh-positive), and after any sensitising event.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q123$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 625, $s$A classic clinical feature of acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema is:$s$, $s$["Pink, frothy sputum with severe dyspnoea and orthopnoea", "Painless haematuria", "Unilateral leg swelling with a normal respiratory rate", "Dry cough with no dyspnoea"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pink, frothy sputum with severe dyspnoea and orthopnoea
In acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, the left ventricle fails and pressure backs up into the lung capillaries. Fluid leaks into the alveoli and mixes with air, producing PINK (blood-stained), FROTHY sputum. The patient is severely breathless, cannot lie flat (orthopnoea), is sweaty and anxious, and has widespread crackles, often with wheeze ("cardiac asthma").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Painless haematuria — Painless haematuria is a urinary sign (kidney or bladder tumour, stones), not pulmonary oedema.
• Unilateral leg swelling with a normal respiratory rate — One swollen leg with normal breathing suggests a DVT, not pulmonary oedema.
• Dry cough with no dyspnoea — A dry cough without breathlessness does not match the severe distress of acute pulmonary oedema.

⭐ Key point: Treat by sitting the patient upright with legs down, giving oxygen or CPAP, GTN (if SBP is above 90–100) and IV furosemide, and monitoring urine output.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q51$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 626, $s$For a patient with an acute COPD exacerbation and a history of chronic CO2 retention, which initial oxygen delivery approach is most appropriate?$s$, $s$["Controlled oxygen via Venturi mask titrated to a target SpO2 of about 88-92%", "Non-rebreather mask at 15 L/min regardless of saturation", "Room air only with no supplemental oxygen under any circumstance", "Nasal cannula at 6 L/min titrated to SpO2 100%"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Controlled oxygen via Venturi mask titrated to a target SpO2 of about 88-92%
Patients with COPD who retain CO₂ may depend partly on low oxygen to drive their breathing. Too much oxygen can raise CO₂ further (through less breathing drive, V/Q mismatch and the Haldane effect), causing CO₂ narcosis, acidosis and drowsiness. A Venturi mask gives an EXACT, fixed oxygen percentage (24% or 28% to start), titrated to a target SpO₂ of 88–92%. Check an ABG within 30–60 minutes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Non-rebreather mask at 15 L/min regardless of saturation — A non-rebreather at 15 L/min gives very high oxygen and risks CO₂ narcosis; it is for other emergencies.
• Room air only with no supplemental oxygen under any circumstance — Withholding oxygen completely is dangerous; hypoxia kills faster than CO₂ retention.
• Nasal cannula at 6 L/min titrated to SpO2 100% — Aiming for 100% saturation in a CO₂ retainer causes oxygen-induced hypercapnia.

⭐ Key point: COPD target SpO₂ is 88–92%. If pH is below 7.35 with a high PaCO₂ despite treatment, start BiPAP (NIV).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q56$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 627, $s$The target SpO₂ in acute severe asthma treatment is:$s$, $s$["100%", "97–99%", "93–95%", "85–90%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 93–95%
In ACUTE SEVERE ASTHMA, the British Thoracic Society/SIGN guideline says give controlled oxygen to keep SpO2 at 94–98%; many exam sources (and this question) quote a target of about 93–95%. The point is: give enough oxygen to correct hypoxaemia, but avoid aiming for 100%, because high-flow oxygen can mask deterioration and unnecessary hyperoxia has no benefit. Oxygen is used to drive nebulised salbutamol in asthma. Life-threatening asthma signs: SpO2 under 92%, PEF under 33%, silent chest, cyanosis, exhaustion, confusion, normal or rising PaCO2 — these need urgent senior/ICU review.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100% — 100% is not the target; excessive oxygen gives no extra benefit and may hide worsening.
• 97–99% — 97–99% is higher than needed; the guideline target range is lower.
• 85–90% — 85–90% is too LOW for asthma; it is close to the COPD/CO2-retainer range (88–92%) and means dangerous hypoxaemia in asthma.

⭐ Key point: Acute asthma: target SpO2 about 93–95% (BTS 94–98%). SpO2 below 92% = life-threatening.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q51$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 628, $s$Non-invasive ventilation (NIV) such as BiPAP is generally contraindicated in a patient who:$s$, $s$["Has mild COPD exacerbation with good cooperation", "Has a reduced level of consciousness and cannot protect their airway", "Is able to tolerate the mask comfortably", "Has stable haemodynamics"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Has a reduced level of consciousness and cannot protect their airway
NIV (BiPAP/CPAP) uses a tight face or nasal mask; the patient must be able to breathe on their own, cooperate, and PROTECT THEIR AIRWAY (cough, swallow, clear secretions). A patient with REDUCED CONSCIOUSNESS (e.g. GCS below about 8, not due to CO2 narcosis that may quickly improve) cannot protect the airway, so there is a high risk of vomiting and ASPIRATION into the lungs under the mask; such patients need intubation. Other contraindications: respiratory arrest, haemodynamic instability, facial trauma/burns, recent upper GI or facial surgery, copious secretions, vomiting, undrained pneumothorax, inability to fit the mask.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Has mild COPD exacerbation with good cooperation — A MILD COPD exacerbation in a cooperative patient is actually a classic INDICATION (especially with hypercapnic acidosis).
• Is able to tolerate the mask comfortably — Being able to TOLERATE THE MASK is needed for NIV to work — it supports use, not contraindicates it.
• Has stable haemodynamics — STABLE HAEMODYNAMICS is a requirement for safe NIV, not a contraindication.

⭐ Key point: NIV needs an alert, cooperative patient who can protect their airway. Reduced consciousness = contraindication → intubate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q57$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 629, $s$Using the ABCDE framework to prioritise care for a newly admitted critically ill patient, which finding would take priority over the others?$s$, $s$["Mild abrasions on the forearm", "Stridor with visible respiratory distress", "A blood pressure of 128/82", "A pain score of 3/10"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stridor with visible respiratory distress
The ABCDE approach (Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability, Exposure) puts problems in order of how quickly they kill. AIRWAY comes first: a blocked airway can cause death within minutes. STRIDOR is a high-pitched noise on breathing in, caused by narrowing of the upper airway (larynx/trachea) — e.g. swelling, foreign body, anaphylaxis, burns, epiglottitis. Stridor PLUS visible respiratory distress means the airway is in danger now. Action: call for senior/anaesthetic help immediately, sit the patient up, give high-flow oxygen, prepare for airway management (adrenaline nebuliser if croup/anaphylaxis, intubation or surgical airway).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild abrasions on the forearm — Mild forearm ABRASIONS belong to E (Exposure) — the lowest priority here.
• A blood pressure of 128/82 — A BP of 128/82 is a near-normal circulation finding — no urgent action.
• A pain score of 3/10 — A pain score of 3/10 is mild pain; it should be treated but is not life-threatening.

⭐ Key point: ABCDE: AIRWAY first. Stridor + respiratory distress = threatened airway → highest priority.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q61$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 630, $s$Indications for intubation in the critical care unit include all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Airway protection in a patient with reduced consciousness", "Respiratory failure requiring mechanical ventilation", "Anticipated airway compromise (e.g. severe burns/oedema)", "Mild, well-controlled hypertension"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mild, well-controlled hypertension
Common INDICATIONS for INTUBATION in ICU: (1) AIRWAY PROTECTION — reduced consciousness (GCS ≤ 8), loss of gag/cough reflex, risk of aspiration; (2) RESPIRATORY FAILURE needing mechanical ventilation — hypoxaemia not responding to oxygen/NIV, rising PaCO2 with acidosis, exhaustion; (3) ANTICIPATED AIRWAY COMPROMISE — airway burns, inhalation injury, angioedema, anaphylaxis, neck haematoma, epiglottitis; (4) to allow procedures, deep sedation or controlled ventilation (e.g. raised ICP, severe shock). Mild, well-controlled HYPERTENSION has nothing to do with the airway or breathing and is never an indication for intubation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Airway protection in a patient with reduced consciousness — Airway PROTECTION in reduced consciousness IS an indication.
• Respiratory failure requiring mechanical ventilation — RESPIRATORY FAILURE needing ventilation IS an indication.
• Anticipated airway compromise (e.g. severe burns/oedema) — Anticipated airway compromise (BURNS/OEDEMA) IS an indication — intubate early before swelling makes it impossible.

⭐ Key point: Intubate for airway protection, respiratory failure or threatened airway — NOT for controlled hypertension.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q106$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 631, $s$Chronic bronchitis, one of the components of COPD, is clinically defined as:$s$, $s$["A productive cough occurring on most days for at least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years", "A single episode of productive cough lasting more than 2 weeks", "Cough occurring only at night for one month", "Wheeze without any cough for 6 months"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A productive cough occurring on most days for at least 3 months in each of 2 consecutive years
CHRONIC BRONCHITIS is defined clinically as a PRODUCTIVE COUGH occurring on most days for at least 3 MONTHS in each of 2 CONSECUTIVE YEARS, after other causes are excluded.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A single episode of productive cough lasting more than 2 weeks — A single 2-week episode is acute bronchitis.
• Cough occurring only at night for one month — Night cough for 1 month doesn't meet the definition.
• Wheeze without any cough for 6 months — Wheeze without cough doesn't meet the definition.

⭐ Key point: Smoking is the main cause; stopping smoking is the most effective intervention.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q103$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 632, $s$Contraindications to chest physiotherapy include all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Untreated pneumothorax", "Active haemoptysis", "Uncontrolled raised intracranial pressure", "Mild, stable chronic bronchitis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mild, stable chronic bronchitis
CHEST PHYSIOTHERAPY (percussion, vibration, postural drainage) can be harmful in certain conditions. Contraindications include: UNTREATED PNEUMOTHORAX (could worsen it), ACTIVE HAEMOPTYSIS (coughing blood — could increase bleeding), UNCONTROLLED RAISED ICP (head-down positions and coughing raise ICP), unstable spinal injury, rib fractures and haemodynamic instability. MILD, STABLE CHRONIC BRONCHITIS is NOT a contraindication — physiotherapy can help clear secretions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Untreated pneumothorax — Untreated pneumothorax IS a contraindication.
• Active haemoptysis — Active haemoptysis IS a contraindication.
• Uncontrolled raised intracranial pressure — Uncontrolled raised ICP IS a contraindication.

⭐ Key point: Always assess the patient's stability before chest physiotherapy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q199$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 633, $s$The most common trigger for an asthma exacerbation is:$s$, $s$["Viral respiratory infection", "Food allergy", "Exercise", "Cold weather alone"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Viral respiratory infection
VIRAL RESPIRATORY INFECTIONS — especially RHINOVIRUS (common cold), influenza and RSV — are the MOST COMMON trigger of ASTHMA EXACERBATIONS in both children and adults (responsible for most attacks). The virus inflames the airway lining, increases mucus production and makes the airways more "twitchy" (hyper-reactive), causing bronchospasm. Exacerbations are more frequent in the rainy/cold season when viral infections spread.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Food allergy — Food allergy can trigger asthma in some people, but it is not the most common trigger.
• Exercise — Exercise can cause bronchoconstriction (exercise-induced asthma), but it rarely causes severe exacerbations requiring hospital care.
• Cold weather alone — Cold weather is a trigger, often because it increases viral infections, but it is not the most common trigger on its own.

⭐ Key point: Prevention: regular inhaled corticosteroids, annual influenza vaccination, hand hygiene, avoiding smoke and allergens, and a written asthma action plan.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q58$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 634, $s$A 19-year-old comes into the emergency department with acute asthma. His respiratory rate is 44 breaths/minute, and he appears to be in acute respiratory distress. Which of the following actions should be taken FIRST?$s$, $s$["Take a full medication history.", "Apply a cardiac monitor to the client.", "Provide emotional support to the client.", "Give a bronchodilator by nebulizer."]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Give a bronchodilator by nebulizer.
A respiratory rate of 44/min with ACUTE RESPIRATORY DISTRESS in asthma is a medical emergency. The FIRST action is to GIVE a BRONCHODILATOR (e.g. salbutamol) by NEBULISER (with oxygen) to open the airways. Other actions (monitoring, history, emotional support) follow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Take a full medication history. — A full medication history delays treatment.
• Apply a cardiac monitor to the client. — A cardiac monitor is useful but not the first priority.
• Provide emotional support to the client. — Emotional support is important but doesn't treat the airway obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Also give oxygen (target SpO2 94–98%), ipratropium, and systemic steroids.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q60$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 635, $s$What is the priority nursing intervention for airway management in an unconscious patient?$s$, $s$["Position the patient supine and flat", "Insert an oral airway only if the gag reflex is present", "Elevate the legs above the level of the heart", "Position the patient laterally (recovery position) or semi-prone with the head of bed elevated to maintain a patent airway"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Position the patient laterally (recovery position) or semi-prone with the head of bed elevated to maintain a patent airway
An UNCONSCIOUS patient may lose airway reflexes; the tongue can fall back and block the airway, and vomit or secretions can be aspirated. Positioning the patient LATERALLY (RECOVERY POSITION) or semi-prone (with the head of bed elevated where appropriate) helps keep the AIRWAY PATENT, allows secretions to drain from the mouth, and reduces aspiration risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Position the patient supine and flat — Supine and flat increases airway obstruction and aspiration risk.
• Insert an oral airway only if the gag reflex is present — Oral airways are inserted only if the gag reflex is ABSENT (otherwise they cause gagging and vomiting).
• Elevate the legs above the level of the heart — Elevating the legs doesn't protect the airway.

⭐ Key point: Have suction ready, and consider airway adjuncts or intubation if the airway cannot be maintained (GCS ≤8).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q93$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 636, $s$The following are absolute indications for intubation in life-threatening asthma EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Cardiopulmonary arrest", "Decreasing level of consciousness", "Mild wheeze with normal speech", "Severe refractory hypoxaemia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mild wheeze with normal speech
In LIFE-THREATENING ASTHMA, intubation is needed when the patient is failing despite maximal treatment. Indications include: CARDIOPULMONARY ARREST, DECREASING LEVEL OF CONSCIOUSNESS (drowsiness, confusion, coma — from high CO2 and low oxygen), SEVERE REFRACTORY HYPOXAEMIA, exhaustion with poor respiratory effort, and a rising PaCO2 with worsening acidosis. A patient with MILD WHEEZE and NORMAL SPEECH has MILD asthma and does NOT need intubation — intubation would expose them to unnecessary serious risks (barotrauma, air trapping, hypotension).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cardiopulmonary arrest — Cardiopulmonary arrest IS an absolute indication.
• Decreasing level of consciousness — Decreasing consciousness IS an indication.
• Severe refractory hypoxaemia — Severe refractory hypoxaemia IS an indication.

⭐ Key point: Intubating an asthmatic is high-risk; it should be done by the most experienced person, with ketamine as a useful induction agent and ventilator settings that allow long expiratory times.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q105$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 637, $s$A ventilated patient develops sudden respiratory distress, high-pressure alarm, and coarse rhonchi audible without a stethoscope. The most appropriate first nursing action is:$s$, $s$["Sedate the patient more deeply", "Immediately call for tube reintubation without assessment", "Increase the FiO2 only", "Attempt suctioning to clear the airway"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Attempt suctioning to clear the airway
SUDDEN RESPIRATORY DISTRESS with a HIGH-PRESSURE alarm and COARSE RHONCHI (audible without a stethoscope) suggest SECRETIONS blocking the airway. The most appropriate first action is to ATTEMPT SUCTIONING to clear the airway.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sedate the patient more deeply — Sedation doesn't treat the secretions.
• Immediately call for tube reintubation without assessment — Re-intubation without assessment is premature.
• Increase the FiO2 only — Increasing FiO2 alone doesn't remove the obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Pre-oxygenate before suctioning and limit each pass to 10–15 seconds.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q120$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 638, $s$A patient with COPD exacerbation and cardiogenic pulmonary edema who is alert, cooperative, and hemodynamically stable may first be tried on which form of ventilatory support before invasive intubation?$s$, $s$["Immediate invasive mechanical ventilation", "High-frequency oscillatory ventilation", "Negative pressure ventilation", "Noninvasive positive pressure ventilation (BiPAP)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Noninvasive positive pressure ventilation (BiPAP)
For an ALERT, COOPERATIVE, HAEMODYNAMICALLY STABLE patient with COPD exacerbation or CARDIOGENIC PULMONARY OEDEMA, NON-INVASIVE POSITIVE PRESSURE VENTILATION (BiPAP or CPAP) should be tried first. It reduces the work of breathing, improves gas exchange, and reduces the need for intubation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate invasive mechanical ventilation — Immediate invasive ventilation isn't needed for stable patients.
• High-frequency oscillatory ventilation — High-frequency oscillatory ventilation is a rescue mode for severe ARDS.
• Negative pressure ventilation — Negative pressure ventilation is rarely used today.

⭐ Key point: Monitor closely and intubate if NIV fails.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q132$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 639, $s$A ventilated patient with bilateral infiltrates on PEEP 10 cmH2O has PaO2 75 mmHg on FiO2 1.0. Using the P/F ratio, how is this classified?$s$, $s$["Severe ARDS", "Mild ARDS", "Normal oxygenation (no ARDS)", "Moderate ARDS"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Severe ARDS
P/F RATIO = PaO2 ÷ FiO2. Here: 75 mmHg ÷ 1.0 (100% oxygen) = 75. With bilateral infiltrates on PEEP ≥5 (Berlin definition), a P/F of 100 or less is SEVERE ARDS. This means that even on 100% oxygen, the blood oxygen is very low — there is a lot of shunt (blood passing collapsed or fluid-filled alveoli).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild ARDS — Mild ARDS is 200–300.
• Normal oxygenation (no ARDS) — Normal oxygenation would be above about 300–400 (on 100% oxygen a healthy person's PaO2 would be over 500).
• Moderate ARDS — Moderate ARDS is 100–200.

⭐ Key point: Severe ARDS treatments: lung-protective ventilation, higher PEEP, PRONE POSITIONING (16+ hours a day), short-term neuromuscular blockade, conservative fluids, and ECMO referral if refractory.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q138 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 640, $s$A patient with a suspected overdose of unknown substance is unconscious with a GCS of 6 and shallow, slow respirations. What is the most immediate nursing priority?$s$, $s$["Prepare for airway protection (e.g., positioning, suction, possible intubation) and provide ventilatory support", "Send the patient for an urgent CT scan of the head", "Administer activated charcoal orally", "Obtain a detailed medication history from family"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Prepare for airway protection (e.g., positioning, suction, possible intubation) and provide ventilatory support
A GCS of 6 means the patient is deeply unconscious and CANNOT PROTECT THE AIRWAY (risk of obstruction by the tongue and aspiration of vomit). SHALLOW, SLOW BREATHING means RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION with high CO2 and low oxygen. The most immediate priority is AIRWAY and BREATHING: position the patient (head tilt/chin lift, recovery position if appropriate), suction, give oxygen, support breathing with bag-mask ventilation, and prepare for INTUBATION. Also check glucose and consider naloxone if opioid toxicity is possible.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Send the patient for an urgent CT scan of the head — A CT scan is important but must not happen before the airway is secured.
• Administer activated charcoal orally — Activated charcoal must NEVER be given to a patient who cannot protect their airway (aspiration risk).
• Obtain a detailed medication history from family — A medication history is helpful, but ABC comes first.

⭐ Key point: "GCS 8 — intubate": a GCS of 8 or less usually means the airway needs protection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q149$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 641, $s$A patient six hours post-thyroidectomy develops increasing neck swelling and stridor. The nurse's priority action is to:$s$, $s$["Call for immediate help, sit the patient upright, give high-flow oxygen, and prepare for emergency wound release if instructed", "Reassure the patient this is normal post-operative swelling with no action needed", "Discharge the patient home for outpatient follow-up", "Apply a tight neck dressing to control swelling"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Call for immediate help, sit the patient upright, give high-flow oxygen, and prepare for emergency wound release if instructed
INCREASING NECK SWELLING and STRIDOR after THYROIDECTOMY suggest a HAEMATOMA (bleeding) compressing the airway. This is a life-threatening emergency. CALL FOR IMMEDIATE HELP, SIT THE PATIENT UPRIGHT, give HIGH-FLOW OXYGEN, and prepare to RELEASE the wound (open skin and muscle sutures — "SCOOP": skin exposure, cut sutures, open skin, open muscles, pack wound) to evacuate the blood if instructed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reassure the patient this is normal post-operative swelling with no action needed — It is not normal swelling.
• Discharge the patient home for outpatient follow-up — Discharge would be fatal.
• Apply a tight neck dressing to control swelling — A tight dressing would worsen airway compression.

⭐ Key point: Keep a suture removal kit at the bedside after thyroid surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q168$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 642, $s$A patient is witnessed to regurgitate and aspirate during induction of anaesthesia. What is the immediate first step?$s$, $s$["Administer a full dose of intravenous antibiotics before any airway intervention", "Delay any intervention until a chest X-ray confirms aspiration", "Place the patient head-down in the lateral position and suction the airway immediately", "Perform saline lavage of the trachea to dilute the acid"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Place the patient head-down in the lateral position and suction the airway immediately
When ASPIRATION is witnessed during induction, the immediate goal is to STOP MORE stomach contents entering the lungs and CLEAR THE AIRWAY: tilt the patient HEAD-DOWN and turn them to the LATERAL (side) position so gravity drains material out of the mouth, and SUCTION the airway immediately. Then secure the airway with a cuffed tube and suction through it before ventilating.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer a full dose of intravenous antibiotics before any airway intervention — Antibiotics are not given before airway protection, and are not always needed.
• Delay any intervention until a chest X-ray confirms aspiration — Waiting for an X-ray delays life-saving action.
• Perform saline lavage of the trachea to dilute the acid — Saline lavage spreads the acid further and is not recommended.

⭐ Key point: Steroids are not recommended for aspiration. Monitor for hypoxia and ARDS.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q178$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 643, $s$In patients with normal cardiopulmonary status, increasing the ________ is the treatment of choice in improving the patient's oxygenation.$s$, $s$["FiO2", "PSV", "VT", "PEEP"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: FiO2
Oxygenation is controlled by FiO₂ (oxygen concentration) and PEEP (mean airway pressure). In patients with NORMAL heart and lung function, simply increasing the FiO₂ is the quickest and easiest way to improve oxygenation. PEEP is added mainly when alveoli are collapsed or flooded (ARDS, oedema).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• PSV — Pressure support helps ventilation and the work of breathing, not mainly oxygenation.
• VT — Tidal volume mainly affects CO₂ removal (ventilation).
• PEEP — PEEP is the treatment of choice when the lungs are damaged, not in normal cardiopulmonary status.

⭐ Key point: Use the lowest FiO₂ that achieves the target SpO₂ (avoid oxygen toxicity, ideally FiO₂ below 0.6).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q33$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, true, 644, $s$In a ventilated life-threatening asthma patient, an inspiratory pressure target is generally set at:$s$, $s$["30–35cmH2O", "80–90cmH2O", "10–15cmH2O", "50–60cmH2O"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 30–35cmH2O
In ventilated life-threatening asthma, the airways are very narrow, so high pressures are needed. With pressure-controlled ventilation, the inspiratory pressure target is usually around 30–35 cmH₂O. This gives enough ventilation while trying to limit barotrauma.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 80–90cmH2O — 80–90 cmH₂O would cause barotrauma (pneumothorax).
• 10–15cmH2O — 10–15 cmH₂O is too low to ventilate tight airways.
• 50–60cmH2O — 50–60 cmH₂O is too high.

⭐ Key point: Asthma ventilation: low rate, long expiratory time, small tidal volumes, minimal PEEP, and permissive hypercapnia, with a watch for auto-PEEP.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q32$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 645, $s$Normal arterial partial pressure of oxygen (PaO2) in a healthy adult breathing room air is approximately:$s$, $s$["40-45 mmHg", "60-65 mmHg", "80-100 mmHg", "120-140 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 80-100 mmHg
In a healthy adult breathing room air (FiO₂ 21%) at sea level, the normal PaO₂ is 80–100 mmHg (10.7–13.3 kPa). It falls slightly with age. PaO₂ is the pressure of oxygen dissolved in arterial blood, and it drives oxygen onto haemoglobin. Below 80 mmHg is hypoxaemia; below 60 mmHg (8 kPa) is respiratory failure, because the oxygen–haemoglobin curve becomes steep and saturation falls quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 40-45 mmHg — 40–45 mmHg is close to the normal VENOUS PO₂ (about 40 mmHg), not arterial.
• 60-65 mmHg — 60–65 mmHg is hypoxaemia; 60 mmHg is the cut-off for respiratory failure.
• 120-140 mmHg — 120–140 mmHg is higher than normal and is only seen when breathing extra oxygen.

⭐ Key point: Normal PaO₂ 80–100 mmHg. PaO₂ below 60 mmHg = respiratory failure (type 1 if PaCO₂ is normal or low; type 2 if PaCO₂ is above 45 mmHg).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q2$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 646, $s$A patient develops sudden dyspnoea, pleuritic chest pain, and tachycardia after prolonged bed rest post-surgery. The nurse should have a high index of suspicion for:$s$, $s$["Simple anxiety", "Pulmonary embolism", "Common cold", "Gastritis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pulmonary embolism
After surgery and bed rest, blood flows slowly in the leg veins (stasis), surgery makes the blood clot more easily, and veins may be damaged. These three factors (Virchow's triad) cause a DVT. A piece of clot can travel to the lungs as a pulmonary embolism, causing SUDDEN breathlessness, sharp chest pain worse on breathing in (pleuritic), fast pulse, low oxygen, and sometimes haemoptysis or collapse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple anxiety — Anxiety should never be assumed until serious causes like PE are excluded.
• Common cold — A common cold causes runny nose and sore throat, not sudden pleuritic pain.
• Gastritis — Gastritis causes epigastric burning, not sudden breathlessness with tachycardia.

⭐ Key point: Prevent PE with early mobilisation, leg exercises, compression stockings and LMWH. Suspect PE with any sudden breathlessness after surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q53$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 647, $s$A patient with severe emphysema is classically described as a 'pink puffer.' This term reflects:$s$, $s$["Cyanosis with peripheral oedema", "A thin body habitus, pursed-lip breathing, and relatively preserved oxygenation until late disease", "Obesity with cor pulmonale", "A productive cough with copious purulent sputum"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A thin body habitus, pursed-lip breathing, and relatively preserved oxygenation until late disease
In emphysema, the alveolar walls are destroyed and the lungs over-inflate. The patient works very hard to breathe: they are thin (they burn many calories breathing), barrel-chested, use accessory muscles, and breathe out through pursed lips to keep the airways open. Because they breathe so hard, oxygen levels stay fairly normal until late, so they look "pink" and "puff". This is the opposite of the "blue bloater".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cyanosis with peripheral oedema — Cyanosis with peripheral oedema describes the "blue bloater" (chronic bronchitis with right heart failure).
• Obesity with cor pulmonale — Obesity with cor pulmonale also fits the blue bloater, not the pink puffer.
• A productive cough with copious purulent sputum — A productive cough with lots of sputum is typical of chronic bronchitis (blue bloater).

⭐ Key point: Pink puffer = emphysema (thin, breathless, near-normal O₂). Blue bloater = chronic bronchitis (overweight, cyanosed, oedema, CO₂ retention).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q55$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 648, $s$A key clinical benefit of BiPAP in a patient with a COPD exacerbation and respiratory failure is that it:$s$, $s$["Reduces the work of breathing by providing inspiratory pressure support while maintaining alveolar recruitment with EPAP", "Eliminates the need for any pharmacologic bronchodilator therapy", "Cures the underlying airway obstruction", "Requires no patient cooperation or spontaneous breathing effort"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reduces the work of breathing by providing inspiratory pressure support while maintaining alveolar recruitment with EPAP
BiPAP gives two pressures. IPAP (inspiratory pressure) pushes air in on each breath, helping the tired breathing muscles, increasing tidal volume and blowing off CO₂. EPAP (expiratory pressure, which acts like PEEP) keeps the small airways and alveoli open at the end of each breath, so less work is needed for the next breath. In COPD exacerbations it lowers CO₂, corrects the pH and reduces the need for intubation and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Eliminates the need for any pharmacologic bronchodilator therapy — BiPAP does not replace bronchodilators; nebulisers, steroids and antibiotics are still needed.
• Cures the underlying airway obstruction — BiPAP supports breathing while the exacerbation is treated; it does not cure the disease.
• Requires no patient cooperation or spontaneous breathing effort — BiPAP needs a cooperative, spontaneously breathing patient who can protect their airway.

⭐ Key point: Start NIV in COPD when pH is 7.25–7.35 with a high PaCO₂. Check the ABG at 1 hour; no improvement means the patient may need intubation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q60$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 649, $s$What PaO2/FiO2 ratio threshold, with PEEP of at least 5 cmH2O, defines severe ARDS under the Berlin definition?$s$, $s$["300 or less", "200 or less", "100 or less", "150 or less"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 100 or less
The Berlin definition grades ARDS by the PaO₂/FiO₂ (P/F) ratio, with PEEP or CPAP of at least 5 cmH₂O. SEVERE is P/F of 100 or less, moderate is above 100 to 200, and mild is above 200 to 300. Example: PaO₂ 70 mmHg on FiO₂ 0.8 gives 70 ÷ 0.8 = 87.5, which is severe ARDS. Severe ARDS has the highest death rate and needs prone positioning and sometimes ECMO.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 300 or less — 300 or less is the upper cut-off for MILD ARDS (the threshold for any ARDS).
• 200 or less — 200 or less is the cut-off for MODERATE ARDS.
• 150 or less — 150 or less is the cut-off used to start PRONE positioning, not the Berlin severe category.

⭐ Key point: P/F ratio = PaO₂ (mmHg) ÷ FiO₂ (as a decimal). Normal is about 400–500 (100 ÷ 0.21 ≈ 476).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q61$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 650, $s$A GCS below what value is generally used as an indication for intubation?$s$, $s$["<12", "<10", "<8", "<5"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: <8
A GCS of 8 or less is the general sign that the patient cannot protect their own airway. The tongue may fall back and block the airway, the gag and cough reflexes are weak, and the patient may aspirate vomit. Intubation protects the airway, prevents aspiration, and allows control of oxygen and CO₂ (important in head injury). Say "GCS less than 8, intubate".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• <12 — Below 12 is moderate impairment; many patients can still protect their airway.
• <10 — Below 10 is too high a threshold for routine intubation.
• <5 — Below 5 is too low; waiting this long risks aspiration and hypoxic brain damage.

⭐ Key point: Also intubate for a rapidly falling GCS, poor oxygenation or ventilation, seizures that can't be controlled, or before transfer of a deteriorating head-injured patient.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q84$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 651, $s$What is Mendelson's syndrome?$s$, $s$["Chemical pneumonitis resulting from aspiration of acidic gastric contents into the lungs", "Bacterial pneumonia acquired during mechanical ventilation", "An allergic reaction to inhalational anaesthetic agents", "Pulmonary embolism occurring after prolonged bed rest"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Chemical pneumonitis resulting from aspiration of acidic gastric contents into the lungs
Mendelson's syndrome is a CHEMICAL pneumonitis caused by inhaling acidic stomach contents into the lungs, classically during general anaesthesia in obstetrics (Curtis Mendelson, 1946). Stomach acid (especially with pH below 2.5 and volume over 25 mL) burns the lung lining, causing wheeze, breathlessness, hypoxia and ARDS within hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bacterial pneumonia acquired during mechanical ventilation — VAP is a bacterial infection after 48 hours of ventilation, not chemical aspiration.
• An allergic reaction to inhalational anaesthetic agents — An allergic reaction to anaesthetic gases is a different problem.
• Pulmonary embolism occurring after prolonged bed rest — PE after bed rest is a blood clot, not aspiration.

⭐ Key point: Prevent aspiration with fasting, antacids or H₂ blockers (ranitidine or sodium citrate in obstetrics), rapid sequence induction, and extubating only when the patient is awake.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q108$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 652, $s$Pulmonary oxygen toxicity is most likely to develop with which exposure pattern?$s$, $s$["FiO2 greater than about 60% sustained for more than 24-48 hours", "Brief exposure to 100% oxygen for less than 5 minutes during pre-oxygenation", "FiO2 of 21% (room air) for any duration", "Intermittent low-flow nasal cannula oxygen at 2 L/min"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: FiO2 greater than about 60% sustained for more than 24-48 hours
Oxygen toxicity damages the lung lining when a high oxygen concentration (FiO₂ above about 60%) is breathed for a long time (more than 24–48 hours). Free radicals injure the alveoli, causing inflammation, fluid leak and eventually fibrosis, much like ARDS. Absorption collapse (atelectasis) can also happen, because nitrogen is washed out of the alveoli.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Brief exposure to 100% oxygen for less than 5 minutes during pre-oxygenation — A brief exposure to 100% oxygen (such as pre-oxygenation for intubation) is safe.
• FiO2 of 21% (room air) for any duration — Room air (21%) does not cause oxygen toxicity.
• Intermittent low-flow nasal cannula oxygen at 2 L/min — Low-flow 2 L/min nasal oxygen gives only about 28% and does not cause toxicity.

⭐ Key point: Give the LOWEST FiO₂ that achieves the target SpO₂ (usually 94–98%, or 88–92% in CO₂ retainers). Use PEEP to allow a lower FiO₂ in ventilated patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q109$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 653, $s$A properly functioning underwater-seal drain typically shows:$s$, $s$["Continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber", "No movement at all in the water-seal chamber", "Fluctuation (tidaling) and intermittent bubbling with respiration", "A water level that never moves"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fluctuation (tidaling) and intermittent bubbling with respiration
In a working underwater-seal drain, the water level in the water-seal chamber rises and falls with breathing (swinging or tidaling), because pressure in the chest changes with each breath. This proves the tube is open and connected to the pleural space. INTERMITTENT bubbling, especially on coughing or breathing out, is expected while air is still leaving the chest after a pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Continuous bubbling in the water-seal chamber — CONTINUOUS bubbling in the water-seal chamber suggests an air leak (from the lung or the system) and must be checked.
• No movement at all in the water-seal chamber — No movement at all suggests the tube is blocked or kinked, or the lung has fully re-expanded.
• A water level that never moves — A water level that never moves means no swinging, so the drain may not be working.

⭐ Key point: Swinging = the drain is open. No swing = check for kinks, clots, or lung re-expansion. Continuous bubbling = look for an air leak. Never lift the bottle above the chest.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q129$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 654, $s$A characteristic sign of croup is:$s$, $s$["A barking cough with inspiratory stridor", "A productive cough with green sputum", "Expiratory wheeze only", "High fever with drooling"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A barking cough with inspiratory stridor
Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) is a viral infection (usually parainfluenza) of the larynx and trachea in children aged 6 months to 3 years. Swelling below the vocal cords narrows the airway, causing a BARKING ("seal-like") cough, hoarse voice and INSPIRATORY STRIDOR (a harsh sound when breathing in). It is often worse at night, with a mild fever.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A productive cough with green sputum — A productive cough with green sputum suggests a chest infection.
• Expiratory wheeze only — Expiratory wheeze suggests asthma or bronchiolitis (lower airway).
• High fever with drooling — High fever with drooling suggests EPIGLOTTITIS, which is more serious.

⭐ Key point: Treat croup with oral dexamethasone (0.15 mg/kg); give nebulised adrenaline for severe stridor. Keep the child calm on the parent's lap.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q147$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 655, $s$A sickle cell disease patient presented with chest pain, low Spo2, cough, dyspnea, which of these is the cause?$s$, $s$["Acute Asthmatic", "Acute Chest Syndrome", "Vaso-Occlusive Crisis", "Acute Anaemia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute Chest Syndrome
Acute chest syndrome is a serious complication of sickle cell disease. Sickled cells block the small lung vessels (plus infection or fat embolism), causing chest pain, cough, breathlessness, low SpO₂, fever, and a NEW shadow on the chest X-ray. It is the leading cause of death in sickle cell disease and needs urgent treatment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute Asthmatic — Acute asthma causes wheeze and breathlessness, but this picture in sickle cell disease points to acute chest syndrome.
• Vaso-Occlusive Crisis — A vaso-occlusive crisis causes pain (often bones and joints) but not hypoxia with lung signs.
• Acute Anaemia — Acute anaemia causes tiredness, pallor and breathlessness, but not chest pain with a new lung shadow.

⭐ Key point: Treatment: oxygen, pain relief, IV fluids (careful), antibiotics, incentive spirometry, and blood or exchange transfusion if severe. Monitor SpO₂ closely.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q149$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 656, $s$Manual hyperinflation$s$, $s$["Involve the use of expiratory pause of 5-10 seconds", "Is used to provide fast and slow expiratory breath", "Technique is not used in the management of patient's self-ventilating", "Technique is used in the management of ventilated patients"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Technique is used in the management of ventilated patients
Manual hyperinflation (MHI) is a chest physiotherapy technique used in VENTILATED (intubated) patients. The patient is disconnected and given slow, deep breaths with a manual bag (larger than normal tidal volume), followed by a short inspiratory hold and then a quick release. This mimics a cough, re-expands collapsed lung areas, and moves secretions up to be suctioned.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Involve the use of expiratory pause of 5-10 seconds — The hold is short (inspiratory pause of 2–3 seconds), not a 5–10 second expiratory pause.
• Is used to provide fast and slow expiratory breath — MHI uses a slow deep breath in and a FAST release, not "fast and slow expiratory breaths".
• Technique is not used in the management of patient's self-ventilating — It is used mainly in intubated patients, but this statement is weaker than the correct one, which describes its main use.

⭐ Key point: Avoid MHI in unstable patients, those with high PEEP needs, undrained pneumothorax, bronchospasm or raised ICP. Monitor BP and SpO₂ during it.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q199$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 657, $s$Ipratropium bromide is used in the management of:$s$, $s$["Acute gastroenteritis", "Acute peptic ulcer disease", "Acute bronchopneumonia", "Acute asthmatic attack"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute asthmatic attack
IPRATROPIUM BROMIDE (Atrovent) is an inhaled ANTICHOLINERGIC (antimuscarinic) bronchodilator. It blocks acetylcholine at muscarinic receptors in the airway smooth muscle, which RELAXES the bronchi and reduces mucus secretion. In an ACUTE ASTHMA ATTACK it is given by nebuliser (e.g. 500 micrograms) together with salbutamol for moderate–severe or life-threatening attacks; the combination gives better bronchodilation than salbutamol alone. It is also widely used in COPD. Side effects: dry mouth, urinary retention, and blurred vision/glaucoma if mist gets into the eyes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute gastroenteritis — ACUTE GASTROENTERITIS is treated with fluids/ORS; ipratropium has no role.
• Acute peptic ulcer disease — PEPTIC ULCER disease is treated with PPIs, H2 blockers and H. pylori eradication, not inhaled ipratropium.
• Acute bronchopneumonia — ACUTE BRONCHOPNEUMONIA needs antibiotics, oxygen and chest care; ipratropium is not the treatment for infection (unless there is bronchospasm from co-existing COPD/asthma).

⭐ Key point: Ipratropium = inhaled ANTICHOLINERGIC bronchodilator, given with salbutamol in ACUTE ASTHMA and COPD.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q36$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 658, $s$Salbutamol relieves bronchospasm mainly by acting as a:$s$, $s$["Beta-2 adrenergic agonist causing bronchial smooth muscle relaxation", "Anticholinergic that dries secretions", "Corticosteroid reducing inflammation only", "Mucolytic that thins mucus"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Beta-2 adrenergic agonist causing bronchial smooth muscle relaxation
SALBUTAMOL (albuterol) is a short-acting BETA-2 ADRENERGIC AGONIST (SABA). Beta-2 receptors are found in bronchial smooth muscle. When salbutamol stimulates them, cAMP rises and the smooth muscle RELAXES, so the airways open (BRONCHODILATION) within minutes; effect lasts 4–6 hours. It is the first-line "reliever" for acute asthma and bronchospasm. Side effects come from beta stimulation: TACHYCARDIA, palpitations, fine TREMOR, anxiety and HYPOKALAEMIA (it drives potassium into cells — this is also why nebulised salbutamol can be used to lower high potassium).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anticholinergic that dries secretions — ANTICHOLINERGICS (e.g. ipratropium) work by blocking muscarinic receptors and drying secretions — that is not how salbutamol works.
• Corticosteroid reducing inflammation only — CORTICOSTEROIDS (e.g. hydrocortisone, prednisolone, budesonide) reduce inflammation over hours; salbutamol is not a steroid.
• Mucolytic that thins mucus — MUCOLYTICS (e.g. acetylcysteine, carbocisteine) thin mucus; salbutamol does not.

⭐ Key point: Salbutamol = BETA-2 AGONIST → bronchial smooth muscle relaxation. Side effects: tremor, tachycardia, hypokalaemia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q37$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 659, $s$A ventilated patient's SpO2 drops from 96% to 88% acutely. Using a systematic differential for displacement, obstruction, pneumothorax, and equipment failure, the nurse should assess for all of these EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Tube displacement or dislodgement", "Airway obstruction from secretions", "Pneumothorax", "Recent oral care"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Recent oral care
When a ventilated patient suddenly desaturates, use the DOPE mnemonic: DISPLACEMENT of the tube (slipped into the right main bronchus or out of the trachea), OBSTRUCTION of the tube (secretions, blood, kinking, biting), PNEUMOTHORAX (especially tension pneumothorax from high pressures), EQUIPMENT failure (ventilator, oxygen supply, disconnected circuit). Immediate action: disconnect from the ventilator and hand-ventilate with 100% oxygen with a bag-valve device, check tube position and breath sounds, pass a suction catheter, look at chest movement, check the equipment. RECENT ORAL CARE is part of routine VAP prevention and is not one of the causes in this emergency differential (though careless oral care can move the tube, which is "displacement").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tube displacement or dislodgement — Tube DISPLACEMENT is the "D" in DOPE and must be checked.
• Airway obstruction from secretions — Airway OBSTRUCTION from secretions is the "O" in DOPE — suction is a key step.
• Pneumothorax — PNEUMOTHORAX is the "P" in DOPE; a tension pneumothorax needs immediate decompression.

⭐ Key point: Sudden desaturation on a ventilator = DOPE: Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q52$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 660, $s$The most common complication following thoracotomy is:$s$, $s$["Wound dehiscence", "Atelectasis", "Bronchopleural fistula", "Pericardial tamponade"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Atelectasis
After THORACOTOMY (opening the chest), the commonest complication is ATELECTASIS — collapse of alveoli/lung segments. Reasons: severe incision pain makes the patient breathe shallowly and avoid coughing; anaesthesia and opioids reduce deep breaths; secretions build up and block small airways. Atelectasis causes low SpO2, fever in the first 1–2 days and can lead to pneumonia. Prevention is a key nursing task: good PAIN CONTROL (epidural/PCA), early mobilisation, deep breathing exercises, incentive spirometry, supported coughing (splinting the wound with a pillow), chest physiotherapy, and managing chest drains.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wound dehiscence — WOUND DEHISCENCE (wound opening) is possible but uncommon after thoracotomy.
• Bronchopleural fistula — BRONCHOPLEURAL FISTULA is a serious complication (mainly after pneumonectomy/lobectomy) but much less common.
• Pericardial tamponade — PERICARDIAL TAMPONADE is rare after thoracotomy; it is more associated with cardiac surgery.

⭐ Key point: Commonest post-thoracotomy complication = ATELECTASIS. Prevent with pain control, deep breathing, coughing, early mobilisation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q55$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 661, $s$A patient with respiratory failure is receiving oxygenation and ventilation support. The nurse knows that the main goal of this therapy is to:$s$, $s$["Keep the patient asleep and comfortable", "Reduce the workload of the heart", "Reverse the underlying cause of the respiratory failure", "Maintain adequate gas exchange and support vital organ function"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Maintain adequate gas exchange and support vital organ function
RESPIRATORY FAILURE means the lungs cannot keep normal blood gases: Type 1 — low PaO2 (below 60 mmHg/8 kPa) with normal or low CO2; Type 2 — low PaO2 plus high PaCO2 (above 50 mmHg/6.5 kPa). Oxygen therapy and ventilation (NIV or mechanical) are SUPPORTIVE: their main goal is to MAINTAIN ADEQUATE GAS EXCHANGE (oxygenation and CO2 removal) so that the brain, heart, kidneys and other VITAL ORGANS keep working, while the medical team treats the cause and the lungs recover. They "buy time".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Keep the patient asleep and comfortable — Keeping the patient asleep and comfortable is a part of sedation management, not the main goal of ventilation.
• Reduce the workload of the heart — Ventilation may reduce the work of BREATHING and can affect the heart, but reducing cardiac workload is not the main aim.
• Reverse the underlying cause of the respiratory failure — Ventilation itself does NOT reverse the cause (e.g. pneumonia needs antibiotics, asthma needs bronchodilators); it supports the patient while the cause is treated.

⭐ Key point: Goal of oxygen/ventilation in respiratory failure = maintain ADEQUATE GAS EXCHANGE and protect vital organs while the cause is treated.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q56$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 662, $s$A patient with pulmonary fibrosis has restrictive lung disease. On spirometry, the nurse would expect:$s$, $s$["Reduced FEV1 with a normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio, and reduced total lung capacity", "Increased total lung capacity with a low FEV1/FVC ratio", "Normal spirometry results in all cases", "Increased FVC with normal FEV1"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reduced FEV1 with a normal or increased FEV1/FVC ratio, and reduced total lung capacity
In RESTRICTIVE lung disease (pulmonary fibrosis, chest wall deformity, obesity, neuromuscular weakness) the lungs cannot EXPAND fully. Both FEV1 (air blown out in 1 second) and FVC (total air blown out) are REDUCED, but they fall roughly together, so the FEV1/FVC RATIO is NORMAL or even HIGH (above 70–80%, because stiff lungs empty quickly). TOTAL LUNG CAPACITY is REDUCED — this is the hallmark of restriction. Gas transfer (DLCO) is also reduced in fibrosis. In OBSTRUCTIVE disease (asthma, COPD) FEV1 falls much more than FVC, so the ratio is LOW (below 0.7), and TLC is normal or increased (air trapping).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased total lung capacity with a low FEV1/FVC ratio — High TLC with a LOW FEV1/FVC ratio describes OBSTRUCTIVE disease such as emphysema, not fibrosis.
• Normal spirometry results in all cases — Spirometry is NOT normal in significant restrictive disease; volumes are reduced.
• Increased FVC with normal FEV1 — FVC is DECREASED, not increased, in restrictive disease.

⭐ Key point: Restrictive = low FEV1 and FVC, NORMAL/HIGH ratio, LOW TLC. Obstructive = LOW ratio (<0.7), high TLC.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q58$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 663, $s$In status asthmaticus, oxygen is given to keep the patient's SpO2 at:$s$, $s$["75%", "88%", "Greater than 92%", "Exactly 90%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Greater than 92%
In STATUS ASTHMATICUS (severe asthma attack not responding to usual treatment), the patient is hypoxic because of bronchospasm, mucus plugging and V/Q mismatch. Oxygen should be given to keep SpO2 ABOVE 92% (guidelines: 94–98%). Low oxygen in asthma is a sign of LIFE-THREATENING severity, and a falling SpO2 means the patient is tiring. Other treatment: nebulised salbutamol (back-to-back) with ipratropium, systemic steroids, IV magnesium sulphate, and ICU review for possible intubation if exhausted or PaCO2 rising.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 75% — 75% is severe, dangerous hypoxaemia — far too low.
• 88% — 88% is the lower target used for COPD patients at risk of CO2 retention, not for asthma.
• Exactly 90% — "Exactly 90%" is too low and too rigid; a target range above 92% is used.

⭐ Key point: Status asthmaticus: keep SpO2 > 92% (94–98%). SpO2 < 92% = life-threatening.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q62$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 664, $s$The nurse is preparing to suction the airway of an unconscious patient. The nurse should be aware that excessive suctioning can lead to:$s$, $s$["Hypertension and tachycardia", "Hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis", "Hypoxemia and cardiac arrhythmias", "Increased intracranial pressure"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypoxemia and cardiac arrhythmias
SUCTIONING removes secretions, but it also removes OXYGEN from the airway and lungs, and the catheter stimulates the vagus nerve. Excessive or prolonged suctioning can therefore cause HYPOXAEMIA (low blood oxygen) and CARDIAC ARRHYTHMIAS (bradycardia from vagal stimulation, or tachyarrhythmias from hypoxia). Other risks: atelectasis (lung collapse), mucosal trauma and bleeding, infection, bronchospasm and raised ICP. Safe practice: suction ONLY when needed (secretions heard/seen, falling SpO2, high airway pressures), PRE-OXYGENATE with 100% O2 for 30–60 seconds, use the right catheter size (no more than half the ETT diameter), apply suction for no more than 10–15 SECONDS, use pressure 80–150 mmHg (adults), monitor SpO2 and ECG.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension and tachycardia — HYPERTENSION and TACHYCARDIA can occur briefly but are not the classic main danger.
• Hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis — Suctioning does NOT cause hyperventilation and respiratory alkalosis; it takes away ventilation.
• Increased intracranial pressure — Suctioning can raise ICP temporarily, but the most important general risks are hypoxaemia and arrhythmias.

⭐ Key point: Excessive suctioning → HYPOXAEMIA + ARRHYTHMIAS (bradycardia). Pre-oxygenate; max 10–15 s per pass.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q94$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 665, $s$Pulse oximetry (SpO2) shows a clinically significant drop only after PaO2 falls below approximately:$s$, $s$["100 mmHg", "80 mmHg", "60 mmHg", "40 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 60 mmHg
This is explained by the OXYGEN–HAEMOGLOBIN DISSOCIATION CURVE, which is S-shaped. At the top the curve is FLAT: PaO2 can fall from 100 to about 60 mmHg while SpO2 only falls from about 98–99% to about 90%. Below a PaO2 of about 60 mmHg (8 kPa) the curve becomes STEEP, so a small further fall in PaO2 causes a LARGE fall in SpO2. So pulse oximetry shows a clinically significant drop only after PaO2 falls below about 60 mmHg — which is also the definition of respiratory failure (Type 1). Useful pairs: PaO2 60 ≈ SpO2 90%; PaO2 40 ≈ SpO2 75% (mixed venous); PaO2 27 ≈ SpO2 50% (P50). This means a normal SpO2 can hide a falling PaO2, and also that SpO2 tells nothing about CO2 — check ABG.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100 mmHg — 100 mmHg is a normal PaO2 with SpO2 about 98%.
• 80 mmHg — 80 mmHg still gives SpO2 about 95% — no significant drop.
• 40 mmHg — At 40 mmHg the SpO2 is already very low (about 75%); the significant drop begins earlier, at about 60.

⭐ Key point: SpO2 falls steeply once PaO2 < 60 mmHg (PaO2 60 ≈ SpO2 90%). SpO2 says nothing about CO2.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q103$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 666, $s$Why is humidification important during prolonged or high-flow oxygen therapy?$s$, $s$["Dry medical oxygen can irritate and dry the airway mucosa, impairing mucociliary clearance and causing discomfort", "Humidification increases the FiO2 delivered to the patient", "Humidification eliminates the need for regular oxygen saturation monitoring", "Humidification prevents oxygen toxicity entirely"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dry medical oxygen can irritate and dry the airway mucosa, impairing mucociliary clearance and causing discomfort
Medical oxygen from cylinders and wall supply is completely DRY. Normally the nose and upper airway warm and humidify air. With HIGH-FLOW or PROLONGED oxygen (or when the nose is bypassed by an ETT or tracheostomy), dry gas DRIES the mucosa of the nose, mouth and airways. This causes discomfort, sore throat, nosebleeds, thick sticky secretions and damage to the CILIA, so MUCOCILIARY CLEARANCE is impaired — secretions build up and can block airways and tubes, raising the risk of atelectasis and infection. Humidification (bubble humidifier, heated humidifier, HME filter) prevents this. Usually needed for flow above about 4 L/min, high-flow nasal cannula, and all artificial airways.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Humidification increases the FiO2 delivered to the patient — Humidification does NOT increase FiO2; it adds water vapour, not oxygen.
• Humidification eliminates the need for regular oxygen saturation monitoring — It does NOT replace SpO2 monitoring; oxygen saturation must still be checked.
• Humidification prevents oxygen toxicity entirely — It does NOT prevent oxygen toxicity; that depends on high FiO2 for long periods. Only limiting FiO2 prevents toxicity.

⭐ Key point: Humidify high-flow/prolonged oxygen — dry gas damages mucosa and mucociliary clearance and thickens secretions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q104$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 667, $s$Chest wall mobilisation as an airway clearance component works by:$s$, $s$["Expanding the thorax and loosening secretions", "Directly killing bacteria in the lungs", "Replacing the need for coughing entirely", "Only working on the left lung"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Expanding the thorax and loosening secretions
CHEST WALL MOBILISATION exercises combine active movements of the trunk and arms (stretching, rotation, side flexion) with DEEP BREATHING. They EXPAND THE THORAX (increase chest wall and lung expansion and ventilation, especially of the bases), improve flexibility of the ribs and spine after surgery or immobility, help re-open collapsed alveoli, and LOOSEN SECRETIONS so they can be moved up and cleared by coughing or huffing. They are part of chest physiotherapy for patients after thoracic/abdominal surgery, those with pain-limited breathing, and in chronic lung disease.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Directly killing bacteria in the lungs — Physical therapy cannot KILL BACTERIA — antibiotics treat infection.
• Replacing the need for coughing entirely — It does NOT replace coughing; it helps secretions move so that coughing/huffing can clear them.
• Only working on the left lung — It works on BOTH lungs, not only the left lung.

⭐ Key point: Chest wall mobilisation = expands the thorax + loosens secretions, helping coughing clear the airways.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q199$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 668, $s$Which lung volume cannot be measured directly by simple spirometry and requires methods like helium dilution or body plethysmography?$s$, $s$["Tidal volume", "Inspiratory reserve volume", "Expiratory reserve volume", "Residual volume"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Residual volume
SIMPLE SPIROMETRY measures air moving IN and OUT of the lungs. RESIDUAL VOLUME (RV) is the air that STAYS in the lungs after a maximal breath out — it never leaves, so a spirometer cannot measure it. It must be measured indirectly using HELIUM DILUTION, nitrogen washout, or BODY PLETHYSMOGRAPHY (sitting in a sealed box). Because RV cannot be measured by spirometry, any capacity that includes it (functional residual capacity, total lung capacity) also cannot be measured by spirometry.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tidal volume — Tidal volume (normal breath) can be measured by spirometry.
• Inspiratory reserve volume — Inspiratory reserve volume (extra air breathed in) can be measured.
• Expiratory reserve volume — Expiratory reserve volume (extra air breathed out) can be measured.

⭐ Key point: Residual volume rises in obstructive diseases (COPD, asthma — air trapping) and falls in restrictive diseases (fibrosis, chest wall problems).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q4$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 669, $s$Which structure connects the pharynx to the bronchi, serving as the main airway?$s$, $s$["Oesophagus", "Trachea", "Aorta", "Ureter"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Trachea
The TRACHEA (windpipe) is a tube about 10–12 cm long, supported by C-shaped cartilage rings, that runs from the LARYNX (below the pharynx) down into the chest, where it divides (at the carina) into the RIGHT and LEFT MAIN BRONCHI. It is the MAIN AIRWAY carrying air to and from the lungs. Its lining has cilia and mucus that trap and move particles upwards.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oesophagus — The oesophagus carries food from the pharynx to the stomach.
• Aorta — The aorta is the main artery carrying blood from the heart.
• Ureter — The ureter carries urine from the kidney to the bladder.

⭐ Key point: The right main bronchus is wider, shorter and more vertical than the left — so ET tubes inserted too far and aspirated material usually enter the right side.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q8$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 670, $s$The air bronchogram sign on chest X-ray is most associated with:$s$, $s$["Pneumothorax", "Consolidation/pneumonia", "Rib fracture", "Cardiomegaly"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Consolidation/pneumonia
An AIR BRONCHOGRAM appears as dark, branching tubes (air-filled bronchi) seen against a WHITE background of lung. Normally, bronchi are not visible because the surrounding lung is also full of air (both dark). When the ALVEOLI around the bronchi become filled with FLUID, PUS, blood or cells (CONSOLIDATION), the lung turns white, and the air-filled bronchi stand out. It is classic for PNEUMONIA, and is also seen in pulmonary oedema, ARDS and lung haemorrhage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pneumothorax — A pneumothorax shows a dark area with NO lung markings and a visible lung edge.
• Rib fracture — A rib fracture shows a break or step in the bone outline.
• Cardiomegaly — Cardiomegaly shows an enlarged heart shadow (cardiothoracic ratio above 0.5).

⭐ Key point: Consolidation + air bronchograms + fever and cough strongly suggests pneumonia — take sputum and blood cultures and start antibiotics.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q44$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 671, $s$A patient on oxygen therapy complains of a dry nose and throat. The nurse should:$s$, $s$["Discontinue the oxygen therapy", "Increase the oxygen flow rate", "Add a humidifier to the oxygen system", "Switch the patient to a non-rebreather mask"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Add a humidifier to the oxygen system
OXYGEN from cylinders or wall supplies is very DRY. It dries the nose and throat, causing discomfort, nosebleeds and thick secretions. The nurse should ADD A HUMIDIFIER to the oxygen system (especially at higher flows), and provide mouth care and water-based lubricant for the lips/nose.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Discontinue the oxygen therapy — Stopping oxygen may cause hypoxia.
• Increase the oxygen flow rate — Increasing flow would worsen dryness.
• Switch the patient to a non-rebreather mask — Switching masks doesn't address dryness.

⭐ Key point: Low-flow nasal cannula (below 4 L/min) usually doesn't need humidification; higher flows do.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q52$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 672, $s$A patient with acute pulmonary oedema on strict intake and output monitoring produces only 20 mL of urine in 2 hours despite IV furosemide 80 mg. What is the priority nursing action?$s$, $s$["Administer another diuretic dose without further assessment", "Assess for hypotension and reassess renal perfusion, and notify the physician", "Increase IV fluids to promote diuresis", "Discontinue oxygen therapy"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assess for hypotension and reassess renal perfusion, and notify the physician
Only 20 mL of urine in 2 hours (about 10 mL/hr) despite IV furosemide 80 mg suggests the kidneys are not responding — possibly due to HYPOTENSION (poor kidney perfusion), AKI, or a blocked catheter. The priority is to ASSESS for HYPOTENSION, REASSESS RENAL PERFUSION (BP, catheter patency, fluid status) and NOTIFY THE PHYSICIAN.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer another diuretic dose without further assessment — Giving more diuretic without assessment may worsen hypotension.
• Increase IV fluids to promote diuresis — More IV fluids could worsen pulmonary oedema.
• Discontinue oxygen therapy — Discontinuing oxygen is dangerous in pulmonary oedema.

⭐ Key point: Check the catheter for blockage first.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q53$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 673, $s$A patient's chest tube water seal chamber shows fluid that stops fluctuating ('swinging') with respiration. This most likely indicates:$s$, $s$["The lung has fully re-expanded, or the tube is blocked/kinked", "Normal ongoing function", "An air leak", "Excessive suction"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The lung has fully re-expanded, or the tube is blocked/kinked
In a chest drain system, fluid in the WATER SEAL chamber normally FLUCTUATES ("SWINGS") up and down with breathing. If the swinging STOPS, it means either the LUNG has FULLY RE-EXPANDED (no space left), or the TUBE is BLOCKED or KINKED.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal ongoing function — It is not normal ongoing function.
• An air leak — An air leak causes bubbling.
• Excessive suction — Excessive suction would cause continuous bubbling in the suction chamber.

⭐ Key point: If swinging stops, assess the patient (breath sounds, SpO2) and check the tubing for kinks and clots.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q57$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 674, $s$Disadvantages of endotracheal intubation includes all of the following EXCEPT$s$, $s$["Loss of the protective function of the upper airway", "Loss of phonation (voice)", "Damage to the subglottic area", "Decreased airway resistance"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Decreased airway resistance
Disadvantages of ENDOTRACHEAL INTUBATION include: LOSS of the upper airway's PROTECTIVE functions (warming, humidifying, filtering air, cough), LOSS of PHONATION (cannot speak), and DAMAGE to the SUBGLOTTIC area (stenosis, ulceration). DECREASED AIRWAY RESISTANCE is NOT a disadvantage — the tube actually INCREASES resistance compared with the natural airway.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Loss of the protective function of the upper airway — Loss of protective function IS a disadvantage.
• Loss of phonation (voice) — Loss of phonation IS a disadvantage.
• Damage to the subglottic area — Subglottic damage IS a disadvantage.

⭐ Key point: Provide humidification and alternative communication methods for intubated patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q59$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 675, $s$A patient who has severe shortness of breath(SOB) and his/her ABGs shows high level of carbon dioxide (CO2) 55mm hg and low level of (Sao2) 40mmhg, which shows type II respiratory failure. Which Approach is best to treat the patient?$s$, $s$["Delivering Oxygen through face mask", "Delivering oxygen through CPAP machine", "Delivering oxygen through BIPAP machine", "None of them"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Delivering oxygen through BIPAP machine
TYPE II RESPIRATORY FAILURE (high CO2, low O2) requires ventilatory support to remove CO2. BiPAP (bilevel positive airway pressure) provides higher inspiratory pressure (to increase tidal volume and blow off CO2) and lower expiratory pressure (to keep airways open). It is the best non-invasive approach.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delivering Oxygen through face mask — A face mask alone doesn't remove CO2 and may worsen hypercapnia.
• Delivering oxygen through CPAP machine — CPAP improves oxygenation but doesn't assist ventilation as effectively.
• None of them — BiPAP is an appropriate option.

⭐ Key point: BiPAP reduces intubation and mortality in COPD exacerbations with acidosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q60$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 676, $s$Type I respiratory failure is also known as:$s$, $s$["Ventilatory failure", "Hypercapnic failure", "Hypoxaemic failure", "Septicaemic failure"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypoxaemic failure
TYPE I RESPIRATORY FAILURE is also called HYPOXAEMIC respiratory failure. It is defined by a LOW PaO2 (below 60 mmHg / 8 kPa) with a NORMAL or LOW PaCO2. It is a problem of GAS EXCHANGE/oxygenation (V/Q mismatch, shunt, diffusion problems), e.g. pneumonia, pulmonary oedema, ARDS, pulmonary embolism, asthma (early). The CO2 is normal or low because breathing effort is usually increased.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ventilatory failure — VENTILATORY failure is TYPE II (the "pump" fails to move enough air).
• Hypercapnic failure — HYPERCAPNIC failure (high CO2) is TYPE II.
• Septicaemic failure — "Septicaemic failure" is not a recognised classification of respiratory failure.

⭐ Key point: Treat Type I with oxygen, PEEP/CPAP and treatment of the cause; Type II needs ventilatory support (NIV or intubation).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q63$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 677, $s$All are clinical features of pulmonary oedema EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Cough with pink, frothy sputum", "Extreme dyspnoea", "Inspiratory and expiratory wheezing", "Syncope"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Syncope
PULMONARY OEDEMA is fluid in the lung tissue and alveoli, usually from LEFT HEART FAILURE (high pressure in the lung capillaries pushes fluid out). Classic features: EXTREME DYSPNOEA (especially lying flat — orthopnoea — and waking breathless at night), COUGH with PINK, FROTHY SPUTUM (fluid mixed with air and a little blood), WHEEZING (fluid narrows the airways — "cardiac asthma"), crackles at both lung bases, sweating, anxiety, tachycardia and low SpO2. SYNCOPE (fainting) is NOT a typical feature of pulmonary oedema; it suggests arrhythmia, aortic stenosis, or other causes of sudden reduced brain blood flow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cough with pink, frothy sputum — Pink, frothy sputum IS a classic feature.
• Extreme dyspnoea — Extreme dyspnoea IS a classic feature.
• Inspiratory and expiratory wheezing — Wheezing IS a feature ("cardiac asthma").

⭐ Key point: Treatment: sit the patient upright, give oxygen/CPAP, IV furosemide and nitrates, and treat the cause (e.g. MI, hypertension, arrhythmia).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q71$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 678, $s$Kinking of the endotracheal tube, another cause of obstruction, is most likely to occur:$s$, $s$["Only inside the ventilator circuit", "In the trachea below the carina", "Only with tracheostomy tubes", "At the point where the tube exits the mouth or nose if not properly secured, or if the patient's head is turned sharply"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: At the point where the tube exits the mouth or nose if not properly secured, or if the patient's head is turned sharply
ET tube KINKING usually happens where the tube EXITS the MOUTH or NOSE, especially if it isn't secured properly or the patient's head is turned sharply. Kinking causes high airway pressures and poor ventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only inside the ventilator circuit — Kinking isn't only inside the circuit.
• In the trachea below the carina — It doesn't occur below the carina.
• Only with tracheostomy tubes — It isn't only with tracheostomy tubes.

⭐ Key point: Secure the tube properly and support the tubing with holders.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q101$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 679, $s$Fatigue combined with somnolence in a severe asthmatic is classified as:$s$, $s$["An absolute indication for intubation", "A relative indication to strongly consider intubation", "A sign of successful treatment", "Not clinically significant"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A relative indication to strongly consider intubation
In SEVERE ASTHMA, FATIGUE (exhaustion) with SOMNOLENCE (drowsiness) means the breathing muscles are tiring and CO2 is rising — the patient is heading towards respiratory failure. This is a RELATIVE INDICATION to STRONGLY CONSIDER INTUBATION (and an urgent ICU review). ABSOLUTE indications are cardiac or respiratory arrest, coma, or severe refractory hypoxaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An absolute indication for intubation — It is not an absolute indication on its own; absolute ones include arrest and coma.
• A sign of successful treatment — Drowsiness and fatigue are signs of DETERIORATION, not successful treatment.
• Not clinically significant — They are highly significant warning signs.

⭐ Key point: Other danger signs in asthma: silent chest, cyanosis, SpO2 below 92%, normal or rising PaCO2, bradycardia and hypotension.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q107$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 680, $s$The nurse calls for you to help extubate a female patient who is orally intubated. All of the following equipment is required for this procedure EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["New endotracheal tube", "Suction equipment", "Bag-valve mask", "Arterial blood gas kit"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Arterial blood gas kit
EXTUBATION can fail suddenly (laryngospasm, airway oedema, aspiration, weakness), so emergency equipment must be ready: SUCTION equipment (to clear the mouth, throat and tube before removal), a BAG-VALVE-MASK with oxygen (to support breathing if needed), and a NEW ENDOTRACHEAL TUBE with laryngoscope (in case RE-INTUBATION is needed). Also: oxygen delivery device (face mask or nasal cannula), syringe to deflate the cuff, and monitoring. An ARTERIAL BLOOD GAS KIT is not required for the extubation procedure itself (an ABG may be checked later if needed).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• New endotracheal tube — A new ET tube IS needed for possible re-intubation.
• Suction equipment — Suction IS needed.
• Bag-valve mask — A bag-valve-mask IS needed.

⭐ Key point: After extubation, sit the patient up, give oxygen, encourage coughing, and watch closely for stridor and respiratory distress.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q108$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 681, $s$The equipment needed for nasotracheal intubation includes the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Magill forceps", "Laryngoscope", "Endotracheal tube", "Chromic 2/0 suture"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Chromic 2/0 suture
NASOTRACHEAL INTUBATION passes the tube through the nose into the trachea (used in oral/maxillofacial surgery or when the mouth can't be opened). Equipment includes: LARYNGOSCOPE (to see the vocal cords), ENDOTRACHEAL TUBE (often a smaller, softer nasal tube), MAGILL FORCEPS (to guide the tube tip through the vocal cords), lubricant, nasal vasoconstrictor spray (to reduce bleeding), suction and oxygen. A CHROMIC 2/0 SUTURE (an absorbable surgical stitch) is NOT needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Magill forceps — Magill forceps ARE needed.
• Laryngoscope — A laryngoscope IS needed.
• Endotracheal tube — An ET tube IS needed.

⭐ Key point: Nasotracheal intubation is contraindicated in suspected basal skull fracture and severe clotting problems.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q111$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 682, $s$Which of the following is classified as a MAJOR/SEVERE contrast reaction?$s$, $s$["Mild urticaria", "Metallic taste", "Laryngeal oedema and respiratory arrest", "Mild nausea"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Laryngeal oedema and respiratory arrest
CONTRAST REACTIONS are graded as mild, moderate or severe. SEVERE (major) reactions are life-threatening: LARYNGEAL OEDEMA (airway swelling), severe bronchospasm, profound hypotension, seizures, cardiac arrest and RESPIRATORY ARREST. These require immediate emergency treatment (adrenaline IM, oxygen, airway management, fluids, CPR).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild urticaria — Mild urticaria is a MILD reaction.
• Metallic taste — A metallic taste is a normal physiological effect.
• Mild nausea — Mild nausea is a MILD reaction.

⭐ Key point: Ask about previous contrast reactions, allergies, asthma and kidney function before giving contrast.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q41$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 683, $s$To increase SpO2 in a mechanical ventilated patient, which of the following parameters should be adjusted:$s$, $s$["Fio2 and R/R", "Vt and R/R", "Vt and PEEP", "PEEP and FiO2"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: PEEP and FiO2
OXYGENATION (SpO2, PaO2) on a ventilator is improved mainly by increasing the FiO2 (fraction of inspired oxygen) and PEEP (positive end-expiratory pressure). FiO2 increases the oxygen in each breath; PEEP keeps alveoli open (recruits collapsed alveoli), reducing shunt and improving oxygen transfer.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fio2 and R/R — Rate mainly affects CO2.
• Vt and R/R — Tidal volume and rate mainly affect CO2.
• Vt and PEEP — Tidal volume affects CO2 more than oxygen.

⭐ Key point: Oxygenation = FiO2 + PEEP. Ventilation (CO2) = rate + tidal volume.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q51$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 684, $s$All of the following are synonyms for (or terms linked to) ARDS EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Shock lung syndrome", "Diffuse alveolar damage", "Acute lung injury", "Acute liver injury"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute liver injury
ARDS (Acute Respiratory Distress Syndrome) is also known as or linked to: SHOCK LUNG syndrome (old name), DIFFUSE ALVEOLAR DAMAGE (the pathological finding), ACUTE LUNG INJURY (older term for milder forms), "wet lung" and "stiff lung". ACUTE LIVER INJURY is a LIVER condition, unrelated to ARDS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shock lung syndrome — Shock lung is an old name for ARDS.
• Diffuse alveolar damage — Diffuse alveolar damage is the typical pathology of ARDS.
• Acute lung injury — Acute lung injury was the older term for mild ARDS.

⭐ Key point: The Berlin definition (2012) replaced "acute lung injury" with mild ARDS.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q52$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 685, $s$1. Which One of the following is NOT a cause of acute ventilation failure?$s$, $s$["Neurological impairment", "Neuromuscular impairment", "Primary respiratory impairment", "Appendectomy"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Appendectomy
ACUTE VENTILATORY FAILURE is caused by problems with the breathing "pump" or lungs: NEUROLOGICAL impairment (e.g. head injury, stroke, drug overdose depressing the breathing centre), NEUROMUSCULAR impairment (e.g. Guillain-Barré, myasthenia gravis, spinal cord injury), and PRIMARY RESPIRATORY problems (e.g. COPD, asthma, pneumonia). An APPENDECTOMY is a surgical procedure, not a cause of ventilatory failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Neurological impairment — Neurological impairment IS a cause.
• Neuromuscular impairment — Neuromuscular impairment IS a cause.
• Primary respiratory impairment — Primary respiratory impairment IS a cause.

⭐ Key point: Post-operative patients can develop ventilatory failure from opioids, pain or atelectasis — but the surgery itself is not the cause.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q53$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 686, $s$A nurse is assessing a patient with a known history of COPD. The nurse should be aware that the patient is at risk for Type II respiratory failure due to:$s$, $s$["An increase in gas exchange efficiency", "A decrease in lung compliance", "Chronic carbon dioxide retention", "A hyperactive respiratory drive"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Chronic carbon dioxide retention
Patients with COPD often have CHRONIC CARBON DIOXIDE RETENTION due to airway obstruction, air trapping, V/Q mismatch, and tired respiratory muscles. This puts them at risk of TYPE II (hypercapnic) respiratory failure, especially during exacerbations or with excessive oxygen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An increase in gas exchange efficiency — COPD reduces gas exchange efficiency.
• A decrease in lung compliance — COPD (emphysema) often increases compliance; reduced compliance is more typical of fibrosis.
• A hyperactive respiratory drive — COPD patients do not have a hyperactive drive causing type II failure.

⭐ Key point: Target SpO2 88–92% in COPD patients at risk of CO2 retention.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q56$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 687, $s$A patient recovering from thoracotomy has thick, tenacious secretions and a weak cough. The most appropriate nursing intervention is to:$s$, $s$["Withhold analgesia to encourage a stronger cough", "Provide adequate analgesia, encourage deep breathing/coughing (splinting the incision), and consider chest physiotherapy", "Discourage any mobilisation", "Avoid humidified oxygen"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Provide adequate analgesia, encourage deep breathing/coughing (splinting the incision), and consider chest physiotherapy
After THORACOTOMY, pain limits deep breathing and coughing, so secretions build up, risking atelectasis and pneumonia. Nursing interventions: ADEQUATE ANALGESIA (so the patient can breathe deeply and cough), DEEP BREATHING and COUGHING exercises with SPLINTING of the incision (pillow support), CHEST PHYSIOTHERAPY, humidified oxygen (loosens secretions), early mobilisation, and incentive spirometry.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Withhold analgesia to encourage a stronger cough — Withholding analgesia prevents effective coughing.
• Discourage any mobilisation — Mobilisation should be encouraged.
• Avoid humidified oxygen — Humidified oxygen helps thin secretions.

⭐ Key point: Epidural or paravertebral analgesia is often used after thoracotomy for good pain control.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q62$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 688, $s$What is the appropriate amount of time to suction a patient in a single interval?$s$, $s$["No more than 20 – 30 seconds", "No more than 10 – 20 seconds", "No more than 5 – 10 seconds", "No more than 10 – 15 seconds"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: No more than 10 – 15 seconds
Each SUCTION PASS should last NO MORE THAN 10–15 SECONDS. Longer suctioning removes oxygen from the lungs, causing HYPOXIA, bradycardia, arrhythmias, atelectasis and mucosal trauma.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No more than 20 – 30 seconds — 20–30 seconds is too long.
• No more than 10 – 20 seconds — 10–20 seconds could be too long.
• No more than 5 – 10 seconds — 5–10 seconds may be too short to clear secretions effectively.

⭐ Key point: Pre-oxygenate before suctioning, apply suction only while withdrawing, and allow recovery between passes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q63$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 689, $s$A patient with COPD should be educated to seek urgent medical attention for which warning sign?$s$, $s$["A significant increase in breathlessness, a change in sputum colour/volume, or new fever suggesting an exacerbation or infection", "Occasional mild breathlessness identical to their usual baseline", "Feeling well with no change in symptoms", "A single missed dose of an as-needed bronchodilator with no symptoms"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A significant increase in breathlessness, a change in sputum colour/volume, or new fever suggesting an exacerbation or infection
COPD patients should seek urgent help for signs of an EXACERBATION: significantly INCREASED BREATHLESSNESS, CHANGE in SPUTUM colour (yellow/green) or VOLUME, NEW FEVER, chest pain, confusion, or drowsiness. Early treatment (steroids, antibiotics) can prevent hospital admission.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Occasional mild breathlessness identical to their usual baseline — Usual baseline breathlessness doesn't need urgent care.
• Feeling well with no change in symptoms — Feeling well needs no urgent attention.
• A single missed dose of an as-needed bronchodilator with no symptoms — One missed as-needed dose with no symptoms isn't urgent.

⭐ Key point: Provide a written COPD action plan, and ensure inhaler technique and vaccinations are up to date.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q102$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 690, $s$Hypoxia during general anaesthesia refers to:$s$, $s$["Inadequate oxygen delivery to tissues", "Excess oxygen delivery to tissues", "Elevated body temperature", "Low blood glucose"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inadequate oxygen delivery to tissues
HYPOXIA means INADEQUATE OXYGEN DELIVERY to (or use by) the TISSUES. During general anaesthesia, causes include: airway obstruction or misplaced tube (e.g. oesophageal intubation), hypoventilation, low FiO2 or oxygen supply failure, disconnection, laryngospasm or bronchospasm, aspiration, atelectasis, pneumothorax, and reduced cardiac output. Hypoxia can rapidly cause brain injury, arrhythmias and cardiac arrest. (Strictly, HYPOXAEMIA is low oxygen in the blood; HYPOXIA is low oxygen at the tissue level.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Excess oxygen delivery to tissues — Excess oxygen delivery is HYPEROXIA.
• Elevated body temperature — Elevated body temperature is HYPERTHERMIA.
• Low blood glucose — Low blood glucose is HYPOGLYCAEMIA.

⭐ Key point: Continuous pulse oximetry, capnography and an oxygen analyser are essential safety monitors during anaesthesia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q109$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 691, $s$What is the recommended maximum suction pressure for children?$s$, $s$["130 mmHg", "140 mmHg", "120 mmHg", "150 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 120 mmHg
SUCTION PRESSURE must be high enough to clear secretions but low enough to avoid damaging the delicate airway lining (bleeding, oedema) and causing hypoxia or atelectasis. For CHILDREN, the recommended maximum is about 80–120 mmHg (here, 120 mmHg is the maximum given). For adults, about 100–150 mmHg; for neonates, about 60–100 mmHg.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 130 mmHg — 130 mmHg is above the recommended paediatric maximum.
• 140 mmHg — 140 mmHg is too high for children.
• 150 mmHg — 150 mmHg is the upper adult limit — too high for children.

⭐ Key point: Use the smallest effective pressure and catheter size, pre-oxygenate, limit each pass to 5–10 seconds in children, and apply suction only while withdrawing the catheter.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q147$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 692, $s$Intellectual standards such as clarity, accuracy, precision, relevance, and logic are used in critical thinking primarily to:$s$, $s$["Assess and improve the quality of one's own reasoning", "Serve as hospital policy documents", "Replace the need for clinical experience", "Determine nurse staffing ratios"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assess and improve the quality of one's own reasoning
INTELLECTUAL STANDARDS (Paul and Elder) — CLARITY, ACCURACY, PRECISION, RELEVANCE, DEPTH, BREADTH, LOGIC, SIGNIFICANCE and FAIRNESS — are criteria nurses use to CHECK and IMPROVE the QUALITY of their own THINKING and REASONING. For example: "Is my information accurate? Is it relevant to this patient's problem? Is my conclusion logical?" This helps nurses make sound clinical judgements and avoid errors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Serve as hospital policy documents — They are not hospital policy documents.
• Replace the need for clinical experience — They do not replace clinical experience; they help nurses learn from it.
• Determine nurse staffing ratios — They are not used to determine staffing ratios.

⭐ Key point: Critical thinking combines knowledge, experience, intellectual standards and reflection to make safe decisions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q151$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 693, $s$How many postural drainage positions are used, corresponding to the lung's bronchopulmonary segments?$s$, $s$["6", "8", "10", "12"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 12
POSTURAL DRAINAGE uses GRAVITY to help drain secretions from specific lung segments into the larger airways, where they can be coughed up or suctioned. The patient is placed in different positions so the affected segment is uppermost and its bronchus points downwards. Commonly, 12 positions are described, corresponding to the main bronchopulmonary segments of both lungs (e.g. upper lobe apical, posterior and anterior segments; middle lobe/lingula; lower lobe superior, anterior, lateral and posterior basal segments).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 6 — 6 positions are too few to cover all segments.
• 8 — 8 positions are too few.
• 10 — 10 positions are too few.

⭐ Key point: Postural drainage is often combined with percussion and vibration. Head-down positions are avoided in raised ICP, recent surgery, haemoptysis and severe breathlessness.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q200$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 694, $s$For a patient with an acute COPD exacerbation presenting with respiratory acidosis (pH 7.30, PaCO2 60 mmHg) but still conscious and able to protect the airway, which intervention is generally tried before invasive intubation?$s$, $s$["High-dose intravenous sedation to reduce work of breathing", "Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (e.g., BiPAP)", "Immediate elective tracheostomy", "Therapeutic bronchoalveolar lavage"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Non-invasive positive pressure ventilation (e.g., BiPAP)
This COPD patient has ACUTE HYPERCAPNIC (type 2) respiratory failure — pH 7.30 (acidaemia) with PaCO2 60 mmHg — but is still CONSCIOUS and able to PROTECT THE AIRWAY. This is the classic indication for NON-INVASIVE VENTILATION (NIV/BiPAP) via a tight-fitting face mask. BiPAP gives a higher pressure during inspiration (IPAP), which helps the tired breathing muscles take bigger breaths and blow off CO2, and a lower pressure during expiration (EPAP), which keeps small airways open. Trials show NIV in COPD exacerbations REDUCES the need for intubation, ICU stay and death. Reassess with an ABG after 1–2 hours: if pH and PaCO2 are not improving, intubation should be considered.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High-dose intravenous sedation to reduce work of breathing — High-dose sedation would depress breathing further, worsen CO2 retention and could cause respiratory arrest.
• Immediate elective tracheostomy — Tracheostomy is a long-term airway for prolonged ventilation, not an emergency first step in an alert patient.
• Therapeutic bronchoalveolar lavage — Bronchoalveolar lavage is a diagnostic/therapeutic bronchoscopy procedure, not treatment for hypercapnic failure.

⭐ Key point: Contraindications to NIV: respiratory arrest, unable to protect the airway, vomiting, facial trauma, untreated pneumothorax, severe haemodynamic instability, and copious secretions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q71$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 695, $s$Type II (hypercapnic) respiratory failure, defined by PaCO2 above about 45-50 mmHg with acidaemia, is most typically caused by:$s$, $s$["Alveolar hypoventilation, such as in COPD, neuromuscular disease, or CNS depression", "Isolated diffusion impairment from pulmonary fibrosis alone", "A pure right-to-left cardiac shunt", "High-altitude exposure alone"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Alveolar hypoventilation, such as in COPD, neuromuscular disease, or CNS depression
TYPE II (HYPERCAPNIC) RESPIRATORY FAILURE is defined by a HIGH PaCO2 (above about 45–50 mmHg) with acidaemia, often with low oxygen too. It is caused by ALVEOLAR HYPOVENTILATION — not enough air moving in and out of the alveoli to remove CO2. Causes: COPD (tired muscles, air trapping), NEUROMUSCULAR DISEASE (Guillain–Barré, myasthenia gravis, motor neurone disease, spinal cord injury), CNS DEPRESSION (opioids, sedatives, head injury), chest wall problems (kyphoscoliosis, obesity hypoventilation, flail chest).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Isolated diffusion impairment from pulmonary fibrosis alone — Isolated diffusion impairment (fibrosis) mainly causes low oxygen (TYPE I) with normal or low CO2.
• A pure right-to-left cardiac shunt — A pure right-to-left shunt causes low oxygen (Type I); CO2 is usually normal or low.
• High-altitude exposure alone — High altitude causes hypoxaemia with LOW CO2 (hyperventilation).

⭐ Key point: Type I = low O2, normal/low CO2 (gas exchange problem). Type II = high CO2 (ventilation/"pump" problem).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q79$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 696, $s$A patient's endotracheal tube cuff, inflated with air, is left in place during a prolonged case using nitrous oxide. A recognized risk of this combination is:$s$, $s$["Reduced cuff pressure requiring frequent reinflation", "Cuff deflation over time", "Progressive cuff overinflation as nitrous oxide diffuses into the cuff, risking tracheal mucosal ischaemia", "Cuff material degradation"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Progressive cuff overinflation as nitrous oxide diffuses into the cuff, risking tracheal mucosal ischaemia
NITROUS OXIDE (N2O) is much more soluble in blood than nitrogen. During anaesthesia, it DIFFUSES into air-filled spaces — including the air-filled endotracheal tube CUFF — faster than nitrogen can leave. So the cuff volume and PRESSURE INCREASE progressively. High cuff pressure (above 30 cmH2O) compresses the tracheal blood supply, causing TRACHEAL MUCOSAL ISCHAEMIA, sore throat, ulceration, and later stenosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reduced cuff pressure requiring frequent reinflation — The cuff pressure INCREASES, not decreases.
• Cuff deflation over time — The cuff over-inflates rather than deflates.
• Cuff material degradation — Material degradation is not the issue.

⭐ Key point: Check cuff pressure regularly with a manometer during nitrous oxide anaesthesia, or fill the cuff with saline or the anaesthetic gas mixture.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q100$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 697, $s$A sudden high-pressure alarm accompanied by absent chest rise, difficulty bagging, and inability to pass a suction catheter most strongly suggests:$s$, $s$["Endotracheal tube obstruction (e.g., mucus plug, kinking, biting)", "Correct tube placement with normal function", "Ventilator circuit disconnection", "Tension pneumothorax with hyperresonance"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Endotracheal tube obstruction (e.g., mucus plug, kinking, biting)
A SUDDEN HIGH-PRESSURE alarm with ABSENT CHEST RISE, DIFFICULTY BAGGING, and INABILITY TO PASS A SUCTION CATHETER strongly suggests the ENDOTRACHEAL TUBE IS OBSTRUCTED: mucus plug, blood clot, kinking, or the patient biting the tube. If the obstruction can't be cleared, the tube may need replacing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Correct tube placement with normal function — Correct tube function would allow ventilation and suction.
• Ventilator circuit disconnection — Circuit disconnection causes LOW pressure alarms.
• Tension pneumothorax with hyperresonance — Tension pneumothorax causes high pressures, but a suction catheter would still pass down the tube.

⭐ Key point: Follow DOPE: Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment failure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q107$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 698, $s$A mechanically ventilated patient's high-pressure alarm sounds repeatedly. On assessment, breath sounds are decreased on the right with tracheal deviation to the left and hyper-resonance on percussion. The nurse should suspect:$s$, $s$["Mucus plug in the right main bronchus", "Pulmonary oedema", "Normal ventilator function", "Tension pneumothorax on the right"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tension pneumothorax on the right
High-pressure alarms, DECREASED BREATH SOUNDS on the RIGHT, TRACHEAL DEVIATION to the LEFT (away from the affected side), and HYPER-RESONANCE on the right suggest a TENSION PNEUMOTHORAX on the RIGHT. This is life-threatening; it needs immediate needle decompression and a chest drain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mucus plug in the right main bronchus — A mucus plug causes dullness, not hyper-resonance, and doesn't push the trachea away.
• Pulmonary oedema — Pulmonary oedema causes crackles, not tracheal deviation.
• Normal ventilator function — This is not normal.

⭐ Key point: Ventilated patients are at high risk of tension pneumothorax — act on clinical signs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q129$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 699, $s$A patient came to the ER with Difficulty in breathing; He was intubated and placed on Mechanical ventilation. His ABGs report showed that his PCO2 is 55 mm Hg. To wash out CO2 which of the following parameter(s) should be adjusted:$s$, $s$["Vt and PEEP", "Vt and R/R", "Fio2 and R/R", "PEEP and FiO2"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vt and R/R
CO2 removal depends on MINUTE VENTILATION = TIDAL VOLUME (Vt) × RESPIRATORY RATE (R/R). A PaCO2 of 55 mmHg is high (normal 35–45). To "wash out" CO2, INCREASE the RESPIRATORY RATE and/or TIDAL VOLUME. In practice, the rate is usually increased first (within limits that avoid air trapping), while tidal volume is kept within lung-protective limits (6–8 mL/kg PBW; plateau ≤30 cmH2O). Rough guide: new rate = current rate × current PaCO2 ÷ target PaCO2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vt and PEEP — PEEP mainly improves OXYGENATION; tidal volume affects CO2, but PEEP does not significantly.
• Fio2 and R/R — FiO2 affects OXYGEN only; it does not wash out CO2.
• PEEP and FiO2 — PEEP and FiO2 both control OXYGENATION, not ventilation.

⭐ Key point: Remember: OXYGENATION = FiO2 + PEEP; VENTILATION (CO2) = RATE + TIDAL VOLUME.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q131$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 700, $s$A patient is extubated successfully but develops increasing stridor and respiratory distress within an hour. The nurse should anticipate:$s$, $s$["Post-extubation laryngeal oedema/airway swelling, potentially requiring nebulised adrenaline, steroids, or reintubation", "No specific concern, since extubation was successful", "No monitoring is needed for the next several hours", "Discharge planning"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Post-extubation laryngeal oedema/airway swelling, potentially requiring nebulised adrenaline, steroids, or reintubation
After extubation, the larynx may be SWOLLEN (laryngeal oedema) from the tube, causing STRIDOR and respiratory distress within hours. Treatment: NEBULISED ADRENALINE (reduces swelling), STEROIDS (dexamethasone), sitting upright, humidified oxygen, and possibly REINTUBATION.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No specific concern, since extubation was successful — Stridor is a serious concern.
• No monitoring is needed for the next several hours — Close monitoring is needed.
• Discharge planning — Discharge planning is inappropriate.

⭐ Key point: A cuff leak test before extubation helps predict laryngeal oedema.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q137$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 701, $s$A patient with pulmonary oedema is started on BiPAP. What is the expected physiological benefit?$s$, $s$["Increases preload to improve cardiac output", "Eliminates the need for diuretic therapy", "Increases venous return to the right heart", "Reduces work of breathing and improves alveolar recruitment via positive pressure"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reduces work of breathing and improves alveolar recruitment via positive pressure
BiPAP in PULMONARY OEDEMA: the positive pressure REDUCES the WORK OF BREATHING, RECRUITS (re-opens) alveoli, pushes fluid out of the alveoli, and reduces preload and afterload of the left ventricle, improving heart function.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increases preload to improve cardiac output — BiPAP reduces preload.
• Eliminates the need for diuretic therapy — It doesn't replace diuretics.
• Increases venous return to the right heart — It reduces venous return.

⭐ Key point: Use BiPAP/CPAP early in cardiogenic pulmonary oedema with IV nitrates.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q139$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 702, $s$A patient with acute respiratory failure requires emergent intubation. Which pre-intubation nursing action is most important to optimize outcomes?$s$, $s$["Sedate the patient heavily without any oxygen supplementation", "Pre-oxygenate with high-flow oxygen or bag-valve-mask prior to intubation", "Delay intubation until the patient is fully alert", "Withhold oxygen until the tube is placed to avoid hyperoxia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pre-oxygenate with high-flow oxygen or bag-valve-mask prior to intubation
During intubation, the patient stops breathing for a period (apnoea). PRE-OXYGENATION — breathing 100% oxygen for 3–5 minutes (via a tight-fitting mask, high-flow oxygen, or non-invasive ventilation) before induction — fills the lungs with oxygen (replaces nitrogen in the functional residual capacity). This creates an oxygen "reservoir" that lets the patient tolerate apnoea for longer before oxygen levels fall dangerously. It is the most important step to prevent hypoxia and cardiac arrest during emergency intubation, especially in critically ill patients who desaturate quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sedate the patient heavily without any oxygen supplementation — Heavy sedation without oxygen causes severe hypoxia.
• Delay intubation until the patient is fully alert — Delaying intubation until the patient is fully alert is inappropriate in respiratory failure.
• Withhold oxygen until the tube is placed to avoid hyperoxia — Withholding oxygen is dangerous; avoiding hypoxia is far more important than avoiding temporary hyperoxia.

⭐ Key point: Also use apnoeic oxygenation (high-flow nasal oxygen during intubation), position the patient head-up, and have suction, airway adjuncts and a difficult airway plan ready.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q147$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 703, $s$A patient's SpO2 reads 99% but the patient appears cyanotic, confused, and has recently been exposed to carbon monoxide. The nurse should recognise that:$s$, $s$["The SpO2 reading is definitely accurate and reassuring", "No further assessment is needed given the good SpO2", "Cyanosis always confirms the oximeter is broken", "Standard pulse oximetry cannot distinguish oxyhaemoglobin from carboxyhaemoglobin, so a falsely reassuring high reading is possible in CO poisoning"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Standard pulse oximetry cannot distinguish oxyhaemoglobin from carboxyhaemoglobin, so a falsely reassuring high reading is possible in CO poisoning
CARBON MONOXIDE (CO) binds strongly to haemoglobin, forming CARBOXYHAEMOGLOBIN (COHb), which cannot carry oxygen. STANDARD PULSE OXIMETERS use only two wavelengths of light and CANNOT tell COHb from oxyhaemoglobin — both look "red" to the sensor. So the oximeter shows a FALSELY HIGH SpO2 (e.g. 99%), while the patient is actually severely hypoxic at the tissue level. Confusion is a sign of brain hypoxia. Measure COHb with a CO-oximeter (on a blood gas analyser), and give 100% oxygen via a non-rebreather mask.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The SpO2 reading is definitely accurate and reassuring — The SpO2 reading is NOT reliable in CO poisoning.
• No further assessment is needed given the good SpO2 — Further assessment and treatment are urgently needed.
• Cyanosis always confirms the oximeter is broken — Cyanosis does not mean the oximeter is broken; it shows the patient is unwell (CO victims may also appear cherry-red).

⭐ Key point: Generators, charcoal stoves and fires in closed spaces are common causes of CO poisoning in Nigeria. Consider hyperbaric oxygen for severe cases.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q152$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 704, $s$A patient with acute severe asthma has a silent chest on auscultation. The nurse should interpret this as:$s$, $s$["Normal findings for an asthmatic patient", "An ominous sign of minimal air movement/impending respiratory failure", "A sign of over-treatment with bronchodilators", "A reassuring sign that the asthma is resolving"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An ominous sign of minimal air movement/impending respiratory failure
In ACUTE SEVERE ASTHMA, a SILENT CHEST means so little air is moving that wheeze can no longer be heard. It is an OMINOUS (very dangerous) sign of severe airway obstruction, exhaustion and IMPENDING RESPIRATORY FAILURE. The patient needs urgent senior help and ICU review.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal findings for an asthmatic patient — It is not normal in an asthmatic.
• A sign of over-treatment with bronchodilators — It is not a sign of over-treatment.
• A reassuring sign that the asthma is resolving — It is not reassuring.

⭐ Key point: Other life-threatening signs: SpO2 below 92%, PEF below 33%, cyanosis, exhaustion, confusion, hypotension.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q161$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 705, $s$A patient with acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema presents with pink frothy sputum, orthopnea, and bilateral crackles. Which hemodynamic mechanism best explains the fluid accumulation?$s$, $s$["Increased pulmonary capillary permeability from sepsis", "Increased pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure from left ventricular failure", "Decreased plasma oncotic pressure from hypoalbuminemia", "Lymphatic obstruction from mediastinal malignancy"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increased pulmonary capillary hydrostatic pressure from left ventricular failure
In CARDIOGENIC PULMONARY OEDEMA, the LEFT VENTRICLE FAILS, so pressure builds up in the left atrium and the lung veins. This INCREASED PULMONARY CAPILLARY HYDROSTATIC PRESSURE pushes fluid out of the capillaries into the lung tissue and alveoli, causing PINK FROTHY SPUTUM, ORTHOPNOEA and BILATERAL CRACKLES.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased pulmonary capillary permeability from sepsis — Increased capillary permeability is the mechanism in ARDS (non-cardiogenic).
• Decreased plasma oncotic pressure from hypoalbuminemia — Low oncotic pressure causes oedema in liver or kidney disease.
• Lymphatic obstruction from mediastinal malignancy — Lymphatic obstruction is a rare cause.

⭐ Key point: Treat with upright positioning, oxygen/CPAP, IV nitrates and diuretics.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q162$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 706, $s$A patient with a history of asthma receiving nebulized acetylcysteine should be monitored closely for:$s$, $s$["Bronchospasm", "Hypertension", "Hyperkalemia", "Serotonin syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bronchospasm
ACETYLCYSTEINE (N-acetylcysteine) given by NEBULISER breaks down thick mucus (mucolytic). However, it can IRRITATE the airways and trigger BRONCHOSPASM — especially in patients with ASTHMA or reactive airways. Signs: wheeze, chest tightness, increased work of breathing, falling SpO2. Many protocols give a bronchodilator (e.g. salbutamol) before or with nebulised acetylcysteine, and monitor closely.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension — Acetylcysteine does not typically cause hypertension.
• Hyperkalemia — It does not cause hyperkalaemia.
• Serotonin syndrome — It does not cause serotonin syndrome.

⭐ Key point: IV acetylcysteine (for paracetamol overdose) can cause anaphylactoid reactions (rash, bronchospasm, hypotension) — slow or pause the infusion if this occurs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q177$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 707, $s$A patient develops sudden chest pain and dyspnoea after central line insertion. A chest X-ray shows a hyperlucent area with absent lung markings on the same side. This confirms:$s$, $s$["Successful catheter placement with no complication", "Pleural effusion", "Pneumothorax, a recognised complication of central line insertion", "Normal post-procedure findings"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pneumothorax, a recognised complication of central line insertion
During CENTRAL LINE insertion (especially SUBCLAVIAN and, less often, internal jugular), the needle can puncture the LUNG, letting air into the pleural space — a PNEUMOTHORAX. Symptoms: SUDDEN CHEST PAIN, BREATHLESSNESS, low SpO2, reduced breath sounds. The chest X-ray shows a HYPERLUCENT (darker) area with ABSENT LUNG MARKINGS beyond a visible lung edge on the same side.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Successful catheter placement with no complication — These findings indicate a complication, not successful placement.
• Pleural effusion — A pleural effusion shows WHITE (opaque) fluid, not a hyperlucent area.
• Normal post-procedure findings — These are not normal post-procedure findings.

⭐ Key point: Always obtain a chest X-ray after subclavian or jugular line insertion (to check tip position and exclude pneumothorax). Ultrasound-guided insertion reduces this risk.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q188$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 708, $s$A patient with a large pleural effusion undergoes therapeutic thoracentesis. As a general safety principle, fluid removal in a single session is often limited to approximately:$s$, $s$["Around 1–1.5 litres, to reduce the risk of re-expansion pulmonary oedema", "10 litres", "No limit at all, regardless of volume", "Only 10ml"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Around 1–1.5 litres, to reduce the risk of re-expansion pulmonary oedema
Removing a large volume of pleural fluid too quickly allows the collapsed lung to RE-EXPAND suddenly. The damaged lung capillaries can then leak fluid, causing RE-EXPANSION PULMONARY OEDEMA — breathlessness, cough, frothy sputum and hypoxia. To reduce this risk, fluid removal in a SINGLE SESSION is usually limited to about 1–1.5 LITRES. The procedure should be stopped earlier if the patient develops cough, chest tightness, pain, or hypotension.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 litres — Removing 10 litres at once would be very dangerous.
• No limit at all, regardless of volume — There is a recommended limit.
• Only 10ml — 10 mL is far too little to relieve symptoms (but enough for diagnostic analysis).

⭐ Key point: For large effusions, drain slowly over time (e.g. clamping the drain intermittently per protocol) and monitor the patient closely.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q190$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 709, $s$A sustained rise in peak and plateau airway pressures unresponsive to suctioning and repositioning should prompt the nurse to:$s$, $s$["Wait until the next shift to report it", "Simply increase sedation and not report it", "Notify the physician promptly, as this may indicate worsening lung compliance, pneumothorax, or other significant pathology", "Decrease the tidal volume without an order"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Notify the physician promptly, as this may indicate worsening lung compliance, pneumothorax, or other significant pathology
A SUSTAINED RISE in BOTH PEAK and PLATEAU pressures means the LUNGS or CHEST WALL have become STIFFER (reduced COMPLIANCE) — not just a simple airway blockage. If suctioning and repositioning have not helped, the cause may be serious: PNEUMOTHORAX, worsening ARDS or pulmonary oedema, right main bronchus intubation, atelectasis, or abdominal distension. The nurse should NOTIFY THE PHYSICIAN PROMPTLY and assess the patient (breath sounds, chest movement, SpO2, BP, tube position).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wait until the next shift to report it — Waiting until the next shift delays treatment of potentially life-threatening problems.
• Simply increase sedation and not report it — Increasing sedation without reporting may hide the problem.
• Decrease the tidal volume without an order — Changing tidal volume without an order is outside the nurse's scope (unless covered by protocol).

⭐ Key point: High peak + normal plateau = airway resistance problem. High peak + high plateau = compliance problem.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q101$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 710, $s$Which patient characteristic is most predictive of difficult bag-mask ventilation, independent of intubation difficulty?$s$, $s$["Normal Mallampati score", "Short neck circumference only", "Full dentition", "Obesity (BMI significantly elevated), presence of a beard, or edentulous state"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Obesity (BMI significantly elevated), presence of a beard, or edentulous state
DIFFICULT BAG-MASK VENTILATION is predicted by: OBESITY (high BMI — heavy chest and abdomen, soft tissue in the airway), a BEARD (prevents a good mask seal), being EDENTULOUS (no teeth — cheeks sink in, making a seal difficult), age over 55, history of snoring or sleep apnoea, and limited jaw protrusion. Memory aid: "BOOTS" — Beard, Obese, Older, Toothless, Snores.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal Mallampati score — A normal Mallampati score suggests easier intubation and ventilation.
• Short neck circumference only — A short neck may contribute, but neck circumference alone is not the best predictor.
• Full dentition — Full dentition helps the mask seal.

⭐ Key point: Prepare: oropharyngeal/nasopharyngeal airways, two-person technique, and a supraglottic device.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q104$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 711, $s$A capnography waveform showing a gradual upsloping/prolonged expiratory phase (shark-fin pattern) in a ventilated patient suggests:$s$, $s$["Bronchospasm/airway narrowing causing delayed expiratory airflow", "Normal airway function", "Complete ETT obstruction", "Esophageal intubation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bronchospasm/airway narrowing causing delayed expiratory airflow
A "SHARK-FIN" capnograph waveform has a slow, gradually UPSLOPING expiratory phase without a flat plateau. It shows that air is leaving the lungs slowly and unevenly through NARROWED AIRWAYS — BRONCHOSPASM (asthma, COPD) or partial airway obstruction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal airway function — Normal airway function shows a rectangular waveform with a flat plateau.
• Complete ETT obstruction — Complete obstruction would show NO waveform.
• Esophageal intubation — Oesophageal intubation would show a waveform that quickly disappears.

⭐ Key point: Treat bronchospasm and watch the waveform return to a normal shape.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q109$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 712, $s$A ventilated patient with bilateral infiltrates on PEEP 10 cmH2O has PaO2 55 mmHg on FiO2 0.4. Using the P/F ratio, how is this classified?$s$, $s$["Normal oxygenation (no ARDS)", "Severe ARDS", "Mild ARDS", "Moderate ARDS"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Moderate ARDS
The P/F RATIO compares the oxygen level in the blood (PaO2) with the oxygen given (FiO2). P/F = PaO2 ÷ FiO2. Here: 55 ÷ 0.4 = 137.5. In a patient with BILATERAL infiltrates on at least 5 cmH2O PEEP (Berlin definition of ARDS), severity is: MILD = 200–300; MODERATE = 100–200; SEVERE = 100 or less. 137.5 is MODERATE ARDS. (A healthy person on room air has a P/F around 400–500: 95 ÷ 0.21 ≈ 450.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal oxygenation (no ARDS) — Normal oxygenation would require a P/F above about 300–400.
• Severe ARDS — Severe ARDS would require a P/F of 100 or less.
• Mild ARDS — Mild ARDS is 200–300.

⭐ Key point: Moderate-to-severe ARDS (especially P/F below 150) benefits from prone positioning, higher PEEP, and possibly neuromuscular blockade, along with lung-protective ventilation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q111 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 713, $s$A rising PaCO₂ in an asthmatic patient not yet ventilated is a sign of:$s$, $s$["Approaching respiratory muscle exhaustion and failure", "A normal, reassuring finding", "Improving ventilation", "Overtreatment with oxygen"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Approaching respiratory muscle exhaustion and failure
In an asthma attack, patients usually breathe fast and have a LOW PaCO2. A RISING or "NORMAL" PaCO2 is a DANGER SIGN: it means the patient can no longer breathe hard enough — the RESPIRATORY MUSCLES are becoming EXHAUSTED, and RESPIRATORY FAILURE is approaching. The patient may need ICU care and intubation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal, reassuring finding — A rising CO2 is not reassuring.
• Improving ventilation — It shows WORSENING, not improving, ventilation.
• Overtreatment with oxygen — Oxygen does not usually cause CO2 rise in asthma (unlike some COPD patients).

⭐ Key point: Other danger signs: silent chest, exhaustion, confusion, cyanosis, bradycardia, hypotension.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q113$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 714, $s$Biting on the endotracheal tube by an inadequately sedated patient can cause acute obstruction. An appropriate intervention includes:$s$, $s$["Deflating the ETT cuff", "Immediately extubating the patient", "Increasing the ventilator rate", "Inserting a bite block and reassessing sedation/analgesia level"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inserting a bite block and reassessing sedation/analgesia level
A patient BITING on the ET tube can block it, causing high airway pressures, hypoxia and distress. Appropriate interventions: insert a BITE BLOCK (or oral airway) to stop the teeth closing on the tube, and REASSESS SEDATION and ANALGESIA (the patient may be agitated, in pain, or waking).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Deflating the ETT cuff — Deflating the cuff does not stop biting and risks aspiration.
• Immediately extubating the patient — Immediate extubation is not appropriate.
• Increasing the ventilator rate — Increasing the rate does not relieve the obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Never leave a patient biting a tube; complete obstruction causes rapid hypoxia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q119$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 715, $s$Which laboratory/clinical finding suggests early ventilator-associated pneumonia in an intubated patient?$s$, $s$["New or worsening infiltrate on chest x-ray with fever, leukocytosis, and purulent secretions", "Clear chest x-ray with normal WBC", "Decreased secretions and improved oxygenation", "Bradycardia with hypothermia only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: New or worsening infiltrate on chest x-ray with fever, leukocytosis, and purulent secretions
VENTILATOR-ASSOCIATED PNEUMONIA (VAP) is pneumonia that develops more than 48 hours after intubation. Signs: a NEW or WORSENING INFILTRATE on chest X-ray, plus FEVER, LEUKOCYTOSIS (high WBC), and PURULENT (thick, yellow-green) SECRETIONS, often with worsening oxygenation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Clear chest x-ray with normal WBC — A clear X-ray and normal WBC do not suggest VAP.
• Decreased secretions and improved oxygenation — Decreased secretions and improved oxygenation suggest improvement.
• Bradycardia with hypothermia only — Bradycardia and hypothermia alone are non-specific.

⭐ Key point: Take respiratory cultures (e.g. tracheal aspirate) before starting antibiotics.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q121$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 716, $s$A patient with acute respiratory failure is started on noninvasive positive pressure ventilation. Which finding indicates the trial is failing and intubation should be considered?$s$, $s$["Improved oxygen saturation", "A decreasing respiratory rate toward normal", "Improving mental status", "Worsening acidosis, a rising PaCO2, or hemodynamic instability despite noninvasive ventilation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Worsening acidosis, a rising PaCO2, or hemodynamic instability despite noninvasive ventilation
NIV is failing if the patient shows: WORSENING ACIDOSIS (falling pH), a RISING PaCO2, increasing breathing difficulty, falling SpO2, worsening consciousness, inability to protect the airway or clear secretions, or HAEMODYNAMIC INSTABILITY. These indicate that intubation should be considered without delay.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved oxygen saturation — Improved oxygenation suggests NIV is working.
• A decreasing respiratory rate toward normal — A decreasing respiratory rate suggests improvement.
• Improving mental status — Improving mental status suggests improvement.

⭐ Key point: Reassess blood gases 1–2 hours after starting NIV. Delayed intubation increases mortality.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q126$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 717, $s$The nurse is assessing an intubated patient's response to increasing FiO2 from 40% to 60% for a SpO2 of 89%. After 10-15 minutes, SpO2 remains 89%. What should the nurse do next?$s$, $s$["Discontinue PEEP", "Continue increasing FiO2 indefinitely without further assessment", "Decrease FiO2 back to 40%", "Reassess the patient thoroughly, including breath sounds, secretions, tube position, and hemodynamics, and notify the physician if hypoxemia persists"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reassess the patient thoroughly, including breath sounds, secretions, tube position, and hemodynamics, and notify the physician if hypoxemia persists
If SpO2 does NOT improve after increasing FiO2, the cause may not simply be low oxygen delivery. The nurse should REASSESS THE PATIENT thoroughly: breath sounds (pneumothorax, collapse), secretions (suction), tube position (displacement, right main bronchus), ventilator circuit, haemodynamics (shock, poor perfusion), and probe placement; then NOTIFY THE PHYSICIAN.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Discontinue PEEP — Discontinuing PEEP would worsen oxygenation.
• Continue increasing FiO2 indefinitely without further assessment — Increasing FiO2 indefinitely without assessment risks oxygen toxicity and misses the cause.
• Decrease FiO2 back to 40% — Decreasing FiO2 would worsen hypoxia.

⭐ Key point: Hypoxia that doesn't respond to oxygen suggests shunt (e.g. collapse, consolidation, ARDS) — PEEP and recruitment may help.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q128$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 718, $s$In an intubated patient, an increase in peak inspiratory pressure with an unchanged plateau pressure most likely indicates a problem with:$s$, $s$["Lung compliance, such as worsening ARDS", "The set FiO2", "The set PEEP", "Airway resistance, such as secretions, bronchospasm, or a kinked tube"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Airway resistance, such as secretions, bronchospasm, or a kinked tube
An increase in PEAK pressure with an UNCHANGED PLATEAU pressure means the problem is in the AIRWAYS — INCREASED AIRWAY RESISTANCE, such as SECRETIONS, BRONCHOSPASM, a KINKED tube, or the patient biting the tube.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lung compliance, such as worsening ARDS — Worsening compliance would raise BOTH peak and plateau.
• The set FiO2 — FiO2 does not affect airway pressures.
• The set PEEP — PEEP changes would affect both pressures.

⭐ Key point: Suction, check the tube, and give bronchodilators if needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q132$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 719, $s$Initial management of hypoxia in a critically ill anaesthetized or ventilated patient should begin with:$s$, $s$["Sedation to reduce oxygen demand", "Diuretic administration", "Immediate intubation regardless of the cause", "Ensuring airway patency and delivering a high fraction of inspired oxygen while identifying the underlying cause"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ensuring airway patency and delivering a high fraction of inspired oxygen while identifying the underlying cause
For HYPOXIA in a critically ill, anaesthetised or ventilated patient, start by ENSURING AIRWAY PATENCY (check the tube or airway, suction) and DELIVERING HIGH FiO2 (100% oxygen), while finding the UNDERLYING CAUSE (DOPES: Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment failure, Stacked breaths).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sedation to reduce oxygen demand — Sedation doesn't treat hypoxia.
• Diuretic administration — Diuretics are only for fluid overload.
• Immediate intubation regardless of the cause — Intubation may be needed, but airway and oxygen come first.

⭐ Key point: Systematic approach: Airway, Breathing, Circulation, then specific causes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q137$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 720, $s$A sudden, sustained loss of the capnography waveform in an intubated, mechanically ventilated patient most urgently suggests:$s$, $s$["A need to increase sedation only", "Accidental extubation, tube dislodgement, or complete airway/circuit disconnection requiring immediate assessment", "A benign, expected finding requiring no action", "Improvement in the patient's respiratory status"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Accidental extubation, tube dislodgement, or complete airway/circuit disconnection requiring immediate assessment
A SUDDEN LOSS of the capnography waveform in a ventilated patient means no exhaled CO2 is detected. The most urgent causes are: ACCIDENTAL EXTUBATION, TUBE DISLODGEMENT, complete airway obstruction, or CIRCUIT DISCONNECTION. Cardiac arrest is another important cause. This needs IMMEDIATE ASSESSMENT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A need to increase sedation only — It does not just mean more sedation is needed.
• A benign, expected finding requiring no action — It is never benign.
• Improvement in the patient's respiratory status — It does not indicate improvement.

⭐ Key point: "No trace = wrong place" (or no circulation).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q138$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 721, $s$In Volume Control ventilation, a form of AC, if a patient's airway resistance suddenly increases from bronchospasm, what happens to peak airway pressure?$s$, $s$["It decreases", "The ventilator stops delivering volume", "It increases, since the set volume is delivered regardless of resistance", "It remains constant"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It increases, since the set volume is delivered regardless of resistance
In VOLUME CONTROL, the ventilator always delivers the SET TIDAL VOLUME, regardless of resistance. If airway RESISTANCE suddenly increases (bronchospasm), the ventilator must push harder, so the PEAK AIRWAY PRESSURE INCREASES.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It decreases — Peak pressure does not decrease with increased resistance.
• The ventilator stops delivering volume — The ventilator continues to deliver volume (unless the pressure limit is reached).
• It remains constant — Peak pressure does not stay constant.

⭐ Key point: Set the high-pressure alarm appropriately (usually 10 cmH2O above the usual peak).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q140$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 722, $s$A ventilated patient with bilateral infiltrates on PEEP 10 cmH2O has PaO2 75 mmHg on FiO2 0.3. Using the P/F ratio, how is this classified?$s$, $s$["Moderate ARDS", "Normal oxygenation (no ARDS)", "Mild ARDS", "Severe ARDS"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mild ARDS
P/F RATIO = PaO2 ÷ FiO2. Here: 75 ÷ 0.3 = 250. The Berlin definition of ARDS (with bilateral infiltrates, PEEP ≥5, within 1 week, not explained by heart failure) grades severity by P/F ratio: MILD 200–300; MODERATE 100–200; SEVERE 100 or less. 250 falls in the MILD range. (Normal P/F on room air is about 400–500.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Moderate ARDS — Moderate ARDS would need a P/F between 100 and 200.
• Normal oxygenation (no ARDS) — Normal oxygenation would need a P/F above 300 (ideally about 400+).
• Severe ARDS — Severe ARDS would need a P/F of 100 or less.

⭐ Key point: Remember to convert FiO2 to a decimal (30% = 0.3) and use PaO2 in mmHg. If using kPa, multiply by 7.5 first.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q142 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 723, $s$A patient's oxygen saturation probe consistently reads falsely low despite the patient appearing clinically well. The nurse should first consider:$s$, $s$["Ignoring the reading entirely with no clinical correlation", "Immediately intubating the patient", "Technical causes such as poor peripheral perfusion, nail polish, motion artefact, or probe malposition before assuming true hypoxaemia", "Assuming the reading is always accurate with no further check"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Technical causes such as poor peripheral perfusion, nail polish, motion artefact, or probe malposition before assuming true hypoxaemia
A pulse oximeter can give FALSELY LOW readings for TECHNICAL reasons: POOR PERIPHERAL PERFUSION (cold hands, vasoconstriction, low BP), dark NAIL POLISH or artificial nails, MOTION ARTEFACT (shivering, moving), PROBE MALPOSITION or a probe that is too big/small, bright ambient light, and some abnormal haemoglobins. If the patient looks clinically WELL (pink, alert, breathing comfortably), the nurse should FIRST check these technical causes (look at the waveform quality, reposition or change the probe, warm the hand, try another site such as the ear lobe) — and confirm with an arterial blood gas if in doubt.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignoring the reading entirely with no clinical correlation — Readings should never be ignored without clinical correlation.
• Immediately intubating the patient — Intubation is not appropriate for a clinically well patient with a possibly false reading.
• Assuming the reading is always accurate with no further check — Assuming the reading is always accurate risks wrong treatment.

⭐ Key point: Check the plethysmograph (waveform): a poor, irregular waveform suggests an unreliable reading. Pulse oximeters may OVER-estimate oxygen levels in darker-skinned patients — be aware.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q154$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 724, $s$A patient with severe Mendelson's syndrome develops worsening bilateral infiltrates, refractory hypoxaemia, and reduced lung compliance over 24-48 hours. This most likely represents progression to which complication?$s$, $s$["Bronchiectasis", "Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)", "Pulmonary embolism", "Chronic bronchitis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)
MENDELSON'S SYNDROME (aspiration of acidic stomach contents) causes a chemical injury to the lungs. In severe cases, the injury progresses over 24–48 hours to ACUTE RESPIRATORY DISTRESS SYNDROME (ARDS): worsening BILATERAL INFILTRATES, REFRACTORY HYPOXAEMIA (poor response to oxygen), and REDUCED LUNG COMPLIANCE (stiff lungs), not caused by heart failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bronchiectasis — Bronchiectasis is a chronic condition developing over years.
• Pulmonary embolism — Pulmonary embolism causes sudden hypoxia but not usually bilateral infiltrates and low compliance.
• Chronic bronchitis — Chronic bronchitis is a long-term condition, not an acute complication.

⭐ Key point: Aspiration is a direct (pulmonary) cause of ARDS. Treat with lung-protective ventilation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q163$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 725, $s$A postoperative patient experiences sudden onset of severe chest pain and shortness of breath. The nurse suspects:$s$, $s$["Infection", "Pneumonia", "Heart failure", "Pulmonary embolism"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pulmonary embolism
A post-operative patient has a high risk of DEEP VEIN THROMBOSIS (immobility, surgery-related clotting changes). A clot can travel to the lungs, causing PULMONARY EMBOLISM (PE): SUDDEN severe CHEST PAIN (often pleuritic), SHORTNESS OF BREATH, tachycardia, hypoxia, and possibly collapse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Infection — Infection develops more gradually, with fever.
• Pneumonia — Pneumonia develops over days with fever and cough.
• Heart failure — Heart failure develops more gradually, with orthopnoea and oedema.

⭐ Key point: Prevent with early mobilisation, compression stockings and anticoagulant prophylaxis. Treat suspected PE with oxygen, anticoagulation and CTPA.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q177$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 726, $s$A patient aspirates gastric contents during induction of anaesthesia. Which clinical picture is most typical in the following minutes?$s$, $s$["Isolated bradycardia with normal oxygen saturation", "Gradual onset of unilateral leg swelling over several days", "Sudden dyspnoea, wheeze, tachycardia, and falling oxygen saturation", "Painless jaundice developing over the next week"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sudden dyspnoea, wheeze, tachycardia, and falling oxygen saturation
When acidic stomach contents are aspirated during induction, the lungs react within MINUTES: the airways go into spasm (WHEEZE, high airway pressures), the alveoli are irritated and begin to leak, and gas exchange is impaired. Typical picture: SUDDEN DYSPNOEA (or difficulty ventilating if intubated), WHEEZE, TACHYCARDIA, coughing, and a FALLING OXYGEN SATURATION. Over the next hours, chest X-ray infiltrates and a chemical pneumonitis (possibly ARDS) develop.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Isolated bradycardia with normal oxygen saturation — Isolated bradycardia with normal oxygen saturation does not fit aspiration.
• Gradual onset of unilateral leg swelling over several days — Gradual leg swelling over days suggests DVT.
• Painless jaundice developing over the next week — Painless jaundice developing over a week is unrelated to aspiration.

⭐ Key point: Immediate management: head-down and lateral, suction, secure the airway with a cuffed tube, suction the trachea, give 100% oxygen and support ventilation. Antibiotics are not routine unless infection develops.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q190$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 727, $s$A patient with COPD receiving oxygen therapy develops worsening drowsiness and a rising respiratory rate that then slows dramatically. The nurse should suspect:$s$, $s$["CO2 narcosis from excessive oxygen therapy suppressing hypoxic respiratory drive", "Normal sleep pattern", "A simple anxiety reaction", "Improved oxygenation with no concern"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: CO2 narcosis from excessive oxygen therapy suppressing hypoxic respiratory drive
In some patients with COPD, giving too much OXYGEN can raise the CO2 level: high oxygen worsens V/Q mismatch (releasing hypoxic vasoconstriction), reduces CO2 carriage by haemoglobin (Haldane effect), and in some patients reduces respiratory drive. The rising CO2 causes CO2 NARCOSIS: DROWSINESS, confusion, and eventually SLOW breathing and respiratory failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal sleep pattern — It is not normal sleep.
• A simple anxiety reaction — It is not anxiety.
• Improved oxygenation with no concern — It is a dangerous situation.

⭐ Key point: Target SpO2 88–92% in COPD patients at risk of CO2 retention.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q198$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 728, $s$Which of the following units of data is the largest?$s$, $s$["Gigabyte (GB)", "Megabyte (MB)", "Kilobyte (KB)", "Terabyte (TB)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Terabyte (TB)
Units of data storage, from smallest to largest: BIT → BYTE (8 bits) → KILOBYTE (KB, about 1,000 bytes) → MEGABYTE (MB, about 1,000 KB) → GIGABYTE (GB, about 1,000 MB) → TERABYTE (TB, about 1,000 GB) → petabyte (PB). So the TERABYTE is the LARGEST of the options. (In binary, each step is 1,024.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Gigabyte (GB) — A gigabyte is about 1/1000 of a terabyte.
• Megabyte (MB) — A megabyte is about one-millionth of a terabyte.
• Kilobyte (KB) — A kilobyte is very small (about a billionth of a terabyte).

⭐ Key point: A typical text document is a few KB; a photo a few MB; a film a few GB; large hospital servers store TBs of data (e.g. imaging).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q39$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 729, $s$Which technique is used for airway clearance and lung inflation in a ventilated ICU patient?$s$, $s$["Manual hyperinflation", "Inspiratory muscle training", "Diaphragmatic breathing", "Pursed-lip breathing"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Manual hyperinflation
MANUAL HYPERINFLATION (MHI, "bagging") is a physiotherapy technique for VENTILATED patients. The patient is disconnected from the ventilator and given slow, deep breaths with a manual resuscitation bag, often with a brief inspiratory hold, followed by a QUICK RELEASE of the bag. The large breaths RE-INFLATE collapsed alveoli (improving lung volume and compliance), and the fast expiratory flow MOBILISES SECRETIONS towards the large airways, where they can be suctioned (AIRWAY CLEARANCE).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Inspiratory muscle training — Inspiratory muscle training strengthens the breathing muscles for weaning; it does not directly clear secretions.
• Diaphragmatic breathing — Diaphragmatic breathing is an active technique for awake, cooperative patients.
• Pursed-lip breathing — Pursed-lip breathing helps awake COPD patients slow breathing and reduce air trapping.

⭐ Key point: MHI risks include pneumothorax, haemodynamic instability, loss of PEEP and raised ICP; use a manometer and avoid it in unstable patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q164$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 730, $s$Accidental displacement of a chest drain while turning a patient may cause:$s$, $s$["Pneumothorax", "Internal bleeding", "Pneumonia", "Atelectasis only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pneumothorax
A CHEST DRAIN removes air or fluid from the pleural space and allows the lung to stay expanded. If the drain is ACCIDENTALLY DISPLACED or pulled out (e.g. while turning a patient), air from the atmosphere can be sucked into the pleural space through the open wound, causing the lung to collapse — a PNEUMOTHORAX (which can become a tension pneumothorax in a ventilated patient). The nurse should immediately cover the site with a sterile occlusive dressing (often taped on three sides to allow air out), assess breathing and SpO2, and call the doctor urgently.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Internal bleeding — Internal bleeding is not the main risk of displacement.
• Pneumonia — Pneumonia may develop later from poor lung expansion, but it is not the immediate risk.
• Atelectasis only — Atelectasis may follow, but pneumothorax is the immediate, main risk.

⭐ Key point: Secure the drain and tubing well, plan turns with enough staff, and keep the drain below chest level.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q170$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 731, $s$An important adverse effect of manual hyperinflation (bagging) is:$s$, $s$["Hypoglycaemia", "Increased intracranial compliance", "Pneumothorax", "Pulmonary embolism"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pneumothorax
MANUAL HYPERINFLATION gives LARGE TIDAL VOLUMES at HIGH PRESSURE. If the airway pressure is too high, or the lungs are fragile (e.g. emphysema, ARDS, bullae), the alveoli can rupture and air leaks into the pleural space, causing a PNEUMOTHORAX (barotrauma). Other complications: hypotension (from high chest pressure reducing venous return), loss of PEEP and alveolar collapse, raised ICP, and hypoxia during disconnection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypoglycaemia — Hypoglycaemia is not related to manual hyperinflation.
• Increased intracranial compliance — MHI does not increase intracranial compliance; it can raise ICP.
• Pulmonary embolism — Pulmonary embolism is not a typical complication of MHI.

⭐ Key point: Use a manometer to keep peak pressure below about 40 cmH2O, include a PEEP valve, and avoid MHI with an undrained pneumothorax or unstable haemodynamics.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q172$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 732, $s$Which organism is commonly associated with late-onset ventilator-associated pneumonia in the ICU?$s$, $s$["Clostridium tetani", "Neisseria meningitidis", "Streptococcus pyogenes", "Pseudomonas aeruginosa"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pseudomonas aeruginosa
LATE-ONSET VAP (developing 5 or more days after intubation) is commonly caused by hospital-acquired, often MULTIDRUG-RESISTANT organisms, such as PSEUDOMONAS AERUGINOSA, Acinetobacter baumannii, MRSA, Klebsiella and other Enterobacterales. These organisms colonise the ICU environment, equipment and patients' oropharynx. Pseudomonas is particularly resistant and needs specific antibiotics (e.g. piperacillin-tazobactam, meropenem, ceftazidime).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Clostridium tetani — Clostridium tetani causes tetanus.
• Neisseria meningitidis — Neisseria meningitidis causes meningitis and septicaemia.
• Streptococcus pyogenes — Streptococcus pyogenes causes throat, skin and soft-tissue infections.

⭐ Key point: Early-onset VAP (within 4 days) is usually caused by community organisms such as Streptococcus pneumoniae, Haemophilus influenzae and MSSA.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q191$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 733, $s$A ventilated patient has a peak airway pressure of 45 cmH₂O and a plateau pressure of 20 cmH₂O. The most likely problem is:$s$, $s$["Decreased lung compliance (ARDS, pulmonary oedema, fibrosis)", "Increased airway resistance (bronchospasm, secretions, biting)", "Endobronchial intubation into the right main bronchus", "Tension pneumothorax with mediastinal shift"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increased airway resistance (bronchospasm, secretions, biting)
PEAK PRESSURE reflects both airway RESISTANCE and lung COMPLIANCE. PLATEAU PRESSURE reflects only COMPLIANCE (alveolar pressure). The DIFFERENCE between them (peak minus plateau) is caused by AIRWAY RESISTANCE. Here, peak is 45 and plateau is 20, so the difference is 25 cmH2O — very high (normally under about 10). The plateau is normal, so the lungs themselves are not stiff. The problem is INCREASED AIRWAY RESISTANCE: BRONCHOSPASM, SECRETIONS or a mucus plug, a kinked tube, or the patient BITING the tube. Actions: check the tube, suction, give bronchodilators, and insert a bite block if needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Decreased lung compliance (ARDS, pulmonary oedema, fibrosis) — Decreased compliance (ARDS, oedema, fibrosis) would raise BOTH peak and plateau pressures, keeping the difference normal.
• Endobronchial intubation into the right main bronchus — Right main bronchus intubation reduces compliance (all the volume goes into one lung), raising the plateau pressure as well.
• Tension pneumothorax with mediastinal shift — A tension pneumothorax reduces compliance and raises both pressures, and is accompanied by hypotension and absent breath sounds.

⭐ Key point: High peak + normal plateau = RESISTANCE problem (airway). High peak + high plateau = COMPLIANCE problem (lungs, chest wall, abdomen).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q4$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 734, $s$A ventilated patient suddenly triggers the high-pressure alarm. SpO₂ is falling, BP is 70/40 mmHg, breath sounds are absent on the left, and the trachea is deviated to the right. The immediate action is:$s$, $s$["Disconnect, bag with 100% oxygen, and wait for an urgent chest X-ray before acting", "Needle decompression of the left chest, followed by a chest drain", "Pass a suction catheter to clear a possible tube blockage", "Withdraw the endotracheal tube by 2 cm"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Needle decompression of the left chest, followed by a chest drain
These signs show a TENSION PNEUMOTHORAX on the LEFT: high airway pressure, falling SpO2, severe HYPOTENSION (70/40), ABSENT breath sounds on the left, and the TRACHEA pushed to the RIGHT (away from the affected side). Positive-pressure ventilation forces air into the pleural space with every breath, and the pressure builds rapidly, compressing the lung and heart and stopping blood return. This is immediately life-threatening. The treatment is IMMEDIATE NEEDLE DECOMPRESSION of the LEFT chest (2nd intercostal space mid-clavicular line, or 4th–5th intercostal space anterior to mid-axillary line), followed by a CHEST DRAIN. Do not wait for an X-ray — it is a clinical diagnosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Disconnect, bag with 100% oxygen, and wait for an urgent chest X-ray before acting — Waiting for an X-ray wastes time; the patient may arrest.
• Pass a suction catheter to clear a possible tube blockage — A blocked tube causes high pressures, but would not cause tracheal deviation and unilateral absent breath sounds.
• Withdraw the endotracheal tube by 2 cm — Withdrawing the tube treats right main bronchus intubation (which reduces breath sounds on the LEFT), but it would not explain tracheal deviation and shock.

⭐ Key point: Tension pneumothorax: trachea deviated AWAY from the affected side, absent breath sounds, hyper-resonance, distended neck veins, hypotension.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q10$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 735, $s$The recommended endotracheal tube cuff pressure for an adult is:$s$, $s$["25–35 mmHg", "20–30 cmH₂O", "10–15 cmH₂O", "30–40 cmH₂O"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 20–30 cmH₂O
The ENDOTRACHEAL TUBE CUFF seals the trachea to prevent air leaks and stop secretions from entering the lungs. The recommended pressure is 20–30 cmH2O. Below 20 cmH2O, secretions can leak past the cuff into the lungs (micro-aspiration), increasing the risk of ventilator-associated pneumonia. Above 30 cmH2O, the cuff squeezes the tracheal lining and blocks its blood supply (the capillary perfusion pressure is about 30 cmH2O), which can cause ulceration, tracheal stenosis or tracheo-oesophageal fistula.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 25–35 mmHg — 25–35 mmHg is too high (about 34–48 cmH2O) and risks tracheal damage.
• 10–15 cmH₂O — 10–15 cmH2O is too low and risks aspiration.
• 30–40 cmH₂O — 30–40 cmH2O is too high and risks tracheal ischaemia.

⭐ Key point: Check cuff pressure with a manometer at least every 8 hours (or per policy), and after repositioning or moving the tube. Never estimate it by squeezing the pilot balloon.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q12$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 736, $s$After a large dose of a local anaesthetic spray (benzocaine), a patient becomes cyanosed. SpO₂ stays around 85% despite high-flow oxygen, PaO₂ on the blood gas is 300 mmHg, and the blood looks chocolate-brown. The diagnosis and treatment are:$s$, $s$["Carbon monoxide poisoning — hyperbaric oxygen", "Sulfhaemoglobinaemia — IV methylene blue", "Cyanide poisoning — hydroxocobalamin", "Methaemoglobinaemia — IV methylene blue"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Methaemoglobinaemia — IV methylene blue
Some drugs (e.g. benzocaine and other local anaesthetics, dapsone, nitrates) oxidise the iron in haemoglobin from the ferrous (Fe²⁺) to the ferric (Fe³⁺) form, creating METHAEMOGLOBIN, which cannot carry oxygen. Key features: CYANOSIS that doesn't respond to oxygen, SpO2 stuck around 85% (regardless of the true level), a HIGH PaO2 on the blood gas (oxygen is dissolved in plasma normally), and CHOCOLATE-BROWN blood. Treatment: IV METHYLENE BLUE (1–2 mg/kg), which converts methaemoglobin back to normal haemoglobin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Carbon monoxide poisoning — hyperbaric oxygen — Carbon monoxide poisoning gives a falsely NORMAL SpO2 and cherry-red (not chocolate-brown) blood.
• Sulfhaemoglobinaemia — IV methylene blue — Sulfhaemoglobinaemia does NOT respond to methylene blue.
• Cyanide poisoning — hydroxocobalamin — Cyanide poisoning prevents cells from using oxygen, but the blood is bright red, not chocolate-brown, and SpO2 is usually normal.

⭐ Key point: Methylene blue is contraindicated in G6PD deficiency (common in Nigeria) — it can cause haemolysis. Use exchange transfusion or ascorbic acid instead.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q28$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 737, $s$A 'shark-fin' capnograph waveform, with a slow sloping upstroke and no flat plateau, indicates:$s$, $s$["Cardiac arrest with low pulmonary flow", "Lower airway obstruction (bronchospasm or COPD)", "Oesophageal intubation with gastric gas", "Rebreathing from exhausted soda lime"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lower airway obstruction (bronchospasm or COPD)
A "SHARK-FIN" capnograph waveform has a slow, SLOPING upstroke and no flat plateau (it looks like a shark's fin). This shows that exhaled air is coming out slowly and unevenly from narrowed airways — LOWER AIRWAY OBSTRUCTION, such as BRONCHOSPASM (asthma) or COPD. The more obstruction, the steeper the slope.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cardiac arrest with low pulmonary flow — Cardiac arrest with low pulmonary flow causes a LOW CO2 trace (low height), but a normal shape.
• Oesophageal intubation with gastric gas — Oesophageal intubation gives a few waveforms that fade to zero.
• Rebreathing from exhausted soda lime — Rebreathing causes the baseline to rise above zero.

⭐ Key point: Treat the bronchospasm and watch the waveform improve as obstruction resolves.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q30$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 738, $s$A patient has an underwater-seal chest drain for a pneumothorax. The water-seal chamber is bubbling CONTINUOUSLY, during both inspiration and expiration. This means:$s$, $s$["The drain tube is blocked by a clot or kinked under the patient", "A persistent air leak — from the lung or a loose connection", "A normal finding in every drain inserted for a pneumothorax", "The lung has fully re-expanded and the drain can be removed"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A persistent air leak — from the lung or a loose connection
In an underwater-seal chest drain, BUBBLING in the water-seal chamber shows that AIR is escaping from the chest. In a pneumothorax drain, intermittent bubbling (with expiration or coughing) is expected while air is leaving the pleural space. CONTINUOUS BUBBLING (during both inspiration and expiration) indicates a PERSISTENT AIR LEAK, either from the LUNG (e.g. a bronchopleural fistula) or a LOOSE CONNECTION/hole in the system.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The drain tube is blocked by a clot or kinked under the patient — A blocked drain would stop bubbling and swinging.
• A normal finding in every drain inserted for a pneumothorax — Continuous bubbling is NOT a normal finding.
• The lung has fully re-expanded and the drain can be removed — If the lung had fully re-expanded, bubbling would STOP.

⭐ Key point: Check the system for leaks by briefly clamping the tubing at different points: if bubbling stops when clamped close to the patient, the leak is from the patient; if it continues, the leak is in the system.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q37$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 739, $s$A Venturi mask delivers a fixed, predictable FiO₂ mainly because:$s$, $s$["Its tight face seal stops room air entering, so inspired gas comes only from the device", "Its reservoir bag stores pure oxygen so that each breath contains a fixed oxygen fraction", "Turning the oxygen flow up to 15 L/min raises the FiO₂ above the colour-coded value", "It entrains a fixed ratio of room air, giving a total flow above the patient's peak inspiratory flow"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It entrains a fixed ratio of room air, giving a total flow above the patient's peak inspiratory flow
A VENTURI MASK uses a jet of oxygen passing through a narrow opening to ENTRAIN (pull in) a FIXED RATIO of ROOM AIR through side ports. This creates a high TOTAL FLOW of gas that is HIGHER than the patient's PEAK INSPIRATORY FLOW, so the patient doesn't draw in extra room air. This gives a FIXED, PREDICTABLE FiO2 (e.g. 24%, 28%, 35%, 40%, 60%), regardless of the patient's breathing pattern.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Its tight face seal stops room air entering, so inspired gas comes only from the device — Venturi masks are NOT tightly sealed; they have open side ports.
• Its reservoir bag stores pure oxygen so that each breath contains a fixed oxygen fraction — A reservoir bag is used in a NON-REBREATHER mask, not a Venturi mask.
• Turning the oxygen flow up to 15 L/min raises the FiO₂ above the colour-coded value — Turning up the oxygen flow above the recommended rate does NOT significantly raise the FiO2 above the set value.

⭐ Key point: Venturi masks are ideal for patients with COPD who need controlled oxygen therapy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q40$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 740, $s$An asthmatic patient is exhausted, with SpO₂ 91% on air, a silent chest, peak flow 28% of predicted and a PaCO₂ of 45 mmHg (6.0 kPa). This attack is best classified as:$s$, $s$["Acute severe asthma", "Moderate exacerbation", "Life-threatening asthma", "Near-fatal asthma"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Life-threatening asthma
Asthma severity (BTS/SIGN guidelines): LIFE-THREATENING asthma is present if ANY of these: SpO2 below 92%, PEF below 33% of best or predicted, SILENT CHEST, cyanosis, poor respiratory effort, EXHAUSTION, altered consciousness, arrhythmia, hypotension, or a NORMAL PaCO2 (4.6–6.0 kPa / 35–45 mmHg) in a breathless asthmatic. Here: SpO2 91%, silent chest, PEF 28%, exhaustion, PaCO2 45 mmHg (6.0 kPa — "normal", which is dangerous because it shows the patient is tiring).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute severe asthma — Acute severe asthma has PEF 33–50%, RR ≥25, HR ≥110, or inability to complete sentences, without life-threatening features.
• Moderate exacerbation — Moderate exacerbation has PEF over 50% with no severe features.
• Near-fatal asthma — NEAR-FATAL asthma is defined by a RAISED PaCO2 (above 6.0 kPa) and/or needing mechanical ventilation.

⭐ Key point: Life-threatening asthma: call ICU early, high-flow oxygen, back-to-back salbutamol and ipratropium, IV hydrocortisone, IV magnesium, and prepare for intubation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q61$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 741, $s$A patient's PaO₂ is 75 mmHg on FiO₂ 0.6 with PEEP 10 cmH₂O, and chest X-ray shows bilateral opacities within a week of severe pancreatitis, not explained by heart failure. By the Berlin definition this is:$s$, $s$["Mild ARDS", "Severe ARDS", "Not ARDS, because the PaO₂/FiO₂ ratio must be below 100", "Moderate ARDS"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Moderate ARDS
The BERLIN DEFINITION of ARDS requires: onset WITHIN 1 WEEK of a known insult (here, pancreatitis); BILATERAL OPACITIES on chest X-ray; respiratory failure NOT fully explained by heart failure or fluid overload; and PaO2/FiO2 ratio on PEEP ≥5. Severity: MILD 200–300, MODERATE 100–200, SEVERE ≤100. Calculation: PaO2 75 ÷ FiO2 0.6 = 125. This is MODERATE ARDS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild ARDS — Mild ARDS has a ratio of 200–300.
• Severe ARDS — Severe ARDS has a ratio of 100 or less.
• Not ARDS, because the PaO₂/FiO₂ ratio must be below 100 — ARDS is defined by a ratio of 300 or less (with PEEP ≥5), not below 100.

⭐ Key point: Severe ARDS benefits from prone positioning (at least 16 hours/day) and possibly neuromuscular blockade, alongside lung-protective ventilation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q63$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 742, $s$A tall, thin 20-year-old smoker has sudden left-sided pleuritic chest pain and breathlessness. On the left there is reduced chest expansion, HYPER-resonant percussion and reduced breath sounds; the trachea is central and BP is normal. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Left lower lobe pneumonia", "Left lower lobe collapse", "Primary spontaneous pneumothorax", "Large left pleural effusion"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Primary spontaneous pneumothorax
A PRIMARY SPONTANEOUS PNEUMOTHORAX typically occurs in TALL, THIN, young men who SMOKE, without an underlying lung disease (small blebs at the lung apex burst). Signs: SUDDEN PLEURITIC CHEST PAIN and BREATHLESSNESS, REDUCED chest EXPANSION, HYPER-RESONANT percussion (more air), and REDUCED BREATH SOUNDS on the affected side. The TRACHEA is CENTRAL and BP is NORMAL, so it is NOT a tension pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Left lower lobe pneumonia — Pneumonia causes fever, DULL percussion and bronchial breathing.
• Left lower lobe collapse — Lobar collapse causes DULL percussion and the trachea pulled TOWARDS the affected side.
• Large left pleural effusion — A pleural effusion causes STONY DULL percussion.

⭐ Key point: Small, minimally symptomatic pneumothoraces may be observed; larger or symptomatic ones need needle aspiration or a chest drain. Advise stopping smoking and avoiding flying until resolved.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q64$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 743, $s$On examination of the right chest: reduced expansion, STONY dull percussion, absent breath sounds, reduced vocal resonance, and the trachea is pushed to the LEFT. The most likely finding is:$s$, $s$["Right tension pneumothorax", "Consolidation of the right lower lobe", "A large right pleural effusion", "Collapse of the right lung"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A large right pleural effusion
A LARGE PLEURAL EFFUSION (fluid in the pleural space) causes: REDUCED expansion, STONY DULL percussion (characteristic of fluid), ABSENT breath sounds, REDUCED vocal resonance (fluid blocks sound), and if large, it PUSHES the TRACHEA AWAY from the affected side (here, to the LEFT).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right tension pneumothorax — Tension pneumothorax causes HYPER-resonant percussion, not stony dull.
• Consolidation of the right lower lobe — Consolidation causes DULL (not stony dull) percussion, bronchial breathing and increased vocal resonance; the trachea is central.
• Collapse of the right lung — Lung collapse PULLS the trachea TOWARDS the affected side.

⭐ Key point: Pleural fluid may be drained by thoracentesis or a chest drain. Analyse the fluid (protein, LDH, glucose, cells, culture) to find the cause.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q65$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 744, $s$A post-operative patient with a weak cough has the trachea pulled to the RIGHT, dull percussion and reduced breath sounds over the right lower chest, and a raised right hemidiaphragm on X-ray. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Right-sided tension pneumothorax", "Right lower lobe collapse (atelectasis)", "Large right-sided pleural effusion", "Right lower lobe consolidation (pneumonia)"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Right lower lobe collapse (atelectasis)
After surgery, a WEAK COUGH allows secretions to block the airways, causing the lung beyond to COLLAPSE (ATELECTASIS). Collapse REDUCES lung volume, so the TRACHEA is PULLED TOWARDS the affected side (here, the RIGHT), the HEMIDIAPHRAGM is RAISED on the same side, and there is DULL percussion with REDUCED breath sounds.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right-sided tension pneumothorax — Tension pneumothorax pushes the trachea AWAY and causes hyper-resonance.
• Large right-sided pleural effusion — A large effusion pushes the trachea AWAY.
• Right lower lobe consolidation (pneumonia) — Consolidation causes bronchial breathing and increased vocal resonance, with a central trachea.

⭐ Key point: Prevent post-operative atelectasis with good analgesia, early mobilisation, deep breathing, incentive spirometry and chest physiotherapy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q66$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 745, $s$A febrile patient has dull percussion, BRONCHIAL breathing, increased vocal resonance and coarse crackles over the right lower zone, with a central trachea. These signs indicate:$s$, $s$["Pneumothorax", "Lobar collapse", "Pleural effusion", "Lobar consolidation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lobar consolidation
LOBAR CONSOLIDATION (e.g. pneumonia) fills the alveoli with fluid and inflammatory cells, but the airways stay open. Sound travels well through solid lung, causing: DULL percussion, BRONCHIAL BREATHING (harsh breath sounds, like those over the trachea), INCREASED VOCAL RESONANCE, and COARSE CRACKLES. The trachea is CENTRAL (no volume change). FEVER suggests infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pneumothorax — Pneumothorax causes hyper-resonance and reduced breath sounds.
• Lobar collapse — Lobar collapse pulls the trachea towards the affected side, with reduced breath sounds.
• Pleural effusion — Pleural effusion causes stony dull percussion and absent breath sounds, with reduced vocal resonance.

⭐ Key point: Treat pneumonia with antibiotics according to severity (CURB-65 score), oxygen and fluids.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q67$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 746, $s$A 60-year-old returned from a hotel stay with air-conditioning 5 days ago. He has high fever, dry cough, confusion, diarrhoea, Na⁺ 126 mmol/L and raised liver enzymes. The most likely organism is:$s$, $s$["Klebsiella pneumoniae", "Mycoplasma pneumoniae", "Streptococcus pneumoniae", "Legionella pneumophila"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Legionella pneumophila
LEGIONELLA PNEUMOPHILA (Legionnaires' disease) grows in water systems such as AIR-CONDITIONING units, cooling towers and hot water systems (e.g. hotels, hospitals). Features: HIGH FEVER, DRY COUGH, CONFUSION, DIARRHOEA, HYPONATRAEMIA (low sodium, here 126), and RAISED LIVER ENZYMES. It is often severe in older people and smokers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Klebsiella pneumoniae — Klebsiella causes "redcurrant jelly" sputum, often in alcoholics.
• Mycoplasma pneumoniae — Mycoplasma affects younger people, with milder symptoms, and may cause haemolysis or rash.
• Streptococcus pneumoniae — Streptococcus pneumoniae is the commonest cause of pneumonia, with rusty sputum and lobar consolidation, but not usually this combination of features.

⭐ Key point: Diagnose with a urinary Legionella antigen test. Treat with a macrolide (e.g. azithromycin) or a fluoroquinolone. It is not spread person-to-person.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q68$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 747, $s$A patient with HIV (CD4 count 80 cells/µL) has 3 weeks of dry cough and fever and desaturates on walking. Chest X-ray shows bilateral perihilar 'ground-glass' shadowing, and LDH is high. The most likely diagnosis and treatment are:$s$, $s$["CMV pneumonitis — IV ganciclovir with CMV immunoglobulin", "Pneumocystis pneumonia — high-dose co-trimoxazole ± steroids", "Pneumococcal pneumonia — high-dose IV amoxicillin alone", "Pulmonary tuberculosis — rifampicin-based four-drug therapy"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pneumocystis pneumonia — high-dose co-trimoxazole ± steroids
PNEUMOCYSTIS JIROVECII PNEUMONIA (PCP) is a fungal lung infection that occurs when the CD4 count is very low (usually below 200 cells/µL — here 80). It develops SLOWLY over weeks, with DRY COUGH, fever and breathlessness. A classic sign is DESATURATION ON WALKING (exercise-induced hypoxia), even when the oxygen level at rest looks acceptable. The chest X-ray typically shows BILATERAL PERIHILAR "GROUND-GLASS" shadowing (it may be normal early), and the LDH is often high. Treatment: HIGH-DOSE CO-TRIMOXAZOLE (trimethoprim-sulfamethoxazole) for 21 days, PLUS STEROIDS (prednisolone) if the patient is significantly hypoxic (PaO2 below 70 mmHg / 9.3 kPa), which reduces lung inflammation and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CMV pneumonitis — IV ganciclovir with CMV immunoglobulin — CMV pneumonitis is less common and occurs at even lower CD4 counts (usually below 50); PCP is the classic picture here.
• Pneumococcal pneumonia — high-dose IV amoxicillin alone — Pneumococcal pneumonia has a rapid onset (days), with productive cough, high fever and lobar consolidation, not bilateral ground-glass shadowing over weeks.
• Pulmonary tuberculosis — rifampicin-based four-drug therapy — TB in advanced HIV can look atypical, but the slow dry cough, exercise desaturation, perihilar ground-glass change and raised LDH are classic for PCP.

⭐ Key point: Prevent PCP with daily co-trimoxazole prophylaxis when the CD4 count is below 200, and start antiretroviral therapy (usually within 2 weeks of PCP treatment).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q71$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 748, $s$A drunk patient vomits and aspirates while lying flat on his back. The lung segment most likely to be affected is the:$s$, $s$["Basal segments of the left lower lobe", "Superior (apical) segment of the right lower lobe", "Lingula of the left upper lobe", "Right middle lobe"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Superior (apical) segment of the right lower lobe
When a patient ASPIRATES while LYING FLAT ON THE BACK (supine), gravity carries the vomit into the most DEPENDENT (lowest) parts of the lungs in that position, which are the POSTERIOR segments. The RIGHT main bronchus is WIDER, SHORTER and MORE VERTICAL than the left, so aspirated material more often goes to the right lung. In the supine position, the first posterior branch it meets is the SUPERIOR (apical) SEGMENT OF THE RIGHT LOWER LOBE (also the posterior segment of the right upper lobe).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Basal segments of the left lower lobe — The basal segments of the left lower lobe are affected when the patient aspirates UPRIGHT, and the left side is less common.
• Lingula of the left upper lobe — The lingula (left upper lobe) is an anterior structure, not dependent when supine.
• Right middle lobe — The right middle lobe is affected when aspiration happens in the PRONE position or when upright, not supine.

⭐ Key point: Upright aspiration → right lower lobe basal segments. Supine aspiration → superior segment of the right lower lobe and posterior segment of the right upper lobe.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q72$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 749, $s$Two days after a closed fracture of the femoral shaft, a young man becomes confused and hypoxaemic, with a petechial rash over the axillae, chest and conjunctivae. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Hypovolaemic shock from concealed bleeding", "Pulmonary thromboembolism", "Fat embolism syndrome", "Septic shock"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fat embolism syndrome
FAT EMBOLISM SYNDROME typically occurs 24–72 HOURS after a fracture of a LONG BONE (especially the femur) or the pelvis. Fat droplets from the bone marrow enter the circulation and lodge in small vessels in the lungs, brain and skin, causing inflammation. The classic triad is: RESPIRATORY problems (HYPOXAEMIA, breathlessness), NEUROLOGICAL changes (CONFUSION, drowsiness, seizures), and a PETECHIAL RASH (especially over the AXILLAE, CHEST, neck and CONJUNCTIVAE). The rash is pathognomonic but appears in only about half of patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypovolaemic shock from concealed bleeding — Hypovolaemic shock from bleeding could cause confusion, but not a petechial rash and hypoxaemia with these features.
• Pulmonary thromboembolism — Pulmonary embolism causes hypoxia and breathlessness, but not a petechial rash.
• Septic shock — Septic shock causes fever, hypotension and possibly a rash, but the timing after a femoral fracture and this specific pattern point to fat embolism.

⭐ Key point: Prevention: early immobilisation and fixation of long bone fractures. Treatment is supportive (oxygen, ventilation if needed, fluids). Most patients recover.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q73$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 750, $s$A patient with a known LEFT upper lobe cavity suddenly coughs up about 300 mL of fresh blood. While preparing for definitive airway management, the patient should be positioned:$s$, $s$["Prone", "Flat on the back", "Lying on the RIGHT side (bleeding side up)", "Lying on the LEFT side (bleeding side down)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lying on the LEFT side (bleeding side down)
In MASSIVE HAEMOPTYSIS (coughing up large amounts of blood, e.g. 300 mL), the danger is not blood loss itself, but DROWNING — blood filling and blocking the airways of the GOOD lung, causing asphyxia. The patient should be positioned LYING ON THE SIDE OF THE BLEEDING (here, the LEFT side DOWN). This uses gravity to keep blood in the bleeding (left) lung and stops it from spilling over into the healthy (right) lung, protecting gas exchange. Then prepare for definitive airway management (e.g. intubation, possibly into the healthy lung), bronchoscopy, or bronchial artery embolisation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prone — Prone positioning does not protect the healthy lung and makes airway management difficult.
• Flat on the back — Lying flat on the back allows blood to spread into both lungs.
• Lying on the RIGHT side (bleeding side up) — Lying on the RIGHT side (bleeding side UP) would cause blood to drain into the healthy right lung, causing asphyxia.

⭐ Key point: "BLEEDING SIDE DOWN" in massive haemoptysis. Give oxygen, have strong suction ready, cross-match blood, and correct any clotting problem.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q74$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 751, $s$A 65-year-old long-term smoker has FEV₁ 1.2 L and FVC 3.0 L (FEV₁/FVC 40%), with no significant improvement after a bronchodilator. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Asthma", "Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis", "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease", "Obesity hypoventilation syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease
Spirometry measures how much and how fast a person can breathe out. FEV1 is the volume breathed out in the first second, and FVC is the total volume breathed out. An FEV1/FVC RATIO BELOW 0.7 (70%) shows OBSTRUCTION (narrow airways slow down the outflow). Here, the ratio is 40%, which is severe obstruction. "NO SIGNIFICANT IMPROVEMENT AFTER A BRONCHODILATOR" means the obstruction is NOT REVERSIBLE. In a long-term SMOKER over 40, this is typical of CHRONIC OBSTRUCTIVE PULMONARY DISEASE (COPD).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Asthma — Asthma shows obstruction that IS significantly reversible after a bronchodilator (usually an FEV1 increase of more than 12% and 200 mL).
• Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis — Pulmonary fibrosis causes a RESTRICTIVE pattern (both FEV1 and FVC reduced, but the ratio is normal or high).
• Obesity hypoventilation syndrome — Obesity hypoventilation causes a restrictive pattern and high CO2, not a severely reduced FEV1/FVC ratio.

⭐ Key point: COPD severity is graded by FEV1 (% predicted). Smoking cessation is the most effective intervention to slow progression.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q75$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 752, $s$A 68-year-old has progressive breathlessness over 2 years, finger clubbing, and fine bibasal 'Velcro-like' end-inspiratory crackles. Spirometry is restrictive and CT shows basal honeycombing. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Chronic heart failure", "Bronchiectasis", "COPD", "Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis
IDIOPATHIC PULMONARY FIBROSIS (IPF) is progressive scarring of the lungs of unknown cause, usually in people over 60. Features: PROGRESSIVE BREATHLESSNESS over months to years, dry cough, FINGER CLUBBING, FINE BIBASAL "VELCRO-LIKE" END-INSPIRATORY CRACKLES (from stiff alveoli popping open), a RESTRICTIVE pattern on spirometry (small lung volumes), and CT showing BASAL HONEYCOMBING (cystic scarring at the bases).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chronic heart failure — Heart failure causes basal crackles, but not clubbing or honeycombing on CT, and usually responds to diuretics.
• Bronchiectasis — Bronchiectasis causes chronic productive cough with copious sputum and coarse crackles, with dilated airways on CT.
• COPD — COPD causes an OBSTRUCTIVE pattern and does not cause clubbing (clubbing in COPD suggests lung cancer).

⭐ Key point: IPF treatment includes antifibrotic drugs (pirfenidone, nintedanib), oxygen, pulmonary rehabilitation and lung transplant in selected patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q76$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 753, $s$A 35-year-old has a severe sore throat, fever, drooling, a muffled 'hot potato' voice and soft inspiratory stridor. He sits leaning forward and cannot swallow his saliva. The most likely diagnosis and key nursing action are:$s$, $s$["Acute asthma — salbutamol nebuliser with the patient lying flat", "Epiglottitis — keep upright, don't examine the throat, call airway help", "Viral croup — nebulised adrenaline, oral dexamethasone, then discharge", "Quinsy — examine the throat with a tongue depressor and drain it"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Epiglottitis — keep upright, don't examine the throat, call airway help
ACUTE EPIGLOTTITIS (in adults, called supraglottitis) is a bacterial infection causing rapid swelling of the epiglottis, which can COMPLETELY BLOCK THE AIRWAY. Features: severe SORE THROAT, FEVER, DROOLING (can't swallow saliva), a MUFFLED "HOT POTATO" VOICE, STRIDOR, and SITTING UPRIGHT, LEANING FORWARD (tripod position) to keep the airway open. Key nursing actions: KEEP THE PATIENT UPRIGHT and calm, DO NOT EXAMINE THE THROAT (this can trigger complete obstruction), give oxygen, and CALL FOR EXPERT AIRWAY HELP (anaesthetist and ENT) immediately. The airway may need to be secured in theatre.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute asthma — salbutamol nebuliser with the patient lying flat — Asthma causes wheeze, not drooling and muffled voice; lying flat would worsen breathing.
• Viral croup — nebulised adrenaline, oral dexamethasone, then discharge — Croup is mostly a childhood viral illness with a barking cough, and discharging a patient with stridor and drooling would be dangerous.
• Quinsy — examine the throat with a tongue depressor and drain it — Quinsy (peritonsillar abscess) causes trismus and a muffled voice, but examining the throat with a tongue depressor in suspected epiglottitis can cause complete obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Give IV antibiotics (e.g. ceftriaxone) once the airway is safe. Have emergency airway equipment, including surgical airway (cricothyroidotomy) kit, ready.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q78$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 754, $s$A 55-year-old on lisinopril develops rapidly increasing swelling of the lips and tongue, with no urticaria, no itch and no wheeze. BP is normal. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Ludwig's angina (floor-of-mouth sepsis)", "Anaphylaxis (IgE-mediated reaction)", "Hereditary C1-esterase angioedema", "ACE-inhibitor (bradykinin) angioedema"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ACE-inhibitor (bradykinin) angioedema
ACE INHIBITORS (e.g. lisinopril, enalapril) block the enzyme that breaks down BRADYKININ. Bradykinin builds up, causing blood vessels to leak and producing ANGIOEDEMA: rapid, deep SWELLING of the LIPS, TONGUE, face or throat. It is NOT an allergic (histamine) reaction, so there is NO URTICARIA (hives), NO ITCH and NO WHEEZE, and BP is usually normal. It can happen at any time, even after years of taking the drug. It is more common in people of African descent. The swelling can block the airway.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ludwig's angina (floor-of-mouth sepsis) — Ludwig's angina is a severe infection of the floor of the mouth (usually from a dental infection), with fever and a hard swollen neck.
• Anaphylaxis (IgE-mediated reaction) — Anaphylaxis is an allergic reaction with urticaria, itching, wheeze, and often hypotension.
• Hereditary C1-esterase angioedema — Hereditary C1-esterase angioedema is a genetic condition with recurrent swelling, usually starting in childhood or adolescence, often with a family history.

⭐ Key point: Stop the ACE inhibitor for life. Protect the airway (may need early intubation). Adrenaline, antihistamines and steroids are often less effective; icatibant or fresh frozen plasma may help.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q79$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 755, $s$A patient with an exacerbation of COPD is alert, with RR 28, pH 7.30, PaCO₂ 62 mmHg and SpO₂ 86% after nebulisers and steroids. The most appropriate next step is:$s$, $s$["Give 15 L/min via non-rebreather to keep SpO₂ above 98%", "Intubate and ventilate immediately in the emergency room", "Continue nebulisers only and repeat the gas in 12 hours", "Start NIV (BiPAP) with oxygen titrated to SpO₂ 88–92%"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Start NIV (BiPAP) with oxygen titrated to SpO₂ 88–92%
This COPD patient has an ACUTE RESPIRATORY ACIDOSIS (pH 7.30, PaCO2 62 mmHg) and a high respiratory rate despite initial treatment (nebulisers and steroids). This is the main indication for NON-INVASIVE VENTILATION (NIV, BiPAP): pH below 7.35 and PaCO2 above 45 mmHg (6 kPa) after standard medical treatment. NIV supports breathing, reduces the work of breathing, and helps blow off CO2. Oxygen should be TITRATED to a TARGET SpO2 of 88–92%, because too much oxygen can worsen CO2 retention in some COPD patients. The patient is ALERT, so can protect the airway and cooperate with NIV.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give 15 L/min via non-rebreather to keep SpO₂ above 98% — High-flow oxygen to keep SpO2 above 98% can worsen CO2 retention and acidosis in COPD.
• Intubate and ventilate immediately in the emergency room — Immediate intubation is not needed; NIV reduces the need for intubation and mortality. Intubation is for NIV failure or contraindications.
• Continue nebulisers only and repeat the gas in 12 hours — Waiting 12 hours without ventilatory support risks worsening acidosis. Reassess blood gases within 1–2 hours of starting NIV.

⭐ Key point: NIV contraindications include: inability to protect the airway, vomiting, recent facial or upper GI surgery, untreated pneumothorax, and severe haemodynamic instability.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q80$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 756, $s$A man was rescued from a burning house after 20 minutes in a smoke-filled room. Which finding is the strongest indication for EARLY intubation?$s$, $s$["A carboxyhaemoglobin level of 8%", "SpO₂ of 97% on oxygen", "Superficial facial burns alone", "A hoarse voice with inspiratory stridor"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A hoarse voice with inspiratory stridor
Smoke and heat can cause rapid SWELLING of the upper airway (inhalation injury). HOARSENESS and INSPIRATORY STRIDOR show the airway is ALREADY narrowing. The swelling worsens over hours, especially with fluid resuscitation, and can completely block the airway. EARLY INTUBATION (before the airway closes) is essential. Waiting may make intubation impossible.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A carboxyhaemoglobin level of 8% — A COHb level of 8% is mildly raised (smokers can have up to 10%); it is treated with oxygen, not intubation.
• SpO₂ of 97% on oxygen — SpO2 of 97% on oxygen doesn't rule out airway injury, and pulse oximetry can be falsely normal with CO.
• Superficial facial burns alone — Superficial facial burns alone don't require intubation, but should be monitored for signs of airway injury.

⭐ Key point: Other warning signs of inhalation injury: burns in an enclosed space, singed nasal hair, soot in the mouth or sputum, facial burns, and altered consciousness.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q152$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 757, $s$A family is found drowsy at home with a generator running indoors. They have headache, nausea and confusion. SpO₂ reads 98%, and carboxyhaemoglobin is 28%. The most appropriate treatment is:$s$, $s$["100% oxygen via a tight non-rebreather mask", "Oxygen titrated to an SpO₂ of 94–98%", "IV hydroxocobalamin 5 g over 15 minutes", "IV methylene blue 1–2 mg/kg over 5 minutes"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 100% oxygen via a tight non-rebreather mask
CARBON MONOXIDE (CO) from running a generator INDOORS (common in Nigeria) binds haemoglobin more strongly than oxygen. Symptoms: HEADACHE, NAUSEA, CONFUSION, drowsiness. SpO2 reads FALSELY NORMAL (98%) because standard oximeters can't tell COHb from oxygenated haemoglobin. COHb of 28% confirms significant poisoning. Treatment: 100% OXYGEN via a TIGHT-FITTING NON-REBREATHER MASK (with a reservoir). This reduces the half-life of COHb from about 4–6 hours to about 60–90 minutes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oxygen titrated to an SpO₂ of 94–98% — Titrating oxygen to SpO2 94–98% is wrong, as SpO2 is unreliable; high-flow oxygen is needed.
• IV hydroxocobalamin 5 g over 15 minutes — Hydroxocobalamin treats CYANIDE poisoning.
• IV methylene blue 1–2 mg/kg over 5 minutes — Methylene blue treats METHAEMOGLOBINAEMIA.

⭐ Key point: Consider hyperbaric oxygen for severe cases (e.g. loss of consciousness, neurological signs, pregnancy, very high COHb). Educate families never to run generators indoors.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q153$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 758, $s$A farmer who drank a pesticide has pinpoint pupils, drooling, wheezing with copious bronchial secretions, vomiting, diarrhoea, HR 45 and muscle twitching. Atropine is being given in doubling doses. The best endpoint for adequate atropinisation is:$s$, $s$["Complete disappearance of all muscle fasciculations", "A clear chest, with HR above 80 and systolic BP above 80", "Fully dilated pupils that no longer react to light", "A heart rate above 120/min with a dry, flushed skin"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A clear chest, with HR above 80 and systolic BP above 80
ORGANOPHOSPHATE pesticides block acetylcholinesterase, causing ACETYLCHOLINE to build up. Features: PINPOINT PUPILS, DROOLING, WHEEZING with COPIOUS BRONCHIAL SECRETIONS (bronchorrhoea), VOMITING, DIARRHOEA, BRADYCARDIA, and MUSCLE TWITCHING. ATROPINE blocks the muscarinic effects. It is given in DOUBLING DOSES every 5 minutes until "ATROPINISATION" is achieved: a CLEAR CHEST (no wheeze or crackles from secretions), HEART RATE ABOVE 80, SYSTOLIC BP ABOVE 80, dry axillae and pupils no longer pinpoint.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Complete disappearance of all muscle fasciculations — Atropine does NOT treat muscle fasciculations (a nicotinic effect); pralidoxime is used for this.
• Fully dilated pupils that no longer react to light — Fully dilated pupils are not the target; this may suggest atropine toxicity.
• A heart rate above 120/min with a dry, flushed skin — A heart rate above 120 and dry flushed skin suggest atropine TOXICITY.

⭐ Key point: Once atropinised, give an atropine infusion (10–20% of the total loading dose per hour). Remove contaminated clothing and wash the skin. Staff should wear gloves.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q155$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 759, $s$A baby born at 28 weeks to a mother who did not receive antenatal steroids develops grunting, nasal flaring, chest recession and cyanosis within an hour of birth. Chest X-ray shows a diffuse 'ground-glass' appearance with air bronchograms. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Transient tachypnoea of the newborn", "Meconium aspiration syndrome", "Respiratory distress syndrome", "Congenital diaphragmatic hernia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory distress syndrome
RESPIRATORY DISTRESS SYNDROME (RDS) occurs in PREMATURE babies (here 28 weeks) because their lungs lack SURFACTANT, so the alveoli collapse. Risk is higher without ANTENATAL STEROIDS. Features within hours of birth: GRUNTING, NASAL FLARING, CHEST RECESSION, CYANOSIS. Chest X-ray: diffuse "GROUND-GLASS" appearance with AIR BRONCHOGRAMS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Transient tachypnoea of the newborn — TTN occurs in term babies, often after caesarean section, and resolves quickly.
• Meconium aspiration syndrome — Meconium aspiration occurs in term or post-term babies with meconium-stained fluid.
• Congenital diaphragmatic hernia — Diaphragmatic hernia shows bowel in the chest on X-ray.

⭐ Key point: Treat with surfactant, CPAP or ventilation, and oxygen. Prevent with antenatal steroids.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q171$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 760, $s$A hypotensive patient (BP 80/50 mmHg) with acute severe asthma needs intubation. The most suitable induction agent is:$s$, $s$["Midazolam in high dose", "Ketamine", "Thiopental", "Propofol"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ketamine
KETAMINE is the most suitable induction agent here for two reasons. (1) It STIMULATES the sympathetic nervous system (releases noradrenaline), so it MAINTAINS or RAISES heart rate and BP — important in a patient who is already HYPOTENSIVE (80/50). (2) It is a BRONCHODILATOR (relaxes airway smooth muscle), which helps in ACUTE SEVERE ASTHMA. Most other induction agents lower BP, and some can worsen bronchospasm. Ketamine also preserves respiratory drive and gives pain relief.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Midazolam in high dose — High-dose midazolam causes vasodilation and myocardial depression, worsening hypotension, and its onset is slow and unpredictable.
• Thiopental — Thiopental lowers BP (myocardial depression and vasodilation) and can release histamine, which may worsen bronchospasm.
• Propofol — Propofol causes significant vasodilation and hypotension, which would be dangerous here (though it has mild bronchodilator properties).

⭐ Key point: Ketamine increases secretions (consider glycopyrrolate) and can cause emergence reactions (hallucinations) — benzodiazepines reduce this. In asthma, set the ventilator to allow long expiratory times to avoid air trapping after intubation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q183$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 761, $s$During a rapid IV vancomycin infusion, a patient develops flushing, itching and a red rash on the face, neck and upper chest, with mild hypotension. There is no wheeze or airway swelling. The correct response is:$s$, $s$["Anaphylaxis — give IM adrenaline and never use vancomycin again", "Infusion ('red man') reaction — pause, give an antihistamine, restart slower", "Serum sickness — stop the drug and start a course of oral steroids", "Stevens–Johnson syndrome — stop all drugs and refer to dermatology"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Infusion ('red man') reaction — pause, give an antihistamine, restart slower
VANCOMYCIN INFUSION REACTION (previously called "RED MAN SYNDROME") happens when vancomycin is infused TOO QUICKLY. The drug directly causes mast cells to release HISTAMINE — it is NOT a true allergy (not IgE-mediated). Features: FLUSHING, ITCHING and a RED RASH on the FACE, NECK and UPPER CHEST, sometimes mild HYPOTENSION, without wheeze or airway swelling. Management: PAUSE the infusion, give an ANTIHISTAMINE (e.g. chlorphenamine), and when symptoms settle, RESTART at a SLOWER rate (over at least 60 minutes per 1 g, or longer).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anaphylaxis — give IM adrenaline and never use vancomycin again — Anaphylaxis involves wheeze, airway swelling, severe hypotension or collapse; IM adrenaline would be given. This reaction lacks those features.
• Serum sickness — stop the drug and start a course of oral steroids — Serum sickness appears days to weeks after starting a drug, with fever, rash and joint pain.
• Stevens–Johnson syndrome — stop all drugs and refer to dermatology — Stevens–Johnson syndrome involves blistering skin and mucous membrane damage, developing over days.

⭐ Key point: Prevent infusion reactions by infusing vancomycin slowly (at least 60 minutes per gram; maximum 10 mg/min). Document the reaction as an "infusion reaction", not a drug allergy, so the patient does not lose an important antibiotic option.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q193$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 762, $s$Thoracotomy is generally indicated for massive haemothorax when drainage exceeds:$s$, $s$[">2500ml in total, regardless of the rate", ">500ml in total or >100ml/hr for 1 hour", ">1500ml or >200ml/hr for 3 hours", ">1000ml in total or any bright red blood"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: >1500ml or >200ml/hr for 3 hours
A MASSIVE HAEMOTHORAX (large amount of blood in the chest) may need surgery to stop the bleeding. ATLS guidance: urgent THORACOTOMY is indicated when the INITIAL chest drain output is MORE THAN 1500 mL of blood, OR when ONGOING drainage exceeds 200 mL PER HOUR for 2–4 HOURS (the question uses 3 hours), or when the patient needs repeated blood transfusions to stay stable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• >2500ml in total, regardless of the rate — There is no rule of "2500 mL regardless of rate"; waiting this long is dangerous.
• >500ml in total or >100ml/hr for 1 hour — 500 mL total or 100 mL/hr for 1 hour is too low a threshold; many patients stop bleeding without surgery at these amounts.
• >1000ml in total or any bright red blood — 1000 mL total is below the 1500 mL threshold, and any bright red blood alone does not automatically mean thoracotomy.

⭐ Key point: The patient's physiology also matters: a patient who stays unstable despite resuscitation needs surgery even below these numbers.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q5$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 763, $s$A patient with a left haemothorax drained by chest tube becomes hypotensive and the drainage suddenly stops. The nurse should first suspect:$s$, $s$["Air leak from the underwater seal causing pneumothorax", "Vasovagal reaction to the presence of the chest drain", "That the bleeding has stopped and the lung is re-expanded", "A clotted or kinked tube with blood re-accumulating in the chest"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A clotted or kinked tube with blood re-accumulating in the chest
The drain had been removing blood. If drainage SUDDENLY STOPS while the patient becomes HYPOTENSIVE, the most likely cause is that the tube is BLOCKED (by a clot) or KINKED. Blood is then building up in the chest again (re-accumulating), compressing the lung and causing hypovolaemia. Check the tubing for kinks and clots, check the patient (breath sounds, oxygen saturation, trachea), and inform the doctor urgently. A chest X-ray may be needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Air leak from the underwater seal causing pneumothorax — An air leak would cause bubbling in the water seal and possible pneumothorax, but doesn't explain blood loss and stopped drainage.
• Vasovagal reaction to the presence of the chest drain — A vasovagal reaction may cause brief hypotension, but it does not explain why drainage suddenly stopped.
• That the bleeding has stopped and the lung is re-expanded — If the bleeding had stopped, the patient would be STABLE or improving, not becoming hypotensive.

⭐ Key point: Never assume a sudden stop in drainage is good news if the patient is getting worse. Always look at the patient and the system together.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q8$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 764, $s$Autotransfusion from a chest drain collection system is most appropriate when:$s$, $s$["A known malignant pleural effusion with recent bleeding", "Blood that has been in the collection chamber over 24 hours", "A penetrating injury that has also perforated the bowel", "A large, uncontaminated haemothorax within a few hours of injury"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A large, uncontaminated haemothorax within a few hours of injury
AUTOTRANSFUSION means collecting the patient's own blood from the chest drain, filtering it and giving it back into a vein. It is most appropriate for a LARGE, UNCONTAMINATED HAEMOTHORAX (e.g. from a chest injury without bowel damage) collected WITHIN A FEW HOURS (usually within 4–6 hours) of injury. It reduces the need for donor blood and avoids transfusion reactions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A known malignant pleural effusion with recent bleeding — Malignant effusion blood may contain cancer cells, which could spread if reinfused.
• Blood that has been in the collection chamber over 24 hours — Blood that has stood for over 24 hours may be contaminated and its cells and clotting factors broken down.
• A penetrating injury that has also perforated the bowel — If the bowel is perforated, the blood is contaminated with gut bacteria, which could cause severe sepsis if reinfused.

⭐ Key point: Autotransfusion requires a special collection system with an anticoagulant and filter. Follow the manufacturer's time limits.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q9$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 765, $s$Which finding distinguishes ARDS (shock lung) from cardiogenic pulmonary oedema?$s$, $s$["Unilateral infiltrates with a large heart shadow", "Rapid improvement with furosemide alone", "Raised wedge pressure above 25 mmHg", "Normal wedge pressure with bilateral infiltrates"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Normal wedge pressure with bilateral infiltrates
ARDS and cardiogenic pulmonary oedema both cause bilateral infiltrates on X-ray. The key difference: in ARDS the problem is LEAKY lung capillaries, not a failing heart, so the PULMONARY CAPILLARY WEDGE PRESSURE (PCWP — a measure of left heart filling pressure) is NORMAL (usually 18 mmHg or less). In cardiogenic oedema, high left heart pressure pushes fluid into the lungs, so the wedge pressure is HIGH.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Unilateral infiltrates with a large heart shadow — Unilateral infiltrates with a large heart suggest other causes; ARDS is bilateral, and a large heart suggests heart failure.
• Rapid improvement with furosemide alone — Rapid improvement with furosemide suggests cardiogenic oedema; ARDS does not resolve quickly with diuretics.
• Raised wedge pressure above 25 mmHg — A raised wedge pressure (over 25 mmHg) suggests cardiogenic pulmonary oedema.

⭐ Key point: Today, echocardiography is more commonly used than a pulmonary artery catheter to assess heart function.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q15$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 766, $s$Massive haemothorax is defined by an initial drainage of at least:$s$, $s$["500 mL", "3000 mL", "1500 mL", "200 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1500 mL
A MASSIVE HAEMOTHORAX is defined as more than 1500 mL of blood (about one-third or more of the patient's blood volume) collecting in the chest, usually detected as the initial chest drain output. It causes hypovolaemic shock AND compresses the lung, causing breathing difficulty.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 500 mL — 500 mL is a moderate haemothorax.
• 3000 mL — 3000 mL is far above the threshold; waiting for this would be fatal.
• 200 mL — 200 mL relates to the hourly rate threshold (200 mL/hour), not the definition of initial massive drainage.

⭐ Key point: Massive haemothorax: dull percussion, reduced breath sounds, shock and flat neck veins (due to blood loss). Treat with blood, chest drain and surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q18$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 767, $s$A ventilated patient with pulmonary hypertension needs suctioning. The best approach is:$s$, $s$["Pre-oxygenate and use short, closed suction to avoid hypoxia", "Avoid suction completely to protect the pulmonary vessels", "Disconnect the ventilator and bag with room air", "Long open suction to clear all secretions at once"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pre-oxygenate and use short, closed suction to avoid hypoxia
Suctioning can cause hypoxia, coughing and agitation, which RAISE pulmonary vascular resistance and can trigger a pulmonary hypertensive crisis. The best approach: PRE-OXYGENATE (100% oxygen for 30–60 seconds before), use a CLOSED (in-line) suction system that keeps the ventilator connected and PEEP maintained, and make each pass SHORT (no more than 10–15 seconds). Consider sedation or an extra dose of analgesia before suctioning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Avoid suction completely to protect the pulmonary vessels — Suction cannot be avoided; secretions blocking the tube cause hypoxia, which is also dangerous.
• Disconnect the ventilator and bag with room air — Disconnecting and bagging with room air (21% oxygen) causes hypoxia and loss of PEEP.
• Long open suction to clear all secretions at once — Long open suction causes prolonged hypoxia and loss of PEEP, which may cause a crisis.

⭐ Key point: Watch the SpO2, BP and heart rate during suctioning. Stop if the patient deteriorates.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q19$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 768, $s$Acute chest syndrome in sickle cell disease presents with:$s$, $s$["Isolated limb pain without any fever or hypoxia", "Pallor and a rapidly enlarging spleen only", "New lung infiltrate with chest pain, fever, hypoxia", "Haematuria with loin pain and normal oxygen"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: New lung infiltrate with chest pain, fever, hypoxia
ACUTE CHEST SYNDROME (ACS) is a serious, potentially fatal complication of sickle cell disease. It is defined as a NEW INFILTRATE on chest X-ray PLUS one or more of: CHEST PAIN, FEVER, HYPOXIA, cough, wheeze or breathing difficulty. It is caused by infection, fat embolism from infarcted bone marrow, or sickling in the lung vessels, and can rapidly progress to respiratory failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Isolated limb pain without any fever or hypoxia — Isolated limb pain without fever or hypoxia describes a simple vaso-occlusive (painful) crisis.
• Pallor and a rapidly enlarging spleen only — Pallor with a rapidly enlarging spleen describes splenic sequestration.
• Haematuria with loin pain and normal oxygen — Haematuria with loin pain suggests renal papillary necrosis, not ACS.

⭐ Key point: Treat ACS with oxygen, antibiotics (including a macrolide for atypical organisms), analgesia, incentive spirometry, careful fluids, and blood transfusion (simple or exchange) if severe.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q109$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 769, $s$In the design of an ICU, each bed space should have:$s$, $s$["Space, oxygen, air, suction and several power outlets", "No hand-washing basin, to save floor space", "Oxygen outlets shared with the next bed space", "A single power socket for all the equipment"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Space, oxygen, air, suction and several power outlets
Each ICU bed space should have: enough SPACE (for equipment and staff to work, e.g. around 20–25 m²), OXYGEN outlets, medical AIR outlets, SUCTION outlets (usually at least 2 each), and SEVERAL POWER OUTLETS (often 12–16, including emergency supply) for monitors, ventilators, pumps and other equipment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No hand-washing basin, to save floor space — Hand-washing basins are essential for infection control.
• Oxygen outlets shared with the next bed space — Shared oxygen outlets risk cross-infection and supply problems.
• A single power socket for all the equipment — A single socket is unsafe and inadequate for ICU equipment.

⭐ Key point: Good design also includes natural light, privacy, and space for family.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q173$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 770, $s$A patient aspirates acidic gastric contents during an emergency intubation. Within hours, the most likely picture is:$s$, $s$["A normal chest X-ray with no respiratory symptoms at all", "A unilateral pleural effusion with a shift of the trachea", "Wheeze, hypoxaemia and bilateral infiltrates (chemical pneumonitis)", "Lobar consolidation with a high fever within the first hour"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Wheeze, hypoxaemia and bilateral infiltrates (chemical pneumonitis)
After aspirating acid, within hours the patient develops CHEMICAL PNEUMONITIS: WHEEZE (bronchospasm), HYPOXAEMIA (low oxygen), tachypnoea, and BILATERAL INFILTRATES on chest X-ray (often in the dependent lung areas, e.g. the right lower lobe).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal chest X-ray with no respiratory symptoms at all — Aspiration of acid causes symptoms; the X-ray may later show infiltrates.
• A unilateral pleural effusion with a shift of the trachea — Pleural effusion with tracheal shift is not typical.
• Lobar consolidation with a high fever within the first hour — Lobar consolidation with high fever in the first hour suggests bacterial pneumonia, which develops later.

⭐ Key point: Treatment is supportive: oxygen, suction, bronchodilators, and ventilation if needed. Steroids are not recommended.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q182$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 771, $s$Incentive spirometry after thoracic or abdominal surgery mainly prevents:$s$, $s$["Urinary retention", "Atelectasis and pneumonia", "Pressure ulcers", "Deep vein thrombosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Atelectasis and pneumonia
After THORACIC or ABDOMINAL SURGERY, patients breathe shallowly because of pain, anaesthetic effects and lying in bed. Shallow breathing allows small airways and alveoli to collapse (ATELECTASIS), and secretions collect, which can lead to PNEUMONIA. INCENTIVE SPIROMETRY uses a device that shows the patient how deeply they are breathing in, encouraging slow, deep breaths (sustained maximal inspiration). This re-expands the alveoli and helps clear secretions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Urinary retention — Urinary retention is prevented by early mobilisation, good pain control and bladder monitoring, not spirometry.
• Pressure ulcers — Pressure ulcers are prevented by repositioning, good nutrition and pressure-relieving devices.
• Deep vein thrombosis — DVT is prevented by early mobilisation, compression stockings and anticoagulants.

⭐ Key point: Teach before surgery: hold the device upright, seal lips around the mouthpiece, breathe in slowly and deeply, hold for 3–5 seconds, then breathe out. Repeat about 10 times every hour while awake, and support the wound with a pillow when coughing.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q190$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 772, $s$A haemopneumothorax should be managed with:$s$, $s$["Needle aspiration only, repeated daily until the chest is dry", "A small pigtail catheter for air only, kept clamped between checks", "Observation with high-flow oxygen therapy and a repeat X-ray", "A large-bore drain in the safe triangle on an underwater seal"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A large-bore drain in the safe triangle on an underwater seal
A haemopneumothorax contains both blood and air. Blood can clot and block small tubes, so a LARGE-BORE chest drain (usually 28–32 Fr in trauma) is inserted in the SAFE TRIANGLE (4th–5th intercostal space, mid-axillary line) and connected to an UNDERWATER SEAL, allowing both blood and air to drain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Needle aspiration only, repeated daily until the chest is dry — Needle aspiration won't drain blood effectively.
• A small pigtail catheter for air only, kept clamped between checks — A small pigtail catheter blocks with blood, and drains should not be kept clamped.
• Observation with high-flow oxygen therapy and a repeat X-ray — Observation alone is unsafe; blood and air need draining.

⭐ Key point: Record the initial blood volume drained. Above 1500 mL, or above 200 mL/hour for 2–4 hours, suggests the need for surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q5$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 773, $s$A patient with status asthmaticus on the ventilator has rising airway pressures and falling BP. The nurse suspects:$s$, $s$["Fluid overload from the IV aminophylline infusion", "A normal response to the nebulised bronchodilators", "Auto-PEEP from air trapping; briefly disconnect to allow exhalation", "The endotracheal tube has slipped into the right main bronchus"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Auto-PEEP from air trapping; briefly disconnect to allow exhalation
In ventilated status asthmaticus, air can't escape fully from narrowed airways (AIR TRAPPING), building up AUTO-PEEP. Pressure in the chest rises, squeezing the heart and veins, so venous return falls, causing HYPOTENSION, while airway pressures RISE. Briefly DISCONNECTING the patient from the ventilator lets trapped air out, and BP often improves at once.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fluid overload from the IV aminophylline infusion — Fluid overload from aminophylline isn't the likely cause.
• A normal response to the nebulised bronchodilators — Rising pressures and falling BP aren't a normal response.
• The endotracheal tube has slipped into the right main bronchus — Right main bronchus intubation would cause one-sided breath sounds, not this specific pattern.

⭐ Key point: Prevent auto-PEEP: low rate, low tidal volume, long expiratory time (I:E 1:4 or more), and permissive hypercapnia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q18$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 774, $s$BiPAP for acute hypercapnic COPD exacerbation works mainly by:$s$, $s$["IPAP boosts tidal volume to clear CO₂; EPAP splints the airways", "EPAP alone delivers a high, fixed oxygen fraction to the patient", "It removes the need for any arterial blood gas monitoring", "It sedates the patient so that the breathing rate falls"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IPAP boosts tidal volume to clear CO₂; EPAP splints the airways
BiPAP gives two pressures. IPAP (inspiratory) boosts each breath, increasing TIDAL VOLUME, which blows off CO₂ and rests the tired breathing muscles. EPAP (expiratory, like PEEP) SPLINTS the small airways open, reducing air trapping and the work of breathing. This lowers CO₂ and corrects acidosis in COPD exacerbations.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• EPAP alone delivers a high, fixed oxygen fraction to the patient — EPAP alone doesn't deliver a fixed FiO₂.
• It removes the need for any arterial blood gas monitoring — ABGs are still needed to monitor the response.
• It sedates the patient so that the breathing rate falls — BiPAP doesn't sedate patients.

⭐ Key point: Check an ABG after 1–2 hours. If the pH isn't improving, consider intubation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q20$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 775, $s$Immediately after a jugular central line insertion, the patient becomes breathless with reduced breath sounds on that side. The nurse suspects:$s$, $s$["Air embolism of the coronary arteries", "Arterial puncture with haematoma only", "Pneumothorax", "Allergic reaction to chlorhexidine"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pneumothorax
During jugular (or subclavian) central line insertion, the needle can puncture the top of the lung, causing a PNEUMOTHORAX. Sudden breathlessness with REDUCED BREATH SOUNDS on that side immediately after insertion strongly suggests this.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Air embolism of the coronary arteries — A coronary air embolism isn't the typical presentation.
• Arterial puncture with haematoma only — An arterial puncture causes bleeding and a haematoma, not reduced breath sounds.
• Allergic reaction to chlorhexidine — A chlorhexidine allergy causes rash or anaphylaxis, not one-sided reduced breath sounds.

⭐ Key point: Get an urgent chest X-ray. A tension pneumothorax (low BP, tracheal shift) needs immediate decompression.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q25$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 776, $s$An underwater seal chest drain stops swinging and the patient is comfortable with normal breath sounds. This most likely means:$s$, $s$["The patient has tension pneumothorax", "The lung has fully re-expanded", "There is a large air leak", "The drain is in the abdomen"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The lung has fully re-expanded
Swinging (tidaling) shows the drain is open to the pleural space. If swinging STOPS but the patient is COMFORTABLE with NORMAL breath sounds, the LUNG has probably fully RE-EXPANDED and is sealing the end of the tube. Confirm with a chest X-ray.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient has tension pneumothorax — A tension pneumothorax would cause distress and absent breath sounds.
• There is a large air leak — A large air leak would cause continuous bubbling.
• The drain is in the abdomen — A drain in the abdomen wouldn't drain the chest properly from the start.

⭐ Key point: If swinging stops with distress or worsening breath sounds, suspect a BLOCKED or kinked drain and call for help.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q29$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 777, $s$Chest drain bubbling that is continuous during both inspiration and expiration in a ventilated patient suggests:$s$, $s$["Normal function of the drain in a ventilated patient", "Complete re-expansion of the lung on that side", "A large air leak from the lung (bronchopleural fistula) or system", "Blockage of the drain by a large blood clot"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A large air leak from the lung (bronchopleural fistula) or system
In a ventilated patient, the lungs are under positive pressure. CONTINUOUS bubbling through both inspiration and expiration suggests a LARGE AIR LEAK, either from the LUNG (a bronchopleural fistula, a hole between an airway and the pleural space) or from the drainage SYSTEM (loose connections).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal function of the drain in a ventilated patient — Continuous bubbling isn't normal function.
• Complete re-expansion of the lung on that side — Complete re-expansion would REDUCE bubbling.
• Blockage of the drain by a large blood clot — A blocked drain would STOP bubbling and swinging.

⭐ Key point: Check all connections first (clamp briefly near the chest: if bubbling stops, the leak is in the patient). Report persistent leaks.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q30$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 778, $s$Signs of a developing tension pneumothorax in a ventilated patient include:$s$, $s$["Hypertension and bradycardia only", "Rising airway pressures, falling SpO₂, hypotension", "Falling airway pressures with a rising SpO₂", "An increased tidal volume with no other change"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Rising airway pressures, falling SpO₂, hypotension
In a ventilated patient, a developing tension pneumothorax causes RISING AIRWAY PRESSURES (the lung can't inflate), FALLING SpO₂, HYPOTENSION (reduced venous return), a raised JVP, absent breath sounds on the affected side, and tracheal deviation. Positive pressure ventilation makes it worsen quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension and bradycardia only — High BP with slow pulse isn't typical.
• Falling airway pressures with a rising SpO₂ — Falling pressures and rising SpO₂ are the opposite.
• An increased tidal volume with no other change — Increased tidal volume isn't a sign.

⭐ Key point: Treat immediately with needle decompression, followed by a chest drain.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q39$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 779, $s$A child with acute severe asthma becomes quiet with a 'silent chest' and drowsiness. This indicates:$s$, $s$["A normal response to inhaled salbutamol", "That the child is ready for discharge home", "Life-threatening asthma needing immediate escalation", "Improvement in the attack after nebulisers"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Life-threatening asthma needing immediate escalation
In acute asthma, a SILENT CHEST means so little air is moving that wheeze can no longer be heard. Together with DROWSINESS (from rising CO2 and low oxygen), poor effort, cyanosis or exhaustion, this is LIFE-THREATENING ASTHMA. The child is close to respiratory arrest. Call for senior help/ICU immediately, give high-flow oxygen, continuous nebulised salbutamol with ipratropium, IV steroids, and consider IV magnesium sulphate or IV salbutamol/aminophylline. Prepare for possible intubation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal response to inhaled salbutamol — A quiet chest is NOT a good response. It means airflow is critically reduced. Improvement shows as better air entry, less work of breathing and better alertness.
• That the child is ready for discharge home — This child is critically ill and must never be discharged.
• Improvement in the attack after nebulisers — Silence here is due to exhaustion and almost no air entry, not recovery.

⭐ Key point: Warning signs of life-threatening asthma: silent chest, cyanosis, SpO2 below 92%, poor respiratory effort, exhaustion, confusion/drowsiness, hypotension. A "normal" or rising CO2 in a breathless asthmatic is also a danger sign.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q107$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 780, $s$Which precaution is most important in reducing the risk of anaesthetic death related to a difficult or failed airway?$s$, $s$["Relying only on the surgeon's assessment of the patient's airway", "Proceeding with induction without any airway assessment to save time", "Thorough airway assessment and a ready difficult airway plan and equipment", "Avoiding pre-oxygenation so that the procedure is completed faster"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Thorough airway assessment and a ready difficult airway plan and equipment
Many anaesthetic deaths are due to failure to secure the airway ("can't intubate, can't oxygenate"). The most important precaution is a THOROUGH AIRWAY ASSESSMENT before anaesthesia (e.g. mouth opening, Mallampati score, neck movement, thyromental distance, previous difficult intubation) and having a READY DIFFICULT AIRWAY PLAN AND EQUIPMENT (video laryngoscope, bougie, supraglottic airway devices, front-of-neck access kit), with skilled help available.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Relying only on the surgeon's assessment of the patient's airway — The surgeon's assessment alone is not enough; airway assessment is the anaesthetist's responsibility.
• Proceeding with induction without any airway assessment to save time — Skipping assessment to save time increases the risk of an unexpected difficult airway.
• Avoiding pre-oxygenation so that the procedure is completed faster — Pre-oxygenation is vital; it gives extra time before oxygen levels fall if intubation is difficult.

⭐ Key point: Follow difficult airway algorithms (e.g. Difficult Airway Society guidelines). Always have suction and oxygen ready.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q182$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 781, $s$The usual first priority when aspiration of gastric content is witnessed during induction is:$s$, $s$["Give IV steroids as the first and most important step", "Head-down tilt, suction and a cuffed tube to secure the airway", "Wait for a chest X-ray before doing anything else", "Sit the patient upright and give oral fluids to wash the throat"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Head-down tilt, suction and a cuffed tube to secure the airway
When aspiration of stomach contents is SEEN during induction, the priority is to limit further aspiration and protect the airway: tilt the patient HEAD-DOWN (so fluid drains out of the mouth rather than into the lungs), SUCTION the mouth and pharynx, and secure the airway with a CUFFED ENDOTRACHEAL TUBE (the cuff seals the trachea). Then suction through the tube before ventilating if possible, and give 100% oxygen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give IV steroids as the first and most important step — Steroids are NOT recommended for aspiration; they do not improve outcome.
• Wait for a chest X-ray before doing anything else — Waiting for a chest X-ray delays urgent airway management.
• Sit the patient upright and give oral fluids to wash the throat — Sitting the patient up and giving oral fluids to an anaesthetised patient would cause more aspiration.

⭐ Key point: Bronchoscopy may be needed to remove solid particles. Antibiotics are not given routinely unless infection develops. Monitor for hypoxia and ARDS.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q185$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 782, $s$Which NANDA-I diagnosis is most appropriate for a patient who is unconscious with an absent gag reflex and is receiving enteral tube feedings, placing them at risk of food or fluid entering the airway?$s$, $s$["Risk for Suffocation", "Impaired Swallowing", "Risk for Aspiration", "Ineffective Airway Clearance"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Risk for Aspiration
Risk for Aspiration fits this patient exactly. He is UNCONSCIOUS (can't protect his airway), has NO GAG REFLEX (can't stop secretions or food entering the trachea), and is on TUBE FEEDS (stomach contents can come back up and be inhaled). It is a "risk" diagnosis because aspiration hasn't happened yet but is likely.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Risk for Suffocation — Risk for Suffocation relates to things blocking breathing from outside (pillows, choking on objects), not tube feeds entering the airway.
• Impaired Swallowing — Impaired Swallowing is about difficulty swallowing in an awake patient; this patient isn't swallowing at all.
• Ineffective Airway Clearance — Ineffective Airway Clearance is an actual problem with clearing secretions, not the specific risk of feeds entering the airway.

⭐ Key point: Prevent aspiration: head of bed 30–45°, check tube position, check gastric residual volumes as per policy, keep the cuff inflated if intubated, and do mouth care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q62$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 783, $s$The priority nursing care of an unconscious patient is to:$s$, $s$["Restrict all IV and enteral fluids", "Keep the airway patent and prevent aspiration", "Begin oral feeding to maintain nutrition", "Remove all monitoring to allow rest"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Keep the airway patent and prevent aspiration
An unconscious patient loses the protective reflexes (cough and gag), and the tongue may fall back and block the throat. The airway is therefore always the FIRST priority. Position the patient on the side (recovery or lateral position) or head-up, suction secretions, use an airway adjunct if needed, and intubate if GCS is 8 or less. Without a clear airway, the patient dies within minutes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Restrict all IV and enteral fluids — Fluids are needed (IV or enteral) to prevent dehydration; they shouldn't be restricted.
• Begin oral feeding to maintain nutrition — Oral feeding is dangerous in an unconscious patient: high risk of aspiration.
• Remove all monitoring to allow rest — Monitoring is essential in unconscious patients to detect deterioration.

⭐ Key point: Priorities always follow ABC: Airway, Breathing, Circulation. Other unconscious care (eyes, mouth, skin, nutrition) comes after the airway is safe.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q72$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 784, $s$A ventilated patient suddenly has high pressure alarms, low tidal volume and cannot pass a suction catheter. The nurse should first:$s$, $s$["Give a sedative bolus and wait for 10 minutes", "Check the ventilator settings and do nothing else", "Suspect a blocked tube, remove it and bag-mask ventilate", "Increase the PEEP to open up the small airways"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Suspect a blocked tube, remove it and bag-mask ventilate
A high-pressure alarm with low tidal volume and a suction catheter that WON'T PASS down the tube means the endotracheal tube is BLOCKED (thick secretions, blood clot, kink or biting). The patient is not being ventilated and will quickly become hypoxic. If you can't clear the tube, remove it and give oxygen by bag-mask ventilation, then re-intubate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give a sedative bolus and wait for 10 minutes — Sedation and waiting 10 minutes wastes time while the patient becomes hypoxic.
• Check the ventilator settings and do nothing else — Checking settings without acting won't fix a blocked tube.
• Increase the PEEP to open up the small airways — Increasing PEEP won't help if the tube is blocked; gas can't get through.

⭐ Key point: A suction catheter that won't pass = blocked or kinked tube (or the patient biting it). Disconnect, bag with 100% O₂, and call for help.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q86$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 785, $s$The DOPE mnemonic for a ventilated patient deteriorating stands for:$s$, $s$["Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment", "Delirium, Over-sedation, Pain, Exhaustion", "Drugs, Oxygen, Pulse and Electrolytes", "Dehydration, Oedema, Pain and Embolism"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment
DOPE helps you remember the causes of sudden deterioration in an intubated, ventilated patient. D = DISPLACEMENT (the tube has moved into the oesophagus, a main bronchus, or out). O = OBSTRUCTION (secretions, blood, kink, biting). P = PNEUMOTHORAX (especially a tension pneumothorax). E = EQUIPMENT failure (ventilator, oxygen supply, circuit disconnection).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delirium, Over-sedation, Pain, Exhaustion — Delirium, over-sedation, pain and exhaustion are not DOPE.
• Drugs, Oxygen, Pulse and Electrolytes — Drugs, oxygen, pulse and electrolytes are not DOPE.
• Dehydration, Oedema, Pain and Embolism — Dehydration, oedema, pain and embolism are not DOPE.

⭐ Key point: Response: disconnect from the ventilator, hand-ventilate with 100% oxygen, check tube position and EtCO₂, pass a suction catheter, listen to the chest, and check the equipment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q87 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 786, $s$Prevention of endotracheal tube blockage includes:$s$, $s$["Dry inspired gases to reduce secretions", "Suctioning every 15 minutes as routine", "Instilling large saline volumes routinely", "Humidified gases and suction when needed"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Humidified gases and suction when needed
Normally, the nose warms and moistens the air we breathe. An endotracheal tube bypasses the nose, so dry gas would dry out the airway lining, making secretions thick and sticky, and they can block the tube. HUMIDIFIED gases (from a heated humidifier or an HME filter) keep secretions thin. Suction when needed (not on a fixed schedule) clears them.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dry inspired gases to reduce secretions — Dry gases make secretions thicker and cause blockage.
• Suctioning every 15 minutes as routine — Suctioning every 15 minutes is too frequent; it damages the airway lining and causes hypoxia.
• Instilling large saline volumes routinely — Large volumes of saline are not recommended routinely; they can push germs into the lungs.

⭐ Key point: Signs that the patient needs suction: visible secretions, coarse crackles, a rising airway pressure, falling SpO₂, a sawtooth pattern on the ventilator flow curve.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q88$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 787, $s$Carboprost (15-methyl PGF2α) should be avoided in:$s$, $s$["Diabetes", "Anaemia", "Hypertension", "Asthma"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Asthma
Carboprost is a prostaglandin F2α drug used for PPH caused by uterine atony. It contracts smooth muscle, including the uterus AND the airways. In people with ASTHMA, it can cause severe BRONCHOSPASM (wheezing and respiratory failure), so it should be avoided.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diabetes — Diabetes is not a contraindication to carboprost.
• Anaemia — Anaemia is not a contraindication.
• Hypertension — Hypertension is a caution but not the main contraindication.

⭐ Key point: Carboprost is given 250 micrograms IM (or into the uterine muscle), repeated every 15 minutes, up to 8 doses. Side effects: diarrhoea, vomiting, fever.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q130$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 788, $s$Standards of practice are used in court to judge whether:$s$, $s$["The patient had paid all the hospital bills", "She was the best nurse in the whole hospital", "She acted as a reasonably competent nurse would", "A doctor was present when she gave care"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: She acted as a reasonably competent nurse would
In a negligence case, the court asks whether the nurse acted as a REASONABLY COMPETENT nurse with similar training would have acted in the same situation (the "reasonable nurse" standard). Standards of practice (policies, guidelines, the code of conduct, expert evidence) are used to judge this.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient had paid all the hospital bills — Whether bills were paid has nothing to do with the standard of care.
• She was the best nurse in the whole hospital — The court doesn't expect the "best" nurse, only a reasonably competent one.
• A doctor was present when she gave care — A doctor's presence doesn't decide whether the nurse met the standard.

⭐ Key point: Following policies and guidelines and documenting your care clearly are your best defence.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q159$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 789, $s$After chest drain insertion, the nurse observes for:$s$, $s$["Only the colour of the dressing at the site", "Nothing at all until the chest X-ray is done", "Swinging, bubbling, drainage volume and breathing", "Only the total volume drained at 24 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Swinging, bubbling, drainage volume and breathing
After chest drain insertion, observe: SWINGING (water level rising and falling with breathing shows the drain is working), BUBBLING (air leaking from the lung), DRAINAGE volume and colour (blood, fluid, pus), and the patient's BREATHING (rate, SpO₂, chest movement, pain). Record drainage hourly at first. Also check the site, dressing and all connections.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only the colour of the dressing at the site — The dressing alone is not enough.
• Nothing at all until the chest X-ray is done — Monitoring must start immediately, not wait for the X-ray.
• Only the total volume drained at 24 hours — A 24-hour total alone misses sudden bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Report more than 100–200 mL of blood per hour, sudden cessation of drainage with distress, or new continuous bubbling.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q26$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 790, $s$A chest drain should not be clamped during transport because:$s$, $s$["The drain is more likely to fall out", "It increases the risk of infection", "A tension pneumothorax may develop", "The fluid inside the tube will clot"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A tension pneumothorax may develop
A drain inserted for a pneumothorax lets air escape continuously. If it is clamped, air builds up in the pleural space with nowhere to go, and a TENSION PNEUMOTHORAX can develop: the lung collapses, the mediastinum shifts, and the patient can collapse with low BP. Instead, keep the bottle BELOW chest level during transport.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The drain is more likely to fall out — Clamping doesn't affect whether the drain falls out.
• It increases the risk of infection — Infection risk isn't the main reason not to clamp.
• The fluid inside the tube will clot — Fluid in the tube doesn't clot because of clamping; the danger is trapped air.

⭐ Key point: Only clamp briefly when changing the bottle, finding a leak, or under medical instruction before removal. Never clamp a bubbling drain.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q27$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 791, $s$Underwater seal drainage works because:$s$, $s$["The water seal lets air out but not back in", "Suction is always applied to the bottle", "The bottle is kept above the chest level", "The drain tube is clamped between checks"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The water seal lets air out but not back in
The water seal acts as a ONE-WAY VALVE. The end of the tube sits 2–3 cm under sterile water. When the patient breathes out or coughs, pressure in the chest rises and air bubbles out through the water. When they breathe in, pressure falls, but air can't come back into the chest, because water rises in the tube and blocks it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Suction is always applied to the bottle — Suction is not always applied; many drains work by gravity alone.
• The bottle is kept above the chest level — The bottle must be kept BELOW chest level, never above.
• The drain tube is clamped between checks — Drains should not be routinely clamped.

⭐ Key point: Fill the water seal chamber to the marked line. Too much water makes it harder for air to escape.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q28$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 792, $s$When a chest drain accidentally falls out, the nurse should first:$s$, $s$["Clamp the drain tube and replace it on the bed", "Push the drain back into the chest through the same hole", "Seal the site with a sterile occlusive dressing; call for help", "Leave the site open so that any air can escape freely"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Seal the site with a sterile occlusive dressing; call for help
If a chest drain falls out, air can be sucked into the chest through the hole, causing a pneumothorax. First, quickly SEAL THE SITE with a sterile occlusive dressing (some guidelines use a three-sided dressing so air can escape but not enter). Then CALL FOR HELP, give oxygen and monitor for respiratory distress and tension pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Clamp the drain tube and replace it on the bed — Clamping the drain and putting it on the bed does nothing to seal the hole.
• Push the drain back into the chest through the same hole — Pushing the drain back in risks infection and injury; never re-insert it.
• Leave the site open so that any air can escape freely — Leaving the site open lets air enter the chest.

⭐ Key point: Always keep sterile occlusive dressings and Spencer Wells clamps at the bedside of a patient with a chest drain.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q36$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 793, $s$Equipment for emergency intubation in ICU should be:$s$, $s$["Checked only on the day it is needed", "Checked daily, on a dedicated airway trolley", "Kept in a locked store away from the unit", "Shared with the operating theatre suite"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Checked daily, on a dedicated airway trolley
Emergency intubation can be needed at any time in ICU. Equipment must be CHECKED DAILY (and after every use) and kept together on a dedicated AIRWAY TROLLEY: laryngoscopes (working lights), ETTs of various sizes, bougie, stylet, syringes, suction, bag-valve-mask, oropharyngeal and supraglottic airways, waveform capnography, and drugs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Checked only on the day it is needed — Checking only on the day of use risks missing faulty equipment in an emergency.
• Kept in a locked store away from the unit — Locking it away delays access in emergencies.
• Shared with the operating theatre suite — Sharing with theatre means it may not be available when needed.

⭐ Key point: Use a checklist and record daily checks with a signature. A difficult airway trolley should also be available.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q37$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 794, $s$Before a tracheostomy tube change, the nurse prepares:$s$, $s$["A urinary catheter set and sterile water", "Only a new dressing and tapes for the stoma site", "Spare tubes (same and smaller), suction, oxygen, dilators", "No special equipment, as changes are routine"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Spare tubes (same and smaller), suction, oxygen, dilators
Changing a tracheostomy tube can go wrong. The tube may not go back in, and the stoma can close quickly. Before changing, prepare: SPARE TUBES (the same size and one size smaller), SUCTION, OXYGEN with a bag-valve-mask, tracheal DILATORS, lubricant, new tapes and dressings, and an experienced second person.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A urinary catheter set and sterile water — A urinary catheter set isn't needed (though some use a catheter as a guide, this is not the core list).
• Only a new dressing and tapes for the stoma site — A new dressing and tapes alone isn't enough preparation.
• No special equipment, as changes are routine — Special equipment is always needed.

⭐ Key point: The FIRST tracheostomy change (usually after 5–7 days, once the tract has formed) is done by an experienced doctor.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q38$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 795, $s$Emergency equipment at a tracheostomy bedside includes:$s$, $s$["A defibrillator only, checked daily", "Spare tube, dilators, suction and oxygen", "A chest drain set and underwater seal", "An epidural pack and local anaesthetic"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Spare tube, dilators, suction and oxygen
At every tracheostomy patient's bedside, emergency equipment must be ready in case the tube comes out or blocks: a SPARE TUBE (same size and one size smaller), tracheal DILATORS, SUCTION and catheters, OXYGEN and a bag-valve-mask, and the tracheostomy emergency algorithm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A defibrillator only, checked daily — A defibrillator alone isn't enough for airway emergencies.
• A chest drain set and underwater seal — A chest drain set isn't needed for tracheostomy emergencies.
• An epidural pack and local anaesthetic — An epidural pack isn't related.

⭐ Key point: A displaced or blocked tracheostomy is an airway emergency. Follow the national tracheostomy safety algorithm.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q39$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 796, $s$Mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO₂) below 60% suggests:$s$, $s$["Normal tissue oxygen extraction", "Hyperoxia from high FiO₂", "Inadequate oxygen delivery for demand", "Excess oxygen delivery to the tissues"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inadequate oxygen delivery for demand
Mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO₂) shows how much oxygen is left in the blood returning to the heart after the tissues have used what they need. Normal is 60–80%. A low SvO₂ (below 60%) means the tissues are extracting MORE oxygen than usual, because DELIVERY is too low for demand: low cardiac output, anaemia, hypoxia, or high demand (fever, shivering).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal tissue oxygen extraction — Normal extraction gives an SvO₂ of 60–80%.
• Hyperoxia from high FiO₂ — High FiO₂ would raise, not lower, SvO₂.
• Excess oxygen delivery to the tissues — Excess oxygen delivery would raise SvO₂.

⭐ Key point: Low SvO₂ → improve oxygen delivery (fluids, inotropes, blood, oxygen) or reduce demand (sedation, cooling).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q55$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 797, $s$Auto-PEEP is most likely in:$s$, $s$["Normal lungs after elective surgery", "ARDS ventilated at a low rate", "Asthma or COPD with short expiratory time", "Pulmonary fibrosis with stiff lungs"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Asthma or COPD with short expiratory time
Auto-PEEP (intrinsic PEEP) happens when the patient can't breathe out fully before the next breath starts, so air gets trapped. It is most likely when there is AIRFLOW OBSTRUCTION (asthma, COPD), so expiration is slow, combined with a SHORT expiratory time (high rate, long inspiration).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal lungs after elective surgery — Normal lungs empty easily; auto-PEEP is unlikely.
• ARDS ventilated at a low rate — ARDS ventilated at a low rate gives plenty of time to breathe out.
• Pulmonary fibrosis with stiff lungs — Stiff lungs (fibrosis) empty quickly.

⭐ Key point: Treat auto-PEEP: reduce the rate, shorten inspiration (I:E of 1:3 or 1:4), use bronchodilators, and suction secretions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q72$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 798, $s$A sudden fall in SpO₂ with absent breath sounds on the left after intubation suggests:$s$, $s$["Right main bronchus intubation", "A left-sided pneumothorax in every case", "Severe bronchospasm on both sides", "A massive pulmonary embolism"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Right main bronchus intubation
The right main bronchus is shorter, wider and more vertical than the left. If the ETT is pushed in too far, it usually goes into the RIGHT main bronchus. Only the right lung is ventilated, so breath sounds are absent on the LEFT, the left lung collapses, and SpO₂ falls.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A left-sided pneumothorax in every case — A left pneumothorax could cause absent sounds, but right main bronchus intubation is the most likely cause right after intubation.
• Severe bronchospasm on both sides — Bronchospasm causes wheeze on both sides.
• A massive pulmonary embolism — A massive PE doesn't cause one-sided absent breath sounds.

⭐ Key point: Normal ETT depth at the teeth: about 21 cm in women, 23 cm in men. Confirm with a chest X-ray (tip 3–5 cm above the carina).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q75$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 799, $s$Non-invasive ventilation is contraindicated in:$s$, $s$["Inability to protect the airway, or vomiting", "COPD with hypercapnic respiratory failure", "Acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema", "Obesity hypoventilation syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inability to protect the airway, or vomiting
NIV (CPAP or BiPAP via a face mask) requires the patient to protect their own airway, cooperate and breathe spontaneously. It is CONTRAINDICATED if the patient can't PROTECT THE AIRWAY (very low GCS, poor cough), is VOMITING (aspiration risk with a sealed mask), has facial trauma or burns, recent upper GI surgery, an undrained pneumothorax, or is in cardiac arrest.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• COPD with hypercapnic respiratory failure — COPD with hypercapnic respiratory failure is a main INDICATION for NIV.
• Acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema — Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema is an INDICATION (CPAP).
• Obesity hypoventilation syndrome — Obesity hypoventilation syndrome is an INDICATION.

⭐ Key point: If NIV is used and the patient isn't improving within 1–2 hours (ABG), prepare for intubation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q76$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 800, $s$In a tension pneumothorax on X-ray, the trachea deviates to the:$s$, $s$["Same (ipsilateral) side", "Opposite (contralateral) side", "Remains exactly midline", "Cannot be assessed on X-ray"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Opposite (contralateral) side
In a tension pneumothorax, air keeps entering the pleural space and can't escape. Pressure builds up and PUSHES the mediastinum, heart and TRACHEA to the OPPOSITE (contralateral) side. This compresses the other lung and kinks the great veins, so blood can't return to the heart, causing shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Same (ipsilateral) side — The trachea moves AWAY from, not towards, the affected side.
• Remains exactly midline — In tension pneumothorax the trachea shifts; it doesn't stay midline.
• Cannot be assessed on X-ray — Tracheal deviation CAN be seen on X-ray (but the diagnosis is clinical; don't wait for an X-ray).

⭐ Key point: Trachea pushed AWAY: tension pneumothorax, large effusion. Trachea pulled TOWARDS: lobar collapse, pneumonectomy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q121$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 801, $s$Air bronchograms on chest X-ray indicate:$s$, $s$["A pneumothorax with collapse of the lung", "A pleural effusion at the lung base", "Air-filled bronchi within consolidated lung", "Normal lung with clear, aerated air spaces"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Air-filled bronchi within consolidated lung
Normally, the airways (bronchi) contain air and are surrounded by air-filled lung, so they can't be seen on X-ray. When the alveoli around them fill with fluid, pus or blood (CONSOLIDATION), the white lung makes the AIR-FILLED BRONCHI stand out as dark branching lines. These are air bronchograms, seen in pneumonia, pulmonary oedema and ARDS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A pneumothorax with collapse of the lung — In pneumothorax, the lung is collapsed and there are no lung markings.
• A pleural effusion at the lung base — A pleural effusion is fluid outside the lung, without air bronchograms.
• Normal lung with clear, aerated air spaces — Normal lung doesn't show air bronchograms.

⭐ Key point: Air bronchograms = the lung tissue is filled (consolidated) but the airways are still open.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q125$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 802, $s$Bat-wing perihilar shadowing with Kerley B lines suggests:$s$, $s$["Right lower lobe pneumonia", "Tension pneumothorax", "Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema", "Massive pulmonary embolism"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema
Cardiogenic pulmonary oedema (from left heart failure) shows on X-ray: "BAT-WING" (butterfly) shadowing around both hila, KERLEY B LINES (short horizontal lines at the lung edges from swollen lymphatics), UPPER LOBE VENOUS DIVERSION, cardiomegaly, and pleural effusions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right lower lobe pneumonia — Right lower lobe pneumonia shows one area of consolidation.
• Tension pneumothorax — Tension pneumothorax shows a black hemithorax with mediastinal shift.
• Massive pulmonary embolism — PE often shows a nearly normal X-ray.

⭐ Key point: Treat cardiogenic pulmonary oedema: sit up, oxygen or CPAP, nitrates, diuretics.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q126$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 803, $s$A CVP line check X-ray is also used to exclude:$s$, $s$["An acute ischaemic stroke", "Pneumothorax after insertion", "Acute appendicitis", "A fractured femoral neck"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pneumothorax after insertion
After inserting a central line through the jugular or subclavian vein, a chest X-ray is taken to check the TIP POSITION and to EXCLUDE a PNEUMOTHORAX, because the needle can puncture the lung. Subclavian lines carry the highest pneumothorax risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An acute ischaemic stroke — Stroke is diagnosed by CT or MRI of the brain.
• Acute appendicitis — Appendicitis is diagnosed clinically, by ultrasound or CT.
• A fractured femoral neck — A fractured femoral neck is seen on a hip X-ray.

⭐ Key point: Ultrasound-guided insertion reduces the risk of pneumothorax and arterial puncture.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q133$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 804, $s$When transferring a ventilated patient for CT, the nurse should:$s$, $s$["Stop all infusions until the patient returns", "Portable monitoring, oxygen, suction and emergency drugs", "Disconnect all monitoring to make the transfer faster", "Send the patient without a nurse to save staff"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Portable monitoring, oxygen, suction and emergency drugs
Transferring a ventilated patient to CT is a high-risk time: tubes can be dislodged, monitors and pumps can fail, and the patient can deteriorate away from the ICU. Take PORTABLE MONITORING (ECG, SpO₂, BP, EtCO₂), a transport ventilator, OXYGEN (enough for the trip plus extra), SUCTION, a bag-valve-mask, and EMERGENCY DRUGS, with a trained nurse and doctor.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stop all infusions until the patient returns — Stopping infusions (such as vasopressors or sedation) can cause collapse or awareness.
• Disconnect all monitoring to make the transfer faster — Disconnecting monitoring hides deterioration.
• Send the patient without a nurse to save staff — Ventilated patients must be accompanied by trained staff.

⭐ Key point: Use a transfer checklist. Secure all tubes and lines, and check oxygen and battery levels before leaving.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q138$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 805, $s$Percussion during airway clearance techniques is performed using:$s$, $s$["Flat, open-palm slapping", "Mechanical hammers only", "Cupped hands", "A closed fist"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cupped hands
Percussion uses CUPPED HANDS (fingers and thumb together, palm hollowed) to clap rhythmically over the chest wall. The cupped shape traps a cushion of air, which creates a vibration that loosens secretions without hurting the patient. It makes a hollow "clopping" sound.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flat, open-palm slapping — Flat, open palms slap the skin and cause pain and redness.
• Mechanical hammers only — Mechanical percussors exist, but "hammers only" is incorrect.
• A closed fist — A closed fist would hurt and could injure the patient.

⭐ Key point: Percuss over a thin layer of clothing or a towel, avoid bony areas (spine, sternum) and the kidneys, and stop if the patient becomes unstable.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q145$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 806, $s$In COPD patients, excessive carbohydrate feeding can:$s$, $s$["Raise CO₂ production and worsen respiratory failure", "Lower the PaCO₂ by improving ventilation", "Improve weaning by giving energy to the muscles", "Reduce the oxygen consumption of the body"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Raise CO₂ production and worsen respiratory failure
When the body burns carbohydrate, it makes more CO₂ than when it burns fat (a higher respiratory quotient). Excess carbohydrate (overfeeding) INCREASES CO₂ production. In COPD patients who already struggle to breathe out CO₂, this can raise the PaCO₂, worsen respiratory failure, and make weaning harder.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lower the PaCO₂ by improving ventilation — Extra carbohydrate RAISES PaCO₂, not lowers it.
• Improve weaning by giving energy to the muscles — It can make weaning harder.
• Reduce the oxygen consumption of the body — It increases oxygen consumption and CO₂ production.

⭐ Key point: Avoid overfeeding in COPD and ventilated patients. A balanced feed with some fat helps.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q153$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 807, $s$Suctioning should be limited to about:$s$, $s$["60 seconds or more each time", "2 minutes for every pass", "As long as secretions appear", "10–15 seconds, with pre-oxygenation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10–15 seconds, with pre-oxygenation
Each suction pass should last only 10–15 SECONDS. Suction removes oxygen along with secretions, so long passes cause HYPOXIA, arrhythmias, bradycardia (vagal stimulation), raised ICP and lung collapse. PRE-OXYGENATE with 100% oxygen for 30–60 seconds before suctioning, and allow recovery between passes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 60 seconds or more each time — 60 seconds or more is far too long.
• 2 minutes for every pass — 2 minutes is dangerous.
• As long as secretions appear — Suctioning "as long as secretions appear" causes serious hypoxia.

⭐ Key point: Apply suction only while withdrawing the catheter, never while inserting it.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q155$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 808, $s$Suction pressure for adults is usually:$s$, $s$["300–400 mmHg", "100–150 mmHg", "500 mmHg", "20–30 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 100–150 mmHg
For adults, suction pressure is usually 100–150 mmHg (about 13–20 kPa). This is strong enough to remove secretions without damaging the delicate airway lining. Higher pressures cause mucosal trauma, bleeding and lung collapse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 300–400 mmHg — 300–400 mmHg is far too high and causes damage.
• 500 mmHg — 500 mmHg is dangerously high.
• 20–30 mmHg — 20–30 mmHg is too weak to clear secretions.

⭐ Key point: Children use about 80–100 mmHg; neonates use 60–80 mmHg.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q156$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 809, $s$Manual hyperoxygenation before suction is used to:$s$, $s$["Reduce the cardiac output and heart rate", "Increase the CO₂ level in the blood", "Prevent hypoxaemia during suction", "Help to remove the endotracheal tube"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Prevent hypoxaemia during suction
Suctioning removes oxygen-rich air from the lungs along with secretions, causing a drop in oxygen levels. Giving 100% oxygen (by the ventilator or a bag) for 30–60 SECONDS before suction raises the oxygen reserve, PREVENTING HYPOXAEMIA during suction. It is also given after suction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reduce the cardiac output and heart rate — Hyperoxygenation doesn't aim to reduce cardiac output.
• Increase the CO₂ level in the blood — It doesn't increase CO₂.
• Help to remove the endotracheal tube — It doesn't help remove the ETT.

⭐ Key point: Use the ventilator's oxygen boost function, or closed (in-line) suction, which keeps PEEP and oxygen on.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q160$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 810, $s$A critical care nurse while performing a suctioning procedure noticed some signs of hypercapnia in the patient. The MOST appropriate intervention to be taken is:$s$, $s$["Stop and document the findings", "Stop the procedure and inform the seniors", "Hyper oxygenate and intubate if possible", "Reassure the patient"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hyper oxygenate and intubate if possible
Signs of hypercapnia (rising CO₂, flushing, drowsiness, high BP, fast heart rate) during suctioning show the patient isn't ventilating adequately and is becoming hypoxic. The keyed answer is to HYPER-OXYGENATE (give 100% oxygen and ventilate) and secure the airway by INTUBATING if the patient isn't already intubated and can't ventilate adequately.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stop and document the findings — Stopping and documenting alone doesn't treat the patient.
• Stop the procedure and inform the seniors — Stopping and informing seniors is reasonable but doesn't immediately support the patient's breathing.
• Reassure the patient — Reassurance alone doesn't treat hypercapnia.

⭐ Key point: During suction: pre-oxygenate, keep passes to 10–15 seconds, watch SpO₂, heart rate and colour, and stop if the patient deteriorates.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q22$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 811, $s$Cardiopulmonary arrest in an asthmatic patient is classified as which type of intubation indication?$s$, $s$["Contraindicated", "Not an indication at all", "Relative", "Absolute"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Absolute
Cardiac or respiratory ARREST is an ABSOLUTE indication for securing the airway and ventilating. The patient isn't breathing or circulating oxygen, so intubation (or another advanced airway) is needed immediately to give oxygen and support resuscitation. In asthma, very high airway pressures are expected.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Contraindicated — Arrest is not a contraindication; it is an emergency need.
• Not an indication at all — It is clearly an indication.
• Relative — It is not just relative; it is absolute.

⭐ Key point: Other absolute indications: inability to protect the airway (GCS 8 or less), impending airway obstruction, and failure of oxygenation or ventilation despite other support.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q24$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 812, $s$A 16-year old patient with cystic fibrosis is admitted with increased shortness of breath and possible pneumonia. Which nursing activity is most important to include in the patient's care?$s$, $s$["Place the patient in a private room to decrease the risk of further infection.", "Plan activities to allow at least 8 hours of uninterrupted sleep.", "Allow the patient to decide whether she needs aerosolized medications.", "Perform postural drainage and chest physiotherapy every 4 hours."]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Perform postural drainage and chest physiotherapy every 4 hours.
Cystic fibrosis causes THICK, sticky mucus in the airways, which blocks them and causes repeated infections. During an exacerbation with pneumonia, regular POSTURAL DRAINAGE and CHEST PHYSIOTHERAPY (every 4 hours, plus airway clearance techniques) is the most important nursing activity to clear secretions and improve breathing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Place the patient in a private room to decrease the risk of further infection. — A private room is used for infection control (especially to protect other CF patients), but it isn't the MOST important activity here.
• Plan activities to allow at least 8 hours of uninterrupted sleep. — Sleep is important, but clearing secretions comes first.
• Allow the patient to decide whether she needs aerosolized medications. — Nebulised medicines are prescribed and shouldn't be left to the patient to decide.

⭐ Key point: CF patients also need antibiotics, nebulised mucolytics (dornase alfa, hypertonic saline), high-calorie nutrition and pancreatic enzymes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q28$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 813, $s$Vibration, as an airway clearance technique, is applied:$s$, $s$["Only during coughing", "During inspiration only", "During expiration only", "Throughout the whole breathing cycle"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: During expiration only
VIBRATION is a chest physiotherapy technique where the hands are placed on the chest wall and fine, rapid shaking movements are applied during EXPIRATION (breathing out). Vibrating while air is leaving helps loosen secretions and move them towards the larger airways.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only during coughing — It isn't applied only during coughing.
• During inspiration only — It isn't applied during inspiration (it would restrict the breath in).
• Throughout the whole breathing cycle — It isn't applied throughout the whole cycle.

⭐ Key point: Vibration is often combined with deep breaths, postural drainage and coughing or suctioning.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q29$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 814, $s$During a routine respiratory assessment of a mechanically ventilated patient, the nurse notes new asymmetric chest expansion and absent breath sounds on the right, with tracheal deviation to the left. This most urgently suggests:$s$, $s$["Right mainstem intubation", "Mucus plugging of the left mainstem bronchus", "Right-sided tension pneumothorax", "A normal variant"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Right-sided tension pneumothorax
In a ventilated patient, new ABSENT breath sounds on the right, ASYMMETRIC chest expansion, and the trachea DEVIATED to the LEFT (AWAY from the right side) are classic signs of a RIGHT TENSION PNEUMOTHORAX. Positive pressure ventilation makes it worse quickly. It is an emergency needing needle decompression and a chest drain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right mainstem intubation — Right mainstem intubation causes absent breath sounds on the LEFT, and the trachea isn't pushed away.
• Mucus plugging of the left mainstem bronchus — A left bronchus mucus plug would reduce LEFT breath sounds, and may PULL the trachea toward the left.
• A normal variant — These findings aren't normal.

⭐ Key point: Tension pneumothorax in a ventilated patient: high airway pressures, falling SpO₂, falling BP, absent breath sounds, tracheal shift. Decompress immediately.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q30$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 815, $s$A patient requires a low, consistent concentration of oxygen. The most appropriate oxygen delivery device for this purpose is a:$s$, $s$["Simple face mask", "Non-rebreather mask", "Nasal cannula", "Venturi mask"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Venturi mask
A VENTURI mask delivers a PRECISE, CONSISTENT oxygen concentration (24%, 28%, 31%, 35%, 40% or 60%), whatever the patient's breathing pattern. It uses a jet of oxygen to draw in a set amount of room air, providing a high total flow. It is ideal for patients who need a low, accurate FiO₂, such as COPD patients who retain CO₂.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple face mask — A simple face mask gives a variable FiO₂ (40–60%) that changes with breathing.
• Non-rebreather mask — A non-rebreather gives a HIGH, variable FiO₂ (60–90%).
• Nasal cannula — A nasal cannula gives a variable FiO₂ (24–44%) that depends on breathing.

⭐ Key point: COPD target SpO₂ is 88–92%. Start with a 24% or 28% Venturi mask.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q34$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 816, $s$A history of previous ICU admission or intubation for asthma indicates:$s$, $s$["Asthma has likely resolved", "High risk of a fatal or near-fatal future attack", "The patient no longer needs controller medication", "No increased future risk"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: High risk of a fatal or near-fatal future attack
A history of previous ICU admission or intubation for asthma is one of the strongest PREDICTORS of a future FATAL or NEAR-FATAL asthma attack. These patients have severe, unstable asthma and must be treated as high risk: early aggressive treatment, a low threshold for admission, and an action plan.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Asthma has likely resolved — It doesn't mean asthma has resolved.
• The patient no longer needs controller medication — They need controller medication, not less.
• No increased future risk — They have an increased, not normal, future risk.

⭐ Key point: Other risk factors for fatal asthma: frequent admissions, heavy use of reliever inhalers, poor adherence, and psychosocial problems.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q36$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 817, $s$Both peak and plateau pressures rise together in a ventilated patient. This points to:$s$, $s$["Thick secretions inside the endotracheal tube only", "Reduced compliance, e.g. pneumothorax or endobronchial intubation", "A large leak in the ventilator circuit tubing", "Increased airway resistance from a bitten tube"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reduced compliance, e.g. pneumothorax or endobronchial intubation
If BOTH peak and plateau pressures rise together, the problem is in the LUNGS or chest wall: REDUCED COMPLIANCE (stiffer lungs). Causes: pneumothorax, endobronchial (right main bronchus) intubation, pulmonary oedema, ARDS, atelectasis, abdominal distension or a stiff chest wall.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Thick secretions inside the endotracheal tube only — Secretions inside the tube raise PEAK only (resistance).
• A large leak in the ventilator circuit tubing — A circuit leak LOWERS pressures.
• Increased airway resistance from a bitten tube — A bitten tube raises PEAK only (resistance).

⭐ Key point: High peak and high plateau → examine the chest, check tube position, consider pneumothorax, and get a chest X-ray.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q39$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 818, $s$Nasal airway is not indicated in one of the following$s$, $s$["Pediatric patient", "Comatose patient", "Full conscious patient", "Basal skull fracture"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Basal skull fracture
A NASAL (nasopharyngeal) airway is NOT used in suspected BASAL SKULL FRACTURE (signs: raccoon eyes, Battle's sign, CSF leak from the nose or ears). The fracture can break the bone between the nose and brain (cribriform plate), and the tube could pass INTO the skull and brain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pediatric patient — Children can receive nasal airways (with the correct size).
• Comatose patient — Comatose patients can use nasal airways (they're often tolerated better than oral airways).
• Full conscious patient — Fully conscious patients can tolerate a nasal airway better than an oral airway, if they need one.

⭐ Key point: Nasal airway size: from the nostril to the earlobe (tragus). Lubricate it, and insert it along the floor of the nose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q143$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 819, $s$If manual bagging of a patient with a suspected blocked ETT meets high resistance and chest rise is absent despite confirmed tube position, the appropriate next step is:$s$, $s$["Administer a bronchodilator via metered-dose inhaler through the tube", "Wait for the physician without further action", "Increase bagging force and continue indefinitely", "Prepare to remove/change the endotracheal tube emergently"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Prepare to remove/change the endotracheal tube emergently
If bag ventilation meets HIGH RESISTANCE and the chest DOESN'T RISE even though the tube is confirmed to be in the right place, the ETT is likely BLOCKED (thick secretions, blood clot, kink). If suction can't clear it, PREPARE TO REMOVE or CHANGE the ETT urgently, and oxygenate by bag-mask until a new tube is placed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer a bronchodilator via metered-dose inhaler through the tube — A bronchodilator won't help if the tube itself is blocked.
• Wait for the physician without further action — Waiting without action allows life-threatening hypoxia.
• Increase bagging force and continue indefinitely — Bagging harder can cause barotrauma and won't clear a blocked tube.

⭐ Key point: If the patient can't be ventilated, remove the tube and use bag-mask ventilation. A patent airway saves lives, not the tube itself.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q146$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 820, $s$On a chest X-ray, the tip of an adult endotracheal tube should lie about:$s$, $s$["At the level of the vocal cords", "In the right main bronchus", "Below the carina in the left bronchus", "3–5 cm above the carina"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3–5 cm above the carina
On a chest X-ray, the tip of an adult ETT should lie about 3–5 cm ABOVE the CARINA (where the trachea divides into the two main bronchi), roughly at the level of the medial ends of the clavicles. This allows ventilation of both lungs and room for the tube to move a little with head movement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• At the level of the vocal cords — At the vocal cords, the tube is too high and can slip out.
• In the right main bronchus — In the right main bronchus, only the right lung is ventilated.
• Below the carina in the left bronchus — In the left bronchus, only the left lung is ventilated.

⭐ Key point: A tube that is too deep usually goes into the RIGHT main bronchus (it is shorter, wider and more vertical).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q200$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 821, $s$A patient is experiencing respiratory failure. Arterial Blood Gas (ABG) results show a PaO₂ of 55 mmHg. Which type of respiratory failure is this most likely to be?$s$, $s$["Mixed Respiratory Failure", "There is not enough information to determine the type", "Type II (Hypercapnic) Respiratory Failure", "Type I (Hypoxemic) Respiratory Failure"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Type I (Hypoxemic) Respiratory Failure
TYPE I (hypoxaemic) respiratory failure means a LOW PaO₂ (below 60 mmHg or 8 kPa) with a NORMAL or LOW PaCO₂. It is caused by problems with oxygen transfer: pneumonia, pulmonary oedema, ARDS, PE. A PaO₂ of 55 mmHg is hypoxaemic, and with no raised CO₂ mentioned, this is most likely type I.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mixed Respiratory Failure — Mixed failure needs both low O₂ and a raised CO₂ in a specific context.
• There is not enough information to determine the type — Strictly, the PaCO₂ is needed to be certain, but the question looks for the type based on low PaO₂.
• Type II (Hypercapnic) Respiratory Failure — Type II requires a HIGH PaCO₂ (above 45–50 mmHg).

⭐ Key point: Type I = oxygenation failure. Type II = ventilation failure (high CO₂). Always check PaCO₂.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q22$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 822, $s$Decreasing level of consciousness in a severe asthmatic patient is classified as:$s$, $s$["A relative indication for intubation", "A sign of improvement", "Not related to asthma severity", "An absolute indication for intubation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An absolute indication for intubation
In severe asthma, a FALLING level of consciousness means the patient is exhausted, with rising CO₂ and hypoxia, and can no longer protect the airway or keep breathing. It is an ABSOLUTE indication for intubation and ventilation, and a sign of imminent respiratory arrest.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A relative indication for intubation — It isn't relative; it demands immediate action.
• A sign of improvement — It's a sign of deterioration, not improvement.
• Not related to asthma severity — It IS related to asthma severity (a life-threatening feature).

⭐ Key point: Other life-threatening asthma signs: silent chest, cyanosis, exhaustion, bradycardia, hypotension, PEF below 33%, SpO₂ below 92%, and a normal or rising PaCO₂.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q23$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 823, $s$A 7-year-old client is brought to the E.R. He’s tachypneic and afebrile and has a respiratory rate of 36 breaths/minute and a nonproductive cough. He recently had a cold. From his history, the client may have which of the following?$s$, $s$["Acute asthma", "Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD)", "Emphysema", "Bronchial pneumonia"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute asthma
A 7-year-old with fast breathing (RR 36), NO fever, a dry (non-productive) cough, and a recent cold (a viral trigger) most likely has ACUTE ASTHMA. Viral infections are common triggers. Listen for wheeze and check for a history of atopy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chronic obstructive pulmonary disease (COPD) — COPD occurs in adults, usually smokers.
• Emphysema — Emphysema is an adult disease (part of COPD).
• Bronchial pneumonia — Bronchial pneumonia usually causes FEVER and a productive cough.

⭐ Key point: Treat acute asthma in children: salbutamol (spacer or nebuliser), oral prednisolone, oxygen if SpO₂ is below 94%, and escalate if severe.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q26$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 824, $s$A patient with respiratory failure has a PaO₂ of 45 mmHg despite receiving oxygen via a non-rebreather mask. The nurse anticipates that the next step in the patient's care will be:$s$, $s$["Weaning the patient from oxygen", "Intubation and mechanical ventilation", "Transferring the patient to a regular medical floor", "Increasing the oxygen flow rate to 15 L/min"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intubation and mechanical ventilation
A PaO₂ of 45 mmHg while on a non-rebreather mask (the highest-concentration oxygen without ventilation) shows SEVERE respiratory failure that isn't responding to oxygen alone. The next step is INTUBATION and MECHANICAL VENTILATION (or NIV in selected cases) to support oxygenation and breathing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Weaning the patient from oxygen — Weaning from oxygen is the opposite of what is needed.
• Transferring the patient to a regular medical floor — Transfer to a regular ward is unsafe.
• Increasing the oxygen flow rate to 15 L/min — A non-rebreather is already at 15 L/min; more flow won't help much.

⭐ Key point: Signs that oxygen therapy is failing: rising RR, falling SpO₂ despite high FiO₂, exhaustion, confusion, rising CO₂.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q30$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 825, $s$Which route of intubation is preferable in children undergoing intensive care and requiring prolonged intubation?$s$, $s$["Laryngotracheal intubation", "Nasotracheal intubation", "Tracheostomy", "Orotracheal intubation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Nasotracheal intubation
For children who need PROLONGED intubation in intensive care, NASOTRACHEAL intubation is traditionally preferred. The tube is held more firmly by the nose, moves less with mouth and head movement, is better tolerated (less gagging and biting), allows easier mouth care, and reduces the risk of accidental extubation. (The old answer key said "tracheostomy"; a tracheostomy is a surgical airway, not a route of intubation, so the key has been corrected to nasotracheal.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Laryngotracheal intubation — Laryngotracheal intubation isn't a standard route category.
• Tracheostomy — Tracheostomy is a surgical airway considered for VERY prolonged ventilation, not a route of intubation.
• Orotracheal intubation — Orotracheal intubation is used for emergencies but is less stable for long-term use in children.

⭐ Key point: Avoid nasal routes in suspected basal skull fracture and coagulopathy (risk of nosebleeds).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q33 · answer key corrected$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 826, $s$Patient was wheeled into ICU from A&E unit, during assessment percussing the chest bilaterally provided hyper-resonance or dullness, this is an evidence for internal injuries such as:$s$, $s$["Atelectasis", "Asthma", "Haematoma", "Pneumothorax"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pneumothorax
Percussing the chest gives clues about what's inside. HYPER-RESONANCE (a hollow, drum-like sound) means extra air, as in PNEUMOTHORAX. DULLNESS means fluid or solid tissue, as in haemothorax, effusion or consolidation. After trauma, hyper-resonance with reduced breath sounds strongly suggests pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Atelectasis — Atelectasis causes dullness, not hyper-resonance.
• Asthma — Asthma may cause slight hyper-resonance but isn't an internal injury.
• Haematoma — A haematoma causes dullness, but the question's hyper-resonance points to pneumothorax.

⭐ Key point: After chest trauma: hyper-resonance = pneumothorax; stony dullness = haemothorax or effusion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q34$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 827, $s$The critical care nurse while managing patient's airways knows that, the most appropriate head position to prevent falling of tongue thus preventing obstruction of the upper airway is:$s$, $s$["Sniffing position", "Right lateral position", "Dorsal position", "Left lateral position"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sniffing position
The SNIFFING position (the neck slightly bent forward and the head tilted back, like sniffing the morning air) lines up the mouth, pharynx and larynx, and lifts the tongue away from the back of the throat, opening the upper airway. It is used for airway management and intubation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right lateral position — The right lateral position is a recovery position option, but not the classic head position for airway alignment.
• Dorsal position — The dorsal (supine) position alone lets the tongue fall back.
• Left lateral position — The left lateral position is a recovery position, used to drain secretions.

⭐ Key point: For an unconscious patient who is breathing, the recovery (lateral) position protects the airway. For airway opening, use head tilt–chin lift or jaw thrust.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q35$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 828, $s$A burns patient from a house fire has soot in the mouth, a hoarse voice and singed nasal hair. The nurse should prepare for:$s$, $s$["Early intubation before airway oedema develops", "Delaying airway care until fluids are given", "Discharge after 4 hours of observation", "Nasal cannula oxygen at 1 L/min only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Early intubation before airway oedema develops
Soot in the mouth, a hoarse voice and singed nasal hair after a house fire suggest INHALATION INJURY. Heat and smoke cause rapid SWELLING of the upper airway, which can block it within hours, especially as fluids are given. EARLY INTUBATION (before oedema develops) by an experienced operator secures the airway safely.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delaying airway care until fluids are given — Delaying airway care risks complete obstruction.
• Discharge after 4 hours of observation — Early discharge is dangerous; swelling progresses over 24 hours.
• Nasal cannula oxygen at 1 L/min only — Low-flow oxygen is inadequate; give 100% oxygen (CO poisoning) and secure the airway.

⭐ Key point: Other signs of airway burn: stridor, wheeze, carbonaceous sputum, facial burns, and burns in an enclosed space.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q120$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 829, $s$Ketamine is useful for induction in a shocked asthmatic patient because it:$s$, $s$["Has no effect on airway reflexes", "Maintains blood pressure and causes bronchodilation", "Lowers BP and causes bronchospasm", "Reverses opioid-induced breathing problems"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Maintains blood pressure and causes bronchodilation
KETAMINE is an NMDA receptor antagonist. It MAINTAINS BLOOD PRESSURE (it stimulates the sympathetic system, making it useful in shocked patients) and causes BRONCHODILATION (useful in asthma). It also preserves airway reflexes better than other induction agents.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Has no effect on airway reflexes — It largely PRESERVES airway reflexes, so the statement "no effect" is wrong.
• Lowers BP and causes bronchospasm — It RAISES BP and causes bronchodilation, not the opposite.
• Reverses opioid-induced breathing problems — It doesn't reverse opioids.

⭐ Key point: Ketamine can cause emergence phenomena (hallucinations, agitation) in adults; benzodiazepines can reduce them. It increases secretions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q160$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 830, $s$Needle decompression for tension pneumothorax is performed at the:$s$, $s$["2nd ICS posterior axillary line", "2nd ICS midclavicular line", "5th ICS anterior axillary line", "Xiphoid process"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2nd ICS midclavicular line
Needle decompression for TENSION PNEUMOTHORAX is classically done at the 2nd INTERCOSTAL SPACE in the MID-CLAVICULAR line (just above the 3rd rib), using a large-bore cannula. Current ATLS guidance prefers the 4th–5th intercostal space in the mid-axillary line in adults (thinner chest wall). Either way, a chest drain must follow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2nd ICS posterior axillary line — The 2nd ICS posterior axillary line isn't the standard site.
• 5th ICS anterior axillary line — The 5th ICS ANTERIOR axillary line is near the newer site, but the classic keyed answer is the 2nd ICS mid-clavicular line.
• Xiphoid process — The xiphoid process is the site for pericardiocentesis.

⭐ Key point: Tension pneumothorax is a clinical diagnosis. Decompress immediately; don't wait for an X-ray.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q24$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 831, $s$Which ventilator practice should be avoided in a hemodynamically unstable patient with status asthmaticus, due to risk of breath stacking?$s$, $s$["Low tidal volume ventilation", "A permissive hypercapnia strategy", "Adequate sedation to reduce patient-ventilator dyssynchrony", "High respiratory rates with short expiratory time"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: High respiratory rates with short expiratory time
In status asthmaticus, the narrowed airways need a LONG time to breathe out. HIGH respiratory rates with SHORT expiratory times don't allow full exhalation, so air builds up with each breath (BREATH STACKING, auto-PEEP). This raises intrathoracic pressure, reduces venous return, and can cause hypotension, barotrauma and cardiac arrest.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low tidal volume ventilation — Low tidal volume ventilation is RECOMMENDED in asthma.
• A permissive hypercapnia strategy — Permissive hypercapnia is RECOMMENDED (accepting high CO₂ to avoid over-inflation).
• Adequate sedation to reduce patient-ventilator dyssynchrony — Adequate sedation is RECOMMENDED to reduce dyssynchrony.

⭐ Key point: If a ventilated asthmatic suddenly becomes hypotensive, disconnect them from the ventilator briefly to let trapped air escape.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q25$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 832, $s$How many bronchopulmonary segments does the right lung have?$s$, $s$["12", "8", "6", "10"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10
The RIGHT lung has 3 lobes and 10 bronchopulmonary segments: upper lobe 3, middle lobe 2, lower lobe 5. The LEFT lung has 2 lobes and usually 8–10 segments (some segments are combined).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 12 — 12 is too many.
• 8 — 8 is the number often given for the left lung.
• 6 — 6 is too few.

⭐ Key point: Each segment has its own bronchus and artery, so it can be drained (postural drainage) or surgically removed separately.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q27$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 833, $s$A 40-year-old woman is involved in an equestrian accident. She presents with chest pain, shortness of breath, and difficulty breathing. Her arterial oxygen saturation is 85% and her respiratory rate is 40b/minute. A chest tube is placed and the nurse observes a return of 300 mls blood into the system. What is the nurse's next line of action?$s$, $s$["Explain to the patient what is going on to alley anxiety.", "Place the patient on oxygen.", "Clamp the tube and notify surgeon immediately.", "Call for a chest x-ray."]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Place the patient on oxygen.
This patient is HYPOXIC (SpO₂ 85%) and breathing fast (RR 40) after chest trauma. Following ABCDE, the immediate priority is to give high-flow OXYGEN. A first drainage of 300 mL of blood is NOT massive (massive haemothorax is 1500 mL or more at insertion, or more than 200 mL/hour for 2–4 hours), and chest drains should NOT be clamped (clamping traps blood and air). Continue to monitor drainage and inform the surgeon. (The old answer key said "clamp the tube", which is unsafe; it has been corrected.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Explain to the patient what is going on to alley anxiety. — Explanation reduces anxiety but doesn't treat hypoxia.
• Clamp the tube and notify surgeon immediately. — Clamping the tube is unsafe, and 300 mL isn't massive bleeding.
• Call for a chest x-ray. — A chest X-ray is useful later, but oxygen comes first.

⭐ Key point: Massive haemothorax: 1500 mL or more on insertion, or ongoing more than 200 mL/hour. This needs blood transfusion and surgery (thoracotomy).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q28 · answer key corrected$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 834, $s$A minimum respiratory rate of what value should be maintained during MgSO4 therapy?$s$, $s$[">12/min", ">6/min", "No specific value is required", ">30/min"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: >12/min
During magnesium sulphate therapy, the RESPIRATORY RATE must stay ABOVE 12 breaths per minute. A rate below 12 suggests magnesium toxicity depressing breathing. Stop the infusion, give oxygen, check reflexes and magnesium level, and give calcium gluconate if needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• >6/min — Above 6/min is too low a threshold; toxicity would already be dangerous.
• No specific value is required — A specific value IS required.
• >30/min — Above 30/min isn't the safety threshold.

⭐ Key point: Also check hourly: knee reflexes present and urine output above 30 mL/hour.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q29$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 835, $s$A nurse is assessing a patient with respiratory failure. The nurse notes that the patient's respiratory rate is 35 breaths per minute. This is an example of:$s$, $s$["Tachypnea", "Eupnea", "Bradypnea", "Apnea"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tachypnea
TACHYPNOEA means an abnormally FAST breathing rate, usually above 20 breaths per minute in adults. A rate of 35 is tachypnoea, a sign of respiratory distress (and often the earliest sign of deterioration).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Eupnea — Eupnoea is NORMAL breathing (12–20/min).
• Bradypnea — Bradypnoea is SLOW breathing (below 12/min).
• Apnea — Apnoea is NO breathing.

⭐ Key point: Respiratory rate is one of the most sensitive early warning signs of deterioration. Always count it for a full minute.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q32$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 836, $s$During insertion of a subclavian central line for TPN, the patient suddenly develops sharp chest pain, dyspnea, and decreased breath sounds on the affected side. The nurse should suspect:$s$, $s$["Catheter occlusion", "Pneumothorax", "Hyperglycemia", "Air embolism"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pneumothorax
During SUBCLAVIAN (or jugular) central line insertion, the needle can puncture the top of the lung, causing a PNEUMOTHORAX. Sudden sharp chest pain, breathlessness and reduced breath sounds on the same side are classic signs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Catheter occlusion — Catheter occlusion stops the line working; it doesn't cause chest pain and breathlessness.
• Hyperglycemia — Hyperglycaemia doesn't cause these sudden symptoms.
• Air embolism — Air embolism can cause sudden breathlessness and chest pain, but reduced breath sounds on one side point to pneumothorax.

⭐ Key point: Always get a chest X-ray after subclavian or jugular line insertion. Ultrasound guidance reduces this risk.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q34$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 837, $s$A patient is receiving oxygen via a non-rebreather mask. The nurse notices the reservoir bag is completely deflating with each inspiration. The most appropriate nursing action is to:$s$, $s$["Clamp the oxygen tubing", "Replace the mask with a simple face mask", "Loosen the mask to allow more room air to enter", "Increase the oxygen flow rate to the mask"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increase the oxygen flow rate to the mask
The reservoir bag of a non-rebreather mask should stay at least partly INFLATED throughout breathing (about one-third to one-half full on inspiration). If it COLLAPSES completely with each breath in, the patient isn't getting enough oxygen. INCREASE the OXYGEN FLOW (usually to 15 L/min) so the bag stays inflated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Clamp the oxygen tubing — Clamping the tubing would stop the oxygen.
• Replace the mask with a simple face mask — A simple face mask gives LESS oxygen.
• Loosen the mask to allow more room air to enter — Loosening the mask lets in room air, lowering the oxygen concentration.

⭐ Key point: Fill the reservoir bag before putting the mask on the patient, and use 10–15 L/min.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q35$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 838, $s$A patient requires mechanical ventilation for airway protection due to a decreased level of consciousness (GCS 6) after a head injury. This is an example of which broad indication for ventilation?$s$, $s$["Primary ventilatory (CO2) failure", "Inability to protect the airway", "Primary oxygenation failure", "Post-anesthesia recovery only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inability to protect the airway
A GCS of 6 means the patient can't protect their airway: the gag and cough reflexes are weak, and the tongue may block the throat. Intubation and ventilation are needed to PROTECT THE AIRWAY from obstruction and aspiration, even if oxygenation and CO₂ are initially normal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Primary ventilatory (CO2) failure — Primary ventilatory failure is when CO₂ rises because of poor breathing.
• Primary oxygenation failure — Primary oxygenation failure is when O₂ is low despite oxygen.
• Post-anesthesia recovery only — This isn't post-anaesthesia recovery.

⭐ Key point: Indications for intubation: can't protect the airway, can't oxygenate, can't ventilate, or the expected clinical course (such as transfer or worsening).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q36$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 839, $s$A ventilated patient suddenly desaturates. The 'DOPE' mnemonic for the cause stands for:$s$, $s$["Disconnection, Oxygen, PEEP, Exhaustion", "Drugs, Oxygen, Pressure, Electrolytes", "Dehydration, Oedema, Pneumonia, Embolism", "Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment failure"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment failure
DOPE helps troubleshoot sudden desaturation in a ventilated patient. D = DISPLACEMENT of the tube (into the oesophagus, a main bronchus or out). O = OBSTRUCTION (secretions, kink, biting). P = PNEUMOTHORAX (especially tension). E = EQUIPMENT failure (ventilator, oxygen supply, circuit).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Disconnection, Oxygen, PEEP, Exhaustion — Disconnection, Oxygen, PEEP, Exhaustion isn't DOPE.
• Drugs, Oxygen, Pressure, Electrolytes — Drugs, Oxygen, Pressure, Electrolytes isn't DOPE.
• Dehydration, Oedema, Pneumonia, Embolism — Dehydration, Oedema, Pneumonia, Embolism isn't DOPE.

⭐ Key point: First step: disconnect from the ventilator and hand-ventilate with 100% oxygen while checking DOPE.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q38 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 840, $s$A nurse is caring for an unconscious patient. Which of the following is a priority nursing intervention to prevent complications?$s$, $s$["Performing frequent oral care and airway suctioning", "Leaving the patient in the same position for extended periods", "Administering oral fluids every hour", "Encouraging the patient to cough and deep breathe"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Performing frequent oral care and airway suctioning
In an unconscious patient, the AIRWAY is the priority. They can't clear secretions or protect against aspiration. Frequent ORAL CARE (reduces bacteria and VAP) and AIRWAY SUCTIONING (removes secretions) prevent aspiration pneumonia and airway obstruction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Leaving the patient in the same position for extended periods — Leaving the patient in one position causes pressure ulcers and lung collapse.
• Administering oral fluids every hour — Oral fluids in an unconscious patient cause aspiration.
• Encouraging the patient to cough and deep breathe — An unconscious patient can't cough and deep breathe on request.

⭐ Key point: Unconscious patient care: airway, breathing, circulation, eyes, mouth, skin, nutrition, elimination, mobility and family support.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q71$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B1 · Airway, Oxygen Therapy & Respiratory Care$s$, false, 841, $s$For a 4-year-old, the uncuffed endotracheal tube size (age ÷ 4 + 4) is:$s$, $s$["3.0 mm internal diameter", "8.0 mm internal diameter", "7.5 mm internal diameter", "5.0 mm internal diameter"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 5.0 mm internal diameter
Formula for uncuffed ETT size (internal diameter) in children over 1 year: age ÷ 4 + 4. For a 4-year-old: 4 ÷ 4 + 4 = 1 + 4 = 5.0 mm. (For cuffed tubes: age ÷ 4 + 3.5 = 4.5 mm.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 3.0 mm internal diameter — 3.0 mm is the size for a newborn.
• 8.0 mm internal diameter — 8.0 mm is an adult male size.
• 7.5 mm internal diameter — 7.5 mm is an adult size.

⭐ Key point: Oral tube length is about age ÷ 2 + 12 cm (14 cm for a 4-year-old). Keep half a size above and below ready.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q136$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 842, $s$A patient is on a ventilator for respiratory failure. The nurse knows that a high pressure alarm on the ventilator may indicate:$s$, $s$["A leak in the circuit", "The patient is disconnected from the ventilator", "A kinked endotracheal tube or the patient is coughing", "The patient's lung has re-expanded"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A kinked endotracheal tube or the patient is coughing
The high-pressure alarm sounds when the pressure needed to deliver the breath rises above the set limit. Something is blocking or resisting airflow: a kinked or bitten ETT, secretions (needs suction), coughing, the patient fighting the ventilator, bronchospasm, a pneumothorax, or water in the tubing. Check the patient first, then the tube, then the circuit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A leak in the circuit — A leak in the circuit causes a LOW-pressure alarm (pressure can't build up).
• The patient is disconnected from the ventilator — Disconnection causes a LOW-pressure or low-volume alarm.
• The patient's lung has re-expanded — Re-expansion of the lung would improve compliance and lower pressure, not trigger a high-pressure alarm.

⭐ Key point: HIGH pressure = something blocking (kink, secretions, cough, biting, bronchospasm, pneumothorax). LOW pressure = something leaking (disconnection, cuff leak, circuit leak).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q101$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 843, $s$If a ventilated patient acutely desaturates and the cause is not immediately identified, an appropriate first-line nursing action while troubleshooting is to:$s$, $s$["Leave the patient on the ventilator and wait", "Disconnect from the ventilator and manually ventilate with a bag-valve device on 100% oxygen while assessing for the cause", "Immediately extubate", "Turn off all alarms to focus"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Disconnect from the ventilator and manually ventilate with a bag-valve device on 100% oxygen while assessing for the cause
When a ventilated patient suddenly desaturates and you don't know why, take the ventilator out of the problem. DISCONNECT the patient and hand-ventilate with a bag-valve device on 100% oxygen. This checks whether the problem is the ventilator or the patient. Squeezing the bag also tells you about the airway: a hard bag suggests blockage or pneumothorax. Then work through DOPE and call for help.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Leave the patient on the ventilator and wait — Waiting while the patient's oxygen falls can lead to cardiac arrest.
• Immediately extubate — Extubating without a clear reason may remove a working airway and make things worse.
• Turn off all alarms to focus — Alarms are important information; turning them off is unsafe.

⭐ Key point: Keep a bag-valve-mask connected to oxygen at EVERY ventilated patient's bedside, always checked and ready.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q105$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 844, $s$Intrinsic PEEP (PEEPi/auto-PEEP) develops in life-threatening asthma because:$s$, $s$["Air cannot fully leave the lungs before the next breath", "The ventilator is set incorrectly in all cases", "The patient is over-oxygenated", "It only occurs in healthy lungs"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Air cannot fully leave the lungs before the next breath
In severe (life-threatening) ASTHMA, the airways are narrowed by bronchospasm, swelling and mucus plugs. Breathing OUT is slow because narrow airways close early during expiration. If the next breath starts before the lungs have fully emptied, air is TRAPPED and builds up with each breath — this is DYNAMIC HYPERINFLATION, and the pressure left in the alveoli at the end of expiration is INTRINSIC PEEP (auto-PEEP). It raises intrathoracic pressure, reduces venous return (HYPOTENSION, even cardiac arrest — PEA), increases work of breathing, and causes BAROTRAUMA/pneumothorax. Management on the ventilator: LOW respiratory rate, LONG expiratory time (I:E 1:4 or 1:5), smaller tidal volumes, treat bronchospasm; if the patient suddenly becomes hypotensive, briefly DISCONNECT the ventilator to let trapped air out.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The ventilator is set incorrectly in all cases — Ventilator setting can make auto-PEEP worse, but it is caused mainly by the disease (airflow obstruction), not "incorrect settings in all cases".
• The patient is over-oxygenated — Over-oxygenation does not cause air trapping.
• It only occurs in healthy lungs — Auto-PEEP happens in OBSTRUCTED lungs (asthma, COPD), not in healthy lungs.

⭐ Key point: Auto-PEEP = air cannot fully leave the lungs before the next breath → air trapping. Treat with low rate, long expiration.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q107$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 845, $s$A normal I:E ratio for a patient without obstructive lung disease is approximately:$s$, $s$["2:1", "1:4", "1:2", "4:1"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1:2
The I:E RATIO compares the time spent breathing IN (inspiration) with the time spent breathing OUT (expiration). In normal breathing, expiration is passive and takes longer, so a normal I:E ratio is about 1:2 (range 1:1.5 to 1:3). On the ventilator 1:2 is the usual starting setting. In OBSTRUCTIVE disease (asthma, COPD), a LONGER expiratory time (1:3 to 1:5) is used to let trapped air out and prevent auto-PEEP. INVERSE ratio ventilation (e.g. 2:1) is sometimes used in severe ARDS to improve oxygenation, but it is uncomfortable and needs heavy sedation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2:1 — 2:1 is INVERSE ratio — inspiration longer than expiration; not normal.
• 1:4 — 1:4 is the long expiratory time used in obstructive disease, longer than normal.
• 4:1 — 4:1 is extreme inverse ratio, not normal.

⭐ Key point: Normal I:E = 1:2. Obstructive disease: 1:3–1:5. Inverse ratio (2:1) only in severe ARDS.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q113$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 846, $s$To assess for "E" (equipment failure) in the DOPE mnemonic, the nurse should:$s$, $s$["Increase the ventilator's tidal volume setting", "Assume the ETT is dislodged", "Order a stat chest x-ray before any other action", "Disconnect the patient from the ventilator and manually ventilate with a bag-valve device connected to 100% oxygen"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Disconnect the patient from the ventilator and manually ventilate with a bag-valve device connected to 100% oxygen
In the DOPE mnemonic, "E" = EQUIPMENT FAILURE (ventilator malfunction, circuit disconnection, oxygen supply failure). To assess, DISCONNECT the patient from the ventilator and MANUALLY VENTILATE with a bag-valve device connected to 100% OXYGEN. If the patient improves, the ventilator or circuit is the problem.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the ventilator's tidal volume setting — Increasing tidal volume doesn't assess equipment.
• Assume the ETT is dislodged — Assuming the tube is dislodged skips assessment.
• Order a stat chest x-ray before any other action — An X-ray delays urgent action.

⭐ Key point: Always keep a bag-valve-mask and oxygen at every ventilated patient's bedside.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q101$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 847, $s$Which mode combines a set mandatory volume-targeted breath with a decelerating pressure waveform, aiming to guarantee tidal volume while limiting peak pressure?$s$, $s$["Standard SIMV", "CPAP", "Pressure-regulated volume control (PRVC)", "High-frequency jet ventilation"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pressure-regulated volume control (PRVC)
PRESSURE-REGULATED VOLUME CONTROL (PRVC) is a "dual" mode: the clinician sets a TARGET TIDAL VOLUME, and the ventilator delivers each breath with a DECELERATING (pressure-control) flow pattern, automatically adjusting the inspiratory PRESSURE breath by breath to achieve the target volume at the LOWEST possible pressure. This aims to GUARANTEE TIDAL VOLUME while LIMITING PEAK PRESSURE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Standard SIMV — Standard SIMV doesn't automatically adjust pressure.
• CPAP — CPAP gives no mandatory breaths.
• High-frequency jet ventilation — High-frequency jet ventilation uses very small, rapid breaths.

⭐ Key point: In PRVC, watch for rising pressures (worsening compliance) and patient effort affecting volumes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q110$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 848, $s$During a spontaneous breathing trial a patient breathes at 16/min with a tidal volume of 500 mL. What is the rapid shallow breathing index (RSBI)?$s$, $s$["8 — likely to fail weaning", "32 — likely to wean successfully", "32 — likely to fail weaning", "31 — likely to wean successfully"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 32 — likely to wean successfully
RSBI = RESPIRATORY RATE ÷ TIDAL VOLUME (in LITRES) = 16 ÷ 0.5 = 32. An RSBI BELOW 105 predicts the patient is LIKELY TO WEAN SUCCESSFULLY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 8 — likely to fail weaning — 8 is a calculation error (16 ÷ 2).
• 32 — likely to fail weaning — 32 is correct, but it predicts SUCCESS, not failure.
• 31 — likely to wean successfully — 31 is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: RSBI above 105 = fast, shallow breathing = likely failure. Assess the whole patient, not just RSBI.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q99 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 849, $s$A female patient with ARDS is 170 cm tall and weighs 87 kg. Using lung-protective ventilation at 6 mL/kg predicted body weight, what tidal volume should be set?$s$, $s$["About 369 mL", "About 522 mL", "About 492 mL", "About 615 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: About 369 mL
FEMALE PBW = 45.5 + 0.91 × (height in cm − 152.4) = 45.5 + 0.91 × (170 − 152.4) = 45.5 + 0.91 × 17.6 = 45.5 + 16.0 = 61.5 kg. Tidal volume at 6 mL/kg = 6 × 61.5 = 369 mL. Her actual weight (87 kg) is NOT used, because lung size depends on height and sex, not body fat.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• About 522 mL — 522 mL is 6 mL/kg of her ACTUAL weight — too high.
• About 492 mL — 492 mL is 8 mL/kg PBW.
• About 615 mL — 615 mL is 10 mL/kg PBW.

⭐ Key point: Using actual weight in obese patients causes over-inflation and lung injury.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q103 · concept asked in 6 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 850, $s$For a hemodynamically unstable ARDS patient requiring precise control of airway pressures to minimize barotrauma risk while sedated, which mode is often preferred initially?$s$, $s$["T-piece trial", "CPAP alone", "Pressure Control Ventilation, or volume control with a plateau pressure limit, with controlled mandatory breaths", "Pressure Support Ventilation with a low backup rate"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pressure Control Ventilation, or volume control with a plateau pressure limit, with controlled mandatory breaths
For a HAEMODYNAMICALLY UNSTABLE, SEDATED ARDS patient, CONTROLLED MANDATORY ventilation is preferred initially: PRESSURE CONTROL VENTILATION (which limits peak airway pressure) or VOLUME CONTROL with a PLATEAU PRESSURE LIMIT (≤30 cmH2O). This gives precise control of pressures and volumes, minimising barotrauma, while ensuring adequate ventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• T-piece trial — A T-piece trial is a weaning test, not for unstable patients.
• CPAP alone — CPAP alone gives no breaths — inadequate for a sedated patient.
• Pressure Support Ventilation with a low backup rate — Pressure support requires a reliable respiratory drive; a low backup rate is unsafe in an unstable, sedated patient.

⭐ Key point: In pressure control, monitor exhaled tidal volume closely — it changes with compliance.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q104$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 851, $s$What is the recommended action if a ventilated patient's exhaled tidal volume suddenly drops significantly with a normal peak pressure and an audible air leak at the mouth?$s$, $s$["Increase sedation", "Suspect cuff leak or partial tube displacement and assess cuff pressure and tube position immediately", "Increase PEEP", "Assume mucus plug and suction aggressively"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Suspect cuff leak or partial tube displacement and assess cuff pressure and tube position immediately
A SUDDEN DROP in EXHALED TIDAL VOLUME with a NORMAL (or low) PEAK PRESSURE and an AUDIBLE AIR LEAK at the mouth means gas is escaping around the tube: a CUFF LEAK (under-inflated or damaged cuff) or PARTIAL TUBE DISPLACEMENT (tube pulled up so the cuff is at or above the vocal cords). Immediately check CUFF PRESSURE (20–30 cmH2O) and TUBE POSITION (marking at the teeth/lips, chest movement, breath sounds, capnography).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase sedation — Increasing sedation doesn't fix a leak.
• Increase PEEP — Increasing PEEP doesn't fix a leak.
• Assume mucus plug and suction aggressively — A mucus plug causes HIGH pressures, not a leak.

⭐ Key point: If the tube has slipped, call for help — re-positioning or re-intubation may be needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q105$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 852, $s$The DOPE mnemonic used to rapidly assess a deteriorating ventilated patient stands for:$s$, $s$["Distress, Obstruction, Pain, Emergency", "Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment failure", "Dislodgement, Oxygen, Pressure, Extubation", "Displacement, Overventilation, Pneumonia, Edema"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment failure
The DOPE mnemonic helps rapidly find the cause of deterioration in a ventilated patient: D = DISPLACEMENT of the tube (extubation, right main bronchus); O = OBSTRUCTION of the tube (secretions, kinking, biting); P = PNEUMOTHORAX (especially tension); E = EQUIPMENT FAILURE (ventilator, circuit, oxygen supply). Some add "S" for Stacked breaths (auto-PEEP).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Distress, Obstruction, Pain, Emergency — "Distress, Obstruction, Pain, Emergency" is incorrect.
• Dislodgement, Oxygen, Pressure, Extubation — "Dislodgement, Oxygen, Pressure, Extubation" is incorrect.
• Displacement, Overventilation, Pneumonia, Edema — "Displacement, Overventilation, Pneumonia, Edema" is incorrect.

⭐ Key point: If in doubt, disconnect from the ventilator and hand-ventilate with 100% oxygen while you work through DOPE.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q112 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 853, $s$A ventilated ARDS patient has a plateau pressure of 28 cmH2O and PEEP of 12 cmH2O. What is the driving pressure?$s$, $s$["16 cmH2O — within the safe target (≤ 15)", "16 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury", "28 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury", "40 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 16 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury
DRIVING PRESSURE = PLATEAU − PEEP = 28 − 12 = 16 cmH2O. This is ABOVE the safe target of 15 cmH2O, so it is TOO HIGH and increases the risk of lung injury. Consider reducing the tidal volume or adjusting PEEP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 16 cmH2O — within the safe target (≤ 15) — 16 is correct, but it is NOT within the safe target.
• 28 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury — 28 is the plateau pressure.
• 40 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury — 40 adds plateau and PEEP.

⭐ Key point: Even when plateau is below 30, a driving pressure above 15 is a warning sign.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q117 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 854, $s$A ventilated patient becomes agitated and is pulling at the endotracheal tube. The nurse's priority assessment is to:$s$, $s$["Immediately extubate the patient", "Assess for a treatable cause of agitation such as hypoxia, pain, or ventilator asynchrony before increasing restraint or sedation alone", "Increase the ventilator rate", "Apply four-point restraints without further assessment"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assess for a treatable cause of agitation such as hypoxia, pain, or ventilator asynchrony before increasing restraint or sedation alone
AGITATION in a ventilated patient may be caused by HYPOXIA, PAIN, VENTILATOR ASYNCHRONY, a full bladder, delirium, or anxiety. The priority is to ASSESS for a TREATABLE CAUSE before increasing restraints or sedation. Ensure the tube is secure and the patient is safe.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediately extubate the patient — Immediate extubation without assessment is unsafe.
• Increase the ventilator rate — Increasing the ventilator rate may worsen asynchrony.
• Apply four-point restraints without further assessment — Restraints without assessment may harm the patient.

⭐ Key point: Use validated tools: CPOT/BPS for pain, RASS for sedation, CAM-ICU for delirium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q126$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 855, $s$A mechanically ventilated patient in status asthmaticus develops sudden hypotension and increased peak airway pressures. The nurse should first suspect:$s$, $s$["Hypoglycemia", "Auto-PEEP causing decreased venous return (breath stacking)", "Ventilator-associated pneumonia", "A normal expected ventilator finding"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Auto-PEEP causing decreased venous return (breath stacking)
In STATUS ASTHMATICUS on a ventilator, the narrowed airways slow exhalation. If each new breath starts before the last one is fully exhaled, air builds up (AUTO-PEEP / BREATH STACKING). This raises pressure inside the chest, which REDUCES VENOUS RETURN to the heart (causing SUDDEN HYPOTENSION) and makes the lungs stiff (INCREASED PEAK PRESSURES). It can also cause barotrauma (pneumothorax). Immediate action: briefly DISCONNECT the ventilator to let the trapped air escape (and gently press on the chest), then reduce the rate and tidal volume and lengthen expiratory time. Also check for tension pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypoglycemia — Hypoglycaemia does not cause high airway pressures.
• Ventilator-associated pneumonia — VAP develops over days, not suddenly with hypotension.
• A normal expected ventilator finding — Sudden hypotension and high pressures are never normal.

⭐ Key point: Prevent with a low rate (10–12), small tidal volume, long expiration (I:E 1:4 or more), aggressive bronchodilators, and permissive hypercapnia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q140$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 856, $s$A ventilated patient has tidal volume 400 mL, plateau pressure 22 cmH2O and PEEP 12 cmH2O. What is the static lung compliance?$s$, $s$["25 mL/cmH2O", "18.2 mL/cmH2O", "40 mL/cmH2O", "11.8 mL/cmH2O"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 40 mL/cmH2O
STATIC COMPLIANCE shows how easily the lungs and chest wall stretch. Formula: COMPLIANCE = TIDAL VOLUME ÷ (PLATEAU PRESSURE − PEEP). Here: 400 mL ÷ (22 − 12) cmH2O = 400 ÷ 10 = 40 mL/cmH2O. The bottom part (22 − 12 = 10) is also the DRIVING PRESSURE, which is within the safe target (≤15). Normal static compliance in a ventilated adult is about 50–100 mL/cmH2O, so 40 shows MILDLY REDUCED compliance (slightly stiff lungs).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 25 mL/cmH2O — 25 mL/cmH2O is a calculation error (400 ÷ 16).
• 18.2 mL/cmH2O — 18.2 mL/cmH2O divides by the plateau pressure alone (400 ÷ 22), forgetting to subtract PEEP.
• 11.8 mL/cmH2O — 11.8 mL/cmH2O divides by plateau plus PEEP (400 ÷ 34) — the wrong formula.

⭐ Key point: Watch compliance trends: a falling value means stiffer lungs (worsening ARDS, oedema, pneumothorax, abdominal distension); a rising value means improvement.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q141 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 857, $s$A ventilator is set at 10 breaths/min with an inspiratory time of 1.2 seconds. What is the I:E ratio?$s$, $s$["1:0.8", "4:1", "1:6", "1:4"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1:4
STEP 1 — Total time for ONE breath: 60 seconds ÷ 10 breaths/min = 6 seconds per breath. STEP 2 — Expiratory time = total time − inspiratory time = 6 − 1.2 = 4.8 seconds. STEP 3 — I:E ratio = 1.2 : 4.8. Divide both by 1.2 to simplify: 1 : 4. A long expiratory time like this gives plenty of time to breathe out, which is helpful in asthma and COPD to prevent air trapping.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1:0.8 — 1:0.8 would mean expiration is SHORTER than inspiration — incorrect.
• 4:1 — 4:1 is an "inverse ratio" (inspiration four times longer than expiration) — incorrect.
• 1:6 — 1:6 is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Normal I:E is about 1:2. Increasing the rate or the inspiratory time shortens expiration and raises the risk of auto-PEEP.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q142 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 858, $s$The peak pressure alarm is sounding on the ventilator of a client with a recent tracheostomy. What intervention should be done first?$s$, $s$["Decrease the sensitivity of the alarm", "Suction the client", "Ensure that the connecting tubing is not kinked", "Assess the client's respiratory status"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assess the client's respiratory status
When ANY ventilator alarm sounds, the FIRST action is to ASSESS THE PATIENT (look, listen, feel — respiratory distress, SpO2, chest movement, breath sounds, colour, tube/tracheostomy position). The patient's condition guides further actions. A peak pressure alarm in a patient with a recent tracheostomy may be due to secretions, biting, coughing, tube displacement, or kinked tubing — but the patient comes first.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Decrease the sensitivity of the alarm — Decreasing alarm sensitivity hides the problem and is unsafe.
• Suction the client — Suctioning may be needed, but only after assessment.
• Ensure that the connecting tubing is not kinked — Checking tubing is important, but assessment of the patient comes first.

⭐ Key point: If you cannot quickly fix a ventilator problem and the patient is distressed, disconnect and hand-ventilate with 100% oxygen while calling for help.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q110$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 859, $s$A ventilated patient has a tidal volume of 420 mL and a rate of 24 breaths/min. What is the minute ventilation?$s$, $s$["17.5 L/min", "12.48 L/min", "10.08 L/min", "100.8 L/min"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10.08 L/min
MINUTE VENTILATION (VE) is the total volume of air moved in and out of the lungs in one minute. Formula: VE = TIDAL VOLUME × RESPIRATORY RATE. Here: 420 mL × 24 breaths/min = 10,080 mL/min. Convert to litres: 10,080 ÷ 1,000 = 10.08 L/min. Minute ventilation mainly controls CO2 removal: higher VE lowers PaCO2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 17.5 L/min — 17.5 L/min is a calculation error.
• 12.48 L/min — 12.48 L/min is a calculation error.
• 100.8 L/min — 100.8 L/min is a decimal place error (ten times too high).

⭐ Key point: Normal adult resting minute ventilation is about 5–8 L/min. A high VE need (e.g. above 10 L/min) can indicate high CO2 production (sepsis, fever) or high dead space, and may make weaning harder.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q124 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 860, $s$Which ventilator waveform finding indicates air trapping (auto-PEEP)?$s$, $s$["The expiratory flow returns to zero and stays flat before the next breath", "The inspiratory flow pattern is square (constant) in volume control", "The pressure–time curve shows a flat plateau during an inspiratory pause", "The expiratory flow does not return to zero before the next breath begins"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The expiratory flow does not return to zero before the next breath begins
AUTO-PEEP (intrinsic PEEP, air trapping) occurs when the patient does not have enough time to breathe out fully before the next breath begins. On the FLOW–TIME waveform, normal expiration shows flow returning to ZERO (the baseline) before the next breath. In air trapping, the EXPIRATORY FLOW DOES NOT RETURN TO ZERO — the next breath starts while air is still flowing out. This trapped air increases pressure in the alveoli, reduces venous return (causing hypotension) and increases the risk of barotrauma.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The expiratory flow returns to zero and stays flat before the next breath — Expiratory flow returning to zero and staying flat is NORMAL — exhalation is complete.
• The inspiratory flow pattern is square (constant) in volume control — A square inspiratory flow pattern is a normal setting in volume control; it does not indicate air trapping.
• The pressure–time curve shows a flat plateau during an inspiratory pause — A flat plateau during an inspiratory pause is used to measure plateau pressure; it does not indicate air trapping.

⭐ Key point: Auto-PEEP is common in asthma and COPD. Measure it with an END-EXPIRATORY hold. Treat by lengthening expiratory time (lower rate, shorter inspiratory time) and treating bronchospasm.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q8$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, true, 861, $s$The ventilator is delivering breaths without any patient effort (auto-triggering). The most common cause is:$s$, $s$["A circuit leak or water in the tubing with a very sensitive trigger", "A trigger set too insensitive, so efforts are missed", "Deep sedation causing the ventilator to compensate", "Severe metabolic acidosis driving genuine extra breaths"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A circuit leak or water in the tubing with a very sensitive trigger
AUTO-TRIGGERING happens when the ventilator "thinks" the patient is trying to breathe, but they are not, so it delivers extra breaths. The most common causes are: a CIRCUIT LEAK (e.g. loose connections, cuff leak, chest drain air leak) or WATER (condensation) moving in the tubing, especially when the TRIGGER is set TOO SENSITIVE. Cardiac oscillations (heartbeats) can also cause auto-triggering. This can cause hyperventilation, respiratory alkalosis and air trapping.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A trigger set too insensitive, so efforts are missed — A trigger set too INSENSITIVE causes MISSED patient efforts, not extra breaths.
• Deep sedation causing the ventilator to compensate — Deep sedation reduces the patient's own breathing; the ventilator does not "compensate" by auto-triggering.
• Severe metabolic acidosis driving genuine extra breaths — Metabolic acidosis causes GENUINE extra breathing efforts by the patient, not auto-triggering.

⭐ Key point: Fix auto-triggering: check and fix leaks, drain water from the circuit, and make the trigger less sensitive.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q18$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 862, $s$Which of the following pathogens is a common cause of ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) in the ICU?$s$, $s$["Pseudomonas aeruginosa", "Common cold rhinovirus only", "Malaria parasite", "Tapeworm"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pseudomonas aeruginosa
Pseudomonas aeruginosa is one of the commonest causes of VAP, especially late VAP (after 5 or more days on the ventilator). It is a Gram-negative bacterium that lives in moist places (sinks, ventilator circuits, humidifiers) and is often resistant to many antibiotics. Other VAP germs: Staphylococcus aureus (including MRSA), Klebsiella, Acinetobacter, and E. coli.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Common cold rhinovirus only — Rhinovirus causes the common cold, not typical VAP.
• Malaria parasite — The malaria parasite infects red blood cells and liver cells, not the lungs via the ventilator.
• Tapeworm — Tapeworm is a gut parasite and has nothing to do with VAP.

⭐ Key point: Prevent VAP with the bundle: head up 30–45°, daily sedation hold, chlorhexidine mouth care, subglottic suction, cuff pressure 20–30 cmH₂O, and hand hygiene.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q25$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 863, $s$What is the normal recommended tidal volume range for lung-protective ventilation in ARDS, based on predicted body weight?$s$, $s$["10-12 mL/kg", "15-18 mL/kg", "2-4 mL/kg", "6-8 mL/kg"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 6-8 mL/kg
Lung-protective ventilation uses SMALL tidal volumes of 6–8 mL/kg PREDICTED body weight (starting at 6 mL/kg in ARDS), with a plateau pressure of 30 cmH₂O or less. ARDS lungs are "baby lungs": only a small part works, so large breaths over-stretch and damage it (volutrauma). The ARDSNet trial showed that 6 mL/kg instead of 12 mL/kg reduced deaths.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10-12 mL/kg — 10–12 mL/kg was the old practice and increases lung injury and death.
• 15-18 mL/kg — 15–18 mL/kg is dangerously large and would cause barotrauma and volutrauma.
• 2-4 mL/kg — 2–4 mL/kg is too small for normal ventilation (only used in special settings like ECMO).

⭐ Key point: Use PREDICTED body weight (from height and sex), not actual weight. An obese patient's lungs are no bigger than a thin patient's of the same height.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q62 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 864, $s$Signs of weaning intolerance during a trial include all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Use of accessory muscles and paradoxical (abdominal) breathing", "Diaphoresis and anxiety", "Improved mentation and stable heart rate", "Significant increase in heart rate or blood pressure"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Improved mentation and stable heart rate
The question asks which is NOT a sign of failing a weaning trial. Improved mentation (more alert) with a stable heart rate is a GOOD sign that the patient is tolerating the trial. Signs of failure include fast breathing (RR above 35), accessory muscle use, see-saw (paradoxical) breathing, sweating, anxiety or agitation, a rise in HR or BP of more than 20%, falling SpO₂ (below 90%) and rising CO₂.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Use of accessory muscles and paradoxical (abdominal) breathing — Accessory muscle use and paradoxical breathing ARE signs of failure (respiratory muscle fatigue).
• Diaphoresis and anxiety — Sweating (diaphoresis) and anxiety ARE signs of failure.
• Significant increase in heart rate or blood pressure — A significant rise in HR or BP IS a sign of failure.

⭐ Key point: If a spontaneous breathing trial fails, put the patient back on comfortable support, look for the reason (fluid overload, secretions, weakness, low phosphate, sedation), and try again the next day.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q104$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 865, $s$Which parameter setting is primarily responsible for oxygenation rather than ventilation (CO2 removal)?$s$, $s$["Respiratory rate", "Tidal volume", "I:E ratio", "FiO2 and PEEP"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: FiO2 and PEEP
Oxygenation (getting O₂ into the blood) is controlled mainly by FiO₂ (the oxygen concentration) and PEEP (keeps the alveoli open for gas exchange and increases surface area). Ventilation (removing CO₂) is controlled by MINUTE ventilation: respiratory rate × tidal volume. So to raise a low PaO₂, increase FiO₂ or PEEP; to lower a high PaCO₂, increase the rate or tidal volume.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory rate — Respiratory rate mainly changes CO₂ removal (ventilation).
• Tidal volume — Tidal volume mainly changes CO₂ removal (ventilation).
• I:E ratio — I:E ratio has some effect on oxygenation but is not the main control; it is adjusted for special cases.

⭐ Key point: O₂ problem → change FiO₂ or PEEP. CO₂ problem → change rate or tidal volume. Memorise this for ventilator questions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q111$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 866, $s$A key element of the "ventilator bundle" (VAP prevention bundle) includes all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Head-of-bed elevation 30-45 degrees", "Daily sedation vacation and readiness-to-wean assessment", "Peptic ulcer disease and deep vein thrombosis prophylaxis", "Routine daily scheduled ventilator circuit changes"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Routine daily scheduled ventilator circuit changes
The question asks which is NOT part of the VAP bundle. Routine, scheduled ventilator circuit changes do NOT reduce VAP. Breaking the circuit may actually increase contamination. Circuits are changed only when visibly soiled or faulty. The VAP bundle includes head-of-bed elevation, daily sedation holds with weaning assessment, peptic ulcer and DVT prophylaxis, and chlorhexidine oral care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Head-of-bed elevation 30-45 degrees — Head-of-bed elevation to 30–45° IS part of the bundle; it reduces aspiration.
• Daily sedation vacation and readiness-to-wean assessment — Daily sedation vacation and readiness-to-wean assessment IS part of the bundle; it shortens ventilation.
• Peptic ulcer disease and deep vein thrombosis prophylaxis — Peptic ulcer and DVT prophylaxis ARE part of the original IHI ventilator bundle.

⭐ Key point: Other VAP measures: subglottic suction, cuff pressure 20–30 cmH₂O, closed suction, hand hygiene, and early extubation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q130$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 867, $s$In which mode of ventilator the patient will initiate his/her own breath and does not depend on ventilator. Ventilator will only assess the patient efforts.$s$, $s$["AC mood", "SIMV mood", "Spontaneous mood", "VCV mood"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Spontaneous mood
In SPONTANEOUS mode (e.g. CPAP or Pressure Support/PSV), the PATIENT starts EVERY breath and controls the rate and depth; the ventilator does not give mandatory breaths. It only senses the patient's effort (trigger) and gives support — a set pressure (pressure support) and/or PEEP — to make breathing easier. There is no backup rate unless apnoea ventilation is set, so it is used for alert patients with a good respiratory drive, especially during WEANING (spontaneous breathing trials).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• AC mood — ASSIST-CONTROL (AC) gives a set number of full mandatory breaths; every patient-triggered breath also gets a full set breath, so the patient depends on the ventilator.
• SIMV mood — SIMV gives a set number of mandatory breaths synchronised with the patient; between them the patient can breathe spontaneously — but it still delivers mandatory breaths.
• VCV mood — VCV (volume-controlled ventilation) delivers a set tidal volume at a set rate — a controlled mode, not patient-driven.

⭐ Key point: SPONTANEOUS mode (CPAP/PSV): patient starts all breaths; ventilator only supports — used for weaning.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q60$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 868, $s$While monitoring during ventilator support, which one of the following is NOT a responsibility of nurse;$s$, $s$["Check any indications of air leak", "Do suctioning if needed", "Arterial blood gas measurement", "Change ventilator setting frequently"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Change ventilator setting frequently
During mechanical ventilation the NURSE's responsibilities include: monitoring the patient (SpO2, vital signs, chest movement, breath sounds, comfort, sedation level), checking for AIR LEAKS (cuff pressure 20–30 cmH2O, circuit connections), SUCTIONING when there are secretions (as needed, not routinely), checking and responding to alarms, collecting/assisting with ABG samples and reporting results, oral care and VAP prevention, and accurate documentation. CHANGING VENTILATOR SETTINGS is NOT a routine nursing task — settings (FiO2, PEEP, mode, rate, tidal volume) are ordered by the doctor/intensivist (or respiratory therapist per protocol), and changing them "frequently" without orders would be unsafe and unstable for the patient.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Check any indications of air leak — Checking for AIR LEAKS IS a nursing responsibility (cuff pressure, circuit).
• Do suctioning if needed — SUCTIONING as needed IS a nursing responsibility.
• Arterial blood gas measurement — ARTERIAL BLOOD GAS sampling/monitoring IS part of nursing care (taking samples from an arterial line and reporting results).

⭐ Key point: Nurses monitor, suction, check leaks and ABGs — they do NOT change ventilator settings without orders.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q63$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 869, $s$SIMV, as a mode of mechanical ventilation, stands for and allows:$s$, $s$["Synchronized Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation — combines mandatory breaths with the ability for the patient to breathe spontaneously between them", "Simple Immediate Manual Ventilation — a purely manual bag-valve technique", "Static Inspiratory Muscle Ventilation — used only for muscle training", "Single Inflation Manual Volume — a one-time inflation mode"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Synchronized Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation — combines mandatory breaths with the ability for the patient to breathe spontaneously between them
SIMV = SYNCHRONISED INTERMITTENT MANDATORY VENTILATION. The ventilator gives a SET NUMBER of MANDATORY breaths per minute (set volume or pressure), timed (SYNCHRONISED) with the patient's own effort to avoid "breath stacking" and fighting the ventilator. BETWEEN the mandatory breaths, the patient can take their OWN SPONTANEOUS breaths, which can be helped by pressure support. It was used for weaning (gradually lowering the mandatory rate), keeping respiratory muscles active. Watch for increased work of breathing if the mandatory rate is set too low.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple Immediate Manual Ventilation — a purely manual bag-valve technique — "Simple Immediate Manual Ventilation" is not a real term; manual bag-valve ventilation is not SIMV.
• Static Inspiratory Muscle Ventilation — used only for muscle training — "Static Inspiratory Muscle Ventilation" is not a real ventilation mode.
• Single Inflation Manual Volume — a one-time inflation mode — "Single Inflation Manual Volume" is not a real ventilation mode.

⭐ Key point: SIMV = Synchronised Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation: set mandatory breaths + spontaneous breaths in between.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q110$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 870, $s$What is the purpose of measuring plateau pressure via an inspiratory hold maneuver?$s$, $s$["To measure airway resistance directly", "To calculate the respiratory rate", "To determine FiO2 requirements", "To estimate alveolar distending pressure and assess risk of barotrauma, independent of airway resistance"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: To estimate alveolar distending pressure and assess risk of barotrauma, independent of airway resistance
On a volume-controlled ventilator, the PEAK pressure is made of two parts: pressure to push gas through the AIRWAYS (resistance) and pressure to stretch the ALVEOLI (compliance). An INSPIRATORY HOLD (pause of about 0.5–1 second at the end of inspiration) stops gas flow, so the airway resistance part disappears; the pressure measured then is the PLATEAU PRESSURE (Pplat), which reflects the pressure inside the ALVEOLI (alveolar distending pressure). Keeping Pplat BELOW 30 cmH2O reduces the risk of BAROTRAUMA/volutrauma (ventilator-induced lung injury, pneumothorax) — a key part of lung-protective ventilation in ARDS (tidal volume 6 mL/kg ideal body weight). Driving pressure = Pplat − PEEP (keep < 15). High peak but normal plateau = airway problem (secretions, bronchospasm, kinked tube); both high = stiff lungs or pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To measure airway resistance directly — AIRWAY RESISTANCE is estimated from (peak − plateau) ÷ flow, not by plateau alone.
• To calculate the respiratory rate — RESPIRATORY RATE is simply counted or set; it does not need an inspiratory hold.
• To determine FiO2 requirements — FiO2 is set from SpO2/PaO2 needs; plateau pressure does not determine it.

⭐ Key point: Plateau pressure (inspiratory hold) = alveolar pressure. Keep < 30 cmH2O to prevent barotrauma.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q112$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 871, $s$A ventilator's "sensitivity" can be set to trigger by flow or by pressure change. Flow triggering is generally preferred because it:$s$, $s$["Always delivers a larger tidal volume", "Eliminates the need for PEEP", "Prevents all forms of asynchrony", "Requires less patient work of breathing to initiate a breath than pressure triggering"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Requires less patient work of breathing to initiate a breath than pressure triggering
FLOW TRIGGERING detects a small change in flow when the patient starts to breathe. It requires LESS WORK from the patient than PRESSURE TRIGGERING (which requires the patient to lower the pressure in the circuit).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Always delivers a larger tidal volume — It doesn't always deliver larger volumes.
• Eliminates the need for PEEP — It doesn't eliminate PEEP.
• Prevents all forms of asynchrony — It doesn't prevent all asynchrony.

⭐ Key point: Set sensitivity carefully to avoid auto-triggering.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q102$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 872, $s$Before caring for a patient on a mechanical ventilator, the nurse should be familiar with all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["The ventilator mode and settings in use", "Alarm limits and troubleshooting", "The patient's dietary preferences prior to admission", "Signs of a blocked or displaced endotracheal tube"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The patient's dietary preferences prior to admission
Before caring for a ventilated patient, the nurse must know: the VENTILATOR MODE and SETTINGS, ALARM LIMITS and TROUBLESHOOTING, and signs of a BLOCKED or DISPLACED ET tube. The patient's DIETARY PREFERENCES before admission are not essential for ventilator safety.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The ventilator mode and settings in use — Mode and settings are essential.
• Alarm limits and troubleshooting — Alarm limits are essential.
• Signs of a blocked or displaced endotracheal tube — Tube problems are essential.

⭐ Key point: Always have a bag-valve-mask and suction at the bedside.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q105$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 873, $s$Which are the Ventilator Parameters adjusted to maintain the optimum Minute Ventilation?$s$, $s$["FiO2", "PEEP", "Tidal Volume and Respiratory Rate", "Both A and B"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tidal Volume and Respiratory Rate
MINUTE VENTILATION (VE) is the total amount of air moved in and out of the lungs each minute: VE = TIDAL VOLUME (Vt) × RESPIRATORY RATE (RR). On a ventilator, VE is controlled by adjusting the TIDAL VOLUME and the RESPIRATORY RATE. Minute ventilation determines how much CO2 is removed: increasing Vt or RR lowers PaCO2; decreasing them raises PaCO2. Normal adult VE is about 5–8 L/min.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• FiO2 — FiO2 (fraction of inspired oxygen) controls OXYGENATION, not minute ventilation.
• PEEP — PEEP (positive end-expiratory pressure) improves OXYGENATION by keeping alveoli open; it does not set minute ventilation.
• Both A and B — "Both A and B" (FiO2 and PEEP) are both oxygenation controls, not ventilation controls.

⭐ Key point: Remember: OXYGENATION = FiO2 + PEEP; VENTILATION (CO2) = rate + tidal volume. Keep tidal volume lung-protective (6–8 mL/kg predicted body weight).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q59$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 874, $s$In a patient with severe air-trapping from status asthmaticus, which ventilator strategy helps reduce auto-PEEP?$s$, $s$["Prolong inspiratory time", "Increase PEEP above intrinsic level", "Prolong expiratory time (lower rate, higher inspiratory flow)", "Increase respiratory rate"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Prolong expiratory time (lower rate, higher inspiratory flow)
In STATUS ASTHMATICUS, air trapping (auto-PEEP) occurs because exhalation is slow. PROLONGING EXPIRATORY TIME allows more complete exhalation: LOWER the RATE (more time for each breath) and use a HIGHER INSPIRATORY FLOW (delivers the breath faster, leaving more time to exhale).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prolong inspiratory time — Prolonging inspiratory time shortens expiration, worsening air trapping.
• Increase PEEP above intrinsic level — Increasing PEEP above intrinsic PEEP can worsen hyperinflation.
• Increase respiratory rate — Increasing the rate shortens expiration.

⭐ Key point: Accept permissive hypercapnia to avoid dangerous air trapping.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q103$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 875, $s$How often should ventilator circuit tubing be routinely changed to reduce infection risk, according to current VAP-prevention guidance?$s$, $s$["Every 24 hours routinely", "Every 48 hours routinely", "Every 4 hours routinely", "Only when visibly soiled or malfunctioning, not on a fixed schedule"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Only when visibly soiled or malfunctioning, not on a fixed schedule
Current VAP-prevention guidelines (e.g. CDC, SHEA) recommend that VENTILATOR CIRCUITS should NOT be changed routinely on a fixed schedule. They should be changed ONLY WHEN VISIBLY SOILED (blood, vomit, secretions) or MALFUNCTIONING. Studies showed that routine frequent changes did NOT reduce VAP — in fact, each change opens the circuit, exposing the patient to contamination, loss of PEEP and lung derecruitment, and may increase VAP risk. Frequent changes also increase cost and staff workload.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Every 24 hours routinely — Routine changes every 24 hours are not recommended.
• Every 48 hours routinely — Routine changes every 48 hours are not recommended.
• Every 4 hours routinely — Changes every 4 hours would be excessive and harmful.

⭐ Key point: Drain condensate in the circuit away from the patient (never back towards them), keep HMEs changed per manufacturer's instructions, and minimise disconnections.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q106$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 876, $s$Which of the following is an absolute indication for initiating invasive mechanical ventilation?$s$, $s$["Apnea with respiratory arrest", "Mild hypoxemia responsive to nasal cannula oxygen", "Chronic stable COPD with SpO2 90%", "Post-operative sedation for 1 hour"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Apnea with respiratory arrest
An ABSOLUTE indication for invasive mechanical ventilation is APNOEA with RESPIRATORY ARREST — the patient is not breathing at all, so they will die within minutes without ventilation. Other strong indications include: inability to protect the airway (GCS ≤8), severe respiratory failure not responding to oxygen or NIV (hypoxaemia, hypercapnia with acidosis, exhaustion), severe shock or cardiac arrest, and the need for deep sedation or paralysis (e.g. raised ICP management).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild hypoxemia responsive to nasal cannula oxygen — Mild hypoxaemia that responds to nasal cannula oxygen does not need ventilation.
• Chronic stable COPD with SpO2 90% — Chronic stable COPD with SpO2 90% is often the patient's baseline; ventilation is not needed.
• Post-operative sedation for 1 hour — Short post-operative sedation usually resolves without invasive ventilation; observe and support as needed.

⭐ Key point: Invasive ventilation carries risks (VAP, barotrauma, sedation, delirium) — so the indication must be clear, and weaning should start as early as possible.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q107$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 877, $s$Which type of ventilator generates a negative pressure around the thorax to draw air into the lungs, historically exemplified by the "iron lung"?$s$, $s$["Positive pressure ventilator", "High-frequency oscillatory ventilator", "Pressure-cycled ventilator", "Negative pressure ventilator"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Negative pressure ventilator
A NEGATIVE PRESSURE VENTILATOR works by creating NEGATIVE (sub-atmospheric) PRESSURE around the outside of the chest. This pulls the chest wall outwards, which lowers the pressure inside the lungs and DRAWS AIR IN — similar to normal breathing. The classic example is the "IRON LUNG", a sealed metal tank widely used during POLIO epidemics in the 1940s–1950s, when polio paralysed the breathing muscles. Modern forms include cuirass (chest shell) ventilators.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Positive pressure ventilator — Positive pressure ventilators (used in most ICUs today) PUSH air INTO the lungs through a tube or mask.
• High-frequency oscillatory ventilator — High-frequency oscillatory ventilators deliver very small, very rapid oscillating breaths (a type of positive pressure).
• Pressure-cycled ventilator — Pressure-cycled ventilators deliver breaths until a set pressure is reached (positive pressure).

⭐ Key point: Positive pressure ventilation replaced negative pressure ventilation because it provides better control and access to the patient.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q108$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 878, $s$A patient in SIMV mode breathes spontaneously between mandatory breaths. What determines the tidal volume of these spontaneous breaths?$s$, $s$["The set tidal volume on the ventilator", "The set PEEP level", "The patient's own effort, with support only from pressure support if added", "The inspiratory flow rate alone"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The patient's own effort, with support only from pressure support if added
In SIMV, the mandatory breaths have the SET tidal volume (or pressure), but the SPONTANEOUS breaths in between depend on the PATIENT'S OWN EFFORT and lung mechanics. If PRESSURE SUPPORT is added, the ventilator helps each spontaneous breath with a set pressure, increasing the volume.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The set tidal volume on the ventilator — The set tidal volume applies only to mandatory breaths.
• The set PEEP level — PEEP doesn't determine tidal volume.
• The inspiratory flow rate alone — Flow rate alone doesn't determine spontaneous volume.

⭐ Key point: Monitor spontaneous tidal volumes in SIMV — small volumes may indicate fatigue.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q112$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 879, $s$A ventilated ARDS patient has a plateau pressure of 15 cmH2O and PEEP of 5 cmH2O. What is the driving pressure?$s$, $s$["10 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury", "20 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury", "10 cmH2O — within the safe target (≤ 15)", "15 cmH2O — within the safe target (≤ 15)"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10 cmH2O — within the safe target (≤ 15)
DRIVING PRESSURE = PLATEAU PRESSURE − PEEP = 15 − 5 = 10 cmH2O. The safe target is 15 cmH2O or LESS, so 10 is WITHIN the safe target. Lower driving pressure means less stretch on the lungs with each breath and a lower risk of ventilator-induced lung injury.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury — 10 is correct, but it is SAFE, not too high.
• 20 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury — 20 adds plateau and PEEP.
• 15 cmH2O — within the safe target (≤ 15) — 15 is the plateau pressure, not the driving pressure.

⭐ Key point: Driving pressure above 15 cmH2O is linked to higher mortality in ARDS.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q101$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 880, $s$A potential complication of Assist-Control ventilation in a patient who is tachypneic from anxiety or pain is:$s$, $s$["Inability to trigger any breaths", "Hypoventilation and hypercapnia", "Respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation, along with breath stacking and auto-PEEP", "Automatic apnea"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory alkalosis from hyperventilation, along with breath stacking and auto-PEEP
In ASSIST-CONTROL (AC) ventilation, EVERY breath the patient triggers is given as a FULL breath (full set volume or pressure). If the patient breathes fast because of ANXIETY or PAIN (e.g. 35/min), each breath delivers a full tidal volume, causing excessive minute ventilation: RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS (CO2 too low). There may also not be enough time to exhale, causing BREATH STACKING and AUTO-PEEP, which can cause hypotension and barotrauma.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Inability to trigger any breaths — AC does allow patients to trigger breaths.
• Hypoventilation and hypercapnia — Hypoventilation is not the problem with rapid triggering in AC.
• Automatic apnea — AC provides backup breaths, so apnoea is not a risk.

⭐ Key point: Treat the cause of tachypnoea (pain, anxiety, fever, hypoxia) and consider a different mode (e.g. pressure support or SIMV).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q102$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 881, $s$The mode of Mechanical Ventilation which does not allow the patient to take spontaneous breaths is:$s$, $s$["AC Mode (Assist Control Mode)", "CMV (Control Mandatory Ventilation)", "SIMV (Synchronous intermittent Mandatory Ventilation)", "PCV (Pressure Control Mode)"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: CMV (Control Mandatory Ventilation)
In CONTROLLED MANDATORY VENTILATION (CMV), the ventilator delivers ALL breaths at a set rate and volume/pressure, and the patient CANNOT TRIGGER any breaths (the trigger is switched off). It is used in deeply sedated or paralysed patients. It is uncomfortable for awake patients ("fighting the ventilator").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• AC Mode (Assist Control Mode) — AC allows patients to trigger extra (full) breaths.
• SIMV (Synchronous intermittent Mandatory Ventilation) — SIMV allows spontaneous breaths between mandatory breaths.
• PCV (Pressure Control Mode) — Pressure Control (as commonly used in AC mode) allows patient-triggered breaths.

⭐ Key point: Many modern ventilators use AC rather than pure CMV.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q106$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 882, $s$A key nursing assessment component specific to a patient receiving neuromuscular blockade for ventilator synchrony is:$s$, $s$["Discontinuing all monitoring since the patient cannot move", "Assessing verbal pain reports", "Allowing the patient to ambulate", "Using a peripheral nerve stimulator, such as train-of-four, to monitor level of blockade and ensuring adequate concurrent sedation and analgesia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Using a peripheral nerve stimulator, such as train-of-four, to monitor level of blockade and ensuring adequate concurrent sedation and analgesia
Patients receiving NEUROMUSCULAR BLOCKADE (paralysis) CANNOT MOVE, BREATHE, or show signs of distress or pain. Key nursing assessments: use a PERIPHERAL NERVE STIMULATOR (TRAIN-OF-FOUR, TOF) to monitor the depth of blockade (avoid excessive paralysis), and ENSURE ADEQUATE SEDATION and ANALGESIA (paralysed patients can be awake and in pain without showing it). Also: eye care (corneal protection), pressure area care, DVT prevention.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Discontinuing all monitoring since the patient cannot move — Monitoring must continue.
• Assessing verbal pain reports — Paralysed patients cannot report pain verbally.
• Allowing the patient to ambulate — Paralysed patients cannot ambulate.

⭐ Key point: Usually aim for 1–2 twitches out of 4 on TOF. Use BIS monitoring if available to assess sedation depth.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q108$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 883, $s$A male patient with ARDS is 185 cm tall and weighs 125 kg. Using lung-protective ventilation at 8 mL/kg predicted body weight, what tidal volume should be set?$s$, $s$["About 1000 mL", "About 637 mL", "About 797 mL", "About 478 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: About 637 mL
MALE PBW = 50 + 0.91 × (height in cm − 152.4) = 50 + 0.91 × (185 − 152.4) = 50 + 0.91 × 32.6 = 50 + 29.7 = 79.7 kg. Tidal volume at 8 mL/kg = 8 × 79.7 = 637 mL. His actual weight (125 kg) is NOT used.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• About 1000 mL — 1,000 mL is 8 mL/kg of actual weight — dangerously high.
• About 797 mL — 797 mL is 10 mL/kg PBW.
• About 478 mL — 478 mL is 6 mL/kg PBW.

⭐ Key point: Tall patients have larger lungs, so their PBW and tidal volumes are larger, regardless of actual weight.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q111 · concept asked in 6 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 884, $s$A patient with severe asthma on the ventilator develops sudden hypotension and difficulty bagging. The nurse should consider disconnecting the patient from the ventilator briefly to allow full exhalation, since this presentation may reflect:$s$, $s$["Normal ventilator function", "Improved lung compliance", "An unrelated cardiac event only", "Severe auto-PEEP/breath stacking causing dynamic hyperinflation and reduced venous return"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Severe auto-PEEP/breath stacking causing dynamic hyperinflation and reduced venous return
In SEVERE ASTHMA, narrowed airways slow exhalation. If the ventilator gives the next breath before exhalation is complete, air builds up (BREATH STACKING / AUTO-PEEP), causing DYNAMIC HYPERINFLATION. The raised pressure in the chest REDUCES VENOUS RETURN, causing HYPOTENSION, and makes the lungs very stiff (DIFFICULT BAGGING). Briefly DISCONNECTING the ventilator allows trapped air to escape, which can quickly restore BP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal ventilator function — This is not normal ventilator function.
• Improved lung compliance — Compliance is worse, not improved.
• An unrelated cardiac event only — It is not an unrelated cardiac event; it is caused by ventilation.

⭐ Key point: Prevent with a low rate, low tidal volume, long expiratory time, and bronchodilators; accept higher CO2.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q113$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 885, $s$A male patient with ARDS is 170 cm tall and weighs 101 kg. Using lung-protective ventilation at 8 mL/kg predicted body weight, what tidal volume should be set?$s$, $s$["About 660 mL", "About 528 mL", "About 808 mL", "About 396 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: About 528 mL
MALE PBW = 50 + 0.91 × (170 − 152.4) = 50 + 0.91 × 17.6 = 50 + 16.0 = 66 kg. Tidal volume at 8 mL/kg = 8 × 66 = 528 mL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• About 660 mL — 660 mL is 10 mL/kg PBW.
• About 808 mL — 808 mL is 8 mL/kg of actual weight (101 kg).
• About 396 mL — 396 mL is 6 mL/kg PBW.

⭐ Key point: Recalculate if the height was estimated — measure the height (e.g. heel to crown) accurately.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q114 · concept asked in 6 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 886, $s$Auto-PEEP (intrinsic PEEP), sometimes seen in patients with severe airflow obstruction on a ventilator, occurs when:$s$, $s$["PEEP is set too low", "Incomplete exhalation traps air in the lungs before the next breath, causing progressive hyperinflation", "There is no relationship to ventilator settings", "The patient fully exhales before the next breath is delivered"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Incomplete exhalation traps air in the lungs before the next breath, causing progressive hyperinflation
AUTO-PEEP (intrinsic PEEP) occurs when EXHALATION is INCOMPLETE — air is TRAPPED in the lungs before the next breath begins. This causes progressive HYPERINFLATION. It is common in severe airflow obstruction (asthma, COPD), high respiratory rates, and long inspiratory times.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• PEEP is set too low — Setting PEEP too low is not the cause.
• There is no relationship to ventilator settings — Auto-PEEP is strongly related to ventilator settings (rate, I:E ratio, tidal volume).
• The patient fully exhales before the next breath is delivered — If the patient fully exhales, there is no auto-PEEP.

⭐ Key point: Detect it with the expiratory flow waveform (not returning to zero) and an end-expiratory hold.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q115$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 887, $s$During a spontaneous breathing trial a patient breathes at 28/min with a tidal volume of 250 mL. What is the rapid shallow breathing index (RSBI)?$s$, $s$["7 — likely to fail weaning", "9 — likely to wean successfully", "112 — likely to wean successfully", "112 — likely to fail weaning"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 112 — likely to fail weaning
RSBI = RESPIRATORY RATE ÷ TIDAL VOLUME (LITRES) = 28 ÷ 0.25 = 112. An RSBI ABOVE 105 suggests RAPID, SHALLOW breathing, so weaning is LIKELY TO FAIL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 7 — likely to fail weaning — 7 is a calculation error.
• 9 — likely to wean successfully — 9 is a calculation error.
• 112 — likely to wean successfully — 112 is correct, but it predicts FAILURE, not success.

⭐ Key point: Look for reversible causes of weaning failure: fluid overload, weakness, secretions, sedation, anxiety, electrolyte problems.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q116 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 888, $s$Which mode of mechanical ventilation delivers a preset volume/pressure with every breath, whether patient-triggered or machine-triggered, providing full support?$s$, $s$["Assist-Control (AC)", "CPAP with no additional support", "Pressure Support Ventilation alone at low levels", "T-piece trial"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assist-Control (AC)
In ASSIST-CONTROL (AC) mode, the ventilator delivers a PRESET VOLUME (volume AC) or PRESSURE (pressure AC) with EVERY breath. If the patient does not breathe, the machine gives breaths at the set rate. If the patient TRIGGERS a breath, the ventilator still delivers the FULL set breath. So AC provides FULL ventilatory support and minimises the patient's work of breathing — useful at the start of ventilation, in shock, severe ARDS, or when muscles are fatigued.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CPAP with no additional support — CPAP alone gives NO breaths — only a continuous pressure; the patient does all the breathing work.
• Pressure Support Ventilation alone at low levels — Low-level pressure support only assists breaths the patient starts; it is partial support.
• T-piece trial — A T-piece trial gives NO support — it tests the patient's ability to breathe alone.

⭐ Key point: Watch for respiratory alkalosis and auto-PEEP in AC if the patient breathes fast; consider sedation, analgesia or a mode change.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q118$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 889, $s$Pressure support ventilation (PSV) requires which patient characteristic to function safely?$s$, $s$["Presence of a tracheostomy only", "Complete absence of spontaneous breathing", "Intact spontaneous respiratory drive", "Paralysis from neuromuscular blockade"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intact spontaneous respiratory drive
In PRESSURE SUPPORT VENTILATION (PSV), the ventilator only HELPS breaths that the PATIENT STARTS. It gives NO mandatory breaths. If the patient's breathing drive is weak or absent (deep sedation, brain injury, neuromuscular blockade), there will be no breaths and the patient can become apnoeic. So PSV requires an INTACT SPONTANEOUS RESPIRATORY DRIVE. It is mainly used for weaning and in awake, spontaneously breathing patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Presence of a tracheostomy only — A tracheostomy is not required; PSV can be used with an ET tube or NIV mask.
• Complete absence of spontaneous breathing — PSV cannot be used when there is no spontaneous breathing.
• Paralysis from neuromuscular blockade — Paralysed patients cannot trigger breaths, so PSV would fail.

⭐ Key point: Always set the apnoea alarm and backup ventilation when using PSV.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q119$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 890, $s$In SIMV (Synchronized Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation) mode, compared to Assist-Control, the patient:$s$, $s$["Receives full ventilator support for every single breath", "Can take spontaneous breaths between mandatory synchronized breaths, at their own tidal volume", "Cannot breathe spontaneously at all", "Is always fully paralysed"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Can take spontaneous breaths between mandatory synchronized breaths, at their own tidal volume
In SIMV, the ventilator gives a SET NUMBER of mandatory breaths, synchronised with the patient's efforts. BETWEEN these breaths, the patient CAN take SPONTANEOUS breaths at their OWN TIDAL VOLUME (often with pressure support). In AC, every triggered breath gets full support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Receives full ventilator support for every single breath — Full support for every breath is AC.
• Cannot breathe spontaneously at all — The patient can breathe spontaneously in SIMV.
• Is always fully paralysed — Patients are not paralysed in SIMV.

⭐ Key point: SIMV was used for weaning, but spontaneous breathing trials are more effective.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q122$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 891, $s$When planning nutrition for a mechanically ventilated COPD patient with difficulty weaning, which principle is important?$s$, $s$["Provide the highest possible carbohydrate load to maximise energy delivery", "Restrict all protein intake to reduce respiratory workload", "Avoid overfeeding, particularly excess carbohydrate, since it increases CO2 production and ventilatory demand", "Nutrition has no effect on ventilatory demand in COPD"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Avoid overfeeding, particularly excess carbohydrate, since it increases CO2 production and ventilatory demand
In ventilated COPD patients, OVERFEEDING — especially EXCESS CARBOHYDRATE — increases CO2 PRODUCTION (carbohydrate metabolism produces more CO2 than fat). This increases the work of breathing and makes WEANING harder.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Provide the highest possible carbohydrate load to maximise energy delivery — A high carbohydrate load increases CO2 production.
• Restrict all protein intake to reduce respiratory workload — Protein is needed for muscle strength.
• Nutrition has no effect on ventilatory demand in COPD — Nutrition does affect ventilatory demand.

⭐ Key point: Provide balanced nutrition (adequate protein, avoid overfeeding).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q124$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 892, $s$Using EtCO2 to guide ventilator adjustments in a patient managed with permissive hypercapnia, such as an ARDS lung-protective strategy, provides the benefit of:$s$, $s$["Allowing noninvasive, continuous trending of ventilation adequacy to help balance lung-protective settings against acceptable CO2 elevation", "Forcing normalization of PaCO2 at all times", "Preventing any hypercapnia from occurring", "Eliminating the need to check arterial blood gases entirely"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Allowing noninvasive, continuous trending of ventilation adequacy to help balance lung-protective settings against acceptable CO2 elevation
In PERMISSIVE HYPERCAPNIA, higher CO2 is accepted to protect the lungs. EtCO2 allows CONTINUOUS, NON-INVASIVE TRENDING of ventilation, helping the team balance lung-protective settings with acceptable CO2 levels between ABGs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Forcing normalization of PaCO2 at all times — PaCO2 normalisation isn't the goal.
• Preventing any hypercapnia from occurring — Hypercapnia is accepted, not prevented.
• Eliminating the need to check arterial blood gases entirely — ABGs are still needed (EtCO2 underestimates PaCO2 in lung disease).

⭐ Key point: Track the PaCO2–EtCO2 gradient with paired ABGs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q128$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 893, $s$The mode of Mechanical Ventilation in which there is a Preset Breath rate and tidal volume but the patient is able to initiate extra breath which is assisted by the ventilator is called:$s$, $s$["ACV", "CMV", "PSV", "SIMV"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ACV
In ASSIST-CONTROL VENTILATION (ACV), the clinician sets a MINIMUM (preset) RATE and a TIDAL VOLUME (or pressure). The ventilator delivers these mandatory breaths, but the patient CAN TRIGGER EXTRA breaths; each extra breath is ASSISTED with the FULL set tidal volume. This combines guaranteed minimum ventilation with patient comfort.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CMV — CMV (controlled mandatory ventilation) does NOT allow the patient to trigger any breaths.
• PSV — PSV has no preset rate or tidal volume — the patient controls both.
• SIMV — In SIMV, extra breaths are at the patient's OWN tidal volume (sometimes pressure-supported), not the full set volume.

⭐ Key point: ACV is one of the most common modes for initial ventilation in ICU.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q133$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 894, $s$During a spontaneous breathing trial a patient breathes at 16/min with a tidal volume of 400 mL. What is the rapid shallow breathing index (RSBI)?$s$, $s$["25 — likely to wean successfully", "6.4 — likely to fail weaning", "40 — likely to fail weaning", "40 — likely to wean successfully"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 40 — likely to wean successfully
RAPID SHALLOW BREATHING INDEX (RSBI) = RESPIRATORY RATE ÷ TIDAL VOLUME (in LITRES). Here: 400 mL = 0.4 L, so 16 ÷ 0.4 = 40. An RSBI BELOW 105 suggests the patient is breathing at a comfortable rate with good-sized breaths, so they are LIKELY TO WEAN SUCCESSFULLY. A high RSBI (fast, shallow breathing) suggests the muscles are tiring.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 25 — likely to wean successfully — 25 is a calculation error.
• 6.4 — likely to fail weaning — 6.4 is a calculation error (16 × 0.4).
• 40 — likely to fail weaning — 40 is correct, but it predicts SUCCESS, not failure.

⭐ Key point: RSBI is measured during a short period of spontaneous breathing (on minimal support). Combine it with other signs: alertness, cough strength, secretions, oxygenation and haemodynamic stability.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q134 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 895, $s$A patient on PEEP (Positive End-Expiratory Pressure) develops sudden hypotension after the PEEP level is increased. The most likely explanation is:$s$, $s$["An allergic reaction to the ventilator tubing", "PEEP has no cardiovascular effects", "Increased intrathoracic pressure reducing venous return and cardiac output", "Coincidental, unrelated hypotension"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increased intrathoracic pressure reducing venous return and cardiac output
Increasing PEEP RAISES INTRATHORACIC PRESSURE, which squeezes the veins returning blood to the heart, REDUCING VENOUS RETURN and CARDIAC OUTPUT — causing HYPOTENSION, especially in hypovolaemic patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An allergic reaction to the ventilator tubing — An allergic reaction to tubing is not likely.
• PEEP has no cardiovascular effects — PEEP does have cardiovascular effects.
• Coincidental, unrelated hypotension — The timing suggests a link to PEEP.

⭐ Key point: Give fluids and adjust PEEP if hypotension occurs; watch BP after every PEEP change.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q135$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 896, $s$A ventilated patient has a tidal volume of 350 mL and a rate of 24 breaths/min. What is the minute ventilation?$s$, $s$["14.58 L/min", "10.8 L/min", "8.4 L/min", "84 L/min"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 8.4 L/min
MINUTE VENTILATION = TIDAL VOLUME × RESPIRATORY RATE. Here: 350 mL × 24 breaths/min = 8,400 mL/min. Convert to litres: 8,400 ÷ 1,000 = 8.4 L/min. This is at the upper end of normal (about 5–8 L/min), but notice the breathing pattern is fast and fairly shallow — more of each breath is "wasted" on dead space, so alveolar ventilation is lower than it appears.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 14.58 L/min — 14.58 L/min is a calculation error.
• 10.8 L/min — 10.8 L/min is a calculation error.
• 84 L/min — 84 L/min is a decimal place error (ten times too high).

⭐ Key point: Alveolar ventilation = (tidal volume − dead space) × rate. With 150 mL dead space: (350 − 150) × 24 = 4.8 L/min.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q136 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 897, $s$A patient with ARDS on mechanical ventilation has a plateau pressure consistently above 30cmH2O. The nurse understands this is concerning because:$s$, $s$["It has no clinical significance", "It indicates the patient is ready for extubation", "It indicates optimal lung compliance", "It increases the risk of ventilator-induced lung injury (barotrauma), and settings should be adjusted to keep plateau pressure below this threshold"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It increases the risk of ventilator-induced lung injury (barotrauma), and settings should be adjusted to keep plateau pressure below this threshold
PLATEAU PRESSURE reflects the pressure in the ALVEOLI at the end of inspiration. When it is consistently ABOVE 30 cmH2O, the alveoli are being OVER-STRETCHED. This increases the risk of VENTILATOR-INDUCED LUNG INJURY: BAROTRAUMA (pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum, surgical emphysema) and VOLUTRAUMA (inflammation and leakage from over-distension). Lung-protective ventilation keeps plateau pressure at or below 30 cmH2O (by reducing tidal volume towards 4–6 mL/kg PBW).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It has no clinical significance — A high plateau pressure IS clinically significant.
• It indicates the patient is ready for extubation — It does not indicate readiness for extubation.
• It indicates optimal lung compliance — High plateau pressure indicates POOR (low) compliance, not optimal compliance.

⭐ Key point: Also keep the driving pressure (plateau − PEEP) at or below 15 cmH2O.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q100$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 898, $s$A female patient with ARDS is 175 cm tall and weighs 101 kg. Using lung-protective ventilation at 8 mL/kg predicted body weight, what tidal volume should be set?$s$, $s$["About 529 mL", "About 661 mL", "About 396 mL", "About 808 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: About 529 mL
Use PREDICTED BODY WEIGHT (PBW) — based on HEIGHT and SEX, not actual weight. FEMALE PBW = 45.5 + 0.91 × (height in cm − 152.4). Here: 175 − 152.4 = 22.6; 0.91 × 22.6 = 20.57; 45.5 + 20.57 = 66.07 kg. Tidal volume at 8 mL/kg = 8 × 66.07 = 528.5 ≈ 529 mL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• About 661 mL — 661 mL is 10 mL/kg PBW (too high).
• About 396 mL — 396 mL is 6 mL/kg PBW (a different target).
• About 808 mL — 808 mL is 8 mL/kg of her ACTUAL weight (101 kg) — dangerously high.

⭐ Key point: Lung-protective ventilation usually starts at 6 mL/kg PBW and stays between 4–8 mL/kg.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q103 · concept asked in 6 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 899, $s$During a spontaneous breathing trial a patient breathes at 16/min with a tidal volume of 350 mL. What is the rapid shallow breathing index (RSBI)?$s$, $s$["46 — likely to fail weaning", "46 — likely to wean successfully", "5.6 — likely to fail weaning", "22 — likely to wean successfully"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 46 — likely to wean successfully
RAPID SHALLOW BREATHING INDEX (RSBI) = RESPIRATORY RATE ÷ TIDAL VOLUME in LITRES. Here: 16 ÷ 0.35 = 45.7 ≈ 46. An RSBI BELOW 105 suggests the patient is LIKELY TO WEAN SUCCESSFULLY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 46 — likely to fail weaning — 46 is correct, but a low RSBI predicts SUCCESS, not failure.
• 5.6 — likely to fail weaning — 5.6 is a calculation error.
• 22 — likely to wean successfully — 22 is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: RSBI above 105 suggests fast, shallow breathing and likely weaning failure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q105 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 900, $s$A ventilated ARDS patient has a plateau pressure of 26 cmH2O and PEEP of 10 cmH2O. What is the driving pressure?$s$, $s$["26 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury", "16 cmH2O — within the safe target (≤ 15)", "36 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury", "16 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 16 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury
DRIVING PRESSURE = PLATEAU PRESSURE − PEEP. Here: 26 − 10 = 16 cmH2O. The target is 15 cmH2O or LESS. A driving pressure above 15 is linked to higher mortality in ARDS, so 16 is slightly TOO HIGH and increases the risk of lung injury — consider reducing the tidal volume.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 26 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury — 26 is the plateau pressure, not the driving pressure.
• 16 cmH2O — within the safe target (≤ 15) — 16 is correct, but it is ABOVE the safe target, not within it.
• 36 cmH2O — too high (> 15), increasing the risk of lung injury — 36 adds plateau and PEEP.

⭐ Key point: Lung-protective targets: tidal volume 4–8 mL/kg PBW, plateau ≤30, driving pressure ≤15 cmH2O.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q106 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 901, $s$The goal of using LOW tidal volume, LOW rate, and LOW PEEP together in ventilated asthma can be summarised as:$s$, $s$["Maximising lung volumes at all costs", "Ensuring the patient breathes exactly like a healthy person", "'Less trapping' — minimising further air trapping and barotrauma", "Achieving the highest possible peak pressures"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 'Less trapping' — minimising further air trapping and barotrauma
In ventilated ASTHMA, the narrowed airways slow exhalation, causing AIR TRAPPING (auto-PEEP), which can cause hypotension and pneumothorax. The strategy is to minimise trapping: LOW tidal volume (less air to exhale), LOW rate (more time to exhale), and LOW extrinsic PEEP (less pressure to trap air), accepting a higher CO2 (permissive hypercapnia). This can be summarised as "LESS TRAPPING".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Maximising lung volumes at all costs — Maximising lung volumes would worsen air trapping.
• Ensuring the patient breathes exactly like a healthy person — The aim is not normal breathing, but to avoid harm.
• Achieving the highest possible peak pressures — High peak pressures increase the risk of barotrauma.

⭐ Key point: Watch for auto-PEEP using the flow waveform and an expiratory hold.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q107$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 902, $s$In mechanically ventilated patients, monitoring of peak and plateau airway pressures is important because:$s$, $s$["Airway pressures have no relationship to lung mechanics", "A rising peak pressure with a normal plateau pressure suggests increased airway resistance, while a rise in both suggests decreased lung/chest wall compliance", "High airway pressures always indicate ventilator malfunction and never reflect patient pathology", "These pressures only need to be checked once per week"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A rising peak pressure with a normal plateau pressure suggests increased airway resistance, while a rise in both suggests decreased lung/chest wall compliance
PEAK pressure reflects both airway RESISTANCE and lung/chest wall COMPLIANCE. PLATEAU pressure reflects only COMPLIANCE (alveolar pressure). A RISING PEAK with a NORMAL PLATEAU suggests INCREASED AIRWAY RESISTANCE (e.g. secretions, bronchospasm, kinked or bitten tube). A rise in BOTH suggests DECREASED COMPLIANCE (e.g. pneumothorax, pulmonary oedema, ARDS, abdominal distension).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Airway pressures have no relationship to lung mechanics — Airway pressures are directly related to lung mechanics.
• High airway pressures always indicate ventilator malfunction and never reflect patient pathology — High pressures usually reflect patient problems, not just equipment.
• These pressures only need to be checked once per week — Airway pressures should be monitored continuously and recorded regularly.

⭐ Key point: Comparing peak and plateau helps quickly identify the cause of a high-pressure alarm.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q108$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 903, $s$Which complication should the ICU nurse monitor for in a mechanically ventilated patient with severe emphysema due to air trapping?$s$, $s$["Reduced airway resistance", "Hyperkalemia from hyperventilation", "Auto-PEEP (intrinsic PEEP) and breath stacking", "Immediate pulmonary oedema"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Auto-PEEP (intrinsic PEEP) and breath stacking
In SEVERE EMPHYSEMA, the airways collapse during expiration, so air leaves very slowly. If the next breath begins before the lungs have emptied, air builds up (AIR TRAPPING): AUTO-PEEP (intrinsic PEEP) and BREATH STACKING. This increases lung volume and chest pressure, causing reduced venous return (hypotension), barotrauma (pneumothorax) and increased work of breathing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reduced airway resistance — Air trapping does not reduce airway resistance.
• Hyperkalemia from hyperventilation — Hyperventilation does not cause hyperkalaemia; alkalosis lowers potassium.
• Immediate pulmonary oedema — Immediate pulmonary oedema is not the typical complication of air trapping.

⭐ Key point: Reduce auto-PEEP by lowering the rate, shortening inspiratory time, and treating bronchospasm.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q112$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 904, $s$A ventilator is set at 15 breaths/min with an inspiratory time of 0.8 seconds. What is the I:E ratio?$s$, $s$["1:4", "1:0.8", "4:1", "1:6"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1:4
STEP 1 — Total breath cycle time: 60 seconds ÷ 15 breaths = 4 seconds per breath. STEP 2 — Expiratory time = total time − inspiratory time = 4 − 0.8 = 3.2 seconds. STEP 3 — I:E ratio = 0.8 : 3.2 = 1 : 4.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1:0.8 — 1:0.8 is incorrect.
• 4:1 — 4:1 is an inverse ratio (longer inspiration than expiration).
• 1:6 — 1:6 is incorrect.

⭐ Key point: Normal I:E is about 1:2. A longer expiratory time (1:3–1:5) is used in asthma and COPD.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q115 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 905, $s$A ventilated patient has tidal volume 450 mL, plateau pressure 32 cmH2O and PEEP 12 cmH2O. What is the static lung compliance?$s$, $s$["10.2 mL/cmH2O", "22.5 mL/cmH2O", "14.1 mL/cmH2O", "44.4 mL/cmH2O"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 22.5 mL/cmH2O
STATIC COMPLIANCE measures how easily the lungs and chest wall stretch, without the effect of airflow. Formula: COMPLIANCE = TIDAL VOLUME ÷ (PLATEAU PRESSURE − PEEP). Here: 450 mL ÷ (32 − 12) cmH2O = 450 ÷ 20 = 22.5 mL/cmH2O. Normal static compliance in ventilated adults is about 50–100 mL/cmH2O, so 22.5 shows STIFF lungs (as in ARDS, pulmonary oedema, fibrosis) or a stiff chest wall/abdomen. Note the plateau pressure (32) is also above the safe limit of 30.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10.2 mL/cmH2O — 10.2 mL/cmH2O is a calculation error.
• 14.1 mL/cmH2O — 14.1 mL/cmH2O divides by the plateau pressure alone (450 ÷ 32), forgetting to subtract PEEP.
• 44.4 mL/cmH2O — 44.4 mL/cmH2O is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Falling compliance suggests worsening lung disease, pneumothorax, or abdominal distension; rising compliance suggests improvement.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q117 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 906, $s$A short course of continuous cisatracurium infusion may be used in a patient with severe ARDS and ventilator dyssynchrony because it can:$s$, $s$["Reduce oxygen consumption and improve ventilator synchrony/lung compliance in carefully selected patients", "Be safely continued indefinitely with no monitoring", "Cure the underlying lung injury", "Eliminate the need for any sedation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reduce oxygen consumption and improve ventilator synchrony/lung compliance in carefully selected patients
In SEVERE ARDS with ventilator dyssynchrony (the patient "fighting" the ventilator), a SHORT course (usually up to 48 hours) of a neuromuscular blocking agent such as CISATRACURIUM may be used in carefully selected patients. It REDUCES OXYGEN CONSUMPTION by the breathing muscles and IMPROVES VENTILATOR SYNCHRONY, allowing lung-protective ventilation and better oxygenation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Be safely continued indefinitely with no monitoring — It cannot be continued indefinitely; long use causes ICU-acquired weakness.
• Cure the underlying lung injury — It does not cure lung injury.
• Eliminate the need for any sedation — The patient must ALWAYS be adequately sedated — paralysis without sedation is cruel (awareness).

⭐ Key point: Monitor the depth of paralysis (train-of-four), give eye care, prevent pressure injury, and ensure deep sedation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q118$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 907, $s$A female patient with ARDS is 170 cm tall and weighs 97 kg. Using lung-protective ventilation at 6 mL/kg predicted body weight, what tidal volume should be set?$s$, $s$["About 582 mL", "About 615 mL", "About 369 mL", "About 492 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: About 369 mL
FEMALE PBW = 45.5 + 0.91 × (height in cm − 152.4). Here: 170 − 152.4 = 17.6; 0.91 × 17.6 = 16.0; 45.5 + 16.0 = 61.5 kg. Tidal volume at 6 mL/kg = 6 × 61.5 = 369 mL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• About 582 mL — 582 mL is 6 mL/kg of her ACTUAL weight (97 kg).
• About 615 mL — 615 mL is 10 mL/kg PBW.
• About 492 mL — 492 mL is 8 mL/kg PBW.

⭐ Key point: Always use PBW; using actual weight in obese patients causes dangerous over-inflation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q120 · concept asked in 6 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 908, $s$A patient on mechanical ventilation develops sudden increased work of breathing, tachycardia, and diaphoresis but the ventilator is not alarming. The nurse should:$s$, $s$["Immediately assess the patient directly rather than relying solely on the ventilator display, since clinical status can deteriorate before alarms trigger", "Increase the FiO2 without further assessment", "Assume the ventilator is malfunctioning and disconnect it", "Wait for an alarm to sound before intervening"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Immediately assess the patient directly rather than relying solely on the ventilator display, since clinical status can deteriorate before alarms trigger
Ventilator alarms only detect changes in machine parameters. A patient may DETERIORATE (increased work of breathing, tachycardia, sweating — signs of distress) before any alarm sounds. The nurse should IMMEDIATELY ASSESS THE PATIENT directly: airway, breathing, circulation, tube position, secretions, pain, anxiety, ventilator settings, and synchrony.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the FiO2 without further assessment — Increasing FiO2 without assessment may hide the cause.
• Assume the ventilator is malfunctioning and disconnect it — Disconnecting the ventilator without reason could cause harm.
• Wait for an alarm to sound before intervening — Waiting for an alarm delays care.

⭐ Key point: "Treat the patient, not the monitor."$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q122$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 909, $s$One of the following modes of ventilation has the risk of the patient getting respiratory alkalosis.$s$, $s$["Pressure Control Mode", "Assist Control Mode", "Controlled Mandatory Ventilation", "Synchronous Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assist Control Mode
In ASSIST-CONTROL (AC) mode, EVERY breath the patient triggers is a FULL mandatory breath (set tidal volume or pressure). If the patient breathes rapidly (from pain, anxiety, fever or sepsis), each extra breath delivers a full volume, causing HYPERVENTILATION and RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS. It can also cause air trapping.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pressure Control Mode — Pressure Control delivers a set pressure; the risk depends on the set rate.
• Controlled Mandatory Ventilation — Controlled mandatory ventilation doesn't allow patient-triggered breaths.
• Synchronous Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation — SIMV allows spontaneous breaths at the patient's own volume.

⭐ Key point: If a patient on AC breathes fast, treat the cause (pain, anxiety) and consider changing the mode.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q123$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 910, $s$A ventilated patient has tidal volume 480 mL, plateau pressure 22 cmH2O and PEEP 8 cmH2O. What is the static lung compliance?$s$, $s$["34.3 mL/cmH2O", "21.8 mL/cmH2O", "16 mL/cmH2O", "29.2 mL/cmH2O"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 34.3 mL/cmH2O
STATIC COMPLIANCE = TIDAL VOLUME ÷ (PLATEAU PRESSURE − PEEP). Here: 480 mL ÷ (22 − 8) cmH2O = 480 ÷ 14 = 34.3 mL/cmH2O. Check: 14 × 34.3 ≈ 480. ✓ This is BELOW normal (50–100), meaning the lungs are somewhat stiff. The driving pressure (22 − 8 = 14) is within the safe target (≤15).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 21.8 mL/cmH2O — 21.8 mL/cmH2O divides by the plateau alone (480 ÷ 22), forgetting to subtract PEEP.
• 16 mL/cmH2O — 16 mL/cmH2O is a calculation error.
• 29.2 mL/cmH2O — 29.2 mL/cmH2O is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Track compliance trends over time to judge whether the lung condition is improving or worsening.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q125 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 911, $s$A ventilated patient who has been sedated and immobile for 10 days is being considered for early mobilisation. The primary rationale is to:$s$, $s$["Increase the risk of complications", "Reduce ICU-acquired weakness, shorten ventilator days, and speed functional recovery", "Replace the need for any physiotherapy input", "Have no measurable effect on outcomes"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reduce ICU-acquired weakness, shorten ventilator days, and speed functional recovery
EARLY MOBILISATION in ICU patients REDUCES ICU-ACQUIRED WEAKNESS, SHORTENS the time on the VENTILATOR and in ICU, reduces DELIRIUM, and improves FUNCTIONAL RECOVERY after discharge. It is safe when the patient meets stability criteria.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the risk of complications — Early mobilisation reduces, not increases, complications.
• Replace the need for any physiotherapy input — It requires physiotherapy input, not replaces it.
• Have no measurable effect on outcomes — It has measurable benefits.

⭐ Key point: Mobilisation progresses from in-bed exercises to sitting, standing and walking.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q127$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 912, $s$The head of bed for a mechanically ventilated patient should be maintained at what elevation to reduce aspiration and VAP risk, absent contraindications?$s$, $s$["0-10 degrees", "30-45 degrees", "Flat (supine) at all times", "Trendelenburg position"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 30-45 degrees
Keeping the HEAD OF THE BED raised to 30–45 DEGREES (unless contraindicated, e.g. unstable spine or severe hypotension) uses gravity to reduce REFLUX of stomach contents up the oesophagus and their ASPIRATION past the endotracheal tube cuff into the lungs. This is a key part of the VAP prevention bundle. It also improves lung expansion and oxygenation, and can help lower intracranial pressure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0-10 degrees — 0–10 degrees is almost flat and increases reflux and aspiration.
• Flat (supine) at all times — Lying flat at all times greatly increases aspiration and VAP risk.
• Trendelenburg position — Trendelenburg (head-down) increases reflux, aspiration and intracranial pressure.

⭐ Key point: Patients often slide down the bed — check the angle regularly (many beds have an angle indicator) and document it. Other VAP bundle elements: daily sedation hold and SBT, oral care, subglottic suction, cuff pressure 20–30 cmH2O.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q129$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 913, $s$What is the most effective strategy to prevent oxygen toxicity in a mechanically ventilated patient?$s$, $s$["Increase FiO2 progressively regardless of oxygen saturation trends", "Titrate to the lowest FiO2 that maintains an acceptable target oxygen saturation, weaning as tolerated", "Maintain FiO2 at 100% throughout the admission to ensure adequate oxygenation", "Avoid all pulse oximetry and ABG monitoring to reduce patient disturbance"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Titrate to the lowest FiO2 that maintains an acceptable target oxygen saturation, weaning as tolerated
OXYGEN TOXICITY (lung damage from high FiO2 over long periods) is prevented by TITRATING to the LOWEST FiO2 that maintains the TARGET SpO2 (usually 92–96%, or 88–92% in COPD), and WEANING oxygen as tolerated. Using PEEP can help reduce the FiO2 needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase FiO2 progressively regardless of oxygen saturation trends — Increasing FiO2 regardless of saturation causes toxicity.
• Maintain FiO2 at 100% throughout the admission to ensure adequate oxygenation — 100% FiO2 for long periods causes lung damage and absorption atelectasis.
• Avoid all pulse oximetry and ABG monitoring to reduce patient disturbance — Monitoring is essential.

⭐ Key point: Prolonged FiO2 above 0.6 increases the risk of toxicity.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q130$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 914, $s$A ventilated patient has a tidal volume of 420 mL and a rate of 16 breaths/min. What is the minute ventilation?$s$, $s$["6.72 L/min", "67.2 L/min", "8.32 L/min", "26.25 L/min"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 6.72 L/min
MINUTE VENTILATION = TIDAL VOLUME × RESPIRATORY RATE. Here: 420 mL × 16 breaths/min = 6,720 mL/min = 6.72 L/min. This is within the normal adult range (about 5–8 L/min). Minute ventilation determines how much CO2 is removed — if PaCO2 is high, minute ventilation can be increased by raising the rate or tidal volume (within safe limits).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 67.2 L/min — 67.2 L/min is a decimal place error (ten times too high).
• 8.32 L/min — 8.32 L/min is a calculation error.
• 26.25 L/min — 26.25 L/min is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Alveolar ventilation (the part that reaches the alveoli) = (tidal volume − dead space) × rate — fast, shallow breathing wastes more ventilation on dead space.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q131 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 915, $s$A ventilator mode where the patient controls their own respiratory rate and tidal volume with only pressure support and PEEP applied is best described as:$s$, $s$["Pressure Support Ventilation, often combined with CPAP", "Assist-Control", "SIMV with 100% mandatory rate", "Pressure Control Ventilation with a fixed rate"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pressure Support Ventilation, often combined with CPAP
PRESSURE SUPPORT VENTILATION (PSV), often combined with CPAP/PEEP, is a SPONTANEOUS mode: the PATIENT CONTROLS their own RESPIRATORY RATE and TIDAL VOLUME; the ventilator provides set PRESSURE SUPPORT to help each breath and PEEP to keep the alveoli open. It is commonly used for weaning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assist-Control — Assist-Control delivers full mandatory breaths.
• SIMV with 100% mandatory rate — SIMV with 100% mandatory rate is fully controlled.
• Pressure Control Ventilation with a fixed rate — Pressure Control with a fixed rate is controlled by the ventilator.

⭐ Key point: PSV requires an intact respiratory drive; set apnoea backup ventilation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q133$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 916, $s$A curare cleft, a small notch or dip during the alveolar plateau of the capnogram in a mechanically ventilated patient, typically represents:$s$, $s$["The patient's spontaneous inspiratory effort occurring during a mandatory ventilator breath, suggesting inadequate paralysis or ventilator asynchrony", "Complete airway obstruction", "A ventilator malfunction requiring immediate circuit replacement", "Esophageal intubation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The patient's spontaneous inspiratory effort occurring during a mandatory ventilator breath, suggesting inadequate paralysis or ventilator asynchrony
A "CURARE CLEFT" is a small NOTCH or dip in the plateau of the capnogram. It occurs when the patient makes a SPONTANEOUS INSPIRATORY EFFORT during a mandatory breath (e.g. as neuromuscular blockade wears off), drawing fresh gas past the sensor. It suggests INADEQUATE PARALYSIS or VENTILATOR ASYNCHRONY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Complete airway obstruction — Complete airway obstruction shows no waveform.
• A ventilator malfunction requiring immediate circuit replacement — It is not a ventilator malfunction.
• Esophageal intubation — Oesophageal intubation shows disappearing waveforms.

⭐ Key point: Reassess sedation and paralysis levels; consider a train-of-four test.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q134$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 917, $s$A male patient with ARDS is 160 cm tall and weighs 82 kg. Using lung-protective ventilation at 8 mL/kg predicted body weight, what tidal volume should be set?$s$, $s$["About 455 mL", "About 341 mL", "About 656 mL", "About 569 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: About 455 mL
MALE PBW = 50 + 0.91 × (height in cm − 152.4). Here: 160 − 152.4 = 7.6; 0.91 × 7.6 = 6.9; 50 + 6.9 = 56.9 kg. Tidal volume at 8 mL/kg = 8 × 56.9 = 455 mL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• About 341 mL — 341 mL is 6 mL/kg PBW.
• About 656 mL — 656 mL is 8 mL/kg of ACTUAL weight (82 kg).
• About 569 mL — 569 mL is 10 mL/kg PBW.

⭐ Key point: Short patients have small PBWs and need small tidal volumes, regardless of actual weight.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q135 · concept asked in 6 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 918, $s$When assessing a mechanically ventilated patient's readiness for a spontaneous breathing trial, the nurse should confirm:$s$, $s$["The patient requires increasing FiO2", "The patient is deeply sedated", "Hemodynamic stability, adequate oxygenation on minimal support, and the ability to initiate spontaneous breaths", "The patient has a fever"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hemodynamic stability, adequate oxygenation on minimal support, and the ability to initiate spontaneous breaths
Before a SPONTANEOUS BREATHING TRIAL (SBT), confirm: the cause of respiratory failure is improving; HAEMODYNAMIC STABILITY (minimal or no vasopressors); ADEQUATE OXYGENATION on minimal support (e.g. FiO2 ≤0.4, PEEP ≤5–8); the ABILITY TO INITIATE SPONTANEOUS BREATHS; adequate consciousness and cough.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient requires increasing FiO2 — Increasing FiO2 requirements indicate the patient isn't ready.
• The patient is deeply sedated — Deep sedation prevents spontaneous breathing.
• The patient has a fever — Fever increases oxygen demand and is a relative contraindication.

⭐ Key point: An SBT usually lasts 30–120 minutes on low pressure support or T-piece.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q139$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 919, $s$A low-pressure or low-exhaled-volume alarm on the ventilator most often signals:$s$, $s$["A leak or disconnection in the ventilator circuit or cuff", "Breath stacking", "Bronchospasm", "Mucus plugging"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A leak or disconnection in the ventilator circuit or cuff
A LOW-PRESSURE or LOW EXHALED-VOLUME alarm means the ventilator cannot build up the expected pressure, or less gas is coming back than was delivered — GAS IS ESCAPING somewhere. The commonest causes are a LEAK or DISCONNECTION in the circuit (loose connection, disconnected tubing, open suction port, cracked humidifier chamber), a CUFF LEAK (under-inflated or burst cuff, or the tube slipped up above the vocal cords), a large air leak through a chest drain (bronchopleural fistula), or accidental extubation. The nurse should look at the patient first, then trace the circuit from the patient to the ventilator, check the cuff pressure and tube position, and bag the patient with 100% oxygen if the cause is not found quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Breath stacking — Breath stacking (air trapping) usually causes HIGH pressures and auto-PEEP, not low-pressure alarms.
• Bronchospasm — Bronchospasm causes HIGH peak pressure alarms.
• Mucus plugging — Mucus plugging causes HIGH pressure alarms (or low volume in pressure modes, but not usually a low-pressure alarm).

⭐ Key point: High-pressure alarm = obstruction or stiff lungs (secretions, biting, bronchospasm, pneumothorax, coughing). Low-pressure alarm = leak or disconnection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q141$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 920, $s$A patient on mechanical ventilation for ARDS is being managed with low tidal volumes (around 6ml/kg predicted body weight). The rationale for this strategy is primarily to:$s$, $s$["Maximise oxygen delivery regardless of pressure", "Increase patient comfort only", "Reduce the work of the ventilator motor", "Minimise ventilator-induced lung injury (volutrauma/barotrauma) in already-injured lungs"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Minimise ventilator-induced lung injury (volutrauma/barotrauma) in already-injured lungs
In ARDS, large parts of the lungs are collapsed or filled with fluid, leaving only a small area of healthy, open lung (the "baby lung"). Normal-sized breaths would OVER-STRETCH this small area, causing more damage (VOLUTRAUMA — injury from over-distension; BAROTRAUMA — pressure injury such as pneumothorax; and BIOTRAUMA — release of inflammatory chemicals). The ARDSNet trial showed that LOW TIDAL VOLUMES (6 mL/kg PREDICTED body weight) with plateau pressure ≤30 cmH2O REDUCED DEATHS. The aim is to MINIMISE VENTILATOR-INDUCED LUNG INJURY. The patient's CO2 may rise (permissive hypercapnia), which is accepted if the pH stays above about 7.20.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Maximise oxygen delivery regardless of pressure — Oxygen delivery matters, but not "regardless of pressure" — high pressures injure the lungs.
• Increase patient comfort only — Comfort is important, but it is not the main reason for low tidal volumes.
• Reduce the work of the ventilator motor — The goal is lung protection, not reducing the ventilator motor's work.

⭐ Key point: Lung-protective bundle: Vt 6 mL/kg PBW, plateau ≤30, driving pressure ≤15, adequate PEEP, prone positioning for P/F <150.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q143$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 921, $s$Which is a key intervention to prevent ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP)?$s$, $s$["Elevating the head of the bed 30–45°", "Daily chest X-rays", "Prophylactic antibiotics for all ventilated patients", "Routine 2-hourly suctioning whether needed or not"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Elevating the head of the bed 30–45°
ELEVATING the HEAD OF THE BED to 30–45° is a key part of the VAP prevention bundle. Lying flat allows stomach contents to reflux up the oesophagus and be aspirated past the endotracheal tube cuff into the lungs, where bacteria can cause pneumonia. Head-up positioning uses gravity to reduce reflux and micro-aspiration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Daily chest X-rays — Daily chest X-rays do not prevent VAP; they are used for diagnosis.
• Prophylactic antibiotics for all ventilated patients — Prophylactic antibiotics for all ventilated patients increase antibiotic resistance and are not recommended routinely.
• Routine 2-hourly suctioning whether needed or not — Routine suctioning when not needed causes trauma, hypoxia and infection risk; suction only when indicated.

⭐ Key point: The VAP bundle: head-of-bed 30–45°, daily sedation hold and SBT, oral care (chlorhexidine/toothbrushing), subglottic secretion drainage, cuff pressure 20–30 cmH2O, DVT and stress ulcer prophylaxis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q190$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 922, $s$In volume-controlled ventilation (VC-CMV), which of the following is NOT set by the nurse or doctor but changes breath by breath with the patient's lung compliance and airway resistance?$s$, $s$["Peak inspiratory pressure", "Mandatory respiratory rate", "Inspiratory flow rate", "Tidal volume"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Peak inspiratory pressure
In VOLUME-CONTROLLED ventilation, the clinician SETS the tidal volume, the mandatory rate, the inspiratory flow rate (and pattern), PEEP and FiO2. The ventilator then pushes in that exact volume every breath, and the PRESSURE needed to do so is whatever the lungs demand. So the PEAK INSPIRATORY PRESSURE is NOT set — it changes breath by breath with the patient's lung COMPLIANCE (stiffness) and AIRWAY RESISTANCE. If the lungs become stiffer (e.g. ARDS, pneumothorax) or the airway narrower (e.g. secretions, bronchospasm, biting), the peak pressure rises. This is why the nurse must watch peak pressure and set the high-pressure alarm in volume modes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mandatory respiratory rate — The mandatory respiratory rate is SET by the clinician; it is the minimum number of breaths the ventilator gives.
• Inspiratory flow rate — The inspiratory flow rate is SET in volume control (it determines how fast the volume is delivered).
• Tidal volume — Tidal volume is the main SET variable in volume control; it does not change from breath to breath.

⭐ Key point: Volume control: volume fixed, pressure varies — watch the PRESSURE. Pressure control: pressure fixed, volume varies — watch the exhaled TIDAL VOLUME.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q1$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 923, $s$A patient on pressure-controlled ventilation (inspiratory pressure 20 cmH₂O, PEEP 8) suddenly drops exhaled tidal volume from 450 mL to 280 mL. The settings are unchanged and the cuff is not leaking. The most likely cause is:$s$, $s$["A trigger sensitivity set too high, causing auto-cycling", "Reduced compliance or raised resistance (e.g. mucus plug, bronchospasm)", "A fall in respiratory drive from increased sedation", "Improved lung compliance as alveoli are recruited by PEEP"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reduced compliance or raised resistance (e.g. mucus plug, bronchospasm)
In PRESSURE-CONTROLLED ventilation, the ventilator delivers a set PRESSURE, and the TIDAL VOLUME that results depends on the patient's lung COMPLIANCE and AIRWAY RESISTANCE. If the settings have not changed and there is no leak, a sudden FALL in exhaled tidal volume (from 450 to 280 mL) means the same pressure now moves less air. This happens when compliance has DECREASED (stiffer lungs, e.g. pneumothorax, worsening oedema, abdominal distension) or RESISTANCE has INCREASED (e.g. a MUCUS PLUG, BRONCHOSPASM, kinked or bitten tube). The nurse should assess the patient: suction, check breath sounds, check the tube for kinks or biting, and look for bronchospasm or pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A trigger sensitivity set too high, causing auto-cycling — A trigger set too sensitive causes auto-cycling (extra breaths), not a smaller tidal volume with each breath.
• A fall in respiratory drive from increased sedation — Reduced respiratory drive from sedation does not reduce the volume of mandatory pressure-controlled breaths, because the ventilator still delivers the set pressure.
• Improved lung compliance as alveoli are recruited by PEEP — Improved compliance from recruitment would INCREASE the tidal volume for the same pressure, not reduce it.

⭐ Key point: In pressure modes, set a LOW tidal volume / low minute volume alarm — it is your warning that compliance or resistance has worsened.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q2$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 924, $s$Plateau pressure on a ventilated patient is obtained by:$s$, $s$["An end-inspiratory hold of 0.5–1 s with the patient passive", "Reading the highest pressure reached during a normal breath", "Reading the airway pressure at the very start of inspiration", "An end-expiratory hold of 0.5–1 s with the patient passive"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An end-inspiratory hold of 0.5–1 s with the patient passive
PLATEAU PRESSURE is the pressure in the ALVEOLI at the end of inspiration, when there is NO airflow. It is measured by doing an END-INSPIRATORY HOLD (pause) of about 0.5–1 second: the ventilator closes the valves at the end of the breath, so gas stops flowing, and the pressure due to airway resistance disappears. What remains is the pressure needed to keep the lungs stretched — the plateau pressure. The patient must be PASSIVE (not breathing against the ventilator), or the reading is inaccurate. A plateau pressure above 30 cmH2O increases the risk of lung injury (barotrauma, volutrauma).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reading the highest pressure reached during a normal breath — The highest pressure during a normal breath is the PEAK pressure, which includes the pressure needed to overcome airway resistance.
• Reading the airway pressure at the very start of inspiration — The pressure at the start of inspiration reflects PEEP (plus any auto-PEEP), not plateau pressure.
• An end-expiratory hold of 0.5–1 s with the patient passive — An END-EXPIRATORY hold measures TOTAL PEEP (set PEEP plus auto-PEEP), not plateau pressure.

⭐ Key point: Peak pressure = resistance + compliance; Plateau pressure = compliance (alveolar pressure). Keep plateau pressure at or below 30 cmH2O.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q3$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 925, $s$A ventilated patient has a tidal volume of 500 mL, a plateau pressure of 30 cmH₂O and a PEEP of 10 cmH₂O. Static lung compliance is:$s$, $s$["50 mL/cmH₂O", "12.5 mL/cmH₂O", "25 mL/cmH₂O", "16.7 mL/cmH₂O"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 25 mL/cmH₂O
STATIC COMPLIANCE measures how easily the lungs stretch. It is calculated as: Compliance = Tidal volume ÷ (Plateau pressure − PEEP). Here: 500 mL ÷ (30 − 10) cmH2O = 500 ÷ 20 = 25 mL/cmH2O. Normal static compliance in a ventilated adult is about 50–100 mL/cmH2O, so 25 means the lungs are STIFF (low compliance), as seen in ARDS, pulmonary oedema or fibrosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50 mL/cmH₂O — 50 mL/cmH2O would be obtained if you wrongly divided by 10 (forgetting to subtract PEEP correctly) — it does not match the numbers.
• 12.5 mL/cmH₂O — 12.5 mL/cmH2O would result from dividing 500 by 40 (plateau plus PEEP), which is the wrong formula.
• 16.7 mL/cmH₂O — 16.7 mL/cmH2O would result from dividing 500 by 30 (plateau alone, ignoring PEEP), which is the wrong formula.

⭐ Key point: Falling compliance over time suggests worsening lung disease, pneumothorax, or abdominal distension. Rising compliance suggests improvement or recruitment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q5 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 926, $s$Driving pressure during lung-protective ventilation is calculated as, and should ideally be kept below:$s$, $s$["Peak pressure minus PEEP; below about 30 cmH₂O", "Plateau pressure minus PEEP; below about 30 cmH₂O", "Peak pressure minus plateau pressure; below about 15 cmH₂O", "Plateau pressure minus PEEP; below about 15 cmH₂O"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Plateau pressure minus PEEP; below about 15 cmH₂O
DRIVING PRESSURE is the pressure that actually stretches the lungs during each breath. It is calculated as PLATEAU PRESSURE MINUS PEEP. It reflects the tidal volume relative to the amount of open, functioning lung ("baby lung" in ARDS). Research shows that a driving pressure ABOVE ABOUT 15 cmH2O is linked to higher mortality in ARDS. Keeping driving pressure below 15 cmH2O, by reducing tidal volume or optimising PEEP, protects the lungs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Peak pressure minus PEEP; below about 30 cmH₂O — Peak minus PEEP includes airway resistance, so it does not reflect lung stretch. 30 cmH2O is the limit for PLATEAU pressure, not driving pressure.
• Plateau pressure minus PEEP; below about 30 cmH₂O — Plateau minus PEEP is the correct formula, but the target is below about 15, not 30 cmH2O.
• Peak pressure minus plateau pressure; below about 15 cmH₂O — Peak minus plateau reflects AIRWAY RESISTANCE, not driving pressure.

⭐ Key point: Lung-protective ventilation: tidal volume 4–8 mL/kg predicted body weight (start at 6), plateau pressure ≤30 cmH2O, driving pressure ≤15 cmH2O.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q6$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 927, $s$A man 170 cm tall who weighs 90 kg develops ARDS. Using predicted body weight (male PBW = 50 + 0.91 × [height in cm − 152.4]), his initial tidal volume at 6 mL/kg is closest to:$s$, $s$["330 mL", "540 mL", "400 mL", "480 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 400 mL
Lung size depends on HEIGHT and SEX, not on actual weight (fat does not make the lungs bigger). So tidal volume is calculated from PREDICTED BODY WEIGHT (PBW). For a male: PBW = 50 + 0.91 × (height in cm − 152.4). Here: 170 − 152.4 = 17.6; 0.91 × 17.6 = 16.0; 50 + 16.0 = 66 kg. Tidal volume at 6 mL/kg = 6 × 66 = 396 mL, which is closest to 400 mL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 330 mL — 330 mL would correspond to only 5 mL/kg of PBW — lower than the starting target.
• 540 mL — 540 mL is 6 mL/kg of his ACTUAL weight (90 kg), which would over-distend his lungs.
• 480 mL — 480 mL is about 7.3 mL/kg PBW — higher than the 6 mL/kg starting target.

⭐ Key point: For females: PBW = 45.5 + 0.91 × (height in cm − 152.4). Always measure height accurately; never use actual body weight for tidal volume.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q7$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 928, $s$A ventilated patient with severe asthma has rising auto-PEEP and falling blood pressure. The most effective ventilator change is to:$s$, $s$["Raise the rate to wash out the retained CO₂ more quickly", "Lengthen inspiration by changing I:E from 1:3 to 1:1", "Lower the rate to lengthen expiration, accepting a higher PaCO₂", "Raise the tidal volume to improve alveolar ventilation"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lower the rate to lengthen expiration, accepting a higher PaCO₂
In severe asthma, the airways are narrow, so air takes a long time to get out. If the ventilator gives the next breath too soon, air gets TRAPPED (auto-PEEP), which raises chest pressure, reduces blood return to the heart and causes HYPOTENSION. The most effective change is to LOWER THE RESPIRATORY RATE, which gives more time for EXPIRATION (longer expiratory time, e.g. I:E of 1:4 or 1:5). This may cause a higher PaCO2, which is ACCEPTED (PERMISSIVE HYPERCAPNIA), as long as the pH stays safe (above about 7.2).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Raise the rate to wash out the retained CO₂ more quickly — Increasing the rate shortens expiration, worsening air trapping and hypotension.
• Lengthen inspiration by changing I:E from 1:3 to 1:1 — Lengthening inspiration (I:E 1:1) SHORTENS expiration, worsening air trapping.
• Raise the tidal volume to improve alveolar ventilation — Increasing tidal volume adds more air that cannot escape, worsening air trapping.

⭐ Key point: If BP falls severely from air trapping, briefly DISCONNECT the ventilator to let the trapped air out (and press on the chest if needed). Also treat bronchospasm aggressively.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q9$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 929, $s$The main reason PEEP improves oxygenation in ARDS is that it:$s$, $s$["It keeps alveoli open at end-expiration, raising FRC and reducing shunt", "It raises minute ventilation, so more CO₂ is cleared each breath", "It reduces the inspiratory effort needed to trigger each breath", "It lowers intrathoracic pressure, improving venous return"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It keeps alveoli open at end-expiration, raising FRC and reducing shunt
In ARDS, many alveoli COLLAPSE at the end of each breath (because the lungs are inflamed and surfactant is damaged). Blood passes these collapsed alveoli without picking up oxygen — this is called SHUNT, the main cause of hypoxia in ARDS. PEEP (positive end-expiratory pressure) keeps pressure in the lungs at the end of expiration, so it KEEPS ALVEOLI OPEN, increases the FUNCTIONAL RESIDUAL CAPACITY (FRC — the volume of air in the lungs at the end of expiration), and REDUCES SHUNT, improving oxygenation. It also prevents the repeated opening and closing of alveoli that injures the lung (atelectrauma).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It raises minute ventilation, so more CO₂ is cleared each breath — PEEP does not mainly increase minute ventilation or CO2 clearance; CO2 is controlled by rate and tidal volume.
• It reduces the inspiratory effort needed to trigger each breath — PEEP may help reduce the work of breathing in some patients (e.g. with auto-PEEP), but this is not why it improves oxygenation in ARDS.
• It lowers intrathoracic pressure, improving venous return — PEEP INCREASES intrathoracic pressure, which can REDUCE venous return (and BP).

⭐ Key point: Oxygenation is controlled mainly by FiO2 and PEEP (mean airway pressure). Ventilation (CO2) is controlled by rate and tidal volume.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q13$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 930, $s$A ventilated patient has PaO₂ 55 mmHg on FiO₂ 0.6 and PEEP 8 cmH₂O, with PaCO₂ 40 mmHg and pH 7.40. Which change will most directly improve oxygenation?$s$, $s$["Increase the PEEP stepwise (with FiO₂ as needed)", "Shorten the inspiratory time", "Increase the respiratory rate", "Increase the tidal volume to 10 mL/kg"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increase the PEEP stepwise (with FiO₂ as needed)
This patient has HYPOXAEMIA (PaO2 55 mmHg on FiO2 0.6) but NORMAL CO2 and pH. So the problem is OXYGENATION, not ventilation. Oxygenation is improved by increasing PEEP (to recruit collapsed alveoli and reduce shunt) and/or FiO2. INCREASING PEEP STEPWISE (with FiO2 as needed, e.g. following the ARDSnet PEEP/FiO2 table) will most directly improve oxygenation. Watch BP (PEEP can reduce venous return) and plateau pressure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shorten the inspiratory time — Shortening inspiratory time REDUCES mean airway pressure and may worsen oxygenation.
• Increase the respiratory rate — Increasing the respiratory rate mainly lowers CO2; the CO2 is already normal here.
• Increase the tidal volume to 10 mL/kg — 10 mL/kg tidal volume causes lung injury (volutrauma) in ARDS. Lung-protective ventilation uses about 6 mL/kg.

⭐ Key point: To raise PaO2: increase FiO2 and PEEP (mean airway pressure), consider prone positioning in moderate–severe ARDS. To lower PaCO2: increase rate or tidal volume.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q14$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 931, $s$Which mode delivers a set number of mandatory breaths synchronised to the patient's effort, while allowing spontaneous breaths in between at the patient's own tidal volume (usually pressure-supported)?$s$, $s$["Pressure support ventilation (PSV)", "Assist-control ventilation (A/C)", "Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP)", "Synchronised intermittent mandatory ventilation (SIMV)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Synchronised intermittent mandatory ventilation (SIMV)
SIMV (Synchronised Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation) delivers a SET NUMBER of MANDATORY breaths (volume or pressure controlled), timed (SYNCHRONISED) with the patient's own breathing efforts so they do not "fight" the ventilator. Between these mandatory breaths, the patient can take SPONTANEOUS breaths at their OWN tidal volume, usually helped by pressure support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pressure support ventilation (PSV) — Pressure support ventilation (PSV) gives NO mandatory breaths; every breath is started by the patient and supported by a set pressure.
• Assist-control ventilation (A/C) — In Assist-control (A/C), EVERY breath the patient triggers is a FULL mandatory breath (same set volume or pressure); there are no "spontaneous" breaths at the patient's own volume.
• Continuous positive airway pressure (CPAP) — CPAP gives continuous pressure only, with no mandatory breaths; all breaths are spontaneous.

⭐ Key point: SIMV was used for weaning, but evidence shows that PSV and spontaneous breathing trials are more effective for weaning.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q16$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 932, $s$In pressure support ventilation (PSV), the ventilator ends inspiration (cycles to expiration) when:$s$, $s$["The preset tidal volume for that breath has been delivered", "Inspiratory flow falls to a set fraction of peak flow (e.g. 25%)", "Airway pressure rises to reach the high-pressure alarm limit", "The preset inspiratory time for that breath has elapsed"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inspiratory flow falls to a set fraction of peak flow (e.g. 25%)
PRESSURE SUPPORT VENTILATION (PSV) is a spontaneous mode: the patient triggers each breath, and the ventilator supports it with a set pressure. Inspiration ends (cycles to expiration) when the patient's INSPIRATORY FLOW falls to a set FRACTION of the PEAK FLOW (commonly 25%, called the "expiratory trigger sensitivity" or "flow cycle"). This lets the patient control the length and size of each breath.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The preset tidal volume for that breath has been delivered — Delivery of a preset tidal volume ends a VOLUME-CONTROLLED breath, not a PSV breath.
• Airway pressure rises to reach the high-pressure alarm limit — Reaching the high-pressure alarm limit ends a breath for SAFETY reasons, but it is not the normal cycling mechanism in PSV.
• The preset inspiratory time for that breath has elapsed — A preset inspiratory time ends a PRESSURE-CONTROLLED breath, not a PSV breath.

⭐ Key point: In COPD, a higher flow cycle (e.g. 40–50%) shortens inspiration and gives more time to breathe out.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q17$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 933, $s$During a spontaneous breathing trial a patient breathes at 30 breaths/min with a tidal volume of 250 mL. The rapid shallow breathing index (RSBI) and its interpretation are:$s$, $s$["7.5 — weaning is likely to succeed", "120 — weaning is likely to fail", "83 — weaning is likely to succeed", "120 — weaning is likely to succeed"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 120 — weaning is likely to fail
The RAPID SHALLOW BREATHING INDEX (RSBI) = Respiratory rate (breaths/min) ÷ Tidal volume (in LITRES). Here: 30 ÷ 0.25 = 120. An RSBI ABOVE 105 suggests that WEANING IS LIKELY TO FAIL, because the patient is breathing fast and shallow — a sign of respiratory muscle fatigue.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 7.5 — weaning is likely to succeed — 7.5 is a wrong calculation (tidal volume not converted to litres properly).
• 83 — weaning is likely to succeed — 83 is a wrong calculation and would suggest likely success.
• 120 — weaning is likely to succeed — 120 is the correct calculation, but it indicates likely FAILURE, not success.

⭐ Key point: RSBI below 105 suggests weaning is more likely to succeed. RSBI is only one guide — always assess the whole patient (mental state, cough, secretions, haemodynamics, gases).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q19 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 934, $s$A heat and moisture exchanger (HME) is in use on a ventilated patient. It should NOT be used together with:$s$, $s$["A mainstream capnography adaptor", "A bacterial/viral filter on the expiratory limb", "An active heated humidifier in the same circuit", "An in-line closed suction catheter"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An active heated humidifier in the same circuit
A HEAT AND MOISTURE EXCHANGER (HME) is a passive device placed near the endotracheal tube. It traps heat and moisture from the patient's exhaled breath and returns them during the next inhalation. It must NOT be used together with an ACTIVE HEATED HUMIDIFIER in the same circuit, because the HME will become SOAKED with water, greatly increasing airway resistance and possibly BLOCKING the airway completely.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A mainstream capnography adaptor — A mainstream capnography adaptor can be used with an HME.
• A bacterial/viral filter on the expiratory limb — A bacterial/viral filter on the expiratory limb can be used with an HME.
• An in-line closed suction catheter — An in-line closed suction catheter can be used with an HME.

⭐ Key point: Change the HME as per the manufacturer's instructions (usually every 24–48 hours), and if it is soiled with secretions. Avoid HMEs in patients with thick secretions, blood in secretions, or large air leaks.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q20$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 935, $s$Inspiratory muscle training in a patient who is difficult to wean aims to:$s$, $s$["Strengthen the diaphragm and aid weaning", "Reduce cough strength to rest the lungs", "Replace the need for ventilator settings", "Increase the patient's sedation needs"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Strengthen the diaphragm and aid weaning
Patients on prolonged mechanical ventilation often develop DIAPHRAGM WEAKNESS (from disuse, sepsis and drugs). This makes weaning difficult. INSPIRATORY MUSCLE TRAINING (IMT) uses a device that provides resistance when the patient breathes in (like weight training for the breathing muscles). Regular IMT STRENGTHENS THE DIAPHRAGM and other inspiratory muscles, improving inspiratory pressure and endurance, and helping the patient WEAN from the ventilator.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reduce cough strength to rest the lungs — IMT does not reduce cough strength; stronger respiratory muscles can actually improve coughing.
• Replace the need for ventilator settings — IMT is a training method; the ventilator settings are still needed and adjusted by the team.
• Increase the patient's sedation needs — IMT does not increase sedation needs; the patient needs to be awake and cooperative to participate.

⭐ Key point: IMT is usually done in short sessions (e.g. sets of 6 breaths, several times a day) by physiotherapists, with careful monitoring of SpO2, heart rate and fatigue.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q189$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 936, $s$Early warning scores (e.g. NEWS2) help the ICU by:$s$, $s$["Replacing routine nursing observations on the ward", "Assessing the performance of the nursing staff", "Calculating drug doses for critically ill patients", "Detecting ward deterioration and triggering early review"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Detecting ward deterioration and triggering early review
EARLY WARNING SCORES (such as NEWS2) combine routine vital signs (respiratory rate, oxygen saturation, BP, pulse, consciousness level, temperature, oxygen use) into a single score. A high or rising score shows the patient is DETERIORATING and TRIGGERS EARLY REVIEW by a doctor or the critical care outreach/ICU team. This allows treatment before cardiac arrest or ICU admission becomes unavoidable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Replacing routine nursing observations on the ward — NEWS2 uses routine observations; it does not replace them.
• Assessing the performance of the nursing staff — It assesses patients, not staff performance.
• Calculating drug doses for critically ill patients — It does not calculate drug doses.

⭐ Key point: NEWS2 score of 5 or more (or 3 in any single parameter) needs urgent review; 7 or more needs emergency response.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q171$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 937, $s$The best teaching method for ICU nurses learning ventilator setup is:$s$, $s$["Watching through the window from outside the room", "A lecture in a classroom with no practical session", "Demonstration with supervised practice and return demonstration", "Reading the ventilator manual alone at home"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Demonstration with supervised practice and return demonstration
Learning to set up a ventilator is a PSYCHOMOTOR (practical, hands-on) skill. The best method is: DEMONSTRATION by an expert, then SUPERVISED PRACTICE by the learner, and finally RETURN DEMONSTRATION (the learner shows they can do it correctly). This builds competence and confidence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Watching through the window from outside the room — Watching through a window gives no hands-on practice.
• A lecture in a classroom with no practical session — A lecture without practice does not develop practical skills.
• Reading the ventilator manual alone at home — Reading a manual alone does not ensure correct, safe practice.

⭐ Key point: Simulation training is also very effective for practical ICU skills.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q197$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 938, $s$Accidental extubation is most likely when:$s$, $s$["Tube ties that are checked every shift", "An agitated patient with a poorly secured tube", "A calm, orientated and cooperative patient", "A deeply sedated patient on a ventilator"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An agitated patient with a poorly secured tube
Accidental (unplanned) extubation happens most often in an AGITATED or delirious patient who is not adequately sedated or monitored, especially if the tube is not securely fixed. The patient may pull the tube out themselves, or it may come out during turning, transfer or coughing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tube ties that are checked every shift — Tube ties checked every shift REDUCE the risk.
• A calm, orientated and cooperative patient — A calm, cooperative patient is at low risk.
• A deeply sedated patient on a ventilator — A deeply sedated patient is less likely to pull out the tube (though it can still happen during movement).

⭐ Key point: Prevent unplanned extubation: secure fixation, check tube position (length at the lips) every shift, manage pain, sedation and delirium, and take care when turning.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q89$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 939, $s$Respiratory muscle training is mainly indicated in:$s$, $s$["An active, undrained pneumothorax", "Unconscious patients only", "Weak inspiratory muscles and difficult weaning", "Patients with normal lungs and strength"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Weak inspiratory muscles and difficult weaning
Respiratory muscle training (inspiratory muscle training) uses a device that adds resistance when the patient breathes in, like weight training for the diaphragm. It strengthens WEAK INSPIRATORY MUSCLES and helps patients who are DIFFICULT TO WEAN from the ventilator, for example after long ventilation or with neuromuscular weakness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An active, undrained pneumothorax — An undrained pneumothorax is a contraindication to respiratory training and positive pressure.
• Unconscious patients only — Unconscious patients can't cooperate with training.
• Patients with normal lungs and strength — Patients with normal strength don't need it.

⭐ Key point: Other weaning aids: early mobilisation, good nutrition, correcting low phosphate and magnesium, and minimising sedation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q192$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 940, $s$The VAP bundle includes head-of-bed elevation of:$s$, $s$["30–45 degrees", "5–10 degrees only", "Flat (0 degrees) to rest the lungs", "90 degrees at all times"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 30–45 degrees
Raising the head of the bed to 30–45° reduces the chance of stomach contents flowing back up and being breathed into the lungs (micro-aspiration) past the ETT cuff. This lowers the risk of ventilator-associated pneumonia. It is a key part of the VAP bundle.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5–10 degrees only — 5–10° is too flat to prevent reflux.
• Flat (0 degrees) to rest the lungs — Lying flat (0°) increases aspiration and VAP.
• 90 degrees at all times — 90° at all times is uncomfortable, causes sacral pressure injuries and sliding down the bed, and isn't required.

⭐ Key point: Check the head-of-bed angle every shift and after turning or procedures. Keep it up unless contraindicated (unstable spine, very low BP).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q6$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 941, $s$Subglottic secretion drainage reduces VAP by:$s$, $s$["Replacing the need for oral care", "Sterilising the endotracheal tube lumen", "Removing pooled secretions above the tube cuff", "Increasing the cuff pressure to 60 cmH₂O"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Removing pooled secretions above the tube cuff
Secretions from the mouth and throat collect above the ETT cuff. They slowly leak down past the cuff into the lungs (micro-aspiration), causing VAP. A special ETT with a suction port just above the cuff allows these pooled secretions to be removed continuously or regularly, reducing VAP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Replacing the need for oral care — It doesn't replace oral care; both are part of the bundle.
• Sterilising the endotracheal tube lumen — It doesn't sterilise the inside of the tube.
• Increasing the cuff pressure to 60 cmH₂O — Cuff pressure should be 20–30 cmH₂O; 60 cmH₂O would damage the trachea.

⭐ Key point: Use a subglottic suction ETT for patients expected to be ventilated more than 48–72 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q7$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 942, $s$Ventilator circuits should be changed:$s$, $s$["Every 6 hours routinely, by the night nurse", "Before every suction, to keep them clean", "Every day at midnight, whatever their state", "Only when visibly soiled or malfunctioning"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Only when visibly soiled or malfunctioning
Studies show that changing ventilator circuits routinely does NOT reduce VAP. Each change opens the system, can spill contaminated condensate into the patient's airway, and increases infection risk. Circuits should be changed only when VISIBLY SOILED or MALFUNCTIONING (or per the manufacturer's instructions).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Every 6 hours routinely, by the night nurse — Changing every 6 hours is unnecessary and increases risk.
• Before every suction, to keep them clean — Changing before every suction is unnecessary and harmful.
• Every day at midnight, whatever their state — Changing every day regardless of state is also unnecessary.

⭐ Key point: Drain circuit condensate away from the patient, and use HME filters or heated humidifiers correctly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q8$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 943, $s$Selective digestive decontamination aims to reduce:$s$, $s$["Diarrhoea caused by enteral feeds", "Gram-negative colonisation and VAP", "Hospital costs and length of stay only", "Gastric acid production and ulcers"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Gram-negative colonisation and VAP
Selective digestive decontamination (SDD) gives non-absorbable antibiotics to the mouth and gut (plus a short course of IV antibiotics) to kill potentially harmful GRAM-NEGATIVE bacteria and yeasts that colonise the gut and throat of ICU patients. This reduces colonisation, VAP and possibly deaths. Its use varies by country, because of concerns about resistance.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diarrhoea caused by enteral feeds — SDD doesn't aim to reduce feed-related diarrhoea.
• Hospital costs and length of stay only — Cost reduction is not its main aim.
• Gastric acid production and ulcers — SDD doesn't reduce stomach acid.

⭐ Key point: SDD is used in some European ICUs. Most units rely on standard VAP bundles.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q20$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 944, $s$PEEP settings in ventilated life-threatening asthma should be:$s$, $s$["Equal to peak inspiratory pressure", "Minimal (<5cmH2O)", "Zero at all times", "High (>15cmH2O)"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Minimal (<5cmH2O)
In severe asthma, air gets trapped in the lungs because the narrowed airways can't empty fully, creating INTRINSIC PEEP (auto-PEEP). Adding a lot of external PEEP would increase lung over-inflation, raise intrathoracic pressure, reduce venous return and cause hypotension or barotrauma. External PEEP is therefore kept MINIMAL (below 5 cmH₂O).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Equal to peak inspiratory pressure — Setting PEEP equal to the peak pressure is dangerous.
• Zero at all times — Zero PEEP at all times isn't the usual approach; a little PEEP may help.
• High (>15cmH2O) — High PEEP (above 15) would worsen air trapping.

⭐ Key point: In asthma ventilation: low rate, small tidal volume, long expiratory time, low PEEP, and permissive hypercapnia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q61$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 945, $s$The negative inspiratory force required for weaning readiness is at least:$s$, $s$["-100cmH2O", "-20cmH2O", "-5cmH2O", "Not relevant to weaning"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: -20cmH2O
Negative inspiratory force (NIF, or maximum inspiratory pressure) measures how strongly the patient can breathe in against a closed airway. A NIF of at least −20 to −30 cmH₂O (more negative is stronger) suggests the breathing muscles are strong enough to wean.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• -100cmH2O — −100 cmH₂O is far stronger than needed and is not the minimum.
• -5cmH2O — −5 cmH₂O is very weak and predicts failure.
• Not relevant to weaning — NIF IS relevant to weaning; it assesses muscle strength.

⭐ Key point: Weaning readiness: FiO₂ 0.4–0.5 or less, PEEP 5–8 or less, a stable BP, awake, cough present, RSBI below 105, NIF −20 or more negative.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q62$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 946, $s$An FiO₂ below what value is generally required before considering weaning?$s$, $s$["<0.5", "<0.8", "FiO₂ is irrelevant to weaning", "<0.2"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: <0.5
Before weaning, the patient's oxygenation should be good on modest support: FiO₂ BELOW 0.5 (50%) (ideally 0.4 or less) and PEEP of 8 cmH₂O or less, with a P/F ratio above 150–200. Needing less oxygen shows the lungs are recovering and could cope with less support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• <0.8 — An FiO₂ of 0.8 is too high; the patient still needs lots of oxygen.
• FiO₂ is irrelevant to weaning — FiO₂ IS relevant; it is one of the key criteria.
• <0.2 — 0.2 is below room air (0.21), so this is impossible.

⭐ Key point: Check the readiness criteria every day, then do a spontaneous breathing trial (SBT).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q63$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 947, $s$One of the following is a risk of keeping high PEEP$s$, $s$["Hypothermia", "Hypertension", "Hypotension", "Hyperthermia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypotension
PEEP keeps pressure in the chest raised even at the end of expiration. HIGH PEEP squeezes the great veins and heart, REDUCING VENOUS RETURN to the right heart. Less blood reaches the heart, so cardiac output and BP fall (HYPOTENSION), especially in hypovolaemic patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypothermia — PEEP doesn't directly cause hypothermia.
• Hypertension — PEEP tends to LOWER BP, not raise it.
• Hyperthermia — PEEP doesn't cause hyperthermia.

⭐ Key point: Other risks of high PEEP: barotrauma (pneumothorax), raised ICP, and reduced kidney and liver blood flow. Give fluids carefully if BP falls.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q64$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 948, $s$Lung-protective ventilation in ARDS uses a tidal volume of:$s$, $s$["12 mL/kg of actual body weight", "6 mL/kg of predicted body weight", "15 mL/kg of predicted weight", "3 mL/kg of actual weight"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 6 mL/kg of predicted body weight
In ARDS, only part of the lung works ("baby lung"), so large breaths over-stretch and injure the healthy parts (volutrauma). Lung-protective ventilation uses a tidal volume of 6 mL/kg PREDICTED (ideal) body weight, with a plateau pressure of 30 cmH₂O or less. This approach (from the ARDSNet trial) reduces deaths.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 12 mL/kg of actual body weight — 12 mL/kg is too big and harms the lungs, and actual weight is the wrong measure.
• 15 mL/kg of predicted weight — 15 mL/kg is dangerously large.
• 3 mL/kg of actual weight — 3 mL/kg of actual weight is too small for routine ventilation.

⭐ Key point: Calculate predicted body weight from height and sex: men = 50 + 0.91 × (height in cm − 152.4); women = 45.5 + 0.91 × (height in cm − 152.4).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q65 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 949, $s$Plateau pressure is measured by:$s$, $s$["An expiratory hold at end-expiration", "Reading the peak airway pressure", "Disconnecting the patient briefly", "An inspiratory hold at end-inspiration"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An inspiratory hold at end-inspiration
Plateau pressure is measured by an INSPIRATORY HOLD (pause of about 0.5–1 second) at the END of INSPIRATION. During the pause there is no airflow, so the airway resistance part disappears, and the pressure reflects the pressure in the ALVEOLI. It shows lung COMPLIANCE (stiffness). Keep it at 30 cmH₂O or less.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An expiratory hold at end-expiration — An expiratory hold measures auto-PEEP (intrinsic PEEP), not plateau pressure.
• Reading the peak airway pressure — Peak pressure includes airway resistance, so it is higher than plateau.
• Disconnecting the patient briefly — Disconnecting the patient doesn't measure plateau pressure.

⭐ Key point: Driving pressure = plateau pressure − PEEP. Keep it below 15 cmH₂O to protect the lungs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q66$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 950, $s$Prone positioning in severe ARDS improves:$s$, $s$["Sedation needs, by improving comfort", "Renal function, by better perfusion", "Oxygenation through better V/Q matching", "Cardiac output, by direct compression"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Oxygenation through better V/Q matching
In severe ARDS, the back (dependent) parts of the lungs collapse under the weight of the heart and lungs when the patient lies on their back. Turning the patient PRONE (face down) opens these areas, makes ventilation more even, and better matches ventilation with blood flow (V/Q matching), improving OXYGENATION. It also reduces lung injury and lowers mortality.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sedation needs, by improving comfort — It isn't used to reduce sedation needs; patients often need more.
• Renal function, by better perfusion — It isn't used to improve kidney function.
• Cardiac output, by direct compression — It doesn't work by compressing the heart.

⭐ Key point: Prone for at least 16 hours a day when the P/F ratio is below 150. Watch pressure areas (face, chest, knees), tubes and lines during turning.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q67$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 951, $s$A P/F ratio of 90 mmHg on PEEP 10 indicates:$s$, $s$["Mild ARDS", "Normal lung function", "Moderate ARDS", "Severe ARDS"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Severe ARDS
The Berlin definition grades ARDS by P/F ratio with PEEP of 5 cmH₂O or more: MILD 200–300, MODERATE 100–200, SEVERE 100 or less. A P/F of 90 on PEEP 10 is SEVERE ARDS. Severe ARDS has the highest death rate and usually needs prone positioning, neuromuscular blockade, and possibly ECMO.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild ARDS — Mild ARDS is P/F 200–300.
• Normal lung function — Normal lungs have a P/F of about 400–500.
• Moderate ARDS — Moderate ARDS is P/F 100–200.

⭐ Key point: P/F ratio = PaO₂ (mmHg) ÷ FiO₂ (as a decimal). Example: PaO₂ 72 on FiO₂ 0.8 = 90.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q68$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 952, $s$Pressure support ventilation means:$s$, $s$["No breaths are delivered unless the patient is apnoeic", "Each spontaneous breath is supported by a set pressure", "All breaths are mandatory at a set rate and volume", "A fixed tidal volume is forced in with each breath"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Each spontaneous breath is supported by a set pressure
Pressure support ventilation (PSV) is a spontaneous mode: the patient starts every breath and decides the rate. The ventilator adds a SET PRESSURE to SUPPORT each breath, making breathing easier. The patient controls their own rate, inspiratory time and, partly, the tidal volume. It is often used for weaning by gradually reducing the support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No breaths are delivered unless the patient is apnoeic — PSV doesn't deliver breaths if the patient stops breathing (apnoea backup is a safety feature, not the mode).
• All breaths are mandatory at a set rate and volume — Mandatory breaths at a set rate and volume describe volume-control mandatory ventilation.
• A fixed tidal volume is forced in with each breath — A fixed tidal volume forced in with each breath describes volume control.

⭐ Key point: Watch for apnoea in PSV, because there are no set breaths. Make sure apnoea alarms and backup ventilation are set.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q69$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 953, $s$A spontaneous breathing trial is considered failed if:$s$, $s$["RR over 35, SpO₂ under 90%, rising HR and distress", "SpO₂ is 96% on 40% oxygen with good air entry", "The patient is calm with a respiratory rate of 18", "The patient obeys commands and coughs well"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: RR over 35, SpO₂ under 90%, rising HR and distress
A spontaneous breathing trial (SBT) tests whether the patient can breathe without much help. It is FAILED if the patient shows signs of struggling: respiratory rate above 35, SpO₂ below 90%, heart rate rising by more than 20%, BP changes, sweating, agitation, accessory muscle use or paradoxical breathing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• SpO₂ is 96% on 40% oxygen with good air entry — SpO₂ of 96% on 40% oxygen with good air entry suggests success.
• The patient is calm with a respiratory rate of 18 — A calm patient with RR 18 is passing the trial.
• The patient obeys commands and coughs well — Obeying commands and coughing well are good signs.

⭐ Key point: If the SBT fails, return to full support, rest the patient, find and fix the cause, and try again the next day.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q70$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 954, $s$The rapid shallow breathing index (RR/VT in litres) predicting weaning success is below:$s$, $s$["20", "500", "300", "105"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 105
The rapid shallow breathing index (RSBI) = respiratory rate ÷ tidal volume in LITRES. Patients who are failing breathe fast and shallow, giving a HIGH RSBI. An RSBI BELOW 105 predicts weaning SUCCESS. Example: RR 25, VT 0.4 L → 25 ÷ 0.4 = 62.5 (good).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20 — 20 is far below the cut-off.
• 500 — 500 is far above it.
• 300 — 300 is far above it.

⭐ Key point: An RSBI above 105 suggests the patient is likely to fail weaning. Use it together with clinical assessment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q71$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 955, $s$High peak pressure with normal plateau pressure suggests:$s$, $s$["Reduced lung compliance, as in ARDS or oedema", "A large pneumothorax on the ventilated side", "Increased airway resistance (secretions, bronchospasm)", "Normal lungs with a normal airway"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increased airway resistance (secretions, bronchospasm)
Peak pressure includes the pressure needed to push air through the AIRWAYS (resistance) plus the pressure to inflate the ALVEOLI (compliance). Plateau pressure reflects the alveoli only. If PEAK is high but PLATEAU is normal, the problem is in the airways: increased RESISTANCE from secretions, bronchospasm, a kinked or bitten tube, or a narrow ETT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reduced lung compliance, as in ARDS or oedema — Low compliance (ARDS, oedema) raises BOTH peak and plateau.
• A large pneumothorax on the ventilated side — A large pneumothorax reduces compliance, raising both peak and plateau.
• Normal lungs with a normal airway — Normal lungs and airway would give normal peak and plateau pressures.

⭐ Key point: High peak with normal plateau = airway problem (suction, bronchodilator, check the tube). High peak with high plateau = lung or chest problem.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q73$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 956, $s$High-flow nasal oxygen provides:$s$, $s$["Heated, humidified oxygen with a small PEEP effect", "Full mechanical ventilation without a tube", "Negative pressure ventilation of the chest", "Only low-flow dry oxygen through the nose"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Heated, humidified oxygen with a small PEEP effect
High-flow nasal oxygen (HFNO) delivers HEATED and HUMIDIFIED oxygen at high flow rates (30–60 L/min) through nasal prongs. The high flow meets the patient's breathing demand, gives an accurate FiO₂ (21–100%), washes CO₂ out of the upper airway, and produces a small PEEP effect (about 3–5 cmH₂O). Humidification makes it comfortable and keeps secretions thin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Full mechanical ventilation without a tube — It is NOT full mechanical ventilation.
• Negative pressure ventilation of the chest — It is not negative pressure ventilation.
• Only low-flow dry oxygen through the nose — It gives HIGH flow, heated and humidified, not low-flow dry oxygen.

⭐ Key point: HFNO is used for type 1 respiratory failure, after extubation, and to pre-oxygenate for intubation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q77$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 957, $s$A ventilated patient's tidal volume falls with low pressure alarms. The nurse checks first for:$s$, $s$["A circuit disconnection or cuff leak", "Coughing against the ventilator", "Severe bronchospasm", "A blocked endotracheal tube"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A circuit disconnection or cuff leak
A low tidal volume with a LOW-PRESSURE alarm means air is ESCAPING from the system. The most likely causes: a CIRCUIT DISCONNECTION (the commonest), a CUFF LEAK (the ETT cuff is deflated or damaged), a leak at the humidifier or water trap, or the tube displaced above the vocal cords.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Coughing against the ventilator — Coughing causes a HIGH-pressure alarm.
• Severe bronchospasm — Bronchospasm causes a HIGH-pressure alarm.
• A blocked endotracheal tube — A blocked ETT causes a HIGH-pressure alarm.

⭐ Key point: Low pressure = leak (check from the patient to the ventilator: tube position, cuff, connections). High pressure = obstruction.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q78$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 958, $s$Daily sedation interruption mainly helps to:$s$, $s$["Reduce ventilation time and ICU stay", "Increase the rate of delirium", "Increase the rate of VAP", "Prolong the time to weaning"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reduce ventilation time and ICU stay
Daily sedation interruption ("sedation hold" or "wake up and breathe") stops sedatives once a day to let the patient wake up. This shows whether they still need sedation and allows weaning assessment. It REDUCES the time on the ventilator and the length of ICU stay, without increasing harm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the rate of delirium — It doesn't increase delirium; less sedation actually reduces delirium.
• Increase the rate of VAP — It REDUCES VAP risk.
• Prolong the time to weaning — It SHORTENS, not prolongs, the time to weaning.

⭐ Key point: Pair daily sedation holds with spontaneous breathing trials (the ABCDE bundle).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q79$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 959, $s$A cuff leak test before extubation assesses:$s$, $s$["Cardiac output", "Laryngeal oedema risk", "Lung compliance", "Gas exchange in lungs"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Laryngeal oedema risk
The cuff leak test checks for swelling of the larynx (laryngeal oedema) before extubation. The ETT cuff is deflated: if air leaks around the tube (a measurable volume, more than about 110 mL or 10–25% of the tidal volume), there is space between the tube and the airway. If there is NO LEAK, the airway may be swollen, with a risk of STRIDOR after extubation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cardiac output — It doesn't measure cardiac output.
• Lung compliance — It doesn't measure lung compliance.
• Gas exchange in lungs — It doesn't measure gas exchange.

⭐ Key point: If there is no cuff leak, consider steroids (methylprednisolone) for 12–24 hours before extubation and have reintubation equipment ready.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q80$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 960, $s$Hypophosphataemia in ICU may cause:$s$, $s$["Hypercalcaemia with kidney stones and polyuria", "Respiratory muscle weakness and failed weaning", "Tetany only, without any muscle weakness", "Severe hypertension with headache and blurring"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory muscle weakness and failed weaning
Phosphate is needed to make ATP (energy) for muscles, including the diaphragm. Low phosphate causes RESPIRATORY MUSCLE WEAKNESS, FAILED WEANING from the ventilator, heart failure, confusion, seizures and red cell breakdown. It is common in refeeding syndrome, DKA treatment (insulin drives phosphate into cells), alcoholism and CRRT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypercalcaemia with kidney stones and polyuria — Low phosphate doesn't cause high calcium with kidney stones.
• Tetany only, without any muscle weakness — Low phosphate causes weakness, not just tetany (low calcium causes tetany).
• Severe hypertension with headache and blurring — Low phosphate doesn't cause severe hypertension.

⭐ Key point: Check phosphate in patients who are hard to wean. Normal phosphate is 0.8–1.5 mmol/L.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q94$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 961, $s$The mode of Mechanical Ventilation which has the risk of producing Hyper ventilation and Respiratory Alkalosis is:$s$, $s$["Controlled Mandatory Ventilation", "Assist Control Mode", "Synchronous Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation", "Pressure Control Mode"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assist Control Mode
In Assist-Control (AC) ventilation, EVERY breath the patient triggers gets a FULL set tidal volume (or pressure). If the patient breathes fast (pain, anxiety, sepsis, fever), each fast breath gets a full-size breath, so minute ventilation becomes very large, CO₂ is blown off, and RESPIRATORY ALKALOSIS can develop.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Controlled Mandatory Ventilation — Controlled mandatory ventilation delivers only the set rate (the patient can't trigger extra).
• Synchronous Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation — SIMV allows extra spontaneous breaths, but they aren't full-sized machine breaths.
• Pressure Control Mode — Pressure control limits pressure; it can cause alkalosis too, but AC is the classic answer.

⭐ Key point: If a patient on AC is over-breathing, look for and treat the cause (pain, agitation, sepsis), or consider changing the mode.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q25$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 962, $s$HFCC (High-Frequency Chest Wall Oscillation) uses an air-filled vest that squeezes the chest at a rate of:$s$, $s$["50–60 times/second", "1–2 times/second", "Once per minute", "5–20 times/second"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 5–20 times/second
High-frequency chest wall oscillation (HFCWO) uses an inflatable VEST connected to a machine that rapidly inflates and deflates it, squeezing the chest about 5–20 TIMES PER SECOND (5–20 Hz). The rapid vibrations loosen secretions and move them towards the larger airways, where they can be coughed out. It is used in cystic fibrosis and bronchiectasis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50–60 times/second — 50–60 times per second is far faster than the vest works.
• 1–2 times/second — 1–2 times per second is too slow.
• Once per minute — Once per minute is far too slow.

⭐ Key point: HFCWO is an airway clearance technique that doesn't need a physiotherapist, so patients can use it at home.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q26$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 963, $s$The nurse is caring for a patient with a UWSD. The nurse notices the water-seal chamber has a small amount of intermittent bubbling. This is most likely caused by:$s$, $s$["A blockage in the drainage system", "A kink in the tubing", "The patient's respirations", "A large air leak"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The patient's respirations
A small amount of INTERMITTENT bubbling in the water-seal chamber, especially when the patient coughs or breathes out, is NORMAL when draining a pneumothorax. It shows air is still leaving the pleural space as the patient breathes. It usually decreases as the lung heals.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A blockage in the drainage system — A blockage would STOP bubbling and swinging.
• A kink in the tubing — A kink would also stop drainage.
• A large air leak — A LARGE air leak would cause CONTINUOUS, vigorous bubbling.

⭐ Key point: Continuous bubbling = a significant air leak (from the lung or a loose connection). No swing = blockage or the lung has fully expanded.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q31$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 964, $s$Is initiated by the patient, but controlled and ended by the ventilator.$s$, $s$["Mandatory breath", "Spontaneous breath", "Assisted breath", "None of them"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assisted breath
An ASSISTED breath is STARTED (triggered) by the patient's effort, but the ventilator then CONTROLS how much it delivers (set volume or pressure) and ENDS (cycles) the breath. Assist-control and SIMV mandatory breaths can be assisted breaths.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mandatory breath — A mandatory breath is started, controlled and ended by the ventilator.
• Spontaneous breath — A spontaneous breath is started and ended by the patient (perhaps with pressure support).
• None of them — One of the options (assisted breath) fits.

⭐ Key point: Breath types: mandatory (machine controls all), assisted (patient triggers, machine controls), spontaneous (patient controls).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q32$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 965, $s$A ventilated ARDS patient has plateau pressure 35 cmH₂O on tidal volume 8 mL/kg. The priority change is to:$s$, $s$["Switch to high inspiratory flow to shorten inspiration", "Reduce tidal volume toward 6 mL/kg predicted body weight", "Increase tidal volume to improve CO₂ clearance", "Remove all PEEP to lower the plateau pressure"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reduce tidal volume toward 6 mL/kg predicted body weight
Lung-protective ventilation in ARDS aims for a plateau pressure of 30 cmH₂O or LESS. A plateau of 35 means the lungs are being over-stretched, risking volutrauma. The priority is to REDUCE the tidal volume to 6 mL/kg PREDICTED body weight (or lower, down to 4 mL/kg if needed), accepting a higher CO₂ (permissive hypercapnia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Switch to high inspiratory flow to shorten inspiration — High inspiratory flow doesn't lower plateau pressure.
• Increase tidal volume to improve CO₂ clearance — Increasing tidal volume would RAISE the plateau further.
• Remove all PEEP to lower the plateau pressure — Removing PEEP would cause alveolar collapse and worsen hypoxia.

⭐ Key point: Plateau pressure ≤30, driving pressure (plateau − PEEP) ≤15, and tidal volume 6 mL/kg predicted body weight.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q37$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 966, $s$On a volume-controlled ventilator, peak pressure rises sharply but plateau pressure is unchanged. The likely cause is:$s$, $s$["Raised airway resistance, e.g. secretions, kinked tube or bronchospasm", "A large air leak around a deflated cuff", "Reduced lung compliance, e.g. pulmonary oedema or ARDS", "Tension pneumothorax with lung collapse"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Raised airway resistance, e.g. secretions, kinked tube or bronchospasm
PEAK pressure = pressure to overcome airway RESISTANCE + pressure to inflate the alveoli. PLATEAU pressure = alveolar pressure only (no flow, so no resistance). If PEAK rises but PLATEAU is unchanged, the extra pressure is due to RAISED AIRWAY RESISTANCE: secretions, a kinked or bitten tube, or bronchospasm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A large air leak around a deflated cuff — An air leak would cause LOW pressures.
• Reduced lung compliance, e.g. pulmonary oedema or ARDS — Reduced compliance (oedema, ARDS) raises BOTH peak and plateau.
• Tension pneumothorax with lung collapse — Tension pneumothorax reduces compliance, raising both peak and plateau.

⭐ Key point: High peak, normal plateau → suction, check the tube, give a bronchodilator.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q38$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 967, $s$Intrinsic PEEP (auto-PEEP) in a ventilated asthmatic is best measured by:$s$, $s$["An end-inspiratory hold manoeuvre", "An end-expiratory hold manoeuvre", "Measuring peak flow before suction", "A cuff leak test"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An end-expiratory hold manoeuvre
Intrinsic PEEP (auto-PEEP) is pressure left in the alveoli at the end of expiration because air is trapped. It's measured with an END-EXPIRATORY HOLD: the ventilator closes the valves at the end of expiration, so pressure in the alveoli equalises with the circuit and can be read. Total PEEP − set PEEP = intrinsic PEEP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An end-inspiratory hold manoeuvre — An end-INSPIRATORY hold measures PLATEAU pressure.
• Measuring peak flow before suction — Peak flow isn't measured on a ventilated patient this way.
• A cuff leak test — The cuff leak test checks for laryngeal swelling.

⭐ Key point: Treat auto-PEEP: lower the rate, shorten inspiration (allow more expiratory time), and give bronchodilators.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q40$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 968, $s$The nurse is monitoring the drainage from a UWSD. The nurse should report which of the following findings to the physician immediately?$s$, $s$["A sudden increase of bloody drainage", "Clear, serous fluid", "The presence of a small amount of drainage", "Intermittent bubbling in the water-seal chamber"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A sudden increase of bloody drainage
A SUDDEN INCREASE in BLOODY (especially bright red) drainage from a chest drain suggests active BLEEDING (haemorrhage), for example from a damaged vessel. It can quickly cause shock. Report it IMMEDIATELY (typically more than 100–200 mL/hour, or more than 1500 mL at insertion).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Clear, serous fluid — Clear serous fluid is normal drainage for an effusion.
• The presence of a small amount of drainage — A small amount of drainage is expected.
• Intermittent bubbling in the water-seal chamber — Intermittent bubbling (with breathing or coughing) is normal with a pneumothorax.

⭐ Key point: Record drainage hourly. Also report sudden cessation with distress (blockage) or new continuous bubbling (air leak).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q24$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 969, $s$Tidal volume 500 mL, plateau pressure 30 cmH₂O and PEEP 10 cmH₂O give a static compliance of:$s$, $s$["12.5 mL/cmH₂O", "16.7 mL/cmH₂O", "50 mL/cmH₂O", "25 mL/cmH₂O"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 25 mL/cmH₂O
Static compliance = tidal volume ÷ (plateau pressure − PEEP) = 500 ÷ (30 − 10) = 500 ÷ 20 = 25 mL/cmH₂O. Normal compliance is about 50–100 mL/cmH₂O, so this shows STIFF lungs (as in ARDS or pulmonary oedema).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 12.5 mL/cmH₂O — 12.5 comes from a calculation error.
• 16.7 mL/cmH₂O — 16.7 = 500 ÷ 30, which ignores PEEP.
• 50 mL/cmH₂O — 50 mL/cmH₂O is normal, not the result here.

⭐ Key point: Driving pressure = plateau − PEEP (here 20). Keep it below 15 if possible to protect the lungs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q37 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 970, $s$In ARDS, driving pressure (plateau minus PEEP) is linked to survival when kept:$s$, $s$["Above about 25 cmH₂O", "Above the peak pressure", "Below about 15 cmH₂O", "Exactly equal to the PEEP"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Below about 15 cmH₂O
DRIVING PRESSURE (plateau pressure minus PEEP) reflects how much the lungs are stretched with each breath relative to their size. Studies show that keeping it BELOW about 15 cmH₂O is linked to BETTER SURVIVAL in ARDS. A high driving pressure means more stress and injury to the lungs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Above about 25 cmH₂O — Above 25 cmH₂O is linked to higher mortality.
• Above the peak pressure — Driving pressure can't be above peak pressure.
• Exactly equal to the PEEP — Driving pressure equal to PEEP makes no sense.

⭐ Key point: Lower the driving pressure by reducing tidal volume or optimising PEEP.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q38$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 971, $s$Prone positioning in moderate–severe ARDS is recommended when PaO₂/FiO₂ is below 150 mmHg, for at least:$s$, $s$["1 hour a day", "4 hours a week", "16 hours a day", "30 minutes after each meal"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 16 hours a day
In moderate to severe ARDS (P/F ratio below 150 mmHg), PRONE positioning (lying face down) improves oxygenation and survival. It must be done for long periods: at least 16 HOURS a day. It works by opening collapsed lung areas at the back and improving V/Q matching.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1 hour a day — 1 hour a day is too short to work.
• 4 hours a week — 4 hours a week is far too short.
• 30 minutes after each meal — 30 minutes after meals is unrelated to ARDS treatment.

⭐ Key point: Nursing in prone position: protect the eyes, face and pressure areas, secure the ETT and lines, and continue enteral feeding with caution.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q39$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 972, $s$A ventilated patient has PaO₂ 90 mmHg on FiO₂ 0.6 with PEEP 8. By the Berlin definition this ARDS is:$s$, $s$["Severe (P/F ratio below 100)", "Moderate (P/F ratio 100–200)", "Not ARDS, as P/F is above 300", "Mild (P/F ratio 200–300)"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Moderate (P/F ratio 100–200)
P/F ratio = PaO₂ ÷ FiO₂ = 90 ÷ 0.6 = 150 mmHg. By the Berlin definition (with PEEP 5 or above): mild 200–300, MODERATE 100–200, severe 100 or less. A P/F of 150 means MODERATE ARDS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Severe (P/F ratio below 100) — Severe ARDS is a P/F of 100 or less.
• Not ARDS, as P/F is above 300 — The P/F is 150, not above 300.
• Mild (P/F ratio 200–300) — Mild ARDS is 200–300.

⭐ Key point: A P/F below 150 means moderate to severe ARDS: consider prone positioning and neuromuscular blockade.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q40$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 973, $s$A patient is on the AC volume ventilation after open heart surgery, which of the following is to be taken care of during this mode to pneumothorax?$s$, $s$["keeping the patient sedated", "high peak inspiratory pressure", "delivering low PEEP", "patient effort to breath more comfortable"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: high peak inspiratory pressure
In assist-control volume ventilation, the machine forces a set volume into the lungs. If the lungs are stiff or the volume is too large, the PEAK INSPIRATORY PRESSURE rises, increasing the risk of BAROTRAUMA and PNEUMOTHORAX (especially after open heart surgery, with fragile lungs and chest drains). Watch the peak and plateau pressures closely.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• keeping the patient sedated — Sedation is used for comfort, but it isn't the main way to prevent pneumothorax.
• delivering low PEEP — Low PEEP doesn't cause pneumothorax (high PEEP increases the risk).
• patient effort to breath more comfortable — Patient comfort with breathing isn't the key monitoring point for pneumothorax.

⭐ Key point: Signs of pneumothorax on a ventilator: rising peak pressures, falling SpO₂, falling BP, absent breath sounds on one side.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q42$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 974, $s$The process of attracting qualified persons to apply for jobs that are open is referred to as:$s$, $s$["Training", "Recruitment", "Transfer", "Human resource management"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Recruitment
RECRUITMENT is the process of ATTRACTING qualified people to APPLY for vacant jobs, through advertising, job fairs, referrals, or recruitment agencies. It is followed by SELECTION (choosing the best applicant through interviews and tests).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Training — Training develops existing staff skills.
• Transfer — Transfer moves staff to another position or unit.
• Human resource management — Human resource management is the whole field (recruitment, training, appraisal, welfare), not the specific process.

⭐ Key point: HR process: workforce planning → recruitment → selection → orientation → training → appraisal → retention.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q169$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 975, $s$Which of the following mode of Mechanical Ventilation cannot be used for weaning off patients from Mechanical Ventilation:$s$, $s$["Synchronized intermittent mandatory ventilation (SIMV)", "Pressure support ventilation (PSV)", "Controlled Mechanical ventilation (CMV)", "Assist-control ventilation (ACV)"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Controlled Mechanical ventilation (CMV)
CONTROLLED MECHANICAL VENTILATION (CMV) delivers ALL breaths at a set rate and volume; the patient can't trigger breaths or breathe on their own. Because the patient does no breathing work, CMV can't be used to assess or train the breathing muscles, so it is unsuitable for weaning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Synchronized intermittent mandatory ventilation (SIMV) — SIMV allows spontaneous breaths between mandatory breaths; it has been used for weaning.
• Pressure support ventilation (PSV) — PSV supports the patient's own breaths; it is the main weaning mode.
• Assist-control ventilation (ACV) — Assist-control lets the patient trigger breaths and can be used before weaning trials.

⭐ Key point: Weaning usually uses pressure support ventilation with gradual reduction, or T-piece spontaneous breathing trials.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q22$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 976, $s$The peak inspiratory pressure safety limit during mechanical ventilation for asthma is:$s$, $s$["<20cmH2O", "<60cmH2O", "No limit needed", "<40cmH2O"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: <40cmH2O
In ventilated severe asthma, the lungs are over-inflated and fragile. High peak pressures risk BAROTRAUMA (pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum). The peak inspiratory pressure is usually kept BELOW about 40 cmH₂O (and plateau pressure below 30) to reduce this risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• <20cmH2O — Below 20 cmH₂O is too low for narrowed asthmatic airways.
• <60cmH2O — Below 60 cmH₂O allows dangerously high pressures.
• No limit needed — A limit IS needed.

⭐ Key point: Asthma ventilation: low rate, long expiratory time, small tidal volumes, and permissive hypercapnia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q23$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 977, $s$A nurse is caring for a patient with a hemothorax. What is the most appropriate action if the patient's chest tube drainage decreases significantly and the patient's condition worsens?$s$, $s$["Milk the tubing to dislodge the clot", "Notify the physician immediately", "Clamp the chest tube to prevent further drainage", "Increase the suction to the UWSD system"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Notify the physician immediately
In a haemothorax, a SUDDEN DECREASE in chest drain output while the patient's condition WORSENS (more breathlessness, low BP, falling SpO₂) suggests the tube is BLOCKED (by clot), so blood is collecting in the chest, or there is ongoing bleeding. NOTIFY THE PHYSICIAN IMMEDIATELY for urgent assessment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Milk the tubing to dislodge the clot — Milking or stripping tubing may be done per local policy, but notifying the doctor is the priority with a deteriorating patient.
• Clamp the chest tube to prevent further drainage — Clamping would trap blood and air in the chest.
• Increase the suction to the UWSD system — Increasing suction won't clear a blocked tube and isn't the right action.

⭐ Key point: A drain that stops draining with a worsening patient is an emergency (blocked drain, tension, tamponade).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q26$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 978, $s$During a spontaneous breathing trial, the rapid shallow breathing index (f/VT) that predicts weaning failure is above:$s$, $s$["25 breaths/min/L", "50 breaths/min/L", "10 breaths/min/L", "105 breaths/min/L"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 105 breaths/min/L
The rapid shallow breathing index (RSBI) = respiratory rate ÷ tidal volume in litres. An RSBI ABOVE 105 predicts WEANING FAILURE: the patient is breathing fast and shallow. For example, RR 35 with VT 0.25 L = 140, which is likely to fail.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 25 breaths/min/L — 25 is far below the cut-off.
• 50 breaths/min/L — 50 is a good value.
• 10 breaths/min/L — 10 is unrealistically low.

⭐ Key point: RSBI below 105 predicts success, but use it with clinical judgement.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q37$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B2 · Mechanical Ventilation & Weaning$s$, false, 979, $s$Shortly after extubation a patient develops stridor. The first-line nursing treatment is:$s$, $s$["Give oral antibiotics and observe for a day", "Deflate the cuff of the removed tube", "Sit upright, give oxygen and nebulised adrenaline", "Lie flat and give a large IV fluid bolus"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sit upright, give oxygen and nebulised adrenaline
STRIDOR after extubation is usually caused by LARYNGEAL OEDEMA (swelling of the larynx from the tube). First-line treatment: SIT the patient UPRIGHT, give OXYGEN (humidified), and give NEBULISED ADRENALINE (which shrinks the swollen tissue). IV steroids (dexamethasone) may also be given. Prepare for reintubation if it worsens.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give oral antibiotics and observe for a day — Antibiotics don't treat swelling, and observing for a day is unsafe.
• Deflate the cuff of the removed tube — The tube has already been removed; its cuff can't be deflated.
• Lie flat and give a large IV fluid bolus — Lying flat and a fluid bolus would worsen airway swelling and breathing.

⭐ Key point: A cuff-leak test before extubation can identify patients at risk of post-extubation stridor.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q39$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 980, $s$A patient post-myocardial infarction develops new-onset crackles bilaterally, a third heart sound (S3), and worsening dyspnoea. This most likely indicates:$s$, $s$["Simple anxiety", "Acute left ventricular failure/pulmonary oedema", "Normal post-MI recovery", "Gastro-oesophageal reflux"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute left ventricular failure/pulmonary oedema
After an MI, the damaged left ventricle may fail to pump forward. Blood backs up into the lungs (pulmonary oedema), causing new bilateral crackles and worsening breathlessness. An S3 (third heart sound, "Ken-tuc-ky") comes from blood rushing into a stiff, overfilled ventricle, a classic sign of LV failure. This is acute left ventricular failure (Killip class II–III).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple anxiety — Anxiety does not produce crackles and an S3.
• Normal post-MI recovery — These are signs of a complication, not normal recovery.
• Gastro-oesophageal reflux — Reflux causes heartburn, not crackles and an S3.

⭐ Key point: Post-MI complications include arrhythmias, heart failure and cardiogenic shock, papillary muscle rupture (a new murmur), ventricular rupture, and pericarditis (including Dressler's syndrome).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q67$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 981, $s$The most common cause of right-sided heart failure is:$s$, $s$["Chronic left-sided heart failure", "Aortic stenosis", "Hyperthyroidism", "Anemia"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Chronic left-sided heart failure
The commonest cause of right heart failure is LEFT heart failure. When the left side fails, pressure backs up into the lungs (pulmonary hypertension). The right ventricle then has to pump against this high pressure and eventually fails too. Signs of right heart failure: raised JVP, swollen ankles (pitting oedema), enlarged liver, ascites.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Aortic stenosis — Aortic stenosis affects the left ventricle first; it can lead to right failure, but it is not the commonest cause.
• Hyperthyroidism — Hyperthyroidism can cause high-output failure but is not the commonest cause of right failure.
• Anemia — Anaemia can cause high-output failure but is uncommon as a cause of right failure.

⭐ Key point: Right heart failure caused by LUNG disease (COPD, PE, pulmonary hypertension) is called cor pulmonale.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q68$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 982, $s$What is the typical initial fluid resuscitation approach for a patient in hypovolemic shock?$s$, $s$["A rapid bolus of isotonic crystalloid solution while closely monitoring the hemodynamic response", "Withholding fluids until blood pressure normalizes spontaneously", "Administering diuretics to reduce circulating volume", "A slow maintenance-rate infusion of hypotonic fluid"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A rapid bolus of isotonic crystalloid solution while closely monitoring the hemodynamic response
Hypovolaemic shock means there is not enough fluid in the blood vessels. The first treatment is to replace volume quickly with an isotonic crystalloid (0.9% saline or Hartmann's), for example a 500 mL bolus over 15 minutes (or 20 mL/kg), then reassess HR, BP, capillary refill, urine output and mental state. Repeat if needed. If the cause is bleeding, give BLOOD early and stop the bleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Withholding fluids until blood pressure normalizes spontaneously — Waiting for the BP to recover by itself lets organs suffer and the patient may die.
• Administering diuretics to reduce circulating volume — Diuretics REMOVE fluid and would make hypovolaemia worse.
• A slow maintenance-rate infusion of hypotonic fluid — A slow drip of hypotonic fluid (5% dextrose) does not stay in the vessels and is far too slow.

⭐ Key point: Use two large-bore cannulas (14–16 G). In trauma, limit crystalloid and give blood products early (permissive hypotension, except in head injury).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q114$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 983, $s$Stroke volume is defined as:$s$, $s$["The volume of blood ejected by the ventricle with each heartbeat", "The total blood volume in the body", "The heart rate over one minute", "The pressure generated during diastole"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The volume of blood ejected by the ventricle with each heartbeat
STROKE VOLUME (SV) is the volume of blood ejected by the VENTRICLE with EACH heartbeat. Normal is about 60–100 mL (about 70 mL average). It is found by: SV = end-diastolic volume − end-systolic volume. CARDIAC OUTPUT = SV × heart rate (about 70 mL × 70 beats/min ≈ 5 L/min). Stroke volume depends on three things: PRELOAD (filling — how much the ventricle is stretched), AFTERLOAD (resistance the ventricle pumps against) and CONTRACTILITY (strength of contraction). In shock, SV falls; nurses support it with fluids (preload), vasodilators (afterload) or inotropes (contractility).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The total blood volume in the body — Total blood volume in the body is about 5 litres (70 mL/kg) — not stroke volume.
• The heart rate over one minute — Heart rate over one minute is the PULSE/heart rate, not the volume pumped.
• The pressure generated during diastole — Pressure during diastole is the DIASTOLIC blood pressure, not stroke volume.

⭐ Key point: Stroke volume = blood ejected per beat (60–100 mL). CO = SV × HR. SV depends on preload, afterload, contractility.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q64$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 984, $s$Normal central venous pressure (CVP) is generally in the range of:$s$, $s$["2–8 mmHg", "20–30 mmHg", "40–50 mmHg", "Below 0 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2–8 mmHg
CENTRAL VENOUS PRESSURE (CVP) is the pressure in the superior vena cava/right atrium, measured through a central line. It reflects RIGHT-SIDED filling pressure and is used (with other signs) to guide fluid therapy. Normal is about 2–8 mmHg (about 3–10 cmH2O). The transducer must be LEVELLED at the phlebostatic axis (4th intercostal space, mid-axillary line) and zeroed, and readings are taken at end-expiration. LOW CVP suggests hypovolaemia (dehydration, bleeding). HIGH CVP suggests fluid overload, right heart failure, cardiac tamponade, tension pneumothorax, pulmonary hypertension, or high PEEP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20–30 mmHg — 20–30 mmHg is very HIGH — e.g. tamponade or severe right heart failure.
• 40–50 mmHg — 40–50 mmHg is impossible as a normal venous pressure.
• Below 0 mmHg — Below 0 mmHg would indicate severe hypovolaemia or a measurement error.

⭐ Key point: Normal CVP ≈ 2–8 mmHg (3–10 cmH2O). Level the transducer at the 4th ICS, mid-axillary line.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q65$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 985, $s$All are indications for Adrenaline use EXCEPT$s$, $s$["Cardiac resuscitation", "Anaphylaxis", "Symptomatic bradycardia", "Heart failure"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Heart failure
ADRENALINE (epinephrine) is used in: CARDIAC RESUSCITATION (1 mg IV every 3–5 minutes), ANAPHYLAXIS (0.5 mg IM in adults), and SYMPTOMATIC BRADYCARDIA (as an infusion if atropine fails). It is NOT a standard treatment for HEART FAILURE (it increases heart workload and oxygen demand; although it may be used as an inotrope in cardiogenic shock, it is not a routine heart failure treatment).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cardiac resuscitation — Cardiac resuscitation IS an indication.
• Anaphylaxis — Anaphylaxis IS an indication.
• Symptomatic bradycardia — Symptomatic bradycardia IS an indication.

⭐ Key point: Know the correct route and dose for each indication — IV adrenaline in anaphylaxis is dangerous outside of cardiac arrest or specialist settings.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q36$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 986, $s$Mr. Amir (RN in Cardiac ICU) obtained cardiac output of a patient having 100 kg weight and report to the head nurse that the cardiac output is 3.5 Liter per minute. To confirm, whether the cardiac output is normal or abnormal, a more accurate measurement used is:$s$, $s$["Stroke volume", "Heart rate", "PCWP", "Cardiac Index"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cardiac Index
CARDIAC OUTPUT (CO) depends on body size — a large person needs more blood flow. CARDIAC INDEX (CI) = CO ÷ BODY SURFACE AREA (BSA), which adjusts for body size. Normal CI is about 2.5–4.0 L/min/m². For a 100 kg patient, a CO of 3.5 L/min may give a low CI (about 1.5–1.6), indicating low output.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stroke volume — Stroke volume alone doesn't adjust for body size.
• Heart rate — Heart rate alone doesn't adjust for body size.
• PCWP — PCWP measures left heart filling, not output.

⭐ Key point: CI below 2.2 L/min/m² with signs of hypoperfusion suggests cardiogenic shock.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q64$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 987, $s$Primary (essential) hypertension accounts for approximately what percentage of hypertension cases?$s$, $s$["5–15%", "50%", "85–95%", "100%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 85–95%
PRIMARY (ESSENTIAL) HYPERTENSION has no single identifiable cause; it results from a mix of genetic, lifestyle and environmental factors (age, family history, high salt intake, obesity, inactivity, alcohol, stress). It accounts for about 85–95% of all hypertension cases. SECONDARY hypertension (about 5–15%) is caused by an identifiable condition: kidney disease, renal artery stenosis, Conn's syndrome, Cushing's syndrome, phaeochromocytoma, thyroid disease, coarctation of the aorta, pregnancy, or drugs (e.g. steroids, oral contraceptives, NSAIDs).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5–15% — 5–15% is the proportion of SECONDARY hypertension.
• 50% — 50% underestimates primary hypertension.
• 100% — 100% ignores secondary causes.

⭐ Key point: Suspect secondary hypertension in young patients, sudden onset, very high or resistant BP, or low potassium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q67$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 988, $s$CVP that is low most likely indicates the need for:$s$, $s$["Diuretics", "Fluid administration", "Vasoconstrictors only", "No action needed"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fluid administration
CENTRAL VENOUS PRESSURE (CVP) reflects the pressure in the right atrium and is used as a rough guide to the patient's blood volume and right heart filling. Normal CVP is about 2–8 mmHg (or 3–10 cmH2O). A LOW CVP suggests HYPOVOLAEMIA (low blood volume — e.g. dehydration, bleeding, vasodilation), so FLUID ADMINISTRATION (fluid challenge) is usually indicated, with careful reassessment of BP, heart rate, urine output and CVP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diuretics — Diuretics would remove more fluid and worsen hypovolaemia.
• Vasoconstrictors only — Vasoconstrictors alone don't correct a low volume; fluids are needed first.
• No action needed — A low CVP with signs of poor perfusion needs action.

⭐ Key point: CVP trends and the response to a fluid challenge are more useful than a single reading.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q126$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 989, $s$A patient with a haemothorax and hypovolemic shock requires which priority intervention alongside chest tube drainage?$s$, $s$["Diuretic therapy", "Fluid resuscitation and blood product transfusion as indicated", "Immediate extubation", "Withholding IV access until surgery"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fluid resuscitation and blood product transfusion as indicated
A HAEMOTHORAX with HYPOVOLAEMIC SHOCK means significant blood loss into the chest. Along with CHEST TUBE drainage (to remove blood and re-expand the lung), the priority is FLUID RESUSCITATION and BLOOD PRODUCT TRANSFUSION to restore circulating volume.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diuretic therapy — Diuretics would worsen hypovolaemia.
• Immediate extubation — Extubation is inappropriate in a shocked patient.
• Withholding IV access until surgery — IV access is essential for resuscitation.

⭐ Key point: Massive haemothorax (over 1,500 mL initially or over 200 mL/hr for 2–4 hours) may need thoracotomy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q57$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 990, $s$The primary hemodynamic benefit of IABP balloon inflation during diastole is:$s$, $s$["Increased afterload", "Increased coronary artery perfusion (diastolic augmentation)", "Decreased myocardial oxygen supply", "Decreased diastolic pressure"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increased coronary artery perfusion (diastolic augmentation)
An INTRA-AORTIC BALLOON PUMP (IABP) INFLATES during DIASTOLE (when the aortic valve closes), pushing blood back towards the heart and INCREASING CORONARY ARTERY PERFUSION (diastolic augmentation). It DEFLATES just before systole, reducing afterload and helping the heart eject blood.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased afterload — IABP DECREASES afterload (on deflation).
• Decreased myocardial oxygen supply — IABP INCREASES myocardial oxygen supply.
• Decreased diastolic pressure — IABP INCREASES diastolic pressure.

⭐ Key point: Timing is set using the ECG (inflate at the T wave/dicrotic notch, deflate before the R wave).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q65$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 991, $s$A 58-year-old man in the ICU suddenly complains of crushing chest pain radiating to his left arm, with diaphoresis and nausea. The nurse's FIRST priority action is to:$s$, $s$["Give the patient a glass of water and reassess in 30 minutes", "Obtain a 12-lead ECG and vital signs while alerting the physician immediately", "Ask the patient to walk to the bathroom to see if pain resolves", "Document the complaint and continue the routine round"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Obtain a 12-lead ECG and vital signs while alerting the physician immediately
CRUSHING CHEST PAIN radiating to the LEFT ARM with SWEATING and NAUSEA suggests an ACUTE CORONARY SYNDROME. The FIRST priority is to obtain a 12-LEAD ECG and VITAL SIGNS while ALERTING THE PHYSICIAN IMMEDIATELY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give the patient a glass of water and reassess in 30 minutes — Waiting 30 minutes delays treatment.
• Ask the patient to walk to the bathroom to see if pain resolves — Walking increases heart workload.
• Document the complaint and continue the routine round — Documenting and continuing the round ignores an emergency.

⭐ Key point: Give aspirin (if not contraindicated), oxygen if hypoxic, nitrates for pain (if BP allows), and prepare for reperfusion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q69$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 992, $s$A 6 kg child in shock is prescribed a 20 mL/kg bolus of normal saline. What volume should be given?$s$, $s$["240 mL", "60 mL", "120 mL", "180 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 120 mL
Fluid boluses for children in shock are worked out by WEIGHT, because a child's blood volume is about 70–80 mL/kg. Formula: VOLUME = mL/kg × WEIGHT. Here: 20 mL/kg × 6 kg = 120 mL of 0.9% sodium chloride. Check: 20 × 6 = 120. ✓ For a 6 kg baby, 120 mL is about a quarter of the whole blood volume, so it is a significant amount. Give it quickly (over 5–10 minutes, using a syringe push or pressure bag), then REASSESS heart rate, capillary refill time, BP, conscious level, urine output and signs of overload (crackles, enlarged liver) before giving any more.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 240 mL — 240 mL is 40 mL/kg — double the dose; too much for one bolus in a small baby.
• 60 mL — 60 mL is only 10 mL/kg — half the prescribed dose.
• 180 mL — 180 mL is 30 mL/kg — more than prescribed.

⭐ Key point: In children with severe malnutrition or heart disease, smaller boluses (10 mL/kg) are given more slowly, because their hearts can easily fail with fluid overload.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q1 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 993, $s$A patient's blood pressure is 138/94 mmHg. What is the mean arterial pressure (MAP)?$s$, $s$["123 mmHg", "44 mmHg", "109 mmHg", "116 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 109 mmHg
MEAN ARTERIAL PRESSURE (MAP) = (SYSTOLIC + 2 × DIASTOLIC) ÷ 3. Here: (138 + 2 × 94) ÷ 3 = (138 + 188) ÷ 3 = 326 ÷ 3 = 108.7, which rounds to 109 mmHg. Diastole counts twice because the heart spends about two-thirds of each cycle in diastole.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 123 mmHg — 123 mmHg is too high (close to systolic).
• 44 mmHg — 44 mmHg is the PULSE PRESSURE (138 − 94).
• 116 mmHg — 116 mmHg is the simple average ((138 + 94) ÷ 2) — not the correct MAP formula.

⭐ Key point: MAP below 65 mmHg means organs (kidneys, brain, heart) may not be perfused adequately.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q20 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 994, $s$A patient with flash pulmonary oedema secondary to a hypertensive emergency should receive which class of medication as a primary intervention?$s$, $s$["IV vasodilators (e.g., nitroglycerin, nitroprusside) to rapidly reduce afterload", "IV antibiotics", "Oral ACE inhibitors", "Beta-blockers to slow heart rate"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV vasodilators (e.g., nitroglycerin, nitroprusside) to rapidly reduce afterload
In FLASH PULMONARY OEDEMA from a HYPERTENSIVE EMERGENCY, very high BP (afterload) makes it difficult for the left ventricle to pump blood forward. Pressure backs up into the lungs, causing sudden fluid flooding. The primary treatment is IV VASODILATORS such as NITROGLYCERIN (GTN) or NITROPRUSSIDE, which rapidly REDUCE AFTERLOAD (and preload), allowing the heart to empty and fluid to move out of the lungs. Also give oxygen, CPAP/NIV, and diuretics.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV antibiotics — Antibiotics treat infection, not pulmonary oedema.
• Oral ACE inhibitors — Oral ACE inhibitors act too slowly in an emergency.
• Beta-blockers to slow heart rate — Beta-blockers could worsen acute heart failure.

⭐ Key point: Sit the patient upright, give high-flow oxygen or CPAP, and start IV nitrates while monitoring BP closely.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q171$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 995, $s$What is the normal range for systemic vascular resistance (SVR) in an adult?$s$, $s$["800–1200 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵", "1600–2000 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵", "400–600 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵", "200–400 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 800–1200 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵
SYSTEMIC VASCULAR RESISTANCE (SVR) is the resistance that the left ventricle must pump against — mainly determined by the tone of small arteries (arterioles). Normal SVR in adults is about 800–1200 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵. It is calculated as SVR = 80 × (MAP − CVP) ÷ CO. HIGH SVR (vasoconstriction) is seen in hypovolaemic and cardiogenic shock; LOW SVR (vasodilation) in septic, anaphylactic and neurogenic shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1600–2000 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵ — 1600–2000 is HIGH (vasoconstriction).
• 400–600 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵ — 400–600 is LOW (vasodilation, e.g. sepsis).
• 200–400 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵ — 200–400 is very low.

⭐ Key point: SVR index (SVRI) adjusts for body size: normal about 1970–2390 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵·m².$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q188$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 996, $s$A patient's BP is 230/130 mmHg with severe headache, confusion, papilloedema and new proteinuria. The appropriate initial target for blood pressure reduction is:$s$, $s$["Give sublingual nifedipine to bring the BP down as fast as possible", "Lower MAP by no more than about 25% in the first hour with an IV agent", "Bring the BP down to below 140/90 mmHg within the first hour", "Lower the systolic BP to 120 mmHg within 6 hours with an IV agent"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lower MAP by no more than about 25% in the first hour with an IV agent
This is a HYPERTENSIVE EMERGENCY (very high BP with organ damage: brain — confusion and papilloedema; kidneys — proteinuria). The BP must be lowered, but NOT TOO FAST — the brain and kidneys have adapted to high pressure, and a sudden drop can cause stroke, blindness or kidney failure. The target: lower the MAP by NO MORE THAN about 25% in the FIRST HOUR, using a controllable IV agent (e.g. labetalol, nicardipine, sodium nitroprusside), then gradually to about 160/100 over the next 2–6 hours, and to normal over 24–48 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give sublingual nifedipine to bring the BP down as fast as possible — Sublingual nifedipine causes an unpredictable, rapid BP drop, which can cause stroke.
• Bring the BP down to below 140/90 mmHg within the first hour — Lowering to below 140/90 in the first hour is too fast and risks organ ischaemia.
• Lower the systolic BP to 120 mmHg within 6 hours with an IV agent — 120 mmHg systolic within 6 hours is too aggressive.

⭐ Key point: Exceptions where faster BP lowering is needed: aortic dissection, eclampsia and acute pulmonary oedema.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q48$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 997, $s$Beck's triad in cardiac tamponade after chest trauma consists of:$s$, $s$["Tachycardia, fever and pericardial rub", "Hypotension, raised JVP and muffled heart sounds", "Hypertension, bradycardia and irregular breathing", "Hypotension, low JVP and loud heart sounds"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypotension, raised JVP and muffled heart sounds
BECK'S TRIAD is the classic sign of CARDIAC TAMPONADE: HYPOTENSION (the compressed heart can't fill, so output falls), RAISED JVP (blood backs up into the veins) and MUFFLED HEART SOUNDS (fluid around the heart muffles sounds). Pulsus paradoxus (a fall in systolic BP of more than 10 mmHg on inspiration) is another sign.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tachycardia, fever and pericardial rub — Tachycardia, fever and pericardial rub suggest pericarditis.
• Hypertension, bradycardia and irregular breathing — Hypertension, bradycardia and irregular breathing are Cushing's triad (raised ICP).
• Hypotension, low JVP and loud heart sounds — Low JVP and loud heart sounds suggest hypovolaemia, not tamponade.

⭐ Key point: Treat tamponade with pericardiocentesis or surgery. In trauma, a FAST scan shows fluid around the heart.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q39 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 998, $s$Haemodynamic monitoring in cardiogenic shock classically shows:$s$, $s$["Low systemic vascular resistance", "Normal filling pressures with high cardiac output", "Low pulmonary capillary wedge pressure and high cardiac output", "High pulmonary capillary wedge pressure and low cardiac output"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: High pulmonary capillary wedge pressure and low cardiac output
In cardiogenic shock the heart muscle is too weak to pump (often after a big MI). Because it can't empty properly, pressure builds up behind it, so the wedge pressure (PCWP) RISES (usually above 18 mmHg). At the same time, the amount pumped out, the cardiac output, FALLS (cardiac index below 2.2). The body tightens the arteries (high SVR) to protect BP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low systemic vascular resistance — Low SVR is seen in septic, anaphylactic and neurogenic shock, not cardiogenic.
• Normal filling pressures with high cardiac output — Normal filling with high output is the opposite of pump failure.
• Low pulmonary capillary wedge pressure and high cardiac output — Low wedge pressure with high output fits early septic shock, not cardiogenic.

⭐ Key point: Cardiogenic shock profile: low CO, high PCWP, high SVR, high CVP, with cold clammy skin and crackles in the lungs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q3 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 999, $s$The door-to-balloon target for primary PCI in STEMI is within:$s$, $s$["30 minutes of discharge", "6 hours of arrival", "24 hours of the onset", "90 minutes"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 90 minutes
In STEMI, a coronary artery is completely blocked and "time is muscle": every minute of delay kills more heart muscle. The target for primary PCI (opening the artery with a balloon and stent) is within 90 minutes of first medical contact or arrival (door-to-balloon). If PCI can't be done within 120 minutes, thrombolysis should be given within 30 minutes of arrival (door-to-needle).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 30 minutes of discharge — "30 minutes of discharge" makes no sense; discharge is at the END of care.
• 6 hours of arrival — 6 hours is far too slow and would allow much more muscle to die.
• 24 hours of the onset — 24 hours from onset is too late for emergency reperfusion in most patients.

⭐ Key point: STEMI targets: ECG within 10 minutes of arrival, door-to-needle under 30 minutes, door-to-balloon under 90 minutes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q7$s$);
insert into public.admin_study (collection, area, must, sort, q, opts, ans_idx, answer, exp, ref) values
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 1000, $s$Hypertensive emergency differs from hypertensive urgency because emergency has:$s$, $s$["A history of hypertension", "Symptoms of headache only", "A systolic BP above 180", "Acute end-organ damage"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute end-organ damage
The difference is end-organ damage. In hypertensive EMERGENCY, very high BP (usually above 180/120) is actively damaging organs now: brain (encephalopathy, stroke), heart (ACS, pulmonary oedema), kidneys (AKI), aorta (dissection), eyes (papilloedema), or pregnancy (eclampsia). Hypertensive URGENCY has very high BP WITHOUT acute organ damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A history of hypertension — A history of hypertension is common in both and doesn't separate them.
• Symptoms of headache only — A headache alone doesn't prove organ damage; urgency can also cause headache.
• A systolic BP above 180 — Both can have systolic BP above 180; the number alone doesn't decide.

⭐ Key point: Emergency = high BP + organ damage → ICU and IV drugs, lowering BP carefully. Urgency = high BP alone → oral drugs, lowering BP over 24–48 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q11$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, true, 1001, $s$Pulse pressure variation above 13% in a ventilated patient suggests:$s$, $s$["The patient is likely to be fluid responsive", "The patient is already fluid overloaded", "Cardiac tamponade is developing", "The arterial line trace is damped"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The patient is likely to be fluid responsive
In a patient on controlled ventilation, positive pressure breaths reduce venous return a little with each breath. If the patient is "on the steep part" of the Starling curve (needs fluid), this makes the pulse pressure vary a lot between breaths. A pulse pressure variation (PPV) above 13% suggests the patient is likely to respond to fluid (cardiac output will rise).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient is already fluid overloaded — Fluid overload would show low variation.
• Cardiac tamponade is developing — Tamponade isn't diagnosed by PPV.
• The arterial line trace is damped — A damped trace reduces the accuracy of PPV; it doesn't explain high PPV.

⭐ Key point: PPV is only valid in sinus rhythm, with controlled ventilation, tidal volumes of about 8 mL/kg, and a closed chest.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q56 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1002, $s$The average lifespan of a red blood cell is approximately:$s$, $s$["10 days", "40 days", "120 days", "300 days"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 120 days
Red blood cells live for about 120 days. They have no nucleus, so they cannot repair themselves. As they age, they become stiff and are removed by macrophages in the spleen (and the liver). The haem is turned into bilirubin, and the iron is recycled to make new red cells in the bone marrow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 days — 10 days is about the lifespan of platelets (7–10 days).
• 40 days — 40 days is too short for a red cell.
• 300 days — 300 days is far too long; no blood cell lives this long in the circulation.

⭐ Key point: Red cells live 120 days, platelets 7–10 days, and neutrophils only hours to days. Stored red cells for transfusion last 35–42 days in the fridge.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q6$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1003, $s$The AV node's primary function in the cardiac conduction system is to:$s$, $s$["Generate the primary pacemaker impulse", "Briefly delay the electrical impulse, allowing the atria to fully contract before ventricular contraction", "Directly cause ventricular contraction without delay", "Regulate coronary blood flow"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Briefly delay the electrical impulse, allowing the atria to fully contract before ventricular contraction
The AV (atrioventricular) node sits between the atria and ventricles. Its main job is to DELAY the impulse for about 0.1 second. This gives the atria time to finish contracting and fill the ventricles before the ventricles contract. The delay appears on the ECG as the PR interval (normal 0.12–0.20 s). The AV node also protects the ventricles from very fast atrial rates, as in AF.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Generate the primary pacemaker impulse — Generating the primary pacemaker impulse is the job of the SA node (60–100/min).
• Directly cause ventricular contraction without delay — The impulse must be delayed, not passed straight to the ventricles; the bundle of His and Purkinje fibres conduct it rapidly afterwards.
• Regulate coronary blood flow — Coronary blood flow is controlled by the coronary arteries and local metabolites, not the AV node.

⭐ Key point: SA node = pacemaker; AV node = delay (PR interval). If the SA node fails, the AV junction can take over at 40–60/min.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q8$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1004, $s$The objective of medical management of angina is to:$s$, $s$["Increase myocardial oxygen demand and increase supply", "Increase demand and decrease supply", "Decrease myocardial oxygen demand and increase supply", "Maintain the general circulation"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Decrease myocardial oxygen demand and increase supply
Angina is chest pain caused by the heart muscle needing more oxygen than the narrowed coronary arteries can supply. Treatment aims to REDUCE oxygen DEMAND and INCREASE oxygen SUPPLY. Beta-blockers slow the heart rate and nitrates reduce preload (lower demand); nitrates and calcium channel blockers widen the coronary arteries (more supply); aspirin and statins prevent clots and plaque growth.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase myocardial oxygen demand and increase supply — Increasing demand would make the imbalance and the pain worse.
• Increase demand and decrease supply — Increasing demand and decreasing supply is the exact cause of ischaemia, not its treatment.
• Maintain the general circulation — Maintaining the general circulation is too vague and doesn't describe the specific aim.

⭐ Key point: For angina, rest plus GTN sublingual. If pain lasts more than 20 minutes or doesn't respond, treat it as ACS: ECG, troponin and aspirin.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q33$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1005, $s$Which chest X-ray finding is characteristic of cardiogenic pulmonary oedema?$s$, $s$["Bilateral perihilar 'bat-wing' infiltrates with Kerley B lines and cardiomegaly", "A single well-circumscribed nodule in the right upper lobe", "Complete opacification of one hemithorax with mediastinal shift away from it", "A normal chest X-ray in all cases"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bilateral perihilar 'bat-wing' infiltrates with Kerley B lines and cardiomegaly
In cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, the failing left ventricle backs up pressure into the lungs. On X-ray you see: an enlarged heart (cardiomegaly), fluid around the central airways in a "bat-wing" (butterfly) pattern, Kerley B lines (short horizontal lines at the lung edges, from swollen lymphatics), upper-lobe vessel diversion, and often pleural effusions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A single well-circumscribed nodule in the right upper lobe — A single nodule suggests a tumour or granuloma, not pulmonary oedema.
• Complete opacification of one hemithorax with mediastinal shift away from it — Complete whiteness of one side with shift AWAY from it suggests a massive pleural effusion.
• A normal chest X-ray in all cases — The X-ray is usually abnormal in pulmonary oedema, not normal.

⭐ Key point: Pulmonary oedema care: sit the patient upright, oxygen or CPAP, GTN (if BP allows), and IV furosemide. In ARDS, the heart size is usually normal.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q45$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1006, $s$Which ventricle is most sensitive to an increase in afterload?$s$, $s$["A normal ventricle", "A hypertrophied athletic ventricle", "A failing ventricle with poor contractility", "None, afterload never affects stroke volume"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A failing ventricle with poor contractility
Afterload is the resistance the ventricle must pump against. A healthy heart can keep its stroke volume even when afterload rises. A FAILING ventricle with weak contractility cannot overcome extra resistance, so its stroke volume falls sharply when afterload rises. That is why lowering afterload (ACE inhibitors, nitrates, nitroprusside, IABP) improves output in heart failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal ventricle — A normal ventricle can compensate well, so its stroke volume changes little.
• A hypertrophied athletic ventricle — A hypertrophied athletic heart is strong and handles extra afterload well.
• None, afterload never affects stroke volume — Afterload DOES affect stroke volume; it matters most in the failing heart.

⭐ Key point: In heart failure, reduce afterload with vasodilators and avoid high blood pressure. In septic shock, afterload (SVR) is LOW and vasopressors raise it.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q64$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1007, $s$A patient reports substernal chest pressure radiating to the jaw during exertion, relieved within 5 minutes of rest. The nurse should classify this as most consistent with:$s$, $s$["Unstable angina", "Stable angina", "Acute MI", "Pericarditis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stable angina
STABLE angina is chest pain brought on by a predictable amount of exertion (walking, climbing stairs) or emotion. It lasts a few minutes (usually under 10–15 minutes) and goes away with REST or GTN. It is caused by a fixed narrowing of the coronary artery. Here, the pain comes on with exertion and is relieved within 5 minutes of rest, which is the classic stable pattern.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Unstable angina — Unstable angina is pain at rest, new pain, or pain that is getting worse (crescendo); it is an emergency (ACS).
• Acute MI — An acute MI causes pain lasting more than 20 minutes, not relieved by rest, with ECG changes and raised troponin.
• Pericarditis — Pericarditis causes sharp pain, worse lying down and on breathing, better sitting forward, not related to exertion.

⭐ Key point: Any change in the pattern of angina (at rest, more often, longer, less relief) is unstable angina. Treat it as ACS.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q65$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1008, $s$Which cardiac biomarker is most specific for myocardial injury and remains elevated for 7-14 days after MI?$s$, $s$["CK-MB", "Troponin I/T", "Myoglobin", "LDH"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Troponin I/T
Troponin I and T are proteins found only in heart muscle, so they are the most SPECIFIC markers of heart muscle damage. Levels rise 3–6 hours after the injury, peak at 12–24 hours, and stay raised for 7–14 days. This makes troponin useful to diagnose MI even if the patient comes late. High-sensitivity troponin tests at 0 and 1–3 hours are now standard.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CK-MB — CK-MB is less specific (some is in skeletal muscle) and returns to normal in 2–3 days, so it is used to detect REINFARCTION.
• Myoglobin — Myoglobin rises earliest (1–2 hours) but is found in all muscle, so it is not specific.
• LDH — LDH rises late and is non-specific (blood, liver, muscle); it is no longer used for MI.

⭐ Key point: Troponin can also rise in sepsis, PE, kidney failure, myocarditis and after CPR or defibrillation, so interpret it with the ECG and symptoms.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q66$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1009, $s$It is a flexible catheter, inserted into the femoral artery and passed into the descending aorta 1-2 cm below the left subclavian artery and slightly above the renal arteries.$s$, $s$["Pulmonary Artery Wedge Pressure", "Pulmonary Artery Catheter", "Intra-aortic balloon pump", "None of the above"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intra-aortic balloon pump
The intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) is a long balloon catheter inserted through the femoral artery into the descending thoracic aorta. The tip sits just below the left subclavian artery, and the balloon ends above the renal arteries. It INFLATES in diastole (pushing blood back into the coronary arteries, improving heart muscle oxygen) and DEFLATES just before systole (lowering afterload so the heart pumps more easily). It is used in cardiogenic shock and severe ischaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pulmonary Artery Wedge Pressure — PAWP is a pressure measurement, not a device.
• Pulmonary Artery Catheter — A pulmonary artery catheter goes through a vein into the right heart and pulmonary artery, not into the aorta.
• None of the above — One of the options (IABP) does fit the description, so "none" is wrong.

⭐ Key point: IABP nursing: check leg pulses, colour and warmth (limb ischaemia); check the LEFT radial pulse (the tip may block the left subclavian); check urine output (it may block the renal arteries); keep the leg straight; watch for bleeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q72$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1010, $s$Which one of the following is NOT a function of pulmonary arterial catheter (PAC) which is also called Swan GANZ catheter?$s$, $s$["Continuous cardiac output monitoring", "Measurement of pulmonary artery pressure", "Estimation of diastole filling of left ventricle", "Measurement of right atrium pressure"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Measurement of right atrium pressure
The pulmonary artery catheter is placed mainly to measure pressures in the pulmonary artery, the wedge pressure (which estimates left ventricular filling) and cardiac output. The question's answer key marks "measurement of right atrium pressure" as NOT its function. The reason: right atrial pressure (CVP) is usually measured with an ordinary central venous catheter, and measuring it is not the reason for placing a Swan-Ganz. (Strictly, the PAC's proximal port can show RA pressure too, so read questions like this carefully.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Continuous cardiac output monitoring — Continuous cardiac output monitoring IS a PAC function (thermodilution).
• Measurement of pulmonary artery pressure — Measuring pulmonary artery pressure IS the main PAC function.
• Estimation of diastole filling of left ventricle — Estimating left ventricular diastolic filling IS a PAC function, through the wedge pressure (PAWP).

⭐ Key point: PAC measures PA pressure (15–25/8–15), PAWP (6–12, left heart filling), cardiac output (4–8 L/min) and SvO₂ (60–80%).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q73$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1011, $s$A trauma patient in hypovolaemic shock has lost an estimated 35% of their circulating blood volume. According to classification of haemorrhagic shock, this places them in:$s$, $s$["Class I", "Class II", "Class III", "Class IV"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Class III
Blood loss classes (ATLS): Class I up to 15%, Class II 15–30%, Class III 30–40%, Class IV over 40%. A loss of 35% falls into CLASS III (about 1500–2000 mL in a 70 kg adult). In Class III, the pulse is above 120, systolic BP FALLS, breathing is fast, urine output is low (5–15 mL/h) and the patient is anxious or confused. They need blood transfusion, not just crystalloid.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Class I — Class I (under 15%) has almost normal vital signs.
• Class II — Class II (15–30%) has tachycardia and a narrow pulse pressure, but the BP is usually still normal.
• Class IV — Class IV (over 40%) is immediately life-threatening, with very low BP, lethargy and almost no urine.

⭐ Key point: A falling BP is a LATE sign of blood loss (Class III). Tachycardia, narrowed pulse pressure, cool skin and slow capillary refill come first.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q75$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1012, $s$Early (warm) septic shock differs from hypovolaemic shock by all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Warm extremities", "Flushed skin", "Well-perfused extremities", "Tachycardia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tachycardia
The question asks which feature does NOT differ. Both early (warm) septic shock and hypovolaemic shock cause TACHYCARDIA, so it does not help tell them apart. What differs is the skin: in warm sepsis, the blood vessels are dilated, so the skin is warm, flushed and well-perfused. In hypovolaemic shock, the vessels are clamped down, so the skin is cold, pale and clammy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Warm extremities — Warm extremities ARE different: warm in sepsis, cold in hypovolaemia.
• Flushed skin — Flushed skin IS different: flushed in sepsis, pale in hypovolaemia.
• Well-perfused extremities — Well-perfused extremities ARE different (sepsis) from poorly perfused ones (hypovolaemia).

⭐ Key point: Warm shock (low SVR) = septic, anaphylactic and neurogenic. Cold shock (high SVR) = hypovolaemic, cardiogenic and obstructive. Late septic shock can also become cold.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q76$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1013, $s$Inhaled nitric oxide is used in pulmonary hypertensive crisis primarily because it:$s$, $s$["Selectively dilates the pulmonary vasculature with minimal systemic hypotension", "Increases systemic vascular resistance", "Acts as a potent bronchoconstrictor", "Directly increases myocardial contractility"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Selectively dilates the pulmonary vasculature with minimal systemic hypotension
INHALED NITRIC OXIDE (iNO) is a gas given through the ventilator circuit (usually 5–20 ppm). Because it is breathed in, it reaches only the pulmonary vessels next to VENTILATED alveoli, where it relaxes vascular smooth muscle (through cGMP), DILATING the pulmonary arteries. This lowers PULMONARY artery pressure and improves V/Q matching (better oxygenation). It is quickly inactivated by haemoglobin in the blood, so it has very little effect on SYSTEMIC BP — it is a SELECTIVE pulmonary vasodilator. Uses: pulmonary hypertensive crisis (e.g. after cardiac surgery), persistent pulmonary hypertension of the newborn, right heart failure. Monitor methaemoglobin and NO2 levels; do not stop it suddenly (rebound pulmonary hypertension).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increases systemic vascular resistance — iNO does NOT increase systemic vascular resistance; it is a vasodilator.
• Acts as a potent bronchoconstrictor — It is a BRONCHODILATOR/vasodilator effect, not a bronchoconstrictor.
• Directly increases myocardial contractility — It does not directly increase myocardial contractility; it helps the right ventricle by lowering its afterload.

⭐ Key point: Inhaled NO = SELECTIVE PULMONARY VASODILATOR — lowers pulmonary pressure with little systemic hypotension. Wean slowly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q54$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1014, $s$Which one of the following is NOT the cause of cardiogenic shock?$s$, $s$["CHF", "Cardiomyopathy", "MI", "Spinal Cord Injury"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Spinal Cord Injury
CARDIOGENIC SHOCK is shock caused by the HEART failing as a PUMP — cardiac output falls despite adequate blood volume, so tissues are under-perfused. Causes are cardiac: acute MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION (the commonest, usually when over 40% of the left ventricle is damaged), severe heart failure (CHF), CARDIOMYOPATHY, arrhythmias, valve rupture, myocarditis. SPINAL CORD INJURY (above T6) causes NEUROGENIC SHOCK — a type of distributive shock: loss of sympathetic tone causes vasodilation, HYPOTENSION and BRADYCARDIA with warm, dry skin. So spinal cord injury is NOT a cause of cardiogenic shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CHF — CHF (congestive heart failure) IS a cause — a weak pump cannot keep up cardiac output.
• Cardiomyopathy — CARDIOMYOPATHY IS a cause — diseased heart muscle pumps poorly.
• MI — MI IS the most common cause of cardiogenic shock.

⭐ Key point: Cardiogenic shock = pump failure (MI, CHF, cardiomyopathy). Spinal cord injury → NEUROGENIC shock (low BP + low pulse).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q66$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1015, $s$According to 2017 ACC/AHA criteria, Stage II hypertension is defined as:$s$, $s$["<120/<80", "120–129/<80", "130–139/80–89", "≥140/≥90"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ≥140/≥90
The 2017 ACC/AHA blood pressure categories are: NORMAL — below 120 and below 80; ELEVATED — 120–129 systolic and below 80; STAGE 1 hypertension — 130–139 systolic OR 80–89 diastolic; STAGE 2 hypertension — 140 or higher systolic OR 90 or higher diastolic. Hypertensive crisis — above 180 and/or above 120. Stage 2 usually needs lifestyle change PLUS medication (often two drugs). Remember to diagnose from repeated, correctly measured readings (correct cuff size, patient seated and rested 5 minutes, arm at heart level).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• <120/<80 — Below 120/80 is NORMAL blood pressure.
• 120–129/<80 — 120–129/below 80 is ELEVATED blood pressure (not yet hypertension).
• 130–139/80–89 — 130–139/80–89 is STAGE 1 hypertension.

⭐ Key point: ACC/AHA 2017: Normal <120/<80; Elevated 120–129/<80; Stage 1 130–139 or 80–89; STAGE 2 ≥140 or ≥90.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q68$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1016, $s$The main aim of the Intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) include all except:$s$, $s$["increase renal blood", "increasing cardiac output", "increase myocardial O2 demand", "decrease myocardial O2 demand"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: increase myocardial O2 demand
The INTRA-AORTIC BALLOON PUMP (IABP) is a balloon in the descending thoracic aorta. It INFLATES in DIASTOLE (right after the aortic valve closes, at the dicrotic notch), pushing blood back into the coronary arteries and forward to the body — this INCREASES CORONARY PERFUSION and oxygen supply. It DEFLATES just before SYSTOLE, creating a "vacuum" that LOWERS AFTERLOAD, so the left ventricle pumps more easily — this DECREASES MYOCARDIAL OXYGEN DEMAND and INCREASES CARDIAC OUTPUT. Better output also improves organ blood flow, including the KIDNEYS. So increasing myocardial oxygen demand is the OPPOSITE of what an IABP does. Nursing: check distal pulses of the leg, watch for limb ischaemia, keep the leg straight, check the left radial pulse (balloon too high blocks the left subclavian) and urine output (balloon too low blocks renal arteries).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• increase renal blood — Increased RENAL blood flow IS an effect, from better cardiac output.
• increasing cardiac output — INCREASING CARDIAC OUTPUT IS a main aim, through reduced afterload.
• decrease myocardial O2 demand — DECREASING myocardial oxygen demand IS a main aim — the heart works less.

⭐ Key point: IABP: inflate in diastole (↑ coronary flow), deflate before systole (↓ afterload) → ↓ O2 DEMAND, ↑ cardiac output.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q72$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1017, $s$Which test is performed in order to check collateral circulation, before going to pass arterial line catheter in radial artery;$s$, $s$["Romberg test", "Weber test", "Whisper test", "Allen test"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Allen test
The ALLEN TEST checks that the hand has a good blood supply from the ULNAR artery before the RADIAL artery is punctured or cannulated (arterial line or ABG). Steps: (1) ask the patient to make a tight fist; (2) press firmly on both the radial and ulnar arteries at the wrist; (3) the patient opens the hand — the palm looks pale; (4) RELEASE the ULNAR artery only; (5) colour should return to the palm within about 5–15 seconds (positive/normal test = good collateral flow). If colour takes longer than about 15 seconds, collateral circulation is poor and the radial artery should NOT be used, because if it clots, the hand could become ischaemic.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Romberg test — The ROMBERG test checks balance/proprioception (patient stands with feet together and eyes closed).
• Weber test — The WEBER test uses a tuning fork on the forehead to check for conductive or sensorineural hearing loss.
• Whisper test — The WHISPER test is a simple screening test for hearing.

⭐ Key point: Before a radial arterial line or ABG → ALLEN TEST (checks ulnar collateral flow; colour back within 5–15 s).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q74$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1018, $s$In critical care, what is the primary purpose of a pulmonary artery catheter (PAC)?$s$, $s$["A) Monitoring brain activity", "B) Administering anesthesia", "C) Measuring intracranial pressure (ICP)", "D) Assessing cardiac function and pressures"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: D) Assessing cardiac function and pressures
A PULMONARY ARTERY CATHETER (PAC, Swan-Ganz) is a long, balloon-tipped catheter passed through a central vein, the right atrium and right ventricle into the pulmonary artery. It is used to ASSESS CARDIAC FUNCTION AND PRESSURES: right atrial pressure (CVP), pulmonary artery pressure, pulmonary artery WEDGE pressure (PAWP/PCWP — an estimate of left atrial/left ventricular filling pressure, normal 6–12 mmHg), CARDIAC OUTPUT (by thermodilution), systemic and pulmonary vascular resistance, and mixed venous oxygen saturation (SvO2). It helps guide fluids, inotropes and vasopressors in complex shock, cardiac surgery and severe heart failure. Risks: arrhythmias, infection, pulmonary artery rupture, pulmonary infarction (never leave the balloon wedged).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A) Monitoring brain activity — Monitoring BRAIN activity is done with EEG, not a PAC.
• B) Administering anesthesia — ANAESTHESIA is given by IV drugs, inhaled gases or nerve blocks, not by a PAC.
• C) Measuring intracranial pressure (ICP) — INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE is measured with an ICP monitor (intraventricular drain or parenchymal probe), not a PAC.

⭐ Key point: PAC (Swan-Ganz) = assesses CARDIAC FUNCTION: PA pressure, wedge pressure, cardiac output, SvO2.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q76$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1019, $s$A heart rate above what value is used as an ICU admission threshold?$s$, $s$[">80bpm", ">90bpm", ">110bpm", ">150bpm"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: >110bpm
ICU admission/referral criteria (and early warning scores such as NEWS) use physiological "trigger" values to identify patients who are deteriorating. A HEART RATE persistently above about 110–130 bpm (this question's key: > 110 bpm) together with other abnormal signs is a common threshold for urgent review and possible ICU admission. Other typical triggers: systolic BP below 90 mmHg, respiratory rate above 30 or below 8, SpO2 below 90% on oxygen, GCS falling by 2 or more, urine output below 0.5 mL/kg/h, lactate above 4 mmol/L. Tachycardia is often the earliest sign of shock, sepsis, bleeding or hypoxia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• >80bpm — > 80 bpm is within the NORMAL resting range (60–100).
• >90bpm — > 90 bpm is only mildly raised; it is a SIRS criterion but not an ICU admission threshold by itself.
• >150bpm — > 150 bpm is very high; waiting for this level would delay ICU referral.

⭐ Key point: HR > 110 bpm (with other abnormal signs) is a common ICU admission/referral trigger. Tachycardia is an early warning sign.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q123$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1020, $s$A patient with septic shock is started on vasopressin. Which effect is expected?$s$, $s$["Decreased SVR", "Increased urine output", "Coronary vasodilation", "Vasoconstriction"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vasoconstriction
VASOPRESSIN acts on V1 receptors in blood vessel walls, causing VASOCONSTRICTION. In septic shock, it raises SVR and BP, helping to reduce the dose of noradrenaline needed. It also acts on V2 receptors in the kidneys (water retention).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Decreased SVR — It INCREASES SVR, not decreases it.
• Increased urine output — It may increase urine output in some cases by improving BP, but its main effect is vasoconstriction.
• Coronary vasodilation — It can cause coronary VASOCONSTRICTION (ischaemia risk).

⭐ Key point: Vasopressin dose in septic shock is usually up to 0.03 units/min, not titrated like noradrenaline.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q31$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1021, $s$This is the similarity between pulmonary and systemic circulation$s$, $s$["total capacity", "pulse pressure", "peripheral vascular resistance", "volume of the circulation per minute"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: volume of the circulation per minute
The PULMONARY and SYSTEMIC circulations are connected in series, so the VOLUME of blood pumped per MINUTE (CARDIAC OUTPUT) by the right ventricle (to the lungs) and the left ventricle (to the body) must be the SAME (about 5 L/min). Otherwise blood would build up in one circulation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• total capacity — Total capacity differs (systemic holds much more blood).
• pulse pressure — Pulse pressure differs (pulmonary pressures are much lower).
• peripheral vascular resistance — Vascular resistance differs (pulmonary resistance is much lower).

⭐ Key point: Pulmonary circulation is a low-pressure system (about 25/10 mmHg).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q56$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1022, $s$Immediately after PAC insertion, which action confirms correct catheter placement?$s$, $s$["A chest X-ray", "Auscultation of bowel sounds", "Checking pedal pulses only", "Measuring oral temperature"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A chest X-ray
After inserting a PULMONARY ARTERY CATHETER (PAC, Swan-Ganz), a CHEST X-RAY is taken to CONFIRM CORRECT PLACEMENT — the catheter tip should be in a main pulmonary artery branch (not too far out into a small artery, where it could cause infarction or rupture) — and to exclude complications of central venous access such as PNEUMOTHORAX, haemothorax or catheter knotting. The pressure waveforms seen during insertion (right atrium → right ventricle → pulmonary artery → wedge) also guide placement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Auscultation of bowel sounds — Bowel sounds have nothing to do with PAC position.
• Checking pedal pulses only — Pedal pulses alone do not confirm catheter position.
• Measuring oral temperature — Oral temperature does not confirm position (the PAC itself measures core temperature).

⭐ Key point: Continuously monitor the PA waveform — a permanently "wedged" trace means the catheter has migrated; deflate the balloon and pull back per protocol.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q66$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1023, $s$The amount of blood which the heart pumps in one cardiac contraction is called:$s$, $s$["Pre-load", "After Load", "Stroke Volume", "Cardiac Output"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stroke Volume
STROKE VOLUME (SV) is the amount of blood the LEFT VENTRICLE ejects with ONE contraction (heartbeat) — about 60–100 mL in an adult at rest. It depends on three factors: PRELOAD (how full the ventricle is before contraction), AFTERLOAD (the resistance it pumps against), and CONTRACTILITY (the strength of contraction). CARDIAC OUTPUT = stroke volume × heart rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pre-load — PRELOAD is the stretch/filling of the ventricle at the end of diastole.
• After Load — AFTERLOAD is the resistance the ventricle must overcome to eject blood.
• Cardiac Output — CARDIAC OUTPUT is the amount pumped per MINUTE, not per beat.

⭐ Key point: Stroke volume falls in hypovolaemia, heart failure and tamponade; the body compensates by increasing heart rate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q70$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1024, $s$Which of the following is NOT a hemodynamic effects and benefits of intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP);$s$, $s$["Increase cardiac output", "Decrease o2 demand", "Increase coronary flow", "Increase ventricular wall tension"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increase ventricular wall tension
An INTRA-AORTIC BALLOON PUMP (IABP) is a balloon in the descending aorta that INFLATES during DIASTOLE (pushing blood back towards the coronary arteries → INCREASES CORONARY BLOOD FLOW) and DEFLATES just before SYSTOLE (creating a "vacuum" that lowers aortic pressure → REDUCES AFTERLOAD). Lower afterload means the left ventricle needs less pressure and less WALL TENSION to eject blood, so its OXYGEN DEMAND FALLS and CARDIAC OUTPUT INCREASES. "Increase ventricular wall tension" is the OPPOSITE of what the IABP does, so it is NOT a benefit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase cardiac output — Increased cardiac output IS a benefit.
• Decrease o2 demand — Decreased oxygen demand IS a benefit.
• Increase coronary flow — Increased coronary flow IS a benefit.

⭐ Key point: Nursing care: check limb perfusion on the insertion side, keep the leg straight, monitor timing, watch for bleeding and balloon rupture (blood in the tubing).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q73$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1025, $s$Afterload for the left ventricle is most directly represented by:$s$, $s$["Systemic vascular resistance (SVR)", "Pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR)", "Central venous pressure", "Stroke volume index"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Systemic vascular resistance (SVR)
AFTERLOAD is the RESISTANCE the ventricle must overcome to eject blood. For the LEFT VENTRICLE, afterload is mainly determined by the resistance of the systemic arteries — the SYSTEMIC VASCULAR RESISTANCE (SVR) (plus aortic valve stenosis if present). High SVR (vasoconstriction, hypertension) increases afterload; low SVR (vasodilation, sepsis) reduces it. Normal SVR: about 800–1200 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) — PVR (pulmonary vascular resistance) represents RIGHT ventricular afterload.
• Central venous pressure — CVP reflects RIGHT ventricular PRELOAD.
• Stroke volume index — Stroke volume index reflects the heart's output, not afterload.

⭐ Key point: Afterload reduction (e.g. vasodilators, IABP) helps a failing heart pump more blood.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q74$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1026, $s$Systemic burn shock risk begins above what %TBSA?$s$, $s$[">5%", ">10%", ">20%", ">50%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: >20%
Burns cause local tissue damage, but large burns trigger a SYSTEMIC inflammatory response: capillaries throughout the body become LEAKY, and large amounts of fluid and protein move out of the blood vessels into the tissues ("burn shock" — a form of hypovolaemic and distributive shock). This systemic response usually begins when burns exceed about 20% TBSA in adults (some guidelines use 15%; about 10% in children and elderly). These patients need formal IV fluid resuscitation (e.g. Parkland formula).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• >5% — 5% TBSA is too small to cause systemic shock in most adults.
• >10% — 10% is the usual threshold in children, not adults.
• >50% — 50% is far above the threshold.

⭐ Key point: Monitor urine output hourly (target 0.5–1 mL/kg/hr in adults) to guide fluid resuscitation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q117$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1027, $s$A patient with obstructive shock from massive pulmonary embolism would be expected to show:$s$, $s$["Low blood pressure with signs of right heart strain (e.g. elevated JVP) from obstructed pulmonary blood flow", "Warm, flushed skin with bounding pulses", "Bradycardia with normal blood pressure", "No cardiovascular compromise"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Low blood pressure with signs of right heart strain (e.g. elevated JVP) from obstructed pulmonary blood flow
In OBSTRUCTIVE SHOCK from MASSIVE PE, blood can't flow through the lungs. The right ventricle is strained and fails, causing LOW BP, RAISED JVP, tachycardia and hypoxia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Warm, flushed skin with bounding pulses — Warm, flushed skin is typical of septic shock.
• Bradycardia with normal blood pressure — Bradycardia with normal BP is not typical.
• No cardiovascular compromise — Massive PE causes compromise.

⭐ Key point: Treat with thrombolysis if unstable.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q119$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1028, $s$Warning signs that may precede an eclamptic convulsion include:$s$, $s$["Severe headache and blurred vision", "Improved appetite", "Reduced blood pressure", "Increased urine output only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Severe headache and blurred vision
WARNING SIGNS of impending ECLAMPSIA (seizures) in pre-eclampsia include: SEVERE HEADACHE (often frontal, not relieved by simple painkillers), VISUAL DISTURBANCES (BLURRED VISION, flashing lights, spots), epigastric or right upper quadrant pain (liver involvement), nausea and vomiting, HYPERREFLEXIA and CLONUS, sudden oedema of the face and hands, and rising BP. These signs mean the brain is irritated and seizures may soon occur.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved appetite — Improved appetite is not a warning sign.
• Reduced blood pressure — BP usually RISES, not falls.
• Increased urine output only — Urine output usually FALLS (oliguria), not increases.

⭐ Key point: Give magnesium sulphate, control BP, and plan for delivery when these warning signs appear.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q138$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1029, $s$Which heart chamber pumps oxygenated blood to the entire systemic circulation?$s$, $s$["Right atrium", "Right ventricle", "Left atrium", "Left ventricle"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Left ventricle
The LEFT VENTRICLE receives OXYGENATED blood from the left atrium (which comes from the lungs through the pulmonary veins) and pumps it through the AORTA to the WHOLE BODY (the SYSTEMIC circulation). Because it must push blood against the high systemic pressure, it has the THICKEST muscular wall of all four chambers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right atrium — The right atrium receives DEOXYGENATED blood from the body through the venae cavae.
• Right ventricle — The right ventricle pumps DEOXYGENATED blood to the LUNGS through the pulmonary artery (low-pressure circuit).
• Left atrium — The left atrium receives oxygenated blood from the lungs and passes it to the left ventricle; it does not pump to the body.

⭐ Key point: Left ventricular failure causes blood to back up into the lungs (pulmonary oedema); right ventricular failure causes backup into the body (raised JVP, liver congestion, leg oedema).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q4$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1030, $s$Vital cardiorespiratory centres are located in the:$s$, $s$["Cerebellum", "Medulla oblongata", "Occipital lobe", "Hypothalamus only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Medulla oblongata
The MEDULLA OBLONGATA, the lowest part of the brainstem, contains the VITAL CENTRES: the CARDIAC centre (controls heart rate), the VASOMOTOR centre (controls blood vessel tone and BP) and the RESPIRATORY centre (controls breathing rhythm). It also controls reflexes such as coughing, swallowing and vomiting. Damage (e.g. from brain herniation through the foramen magnum) causes respiratory arrest and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cerebellum — The cerebellum controls balance and coordination.
• Occipital lobe — The occipital lobe processes vision.
• Hypothalamus only — The hypothalamus controls temperature, hunger, thirst and hormones, but not the main cardiorespiratory centres.

⭐ Key point: Raised ICP can compress the medulla, causing Cushing's triad (hypertension, bradycardia, irregular breathing) — a pre-terminal sign.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q8$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1031, $s$A 37 year old male presented to the ER with chest pain, radiating to the left arm and diaphoresis. Upon history taking patient says that he has this problem for almost 3-4 hours and he has arrived here after a long drive. Assessing the patient's condition, the Emergency room Nurse diagnosis this patient as:$s$, $s$["Highly urgent", "Urgent", "Critically ill", "Emergent"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Emergent
CHEST PAIN radiating to the LEFT ARM with SWEATING (diaphoresis) for 3–4 hours suggests an ACUTE MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION (heart attack). This patient needs immediate assessment and treatment — triaged as EMERGENT (life-threatening, needs immediate care). "Time is muscle" — every minute counts.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Highly urgent — "Highly urgent" may underestimate the urgency.
• Urgent — "Urgent" suggests a delay is acceptable — it is not.
• Critically ill — "Critically ill" is a description, not a standard triage category here.

⭐ Key point: Obtain a 12-lead ECG within 10 minutes of arrival, give aspirin, and activate the cardiac team.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q68$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1032, $s$Which of these is not an antihypertensive$s$, $s$["Valsartan", "Atenolol", "Hydrallazine", "None of the above"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: None of the above
All three drugs listed are ANTIHYPERTENSIVES: VALSARTAN — an angiotensin II receptor blocker (ARB), which relaxes blood vessels; ATENOLOL — a beta-blocker, which slows the heart and reduces cardiac output; HYDRALAZINE — a direct arterial vasodilator, often used in pregnancy-related hypertension. Because every listed drug IS an antihypertensive, the correct answer is "NONE OF THE ABOVE" (none of them is a non-antihypertensive).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Valsartan — Valsartan IS an antihypertensive (ARB).
• Atenolol — Atenolol IS an antihypertensive (beta-blocker).
• Hydrallazine — Hydralazine IS an antihypertensive (vasodilator).

⭐ Key point: Main antihypertensive classes: ACE inhibitors (e.g. lisinopril), ARBs (e.g. losartan), calcium channel blockers (e.g. amlodipine, nifedipine), thiazide diuretics, beta-blockers, and others (e.g. methyldopa, hydralazine, labetalol).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q70$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1033, $s$Before delivering cardioversion, the nurse must confirm that:$s$, $s$["The machine is sensing (marking) the R wave in sync mode", "The defibrillator is in unsynchronised mode", "Oxygen is flowing over the chest", "The patient is awake and talking"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The machine is sensing (marking) the R wave in sync mode
SYNCHRONISED CARDIOVERSION is used for unstable tachyarrhythmias with a pulse (e.g. AF, atrial flutter, SVT, VT with a pulse). The shock must be timed to the R WAVE so it does not land on the T wave (the "vulnerable period"), which could trigger ventricular fibrillation. Before shocking, the nurse must confirm that "SYNC" mode is ON and the machine is SENSING (MARKING) each R WAVE on the monitor (usually shown as a dot or arrow above each QRS).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The defibrillator is in unsynchronised mode — Unsynchronised mode is used for VF and pulseless VT, not cardioversion.
• Oxygen is flowing over the chest — Oxygen should be moved AWAY from the chest during shocks (fire risk), not flowing over it.
• The patient is awake and talking — The patient should be SEDATED (cardioversion is painful), not awake and talking.

⭐ Key point: Many defibrillators switch back to unsynchronised mode after each shock — re-select "sync" before every further cardioversion attempt.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q72$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1034, $s$While caring for a client who has sustained an MI, the nurse notes eight PVCs in one minute on the cardiac monitor. The client is receiving an IV infusion of D5W and oxygen at 2 L/minute. The nurse’s first course of action should be to:$s$, $s$["Increase the oxygen concentration.", "Increase the IV infusion rate", "Notify the physician promptly.", "Administer a prescribed analgesic."]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Notify the physician promptly.
PREMATURE VENTRICULAR CONTRACTIONS (PVCs) are extra beats from the ventricles. After an MI, FREQUENT PVCs (e.g. more than 6 per minute, multifocal, couplets or "R on T") show that the damaged heart muscle is electrically IRRITABLE, and may be a warning of VENTRICULAR TACHYCARDIA or VENTRICULAR FIBRILLATION. Eight PVCs per minute is significant. The first action is to NOTIFY THE PHYSICIAN PROMPTLY, while assessing the patient (symptoms, BP, oxygen) and checking for causes (low potassium or magnesium, hypoxia, ischaemia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the oxygen concentration. — Increasing oxygen may help if hypoxic, but it does not address the possible arrhythmia risk on its own.
• Increase the IV infusion rate — Increasing the IV rate is not indicated and could cause fluid overload.
• Administer a prescribed analgesic. — Analgesia may be needed for pain, but it is not the first priority for frequent PVCs.

⭐ Key point: Have a defibrillator nearby, check electrolytes, and review the 12-lead ECG for ischaemia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q75$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1035, $s$A client is brought to the emergency room complaining of pressure in her chest. Her blood pressure is 150/90, pulse 88, respirations 20. The nurse administers nitro-glycerine 0.4 mg sublingually as ordered. After five minutes her blood pressure is 100/60, pulse 96, respirations 20. Which of the following should the critical care nurse do next?$s$, $s$["Notify the physician that the client has become hypotensive, and obtain an order to administer IV fluids.", "Place the client in semi-Fowler’s position, and administer O2 at 4 L.", "Administer a second dose of nitro-glycerine.", "Document the results, and continue to monitor the client."]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Document the results, and continue to monitor the client.
SUBLINGUAL GTN (nitroglycerin) dilates blood vessels, so a FALL in BP is EXPECTED. Here the BP fell from 150/90 to 100/60 — a systolic of 100 is still ACCEPTABLE (the usual concern is a systolic below 90 mmHg or a fall of more than 30 mmHg causing symptoms). The heart rate rose slightly (reflex). The nurse should DOCUMENT the results and CONTINUE to MONITOR the client (BP, pulse, pain level) and report as needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Notify the physician that the client has become hypotensive, and obtain an order to administer IV fluids. — The client is not significantly hypotensive (systolic 100), so IV fluids are not needed.
• Place the client in semi-Fowler’s position, and administer O2 at 4 L. — Positioning and oxygen may be part of care, but are not the specific next step indicated by these findings.
• Administer a second dose of nitro-glycerine. — A second dose may be considered if chest pain continues, but with BP now 100/60 it should be given cautiously only after reassessing pain and BP.

⭐ Key point: Before each GTN dose, check BP (hold if systolic below 90 mmHg) and ask about sildenafil/tadalafil use.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q76$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1036, $s$A patient with well-controlled hypertension and no functional limitation is classified as which ASA physical status?$s$, $s$["ASA I", "ASA II", "ASA III", "ASA IV"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ASA II
The ASA PHYSICAL STATUS classification: ASA I — healthy; ASA II — MILD SYSTEMIC DISEASE without functional limitation (e.g. WELL-CONTROLLED HYPERTENSION, controlled diabetes, smoker); ASA III — severe systemic disease with functional limitation; ASA IV — severe disease that is a constant threat to life; ASA V — moribund; ASA VI — brain-dead organ donor.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• ASA I — ASA I is a healthy patient.
• ASA III — ASA III involves functional limitation.
• ASA IV — ASA IV is a constant threat to life.

⭐ Key point: Add "E" for emergency surgery (e.g. ASA IIE).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q113$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1037, $s$Neurogenic shock is distinguished from other types of shock by:$s$, $s$["Tachycardia", "Bradycardia", "No change in heart rate", "Always presenting with fever"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bradycardia
NEUROGENIC SHOCK happens after spinal cord injury above about T6. The injury cuts off the SYMPATHETIC nervous system, so: blood vessels DILATE (low SVR → HYPOTENSION) and the heart is left under unopposed VAGAL (parasympathetic) control, causing BRADYCARDIA. The skin is typically WARM and DRY (because the vessels are dilated). This combination of hypotension WITH bradycardia distinguishes it from most other types of shock (hypovolaemic, septic, cardiogenic), which usually cause TACHYCARDIA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tachycardia — Tachycardia is typical of OTHER shock types (the body's compensation).
• No change in heart rate — The heart rate does change — it falls.
• Always presenting with fever — Neurogenic shock does not always present with fever; temperature regulation may be impaired, causing hypothermia or hyperthermia.

⭐ Key point: Always exclude bleeding first in trauma patients. Treat with careful fluids, vasopressors (e.g. noradrenaline) and atropine or pacing for symptomatic bradycardia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q121$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1038, $s$A 12 kg child in shock is prescribed a 20 mL/kg bolus of normal saline. What volume should be given?$s$, $s$["360 mL", "480 mL", "240 mL", "120 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 240 mL
Fluid boluses in children: VOLUME = mL/kg × WEIGHT. Here: 20 mL/kg × 12 kg = 240 mL of 0.9% saline. Check: 20 × 12 = 240. ✓ Give it rapidly, then REASSESS heart rate, capillary refill, BP, conscious level and urine output, and look for signs of fluid overload (crackles, enlarged liver, raised JVP) before giving more.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 360 mL — 360 mL is 30 mL/kg — more than prescribed.
• 480 mL — 480 mL is 40 mL/kg — double.
• 120 mL — 120 mL is only 10 mL/kg — half.

⭐ Key point: If shock persists after 40–60 mL/kg of fluid, start inotropes/vasopressors and involve paediatric intensive care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q53 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1039, $s$A patient's blood pressure is 176/56 mmHg. What is the mean arterial pressure (MAP)?$s$, $s$["136 mmHg", "96 mmHg", "116 mmHg", "120 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 96 mmHg
MAP = (176 + 2 × 56) ÷ 3 = (176 + 112) ÷ 3 = 288 ÷ 3 = 96 mmHg. Note the wide pulse pressure (176 − 56 = 120), seen in aortic regurgitation, stiff arteries in the elderly, or sepsis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 136 mmHg — 136 mmHg is too high.
• 116 mmHg — 116 mmHg is the simple average.
• 120 mmHg — 120 mmHg is the pulse pressure.

⭐ Key point: A very low diastolic pressure can reduce coronary perfusion even if MAP looks acceptable.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q58 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1040, $s$A hypovolaemic patient becomes hypotensive after intubation and positive-pressure ventilation mainly because:$s$, $s$["Heart rate falls", "Contractility increases", "Afterload falls to zero", "Increased intrathoracic pressure reduces venous return"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increased intrathoracic pressure reduces venous return
POSITIVE-PRESSURE VENTILATION RAISES the pressure inside the chest. This SQUEEZES the large veins and the right atrium, REDUCING VENOUS RETURN to the heart. In a HYPOVOLAEMIC patient (already low blood volume), this further reduces cardiac output and BP. Induction drugs also cause vasodilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Heart rate falls — Heart rate usually doesn't fall.
• Contractility increases — Contractility doesn't increase.
• Afterload falls to zero — Afterload doesn't fall to zero.

⭐ Key point: Give fluids and have vasopressors ready before intubating hypovolaemic patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q123$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1041, $s$A patient receiving IV nitroglycerin for cardiogenic pulmonary oedema develops a blood pressure of 82/50 mmHg. What is the nurse's priority action?$s$, $s$["Administer a fluid bolus and continue the infusion unchanged", "Switch to sublingual nitroglycerin", "Increase the infusion rate to improve cardiac output", "Stop or slow the infusion and reassess blood pressure"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stop or slow the infusion and reassess blood pressure
GLYCERYL TRINITRATE (GTN, nitroglycerin) dilates veins and arteries, which reduces preload and afterload — helpful in pulmonary oedema. But it also LOWERS BP. A BP of 82/50 mmHg (systolic below 90) is too low and may reduce blood flow to the heart, brain and kidneys. The priority is to STOP or SLOW the infusion and REASSESS the BP (it usually recovers quickly because GTN has a short half-life), then notify the doctor.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer a fluid bolus and continue the infusion unchanged — A fluid bolus could worsen pulmonary oedema, and continuing the infusion unchanged would keep BP low.
• Switch to sublingual nitroglycerin — Sublingual GTN would lower BP further.
• Increase the infusion rate to improve cardiac output — Increasing the infusion would worsen hypotension.

⭐ Key point: Check BP every 5–15 minutes when starting or changing GTN. Avoid GTN in patients who have taken sildenafil or tadalafil.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q151$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1042, $s$A patient receiving an intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) suddenly develops a cold, pulseless left leg. The nurse should suspect:$s$, $s$["Limb ischaemia from the IABP catheter, requiring urgent assessment and intervention", "An allergic reaction to the dressing", "Normal side effect requiring no action", "A simple positioning issue with no urgency"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Limb ischaemia from the IABP catheter, requiring urgent assessment and intervention
An INTRA-AORTIC BALLOON PUMP (IABP) catheter is inserted through the FEMORAL ARTERY into the descending aorta. It can BLOCK or reduce blood flow to the leg, cause clots, or dissect the artery, leading to LIMB ISCHAEMIA: a COLD, PALE, PULSELESS leg with pain, numbness and weakness. This is an emergency — check pulses (Doppler), colour, temperature, sensation and movement, and notify the doctor urgently (the IABP may need removal or repositioning).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An allergic reaction to the dressing — An allergic reaction to the dressing would cause local itching and rash, not a pulseless leg.
• Normal side effect requiring no action — A cold, pulseless leg is never a normal side effect.
• A simple positioning issue with no urgency — It is not a simple positioning issue; it needs urgent action.

⭐ Key point: Check distal pulses and limb perfusion (hourly) in patients with an IABP. Keep the leg straight and avoid hip flexion beyond 30°.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q154$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1043, $s$A patient with cardiac tamponade would be expected to show which combination of findings (Beck's Triad)?$s$, $s$["Fever, cough, wheeze", "Polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss", "Hypotension, jugular venous distension, muffled heart sounds", "Hypertension, bradycardia, loud heart sounds"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypotension, jugular venous distension, muffled heart sounds
BECK'S TRIAD indicates CARDIAC TAMPONADE (fluid compressing the heart): HYPOTENSION (low cardiac output), JUGULAR VENOUS DISTENSION (blood backs up), and MUFFLED HEART SOUNDS (fluid dampens sound).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fever, cough, wheeze — Fever, cough and wheeze suggest respiratory infection.
• Polyuria, polydipsia, weight loss — Polyuria, polydipsia and weight loss suggest diabetes.
• Hypertension, bradycardia, loud heart sounds — Hypertension, bradycardia and loud heart sounds are not tamponade signs.

⭐ Key point: Pulsus paradoxus (BP drop >10 mmHg on inspiration) is another sign. Treat with pericardiocentesis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q167$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1044, $s$A patient's angina is unrelieved after 3 sublingual nitroglycerin tablets taken 5 minutes apart. The priority nursing action is to:$s$, $s$["Administer a 4th tablet and wait 15 more minutes", "Notify the provider/activate emergency response and evaluate for acute MI", "Encourage the patient to walk to reduce anxiety", "Document and reassess in 1 hour"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Notify the provider/activate emergency response and evaluate for acute MI
If chest pain is NOT RELIEVED after 3 sublingual GTN doses 5 minutes apart, it may be an ACUTE MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION (heart attack). NOTIFY THE PROVIDER/ACTIVATE EMERGENCY RESPONSE, perform a 12-lead ECG, give aspirin (if not contraindicated), oxygen if hypoxic, and prepare for cardiac care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer a 4th tablet and wait 15 more minutes — A 4th tablet and waiting delays treatment.
• Encourage the patient to walk to reduce anxiety — Walking increases heart workload.
• Document and reassess in 1 hour — Waiting an hour risks heart muscle death.

⭐ Key point: "Time is muscle" — rapid reperfusion saves heart tissue.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q173$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1045, $s$A woman with an ectopic pregnancy presents with unilateral lower abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding, and signs of hypovolaemic shock. This presentation should be treated as:$s$, $s$["A life-threatening gynaecological emergency requiring urgent assessment and likely surgical intervention", "A urinary tract infection", "Normal early pregnancy discomfort", "A routine outpatient concern"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A life-threatening gynaecological emergency requiring urgent assessment and likely surgical intervention
An ECTOPIC PREGNANCY implants outside the uterus, usually in the fallopian tube. As it grows, the tube can RUPTURE, causing MASSIVE INTERNAL BLEEDING into the abdomen. Unilateral lower abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding and signs of HYPOVOLAEMIC SHOCK (tachycardia, hypotension, pallor, collapse, shoulder tip pain from blood irritating the diaphragm) mean a LIFE-THREATENING emergency. Actions: call for help, two large-bore IV cannulas, crossmatch blood, fluid and blood resuscitation, and URGENT SURGERY (laparoscopy or laparotomy).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A urinary tract infection — A UTI does not cause shock with vaginal bleeding in pregnancy.
• Normal early pregnancy discomfort — Shock is never normal pregnancy discomfort.
• A routine outpatient concern — This is not a routine outpatient concern; delay can be fatal.

⭐ Key point: Think of ectopic pregnancy in any woman of childbearing age with abdominal pain or collapse — do a pregnancy test.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q187$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1046, $s$A patient under spinal anaesthesia for a caesarean section develops sudden hypotension and nausea. The nurse should recognise this as likely due to:$s$, $s$["An allergic reaction requiring adrenaline", "A sign of successful anaesthesia with no action needed", "Sympathetic blockade from the spinal anaesthesia causing vasodilation, typically managed with fluids and/or vasopressors", "Unrelated cardiac disease"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sympathetic blockade from the spinal anaesthesia causing vasodilation, typically managed with fluids and/or vasopressors
SPINAL ANAESTHESIA for caesarean section blocks the SYMPATHETIC nerves (which keep blood vessels constricted). The vessels in the lower body DILATE, blood pools, and BP falls (HYPOTENSION). Low BP reduces blood flow to the brain (causing NAUSEA, dizziness) and to the placenta (risking fetal distress). Management: LEFT UTERINE DISPLACEMENT (to relieve aortocaval compression), IV FLUIDS (co-loading), and VASOPRESSORS (phenylephrine first-line, or ephedrine).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An allergic reaction requiring adrenaline — An allergic reaction would typically cause rash, wheeze and swelling — adrenaline is not the first treatment here.
• A sign of successful anaesthesia with no action needed — Hypotension is common but must be treated, not ignored.
• Unrelated cardiac disease — It is not due to unrelated cardiac disease.

⭐ Key point: Nausea after spinal anaesthesia is often the first sign of hypotension — check the BP immediately.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q198$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1047, $s$A patient with a pulmonary artery catheter (PAC) in place develops a persistently wedged waveform. The nurse's most appropriate immediate action is to:$s$, $s$["Inflate the balloon further to confirm the wedge", "Ignore it since PAC waveforms fluctuate normally", "Deflate the balloon immediately and reposition/notify the physician, as a permanently wedged catheter risks pulmonary infarction", "Increase the IV fluid rate"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Deflate the balloon immediately and reposition/notify the physician, as a permanently wedged catheter risks pulmonary infarction
A PERSISTENTLY WEDGED PA catheter waveform means the catheter tip has migrated into a small pulmonary artery, blocking blood flow and risking PULMONARY INFARCTION or rupture. DEFLATE the balloon (if inflated), reposition/pull back per protocol, and NOTIFY THE PHYSICIAN.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Inflate the balloon further to confirm the wedge — Inflating the balloon further risks rupture.
• Ignore it since PAC waveforms fluctuate normally — Ignoring it risks infarction.
• Increase the IV fluid rate — Increasing IV fluids doesn't fix the catheter position.

⭐ Key point: Never leave the balloon inflated; limit wedge readings to brief periods.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q200$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1048, $s$A patient's blood pressure is 164/58 mmHg. What is the mean arterial pressure (MAP)?$s$, $s$["93 mmHg", "129 mmHg", "106 mmHg", "111 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 93 mmHg
MEAN ARTERIAL PRESSURE (MAP) = (SYSTOLIC + 2 × DIASTOLIC) ÷ 3, or DIASTOLIC + ⅓ × (SYSTOLIC − DIASTOLIC). Here: (164 + 2 × 58) ÷ 3 = (164 + 116) ÷ 3 = 280 ÷ 3 = 93.3 ≈ 93 mmHg.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 129 mmHg — 129 mmHg is far too high; it would be closer to the systolic value and ignores that diastole lasts longer.
• 106 mmHg — 106 mmHg is a calculation error.
• 111 mmHg — 111 mmHg is the simple average of systolic and diastolic ((164 + 58) ÷ 2), which is wrong — the heart spends longer in diastole.

⭐ Key point: A MAP of at least 65 mmHg is usually needed to perfuse vital organs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q36 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1049, $s$A 14 kg child in shock is prescribed a 20 mL/kg bolus of normal saline. What volume should be given?$s$, $s$["420 mL", "280 mL", "140 mL", "560 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 280 mL
Fluid boluses in children are calculated by weight: VOLUME = mL/kg × WEIGHT. Here: 20 mL/kg × 14 kg = 280 mL of 0.9% sodium chloride. Check: 20 × 14 = (20 × 10) + (20 × 4) = 200 + 80 = 280 mL. ✓ Give it quickly (over 5–10 minutes in shock, using a syringe or pressure bag), then REASSESS: heart rate, capillary refill, BP, conscious level, urine output, and look for signs of overload (crackles, enlarged liver).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 420 mL — 420 mL is 30 mL/kg — too much for a single bolus.
• 140 mL — 140 mL is only 10 mL/kg (sometimes used in cardiac disease or severe malnutrition, but not here).
• 560 mL — 560 mL is 40 mL/kg — far too much at once.

⭐ Key point: Some guidelines (e.g. for children with severe malnutrition or heart disease, or in some low-resource settings) recommend smaller boluses (10 mL/kg); follow local protocols and reassess after every bolus.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q44 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1050, $s$A patient's blood pressure is 126/84 mmHg. What is the mean arterial pressure (MAP)?$s$, $s$["98 mmHg", "112 mmHg", "105 mmHg", "42 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 98 mmHg
MEAN ARTERIAL PRESSURE (MAP) is the average pressure in the arteries during one heartbeat. Because the heart spends about TWO-THIRDS of each beat in DIASTOLE, the diastolic pressure counts double. Formula: MAP = (SYSTOLIC + 2 × DIASTOLIC) ÷ 3. Here: (126 + 2 × 84) ÷ 3 = (126 + 168) ÷ 3 = 294 ÷ 3 = 98 mmHg. Another way: MAP = diastolic + ⅓ of pulse pressure = 84 + (42 ÷ 3) = 84 + 14 = 98. ✓

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 112 mmHg — 112 mmHg is not correct.
• 105 mmHg — 105 mmHg is the simple average of systolic and diastolic ((126 + 84) ÷ 2) — wrong, because diastole lasts longer.
• 42 mmHg — 42 mmHg is the PULSE PRESSURE (126 − 84), not the MAP.

⭐ Key point: A MAP of at least 65 mmHg is usually needed to perfuse the brain, kidneys and heart; it is the target in septic shock.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q52 · concept asked in 4 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1051, $s$A patient 3 days post-MI develops a new holosystolic murmur, hypotension, and acute pulmonary edema. This presentation is most consistent with:$s$, $s$["Papillary muscle rupture causing acute mitral regurgitation", "Deep vein thrombosis", "Stable angina", "Pericarditis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Papillary muscle rupture causing acute mitral regurgitation
About 2–7 days after an MI, PAPILLARY MUSCLE RUPTURE can occur when the damaged muscle tears. The mitral valve leaflets lose their support, causing SUDDEN, severe ACUTE MITRAL REGURGITATION: a new HOLOSYSTOLIC (pansystolic) MURMUR at the apex, HYPOTENSION (low output), and ACUTE PULMONARY OEDEMA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Deep vein thrombosis — DVT does not cause a murmur and pulmonary oedema.
• Stable angina — Stable angina is chest pain on exertion without these acute findings.
• Pericarditis — Pericarditis causes chest pain with a pericardial rub, not a holosystolic murmur and pulmonary oedema.

⭐ Key point: This is a surgical emergency: urgent echo, vasodilators, IABP, and valve surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q167$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1052, $s$A patient on haemodialysis develops hypotension, cramping, and nausea during the session. The nurse's priority action is to:$s$, $s$["Ignore symptoms since they are expected", "Increase the ultrafiltration rate to relieve symptoms faster", "Slow or stop ultrafiltration, position the patient appropriately, and consider a fluid bolus per protocol", "Immediately terminate dialysis access permanently"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Slow or stop ultrafiltration, position the patient appropriately, and consider a fluid bolus per protocol
INTRADIALYTIC HYPOTENSION happens when fluid is removed (ULTRAFILTRATION) faster than the body can refill the blood vessels. Symptoms: HYPOTENSION, CRAMPING, NAUSEA, dizziness. The nurse should SLOW or STOP ULTRAFILTRATION, POSITION the patient flat with legs raised (Trendelenburg), and consider a small FLUID BOLUS (e.g. saline) according to protocol.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignore symptoms since they are expected — These symptoms need action.
• Increase the ultrafiltration rate to relieve symptoms faster — Increasing ultrafiltration would worsen hypotension.
• Immediately terminate dialysis access permanently — Terminating dialysis access permanently is inappropriate.

⭐ Key point: Prevent by limiting fluid gain between sessions, careful target weight setting, and avoiding antihypertensives before dialysis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q173$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1053, $s$A post-nephrectomy patient's hourly urine output drops significantly with a rising heart rate and falling blood pressure. The nurse should first suspect:$s$, $s$["Hypovolaemia/bleeding, requiring urgent assessment and fluid resuscitation", "A urinary tract infection", "Normal recovery with no action needed", "Overhydration"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypovolaemia/bleeding, requiring urgent assessment and fluid resuscitation
After NEPHRECTOMY (kidney removal), BLEEDING from the renal vessels is a serious risk. FALLING URINE OUTPUT, RISING HEART RATE and FALLING BP are classic signs of HYPOVOLAEMIA from BLEEDING. The nurse should urgently assess (check wound and drains, vital signs, Hb), give IV fluids, notify the surgeon, and prepare for blood transfusion and possible return to theatre.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A urinary tract infection — A UTI would cause fever and urinary symptoms, not shock.
• Normal recovery with no action needed — These are not signs of normal recovery.
• Overhydration — Overhydration would cause high BP and oedema, not hypotension and tachycardia.

⭐ Key point: Monitor the remaining kidney's function closely after nephrectomy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q176$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1054, $s$A patient in cardiogenic shock with low cardiac output and high SVR would most benefit from an agent that:$s$, $s$["Decreases contractility", "Further increases SVR/afterload, such as phenylephrine", "Causes pure vasoconstriction without inotropic support", "Increases contractility and reduces afterload, such as dobutamine"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increases contractility and reduces afterload, such as dobutamine
In CARDIOGENIC SHOCK, the heart pumps weakly (LOW CARDIAC OUTPUT). The body tries to keep BP up by constricting vessels (HIGH SVR), but this extra resistance (afterload) makes it even harder for the weak heart to pump. The ideal drug INCREASES CONTRACTILITY (stronger heartbeat) and REDUCES AFTERLOAD (opens vessels slightly) — an "inodilator" such as DOBUTAMINE (β1 stimulation for contractility, mild β2 vasodilation) or milrinone. If BP is very low, noradrenaline may be added to maintain perfusion pressure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Decreases contractility — Decreasing contractility (e.g. beta-blockers) would worsen cardiac output.
• Further increases SVR/afterload, such as phenylephrine — Phenylephrine increases SVR/afterload, which further reduces cardiac output.
• Causes pure vasoconstriction without inotropic support — Pure vasoconstriction without inotropic support increases the heart's workload and worsens output.

⭐ Key point: Treat the cause (e.g. urgent coronary revascularisation in MI). Mechanical support (IABP, Impella, ECMO) may be needed if drugs fail.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q186$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1055, $s$A patient with decompensated heart failure and hypotension (cardiogenic shock physiology) may benefit from which inotropic agent to improve contractility and cardiac output?$s$, $s$["Dobutamine", "Verapamil", "Metoprolol", "Furosemide"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dobutamine
In DECOMPENSATED HEART FAILURE with HYPOTENSION (cardiogenic shock physiology), the heart muscle is too weak to pump enough blood. An INOTROPE increases the force of contraction. DOBUTAMINE is a β1-agonist inotrope that INCREASES CONTRACTILITY and CARDIAC OUTPUT, improving organ perfusion. It is often combined with noradrenaline if BP is very low (because dobutamine can cause some vasodilation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Verapamil — Verapamil (a calcium channel blocker) REDUCES contractility and heart rate — dangerous in heart failure with low BP.
• Metoprolol — Metoprolol (a beta-blocker) REDUCES contractility — harmful in acute cardiogenic shock (although useful in stable chronic heart failure).
• Furosemide — Furosemide (a diuretic) removes fluid but does NOT improve contractility, and can lower BP further.

⭐ Key point: Monitor for tachycardia, arrhythmias and hypotension during dobutamine infusion. Milrinone is an alternative inotrope.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q196$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1056, $s$A critical pathway (care map) used in an ICU for post-cardiac-surgery clients is BEST described as:$s$, $s$["A document that replaces the need for nursing assessment", "A standardized, multidisciplinary plan outlining the sequence and timing of interventions expected to achieve outcomes within a specific time frame", "A tool used exclusively for billing purposes", "A plan used only for clients with no comorbidities"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A standardized, multidisciplinary plan outlining the sequence and timing of interventions expected to achieve outcomes within a specific time frame
A CRITICAL PATHWAY (care map) is a STANDARDISED, MULTIDISCIPLINARY plan that outlines the SEQUENCE and TIMING of key INTERVENTIONS expected to achieve OUTCOMES within a specific TIME FRAME for a particular condition or procedure (e.g. post-cardiac surgery). It coordinates care, reduces variation, and identifies "variances" (when the patient does not progress as expected).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A document that replaces the need for nursing assessment — It does not replace nursing assessment.
• A tool used exclusively for billing purposes — It is a clinical tool, not just for billing.
• A plan used only for clients with no comorbidities — It can be adapted for patients with comorbidities.

⭐ Key point: Variances from the pathway are documented and analysed to improve care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q159$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1057, $s$Which of the following is an example of a balance and coordination exercise?$s$, $s$["Kegel exercise", "Ballistic stretching", "Squatting exercise", "Frenkel's exercises"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Frenkel's exercises
FRENKEL'S EXERCISES are a series of slow, controlled, repetitive movements designed to improve BALANCE and COORDINATION in patients with ATAXIA — especially sensory ataxia from loss of proprioception (position sense), e.g. in tabes dorsalis, multiple sclerosis or cerebellar disorders. Patients use their EYES (visual feedback) to guide movements, compensating for poor position sense. They progress from lying, to sitting, to standing, to walking, from simple to complex movements.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Kegel exercise — Kegel exercises strengthen the pelvic floor muscles (for incontinence).
• Ballistic stretching — Ballistic stretching uses bouncing movements to increase flexibility (and risks injury).
• Squatting exercise — Squatting is a strengthening exercise for the legs.

⭐ Key point: Frenkel's exercises require concentration, so they are done in a quiet environment, with short sessions to avoid fatigue.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q162$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1058, $s$Passive exercise is used clinically mainly to:$s$, $s$["Increase muscle tone", "Increase muscle power", "Increase muscle strength", "Maintain joint range of motion"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Maintain joint range of motion
PASSIVE EXERCISE is performed by another person (e.g. nurse, physiotherapist) or a machine, WITHOUT the patient's muscle effort. It is used mainly to MAINTAIN JOINT RANGE OF MOTION and prevent contractures in patients who cannot move themselves (e.g. unconscious or paralysed ICU patients). It does NOT build muscle strength.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase muscle tone — Muscle tone is not increased by passive exercise.
• Increase muscle power — Muscle power requires active exercise.
• Increase muscle strength — Muscle strength requires active (resisted) exercise.

⭐ Key point: Move each joint through its full range, slowly and gently, several times a day.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q163$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1059, $s$Decreased orthostatic tolerance after prolonged bed rest is characterised by:$s$, $s$["Cerebral hyperaemia", "A fall in heart rate on standing", "Hypertension on standing", "Dizziness or faintness when the patient suddenly sits or stands up"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dizziness or faintness when the patient suddenly sits or stands up
After PROLONGED BED REST, the body's ability to adjust blood pressure on standing (ORTHOSTATIC TOLERANCE) decreases. When the patient suddenly sits or stands up, blood pools in the legs, BP drops (orthostatic hypotension) and the patient feels DIZZY or FAINT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cerebral hyperaemia — Cerebral hyperaemia (too much blood to the brain) does not occur; blood flow to the brain decreases.
• A fall in heart rate on standing — The heart rate usually increases on standing.
• Hypertension on standing — BP falls (hypotension) on standing, not rises.

⭐ Key point: Mobilise gradually: sit up, dangle legs, then stand with support.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q166$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1060, $s$Which statement about the Oxford (MRC) scale for grading muscle power is FALSE?$s$, $s$["Grade 0 means no contraction at all", "It is used to measure muscle strength", "Grade 5 means movement against gravity only", "It grades power from 0 to 5"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Grade 5 means movement against gravity only
The OXFORD (MRC) SCALE grades MUSCLE POWER from 0 to 5: 0 = no contraction; 1 = flicker of contraction; 2 = movement with gravity eliminated; 3 = movement AGAINST GRAVITY only; 4 = movement against gravity and SOME resistance; 5 = NORMAL power. The statement "Grade 5 means movement against gravity only" is FALSE — that describes grade 3.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Grade 0 means no contraction at all — Grade 0 means no contraction — true.
• It is used to measure muscle strength — It is used to measure muscle strength — true.
• It grades power from 0 to 5 — It grades power from 0 to 5 — true.

⭐ Key point: The MRC sum score (0–60) is used to diagnose ICU-acquired weakness (below 48).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q167$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1061, $s$The effects of prolonged immobility on the musculoskeletal system include all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Increased muscle mass", "Joint contracture", "Decreased joint stability", "Reduced muscle endurance"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increased muscle mass
PROLONGED IMMOBILITY has many harmful effects on the musculoskeletal system: MUSCLE ATROPHY (DECREASED muscle mass and strength — up to 1–1.5% per day of bed rest), REDUCED MUSCLE ENDURANCE, JOINT CONTRACTURES (shortened muscles and tendons fixing joints in abnormal positions, e.g. foot drop), DECREASED JOINT STABILITY and mobility, and BONE LOSS (disuse osteoporosis, leading to high calcium and risk of kidney stones and fractures). INCREASED MUSCLE MASS does NOT occur with immobility.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Joint contracture — Joint contracture is a recognised effect of immobility.
• Decreased joint stability — Decreased joint stability is a recognised effect.
• Reduced muscle endurance — Reduced muscle endurance is a recognised effect.

⭐ Key point: Prevent with early mobilisation, range-of-motion exercises, correct positioning, splints (e.g. to prevent foot drop) and good nutrition.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q168$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1062, $s$Which barrier method is suitable for a couple when one partner has a latex allergy?$s$, $s$["Male latex condom", "Latex diaphragm", "None — barrier methods cannot be used", "Female condom (nitrile/polyurethane)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Female condom (nitrile/polyurethane)
If one partner has a LATEX ALLERGY, latex barrier methods (latex male condoms, latex diaphragms) can cause reactions ranging from itching and rash to anaphylaxis. The FEMALE CONDOM is made of NITRILE (or polyurethane), which is non-latex, so it is a suitable barrier method that also protects against STIs. Non-latex male condoms (polyurethane or polyisoprene) are also available.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Male latex condom — Male latex condoms would trigger the allergy.
• Latex diaphragm — Latex diaphragms would trigger the allergy.
• None — barrier methods cannot be used — Barrier methods CAN be used if they are made of non-latex materials.

⭐ Key point: Always ask about latex allergy before using latex products; use non-latex gloves and equipment in the ICU for allergic patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q181$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1063, $s$In a central venous pressure (CVP) waveform, the 'a' wave represents:$s$, $s$["Atrial relaxation", "Ventricular relaxation", "Atrial contraction", "Ventricular contraction"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Atrial contraction
The CVP waveform reflects pressure changes in the RIGHT ATRIUM. It has three positive waves and two descents: the "a" WAVE — RIGHT ATRIAL CONTRACTION, which pushes blood into the right ventricle (seen just after the P wave on the ECG); the "c" wave — bulging of the closed tricuspid valve into the atrium at the start of ventricular contraction; the "x" descent — atrial relaxation; the "v" wave — filling of the right atrium against the closed tricuspid valve; the "y" descent — opening of the tricuspid valve and rapid emptying of the atrium.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Atrial relaxation — Atrial relaxation is the x descent.
• Ventricular relaxation — Ventricular relaxation relates to the y descent/v wave period.
• Ventricular contraction — Ventricular contraction produces the c wave.

⭐ Key point: Absent "a" waves occur in atrial fibrillation; large "cannon a" waves occur in complete heart block; large "v" waves suggest tricuspid regurgitation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q183$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1064, $s$In a pulmonary artery pressure waveform, the dicrotic notch represents:$s$, $s$["Opening of the mitral valve", "The peak of ventricular systole", "Closure of the pulmonary valve", "Closure of the tricuspid valve"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Closure of the pulmonary valve
The PULMONARY ARTERY (PA) pressure waveform looks like an arterial waveform. The DICROTIC NOTCH on the downstroke represents CLOSURE OF THE PULMONARY VALVE at the end of right ventricular systole — after this, the pressure falls slowly during diastole. (In the systemic arterial waveform, the dicrotic notch represents AORTIC valve closure.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Opening of the mitral valve — Opening of the mitral valve is not seen on the PA waveform.
• The peak of ventricular systole — The peak of ventricular systole is the top of the waveform, not the notch.
• Closure of the tricuspid valve — Closure of the tricuspid valve is related to the "c" wave on the CVP waveform.

⭐ Key point: Normal PA pressure is about 15–30/5–15 mmHg; normal PA wedge pressure is about 6–12 mmHg.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q184$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1065, $s$Cardiac output is calculated as:$s$, $s$["Blood pressure ÷ heart rate", "Blood pressure × heart rate", "Stroke volume × heart rate", "Stroke volume ÷ heart rate"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stroke volume × heart rate
CARDIAC OUTPUT (CO) is the volume of blood the heart pumps per minute. CO = STROKE VOLUME (SV — the volume pumped with each beat) × HEART RATE (HR — beats per minute). Example: SV 70 mL × HR 80/min = 5,600 mL/min = 5.6 L/min. Normal CO is about 4–8 L/min. Stroke volume depends on PRELOAD (filling), AFTERLOAD (resistance) and CONTRACTILITY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Blood pressure ÷ heart rate — BP ÷ HR is not a formula for cardiac output.
• Blood pressure × heart rate — BP × HR is related to myocardial oxygen demand ("rate-pressure product"), not cardiac output.
• Stroke volume ÷ heart rate — SV ÷ HR is not meaningful.

⭐ Key point: Cardiac index (CI) = CO ÷ body surface area (normal 2.5–4.0 L/min/m²). Also, MAP = CO × SVR (approximately).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q185$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1066, $s$Right ventricular preload is best estimated at the bedside by:$s$, $s$["Central venous pressure (CVP)", "Mean arterial pressure (MAP)", "Cardiac output (CO)", "Systemic vascular resistance (SVR)"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Central venous pressure (CVP)
PRELOAD is the stretch (filling) of the ventricle at the end of diastole. RIGHT VENTRICULAR PRELOAD is estimated at the bedside by the CENTRAL VENOUS PRESSURE (CVP), which reflects right atrial pressure (normal about 2–8 mmHg or 3–10 cmH2O). A low CVP may suggest hypovolaemia; a high CVP may suggest fluid overload, right heart failure, tamponade or high intrathoracic pressure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mean arterial pressure (MAP) — MAP reflects the average arterial pressure perfusing organs.
• Cardiac output (CO) — Cardiac output reflects the heart's pumping, not preload.
• Systemic vascular resistance (SVR) — SVR reflects left ventricular AFTERLOAD.

⭐ Key point: Single CVP values are poor predictors of fluid responsiveness; trends and dynamic tests (e.g. passive leg raising) are more useful.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q187$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1067, $s$A patient has a low cardiac output with a high systemic vascular resistance and adequate filling pressures. Which therapy is most appropriate to improve output?$s$, $s$["Beta-blockers", "Vasodilators (afterload reduction)", "Vasopressors", "Rapid fluid boluses"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vasodilators (afterload reduction)
This patient has LOW CARDIAC OUTPUT with HIGH SVR (the vessels are tightly constricted) and ADEQUATE FILLING PRESSURES (fluids are not needed). The heart is struggling to pump against the high resistance (high AFTERLOAD). VASODILATORS (e.g. nitroprusside, GTN, or inodilators like milrinone/dobutamine) reduce afterload, making it easier for the heart to eject blood, so CARDIAC OUTPUT IMPROVES. BP must be watched closely.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Beta-blockers — Beta-blockers reduce heart rate and contractility, further lowering cardiac output.
• Vasopressors — Vasopressors would increase SVR further, worsening output.
• Rapid fluid boluses — Rapid fluid boluses are not needed when filling is adequate and could cause pulmonary oedema.

⭐ Key point: Low output + high SVR + adequate filling = afterload reduction (± inotropes). Low output + low filling = fluids.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q189$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1068, $s$An arterial line transducer is accidentally left 10 cm BELOW the phlebostatic axis. The displayed blood pressure will be:$s$, $s$["Unchanged, because the system was zeroed earlier", "Falsely low, by about 7 mmHg", "Falsely high, by about 7 mmHg", "Falsely high, by about 10 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Falsely high, by about 7 mmHg
Arterial line transducers must be level with the PHLEBOSTATIC AXIS (4th intercostal space, mid-axillary line — the level of the right atrium). If the transducer is BELOW this level, the weight of the fluid column above the transducer adds pressure, so the reading is FALSELY HIGH. Every 10 cm of water = about 7.4 mmHg. So 10 cm below → FALSELY HIGH by about 7 mmHg.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Unchanged, because the system was zeroed earlier — The reading changes when the transducer level changes, even if zeroed earlier.
• Falsely low, by about 7 mmHg — A transducer placed ABOVE the axis would give a falsely LOW reading.
• Falsely high, by about 10 mmHg — 10 cmH2O ≈ 7.4 mmHg, not 10 mmHg.

⭐ Key point: Re-level the transducer whenever the patient's position changes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q34$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1069, $s$A fast-flush (square-wave) test on an arterial line shows 1½–2 oscillations before the trace returns to the waveform. This indicates:$s$, $s$["An optimally damped system", "A disconnected transducer", "An under-damped system", "An over-damped system"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An optimally damped system
The FAST-FLUSH (square-wave) test checks the dynamic response of an arterial line. After a fast flush, the trace shows a square wave and then OSCILLATIONS before returning to the waveform. 1½–2 oscillations indicate an OPTIMALLY DAMPED system, meaning the readings are accurate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A disconnected transducer — A disconnected transducer shows no proper waveform.
• An under-damped system — An UNDER-DAMPED system shows MANY oscillations (more than 2).
• An over-damped system — An OVER-DAMPED system shows FEW (less than 1½) or no oscillations.

⭐ Key point: Perform the fast-flush test regularly (at least once per shift) and whenever the trace looks abnormal.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q36$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1070, $s$Six hours after cardiac surgery, a patient's BP falls to 80/60 mmHg. Heart rate is 125, JVP is raised, heart sounds are muffled, systolic pressure drops 15 mmHg on inspiration, and chest drain output has suddenly stopped. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Hypovolaemia from concealed bleeding", "Tension pneumothorax", "Cardiac tamponade", "Right ventricular infarction"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cardiac tamponade
After cardiac surgery, bleeding into the pericardium can compress the heart: CARDIAC TAMPONADE. Signs: HYPOTENSION (80/60), TACHYCARDIA (125), RAISED JVP (blood backs up), MUFFLED HEART SOUNDS, PULSUS PARADOXUS (systolic falls more than 10 mmHg on inspiration — here 15), and a SUDDEN STOP in chest drain output (the drains have clotted, so blood is trapped around the heart).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypovolaemia from concealed bleeding — Hypovolaemia from bleeding causes a LOW (not raised) JVP.
• Tension pneumothorax — Tension pneumothorax causes absent breath sounds and tracheal deviation.
• Right ventricular infarction — RV infarction causes raised JVP and hypotension, but would not explain the sudden stop in drain output 6 hours after surgery.

⭐ Key point: Post-cardiac surgery tamponade requires urgent re-opening of the chest (re-sternotomy). Keep drains patent and monitor output hourly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q41$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1071, $s$A patient with an inferior STEMI becomes hypotensive (80/50 mmHg) with a raised JVP and CLEAR lung fields. ST elevation is seen in right-sided lead V4R. The diagnosis and key management are:$s$, $s$["Massive PE — systemic thrombolysis immediately", "RV infarction — IV fluid boluses; avoid nitrates", "Cardiac tamponade — urgent pericardiocentesis", "LV failure — IV furosemide and GTN infusion"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: RV infarction — IV fluid boluses; avoid nitrates
An INFERIOR STEMI (ST elevation in II, III, aVF) is usually caused by RIGHT CORONARY ARTERY occlusion, which also supplies the RIGHT VENTRICLE. RV INFARCTION is suggested by: HYPOTENSION, RAISED JVP, and CLEAR LUNG FIELDS (the right heart fails, so blood doesn't reach the lungs). ST elevation in right-sided lead V4R confirms it. The RV needs adequate filling (PRELOAD) to pump blood, so give IV FLUID BOLUSES. AVOID NITRATES (and diuretics, morphine), which reduce preload and can cause severe hypotension.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Massive PE — systemic thrombolysis immediately — Massive PE causes hypotension and raised JVP, but V4R ST elevation points to RV infarction.
• Cardiac tamponade — urgent pericardiocentesis — Tamponade causes hypotension and raised JVP, but V4R ST elevation points to RV infarction.
• LV failure — IV furosemide and GTN infusion — LV failure causes pulmonary oedema (crackles), not clear lung fields; furosemide and GTN would worsen RV infarction.

⭐ Key point: In all inferior STEMIs, record a right-sided ECG (V4R). Urgent reperfusion (PCI or thrombolysis) is still the main treatment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q42$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1072, $s$A 58-year-old hypertensive man has sudden 'tearing' chest pain radiating to the back. BP is 180/100 mmHg in the right arm and 140/80 mmHg in the left arm, and there is a new early diastolic murmur. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Massive pulmonary thromboembolism", "Acute anterior myocardial infarction", "Stanford type A aortic dissection", "Acute pericarditis with effusion"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stanford type A aortic dissection
AORTIC DISSECTION is a tear in the inner layer of the aorta, allowing blood to split the wall. Classic features: SUDDEN, severe "TEARING" CHEST PAIN radiating to the BACK, a BP DIFFERENCE between arms (more than 20 mmHg — here 40), and a new EARLY DIASTOLIC MURMUR of AORTIC REGURGITATION (the dissection involves the aortic root). This indicates a STANFORD TYPE A dissection (involving the ascending aorta), a surgical emergency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Massive pulmonary thromboembolism — Massive PE causes breathlessness and hypotension, not BP differences between arms or aortic murmurs.
• Acute anterior myocardial infarction — Anterior MI causes central crushing chest pain, but not BP differences between arms or a "tearing" back pain.
• Acute pericarditis with effusion — Pericarditis causes sharp pain relieved by sitting forward, with a pericardial rub.

⭐ Key point: Never give thrombolytics if aortic dissection is suspected. Control heart rate and BP (beta-blockers), give pain relief, and arrange urgent surgery for Type A.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q43$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1073, $s$A 30-year-old has sharp central chest pain that is worse lying flat and relieved by sitting forward. The ECG shows widespread concave ('saddle-shaped') ST elevation with PR depression, and a scratchy sound is heard at the left sternal edge. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Acute pericarditis", "Takotsubo cardiomyopathy", "Benign early repolarisation", "Acute anterior STEMI"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute pericarditis
ACUTE PERICARDITIS (inflammation of the pericardium, often viral) causes: SHARP central chest pain, WORSE LYING FLAT and RELIEVED BY SITTING FORWARD; an ECG showing WIDESPREAD CONCAVE ("SADDLE-SHAPED") ST ELEVATION in many leads (not following one artery), with PR DEPRESSION; and a PERICARDIAL RUB (a scratchy sound) at the left sternal edge.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Takotsubo cardiomyopathy — Takotsubo cardiomyopathy usually occurs after emotional stress, with chest pain and ST changes, but not positional pain and a pericardial rub.
• Benign early repolarisation — Benign early repolarisation causes ST elevation (usually in young men) without symptoms.
• Acute anterior STEMI — Anterior STEMI causes convex ST elevation in specific leads (V1–V4), with reciprocal changes, and no rub.

⭐ Key point: Treat with NSAIDs (e.g. ibuprofen) and colchicine. Watch for pericardial effusion and tamponade.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q44$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1074, $s$Five days after an acute MI, a patient suddenly develops severe breathlessness and pink frothy sputum, with a NEW loud pansystolic murmur at the apex radiating to the axilla. The most likely complication is:$s$, $s$["Papillary muscle rupture (acute mitral regurgitation)", "Ventricular septal rupture (acute left-to-right shunt)", "Dressler syndrome (post-MI autoimmune pericarditis)", "Left ventricular free wall rupture with tamponade"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Papillary muscle rupture (acute mitral regurgitation)
About 2–7 days after an MI, the damaged heart muscle is weakest. PAPILLARY MUSCLE RUPTURE (usually the posteromedial papillary muscle) causes SUDDEN, severe ACUTE MITRAL REGURGITATION: blood flows backwards into the left atrium and lungs, causing ACUTE PULMONARY OEDEMA (severe breathlessness, PINK FROTHY SPUTUM) and a NEW loud PANSYSTOLIC MURMUR at the APEX radiating to the AXILLA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ventricular septal rupture (acute left-to-right shunt) — Ventricular septal rupture also causes a new pansystolic murmur, but it is usually heard at the LEFT STERNAL EDGE, with less pulmonary oedema initially.
• Dressler syndrome (post-MI autoimmune pericarditis) — Dressler syndrome occurs weeks after MI, causing pericarditis, not a murmur and pulmonary oedema.
• Left ventricular free wall rupture with tamponade — Free wall rupture causes sudden tamponade and collapse, not a murmur.

⭐ Key point: Urgent echocardiography confirms the diagnosis. Treatment: emergency surgery, with vasodilators or an intra-aortic balloon pump as a bridge.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q45$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1075, $s$Three weeks after an MI, a patient develops fever, pleuritic chest pain, a pericardial rub and a raised ESR. Echo shows a small pericardial effusion and no new wall-motion abnormality. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Infective endocarditis", "Left ventricular aneurysm", "Dressler syndrome", "Ventricular free wall rupture"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dressler syndrome
DRESSLER SYNDROME (post-MI syndrome) is an AUTOIMMUNE PERICARDITIS that develops 2–10 WEEKS after an MI (or after heart surgery). The immune system reacts to damaged heart tissue. Features: FEVER, PLEURITIC CHEST PAIN, PERICARDIAL RUB, raised ESR, and a PERICARDIAL EFFUSION. Echo shows no new wall-motion problems (it's not a new MI).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Infective endocarditis — Infective endocarditis causes fever and murmurs, with positive blood cultures and vegetations.
• Left ventricular aneurysm — LV aneurysm causes persistent ST elevation and heart failure, not fever and pericarditis.
• Ventricular free wall rupture — Free wall rupture causes sudden collapse and tamponade, not slow onset with fever.

⭐ Key point: Treat with NSAIDs or aspirin and colchicine. Steroids are used if resistant.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q46$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1076, $s$An injecting drug user has fever for 2 weeks, a new pansystolic murmur at the lower left sternal edge that increases on inspiration, splinter haemorrhages, painless red macules on the soles, and multiple round opacities on chest X-ray. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Tricuspid valve endocarditis with septic emboli", "Community-acquired pneumonia with lung abscess", "Mitral valve endocarditis with cerebral emboli", "Acute rheumatic fever with carditis and arthritis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tricuspid valve endocarditis with septic emboli
INJECTING DRUG USERS often get INFECTIVE ENDOCARDITIS on the RIGHT side of the heart, especially the TRICUSPID VALVE, usually caused by Staphylococcus aureus. Features: FEVER, a PANSYSTOLIC MURMUR at the lower LEFT STERNAL EDGE that INCREASES ON INSPIRATION (Carvallo's sign — right-sided murmurs increase with inspiration), SPLINTER HAEMORRHAGES, JANEWAY LESIONS (painless red macules on palms/soles), and SEPTIC EMBOLI to the LUNGS (multiple round opacities on chest X-ray).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Community-acquired pneumonia with lung abscess — Pneumonia with lung abscess doesn't cause a new heart murmur and splinter haemorrhages.
• Mitral valve endocarditis with cerebral emboli — Mitral valve endocarditis would send emboli to the brain and body, not the lungs, and the murmur would be at the apex.
• Acute rheumatic fever with carditis and arthritis — Rheumatic fever causes carditis and arthritis, usually in children.

⭐ Key point: Diagnose with blood cultures (at least 3 sets before antibiotics) and echocardiography (Duke criteria).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q47$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1077, $s$A 19-year-old athlete faints during a football match. A harsh ejection systolic murmur at the left sternal edge gets LOUDER on standing and during the Valsalva manoeuvre, and softer on squatting. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy", "Dilated cardiomyopathy with low output", "Mitral valve prolapse with regurgitation", "Calcific aortic valve stenosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy
HYPERTROPHIC OBSTRUCTIVE CARDIOMYOPATHY (HOCM) is an inherited thickening of the heart muscle, especially the septum, which can block the outflow of blood from the left ventricle. It is a leading cause of SUDDEN DEATH in young ATHLETES. The murmur is a harsh EJECTION SYSTOLIC MURMUR at the left sternal edge. It gets LOUDER when the left ventricle is SMALLER (standing, Valsalva manoeuvre — less blood returning to the heart, so the obstruction is worse) and SOFTER when the ventricle is FULLER (squatting, leg raising).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dilated cardiomyopathy with low output — Dilated cardiomyopathy causes heart failure symptoms, not a dynamic murmur in a young athlete.
• Mitral valve prolapse with regurgitation — Mitral valve prolapse causes a mid-systolic click and late systolic murmur.
• Calcific aortic valve stenosis — Calcific aortic stenosis usually affects older people, and the murmur gets SOFTER with Valsalva.

⭐ Key point: Advise avoiding competitive sports, screen family members, and consider an ICD for high-risk patients. Avoid dehydration and vasodilators.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q53$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1078, $s$A breathless patient has bilateral lung infiltrates and a PaO₂/FiO₂ ratio of 150, an S3 gallop, raised BNP and a pulmonary artery wedge pressure of 28 mmHg. The most likely cause of the infiltrates is:$s$, $s$["Transfusion-related acute lung injury", "Bilateral bacterial pneumonia", "Cardiogenic (hydrostatic) pulmonary oedema", "Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS)"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cardiogenic (hydrostatic) pulmonary oedema
CARDIOGENIC (HYDROSTATIC) PULMONARY OEDEMA happens when the left heart fails and pressure backs up into the lungs. Clues: an S3 GALLOP (sign of heart failure), RAISED BNP (released by stretched heart muscle), and a HIGH PULMONARY ARTERY WEDGE PRESSURE (PAWP 28 mmHg — normal is 6–12, and ARDS requires it to be below 18). The bilateral infiltrates are from high-pressure fluid leakage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Transfusion-related acute lung injury — TRALI occurs within 6 hours of transfusion, with a normal PAWP.
• Bilateral bacterial pneumonia — Bilateral pneumonia would cause fever and a normal PAWP.
• Acute respiratory distress syndrome (ARDS) — ARDS requires that the infiltrates are NOT explained by heart failure; a high PAWP and S3 point to cardiogenic oedema.

⭐ Key point: Treat with oxygen/CPAP, diuretics, nitrates, and treat the cause of heart failure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q54$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1079, $s$Five days after a hip replacement, a patient suddenly becomes breathless with pleuritic pain. HR is 130, BP 75/50 mmHg, JVP raised, lungs clear, and echo shows a dilated, poorly contracting right ventricle. The most likely diagnosis and treatment are:$s$, $s$["Massive pulmonary embolism — systemic thrombolysis", "Right ventricular infarction — IV fluids only", "Cardiac tamponade — pericardiocentesis", "Tension pneumothorax — needle decompression"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Massive pulmonary embolism — systemic thrombolysis
Five days after hip replacement (a major risk for clots), sudden breathlessness, pleuritic pain, TACHYCARDIA, HYPOTENSION, RAISED JVP, CLEAR lungs, and an echo showing a DILATED, poorly contracting RIGHT VENTRICLE (the RV is failing against a blocked pulmonary artery) indicate MASSIVE (high-risk) PULMONARY EMBOLISM. With hypotension (shock), the treatment is SYSTEMIC THROMBOLYSIS (e.g. alteplase) to dissolve the clot quickly, unless contraindicated (then surgical or catheter-directed clot removal).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right ventricular infarction — IV fluids only — RV infarction is caused by coronary artery blockage, usually with ECG changes of inferior MI.
• Cardiac tamponade — pericardiocentesis — Tamponade shows fluid around the heart on echo.
• Tension pneumothorax — needle decompression — Tension pneumothorax shows absent breath sounds and tracheal deviation.

⭐ Key point: Prevent PE after orthopaedic surgery with early mobilisation, compression stockings and anticoagulant prophylaxis (e.g. LMWH).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q55$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1080, $s$A patient in shock has cardiac index 1.6 L/min/m², PAWP 24 mmHg, SVR 1,800 dyn·s·cm⁻⁵ and cold, clammy peripheries. The type of shock is:$s$, $s$["Hypovolaemic shock", "Cardiogenic shock", "Obstructive shock from massive PE", "Septic (distributive) shock"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cardiogenic shock
CARDIOGENIC SHOCK means the heart cannot pump enough blood. Findings: LOW CARDIAC INDEX (1.6 L/min/m² — normal is 2.5–4.0), HIGH PAWP (24 mmHg — the left heart is backing up), HIGH SVR (1800 — the body constricts vessels to maintain BP), and COLD, CLAMMY peripheries.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypovolaemic shock — Hypovolaemic shock has LOW PAWP (empty heart).
• Obstructive shock from massive PE — Obstructive shock from massive PE has a LOW or normal PAWP (the left heart is underfilled), with a high RV pressure.
• Septic (distributive) shock — Septic shock has a LOW SVR (vasodilation), often with warm peripheries and high cardiac output.

⭐ Key point: Treat with inotropes (e.g. dobutamine), revascularisation if due to MI, and mechanical support (e.g. intra-aortic balloon pump).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q56$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1081, $s$A 72-year-old smoker with hypertension has sudden severe back and left flank pain and collapses. BP is 70/40 mmHg, and a tender pulsatile mass is felt in the epigastrium. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Acute pancreatitis", "Perforated peptic ulcer", "Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm", "Left renal colic"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm
A RUPTURED ABDOMINAL AORTIC ANEURYSM (AAA) classically occurs in OLDER men who SMOKE and have HYPERTENSION. Features: SUDDEN SEVERE BACK or FLANK PAIN, COLLAPSE, HYPOTENSION (shock from massive bleeding), and a TENDER, PULSATILE abdominal MASS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute pancreatitis — Acute pancreatitis causes epigastric pain radiating to the back, but not a pulsatile mass.
• Perforated peptic ulcer — Perforated ulcer causes sudden abdominal pain with a rigid abdomen, but not a pulsatile mass.
• Left renal colic — Renal colic causes severe loin-to-groin pain, but not shock and a pulsatile mass.

⭐ Key point: Ruptured AAA is a surgical emergency. Use permissive hypotension (systolic around 80–100 mmHg) to reduce bleeding until surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q57$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1082, $s$A 65-year-old woman develops chest pain and anterior ST elevation a few hours after hearing that her son has died. Coronary angiography shows normal coronary arteries, and the left ventricle is ballooned at the apex with a hypercontractile base. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Anterior STEMI from LAD occlusion", "Takotsubo (stress-induced) cardiomyopathy", "Acute pericarditis", "Acute viral myocarditis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Takotsubo (stress-induced) cardiomyopathy
TAKOTSUBO (stress-induced) CARDIOMYOPATHY is a temporary weakness of the heart muscle triggered by severe EMOTIONAL or physical STRESS (here, the death of her son), especially in older women. It mimics a heart attack (chest pain, ST elevation), but CORONARY ARTERIES ARE NORMAL. The LEFT VENTRICLE BALLOONS at the APEX with a HYPERCONTRACTILE BASE (it looks like a Japanese octopus trap, "takotsubo").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anterior STEMI from LAD occlusion — Anterior STEMI from LAD occlusion would show a blocked artery on angiography.
• Acute pericarditis — Pericarditis causes saddle-shaped ST elevation and positional pain, with no apical ballooning.
• Acute viral myocarditis — Viral myocarditis usually follows a viral illness and doesn't cause typical apical ballooning.

⭐ Key point: Takotsubo usually recovers within weeks with supportive care, but complications (heart failure, arrhythmias, LV thrombus) can occur.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q58$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1083, $s$According to Sepsis-3, septic shock is identified clinically by:$s$, $s$["Systolic BP below 90 mmHg with a qSOFA score of 2 or more after fluids", "A lactate above 2 mmol/L on its own, whatever the blood pressure", "Vasopressors needed for MAP ≥ 65 AND lactate > 2 mmol/L after adequate fluids", "MAP below 65 mmHg with a lactate above 4 mmol/L, before any fluids are given"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vasopressors needed for MAP ≥ 65 AND lactate > 2 mmol/L after adequate fluids
SEPSIS-3 (2016) defines SEPTIC SHOCK as sepsis with profound circulatory and cellular/metabolic problems. It is identified clinically by BOTH: (1) the need for VASOPRESSORS to keep the MEAN ARTERIAL PRESSURE (MAP) at 65 mmHg or more, AND (2) a serum LACTATE above 2 mmol/L — despite ADEQUATE FLUID RESUSCITATION. Patients meeting these criteria have a hospital mortality above 40%.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Systolic BP below 90 mmHg with a qSOFA score of 2 or more after fluids — Systolic BP below 90 with qSOFA ≥2 is not the definition. qSOFA is only a bedside screening tool to identify patients at risk outside ICU.
• A lactate above 2 mmol/L on its own, whatever the blood pressure — A lactate above 2 alone, without vasopressor need, does not define septic shock (it may indicate sepsis with hypoperfusion).
• MAP below 65 mmHg with a lactate above 4 mmol/L, before any fluids are given — The definition requires that fluids have ALREADY been given adequately; a low MAP before fluids may simply reflect hypovolaemia.

⭐ Key point: Sepsis = infection + organ dysfunction (SOFA rise ≥2). Septic shock = sepsis + vasopressors for MAP ≥65 + lactate >2 after fluids.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q141$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1084, $s$A febrile patient with pneumonia has BP 80/40 mmHg, a bounding pulse, warm flushed peripheries, a wide pulse pressure and rapid capillary refill. The haemodynamic pattern is most likely:$s$, $s$["Distributive shock — low SVR with a normal or high cardiac output", "Hypovolaemic shock — low cardiac output with high SVR", "Obstructive shock — low cardiac output with high CVP", "Cardiogenic shock — low cardiac output with high SVR"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Distributive shock — low SVR with a normal or high cardiac output
This patient has signs of DISTRIBUTIVE (SEPTIC) SHOCK, often called "warm shock". Infection triggers massive VASODILATION, so the SYSTEMIC VASCULAR RESISTANCE (SVR) is LOW. The heart pumps harder and faster to compensate, so the CARDIAC OUTPUT is NORMAL or HIGH. This produces: LOW BP (80/40), a BOUNDING PULSE, WARM FLUSHED skin, a WIDE PULSE PRESSURE (low diastolic from vasodilation), and RAPID capillary refill.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypovolaemic shock — low cardiac output with high SVR — Hypovolaemic shock causes vasoconstriction (high SVR), COLD clammy skin, slow capillary refill and a weak thready pulse.
• Obstructive shock — low cardiac output with high CVP — Obstructive shock (e.g. tamponade, PE) causes low cardiac output, high CVP, and cold peripheries.
• Cardiogenic shock — low cardiac output with high SVR — Cardiogenic shock causes low cardiac output, high SVR, and cold, clammy skin.

⭐ Key point: Late septic shock may become "cold" as the heart fails. Treat with fluids, early antibiotics, source control and noradrenaline.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q143$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1085, $s$Five weeks after a normal delivery, a previously healthy woman has worsening breathlessness, orthopnoea, swollen ankles and a resting heart rate of 120. Echo shows a dilated left ventricle with an ejection fraction of 30%, and she has no history of heart disease. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Postpartum anaemia only", "Peripartum cardiomyopathy", "Postpartum pulmonary embolism", "Pre-existing rheumatic mitral stenosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Peripartum cardiomyopathy
PERIPARTUM CARDIOMYOPATHY (PPCM) is heart failure developing in the last month of pregnancy or within 5 months after delivery, in women with no previous heart disease. Features: BREATHLESSNESS, ORTHOPNOEA, SWOLLEN ANKLES, TACHYCARDIA. Echo shows a DILATED LEFT VENTRICLE with REDUCED EJECTION FRACTION (below 45% — here 30%). It is more common in women of African descent (including Nigeria).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Postpartum anaemia only — Anaemia causes tiredness and breathlessness, but not a dilated, poorly contracting heart.
• Postpartum pulmonary embolism — Pulmonary embolism causes sudden breathlessness and chest pain, not gradual heart failure.
• Pre-existing rheumatic mitral stenosis — Rheumatic mitral stenosis would usually show a known history and a characteristic murmur.

⭐ Key point: Treat with heart failure drugs (diuretics, beta-blockers, ACE inhibitors after delivery), and consider bromocriptine. Anticoagulation may be needed. Future pregnancies carry risk.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q169$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1086, $s$Which inotrope increases myocardial contractility mainly through β1 stimulation, but may LOWER blood pressure through β2-mediated vasodilation?$s$, $s$["Noradrenaline", "Phenylephrine", "Vasopressin", "Dobutamine"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dobutamine
DOBUTAMINE is a synthetic catecholamine that mainly stimulates β1 receptors in the heart, INCREASING CONTRACTILITY (stronger heartbeats) and heart rate, so CARDIAC OUTPUT rises. It also stimulates β2 receptors in blood vessels, causing mild VASODILATION, which REDUCES afterload (helpful for a failing heart) but can LOWER BP. So dobutamine is an "INODILATOR". It is used in cardiogenic shock and low cardiac output states, often together with a vasopressor (noradrenaline) if BP is low.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Noradrenaline — Noradrenaline mainly stimulates α1 receptors (vasoconstriction), raising BP; it has only modest β1 effects.
• Phenylephrine — Phenylephrine is a pure α1-agonist — it causes vasoconstriction only, with no inotropic effect, and may cause reflex bradycardia.
• Vasopressin — Vasopressin acts on V1 receptors to constrict blood vessels; it has no inotropic effect.

⭐ Key point: Side effects of dobutamine: tachycardia, arrhythmias (including AF), and hypotension. Monitor ECG and BP continuously. Milrinone is another inodilator (works by a different mechanism).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q188$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1087, $s$A ventilated patient with severe pulmonary hypertension suddenly drops BP to 70/40 with a rising CVP after a large tidal volume increase. The most likely mechanism is:$s$, $s$["Tension pneumothorax from barotrauma, shifting the mediastinum to the right", "Vasodilation from deep sedation, causing a distributive type of shock", "Raised RV afterload from high intrathoracic pressure, causing acute RV failure", "Left ventricular failure from fluid overload, causing acute pulmonary oedema"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Raised RV afterload from high intrathoracic pressure, causing acute RV failure
In pulmonary hypertension the right ventricle (RV) is already pumping against very high pressure in the lung vessels, and it is a thin-walled chamber that copes badly with any extra load. When the ventilator tidal volume is increased a lot, the pressure inside the chest (intrathoracic pressure) rises and the over-stretched alveoli squeeze the small lung capillaries. This sharply RAISES RV AFTERLOAD (the resistance the RV must pump against). The RV cannot empty, it dilates and fails: blood backs up into the veins (CVP rises) and less blood reaches the left heart, so the BP falls. This is ACUTE RV FAILURE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tension pneumothorax from barotrauma, shifting the mediastinum to the right — A tension pneumothorax can cause low BP and high CVP, but the question links the collapse directly to the tidal volume change in a patient with pulmonary hypertension; the classic expected mechanism is RV afterload. Tension pneumothorax would also show absent breath sounds, tracheal deviation and very high airway pressures.
• Vasodilation from deep sedation, causing a distributive type of shock — Vasodilation from sedation causes LOW BP with a LOW or normal CVP, because the veins also dilate. A rising CVP does not fit distributive shock.
• Left ventricular failure from fluid overload, causing acute pulmonary oedema — LV failure from fluid overload causes pulmonary oedema with crackles and pink frothy sputum; it is not caused by increasing tidal volume.

⭐ Key point: In pulmonary hypertension or RV failure on a ventilator: use lung-protective volumes (6 mL/kg ideal body weight), avoid high plateau pressures and excessive PEEP, avoid hypoxia and hypercapnia, keep the BP up (noradrenaline) to perfuse the RV, and consider inhaled nitric oxide.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q1$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1088, $s$In a patient with pulmonary arterial hypertension, which factor most worsens pulmonary vascular resistance and must be avoided in the ICU?$s$, $s$["Normothermia with adequate analgesia", "Hypoxia, hypercapnia and acidosis", "A slightly alkalotic pH from mild hyperventilation", "Mild hyperoxia with normocapnia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypoxia, hypercapnia and acidosis
The lung blood vessels CONSTRICT (narrow) when the patient has HYPOXIA (low oxygen — "hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction"), HYPERCAPNIA (high CO2) and ACIDOSIS (low pH). In a patient with pulmonary hypertension, these three sharply increase pulmonary vascular resistance, which overloads the right ventricle and can trigger a "pulmonary hypertensive crisis" with RV failure and cardiac arrest. Pain, agitation, hypothermia and coughing/suction episodes also worsen it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normothermia with adequate analgesia — Normal temperature with good pain control is GOOD for the patient; pain and cold increase pulmonary resistance.
• A slightly alkalotic pH from mild hyperventilation — A slightly alkalotic pH actually DILATES the pulmonary vessels and lowers resistance. Mild hyperventilation is sometimes used deliberately.
• Mild hyperoxia with normocapnia — Good oxygen with normal CO2 lowers pulmonary resistance. It is helpful, not harmful.

⭐ Key point: Remember: keep the patient "warm, well-oxygenated, slightly alkalotic, pain-free and calm". Avoid hypoxia, hypercapnia, acidosis, pain and hypothermia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q2$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1089, $s$Inhaled nitric oxide is used in ICU patients with acute pulmonary hypertension because it:$s$, $s$["Dilates pulmonary vessels in ventilated lung with little systemic effect", "Increases right ventricular contractility directly as an inotrope", "Reduces blood viscosity and platelet adhesion, preventing thrombosis", "Dilates systemic arteries more than pulmonary arteries, lowering afterload"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dilates pulmonary vessels in ventilated lung with little systemic effect
INHALED NITRIC OXIDE (iNO) is a gas given through the ventilator circuit. It relaxes the smooth muscle of the pulmonary arteries, DILATING THE LUNG VESSELS. Because it is inhaled, it reaches only the parts of the lung that are being VENTILATED, so it improves blood flow to the well-aerated lung (improving oxygenation). Once it enters the blood, it is quickly inactivated by haemoglobin, so it has LITTLE SYSTEMIC EFFECT — it does not lower the general blood pressure. This makes it ideal for acute pulmonary hypertension and RV failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increases right ventricular contractility directly as an inotrope — iNO is a vasodilator, not an inotrope. It helps the RV indirectly by lowering its afterload, not by making it contract harder.
• Reduces blood viscosity and platelet adhesion, preventing thrombosis — iNO is not given to prevent clots or thin the blood (it has only minor effects on platelets).
• Dilates systemic arteries more than pulmonary arteries, lowering afterload — iNO dilates PULMONARY vessels selectively; it has almost no effect on systemic arteries because haemoglobin inactivates it.

⭐ Key point: Never stop iNO suddenly — rebound pulmonary hypertension can occur; wean it slowly. Monitor methaemoglobin levels and NO2 (a toxic by-product).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q3$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1090, $s$A patient with chronic pulmonary hypertension and right heart failure needs fluid management. The ICU nurse should expect:$s$, $s$["Small, careful fluid boluses, as the RV tolerates under- and over-filling poorly", "Large crystalloid boluses to raise preload and push blood through the lungs", "Fluids guided only by hourly urine output, ignoring CVP and the echo", "Strict fluid restriction regardless of the blood pressure or the CVP"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Small, careful fluid boluses, as the RV tolerates under- and over-filling poorly
A failing RIGHT VENTRICLE has a narrow safe range of filling. If it is UNDER-FILLED (too little fluid), it cannot generate enough output and the BP falls. If it is OVER-FILLED (too much fluid), it stretches and bulges to the left, squeezing the left ventricle (interventricular dependence), so cardiac output falls further and the liver and gut become congested. So fluids are given as SMALL, CAREFUL BOLUSES (e.g. 250 mL), each followed by reassessment of BP, CVP, echo findings and urine output.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Large crystalloid boluses to raise preload and push blood through the lungs — Large boluses can over-distend the failing RV, push the septum to the left and cause collapse.
• Fluids guided only by hourly urine output, ignoring CVP and the echo — Urine output alone can mislead; CVP trends and echocardiography are important guides in RV failure.
• Strict fluid restriction regardless of the blood pressure or the CVP — Strict restriction regardless of BP can leave the RV under-filled and cause low cardiac output.

⭐ Key point: In RV failure: careful fluids, keep the systemic BP up (noradrenaline maintains coronary perfusion of the RV), consider inotropes (dobutamine, milrinone) and pulmonary vasodilators. Diuretics if overloaded.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q4$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1091, $s$Shock lung is another name for:$s$, $s$["ARDS developing after shock or major trauma", "Pulmonary oedema caused by left ventricular failure", "Lung collapse caused by a large pleural effusion", "Bronchospasm triggered by a blood transfusion"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ARDS developing after shock or major trauma
"SHOCK LUNG" is an old name for ACUTE RESPIRATORY DISTRESS SYNDROME (ARDS) developing after SHOCK, major TRAUMA, sepsis or massive transfusion. The lungs become inflamed and leaky, filling with protein-rich fluid. This causes severe hypoxia, stiff lungs and bilateral infiltrates on X-ray — not caused by heart failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pulmonary oedema caused by left ventricular failure — Pulmonary oedema from LV failure is cardiogenic, not "shock lung".
• Lung collapse caused by a large pleural effusion — Lung collapse from pleural effusion is a different problem.
• Bronchospasm triggered by a blood transfusion — Bronchospasm with transfusion is an allergic reaction, not ARDS.

⭐ Key point: ARDS (Berlin definition): onset within 1 week, bilateral infiltrates, not fully explained by heart failure or fluid overload, and PaO2/FiO2 ≤300 on PEEP ≥5.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q14$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1092, $s$Sildenafil is used in pulmonary arterial hypertension because it:$s$, $s$["Acts as a potent loop diuretic that reduces right ventricular preload", "Inhibits phosphodiesterase-5, increasing NO-mediated vasodilation", "Blocks endothelin receptors on the pulmonary vascular smooth muscle", "Acts as a beta-blocker that slows the heart and reduces RV workload"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inhibits phosphodiesterase-5, increasing NO-mediated vasodilation
SILDENAFIL is a PHOSPHODIESTERASE-5 (PDE-5) INHIBITOR. Normally, nitric oxide (NO) relaxes blood vessels by increasing a chemical messenger called cGMP; PDE-5 breaks cGMP down. By blocking PDE-5, sildenafil keeps cGMP levels high, so NO-mediated VASODILATION in the lung vessels is stronger and longer-lasting. This lowers pulmonary vascular resistance.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acts as a potent loop diuretic that reduces right ventricular preload — Sildenafil is not a diuretic.
• Blocks endothelin receptors on the pulmonary vascular smooth muscle — Blocking endothelin receptors describes bosentan, ambrisentan and macitentan.
• Acts as a beta-blocker that slows the heart and reduces RV workload — Sildenafil is not a beta-blocker.

⭐ Key point: Sildenafil can cause systemic hypotension, especially with nitrates (never combine). It can also help wean inhaled nitric oxide.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q20$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1093, $s$Intra-abdominal hypertension is:$s$, $s$["When IAP is consistently \\>12 mmHg with organ dysfunction", "When IAP is \\>12 mmHg without organ dysfunction", "When IAP is consistently \\<12 mmHg with organ dysfunction", "When IAP is consistently \\> 5 mmHg without organ dysfunction"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: When IAP is \>12 mmHg without organ dysfunction
INTRA-ABDOMINAL HYPERTENSION (IAH) is defined as a sustained or repeated INTRA-ABDOMINAL PRESSURE (IAP) of 12 mmHg OR MORE. Organ dysfunction is NOT required for IAH. If IAP is above 20 mmHg WITH new organ dysfunction, this is ABDOMINAL COMPARTMENT SYNDROME (ACS).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• When IAP is consistently \>12 mmHg with organ dysfunction — If organ dysfunction is present with very high pressure (over 20), it's ACS, not just IAH.
• When IAP is consistently \<12 mmHg with organ dysfunction — An IAP below 12 is not hypertension.
• When IAP is consistently \> 5 mmHg without organ dysfunction — Normal IAP in critically ill adults is about 5–7 mmHg; above 5 is not IAH.

⭐ Key point: IAH grades: I (12–15), II (16–20), III (21–25), IV (over 25 mmHg).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q61$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1094, $s$Abdominal perfusion pressure (APP) is calculated as:$s$, $s$["Intra-abdominal pressure minus CVP", "Diastolic pressure plus intra-abdominal pressure", "Mean arterial pressure minus intra-abdominal pressure", "Systolic pressure minus intra-abdominal pressure"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mean arterial pressure minus intra-abdominal pressure
ABDOMINAL PERFUSION PRESSURE (APP) = MEAN ARTERIAL PRESSURE (MAP) − INTRA-ABDOMINAL PRESSURE (IAP). It reflects blood flow to the abdominal organs. A target APP of at least 60 mmHg is recommended.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intra-abdominal pressure minus CVP — IAP minus CVP is not the correct formula.
• Diastolic pressure plus intra-abdominal pressure — Diastolic pressure plus IAP is not the correct formula.
• Systolic pressure minus intra-abdominal pressure — Systolic pressure is not used; MAP is.

⭐ Key point: This is similar to cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP = MAP − ICP).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q64$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1095, $s$After a cardiac arrest in ICU, a team debrief is mainly held to:$s$, $s$["Review what went well and what to improve, and support staff", "Prepare a statement to inform the media about the event", "Replace the need for an incident report on the arrest", "Identify the person to blame for the cardiac arrest"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Review what went well and what to improve, and support staff
A TEAM DEBRIEF after a cardiac arrest helps the team REVIEW WHAT WENT WELL and WHAT COULD BE IMPROVED (e.g. communication, equipment, roles), and SUPPORTS STAFF emotionally after a stressful event.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prepare a statement to inform the media about the event — Debriefs are not for media statements.
• Replace the need for an incident report on the arrest — Incident reports may still be required.
• Identify the person to blame for the cardiac arrest — Debriefs focus on learning, not blame.

⭐ Key point: Debriefs should be safe, supportive, and held soon after the event.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q180$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1096, $s$On a pulmonary artery catheter, which reading confirms pulmonary arterial hypertension rather than left heart disease?$s$, $s$["Mean PAP 18 mmHg with a wedge pressure of 8 mmHg", "Mean PAP 40 mmHg with a wedge pressure of 28 mmHg", "Mean PAP 12 mmHg with a high cardiac output and wedge 6", "Mean PAP 40 mmHg with a wedge pressure of 10 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mean PAP 40 mmHg with a wedge pressure of 10 mmHg
Pulmonary hypertension (PH) means a mean pulmonary artery pressure (mPAP) above about 20–25 mmHg. The WEDGE pressure (PAWP) tells you where the problem is. In PULMONARY ARTERIAL (pre-capillary) hypertension, the lung arteries themselves are narrowed, so mPAP is HIGH but the wedge (left atrial) pressure is NORMAL (15 or below). Mean PAP 40 with wedge 10 fits this.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mean PAP 18 mmHg with a wedge pressure of 8 mmHg — mPAP 18 with wedge 8 is normal; there is no pulmonary hypertension.
• Mean PAP 40 mmHg with a wedge pressure of 28 mmHg — mPAP 40 with wedge 28 is PH caused by LEFT heart disease (post-capillary): the high wedge shows pressure backing up from the left heart.
• Mean PAP 12 mmHg with a high cardiac output and wedge 6 — mPAP 12 is normal.

⭐ Key point: Pre-capillary PH: high mPAP + normal wedge. Post-capillary PH (left heart failure, mitral disease): high mPAP + high wedge (above 15).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q1$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1097, $s$The ECG of a patient with long-standing pulmonary hypertension commonly shows:$s$, $s$["Left axis deviation with tall R waves in V5–V6", "ST elevation in V1–V4 with deep Q waves", "A short PR interval with delta waves", "Right axis deviation, P pulmonale and RV strain"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Right axis deviation, P pulmonale and RV strain
Long-standing pulmonary hypertension makes the RIGHT ventricle thicken (hypertrophy) and the right atrium enlarge. The ECG shows RIGHT AXIS DEVIATION, a tall peaked P wave in lead II (P PULMONALE, from a big right atrium), a dominant R wave in V1, and RV STRAIN (T-wave inversion in V1–V3).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Left axis deviation with tall R waves in V5–V6 — Left axis deviation with tall R waves in V5–V6 suggests LEFT ventricular hypertrophy.
• ST elevation in V1–V4 with deep Q waves — ST elevation with Q waves suggests an MI.
• A short PR interval with delta waves — A short PR with delta waves suggests Wolff–Parkinson–White syndrome.

⭐ Key point: An echocardiogram estimates PA pressure and RV function. A right heart catheter confirms PH.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q2$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1098, $s$Which drug should be avoided in severe pulmonary hypertension because it can precipitate RV collapse at induction?$s$, $s$["A low-dose dobutamine infusion started beforehand", "A large propofol bolus causing sudden systemic hypotension", "Small titrated doses of noradrenaline before induction", "Inhaled nitric oxide given through the ventilator"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A large propofol bolus causing sudden systemic hypotension
In severe pulmonary hypertension, the RV depends on good coronary blood flow, which needs an adequate systemic BP. A LARGE PROPOFOL BOLUS causes sudden SYSTEMIC HYPOTENSION. The RV, already working against high lung pressure, loses its own blood supply, fails, and collapses, causing cardiac arrest at induction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A low-dose dobutamine infusion started beforehand — Low-dose dobutamine supports RV contraction and is HELPFUL.
• Small titrated doses of noradrenaline before induction — Titrated noradrenaline keeps systemic BP up and is HELPFUL.
• Inhaled nitric oxide given through the ventilator — Inhaled nitric oxide relaxes the lung vessels and is HELPFUL.

⭐ Key point: In PH, avoid things that raise lung pressures (hypoxia, high CO₂, acidosis, pain) or drop systemic BP. Induce carefully, with vasopressors ready.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q3$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1099, $s$Signs of venous air embolism during central line removal include:$s$, $s$["Bradycardia with high blood pressure and a clear chest", "Fever with rigors starting 24 hours after removal", "Local bruising and swelling at the insertion site only", "Sudden breathlessness, hypotension and a 'mill-wheel' murmur"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sudden breathlessness, hypotension and a 'mill-wheel' murmur
If air enters a vein during central line removal (especially if the patient breathes in, or is sitting up), VENOUS AIR EMBOLISM occurs. Air blocks blood flow through the right heart and lungs, causing SUDDEN BREATHLESSNESS, HYPOTENSION, chest pain, collapse, and sometimes a "MILL-WHEEL" (churning) MURMUR over the heart.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bradycardia with high blood pressure and a clear chest — Bradycardia with high BP and a clear chest isn't air embolism.
• Fever with rigors starting 24 hours after removal — Fever 24 hours later suggests infection.
• Local bruising and swelling at the insertion site only — Local bruising is a minor local complication.

⭐ Key point: Treat air embolism: clamp or occlude the site, place the patient LEFT side down and head down (Durant's position), and give 100% oxygen.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q26$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1100, $s$Pericardiocentesis in tamponade is usually performed via the:$s$, $s$["Right second intercostal space", "Left fifth intercostal space in the mid-axillary line", "Suprasternal notch", "Subxiphoid approach, aiming toward the left shoulder"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Subxiphoid approach, aiming toward the left shoulder
Pericardiocentesis is usually done via the SUBXIPHOID approach: the needle is inserted just below the xiphoid process (lower tip of the sternum) and directed toward the LEFT SHOULDER at about 30–45°. This route avoids the lungs and the coronary arteries. Ultrasound guidance makes it safer.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right second intercostal space — The right 2nd intercostal space risks the great vessels and lung.
• Left fifth intercostal space in the mid-axillary line — The left 5th ICS mid-axillary line risks lung puncture.
• Suprasternal notch — The suprasternal notch is near the great vessels and airway.

⭐ Key point: Monitor the ECG during the procedure. ST changes or ectopics mean the needle has touched the heart.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q27$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1101, $s$Removal of only 30–50 mL of blood during pericardiocentesis in tamponade typically produces:$s$, $s$["No change because more is needed", "Worsening of hypotension", "Marked improvement in blood pressure", "Immediate cardiac arrest"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Marked improvement in blood pressure
The pericardial sac doesn't stretch quickly. In tamponade, the pressure–volume curve is STEEP: a small extra volume causes a large rise in pressure. Removing even a SMALL volume (30–50 mL) moves back down the steep curve, causing a MARKED drop in pericardial pressure and a big improvement in BP and cardiac output.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No change because more is needed — A small volume does make a difference.
• Worsening of hypotension — Removing fluid improves, not worsens, BP.
• Immediate cardiac arrest — Correct drainage relieves tamponade; it doesn't cause arrest.

⭐ Key point: In acute tamponade (trauma, after surgery), even 100–200 mL can be fatal. Slowly accumulating effusions can be much larger.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q28$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1102, $s$In targeted temperature management, the most common recommended target after cardiac arrest is:$s$, $s$["Room temperature with no monitoring needed", "38–39 °C to help fight any infection", "28–30 °C maintained for at least 72 hours", "A constant 32–36 °C, avoiding fever afterwards"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A constant 32–36 °C, avoiding fever afterwards
After cardiac arrest with return of circulation, patients who stay unconscious receive TARGETED TEMPERATURE MANAGEMENT. Current guidance: keep a CONSTANT temperature between 32 and 36 °C (or at least actively prevent fever), then AVOID FEVER (above 37.7 °C) for at least 72 hours, to protect the brain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Room temperature with no monitoring needed — Temperature must be monitored and controlled.
• 38–39 °C to help fight any infection — 38–39 °C is fever, which harms the brain.
• 28–30 °C maintained for at least 72 hours — 28–30 °C is too cold and causes arrhythmias.

⭐ Key point: During TTM, control shivering, monitor for arrhythmias, electrolytes (low K during cooling), glucose and infection. Rewarm slowly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q51$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1103, $s$Intra-abdominal hypertension grade III corresponds to an IAP of:$s$, $s$["12–15 mmHg", "Over 25 mmHg", "16–20 mmHg", "21–25 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 21–25 mmHg
Intra-abdominal hypertension (IAH) is graded by intra-abdominal pressure (IAP). Grade I = 12–15 mmHg, Grade II = 16–20, GRADE III = 21–25, Grade IV = above 25 mmHg. Abdominal compartment syndrome is IAP above 20 with new organ dysfunction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 12–15 mmHg — 12–15 is Grade I.
• Over 25 mmHg — Above 25 is Grade IV.
• 16–20 mmHg — 16–20 is Grade II.

⭐ Key point: Normal IAP is 5–7 mmHg in critically ill adults. Measure it via the bladder.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q61$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1104, $s$Relative adrenal insufficiency in septic shock is suspected when:$s$, $s$["The blood pressure responds well to the first fluid bolus", "The patient has hyperkalaemia with no hypotension", "The blood glucose is persistently high on insulin", "Hypotension persists despite fluids and rising vasopressors"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypotension persists despite fluids and rising vasopressors
RELATIVE adrenal insufficiency (critical illness-related corticosteroid insufficiency) is suspected in septic shock when HYPOTENSION PERSISTS despite adequate FLUIDS and RISING VASOPRESSOR doses. Low-dose IV hydrocortisone (200 mg/day) is recommended in this situation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The blood pressure responds well to the first fluid bolus — A good response to fluids doesn't suggest adrenal insufficiency.
• The patient has hyperkalaemia with no hypotension — High potassium WITHOUT hypotension doesn't fit.
• The blood glucose is persistently high on insulin — Persistently high glucose on insulin doesn't suggest adrenal insufficiency.

⭐ Key point: Hydrocortisone shortens the duration of septic shock. Monitor glucose and stop it when vasopressors are no longer needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q76$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1105, $s$A modified Allen's test is performed before:$s$, $s$["Central venous cannulation in the internal jugular", "Urinary catheterisation in men with retention", "Radial arterial puncture or arterial line insertion", "Lumbar puncture for CSF sampling in meningitis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Radial arterial puncture or arterial line insertion
The MODIFIED ALLEN'S TEST checks that the ULNAR ARTERY can supply blood to the hand on its own, in case the RADIAL ARTERY becomes blocked after puncture or cannulation. Method: compress both radial and ulnar arteries while the patient clenches the fist; the hand goes pale; release the ULNAR artery only. If colour returns within about 5–15 seconds, collateral flow is adequate. It is performed before RADIAL ARTERIAL PUNCTURE (ABG sampling) or ARTERIAL LINE insertion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Central venous cannulation in the internal jugular — Internal jugular central venous cannulation does not involve the hand's arteries.
• Urinary catheterisation in men with retention — Urinary catheterisation is unrelated.
• Lumbar puncture for CSF sampling in meningitis — Lumbar puncture is unrelated.

⭐ Key point: If the test is abnormal (slow colour return), use another site (e.g. the other radial, or the femoral artery).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q194$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1106, $s$On haemodynamic monitoring, a patient shows low cardiac output, high systemic vascular resistance, and a high pulmonary capillary wedge pressure. This pattern is most consistent with:$s$, $s$["Hypovolaemic shock", "Cardiogenic shock", "Distributive (septic) shock", "Normal haemodynamics"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cardiogenic shock
Read each number. LOW cardiac output means the heart is not pumping enough. HIGH SVR means the arteries have clamped down to keep up the BP (a compensation for the low output). HIGH wedge pressure (PCWP above 18) means blood is backing up behind a failing left ventricle. A weak pump with backed-up blood and tight vessels is CARDIOGENIC shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypovolaemic shock — Hypovolaemic shock also has low output and high SVR, but the wedge pressure is LOW, because the tank is empty.
• Distributive (septic) shock — Septic (distributive) shock has LOW SVR (dilated vessels), often with a normal or high cardiac output.
• Normal haemodynamics — Normal haemodynamics would show normal output, SVR and wedge pressure.

⭐ Key point: The wedge pressure tells you about filling. Low output + high wedge = pump failure (cardiogenic). Low output + low wedge = empty tank (hypovolaemic).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q1 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1107, $s$A patient in cardiogenic shock has a low cardiac output despite adequate preload. Which mechanism is most likely responsible?$s$, $s$["Bradycardia-induced increase in stroke volume", "Impaired myocardial contractility", "Excessively low afterload", "Excess venous return"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Impaired myocardial contractility
Cardiac output = heart rate × stroke volume. Stroke volume depends on preload (filling), afterload (resistance) and CONTRACTILITY (squeeze). If the heart is well filled (adequate preload) and the output is still low, the problem must be the SQUEEZE: the damaged heart muscle cannot contract strongly. This is the basic cause of cardiogenic shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bradycardia-induced increase in stroke volume — Bradycardia lowers cardiac output; it doesn't increase stroke volume enough to cause shock.
• Excessively low afterload — Low afterload would make it EASIER for the heart to pump, raising output.
• Excess venous return — Extra venous return increases preload and would raise output in a healthy heart.

⭐ Key point: Treat poor contractility with inotropes (dobutamine, milrinone, adrenaline), and treat the cause (PCI for MI).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q5$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1108, $s$A patient with an inferior STEMI becomes hypotensive after sublingual GTN, with clear lungs and a raised JVP. The likely cause is:$s$, $s$["Cardiac tamponade from free wall rupture of the LV", "Allergic reaction to the GTN tablet given", "Left ventricular failure with pulmonary oedema", "Right ventricular infarction"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Right ventricular infarction
The right coronary artery supplies both the INFERIOR wall of the left ventricle and the RIGHT ventricle. So an inferior STEMI can also cause a right ventricular infarct. A failing RV depends on good filling (it is "preload-dependent"). GTN dilates the veins and reduces filling, so the BP drops suddenly. Clear lungs (the left side isn't overloaded) with a raised JVP (blood backing up behind the right ventricle) is the classic picture.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cardiac tamponade from free wall rupture of the LV — Free wall rupture with tamponade is a later complication (days), with sudden collapse and PEA.
• Allergic reaction to the GTN tablet given — An allergic reaction to GTN is very rare and would show rash or wheeze.
• Left ventricular failure with pulmonary oedema — LV failure would cause crackles and pulmonary oedema, but here the lungs are clear.

⭐ Key point: In inferior MI, record right-sided leads (V4R). If RV infarct is present, give IV fluids and AVOID nitrates, morphine and diuretics.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q6$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1109, $s$A contraindication to thrombolysis in STEMI is:$s$, $s$["Controlled hypertension on treatment", "Ischaemic stroke within 3 months", "Age over 60 years with diabetes", "Previous MI more than 5 years ago"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ischaemic stroke within 3 months
Thrombolytic drugs (streptokinase, alteplase, tenecteplase) dissolve clots everywhere in the body, so anything that could cause dangerous bleeding is a contraindication. An ischaemic stroke within the last 3 months is an absolute contraindication, because the damaged brain area can bleed (haemorrhagic transformation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Controlled hypertension on treatment — Controlled hypertension is not a contraindication; only severe uncontrolled BP (above 180/110) is a relative one.
• Age over 60 years with diabetes — Age over 60 with diabetes is not a contraindication.
• Previous MI more than 5 years ago — An old MI (over 5 years ago) is not a contraindication.

⭐ Key point: Absolute contraindications: any previous brain bleed, ischaemic stroke in 3 months, brain tumour, active bleeding, suspected aortic dissection, major head trauma or surgery in 3 weeks.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q8$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1110, $s$A patient 4 days after MI develops a new harsh pansystolic murmur and acute pulmonary oedema. The nurse suspects:$s$, $s$["Acute pericarditis with a pericardial friction rub", "Normal healing of the infarct with scar formation", "Dressler's syndrome with fever and pleuritic pain", "Papillary muscle or ventricular septal rupture"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Papillary muscle or ventricular septal rupture
Three to five days after an MI, dead heart muscle is at its softest and can tear. A papillary muscle rupture causes sudden severe mitral regurgitation (blood leaks back into the lungs). A ventricular septal rupture makes a hole between the ventricles. Both cause a NEW, loud, harsh pansystolic murmur and sudden pulmonary oedema or shock. They are surgical emergencies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute pericarditis with a pericardial friction rub — Pericarditis causes a scratchy friction rub and sharp pain, not a pansystolic murmur with pulmonary oedema.
• Normal healing of the infarct with scar formation — Normal healing does not produce a new murmur and pulmonary oedema.
• Dressler's syndrome with fever and pleuritic pain — Dressler's syndrome occurs weeks later, with fever, pleuritic pain and a rub.

⭐ Key point: Mechanical complications after MI (papillary muscle rupture, VSD, free wall rupture) usually happen at 3–5 days. Listen to the heart every day after MI.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q10$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1111, $s$In hypertensive emergency (without aortic dissection), the MAP should be reduced in the first hour by no more than:$s$, $s$["5%", "75%", "50%", "25%"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 25%
In chronic hypertension, the brain, heart and kidneys have adapted to high pressure (autoregulation is reset higher). If the BP is dropped too fast, these organs suddenly don't get enough blood and can be damaged: stroke, MI, AKI or blindness. The rule is to lower MAP by no more than 25% in the first hour, then to about 160/100 over the next 2–6 hours, then to normal over 24–48 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5% — 5% is too little to protect organs that are being damaged.
• 75% — 75% is a dangerous, catastrophic drop.
• 50% — 50% is too fast and risks ischaemic damage.

⭐ Key point: Exceptions where BP is lowered faster: aortic dissection (systolic 100–120 within 20 minutes) and severe pre-eclampsia or eclampsia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q12$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1112, $s$A patient with tearing chest pain radiating to the back and unequal arm blood pressures most likely has:$s$, $s$["Oesophageal spasm", "Pulmonary embolism", "Aortic dissection", "Acute pericarditis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Aortic dissection
Aortic dissection is a tear in the inner layer of the aorta. Blood forces its way between the layers, causing sudden, severe, TEARING or ripping pain that goes through to the back (between the shoulder blades). If the tear affects the branches to the arms, the BP and pulses differ between the arms (more than 20 mmHg). The chest X-ray may show a wide mediastinum, and CT angiography confirms it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oesophageal spasm — Oesophageal spasm causes squeezing chest pain, often with swallowing, but normal BP in both arms.
• Pulmonary embolism — PE causes breathlessness and pleuritic pain, not unequal arm pressures.
• Acute pericarditis — Pericarditis causes sharp pain better sitting forward, not tearing back pain with unequal pulses.

⭐ Key point: NEVER give thrombolysis or heparin in suspected dissection. Lower the HR and BP and call the cardiothoracic surgeons (type A involves the ascending aorta and needs surgery).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q13$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1113, $s$In aortic dissection, IV beta-blockers are given before vasodilators to:$s$, $s$["Raise the blood pressure before surgical repair", "Prevent reflex tachycardia that raises shear stress", "Increase the cardiac output and organ perfusion", "Dissolve the intimal flap and close the false lumen"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Prevent reflex tachycardia that raises shear stress
In aortic dissection, the force of each heartbeat (shear stress) pushes the tear further along. Vasodilators like nitroprusside lower BP but cause a REFLEX TACHYCARDIA (the heart speeds up), which increases the force on the aortic wall and can extend the dissection. Giving a beta-blocker first (such as labetalol or esmolol) slows the heart and reduces the force of contraction, so the vasodilator can then lower BP safely.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Raise the blood pressure before surgical repair — The aim is to LOWER the BP, not raise it.
• Increase the cardiac output and organ perfusion — The aim is to reduce the force of pumping, not increase output.
• Dissolve the intimal flap and close the false lumen — No drug dissolves the flap; repair is surgical or with an endovascular stent.

⭐ Key point: Dissection targets: heart rate about 60/min and systolic BP 100–120 mmHg. Give pain relief (opioids), because pain raises HR and BP.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q14$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1114, $s$A patient with new atrial fibrillation (rate 160) becomes hypotensive and confused. The immediate treatment is:$s$, $s$["Synchronised DC cardioversion", "Observation with repeat ECG in 24 hours", "IV furosemide bolus and fluid restriction", "Oral digoxin loading over 24 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Synchronised DC cardioversion
This patient has new AF with a fast rate AND adverse features: low BP and confusion (shock and poor brain perfusion). An UNSTABLE tachyarrhythmia needs immediate SYNCHRONISED DC cardioversion (under sedation if conscious), which resets the heart into normal rhythm. The shock is timed to the R wave so it doesn't land on the T wave and cause VF.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Observation with repeat ECG in 24 hours — Waiting 24 hours with observation would let the patient deteriorate or die.
• IV furosemide bolus and fluid restriction — Furosemide and fluid restriction don't treat the rhythm and would lower BP further.
• Oral digoxin loading over 24 hours — Oral digoxin takes hours to work and is far too slow for an unstable patient.

⭐ Key point: Adverse features that demand cardioversion: shock (low BP), syncope, myocardial ischaemia (chest pain), and heart failure. Stable AF is treated with rate control (beta-blockers) and anticoagulation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q15$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1115, $s$Before elective cardioversion of AF lasting more than 48 hours, the patient needs:$s$, $s$["Only a chest X-ray to check the heart size", "A single loading dose of aspirin on the day", "No preparation, as long as the patient is stable", "3 weeks of anticoagulation, or TOE to exclude thrombus"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3 weeks of anticoagulation, or TOE to exclude thrombus
In AF lasting more than 48 hours, blood pools in the left atrium (especially the appendage) and can form a clot. When normal rhythm returns, the atrium contracts again and can throw the clot to the brain, causing a stroke. To prevent this, the patient needs at least 3 WEEKS of effective anticoagulation BEFORE cardioversion, or a TOE (trans-oesophageal echo) to show there is no clot, then anticoagulation for at least 4 weeks afterwards.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only a chest X-ray to check the heart size — A chest X-ray can't show a clot inside the heart.
• A single loading dose of aspirin on the day — A single dose of aspirin doesn't protect against AF stroke.
• No preparation, as long as the patient is stable — Being stable does not remove the clot risk.

⭐ Key point: Use the CHA₂DS₂-VASc score to decide long-term anticoagulation in AF.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q16$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1116, $s$Cardiogenic shock after a large anterior MI is treated with:$s$, $s$["Routine high-dose diuretics only, until the BP improves", "IV beta-blockers to lower the heart rate and oxygen use", "Urgent revascularisation, inotropes and IABP support", "Large fluid boluses alone to fill the failing ventricle"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Urgent revascularisation, inotropes and IABP support
Cardiogenic shock after a large anterior MI is caused by a big area of dead or stunned heart muscle. The most important treatment is URGENT REVASCULARISATION (PCI or CABG) to restore blood flow to the living muscle. Meanwhile, inotropes (dobutamine, or noradrenaline if BP is very low) support the pump, and mechanical support (IABP or Impella) reduces afterload and improves coronary flow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Routine high-dose diuretics only, until the BP improves — High-dose diuretics alone lower BP further and don't fix the pump.
• IV beta-blockers to lower the heart rate and oxygen use — Beta-blockers WEAKEN contraction and can worsen shock.
• Large fluid boluses alone to fill the failing ventricle — Large fluid boluses would flood the lungs in a failing left ventricle (careful small boluses only if preload is low).

⭐ Key point: The SHOCK trial showed early revascularisation saves lives in cardiogenic shock. Death rate without treatment is very high (about 50%).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q19$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1117, $s$Troponin in acute MI begins to rise within about:$s$, $s$["30 minutes of symptom onset", "7 days after the event", "3–4 hours of symptom onset", "24 hours only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3–4 hours of symptom onset
Troponin is released when heart muscle cells are damaged. It begins to rise in the blood about 3–4 hours after symptoms start (high-sensitivity tests detect it earlier, within 1–3 hours). It peaks at about 24–48 hours and stays raised for up to 10–14 days. That's why troponin is checked on arrival and repeated after a few hours if the first is normal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 30 minutes of symptom onset — At 30 minutes it is often still normal, so a normal early troponin doesn't rule out MI.
• 7 days after the event — By 7 days it is falling, not just starting to rise.
• 24 hours only — It rises much earlier than 24 hours.

⭐ Key point: Never wait for troponin to treat a STEMI. The ECG diagnoses STEMI; troponin confirms damage in NSTEMI.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q20$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1118, $s$Neurogenic shock after high cord injury presents with:$s$, $s$["Hypertension with bradycardia", "Hypotension with tachycardia and cold skin", "Hypotension with bradycardia and warm skin", "Normal blood pressure"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypotension with bradycardia and warm skin
An injury above T6 cuts off the sympathetic nerves that control BP and heart rate. Without sympathetic tone, the blood vessels dilate (low BP) and the vagus nerve slows the heart unopposed (slow pulse). The skin stays WARM and DRY because the vessels are dilated. This is NEUROGENIC SHOCK: the only shock with a slow pulse and warm skin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension with bradycardia — High BP with slow pulse describes Cushing's triad or autonomic dysreflexia.
• Hypotension with tachycardia and cold skin — Low BP with fast pulse and cold skin describes hypovolaemic shock.
• Normal blood pressure — Neurogenic shock causes low BP, not normal.

⭐ Key point: Treat neurogenic shock with fluids (careful), vasopressors (noradrenaline) and atropine for bradycardia. Always rule out bleeding as a cause of low BP in trauma.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q97$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1119, $s$A woman at 34 weeks lies flat for a scan and becomes pale, sweaty and hypotensive. The nurse should:$s$, $s$["Turn her to the left lateral position", "Raise both legs and keep her flat", "Give IV furosemide", "Sit her upright at 90 degrees"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Turn her to the left lateral position
When a woman in late pregnancy lies flat on her back, the heavy uterus presses on the inferior vena cava (IVC). Blood returning to the heart drops, cardiac output falls, and the BP drops. She becomes pale, sweaty, dizzy and hypotensive: supine hypotensive syndrome. TURNING HER TO THE LEFT side moves the uterus off the IVC and restores blood flow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Raise both legs and keep her flat — Raising the legs while still flat doesn't remove the uterus from the IVC.
• Give IV furosemide — Furosemide would reduce blood volume and worsen the hypotension.
• Sit her upright at 90 degrees — Sitting fully upright isn't the standard treatment, and she may faint.

⭐ Key point: In late pregnancy, avoid lying flat. Use a left lateral tilt (or a wedge under the right hip) for procedures and CPR.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q121$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1120, $s$Peripartum cardiomyopathy presents with:$s$, $s$["Sudden tearing chest pain radiating to the back", "Hypertension and seizures without heart failure", "Heart failure in late pregnancy or months after", "Fever with a widespread rash and joint pains"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Heart failure in late pregnancy or months after
Peripartum cardiomyopathy is HEART FAILURE caused by weakness of the heart muscle (ejection fraction below 45%) that develops in the LAST MONTH of pregnancy or within 5 MONTHS after delivery, with no other cause. Symptoms: breathlessness, orthopnoea, swollen legs, cough, tiredness and palpitations, which are easily mistaken for normal pregnancy symptoms. Risk factors: multiple pregnancies, older age, hypertension, twins and African descent. It is unusually common in northern Nigeria, possibly linked to postpartum customs of very high salt intake and heated rooms.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sudden tearing chest pain radiating to the back — Tearing chest pain to the back suggests aortic dissection.
• Hypertension and seizures without heart failure — High BP and seizures WITHOUT heart failure describe eclampsia.
• Fever with a widespread rash and joint pains — Fever with rash and joint pains suggests an infection or autoimmune disease.

⭐ Key point: Treat with heart failure drugs (diuretics, beta-blockers; ACE inhibitors after delivery), anticoagulation if the EF is very low, and bromocriptine in some cases. Advise about future pregnancy risk.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q127 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1121, $s$An arterial line waveform that is 'over-damped' shows:$s$, $s$["A tall, spiky trace with a false high systolic", "A normal trace with a clear dicrotic notch", "An absent trace with a normal cuff pressure", "A flattened trace with a falsely low systolic"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A flattened trace with a falsely low systolic
An over-damped arterial trace looks FLATTENED and rounded, with a poor or absent dicrotic notch. The systolic pressure reads FALSELY LOW and the diastolic may read falsely high (the MAP is usually still fairly accurate). Causes: air bubbles, clots in the catheter, kinked tubing, a loose connection, or a pressure bag that is not inflated enough.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A tall, spiky trace with a false high systolic — A tall, spiky trace with a falsely high systolic is UNDER-damped.
• A normal trace with a clear dicrotic notch — A normal trace with a clear notch is optimally damped.
• An absent trace with a normal cuff pressure — An absent trace suggests disconnection or a blocked line.

⭐ Key point: Use the square-wave (fast flush) test to check damping, then remove bubbles, flush, and check connections.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q24$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1122, $s$Monitoring after pericardiocentesis includes observing for:$s$, $s$["Hyperglycaemia and ketosis from the stress", "Re-accumulation, arrhythmias and pneumothorax", "A widespread skin rash from the antiseptic", "Diarrhoea and dehydration from the drugs"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Re-accumulation, arrhythmias and pneumothorax
After pericardiocentesis, monitor for RE-ACCUMULATION of fluid (returning signs of tamponade: low BP, rising JVP, muffled heart sounds), ARRHYTHMIAS (the needle may have irritated the heart), and PNEUMOTHORAX (the needle may have punctured the lung). A pericardial drain may be left in, and its output recorded.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperglycaemia and ketosis from the stress — Hyperglycaemia and ketosis are not typical complications.
• A widespread skin rash from the antiseptic — A skin rash from antiseptic is minor and not the main concern.
• Diarrhoea and dehydration from the drugs — Diarrhoea and dehydration are not related.

⭐ Key point: If tamponade signs return, the drain may be blocked or fluid is re-accumulating. Inform the doctor urgently.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q40$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1123, $s$The normal CVP range is:$s$, $s$["30–40mmHg", "10–20mmHg", "2–8mmHg", "0–2mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2–8mmHg
Normal central venous pressure (CVP) is about 2–8 mmHg (about 3–10 cmH₂O). It reflects the pressure in the right atrium and big veins, and gives an idea of blood volume and right heart function.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 30–40mmHg — 30–40 mmHg is extremely high and not normal.
• 10–20mmHg — 10–20 mmHg is high (fluid overload or right heart failure).
• 0–2mmHg — 0–2 mmHg is low (suggests hypovolaemia).

⭐ Key point: The TREND of CVP and its response to fluid matters more than a single value.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q41$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1124, $s$CVP should be measured at which point in the respiratory cycle?$s$, $s$["Mid-inspiration", "End of expiration", "End of inspiration", "Timing doesn't matter"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: End of expiration
Breathing changes the pressure inside the chest, which changes the CVP reading. At END-EXPIRATION, the chest is at rest and intrathoracic pressure is closest to atmospheric, so the reading is most accurate and consistent. This applies to both spontaneously breathing and ventilated patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mid-inspiration — Mid-inspiration gives a changing, unreliable reading.
• End of inspiration — End-inspiration gives a falsely LOW reading in spontaneous breathing and a falsely HIGH one in ventilated patients.
• Timing doesn't matter — The timing matters for accuracy.

⭐ Key point: Read CVP at end-expiration, with the patient lying flat and the transducer at the phlebostatic axis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q42$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1125, $s$The Left Ventricular End Diastolic Volume (LVEDV) is also called:$s$, $s$["Afterload", "Preload", "Cardiac Index", "Cardiac Output"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Preload
Preload is the stretch of the ventricle muscle at the end of diastole, just before it contracts. It is determined by the VOLUME of blood in the ventricle at that moment: the left ventricular end-diastolic volume (LVEDV). So LVEDV is another name for preload of the left ventricle.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Afterload — Afterload is the resistance the ventricle must pump against.
• Cardiac Index — Cardiac index is cardiac output divided by body surface area.
• Cardiac Output — Cardiac output is the volume pumped per minute (HR × SV).

⭐ Key point: More preload means more stretch, which means a stronger contraction (Frank–Starling law), up to a point.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q43$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1126, $s$A patient in hypovolaemic shock from acute blood loss would be expected to show which haemodynamic pattern?$s$, $s$["High filling pressures with low SVR", "Low cardiac output, high SVR, low filling pressures", "Normal cardiac output with no haemodynamic change", "High cardiac output, low SVR"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Low cardiac output, high SVR, low filling pressures
In hypovolaemic shock, there isn't enough blood to fill the heart. So: LOW filling pressures (low CVP and wedge pressure), LOW cardiac output (not enough blood to pump), and HIGH SVR (the vessels tighten to keep up BP and send blood to vital organs). The skin is cold, pale and clammy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High filling pressures with low SVR — High filling pressures with low SVR do not fit hypovolaemia.
• Normal cardiac output with no haemodynamic change — Hypovolaemia causes clear haemodynamic changes.
• High cardiac output, low SVR — High cardiac output with low SVR describes early septic shock.

⭐ Key point: Treat hypovolaemic shock by replacing the volume lost: crystalloids, or blood for haemorrhage. Stop any bleeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q44$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1127, $s$What is the primary purpose of a Swan-Ganz catheter in critical care?$s$, $s$["Monitoring blood pressure", "Measuring intracranial pressure (ICP)", "Monitoring cardiac hemodynamics", "Administering insulin"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Monitoring cardiac hemodynamics
A Swan-Ganz (pulmonary artery) catheter is inserted through a large vein into the right side of the heart and pulmonary artery. It measures right atrial pressure, PA pressures, wedge pressure (left heart filling), cardiac output (thermodilution) and mixed venous oxygen. Its main purpose is to MONITOR CARDIAC HAEMODYNAMICS in complex shock or heart failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Monitoring blood pressure — Blood pressure is monitored by a cuff or arterial line.
• Measuring intracranial pressure (ICP) — ICP is measured by an EVD or brain probe.
• Administering insulin — Insulin is given through any IV line; this isn't the purpose of the PA catheter.

⭐ Key point: PA catheters are used less now (echo and less invasive monitors are preferred), but they are still examined.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q45$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1128, $s$Normal pulmonary artery wedge pressure is:$s$, $s$["6–12 mmHg", "0–2 mmHg", "20–30 mmHg", "40–50 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 6–12 mmHg
The pulmonary artery wedge pressure (PAWP or PCWP) is normally 6–12 mmHg. With the balloon inflated, the catheter tip "sees" the pressure beyond it in the left atrium, so it reflects LEFT ventricular filling (preload).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0–2 mmHg — 0–2 mmHg is too low (severe hypovolaemia).
• 20–30 mmHg — 20–30 mmHg is high (left heart failure, fluid overload, pulmonary oedema).
• 40–50 mmHg — 40–50 mmHg is extremely high and not normal.

⭐ Key point: A PAWP above 18 mmHg suggests cardiogenic (hydrostatic) pulmonary oedema; in ARDS the PAWP is usually normal.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q46$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1129, $s$Normal cardiac index is about:$s$, $s$["10–12 L/min/m²", "6–8 L/min/m²", "2.5–4.0 L/min/m²", "0.5–1.0 L/min/m²"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2.5–4.0 L/min/m²
The cardiac index (CI) is the cardiac output divided by the body surface area (CO ÷ BSA). It makes the output comparable between big and small people. The normal CI is 2.5–4.0 L/min/m². A CI below 2.2 suggests cardiogenic shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10–12 L/min/m² — 10–12 is far too high.
• 6–8 L/min/m² — 6–8 is too high for CI (it's more like a high cardiac output in litres per minute).
• 0.5–1.0 L/min/m² — 0.5–1.0 is dangerously low.

⭐ Key point: Normal cardiac output is 4–8 L/min. Normal stroke volume is 60–100 mL.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q47$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1130, $s$Systemic vascular resistance is high in:$s$, $s$["Cardiogenic and hypovolaemic shock", "Anaphylactic shock after a drug", "Neurogenic shock after cord injury", "Early (warm) septic shock"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cardiogenic and hypovolaemic shock
Systemic vascular resistance (SVR) rises when the arteries constrict. In CARDIOGENIC and HYPOVOLAEMIC shock, cardiac output falls, so the body tightens the arteries (via adrenaline, noradrenaline and angiotensin) to maintain blood pressure. This is compensatory vasoconstriction, causing high SVR and cold, pale skin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anaphylactic shock after a drug — Anaphylactic shock has LOW SVR (vasodilation).
• Neurogenic shock after cord injury — Neurogenic shock has LOW SVR (lost sympathetic tone).
• Early (warm) septic shock — Early septic shock has LOW SVR (vasodilation).

⭐ Key point: High SVR shocks (cold) = hypovolaemic, cardiogenic, obstructive. Low SVR shocks (warm) = septic, anaphylactic, neurogenic.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q48$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1131, $s$Early septic (warm) shock shows:$s$, $s$["High cardiac output and low SVR", "Low wedge and high SVR", "Low cardiac output and high SVR", "Normal output and high SVR"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: High cardiac output and low SVR
In early (warm) septic shock, inflammatory chemicals widen the blood vessels, so SVR FALLS. The heart pumps harder and faster to compensate, so cardiac output RISES (a hyperdynamic state). The skin is warm and flushed, with bounding pulses, but organs are still under-perfused.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low wedge and high SVR — Low wedge with high SVR describes hypovolaemia.
• Low cardiac output and high SVR — Low output with high SVR describes cardiogenic or hypovolaemic shock (or late sepsis).
• Normal output and high SVR — Normal output with high SVR doesn't fit early sepsis.

⭐ Key point: Late septic shock can become "cold", with low cardiac output from septic cardiomyopathy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q49$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1132, $s$When floating a PA catheter, the waveform changes from RV to PA by:$s$, $s$["A fall in systolic pressure towards zero", "A rise in diastolic pressure with a dicrotic notch", "A complete loss of all pulsation", "A flat trace with very large A waves"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A rise in diastolic pressure with a dicrotic notch
As the PA catheter passes from the right ventricle into the pulmonary artery, the waveform changes. In the RV, the diastolic pressure is close to zero. In the PA, the pulmonary valve closes and keeps pressure up, so the DIASTOLIC PRESSURE RISES (to about 8–15 mmHg), and a DICROTIC NOTCH appears (pulmonary valve closure). The systolic pressure stays about the same.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A fall in systolic pressure towards zero — The systolic pressure doesn't fall to zero.
• A complete loss of all pulsation — Losing all pulsation would suggest a problem (such as a wedged or blocked catheter).
• A flat trace with very large A waves — A flat trace with large A waves isn't the PA trace.

⭐ Key point: The waveform sequence while floating: RA (low, small waves), RV (tall, square, diastole near zero), PA (diastole raised, dicrotic notch), wedge (low, flat-ish, like the atrium).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q50$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1133, $s$The balloon of a PA catheter should be inflated:$s$, $s$["With no more than 1.5 mL of air, briefly", "With 10 mL of saline for a firm wedge", "Continuously for hours to monitor PAWP", "With water to improve the wedge trace"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: With no more than 1.5 mL of air, briefly
The PA catheter balloon is inflated with no more than 1.5 mL of AIR (the volume marked on the catheter), only BRIEFLY (just long enough to read the wedge pressure, under about 15 seconds), then allowed to deflate passively. Over-inflation or prolonged wedging can rupture the pulmonary artery or cause pulmonary infarction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• With 10 mL of saline for a firm wedge — Saline is never used; it can't be quickly removed, and the balloon may burst.
• Continuously for hours to monitor PAWP — Continuous inflation risks pulmonary infarction.
• With water to improve the wedge trace — Water is never used.

⭐ Key point: If the wedge trace appears with LESS than the full volume, the catheter has moved too far; inform the doctor.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q51$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1134, $s$A permanent wedge trace on a PA catheter without balloon inflation means:$s$, $s$["The balloon has burst and must be replaced", "Cardiac tamponade around the catheter", "Normal function of the catheter", "The catheter has migrated distally; pull it back"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The catheter has migrated distally; pull it back
If the PA catheter shows a wedge trace when the balloon is NOT inflated, the catheter tip has moved further into a small pulmonary artery branch (spontaneous wedging) and is blocking it. This can cause a pulmonary infarction or artery rupture. The catheter needs to be pulled back by the doctor; inform them immediately.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The balloon has burst and must be replaced — A burst balloon wouldn't cause a permanent wedge trace.
• Cardiac tamponade around the catheter — Tamponade doesn't cause a wedge trace.
• Normal function of the catheter — A continuous wedge trace without inflation is NOT normal.

⭐ Key point: Continuously display the PA trace on the monitor so migration is spotted early.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q52$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1135, $s$The thermodilution method calculates cardiac output from:$s$, $s$["The temperature change of cold injectate", "The heart rate measured over one minute", "The blood pressure measured by the cuff", "The ECG voltage in the chest leads"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The temperature change of cold injectate
In thermodilution, a known amount of cold (or room temperature) saline is injected quickly into the proximal (right atrial) port. A thermistor near the catheter tip in the pulmonary artery measures the temperature change as the cold fluid passes. The computer calculates cardiac output from the curve: a fast, small temperature change means high output; a slow, large change means low output.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The heart rate measured over one minute — Heart rate alone doesn't give cardiac output.
• The blood pressure measured by the cuff — Cuff BP doesn't measure cardiac output.
• The ECG voltage in the chest leads — ECG voltage doesn't measure cardiac output.

⭐ Key point: Inject 10 mL smoothly and quickly, at the same point in the breathing cycle, and average 3 readings that are within 10% of each other.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q53$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1136, $s$CVP is raised in:$s$, $s$["Vasodilation from early septic shock", "Dehydration from vomiting and diarrhoea", "Right heart failure, fluid overload, tamponade", "Hypovolaemia from acute haemorrhage"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Right heart failure, fluid overload, tamponade
CVP rises when blood backs up in the right atrium and big veins. Causes: RIGHT HEART FAILURE (the RV can't pump blood forward), FLUID OVERLOAD (too much volume), cardiac TAMPONADE (fluid around the heart squeezes it), tension pneumothorax, high PEEP, and PE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vasodilation from early septic shock — Vasodilation in early sepsis LOWERS CVP.
• Dehydration from vomiting and diarrhoea — Dehydration LOWERS CVP.
• Hypovolaemia from acute haemorrhage — Haemorrhage LOWERS CVP.

⭐ Key point: Always interpret CVP with the patient's clinical picture and its trend over time.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q54$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1137, $s$A passive leg raise test assesses:$s$, $s$["Spinal reflexes after cord injury", "The risk of deep vein thrombosis", "Leg circulation in arterial disease", "Fluid responsiveness without giving fluid"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fluid responsiveness without giving fluid
A passive leg raise (PLR) moves the patient from a semi-recumbent position to lying flat with the legs raised at 45°. This shifts about 300 mL of blood from the legs to the heart, like a temporary "fluid bolus" that can be reversed. If cardiac output (or stroke volume) rises by 10% or more, the patient is likely FLUID RESPONSIVE, without giving any actual fluid.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Spinal reflexes after cord injury — PLR doesn't test spinal reflexes.
• The risk of deep vein thrombosis — It doesn't assess DVT risk.
• Leg circulation in arterial disease — It isn't for diagnosing arterial disease in the legs.

⭐ Key point: Measure the cardiac output change directly (not just BP), within 1–2 minutes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q57$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1138, $s$Inotropes increase:$s$, $s$["Venous capacity", "Systemic vascular resistance only", "Myocardial contractility", "Heart block"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Myocardial contractility
Inotropes increase the FORCE of heart muscle contraction (contractility), so the heart pumps more blood with each beat (higher stroke volume and cardiac output). Examples: dobutamine, adrenaline, milrinone, dopamine, and levosimendan. They are used in low cardiac output states such as cardiogenic shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Venous capacity — Increasing venous capacity is a vasodilator effect (like GTN).
• Systemic vascular resistance only — Raising SVR is the main effect of vasopressors.
• Heart block — Inotropes don't cause heart block; some may cause arrhythmias.

⭐ Key point: Inotropes increase myocardial oxygen demand. Monitor for tachycardia, arrhythmias and ischaemia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q58$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1139, $s$Vasopressors mainly increase:$s$, $s$["SVR and blood pressure", "Contractility only", "Venous capacity", "Heart block"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: SVR and blood pressure
Vasopressors tighten (constrict) the blood vessels, increasing SYSTEMIC VASCULAR RESISTANCE and therefore BLOOD PRESSURE. They are used in vasodilated shock (septic, anaphylactic, neurogenic). Noradrenaline is first-line in septic shock; vasopressin is often added second.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Contractility only — Raising contractility alone is an inotrope effect.
• Venous capacity — Increasing venous capacity is a venodilator effect.
• Heart block — Vasopressors don't cause heart block.

⭐ Key point: Give vasopressors through a central line where possible, via a dedicated lumen, never flushed, with continuous arterial BP monitoring.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q59$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1140, $s$The intra-aortic balloon pump inflates during:$s$, $s$["Only during cardiac arrest", "Systole, increasing the afterload", "Diastole, improving coronary flow", "Both phases equally throughout"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Diastole, improving coronary flow
The intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP) INFLATES during DIASTOLE (just after the aortic valve closes, at the dicrotic notch). This pushes blood back toward the coronary arteries, improving coronary blood flow and oxygen supply to the heart muscle. It DEFLATES just before systole, which lowers the pressure in the aorta (afterload), so the heart can pump more easily.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only during cardiac arrest — The IABP is not used during cardiac arrest in this way.
• Systole, increasing the afterload — Inflating in systole would INCREASE afterload and harm the heart.
• Both phases equally throughout — It inflates and deflates at different times, not both phases equally.

⭐ Key point: IABP improves coronary perfusion (diastolic augmentation) and reduces afterload. It is timed to the ECG or arterial trace.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q60$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1141, $s$Immediate hemostatic responds to the damage vessel is$s$, $s$["Platelet aggregation", "Platelet viscous metamorphosis", "Platelet adhesion", "Vasoconstriction"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vasoconstriction
When a blood vessel is damaged, the FIRST and IMMEDIATE response is VASOCONSTRICTION: the vessel wall muscle contracts, narrowing the vessel and reducing blood loss within seconds. Next, platelets stick to the damaged wall (adhesion), clump together (aggregation) to form a plug, and then the clotting cascade forms fibrin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Platelet aggregation — Platelet aggregation comes after adhesion.
• Platelet viscous metamorphosis — "Viscous metamorphosis" is an old term for platelets changing shape and fusing, which comes after adhesion.
• Platelet adhesion — Platelet adhesion comes after vasoconstriction.

⭐ Key point: Haemostasis steps: 1) vasoconstriction, 2) platelet plug, 3) coagulation (fibrin clot), 4) clot breakdown (fibrinolysis).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q15$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1142, $s$Which class of medication reduces mortality in chronic heart failure with reduced ejection fraction by blocking the renin-angiotensin system?$s$, $s$["ACE inhibitors", "Loop diuretics alone", "Calcium channel blockers (non-dihydropyridine)", "Short-acting nitrates"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ACE inhibitors
ACE inhibitors (such as enalapril, ramipril, lisinopril) block the conversion of angiotensin I to angiotensin II. This lowers afterload (vasodilation), reduces aldosterone (less salt and water retention), and slows harmful heart remodelling. They REDUCE DEATHS and hospital admissions in heart failure with reduced ejection fraction (HFrEF).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Loop diuretics alone — Loop diuretics relieve fluid overload and symptoms but don't reduce mortality on their own.
• Calcium channel blockers (non-dihydropyridine) — Non-dihydropyridine calcium channel blockers (verapamil, diltiazem) can WORSEN HFrEF.
• Short-acting nitrates — Short-acting nitrates relieve symptoms but don't reduce mortality.

⭐ Key point: Mortality-reducing HFrEF drugs: ACE inhibitors (or ARBs/ARNI), beta-blockers, mineralocorticoid antagonists (spironolactone), and SGLT2 inhibitors.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q41$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1143, $s$Hemodynamic pressure monitoring provides information about the following parameters:$s$, $s$["Fluid balance and Stroke Volume", "Blood Volume and Stroke Volume", "Blood Volume and Heart Rate", "Blood volume and fluid balance"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Blood volume and fluid balance
Haemodynamic pressure monitoring (CVP, PA pressures, arterial pressure) gives information about the circulation, especially the patient's BLOOD VOLUME (filling status) and FLUID BALANCE (whether they are dry or overloaded), helping guide fluid therapy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fluid balance and Stroke Volume — Stroke volume is measured by cardiac output monitoring, not pressure monitoring alone.
• Blood Volume and Stroke Volume — Stroke volume is not directly measured by pressure.
• Blood Volume and Heart Rate — Heart rate is measured by the ECG.

⭐ Key point: Pressures are only one part of the picture. Combine them with clinical signs, urine output, lactate and fluid responsiveness tests.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q43$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1144, $s$Post-thyroidectomy, a nurse observes hypotension and tachycardia. The most probable cause is:$s$, $s$["Hypothyroidism", "Laryngospasm", "Anxiety", "Hemorrhage"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hemorrhage
After thyroidectomy, the neck has many blood vessels. Bleeding (HAEMORRHAGE) can cause HYPOVOLAEMIA (low BP, fast pulse) and, importantly, a neck haematoma that can COMPRESS the airway. Hypotension with tachycardia most likely means bleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypothyroidism — Hypothyroidism develops slowly and causes slow pulse, not acute hypotension with tachycardia.
• Laryngospasm — Laryngospasm causes breathing problems and stridor, not mainly low BP and fast pulse.
• Anxiety — Anxiety can cause a fast pulse but not hypotension.

⭐ Key point: Post-thyroidectomy: check the neck and dressing for swelling, watch for stridor, keep stitch-cutters or clip removers at the bedside, and watch for low calcium (tingling, tetany).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q44$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1145, $s$When the delivery button is pushed, there will be a slight delay in firing because the machine is sensing the R wave in order to deliver the energy at the precise moment. This phenomenon is known as:$s$, $s$["Defibrillation", "Synchronized Cardio Version", "Cardio Version", "A Synchronized Cardio Version"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Synchronized Cardio Version
In SYNCHRONISED CARDIOVERSION, the defibrillator is set to SYNC mode, so it detects the patient's R wave and times the shock to land on it. This avoids shocking during the vulnerable T wave, which could cause VF. That's why there's a slight DELAY after pressing the button. It is used for unstable tachycardias WITH a pulse (AF, flutter, SVT, VT with a pulse).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Defibrillation — Defibrillation is UNSYNCHRONISED (given immediately), used for VF and pulseless VT.
• Cardio Version — "Cardioversion" alone is incomplete; the R-wave timing is what makes it synchronised.
• A Synchronized Cardio Version — "A synchronized cardioversion" is a duplicate distractor of the correct answer.

⭐ Key point: Turn SYNC on for cardioversion. It often switches OFF after each shock, so check it before the next one.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q47$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1146, $s$A paradoxical pulse occurs in a client who had a coronary artery bypass graft (CABG) surgery two (2) days ago. Which of the following surgical complications should the nurse suspect?$s$, $s$["Left-sided heart failure", "Pericardial tamponade", "Aortic regurgitation", "Complete heart block"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pericardial tamponade
PULSUS PARADOXUS is an exaggerated fall in systolic BP (more than 10 mmHg) during inspiration. After cardiac surgery, blood can collect around the heart (the pericardium), squeezing it so it can't fill: CARDIAC TAMPONADE. Other signs: low BP, raised JVP, muffled heart sounds (Beck's triad), and a sudden fall in chest drain output (clot blocking the drain).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Left-sided heart failure — Left heart failure causes pulmonary oedema and crackles, not typically pulsus paradoxus.
• Aortic regurgitation — Aortic regurgitation causes a wide pulse pressure and a diastolic murmur.
• Complete heart block — Complete heart block causes a slow pulse, not pulsus paradoxus.

⭐ Key point: Post-cardiac surgery tamponade is a surgical emergency: return to theatre or reopen the chest.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q49$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1147, $s$IV furosemide is administered for acute pulmonary oedema. Besides diuresis, what additional early therapeutic effect makes it useful in this emergency?$s$, $s$["Venodilation reducing preload", "Afterload reduction via arterial vasodilation", "Positive inotropy", "Bronchodilation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Venodilation reducing preload
IV furosemide works in TWO ways in acute pulmonary oedema. EARLY (within 5–15 minutes, before urine output increases), it causes VENODILATION, widening the veins so more blood is held in them. This reduces PRELOAD (blood returning to the heart) and lowers pressure in the lung capillaries, easing breathlessness. LATER, its diuretic effect removes excess fluid.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Afterload reduction via arterial vasodilation — Arterial vasodilation (afterload reduction) isn't its main early effect.
• Positive inotropy — Furosemide has no positive inotropic effect.
• Bronchodilation — Furosemide doesn't cause bronchodilation.

⭐ Key point: GTN (nitrates) also reduces preload quickly. Sit the patient upright and give oxygen or CPAP.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q50$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1148, $s$Which of the following statement is false?$s$, $s$["Hypertensive crises are categorized as either hypertensive emergency or hypertensive urgency", "Hypertensive emergencies require an immediate but gradual reduction in BP over a period of several minutes to several hours using intravenous antihypertensive agents.", "Hypertensive urgency is associated with end organ injury or damage", "Hypertensive emergency are extreme elevations in BP that a accompanied by acute or progressing target-organ damage"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypertensive urgency is associated with end organ injury or damage
This question asks for the FALSE statement. Hypertensive URGENCY is severe high BP WITHOUT acute end-organ damage. The statement that urgency "is associated with end organ damage" is FALSE, because organ damage defines a hypertensive EMERGENCY. The other three statements are true.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertensive crises are categorized as either hypertensive emergency or hypertensive urgency — It is TRUE that hypertensive crises are divided into emergency and urgency.
• Hypertensive emergencies require an immediate but gradual reduction in BP over a period of several minutes to several hours using intravenous antihypertensive agents. — It is TRUE that hypertensive emergencies need immediate but gradual BP reduction with IV drugs.
• Hypertensive emergency are extreme elevations in BP that a accompanied by acute or progressing target-organ damage — It is TRUE that hypertensive emergencies involve extreme BP with acute or progressing organ damage.

⭐ Key point: Emergency = high BP + organ damage (IV drugs, ICU). Urgency = high BP alone (oral drugs, gradual reduction).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q51$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1149, $s$A patient is undergoing cardiac catheterization. Which of the following sensations reported by the patient during the procedure has the highest priority?$s$, $s$["Warm, flushed feeling", "Pressure at the insertion site", "Urge to cough", "Chest pain"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Chest pain
CHEST PAIN during cardiac catheterisation may mean myocardial ISCHAEMIA (the catheter or contrast may be blocking a coronary artery), a coronary artery dissection, spasm, or an MI. It is the highest-priority sensation, because it could be life-threatening. Report it immediately and record a 12-lead ECG.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Warm, flushed feeling — A warm, flushed feeling is NORMAL when contrast is injected.
• Pressure at the insertion site — Pressure at the insertion site is expected.
• Urge to cough — An urge to cough can happen with contrast and is usually harmless.

⭐ Key point: After cardiac catheterisation: check the puncture site for bleeding or haematoma, check distal pulses, and keep the limb straight as instructed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q53$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1150, $s$Stimulation of the vagus nerve (CN X), for example during suctioning or straining, would be expected to cause which cardiovascular response?$s$, $s$["Tachycardia and hypertension", "No cardiovascular effect", "Bradycardia, and potentially hypotension", "Isolated increase in respiratory rate"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bradycardia, and potentially hypotension
The VAGUS NERVE (CN X) is part of the parasympathetic nervous system. Stimulating it (suction, straining, vomiting, pressure on the eyes or the carotid sinus) slows the SA node and AV conduction, causing BRADYCARDIA and possibly HYPOTENSION. In severe cases it can cause asystole.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tachycardia and hypertension — Tachycardia and hypertension are SYMPATHETIC effects.
• No cardiovascular effect — Vagal stimulation does have cardiovascular effects.
• Isolated increase in respiratory rate — It doesn't simply increase breathing rate.

⭐ Key point: Keep suction passes short, pre-oxygenate, and keep atropine available for patients prone to vagal bradycardia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q54$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1151, $s$Which of the following pairs is correct?$s$, $s$["Arterial dilators: reduce cardiac output", "Arterial dilators: increase afterload", "Vasodilators: reduce preload", "Venodilators: increase blood volume returning to the heart"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vasodilators: reduce preload
VASODILATORS widen blood vessels. Venodilators (like GTN) increase the capacity of the veins, so more blood pools there and less returns to the heart, which REDUCES PRELOAD. This is why nitrates help in pulmonary oedema and angina.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Arterial dilators: reduce cardiac output — Arterial dilators REDUCE afterload, which usually INCREASES cardiac output.
• Arterial dilators: increase afterload — Arterial dilators DECREASE (not increase) afterload.
• Venodilators: increase blood volume returning to the heart — Venodilators DECREASE (not increase) the volume returning to the heart.

⭐ Key point: Venodilators reduce preload (nitrates); arterial dilators reduce afterload (hydralazine); some do both (nitroprusside, ACE inhibitors).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q55$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1152, $s$A client is scheduled for a cardiac catheterization using a radiopaque dye. Which of the following assessments is most critical before the procedure?$s$, $s$["Allergy to iodine or shellfish", "Height and weight", "Intake and output", "Baseline peripheral pulse rates"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Allergy to iodine or shellfish
Cardiac catheterisation uses an iodine-based radiopaque contrast (dye). The most critical pre-procedure check is for ALLERGY to IODINE or contrast (and a history of severe allergies). Shellfish allergy is traditionally asked about, although it's not a reliable predictor. A known allergy may need pre-medication (steroids, antihistamines) or alternative plans. Kidney function is also important.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Height and weight — Height and weight matter for drug doses but are less critical.
• Intake and output — Intake and output is routine monitoring.
• Baseline peripheral pulse rates — Baseline peripheral pulses are important for comparison after the procedure, but allergy is most critical.

⭐ Key point: Also check: creatinine (contrast nephropathy), anticoagulants, metformin, and that consent is signed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q56$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1153, $s$A patient has cardiac output 4 L/min and body surface area 2 m². The cardiac index is:$s$, $s$["4.0 L/min/m², normal", "2.0 L/min/m², below normal", "8.0 L/min/m², above normal", "0.5 L/min/m², incompatible with life"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2.0 L/min/m², below normal
Cardiac index (CI) = cardiac output ÷ body surface area. Here: 4 L/min ÷ 2 m² = 2.0 L/min/m². Normal CI is 2.5–4.0 L/min/m², so 2.0 is BELOW NORMAL. Although a cardiac output of 4 L/min looks normal, it is too low for this large person.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 4.0 L/min/m², normal — 4.0 would mean no division by body surface area.
• 8.0 L/min/m², above normal — 8.0 comes from multiplying instead of dividing.
• 0.5 L/min/m², incompatible with life — 0.5 comes from dividing the wrong way round (BSA ÷ CO).

⭐ Key point: A cardiac index below 2.2 with signs of poor perfusion suggests cardiogenic shock.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q57$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1154, $s$MAP 65 mmHg, CVP 5 mmHg and cardiac output 6 L/min give an SVR of about:$s$, $s$["2400 dyn·s/cm⁵, high, as in hypovolaemia", "80 dyn·s/cm⁵, low, as in heart block", "800 dyn·s/cm⁵, low, as in distributive shock", "1600 dyn·s/cm⁵, high, as in cardiogenic shock"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 800 dyn·s/cm⁵, low, as in distributive shock
SVR = (MAP − CVP) × 80 ÷ cardiac output. Here: (65 − 5) × 80 ÷ 6 = 60 × 80 ÷ 6 = 4800 ÷ 6 = 800 dyn·s/cm⁵. This is at the LOWER limit of normal (800–1200), which points to vasodilation, as seen in DISTRIBUTIVE shock (sepsis, anaphylaxis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2400 dyn·s/cm⁵, high, as in hypovolaemia — 2400 would be very high (vasoconstricted).
• 80 dyn·s/cm⁵, low, as in heart block — 80 forgets to multiply by 80.
• 1600 dyn·s/cm⁵, high, as in cardiogenic shock — 1600 comes from a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Low SVR = distributive shock, treated with vasopressors. High SVR = hypovolaemic or cardiogenic shock.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q58$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1155, $s$In a fully ventilated patient in sinus rhythm, pulse pressure variation above 13% suggests:$s$, $s$["The arterial line trace is over-damped", "Severe left ventricular systolic failure", "The patient is already fluid overloaded", "The patient is likely to respond to a fluid bolus"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The patient is likely to respond to a fluid bolus
In a patient on controlled ventilation in SINUS rhythm, positive pressure breaths reduce venous return slightly with each breath. If the patient needs fluid (preload-dependent), the pulse pressure varies a lot between breaths. Pulse pressure variation (PPV) above 13% suggests the patient is likely to RESPOND to a FLUID BOLUS (cardiac output will rise).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The arterial line trace is over-damped — An over-damped trace doesn't produce high PPV.
• Severe left ventricular systolic failure — Severe LV failure tends to show LOW PPV.
• The patient is already fluid overloaded — Fluid overload shows LOW PPV.

⭐ Key point: PPV is valid only with controlled ventilation, tidal volume of about 8 mL/kg, sinus rhythm and a closed chest.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q59 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1156, $s$A passive leg raise predicts fluid responsiveness when it raises stroke volume or cardiac output by more than:$s$, $s$["10%", "100%", "50%", "1%"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10%
A passive leg raise (PLR) lifts the legs to 45° from a semi-recumbent position, returning about 300 mL of blood to the heart, like a reversible fluid challenge. If the STROKE VOLUME or CARDIAC OUTPUT rises by more than 10%, the patient is likely fluid responsive and may benefit from fluids.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100% — 100% is an impossible rise.
• 50% — 50% is far too high a threshold.
• 1% — 1% is too small to be meaningful.

⭐ Key point: Measure the response with a cardiac output monitor or echo, not just BP, and read it within 1–2 minutes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q60$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1157, $s$A 20 year male presented to ER with motor bike accident. The Nurse is performing initial assessment of the patient in triage and finds out that the patient has delayed capillary refill, Cool, Moist skin, Decreasing blood pressure and increasing heart rate. What is the suspected medical diagnose of this patient?$s$, $s$["Hypotension", "Hemorrhage", "Shock", "Acute Renal Failure(ARF)"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Shock
This patient has signs of poor tissue perfusion: delayed capillary refill, cool and moist (clammy) skin, falling BP and a rising heart rate. Together, these describe SHOCK (circulatory failure). After a motorbike crash, it is most likely hypovolaemic (haemorrhagic) shock from bleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypotension — Hypotension is one sign, not the full clinical diagnosis.
• Hemorrhage — Haemorrhage is the likely CAUSE, but the clinical state described is shock.
• Acute Renal Failure(ARF) — Acute renal failure isn't diagnosed from these findings (it may follow shock).

⭐ Key point: Shock = inadequate tissue perfusion. Look for bleeding: "on the floor and four more" (chest, abdomen, pelvis, long bones).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q105$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1158, $s$A trauma patient with a systolic blood pressure of 85mmHg, heart rate 128, and cool clammy skin after a motor vehicle accident is most likely in which type of shock?$s$, $s$["Hypovolaemic shock", "Anaphylactic shock", "Cardiogenic shock", "Neurogenic shock"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypovolaemic shock
After trauma, a low BP (systolic 85), fast heart rate (128), and COOL, CLAMMY skin indicate HYPOVOLAEMIC shock, most likely from blood loss. The body tightens blood vessels to protect vital organs (cool skin) and speeds the heart to compensate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anaphylactic shock — Anaphylactic shock follows an allergen, with warm, flushed skin, rash and wheeze.
• Cardiogenic shock — Cardiogenic shock follows heart failure or MI, with pulmonary oedema.
• Neurogenic shock — Neurogenic shock (spinal injury) has SLOW pulse and WARM skin.

⭐ Key point: Treat haemorrhagic shock: control bleeding, give blood products early, tranexamic acid within 3 hours, and keep the patient warm.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q110$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1159, $s$Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity with cardiovascular collapse is treated with:$s$, $s$["IV calcium gluconate 10 mL only", "20% lipid emulsion 1.5 mL/kg IV bolus", "IV naloxone 0.4 mg", "IV atropine 3 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 20% lipid emulsion 1.5 mL/kg IV bolus
Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST) happens when too much local anaesthetic (such as bupivacaine) enters the blood. Early signs: tingling around the mouth, metallic taste, tinnitus, confusion, then seizures, arrhythmias and cardiac arrest. Treatment with cardiovascular collapse: 20% LIPID EMULSION, 1.5 mL/kg IV bolus, then an infusion of 15 mL/kg/hour. The lipid soaks up the drug from the heart.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV calcium gluconate 10 mL only — Calcium gluconate alone doesn't treat LAST.
• IV naloxone 0.4 mg — Naloxone reverses opioids.
• IV atropine 3 mg — Atropine doesn't reverse LAST.

⭐ Key point: Prevent LAST: calculate the maximum dose, aspirate before injecting, and inject slowly in small amounts. Continue CPR, which may be prolonged.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q160$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1160, $s$ST-segment elevation in leads II, III, and aVF is most consistent with a myocardial infarction affecting the:$s$, $s$["Anterior wall", "Inferior wall", "Lateral wall", "Posterior wall exclusively"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inferior wall
Leads II, III and aVF view the INFERIOR wall of the left ventricle. ST elevation in these leads means an INFERIOR MI, usually caused by blockage of the right coronary artery. Watch for bradycardia, heart block and right ventricular infarction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anterior wall — An anterior MI shows changes in V1–V4.
• Lateral wall — A lateral MI shows changes in I, aVL, V5–V6.
• Posterior wall exclusively — A posterior MI shows ST depression in V1–V3 with tall R waves.

⭐ Key point: In an inferior MI, check right-sided leads (V4R). If the RV is involved, give fluids and avoid nitrates.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q46$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1161, $s$A heart rate below what value is used as an ICU admission threshold?$s$, $s$["<90bpm", "<55bpm", "<70bpm", "<40bpm"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: <40bpm
ICU admission and outreach ("call criteria") use physiological thresholds that suggest a patient is dangerously unstable. A heart rate BELOW 40 bpm (or above 140–150) is commonly used. Such a slow rate may cause low cardiac output and signals conditions like complete heart block, hypoxia or drug toxicity. (The old answer key said below 55 bpm; the usual threshold is below 40, so the key has been corrected.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• <90bpm — Below 90 bpm is normal.
• <55bpm — Below 55 bpm is mild bradycardia and often normal (for example in athletes).
• <70bpm — Below 70 bpm is normal.

⭐ Key point: Other common triggers: systolic BP below 90, RR below 8 or above 30, SpO₂ below 90%, a fall in GCS of 2 or more, seizures.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q48 · answer key corrected$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1162, $s$A nurse is assessing Blood pressure of the patient, she knows that the invasive method of measuring Blood pressure is:$s$, $s$["Cardiac Monitor", "PA catheter", "Arterial line", "Blood pressure cuff"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Arterial line
An ARTERIAL LINE is a catheter placed in an artery (usually the radial) and connected to a pressure transducer. It measures BP DIRECTLY and CONTINUOUSLY (beat by beat), making it the INVASIVE method of BP measurement. It's used in shock, on vasopressors, and when frequent ABGs are needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cardiac Monitor — A cardiac monitor shows the ECG, not invasive BP.
• PA catheter — A PA catheter measures pressures in the pulmonary artery, not systemic BP.
• Blood pressure cuff — A BP cuff is NON-invasive.

⭐ Key point: Arterial line care: level and zero the transducer, keep the pressure bag at 300 mmHg, check hand perfusion, and label the line clearly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q50$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1163, $s$The purpose of inserting central venous pressure (CVP) into the major vein of body such as jugular vein, subclavian vein, and femoral is to monitor the pressure in;$s$, $s$["Pulmonary artery", "Right ventricle", "Left atrium", "Left ventricle"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Right ventricle
A CVP catheter sits in the superior vena cava near the right atrium. It measures RIGHT ATRIAL pressure, which reflects the FILLING pressure of the RIGHT VENTRICLE (right ventricular end-diastolic pressure, because the tricuspid valve is open in diastole). It guides fluid status and right heart function.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pulmonary artery — The pulmonary artery is measured with a PA catheter.
• Left atrium — The left atrium is estimated by the wedge pressure (PAWP).
• Left ventricle — The left ventricle is estimated indirectly by the wedge pressure.

⭐ Key point: Normal CVP is 2–8 mmHg. A high CVP means fluid overload, right heart failure or tamponade; a low CVP means hypovolaemia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q52$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1164, $s$A nurse is caring for a post- CABG patient. While performing hemodynamic monitoring, he noted that Pulmonary Artery pressure is 31/18. The nurse would understand which of the following abnormality as the cause of the this PA pressure reading:$s$, $s$["Pulmonary valve stenosis", "Right sided heart failure", "Pulmonary infection", "Left side heart failure"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Left side heart failure
Normal pulmonary artery pressure is about 15–25 / 8–15 mmHg. A PA pressure of 31/18 is RAISED. After CABG, the commonest cause is LEFT SIDED HEART FAILURE: the weakened left ventricle can't pump effectively, so pressure backs up into the left atrium, the lung veins and the pulmonary artery (post-capillary pulmonary hypertension).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pulmonary valve stenosis — Pulmonary valve stenosis causes a pressure DROP across the valve, with low PA pressure.
• Right sided heart failure — Right heart failure alone doesn't raise PA pressure (the RV can't generate pressure).
• Pulmonary infection — A lung infection can raise PA pressure slightly, but LV failure is the most likely cause here.

⭐ Key point: Check the wedge pressure: a high PAWP confirms the left heart is the cause.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q53$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1165, $s$Malignant hypertension is defined as a medical emergency involving:$s$, $s$["Low blood pressure with organ damage", "Only diastolic pressure elevation", "Any blood pressure reading above normal", "SBP>180, DBP>120, plus organ damage"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: SBP>180, DBP>120, plus organ damage
MALIGNANT hypertension (a hypertensive emergency) is very high BP (typically systolic above 180 and/or diastolic above 120 mmHg) WITH acute ORGAN DAMAGE: the brain (encephalopathy, stroke), eyes (papilloedema, retinal haemorrhages), kidneys (AKI), and heart (pulmonary oedema, ACS).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low blood pressure with organ damage — Low BP with organ damage is shock.
• Only diastolic pressure elevation — It involves both systolic and diastolic pressure.
• Any blood pressure reading above normal — Any high reading without organ damage isn't malignant hypertension.

⭐ Key point: Lower BP gradually: by no more than 25% in the first hour, using IV drugs in the ICU.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q54$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1166, $s$A patient's ECG shows ST-segment depression in the anterior leads during an episode of chest pain, which resolves when the pain resolves. This pattern most likely represents:$s$, $s$["Normal variant with no clinical significance", "Myocardial ischaemia", "Old, resolved myocardial infarction with no current significance", "Ventricular hypertrophy only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Myocardial ischaemia
ST-segment DEPRESSION that appears DURING chest pain and goes away when the pain goes is the classic sign of reversible MYOCARDIAL ISCHAEMIA (lack of oxygen to the heart muscle, not yet an infarction), as in angina or NSTE-ACS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal variant with no clinical significance — It isn't a normal variant; it's linked to symptoms.
• Old, resolved myocardial infarction with no current significance — An old MI shows Q waves, not transient ST depression.
• Ventricular hypertrophy only — Ventricular hypertrophy can cause fixed ST changes (strain), not changes that come and go with pain.

⭐ Key point: Dynamic ST changes with chest pain need urgent assessment: troponin, aspirin, anticoagulation and cardiology review.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q56 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1167, $s$An arterial line trace becomes over-damped. The nurse expects:$s$, $s$["A correct systolic but zero diastolic reading", "An accurate trace but a slow heart rate display", "Falsely low systolic and falsely high diastolic readings", "Falsely high systolic and falsely low diastolic readings"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Falsely low systolic and falsely high diastolic readings
An OVER-DAMPED arterial line (from air bubbles, clots, kinks, soft tubing or a low flush bag) blunts the waveform. The SYSTOLIC reads FALSELY LOW and the DIASTOLIC reads FALSELY HIGH (the MAP is usually still roughly correct). The trace looks flattened, with a poor dicrotic notch.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A correct systolic but zero diastolic reading — A correct systolic with zero diastolic doesn't describe damping.
• An accurate trace but a slow heart rate display — An over-damped trace isn't accurate.
• Falsely high systolic and falsely low diastolic readings — Falsely high systolic and falsely low diastolic describes UNDER-damping.

⭐ Key point: Check damping with the fast-flush (square wave) test, then remove bubbles, flush and check the system.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q57$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1168, $s$The fast-flush (square wave) test on a pressure line checks:$s$, $s$["Whether the catheter tip is intravascular", "The correct zero level of the transducer", "The dynamic response (damping) of the system", "The patient's fluid responsiveness"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The dynamic response (damping) of the system
The fast-flush (square wave) test checks the DYNAMIC RESPONSE (damping) of the pressure monitoring system. Pull the flush valve briefly, release it, and watch the trace: an optimally damped system shows 1–2 oscillations before returning to the waveform. More oscillations = under-damped; a slow return with no oscillation = over-damped.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Whether the catheter tip is intravascular — It doesn't check whether the catheter is in the vessel.
• The correct zero level of the transducer — Zeroing is a separate procedure.
• The patient's fluid responsiveness — Fluid responsiveness is tested by PLR or PPV.

⭐ Key point: Do the square wave test at the start of each shift and whenever the waveform looks wrong.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q58$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1169, $s$If a CVP transducer is placed below the phlebostatic axis, the reading will be:$s$, $s$["Falsely high", "Unchanged", "Zero", "Falsely low"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Falsely high
The transducer must be at the level of the right atrium (the phlebostatic axis: 4th intercostal space, mid-axillary line). If it is placed BELOW this level, the column of fluid above it adds extra pressure, so the reading is FALSELY HIGH. If it is placed above, the reading is falsely low.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Unchanged — The reading will change.
• Zero — It won't read zero.
• Falsely low — Falsely low readings happen when the transducer is ABOVE the axis.

⭐ Key point: Every 1 cm difference in height changes the reading by about 0.74 mmHg. Re-level after every position change.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q59$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1170, $s$After cardiac surgery, chest drain output suddenly stops while CVP rises and BP falls with pulsus paradoxus. The nurse suspects:$s$, $s$["Hypovolaemia needing fluid restriction", "Right-sided pneumothorax only", "Recovery of normal clotting function", "Cardiac tamponade from clotted drains"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cardiac tamponade from clotted drains
After cardiac surgery, if chest drain output SUDDENLY STOPS (the drains are blocked by clot) while CVP RISES, BP FALLS and PULSUS PARADOXUS appears, blood is collecting around the heart and squeezing it: CARDIAC TAMPONADE. It is a life-threatening emergency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypovolaemia needing fluid restriction — Hypovolaemia would cause LOW CVP, and fluid restriction would be wrong.
• Right-sided pneumothorax only — A pneumothorax doesn't usually cause pulsus paradoxus with a rising CVP and blocked drains.
• Recovery of normal clotting function — Stopped drainage with collapse isn't recovery.

⭐ Key point: Milk or strip the drains as per policy, call the surgeon immediately, and prepare for re-sternotomy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q60$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1171, $s$The monophasic shock energy is used:$s$, $s$["100-200 J", "150-200 J", "200-300 J", "120-200 J"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 200-300 J
MONOPHASIC defibrillators send current in one direction and need HIGHER energy than biphasic machines. They usually deliver 200–360 J for defibrillation; this question's key uses 200–300 J. Many guidelines recommend 360 J for monophasic shocks in VF and pulseless VT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100-200 J — 100–200 J is lower, closer to biphasic energies.
• 150-200 J — 150–200 J is the typical BIPHASIC range.
• 120-200 J — 120–200 J is the typical BIPHASIC range.

⭐ Key point: Biphasic defibrillators are more effective at lower energy (150–200 J). Follow the manufacturer's recommended setting.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q114$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1172, $s$A woman with severe mitral stenosis and mitral regurgitation has a pulmonary artery catheter inserted. The physician orders pulmonary artery pressure monitoring, including pulmonary capillary wedge pressures. The purpose of this is to help assess the:$s$, $s$["Peripheral arterial pressure.", "Pressure from fluid within the left ventricle.", "Degree of coronary artery stenosis.", "Oxygen and carbon dioxide concentration is the blood."]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pressure from fluid within the left ventricle.
The pulmonary capillary wedge pressure (PCWP) reflects LEFT ATRIAL pressure, which (when the mitral valve is open in diastole) reflects LEFT VENTRICULAR filling pressure (preload). In mitral valve disease, it helps assess how much pressure is building up behind the valve and guides fluid and drug therapy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Peripheral arterial pressure. — Peripheral arterial pressure is measured by an arterial line.
• Degree of coronary artery stenosis. — Coronary artery narrowing is assessed by coronary angiography.
• Oxygen and carbon dioxide concentration is the blood. — Blood gases are measured by an ABG.

⭐ Key point: In mitral stenosis, PCWP overestimates LV filling, because pressure builds up behind the narrowed valve.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q41$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1173, $s$A patient has a PAWP of 22 mmHg, cardiac index of 1.8 L/min/m2, and elevated SVR. This hemodynamic profile is most consistent with:$s$, $s$["Cardiogenic shock", "Distributive shock", "Hypovolemic shock", "Normal hemodynamics"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cardiogenic shock
A HIGH wedge pressure (PAWP 22, normal 6–12) means blood is backing up behind a failing LEFT VENTRICLE. A LOW CARDIAC INDEX (1.8, normal 2.5–4.0) means the heart isn't pumping enough. HIGH SVR shows the vessels are clamping down to compensate. This is CARDIOGENIC SHOCK.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Distributive shock — Distributive (septic) shock has LOW SVR.
• Hypovolemic shock — Hypovolaemic shock has LOW PAWP.
• Normal hemodynamics — These values aren't normal.

⭐ Key point: Treat cardiogenic shock: inotropes, careful fluids, reperfusion (PCI for MI), mechanical support (IABP).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q42$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1174, $s$Which gas is used to inflate balloon during diastole in Intra-aortic Balloon pump (IABP) machine?$s$, $s$["Helium", "Chlorine", "Iodine", "Hydrogen"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Helium
The IABP balloon is inflated with HELIUM gas. Helium is very light (low density), so it moves in and out of the balloon very quickly, allowing rapid inflation and deflation with each heartbeat. It is also quickly absorbed into the blood if the balloon ruptures, reducing the risk of gas embolism.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chlorine — Chlorine is a toxic gas.
• Iodine — Iodine isn't a gas used in medical balloons.
• Hydrogen — Hydrogen is explosive and dangerous.

⭐ Key point: If blood appears in the IABP gas tubing, the balloon has ruptured: stop the pump and inform the doctor immediately.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q45$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1175, $s$Which of the following is NOT a reversible cause of Cardiac Arrest?$s$, $s$["Hypoxia", "Hypovolemia", "Tension pneumothorax", "Hypertension"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypertension
The reversible causes of cardiac arrest are the 4 Hs and 4 Ts. 4 Hs: HYPOXIA, HYPOVOLAEMIA, HYPO/HYPERKALAEMIA (and other metabolic causes), HYPOTHERMIA. 4 Ts: TENSION PNEUMOTHORAX, TAMPONADE, TOXINS, THROMBOSIS (coronary or pulmonary). HYPERTENSION is NOT one of them.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypoxia — Hypoxia IS a reversible cause.
• Hypovolemia — Hypovolaemia IS a reversible cause.
• Tension pneumothorax — Tension pneumothorax IS a reversible cause.

⭐ Key point: During CPR, actively look for and treat the 4 Hs and 4 Ts.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q50$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1176, $s$Rapid deflation of Balloon of IABP will decrease:$s$, $s$["Contraction", "Pre-load", "Heart Rate", "After Load"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: After Load
The IABP DEFLATES rapidly just before the heart contracts (systole). This suddenly lowers the pressure in the aorta, so the left ventricle pushes blood out against less resistance: AFTERLOAD is REDUCED. This lowers the heart's oxygen needs and improves cardiac output.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Contraction — Deflation doesn't decrease contraction.
• Pre-load — Deflation doesn't mainly affect preload.
• Heart Rate — Deflation doesn't directly change heart rate.

⭐ Key point: IABP: inflate in diastole (improves coronary flow), deflate before systole (reduces afterload).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q52$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1177, $s$Elective surgery is generally considered for cancellation when blood pressure exceeds:$s$, $s$["SBP >120 AND DBP >80", "SBP >180 AND DBP >110", "Any blood pressure above normal", "SBP >100 alone"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: SBP >180 AND DBP >110
Elective (non-urgent) surgery is often postponed if the BP is very high, commonly a SYSTOLIC above 180 AND a DIASTOLIC above 110 mmHg (or systolic 200 or more alone). Very high BP increases the risk of stroke, MI, bleeding and BP swings during anaesthesia. The BP is controlled first.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• SBP >120 AND DBP >80 — 120/80 is normal BP.
• Any blood pressure above normal — Any BP above normal doesn't justify cancellation.
• SBP >100 alone — A systolic above 100 alone is normal.

⭐ Key point: Emergency surgery isn't delayed for high BP; it is managed during the operation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q53$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1178, $s$With an intra-aortic balloon pump, the balloon should inflate at:$s$, $s$["The upstroke of the arterial waveform", "The peak of the R wave on the ECG", "The start of atrial contraction (P wave)", "The dicrotic notch (aortic valve closure)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The dicrotic notch (aortic valve closure)
The IABP balloon should INFLATE at the DICROTIC NOTCH on the arterial waveform, which marks AORTIC VALVE CLOSURE (the start of diastole). Inflating then pushes blood back into the coronary arteries, improving heart muscle blood flow. It deflates just before the next systole.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The upstroke of the arterial waveform — Inflating on the upstroke (systole) would block the heart's ejection, which is dangerous.
• The peak of the R wave on the ECG — Inflating at the peak of the R wave is wrong timing (the R wave triggers deflation timing).
• The start of atrial contraction (P wave) — Inflating at the P wave is wrong timing.

⭐ Key point: Wrong timing harms the heart. Early inflation raises afterload; late deflation increases cardiac work.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q57$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1179, $s$ST depression in V1–V3 with tall R waves in a patient with chest pain suggests:$s$, $s$["Anterior wall ischaemia only", "Normal early repolarisation", "Posterior MI; record leads V7–V9", "Right bundle branch block only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Posterior MI; record leads V7–V9
ST DEPRESSION in V1–V3 with TALL R WAVES in a patient with chest pain suggests a POSTERIOR MI. The front leads see the back of the heart "in reverse". Record posterior leads (V7–V9): ST elevation there confirms a posterior STEMI, which needs urgent reperfusion (PCI).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anterior wall ischaemia only — Anterior ischaemia doesn't explain the tall R waves.
• Normal early repolarisation — Early repolarisation causes ST elevation, not depression.
• Right bundle branch block only — Right bundle branch block causes a wide QRS with an RSR' pattern.

⭐ Key point: A posterior MI is often missed. Think of it when there is ST depression in V1–V3.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q58$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1180, $s$Beck's triad of cardiac tamponade is:$s$, $s$["Hypotension, raised neck veins and muffled heart sounds", "Hypertension, bradycardia and irregular breathing", "Chest pain, ST elevation and raised troponin", "Fever, new murmur and splinter haemorrhages"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypotension, raised neck veins and muffled heart sounds
BECK'S TRIAD of cardiac tamponade: 1) HYPOTENSION (the squeezed heart can't fill and pump), 2) RAISED neck veins (JVP; blood can't enter the heart), 3) MUFFLED heart sounds (fluid around the heart). Pulsus paradoxus is another sign.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension, bradycardia and irregular breathing — Hypertension, bradycardia and irregular breathing is Cushing's triad (raised ICP).
• Chest pain, ST elevation and raised troponin — Chest pain, ST elevation and raised troponin describe MI.
• Fever, new murmur and splinter haemorrhages — Fever, a new murmur and splinter haemorrhages describe infective endocarditis.

⭐ Key point: Treat tamponade urgently with pericardiocentesis or surgical drainage.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q60 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1181, $s$A patient with a high spinal cord injury develops hypotension WITH bradycardia (rather than the expected compensatory tachycardia). This is most consistent with:$s$, $s$["Hypovolaemic shock", "Cardiogenic shock", "Septic shock", "Neurogenic shock"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Neurogenic shock
A high spinal cord injury (above T6) cuts the sympathetic nerves that control the blood vessels and heart. The vessels DILATE (low BP), and the unopposed vagus nerve SLOWS the heart. So the patient has HYPOTENSION with BRADYCARDIA (instead of the usual fast pulse in shock), and warm, dry skin: NEUROGENIC SHOCK.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypovolaemic shock — Hypovolaemic shock causes a FAST pulse and cold skin.
• Cardiogenic shock — Cardiogenic shock usually causes a fast pulse with signs of heart failure.
• Septic shock — Septic shock usually causes a fast pulse.

⭐ Key point: In trauma, always look for bleeding too, as both can coexist. Treat neurogenic shock with fluids, vasopressors and atropine.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q101$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1182, $s$A prolonged capillary refill time (greater than 2–3 seconds) in a shocked patient suggests:$s$, $s$["Adequate peripheral perfusion", "Only a cold ambient room temperature, never clinical significance", "Improved cardiac output", "Reduced peripheral perfusion"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reduced peripheral perfusion
A capillary refill time longer than 2–3 seconds means the blood is slow to return to the skin after pressure. This shows REDUCED PERIPHERAL PERFUSION from vasoconstriction (the body diverting blood to vital organs in shock) or low cardiac output.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adequate peripheral perfusion — A prolonged refill means POOR, not adequate, perfusion.
• Only a cold ambient room temperature, never clinical significance — A cold room can prolong it, but in a shocked patient it is clinically significant.
• Improved cardiac output — It suggests REDUCED, not improved, cardiac output.

⭐ Key point: Check capillary refill on the sternum or fingertip (press for 5 seconds). Use it together with other signs of perfusion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q111$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1183, $s$A 30 years old male admitted to ICU with RTA (road traffic accident), she is unconscious and is on ventilator with Tidal volume=550 and FiO2=90%. She has CVP line inserted because of failure to insert I/V cannula, IV bolus of 1 litre and blood was infused. You are measuring his CVP which is 12cm of H2O, what does this indicate?$s$, $s$["Normal volume", "Hypovolemia", "Mixed state", "Hypervolemia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypervolemia
Normal CVP is about 3–10 cmH₂O (or 2–8 mmHg). A CVP of 12 cmH₂O (about 9 mmHg) after 1 litre of fluid and blood is above the normal range and, in this question, indicates HYPERVOLAEMIA (volume overload). The trend after a fluid challenge is more useful than a single reading.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal volume — Normal volume would be within 3–10 cmH₂O.
• Hypovolemia — Hypovolaemia would give a LOW CVP.
• Mixed state — "Mixed state" isn't a recognised interpretation.

⭐ Key point: Always note whether the CVP is in mmHg or cmH₂O (1 mmHg ≈ 1.36 cmH₂O).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q113$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1184, $s$Compared with the other agents listed, which is most likely to cause direct, dose-dependent myocardial contractile depression rather than predominantly vasodilation?$s$, $s$["Isoflurane", "Halothane", "Desflurane", "Sevoflurane at low concentrations"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Halothane
HALOTHANE directly DEPRESSES heart muscle CONTRACTILITY in a dose-dependent way (a negative inotropic effect), lowering cardiac output and BP. Newer agents (isoflurane, desflurane, sevoflurane) lower BP mainly by VASODILATION, with less direct heart depression.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Isoflurane — Isoflurane mainly causes vasodilation.
• Desflurane — Desflurane mainly causes vasodilation (and can raise heart rate).
• Sevoflurane at low concentrations — Sevoflurane at low doses causes mild vasodilation with little heart depression.

⭐ Key point: Halothane is the classic answer for myocardial depression, arrhythmias with adrenaline, and hepatitis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q151$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1185, $s$In shockable cardiac arrest, the first dose of amiodarone is:$s$, $s$["50 mg IV after every shock", "900 mg IV after the first shock", "300 mg IV after the third shock", "150 mg IV before the first shock"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 300 mg IV after the third shock
In shockable cardiac arrest (VF or pulseless VT), give AMIODARONE 300 mg IV AFTER the THIRD shock. A further 150 mg may be given after the fifth shock. It helps stabilise the heart rhythm and improve the chance of successful defibrillation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50 mg IV after every shock — 50 mg after every shock isn't the regimen.
• 900 mg IV after the first shock — 900 mg after the first shock is far too much and too early.
• 150 mg IV before the first shock — 150 mg before the first shock is the wrong dose and timing.

⭐ Key point: Give adrenaline 1 mg after the third shock as well, then every 3–5 minutes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q159$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1186, $s$A nurse assesses a client's exercise habits, activities of daily living, and any factors that interfere with the client's desired activity level (such as dyspnea or chest pain). This corresponds to which Gordon functional health pattern?$s$, $s$["Activity-exercise pattern", "Self-perception-self-concept pattern", "Nutritional-metabolic pattern", "Sleep-rest pattern"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Activity-exercise pattern
Gordon's ACTIVITY-EXERCISE pattern assesses the patient's exercise habits, activities of daily living (ADLs), leisure activities, and factors that limit activity (breathlessness, chest pain, fatigue, weakness).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Self-perception-self-concept pattern — The self-perception–self-concept pattern assesses how the patient views themselves.
• Nutritional-metabolic pattern — The nutritional-metabolic pattern assesses diet, fluid and metabolism.
• Sleep-rest pattern — The sleep-rest pattern assesses sleep and rest.

⭐ Key point: Gordon's 11 functional health patterns give a complete, holistic nursing assessment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q182$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B3 · Cardiovascular Care, Haemodynamics & Shock$s$, false, 1187, $s$The assessment approach in which the cardiovascular system is assessed before the genito-urinary system is the:$s$, $s$["Systematic approach", "Cephalocaudal approach", "Body systems approach", "Approach by order of priority"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Body systems approach
The BODY SYSTEMS approach assesses one body SYSTEM at a time (cardiovascular, respiratory, neurological, gastrointestinal, genitourinary and so on). Assessing the cardiovascular system before the genitourinary system follows this approach.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Systematic approach — "Systematic approach" is general and doesn't specify the order by system.
• Cephalocaudal approach — The cephalocaudal (head-to-toe) approach examines from head down, by body region.
• Approach by order of priority — The order-of-priority approach assesses the most urgent problems first (ABCDE).

⭐ Key point: ICU assessment often combines ABCDE (priority) with a systems review (body systems).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q191$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, true, 1188, $s$How is a tension pneumothorax causing PEA arrest managed?$s$, $s$["Defibrillation", "IV fluids alone", "Atropine administration", "Immediate needle decompression followed by chest tube insertion"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Immediate needle decompression followed by chest tube insertion
Tension pneumothorax causes PEA by trapping air that crushes the lung and pushes the heart and great veins aside, so blood cannot return to the heart. It is one of the reversible "4 Ts". The cause must be relieved IMMEDIATELY with needle decompression (2nd ICS mid-clavicular line, or 4th–5th ICS mid-axillary line in adults), followed by a chest drain. Continue CPR while this is done.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Defibrillation — Defibrillation does not work for PEA; it is only for VF and pulseless VT.
• IV fluids alone — IV fluids alone cannot overcome the blockage to blood return.
• Atropine administration — Atropine is no longer recommended for PEA and does not treat the cause.

⭐ Key point: The reversible causes of cardiac arrest are the 4 Hs (Hypoxia, Hypovolaemia, Hypo/Hyperkalaemia, Hypothermia) and the 4 Ts (Tension pneumothorax, Tamponade, Toxins, Thrombosis).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q57$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, true, 1189, $s$One of these is an indication for a permanent pacemaker$s$, $s$["Tachyarrthymias", "Carotid sinus syndrome", "Complete heart block", "Myocardial infarction"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Complete heart block
Complete (third-degree) heart block means NO impulses pass from the atria to the ventricles. The ventricles beat on their own slow escape rhythm (often 30–40/min), causing dizziness, fainting (Stokes–Adams attacks), heart failure or sudden death. Because it rarely recovers (except after an inferior MI or from drugs), a PERMANENT pacemaker is the definitive treatment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tachyarrthymias — Tachyarrhythmias are usually treated with drugs, ablation or an ICD, not a standard pacemaker.
• Carotid sinus syndrome — Carotid sinus syndrome can need a pacemaker in severe cases, but complete heart block is the classic, definite indication.
• Myocardial infarction — An MI alone is not an indication; temporary pacing may be needed for some blocks after MI.

⭐ Key point: Indications for a permanent pacemaker: complete heart block, Mobitz II block, symptomatic sick sinus syndrome, and symptomatic bradycardia without a reversible cause.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q69$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, true, 1190, $s$a 62 years old male patient has an ECG done that shows PR interval to be fixed, but drop QRS randomly. It indiciates?$s$, $s$["first degree block", "second degree type-I block", "second degree type-II block", "third degree block"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: second degree type-II block
Second-degree TYPE II (Mobitz II) heart block: the PR interval stays the SAME (fixed) for each conducted beat, but suddenly a P wave is NOT followed by a QRS (a dropped beat), without warning. The block is usually below the AV node (in the His–Purkinje system), so it can suddenly progress to complete heart block. It needs pacing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• first degree block — First-degree block has a long PR (above 0.20 s) but NO dropped beats.
• second degree type-I block — Second-degree type I (Wenckebach) has a PR that gets LONGER and LONGER until a beat drops, then the cycle repeats.
• third degree block — Third-degree block has NO relationship between P waves and QRS complexes (they march independently).

⭐ Key point: Wenckebach = "longer, longer, longer, drop" (usually benign). Mobitz II = "fixed PR, sudden drop" (dangerous; prepare for pacing).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q70$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, true, 1191, $s$A patient with a permanent pacemaker shows a rhythm strip with pacer spikes not followed by a QRS complex. This indicates:$s$, $s$["Normal pacemaker function", "Failure to capture — the pacemaker impulse is not depolarising the myocardium", "Battery is fully charged with no issue", "Over-sensing with no clinical relevance"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Failure to capture — the pacemaker impulse is not depolarising the myocardium
Each pacing spike should be immediately followed by a QRS complex (ventricular pacing) or a P wave (atrial pacing). This means the impulse has "captured" the heart. A spike with NO QRS after it is FAILURE TO CAPTURE: the electrical output is not making the heart muscle contract. Causes: lead displacement, a lead fracture, energy output too low, scar tissue at the lead tip, low battery, high potassium, or drugs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal pacemaker function — Normal function means every spike is followed by a complex.
• Battery is fully charged with no issue — A battery problem is one possible CAUSE of failure to capture; the strip does not show a healthy battery.
• Over-sensing with no clinical relevance — Over-sensing means the pacemaker detects signals it shouldn't and fails to pace (no spikes when needed); it is clinically important.

⭐ Key point: Pacemaker problems: failure to PACE (no spike), failure to CAPTURE (spike with no complex), and failure to SENSE (spikes in the wrong place). Check the patient's pulse and BP first, then call for help.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q71$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, true, 1192, $s$What is the currently recommended target temperature range for targeted temperature management after cardiac arrest?$s$, $s$["40 to 42 degrees C", "20 to 25 degrees C", "32 to 36 degrees C", "38 to 39 degrees C"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 32 to 36 degrees C
After return of circulation following cardiac arrest, patients who remain unconscious are kept at a controlled temperature to protect the brain. Current guidance is a target between 32 and 36 °C for at least 24 hours, and above all to AVOID FEVER (above 37.7 °C) for at least 72 hours. Fever after cardiac arrest worsens brain damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 40 to 42 degrees C — 40–42 °C is hyperthermia, which harms the brain.
• 20 to 25 degrees C — 20–25 °C is severe hypothermia and causes arrhythmias and cardiac arrest.
• 38 to 39 degrees C — 38–39 °C is fever, which is exactly what should be prevented.

⭐ Key point: During cooling, watch for shivering (treat with sedation or paralysis), bradycardia, low potassium (which rises on rewarming), high glucose, bleeding and infection. Rewarm slowly, at about 0.25–0.5 °C per hour.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q110$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, true, 1193, $s$CPR may be stopped in all of the following situations EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Return of spontaneous circulation", "A qualified person takes over", "The rescuer feels the situation is hopeless", "A physician pronounces death"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The rescuer feels the situation is hopeless
Once CPR has started it should continue until one of these happens: (1) RETURN OF SPONTANEOUS CIRCULATION (ROSC) — the patient has a pulse; (2) a QUALIFIED person (or team, e.g. ambulance/resuscitation team) TAKES OVER; (3) a PHYSICIAN or authorised person PRONOUNCES DEATH or stops resuscitation; (4) the rescuer is physically EXHAUSTED and cannot go on, or the scene becomes UNSAFE; or (5) a valid DNACPR order is presented. Simply FEELING that the situation is hopeless is NOT a valid reason for the rescuer to stop — that is a personal emotional judgement, not a clinical decision. Survival is possible even after prolonged CPR, e.g. in hypothermia or drowning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Return of spontaneous circulation — ROSC IS a reason to stop compressions and start post-resuscitation care.
• A qualified person takes over — A qualified person TAKING OVER IS a reason to stop (they continue CPR).
• A physician pronounces death — A physician PRONOUNCING DEATH IS a reason to stop.

⭐ Key point: Stop CPR for ROSC, handover, death pronounced, exhaustion, unsafe scene or valid DNACPR — NOT because you "feel" it is hopeless.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q67$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, true, 1194, $s$How often should rescuers performing chest compressions switch during ongoing CPR?$s$, $s$["Every 15 minutes", "Every 30 seconds", "Rescuers should never switch during an arrest", "About every 2 minutes, to prevent fatigue and maintain compression quality"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: About every 2 minutes, to prevent fatigue and maintain compression quality
Good-quality chest compressions need: rate 100–120/min, depth 5–6 cm in adults, full chest recoil, minimal interruptions. Compressions are tiring, and quality (especially depth) falls after about 1–2 minutes, often without the rescuer noticing. So guidelines (AHA/ERC) recommend SWITCHING THE COMPRESSOR ABOUT EVERY 2 MINUTES — usually at the rhythm check — with the change taking less than 5–10 seconds. Have the next compressor ready and standing by.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Every 15 minutes — Every 15 minutes is far too long; fatigue would reduce compression quality long before then.
• Every 30 seconds — Every 30 seconds is too frequent; constant switching causes too many interruptions.
• Rescuers should never switch during an arrest — Never switching is wrong; it leads to tired, shallow compressions and lower survival.

⭐ Key point: Switch compressors about EVERY 2 MINUTES (at rhythm checks) with minimal pause, to prevent fatigue.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q105$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, true, 1195, $s$Amiodarone is primarily indicated in cardiac arrest management for:$s$, $s$["Asystole only", "Shockable rhythms (VF/pulseless VT) refractory to defibrillation", "Bradycardia", "Hypertensive emergency"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Shockable rhythms (VF/pulseless VT) refractory to defibrillation
In CARDIAC ARREST, AMIODARONE is used for SHOCKABLE RHYTHMS — VENTRICULAR FIBRILLATION (VF) and PULSELESS VENTRICULAR TACHYCARDIA (pVT) — that persist after defibrillation (refractory). The dose is 300 mg IV after the third shock, then 150 mg after the fifth shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Asystole only — Asystole is a non-shockable rhythm; amiodarone is not indicated.
• Bradycardia — Bradycardia is treated with atropine, pacing, or adrenaline infusion.
• Hypertensive emergency — Hypertensive emergency is treated with antihypertensives.

⭐ Key point: High-quality CPR and early defibrillation are the most important treatments in VF/pVT.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q35$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, true, 1196, $s$Which ECG change is classically associated with hypercalcemia?$s$, $s$["Shortened QT interval", "Prolonged QT interval", "Peaked T waves", "Prominent U waves"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Shortened QT interval
CALCIUM affects how quickly the heart muscle repolarises (recovers) after each beat. HYPERCALCAEMIA (high calcium) SPEEDS UP repolarisation, so the ST segment and QT interval become SHORT — a SHORTENED QT INTERVAL is the classic ECG sign. Very high calcium can also cause bradycardia, heart block and arrhythmias. (HYPOcalcaemia does the opposite — it PROLONGS the QT.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prolonged QT interval — A PROLONGED QT is seen with HYPOcalcaemia, hypokalaemia, hypomagnesaemia and many drugs.
• Peaked T waves — PEAKED T waves are a sign of HYPERkalaemia.
• Prominent U waves — Prominent U waves are a sign of HYPOkalaemia.

⭐ Key point: Common causes of hypercalcaemia in ICU: cancer (bone metastases, myeloma), hyperparathyroidism, immobilisation and thiazide diuretics. Treat with IV fluids (0.9% saline) and bisphosphonates.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q2$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, true, 1197, $s$A first-degree heart block on ECG is characterised by:$s$, $s$["Complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes", "A prolonged PR interval with every P wave still followed by a QRS complex", "Absent P waves entirely", "A widened QRS with no P waves"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A prolonged PR interval with every P wave still followed by a QRS complex
In FIRST-DEGREE HEART BLOCK, electrical impulses travel through the AV node more SLOWLY than normal, so the PR INTERVAL is PROLONGED (more than 0.20 seconds — five small squares). However, EVERY P WAVE is still FOLLOWED by a QRS complex — no beats are dropped. It is usually harmless and often seen with drugs (beta-blockers, digoxin, calcium channel blockers), high vagal tone, or inferior MI.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes — Complete dissociation between P waves and QRS complexes is THIRD-DEGREE (complete) heart block.
• Absent P waves entirely — Absent P waves are seen in atrial fibrillation or junctional rhythms.
• A widened QRS with no P waves — A wide QRS with no P waves suggests a ventricular rhythm.

⭐ Key point: Second-degree block = some P waves not followed by QRS (Mobitz I with progressive PR lengthening; Mobitz II with constant PR). Mobitz II and third-degree blocks are dangerous and may need pacing.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q73$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, true, 1198, $s$A patient's ECG shows ST-segment depression in the anterior leads during an episode of chest pain, which resolves when the pain resolves. This pattern most likely represents:$s$, $s$["Normal variant with no clinical significance", "Old, resolved myocardial infarction with no current significance", "Ventricular hypertrophy only", "Myocardial ischaemia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Myocardial ischaemia
ST-SEGMENT DEPRESSION during chest pain that RESOLVES when the pain goes away indicates MYOCARDIAL ISCHAEMIA — the heart muscle is temporarily short of oxygen (e.g. unstable angina or demand ischaemia). It is a warning sign of coronary artery disease and possible impending MI.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal variant with no clinical significance — It is not a normal variant.
• Old, resolved myocardial infarction with no current significance — An old MI shows Q waves, not transient ST depression with pain.
• Ventricular hypertrophy only — Ventricular hypertrophy causes persistent changes, not transient changes with pain.

⭐ Key point: Treat as acute coronary syndrome: aspirin, anticoagulation, nitrates, troponin testing, and cardiology review.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q175 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, true, 1199, $s$A 22-year-old has palpitations with an irregular, very fast (rate 260) broad-complex tachycardia whose QRS shape varies from beat to beat. A previous ECG showed a short PR interval and a slurred upstroke (delta wave). Which drug is CONTRAINDICATED?$s$, $s$["Flecainide", "Verapamil", "Synchronised DC cardioversion", "Procainamide"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Verapamil
This patient has WOLFF-PARKINSON-WHITE (WPW) syndrome (short PR, delta wave) with ATRIAL FIBRILLATION conducted down the ACCESSORY PATHWAY, causing an irregular, very fast, BROAD-COMPLEX tachycardia with varying QRS shapes. AV nodal blocking drugs such as VERAPAMIL (and adenosine, digoxin, beta-blockers) are CONTRAINDICATED, because blocking the AV node forces MORE impulses down the accessory pathway, which can cause VENTRICULAR FIBRILLATION and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flecainide — Flecainide slows conduction in the accessory pathway; it can be used in stable patients.
• Synchronised DC cardioversion — Synchronised DC cardioversion is the treatment of choice if the patient is unstable.
• Procainamide — Procainamide slows conduction in the accessory pathway; it can be used.

⭐ Key point: In WPW with AF, avoid AV nodal blockers ("ABCD": Adenosine, Beta-blockers, Calcium channel blockers, Digoxin).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q49$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, true, 1200, $s$A patient with hypomagnesaemia who is taking a QT-prolonging drug develops a polymorphic ventricular tachycardia whose QRS axis appears to twist around the baseline. A pulse is present. The first-line drug is:$s$, $s$["IV magnesium sulphate 2 g", "IV adenosine 6 mg", "IV amiodarone 300 mg", "IV lidocaine"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV magnesium sulphate 2 g
This is TORSADES DE POINTES ("twisting of the points") — a polymorphic VT where the QRS axis twists around the baseline. It happens with a LONG QT interval, often due to HYPOMAGNESAEMIA, hypokalaemia, or QT-PROLONGING DRUGS (e.g. some antibiotics, antipsychotics, antiarrhythmics). The first-line treatment is IV MAGNESIUM SULPHATE 2 g (8 mmol) over 10–15 minutes, which stabilises the heart muscle even if magnesium levels are normal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV adenosine 6 mg — Adenosine is for SVT; it does not treat torsades.
• IV amiodarone 300 mg — Amiodarone PROLONGS the QT interval and can worsen torsades.
• IV lidocaine — Lidocaine is not first-line for torsades.

⭐ Key point: Stop QT-prolonging drugs, correct potassium (aim above 4.5 mmol/L), and use overdrive pacing or isoprenaline if torsades recurs. If pulseless, defibrillate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q50$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, true, 1201, $s$Which ECG finding, though neither sensitive nor specific, is classically associated with acute PE and right heart strain?$s$, $s$["Delta wave", "S1Q3T3 pattern", "Prolonged QT interval", "U waves"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: S1Q3T3 pattern
The S1Q3T3 pattern (a deep S wave in lead I, a Q wave and inverted T wave in lead III) reflects RIGHT HEART STRAIN from a large pulmonary embolism. It is classically associated with PE, but it is neither sensitive nor specific. The commonest ECG finding in PE is sinus tachycardia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delta wave — A delta wave is seen in Wolff–Parkinson–White syndrome.
• Prolonged QT interval — A prolonged QT is seen with drugs and electrolyte problems.
• U waves — U waves are seen in hypokalaemia.

⭐ Key point: Other PE ECG findings: sinus tachycardia, right bundle branch block, right axis deviation, T inversion in V1–V4.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q44$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1202, $s$A key nursing precaution when administering IV potassium chloride is to:$s$, $s$["Give it as a rapid IV push for fast correction", "Never give it as an undiluted rapid IV push, due to risk of fatal arrhythmia", "Administer it intramuscularly for safety", "Mix it with any other IV drug freely"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Never give it as an undiluted rapid IV push, due to risk of fatal arrhythmia
IV POTASSIUM CHLORIDE (KCl) is a HIGH-ALERT medication. Given as an UNDILUTED RAPID IV PUSH, it causes a sudden high potassium level in the heart, leading to FATAL ARRHYTHMIAS and CARDIAC ARREST. It must ALWAYS be DILUTED, given by INFUSION PUMP, at a controlled rate (usually no more than 10 mmol/hr peripherally, up to 20 mmol/hr centrally with ECG monitoring).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give it as a rapid IV push for fast correction — Rapid IV push is never acceptable.
• Administer it intramuscularly for safety — IM potassium is not used.
• Mix it with any other IV drug freely — KCl should not be mixed freely with other drugs.

⭐ Key point: Concentrated KCl ampoules should be stored securely and separately in clinical areas to prevent fatal errors.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q30$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1203, $s$A patient comes with severe chest pain, radiating to jaw and shoulder. His ECG shows ST elevations in lead II, lead III and lead AVF. What does it show?$s$, $s$["Posterior MI", "Anterior MI", "Septal MI", "Inferior MI"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inferior MI
ST ELEVATION in leads II, III and aVF (the INFERIOR leads, which look at the bottom wall of the heart) indicates an INFERIOR MYOCARDIAL INFARCTION. It is usually caused by blockage of the RIGHT CORONARY ARTERY (in about 80–90% of people). Reciprocal ST depression may be seen in leads I and aVL. Inferior MI can be complicated by bradycardia and heart block (the RCA supplies the SA and AV nodes) and RIGHT VENTRICULAR infarction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Posterior MI — Posterior MI shows ST DEPRESSION with tall R waves in V1–V3 (and ST elevation in V7–V9).
• Anterior MI — Anterior MI shows ST elevation in V1–V4 (LAD artery).
• Septal MI — Septal MI shows ST elevation in V1–V2.

⭐ Key point: In inferior MI, record right-sided leads (V4R) — if RV infarction is present, avoid nitrates and give fluids for hypotension.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q65$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1204, $s$Concerning the ECG, all of the following are incorrect EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["A waveform moving toward the top of the paper is a negative deflection", "A waveform moving toward the bottom of the paper is a positive deflection", "The ECG waveform shows the heart's mechanical pumping force", "Time and rate are measured on the horizontal axis and voltage on the vertical axis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Time and rate are measured on the horizontal axis and voltage on the vertical axis
On an ECG, TIME (and rate) is measured on the HORIZONTAL axis (each small square = 0.04 seconds at standard speed) and VOLTAGE (amplitude) on the VERTICAL axis (each small square = 0.1 mV). This statement is CORRECT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A waveform moving toward the top of the paper is a negative deflection — A waveform moving upwards is POSITIVE, not negative.
• A waveform moving toward the bottom of the paper is a positive deflection — A waveform moving downwards is NEGATIVE, not positive.
• The ECG waveform shows the heart's mechanical pumping force — The ECG shows ELECTRICAL activity, not mechanical pumping.

⭐ Key point: Pulseless electrical activity (PEA) shows that electrical activity can occur without effective pumping.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q68$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1205, $s$For an unconscious choking adult, the correct approach is to:$s$, $s$["Start CPR, checking the mouth before each breath", "Wait for the ambulance without doing anything", "Perform abdominal thrusts only", "Place immediately in recovery position"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Start CPR, checking the mouth before each breath
For an UNCONSCIOUS CHOKING ADULT, call for help and START CPR immediately (30 compressions : 2 breaths). Chest compressions raise the pressure inside the chest and may dislodge the obstruction. BEFORE EACH ATTEMPT AT BREATHS, LOOK IN THE MOUTH and remove any VISIBLE object with your fingers (no blind finger sweeps). Continue until the object is cleared and the patient breathes, or help arrives.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wait for the ambulance without doing anything — Waiting for the ambulance without action allows brain damage and death from hypoxia.
• Perform abdominal thrusts only — Abdominal thrusts (Heimlich manoeuvre) are for CONSCIOUS choking adults; once unconscious, CPR is started.
• Place immediately in recovery position — The recovery position does nothing to clear a blocked airway.

⭐ Key point: For a CONSCIOUS choking adult: encourage coughing; if ineffective, give up to 5 back blows then up to 5 abdominal thrusts, repeating.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q69$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1206, $s$A condition characterised by a prolonged PR interval more than 0.2 secs in ECG is called:$s$, $s$["Second degree AV block", "Mobiz Type II AV block", "First degree Av block", "Complete block"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: First degree Av block
FIRST-DEGREE AV BLOCK is defined by a PROLONGED PR INTERVAL of MORE THAN 0.20 SECONDS (more than 5 small squares on the ECG). Every P wave is still followed by a QRS (no dropped beats); conduction through the AV node is simply delayed. It is usually benign, often caused by drugs (beta-blockers, digoxin, calcium channel blockers), high vagal tone, or inferior MI.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Second degree AV block — Second-degree AV block has some P waves NOT followed by a QRS.
• Mobiz Type II AV block — Mobitz type II is a form of second-degree block with dropped beats and a constant PR.
• Complete block — Complete (third-degree) block has NO relationship between P waves and QRS complexes.

⭐ Key point: Normal PR interval: 0.12–0.20 seconds (3–5 small squares).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q75$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1207, $s$On an ECG, the P wave reflects atrial depolarisation initiated by the:$s$, $s$["Left bundle branch", "AV node", "Bachmann's bundle", "SA node"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: SA node
The SINOATRIAL (SA) NODE, in the wall of the right atrium, is the heart's natural PACEMAKER. It generates electrical impulses (normally 60–100 per minute) that spread across both atria, causing ATRIAL DEPOLARISATION — seen as the P WAVE on the ECG — followed by atrial contraction. The impulse then travels to the AV node.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Left bundle branch — The left bundle branch conducts impulses through the ventricles (part of the QRS).
• AV node — The AV node delays the impulse between atria and ventricles (the PR segment).
• Bachmann's bundle — Bachmann's bundle carries the impulse from the right to left atrium, but does not INITIATE it.

⭐ Key point: In sinus rhythm, every QRS is preceded by an upright P wave in lead II.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q76$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1208, $s$Early ECG changes of hyperkalaemia typically include:$s$, $s$["Flattened T waves", "Tall, peaked T waves", "Prolonged QT interval as the earliest change", "U waves"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tall, peaked T waves
HYPERKALAEMIA affects heart conduction. EARLY ECG changes: TALL, PEAKED (tented) T WAVES. As potassium rises further: flattened or absent P waves, prolonged PR interval, WIDE QRS complexes, a "sine wave" pattern, and finally ventricular fibrillation or asystole.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flattened T waves — Flattened T waves are seen in HYPOkalaemia.
• Prolonged QT interval as the earliest change — Prolonged QT is not the earliest change of hyperkalaemia (it is seen with hypocalcaemia, hypokalaemia).
• U waves — U waves are seen in HYPOkalaemia.

⭐ Key point: Treat severe hyperkalaemia: calcium gluconate (protect the heart), insulin-dextrose and salbutamol (shift potassium into cells), then remove potassium (binders, dialysis).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q25$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1209, $s$A patient with Torsades de Pointes is prescribed magnesium sulfate. Which ECG change confirms efficacy?$s$, $s$["Resolution of U waves", "Narrowed QRS complex", "Shortened QT interval", "Absent P waves"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Shortened QT interval
TORSADES DE POINTES is a polymorphic VT associated with a PROLONGED QT interval. MAGNESIUM SULPHATE stabilises the heart cell membranes and suppresses the early after-depolarisations that trigger torsades. Its effectiveness is confirmed when the arrhythmia stops and the QT INTERVAL SHORTENS (returns towards normal), reducing the risk of recurrence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Resolution of U waves — U waves relate to potassium, not the main target.
• Narrowed QRS complex — Narrowed QRS relates to sodium channel blockade (e.g. TCA overdose).
• Absent P waves — Absent P waves are seen in AF or hyperkalaemia.

⭐ Key point: Also correct potassium (keep above 4.5 mmol/L) and stop QT-prolonging drugs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q31$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1210, $s$An ETCO₂ reading below what value after 20 minutes of CPR may prompt consideration of stopping resuscitation?$s$, $s$["<10mmHg", "<25mmHg", "<40mmHg", "<60mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: <10mmHg
END-TIDAL CO2 (ETCO2) during CPR reflects the amount of blood the chest compressions are pushing through the lungs (cardiac output). Good-quality CPR usually produces an ETCO2 of at least 10–20 mmHg. An ETCO2 persistently BELOW 10 mmHg after about 20 MINUTES of good-quality CPR (in an intubated patient) suggests a very low chance of return of spontaneous circulation (ROSC), and may be one factor in the team's decision to stop resuscitation. It should never be used alone.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• <25mmHg — 25 mmHg suggests reasonable blood flow during CPR — not a reason to stop.
• <40mmHg — 40 mmHg is a normal value (often seen after ROSC).
• <60mmHg — 60 mmHg is high.

⭐ Key point: A sudden rise in ETCO2 (e.g. to 35–40 mmHg) during CPR is often the first sign of ROSC. ETCO2 also confirms correct tube placement.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q74$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1211, $s$Cardiopulmonary Resuscitation (CPR)$s$, $s$["CPR for the infant victim will be 30 compressions to 2 breaths.", "CPR ratio for the adult will be 15 compressions to 2 breaths.", "CPR for the neonate victim will be 30 compressions to 2 breaths.", "CPR ratio for the child and infant will be 15 compressions to 2 breaths."]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: CPR ratio for the child and infant will be 15 compressions to 2 breaths.
CPR compression-to-ventilation ratios: ADULTS — 30:2 (one or two rescuers). CHILDREN and INFANTS — 30:2 with one rescuer, but 15:2 with TWO HEALTH-CARE RESCUERS (because children's arrests are usually caused by breathing problems, so they need more ventilation). NEWBORNS — 3:1. So the statement "CPR ratio for the child and infant will be 15 compressions to 2 breaths" (for two rescuers) is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CPR for the infant victim will be 30 compressions to 2 breaths. — Infant CPR by two health-care rescuers is 15:2, not 30:2.
• CPR ratio for the adult will be 15 compressions to 2 breaths. — The adult ratio is 30:2, not 15:2.
• CPR for the neonate victim will be 30 compressions to 2 breaths. — Newborn CPR is 3:1, not 30:2.

⭐ Key point: Infant compressions: two-thumb technique (two rescuers) or two fingers (one rescuer), at least one-third of chest depth (about 4 cm), rate 100–120/min.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q148$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1212, $s$A ventilated patient develops a new cardiac dysrhythmia, such as new atrial fibrillation with rapid ventricular response, along with desaturation. The nurse should:$s$, $s$["Wait for the next scheduled assessment", "Decrease FiO2", "Treat this as an isolated cardiac issue unrelated to ventilation", "Assess for hypoxemia or ventilatory causes contributing to the dysrhythmia and notify the physician promptly given the combined instability"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assess for hypoxemia or ventilatory causes contributing to the dysrhythmia and notify the physician promptly given the combined instability
In a VENTILATED patient, a NEW ARRHYTHMIA (e.g. rapid AF) together with DESATURATION suggests a COMBINED problem — HYPOXIA, high CO2, electrolyte imbalance, tube problems, pneumothorax, or sepsis can cause both. The nurse should ASSESS for HYPOXAEMIA and VENTILATORY causes (check tube, breath sounds, ventilator, ABG, electrolytes) and NOTIFY THE PHYSICIAN PROMPTLY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wait for the next scheduled assessment — Waiting delays treatment of a serious problem.
• Decrease FiO2 — Decreasing FiO2 would worsen hypoxia.
• Treat this as an isolated cardiac issue unrelated to ventilation — The arrhythmia may be caused by ventilation problems, not an isolated cardiac issue.

⭐ Key point: Check potassium and magnesium in new arrhythmias in ICU patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q110$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1213, $s$A patient with a newly inserted temporary transvenous pacemaker shows pacer spikes with no captured QRS complex at times, worsening when the patient takes a deep breath. This is most likely due to:$s$, $s$["Battery failure only", "Improved lead contact", "Normal pacemaker function with no concern", "Lead micro-displacement affecting capture with patient movement/position change, requiring evaluation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lead micro-displacement affecting capture with patient movement/position change, requiring evaluation
FAILURE TO CAPTURE (pacing spikes without QRS complexes) that WORSENS WITH BREATHING suggests the pacing LEAD has MICRO-DISPLACED — it moves away from the heart wall with breathing and position changes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Battery failure only — Battery failure would cause no spikes or reduced output.
• Improved lead contact — Improved contact would improve capture.
• Normal pacemaker function with no concern — Failure to capture is never normal.

⭐ Key point: Check connections, increase output per protocol, and arrange a chest X-ray.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q180$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1214, $s$A patient with severe sepsis has a central venous oxygen saturation (ScvO2) that is persistently low despite fluid resuscitation. This finding suggests:$s$, $s$["Complete resolution of shock", "No clinical significance", "An imbalance between oxygen delivery and tissue oxygen demand/consumption, prompting further optimisation of resuscitation", "Excellent tissue oxygen delivery relative to demand"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An imbalance between oxygen delivery and tissue oxygen demand/consumption, prompting further optimisation of resuscitation
CENTRAL VENOUS OXYGEN SATURATION (ScvO2) is measured from blood in the superior vena cava. It reflects the BALANCE between OXYGEN DELIVERY (cardiac output, haemoglobin, SaO2) and OXYGEN CONSUMPTION by the tissues. Normal is about 70% or more. A PERSISTENTLY LOW ScvO2 (below 70%) despite fluids means the tissues are extracting a lot of oxygen because DELIVERY IS NOT ENOUGH for their needs. This prompts further optimisation: more fluids if still responsive, inotropes (e.g. dobutamine) if cardiac output is low, blood transfusion if Hb is very low, and reducing oxygen demand (control fever, pain, agitation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Complete resolution of shock — Low ScvO2 shows ongoing shock, not resolution.
• No clinical significance — It is clinically significant.
• Excellent tissue oxygen delivery relative to demand — Low ScvO2 means POOR delivery relative to demand, not excellent delivery.

⭐ Key point: A HIGH ScvO2 with high lactate in sepsis may mean the cells cannot use oxygen (cytopathic hypoxia) or there is shunting — interpret with lactate and the clinical picture.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q149$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1215, $s$A patient with an inferior wall MI should be monitored closely for which complication, due to the shared blood supply from the right coronary artery?$s$, $s$["Pulmonary embolism", "Right ventricular infarction and bradyarrhythmias/heart block", "Isolated left bundle branch block", "Anterior wall extension"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Right ventricular infarction and bradyarrhythmias/heart block
The RIGHT CORONARY ARTERY (RCA) supplies the INFERIOR wall of the left ventricle in most people. It also supplies the RIGHT VENTRICLE, the SINOATRIAL (SA) node (in about 60%), and the ATRIOVENTRICULAR (AV) node (in about 90%). So an INFERIOR MI (RCA blockage) can cause RIGHT VENTRICULAR INFARCTION (hypotension, raised JVP, clear lungs) and BRADYARRHYTHMIAS/HEART BLOCK (sinus bradycardia, AV block).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pulmonary embolism — PE is not directly linked to inferior MI.
• Isolated left bundle branch block — Isolated LBBB is more associated with anterior MI.
• Anterior wall extension — Anterior wall extension is unlikely with RCA occlusion.

⭐ Key point: In inferior MI: record right-sided leads (V4R), avoid nitrates if RV infarction, and have atropine and pacing ready.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q165$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1216, $s$A patient two hours post-laparoscopic nephrectomy reports shoulder tip pain. The nurse should recognize this as most likely related to:$s$, $s$["Myocardial infarction", "Renal colic", "Residual carbon dioxide gas used for pneumoperitoneum during laparoscopy, irritating the diaphragm", "Wound infection"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Residual carbon dioxide gas used for pneumoperitoneum during laparoscopy, irritating the diaphragm
In LAPAROSCOPIC surgery, CARBON DIOXIDE gas is used to inflate the abdomen (PNEUMOPERITONEUM). After surgery, some residual gas remains and IRRITATES THE DIAPHRAGM. Pain from the diaphragm is "referred" to the SHOULDER TIP via the phrenic nerve (C3–C5). This is common and usually resolves within 24–72 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Myocardial infarction — MI would cause central chest pain, often with sweating and ECG changes.
• Renal colic — Renal colic causes loin-to-groin pain.
• Wound infection — Wound infection causes local pain, redness and discharge, usually after a few days.

⭐ Key point: Shoulder tip pain can be eased by early mobilisation, analgesia, and positioning; but always rule out other causes (e.g. bleeding).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q179$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1217, $s$When an unstable patient with fast atrial fibrillation is cardioverted, 'sync' mode is used so that the shock is NOT delivered during:$s$, $s$["The PR interval", "The T wave", "The P wave", "The R wave"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The T wave
During SYNCHRONISED CARDIOVERSION, the defibrillator detects the R wave and delivers the shock AT THE SAME TIME AS the R wave (the QRS complex). This avoids delivering the shock during the T WAVE, which is the "VULNERABLE PERIOD" when the ventricles are repolarising. A shock during the T wave can trigger VENTRICULAR FIBRILLATION (R-on-T phenomenon).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The PR interval — The PR interval is not the vulnerable period.
• The P wave — The P wave (atrial depolarisation) is not the vulnerable period.
• The R wave — The shock IS delivered on the R wave (it's synchronised to it).

⭐ Key point: Check that the defibrillator is marking the R waves before shocking, and re-select "sync" after each shock if needed (some machines default back to unsynchronised).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q32$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1218, $s$During transcutaneous pacing, every pacing spike is followed by a wide QRS complex. The best way to confirm that the heart is actually pumping (mechanical capture) is to:$s$, $s$["Feel the femoral pulse or look at the arterial line trace", "Feel the carotid pulse", "Increase the current by 50% above the capture threshold", "Check that each spike is followed by a QRS on the monitor"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Feel the femoral pulse or look at the arterial line trace
During TRANSCUTANEOUS PACING, ELECTRICAL CAPTURE (a wide QRS after each spike) does NOT guarantee MECHANICAL CAPTURE (the heart actually contracting and pumping blood). The best way to confirm mechanical capture is to feel the FEMORAL PULSE or look at the ARTERIAL LINE TRACE for a pulse that matches the pacing rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Feel the carotid pulse — The CAROTID pulse is unreliable during transcutaneous pacing, because the pacing current causes muscle twitching in the neck that can be mistaken for a pulse.
• Increase the current by 50% above the capture threshold — Increasing the current is done after capture is confirmed (usually by 5–10 mA above threshold), not to confirm mechanical capture.
• Check that each spike is followed by a QRS on the monitor — A QRS after each spike shows ELECTRICAL capture only.

⭐ Key point: Give analgesia and sedation during transcutaneous pacing — it is painful.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q33$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1219, $s$An elderly man has dizzy spells. The pulse is 34/min and regular. On the ECG, P waves occur at 80/min, QRS complexes at 34/min, there is no consistent PR interval, and the QRS complexes are wide. The rhythm is:$s$, $s$["Second-degree AV block, Mobitz type I (Wenckebach)", "Sinus bradycardia", "Third-degree (complete) heart block", "Second-degree AV block, Mobitz type II"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Third-degree (complete) heart block
In COMPLETE (third-degree) HEART BLOCK, NO impulses from the atria reach the ventricles. The atria and ventricles beat INDEPENDENTLY: P waves occur regularly (here at 80/min), QRS complexes occur regularly but slower (here 34/min), and there is NO CONSISTENT PR INTERVAL (the P waves "march through" the QRS complexes). WIDE QRS complexes mean the escape rhythm comes from the ventricles (unstable, slow, and dangerous).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Second-degree AV block, Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) — Mobitz I shows progressively lengthening PR intervals until a QRS is dropped.
• Sinus bradycardia — Sinus bradycardia has a P wave before each QRS with a constant PR interval.
• Second-degree AV block, Mobitz type II — Mobitz II shows a constant PR interval with occasional dropped QRS complexes.

⭐ Key point: Treat with atropine (may not work in wide-complex escape), transcutaneous pacing, isoprenaline or adrenaline, and then a permanent pacemaker.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q51$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1220, $s$An ECG shows a constant PR interval of 0.20 s. Every few beats, a P wave is suddenly not followed by a QRS, without any progressive PR lengthening beforehand. This rhythm is:$s$, $s$["Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) second-degree AV block — usually benign", "First-degree AV block with intermittent sinus arrest — usually benign", "Mobitz type II second-degree AV block — high risk of complete block", "Third-degree (complete) heart block with a junctional escape rhythm"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mobitz type II second-degree AV block — high risk of complete block
In MOBITZ TYPE II second-degree AV block, the PR interval is CONSTANT (here 0.20 s), but suddenly a P wave is NOT followed by a QRS (a dropped beat), WITHOUT any progressive PR lengthening beforehand. The block is usually below the AV node (in the His-Purkinje system). It is dangerous because it can suddenly progress to COMPLETE HEART BLOCK.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mobitz type I (Wenckebach) second-degree AV block — usually benign — Mobitz I (Wenckebach) shows PROGRESSIVE PR lengthening before the dropped beat; it is usually benign.
• First-degree AV block with intermittent sinus arrest — usually benign — First-degree AV block has a prolonged PR interval (over 0.20 s) but NO dropped beats; sinus arrest shows absent P waves.
• Third-degree (complete) heart block with a junctional escape rhythm — Complete heart block shows NO relationship between P waves and QRS complexes.

⭐ Key point: Mobitz II usually needs a permanent pacemaker. Have transcutaneous pacing ready.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q52$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1221, $s$An ECG shows ST elevation in leads II, III and aVF, with reciprocal ST depression in leads I and aVL. The culprit artery is most likely the:$s$, $s$["Left main stem", "Left circumflex artery", "Left anterior descending artery", "Right coronary artery"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Right coronary artery
An INFERIOR MI shows ST ELEVATION in leads II, III and aVF (which look at the bottom/inferior wall of the heart). RECIPROCAL ST DEPRESSION in leads I and aVL (which look at the opposite, lateral wall) supports it. The inferior wall is supplied by the RIGHT CORONARY ARTERY in about 80–90% of people.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Left main stem — Left main stem occlusion causes widespread ST changes and often ST elevation in aVR.
• Left circumflex artery — The left circumflex artery causes lateral changes (I, aVL, V5–V6), sometimes inferior changes.
• Left anterior descending artery — The LAD causes anterior changes (V1–V4).

⭐ Key point: With inferior MI, record right-sided leads (V4R) to check for RV infarction, and watch for heart block and bradycardia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q60$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1222, $s$An ECG shows flattened T waves, ST depression, a prominent extra wave after the T wave, and an apparently long QT interval. The most likely electrolyte disturbance is:$s$, $s$["Hypocalcaemia", "Hypokalaemia", "Hyperkalaemia", "Hypermagnesaemia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypokalaemia
HYPOKALAEMIA (low potassium) causes ECG changes: FLATTENED T WAVES, ST DEPRESSION, prominent U WAVES (a wave after the T wave), and an apparently LONG QT (actually QU) interval. It increases the risk of arrhythmias, especially with digoxin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypocalcaemia — Hypocalcaemia causes a long QT interval, but not U waves or flattened T waves.
• Hyperkalaemia — Hyperkalaemia causes PEAKED T waves, wide QRS and flattened P waves.
• Hypermagnesaemia — Hypermagnesaemia causes bradycardia and heart block.

⭐ Key point: Replace potassium (oral or IV). Correct magnesium too, as low magnesium makes potassium hard to correct.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q102$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1223, $s$An elderly man found on the floor after a 2-day 'long lie' passes dark brown urine. Dipstick is strongly positive for blood, but microscopy shows almost no red cells. CK is 60,000 U/L and K⁺ 6.4 mmol/L. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction", "Rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria", "Acute glomerulonephritis", "Ureteric stone"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Rhabdomyolysis with myoglobinuria
RHABDOMYOLYSIS is breakdown of muscle, often after a "LONG LIE" (prolonged immobility), crush injury, or extreme exercise. Muscle cells release MYOGLOBIN, which turns urine DARK BROWN. The urine dipstick reads POSITIVE for BLOOD (the test can't tell myoglobin from haemoglobin), but MICROSCOPY shows almost NO RED CELLS. Very high CREATINE KINASE (CK — 60,000) and HYPERKALAEMIA (K⁺ 6.4 — released from muscle) are typical. Myoglobin can damage the kidneys.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute haemolytic transfusion reaction — A haemolytic transfusion reaction occurs during transfusion.
• Acute glomerulonephritis — Glomerulonephritis shows red cells and red cell casts in the urine.
• Ureteric stone — A ureteric stone causes colicky pain and red cells in the urine.

⭐ Key point: Treat with aggressive IV fluids (aim urine output 200–300 mL/h), correct potassium and monitor kidney function.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q116$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1224, $s$A woman at 30 weeks of pregnancy has a cardiac arrest on the ward. CPR with manual left uterine displacement has started. If there is no return of spontaneous circulation, resuscitative hysterotomy (perimortem caesarean section) should be started:$s$, $s$["After 15 minutes of unsuccessful CPR", "After 30 minutes of CPR and senior review", "After 10 minutes of unsuccessful CPR", "By 4 minutes, to deliver the baby by 5 minutes"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: By 4 minutes, to deliver the baby by 5 minutes
During cardiac arrest in a pregnant woman (over 20 weeks), the uterus compresses major blood vessels, making CPR less effective. If there is NO RETURN OF CIRCULATION, RESUSCITATIVE HYSTEROTOMY (perimortem caesarean) should be STARTED BY 4 MINUTES, so that the baby is DELIVERED BY 5 MINUTES after arrest. This improves the mother's chance of survival (relieves compression) and the baby's.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• After 15 minutes of unsuccessful CPR — 15 minutes is too late.
• After 30 minutes of CPR and senior review — 30 minutes is far too late.
• After 10 minutes of unsuccessful CPR — 10 minutes is too late.

⭐ Key point: Perform it at the site of arrest — do not move the patient to theatre.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q170$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1225, $s$During pericardiocentesis, the ECG shows ST elevation and ventricular ectopics. This suggests:$s$, $s$["The needle is touching the myocardium and must be withdrawn", "The tamponade has resolved and the heart is refilling", "A normal response to the injected local anaesthetic", "The effusion has been successfully and fully drained"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The needle is touching the myocardium and must be withdrawn
During PERICARDIOCENTESIS, the needle is inserted into the fluid around the heart. If the ECG shows ST ELEVATION (injury current) or VENTRICULAR ECTOPICS, the needle is TOUCHING THE MYOCARDIUM (heart muscle). It must be WITHDRAWN slightly to avoid puncturing the heart or coronary arteries.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The tamponade has resolved and the heart is refilling — Resolution of tamponade would show improved BP, not ST elevation and ectopics.
• A normal response to the injected local anaesthetic — Local anaesthetic does not cause ST elevation in this way.
• The effusion has been successfully and fully drained — Successful drainage would improve haemodynamics, not cause ECG changes.

⭐ Key point: Pericardiocentesis is done under ultrasound guidance with continuous ECG monitoring.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q38$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1226, $s$The compression to ventilation ratio in newborn resuscitation is:$s$, $s$["3:1, about 90 compressions and 30 breaths/min", "15:2, as in older children with two rescuers", "30:2, as in adult basic life support", "5:1, with continuous compressions throughout"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3:1, about 90 compressions and 30 breaths/min
In newborn resuscitation, the ratio is 3 CHEST COMPRESSIONS to 1 VENTILATION BREATH (3:1). In one minute, this gives about 90 compressions and 30 breaths — a total of 120 "events" per minute. Newborns usually arrest because of a BREATHING problem (asphyxia), not a primary heart problem, so ventilation is given a larger share than in adults. Use the two-thumb technique on the lower third of the sternum, compressing about one-third of the chest depth. Reassess the heart rate every 30–60 seconds.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 15:2, as in older children with two rescuers — 15:2 is used for infants and children (beyond the newborn period) when two health workers are performing CPR.
• 30:2, as in adult basic life support — 30:2 is the adult ratio (and the single-rescuer paediatric ratio).
• 5:1, with continuous compressions throughout — 5:1 with continuous compressions is not the recommended newborn ratio.

⭐ Key point: Count aloud "One-and-two-and-three-and-BREATHE-and". Increase oxygen to 100% once compressions are started. Stop compressions when the heart rate rises above 60/min.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q89$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1227, $s$A Do-Not-Attempt-Resuscitation order means:$s$, $s$["Only the family may give medications", "All treatment, including feeding, is stopped", "The patient is discharged from ICU at once", "No CPR, but all other appropriate care continues"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: No CPR, but all other appropriate care continues
A DO-NOT-ATTEMPT-CARDIOPULMONARY-RESUSCITATION (DNACPR) order means that if the patient's heart stops or they stop breathing, CPR (chest compressions, defibrillation, emergency ventilation) WILL NOT be attempted, because it would not work or would not be in the patient's best interests (or the patient has refused it). However, ALL OTHER APPROPRIATE CARE CONTINUES: pain relief, comfort, nutrition and hydration, antibiotics, oxygen, monitoring, and even ICU treatment if appropriate. DNACPR is not the same as "do not treat".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only the family may give medications — Medicines are prescribed and given by health workers as usual; the family does not take over.
• All treatment, including feeding, is stopped — Feeding and other appropriate treatments continue. Stopping them is a separate decision.
• The patient is discharged from ICU at once — A DNACPR order does not mean immediate discharge from ICU; the patient may still benefit from ICU care.

⭐ Key point: DNACPR decisions should be made by the senior doctor, discussed with the patient and/or family, clearly documented, communicated to the team, and reviewed regularly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q146$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1228, $s$Autonomic dysfunction in severe tetanus presents with:$s$, $s$["Polyuria and severe hypokalaemia", "Hypoglycaemia and drowsiness", "Labile BP, tachycardia, sweating and arrhythmias", "Persistent hypothermia and bradycardia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Labile BP, tachycardia, sweating and arrhythmias
Severe tetanus affects the AUTONOMIC nervous system (sympathetic overactivity), causing LABILE (swinging) BP, TACHYCARDIA, heavy SWEATING, fever and ARRHYTHMIAS. This autonomic instability is a major cause of death in ICU-managed tetanus.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Polyuria and severe hypokalaemia — Polyuria and hypokalaemia aren't typical.
• Hypoglycaemia and drowsiness — Hypoglycaemia and drowsiness aren't typical.
• Persistent hypothermia and bradycardia — Persistent hypothermia and bradycardia aren't typical (though sudden bradycardia and cardiac arrest can occur).

⭐ Key point: Magnesium sulphate infusion helps control both spasms and autonomic instability.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q55$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1229, $s$A hypothermic patient (30 °C) in cardiac arrest should have:$s$, $s$["CPR continued while rewarming; 'not dead until warm and dead'", "Adrenaline every 1 minute at full dose", "CPR stopped immediately as futile", "Defibrillation repeated every minute without rewarming"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: CPR continued while rewarming; 'not dead until warm and dead'
In hypothermic cardiac arrest (30 °C), continue CPR while REWARMING (warm IV fluids, forced-air warming, and ECMO or cardiopulmonary bypass if available). The cold protects the brain, so good recovery is possible even after prolonged CPR: "not dead until WARM and dead".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adrenaline every 1 minute at full dose — Adrenaline is WITHHELD below 30 °C (and given at longer intervals between 30 and 35 °C).
• CPR stopped immediately as futile — Stopping CPR early is wrong; hypothermia protects the brain.
• Defibrillation repeated every minute without rewarming — Defibrillation may not work below 30 °C; give up to 3 shocks, then focus on rewarming.

⭐ Key point: Rewarm to at least 32–35 °C before deciding to stop resuscitation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q80$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1230, $s$In neonatal resuscitation, after drying and stimulating, the next assessment is:$s$, $s$["Core temperature only", "Non-invasive blood pressure", "Breathing and heart rate", "Blood glucose level"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Breathing and heart rate
After drying, warming and stimulating a newborn, the next step is to assess BREATHING (is the baby breathing effectively, gasping or apnoeic?) and HEART RATE (with a stethoscope at the chest or by palpating the umbilical cord). These decide whether resuscitation is needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Core temperature only — Temperature is important but doesn't guide immediate resuscitation.
• Non-invasive blood pressure — BP isn't routinely measured at birth.
• Blood glucose level — Glucose is checked later.

⭐ Key point: If the baby is apnoeic, gasping, or HR is below 100, give 5 inflation breaths, then ventilate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q81$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1231, $s$A patient on continuous ECG monitoring develops wide, bizarre QRS complexes at a rate of 160/min with no discernible P waves, and is hypotensive. This rhythm most likely represents:$s$, $s$["Ventricular tachycardia", "Sinus tachycardia", "First-degree heart block", "Normal sinus rhythm"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ventricular tachycardia
Ventricular tachycardia (VT) starts in the ventricles, not the normal conduction system, so the QRS is WIDE (more than 0.12 s) and bizarre. There are no normal P waves before each QRS, and the rate is fast (usually 120–250/min). Low BP means this is UNSTABLE VT: the heart beats too fast and too inefficiently to fill and pump properly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sinus tachycardia — Sinus tachycardia has a normal P wave before every narrow QRS.
• First-degree heart block — First-degree heart block is a long PR interval with a normal rate and QRS.
• Normal sinus rhythm — Normal sinus rhythm has a rate of 60–100 with normal P waves and narrow QRS.

⭐ Key point: VT with a pulse and adverse signs (low BP, chest pain, confusion, heart failure) needs synchronised cardioversion. Pulseless VT needs defibrillation and CPR.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q2$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1232, $s$Torsades de pointes, a polymorphic ventricular tachycardia, is classically associated with:$s$, $s$["A shortened QT interval", "Normal QT interval in all cases", "Complete heart block only", "A prolonged QT interval"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A prolonged QT interval
Torsades de pointes ("twisting of the points") is a polymorphic VT in which the QRS complexes twist around the baseline, changing in height. It happens when the QT interval is PROLONGED, meaning the heart takes too long to recover between beats. Causes: low magnesium, low potassium, low calcium, drugs (amiodarone, haloperidol, erythromycin, ondansetron, methadone) and inherited long QT syndrome.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A shortened QT interval — A SHORT QT interval does not cause torsades.
• Normal QT interval in all cases — Torsades is defined by a long QT, so "normal QT in all cases" is wrong.
• Complete heart block only — Complete heart block can trigger torsades because the slow rate lengthens the QT, but it is not the classic link.

⭐ Key point: Normal QTc is under 440 ms in men and 460 ms in women. Above 500 ms is high risk. Check QT before and during QT-prolonging drugs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q4$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1233, $s$Successful reperfusion after thrombolysis is suggested by:$s$, $s$["Complete heart block develops with a slow ventricular rate", "ST elevation falls by over 50% and the pain settles", "Chest pain persists with steadily rising ST segments", "New Q waves appear in the same leads within an hour"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ST elevation falls by over 50% and the pain settles
When the clot is dissolved and blood flows again (reperfusion), the heart muscle stops being injured. The ST elevation falls quickly (by more than 50% within 60–90 minutes) and the chest pain eases. Reperfusion arrhythmias, such as accelerated idioventricular rhythm, may also briefly appear and are a good sign.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Complete heart block develops with a slow ventricular rate — Complete heart block is a complication, not a sign of success.
• Chest pain persists with steadily rising ST segments — Ongoing pain with rising ST elevation means the artery is still blocked; the patient needs rescue PCI.
• New Q waves appear in the same leads within an hour — New Q waves show dead muscle (infarction), not successful reperfusion.

⭐ Key point: If ST elevation does not fall by 50% within 90 minutes of thrombolysis, refer for rescue PCI.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q9$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1234, $s$Adenosine is used in narrow-complex regular tachycardia (SVT). The nurse should warn the patient about:$s$, $s$["Permanent bradycardia needing a pacemaker", "Long-lasting drowsiness for several hours after", "Severe hypertension lasting for several hours", "Brief chest tightness and a sense of impending doom"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Brief chest tightness and a sense of impending doom
Adenosine briefly blocks the AV node for a few seconds to stop SVT. Its half-life is under 10 seconds, so it must be pushed RAPIDLY into a large vein (antecubital), followed immediately by a fast saline flush. As it works, the heart may pause for a few seconds, and the patient often feels chest tightness, flushing, breathlessness and a frightening "sense of impending doom". Warn the patient beforehand that it lasts only seconds.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Permanent bradycardia needing a pacemaker — Adenosine works for seconds; it does not cause permanent bradycardia.
• Long-lasting drowsiness for several hours after — Its effects pass in seconds, not hours.
• Severe hypertension lasting for several hours — It causes brief flushing and possible low BP, not hours of high BP.

⭐ Key point: Doses: 6 mg rapid IV, then 12 mg, then a further 12 mg (some guidelines 18 mg). Avoid in severe asthma (bronchospasm). Run an ECG strip during the injection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q17$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1235, $s$Family presence during resuscitation, when supported by a staff member, has been shown to:$s$, $s$["It always disrupts the resuscitation team's work", "It increases legal claims in every case", "Helps relatives cope, without disrupting care", "It lengthens the resuscitation time considerably"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Helps relatives cope, without disrupting care
Studies show that when family members are present during resuscitation, supported by a dedicated staff member who explains what is happening, they cope BETTER afterwards: less anxiety, depression and PTSD-like symptoms, and an easier grieving process if the patient dies. Care is not disrupted, and there is no increase in legal claims.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It always disrupts the resuscitation team's work — Family presence does not usually disrupt the team when the family is supported.
• It increases legal claims in every case — Legal claims do not increase; families who see the effort made often feel reassured.
• It lengthens the resuscitation time considerably — Resuscitation time is not lengthened.

⭐ Key point: Offer family presence where possible, assign a staff member to stay with them, and allow them to leave at any time.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q70$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1236, $s$A baby is brought in apnoeic and pulseless at home. The nurse should:$s$, $s$["Give oxygen by mask and observe", "Wait for the doctor to confirm death", "Ask the parents what happened first", "Start paediatric CPR and call the team"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Start paediatric CPR and call the team
A baby who is not breathing and has no pulse is in cardiac arrest. The nurse must START CPR immediately and call for help (the resuscitation team). In infants, give 5 rescue breaths first, then chest compressions at a ratio of 15:2 (two rescuers; 30:2 for a lone rescuer), with two thumbs encircling the chest or two fingers on the lower sternum, 100–120 compressions per minute.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give oxygen by mask and observe — Oxygen by mask alone won't help a baby with no breathing and no pulse.
• Wait for the doctor to confirm death — Waiting for the doctor wastes vital minutes.
• Ask the parents what happened first — Asking the parents can wait; resuscitation comes first.

⭐ Key point: In children, cardiac arrest is usually caused by hypoxia (breathing problems), so rescue breaths are very important.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q109$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1237, $s$TENS (transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation) should not be placed:$s$, $s$["On the lower back over the muscles", "On the legs over the thigh muscles", "On the arms over the forearm muscles", "Over a pacemaker or the front of the neck"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Over a pacemaker or the front of the neck
TENS sends electrical pulses through the skin. It should NOT be placed OVER A PACEMAKER (it can interfere with how the pacemaker senses and fires), or over the FRONT OF THE NECK (it can stimulate the carotid sinus, causing a drop in BP and heart rate, or cause laryngeal spasm). Other places to avoid: the eyes, broken skin, the pregnant abdomen (except approved labour TENS), and over tumours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• On the lower back over the muscles — The lower back is a common, safe site for TENS.
• On the legs over the thigh muscles — The thigh muscles are a safe site.
• On the arms over the forearm muscles — The forearm muscles are a safe site.

⭐ Key point: Patients with pacemakers or implanted defibrillators should generally avoid TENS unless a cardiologist approves it.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q194$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1238, $s$Over-insertion of the guidewire during central line placement risks causing:$s$, $s$["Improved cardiac function", "No clinical consequence", "Arrhythmias", "Immediate cure of the underlying condition"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Arrhythmias
The guidewire passes through the vein into the right atrium. If inserted too far, the tip touches the heart wall and irritates the conduction system, triggering ARRHYTHMIAS (atrial or ventricular ectopics, even VT). The patient must be on an ECG monitor during insertion, and the wire is pulled back if arrhythmias occur.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved cardiac function — Irritating the heart doesn't improve cardiac function.
• No clinical consequence — Over-insertion does have consequences.
• Immediate cure of the underlying condition — It doesn't cure anything.

⭐ Key point: Other central line insertion complications: pneumothorax, arterial puncture, bleeding, air embolism and malposition. Get a chest X-ray after neck and chest lines.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q22$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1239, $s$During pericardiocentesis, the nurse monitors:$s$, $s$["Only the core temperature", "Only the capillary blood glucose", "Only the hourly urine output", "ECG for arrhythmias, with BP and SpO₂"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ECG for arrhythmias, with BP and SpO₂
During pericardiocentesis, a needle is inserted into the pericardial sac (usually below the xiphisternum, aimed at the left shoulder) to remove fluid. If the needle touches the heart muscle, it can cause ARRHYTHMIAS (ventricular ectopics, VT) or puncture the heart. The nurse monitors the ECG continuously, plus BP and SpO₂, and has resuscitation equipment ready.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only the core temperature — Temperature alone is not the main concern.
• Only the capillary blood glucose — Blood glucose isn't the main concern.
• Only the hourly urine output — Urine output isn't the main concern during the procedure.

⭐ Key point: After successful pericardiocentesis, BP usually rises and the heart rate falls quickly. Watch for the fluid re-accumulating.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q29$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1240, $s$Pneumoperitoneum after laparoscopy usually:$s$, $s$["Never occurs", "Resolves within a few days", "Always means perforation", "Requires surgery"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Resolves within a few days
During laparoscopy, the abdomen is inflated with CO₂ gas. Small amounts can remain after surgery, showing as free air under the diaphragm on X-ray. This is EXPECTED and usually RESOLVES within a few days (CO₂ is absorbed quickly). The patient may have shoulder-tip pain from diaphragm irritation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Never occurs — It does occur commonly after laparoscopy.
• Always means perforation — It doesn't always mean perforation after recent surgery.
• Requires surgery — It doesn't usually need surgery.

⭐ Key point: Free air plus signs of peritonitis (fever, rigid abdomen, rising inflammatory markers) after surgery needs urgent review for a leak or perforation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q140$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1241, $s$Tall-tented T waves and widened QRS are seen in:$s$, $s$["Hyperglycemia", "Hyperkalemia", "Hyperphosphatemia", "Hyponatremia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hyperkalemia
High potassium (hyperkalaemia) changes the heart's electrical recovery. As potassium rises, the ECG shows TALL, peaked (tented) T WAVES first, then flattened or lost P waves, a long PR interval, and WIDENING of the QRS. Very high levels give a sine-wave pattern, then VF or asystole.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperglycemia — Hyperglycaemia doesn't cause these ECG changes.
• Hyperphosphatemia — Hyperphosphataemia doesn't cause these changes directly.
• Hyponatremia — Hyponatraemia doesn't cause tall T waves.

⭐ Key point: Treat hyperkalaemia with ECG changes as an emergency: calcium gluconate first, then insulin with glucose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q4$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1242, $s$A patient with end-stage renal disease has a serum K⁺ of 6.8 mEq/L and ECG shows widened QRS complexes. Which intervention is MOST urgent?$s$, $s$["Give calcium gluconate 10% IV", "Administer sodium polystyrene sulfonate orally", "Initiate hemodialysis", "Infuse 10 units of regular insulin IV with 50% dextrose"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Give calcium gluconate 10% IV
K⁺ 6.8 with a WIDENED QRS shows the heart is in danger of a fatal arrhythmia. The MOST urgent step is IV CALCIUM GLUCONATE 10% (30 mL), which stabilises the heart muscle membrane within minutes. It doesn't lower potassium, but it buys time for the other treatments.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer sodium polystyrene sulfonate orally — Oral binders (sodium polystyrene) work over hours to days.
• Initiate hemodialysis — Haemodialysis is the definitive treatment but takes time to arrange; calcium comes first.
• Infuse 10 units of regular insulin IV with 50% dextrose — Insulin with dextrose (shifting K⁺ into cells) is the next step after calcium.

⭐ Key point: Order: protect the heart (calcium), shift K⁺ into cells (insulin and glucose, salbutamol), remove K⁺ (dialysis, binders, diuretics).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q8$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1243, $s$About cardiac pacemaker$s$, $s$["When viewed on a chest radiograph, the pacemaker lead should terminate in the right ventricular apex", "The right internal jugular and the left subclavian veins are often preferred", "Cardiac pacemakers can be inserted by trans venous only", "Most often the trans venous approach is used"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cardiac pacemakers can be inserted by trans venous only
This question asks for the statement that is NOT correct. "Cardiac pacemakers can be inserted by transvenous approach ONLY" is FALSE, because pacemakers can also be placed EPICARDIALLY (surgically on the heart surface, such as after cardiac surgery), TRANSCUTANEOUSLY (external pads) and transoesophageally. The other statements are true.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• When viewed on a chest radiograph, the pacemaker lead should terminate in the right ventricular apex — It is TRUE that the pacing lead on X-ray should end at the right ventricular apex.
• The right internal jugular and the left subclavian veins are often preferred — It is TRUE that the right internal jugular and left subclavian veins are often used.
• Most often the trans venous approach is used — It is TRUE that the transvenous route is used most often.

⭐ Key point: Temporary pacing options: transcutaneous (emergency, pads), transvenous (wire via a central vein), and epicardial (wires after cardiac surgery).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q42$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1244, $s$The required dose of Amiodarone in Refractory VF and Pulseless VT:$s$, $s$["150 mg", "300 mg", "100 mg", "250 mg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 300 mg
In shock-resistant (refractory) VF or pulseless VT, AMIODARONE 300 mg IV is given after the 3rd shock. A further 150 mg is given after the 5th shock. Amiodarone stabilises the heart's electrical activity and helps defibrillation succeed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 150 mg — 150 mg is the SECOND dose (after the 5th shock).
• 100 mg — 100 mg is not the standard dose.
• 250 mg — 250 mg is not the standard dose.

⭐ Key point: Lidocaine 1 mg/kg is an alternative if amiodarone isn't available. Give adrenaline 1 mg every 3–5 minutes as well.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q45$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1245, $s$During CPR, the recommended compression-to-ventilation ratio for a single rescuer performing adult CPR without an advanced airway is:$s$, $s$["30:2", "Continuous compressions with no ventilation", "5:1", "15:2"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 30:2
For adult CPR without an advanced airway, the ratio is 30 COMPRESSIONS to 2 BREATHS (30:2), for both single rescuers and teams. Compressions are 5–6 cm deep at 100–120 per minute, allowing full chest recoil and minimising interruptions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Continuous compressions with no ventilation — Continuous compressions without breaths are used by untrained bystanders (compression-only CPR) or once an advanced airway is in place (with ventilation at 10 per minute).
• 5:1 — 5:1 is an old, outdated ratio.
• 15:2 — 15:2 is the ratio for CHILDREN with two rescuers.

⭐ Key point: Once an advanced airway (ETT or supraglottic) is in place, give continuous compressions with 10 breaths per minute.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q46$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1246, $s$Electrocariaogram strip of a patient shows: P wave and QRS complexes --regular, PR-Interval - 0.14 seconds, QRS complexes measure- 0.08 seconds, overall hert beat rate- 82beats per minutes. This cardiac rhythm is____________$s$, $s$["First-degree heart block", "Normal sinus rhythm", "Sinus tachycardia", "Sinus bradycardia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Normal sinus rhythm
Check each part of the ECG strip. Regular P waves and QRS complexes, with each P followed by a QRS. PR interval 0.14 s (normal 0.12–0.20). QRS 0.08 s (normal under 0.12, narrow). Heart rate 82/min (normal 60–100). Everything is normal, so this is NORMAL SINUS RHYTHM.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• First-degree heart block — First-degree heart block has a PR interval LONGER than 0.20 s.
• Sinus tachycardia — Sinus tachycardia has a rate ABOVE 100.
• Sinus bradycardia — Sinus bradycardia has a rate BELOW 60.

⭐ Key point: The ECG checklist: rate, rhythm (regular?), P waves (present before each QRS?), PR interval, QRS width, ST segment and T waves.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q48$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1247, $s$A patient with suspected myocardial infarction has ECG changes showing ST elevation in leads II, III, and aVF. This localises the infarction to the:$s$, $s$["Inferior wall", "Posterior wall only", "Anterior wall", "Lateral wall"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inferior wall
ECG leads II, III and aVF look at the INFERIOR (bottom) wall of the heart. ST elevation in these leads means an INFERIOR MI, usually from blockage of the right coronary artery (RCA). The RCA also supplies the right ventricle and the AV node, so watch for RV infarction and heart block.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Posterior wall only — A posterior MI shows ST DEPRESSION in V1–V3 (with tall R waves).
• Anterior wall — An anterior MI shows changes in V1–V4.
• Lateral wall — A lateral MI shows changes in I, aVL, V5 and V6.

⭐ Key point: In an inferior MI, record V4R to check for RV involvement, and avoid nitrates if the RV is involved.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q52$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1248, $s$A nurse notices frequent artifacts on the ECG monitor for a client whose leads are connected by cable to a console at the bedside. The nurse examines the client to determine the cause. Which of the following items is unlikely to be responsible for the artifact?$s$, $s$["Tightly secured cable connections.", "Leads applied over hairy areas.", "Frequent movement of the client.", "Leads applied to the limbs."]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tightly secured cable connections.
Artefact (interference) on the ECG comes from things that disturb the electrical signal: patient MOVEMENT (shivering, tremor), electrodes on HAIRY skin (poor contact), electrodes on the LIMBS (more muscle movement), loose connections and dried gel. TIGHTLY SECURED cable connections REDUCE artefact, so they are UNLIKELY to be the cause.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Leads applied over hairy areas. — Hairy areas DO cause artefact (poor contact).
• Frequent movement of the client. — Frequent movement DOES cause artefact.
• Leads applied to the limbs. — Limb leads DO cause more artefact from muscle movement.

⭐ Key point: Reduce artefact: clip hair, clean and dry the skin, use fresh electrodes, place them over bone on the chest, and secure the cables.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q41$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1249, $s$As a nurse performing cardio-pulmonary resuscitation on a cardiac arrest patient, you are aware that the correct cardiac compression to optimised cardiac function is:$s$, $s$["110 compressions per minute", "115 compressions per minute", "120 Compressions per minute", "100 Compressions per minute"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 120 Compressions per minute
Current guidelines recommend chest compressions at 100–120 per MINUTE, with the aim of about 120/min at the upper end. The keyed answer is 120. Push 5–6 cm deep in adults, allow full chest recoil, and minimise pauses.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 110 compressions per minute — 110 per minute is within the range, but the keyed answer is the upper target.
• 115 compressions per minute — 115 per minute is within the range, but not the keyed answer.
• 100 Compressions per minute — 100 per minute is the lower limit, not the optimum here.

⭐ Key point: Change the person doing compressions every 2 minutes to avoid fatigue. Use capnography to check compression quality.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q45$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1250, $s$In neonatal resuscitation, chest compressions start when heart rate stays below 60/min despite:$s$, $s$["30 seconds of effective ventilation", "5 minutes of free-flow oxygen", "One minute of drying only", "Two doses of adrenaline"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 30 seconds of effective ventilation
In neonatal resuscitation, ventilation is the key step. If the heart rate stays BELOW 60/min despite 30 SECONDS of EFFECTIVE VENTILATION (with the chest visibly moving), start CHEST COMPRESSIONS at a 3:1 ratio with ventilation (90 compressions and 30 breaths per minute).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5 minutes of free-flow oxygen — Free-flow oxygen alone isn't effective ventilation.
• One minute of drying only — Drying alone isn't enough to decide on compressions.
• Two doses of adrenaline — Adrenaline comes AFTER compressions, not before.

⭐ Key point: Recheck the heart rate every 30 seconds. If it stays below 60 despite compressions, give adrenaline via an umbilical venous catheter.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q135$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1251, $s$Which of the following is ethical issue during CPR:$s$, $s$["Refusal and discontinuation of treatment", "Family presence during CPR", "Consent for Treatment", "Trained healthcare providers"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Family presence during CPR
FAMILY PRESENCE DURING CPR is one of the most debated ethical issues in resuscitation. Benefits: families may cope better, see that everything was done, and be present at death. Concerns: possible distress, interference, and staff discomfort. Many guidelines now support offering it, with a dedicated staff member to support the family.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Refusal and discontinuation of treatment — Refusal and discontinuation of treatment are ethical issues, but the keyed answer focuses on CPR family presence.
• Consent for Treatment — Consent for treatment is an ethical issue, but not specific to CPR here.
• Trained healthcare providers — Trained providers is a staffing matter, not an ethical issue.

⭐ Key point: Offer family presence where appropriate, with a staff member to explain what's happening and support them.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q168$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1252, $s$A nurse is assessing an electrocardiogram rhythm strip. The P waves and QRS complexes are regular. The PR interval is 0.16 second, and QRS complexes measure 0.06 second. The overall heart rate is 64 beats per minute. The nurse assesses the cardiac rhythm as:$s$, $s$["Sinus bradycardia", "Sick sinus syndrome", "Normal sinus rhythm", "First-degree heart block"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Normal sinus rhythm
Check each part: rhythm REGULAR; rate 64 (normal 60–100); PR interval 0.16 s (normal 0.12–0.20); QRS 0.06 s (normal, under 0.12). Every value is normal, so this is NORMAL SINUS RHYTHM.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sinus bradycardia — Sinus bradycardia has a rate BELOW 60.
• Sick sinus syndrome — Sick sinus syndrome shows alternating slow and fast rhythms, or pauses.
• First-degree heart block — First-degree heart block has a PR interval ABOVE 0.20 s.

⭐ Key point: ECG interpretation: rate, rhythm, P waves, PR interval, QRS width, ST segment, T waves.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q54$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1253, $s$The compression depth recommended for an infant during CPR is approximately:$s$, $s$["5–6cm", "1cm", "8cm", "4cm"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 4cm
For an INFANT, chest compressions should be about ONE-THIRD of the chest depth, approximately 4 cm. Use two thumbs (encircling technique) or two fingers on the lower half of the sternum, at 100–120 compressions per minute.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5–6cm — 5–6 cm is the depth for ADULTS.
• 1cm — 1 cm is too shallow to circulate blood.
• 8cm — 8 cm is far too deep for an infant.

⭐ Key point: For a child (1 year to puberty), compress about 5 cm (one-third of the chest depth).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q122$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B4 · Resuscitation (BLS/ACLS), ECG & Arrhythmias$s$, false, 1254, $s$The defibrillation energy for a child in VF is:$s$, $s$["360 J for all ages", "40 J/kg", "0.4 J/kg", "4 J/kg"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 4 J/kg
For a child in VF or pulseless VT, the defibrillation energy is 4 JOULES per KILOGRAM (for example, 4 × 20 kg = 80 J). Then resume CPR immediately for 2 minutes before checking the rhythm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 360 J for all ages — 360 J for all ages is an old adult monophasic dose, far too high for children.
• 40 J/kg — 40 J/kg is ten times too high.
• 0.4 J/kg — 0.4 J/kg is ten times too low.

⭐ Key point: Use paediatric pads (or adult pads placed front and back) in small children.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q134$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1255, $s$A patient opens their eyes only to painful stimuli, makes incomprehensible sounds, and withdraws from pain. What is the calculated GCS score?$s$, $s$["6", "10", "12", "8"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 8
Add up the three parts of the GCS. EYES open only to pain = E2. VERBAL: incomprehensible sounds (moaning, groaning, no words) = V2. MOTOR: withdraws from pain (pulls the limb away) = M4. Total = 2 + 2 + 4 = 8. A GCS of 8 is a severe brain injury, and the airway may need protection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 6 — 6 would mean a lower motor or verbal score (for example abnormal flexion, M3).
• 10 — 10 would need higher scores, such as confused words (V4) or localising pain (M5).
• 12 — 12 would need much better scores, such as eyes opening to voice and localising pain.

⭐ Key point: GCS: Eyes 1–4, Verbal 1–5, Motor 1–6 (total 3–15). Motor: 6 obeys, 5 localises, 4 withdraws, 3 abnormal flexion (decorticate), 2 extension (decerebrate), 1 none.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q83$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1256, $s$Cushing's triad, a late sign of rising intracranial pressure, consists of which three findings?$s$, $s$["Hypotension, tachycardia, and tachypnea", "Hypertension with tachycardia and hyperthermia", "Hypertension with widened pulse pressure, bradycardia, and irregular respirations", "Hypotension, bradycardia, and apnea"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypertension with widened pulse pressure, bradycardia, and irregular respirations
Cushing's triad is a LATE sign of raised ICP and approaching brainstem herniation. 1) HYPERTENSION with a WIDE pulse pressure: the body raises BP to push blood into the brain against high pressure. 2) BRADYCARDIA: a reflex response to the high BP and brainstem compression. 3) IRREGULAR breathing (Cheyne–Stokes, apnoea): compression of the breathing centre in the brainstem.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypotension, tachycardia, and tachypnea — Low BP, fast pulse and fast breathing describe SHOCK, not raised ICP.
• Hypertension with tachycardia and hyperthermia — High BP with fast pulse and fever does not fit; the pulse is SLOW in Cushing's triad.
• Hypotension, bradycardia, and apnea — Low BP, slow pulse and apnoea is a pre-terminal picture, not Cushing's triad.

⭐ Key point: Cushing's triad means herniation is near. Call for help at once and prepare for urgent ICP-lowering treatment (osmotherapy, neurosurgery).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q86 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1257, $s$What are common initial presenting symptoms of Myasthenia Gravis?$s$, $s$["Foot drop", "Urinary retention", "Severe headache", "Ptosis and diplopia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ptosis and diplopia
Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease in which antibodies block the acetylcholine receptors at the neuromuscular junction. Muscles tire quickly with use and recover with rest. The EYE muscles are usually affected first: drooping eyelids (PTOSIS) and double vision (DIPLOPIA) are the commonest first symptoms (in about 2 out of 3 patients). Weakness is typically worse at the end of the day.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Foot drop — Foot drop is more typical of a peripheral nerve problem (such as common peroneal nerve palsy).
• Urinary retention — Urinary retention is not a feature of myasthenia (smooth muscle is spared).
• Severe headache — Severe headache is not a feature of myasthenia.

⭐ Key point: In a myasthenic crisis (breathing or swallowing failure), monitor FVC, protect the airway, and give IVIG or plasma exchange. Avoid aminoglycosides and magnesium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q94$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1258, $s$What is the first step in the pharmacologic management approach to ICU delirium?$s$, $s$["Immediately start high-dose benzodiazepines", "Start antipsychotics for all patients regardless of cause", "Sedate to RASS -5 to prevent movement", "Identify and treat underlying reversible causes (pain, hypoxia, infection, metabolic derangement) before adding antipsychotics"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Identify and treat underlying reversible causes (pain, hypoxia, infection, metabolic derangement) before adding antipsychotics
DELIRIUM is an acute change in attention and thinking, with a fluctuating course. It is almost always caused by something — so the FIRST step is to look for and TREAT REVERSIBLE CAUSES. Memory aid "I WATCH DEATH" or "PINCH ME": Pain, Infection (sepsis, UTI, pneumonia), Nutrition/dehydration, Constipation, HYPOXIA, Medications (benzodiazepines, anticholinergics, opioids), Environment, Electrolyte/metabolic disturbances (sodium, glucose, calcium), urinary retention, withdrawal (alcohol). Also use non-drug measures: reorientation, clocks and calendars, glasses and hearing aids, family visits, sleep at night/light in day, early mobilisation (ABCDEF bundle). ANTIPSYCHOTICS (e.g. haloperidol, quetiapine) are only for severe agitation that puts the patient or staff at risk, at the lowest dose for the shortest time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediately start high-dose benzodiazepines — High-dose BENZODIAZEPINES can CAUSE or WORSEN delirium (except in alcohol/benzodiazepine withdrawal).
• Start antipsychotics for all patients regardless of cause — Antipsychotics for ALL patients regardless of cause is wrong; they don't fix the cause and have side effects (QT prolongation, sedation).
• Sedate to RASS -5 to prevent movement — Deep sedation to RASS −5 increases delirium, ventilator days and mortality; the target is light sedation (RASS 0 to −2).

⭐ Key point: ICU delirium → FIRST find and treat the cause (pain, hypoxia, infection, metabolic, drugs). Antipsychotics only for severe agitation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q83$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1259, $s$Status epilepticus is defined as a seizure lasting longer than:$s$, $s$["30 seconds", "2 minutes", "5 minutes", "15 minutes"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 5 minutes
STATUS EPILEPTICUS (SE) is now defined as a seizure lasting MORE THAN 5 MINUTES, or two or more seizures without full recovery of consciousness in between. Older textbooks said 30 minutes, but the definition was shortened because most seizures stop within 2–3 minutes; after 5 minutes they are unlikely to stop on their own, and after about 30 minutes there is risk of permanent brain damage. Treatment timeline: 0–5 min — ABC, oxygen, check glucose, IV access; 5–20 min — BENZODIAZEPINE (IV lorazepam 4 mg, or buccal/IV midazolam, or diazepam), may repeat once; 20–40 min — second-line drug (levetiracetam, phenytoin/fosphenytoin, valproate); over 40 min (refractory) — anaesthetic agents and intubation in ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 30 seconds — 30 seconds is an ordinary short seizure.
• 2 minutes — 2 minutes is still within the usual length of a self-limiting seizure.
• 15 minutes — 15 minutes is longer than the current definition; waiting this long delays treatment.

⭐ Key point: Status epilepticus = seizure > 5 MINUTES (or repeated without recovery). First-line: IV lorazepam/midazolam.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q84$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1260, $s$When does primary brain injury occur?$s$, $s$["At the moment of impact", "In the first hour after injury", "In the first 4 hours after injury", "Only if the patient is in coma"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: At the moment of impact
PRIMARY BRAIN INJURY is the damage caused at the MOMENT OF IMPACT — the direct mechanical force on the brain tissue: contusions, lacerations, skull fractures, diffuse axonal injury, tearing of blood vessels. It happens in milliseconds and cannot be reversed by treatment; only prevention (helmets, seatbelts, road safety) helps. SECONDARY BRAIN INJURY develops minutes, hours or days later from hypoxia, hypotension, swelling, raised ICP, seizures, fever, infection and metabolic problems — this is what medical and nursing care tries to prevent.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• In the first hour after injury — "In the first hour" describes the time when SECONDARY injury may begin, not primary injury.
• In the first 4 hours after injury — "In the first 4 hours" is also the secondary-injury window, not the primary event.
• Only if the patient is in coma — Primary injury happens in every brain injury at impact, whether or not the patient is in coma.

⭐ Key point: PRIMARY brain injury = at the MOMENT OF IMPACT (cannot be undone). SECONDARY = later, preventable.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q96$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1261, $s$Which ICP monitoring device is considered the gold standard because it also allows therapeutic drainage of cerebrospinal fluid?$s$, $s$["Subarachnoid bolt", "Epidural sensor", "Intraventricular catheter (external ventricular drain)", "Intraparenchymal fiberoptic monitor"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intraventricular catheter (external ventricular drain)
The INTRAVENTRICULAR CATHETER (EXTERNAL VENTRICULAR DRAIN, EVD) is placed in a lateral ventricle. It is the GOLD STANDARD for ICP monitoring because it is accurate, can be recalibrated, AND allows THERAPEUTIC DRAINAGE of CSF to lower ICP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Subarachnoid bolt — Subarachnoid bolts are less accurate and can't drain CSF.
• Epidural sensor — Epidural sensors are less accurate.
• Intraparenchymal fiberoptic monitor — Intraparenchymal monitors are accurate but can't drain CSF.

⭐ Key point: EVDs carry a risk of infection (ventriculitis) — maintain strict asepsis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q86$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1262, $s$Cerebral Perfusion Pressure (CPP) is calculated as:$s$, $s$["Mean Arterial Pressure minus Intracranial Pressure", "Systolic Blood Pressure plus Intracranial Pressure", "Heart Rate divided by Intracranial Pressure", "Diastolic Blood Pressure minus Heart Rate"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mean Arterial Pressure minus Intracranial Pressure
CEREBRAL PERFUSION PRESSURE (CPP) = MEAN ARTERIAL PRESSURE (MAP) − INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP). It represents the pressure driving blood into the brain. Target CPP is usually 60–70 mmHg in TBI.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Systolic Blood Pressure plus Intracranial Pressure — Systolic plus ICP is incorrect.
• Heart Rate divided by Intracranial Pressure — Heart rate divided by ICP is incorrect.
• Diastolic Blood Pressure minus Heart Rate — Diastolic minus heart rate is incorrect.

⭐ Key point: Low CPP causes brain ischaemia; raise MAP or lower ICP.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q88$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1263, $s$Therapeutic hyperventilation used briefly as a temporizing measure for acute herniation should target a PaCO2 of approximately what value?$s$, $s$["15-20 mmHg", "45-50 mmHg", "30-35 mmHg", "55-60 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 30-35 mmHg
In an EMERGENCY of acute BRAIN HERNIATION (e.g. a blown pupil, Cushing's response), brief HYPERVENTILATION lowers PaCO2. Low CO2 makes the brain's blood vessels CONSTRICT, reducing cerebral blood volume and quickly lowering ICP. The target is a PaCO2 of about 30–35 mmHg. This is only a short-term "bridge" while giving osmotherapy (mannitol or hypertonic saline) and arranging CT and neurosurgery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 15-20 mmHg — 15–20 mmHg causes severe vasoconstriction, cutting off blood supply and causing brain ischaemia.
• 45-50 mmHg — 45–50 mmHg (high CO2) causes vasodilation and RAISES ICP.
• 55-60 mmHg — 55–60 mmHg would raise ICP even more.

⭐ Key point: Avoid prolonged or routine hyperventilation in brain injury — it can worsen ischaemia. Normal target PaCO2 is 35–45 mmHg.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q93$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1264, $s$Diazepam, as an alternative first-line agent for status epilepticus, can be given via all of the following routes EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["IV", "Rectal", "Buccal or nasal", "Oral tablet during active seizure"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Oral tablet during active seizure
DIAZEPAM can be given for status epilepticus via: IV, RECTAL (e.g. rectal tubes), and sometimes buccal/nasal (though buccal/nasal midazolam is more common). It should NOT be given as an ORAL TABLET during an active seizure — the patient cannot swallow safely (choking and aspiration risk) and absorption would be too slow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV — IV is a fast route for diazepam.
• Rectal — Rectal diazepam is used, especially in children and outside hospital.
• Buccal or nasal — Buccal or nasal routes are used (mainly midazolam).

⭐ Key point: Never put anything in the mouth of a fitting patient.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q84$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1265, $s$What is the recommended external anatomical reference point used to zero and level an external ventricular drain (EVD)?$s$, $s$["Xiphoid process", "Umbilicus", "Sternal angle", "Tragus of the ear (level of the foramen of Monro)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tragus of the ear (level of the foramen of Monro)
An EXTERNAL VENTRICULAR DRAIN (EVD) is levelled and zeroed at the level of the FORAMEN OF MONRO (the opening connecting the lateral and third ventricles). The external landmark is the TRAGUS of the ear (the small cartilage flap in front of the ear canal), or the outer canthus of the eye in some units.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Xiphoid process — The xiphoid process is not used.
• Umbilicus — The umbilicus is not used.
• Sternal angle — The sternal angle is used for JVP assessment, not EVD.

⭐ Key point: Re-level the EVD every time the patient's head position changes; wrong levelling causes over- or under-drainage of CSF.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q85 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1266, $s$A Glasgow Coma Scale score in which range classifies a traumatic brain injury as severe?$s$, $s$["13-15", "9-12", "3-8", "3-5 only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3-8
TRAUMATIC BRAIN INJURY (TBI) severity is classified using the GLASGOW COMA SCALE (GCS) after resuscitation: MILD = 13–15; MODERATE = 9–12; SEVERE = 3–8. A GCS of 3–8 means the patient is in a coma, usually cannot protect the airway (needs intubation), and needs ICU care, CT scan and often ICP monitoring. Severe TBI carries a high risk of death and long-term disability.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 13-15 — 13–15 is MILD TBI.
• 9-12 — 9–12 is MODERATE TBI.
• 3-5 only — "3–5 only" is too narrow; severe TBI includes GCS 3 to 8.

⭐ Key point: Prevent secondary brain injury: avoid hypoxia (SpO2 above 94%), hypotension (systolic above 100–110 mmHg), hypo/hypercapnia, fever, hypo/hyperglycaemia and seizures.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q89$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1267, $s$A head-injured patient has BP 140/80 mmHg and ICP 30 mmHg. What is the cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP)?$s$, $s$["110 mmHg", "70 mmHg", "50 mmHg", "130 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 70 mmHg
CEREBRAL PERFUSION PRESSURE (CPP) is the pressure pushing blood into the brain: CPP = MAP − ICP. STEP 1 — MAP = (systolic + 2 × diastolic) ÷ 3 = (140 + 160) ÷ 3 = 300 ÷ 3 = 100 mmHg. STEP 2 — CPP = 100 − 30 = 70 mmHg. A CPP of 60–70 mmHg is the usual target in traumatic brain injury, so CPP is acceptable here — BUT the ICP of 30 mmHg is HIGH (normal 5–15; treatment usually starts above 20–22 mmHg), so measures to lower ICP are still needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 110 mmHg — 110 mmHg is systolic minus ICP (140 − 30) — wrong, it should use MAP.
• 50 mmHg — 50 mmHg is diastolic minus ICP (80 − 30).
• 130 mmHg — 130 mmHg is not a valid calculation.

⭐ Key point: To lower ICP: head up 30°, neck straight, avoid hypoxia and high CO2, sedation and analgesia, treat fever and seizures, osmotherapy (mannitol or hypertonic saline), CSF drainage.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q7 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1268, $s$A patient with a severe traumatic brain injury requires sedation for ventilator tolerance. The team performs a 'neuro wake-up' assessment periodically. The primary purpose of this is to:$s$, $s$["Discharge the patient sooner regardless of status", "Allow accurate ongoing neurological assessment despite the need for sedation", "Increase sedation requirements", "Reduce the need for any imaging"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Allow accurate ongoing neurological assessment despite the need for sedation
Patients with SEVERE TRAUMATIC BRAIN INJURY often need sedation to tolerate the ventilator, control ICP and reduce brain oxygen demand. But sedation HIDES the neurological examination — the team cannot tell if the brain is getting worse (e.g. a new bleed). A "NEURO WAKE-UP" test means briefly stopping or reducing sedation to perform an ACCURATE NEUROLOGICAL ASSESSMENT (GCS, pupils, limb movements, obeying commands), then restarting sedation. It must be done only when the ICP is stable and controlled, because waking can raise ICP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Discharge the patient sooner regardless of status — Wake-up tests are not done to discharge patients sooner regardless of their state.
• Increase sedation requirements — The purpose is not to increase sedation requirements.
• Reduce the need for any imaging — It does not replace imaging; CT is still needed when deterioration is suspected.

⭐ Key point: Stop the wake-up test immediately if ICP rises significantly, the patient becomes distressed, or vital signs become unstable. Many units use continuous ICP monitoring and pupillometry instead in very unstable patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q143$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1269, $s$A patient recovering from a head injury is assessed to have decerebrate posturing (extension of all four limbs) in response to painful stimuli. This indicates:$s$, $s$["More severe brainstem dysfunction, generally a worse prognosis than decorticate posturing", "A better prognosis than decorticate posturing", "Normal neurological function", "No clinical relevance"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: More severe brainstem dysfunction, generally a worse prognosis than decorticate posturing
DECEREBRATE POSTURING is EXTENSION of all four limbs to painful stimuli (arms straightened and rotated inwards, legs extended, toes pointed). It indicates damage at the level of the BRAINSTEM (midbrain/pons) — LOWER and MORE SEVERE than decorticate posturing (flexion of the arms, which indicates damage above the brainstem). Decerebrate posturing carries a WORSE PROGNOSIS. On the GCS, decerebrate = motor score 2; decorticate = 3.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A better prognosis than decorticate posturing — Decerebrate has a WORSE, not better, prognosis than decorticate.
• Normal neurological function — It is never normal.
• No clinical relevance — It is very clinically relevant — it may signal impending herniation.

⭐ Key point: A change from decorticate to decerebrate posturing is a sign of worsening brain injury — report immediately.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q153$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1270, $s$A patient sustains a rotational acceleration-deceleration injury in a high-speed motor vehicle collision. The initial CT is normal, but the patient remains comatose for several days. Which type of head injury is most consistent with this presentation?$s$, $s$["Concussion", "Depressed skull fracture", "Diffuse axonal injury", "Epidural hematoma"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Diffuse axonal injury
DIFFUSE AXONAL INJURY (DAI) is caused by ROTATIONAL ACCELERATION-DECELERATION forces (e.g. high-speed car crashes). The brain twists and shears inside the skull, tearing AXONS (nerve fibres) throughout the brain. Classic picture: the patient is in a COMA from the moment of injury, but the INITIAL CT may be NORMAL or show only small bleeds (MRI is more sensitive). Prognosis is often poor.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Concussion — Concussion is a mild, temporary brain dysfunction; patients recover consciousness quickly.
• Depressed skull fracture — Depressed skull fracture would show on CT.
• Epidural hematoma — Epidural haematoma would show as a lens-shaped bleed on CT, often with a lucid interval.

⭐ Key point: DAI is graded by location (grade I: white matter; II: corpus callosum; III: brainstem). Care is supportive: prevent secondary brain injury (oxygen, BP, ICP control).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q161$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1271, $s$A patient with a chronic spinal cord injury at T4 develops a sudden pounding headache, severe hypertension, profuse sweating and flushing above the injury level, and bradycardia after their urinary catheter becomes blocked. This presentation is most consistent with$s$, $s$["spinal shock", "a simple tension headache", "Cushing's triad from raised intracranial pressure", "autonomic dysreflexia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: autonomic dysreflexia
AUTONOMIC DYSREFLEXIA occurs in spinal cord injuries at or above T6. A stimulus below the injury (most commonly a BLOCKED URINARY CATHETER or full bladder) triggers a massive sympathetic reflex, causing SEVERE HYPERTENSION. The body tries to lower it, causing BRADYCARDIA, POUNDING HEADACHE, and FLUSHING and SWEATING ABOVE the injury level.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• spinal shock — Spinal shock is a temporary loss of reflexes right after injury.
• a simple tension headache — A tension headache does not cause severe hypertension and bradycardia.
• Cushing's triad from raised intracranial pressure — Cushing's triad is caused by raised intracranial pressure, not a blocked catheter.

⭐ Key point: Sit the patient up, find and remove the trigger (check catheter, bowels, skin), and give antihypertensives if needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q174 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1272, $s$What is considered the normal range for brain tissue oxygen tension (PbtO2) measured by a Licox monitor?$s$, $s$["100-150 mmHg", "5-10 mmHg", "60-100 mmHg", "20-40 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 20-40 mmHg
Brain tissue oxygen tension (PbtO2) measures the oxygen level in the brain tissue. The normal range is about 20–40 mmHg (often quoted as 25–35). Values below 20 mmHg indicate brain tissue hypoxia, and below 10–15 mmHg indicate severe hypoxia with high risk of brain damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100-150 mmHg — 100–150 mmHg is far too high (it's like arterial PaO2 on extra oxygen).
• 5-10 mmHg — 5–10 mmHg indicates severe brain hypoxia.
• 60-100 mmHg — 60–100 mmHg is the normal arterial PaO2, not brain tissue.

⭐ Key point: Treatment targets: keep PbtO2 above 20 mmHg.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q43$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1273, $s$Abnormal flexion (decorticate posturing) corresponds to which motor score on the Glasgow Coma Scale?$s$, $s$["6", "5", "4", "3"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3
In the GCS motor score, ABNORMAL FLEXION (decorticate posturing: arms bent up to the chest, wrists flexed, legs extended) scores M3. It shows damage above the brainstem (cerebral hemispheres or internal capsule).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 6 — M6 means obeying commands.
• 5 — M5 means localising to pain.
• 4 — M4 means normal flexion (withdrawal) from pain.

⭐ Key point: GCS motor: 6 obeys, 5 localises, 4 withdraws, 3 abnormal flexion (decorticate), 2 extension (decerebrate), 1 none.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q62 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, true, 1274, $s$After the initial assessment of a patient with suspected stroke, the next immediate step is:$s$, $s$["Obtaining a neurosurgical consultation", "Preparing for carotid Doppler ultrasonography", "Administering tissue plasminogen activator", "Obtaining a non-contrast CT scan of the head"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Obtaining a non-contrast CT scan of the head
After initial assessment (ABC, glucose), the next immediate step in suspected stroke is an urgent NON-CONTRAST CT of the head. It distinguishes ISCHAEMIC stroke (a clot, which may be treated with thrombolysis) from HAEMORRHAGIC stroke (a bleed, where thrombolysis would be fatal). Time is brain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Obtaining a neurosurgical consultation — Neurosurgical consultation may follow if CT shows a bleed.
• Preparing for carotid Doppler ultrasonography — Carotid Doppler is done later to look for the cause.
• Administering tissue plasminogen activator — tPA (thrombolysis) can only be given AFTER CT excludes bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Thrombolysis for ischaemic stroke should be given within 4.5 hours of symptom onset, ideally within 60 minutes of arrival.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q69$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1275, $s$Cerebrospinal fluid circulates within the$s$, $s$["epidural space", "subdural space", "subarachnoid space", "potential space between periosteum and dura"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: subarachnoid space
CSF flows in the SUBARACHNOID space, between the arachnoid mater and the pia mater, and in the ventricles and central canal. It is made mainly by the choroid plexus (about 500 mL/day; about 150 mL present at a time) and absorbed by the arachnoid villi into the venous sinuses. It cushions the brain and removes waste. A lumbar puncture samples CSF from this space below L1–L2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• epidural space — The epidural space lies outside the dura and contains fat and veins; epidural anaesthesia goes here, not CSF.
• subdural space — The subdural space lies between the dura and arachnoid; a subdural haematoma collects here.
• potential space between periosteum and dura — The space between the periosteum and dura is the epidural space in the skull, where an extradural haematoma forms.

⭐ Key point: Subarachnoid = CSF. A subarachnoid haemorrhage puts blood into the CSF, giving a thunderclap headache and neck stiffness.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q12$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1276, $s$A patient presents with ptosis, a pupil that is fixed and dilated, and the eye deviated "down and out." Which cranial nerve palsy best explains this presentation?$s$, $s$["Cranial nerve III (oculomotor)", "Cranial nerve IV (trochlear)", "Cranial nerve VI (abducens)", "Cranial nerve VII (facial)"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cranial nerve III (oculomotor)
The oculomotor nerve (CN III) moves most eye muscles, lifts the eyelid (levator palpebrae) and carries parasympathetic fibres that constrict the pupil. When it is paralysed, the unopposed lateral rectus (CN VI) and superior oblique (CN IV) pull the eye "down and out". The lid droops (ptosis), and the pupil is fixed and dilated. In head injury, a new dilated pupil means the uncus is herniating and pressing on CN III.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cranial nerve IV (trochlear) — A CN IV (trochlear) palsy causes vertical double vision, especially going downstairs, without ptosis or a dilated pupil.
• Cranial nerve VI (abducens) — A CN VI (abducens) palsy stops the eye moving outwards; the eye turns in.
• Cranial nerve VII (facial) — A CN VII (facial) palsy causes facial weakness and inability to close the eye, not ptosis with a dilated pupil.

⭐ Key point: A fixed dilated pupil on one side after head injury is a NEUROSURGICAL EMERGENCY (raised ICP and herniation). Report it at once.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q13$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1277, $s$A Processor acts like a/an$s$, $s$["HEART", "ARM", "BRAIN", "KIDNEY"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: BRAIN
The processor (CPU, central processing unit) is the "brain" of the computer. It carries out instructions, does calculations and controls all other parts, just as the brain controls the body. Its speed is measured in gigahertz (GHz).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• HEART — The heart pumps blood; in a computer, the power supply is a better comparison.
• ARM — An arm is like an output device (it carries out actions), not the processor.
• KIDNEY — The kidney filters waste; there is no CPU comparison with the kidney.

⭐ Key point: CPU = brain, RAM = short-term memory (working desk), hard disk = long-term storage (filing cabinet), and input/output devices = the senses and hands.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q46$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1278, $s$The following statement regarding extradural haematoma are true EXCEPT$s$, $s$["It is often caused by a skull fracture with laceration of the middle meningeal artery", "It commonly occurs at the pterion", "There is often a lucid interval", "Prognosis is poor"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Prognosis is poor
The question asks which statement is NOT true. Extradural haematoma usually has a GOOD prognosis if it is found and drained quickly, because the brain beneath is often not badly damaged. The statement "prognosis is poor" is therefore false. Delay is what makes it fatal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It is often caused by a skull fracture with laceration of the middle meningeal artery — It IS usually caused by a skull fracture tearing the middle meningeal artery (true).
• It commonly occurs at the pterion — It DOES commonly occur at the pterion, the thin part of the temporal skull (true).
• There is often a lucid interval — There IS often a lucid interval: knocked out, wakes up and talks, then quickly deteriorates (true).

⭐ Key point: Extradural = arterial bleed, lens-shaped (biconvex) on CT, lucid interval, emergency surgery. Subdural = venous bleed, crescent-shaped, often in elderly and alcoholics.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q85$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1279, $s$The Kellie-Monro (Monro-Kellie) Doctrine states which of the following?$s$, $s$["The skull is a rigid, non-expansile compartment; an increase in volume of one intracranial component requires a reciprocal decrease in another to keep ICP constant", "The brain can expand infinitely without pressure changes", "CSF volume is fixed and cannot change", "Cerebral blood flow is independent of ICP"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The skull is a rigid, non-expansile compartment; an increase in volume of one intracranial component requires a reciprocal decrease in another to keep ICP constant
The Monro–Kellie doctrine says the skull is a rigid, closed box containing three things: brain (about 80%), blood (about 10%) and CSF (about 10%). The total volume must stay constant. If one part increases (a tumour, a bleed, swelling), another must decrease, for example CSF is pushed into the spinal canal or venous blood leaves. When these compensations run out, ICP rises steeply.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The brain can expand infinitely without pressure changes — The brain cannot expand freely inside the rigid skull.
• CSF volume is fixed and cannot change — CSF volume does change; displacing CSF is the first compensation.
• Cerebral blood flow is independent of ICP — Cerebral blood flow depends on CPP (MAP − ICP), so it is not independent of ICP.

⭐ Key point: The ICP volume–pressure curve is flat at first, then steep. Once compensation is used up, a small extra volume (coughing, suction) causes a big rise in ICP.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q87$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1280, $s$Signs of raised intracranial pressure include all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Early-morning headache", "Nausea and vomiting", "Papilloedema", "Loss of hearing"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Loss of hearing
The question asks for the sign that does NOT belong. Loss of hearing is not a sign of raised ICP. Raised ICP classically causes headache that is worse in the morning (CO₂ rises during sleep and on lying down), nausea and vomiting (often projectile), papilloedema (swollen optic discs), visual changes, a falling GCS, pupil changes and Cushing's triad late.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Early-morning headache — Early-morning headache IS a classic sign of raised ICP.
• Nausea and vomiting — Nausea and vomiting ARE classic signs of raised ICP.
• Papilloedema — Papilloedema IS a classic sign of raised ICP.

⭐ Key point: The EARLIEST and most sensitive sign of raised ICP is a change in the level of consciousness (restlessness, confusion, drowsiness).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q88$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1281, $s$The signs of an upper motor neuron lesion include all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Muscular spasticity", "Brisk deep tendon reflexes", "Positive Babinski response", "Fibrillations"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fibrillations
The question asks for the sign that does NOT belong. Fibrillations (tiny spontaneous twitches of single muscle fibres, seen on EMG) and fasciculations are signs of a LOWER motor neurone lesion, not an upper one. Upper motor neurone lesions (brain or spinal cord: stroke, MS, spinal injury) cause spasticity, increased (brisk) reflexes, clonus and a positive Babinski sign, with little muscle wasting.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Muscular spasticity — Spasticity IS an upper motor neurone sign.
• Brisk deep tendon reflexes — Brisk deep tendon reflexes ARE an upper motor neurone sign.
• Positive Babinski response — A positive Babinski sign (big toe goes up) IS an upper motor neurone sign.

⭐ Key point: UMN = spastic, brisk reflexes, upgoing toe. LMN = floppy (flaccid), lost reflexes, wasting, fasciculations and fibrillations (as in GBS, polio and peripheral nerve injury).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q89$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1282, $s$A Licox brain tissue oxygen monitor measures which of the following?$s$, $s$["Brain tissue oxygen tension (PbtO2)", "Intracranial pressure only", "Cerebral blood flow velocity", "Serum oxygen saturation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Brain tissue oxygen tension (PbtO2)
The Licox probe is placed in the brain tissue (white matter) and measures the partial pressure of oxygen there (PbtO₂), and also brain temperature. It shows whether the brain is getting enough oxygen. Normal PbtO₂ is about 20–35 mmHg; below 20 needs treatment and below 10–15 means serious brain hypoxia with a poor outcome.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intracranial pressure only — ICP is measured by a separate probe or an EVD; Licox measures oxygen.
• Cerebral blood flow velocity — Cerebral blood flow velocity is measured by transcranial Doppler.
• Serum oxygen saturation — Serum (arterial) oxygen saturation is measured by an ABG or pulse oximeter.

⭐ Key point: To improve low PbtO₂: raise FiO₂, raise CPP (MAP), treat high ICP, correct anaemia, avoid hyperventilation (low CO₂), and treat fever and seizures.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q90$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1283, $s$Causes of Brain death are EXCEPT$s$, $s$["An acute catastrophic brain injury.", "Abrupt loss of cerebral perfusion", "Extracranial brain injury, as seen in patients following cardiopulmonary arrest", "Good brain oxygenation."]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Good brain oxygenation.
The question asks which is NOT a cause of brain death. Good brain oxygenation keeps brain cells alive; it is the opposite of what causes brain death. Brain death happens when the whole brain, including the brainstem, is irreversibly destroyed, usually by severe injury, swelling or loss of blood and oxygen supply.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An acute catastrophic brain injury. — An acute catastrophic brain injury (trauma, a huge bleed) IS a cause.
• Abrupt loss of cerebral perfusion — Abrupt loss of cerebral perfusion (very high ICP stopping blood flow) IS a cause.
• Extracranial brain injury, as seen in patients following cardiopulmonary arrest — Brain injury from outside the skull, such as lack of oxygen after cardiac arrest (hypoxic-ischaemic injury), IS a cause.

⭐ Key point: Before testing for brain death, exclude reversible causes: sedative drugs, hypothermia, severe metabolic or endocrine problems, and neuromuscular blockers.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q91$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1284, $s$Brudzinski's sign is positive when which of the following occurs?$s$, $s$["Pain on hip flexion with knee extension", "Involuntary flexion of the hips and knees when the examiner passively flexes the patient's neck", "Dilation of the pupils with light exposure", "Rigid extension of the leg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Involuntary flexion of the hips and knees when the examiner passively flexes the patient's neck
Brudzinski's sign tests for meningeal irritation (meningitis, subarachnoid haemorrhage). With the patient lying flat, the examiner gently bends the patient's head forward toward the chest. If the patient's hips and knees bend up on their own (involuntarily), the sign is positive. Bending the neck stretches the inflamed meninges, and the body bends the legs to reduce the stretch.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pain on hip flexion with knee extension — Pain on hip flexion with the knee straight describes a different test (Kernig's sign checks pain or resistance when straightening the knee with the hip bent).
• Dilation of the pupils with light exposure — Pupils dilating with light is abnormal but has nothing to do with Brudzinski's sign (pupils normally constrict).
• Rigid extension of the leg — A rigid straight leg is not the Brudzinski response.

⭐ Key point: Meningitis signs: neck stiffness, photophobia, fever, headache, Kernig's and Brudzinski's signs, and a purpuric rash in meningococcal disease. Give antibiotics without delay and use droplet precautions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q92$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1285, $s$What is the recommended treatment for a patient with Guillain-Barré syndrome?$s$, $s$["Antibiotics", "Hospitalization for observation", "Plasmapheresis or immunoglobulin therapy", "No treatment is required"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Plasmapheresis or immunoglobulin therapy
Guillain–Barré syndrome is an autoimmune attack on peripheral nerves, often after an infection. Proven treatments that shorten the illness and improve recovery are PLASMA EXCHANGE (plasmapheresis, which removes the harmful antibodies) or IV IMMUNOGLOBULIN (IVIG, 0.4 g/kg/day for 5 days). Both work equally well; they are not given together. Supportive ICU care (breathing, autonomic monitoring, DVT prevention, pain control, nutrition) is essential.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Antibiotics — Antibiotics do not treat the immune nerve damage (the triggering infection has usually passed).
• Hospitalization for observation — Observation alone is not enough for significant weakness; specific treatment should start early.
• No treatment is required — GBS can paralyse the breathing muscles, so leaving it untreated is dangerous.

⭐ Key point: Steroids do NOT help GBS. Monitor FVC every 4 hours: below 20 mL/kg needs ICU; below 15 mL/kg usually needs intubation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q93$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1286, $s$Non-pharmacological management of delirium focuses on risk factors such as:$s$, $s$["Dehydration, constipation and sleep deprivation", "Reorientation, clear communication and immobility", "Pain control, nutrition and early removal of catheters", "All of the above"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
Delirium usually has several causes at once, so non-drug prevention tackles all the risk factors together. That includes treating dehydration and constipation, protecting sleep (night-time quiet, dim lights, clustered care), reorienting the patient (clock, calendar, glasses, hearing aids, family), communicating clearly, early mobilisation, good pain control, adequate nutrition, and removing catheters and lines early. All the groups listed are correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dehydration, constipation and sleep deprivation — This group is part of the answer but not all of it.
• Reorientation, clear communication and immobility — This group is part of the answer but not all of it.
• Pain control, nutrition and early removal of catheters — This group is part of the answer but not all of it.

⭐ Key point: A useful memory aid for the causes of delirium is "PINCH ME": Pain, Infection, Nutrition, Constipation, Hydration, Medication, Environment. Screen every shift with CAM-ICU.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q98$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1287, $s$Thiamine (vitamin B1) deficiency results in which potential maternal complication of hyperemesis gravidarum?$s$, $s$["Renal failure", "Esophageal rupture", "Wernicke encephalopathy", "Osmotic demyelination syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Wernicke encephalopathy
In hyperemesis gravidarum, prolonged vomiting and poor intake use up thiamine (vitamin B1) stores, especially if IV glucose is given without thiamine. Thiamine deficiency causes Wernicke's encephalopathy: confusion, ataxia (unsteady walking), and eye problems (nystagmus, ophthalmoplegia). It can lead to permanent memory loss (Korsakoff syndrome) or death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal failure — Renal failure can come from dehydration, but it is not caused by thiamine deficiency.
• Esophageal rupture — Oesophageal rupture (Boerhaave syndrome) can follow forceful vomiting, but not thiamine deficiency.
• Osmotic demyelination syndrome — Osmotic demyelination comes from correcting low sodium too fast, not thiamine deficiency.

⭐ Key point: Give THIAMINE before or with IV glucose in any malnourished patient (hyperemesis, alcohol misuse, starvation), and replace fluids and electrolytes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q139$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1288, $s$Which meningeal layer lies closest to the brain surface?$s$, $s$["Dura mater", "Arachnoid mater", "Pia mater", "Periosteum"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pia mater
The brain and spinal cord are covered by three MENINGES. From outside in, the memory aid is "PAD" read backwards — Dura, Arachnoid, Pia (D-A-P from outside). DURA MATER ("tough mother") is the thick outer layer next to the skull. ARACHNOID MATER is the middle, web-like layer; under it is the SUBARACHNOID space containing cerebrospinal fluid (CSF) and big blood vessels. PIA MATER ("gentle mother") is the thinnest, innermost layer, stuck firmly to the brain surface and following every fold (gyri and sulci). Clinical: a subarachnoid haemorrhage bleeds into the space above the pia; lumbar puncture collects CSF from the subarachnoid space; meningitis is inflammation of the arachnoid and pia (leptomeninges).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dura mater — DURA MATER is the OUTERMOST meninx, lying against the inside of the skull — the farthest from the brain.
• Arachnoid mater — ARACHNOID MATER is the MIDDLE layer, separated from the brain by the CSF-filled subarachnoid space.
• Periosteum — PERIOSTEUM covers bone (the skull), so it is outside all three meninges; it is not a meningeal layer at all.

⭐ Key point: Meninges outside → in: Dura, Arachnoid, PIA. Pia is the one stuck on the brain surface.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q11$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1289, $s$The brainstem consists of:$s$, $s$["Midbrain, hindbrain and forebrain", "Hindbrain, ventricle and pons", "Midbrain, pons and forebrain", "Midbrain, pons and medulla"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Midbrain, pons and medulla
The BRAINSTEM connects the brain to the spinal cord and has three parts, top to bottom: MIDBRAIN, PONS and MEDULLA OBLONGATA. It contains the nuclei of cranial nerves III–XII, the centres for breathing, heart rate and blood pressure (mainly the medulla), and the reticular activating system that keeps us awake. That is why brainstem damage or herniation (from raised ICP) causes coma, abnormal pupils, irregular breathing and Cushing's triad (high BP, slow pulse, irregular breathing), and why brainstem reflexes are tested for brain death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Midbrain, hindbrain and forebrain — "Midbrain, hindbrain and forebrain" are the three main developmental divisions of the WHOLE brain; the forebrain (cerebrum, thalamus) is not part of the brainstem.
• Hindbrain, ventricle and pons — A VENTRICLE is a CSF-filled cavity (the fourth ventricle lies behind the pons and medulla), not a part of the brainstem.
• Midbrain, pons and forebrain — The FOREBRAIN (cerebral hemispheres, thalamus, hypothalamus) sits above the brainstem, so it cannot be part of it.

⭐ Key point: Brainstem = MIDBRAIN + PONS + MEDULLA. Medulla holds vital breathing and cardiovascular centres.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q12$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1290, $s$CT head findings suggestive of raised intracranial pressure include:$s$, $s$["Symmetric ventricles with clear sulci", "Midline shift and effacement (compression) of the ventricles", "Normal grey-white matter differentiation only", "Absence of any mass effect"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Midline shift and effacement (compression) of the ventricles
In RAISED INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP), something takes up extra space inside the rigid skull (bleed, tumour, swelling). On CT head this shows as MASS EFFECT: MIDLINE SHIFT (the brain is pushed to one side, a shift over 5 mm is significant), EFFACEMENT (squashing) of the lateral ventricles, loss of sulci (the grooves on the brain surface are flattened by swollen brain), effacement of the basal cisterns, loss of grey–white matter differentiation in diffuse oedema, and signs of herniation. These findings warn of the risk of brain herniation and need urgent neurosurgical review and ICP-lowering measures (head up 30°, normal CO2, osmotherapy such as mannitol or hypertonic saline).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Symmetric ventricles with clear sulci — SYMMETRIC ventricles and CLEAR sulci are NORMAL findings — no pressure effect.
• Normal grey-white matter differentiation only — Normal grey–white differentiation is a normal finding; LOSS of it suggests oedema.
• Absence of any mass effect — ABSENCE of mass effect means no space-occupying pressure, the opposite of raised ICP.

⭐ Key point: Raised ICP on CT = MIDLINE SHIFT, compressed ventricles, effaced sulci/cisterns.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q42$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1291, $s$A patient with a Glasgow Coma Scale eye-opening score of 2 demonstrates:$s$, $s$["Spontaneous eye opening", "Eye opening to voice", "Eye opening to pain only", "No eye opening at all"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Eye opening to pain only
The GLASGOW COMA SCALE (GCS) has three parts, total 3–15. EYE OPENING (E, 1–4): 4 = spontaneous; 3 = to voice/speech; 2 = to PAIN (pressure) only; 1 = none. VERBAL (V, 1–5): 5 oriented, 4 confused, 3 inappropriate words, 2 incomprehensible sounds, 1 none. MOTOR (M, 1–6): 6 obeys commands, 5 localises pain, 4 withdraws (normal flexion), 3 abnormal flexion (decorticate), 2 extension (decerebrate), 1 none. So E2 means EYES OPEN ONLY TO A PAINFUL STIMULUS (e.g. trapezius squeeze, supraorbital pressure, nail-bed pressure).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Spontaneous eye opening — SPONTANEOUS eye opening = E4.
• Eye opening to voice — Eye opening to VOICE = E3.
• No eye opening at all — NO eye opening even to pain = E1.

⭐ Key point: GCS eyes: 4 spontaneous, 3 voice, 2 PAIN, 1 none. Verbal max 5; motor max 6; total 3–15.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q85$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1292, $s$The coup-contrecoup mechanism of brain injury describes which of the following?$s$, $s$["Injury only at the site of direct impact", "Diffuse shearing injury without focal contusion", "Injury at the site of impact and at the opposite pole of the brain from movement within the skull", "A fracture line radiating from the impact point"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Injury at the site of impact and at the opposite pole of the brain from movement within the skull
In a COUP-CONTRECOUP injury, the head is hit (or suddenly stops, e.g. in a car crash). The brain hits the inside of the skull at the point of impact (COUP injury), then rebounds and hits the OPPOSITE side of the skull (CONTRECOUP injury). So bruises (contusions) appear at BOTH the site of impact AND the opposite pole of the brain. Example: a fall onto the back of the head often causes contusions in the FRONTAL and TEMPORAL lobes at the front. Nursing: assess the whole brain function, not just the impact area; watch for delayed swelling and rising ICP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Injury only at the site of direct impact — Injury ONLY at the site of direct impact is a coup injury alone — it ignores the contrecoup part.
• Diffuse shearing injury without focal contusion — DIFFUSE SHEARING without focal contusion describes DIFFUSE AXONAL INJURY (from rotational acceleration), not coup-contrecoup.
• A fracture line radiating from the impact point — A fracture line radiating from the impact point describes a LINEAR SKULL FRACTURE, not brain contusion.

⭐ Key point: Coup-contrecoup = injury at the IMPACT site AND the OPPOSITE side of the brain from rebound inside the skull.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q86$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1293, $s$Autonomic dysreflexia in a patient with a spinal cord injury above T6 is typically triggered by:$s$, $s$["A noxious stimulus below the level of injury, such as a distended bladder", "Mild anxiety only", "Normal ambient room temperature", "Eating a regular meal"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A noxious stimulus below the level of injury, such as a distended bladder
AUTONOMIC DYSREFLEXIA (AD) happens in spinal cord injury at or ABOVE T6. A NOXIOUS (irritating) stimulus BELOW the injury level — most commonly a FULL BLADDER (blocked or kinked catheter), then a full bowel/constipation, pressure ulcer, tight clothes, ingrown toenail, sunburn — causes a massive sympathetic reflex below the injury. Blood vessels constrict, causing SEVERE HYPERTENSION (can be over 200 mmHg — risk of stroke, seizure, death). The brain tries to respond through the vagus (BRADYCARDIA) and vasodilation above the lesion (POUNDING HEADACHE, FLUSHING and SWEATING above the injury, nasal congestion), but the message cannot pass down the damaged cord. Action: SIT THE PATIENT UP (lower the legs), loosen tight clothing, find and remove the cause (check catheter, empty bladder, check bowel), monitor BP every 2–5 minutes, give antihypertensive (e.g. nifedipine, GTN) if BP stays high.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild anxiety only — MILD ANXIETY alone does not trigger this dangerous reflex.
• Normal ambient room temperature — Normal room temperature is not a noxious stimulus.
• Eating a regular meal — Eating a normal meal is not a trigger (although a full bowel/constipation can be).

⭐ Key point: Autonomic dysreflexia (SCI ≥ T6) = noxious stimulus below injury, usually FULL BLADDER → severe hypertension. SIT UP, find the cause.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q87$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1294, $s$Bilaterally pinpoint pupils in an unresponsive patient may suggest:$s$, $s$["Opioid toxicity or a pontine lesion", "Anticholinergic drug effect", "Normal resting pupil size in bright light only", "Complete brain death in all cases"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Opioid toxicity or a pontine lesion
PINPOINT PUPILS (very small, under 2 mm) on both sides in an unconscious patient suggest two main causes: (1) OPIOID TOXICITY (morphine, heroin, fentanyl, tramadol) — the classic triad is pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression and reduced consciousness; give NALOXONE (0.4 mg IV, repeat as needed). Organophosphate poisoning also causes small pupils. (2) A PONTINE LESION (e.g. pontine haemorrhage or infarction) — this damages descending sympathetic fibres, so the pupils constrict; the patient is often deeply comatose with high temperature. Always check blood glucose and consider naloxone trial.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anticholinergic drug effect — ANTICHOLINERGIC drugs (atropine, tricyclic antidepressants, antihistamines) cause DILATED pupils ("blind as a bat, mad as a hatter, hot as a hare, dry as a bone").
• Normal resting pupil size in bright light only — Normal pupils in bright light are small but not pinpoint, and an UNRESPONSIVE patient with pinpoint pupils is not "normal".
• Complete brain death in all cases — BRAIN DEATH usually shows FIXED, mid-sized or DILATED pupils, not pinpoint.

⭐ Key point: Bilateral PINPOINT pupils + coma = OPIOIDS (give naloxone) or PONTINE lesion. Dilated pupils = anticholinergic, brain death, herniation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q89$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1295, $s$Which of the following is a preventable secondary brain injury factor that critical care nurses must aggressively manage after TBI?$s$, $s$["The initial mechanical impact", "Skull fracture location", "Scalp laceration", "Hypotension and hypoxia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypotension and hypoxia
In TRAUMATIC BRAIN INJURY (TBI), PRIMARY injury happens at the moment of impact and cannot be undone. SECONDARY injury develops over hours to days and can be PREVENTED — this is the main focus of critical care. The most damaging secondary factors are HYPOTENSION (systolic below 90–110 mmHg) and HYPOXIA (SpO2 below 90% or PaO2 below 60 mmHg): even one episode greatly increases death and disability, because the injured brain cannot tolerate low oxygen delivery. Other secondary factors: high or low CO2, fever, seizures, hypo/hyperglycaemia, low sodium, raised ICP, anaemia. Nursing aims: SpO2 ≥ 94%, systolic BP ≥ 100–110 mmHg, CPP 60–70 mmHg, normocapnia (PaCO2 35–45), normothermia, glucose control.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The initial mechanical impact — The INITIAL MECHANICAL IMPACT is the PRIMARY injury — it has already happened and cannot be prevented by nurses.
• Skull fracture location — SKULL FRACTURE LOCATION is fixed at the time of injury; it guides assessment but is not modifiable.
• Scalp laceration — A SCALP LACERATION needs care (it can bleed heavily) but is not a secondary brain injury factor.

⭐ Key point: Prevent SECONDARY brain injury: avoid HYPOTENSION and HYPOXIA (also high/low CO2, fever, seizures, glucose problems).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q90$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1296, $s$The pupillary light reflex tests cranial nerves:$s$, $s$["I and II", "IV and V", "IV and VI", "II and III"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: II and III
The PUPILLARY LIGHT REFLEX has two nerves: the AFFERENT (sensory) limb is the OPTIC NERVE (CN II), which carries the light signal from the retina to the brainstem; the EFFERENT (motor) limb is the OCULOMOTOR NERVE (CN III), whose parasympathetic fibres make the pupil CONSTRICT. Shining light in one eye normally makes BOTH pupils constrict (direct and consensual response). A FIXED, DILATED pupil on one side in a head-injured patient often means CN III is being compressed by uncal HERNIATION — a neurosurgical emergency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• I and II — CN I (olfactory) is smell and CN II is vision; CN I is not part of the light reflex.
• IV and V — CN IV (trochlear) moves the eye down and in; CN V (trigeminal) is facial sensation and chewing — the corneal reflex uses V, not the light reflex.
• IV and VI — CN IV and CN VI (abducens) control eye movements, not pupil size.

⭐ Key point: Pupillary light reflex: CN II in (sensory), CN III out (motor). Unilateral fixed dilated pupil = CN III compression (herniation).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q91$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1297, $s$Your patient's eyes do not open to noxious stimuli and they respond incorrectly when asked questions and localize to pain. What is their GCS?$s$, $s$["7", "8", "9", "10"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 9
Score each part of the GCS: EYES — do not open even to noxious (painful) stimulus = E1. VERBAL — "responds INCORRECTLY when asked questions" is scored by this exam key as INAPPROPRIATE WORDS = V3 (words that do not answer the question). MOTOR — LOCALISES to pain (moves a hand towards the painful stimulus to remove it) = M5. Total: E1 + V3 + M5 = 9. Exam tip: if the stem had said "confused but answers in sentences", that would be V4 (total 10) — so read the verbal description carefully. Remember GCS ≤ 8 = coma/"intubate", 9–12 moderate, 13–15 mild brain injury. Always document each component (E1 V3 M5), not just the total.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 7 — 7 would need lower scores (e.g. E1 V2 M4) — it underestimates this patient.
• 8 — 8 would be E1 V2 M5 or E1 V3 M4 — it does not match "localises to pain" with words.
• 10 — 10 would only be correct if the verbal response were scored as "confused" (V4); the key here takes it as inappropriate words (V3).

⭐ Key point: GCS = E(1–4) + V(1–5) + M(1–6). Here E1 + V3 + M5 = 9. Always report each part.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q92$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1298, $s$What degree of head-of-bed elevation is recommended to reduce intracranial pressure while maintaining cerebral perfusion?$s$, $s$["Flat at 0 degrees", "15 degrees of elevation", "30 degrees of elevation with neutral head and neck alignment", "90 degrees, fully upright"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 30 degrees of elevation with neutral head and neck alignment
To lower INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP) while keeping CEREBRAL PERFUSION, raise the HEAD OF BED to about 30 DEGREES and keep the HEAD and NECK in a NEUTRAL (midline) position. This helps venous blood drain from the brain through the jugular veins, lowering ICP, without dropping BP too much (CPP = MAP − ICP; target CPP 60–70 mmHg). Avoid neck flexion or rotation, tight ETT ties or cervical collars that compress the jugular veins. Other measures: avoid hip flexion over 90°, keep the patient calm and pain-free, cluster nursing care, avoid coughing/straining, normothermia, normal CO2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flat at 0 degrees — FLAT at 0° reduces venous drainage and raises ICP.
• 15 degrees of elevation — 15° is less effective for venous drainage than 30°.
• 90 degrees, fully upright — 90° fully upright can drop MAP and cerebral perfusion pressure, and is uncomfortable/unsafe in unstable patients.

⭐ Key point: Raised ICP → head of bed 30° + NEUTRAL head/neck alignment (no jugular compression).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q93$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1299, $s$Which of the following findings is NOT compatible with a diagnosis of brain death?$s$, $s$["Fixed and dilated pupils", "Absent corneal reflex", "Absent gag reflex", "Spontaneous respiratory effort during apnea testing"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Spontaneous respiratory effort during apnea testing
BRAIN DEATH (brainstem death) means permanent loss of all brain and brainstem function. Criteria include: known cause and exclusion of reversible factors (sedatives, hypothermia, metabolic problems); deep coma with no response; ABSENT brainstem reflexes — FIXED pupils (unreactive to light), ABSENT CORNEAL reflex, absent oculocephalic/oculovestibular reflexes, ABSENT GAG and cough reflexes, no motor response in the cranial nerve area; and a positive APNOEA TEST — no breathing effort when PaCO2 is allowed to rise above 60 mmHg (or 6.65 kPa per UK criteria) and pH is below 7.30. Any SPONTANEOUS BREATHING EFFORT during apnoea testing means the brainstem respiratory centre is still working, so brain death CANNOT be diagnosed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fixed and dilated pupils — FIXED DILATED pupils ARE consistent with brain death.
• Absent corneal reflex — ABSENT CORNEAL reflex IS consistent with brain death.
• Absent gag reflex — ABSENT GAG reflex IS consistent with brain death.

⭐ Key point: Any SPONTANEOUS BREATHING during the apnoea test rules out brain death.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q95$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1300, $s$Which type of ICU delirium is most commonly MISSED by staff?$s$, $s$["Hyperactive", "Hypoactive", "Mixed", "All types are equally easy to spot"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypoactive
Delirium has three types. HYPERACTIVE — agitated, restless, pulling at lines, aggressive; easy to notice. HYPOACTIVE — quiet, withdrawn, sleepy, slow, staring, little movement or speech; the patient seems "calm" or "just tired" or depressed, so it is often MISSED by staff, even though it is the MOST COMMON type in ICU and older patients and has the WORST outcome (higher mortality, longer stay). MIXED — swings between the two. That is why every ICU patient should be screened regularly (e.g. every shift) with a tool like the CAM-ICU or ICDSC, not just those who are agitated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperactive — HYPERACTIVE delirium is the most noticeable type because of agitation, so it is rarely missed.
• Mixed — MIXED delirium is usually noticed during the hyperactive phases.
• All types are equally easy to spot — Not all types are equally easy to spot — hypoactive is clearly the hardest.

⭐ Key point: HYPOACTIVE delirium (quiet, sleepy) is the most commonly MISSED. Screen all patients with CAM-ICU.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q97$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1301, $s$A patient with hyperactive delirium is prescribed quetiapine. The nurse monitors for:$s$, $s$["QT prolongation", "Hypertension", "Hyperglycemia", "A and C"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A and C
QUETIAPINE is an atypical (second-generation) ANTIPSYCHOTIC sometimes used for agitated ICU delirium. Key monitoring: (1) QT PROLONGATION — antipsychotics can lengthen the QT interval, risking torsades de pointes (a dangerous ventricular arrhythmia); check a baseline ECG and repeat it, keep potassium and magnesium normal, avoid other QT-prolonging drugs (e.g. haloperidol, macrolides, ondansetron). (2) HYPERGLYCAEMIA and metabolic effects — atypical antipsychotics can raise blood sugar and lipids; monitor glucose. Other side effects: sedation, ORTHOSTATIC HYPOTENSION (low BP, not high), anticholinergic effects, and rarely neuroleptic malignant syndrome.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• QT prolongation — QT prolongation alone is correct but incomplete, since hyperglycaemia must also be monitored.
• Hypertension — HYPERTENSION is not a typical effect; quetiapine more often causes HYPOTENSION (postural drop) from alpha-blockade.
• Hyperglycemia — Hyperglycaemia alone is correct but incomplete, since QT prolongation is also a key risk.

⭐ Key point: Quetiapine/antipsychotics: monitor QT (ECG, K⁺, Mg²⁺) AND blood GLUCOSE. They cause low BP, not high.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q98$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1302, $s$Encephalopathy toxins implicated in hepatic failure include all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Ammonia", "False neurotransmitters", "GABA", "Insulin"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Insulin
In HEPATIC ENCEPHALOPATHY, the failing liver cannot clear toxins coming from the gut, so they reach the brain. Main toxins: AMMONIA (made by gut bacteria from protein; the key toxin), FALSE NEUROTRANSMITTERS (e.g. octopamine, from altered amino acid balance), increased GABA-ergic tone (GABA and endogenous benzodiazepine-like substances), mercaptans, short-chain fatty acids and manganese. Signs: confusion, sleep reversal, asterixis (flapping tremor), fetor hepaticus, coma. Treatment: find triggers (GI bleed, infection, constipation, dehydration, sedatives, high protein), LACTULOSE (traps ammonia in the gut, aim 2–3 soft stools daily) and RIFAXIMIN. INSULIN is a normal hormone that controls glucose; it is not an encephalopathy toxin (liver failure actually causes HYPOGLYCAEMIA).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ammonia — AMMONIA IS the main toxin — lactulose is used to reduce it.
• False neurotransmitters — FALSE NEUROTRANSMITTERS ARE implicated.
• GABA — GABA IS implicated — increased inhibitory tone in the brain.

⭐ Key point: Hepatic encephalopathy toxins = AMMONIA, false neurotransmitters, GABA. Treat with LACTULOSE + rifaximin. Insulin is not a toxin.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q116$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1303, $s$What proportion of resting cardiac output goes to the brain?$s$, $s$["5%", "10%", "15%", "20%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 15%
Although the BRAIN is only about 2% of body weight, it receives about 15% of the RESTING CARDIAC OUTPUT (about 750 mL/min) and uses about 20% of the body's oxygen. It has no fuel stores, so it depends on a continuous supply of blood, oxygen and glucose. Loss of blood flow causes unconsciousness within seconds and irreversible damage within minutes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5% — 5% is too low.
• 10% — 10% is too low.
• 20% — 20% is the proportion of the body's OXYGEN consumption, not cardiac output.

⭐ Key point: Brain blood flow depends on cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP = MAP − ICP); maintain MAP and control ICP in brain-injured patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q11$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1304, $s$An unresponsive patient shows no abnormal movements, and continuous EEG shows a normal background without epileptiform activity. This clinical picture is best described as what?$s$, $s$["An active tonic-clonic seizure", "Nonconvulsive status epilepticus", "A myoclonic seizure", "Unconsciousness/coma without seizure activity"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Unconsciousness/coma without seizure activity
An UNRESPONSIVE patient with NO ABNORMAL MOVEMENTS and a CONTINUOUS EEG showing a NORMAL BACKGROUND WITHOUT EPILEPTIFORM ACTIVITY is UNCONSCIOUS (COMA) WITHOUT SEIZURE ACTIVITY. The cause must be found (metabolic, toxic, structural).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An active tonic-clonic seizure — An active tonic-clonic seizure shows obvious movements and EEG seizure activity.
• Nonconvulsive status epilepticus — Non-convulsive status epilepticus shows seizure activity on EEG without obvious movements.
• A myoclonic seizure — A myoclonic seizure involves jerks.

⭐ Key point: EEG is important in unexplained coma to rule out non-convulsive seizures.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q83$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1305, $s$An unconscious patient's airway is being protected. The nurse should recognise that airway reflexes (gag/cough) are typically lost when the GCS falls to approximately:$s$, $s$["15", "12", "8 or below", "10, with no significance below this"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 8 or below
When the GCS falls to 8 OR BELOW, protective airway reflexes (gag and cough) are typically lost or impaired. The patient cannot protect their airway from obstruction or aspiration. This is why "GCS 8 — intubate" is a common rule.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 15 — GCS 15 is fully alert.
• 12 — GCS 12 usually maintains airway reflexes.
• 10, with no significance below this — Below 10 is significant, and reflexes are usually lost at 8 or below.

⭐ Key point: Position unconscious patients on their side and have suction ready until the airway is secured.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q84$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1306, $s$All of the following are hyperkinetic involuntary movement disorders EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Chorea", "Athetosis", "Torsion dystonia", "Bradykinesia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bradykinesia
Movement disorders are divided into HYPERKINETIC (too much, unwanted movement) and HYPOKINETIC (too little movement). HYPERKINETIC disorders include: CHOREA (brief, irregular, dance-like jerks — e.g. Huntington's disease), ATHETOSIS (slow, writhing movements of the hands and feet — e.g. cerebral palsy, kernicterus), TORSION DYSTONIA (sustained twisting muscle contractions and abnormal postures), tremor, tics, myoclonus and ballismus. BRADYKINESIA (slowness of movement) is HYPOKINETIC — a key feature of PARKINSON'S DISEASE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chorea — Chorea IS hyperkinetic.
• Athetosis — Athetosis IS hyperkinetic.
• Torsion dystonia — Torsion dystonia IS hyperkinetic.

⭐ Key point: Parkinson's disease features: bradykinesia, rigidity, resting tremor and postural instability.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q87$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1307, $s$What is the normal range for intracranial pressure (ICP) in adults?$s$, $s$["0-5 mmHg", "20-30 mmHg", "30-40 mmHg", "5-15 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 5-15 mmHg
INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP) is the pressure inside the skull. In a resting adult lying down, NORMAL ICP is about 5–15 mmHg (7–20 cmH2O). ICP above 20–22 mmHg is considered RAISED and usually needs treatment, because high ICP reduces cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP = MAP − ICP) and can cause brain ischaemia and herniation. Coughing, straining and suctioning cause brief rises that settle quickly in a normal brain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0-5 mmHg — 0–5 mmHg is low; very low ICP can occur after over-drainage of CSF.
• 20-30 mmHg — 20–30 mmHg is RAISED.
• 30-40 mmHg — 30–40 mmHg is severely raised and dangerous.

⭐ Key point: Nursing measures to keep ICP down: head-up 30°, neck neutral, avoid tight tube ties, minimise suctioning, treat pain, fever and agitation, and avoid hypoxia and high CO2.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q89$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1308, $s$All of the following are levels of impaired consciousness EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Alertness", "Stupor", "Delirium", "Coma"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Alertness
Consciousness ranges from fully ALERT (normal) through various levels of IMPAIRMENT: CONFUSION, DELIRIUM (acute fluctuating confusion with poor attention), lethargy/drowsiness, obtundation, STUPOR (responds only to strong, repeated stimuli) and COMA (cannot be roused, no purposeful response). ALERTNESS is the NORMAL state of full consciousness — awake, attentive, and responding appropriately — so it is NOT a level of impaired consciousness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stupor — Stupor IS a level of impaired consciousness.
• Delirium — Delirium IS a disturbance of consciousness.
• Coma — Coma IS the deepest level of impaired consciousness.

⭐ Key point: Describe consciousness objectively with the GCS (and AVPU in emergencies) rather than vague terms.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q90$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1309, $s$Unconsciousness include all the following EXCEPT$s$, $s$["A state of abnormal cerebral activity", "Unresponsive to internal and external environmental changes", "Depression of cerebral function", "Awareness of self and environment"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Awareness of self and environment
UNCONSCIOUSNESS is a state of abnormal cerebral activity and depressed brain function, where the person is UNRESPONSIVE to internal and external stimuli. AWARENESS of SELF and ENVIRONMENT is the definition of CONSCIOUSNESS — the opposite of unconsciousness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A state of abnormal cerebral activity — Abnormal cerebral activity IS part of unconsciousness.
• Unresponsive to internal and external environmental changes — Unresponsiveness IS part of unconsciousness.
• Depression of cerebral function — Depressed cerebral function IS part of unconsciousness.

⭐ Key point: Causes of unconsciousness: head injury, stroke, hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, drugs, infection, seizures.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q91$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1310, $s$A nurse is performing hygiene care for an unconscious patient. To prevent complications, the nurse should:$s$, $s$["Bathe the patient with cold water to stimulate a response", "Use a cotton swab to clean the patient's ears", "Ensure the patient's head is elevated to at least 30 degrees during care", "Use a firm bristle toothbrush to clean the teeth"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ensure the patient's head is elevated to at least 30 degrees during care
Unconscious patients are at risk of ASPIRATION during care (e.g. mouth care, bathing). ELEVATING THE HEAD to at least 30 degrees (or turning the patient to the side) reduces the risk of aspiration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bathe the patient with cold water to stimulate a response — Cold water bathing causes hypothermia.
• Use a cotton swab to clean the patient's ears — Cotton swabs can damage the ear canal and eardrum.
• Use a firm bristle toothbrush to clean the teeth — Firm bristle brushes can damage gums; use a soft brush.

⭐ Key point: Use suction during mouth care and check the airway.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q92$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1311, $s$What does the brainstem control?$s$, $s$["Feelings", "Balance", "Emotions", "Breathing"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Breathing
The BRAINSTEM (midbrain, pons and medulla oblongata) connects the brain to the spinal cord and controls VITAL, automatic functions: BREATHING (respiratory centres in the medulla and pons), HEART RATE and BLOOD PRESSURE (cardiovascular centres), CONSCIOUSNESS and the sleep–wake cycle (reticular activating system), swallowing, coughing and vomiting reflexes, and most CRANIAL NERVE functions (pupil reactions, eye movements, facial sensation, gag reflex).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Feelings — Feelings and emotions are processed mainly by the limbic system and frontal lobes.
• Balance — Balance and coordination are mainly controlled by the cerebellum.
• Emotions — Emotions are processed by the limbic system (e.g. amygdala).

⭐ Key point: Brainstem death means permanent loss of brainstem reflexes and the ability to breathe; it is legally recognised as death.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q95$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1312, $s$Mr kifakat is on mechanical ventilator in ICU with a diagnosis of Brain death, what is his GCS$s$, $s$["3/15", "6/15", "4/15", "5/15"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3/15
In BRAIN DEATH, there is NO brain function at all. On the GLASGOW COMA SCALE: EYES — no eye opening = 1; VERBAL — no verbal response = 1 (or "T" if intubated); MOTOR — no motor response = 1. The total is the LOWEST possible score: 3/15. (Spinal reflexes may still be present, because the spinal cord can work independently.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 6/15 — 6/15 means some responses are present.
• 4/15 — 4/15 means at least one response (e.g. extension or eye opening to pain).
• 5/15 — 5/15 means some responses are present.

⭐ Key point: Brain death is confirmed by formal brainstem testing by two senior doctors (after excluding drugs, hypothermia and metabolic causes), not by GCS alone.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q96$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1313, $s$Alcohol withdrawal symptoms typically begin how long after the last drink?$s$, $s$["Immediately, within minutes", "6–24 hours", "1–2 weeks", "Only after 1 month"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 6–24 hours
In people dependent on alcohol, WITHDRAWAL symptoms usually begin about 6–24 HOURS after the LAST DRINK, as blood alcohol falls: tremor ("the shakes"), anxiety, sweating, nausea, vomiting, tachycardia, hypertension and insomnia. WITHDRAWAL SEIZURES may occur at 12–48 hours. DELIRIUM TREMENS (confusion, hallucinations, fever, severe autonomic instability) usually appears at 48–72 hours and can be fatal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediately, within minutes — Symptoms do not begin immediately (within minutes).
• 1–2 weeks — 1–2 weeks is too late for the onset of withdrawal.
• Only after 1 month — 1 month is far too late.

⭐ Key point: Ask about alcohol intake on admission, use a withdrawal scale (CIWA-Ar), give benzodiazepines as needed, and give IV thiamine before glucose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q97$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1314, $s$A patient in the ICU is agitated, disoriented, and pulling at IV lines. The nurse FIRST:$s$, $s$["Administers haloperidol", "Applies restraints", "Assesses for pain or urinary retention", "Increases sedation"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assesses for pain or urinary retention
An AGITATED, DISORIENTED ICU patient pulling at lines may be in DELIRIUM, but the agitation often has a TREATABLE CAUSE: PAIN, a FULL BLADDER (URINARY RETENTION — e.g. blocked catheter), hypoxia, hypoglycaemia, sepsis, drug or alcohol withdrawal, or discomfort (positioning, thirst). The FIRST action is to ASSESS for these causes — e.g. use a pain scale (CPOT/BPS), check SpO2 and glucose, check the catheter and bladder (bladder scan), while keeping the patient safe.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administers haloperidol — Haloperidol may be considered later for distressing agitation, but not before looking for the cause.
• Applies restraints — Restraints are a last resort; they can increase agitation and injury.
• Increases sedation — Increasing sedation may mask the cause and worsen delirium.

⭐ Key point: Treat the cause, reorient the patient, involve family, and use the least restrictive measures.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q98$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1315, $s$A patient with alcohol withdrawal delirium should receive:$s$, $s$["Benzodiazepines", "Antipsychotics", "Opioids", "Barbiturates"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Benzodiazepines
ALCOHOL WITHDRAWAL DELIRIUM (DELIRIUM TREMENS) is caused by sudden loss of alcohol's calming effect on GABA receptors, causing over-excitation of the brain. BENZODIAZEPINES (e.g. diazepam, lorazepam, chlordiazepoxide) act on the SAME GABA receptors as alcohol, so they calm the brain, control agitation and tremor, and PREVENT SEIZURES. They are the first-line treatment. IV THIAMINE should also be given to prevent Wernicke's encephalopathy, along with fluids and electrolyte correction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Antipsychotics — Antipsychotics (e.g. haloperidol) may lower the seizure threshold; they are only add-ons for severe hallucinations.
• Opioids — Opioids do not treat alcohol withdrawal.
• Barbiturates — Barbiturates (e.g. phenobarbital) may be used for benzodiazepine-resistant cases, but are not first-line.

⭐ Key point: Monitor sedation and breathing closely during high-dose benzodiazepine treatment; severe cases need ICU.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q99$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1316, $s$A patient with hypoactive delirium is at risk for:$s$, $s$["Falls", "Malnutrition", "Pressure ulcers", "All of the above"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
HYPOACTIVE DELIRIUM is the "quiet" type: the patient is drowsy, withdrawn, slow to respond and inactive. Because they are not disruptive, it is often MISSED. These patients are at risk of: FALLS (confusion and weakness when they try to get up), MALNUTRITION and dehydration (they eat and drink poorly), PRESSURE ULCERS (they lie still for long periods), aspiration, DVT and longer hospital stays. So "ALL OF THE ABOVE" is correct. Hypoactive delirium also carries a higher mortality than the hyperactive type.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Falls — Falls are only one risk.
• Malnutrition — Malnutrition is only one risk.
• Pressure ulcers — Pressure ulcers are only one risk.

⭐ Key point: Screen every patient regularly with CAM-ICU or 4AT to detect hypoactive delirium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q100$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1317, $s$The main complication of FHF, occurring in about 80% of cases, is:$s$, $s$["Cerebral oedema", "Renal calculi", "Pancreatitis", "Peptic ulcer"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cerebral oedema
FULMINANT HEPATIC FAILURE (FHF) is rapid liver failure with encephalopathy in a person without previous liver disease (e.g. from paracetamol overdose or viral hepatitis). The main and most dangerous complication is CEREBRAL OEDEMA (brain swelling), which develops in up to about 80% of patients with severe (grade 3–4) encephalopathy. Ammonia and other toxins that the failing liver cannot clear cause brain cells (astrocytes) to swell. This raises intracranial pressure and can cause brain herniation and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal calculi — Renal calculi (kidney stones) are not a typical complication.
• Pancreatitis — Pancreatitis is not a typical complication.
• Peptic ulcer — Peptic ulcer is not the main complication.

⭐ Key point: Manage with head-up 30°, avoid hypoxia and fever, control ammonia, consider hypertonic saline, and refer early for liver transplant.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q121$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1318, $s$On the MRC muscle strength scale, a score of 0 indicates:$s$, $s$["Normal power", "Movement against gravity only", "Complete paralysis, no movement", "Flicker of movement only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Complete paralysis, no movement
The MRC (Medical Research Council) muscle strength scale grades power from 0 to 5: 0 = NO CONTRACTION (COMPLETE PARALYSIS); 1 = flicker or trace of contraction; 2 = movement with gravity eliminated; 3 = movement against gravity; 4 = movement against gravity and some resistance; 5 = normal power. So 0 means complete paralysis with no movement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal power — Normal power is grade 5.
• Movement against gravity only — Movement against gravity only is grade 3.
• Flicker of movement only — A flicker of movement is grade 1.

⭐ Key point: The MRC sum score (0–60, testing 6 muscle groups on both sides) is used to diagnose ICU-acquired weakness (below 48).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q200$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1319, $s$When titrating sedation in a mechanically ventilated ICU patient, the nurse should primarily use:$s$, $s$["The patient's family's preference alone", "A validated sedation scale (e.g. RASS) to guide titration", "No formal scale — sedation should be maximised at all times", "Blood pressure alone as the only guide"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A validated sedation scale (e.g. RASS) to guide titration
Sedation in ventilated ICU patients should be titrated using a VALIDATED SEDATION SCALE, such as the RICHMOND AGITATION-SEDATION SCALE (RASS) or Sedation-Agitation Scale (SAS). These give an objective target (often RASS −2 to 0 for light sedation), avoiding over-sedation (which prolongs ventilation and causes delirium) and under-sedation (agitation, self-extubation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient's family's preference alone — Family preferences are important but cannot guide titration alone.
• No formal scale — sedation should be maximised at all times — Maximising sedation is harmful.
• Blood pressure alone as the only guide — BP alone is not a reliable guide to sedation depth.

⭐ Key point: Combine sedation scales with pain scales (CPOT, BPS) and delirium screening (CAM-ICU).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q36$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1320, $s$What is the standard approach to bladder management for an unconscious patient?$s$, $s$["No catheterization is needed for an unconscious patient", "Encouraging the patient to void on command", "Indwelling urinary catheter placement with strict intake and output monitoring", "Fluid restriction"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Indwelling urinary catheter placement with strict intake and output monitoring
An UNCONSCIOUS patient cannot void voluntarily and is at risk of urinary retention, incontinence (skin damage) and inaccurate fluid assessment. The standard approach is INDWELLING URINARY CATHETER placement with STRICT INTAKE AND OUTPUT MONITORING (hourly urine output in ICU). Use aseptic insertion and catheter care, and remove the catheter as soon as it is no longer needed to reduce catheter-associated urinary tract infection (CAUTI).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No catheterization is needed for an unconscious patient — Catheterisation is usually needed for accurate monitoring and to prevent retention.
• Encouraging the patient to void on command — An unconscious patient cannot void on command.
• Fluid restriction — Fluid restriction is not the standard approach.

⭐ Key point: CAUTI bundle: aseptic insertion, closed drainage, bag below bladder level, daily review of need.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q83$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1321, $s$Meningeal syndrome includes all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Neck stiffness", "Headache", "Photophobia", "Babinski response"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Babinski response
MENINGEAL SYNDROME (meningism) is caused by irritation of the meninges (e.g. meningitis, subarachnoid haemorrhage). It includes: NECK STIFFNESS, HEADACHE, PHOTOPHOBIA (sensitivity to light), nausea/vomiting, and positive KERNIG'S and BRUDZINSKI'S signs. A BABINSKI RESPONSE (upgoing big toe) is a sign of UPPER MOTOR NEURONE damage (brain or spinal cord), not meningeal irritation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Neck stiffness — Neck stiffness IS part of meningeal syndrome.
• Headache — Headache IS part of meningeal syndrome.
• Photophobia — Photophobia IS part of meningeal syndrome.

⭐ Key point: Kernig's sign: pain on extending the knee with the hip flexed. Brudzinski's sign: neck flexion causes hip and knee flexion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q86$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1322, $s$The nurse is assessing the pupils of an unconscious patient and notes that they are unequal in size. This finding is known as:$s$, $s$["Miosis", "Mydriasis", "Anisocoria", "Hippus"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Anisocoria
ANISOCORIA means UNEQUAL PUPIL SIZES. In an unconscious patient, a new anisocoria (especially a dilated, sluggish pupil) may indicate compression of the third cranial nerve by a mass or raised ICP (e.g. uncal herniation) — a neurosurgical emergency. A small degree of anisocoria (less than 1 mm) can be normal in some people.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Miosis — Miosis means small (constricted) pupils.
• Mydriasis — Mydriasis means large (dilated) pupils.
• Hippus — Hippus is rhythmic pupil constriction and dilation.

⭐ Key point: Report any new pupil inequality immediately in neurological patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q87$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1323, $s$Which statement about delirium and dementia is TRUE?$s$, $s$["Delirium is usually reversible, while dementia is progressive", "Delirium is never reversible", "Dementia has an acute onset over hours", "Delirium and dementia are the same condition"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Delirium is usually reversible, while dementia is progressive
DELIRIUM is an ACUTE, FLUCTUATING disturbance of attention and awareness, usually caused by a medical condition (infection, drugs, hypoxia, metabolic problems). It is often REVERSIBLE when the cause is treated. DEMENTIA is a CHRONIC, PROGRESSIVE decline in memory and thinking over months to years, and is usually NOT reversible. Patients with dementia are at higher risk of delirium.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delirium is never reversible — Delirium is often reversible.
• Dementia has an acute onset over hours — Dementia has a gradual, not acute, onset.
• Delirium and dementia are the same condition — They are different conditions.

⭐ Key point: Screen ICU patients for delirium regularly using CAM-ICU or ICDSC.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q90$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1324, $s$What is the primary purpose of the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) in critical care?$s$, $s$["A) Monitoring blood pressure", "B) Assessing respiratory function", "C) Evaluating neurological status and consciousness", "D) Administering antibiotics"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: C) Evaluating neurological status and consciousness
The GLASGOW COMA SCALE (GCS) is a standard tool to EVALUATE NEUROLOGICAL STATUS and LEVEL OF CONSCIOUSNESS, using three components: eye opening (1–4), verbal response (1–5), and motor response (1–6). Total 3–15. It helps detect changes over time and guides decisions (e.g. intubation when GCS ≤8).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A) Monitoring blood pressure — It doesn't monitor BP.
• B) Assessing respiratory function — It doesn't assess respiratory function directly.
• D) Administering antibiotics — It isn't related to antibiotics.

⭐ Key point: Always report the individual components (E, V, M) as well as the total.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q91$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1325, $s$Skeletal muscles disorder commonly occurs following sepsis, mobility restriction, hyperglycemia and neuromuscular blocking agents is termed$s$, $s$["Critical illness polyneuropathy", "Critical illness myopathy", "Critical illness neuropathy", "Critical care acquired weakness"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Critical care acquired weakness
ICU-ACQUIRED WEAKNESS (critical care-acquired weakness) is generalised, symmetrical muscle weakness developing during critical illness. It includes CRITICAL ILLNESS POLYNEUROPATHY (nerve damage), CRITICAL ILLNESS MYOPATHY (muscle damage), or both. Risk factors: SEPSIS, multi-organ failure, IMMOBILITY, HYPERGLYCAEMIA, NEUROMUSCULAR BLOCKING AGENTS and steroids. The broad term for the clinical syndrome is ICU-acquired weakness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Critical illness polyneuropathy — Critical illness polyneuropathy is one type (nerve).
• Critical illness myopathy — Critical illness myopathy is one type (muscle).
• Critical illness neuropathy — "Critical illness neuropathy" is not the overall term.

⭐ Key point: Prevent with early mobilisation, glucose control, light sedation and minimising neuromuscular blockers.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q92$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1326, $s$A patient has a seizure with uncontrollable rhythmic jerking movements. This is documented as a:$s$, $s$["Tonic seizure", "Absence seizure", "Myoclonic seizure", "Clonic seizure"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Clonic seizure
A CLONIC SEIZURE involves RHYTHMIC, REPETITIVE JERKING movements of the muscles, caused by repeated cycles of muscle contraction and relaxation. In a generalised TONIC-CLONIC seizure, the patient first stiffens (tonic phase, often with a cry and cyanosis), then has rhythmic jerking (clonic phase), followed by a post-ictal period of drowsiness and confusion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tonic seizure — A TONIC seizure involves sustained STIFFENING of muscles, without rhythmic jerking.
• Absence seizure — An ABSENCE seizure is a brief (seconds) loss of awareness with staring, mostly in children.
• Myoclonic seizure — A MYOCLONIC seizure involves sudden, brief, shock-like single jerks, not continuous rhythmic jerking.

⭐ Key point: During a seizure: time it, protect the head, do not restrain or put anything in the mouth, give oxygen, check glucose, and turn to the recovery position afterwards. Seizures longer than 5 minutes need benzodiazepines.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q94$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1327, $s$Which statement about the unconscious patient is NOT correct?$s$, $s$["Respiratory failure can cause unconsciousness", "Poor conscious levels are often accompanied by altered breathing patterns", "A saturation monitor provides all the information needed to assess respiratory function", "Airway protection is a nursing priority"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A saturation monitor provides all the information needed to assess respiratory function
In UNCONSCIOUS patients, SpO2 shows only OXYGENATION, NOT VENTILATION. A patient can have normal SpO2 (especially on oxygen) while CO2 is rising dangerously (hypoventilation). Full respiratory assessment requires observing breathing rate, depth and pattern, airway patency, chest movement, and ABGs or capnography. So the statement that SpO2 provides "all the information" is NOT correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory failure can cause unconsciousness — Respiratory failure CAN cause unconsciousness (hypoxia, high CO2).
• Poor conscious levels are often accompanied by altered breathing patterns — Altered breathing patterns DO accompany poor consciousness.
• Airway protection is a nursing priority — Airway protection IS a nursing priority.

⭐ Key point: Use capnography to monitor ventilation in sedated or unconscious patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q95$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1328, $s$Delirium Risk Factors are as follows EXCEPT$s$, $s$["Obesity", "Preexisting dementia", "Bone fractures", "Infections"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Obesity
DELIRIUM is an acute brain dysfunction with many risk factors. PREDISPOSING factors: older age, PRE-EXISTING DEMENTIA or cognitive impairment, depression, alcohol use, visual/hearing impairment, severe illness. PRECIPITATING factors: INFECTIONS (sepsis, UTI, pneumonia), surgery and BONE FRACTURES (e.g. hip fracture), hypoxia, metabolic disturbances, drugs (benzodiazepines, opioids, anticholinergics), dehydration, urinary retention, constipation, pain, sleep deprivation and immobility. OBESITY is NOT a recognised major risk factor for delirium.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Preexisting dementia — Pre-existing dementia IS one of the strongest risk factors.
• Bone fractures — Bone fractures (especially hip fractures in older people) ARE a risk factor.
• Infections — Infections ARE a common precipitating factor.

⭐ Key point: Prevention bundles (reorientation, sleep promotion, early mobility, hearing aids/glasses, hydration, avoiding deliriogenic drugs) reduce delirium by about a third.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q96$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1329, $s$A patient with Parkinson’s disease and delirium should avoid:$s$, $s$["Haloperidol", "Quetiapine", "Melatonin", "Dexmedetomidine"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Haloperidol
PARKINSON'S DISEASE is caused by a loss of DOPAMINE-producing cells in the brain. HALOPERIDOL is a typical antipsychotic that strongly BLOCKS DOPAMINE (D2) receptors. In a patient with Parkinson's, it can severely WORSEN rigidity, tremor and immobility, cause falls and swallowing problems, and may trigger neuroleptic malignant syndrome. It should be AVOIDED. If an antipsychotic is absolutely needed for distressing delirium, QUETIAPINE (weak dopamine blockade) is preferred at low doses.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Quetiapine — Quetiapine is relatively safe in Parkinson's disease and may be used if needed.
• Melatonin — Melatonin may help regulate sleep and is safe.
• Dexmedetomidine — Dexmedetomidine (an alpha-2 agonist sedative) does not block dopamine and is safe.

⭐ Key point: Also avoid metoclopramide and prochlorperazine in Parkinson's patients; use domperidone for nausea. Never stop Parkinson's medications abruptly (risk of a parkinsonism-hyperpyrexia syndrome).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q98$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1330, $s$What percentage of mechanically ventilated ICU patients may experience delirium?$s$, $s$["Up to 10%", "Up to 30%", "Up to 80%", "0%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Up to 80%
DELIRIUM is very common in ICU, especially in MECHANICALLY VENTILATED patients — studies report it in up to about 60–80%. Contributing factors: severe illness (sepsis), sedative drugs (especially benzodiazepines), sleep deprivation, immobility, hypoxia, metabolic problems, and the ICU environment. Much ICU delirium is the "HYPOACTIVE" type (quiet, withdrawn, drowsy), which is easily missed without routine screening. Delirium is linked to longer ventilation, longer stay, higher mortality and long-term cognitive impairment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Up to 10% — 10% greatly underestimates the problem.
• Up to 30% — 30% underestimates it for ventilated patients.
• 0% — 0% is incorrect — delirium is very common.

⭐ Key point: Screen every shift with CAM-ICU or ICDSC, and use the ABCDEF bundle to prevent and manage delirium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q99$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1331, $s$Post-ICU delirium may lead to:$s$, $s$["Long-term cognitive impairment", "PTSD", "Functional decline", "All of the above"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
Surviving critical illness, especially with delirium, can lead to long-term problems known as POST-INTENSIVE CARE SYNDROME (PICS): LONG-TERM COGNITIVE IMPAIRMENT (memory, attention and problem-solving difficulties — sometimes like mild dementia), mental health problems including PTSD (flashbacks and nightmares of ICU experiences), anxiety and depression, and FUNCTIONAL DECLINE (weakness, difficulty with daily activities, inability to return to work). So "ALL OF THE ABOVE" is correct. Family members can also suffer (PICS-Family).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Long-term cognitive impairment — Long-term cognitive impairment is only one effect.
• PTSD — PTSD is only one effect.
• Functional decline — Functional decline is only one effect.

⭐ Key point: ICU diaries, early rehabilitation, family involvement, and ICU follow-up clinics help patients and families recover.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q100$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1332, $s$A patient does not open the eyes at all, uses inappropriate words and flexes abnormally to pain (decorticate). What is the Glasgow Coma Scale score?$s$, $s$["8/15", "9/15", "7/15", "6/15"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 7/15
GLASGOW COMA SCALE = EYES (1–4) + VERBAL (1–5) + MOTOR (1–6). EYES — does NOT open at all = 1. VERBAL — INAPPROPRIATE WORDS (random words, no conversation) = 3 (orientated 5, confused 4, inappropriate words 3, incomprehensible sounds 2, none 1). MOTOR — ABNORMAL FLEXION to pain (DECORTICATE posturing — arms bent in towards the chest) = 3 (obeys 6, localises 5, normal withdrawal 4, abnormal flexion 3, extension 2, none 1). Total: 1 + 3 + 3 = 7/15. A GCS of 8 or less means severe brain injury and usually the need to secure the airway.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 8/15 — 8/15 overscores one component by 1.
• 9/15 — 9/15 overscores by 2.
• 6/15 — 6/15 underscores by 1.

⭐ Key point: Decorticate (abnormal flexion) = 3; decerebrate (extension) = 2. Always report the parts (E1 V3 M3) — the motor score is the best predictor of outcome.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q10$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1333, $s$A head-injured patient has BP 100/55 mmHg and ICP 10 mmHg. What is the cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP)?$s$, $s$["60 mmHg", "90 mmHg", "80 mmHg", "45 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 60 mmHg
STEP 1 — MAP = (100 + 2 × 55) ÷ 3 = 210 ÷ 3 = 70 mmHg. STEP 2 — CPP = MAP − ICP = 70 − 10 = 60 mmHg. This is at the LOWER limit of the usual target (60–70 mmHg) — the BP is low, so raising MAP (fluids, vasopressors) may be needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 90 mmHg — 90 mmHg is systolic minus ICP.
• 80 mmHg — 80 mmHg is a calculation error.
• 45 mmHg — 45 mmHg is diastolic minus ICP.

⭐ Key point: Avoid hypotension in brain injury — even one episode of systolic BP below 90 mmHg worsens outcome.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q55 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1334, $s$A patient with a subarachnoid haemorrhage is prescribed nimodipine. The rationale for this medication is primarily to:$s$, $s$["Lower blood pressure aggressively as the main goal", "Reduce the risk of cerebral vasospasm and secondary ischaemic injury", "Reduce intracranial pressure directly", "Treat infection"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reduce the risk of cerebral vasospasm and secondary ischaemic injury
After SUBARACHNOID HAEMORRHAGE (SAH), blood around the brain arteries can cause them to narrow — CEREBRAL VASOSPASM — usually between days 4 and 14. This can starve parts of the brain of blood, causing DELAYED CEREBRAL ISCHAEMIA (new confusion, weakness, stroke). NIMODIPINE (a calcium channel blocker, 60 mg orally or by NG tube every 4 hours for 21 days) is given from admission because it IMPROVES OUTCOMES and reduces the risk of ischaemic damage. It acts mainly by protecting brain cells and reducing vasospasm effects.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lower blood pressure aggressively as the main goal — Lowering BP aggressively is NOT the goal — nimodipine can cause hypotension, which must be AVOIDED as it reduces brain blood flow.
• Reduce intracranial pressure directly — Nimodipine does not directly reduce ICP.
• Treat infection — Nimodipine is not an antibiotic.

⭐ Key point: If nimodipine causes hypotension, the dose may be split (30 mg every 2 hours) rather than stopped. Maintain normal blood volume and sodium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q144$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1335, $s$A patient develops new left-sided facial droop, arm weakness, and slurred speech that resolves completely within 20 minutes. This presentation is most consistent with:$s$, $s$["Normal ageing", "A migraine with no further evaluation needed", "A Transient Ischaemic Attack (TIA), which still requires urgent evaluation given high subsequent stroke risk", "A completed stroke with permanent deficit"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A Transient Ischaemic Attack (TIA), which still requires urgent evaluation given high subsequent stroke risk
A TRANSIENT ISCHAEMIC ATTACK (TIA) causes stroke-like symptoms (facial droop, arm weakness, slurred speech) that RESOLVE COMPLETELY, usually within minutes to an hour. Although symptoms go away, a TIA is a WARNING sign: the risk of a full stroke is high, especially in the next 48 hours to 7 days. It needs URGENT evaluation (brain imaging, carotid Doppler, ECG for AF, blood tests) and preventive treatment (antiplatelets, statins, BP control, anticoagulation if AF).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal ageing — Stroke-like symptoms are not normal ageing.
• A migraine with no further evaluation needed — Migraine can cause neurological symptoms, but this should not be assumed without evaluation.
• A completed stroke with permanent deficit — A completed stroke has permanent deficits, which are not present here.

⭐ Key point: Use the FAST test (Face, Arms, Speech, Time) to recognise stroke and TIA, and treat every episode as an emergency.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q156$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1336, $s$A patient with a subarachnoid haemorrhage develops new confusion and weakness on day 7, distinct from their baseline post-bleed status. The nurse should have a high suspicion for:$s$, $s$["Normal recovery progress", "An unrelated finding", "Delayed cerebral ischaemia from vasospasm, a recognised complication in the days following subarachnoid haemorrhage", "Improvement in neurological status"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Delayed cerebral ischaemia from vasospasm, a recognised complication in the days following subarachnoid haemorrhage
After SUBARACHNOID HAEMORRHAGE, blood around the brain arteries triggers VASOSPASM, usually starting around day 3–4, peaking at days 7–10, and resolving by about day 14–21. It can cause DELAYED CEREBRAL ISCHAEMIA (DCI): NEW CONFUSION, reduced consciousness, or FOCAL WEAKNESS that was not present before. On day 7, new confusion and weakness should raise high suspicion. Other causes to exclude: rebleeding, hydrocephalus, hyponatraemia, seizures and infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal recovery progress — New deficits are not normal recovery.
• An unrelated finding — They are directly related to the SAH.
• Improvement in neurological status — New confusion and weakness show deterioration, not improvement.

⭐ Key point: Prevention: nimodipine for 21 days, maintain normal blood volume and sodium, avoid hypotension. Report any new neurological change immediately — urgent CT/CT angiography may be needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q182$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1337, $s$A patient with increased intracranial pressure is being nursed. Which positioning is generally recommended to help reduce ICP?$s$, $s$["Prone position", "Trendelenburg (head down) position", "Head of bed flat, neck flexed", "Head of bed elevated 30 degrees, head in neutral alignment"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Head of bed elevated 30 degrees, head in neutral alignment
To help REDUCE INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP), the head of the bed is ELEVATED to about 30 DEGREES and the HEAD is kept in NEUTRAL ALIGNMENT (not turned or flexed). This uses gravity to improve VENOUS DRAINAGE from the brain through the jugular veins, which lowers ICP, while still maintaining enough blood pressure to the brain. Also avoid tight ET tube ties, cervical collars that compress the neck, hip flexion above 90°, and sudden position changes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prone position — Prone positioning raises intra-abdominal and intrathoracic pressure, which can raise ICP.
• Trendelenburg (head down) position — Trendelenburg (head-down) increases blood volume in the head and raises ICP.
• Head of bed flat, neck flexed — Lying flat with the neck flexed obstructs venous drainage and raises ICP.

⭐ Key point: Other ICP measures: adequate sedation and analgesia, avoid coughing and straining, keep PaCO2 normal, treat fever and seizures, osmotherapy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q193$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1338, $s$A patient opens the eyes to voice, is fully orientated and localises to pain. What is the Glasgow Coma Scale score?$s$, $s$["14/15", "12/15", "13/15", "15/15"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 13/15
GLASGOW COMA SCALE (GCS) = EYES + VERBAL + MOTOR. EYES: opens to VOICE = 3 (spontaneous 4, voice 3, pain 2, none 1). VERBAL: fully ORIENTATED = 5 (orientated 5, confused 4, inappropriate words 3, sounds 2, none 1). MOTOR: LOCALISES to pain = 5 (obeys commands 6, localises 5, withdraws 4, abnormal flexion 3, extension 2, none 1). Total: 3 + 5 + 5 = 13/15.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 14/15 — 14/15 would need eyes opening spontaneously or obeying commands.
• 12/15 — 12/15 underscores one component.
• 15/15 — 15/15 requires eyes open spontaneously, orientated, AND obeying commands.

⭐ Key point: Report GCS by components (e.g. E3 V5 M5 = 13), because the parts give more information than the total. Intubated patients are scored "VT".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q10$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1339, $s$A head-injured patient has BP 120/60 mmHg and ICP 35 mmHg. What is the cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP)?$s$, $s$["25 mmHg", "85 mmHg", "45 mmHg", "115 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 45 mmHg
CEREBRAL PERFUSION PRESSURE (CPP) = MEAN ARTERIAL PRESSURE (MAP) − INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP). STEP 1 — MAP = (systolic + 2 × diastolic) ÷ 3 = (120 + 120) ÷ 3 = 240 ÷ 3 = 80 mmHg. STEP 2 — CPP = 80 − 35 = 45 mmHg. This is LOW (target CPP is usually 60–70 mmHg), so the brain is at risk of ischaemia; ICP is also high (normal 5–15 mmHg; treat above 20–22).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 25 mmHg — 25 mmHg uses the diastolic pressure (60 − 35) instead of MAP.
• 85 mmHg — 85 mmHg uses systolic minus ICP (120 − 35).
• 115 mmHg — 115 mmHg is not a valid calculation.

⭐ Key point: Improve CPP by lowering ICP (head-up 30°, sedation, osmotherapy, CSF drainage) and/or raising MAP (fluids, vasopressors).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q17 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1340, $s$A patient with a head injury has a Glasgow Coma Scale score of 10. This is classified as which severity of traumatic brain injury?$s$, $s$["Severe", "Vegetative state", "Moderate", "Mild"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Moderate
Traumatic brain injury (TBI) is graded by the GLASGOW COMA SCALE (GCS): MILD = GCS 13–15; MODERATE = GCS 9–12; SEVERE = GCS 3–8. A GCS of 10 is MODERATE TBI. These patients can deteriorate, so they need close neurological observation, CT scanning, and neurosurgical review.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Severe — Severe TBI is GCS 8 or less (these patients usually need intubation to protect the airway).
• Vegetative state — A vegetative state is a long-term condition of wakefulness without awareness, not a GCS category.
• Mild — Mild TBI is GCS 13–15.

⭐ Key point: "GCS 8 — intubate." A fall of 2 or more points in GCS needs urgent medical review.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q158$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1341, $s$A patient with myasthenia gravis in a myasthenic crisis presents with worsening muscle weakness and respiratory difficulty. The priority nursing concern is:$s$, $s$["Nutritional intake only", "Close monitoring of respiratory status/vital capacity, with readiness for ventilatory support", "Skin integrity only", "Pain management only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Close monitoring of respiratory status/vital capacity, with readiness for ventilatory support
In MYASTHENIC CRISIS, severe muscle weakness affects the BREATHING muscles (diaphragm, intercostals) and the throat muscles (swallowing, airway protection). This can lead to RESPIRATORY FAILURE quickly. The priority is CLOSE MONITORING of respiratory status — breathing rate, chest movement, SpO2, ability to cough and swallow, and regular VITAL CAPACITY (FVC) and negative inspiratory force measurements — and being READY for VENTILATORY SUPPORT (NIV or intubation). An FVC below 20 mL/kg or a rapid fall indicates the need for ventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nutritional intake only — Nutrition is important, but breathing comes first.
• Skin integrity only — Skin integrity is important, but not the priority.
• Pain management only — Pain management is not the main issue in myasthenic crisis.

⭐ Key point: SpO2 and blood gases may stay normal until late — rely on FVC. Treatment includes IVIG or plasma exchange; avoid drugs that worsen myasthenia (e.g. aminoglycosides, magnesium, some muscle relaxants).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q160$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1342, $s$A patient post-PE thrombolysis develops a sudden severe headache and decreased level of consciousness. The nurse should suspect:$s$, $s$["Intracranial hemorrhage", "An expected medication side effect requiring no action", "Hypoglycemia", "A migraine caused by stress"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intracranial hemorrhage
THROMBOLYSIS dissolves clots everywhere, so the most feared complication is BLEEDING — especially INTRACRANIAL HAEMORRHAGE. A SUDDEN SEVERE HEADACHE with DECREASED CONSCIOUSNESS after thrombolysis strongly suggests brain bleeding. Stop the thrombolytic, call for urgent help, and arrange an urgent CT head.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An expected medication side effect requiring no action — It is not an expected side effect.
• Hypoglycemia — Hypoglycaemia doesn't usually cause a sudden severe headache.
• A migraine caused by stress — A migraine is not likely after thrombolysis.

⭐ Key point: Monitor neurological status closely after thrombolysis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q189$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1343, $s$A patient with a stroke presents with contralateral homonymous hemianopia and no motor weakness. Which cerebral artery territory is most likely affected?$s$, $s$["Posterior cerebral artery", "Anterior cerebral artery", "Anterior spinal artery", "Middle cerebral artery"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Posterior cerebral artery
The POSTERIOR CEREBRAL ARTERY (PCA) supplies the OCCIPITAL LOBE, which contains the primary VISUAL CORTEX. A PCA stroke damages vision from the opposite side, causing CONTRALATERAL HOMONYMOUS HEMIANOPIA — loss of the same half of the visual field in BOTH eyes (e.g. a right PCA stroke causes loss of the left half of vision in each eye). Because the PCA does not supply the main motor areas, there is usually NO MOTOR WEAKNESS. Macular sparing is common.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anterior cerebral artery — The anterior cerebral artery supplies the medial frontal and parietal lobes — strokes cause leg weakness more than arm weakness.
• Anterior spinal artery — The anterior spinal artery supplies the front of the spinal cord — blockage causes paralysis and loss of pain/temperature below the lesion.
• Middle cerebral artery — The middle cerebral artery supplies the lateral cortex — strokes cause face and arm weakness, sensory loss, and aphasia or neglect (visual field loss may also occur, but with motor weakness).

⭐ Key point: Patients with hemianopia may bump into objects on the affected side — place the call bell and belongings on the unaffected side, and teach them to turn the head to scan.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q199$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1344, $s$The head-down (Trendelenburg) position used for postural drainage may cause which complication?$s$, $s$["Raised intracranial pressure", "Deep vein thrombosis", "Neck fracture", "Air embolism"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Raised intracranial pressure
POSTURAL DRAINAGE uses gravity to drain secretions from specific lung segments. The HEAD-DOWN (Trendelenburg) position drains the lower lobes, but it causes blood to pool in the head and reduces venous drainage from the brain, which RAISES INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP). It is therefore contraindicated in head injury, raised ICP, recent neurosurgery, uncontrolled hypertension and recent eye surgery. It can also push abdominal contents up against the diaphragm, increasing breathlessness and the risk of reflux and aspiration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Deep vein thrombosis — DVT is caused by immobility and other factors, not directly by head-down positioning.
• Neck fracture — A neck fracture is not caused by the position.
• Air embolism — Air embolism is not a typical complication of postural drainage.

⭐ Key point: Other contraindications to head-down positioning: severe breathlessness, haemoptysis, recent oesophageal surgery, and gastro-oesophageal reflux.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q171$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1345, $s$What is the usual target Richmond Agitation–Sedation Scale (RASS) score for a lightly sedated ICU patient?$s$, $s$["+1 to +2", "−5 to −4", "−2 to 0", "+3 to +4"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: −2 to 0
The RICHMOND AGITATION–SEDATION SCALE (RASS) ranges from +4 (combative) to −5 (unrousable). The usual target for LIGHT SEDATION in most ventilated ICU patients is −2 to 0: 0 = alert and calm; −1 = drowsy but sustained awakening to voice (more than 10 seconds); −2 = light sedation, briefly awakens to voice with eye contact (less than 10 seconds). Light sedation reduces ventilator days, ICU stay and delirium.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• +1 to +2 — +1 to +2 is restless to agitated.
• −5 to −4 — −5 to −4 is deep sedation (no response to voice), used only for specific indications (e.g. severe ARDS, raised ICP).
• +3 to +4 — +3 to +4 is very agitated to combative.

⭐ Key point: Assess RASS regularly and use daily sedation interruption (spontaneous awakening trials) where appropriate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q195$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1346, $s$Two weeks after a bout of diarrhoea, a 30-year-old develops symmetrical weakness that started in the legs and is ascending, with absent reflexes and mild tingling. CSF shows protein 2.1 g/L and 2 white cells/µL. The diagnosis and the key nursing monitoring are:$s$, $s$["Guillain–Barré syndrome — regular forced vital capacity checks", "Myasthenia gravis — regular checks of ptosis and diplopia", "Transverse myelitis — regular checks for a sensory level", "Botulism — regular checks of pupil size and reactivity"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Guillain–Barré syndrome — regular forced vital capacity checks
GUILLAIN-BARRÉ SYNDROME (GBS) is an autoimmune attack on the peripheral nerves, often 1–4 WEEKS after an infection (e.g. DIARRHOEA from Campylobacter jejuni, or a respiratory infection). Features: SYMMETRICAL WEAKNESS that starts in the LEGS and ASCENDS, ABSENT REFLEXES, mild sensory symptoms (tingling), and CSF showing HIGH PROTEIN with NORMAL white cells (albuminocytological dissociation). The key nursing monitoring is FORCED VITAL CAPACITY (FVC) checks, because weakness can spread to the breathing muscles. An FVC below 20 mL/kg (or falling rapidly) indicates the need for ICU admission and possibly ventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Myasthenia gravis — regular checks of ptosis and diplopia — Myasthenia gravis causes fatigable weakness with ptosis and double vision, with normal reflexes.
• Transverse myelitis — regular checks for a sensory level — Transverse myelitis causes a spinal cord lesion with a sensory LEVEL and bladder problems.
• Botulism — regular checks of pupil size and reactivity — Botulism causes DESCENDING paralysis starting with the eyes and throat, with dilated pupils.

⭐ Key point: SpO2 and blood gases change late in GBS. Also monitor for autonomic instability (BP swings, arrhythmias) and swallowing difficulty.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q81$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1347, $s$A patient with myasthenia gravis took extra doses of pyridostigmine and now has worsening weakness with small pupils, sweating, excess saliva, abdominal cramps, diarrhoea and muscle fasciculations. The most likely problem is:$s$, $s$["Guillain–Barré syndrome", "Cholinergic crisis", "Myasthenic crisis", "Lambert–Eaton myasthenic syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cholinergic crisis
PYRIDOSTIGMINE treats myasthenia gravis by increasing ACETYLCHOLINE at the neuromuscular junction. Too much causes a CHOLINERGIC CRISIS: excess acetylcholine overstimulates muscles (causing FASCICULATIONS and then WEAKNESS, which can affect breathing) and the parasympathetic system ("SLUDGE": Salivation, Lacrimation, Urination, Defecation/DIARRHOEA, GI cramps, Emesis; plus SMALL PUPILS (miosis), SWEATING and bradycardia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Guillain–Barré syndrome — Guillain-Barré syndrome causes ascending weakness, not these cholinergic features.
• Myasthenic crisis — Myasthenic crisis is worsening weakness from too LITTLE treatment (e.g. infection, missed doses); it lacks the cholinergic signs.
• Lambert–Eaton myasthenic syndrome — Lambert-Eaton syndrome causes proximal weakness that improves with use, often with lung cancer.

⭐ Key point: Treat cholinergic crisis by stopping pyridostigmine, giving atropine for the muscarinic effects, and supporting breathing (may need ventilation).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q82$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1348, $s$After eating home-canned food, a patient develops double vision, drooping eyelids, slurred speech and difficulty swallowing, followed by weakness spreading DOWNWARDS to the arms. The pupils are dilated and poorly reactive, and sensation is normal. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Myasthenia gravis", "Botulism", "Guillain–Barré syndrome", "Miller Fisher syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Botulism
BOTULISM is caused by a toxin from Clostridium botulinum, often in HOME-CANNED or preserved foods. The toxin blocks acetylcholine release at nerve endings. Features: a DESCENDING PARALYSIS starting with the CRANIAL NERVES (DOUBLE VISION, DROOPING EYELIDS, SLURRED SPEECH, DIFFICULTY SWALLOWING), then spreading DOWN to the arms, trunk and legs. PUPILS are often DILATED and poorly reactive, and SENSATION is NORMAL. The patient is usually alert, with no fever.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Myasthenia gravis — Myasthenia gravis causes fatigable weakness, but pupils are normal and it is not linked to food.
• Guillain–Barré syndrome — GBS causes ASCENDING weakness and often sensory symptoms, with normal pupils.
• Miller Fisher syndrome — Miller Fisher syndrome (a GBS variant) causes ophthalmoplegia, ataxia and absent reflexes, but not usually dilated pupils with descending weakness after food.

⭐ Key point: Treat with botulinum antitoxin early, supportive care, and monitor FVC (may need ventilation). Notify public health — other people may have eaten the same food.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q83$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1349, $s$A patient with a head injury has a left dilated pupil. Two hours later BP rises from 130/80 to 190/70 mmHg, the pulse falls from 90 to 48, and breathing becomes irregular. These changes represent:$s$, $s$["Beck's triad of cardiac tamponade", "Autonomic dysreflexia from a full bladder", "Neurogenic shock from cord injury", "Cushing's triad of raised intracranial pressure"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cushing's triad of raised intracranial pressure
CUSHING'S TRIAD is a LATE sign of severely RAISED INTRACRANIAL PRESSURE (ICP), showing the brainstem is being compressed (herniation is imminent): (1) RISING SYSTOLIC BP with a WIDENING PULSE PRESSURE (190/70 — the body raises BP to push blood into the squeezed brain); (2) BRADYCARDIA (pulse falls to 48 — a reflex response to the high BP and brainstem compression); (3) IRREGULAR BREATHING (from brainstem compression). The dilated pupil (third nerve compression) also indicates herniation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Beck's triad of cardiac tamponade — Beck's triad (tamponade) involves hypotension, raised JVP and muffled heart sounds.
• Autonomic dysreflexia from a full bladder — Autonomic dysreflexia occurs in spinal injury above T6, triggered by stimuli such as a full bladder.
• Neurogenic shock from cord injury — Neurogenic shock causes LOW BP and bradycardia from loss of sympathetic tone, not high BP.

⭐ Key point: Cushing's triad is a neurosurgical emergency: elevate the head of the bed 30°, ensure oxygenation and normocapnia, give osmotic therapy (mannitol or hypertonic saline), and arrange urgent CT and surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q84$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1350, $s$After a C5 spinal cord injury from a fall, a patient has BP 75/40 mmHg, pulse 48, and warm, dry, pink peripheries. The most likely type of shock is:$s$, $s$["Neurogenic shock", "Hypovolaemic shock", "Septic shock", "Spinal shock"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Neurogenic shock
NEUROGENIC SHOCK occurs after spinal cord injuries above T6 (here, C5). The injury cuts off the SYMPATHETIC nervous system, causing loss of blood vessel tone (VASODILATION, causing HYPOTENSION) and unopposed parasympathetic action on the heart (BRADYCARDIA). Because the vessels are dilated, the skin is WARM, DRY and PINK (unlike other types of shock, which cause cold, clammy skin).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypovolaemic shock — Hypovolaemic shock causes TACHYCARDIA and COLD, CLAMMY skin.
• Septic shock — Septic shock usually causes tachycardia and fever.
• Spinal shock — SPINAL SHOCK is a temporary loss of all reflexes below the injury (flaccid paralysis), not a type of circulatory shock.

⭐ Key point: Treat neurogenic shock with careful fluids (exclude bleeding first), vasopressors (e.g. noradrenaline), and atropine for symptomatic bradycardia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q86$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1351, $s$A young man is hit on the side of the head with a stick, briefly loses consciousness, then is lucid and talking for 2 hours before rapidly deteriorating with a dilated pupil on the side of the injury. CT shows a biconvex (lens-shaped) collection. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Acute subdural haematoma (bridging veins)", "Extradural haematoma (middle meningeal artery)", "Traumatic subarachnoid haemorrhage", "Diffuse axonal injury from shearing"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Extradural haematoma (middle meningeal artery)
An EXTRADURAL (epidural) HAEMATOMA is bleeding between the skull and the dura, usually from the MIDDLE MENINGEAL ARTERY after a blow to the SIDE of the head (temporal bone fracture). Classic course: brief LOSS OF CONSCIOUSNESS, then a LUCID INTERVAL (the patient seems well for hours), then RAPID DETERIORATION as the arterial blood builds up, with a DILATED PUPIL on the same side (third nerve compression). CT shows a BICONVEX (LENS-SHAPED) collection, because the dura is stuck firmly to the skull at the sutures, limiting spread.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute subdural haematoma (bridging veins) — An acute subdural haematoma (from bridging veins) is CRESCENT-shaped and usually follows more severe injury.
• Traumatic subarachnoid haemorrhage — Traumatic subarachnoid haemorrhage shows blood in the sulci and cisterns, not a lens-shaped collection.
• Diffuse axonal injury from shearing — Diffuse axonal injury causes immediate, prolonged coma, often with a normal or near-normal CT.

⭐ Key point: Extradural haematoma is a neurosurgical emergency; prompt surgery has an excellent outcome.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q87$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1352, $s$A 78-year-old alcoholic on warfarin has 3 weeks of fluctuating confusion, headache and drowsiness. He had a minor fall a month ago. CT shows a crescent-shaped collection that is darker (hypodense) than the brain. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Subarachnoid haemorrhage", "Chronic subdural haematoma", "Acute extradural haematoma", "Normal pressure hydrocephalus"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Chronic subdural haematoma
A CHRONIC SUBDURAL HAEMATOMA is slow bleeding from torn BRIDGING VEINS between the brain and the dura. Risk factors: ELDERLY people (brain shrinkage stretches the veins), ALCOHOLICS, people on ANTICOAGULANTS (warfarin), and recurrent falls. Often follows a MINOR FALL weeks earlier. Symptoms are gradual and FLUCTUATING: confusion, headache, drowsiness, personality change. CT shows a CRESCENT-shaped collection that is DARKER (HYPODENSE) than the brain (old blood).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Subarachnoid haemorrhage — SAH causes a sudden severe headache, with blood in the subarachnoid space.
• Acute extradural haematoma — An acute extradural haematoma is lens-shaped and bright (hyperdense), with rapid deterioration.
• Normal pressure hydrocephalus — Normal pressure hydrocephalus causes dementia, gait problems and incontinence, with enlarged ventricles.

⭐ Key point: Reverse anticoagulation (vitamin K and prothrombin complex concentrate) and arrange neurosurgical drainage (burr holes).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q88$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1353, $s$Six days after clipping of a ruptured aneurysm (SAH), a patient becomes newly confused with a right-sided weakness. Repeat CT shows no rebleed and no hydrocephalus. The most likely cause, and the drug given from admission to reduce this risk, are:$s$, $s$["Hydrocephalus — acetazolamide", "Delayed cerebral ischaemia from vasospasm — oral nimodipine", "Rebleeding — tranexamic acid", "Hyponatraemia from SIADH — strict fluid restriction"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Delayed cerebral ischaemia from vasospasm — oral nimodipine
DELAYED CEREBRAL ISCHAEMIA (DCI) from CEREBRAL VASOSPASM (narrowing of brain arteries) is a major complication of SAH. It usually occurs between DAYS 4 and 14, causing NEW CONFUSION or FOCAL NEUROLOGICAL DEFICITS (e.g. right-sided weakness). ORAL NIMODIPINE (a calcium channel blocker, 60 mg every 4 hours for 21 days) is given FROM ADMISSION to reduce the risk of poor outcome from DCI.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hydrocephalus — acetazolamide — Hydrocephalus is excluded by the CT.
• Rebleeding — tranexamic acid — Rebleeding is excluded by the CT (and is unlikely after clipping).
• Hyponatraemia from SIADH — strict fluid restriction — Hyponatraemia in SAH is often due to cerebral salt wasting; fluid restriction is harmful because it increases vasospasm risk.

⭐ Key point: Maintain normal blood volume (euvolaemia), normal sodium and adequate BP. Avoid hypotension.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q90$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1354, $s$A right-handed patient suddenly develops weakness of the right face and arm (more than the leg), right-sided sensory loss and expressive and receptive aphasia. The artery most likely blocked is the:$s$, $s$["Left middle cerebral artery", "Basilar artery", "Left anterior cerebral artery", "Right middle cerebral artery"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Left middle cerebral artery
The MIDDLE CEREBRAL ARTERY (MCA) supplies the side of the brain that controls the FACE and ARM (more than the leg) and sensation. The LEFT hemisphere controls the RIGHT side of the body, and in right-handed people, it contains the LANGUAGE CENTRES (Broca's area for expression, Wernicke's area for understanding). So a LEFT MCA blockage causes: RIGHT face and arm weakness (more than the leg), RIGHT-sided sensory loss, and EXPRESSIVE AND RECEPTIVE APHASIA (global aphasia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Basilar artery — Basilar artery occlusion causes brainstem signs (e.g. coma, quadriplegia, cranial nerve problems).
• Left anterior cerebral artery — The anterior cerebral artery mainly affects the LEG more than the arm.
• Right middle cerebral artery — A RIGHT MCA stroke would cause LEFT-sided weakness and neglect, not aphasia (in right-handed people).

⭐ Key point: Time is brain: check eligibility for thrombolysis (within 4.5 hours) or thrombectomy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q91$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1355, $s$A patient develops sudden vertigo, vomiting, difficulty swallowing and a hoarse voice. There is loss of pain and temperature sensation on the LEFT side of the face and the RIGHT side of the body, a left Horner's syndrome and left-sided ataxia. The likely lesion is:$s$, $s$["Left lateral medullary infarct (PICA / vertebral)", "Left middle cerebral artery territory infarct", "Right internal capsule lacunar infarct", "Basilar artery occlusion with locked-in state"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Left lateral medullary infarct (PICA / vertebral)
This is LATERAL MEDULLARY (WALLENBERG) SYNDROME, caused by blockage of the POSTERIOR INFERIOR CEREBELLAR ARTERY (PICA) or the VERTEBRAL artery. Features: VERTIGO, VOMITING, DIFFICULTY SWALLOWING and HOARSE VOICE (nucleus ambiguus), loss of pain and temperature on the SAME side of the face (LEFT) and the OPPOSITE side of the body (RIGHT) — "crossed" sensory loss, same-side HORNER'S syndrome (LEFT), and same-side ATAXIA (LEFT). Limb strength is usually normal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Left middle cerebral artery territory infarct — A left MCA infarct would cause right-sided weakness and aphasia, without crossed sensory loss.
• Right internal capsule lacunar infarct — A right internal capsule lacunar infarct would cause pure motor or sensory loss on the left side, without brainstem signs.
• Basilar artery occlusion with locked-in state — Basilar occlusion with locked-in syndrome causes quadriplegia with preserved consciousness.

⭐ Key point: Patients are at risk of aspiration; assess swallowing before giving anything by mouth.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q92$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1356, $s$After a brainstem stroke, a patient cannot move any limb or speak, but is fully awake and aware and can blink and move the eyes vertically on command. This is:$s$, $s$["Brainstem death", "Akinetic mutism", "Persistent vegetative state", "Locked-in syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Locked-in syndrome
LOCKED-IN SYNDROME is caused by damage to the front (ventral) part of the PONS (usually basilar artery stroke). The patient is FULLY AWAKE and AWARE (the parts of the brain controlling consciousness are spared) but CANNOT MOVE ANY LIMB or SPEAK (paralysis of all voluntary muscles). They can usually BLINK and move the EYES VERTICALLY, which can be used to communicate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Brainstem death — Brainstem death means no brainstem function and no consciousness.
• Akinetic mutism — Akinetic mutism patients are awake but lack motivation to move or speak; they are not truly paralysed.
• Persistent vegetative state — Persistent vegetative state patients have sleep–wake cycles but no awareness.

⭐ Key point: Always speak to these patients as fully aware. Set up a communication system using eye movements or blinks.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q93$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1357, $s$A farmer with 3 weeks of headache, fever and weight loss develops a sixth cranial nerve palsy. CSF shows raised pressure, lymphocytes, protein 2.5 g/L and a CSF:blood glucose ratio of 0.25. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Viral meningitis", "Tuberculous meningitis", "Subarachnoid haemorrhage", "Acute bacterial (pneumococcal) meningitis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tuberculous meningitis
TUBERCULOUS MENINGITIS (TBM) has a SLOW onset (weeks), with HEADACHE, FEVER, WEIGHT LOSS, and often CRANIAL NERVE PALSIES (commonly the sixth nerve, from thick exudate at the base of the brain). CSF: RAISED PRESSURE, LYMPHOCYTES, VERY HIGH PROTEIN (above 1–2 g/L) and VERY LOW GLUCOSE (CSF:blood ratio below 0.5, here 0.25).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Viral meningitis — Viral meningitis has lymphocytes, but protein is only mildly raised and glucose is NORMAL.
• Subarachnoid haemorrhage — SAH causes a sudden headache with red cells or xanthochromia.
• Acute bacterial (pneumococcal) meningitis — Bacterial (pneumococcal) meningitis has an acute onset (hours to days), with NEUTROPHILS in the CSF.

⭐ Key point: Treat with anti-TB drugs (rifampicin, isoniazid, pyrazinamide, ethambutol) plus dexamethasone. Test for HIV.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q96$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1358, $s$A known alcoholic is admitted confused and unsteady on his feet, with nystagmus and weakness of eye movements. Before giving IV dextrose, the nurse should ensure he receives:$s$, $s$["IV naloxone — this is opioid toxicity", "Oral lactulose — this is hepatic encephalopathy", "IV thiamine — this is Wernicke's encephalopathy", "IV diazepam — this is delirium tremens"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV thiamine — this is Wernicke's encephalopathy
WERNICKE'S ENCEPHALOPATHY is caused by THIAMINE (vitamin B1) deficiency, common in ALCOHOLICS. Classic triad: CONFUSION, ATAXIA (unsteady gait), and eye movement problems (NYSTAGMUS, ophthalmoplegia). Giving IV DEXTROSE before thiamine can WORSEN it, because glucose metabolism uses up the remaining thiamine. So give IV THIAMINE (e.g. Pabrinex) BEFORE or with dextrose.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV naloxone — this is opioid toxicity — Naloxone treats opioid toxicity (small pupils, respiratory depression).
• Oral lactulose — this is hepatic encephalopathy — Lactulose treats hepatic encephalopathy (flapping tremor, liver disease).
• IV diazepam — this is delirium tremens — Diazepam treats delirium tremens (hallucinations, tremor, autonomic overactivity).

⭐ Key point: Untreated Wernicke's can progress to Korsakoff syndrome (permanent memory loss).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q97$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1359, $s$Three days after admission for a fracture, a heavy drinker becomes agitated and disoriented, with visual hallucinations ('insects on the wall'), coarse tremor, sweating, fever, a pulse of 130 and high BP. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Korsakoff syndrome", "Delirium tremens", "Wernicke's encephalopathy", "Hepatic encephalopathy"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Delirium tremens
DELIRIUM TREMENS (DTs) is a severe form of ALCOHOL WITHDRAWAL, usually 48–72 HOURS after the last drink (e.g. after hospital admission). Features: AGITATION, DISORIENTATION, VISUAL HALLUCINATIONS (e.g. insects), COARSE TREMOR, SWEATING, FEVER, TACHYCARDIA and HYPERTENSION. It can be fatal (seizures, arrhythmias).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Korsakoff syndrome — Korsakoff syndrome causes memory loss and confabulation, not acute agitation.
• Wernicke's encephalopathy — Wernicke's encephalopathy causes confusion, ataxia and eye problems, without hallucinations and autonomic overactivity.
• Hepatic encephalopathy — Hepatic encephalopathy causes confusion and flapping tremor, without autonomic overactivity.

⭐ Key point: Treat with benzodiazepines (e.g. chlordiazepoxide, diazepam), IV thiamine, fluids and electrolytes. Prevent by recognising alcohol dependence on admission.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q98$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1360, $s$A patient on an SSRI antidepressant was given tramadol. Within hours she is agitated and sweating, with a temperature of 39.5 °C, dilated pupils, diarrhoea, brisk reflexes and inducible clonus that is worse in the legs. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome", "Malignant hyperthermia", "Serotonin syndrome", "Anticholinergic toxicity"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Serotonin syndrome
SEROTONIN SYNDROME occurs when too much serotonin builds up, often when combining SEROTONERGIC DRUGS (e.g. an SSRI with tramadol). It develops RAPIDLY (within hours). Features: AGITATION, SWEATING, HIGH TEMPERATURE, DILATED PUPILS, DIARRHOEA, BRISK REFLEXES and CLONUS (worse in the LEGS).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Neuroleptic malignant syndrome — NMS develops slowly (days), with "lead-pipe" rigidity and slow reflexes.
• Malignant hyperthermia — Malignant hyperthermia occurs during anaesthesia (triggered by volatile agents or suxamethonium).
• Anticholinergic toxicity — Anticholinergic toxicity causes DRY skin and urinary retention, without clonus.

⭐ Key point: Stop serotonergic drugs, give benzodiazepines, cooling and cyproheptadine (a serotonin antagonist).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q99$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1361, $s$A patient started on IV haloperidol 4 days ago now has a temperature of 40 °C, generalised 'lead-pipe' rigidity, slowed movements, fluctuating BP, sweating and a creatine kinase of 25,000 U/L. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Malignant hyperthermia", "Serotonin syndrome", "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome", "Heat stroke"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Neuroleptic malignant syndrome
NEUROLEPTIC MALIGNANT SYNDROME (NMS) is a rare, life-threatening reaction to ANTIPSYCHOTIC drugs (e.g. HALOPERIDOL), which block dopamine. It develops over DAYS. Features: HIGH FEVER, "LEAD-PIPE" RIGIDITY, slow movements, AUTONOMIC INSTABILITY (fluctuating BP, sweating), altered consciousness, and very HIGH CREATINE KINASE (CK) from muscle breakdown.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Malignant hyperthermia — Malignant hyperthermia occurs during anaesthesia.
• Serotonin syndrome — Serotonin syndrome develops rapidly, with clonus and hyperreflexia.
• Heat stroke — Heat stroke follows heat exposure, without lead-pipe rigidity.

⭐ Key point: Stop the antipsychotic, give cooling, fluids, and consider dantrolene or bromocriptine. Watch for kidney failure from rhabdomyolysis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q100$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1362, $s$According to the Monro-Kellie doctrine, once compensatory mechanisms (CSF displacement, venous compression) are exhausted, further small increases in intracranial volume cause what?$s$, $s$["A linear, predictable rise in ICP", "No change in ICP", "A decrease in ICP", "Sharp, exponential rises in ICP"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sharp, exponential rises in ICP
The MONRO-KELLIE DOCTRINE states that the skull is a rigid box containing brain, blood and CSF. If one increases, others must decrease to keep pressure stable. Initially, CSF and venous blood are pushed out (compensation). Once these are EXHAUSTED, the skull cannot expand, so even SMALL increases in volume cause SHARP, EXPONENTIAL RISES in ICP (shown on the pressure-volume curve).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A linear, predictable rise in ICP — The relationship is not linear; it becomes steeply exponential.
• No change in ICP — ICP rises once compensation is exhausted.
• A decrease in ICP — ICP does not decrease with added volume.

⭐ Key point: At this stage, small things (coughing, suctioning, lying flat) can cause large ICP spikes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q41$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1363, $s$Per the Monro-Kellie doctrine, which two components are displaced early to compensate for a growing intracranial mass lesion?$s$, $s$["CSF into the spinal space and venous blood out of the skull", "Arterial blood only, by constriction of the cerebral arteries", "Brain tissue only, by compression of the normal cortex", "Bone, by remodelling and expansion of the skull sutures"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: CSF into the spinal space and venous blood out of the skull
When a mass lesion (e.g. a haematoma) grows, the brain first compensates by pushing CSF DOWN into the spinal space and squeezing VENOUS BLOOD OUT of the skull (venous sinuses). This keeps ICP stable for a time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Arterial blood only, by constriction of the cerebral arteries — Arterial blood is not the main early compensation; reducing arterial flow would cause ischaemia.
• Brain tissue only, by compression of the normal cortex — Brain tissue cannot be compressed significantly without damage.
• Bone, by remodelling and expansion of the skull sutures — In adults, the skull cannot expand (sutures are fused).

⭐ Key point: When compensation fails, ICP rises sharply and herniation may follow.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q42$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1364, $s$Where is the Licox brain tissue oxygen monitoring catheter typically placed?$s$, $s$["Peripheral muscle tissue of the thigh", "The subarachnoid space over the spine", "The ventricle, solely for CSF sampling", "Uninjured white matter, often frontal"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uninjured white matter, often frontal
The LICOX catheter is placed into the BRAIN TISSUE, usually in the FRONTAL lobe white matter. It is usually put into an area of UNINJURED (or at-risk) brain to reflect overall brain oxygenation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Peripheral muscle tissue of the thigh — Placement is in the brain, not the thigh.
• The subarachnoid space over the spine — It is not placed in the subarachnoid space over the spine.
• The ventricle, solely for CSF sampling — The ventricle is used for external ventricular drains (EVDs), not Licox.

⭐ Key point: Licox also measures brain temperature.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q44$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1365, $s$An external ventricular drain (EVD) that is leveled too LOW relative to the reference point will most likely cause what?$s$, $s$["Over-drainage of CSF, risking ventricular collapse", "Immediate infection of the ventricular catheter", "Under-drainage of CSF, with a rising ICP", "No effect, because drainage depends on ICP only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Over-drainage of CSF, risking ventricular collapse
In an external ventricular drain (EVD), CSF drains when the ICP is higher than the height of the drip chamber. If the system is levelled TOO LOW (e.g. the chamber is lower than intended relative to the reference point at the tragus), CSF will drain too easily: OVER-DRAINAGE. This can collapse the ventricles, tear bridging veins (causing subdural haematoma) or cause herniation upwards.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate infection of the ventricular catheter — Wrong levelling doesn't immediately cause infection.
• Under-drainage of CSF, with a rising ICP — Under-drainage happens when the system is levelled too HIGH.
• No effect, because drainage depends on ICP only — Drainage depends on both ICP AND the height of the system.

⭐ Key point: Re-level the EVD every time the patient's position changes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q45$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1366, $s$According to the Monro-Kellie doctrine, which component can the ICU nurse most directly influence to reduce ICP?$s$, $s$["Cerebral blood volume, via head-up position, neck alignment, PaCO₂ control", "CSF volume, by encouraging the patient to cough strongly", "Brain tissue volume, by giving large volumes of IV fluids", "Skull volume, by positioning the patient completely flat"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cerebral blood volume, via head-up position, neck alignment, PaCO₂ control
Of the three components (brain, blood, CSF), the nurse can most directly influence CEREBRAL BLOOD VOLUME. Measures: HEAD-UP 30°, keeping the NECK STRAIGHT (no tight tube ties or collars compressing the jugular veins) to help venous drainage, and controlling PaCO2 (high CO2 dilates brain vessels; target normal PaCO2 of about 35–40 mmHg).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CSF volume, by encouraging the patient to cough strongly — Coughing RAISES ICP.
• Brain tissue volume, by giving large volumes of IV fluids — Large volumes of IV fluids can increase brain swelling; brain tissue volume is reduced by osmotic therapy (mannitol, hypertonic saline), not fluids.
• Skull volume, by positioning the patient completely flat — Skull volume cannot be changed; lying flat increases ICP.

⭐ Key point: Other nursing measures: avoid hip flexion, cluster care, treat pain and fever, and avoid unnecessary suctioning.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q46$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1367, $s$A Licox reading of PbtO₂ 12 mmHg with normal ICP and MAP indicates:$s$, $s$["Over-drainage from the external ventricular drain", "Hyperoxia, so the FiO₂ should be reduced at once", "Brain tissue hypoxia; review oxygenation, Hb and perfusion", "Normal brain tissue oxygenation needing no action"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Brain tissue hypoxia; review oxygenation, Hb and perfusion
A PbtO2 of 12 mmHg is BELOW the normal range (20–40 mmHg), showing BRAIN TISSUE HYPOXIA, even though ICP and MAP are normal. The nurse should review causes: OXYGENATION (SpO2, PaO2, FiO2), HAEMOGLOBIN (anaemia reduces oxygen delivery), PERFUSION (CPP, cardiac output), PaCO2, and also increased demand (fever, seizures).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Over-drainage from the external ventricular drain — Over-drainage of an EVD causes low ICP, not specifically low PbtO2.
• Hyperoxia, so the FiO₂ should be reduced at once — 12 mmHg is low, not high; reducing oxygen would worsen it.
• Normal brain tissue oxygenation needing no action — A low PbtO2 needs action.

⭐ Key point: Brain hypoxia can occur even with normal ICP and CPP — this is why multimodal monitoring is helpful.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q47$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1368, $s$Before moving a patient with an open external ventricular drain, the nurse should:$s$, $s$["Disconnect the drain from the bag for the transfer", "Clamp per protocol, then re-level and reopen after the move", "Lower the drip chamber to the floor during the move", "Leave it open while sitting the patient upright"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Clamp per protocol, then re-level and reopen after the move
Before moving a patient with an open EVD, CLAMP THE DRAIN (per protocol) to prevent sudden over-drainage or under-drainage from position changes during the move. After the move, RE-LEVEL the system to the tragus and REOPEN it. Never forget to reopen it, or ICP may rise.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Disconnect the drain from the bag for the transfer — Disconnecting the drain risks infection and loss of CSF.
• Lower the drip chamber to the floor during the move — Lowering the chamber to the floor causes massive over-drainage.
• Leave it open while sitting the patient upright — Leaving it open while sitting up changes the pressure relationship and causes over-drainage.

⭐ Key point: Clamped EVDs must be closely monitored; ICP can rise rapidly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q49$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1369, $s$An EVD set at 15 cmH₂O above the tragus means CSF drains when:$s$, $s$["Whenever the patient coughs only", "ICP exceeds about 15 cmH₂O", "ICP falls below 15 cmH₂O", "The patient's BP exceeds 15 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ICP exceeds about 15 cmH₂O
An EVD set at 15 cmH2O above the tragus means CSF drains only when the ICP is HIGHER THAN 15 cmH2O (about 11 mmHg). If ICP is below 15 cmH2O, CSF will not drain. This controls ICP by allowing drainage only when pressure exceeds the set level.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Whenever the patient coughs only — Coughing can transiently raise ICP above the level, but drainage is not limited to coughing.
• ICP falls below 15 cmH₂O — CSF does not drain when ICP falls below the level.
• The patient's BP exceeds 15 mmHg — BP is not the threshold.

⭐ Key point: 1 mmHg = 1.36 cmH2O. Always check whether the order is in cmH2O or mmHg.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q50$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1370, $s$A sudden increase in clear EVD drainage with a falling GCS and headache on sitting up suggests:$s$, $s$["Cerebral vasospasm after subarachnoid bleeding", "A normal response to sitting the patient upright", "A blocked drain with a steadily rising ICP", "Over-drainage causing low ICP or subdural collection"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Over-drainage causing low ICP or subdural collection
A sudden increase in CLEAR EVD drainage, a FALLING GCS and HEADACHE when sitting up suggest OVER-DRAINAGE: too much CSF is being removed, causing LOW ICP (intracranial hypotension). The brain can sag, and bridging veins can tear, causing a SUBDURAL COLLECTION. Check the levelling of the EVD and whether the patient's position changed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cerebral vasospasm after subarachnoid bleeding — Vasospasm causes neurological deterioration, but not increased drainage.
• A normal response to sitting the patient upright — Sitting upright should not cause these changes if the EVD is properly levelled.
• A blocked drain with a steadily rising ICP — A blocked drain would cause REDUCED drainage, not increased.

⭐ Key point: Check the EVD level whenever the patient moves, and look at drainage volumes hourly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q51$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1371, $s$During targeted temperature management, shivering should be prevented because it:$s$, $s$["It improves the accuracy of the temperature probes", "It reduces cerebral oxygen demand and so is beneficial", "It raises metabolic rate and O₂ use, opposing neuroprotection", "It lowers the core temperature far too quickly"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It raises metabolic rate and O₂ use, opposing neuroprotection
SHIVERING is the body's natural way to produce heat. It sharply INCREASES METABOLIC RATE and OXYGEN CONSUMPTION (up to several times normal), increases CO2 production, and raises ICP. This OPPOSES the brain-protective goal of targeted temperature management (TTM), which is to reduce brain metabolism. Shivering is controlled with sedation, analgesia, magnesium, skin warming and, if needed, neuromuscular blockade.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It improves the accuracy of the temperature probes — Shivering does not improve the accuracy of temperature probes.
• It reduces cerebral oxygen demand and so is beneficial — Shivering INCREASES oxygen demand; it is harmful.
• It lowers the core temperature far too quickly — Shivering produces heat, which makes the core temperature harder to lower.

⭐ Key point: Use the Bedside Shivering Assessment Scale (BSAS) to monitor shivering.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q52$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1372, $s$Rewarming after targeted temperature management should be:$s$, $s$["Stopped at 33 °C permanently", "Rapid, 2–3 °C per hour", "As fast as possible with hot fluids", "Slow, about 0.25–0.5 °C per hour"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Slow, about 0.25–0.5 °C per hour
After TTM, rewarming must be SLOW and CONTROLLED, about 0.25–0.5 °C PER HOUR. Rapid rewarming can cause VASODILATION (hypotension), sudden potassium release (hyperkalaemia), raised ICP, seizures and rebound fever. Slow rewarming protects the brain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stopped at 33 °C permanently — Hypothermia is not maintained permanently; it's for a set time (usually 24 hours).
• Rapid, 2–3 °C per hour — 2–3 °C per hour is too fast and dangerous.
• As fast as possible with hot fluids — Rewarming "as fast as possible" with hot fluids is dangerous.

⭐ Key point: Prevent fever (over 37.7 °C) for at least 72 hours after cardiac arrest.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q54$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1373, $s$Guillain-Barré syndrome patients in ICU need serial measurement of:$s$, $s$["Arterial lactate and pH every hour", "Serum calcium and magnesium daily", "Peak expiratory flow rate every 4 hours", "Vital capacity and negative inspiratory force"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vital capacity and negative inspiratory force
GUILLAIN-BARRÉ SYNDROME (GBS) causes ascending muscle weakness that can affect the BREATHING MUSCLES (diaphragm). Serial measurement of VITAL CAPACITY (VC) and NEGATIVE INSPIRATORY FORCE (NIF) detects respiratory weakness early, before blood gases worsen. A VC below 20 mL/kg, NIF weaker than −30 cmH2O, or rapid decline indicates the need for intubation ("20/30/40 rule").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Arterial lactate and pH every hour — Lactate and pH are late signs of respiratory failure.
• Serum calcium and magnesium daily — Calcium and magnesium are not specific monitoring tools for GBS.
• Peak expiratory flow rate every 4 hours — Peak flow measures airway obstruction (asthma), not muscle weakness.

⭐ Key point: SpO2 and blood gases may stay normal until the patient suddenly fails — measure VC regularly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q55$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1374, $s$Autonomic instability in Guillain-Barré syndrome may present as:$s$, $s$["Constant bradycardia with a low temperature", "Swings in BP and heart rate, with arrhythmias", "Hypoglycaemia with recurrent seizures", "Hyperkalaemia with peaked T waves"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Swings in BP and heart rate, with arrhythmias
GBS damages the AUTONOMIC NERVES (that control BP, heart rate, sweating and gut). This causes AUTONOMIC INSTABILITY: large SWINGS in BP (severe high and low) and heart rate (tachycardia or bradycardia), and ARRHYTHMIAS (including sudden asystole, especially during suctioning). Other signs: urinary retention, ileus and abnormal sweating.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Constant bradycardia with a low temperature — Constant bradycardia with low temperature is not typical; autonomic instability causes swings.
• Hypoglycaemia with recurrent seizures — Hypoglycaemia with seizures is not typical of GBS.
• Hyperkalaemia with peaked T waves — Hyperkalaemia is not a feature of autonomic dysfunction in GBS.

⭐ Key point: Monitor ECG continuously, and be prepared for bradycardia during suctioning (have atropine ready).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q56$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1375, $s$The specific treatments that shorten the course of Guillain-Barré syndrome are:$s$, $s$["Pyridostigmine and atropine", "Dantrolene and cooling", "IV immunoglobulin or plasma exchange", "High-dose steroids and antibiotics"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV immunoglobulin or plasma exchange
GBS is an autoimmune disease where antibodies attack the peripheral nerves. IV IMMUNOGLOBULIN (IVIG) or PLASMA EXCHANGE (plasmapheresis) shorten the course by neutralising or removing these antibodies. They are most effective when started within 2 weeks of symptoms.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pyridostigmine and atropine — Pyridostigmine and atropine are used in myasthenia gravis.
• Dantrolene and cooling — Dantrolene and cooling treat malignant hyperthermia.
• High-dose steroids and antibiotics — Steroids do NOT help GBS, and antibiotics are not indicated.

⭐ Key point: IVIG and plasma exchange are equally effective; don't combine them.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q57$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1376, $s$Critical illness polyneuropathy differs from Guillain-Barré syndrome in that it:$s$, $s$["It follows a diarrhoeal illness, with demyelination", "It always affects the facial muscles first", "It causes high CSF protein with no other cause", "It develops in sepsis and MOF, with axonal loss"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It develops in sepsis and MOF, with axonal loss
CRITICAL ILLNESS POLYNEUROPATHY (CIP) develops in patients with SEPSIS and MULTI-ORGAN FAILURE (MOF) during ICU stays. It damages the AXONS (the nerve fibres) of peripheral nerves, causing limb weakness and difficulty weaning from the ventilator.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It follows a diarrhoeal illness, with demyelination — A diarrhoeal illness followed by demyelination describes GBS (e.g. after Campylobacter).
• It always affects the facial muscles first — CIP usually spares facial muscles; it mainly affects the limbs.
• It causes high CSF protein with no other cause — High CSF protein (with normal cells) is typical of GBS, not CIP.

⭐ Key point: Nerve conduction studies show reduced amplitudes in CIP (axonal) and slowed conduction in GBS (demyelinating).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q58$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1377, $s$A key risk factor for ICU-acquired weakness that the nurse can reduce is:$s$, $s$["Early mobilisation and physiotherapy", "Tight glucose control below 10 mmol/L", "Daily sedation holds with SBTs", "Prolonged immobility and deep sedation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Prolonged immobility and deep sedation
ICU-ACQUIRED WEAKNESS (ICU-AW) is muscle and nerve weakness developing in ICU. Key risk factors that the nurse can reduce are PROLONGED IMMOBILITY and DEEP SEDATION. Muscles waste rapidly when not used. Other risk factors: sepsis, multi-organ failure, hyperglycaemia, steroids and neuromuscular blockers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Early mobilisation and physiotherapy — Early mobilisation and physiotherapy PREVENT ICU-AW; they are not risk factors.
• Tight glucose control below 10 mmol/L — Good glucose control reduces risk; it's not a risk factor.
• Daily sedation holds with SBTs — Daily sedation holds reduce risk; they're not a risk factor.

⭐ Key point: The ABCDEF bundle helps prevent ICU-AW: early mobility is "E".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q59$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1378, $s$Hyperemesis gravidarum may lead to which serious neurological complication?$s$, $s$["Guillain-Barré syndrome after infection", "Bell's palsy from dehydration", "Wernicke's encephalopathy (thiamine deficiency)", "Myasthenia gravis triggered by vomiting"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Wernicke's encephalopathy (thiamine deficiency)
HYPEREMESIS GRAVIDARUM is severe, persistent vomiting in pregnancy, causing dehydration, weight loss and electrolyte disturbance. Prolonged vomiting and poor intake lead to THIAMINE (vitamin B1) DEFICIENCY, because the body has small thiamine stores. Thiamine is needed for the brain to use glucose. Deficiency causes WERNICKE'S ENCEPHALOPATHY: CONFUSION, ophthalmoplegia (eye movement problems, nystagmus), and ATAXIA (unsteady gait). Untreated, it can progress to Korsakoff's psychosis (memory loss), coma and death. Giving glucose-containing IV fluid without thiamine can precipitate or worsen it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Guillain-Barré syndrome after infection — Guillain-Barré syndrome follows infections (e.g. Campylobacter), not hyperemesis.
• Bell's palsy from dehydration — Bell's palsy (facial nerve weakness) is more common in pregnancy but is not caused by dehydration from vomiting.
• Myasthenia gravis triggered by vomiting — Myasthenia gravis is an autoimmune disease; vomiting does not trigger it.

⭐ Key point: Give IV or oral thiamine to all women admitted with hyperemesis, before or with dextrose-containing fluids. Also correct potassium, sodium and magnesium, and give anti-emetics and thromboprophylaxis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q138$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1379, $s$A patient with hepatic encephalopathy needs nutrition. The current approach is:$s$, $s$["Fasting until the encephalopathy has resolved fully", "Complete protein restriction until the ammonia normalises", "Normal protein, with branched-chain amino acids if intolerant", "A high-fat, carbohydrate-free diet to reduce ammonia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Normal protein, with branched-chain amino acids if intolerant
In HEPATIC ENCEPHALOPATHY (brain dysfunction from liver failure), ammonia from protein breakdown in the gut contributes to confusion. In the past, protein was restricted. Now we know that protein restriction causes MUSCLE WASTING, and muscle is an important site for removing ammonia. Malnutrition worsens outcome. Current guidelines recommend NORMAL PROTEIN intake (about 1.2–1.5 g/kg/day), with energy of about 35 kcal/kg/day, small frequent meals and a late-evening snack. If the patient truly cannot tolerate dietary protein, BRANCHED-CHAIN AMINO ACIDS (BCAAs) or vegetable and dairy protein may be used.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fasting until the encephalopathy has resolved fully — Fasting causes the body to break down muscle for energy, which INCREASES ammonia production and worsens encephalopathy.
• Complete protein restriction until the ammonia normalises — Complete protein restriction is no longer recommended; it worsens malnutrition and muscle loss.
• A high-fat, carbohydrate-free diet to reduce ammonia — A high-fat, carbohydrate-free diet is not recommended and may worsen nutrition and liver function.

⭐ Key point: Treat encephalopathy with lactulose (to cause 2–3 soft stools daily and reduce ammonia absorption), rifaximin, and correction of precipitating factors (GI bleeding, infection, constipation, electrolyte imbalance, sedatives).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q186$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1380, $s$In the ABCDE primary survey, 'D' stands for:$s$, $s$["Drugs: give analgesia before assessing pain", "Diagnosis: reach a definitive diagnosis first", "Disability: GCS or AVPU, pupils and glucose", "Documentation: write the complete history"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Disability: GCS or AVPU, pupils and glucose
In the primary survey (ABCDE), D = DISABILITY: a quick NEUROLOGICAL assessment. Check the level of consciousness (GCS or AVPU), PUPILS (size, equality, reaction), and blood GLUCOSE (hypoglycaemia can cause reduced consciousness), plus limb movement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Drugs: give analgesia before assessing pain — "Drugs" isn't what D stands for.
• Diagnosis: reach a definitive diagnosis first — "Diagnosis" isn't what D stands for; diagnosis comes later.
• Documentation: write the complete history — "Documentation" isn't what D stands for.

⭐ Key point: E = Exposure: examine the whole body, while preventing hypothermia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q21$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1381, $s$According to the Monro-Kellie doctrine, approximately what proportion of intracranial volume is occupied by brain tissue?$s$, $s$["~80%", "~50%", "~30%", "~10%"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ~80%
The Monro–Kellie doctrine says the skull is a rigid box containing BRAIN tissue (about 80%), BLOOD (about 10%) and CSF (about 10%). If one increases (swelling, bleeding), another must decrease, or ICP rises.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• ~50% — 50% underestimates brain volume.
• ~30% — 30% underestimates brain volume.
• ~10% — 10% is the share of blood or CSF, not brain.

⭐ Key point: Early compensation: CSF is pushed into the spinal canal and venous blood is squeezed out. When this runs out, ICP rises steeply.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q41$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1382, $s$A PbtO2 value below which threshold on Licox monitoring is generally considered critical and warrants urgent intervention?$s$, $s$["Above 40 mmHg", "Above 100 mmHg", "Below 15-20 mmHg", "Below 60 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Below 15-20 mmHg
The Licox probe measures brain tissue oxygen (PbtO₂). Normal is about 20–35 mmHg. A PbtO₂ BELOW about 15–20 mmHg means brain tissue HYPOXIA and needs urgent treatment (raise FiO₂, CPP or haemoglobin, lower ICP, treat fever and seizures). Below 10 is critical.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Above 40 mmHg — Above 40 mmHg isn't critical.
• Above 100 mmHg — Above 100 mmHg isn't a threshold of concern.
• Below 60 mmHg — Below 60 mmHg includes normal values; 60 isn't the threshold.

⭐ Key point: Brain hypoxia worsens outcome after brain injury, even when ICP is controlled.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q42$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1383, $s$A patient's ICP is 28 mmHg and MAP is 80 mmHg. The cerebral perfusion pressure is:$s$, $s$["52 mmHg, below the target of 60–70 mmHg", "108 mmHg, above the target range", "80 mmHg, within the target range", "28 mmHg, normal after head injury"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 52 mmHg, below the target of 60–70 mmHg
CPP = MAP − ICP = 80 − 28 = 52 mmHg. The target is 60–70 mmHg, so 52 is BELOW target. The brain isn't getting enough blood. Treat by LOWERING ICP (head up, sedation, osmotherapy, CSF drainage) and/or RAISING MAP (fluids, vasopressors).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 108 mmHg, above the target range — 108 is MAP + ICP (wrong calculation).
• 80 mmHg, within the target range — 80 is the MAP, not the CPP.
• 28 mmHg, normal after head injury — 28 is the ICP, which is high.

⭐ Key point: ICP of 28 is above the treatment threshold (20–22 mmHg).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q43 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1384, $s$A 'plateau wave' (Lundberg A wave) on ICP monitoring indicates:$s$, $s$["Improving brain compliance after treatment", "Sustained ICP of 50–100 mmHg; compliance exhausted", "A blocked transducer giving falsely low readings", "Normal pulsatile ICP changes with the heartbeat"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sustained ICP of 50–100 mmHg; compliance exhausted
Lundberg A (PLATEAU) waves are sustained rises in ICP to 50–100 mmHg, lasting 5–20 minutes. They show that the brain's COMPLIANCE is EXHAUSTED: it can no longer tolerate extra volume. They are ominous, reduce cerebral perfusion, and need urgent treatment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improving brain compliance after treatment — They show worsening, not improving, compliance.
• A blocked transducer giving falsely low readings — A blocked transducer gives a flat or dampened trace, not plateau waves.
• Normal pulsatile ICP changes with the heartbeat — Normal pulse waves are small, rapid changes with each heartbeat.

⭐ Key point: Lundberg B waves are smaller oscillations (0.5–2 per minute) and may precede A waves. C waves are normal.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q44$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1385, $s$A dampened, flat ICP waveform on an EVD system most likely means:$s$, $s$["Infection of the CSF with raised white cells", "Excellent brain oxygenation and low ICP", "Normal ICP with good intracranial compliance", "Blockage or air in the system, or a collapsed ventricle"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Blockage or air in the system, or a collapsed ventricle
A normal ICP waveform pulsates with each heartbeat. A DAMPENED, FLAT trace on an EVD usually means a technical problem: BLOCKAGE (a clot or debris in the catheter), AIR in the tubing, a closed clamp, a kink, or a COLLAPSED VENTRICLE (from over-drainage) around the catheter tip.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Infection of the CSF with raised white cells — Infection doesn't cause a flat waveform.
• Excellent brain oxygenation and low ICP — A flat trace doesn't indicate good oxygenation.
• Normal ICP with good intracranial compliance — A flat trace isn't normal; normal ICP still pulsates.

⭐ Key point: Check clamps, kinks and air, re-level, and inform the neurosurgeon. NEVER flush toward the patient's brain.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q45$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1386, $s$CSF from an EVD becomes cloudy, the patient febrile, and CSF glucose low. The nurse suspects:$s$, $s$["Ventriculitis", "Subarachnoid haemorrhage", "Normal post-insertion change", "Over-drainage of CSF"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ventriculitis
VENTRICULITIS (infection of the ventricles) is a serious complication of an EVD. Signs: CLOUDY CSF, FEVER, LOW CSF GLUCOSE (bacteria use it), high CSF white cells and protein, neck stiffness, and a falling GCS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Subarachnoid haemorrhage — Subarachnoid haemorrhage gives blood-stained CSF, not cloudy CSF with low glucose.
• Normal post-insertion change — These aren't normal post-insertion changes.
• Over-drainage of CSF — Over-drainage causes headache and low ICP, not cloudy CSF with fever.

⭐ Key point: Send CSF for microscopy, culture and glucose. Use strict asepsis and limit sampling to reduce infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q46$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1387, $s$The ICP threshold that generally triggers treatment in severe traumatic brain injury is above:$s$, $s$["10–15 mmHg", "5–10 mmHg", "40–45 mmHg", "20–22 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 20–22 mmHg
In severe traumatic brain injury, treatment usually starts when ICP is ABOVE 20–22 mmHg (Brain Trauma Foundation guidelines). Normal ICP is about 5–15 mmHg.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10–15 mmHg — 10–15 mmHg is normal.
• 5–10 mmHg — 5–10 mmHg is normal.
• 40–45 mmHg — 40–45 mmHg is far above the threshold; treatment should start well before.

⭐ Key point: Treat raised ICP in steps: head up, sedation and analgesia, normal CO₂, osmotherapy, CSF drainage, then barbiturate coma or decompressive craniectomy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q47$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1388, $s$Hyperosmolar therapy for raised ICP uses:$s$, $s$["Mannitol or hypertonic saline", "Hartmann's solution boluses", "5% dextrose boluses", "Albumin 4.5% only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mannitol or hypertonic saline
HYPEROSMOLAR therapy raises blood osmolality, drawing water OUT of brain tissue and reducing brain swelling and ICP. The agents are MANNITOL (0.25–1 g/kg IV) and HYPERTONIC SALINE (3%, 7.5% or 23.4%).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hartmann's solution boluses — Hartmann's is slightly hypotonic; it doesn't lower ICP.
• 5% dextrose boluses — 5% dextrose is effectively hypotonic and WORSENS brain swelling.
• Albumin 4.5% only — Albumin 4.5% alone doesn't act as hyperosmolar therapy.

⭐ Key point: Monitor sodium and serum osmolality. Mannitol can cause dehydration and kidney injury; hypertonic saline can cause high sodium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q48$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1389, $s$Short-term hyperventilation to PaCO₂ 4–4.5 kPa (30–35 mmHg) lowers ICP by:$s$, $s$["Increased cerebral blood flow and oxygen delivery", "Dilatation of the cerebral veins and sinuses", "Cerebral vasoconstriction, reducing blood volume", "Increased CSF production by the choroid plexus"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cerebral vasoconstriction, reducing blood volume
Lowering PaCO₂ by hyperventilation causes CEREBRAL VASOCONSTRICTION (the brain's arteries narrow). This REDUCES cerebral BLOOD VOLUME, lowering ICP quickly. However, narrowed vessels also reduce blood flow and can cause brain ischaemia, so it is used only briefly for impending herniation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased cerebral blood flow and oxygen delivery — Hyperventilation REDUCES, not increases, cerebral blood flow.
• Dilatation of the cerebral veins and sinuses — It doesn't dilate cerebral veins.
• Increased CSF production by the choroid plexus — It doesn't increase CSF production.

⭐ Key point: The usual target is normal PaCO₂ (4.5–5.0 kPa, 35–40 mmHg). Avoid prolonged hyperventilation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q49$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1390, $s$A combination of hypertension, bradycardia and irregular breathing in a head-injured patient is:$s$, $s$["Beck's triad of cardiac tamponade", "Cushing's triad, a late sign of herniation", "Virchow's triad of venous thrombosis", "Charcot's triad of ascending cholangitis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cushing's triad, a late sign of herniation
HYPERTENSION (with a wide pulse pressure), BRADYCARDIA and IRREGULAR BREATHING in a head-injured patient is CUSHING'S TRIAD: a late sign of severely raised ICP and impending brainstem HERNIATION. It needs immediate escalation and emergency treatment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Beck's triad of cardiac tamponade — Beck's triad (low BP, raised JVP, muffled heart sounds) is cardiac tamponade.
• Virchow's triad of venous thrombosis — Virchow's triad (stasis, endothelial injury, hypercoagulability) explains venous thrombosis.
• Charcot's triad of ascending cholangitis — Charcot's triad (fever, jaundice, RUQ pain) is ascending cholangitis.

⭐ Key point: Don't wait for Cushing's triad. A falling GCS and pupil changes are earlier warning signs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q50$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1391, $s$ICU-Acquired Weakness differs from Guillain-Barré Syndrome in that ICU-AW involves:$s$, $s$["Segmental demyelination of the nerves", "Axonal degeneration without demyelination", "Only facial and bulbar muscle weakness", "No nerve involvement at all, only muscle"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Axonal degeneration without demyelination
ICU-acquired weakness (critical illness polyneuropathy) mainly involves AXONAL DEGENERATION (damage to the nerve fibre itself) WITHOUT significant demyelination. Guillain–Barré syndrome (classic form) involves SEGMENTAL DEMYELINATION (damage to the myelin sheath). Nerve conduction studies help tell them apart.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Segmental demyelination of the nerves — Segmental demyelination is typical of GBS.
• Only facial and bulbar muscle weakness — ICU-AW affects the limbs and respiratory muscles, not mainly the face and bulbar muscles.
• No nerve involvement at all, only muscle — ICU-AW CAN involve nerves (polyneuropathy) as well as muscle (myopathy).

⭐ Key point: ICU-AW risk factors: sepsis, multi-organ failure, high glucose, steroids, prolonged immobility and neuromuscular blockers.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q56$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1392, $s$Which muscle group is almost always SPARED in ICU-Acquired Weakness?$s$, $s$["Facial and ocular muscles", "Hip girdle muscles", "Respiratory muscles", "Proximal limb muscles"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Facial and ocular muscles
ICU-acquired weakness is a symmetrical, flaccid weakness mainly of the PROXIMAL LIMB muscles (shoulders, hips) and the RESPIRATORY muscles, with reduced reflexes. The FACIAL and OCULAR (eye) muscles are almost always SPARED. If facial or eye muscles are weak, consider other causes (GBS, myasthenia, brainstem stroke).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hip girdle muscles — The hip girdle muscles ARE typically affected.
• Respiratory muscles — The respiratory muscles ARE typically affected (causing difficult weaning).
• Proximal limb muscles — The proximal limb muscles ARE typically affected.

⭐ Key point: Assess ICU-AW with the MRC sum score (below 48/60 means weakness).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q57$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1393, $s$A lumbar puncture is generally avoided or delayed in a patient with suspected meningitis if there are signs of raised intracranial pressure, primarily because of the risk of:$s$, $s$["No particular risk exists in this situation at all", "Brain herniation from the pressure change of CSF removal", "A reduced risk of spreading the infection", "Improvement in the patient's neurological outcome"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Brain herniation from the pressure change of CSF removal
When intracranial pressure (ICP) is high, the brain is already squeezed inside the closed skull. A lumbar puncture suddenly removes fluid from BELOW (the spinal canal). This creates a pressure difference between the skull and the spine, and the brain can be pushed downwards through the opening at the base of the skull (the foramen magnum). This is called BRAIN HERNIATION ("coning"). It compresses the brainstem, which controls breathing and heart rate, and can kill the patient within minutes. Signs that make LP unsafe include reduced consciousness (GCS falling), new focal weakness, seizures, papilloedema, unequal or sluggish pupils, and Cushing's signs (high BP, slow pulse, irregular breathing).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No particular risk exists in this situation at all — This is false. Herniation is a real and well-known danger of LP when ICP is raised.
• A reduced risk of spreading the infection — LP does not reduce infection spread, and avoiding it is not about infection risk at all. The concern is pressure, not germs.
• Improvement in the patient's neurological outcome — Removing CSF in raised ICP can worsen the outcome (herniation and death). It does not improve neurological outcome.

⭐ Key point: In suspected meningitis with signs of raised ICP: do NOT delay antibiotics. Take blood cultures, give IV antibiotics (e.g. ceftriaxone) immediately, do a CT scan first, and do the LP later only when it is safe.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q101$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1394, $s$In assessment of unconscious patient which of this is the ideal sequence used?$s$, $s$["EACAB", "ABCDE", "CABDE", "BACDE"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ABCDE
ABCDE is the internationally used sequence for assessing any seriously ill or unconscious patient: AIRWAY (is it open?), BREATHING (rate, SpO₂, chest movement), CIRCULATION (pulse, BP, perfusion, bleeding), DISABILITY (GCS or AVPU, pupils, glucose), EXPOSURE (examine the whole body, keep warm). Each problem is treated as it is found before moving to the next step.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• EACAB — "EACAB" is not a recognised sequence.
• CABDE — CABDE is used in CARDIAC ARREST (compressions first), not for assessing an unconscious patient with a pulse.
• BACDE — BACDE is not a recognised sequence; airway always comes before breathing.

⭐ Key point: In trauma, catastrophic bleeding is treated first: <C>ABCDE. Reassess ABCDE after every intervention.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q63$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1395, $s$Glasgow Coma Scale of E1 V1 M4 means the patient:$s$, $s$["Obeys commands but makes no sound at all", "Is fully conscious and orientated to place", "Opens eyes to speech and is confused in speech", "No eye opening, no voice, and withdraws from pain"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: No eye opening, no voice, and withdraws from pain
Read each part of the score. E1 = eyes do NOT open even to pain. V1 = NO verbal sound at all. M4 = WITHDRAWS from pain (pulls the limb away from the painful stimulus). Total GCS = 1 + 1 + 4 = 6, which is a severe brain injury (GCS 8 or less), so the airway needs protecting.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Obeys commands but makes no sound at all — Obeying commands would be M6, and making no sound is V1; this doesn't match E1 V1 M4.
• Is fully conscious and orientated to place — Fully conscious and orientated would be E4 V5 M6 (GCS 15).
• Opens eyes to speech and is confused in speech — Eyes opening to speech is E3 and confused speech is V4; neither is in this score.

⭐ Key point: Motor scores: M6 obeys, M5 localises, M4 withdraws (normal flexion), M3 abnormal flexion (decorticate), M2 extension (decerebrate), M1 none. The motor score is the best predictor of outcome.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q71$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1396, $s$Hearing is thought to be the last sense lost in unconscious patients, so nurses should:$s$, $s$["Play loud music continuously", "Talk to the patient and explain procedures", "Discuss the prognosis loudly at the bedside", "Avoid speaking near the bed"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Talk to the patient and explain procedures
Hearing is believed to be one of the last senses lost in unconscious and dying patients, and some patients later remember things said around them. Nurses should therefore TALK to the patient: greet them by name, explain what you are about to do, reorient them (day, time, place) and encourage the family to speak to them. This respects dignity and may reduce fear and delirium.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Play loud music continuously — Loud continuous music can be disturbing and stops rest; gentle familiar music may help.
• Discuss the prognosis loudly at the bedside — Discussing a poor prognosis at the bedside can distress the patient.
• Avoid speaking near the bed — Avoiding speaking leaves the patient isolated and frightened.

⭐ Key point: Always assume an unconscious patient can hear. Don't say things at the bedside you wouldn't say if they were awake.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q76$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1397, $s$A positive Babinski sign in an adult (great toe dorsiflexes, toes fan out) indicates:$s$, $s$["A lower motor neurone lesion in the leg", "A normal plantar reflex in an adult", "An upper motor neurone (corticospinal) lesion", "Peripheral neuropathy from diabetes"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An upper motor neurone (corticospinal) lesion
The Babinski sign tests the corticospinal tract (upper motor neurone pathway from the brain to the spinal cord). In a healthy adult, stroking the sole makes the toes curl DOWN (plantar flexion). If the big toe goes UP (dorsiflexes) and the other toes fan out, this is a positive Babinski sign, which means damage to the upper motor neurone: stroke, brain injury, spinal cord injury, or raised ICP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A lower motor neurone lesion in the leg — A lower motor neurone lesion causes absent or reduced reflexes, not a positive Babinski.
• A normal plantar reflex in an adult — In adults, the normal response is toes curling DOWN; an upgoing toe is abnormal.
• Peripheral neuropathy from diabetes — Diabetic peripheral neuropathy reduces reflexes; it doesn't cause a Babinski sign.

⭐ Key point: A positive Babinski is NORMAL in babies under about 1–2 years, because their corticospinal tracts are not yet fully developed (myelinated).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q81$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1398, $s$Babinski sign is tested by:$s$, $s$["Flexing the neck forward towards the chest", "Stroking the lateral sole from heel to toes", "Tapping the patellar tendon with a hammer", "Pricking the big toe with a sterile pin"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stroking the lateral sole from heel to toes
The Babinski sign (plantar reflex) is tested by firmly stroking the OUTER (lateral) edge of the sole with a blunt object (the handle of a reflex hammer or a key), from the HEEL up toward the toes, then across the ball of the foot toward the big toe. Watch the big toe: normal adults show toes curling down; upgoing is a positive Babinski.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flexing the neck forward towards the chest — Flexing the neck tests for Brudzinski's sign (meningeal irritation).
• Tapping the patellar tendon with a hammer — Tapping the patellar tendon tests the knee jerk reflex.
• Pricking the big toe with a sterile pin — Pricking the toe tests pain sensation, not the plantar reflex.

⭐ Key point: Use a blunt object, not a sharp one. Always compare both feet.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q82$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1399, $s$A patient with GBS has absent knee and ankle jerks. This indicates:$s$, $s$["Upper motor neurone involvement in the cord", "Spinal shock from an injury above T6", "Lower motor neurone involvement (areflexia)", "A normal finding in critically ill adults"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lower motor neurone involvement (areflexia)
Guillain–Barré syndrome attacks the PERIPHERAL nerves, which are part of the LOWER motor neurone pathway. Damaged peripheral nerves can't carry the reflex arc, so reflexes are reduced or lost (areflexia), usually early in the illness. GBS causes flaccid (floppy) weakness that usually starts in the legs and moves up.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Upper motor neurone involvement in the cord — Upper motor neurone lesions cause BRISK reflexes, not absent ones.
• Spinal shock from an injury above T6 — Spinal shock happens after spinal cord injury, not GBS.
• A normal finding in critically ill adults — Absent reflexes are an abnormal finding, not normal.

⭐ Key point: GBS: ascending flaccid weakness, absent reflexes, tingling. Monitor breathing (FVC) and the autonomic system (BP and heart rate swings).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q83$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1400, $s$Skin plays a role in thermoregulation mainly through:$s$, $s$["Sweating and changes in skin blood flow", "Absorbing oxygen from the air", "Producing red cells in the skin", "Producing insulin in the dermis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sweating and changes in skin blood flow
The skin controls body temperature in two main ways. SWEATING: sweat evaporates and removes heat. Changes in SKIN BLOOD FLOW: when hot, the skin vessels widen (vasodilation) to lose heat; when cold, they narrow (vasoconstriction) to keep heat in. Shivering also produces heat.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Absorbing oxygen from the air — The skin doesn't absorb significant oxygen from the air.
• Producing red cells in the skin — Red cells are made in the bone marrow, not the skin.
• Producing insulin in the dermis — Insulin is made by the pancreas.

⭐ Key point: Patients with large burns or TEN lose this control and lose heat rapidly. Keep the room warm (28–32 °C) and use warming devices.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q91$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1401, $s$In critically ill patients, skin failure ('acute skin failure') is caused by:$s$, $s$["Skin hypoperfusion during multi-organ failure", "Early mobilisation and turning in bed", "Regular use of moisturisers on the skin", "Excessive bathing with soap and water only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Skin hypoperfusion during multi-organ failure
Acute skin failure happens when the skin's blood supply falls during critical illness: shock, multi-organ failure, vasopressor use, and low oxygen. Like any other organ, the skin is damaged when it isn't perfused. Pressure injuries can develop even with excellent nursing care, because the skin is already failing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Early mobilisation and turning in bed — Early mobilisation and turning PROTECT the skin.
• Regular use of moisturisers on the skin — Moisturisers protect the skin and prevent cracks.
• Excessive bathing with soap and water only — Excessive bathing can dry the skin but doesn't cause acute skin failure.

⭐ Key point: Document skin assessment carefully. Not all pressure injuries in dying or shocked patients are due to poor care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q92$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1402, $s$Isoniazid neuropathy is prevented by giving:$s$, $s$["Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)", "Vitamin K (phytomenadione)", "Folic acid (vitamin B9)", "Ascorbic acid (vitamin C)"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pyridoxine (vitamin B6)
Isoniazid, a key anti-TB drug, interferes with vitamin B6 (pyridoxine) in the body, causing a peripheral neuropathy (tingling and numbness in the hands and feet). Giving pyridoxine (10–25 mg daily) prevents this, especially in people at higher risk: diabetes, HIV, malnutrition, alcohol misuse, pregnancy and breastfeeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vitamin K (phytomenadione) — Vitamin K is for clotting.
• Folic acid (vitamin B9) — Folic acid is for red cell production and pregnancy.
• Ascorbic acid (vitamin C) — Vitamin C does not prevent isoniazid neuropathy.

⭐ Key point: Isoniazid can also cause liver damage (hepatitis), so check LFTs in high-risk patients. In isoniazid overdose (seizures), give high-dose pyridoxine.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q94$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1403, $s$A deep tendon reflex graded 4+ means:$s$, $s$["Normal, as expected", "Very brisk, with clonus", "Sluggish and diminished", "Absent, even with reinforcement"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Very brisk, with clonus
Deep tendon reflexes are graded from 0 to 4+. A grade of 4+ means VERY BRISK, hyperactive reflexes WITH CLONUS (repeated rhythmic jerking when the muscle is stretched, such as the foot beating when the ankle is pushed up). This suggests an upper motor neurone lesion or a hyperexcitable state (such as magnesium deficiency or pre-eclampsia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal, as expected — Normal is 2+.
• Sluggish and diminished — Sluggish or diminished is 1+.
• Absent, even with reinforcement — Absent is 0.

⭐ Key point: In pre-eclampsia, brisk reflexes and clonus suggest a risk of fits. With magnesium therapy, LOSS of reflexes is a sign of toxicity.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q95$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1404, $s$Autonomic dysreflexia in a T4 cord injury patient is triggered by:$s$, $s$["A low blood sugar level", "A full bladder or constipation", "Excess oral fluid intake", "Hypothermia after bathing"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A full bladder or constipation
Autonomic dysreflexia happens in spinal cord injury at T6 or above. A stimulus below the injury, most often a FULL BLADDER (blocked catheter) or CONSTIPATION, triggers a massive sympathetic response. The vessels below the injury constrict, causing dangerously high BP. The body above the injury tries to compensate: slow pulse, flushing, sweating and a pounding headache above the injury.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A low blood sugar level — Low blood sugar doesn't trigger autonomic dysreflexia.
• Excess oral fluid intake — Drinking extra fluid doesn't trigger it directly, although a full bladder does.
• Hypothermia after bathing — Hypothermia doesn't usually trigger it.

⭐ Key point: Act at once: sit the patient upright, loosen clothing, check the catheter or empty the bladder, check for constipation, and give fast-acting BP drugs (GTN, nifedipine) if BP stays high.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q98$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1405, $s$Decerebrate posturing (extension of arms and legs) indicates:$s$, $s$["A cortical lesion only", "An isolated spinal cord injury", "Brain stem damage (midbrain/pons)", "A normal response to pain"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Brain stem damage (midbrain/pons)
Decerebrate posturing is when the arms and legs EXTEND (straighten), the arms rotate inward, the wrists flex and the toes point down. It shows severe damage to the BRAINSTEM (midbrain or upper pons). It is scored M2 on the GCS and has a poor prognosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A cortical lesion only — A cortical (upper brain) lesion causes DECORTICATE posturing (arms flexed to the chest), scored M3.
• An isolated spinal cord injury — A spinal cord injury causes paralysis below the injury, not posturing.
• A normal response to pain — Posturing is never a normal response to pain.

⭐ Key point: Decorticate (flexion, M3) = injury above the brainstem. Decerebrate (extension, M2) = brainstem injury, which is worse. A change from flexion to extension means the patient is getting worse.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q99$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1406, $s$Polyhydramnios with a fetus that cannot swallow suggests:$s$, $s$["Post-maturity beyond 42 weeks", "Bilateral fetal renal agenesis", "Placental insufficiency and IUGR", "Oesophageal atresia or neurological anomaly"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Oesophageal atresia or neurological anomaly
Before birth, the fetus swallows amniotic fluid, which helps keep the fluid volume normal. If the fetus CANNOT SWALLOW, fluid builds up, causing POLYHYDRAMNIOS. Causes: OESOPHAGEAL ATRESIA (the oesophagus is blocked), duodenal atresia, or NEUROLOGICAL problems that affect swallowing (anencephaly, muscle disorders).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Post-maturity beyond 42 weeks — Post-maturity is linked to reduced fluid (oligohydramnios).
• Bilateral fetal renal agenesis — Bilateral renal agenesis causes NO fluid (oligohydramnios), because the fetus makes no urine.
• Placental insufficiency and IUGR — Placental insufficiency with IUGR is linked to reduced fluid (oligohydramnios).

⭐ Key point: At birth, pass an NG tube to check the oesophagus is open. If it won't pass, suspect oesophageal atresia: no feeds, suction the upper pouch.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q133$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1407, $s$An intraventricular pressure monitor has the advantage that it:$s$, $s$["Measures brain tissue oxygen", "Never becomes infected", "Is completely non-invasive", "Measures ICP and allows CSF drainage"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Measures ICP and allows CSF drainage
An intraventricular catheter (EVD) is placed in the lateral ventricle. It is the GOLD STANDARD for ICP measurement, because it measures pressure directly in the CSF and can be re-zeroed. It also allows CSF DRAINAGE to lower ICP and allows CSF sampling.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Measures brain tissue oxygen — Brain tissue oxygen is measured by a Licox probe.
• Never becomes infected — It has the highest infection risk among ICP monitors (ventriculitis).
• Is completely non-invasive — It is highly invasive (inserted through the skull).

⭐ Key point: EVD care: level to the tragus, clamp when moving, strict asepsis, and record CSF volume and colour hourly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q35$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1408, $s$CT scanning is preferred over plain X-ray in head injury because it shows:$s$, $s$["Intracranial bleeding and swelling", "Only wounds of the scalp", "Only disease of the sinuses", "Only fractures of the skull"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intracranial bleeding and swelling
A plain skull X-ray only shows bones. CT of the head shows the BRAIN: intracranial BLEEDING (extradural, subdural, subarachnoid, intracerebral), brain SWELLING (oedema), midline shift and hydrocephalus, as well as skull fractures. These findings decide whether surgery is needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only wounds of the scalp — CT shows much more than scalp wounds.
• Only disease of the sinuses — CT isn't mainly for sinus disease.
• Only fractures of the skull — CT shows more than skull fractures.

⭐ Key point: Non-contrast CT head is the first investigation in head injury and suspected stroke.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q137$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1409, $s$Raised intracranial pressure above what value is a contraindication to physiotherapy?$s$, $s$[">5mmHg", ">20mmHg", ">50mmHg", "ICP is not a consideration"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: >20mmHg
Chest physiotherapy (positioning, percussion, suction, coughing) can temporarily RAISE ICP. When ICP is already HIGH (above 20 mmHg, the treatment threshold), further rises could reduce brain perfusion and cause harm. Physiotherapy is therefore avoided or modified when ICP is above 20 mmHg.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• >5mmHg — 5 mmHg is normal ICP.
• >50mmHg — 50 mmHg is dangerously high; physiotherapy would have stopped long before.
• ICP is not a consideration — ICP IS an important consideration.

⭐ Key point: Cluster care sensibly in head injury, and give sedation before suction or physiotherapy if ICP rises easily.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q143$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1410, $s$Postural drainage is contraindicated in a patient with raised ICP above:$s$, $s$["ICP is not relevant to PD", "5mmHg", "20mmHg", "100mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 20mmHg
Postural drainage uses gravity, sometimes with HEAD-DOWN positions, to drain secretions. Head-down positions RAISE ICP by reducing venous drainage from the brain. When ICP is already above 20 mmHg, these positions are dangerous and contraindicated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• ICP is not relevant to PD — ICP IS relevant to postural drainage.
• 5mmHg — 5 mmHg is normal; postural drainage is fine.
• 100mmHg — 100 mmHg is extremely high; the threshold is much lower.

⭐ Key point: In head-injured patients, keep the head of the bed up at 30° and the neck straight.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q144$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1411, $s$A neonate with seizures should first have checked:$s$, $s$["Hearing response to sound", "Visual response to light", "Hip stability on testing", "Blood glucose and calcium"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Blood glucose and calcium
Seizures in newborns often have TREATABLE metabolic causes, mainly HYPOGLYCAEMIA (low blood sugar) and HYPOCALCAEMIA (low calcium). These must be checked FIRST and corrected quickly, because treating them can stop the seizures and prevent brain damage. Other causes include hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy, infection, bleeding and low magnesium.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hearing response to sound — Hearing isn't urgent in a seizing baby.
• Visual response to light — Visual response isn't relevant.
• Hip stability on testing — Hip stability isn't relevant.

⭐ Key point: Low glucose in a newborn: below 2.6 mmol/L. Treat with 2 mL/kg of 10% dextrose IV.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q189$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1412, $s$Cranial nerves originate directly from the:$s$, $s$["Brain and brainstem", "Peripheral muscles", "Autonomic ganglia only", "Spinal cord only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Brain and brainstem
The 12 pairs of CRANIAL NERVES come directly from the BRAIN and BRAINSTEM (the olfactory and optic nerves from the forebrain; the others from the midbrain, pons and medulla). They control the senses, eye movements, face, swallowing, and the vagus supplies the heart, lungs and gut.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Peripheral muscles — Nerves don't originate in muscles.
• Autonomic ganglia only — Autonomic ganglia are relay stations, not the origin of cranial nerves.
• Spinal cord only — Spinal nerves (31 pairs) come from the spinal cord.

⭐ Key point: Cranial nerve checks in ICU include pupils (CN III), the corneal reflex (V and VII), and the gag and cough reflexes (IX and X).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q13$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1413, $s$A positive Lasègue sign (straight-leg raise) suggests damage to:$s$, $s$["Spinal roots C5–C8 or radial nerve", "Spinal roots L5–S1 or sciatic nerve", "Meninges of the brain", "Anterior horns at the level L5–S1"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Spinal roots L5–S1 or sciatic nerve
Lasègue's sign (the straight-leg raise test): with the patient lying flat, the straight leg is lifted. Pain shooting down the back of the leg between 30° and 70° is a positive sign. It stretches the SCIATIC nerve and the L5–S1 nerve roots, suggesting compression or irritation, usually from a herniated (slipped) lumbar disc.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Spinal roots C5–C8 or radial nerve — The C5–C8 roots and the radial nerve supply the arm.
• Meninges of the brain — The meninges of the brain are tested by neck stiffness, Kernig's and Brudzinski's signs.
• Anterior horns at the level L5–S1 — Anterior horn damage causes weakness, not pain on stretching.

⭐ Key point: A slipped disc with saddle numbness, bladder or bowel dysfunction, and leg weakness suggests cauda equina syndrome, which is a surgical emergency.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q63$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1414, $s$After ROSC, targeted temperature management is used to protect:$s$, $s$["The liver", "The kidneys", "The skin", "The brain"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The brain
After the heart restarts (ROSC), the BRAIN is vulnerable to further damage (post-cardiac arrest brain injury) from swelling, inflammation and free radicals. TARGETED TEMPERATURE MANAGEMENT (keeping temperature at 32–36 °C, and above all AVOIDING FEVER) reduces this damage and improves neurological outcome.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The liver — TTM isn't mainly to protect the liver.
• The kidneys — TTM isn't mainly to protect the kidneys.
• The skin — TTM isn't to protect the skin.

⭐ Key point: After cardiac arrest, prevent fever (above 37.7 °C) for at least 72 hours, and control shivering.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q64$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1415, $s$Babinski's sign, when positive (upgoing toe) in an adult, suggests:$s$, $s$["Upper motor neuron pathology", "A normal finding in all healthy adults", "No clinical significance", "A peripheral nerve injury only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Upper motor neuron pathology
A positive Babinski sign (the big toe goes UP and the toes fan out when the sole is stroked) in an ADULT means damage to the UPPER MOTOR NEURONE pathway (corticospinal tract): stroke, brain or spinal cord injury, tumours or raised ICP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal finding in all healthy adults — It is NOT normal in healthy adults (adults' toes curl down).
• No clinical significance — It is clinically significant.
• A peripheral nerve injury only — Peripheral nerve injury doesn't cause a Babinski sign.

⭐ Key point: Babinski is normal in infants under about 1–2 years, because their corticospinal tracts aren't fully myelinated.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q65$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1416, $s$Which of the following is a FOCAL neurological sign?$s$, $s$["Vomiting", "Generalised drowsiness", "Headache", "Hemiparesis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hemiparesis
A FOCAL neurological sign points to damage in a SPECIFIC part of the brain or nervous system. HEMIPARESIS (weakness of one side of the body) is focal: it means a lesion in the opposite side of the brain (such as a stroke). Other focal signs: unequal pupils, aphasia, visual field loss and facial droop.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vomiting — Vomiting is a general sign (raised ICP, many causes).
• Generalised drowsiness — Generalised drowsiness reflects overall brain dysfunction, not a specific site.
• Headache — Headache is a general symptom.

⭐ Key point: New focal signs in a head-injured or stroke patient need urgent CT and senior review.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q66$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1417, $s$Hemianaesthesia, hemianopia and sensory hemiataxia together suggest damage to the:$s$, $s$["Substantia nigra", "Internal capsule", "Spinal cord", "Thalamus"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Thalamus
The THALAMUS is the brain's sensory relay station. Damage to it (often a stroke) causes loss of sensation on the opposite side of the body (HEMIANAESTHESIA), poor coordination from loss of position sense (SENSORY HEMIATAXIA), and sometimes loss of half the visual field (HEMIANOPIA). Later, thalamic pain syndrome may develop.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Substantia nigra — The substantia nigra is involved in Parkinson's disease (movement).
• Internal capsule — The internal capsule mainly causes MOTOR weakness (hemiparesis).
• Spinal cord — Spinal cord lesions don't cause hemianopia.

⭐ Key point: Thalamic pain syndrome (Dejerine–Roussy) causes severe burning pain on the affected side after a thalamic stroke.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q67$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1418, $s$Muscle wasting (hypotrophy) usually develops with disease of the:$s$, $s$["Lower motor neuron", "Upper motor neuron", "Caudate nucleus", "Cerebellum"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lower motor neuron
MUSCLE WASTING (atrophy) develops in LOWER MOTOR NEURONE disease (damage to anterior horn cells, nerve roots or peripheral nerves), because the muscle loses its nerve supply and the trophic factors that keep it healthy. Wasting is marked and appears early.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Upper motor neuron — Upper motor neurone lesions cause little wasting (only mild disuse atrophy).
• Caudate nucleus — The caudate nucleus is part of the basal ganglia (movement control).
• Cerebellum — Cerebellar lesions cause incoordination, not wasting.

⭐ Key point: LMN signs: wasting, fasciculations, weakness, low tone, and reduced or absent reflexes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q68$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1419, $s$A nurse while conducting a physical examination on newly admitted patient observed that, the patient is photophobic, easily irritated by alarm sounds in the unit. The most likely condition of this patient is:$s$, $s$["Tetanus", "Epilepsy", "Meningitis", "Severe malaria"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Meningitis
PHOTOPHOBIA (discomfort with light) and IRRITABILITY to stimuli (such as alarm sounds) are classic signs of MENINGEAL IRRITATION, found in MENINGITIS (and subarachnoid haemorrhage). Other signs: headache, fever, neck stiffness, and positive Kernig's and Brudzinski's signs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tetanus — Tetanus causes spasms triggered by stimuli, but not photophobia.
• Epilepsy — Epilepsy causes seizures; photophobia isn't typical between seizures.
• Severe malaria — Severe malaria can cause altered consciousness, but photophobia is typical of meningitis.

⭐ Key point: Suspected bacterial meningitis: blood cultures, IV ceftriaxone immediately, and droplet precautions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q70$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1420, $s$Coma (head injury) is classified as severe when the Glasgow Coma Scale is:$s$, $s$["≤12", "≤14", "≤10", "≤8"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ≤8
Head injury severity by GCS: MILD 13–15, MODERATE 9–12, SEVERE 8 OR LESS. A GCS of 8 or less is severe and is also the usual threshold for intubation to protect the airway.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• ≤12 — 12 or less includes moderate injuries.
• ≤14 — 14 or less includes mild injuries.
• ≤10 — 10 or less isn't the standard cut-off.

⭐ Key point: "GCS 8, intubate." Protect the brain: oxygen, normal CO₂, adequate BP, head up 30°.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q71$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1421, $s$Cushing's triad, a late sign of raised intracranial pressure, consists of:$s$, $s$["Normal vital signs with confusion", "Hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respirations", "Hypotension, bradycardia, and regular respirations", "Hypotension, tachycardia, and tachypnoea"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypertension, bradycardia, and irregular respirations
Cushing's triad is a LATE sign of raised ICP and approaching brainstem herniation. It includes HYPERTENSION (with a widened pulse pressure, as the body pushes blood into the brain), BRADYCARDIA (a reflex and brainstem compression), and IRREGULAR RESPIRATIONS (pressure on the breathing centre).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal vital signs with confusion — Normal vital signs with confusion is an early but non-specific picture.
• Hypotension, bradycardia, and regular respirations — Low BP with slow pulse and regular breathing isn't Cushing's triad.
• Hypotension, tachycardia, and tachypnoea — Low BP, fast pulse and fast breathing is shock.

⭐ Key point: Cushing's triad is a pre-terminal sign. Call for help and prepare for emergency ICP-lowering measures.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q72 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1422, $s$Abnormal extension (decerebrate posturing) corresponds to which motor score on the Glasgow Coma Scale?$s$, $s$["2", "4", "3", "5"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2
In the GCS motor score, ABNORMAL EXTENSION (decerebrate posturing: arms and legs straight, arms rotated inwards, wrists flexed) scores M2. It indicates damage at the level of the BRAINSTEM (midbrain or pons) and has a worse prognosis than decorticate posturing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 4 — M4 is withdrawal from pain.
• 3 — M3 is abnormal flexion (decorticate).
• 5 — M5 is localising to pain.

⭐ Key point: A change from flexion (M3) to extension (M2) means the patient is getting worse; report it immediately.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q73 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1423, $s$When managing an unconscious patient, the nurse should be aware that the most significant risk associated with impaired consciousness is:$s$, $s$["Aspiration", "Hypotension", "Hyperglycemia", "Hypothermia"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Aspiration
An unconscious patient loses the protective airway reflexes (gag, cough, swallow). Saliva, vomit or feeds can then go into the lungs, causing ASPIRATION, aspiration pneumonia, or airway obstruction. This is the most significant risk, and it can kill quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypotension — Hypotension may occur but is not the most significant general risk of unconsciousness.
• Hyperglycemia — High glucose is not the main risk.
• Hypothermia — Hypothermia can occur but isn't the most significant risk.

⭐ Key point: Protect the airway: lateral (recovery) position or head-up, suction, nil by mouth, and intubate if GCS is 8 or less.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q74$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1424, $s$What blood pressure threshold must generally be achieved before administering IV tPA for acute ischemic stroke?$s$, $s$["There is no blood pressure limit", "Less than 160/100 mmHg", "Less than 220/120 mmHg", "Less than 185/110 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Less than 185/110 mmHg
Before thrombolysis with IV tPA (alteplase) for ischaemic stroke, the BP must be BELOW 185/110 mmHg, and kept below 180/105 for 24 hours afterwards. High BP greatly increases the risk of bleeding into the brain after tPA. IV labetalol or nicardipine are used to lower it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• There is no blood pressure limit — There IS a BP limit for thrombolysis.
• Less than 160/100 mmHg — 160/100 is lower than the required threshold (not the standard cut-off).
• Less than 220/120 mmHg — 220/120 is the threshold for lowering BP in strokes NOT receiving tPA.

⭐ Key point: Check BP every 15 minutes during and after thrombolysis, and watch for neurological deterioration (bleeding).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q75$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1425, $s$Which glial cell forms tight junctions with capillary endothelial cells and contributes end-feet processes that help maintain the blood-brain barrier?$s$, $s$["Microglia", "Astrocyte", "Schwann cell", "Ependymal cell"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Astrocyte
ASTROCYTES are star-shaped glial cells. Their "end-feet" wrap around brain capillaries and signal the endothelial cells to form TIGHT JUNCTIONS. This creates the BLOOD–BRAIN BARRIER, which protects the brain from toxins and many drugs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Microglia — Microglia are the brain's immune cells (macrophages).
• Schwann cell — Schwann cells make myelin in the PERIPHERAL nervous system.
• Ependymal cell — Ependymal cells line the ventricles and help make CSF.

⭐ Key point: The blood–brain barrier explains why some antibiotics (and drugs) don't reach the brain well. Inflammation (meningitis) makes it leakier.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q76$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1426, $s$A head-injured patient has MAP 80 mmHg and ICP 25 mmHg. The cerebral perfusion pressure is:$s$, $s$["55 mmHg, below target", "25 mmHg, at target", "105 mmHg, above target", "80 mmHg, at target"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 55 mmHg, below target
Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) = MAP − ICP. Here: 80 − 25 = 55 mmHg. The target CPP is 60–70 mmHg, so 55 is BELOW target. The brain isn't getting enough blood. Treat by lowering ICP (head up, sedation, osmotherapy, CSF drainage) and/or raising MAP (vasopressors).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 25 mmHg, at target — 25 is the ICP, not the CPP.
• 105 mmHg, above target — 105 is MAP + ICP (wrong calculation).
• 80 mmHg, at target — 80 is the MAP, not the CPP.

⭐ Key point: Keep ICP below 20–22 mmHg and CPP at 60–70 mmHg in severe brain injury.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q77 · concept asked in 5 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1427, $s$Cushing's triad, a late sign of raised ICP, is:$s$, $s$["Hypotension, tachycardia and rapid shallow breathing", "Hypertension, tachycardia and a fever above 39 °C", "Hypertension with wide pulse pressure, bradycardia and irregular breathing", "Hypotension, bradycardia and warm, dry, flushed skin"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypertension with wide pulse pressure, bradycardia and irregular breathing
Cushing's triad, a late sign of raised ICP, consists of HYPERTENSION with a WIDE pulse pressure, BRADYCARDIA, and IRREGULAR breathing. It shows the brainstem is being compressed and herniation is imminent, so it needs immediate action.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypotension, tachycardia and rapid shallow breathing — Low BP, fast pulse and fast breathing describe shock.
• Hypertension, tachycardia and a fever above 39 °C — High BP with fast pulse and fever doesn't fit; the pulse is SLOW in Cushing's triad.
• Hypotension, bradycardia and warm, dry, flushed skin — Low BP, slow pulse and warm dry skin describe NEUROGENIC shock.

⭐ Key point: Immediate actions: call for help, head up 30°, ensure oxygenation, short hyperventilation only for herniation, mannitol or hypertonic saline, and urgent CT or neurosurgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q78 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1428, $s$When giving mannitol for raised ICP, the nurse should withhold further doses if serum osmolality is above:$s$, $s$["250 mOsm/kg", "200 mOsm/kg", "320 mOsm/kg", "280 mOsm/kg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 320 mOsm/kg
Mannitol draws water out of brain cells by raising serum osmolality. If the serum osmolality rises above 320 mOsm/kg, further mannitol can damage the KIDNEYS (acute kidney injury) and cause fluid and electrolyte problems, with little extra benefit. Withhold further doses at this level and consider hypertonic saline instead.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 250 mOsm/kg — 250 is below normal.
• 200 mOsm/kg — 200 is very low.
• 280 mOsm/kg — 280 is normal (275–295).

⭐ Key point: When giving mannitol: check serum osmolality and sodium, monitor urine output, use a filter (crystals can form), and watch for low BP from diuresis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q79$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1429, $s$In the apnoea test for brainstem death, the test is positive when no breathing occurs with PaCO₂ rising to at least:$s$, $s$["60 mmHg (8 kPa), or 20 mmHg above baseline", "40 mmHg (5.3 kPa), the normal value", "30 mmHg (4 kPa) at any time", "100 mmHg (13 kPa) as a minimum"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 60 mmHg (8 kPa), or 20 mmHg above baseline
In the apnoea test for brainstem death, the patient is disconnected from the ventilator (with oxygen given) to see if a high CO₂ triggers breathing. The test is positive (confirming no breathing drive) if NO breathing occurs when PaCO₂ rises to at least 60 mmHg (8 kPa), or 20 mmHg above baseline. UK guidance uses above 6.0 kPa with a rise of at least 0.5 kPa.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 40 mmHg (5.3 kPa), the normal value — 40 mmHg is the normal PaCO₂ and wouldn't stimulate breathing enough to test.
• 30 mmHg (4 kPa) at any time — 30 mmHg is low.
• 100 mmHg (13 kPa) as a minimum — 100 mmHg isn't required as a minimum.

⭐ Key point: Before testing, exclude sedatives, hypothermia, and metabolic or endocrine causes. Two senior doctors perform the tests, twice.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q80$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1430, $s$The purpose of performing an EBT in a severely jaundiced baby is to urgently reduce the blood level of unconjugated bilirubin so as to prevent$s$, $s$["Brain damage", "Unconjugated bilirubin", "Asphyxia", "Febrile illness"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Brain damage
An exchange blood transfusion (EBT) quickly removes the baby's blood, which is high in unconjugated bilirubin (and antibodies in haemolytic disease), and replaces it with donor blood. Very high unconjugated bilirubin can cross into the brain and cause KERNICTERUS (bilirubin encephalopathy): BRAIN DAMAGE causing deafness, cerebral palsy and learning disability.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Unconjugated bilirubin — "Unconjugated bilirubin" is what the EBT removes; it's not the harm being prevented.
• Asphyxia — Asphyxia isn't caused by bilirubin.
• Febrile illness — Fever isn't caused by bilirubin.

⭐ Key point: Signs of acute bilirubin encephalopathy: lethargy, poor feeding, high-pitched cry, arching (opisthotonus) and seizures.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q123$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1431, $s$A patient in the ICU develops new-onset agitation, disorientation, and pulling at lines, fluctuating throughout the day. The nurse should assess for:$s$, $s$["Normal ICU behaviour requiring no assessment", "Nothing — this is expected and not clinically significant", "Definite dementia with no other cause considered", "ICU delirium, using a validated delirium assessment tool"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ICU delirium, using a validated delirium assessment tool
New-onset AGITATION, DISORIENTATION, pulling at lines, with symptoms that FLUCTUATE through the day, are classic signs of ICU DELIRIUM (acute brain dysfunction). The nurse should assess with a validated tool such as CAM-ICU or ICDSC, then look for causes (infection, drugs, hypoxia, pain, metabolic problems).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal ICU behaviour requiring no assessment — This behaviour needs assessment.
• Nothing — this is expected and not clinically significant — It is clinically significant; delirium increases mortality.
• Definite dementia with no other cause considered — Dementia is chronic, not sudden and fluctuating.

⭐ Key point: Delirium prevention: reorientation, sleep, early mobility, pain control, less sedation, family involvement.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q61$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1432, $s$Upper motor neuron impairment produces which change in muscle tone?$s$, $s$["Flaccidity", "\"Cog-wheel\" rigidity", "Myoclonus", "Spasticity"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Spasticity
Upper motor neurone (UMN) lesions (stroke, spinal cord injury) remove the brain's control over spinal reflexes. Muscle tone INCREASES, causing SPASTICITY: resistance that increases with faster movement ("clasp-knife"), along with brisk reflexes and a Babinski sign.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flaccidity — Flaccidity (floppy tone) is a LOWER motor neurone sign.
• "Cog-wheel" rigidity — Cog-wheel rigidity is seen in Parkinson's disease.
• Myoclonus — Myoclonus is sudden jerking movements, not a tone change.

⭐ Key point: In acute spinal cord injury, tone is initially flaccid (spinal shock), then becomes spastic over weeks.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q62$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1433, $s$What is the generally recommended target cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) in the management of severe traumatic brain injury?$s$, $s$["90-100 mmHg", "60-70 mmHg", "Greater than 100 mmHg", "40-50 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 60-70 mmHg
In severe traumatic brain injury, the brain needs enough blood flow. Guidelines recommend keeping CEREBRAL PERFUSION PRESSURE (CPP = MAP − ICP) at 60–70 mmHg. Too low causes brain ischaemia; too high (above 70) can increase complications like ARDS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 90-100 mmHg — 90–100 mmHg is too high.
• Greater than 100 mmHg — Above 100 is too high.
• 40-50 mmHg — 40–50 mmHg is too low and risks brain ischaemia.

⭐ Key point: Keep ICP below 22 mmHg and CPP at 60–70. Avoid hypotension (systolic below 110 in head injury).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q63$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1434, $s$Which brainstem structure contains the primary neurons responsible for the automatic, rhythmic control of breathing?$s$, $s$["The cerebellum", "The hypothalamus", "The occipital cortex", "The medulla oblongata"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The medulla oblongata
The MEDULLA OBLONGATA (in the brainstem) contains the respiratory centres (the dorsal and ventral respiratory groups) that automatically set the rhythm of breathing. It responds to CO₂ and pH changes through the central chemoreceptors. The pons fine-tunes the pattern.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The cerebellum — The cerebellum controls balance and coordination.
• The hypothalamus — The hypothalamus controls temperature, hunger and hormones.
• The occipital cortex — The occipital cortex processes vision.

⭐ Key point: Damage to the medulla (herniation, brainstem stroke) causes abnormal breathing or apnoea.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q64$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1435, $s$Which pathological reflex occurs in central paralysis (upper motor neuron lesion)?$s$, $s$["Brudzinski", "Neri", "Brown-Séquard", "Babinski"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Babinski
The BABINSKI reflex (the big toe goes up and the toes fan out when the sole is stroked) is the classic PATHOLOGICAL reflex of an UPPER MOTOR NEURONE (central) lesion: damage to the corticospinal tract from stroke, brain injury, MS or spinal cord injury.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Brudzinski — Brudzinski's sign is a meningeal sign.
• Neri — Neri's sign is a less common test for nerve root irritation.
• Brown-Séquard — Brown-Séquard is a syndrome (hemisection of the spinal cord), not a reflex.

⭐ Key point: A positive Babinski is normal in babies under about 1–2 years.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q66$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1436, $s$A patient is assessed as GCS E3 V4 M5. The total GCS score is:$s$, $s$["10", "12", "8", "15"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 12
Add the three parts: Eye opening E3 (opens eyes to speech) + Verbal V4 (confused conversation) + Motor M5 (localises to pain) = 3 + 4 + 5 = 12. A GCS of 12 is a MODERATE head injury (9–12).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 — 10 is wrong; it doesn't match the sum.
• 8 — 8 would be severe.
• 15 — 15 is normal (E4 V5 M6).

⭐ Key point: GCS: Eyes 1–4, Verbal 1–5, Motor 1–6. Total 3–15. Always report the individual parts, not just the total.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q67$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1437, $s$Normal intracranial pressure in an adult is generally considered to be:$s$, $s$["Above 25 mmHg at baseline", "50–60 mmHg", "0–15 mmHg", "30–40 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 0–15 mmHg
Normal intracranial pressure in adults is about 0–15 mmHg (some sources say 5–15). Pressures above 20–22 mmHg need treatment, because they reduce brain perfusion and risk herniation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Above 25 mmHg at baseline — Above 25 mmHg at baseline is HIGH.
• 50–60 mmHg — 50–60 mmHg is dangerously high.
• 30–40 mmHg — 30–40 mmHg is dangerously high.

⭐ Key point: CPP = MAP − ICP. Keep ICP below 22 and CPP at 60–70 mmHg.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q68$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1438, $s$Which is NOT an ancillary investigation for brainstem death?$s$, $s$["Cerebral angiography", "Electromyography", "Electroencephalography", "Transcranial Doppler"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Electromyography
Ancillary (supportive) tests for brainstem death show NO BLOOD FLOW to the brain (cerebral angiography, transcranial Doppler, CT angiography, nuclear perfusion scan) or NO BRAIN ELECTRICAL ACTIVITY (EEG). ELECTROMYOGRAPHY (EMG) tests MUSCLE and peripheral nerve activity, not the brain, so it is NOT an ancillary test for brainstem death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cerebral angiography — Cerebral angiography IS an ancillary test (no brain blood flow).
• Electroencephalography — EEG IS an ancillary test (no brain electrical activity).
• Transcranial Doppler — Transcranial Doppler IS an ancillary test (reverberating or absent flow).

⭐ Key point: In most countries, brainstem death is diagnosed clinically. Ancillary tests are used when clinical tests can't be fully completed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q69$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1439, $s$Voluntary control of breathing (e.g. holding one's breath) originates in the:$s$, $s$["Medulla oblongata", "Pons only", "Cerebral cortex", "Spinal cord"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cerebral cortex
Breathing is automatic (controlled by the medulla), but we can also control it VOLUNTARILY: holding our breath, breathing faster, speaking, singing. This voluntary control comes from the CEREBRAL CORTEX (motor cortex), which can temporarily override the brainstem.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Medulla oblongata — The medulla controls AUTOMATIC breathing.
• Pons only — The pons fine-tunes breathing but isn't the voluntary centre.
• Spinal cord — The spinal cord carries signals but doesn't originate voluntary control.

⭐ Key point: We can't hold our breath until we die: rising CO₂ eventually forces the medulla to make us breathe.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q70$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1440, $s$A client is undergoing a comphrensive admission assessment, the critical care nurse knows that, the single most significant indicator of cerebral perfusion and functioning is called$s$, $s$["Level of consciousness", "Intra cranial pressure", "Glasgow coma scale", "Pupils dilatation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Level of consciousness
LEVEL OF CONSCIOUSNESS (LOC) is the single most sensitive and earliest indicator of how well the brain is perfused and functioning. A subtle change (restlessness, confusion, drowsiness) often appears before other signs of deterioration such as pupil changes or Cushing's triad.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intra cranial pressure — ICP is a measured value, not the clinical indicator.
• Glasgow coma scale — The GCS is a tool for MEASURING level of consciousness.
• Pupils dilatation — Pupil dilatation is a later sign of brain compression.

⭐ Key point: Report ANY change in level of consciousness promptly. It is often the first sign of trouble.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q71$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1441, $s$A polyneuropathic pattern of sensory loss presents as:$s$, $s$["Numbness and analgesia in half of the body", "Analgesia and sensory ataxia in the proximal parts of the extremities", "Pain and sensory ataxia in half of the body", "Numbness and pain in the distal parts of the extremities"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Numbness and pain in the distal parts of the extremities
Polyneuropathy (damage to many peripheral nerves, as in diabetes, alcohol, B12 deficiency or drugs) affects the LONGEST nerves first. Symptoms start in the feet and hands, giving a symmetrical "glove-and-stocking" pattern of NUMBNESS, PAIN, tingling and burning in the DISTAL parts of the limbs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Numbness and analgesia in half of the body — Numbness of half the body suggests a brain lesion (a stroke).
• Analgesia and sensory ataxia in the proximal parts of the extremities — Proximal sensory loss doesn't fit polyneuropathy.
• Pain and sensory ataxia in half of the body — Pain and ataxia in half the body suggest a central lesion.

⭐ Key point: Protect numb feet: check them daily, wear proper footwear, and test bath water temperature.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q72$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1442, $s$One of the tools used for monitoring a polytrauma patient is Glasgow Coma scale. The second parameter has a total of what score?$s$, $s$["3", "5", "6", "15"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 5
The Glasgow Coma Scale has three parameters in this order: 1) EYE opening (maximum 4), 2) VERBAL response (maximum 5), 3) MOTOR response (maximum 6). The SECOND parameter, verbal response, has a maximum score of 5: orientated (5), confused (4), inappropriate words (3), incomprehensible sounds (2), none (1). (The old answer key said 6, which is the motor maximum; it has been corrected to 5.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 3 — 3 isn't the maximum of any GCS part.
• 6 — 6 is the maximum of the THIRD parameter (motor).
• 15 — 15 is the total maximum GCS.

⭐ Key point: Total GCS ranges from 3 to 15. A GCS of 8 or less means severe injury.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q73 · answer key corrected$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1443, $s$The validated clinical tools used in ICU for detecting delirium are:$s$, $s$["Braden and Waterlow scales", "MMSE and AVPU", "CAM-ICU and ICDSC", "RASS and GCS"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: CAM-ICU and ICDSC
The two validated tools for detecting delirium in ICU patients (including those on ventilators) are the CAM-ICU (Confusion Assessment Method for the ICU) and the ICDSC (Intensive Care Delirium Screening Checklist). They check for acute change, inattention, disorganised thinking and altered consciousness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Braden and Waterlow scales — Braden and Waterlow assess pressure ulcer risk.
• MMSE and AVPU — MMSE assesses cognition (dementia) and needs a verbal patient; AVPU assesses consciousness.
• RASS and GCS — RASS assesses sedation level, and GCS assesses consciousness; neither detects delirium.

⭐ Key point: Use RASS first (the patient must be rousable, RASS −3 or higher) before doing CAM-ICU.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q75$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1444, $s$A patient exhibits rhythmic jerking of all four limbs with impaired awareness that correlates with epileptiform discharges on EEG. This is best classified as which of the following?$s$, $s$["Rigors from fever", "Decerebrate posturing only", "A convulsive seizure", "A simple faint (vasovagal syncope)"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A convulsive seizure
RHYTHMIC jerking of all four limbs with IMPAIRED AWARENESS, with matching EPILEPTIFORM DISCHARGES on EEG, is a CONVULSIVE (generalised tonic-clonic) SEIZURE: abnormal, excessive electrical activity in the brain causing visible convulsions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rigors from fever — Rigors (shivering with fever) don't cause epileptiform EEG changes.
• Decerebrate posturing only — Decerebrate posturing is rigid extension, not rhythmic jerking.
• A simple faint (vasovagal syncope) — A vasovagal faint is brief loss of consciousness without epileptiform EEG activity.

⭐ Key point: Seizures lasting more than 5 minutes are status epilepticus: give benzodiazepines.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q76$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1445, $s$Nimodipine is given after aneurysmal subarachnoid haemorrhage to:$s$, $s$["Reduce delayed cerebral ischaemia from vasospasm", "Lower ICP by reducing CSF production", "Prevent rebleeding by clotting the aneurysm", "Treat seizures in the first week"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reduce delayed cerebral ischaemia from vasospasm
After aneurysmal subarachnoid haemorrhage (SAH), the blood around the brain arteries can cause them to spasm (VASOSPASM) from day 3–14, reducing blood flow and causing DELAYED CEREBRAL ISCHAEMIA (stroke-like deficits). NIMODIPINE (a calcium channel blocker, 60 mg orally every 4 hours for 21 days) improves outcomes by reducing this.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lower ICP by reducing CSF production — Nimodipine doesn't lower ICP by reducing CSF.
• Prevent rebleeding by clotting the aneurysm — Nimodipine doesn't prevent rebleeding; securing the aneurysm (coiling or clipping) does.
• Treat seizures in the first week — Nimodipine isn't an anti-seizure drug.

⭐ Key point: Watch for hypotension with nimodipine. Keep the patient normovolaemic, and avoid hypotension, which worsens ischaemia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q77$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1446, $s$Hyperventilation to lower PaCO₂ in traumatic brain injury should be used:$s$, $s$["Routinely to keep PaCO₂ below 3 kPa", "Only briefly as a rescue for signs of herniation", "For all patients in the first 24 hours", "Instead of sedation and head elevation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Only briefly as a rescue for signs of herniation
Lowering PaCO₂ by hyperventilation narrows the brain's blood vessels (vasoconstriction), which quickly lowers ICP, but it also REDUCES brain blood flow and can cause ISCHAEMIA. It should be used ONLY BRIEFLY as a RESCUE measure for signs of impending herniation (such as a blown pupil, Cushing's triad), while other treatments are started. The normal target is PaCO₂ 4.5–5.0 kPa (35–40 mmHg).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Routinely to keep PaCO₂ below 3 kPa — Routine PaCO₂ below 3 kPa (22 mmHg) causes dangerous brain ischaemia.
• For all patients in the first 24 hours — Routine hyperventilation in all patients in the first 24 hours is harmful.
• Instead of sedation and head elevation — It doesn't replace sedation and head elevation.

⭐ Key point: Avoid hypocapnia, hypoxia and hypotension in brain injury.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q78$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1447, $s$Cerebral salt wasting differs from SIADH because in cerebral salt wasting the patient is:$s$, $s$["Hypervolaemic and needs loop diuretics", "Normonatraemic and needs no treatment", "Hypovolaemic and needs sodium and fluid replacement", "Euvolaemic and needs strict fluid restriction"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypovolaemic and needs sodium and fluid replacement
Both cerebral salt wasting (CSW) and SIADH cause low sodium after brain injury, but CSW patients are HYPOVOLAEMIC (dehydrated): the kidneys lose both salt AND water. Treatment is SODIUM and FLUID REPLACEMENT (0.9% or hypertonic saline, fludrocortisone). Fluid restriction would worsen dehydration and brain ischaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypervolaemic and needs loop diuretics — CSW patients are dry, not overloaded; diuretics would be harmful.
• Normonatraemic and needs no treatment — They have LOW sodium, not normal.
• Euvolaemic and needs strict fluid restriction — Euvolaemia with fluid restriction is the SIADH treatment.

⭐ Key point: Telling them apart: check fluid status (weight, CVP, urea, fluid balance). Dry = CSW; normal or wet = SIADH.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q80$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1448, $s$A nurse is monitoring a sedated patient in the ICU using the Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale (RASS). A score of 0 on the RASS indicates:$s$, $s$["Unarousable", "Alert and calm", "Agitated or restless", "Light sedation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Alert and calm
The Richmond Agitation-Sedation Scale (RASS) runs from +4 (combative) to −5 (unrousable). A score of 0 means the patient is ALERT AND CALM: the ideal target for many ICU patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Unarousable — Unrousable is −5.
• Agitated or restless — Agitated or restless is +1 to +2.
• Light sedation — Light sedation is −1 to −2.

⭐ Key point: The usual ICU target is RASS 0 to −1 (alert and calm to drowsy), unless deeper sedation is needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q150$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1449, $s$Documentation of hourly neurological observations for a patient with a head injury is an example of which type of standard in the Donabedian model?$s$, $s$["Structure", "Outcome", "Input", "Process"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Process
In the Donabedian model, PROCESS standards describe the activities done in delivering care. Documenting hourly neurological observations for a head-injured patient is a nursing ACTIVITY, so it is a PROCESS standard.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Structure — Structure standards relate to resources (staff, equipment, facilities).
• Outcome — Outcome standards relate to results (recovery, complications).
• Input — "Input" isn't one of the three Donabedian categories.

⭐ Key point: Structure (resources) → Process (what is done) → Outcome (results).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q164$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1450, $s$The Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) score, which ranks a patient's level of consciousness from 3 to 15, is best classified as which scale of measurement?$s$, $s$["Ratio", "Interval", "Nominal", "Ordinal"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ordinal
The GCS ranks levels of consciousness in ORDER (3 = deepest coma, 15 = fully alert), but the differences between scores aren't equal (a change from 3 to 4 isn't the same as from 14 to 15). It is an ORDINAL scale.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ratio — Ratio scales have a true zero and equal intervals.
• Interval — Interval scales have equal intervals.
• Nominal — Nominal scales are categories without order.

⭐ Key point: Ordinal data are best summarised with the median, not the mean.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q195$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1451, $s$A patient with heart failure and severe hyponatremia (Na⁺ 115 mEq/L) develops seizures. Which intervention should the nurse initiate FIRST?$s$, $s$["Start oral sodium chloride tablets", "Administer 3% hypertonic saline IV", "Give furosemide 40 mg IV", "Restrict oral fluid intake to 500 mL/day"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Administer 3% hypertonic saline IV
A sodium of 115 with SEIZURES is severe, symptomatic hyponatraemia; brain cells are swelling. The FIRST step is 3% HYPERTONIC SALINE IV (often 150 mL over 10–20 minutes, repeated as needed) to raise sodium by 4–6 mmol/L quickly and stop the seizures. Then correct slowly (no more than 8–10 mmol/L in 24 hours).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Start oral sodium chloride tablets — Oral salt tablets work too slowly for seizures.
• Give furosemide 40 mg IV — Furosemide alone doesn't raise sodium quickly enough.
• Restrict oral fluid intake to 500 mL/day — Fluid restriction is too slow for a seizing patient.

⭐ Key point: Monitor sodium every 2–4 hours to avoid over-correction (osmotic demyelination).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q10$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1452, $s$Bilateral damage to the spinal cord at the cervical level may produce:$s$, $s$["monoplegia", "tetraplegia", "hemiplegia", "paraplegia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: tetraplegia
The cervical spinal cord carries nerves for the ARMS and LEGS (and breathing). BILATERAL (both sides) damage at the cervical level causes paralysis of ALL FOUR limbs: TETRAPLEGIA (quadriplegia). High cervical injuries (C3–C5) can also paralyse the diaphragm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• monoplegia — Monoplegia is paralysis of ONE limb.
• hemiplegia — Hemiplegia is paralysis of ONE SIDE of the body (often a stroke).
• paraplegia — Paraplegia is paralysis of the LEGS (thoracic or lumbar injury).

⭐ Key point: "C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive." High cervical injuries need breathing support.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q62$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1453, $s$Central paresis and loss of proprioception on one side with loss of pain and temperature sensation on the opposite side form which syndrome?$s$, $s$["Strümpell–Lorrain", "Argyll Robertson", "Brown-Séquard", "Lambert–Eaton"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Brown-Séquard
BROWN-SÉQUARD syndrome happens with damage to ONE SIDE (hemisection) of the spinal cord (such as from a stab wound). On the SAME side: weakness (central paresis) and loss of position and vibration sense. On the OPPOSITE side: loss of pain and temperature sensation (because these fibres cross over soon after entering the cord).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Strümpell–Lorrain — Strümpell–Lorrain disease is hereditary spastic paraplegia.
• Argyll Robertson — Argyll Robertson pupils are seen in neurosyphilis.
• Lambert–Eaton — Lambert–Eaton syndrome is a neuromuscular junction disorder linked to cancer.

⭐ Key point: Brown-Séquard has the best prognosis of the incomplete spinal cord syndromes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q64$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1454, $s$A nurse is positioning an unconscious patient. The patient's head should be maintained in a:$s$, $s$["Hyperextended position", "Flexed position on the chest", "Rotated position to the side", "Neutral, midline position"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Neutral, midline position
An unconscious patient's head should be kept in a NEUTRAL, MIDLINE position (not turned or bent). This keeps the AIRWAY OPEN and allows good VENOUS DRAINAGE from the brain through the jugular veins, which helps prevent raised ICP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperextended position — Hyperextension can strain the neck and may close the airway in some patients.
• Flexed position on the chest — Flexion onto the chest can block the airway and jugular veins.
• Rotated position to the side — Rotation to the side compresses the jugular veins and raises ICP.

⭐ Key point: Keep the head of the bed up at 30° and avoid tight ETT ties or collars that compress the neck veins.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q65$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1455, $s$This disease condition consists of train of severe seizures with relatively short intervals or no interval between, the patient becomes exhausted and frequently hyperthermia. Death not uncommonly occurs during the attacks.$s$, $s$["Persistent convulsion", "Poor epilepsy", "Status epilepticus", "Meningeal irritation"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Status epilepticus
STATUS EPILEPTICUS is a continuous seizure, or a series of seizures without recovery between them, lasting more than 5 minutes. The patient becomes exhausted, may develop hyperthermia, acidosis and rhabdomyolysis, and death can occur. It is a medical emergency. (The old answer key said "persistent convulsion", which isn't the medical term; it has been corrected to status epilepticus.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Persistent convulsion — "Persistent convulsion" describes the event loosely but isn't the recognised diagnosis.
• Poor epilepsy — "Poor epilepsy" isn't a medical term.
• Meningeal irritation — Meningeal irritation is a sign of meningitis, not repeated seizures.

⭐ Key point: Treatment: ABC, check glucose, benzodiazepine, second-line IV drug, then anaesthesia if refractory.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q67 · answer key corrected$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1456, $s$The onset of delirium is usually rapid, within:$s$, $s$["Months to years", "30 minutes to 1 hour", "Hours to a few days", "Weeks to months"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hours to a few days
Delirium has a RAPID ONSET, developing over HOURS to a FEW DAYS, and FLUCTUATES through the day. It is caused by an acute problem (infection, drugs, hypoxia, metabolic changes). DEMENTIA, in contrast, develops slowly over months to years.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Months to years — Months to years describes dementia.
• 30 minutes to 1 hour — 30 minutes to 1 hour is too fast for typical delirium onset.
• Weeks to months — Weeks to months describes a slower process.

⭐ Key point: Delirium is often reversible if the cause is found and treated. Screen ICU patients every shift.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q68$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1457, $s$A seizure that stops on its own after 3 minutes and the patient regains consciousness would be classified as:$s$, $s$["Cannot be determined", "Epilepsy by definition", "Status epilepticus", "Not status epilepticus"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Not status epilepticus
Status epilepticus is a seizure lasting MORE THAN 5 MINUTES, or repeated seizures without recovery between them. A seizure that STOPS ON ITS OWN after 3 minutes, with the patient waking up, is NOT status epilepticus. It is a single seizure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cannot be determined — It can be classified from the information given.
• Epilepsy by definition — A single seizure doesn't define epilepsy (two or more unprovoked seizures are needed).
• Status epilepticus — It doesn't meet the 5-minute status definition.

⭐ Key point: Treat seizures lasting more than 5 minutes with benzodiazepines. Investigate the cause of any first seizure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q69$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1458, $s$Xanthochromia (yellow discoloration) of the cerebrospinal fluid on lumbar puncture is most suggestive of which condition?$s$, $s$["Guillain-Barre syndrome", "Subarachnoid hemorrhage", "Normal CSF findings", "Bacterial meningitis only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Subarachnoid hemorrhage
XANTHOCHROMIA is yellow discolouration of the CSF caused by the breakdown of red blood cells into BILIRUBIN. It appears about 6–12 hours after bleeding into the CSF, and is the classic sign of SUBARACHNOID HAEMORRHAGE (helping to distinguish it from a traumatic "bloody tap" during the LP).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Guillain-Barre syndrome — Guillain–Barré syndrome shows high protein with normal cells, not xanthochromia.
• Normal CSF findings — Normal CSF is clear and colourless.
• Bacterial meningitis only — Bacterial meningitis gives cloudy CSF with many white cells.

⭐ Key point: Do the LP at least 12 hours after headache onset to detect xanthochromia if CT is negative.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q73$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1459, $s$When assessing the neurological status of an unconscious patient, which component of the Glasgow Coma Scale (GCS) is used to evaluate eye opening?$s$, $s$["Eye Opening", "Pupillary Response", "Motor Response", "Verbal Response"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Eye Opening
The GCS has three parts: EYE OPENING (1–4), VERBAL response (1–5) and MOTOR response (1–6). EYE OPENING is the part used to assess whether and how the eyes open (spontaneously, to voice, to pain, or not at all).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pupillary Response — Pupillary response is checked separately; it isn't part of the GCS.
• Motor Response — Motor response assesses movement.
• Verbal Response — Verbal response assesses speech.

⭐ Key point: Record pupils (size, reaction) alongside the GCS in neurological observations.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q75$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1460, $s$In Guillain–Barré syndrome, elective intubation should be considered when vital capacity falls below:$s$, $s$["50 mL/kg", "15–20 mL/kg", "70 mL/kg", "100 mL/kg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 15–20 mL/kg
In Guillain–Barré syndrome, breathing muscles can weaken quickly. VITAL CAPACITY (VC) is measured regularly. Elective intubation is considered when VC falls BELOW about 15–20 mL/kg (or there is a rapid fall, a weak cough, or bulbar weakness), before respiratory failure happens.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50 mL/kg — 50 mL/kg is near normal.
• 70 mL/kg — 70 mL/kg is normal.
• 100 mL/kg — 100 mL/kg is above normal.

⭐ Key point: SpO₂ falls LATE in neuromuscular weakness. Rely on vital capacity, not oxygen saturation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q77$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1461, $s$Which nursing measure helps reduce raised ICP in a ventilated head-injured patient?$s$, $s$["Head flat with tight tube ties", "Head up 30°, neck midline, loose tube ties", "Frequent, long suction sessions", "Neck flexed down onto the chest"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Head up 30°, neck midline, loose tube ties
To reduce raised ICP: keep the HEAD UP at 30° (helps venous drainage), the NECK in the MIDLINE (no twisting that blocks the jugular veins), and the ETT ties LOOSE (tight ties compress the neck veins). Also avoid hypoxia, high CO₂, fever, hypotension, and coughing or straining.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Head flat with tight tube ties — A flat head and tight ties INCREASE ICP.
• Frequent, long suction sessions — Frequent, long suction RAISES ICP.
• Neck flexed down onto the chest — Flexing the neck onto the chest blocks venous drainage and raises ICP.

⭐ Key point: Cluster care carefully, give sedation before suction if ICP rises, and keep suction passes under 10 seconds.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q78$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1462, $s$For an external ventricular drain, the zero reference point is usually the:$s$, $s$["Umbilicus with the patient flat", "Phlebostatic axis at the 4th intercostal space", "Tragus of the ear (level of the foramen of Monro)", "Sternal notch with the patient upright"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tragus of the ear (level of the foramen of Monro)
For an external ventricular drain (EVD), the zero reference point is the TRAGUS of the ear (approximating the level of the FORAMEN OF MONRO, the opening between the ventricles). The drip chamber is set at a prescribed height (such as 10–20 cmH₂O) above this point.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Umbilicus with the patient flat — The umbilicus isn't the reference.
• Phlebostatic axis at the 4th intercostal space — The phlebostatic axis is for CVP and arterial lines, not EVDs.
• Sternal notch with the patient upright — The sternal notch isn't the reference.

⭐ Key point: CLAMP the EVD before moving or repositioning the patient, then re-level and unclamp, to avoid over- or under-drainage.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q79 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B5 · Neurological Critical Care$s$, false, 1463, $s$A new unilateral fixed dilated pupil in a head-injured patient most likely indicates:$s$, $s$["A normal pupil response to the bright pen torch", "Opioid toxicity, which affects both pupils equally", "Compression of cranial nerve III by uncal herniation on that side", "Optic nerve damage on the opposite side of the head"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Compression of cranial nerve III by uncal herniation on that side
A NEW, UNILATERAL, FIXED, DILATED pupil in a head-injured patient means the OCULOMOTOR nerve (CN III) is being compressed, usually by UNCAL HERNIATION: part of the brain (the uncus) is pushed down through the tentorium by rising ICP. This is a NEUROSURGICAL EMERGENCY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal pupil response to the bright pen torch — A normal pupil constricts to light; a fixed dilated pupil is abnormal.
• Opioid toxicity, which affects both pupils equally — Opioids cause small (pinpoint) pupils on both sides.
• Optic nerve damage on the opposite side of the head — Optic nerve damage on the opposite side doesn't cause a fixed dilated pupil this way.

⭐ Key point: Inform the team immediately, prepare for urgent CT and surgery, and start ICP-lowering measures (mannitol or hypertonic saline).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q80$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1464, $s$On a plain abdominal X-ray, multiple dilated bowel loops with air-fluid levels on an erect film are classic findings of:$s$, $s$["Normal bowel gas pattern", "Intestinal obstruction", "Chronic constipation only", "Gallstones"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intestinal obstruction
In intestinal obstruction, gas and fluid build up above the blockage. The bowel loops stretch (dilated loops), and on an ERECT film, gas sits on top of fluid, forming flat lines called air–fluid levels (like a glass of water). Small bowel loops are central with folds that cross the whole width (valvulae conniventes); large bowel loops are peripheral with folds that cross only partly (haustra).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal bowel gas pattern — A normal bowel gas pattern does not show multiple dilated loops with fluid levels.
• Chronic constipation only — Constipation shows faecal loading, not multiple air–fluid levels.
• Gallstones — Gallstones are often not visible on X-ray (only about 10–15% contain calcium); they don't cause this pattern.

⭐ Key point: Signs of obstruction: colicky pain, vomiting, distension, absolute constipation. Treatment is "drip and suck": IV fluids plus NG tube on free drainage, nil by mouth.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q43$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1465, $s$Kidney failure is a potential postoperative complication in the ICU. Which laboratory test would be most indicative of kidney function?$s$, $s$["Glucose levels", "Potassium levels", "Creatinine levels", "Sodium levels"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Creatinine levels
Creatinine is a waste product of muscle that the kidneys filter at a steady rate. When kidney function falls, creatinine RISES in the blood. It is the standard simple test of kidney function and is used to estimate GFR and to diagnose AKI (KDIGO: a rise of 26.5 µmol/L or more within 48 hours, or 1.5 times baseline, or urine below 0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 hours).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Glucose levels — Glucose measures diabetes control, not kidney function.
• Potassium levels — Potassium often rises in kidney failure and is important to monitor, but it is not the best measure of kidney function itself.
• Sodium levels — Sodium reflects water balance, not kidney filtration.

⭐ Key point: Creatinine rises LATE (24–48 hours after injury). Falling urine output is often the EARLIEST sign of AKI, so measure it hourly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q78$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1466, $s$The following are causes of Normal sized or enlarged Kidneys in CKD Except$s$, $s$["HIVAN", "ADPKD", "Amyloidosis", "Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypertensive Nephrosclerosis
In most CKD the kidneys SHRINK (become small and scarred) as nephrons are lost. However, some causes keep the kidneys NORMAL-SIZED or ENLARGED even in advanced disease — memory aid: "DIABETIC PAH": Diabetic nephropathy, HIV-associated nephropathy (HIVAN), AMYLOIDOSIS (protein deposits), ADPKD (adult polycystic kidney disease — big cyst-filled kidneys), plus myeloma kidney, and sickle-cell nephropathy. HYPERTENSIVE NEPHROSCLEROSIS causes SMALL, shrunken kidneys, because long-standing high BP damages small arteries and causes scarring of the glomeruli. On ultrasound, small kidneys (under about 9 cm) usually mean chronic, irreversible disease.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• HIVAN — HIVAN typically causes LARGE, echogenic kidneys — it does belong to the normal/enlarged group.
• ADPKD — ADPKD causes very LARGE kidneys full of cysts.
• Amyloidosis — AMYLOIDOSIS causes normal or LARGE kidneys due to amyloid deposits.

⭐ Key point: CKD with normal/large kidneys = diabetes, HIVAN, amyloid, ADPKD, myeloma. HYPERTENSIVE nephrosclerosis = SMALL kidneys.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q80$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1467, $s$Which of the following is the most common intrinsic (intrarenal) cause of acute kidney injury in critically ill patients?$s$, $s$["Bladder outlet obstruction", "Renal artery stenosis", "Nephrolithiasis", "Acute tubular necrosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute tubular necrosis
AKI causes are grouped as: PRE-RENAL (low blood flow to kidneys — dehydration, haemorrhage, shock, heart failure), INTRINSIC/intrarenal (damage inside the kidney), and POST-RENAL (blockage of urine flow). In critically ill patients, the most common INTRINSIC cause is ACUTE TUBULAR NECROSIS (ATN). ATN happens when kidney tubule cells die from prolonged ISCHAEMIA (e.g. untreated pre-renal shock/sepsis) or TOXINS (aminoglycosides, IV contrast, rhabdomyolysis/myoglobin, NSAIDs). Signs: oliguria, rising creatinine, muddy brown granular casts in urine, urine sodium over 40 mmol/L. Management: treat the cause, optimise fluids and BP, avoid nephrotoxins, adjust drug doses, dialysis if needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bladder outlet obstruction — BLADDER OUTLET OBSTRUCTION (e.g. enlarged prostate) is a POST-RENAL cause.
• Renal artery stenosis — RENAL ARTERY STENOSIS reduces blood flow to the kidney — usually considered PRE-RENAL/vascular, and usually chronic.
• Nephrolithiasis — NEPHROLITHIASIS (kidney stones) causes POST-RENAL obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Commonest intrinsic AKI in ICU = ACUTE TUBULAR NECROSIS (from ischaemia/sepsis or toxins). Muddy brown casts.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q82$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1468, $s$The most common type of thyroid carcinoma is:$s$, $s$["Follicular", "Medullary", "Papillary", "Anaplastic"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Papillary
PAPILLARY CARCINOMA is the MOST COMMON thyroid cancer, making up about 75–85% of cases. It is more common in women aged 20–50 and after radiation exposure. It grows slowly, spreads mainly through LYMPHATICS to neck nodes, and has an EXCELLENT prognosis (over 90% 10-year survival). Under the microscope it shows "Orphan Annie eye" nuclei and PSAMMOMA bodies. Treatment: thyroidectomy, sometimes radioactive iodine, then levothyroxine. Other types: FOLLICULAR (10–15%, spreads through blood to bone and lungs), MEDULLARY (5%, from C-cells, makes calcitonin, linked with MEN 2), ANAPLASTIC (under 2%, elderly, very aggressive, poor prognosis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Follicular — FOLLICULAR is the second most common (10–15%), spreads by blood.
• Medullary — MEDULLARY is uncommon (about 5%), from parafollicular C cells, calcitonin-secreting.
• Anaplastic — ANAPLASTIC is the rarest and most aggressive, not the most common.

⭐ Key point: Commonest thyroid cancer = PAPILLARY (~80%, lymph spread, good prognosis). Anaplastic = worst.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q115$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1469, $s$The most common cause of lower GI bleeding is:$s$, $s$["Haemorrhoids", "Colorectal cancer", "Diverticular disease", "Angiodysplasia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Diverticular disease
LOWER GI BLEEDING is bleeding from below the ligament of Treitz (mostly the colon), usually seen as fresh red blood per rectum (haematochezia). In adults, the MOST COMMON cause is DIVERTICULAR DISEASE (about 30–40%) — small pouches in the colon wall, especially in older people, where a blood vessel stretched over the pouch can rupture and cause sudden, painless, large-volume bleeding that often stops on its own. Other causes: haemorrhoids, angiodysplasia, colorectal cancer/polyps, colitis (ischaemic, infective, inflammatory bowel disease), post-polypectomy bleeding. Management: resuscitate (ABC, IV access, bloods, cross-match), then colonoscopy or CT angiography; embolisation or surgery if bleeding continues.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Haemorrhoids — HAEMORRHOIDS are very common but usually cause small, bright red bleeding on the tissue — not the commonest cause of significant lower GI bleeding requiring admission.
• Colorectal cancer — COLORECTAL CANCER usually causes slow, chronic bleeding/anaemia rather than being the most common cause.
• Angiodysplasia — ANGIODYSPLASIA is an important cause in the elderly but less common than diverticular disease.

⭐ Key point: Commonest cause of significant lower GI bleeding = DIVERTICULAR DISEASE (painless, large volume, older patients).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q125$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1470, $s$What is the primary purpose of administering potassium chloride infusion to patient with diabetic ketoacidosis recieving IV insulin?$s$, $s$["Treatment of hypepnea", "Prevention of fluccid paralysis", "Replacement of potassium losses", "Treatment of cardiac arrythmias"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Replacement of potassium losses
In DKA, total body POTASSIUM is LOW (lost in urine through osmotic diuresis and vomiting), even if the blood level looks normal or high at first (acidosis and lack of insulin shift potassium OUT of cells). When IV INSULIN is given, it drives potassium back INTO the cells, so the blood potassium can FALL quickly and dangerously. POTASSIUM CHLORIDE infusion is given to REPLACE POTASSIUM LOSSES and prevent hypokalaemia (which can cause arrhythmias and muscle weakness).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Treatment of hypepnea — "Hyperpnoea" (deep breathing) is due to acidosis, not treated with potassium.
• Prevention of fluccid paralysis — Preventing flaccid paralysis is a consequence of preventing hypokalaemia, but the primary purpose is replacement.
• Treatment of cardiac arrythmias — Treating arrhythmias is not the primary purpose; preventing hypokalaemia prevents them.

⭐ Key point: Check potassium before starting insulin; if potassium is below 3.3 mmol/L, give potassium first and delay insulin.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q19$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1471, $s$Elevated ALT and AST primarily indicate:$s$, $s$["Renal dysfunction", "Hepatocellular injury", "Pancreatic dysfunction only", "Coagulopathy directly"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hepatocellular injury
ALT (alanine aminotransferase) and AST (aspartate aminotransferase) are enzymes found inside LIVER CELLS (hepatocytes). When liver cells are damaged, they leak into the blood, so RAISED ALT and AST indicate HEPATOCELLULAR INJURY — e.g. viral hepatitis, paracetamol overdose, ischaemic ("shock") liver, alcoholic or drug-induced liver injury. ALT is more specific to the liver; AST is also found in muscle and heart.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal dysfunction — Kidney function is measured by urea and creatinine.
• Pancreatic dysfunction only — Pancreatic damage is shown by amylase and lipase.
• Coagulopathy directly — Coagulopathy is measured by PT/INR, though liver damage can cause it indirectly.

⭐ Key point: Very high transaminases (in the thousands) suggest acute hepatitis, paracetamol toxicity or shock liver.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q25$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1472, $s$Which mnemonic is commonly used to remember indications for urgent or emergent dialysis?$s$, $s$["SEPSIS", "MONA", "AEIOU, standing for acidosis, electrolyte imbalance, ingestion/intoxication, overload, uremia", "FAST"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: AEIOU, standing for acidosis, electrolyte imbalance, ingestion/intoxication, overload, uremia
The AEIOU mnemonic lists indications for URGENT/EMERGENCY DIALYSIS when medical treatment fails: A = ACIDOSIS (severe metabolic acidosis, e.g. pH below 7.1–7.15); E = ELECTROLYTE imbalance (especially refractory HYPERKALAEMIA); I = INTOXICATION/INGESTION (dialysable poisons such as lithium, methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylates); O = OVERLOAD (fluid overload/pulmonary oedema not responding to diuretics); U = URAEMIA (uraemic pericarditis, encephalopathy, bleeding).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• SEPSIS — SEPSIS relates to sepsis recognition.
• MONA — MONA (Morphine, Oxygen, Nitrates, Aspirin) relates to acute coronary syndrome treatment.
• FAST — FAST relates to stroke recognition.

⭐ Key point: Dialysis decisions are based on the patient's clinical state, not a single creatinine value.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q77$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1473, $s$When titrating CRRT replacement fluid and dialysate flow rates for a patient with severe metabolic acidosis, the priority nursing consideration is:$s$, $s$["Using a bicarbonate- or lactate-buffered replacement fluid to help correct the acidosis gradually", "Rapidly correcting the entire acid-base deficit within the first hour", "Using only solutions with no buffer", "Avoiding any buffer to prevent alkalosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Using a bicarbonate- or lactate-buffered replacement fluid to help correct the acidosis gradually
CONTINUOUS RENAL REPLACEMENT THERAPY (CRRT) can correct metabolic acidosis by removing acids and adding BUFFER (usually BICARBONATE, sometimes lactate) through the replacement fluid and dialysate. The priority is to use a BUFFERED fluid and correct the acidosis GRADUALLY over hours. Rapid correction can cause problems such as sudden shifts in potassium and calcium, rebound alkalosis, and worsening intracellular acidosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rapidly correcting the entire acid-base deficit within the first hour — Rapid correction within one hour is unsafe.
• Using only solutions with no buffer — Unbuffered solutions would fail to correct the acidosis (and may worsen it).
• Avoiding any buffer to prevent alkalosis — Avoiding buffer would leave the acidosis untreated.

⭐ Key point: Monitor ABGs, potassium, ionised calcium, phosphate and magnesium closely during CRRT.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q87$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1474, $s$A patient with known oesophageal varices develops sudden haematemesis and hypotension. The nurse's priority actions include:$s$, $s$["Secure IV access, prepare for fluid/blood resuscitation, and avoid over-transfusion which can raise portal pressure", "Encourage oral fluids immediately", "Give a laxative to clear the bowel", "Discharge home with outpatient follow-up"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Secure IV access, prepare for fluid/blood resuscitation, and avoid over-transfusion which can raise portal pressure
OESOPHAGEAL VARICES are swollen veins in the oesophagus caused by high pressure in the portal vein (portal hypertension), usually from liver cirrhosis. When they bleed, the patient can lose large volumes of blood quickly (HAEMATEMESIS, HYPOTENSION). Priorities: secure the airway if needed, insert TWO LARGE-BORE IV cannulas, take blood (FBC, clotting, crossmatch), and start FLUID and BLOOD RESUSCITATION. However, AVOID OVER-TRANSFUSION: giving too much volume RAISES PORTAL PRESSURE and can restart or worsen bleeding. A restrictive transfusion target (Hb about 70–80 g/L) is used. Give terlipressin and IV antibiotics, and arrange urgent endoscopy for band ligation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Encourage oral fluids immediately — Oral fluids are dangerous (aspiration, vomiting) and delay endoscopy.
• Give a laxative to clear the bowel — Laxatives are not the priority; lactulose may be used later to prevent hepatic encephalopathy, but not in an emergency.
• Discharge home with outpatient follow-up — Discharge would be fatal — this is a life-threatening emergency.

⭐ Key point: Variceal bleeding has a high mortality; terlipressin and prophylactic antibiotics (e.g. ceftriaxone) reduce death.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q145$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1475, $s$Primary hyperparathyroidism, most commonly caused by a solitary parathyroid adenoma, classically presents with which laboratory pattern?$s$, $s$["Low serum calcium with low PTH", "Elevated serum calcium with elevated or inappropriately normal PTH", "Elevated serum calcium with suppressed PTH", "Low serum calcium with elevated PTH"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Elevated serum calcium with elevated or inappropriately normal PTH
PRIMARY HYPERPARATHYROIDISM (usually a parathyroid adenoma) causes HIGH CALCIUM with HIGH or INAPPROPRIATELY NORMAL PTH. Normally, high calcium suppresses PTH; in this condition, PTH is not suppressed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low serum calcium with low PTH — Low calcium with low PTH is hypoparathyroidism.
• Elevated serum calcium with suppressed PTH — High calcium with suppressed PTH suggests other causes (e.g. cancer).
• Low serum calcium with elevated PTH — Low calcium with high PTH is secondary hyperparathyroidism (e.g. kidney disease, vitamin D deficiency).

⭐ Key point: Symptoms: "bones, stones, groans, moans" — bone pain, kidney stones, abdominal pain, depression.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q171$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1476, $s$A patient on CRRT (Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy) develops a sudden drop in filter/circuit pressure with visible clotting in the circuit. The nurse's priority action is to:$s$, $s$["Disconnect the patient permanently from all renal support", "Continue therapy unchanged", "Assess anticoagulation status, notify the team, and prepare for circuit change per protocol, since a clotted circuit stops effective filtration", "Increase the blood flow rate significantly without assessment"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assess anticoagulation status, notify the team, and prepare for circuit change per protocol, since a clotted circuit stops effective filtration
A CLOTTED CRRT circuit STOPS effective filtration and wastes blood (the blood in the circuit is lost). The nurse should ASSESS ANTICOAGULATION (e.g. citrate levels, heparin dose), NOTIFY the team, and PREPARE for a CIRCUIT CHANGE per protocol. Blood may be returned to the patient if possible before the circuit clots completely.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Disconnect the patient permanently from all renal support — Permanently disconnecting renal support is inappropriate.
• Continue therapy unchanged — Continuing unchanged wastes blood.
• Increase the blood flow rate significantly without assessment — Increasing blood flow without assessment is unsafe.

⭐ Key point: Prevent clotting with adequate anticoagulation, good catheter flow, and minimising interruptions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q176$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1477, $s$A 70 kg patient has passed 120 mL of urine in the last 6 hours. What is the urine output and how is it interpreted?$s$, $s$["20 mL/kg/hr — adequate", "1.71 mL/kg/hr — adequate", "0.29 mL/kg/hr — oliguria", "0.29 mL/kg/hr — adequate"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 0.29 mL/kg/hr — oliguria
URINE OUTPUT (mL/kg/hr) = VOLUME ÷ HOURS ÷ WEIGHT. Here: 120 mL ÷ 6 hours = 20 mL/hr; 20 ÷ 70 kg = 0.29 mL/kg/hr. Normal adult urine output is at least 0.5 mL/kg/hr. Below this is OLIGURIA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20 mL/kg/hr — adequate — 20 mL/kg/hr is a units error.
• 1.71 mL/kg/hr — adequate — 1.71 mL/kg/hr is wrong (120 ÷ 70).
• 0.29 mL/kg/hr — adequate — 0.29 is correct, but it is NOT adequate — it shows oliguria.

⭐ Key point: Oliguria for 6 hours or more is a KDIGO criterion for acute kidney injury. Check the catheter, fluid status and BP.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q49 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1478, $s$A critically ill patient presents with severe tachycardia, fever, agitation, and altered mental status after recent thyroid surgery. Which life-threatening endocrine emergency should be suspected?$s$, $s$["Addisonian crisis", "Myxedema coma", "Thyroid storm (thyrotoxic crisis)", "Hypoparathyroid tetany"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Thyroid storm (thyrotoxic crisis)
THYROID STORM (thyrotoxic crisis) is a life-threatening surge of thyroid hormone. It can be triggered by THYROID SURGERY (handling the gland releases stored hormone), infection, trauma, or stopping antithyroid drugs. Features: very HIGH FEVER, severe TACHYCARDIA (often atrial fibrillation), heart failure, AGITATION, delirium or ALTERED MENTAL STATUS, vomiting, diarrhoea and jaundice. Treatment: beta-blocker (propranolol), antithyroid drug (propylthiouracil or carbimazole), iodine at least 1 hour later, steroids (hydrocortisone), cooling and supportive ICU care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Addisonian crisis — Addisonian crisis causes hypotension, low sodium, high potassium and low glucose, usually without high fever and agitation.
• Myxedema coma — Myxoedema coma causes HYPOthermia, bradycardia and drowsiness — the opposite.
• Hypoparathyroid tetany — Hypoparathyroid tetany after thyroidectomy causes tingling, cramps and spasms (low calcium), not fever and tachycardia.

⭐ Key point: Use the Burch–Wartofsky score to assess the likelihood of thyroid storm. Cool the patient, but avoid aspirin (it releases more thyroid hormone from binding proteins).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q146$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1479, $s$A patient with end-stage renal disease who missed a scheduled dialysis session presents with tall peaked T waves on ECG and a serum potassium of 7.2 mEq/L. In addition to emergent pharmacologic treatment, which nursing action is highest priority?$s$, $s$["Encourage oral intake of a high-potassium meal", "Continuous cardiac monitoring and preparation for emergent dialysis", "Administer a potassium supplement", "Delay treatment until the next routinely scheduled dialysis session"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Continuous cardiac monitoring and preparation for emergent dialysis
HYPERKALAEMIA (potassium 7.2 mmol/L) with TALL, PEAKED T WAVES is a MEDICAL EMERGENCY — the heart can suddenly go into ventricular fibrillation or asystole. After urgent drug treatment (IV calcium gluconate to protect the heart, insulin with dextrose and salbutamol to move potassium into cells), the definitive treatment is to REMOVE potassium by DIALYSIS. So the highest priority nursing actions are CONTINUOUS CARDIAC MONITORING and PREPARING for EMERGENCY DIALYSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Encourage oral intake of a high-potassium meal — A high-potassium meal would worsen hyperkalaemia.
• Administer a potassium supplement — Potassium supplements would worsen hyperkalaemia.
• Delay treatment until the next routinely scheduled dialysis session — Waiting for the next scheduled dialysis could be fatal.

⭐ Key point: Educate dialysis patients about potassium-restricted diets and the importance of not missing dialysis sessions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q170$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1480, $s$During a first haemodialysis session for a urea of 60 mmol/L, a patient develops headache, nausea, restlessness, confusion and then a seizure. The most likely cause is:$s$, $s$["Rebound hyperkalaemia after dialysis", "Venous air embolism from the circuit", "Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome", "Hypoglycaemia during dialysis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome
DIALYSIS DISEQUILIBRIUM SYNDROME occurs when urea is removed TOO QUICKLY from the blood during the FIRST dialysis session, especially with very high urea. Urea leaves the brain more slowly, so the brain becomes relatively "salty", drawing water into the brain cells (CEREBRAL OEDEMA). Symptoms: HEADACHE, NAUSEA, RESTLESSNESS, CONFUSION, SEIZURES.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rebound hyperkalaemia after dialysis — Rebound hyperkalaemia doesn't cause these neurological symptoms.
• Venous air embolism from the circuit — Air embolism causes sudden breathlessness and collapse.
• Hypoglycaemia during dialysis — Hypoglycaemia during dialysis may cause confusion but not typically this pattern.

⭐ Key point: Prevent with shorter, gentler first sessions (lower blood flow), aiming to reduce urea by no more than about 30%.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q119$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1481, $s$Which electrolyte abnormality pattern is classically associated with primary adrenal insufficiency due to combined cortisol and aldosterone deficiency?$s$, $s$["Hypernatremia and hypokalemia", "Hyponatremia and hyperkalemia", "Normal sodium with hyperkalemia only", "Hyponatremia and hypokalemia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hyponatremia and hyperkalemia
In primary adrenal insufficiency (Addison's disease), the adrenal cortex fails, so both cortisol AND ALDOSTERONE are lacking. Without aldosterone, the kidneys lose SODIUM (hyponatraemia) and keep POTASSIUM (hyperkalaemia). Low cortisol also causes low glucose and low BP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypernatremia and hypokalemia — High sodium with low potassium is the pattern of TOO MUCH aldosterone (Conn's syndrome).
• Normal sodium with hyperkalemia only — Normal sodium with high potassium alone is incomplete.
• Hyponatremia and hypokalemia — Low sodium with low potassium suggests diuretics or vomiting, not Addison's.

⭐ Key point: Adrenal crisis: give IV hydrocortisone 100 mg and IV saline immediately.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q7$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1482, $s$Acute adrenal (Addisonian) crisis is a life-threatening emergency best managed with immediate administration of which agent, alongside aggressive fluid resuscitation?$s$, $s$["Oral fludrocortisone only", "IV hydrocortisone", "IV levothyroxine", "IV insulin"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV hydrocortisone
In an adrenal (Addisonian) crisis, the body lacks cortisol and can't maintain BP or glucose. IV HYDROCORTISONE (100 mg immediately, then 50 mg every 6 hours or an infusion) replaces cortisol, and at high doses it also has mineralocorticoid (aldosterone-like) effects. It is given with aggressive IV 0.9% saline and dextrose.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral fludrocortisone only — Oral fludrocortisone works too slowly and doesn't replace cortisol in a crisis.
• IV levothyroxine — Levothyroxine is for hypothyroidism.
• IV insulin — Insulin would worsen low glucose.

⭐ Key point: Give hydrocortisone immediately if adrenal crisis is suspected; don't wait for blood results.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q84$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, true, 1483, $s$Which laboratory test is generally considered the most sensitive initial screening test for primary thyroid dysfunction?$s$, $s$["Thyroglobulin", "Total T4", "Serum TSH", "Free T3"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Serum TSH
Serum TSH (thyroid-stimulating hormone) is the most SENSITIVE initial test for primary thyroid problems. Because of the feedback loop, a small change in thyroid hormone causes a LARGE change in TSH. High TSH means hypothyroidism; low TSH means hyperthyroidism.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Thyroglobulin — Thyroglobulin is used to monitor thyroid cancer.
• Total T4 — Total T4 is affected by binding proteins and is less sensitive.
• Free T3 — Free T3 is useful in some hyperthyroid cases but isn't the first test.

⭐ Key point: In critical illness, thyroid tests are often abnormal (sick euthyroid syndrome). Interpret them carefully.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q86$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1484, $s$Which nephron segment is primarily responsible for the countercurrent multiplier system that concentrates urine?$s$, $s$["Bowman's capsule", "Proximal convoluted tubule", "Renal pelvis", "Loop of Henle"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Loop of Henle
The loop of Henle (in the medulla) is the countercurrent multiplier. The descending limb lets water out; the thick ascending limb pumps sodium and chloride out but is impermeable to water. This builds a very salty (hyperosmolar) medulla. Then, under ADH, water leaves the collecting ducts into the salty medulla and the urine becomes concentrated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bowman's capsule — Bowman's capsule collects the filtrate from the glomerulus; it does not concentrate urine.
• Proximal convoluted tubule — The proximal convoluted tubule reabsorbs about 65% of filtered sodium and water and all glucose, but the fluid stays isotonic.
• Renal pelvis — The renal pelvis is a collecting funnel that carries urine to the ureter.

⭐ Key point: Loop of Henle = concentrates urine. Loop diuretics (furosemide) block the Na-K-2Cl pump in the thick ascending limb, which is why they are so powerful.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q9$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1485, $s$The normal glomerular filtration rate (GFR) in a healthy adult is approximately:$s$, $s$["120-125 mL/min", "20-30 mL/min", "500 mL/min", "1000 mL/min"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 120-125 mL/min
Normal GFR is about 120–125 mL/min (about 180 L/day) in a healthy young adult. Almost all of this filtrate (about 99%) is reabsorbed, leaving 1–2 L of urine a day. GFR is the best overall measure of kidney function; it is estimated (eGFR) from serum creatinine, age and sex.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20-30 mL/min — 20–30 mL/min is severely reduced (stage 4 CKD).
• 500 mL/min — 500 mL/min is about the renal plasma flow, not the GFR.
• 1000 mL/min — 1000 mL/min is close to total renal BLOOD flow (about 20% of cardiac output), not the filtration rate.

⭐ Key point: GFR normal ~120 mL/min. Stage 5 CKD (kidney failure) is GFR below 15. Many drugs (vancomycin, gentamicin, enoxaparin) need dose changes when GFR is low.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q11$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1486, $s$Beta cells of the pancreatic islets of Langerhans secrete:$s$, $s$["Insulin", "Glucagon", "Somatostatin", "Pancreatic polypeptide exclusively"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Insulin
In the islets of Langerhans, BETA cells make insulin, which lowers blood glucose by moving it into cells and storing it as glycogen and fat. Beta cells are the most common islet cells (about 70%). They are destroyed in type 1 diabetes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Glucagon — Glucagon is made by ALPHA cells; it raises blood glucose.
• Somatostatin — Somatostatin is made by DELTA cells; it inhibits insulin and glucagon release.
• Pancreatic polypeptide exclusively — Pancreatic polypeptide is made by PP (F) cells, not beta cells.

⭐ Key point: Beta = insulin (lowers glucose). Alpha = glucagon (raises glucose). Glucagon 1 mg IM is used for severe hypoglycaemia when there is no IV access.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q14$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1487, $s$The preferred muscle relaxant in a patient with liver or renal failure is:$s$, $s$["Rocuronium", "Pancuronium", "Vecuronium", "Atracurium"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Atracurium
Atracurium is broken down in the blood by Hofmann elimination (a spontaneous chemical breakdown at normal body temperature and pH) and by ester hydrolysis, NOT by the liver or kidneys. Its action therefore stays predictable in liver or kidney failure. Cisatracurium works the same way and is often preferred in ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rocuronium — Rocuronium is cleared mainly by the liver (and kidneys), so it can last longer in liver failure.
• Pancuronium — Pancuronium is mainly cleared by the kidneys and is very long-acting in renal failure.
• Vecuronium — Vecuronium depends on the liver and kidneys, and its active metabolite builds up in organ failure.

⭐ Key point: Atracurium and cisatracurium are the muscle relaxants of choice in liver or kidney failure. Note that atracurium can release histamine (rash, low BP).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q35$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1488, $s$Which nursing intervention is most effective in preventing contrast-induced nephropathy in an at-risk patient before a CT scan with IV contrast?$s$, $s$["Administering prophylactic antibiotics", "Withholding all oral fluids for 12 hours before the scan", "Administering a loop diuretic prior to the scan", "Ensuring adequate IV isotonic fluid hydration before and after the procedure"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ensuring adequate IV isotonic fluid hydration before and after the procedure
Contrast can damage the kidney tubules and narrow the kidney blood vessels, causing a rise in creatinine 24–72 hours later. The most effective preventive step is good IV HYDRATION with isotonic fluid (0.9% saline or Hartmann's) before and after the scan. This keeps urine flowing, dilutes the contrast and protects the kidneys. Also use the lowest dose of low-osmolar contrast, stop nephrotoxic drugs (NSAIDs), and hold metformin if the GFR is low.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administering prophylactic antibiotics — Antibiotics have no role in preventing contrast kidney injury.
• Withholding all oral fluids for 12 hours before the scan — Withholding fluids causes dehydration, which INCREASES the risk.
• Administering a loop diuretic prior to the scan — Loop diuretics can dehydrate the patient and increase the risk.

⭐ Key point: High-risk patients are those with CKD, diabetes, dehydration, heart failure and older age. Check creatinine at 48–72 hours after contrast.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q44$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1489, $s$What is a common cause of acute kidney injury (AKI) in the ICU?$s$, $s$["Sepsis", "Dehydration", "Nephrotoxic drugs", "All of the above"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
All three are common causes of AKI in ICU, and often several act together. Sepsis is the commonest cause: it causes low BP, inflammation and microcirculation damage. Dehydration or hypovolaemia (bleeding, vomiting, diuretics) reduces kidney blood flow (pre-renal AKI). Nephrotoxic drugs damage the tubules: aminoglycosides (gentamicin), vancomycin, NSAIDs, IV contrast, amphotericin and ACE inhibitors in dehydration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sepsis — Sepsis alone is a cause, but not the only one listed.
• Dehydration — Dehydration alone is a cause, but not the only one listed.
• Nephrotoxic drugs — Nephrotoxic drugs alone are a cause, but not the only one listed.

⭐ Key point: Prevent AKI: keep MAP of 65 or more, keep the patient well hydrated, avoid or monitor nephrotoxic drugs (check trough levels), and watch urine output hourly (aim for at least 0.5 mL/kg/h).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q77$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1490, $s$In Arteriovenous fistula:$s$, $s$["There is mixture of arterial blood", "There is mixture of venous blood", "There is mixture of artery and capillary blood", "There is mixture of arterial and venous blood"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: There is mixture of arterial and venous blood
An arteriovenous (AV) fistula is a surgical joining of an ARTERY directly to a VEIN, usually in the forearm (radial artery to cephalic vein). Arterial blood at high pressure flows straight into the vein, so the vein contains a mixture of arterial and venous blood. The vein enlarges and its wall thickens ("matures" over 6–12 weeks), so it can be needled for haemodialysis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• There is mixture of arterial blood — It is not only arterial blood; the blood goes into a vein.
• There is mixture of venous blood — It is not only venous blood; arterial blood enters the vein directly.
• There is mixture of artery and capillary blood — Capillaries are bypassed in a fistula; there is no artery–capillary mixing.

⭐ Key point: Fistula care: feel for the THRILL (buzz) and listen for the BRUIT (whoosh) daily. Never take BP, put a cannula in, or take blood from the fistula arm.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q80$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1491, $s$A hemodialysis patient develops sudden hypotension, nausea, headache, and confusion approximately 3 hours into a first dialysis session. This presentation is most consistent with:$s$, $s$["Anaphylaxis to the dialyzer membrane", "Air embolism", "Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome", "Hyperkalemic cardiac arrest"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome
Dialysis disequilibrium syndrome happens during or just after the FIRST dialysis sessions, especially when urea is very high and is removed too quickly. Urea falls fast in the blood but slowly in the brain, so water moves INTO brain cells (cerebral oedema). Signs: headache, nausea, vomiting, restlessness, confusion, hypotension, seizures, even coma. Prevent it with short, slow first sessions (low blood flow) and a gradual urea reduction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anaphylaxis to the dialyzer membrane — Dialyser anaphylaxis happens within MINUTES of starting, with wheeze, urticaria and itching.
• Air embolism — Air embolism causes sudden breathlessness, chest pain and collapse, not slowly developing headache and confusion.
• Hyperkalemic cardiac arrest — Hyperkalaemia improves during dialysis (K⁺ is removed), so cardiac arrest from high K⁺ is unlikely 3 hours in.

⭐ Key point: Treat by slowing or stopping dialysis and giving osmotic agents (mannitol or hypertonic saline) if severe. Monitor neuro status during first sessions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q81$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1492, $s$Hypothermia is a common complication of CRRT because:$s$, $s$["CRRT machines actively cool the blood intentionally at all times", "Patients are typically febrile at baseline", "Extracorporeal blood is exposed to room-temperature tubing, filters, and replacement fluids, causing heat loss", "The dialysate is always heated above body temperature"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Extracorporeal blood is exposed to room-temperature tubing, filters, and replacement fluids, causing heat loss
In CRRT, blood leaves the body and runs through long tubing and a filter at room temperature, and large volumes of replacement fluid or dialysate (often several litres per hour) are added. The blood loses heat outside the body and on mixing with cooler fluid, so the patient's temperature falls. Hypothermia can also HIDE a fever, so infection may be missed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CRRT machines actively cool the blood intentionally at all times — CRRT machines do not deliberately cool the blood.
• Patients are typically febrile at baseline — Patients may or may not have fever; this does not explain the cooling.
• The dialysate is always heated above body temperature — Dialysate is warmed TO body temperature (not above it) to reduce heat loss.

⭐ Key point: Prevent CRRT hypothermia with the fluid warmer and blankets or forced-air warmers. Monitor temperature hourly, and remember a normal temperature on CRRT does not rule out infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q82$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1493, $s$A patient s/p TURP has pink-tinged urine and bladder spasms. This is:$s$, $s$["Normal", "Indicative of infection", "Sign of hemorrhage", "Urinary retention"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Normal
After transurethral resection of the prostate (TURP), PINK-tinged urine (light bleeding) and BLADDER SPASMS (from the catheter balloon and the raw area) are expected in the first days. Continuous bladder irrigation through a three-way catheter keeps clots from forming, and anticholinergic drugs (such as oxybutynin) ease the spasms.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Indicative of infection — Infection would cause fever, cloudy smelly urine and rising inflammatory markers, not just pink urine.
• Sign of hemorrhage — Haemorrhage would show bright red urine with clots, falling BP and rising HR; pink urine is not haemorrhage.
• Urinary retention — Retention would cause a full painful bladder with little drainage; check the catheter is draining.

⭐ Key point: After TURP, report bright red urine or clots, a blocked catheter (no drainage with pain), and signs of TURP syndrome (confusion, low sodium, low BP).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q118$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1494, $s$A patient develops severe hypocalcemia with perioral tingling, muscle cramps, and a positive Chvostek sign after total thyroidectomy. Which complication is most likely?$s$, $s$["Inadvertent removal or devascularization of the parathyroid glands", "Thyroid storm", "Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury", "Adrenal insufficiency"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inadvertent removal or devascularization of the parathyroid glands
After TOTAL THYROIDECTOMY the small PARATHYROID GLANDS (usually four, sitting behind the thyroid) can be removed by mistake or lose their blood supply. Parathyroid hormone (PTH) keeps blood calcium up, so without it calcium falls (HYPOCALCAEMIA), usually 24–72 hours after surgery. Low calcium makes nerves and muscles over-excitable: tingling around the mouth and fingers, muscle cramps, CHVOSTEK sign (tapping the facial nerve in front of the ear makes the face twitch), TROUSSEAU sign (BP cuff inflated makes the hand go into spasm), and in severe cases laryngospasm, seizures and arrhythmia (long QT). Nursing: keep IV calcium gluconate 10% at the bedside, check serum calcium, report tingling early.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Thyroid storm — THYROID STORM gives high fever, fast heart rate, agitation and sweating — a hypermetabolic picture, not low-calcium tetany.
• Recurrent laryngeal nerve injury — RECURRENT LARYNGEAL NERVE injury causes HOARSENESS or a weak voice (and stridor if both sides are cut), not tingling or a positive Chvostek sign.
• Adrenal insufficiency — ADRENAL INSUFFICIENCY gives low BP, weakness, low sodium and high potassium; it is not linked to thyroid surgery.

⭐ Key point: Tingling + cramps + Chvostek/Trousseau after thyroidectomy = HYPOCALCAEMIA from parathyroid injury. Give IV calcium gluconate; keep it at the bedside.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q2$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1495, $s$The kidneys are classified as retroperitoneal organs because they:$s$, $s$["Lie behind the parietal peritoneum rather than within the peritoneal cavity", "Are suspended by a mesentery inside the peritoneal cavity", "Float freely within the peritoneal cavity", "Lie entirely anterior to the peritoneum, outside the abdomen"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lie behind the parietal peritoneum rather than within the peritoneal cavity
The PERITONEUM is the thin membrane lining the abdominal cavity. Organs inside it, hung on a mesentery (e.g. stomach, most of the small bowel, transverse colon), are INTRAPERITONEAL. Organs that lie BEHIND the posterior parietal peritoneum, against the back wall of the abdomen, are RETROPERITONEAL. The KIDNEYS are retroperitoneal — covered by peritoneum only on their front surface. Other retroperitoneal organs, memory aid "SAD PUCKER": Suprarenal (adrenal) glands, Aorta/IVC, Duodenum (most), Pancreas (except tail), Ureters, Colon (ascending and descending), Kidneys, Esophagus, Rectum. Clinical link: kidney surgery can be done through the flank without opening the peritoneal cavity, and retroperitoneal bleeding may show as flank bruising (Grey Turner sign).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Are suspended by a mesentery inside the peritoneal cavity — Being SUSPENDED BY A MESENTERY inside the cavity is the definition of an INTRAPERITONEAL organ — the opposite.
• Float freely within the peritoneal cavity — No solid organ "floats freely" in the peritoneal cavity; all are anchored, and the kidneys are fixed in fat and fascia behind the peritoneum.
• Lie entirely anterior to the peritoneum, outside the abdomen — The kidneys lie POSTERIOR (behind) the peritoneum, inside the abdomen — not anterior to it and not outside the abdomen.

⭐ Key point: Retroperitoneal = BEHIND the parietal peritoneum. Kidneys, adrenals, pancreas, aorta, ureters ("SAD PUCKER").$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q10$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1496, $s$Hyperphosphataemia is a common electrolyte disturbance in patients with:$s$, $s$["Chronic kidney disease", "Diabetes insipidus", "Primary hyperaldosteronism", "SIADH"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Chronic kidney disease
The KIDNEYS are the main route for removing PHOSPHATE from the body. In CHRONIC KIDNEY DISEASE (CKD), the glomerular filtration rate falls, so phosphate is retained and blood levels rise (HYPERPHOSPHATAEMIA, normal 0.8–1.5 mmol/L). High phosphate binds calcium, so calcium falls; the failing kidney also makes less active vitamin D. Low calcium and high phosphate drive the parathyroid glands to overwork (secondary hyperparathyroidism), causing renal bone disease, itching and calcium–phosphate deposits in vessels (vascular calcification). Management: low-phosphate diet (limit dairy, cola, processed foods), PHOSPHATE BINDERS taken with meals (calcium carbonate, sevelamer), and dialysis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diabetes insipidus — DIABETES INSIPIDUS causes loss of large amounts of dilute urine and HYPERNATRAEMIA, not high phosphate.
• Primary hyperaldosteronism — PRIMARY HYPERALDOSTERONISM causes HYPOKALAEMIA, sodium retention and hypertension.
• SIADH — SIADH causes water retention and dilutional HYPONATRAEMIA.

⭐ Key point: CKD → high PHOSPHATE + low calcium → secondary hyperparathyroidism. Treat with diet + phosphate binders with meals.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q26$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1497, $s$Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy (CRRT), compared to intermittent haemodialysis, is often preferred in haemodynamically unstable ICU patients because it:$s$, $s$["Removes fluid/solutes more slowly and continuously, causing less haemodynamic stress", "Is always faster at removing toxins", "Requires no vascular access", "Has no risk of complications"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Removes fluid/solutes more slowly and continuously, causing less haemodynamic stress
CRRT (Continuous Renal Replacement Therapy, e.g. CVVH, CVVHD, CVVHDF) runs 24 hours a day and removes fluid and waste SLOWLY and CONTINUOUSLY. Because fluid is removed gently over many hours (e.g. 50–200 mL/hour), there are smaller shifts of fluid and solutes, so BP stays more STABLE. This makes it the preferred method in HAEMODYNAMICALLY UNSTABLE ICU patients (shock, on vasopressors), and in patients with brain injury (less risk of raised ICP from rapid osmotic shifts). Intermittent haemodialysis (IHD) removes the same amount in 3–4 hours, which can cause sudden HYPOTENSION in unstable patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Is always faster at removing toxins — CRRT is NOT faster; IHD is faster at clearing toxins, which is why IHD is preferred for some poisonings and severe hyperkalaemia.
• Requires no vascular access — CRRT DOES need vascular access — a large double-lumen dialysis catheter (femoral, internal jugular).
• Has no risk of complications — CRRT has risks: bleeding from anticoagulation, filter clotting, hypothermia, electrolyte imbalance (low K⁺, low phosphate), line infection.

⭐ Key point: CRRT = slow, continuous removal → less haemodynamic stress → best for unstable ICU patients. IHD is faster but causes more hypotension.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q78$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1498, $s$At 10 years, survival with a living-related renal transplant is approximately:$s$, $s$["8.49%", "55.07%", "74.84%", "100%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 74.84%
Kidney transplant survival depends on the donor type. A LIVING-RELATED (living) donor kidney gives better results than a deceased-donor kidney because the kidney is healthy, carefully matched, and transplanted with very short cold ischaemia time. Long-term data show about 75% (here 74.84%) of patients/grafts surviving at 10 YEARS after living-related transplant, compared with lower figures for deceased donors (around 55–60%). Survival also depends on matching (HLA), immunosuppression adherence, prevention of rejection and infection, and control of BP and diabetes. Nurses play a big role in teaching patients to take immunosuppressants on time and report signs of rejection (fever, reduced urine, graft pain, rising creatinine).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 8.49% — 8.49% is far too low; this would mean almost every transplant fails within 10 years, which is not true.
• 55.07% — 55.07% is closer to deceased-donor 10-year survival, not living-related.
• 100% — 100% is impossible; some grafts are always lost through rejection, infection or death from other causes.

⭐ Key point: Living-related kidney transplant: about 75% survival at 10 years — better than deceased donor (~55–60%).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q79$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1499, $s$Post-nephrectomy donor investigations typically include:$s$, $s$["Chest X-ray, FBC, Electrolytes/Urea/Creatinine, eGFR", "Only a urine dipstick", "No pre-operative investigations are needed", "Bone marrow biopsy"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Chest X-ray, FBC, Electrolytes/Urea/Creatinine, eGFR
A live kidney donor must be fully assessed so that the operation is safe and the remaining single kidney will work well. Standard investigations (pre- and peri-operative) include: CHEST X-RAY (heart and lungs before anaesthesia), FULL BLOOD COUNT (anaemia, infection), ELECTROLYTES, UREA and CREATININE, and eGFR (kidney function — to make sure the remaining kidney is adequate), plus blood group and cross-match, urinalysis, viral screening (HIV, hepatitis B/C), ECG, and CT angiography of the kidneys before surgery. After nephrectomy, creatinine and eGFR are monitored closely because the remaining kidney must take over the full workload.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only a urine dipstick — A urine dipstick ALONE is far too little to assess kidney function and fitness for surgery.
• No pre-operative investigations are needed — Saying "no investigations are needed" is unsafe; major surgery always needs assessment.
• Bone marrow biopsy — BONE MARROW BIOPSY is for blood disorders (e.g. leukaemia), not kidney donation.

⭐ Key point: Kidney donor work-up = CXR, FBC, U&E/creatinine, eGFR, blood group, viral screen, ECG — check the remaining kidney is adequate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q81$s$);
