($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1500, $s$The most common cause of hyperthyroidism is:$s$, $s$["Toxic multinodular goitre", "Solitary toxic adenoma", "Grave's disease", "Thyroiditis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Grave's disease
GRAVES' DISEASE is the MOST COMMON cause of HYPERTHYROIDISM (about 60–80% of cases), especially in women aged 20–50. It is AUTOIMMUNE: antibodies (TSH-receptor antibodies, TRAb) stimulate the thyroid to grow and make too much hormone. Features: diffuse smooth GOITRE (often with a bruit), weight loss, heat intolerance, tremor, palpitations/AF, anxiety, plus features specific to Graves' — EYE disease (exophthalmos, lid lag), pretibial myxoedema and thyroid acropachy. Labs: low TSH, high T4/T3, positive TRAb. Treatment: carbimazole/methimazole or PTU, beta-blockers for symptoms, radioactive iodine or surgery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Toxic multinodular goitre — TOXIC MULTINODULAR GOITRE is the second most common cause, mainly in older people.
• Solitary toxic adenoma — A SOLITARY TOXIC ADENOMA (one "hot" nodule) is less common.
• Thyroiditis — THYROIDITIS (e.g. subacute, postpartum) causes temporary hyperthyroidism from hormone leakage — less common.

⭐ Key point: Commonest cause of hyperthyroidism = GRAVES' DISEASE (autoimmune, TSH-receptor antibodies, eye disease).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q133$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1501, $s$Classic presenting features of appendicitis in a child include periumbilical pain migrating to which quadrant?$s$, $s$["Right upper quadrant", "Left lower quadrant", "Right lower quadrant", "Left upper quadrant"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Right lower quadrant
In APPENDICITIS, pain usually starts as dull PERIUMBILICAL (around the belly button) pain — this is visceral pain, because the appendix shares nerve supply with the midgut (T10). After a few hours, as inflammation reaches the overlying parietal peritoneum, pain MOVES to the RIGHT LOWER QUADRANT (RLQ), over McBURNEY'S POINT (one-third of the way from the anterior superior iliac spine to the umbilicus), and becomes sharp and localised. Other signs: loss of appetite, nausea/vomiting, low-grade fever, rebound tenderness, Rovsing's sign, psoas sign, raised white cells. Children often present late and perforate more often. Nursing: NPO, IV fluids, analgesia, no laxatives or enemas (risk of perforation), prepare for appendicectomy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right upper quadrant — RIGHT UPPER QUADRANT pain suggests gallbladder or liver problems.
• Left lower quadrant — LEFT LOWER QUADRANT pain suggests sigmoid/diverticular disease (adults) or left-sided problems.
• Left upper quadrant — LEFT UPPER QUADRANT pain suggests spleen, stomach or pancreas problems.

⭐ Key point: Appendicitis: periumbilical pain → RIGHT LOWER QUADRANT (McBurney's point), with anorexia, fever, rebound tenderness.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q146$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1502, $s$Titratable acidity refers to renal buffering of secreted hydrogen ions mainly by which urinary buffer?$s$, $s$["Phosphate (HPO4^2- converting to H2PO4-)", "Bicarbonate", "Hemoglobin", "Albumin"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Phosphate (HPO4^2- converting to H2PO4-)
The kidneys get rid of acid by secreting HYDROGEN IONS (H⁺) into the urine. Free H⁺ in urine would make it too acidic, so it must be BUFFERED. "TITRATABLE ACIDITY" is the H⁺ buffered mainly by PHOSPHATE: H⁺ combines with HPO4²⁻ (monohydrogen phosphate) to form H2PO4⁻ (dihydrogen phosphate), which is excreted. Ammonia (NH3 → NH4⁺) is the other main urinary buffer. For each H⁺ excreted, a new bicarbonate is generated and returned to the blood.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bicarbonate — Bicarbonate is reabsorbed and generated by the kidney; it is not the urinary buffer for titratable acidity.
• Hemoglobin — Haemoglobin is a buffer in red blood cells, not urine.
• Albumin — Albumin is a plasma protein buffer, not a urinary buffer.

⭐ Key point: In kidney failure, the kidneys cannot excrete acid, causing metabolic acidosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q7$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1503, $s$Gastrointestinal glandular secretions primarily aid in:$s$, $s$["Chemical breakdown of food and enzymatic digestion", "Producing red blood cells", "Regulating body temperature exclusively", "Filtering blood"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Chemical breakdown of food and enzymatic digestion
GASTROINTESTINAL GLANDULAR SECRETIONS — saliva, gastric juice (acid, pepsin), pancreatic juice (amylase, lipase, proteases, bicarbonate), bile, and intestinal enzymes — are mainly for the CHEMICAL BREAKDOWN of food (ENZYMATIC DIGESTION) into small molecules (sugars, amino acids, fatty acids) that can be absorbed. They also lubricate food (mucus), neutralise acid (bicarbonate) and help protect the gut lining.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Producing red blood cells — Red blood cells are produced in the bone marrow.
• Regulating body temperature exclusively — Body temperature is regulated by the hypothalamus, skin and circulation, not GI secretions.
• Filtering blood — Blood is filtered by the kidneys (and the liver and spleen in other ways).

⭐ Key point: Large losses of GI secretions (vomiting, NG drainage, diarrhoea, fistulas) cause dehydration and electrolyte/acid-base disturbances.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q10$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1504, $s$The functional unit of the kidney responsible for filtration is the:$s$, $s$["Renal pelvis", "Nephron", "Ureter", "Bladder trigone"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Nephron
The NEPHRON is the FUNCTIONAL UNIT of the kidney — each kidney has about 1 million. Each nephron has a GLOMERULUS (a ball of capillaries where blood is FILTERED), Bowman's capsule, proximal tubule, loop of Henle, distal tubule, and collecting duct (where water, salts and wastes are reabsorbed or secreted to form urine).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal pelvis — The renal pelvis collects urine from the calyces — it does not filter.
• Ureter — The ureter carries urine to the bladder.
• Bladder trigone — The bladder trigone is a triangular area of the bladder wall.

⭐ Key point: The glomerular filtration rate (GFR, normal about 90–120 mL/min) measures how well nephrons filter; it falls in kidney disease.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q14$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1505, $s$Kussmaul respiration — deep, rapid breathing — is a compensatory pattern most associated with:$s$, $s$["Metabolic acidosis", "Respiratory acidosis", "Metabolic alkalosis exclusively", "Normal resting breathing"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Metabolic acidosis
KUSSMAUL RESPIRATION is deep, rapid, laboured breathing ("air hunger"). It is the lungs' COMPENSATION for METABOLIC ACIDOSIS: by breathing deeply and quickly, the body BLOWS OFF CARBON DIOXIDE (an acid), which helps RAISE the blood pH towards normal. It is classically seen in DIABETIC KETOACIDOSIS (often with a fruity/acetone smell on the breath), and also in severe kidney failure (uraemic acidosis), lactic acidosis and some poisonings (methanol, ethylene glycol, aspirin).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory acidosis — Respiratory acidosis is caused by HYPOventilation (CO2 retention), not deep fast breathing.
• Metabolic alkalosis exclusively — Metabolic alkalosis causes SLOW, shallow breathing (to retain CO2).
• Normal resting breathing — Kussmaul breathing is abnormal; normal resting breathing is quiet and regular at 12–20/min.

⭐ Key point: A patient with Kussmaul breathing needs an urgent blood gas, glucose, ketones and lactate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q54$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1506, $s$A thoraco-abdominal incision on the right side gives access to a:$s$, $s$["Lacerated liver", "Ruptured spleen", "Oesophagogastric injury", "None of the above"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lacerated liver
A THORACO-ABDOMINAL incision crosses from the chest into the abdomen through the diaphragm. On the RIGHT side, it gives excellent exposure of the LIVER (especially the large right lobe and its posterior segments), the inferior vena cava, and the right side of the diaphragm. It is used for major LIVER LACERATIONS and complex liver resections, where a standard abdominal incision does not give enough access.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ruptured spleen — The SPLEEN is on the LEFT side — a right incision would not reach it.
• Oesophagogastric injury — Oesophagogastric injuries (lower oesophagus, stomach) are usually approached from the LEFT thoraco-abdominal route.
• None of the above — The liver is correctly accessed by this approach, so "none" is wrong.

⭐ Key point: Right lower rib fractures should raise suspicion of liver injury; look for signs of internal bleeding (tachycardia, hypotension, abdominal distension).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q62$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1507, $s$Which mechanism helps maintain glomerular filtration pressure when renal perfusion falls, mediated by angiotensin II?$s$, $s$["Dilation of the efferent arteriole", "Dilation of both arterioles equally", "Constriction of the efferent arteriole", "Constriction of the afferent arteriole"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Constriction of the efferent arteriole
When renal perfusion falls, the kidney releases RENIN, producing ANGIOTENSIN II. Angiotensin II CONSTRICTS the EFFERENT ARTERIOLE (the vessel leaving the glomerulus) more than the afferent. This "backs up" blood in the glomerulus, MAINTAINING GLOMERULAR FILTRATION PRESSURE and GFR despite low blood flow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dilation of the efferent arteriole — Dilating the efferent arteriole would LOWER filtration pressure (this is what ACE inhibitors do).
• Dilation of both arterioles equally — Dilating both arterioles equally would not maintain filtration pressure.
• Constriction of the afferent arteriole — Constricting the afferent arteriole would REDUCE blood entering the glomerulus and lower GFR.

⭐ Key point: ACE inhibitors and ARBs block this mechanism, so they can cause AKI in dehydration or renal artery stenosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q77$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1508, $s$At 10 years, survival on dialysis alone (without transplant) is approximately:$s$, $s$["8.49%", "55.07%", "74.84%", "95%"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 8.49%
Long-term survival on DIALYSIS ALONE (without a kidney transplant) is poor, because of cardiovascular disease, infections and other complications. Registry data show that only about 10% or fewer (around 8.5% in the source used) of patients are still alive after 10 YEARS on dialysis alone. Kidney transplantation gives much better long-term survival and quality of life.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 55.07% — 55% is far too high for 10-year survival on dialysis alone.
• 74.84% — 75% is far too high.
• 95% — 95% is far too high.

⭐ Key point: Survival depends on age, diabetes, heart disease and access to adequate dialysis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q78$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1509, $s$Approximately what percentage of resting cardiac output do the kidneys normally receive?$s$, $s$["40%", "5%", "10%", "20-25%"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 20-25%
The KIDNEYS receive about 20–25% of the RESTING CARDIAC OUTPUT — about 1–1.25 litres of blood per minute — even though they weigh only about 300 g (0.5% of body weight). This huge blood flow is not for the kidneys' own oxygen needs; it is needed to FILTER large volumes of plasma (about 180 litres per day) to remove waste products and regulate fluids, electrolytes and acid–base balance.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 40% — 40% is too high.
• 5% — 5% is too low.
• 10% — 10% is too low.

⭐ Key point: Because the kidneys depend on high blood flow, any hypotension or hypovolaemia quickly reduces GFR and can cause pre-renal AKI — maintain MAP above 65 mmHg.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q80$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1510, $s$A 68-year-old man with benign prostatic hyperplasia presents with anuria and a palpably distended bladder. This is most consistent with which category of AKI?$s$, $s$["Prerenal", "Intrarenal", "Functional", "Postrenal"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Postrenal
BENIGN PROSTATIC HYPERPLASIA (BPH) can BLOCK the outflow of urine from the bladder. ANURIA with a PALPABLY DISTENDED BLADDER shows the kidneys are making urine but it cannot drain out — this is POST-RENAL (obstructive) AKI. Treatment: urinary catheterisation (often suprapubic if urethral fails). Watch for post-obstructive diuresis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prerenal — Pre-renal AKI is due to reduced kidney blood flow.
• Intrarenal — Intrarenal AKI is due to kidney tissue damage.
• Functional — "Functional" is not a standard category here.

⭐ Key point: A bladder scan or palpation can quickly confirm retention.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q82$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1511, $s$What percentage of solitary thyroid nodules are malignant?$s$, $s$["1%", "5%", "15%", "50%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 15%
SOLITARY THYROID NODULES are common (especially in women and older people). Most are benign (e.g. colloid nodules, adenomas, cysts). About 5–15% are MALIGNANT (thyroid cancer). The risk is higher in men, children, older adults, people with a history of radiation to the neck, rapidly growing or hard, fixed nodules, hoarseness (recurrent laryngeal nerve involvement), and enlarged neck lymph nodes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1% — 1% underestimates the cancer risk.
• 5% — 5% is the lower end of the range.
• 50% — 50% greatly overestimates.

⭐ Key point: Assess nodules with thyroid function tests, ultrasound, and fine-needle aspiration (FNA) cytology.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q115$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1512, $s$Which is NOT a marker of acute organ dysfunction?$s$, $s$["Jaundice", "Thrombocytopenia", "Hypotension", "Decreased creatinine level"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Decreased creatinine level
ACUTE ORGAN DYSFUNCTION (as in sepsis) is shown by markers such as: JAUNDICE (raised bilirubin — liver dysfunction), THROMBOCYTOPENIA (low platelets — haematological/clotting dysfunction), HYPOTENSION (cardiovascular dysfunction), RAISED CREATININE or low urine output (kidney dysfunction), low GCS/confusion (brain dysfunction), and low PaO2/FiO2 (lung dysfunction). A DECREASED CREATININE does NOT indicate organ dysfunction — kidney dysfunction causes creatinine to RISE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Jaundice — Jaundice IS a marker of liver dysfunction.
• Thrombocytopenia — Thrombocytopenia IS a marker of haematological dysfunction.
• Hypotension — Hypotension IS a marker of cardiovascular dysfunction.

⭐ Key point: The SOFA score uses these markers to grade organ dysfunction in sepsis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q116$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1513, $s$Calcitonin is a tumour marker specifically associated with which type of thyroid cancer?$s$, $s$["Papillary", "Follicular", "Medullary", "Anaplastic"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Medullary
CALCITONIN is a hormone made by the PARAFOLLICULAR (C) CELLS of the thyroid, which help regulate calcium. MEDULLARY THYROID CANCER arises from these C cells and produces large amounts of calcitonin, so CALCITONIN is used as a TUMOUR MARKER for diagnosis and follow-up (along with CEA). Medullary thyroid cancer can be inherited as part of MULTIPLE ENDOCRINE NEOPLASIA type 2 (MEN 2), linked to the RET gene.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Papillary — Papillary cancer arises from follicular cells; thyroglobulin is used for follow-up.
• Follicular — Follicular cancer arises from follicular cells; thyroglobulin is used.
• Anaplastic — Anaplastic cancer has no specific tumour marker.

⭐ Key point: Patients with medullary thyroid cancer should be screened for phaeochromocytoma before surgery (MEN 2).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q123$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1514, $s$A Mallory-Weiss tear classically occurs after:$s$, $s$["Repeated vomiting", "Chronic constipation", "A single episode of coughing", "Eating spicy food"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Repeated vomiting
A MALLORY-WEISS TEAR is a tear in the lining (mucosa) at the junction of the oesophagus and stomach, caused by a sudden rise in pressure during REPEATED VOMITING or forceful retching — often after heavy alcohol intake, or in hyperemesis gravidarum or bulimia. It typically causes vomiting of bright red blood (haematemesis) after episodes of non-bloody vomiting. Most tears heal on their own within a few days; endoscopic treatment is needed if bleeding continues.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chronic constipation — Chronic constipation is not a cause.
• A single episode of coughing — A single episode of coughing is unlikely to cause it.
• Eating spicy food — Eating spicy food does not cause it.

⭐ Key point: Distinguish from Boerhaave syndrome (full-thickness oesophageal rupture), which causes severe chest pain and surgical emphysema.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q127$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1515, $s$The pyramidal lobe of the thyroid, when present, is a remnant of the:$s$, $s$["Thyroglossal duct", "Parathyroid gland", "Carotid sheath", "Trachea"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Thyroglossal duct
During development, the THYROID gland forms at the base of the tongue and migrates down the neck to its final position in front of the trachea, leaving behind a tube called the THYROGLOSSAL DUCT, which usually disappears. In about 40–50% of people, a small part of thyroid tissue remains along this path, forming the PYRAMIDAL LOBE, which extends upwards from the isthmus. Remnants can also form THYROGLOSSAL CYSTS (midline neck lumps that move upward when sticking out the tongue).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Parathyroid gland — It is not related to the parathyroid glands.
• Carotid sheath — It is not related to the carotid sheath.
• Trachea — It is not related to the trachea.

⭐ Key point: The pyramidal lobe may enlarge in Graves' disease and can be seen on thyroid scans.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q129$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1516, $s$The female-to-male ratio in goitre incidence is approximately:$s$, $s$["1:1", "2:1", "5:1", "10:1"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10:1
GOITRE (enlargement of the thyroid gland) is much more common in WOMEN than men. The female-to-male ratio is often quoted as about 4:1 up to 10:1, depending on the type and population. Reasons include the effects of oestrogen and pregnancy (increased iodine needs) and the higher rate of autoimmune thyroid disease (Graves', Hashimoto's) in women. Iodine deficiency is the most common cause of goitre worldwide.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1:1 — 1:1 is incorrect — goitre is not equally common.
• 2:1 — 2:1 underestimates the difference.
• 5:1 — 5:1 is within the range but lower than the figure used in this exam.

⭐ Key point: Iodised salt has greatly reduced goitre in many countries, including Nigeria.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q131$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1517, $s$Hirschsprung's disease is caused by:$s$, $s$["Excess ganglion cells in the bowel wall", "Absence of ganglion cells in a segment of bowel, causing functional obstruction", "A viral infection of the colon", "An allergic reaction to milk protein"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Absence of ganglion cells in a segment of bowel, causing functional obstruction
HIRSCHSPRUNG'S DISEASE is caused by the ABSENCE of GANGLION CELLS (nerve cells) in a segment of the bowel wall (usually the rectum and sigmoid colon). Without these nerves, the segment cannot relax and push stool along (no peristalsis), causing a FUNCTIONAL OBSTRUCTION. The bowel above becomes dilated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Excess ganglion cells in the bowel wall — It is caused by ABSENCE, not excess, of ganglion cells.
• A viral infection of the colon — It is not caused by a viral infection.
• An allergic reaction to milk protein — It is not caused by milk protein allergy.

⭐ Key point: Diagnosed by rectal biopsy; treated surgically by removing the affected segment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q149$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1518, $s$Neonatal jaundice is usually clinically visible when serum bilirubin exceeds:$s$, $s$["5 mg/dL", "15 mg/dL", "25 mg/dL", "50 mg/dL"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 5 mg/dL
NEONATAL JAUNDICE becomes VISIBLE in the skin when serum bilirubin exceeds about 5 mg/dL (about 85 µmol/L). In adults, jaundice is visible at about 2–3 mg/dL. Jaundice spreads from head to toe as levels rise.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 15 mg/dL — 15 mg/dL is a high level, not the threshold of visibility.
• 25 mg/dL — 25 mg/dL is very high and risks kernicterus.
• 50 mg/dL — 50 mg/dL is extremely high.

⭐ Key point: Visual assessment is unreliable — measure bilirubin (transcutaneous or serum) when jaundice is noted.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q150$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1519, $s$The 'ebb and flow' model of the metabolic response to trauma describes which sequence?$s$, $s$["An initial hypometabolic 'ebb' phase of hypoperfusion followed by a hypermetabolic 'flow' (catabolic) phase", "A single sustained hypermetabolic phase with no early depression", "An initial anabolic phase followed by a hypometabolic phase", "A random, unpredictable pattern with no identifiable phases"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An initial hypometabolic 'ebb' phase of hypoperfusion followed by a hypermetabolic 'flow' (catabolic) phase
The "EBB and FLOW" model describes the metabolic response to trauma: an initial EBB phase (first 24–48 hours) with HYPOMETABOLISM and hypoperfusion (shock), followed by a FLOW phase with HYPERMETABOLISM and catabolism (muscle breakdown) during recovery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A single sustained hypermetabolic phase with no early depression — There is an early depressed phase.
• An initial anabolic phase followed by a hypometabolic phase — The anabolic phase comes later in recovery.
• A random, unpredictable pattern with no identifiable phases — The pattern is predictable.

⭐ Key point: Nutritional support is important during the flow phase.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q197$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1520, $s$Urine passes through the renal collecting system in which correct sequence?$s$, $s$["Minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter", "Major calyx, minor calyx, renal pelvis, ureter", "Renal pelvis, major calyx, minor calyx, ureter", "Minor calyx, renal pelvis, major calyx, ureter"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Minor calyx, major calyx, renal pelvis, ureter
Urine is formed in the nephrons and flows through the collecting ducts into the renal papillae, then through: MINOR CALYX → MAJOR CALYX → RENAL PELVIS → URETER → bladder → urethra. Several minor calyces join to form a major calyx; the major calyces join to form the renal pelvis, which narrows into the ureter.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Major calyx, minor calyx, renal pelvis, ureter — Major before minor calyx is the wrong order.
• Renal pelvis, major calyx, minor calyx, ureter — Renal pelvis before calyces is the wrong order.
• Minor calyx, renal pelvis, major calyx, ureter — The renal pelvis comes after the major calyx.

⭐ Key point: Kidney stones can lodge at narrow points (pelvi-ureteric junction, pelvic brim, vesico-ureteric junction), causing severe colicky pain and obstruction.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q7$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1521, $s$Haustra, the pouch-like segments visible along the colon, are formed largely due to the action of:$s$, $s$["The teniae coli, three longitudinal bands of smooth muscle", "The circular muscle layer alone", "Villi projecting from the mucosa", "The greater omentum"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The teniae coli, three longitudinal bands of smooth muscle
HAUSTRA are the pouch-like bulges seen along the COLON (large intestine). They are formed because the TENIAE COLI — three narrow bands of LONGITUDINAL smooth muscle running along the colon — are SHORTER than the colon wall, so they bunch it up into pouches (like gathering fabric with a drawstring). Haustra help mix and move faeces.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The circular muscle layer alone — The circular muscle layer alone does not create haustra.
• Villi projecting from the mucosa — Villi are found in the SMALL intestine (for absorption), not the colon.
• The greater omentum — The greater omentum is a fatty apron hanging from the stomach.

⭐ Key point: On abdominal X-ray, haustra (incomplete lines across the bowel) identify the LARGE bowel, whereas valvulae conniventes (complete lines) identify the SMALL bowel.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q10$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1522, $s$On imaging of intestinal obstruction, distinguishing small bowel from large bowel obstruction is aided by which feature?$s$, $s$["Small bowel loops show valvulae conniventes (complete transverse lines); large bowel shows haustra (incomplete lines)", "There is no radiological way to distinguish them", "Only CT can ever differentiate them, never plain X-ray", "Large bowel is always more centrally located regardless of distension"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Small bowel loops show valvulae conniventes (complete transverse lines); large bowel shows haustra (incomplete lines)
On ABDOMINAL X-RAY, SMALL BOWEL loops show VALVULAE CONNIVENTES — mucosal folds that cross the ENTIRE WIDTH of the bowel (complete lines, like a "stack of coins"). LARGE BOWEL shows HAUSTRA — folds that do NOT cross the full width (incomplete lines). Small bowel is usually central; large bowel is peripheral (framing the abdomen).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• There is no radiological way to distinguish them — Plain X-ray can distinguish them.
• Only CT can ever differentiate them, never plain X-ray — Plain X-ray can be used; CT gives more detail.
• Large bowel is always more centrally located regardless of distension — Large bowel is usually peripheral, not central.

⭐ Key point: Upper limits of normal diameter: small bowel 3 cm, colon 6 cm, caecum 9 cm (the "3-6-9 rule").$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q42$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1523, $s$Hypertension is the leading cause of end-stage renal disease after which condition?$s$, $s$["Diabetes", "Asthma", "Hepatitis", "Osteoarthritis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Diabetes
DIABETES MELLITUS is the LEADING cause of END-STAGE RENAL DISEASE (ESRD — kidney failure needing dialysis or transplant) worldwide, accounting for about 40% of cases. HYPERTENSION is the SECOND leading cause. Both damage the small blood vessels and filtering units (glomeruli) of the kidneys over many years. In Nigeria and much of Africa, hypertension, diabetes and chronic glomerulonephritis are major causes of CKD.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Asthma — Asthma does not damage the kidneys.
• Hepatitis — Hepatitis B and C can cause certain kidney diseases (glomerulonephritis), but they are not leading causes of ESRD.
• Osteoarthritis — Osteoarthritis does not cause kidney failure (although frequent NSAID use for pain can).

⭐ Key point: Slow kidney damage by good control of blood glucose (HbA1c) and BP (often with ACE inhibitors or ARBs), and screen for protein in the urine.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q66$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1524, $s$Which finding is most suggestive of contrast-induced nephropathy after coronary angiography?$s$, $s$["Hyperkalemia occurring within minutes of injection", "Gross hematuria developing after two weeks", "Immediate anuria during the procedure", "Rise in serum creatinine within 24-72 hours of contrast exposure"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Rise in serum creatinine within 24-72 hours of contrast exposure
CONTRAST-INDUCED NEPHROPATHY (contrast-associated AKI) is a decline in kidney function after IV contrast (e.g. coronary angiography or CT). It is diagnosed by a RISE in SERUM CREATININE (e.g. ≥26.5 µmol/L or ≥25% above baseline) within 24–72 HOURS of contrast exposure. It usually peaks at 3–5 days and often recovers within 1–3 weeks. Risk factors: CKD, diabetes, dehydration, heart failure, large contrast volume, and nephrotoxic drugs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperkalemia occurring within minutes of injection — Hyperkalaemia within minutes is not typical.
• Gross hematuria developing after two weeks — Gross haematuria after two weeks is not typical.
• Immediate anuria during the procedure — Immediate anuria during the procedure is not typical.

⭐ Key point: Prevention: identify at-risk patients, ensure adequate IV hydration (e.g. 0.9% saline before and after), use the lowest contrast dose, and hold nephrotoxic drugs (e.g. NSAIDs, metformin in severe CKD).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q78$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1525, $s$A patient's urine output is measured at 20ml over the last hour, with a weight of 70kg. This represents a urine output of approximately:$s$, $s$["0.3ml/kg/hr, consistent with oliguria requiring assessment", "2ml/kg/hr, a normal finding", "5ml/kg/hr, indicating polyuria", "No clinical significance regardless of the value"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 0.3ml/kg/hr, consistent with oliguria requiring assessment
URINE OUTPUT in mL/kg/hr = VOLUME ÷ HOURS ÷ WEIGHT. Here: 20 mL ÷ 1 hour ÷ 70 kg = 0.29, which is approximately 0.3 mL/kg/hr. The minimum normal adult urine output is 0.5 mL/kg/hr. Below this is OLIGURIA, which requires assessment (check catheter patency, fluid status, BP, and causes of AKI).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2ml/kg/hr, a normal finding — 2 mL/kg/hr would be 140 mL/hr for this patient.
• 5ml/kg/hr, indicating polyuria — 5 mL/kg/hr would be 350 mL/hr (polyuria).
• No clinical significance regardless of the value — Urine output is always clinically significant in ICU.

⭐ Key point: Oliguria for 6 hours or more meets KDIGO criteria for AKI. Always check that the catheter is not blocked before assuming kidney failure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q79$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1526, $s$Acute kidney injury caused by renal artery stenosis reducing blood flow to the kidney is classified as:$s$, $s$["Prerenal", "Intrinsic (renal)", "Postrenal", "Not a cause of AKI"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Prerenal
AKI is classified by where the problem is: PRE-RENAL (reduced blood flow TO the kidney), INTRINSIC/RENAL (damage WITHIN the kidney tissue), or POST-RENAL (blockage of urine flow AFTER the kidney). RENAL ARTERY STENOSIS narrows the artery supplying the kidney, REDUCING BLOOD FLOW, so it is classified as PRE-RENAL (it affects perfusion before blood reaches the kidney tissue). ACE inhibitors can precipitate AKI in bilateral renal artery stenosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intrinsic (renal) — Intrinsic AKI involves direct damage to the kidney tissue (e.g. ATN, glomerulonephritis).
• Postrenal — Post-renal AKI involves obstruction of the urinary tract.
• Not a cause of AKI — Renal artery stenosis IS a cause of AKI.

⭐ Key point: Pre-renal AKI usually improves quickly if blood flow is restored before the tubules are damaged.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q81$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1527, $s$A patient with severe hemorrhage and hypotension develops oliguria and rising creatinine. This mechanism of acute kidney injury is best classified as:$s$, $s$["Nephrotoxic", "Postrenal", "Prerenal", "Intrarenal"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Prerenal
SEVERE HAEMORRHAGE and HYPOTENSION reduce blood flow to the kidneys. The kidneys respond by retaining sodium and water, producing a small amount of concentrated urine (OLIGURIA), and the creatinine rises. This is PRE-RENAL AKI. If hypoperfusion is prolonged, it can progress to acute tubular necrosis (intrinsic AKI).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nephrotoxic — Nephrotoxic AKI is caused by drugs or toxins damaging the kidney.
• Postrenal — Post-renal AKI is caused by obstruction.
• Intrarenal — Intrarenal (intrinsic) AKI involves direct kidney damage.

⭐ Key point: Treat with prompt fluid and blood resuscitation and control of bleeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q82$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1528, $s$A PT (prothrombin time) greater than what value is used as a liver transplant criterion in FHF?$s$, $s$[">15 seconds", ">30 seconds", ">50 seconds", ">100 seconds"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: >50 seconds
KING'S COLLEGE CRITERIA identify patients with FULMINANT HEPATIC FAILURE (FHF) who need LIVER TRANSPLANT. For NON-paracetamol causes, transplant is indicated if PT is above 100 seconds (INR >6.5) alone, OR any 3 of: age under 10 or over 40, unfavourable cause, jaundice for more than 7 days before encephalopathy, PT above 50 SECONDS (INR >3.5), or bilirubin above 300 µmol/L. So a PT above 50 seconds is a recognised transplant criterion (as one of the combined criteria).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• >15 seconds — 15 seconds is close to normal.
• >30 seconds — 30 seconds is prolonged but not the cut-off.
• >100 seconds — 100 seconds is the standalone criterion; but the commonly tested cut-off in the combined criteria is 50 seconds.

⭐ Key point: Refer early to a liver transplant centre when coagulopathy, encephalopathy or acidosis worsen.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q115$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1529, $s$Psammoma bodies on histology are a classic feature found in what percentage of papillary carcinoma cases?$s$, $s$["10%", "25%", "60%", "100%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 60%
PSAMMOMA BODIES are small, round, layered (laminated) clumps of CALCIUM seen under the microscope. They form from dead tumour cells that have calcified. They are a classic feature of PAPILLARY THYROID CARCINOMA, found in roughly HALF of cases (commonly quoted as about 40–60%, so 60% is the closest option). They are also seen in other tumours: papillary serous ovarian cancer, meningioma and mesothelioma (memory aid: "PSaMMoma" — Papillary thyroid, Serous ovarian, Meningioma, Mesothelioma).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10% — 10% underestimates how often they are found.
• 25% — 25% underestimates how often they are found.
• 100% — 100% is incorrect — not every papillary carcinoma shows psammoma bodies.

⭐ Key point: Papillary carcinoma is the most common thyroid cancer, often spreads to neck lymph nodes, and has an excellent prognosis with treatment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q123$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1530, $s$Anti-thyroid drug treatment is typically continued for how long before a patient may be considered cured?$s$, $s$["2 weeks", "3 months", "18 months", "10 years"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 18 months
In GRAVES' DISEASE (the commonest cause of hyperthyroidism), ANTI-THYROID DRUGS such as CARBIMAZOLE or PROPYLTHIOURACIL are usually given for about 12–18 MONTHS. After this course, the drug is stopped, and about 50% of patients stay in long-term REMISSION (effectively "cured"). If hyperthyroidism comes back, definitive treatment with radioactive iodine or thyroidectomy is usually considered.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2 weeks — 2 weeks is far too short — the drugs take 4–8 weeks just to control thyroid levels.
• 3 months — 3 months is too short for lasting remission.
• 10 years — 10 years is not the standard course (though some patients choose long-term low-dose therapy).

⭐ Key point: Warn patients about AGRANULOCYTOSIS — they must report sore throat, fever or mouth ulcers immediately and have an urgent blood count.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q125$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1531, $s$Which of the following is a goitrogenic food capable of blocking iodine uptake?$s$, $s$["Cassava", "Rice", "Chicken", "Eggs"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cassava
GOITROGENS are substances that interfere with thyroid hormone production. CASSAVA contains CYANOGENIC GLYCOSIDES (e.g. linamarin), which the body converts to THIOCYANATE. Thiocyanate BLOCKS the IODINE pump in the thyroid gland, reducing iodine uptake. In areas where iodine intake is low and cassava is a staple food (including parts of Nigeria), this contributes to GOITRE and hypothyroidism. Proper processing (soaking, fermenting, drying, cooking — as in making garri) reduces the toxic content.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rice — Rice is not goitrogenic.
• Chicken — Chicken is not goitrogenic.
• Eggs — Eggs are not goitrogenic; they contain some iodine.

⭐ Key point: Iodised salt is the main public health measure to prevent iodine-deficiency goitre. Other goitrogens include cabbage, cauliflower and millet.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q130$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1532, $s$The anatomical landmark dividing upper from lower GI bleeding is the:$s$, $s$["Pylorus", "Ileocaecal valve", "Ligament of Treitz", "Duodenal bulb"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ligament of Treitz
The LIGAMENT OF TREITZ (suspensory ligament of the duodenum) marks the junction between the duodenum and jejunum. Bleeding ABOVE (proximal to) it is UPPER GI bleeding; bleeding BELOW (distal to) it is LOWER GI bleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pylorus — The pylorus is between the stomach and duodenum.
• Ileocaecal valve — The ileocaecal valve is between the small and large intestine.
• Duodenal bulb — The duodenal bulb is part of the duodenum.

⭐ Key point: Upper GI bleeding causes haematemesis and melaena; lower GI bleeding usually causes fresh red blood per rectum.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q131$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1533, $s$A test that shows positive when the true status is negative gives a:$s$, $s$["True positive", "False negative", "False positive", "True negative"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: False positive
When comparing a test result with the TRUE status: TRUE POSITIVE — test positive, disease present; FALSE POSITIVE — test POSITIVE but disease ABSENT (true status negative); FALSE NEGATIVE — test negative but disease present; TRUE NEGATIVE — test negative, disease absent. A false positive can cause unnecessary anxiety, further tests and treatment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• True positive — A true positive is correctly positive in someone WITH the condition.
• False negative — A false negative is a NEGATIVE result in someone WITH the condition (missed diagnosis).
• True negative — A true negative is correctly negative in someone WITHOUT the condition.

⭐ Key point: SENSITIVITY = ability to detect people WITH the disease (few false negatives). SPECIFICITY = ability to correctly identify people WITHOUT the disease (few false positives).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q171$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1534, $s$Which of the following is the appropriate nursing intervention for a patient with a terminal illness who is passing through the acceptance stage?$s$, $s$["Allowing the patient to cry", "Encouraging unrestricted visiting", "Explaining the patient what is being done", "Being around though not speaking"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Being around though not speaking
In the ACCEPTANCE stage of grief (Kübler-Ross), the patient is often calm, withdrawn and may want QUIET COMPANY. The most appropriate intervention is BEING AROUND THOUGH NOT SPEAKING — a quiet, supportive presence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Allowing the patient to cry — Allowing crying fits the depression stage.
• Encouraging unrestricted visiting — Unrestricted visiting may tire the patient.
• Explaining the patient what is being done — Explaining procedures is always good, but not the specific need here.

⭐ Key point: Stages: denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q186$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1535, $s$A nurse loves caring for the elderly. This represents her:$s$, $s$["Respect", "Dignity", "Fidelity", "Values"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Values
VALUES are a person's deeply held BELIEFS about what is important, desirable and worthwhile. They guide choices and behaviour. A nurse who LOVES caring for the ELDERLY shows a personal VALUE — she values older people and the work of caring for them. Professional nursing values include caring, compassion, respect, integrity, altruism and social justice.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respect — Respect is regard for the worth and rights of others — it can be a value, but the scenario describes what she cherishes.
• Dignity — Dignity is the inherent worth of every person.
• Fidelity — Fidelity is keeping promises and commitments.

⭐ Key point: Nurses should reflect on their own values (values clarification) and avoid imposing them on patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q190$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1536, $s$An 80 kg patient has passed 240 mL of urine in the last 3 hours. What is the urine output and how is it interpreted?$s$, $s$["80 mL/kg/hr — adequate", "1 mL/kg/hr — oliguria", "3 mL/kg/hr — adequate", "1 mL/kg/hr — adequate"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1 mL/kg/hr — adequate
URINE OUTPUT (mL/kg/hr) = VOLUME ÷ HOURS ÷ WEIGHT. Here: 240 mL ÷ 3 hours = 80 mL/hr; 80 ÷ 80 kg = 1 mL/kg/hr. The minimum acceptable adult urine output is 0.5 mL/kg/hr, so 1 mL/kg/hr is ADEQUATE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 80 mL/kg/hr — adequate — 80 mL/kg/hr is a units error.
• 1 mL/kg/hr — oliguria — 1 mL/kg/hr is correct, but it is NOT oliguria.
• 3 mL/kg/hr — adequate — 3 mL/kg/hr is a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Oliguria = less than 0.5 mL/kg/hr for 6 hours or more (KDIGO AKI criterion).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q37 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1537, $s$A 50 kg patient has passed 200 mL of urine in the last 3 hours. What is the urine output and how is it interpreted?$s$, $s$["1.33 mL/kg/hr — adequate", "4 mL/kg/hr — adequate", "1.33 mL/kg/hr — oliguria", "66.7 mL/kg/hr — adequate"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1.33 mL/kg/hr — adequate
URINE OUTPUT = VOLUME ÷ HOURS ÷ WEIGHT = 200 mL ÷ 3 hr ÷ 50 kg = 66.7 mL/hr ÷ 50 = 1.33 mL/kg/hr. This is above the minimum of 0.5 mL/kg/hr, so it is ADEQUATE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 4 mL/kg/hr — adequate — 4 mL/kg/hr is a calculation error.
• 1.33 mL/kg/hr — oliguria — 1.33 is correct, but it is NOT oliguria.
• 66.7 mL/kg/hr — adequate — 66.7 mL/kg/hr confuses mL/hr with mL/kg/hr.

⭐ Key point: In burns, the target is 0.5–1 mL/kg/hr (adults); higher output may mean over-resuscitation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q46 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1538, $s$A patient's renal function tests show a rising creatinine and urea with oliguria following a major surgery complicated by hypotension. This presentation is most consistent with:$s$, $s$["Acute kidney injury, likely pre-renal from hypoperfusion", "Diabetes insipidus", "Chronic kidney disease of long standing", "Normal post-operative recovery"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute kidney injury, likely pre-renal from hypoperfusion
ACUTE KIDNEY INJURY (AKI) is a sudden fall in kidney function, shown by a RISING CREATININE and UREA and/or REDUCED URINE OUTPUT (oliguria). After MAJOR SURGERY complicated by HYPOTENSION, the kidneys have not received enough blood flow (HYPOPERFUSION), so this is most likely PRE-RENAL AKI. If the hypoperfusion lasts long enough, it can progress to acute tubular necrosis (intrinsic AKI). Treatment: restore circulating volume and BP, stop nephrotoxic drugs, monitor fluid balance and electrolytes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diabetes insipidus — Diabetes insipidus causes POLYURIA (large volumes of dilute urine), not oliguria.
• Chronic kidney disease of long standing — Chronic kidney disease develops over months to years.
• Normal post-operative recovery — Rising creatinine with oliguria is not normal recovery.

⭐ Key point: KDIGO AKI criteria: creatinine rise ≥26.5 µmol/L within 48 hours, or ≥1.5 times baseline within 7 days, or urine output <0.5 mL/kg/hr for 6 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q150$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1539, $s$A patient with suspected mushroom poisoning develops severe gastroenteritis 6-24 hours after ingestion, followed by signs of liver failure over the next several days. Which type of mushroom toxin is most likely responsible?$s$, $s$["Ibotenic acid-containing mushrooms", "Muscarine-containing mushrooms", "Amatoxin-containing mushrooms (e.g., Amanita phalloides)", "Psilocybin-containing mushrooms"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Amatoxin-containing mushrooms (e.g., Amanita phalloides)
AMATOXIN-containing mushrooms (e.g. AMANITA PHALLOIDES, "death cap") cause a typical pattern: a symptom-free delay of 6–24 HOURS, then severe GASTROENTERITIS (vomiting, watery diarrhoea), an apparent improvement, then ACUTE LIVER FAILURE (jaundice, coagulopathy, encephalopathy) over the next few days. Amatoxins block protein synthesis in liver cells. It can be fatal; liver transplant may be needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ibotenic acid-containing mushrooms — Ibotenic acid mushrooms cause early (within 2 hours) neurological symptoms (confusion, hallucinations).
• Muscarine-containing mushrooms — Muscarine mushrooms cause early cholinergic symptoms (sweating, salivation).
• Psilocybin-containing mushrooms — Psilocybin causes hallucinations.

⭐ Key point: Late-onset gastrointestinal symptoms (after 6 hours) after eating wild mushrooms suggest dangerous toxins.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q159$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1540, $s$A patient with severe liver disease develops ascites and peripheral oedema. The nurse understands this is partly explained by the liver's reduced synthesis of:$s$, $s$["Insulin only", "Red blood cells", "Albumin, reducing plasma oncotic pressure and allowing fluid to shift into tissues/peritoneal cavity", "White blood cells"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Albumin, reducing plasma oncotic pressure and allowing fluid to shift into tissues/peritoneal cavity
The LIVER makes ALBUMIN, the main protein that holds fluid inside blood vessels (ONCOTIC PRESSURE). In severe liver disease, albumin falls, oncotic pressure drops, and fluid leaks into tissues (PERIPHERAL OEDEMA) and the abdomen (ASCITES). Portal hypertension also contributes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Insulin only — The liver doesn't make insulin (the pancreas does).
• Red blood cells — Red blood cells are made in bone marrow.
• White blood cells — White blood cells are made in bone marrow.

⭐ Key point: Treat ascites with salt restriction, diuretics (spironolactone), and paracentesis with albumin.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q170$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1541, $s$A 55 year old male patient is admitted with a massive GI bleed. The patient is at risk for what type of acute kidney injury?$s$, $s$["Intrinsic renal", "Intra-renal", "Pre-renal", "Post-renal"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pre-renal
A MASSIVE GI BLEED causes HYPOVOLAEMIA (loss of circulating blood). The kidneys receive less blood flow, so the glomerular filtration rate falls — this is PRE-RENAL AKI. At first the kidneys themselves are not damaged; urine output falls, urine is concentrated with low sodium (the kidneys are trying to save water and salt), and the urea rises more than creatinine (also from digested blood in the gut). If hypoperfusion continues, it can progress to acute tubular necrosis (intrinsic damage).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intrinsic renal — Intrinsic renal AKI is direct damage to the kidney tissue (e.g. ATN, glomerulonephritis, nephrotoxins).
• Intra-renal — Intra-renal is another name for intrinsic renal AKI.
• Post-renal — Post-renal AKI is caused by obstruction to urine outflow (e.g. enlarged prostate, blocked catheter).

⭐ Key point: Rapid restoration of blood volume (fluids and blood transfusion) and stopping the bleeding can reverse pre-renal AKI before permanent damage occurs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q175$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1542, $s$A patient with obstructive uropathy from bilateral ureteral stones is treated with nephrostomy tube placement. What immediate postobstructive complication should the nurse monitor for?$s$, $s$["Post-obstructive diuresis with large fluid and electrolyte losses", "Spontaneous resolution of chronic kidney disease", "Immediate anuria", "Hypertension crisis only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Post-obstructive diuresis with large fluid and electrolyte losses
When a long-standing URINARY OBSTRUCTION is RELIEVED (e.g. by nephrostomy tubes or catheter), the kidneys may produce VERY LARGE VOLUMES of urine — POST-OBSTRUCTIVE DIURESIS (often above 200 mL/hr). Reasons: excretion of accumulated urea and salt, and the damaged tubules' temporary inability to concentrate urine. This can quickly cause HYPOVOLAEMIA, low BP and ELECTROLYTE LOSSES (sodium, potassium, magnesium).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Spontaneous resolution of chronic kidney disease — Chronic kidney disease does not resolve spontaneously.
• Immediate anuria — Anuria is unlikely immediately after relief of obstruction.
• Hypertension crisis only — Hypertensive crisis is not the main post-obstructive concern; hypovolaemia is.

⭐ Key point: Measure urine output hourly, check electrolytes frequently, and replace fluids (often a proportion of the output) as prescribed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q196$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1543, $s$A patient with severe cirrhosis of the liver develops hepatorenal syndrome. Which of the following nursing assessment data would support this?$s$, $s$["Oliguria and azotemia", "Metabolic alkalosis", "Decreased urinary concentration", "Weight gain of less than 1kg per week"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Oliguria and azotemia
HEPATORENAL SYNDROME (HRS) is kidney failure in a patient with severe liver disease (cirrhosis, ascites). The kidneys themselves are structurally normal, but severe VASOCONSTRICTION of the kidney blood vessels (caused by vasodilation elsewhere and activation of renin-angiotensin and sympathetic systems) reduces blood flow. Typical findings: OLIGURIA (low urine output) and AZOTAEMIA (rising urea and creatinine), with very low urine sodium, and no improvement after stopping diuretics and giving albumin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metabolic alkalosis — Metabolic alkalosis is not a key feature of HRS.
• Decreased urinary concentration — Urine in HRS is concentrated (low sodium), not dilute.
• Weight gain of less than 1kg per week — Weight gain reflects ascites; slow weight gain is not a specific marker of HRS.

⭐ Key point: Treatment: terlipressin (or noradrenaline) plus IV albumin; liver transplant is the definitive cure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q197$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1544, $s$A patient with long-standing hypertension and diabetes has an eGFR of 25 mL/min/1.73m2 sustained for over three months. This is classified as:$s$, $s$["Nephrotic syndrome", "Chronic kidney disease stage 4", "Acute kidney injury", "Chronic kidney disease stage 2"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Chronic kidney disease stage 4
CHRONIC KIDNEY DISEASE (CKD) is defined as abnormal kidney structure or function lasting MORE THAN 3 MONTHS. It is staged by the eGFR (mL/min/1.73m²): G1 ≥90 (with kidney damage); G2 60–89; G3a 45–59; G3b 30–44; G4 15–29; G5 <15 (kidney failure). An eGFR of 25 sustained for more than 3 months is CKD STAGE 4 (severely reduced function). Hypertension and diabetes are the two commonest causes of CKD.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nephrotic syndrome — Nephrotic syndrome is defined by heavy proteinuria, low albumin, oedema and high lipids — not by the eGFR level.
• Acute kidney injury — Acute kidney injury develops over hours to days, not over 3 months.
• Chronic kidney disease stage 2 — Stage 2 is an eGFR of 60–89.

⭐ Key point: Stage 4 patients should be prepared for renal replacement therapy (dialysis or transplant planning). Avoid nephrotoxic drugs and adjust drug doses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q145$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1545, $s$A patient on long-term oral amiodarone should be monitored for which combination of toxicities?$s$, $s$["Ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity, and red man syndrome", "Serotonin syndrome, tendon rupture, and QT shortening", "Pulmonary fibrosis, thyroid dysfunction, and hepatotoxicity", "Adrenal suppression, hypoglycemia, and priapism"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pulmonary fibrosis, thyroid dysfunction, and hepatotoxicity
AMIODARONE contains a lot of iodine and builds up in tissues for months. Long-term toxicities include: PULMONARY FIBROSIS / pneumonitis (cough, breathlessness — can be fatal); THYROID DYSFUNCTION (both hypothyroidism and hyperthyroidism); and HEPATOTOXICITY (raised liver enzymes, hepatitis). Others: corneal microdeposits, slate-grey skin and photosensitivity, peripheral neuropathy, bradycardia and QT prolongation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity, and red man syndrome — Ototoxicity, nephrotoxicity and red man syndrome are linked to aminoglycosides and vancomycin.
• Serotonin syndrome, tendon rupture, and QT shortening — Serotonin syndrome, tendon rupture (fluoroquinolones) and QT SHORTENING are not amiodarone effects — amiodarone PROLONGS the QT.
• Adrenal suppression, hypoglycemia, and priapism — Adrenal suppression and hypoglycaemia are not typical amiodarone effects; priapism is linked to other drugs.

⭐ Key point: Monitor thyroid function, liver function, chest X-ray/lung function and ECG. Reduce doses of warfarin and digoxin when amiodarone is started.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q151$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1546, $s$A patient recovering from DKA treatment develops cerebral oedema, a rare but serious complication most associated with:$s$, $s$["No relationship to treatment rate", "Overly rapid correction of hyperglycaemia and fluid shifts, particularly in paediatric patients", "Undertreatment of DKA", "Slow, careful correction of fluids and glucose"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Overly rapid correction of hyperglycaemia and fluid shifts, particularly in paediatric patients
CEREBRAL OEDEMA is a rare but serious complication of DKA treatment, mostly in CHILDREN and young adults. It is linked to OVERLY RAPID CORRECTION — giving large volumes of IV fluid quickly and lowering glucose and osmolality too fast. Water moves into the brain cells, which have adapted to the high osmolality, causing them to swell. Warning signs: headache, falling conscious level, irritability, slowing heart rate, rising BP, and abnormal posturing. Treatment: mannitol or hypertonic saline, head-up position, reduce fluid rate, and ICU care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No relationship to treatment rate — The rate of correction IS related to the risk.
• Undertreatment of DKA — Undertreatment is not the main association.
• Slow, careful correction of fluids and glucose — Slow, careful correction REDUCES the risk.

⭐ Key point: In children with DKA, fluids are given more cautiously, and glucose should fall gradually (e.g. no faster than about 5 mmol/L per hour).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q153$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1547, $s$A patient with severe upper GI bleeding is given a proton pump inhibitor (PPI) infusion. The rationale for this treatment is that PPIs:$s$, $s$["Have no role in acute GI bleeding management", "Raise gastric pH, which stabilises clot formation and reduces rebleeding risk from peptic ulcers", "Are used only for long-term prevention, never in acute bleeding", "Directly stop active arterial bleeding without any other intervention"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Raise gastric pH, which stabilises clot formation and reduces rebleeding risk from peptic ulcers
In upper GI bleeding from a PEPTIC ULCER, stomach ACID and the enzyme PEPSIN break down blood clots. PPIs (e.g. omeprazole, pantoprazole infusion) strongly suppress acid production and RAISE the GASTRIC pH (to above 6). At this pH, clots are more STABLE and platelets work better, which REDUCES REBLEEDING after endoscopic treatment, and reduces the need for surgery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Have no role in acute GI bleeding management — PPIs DO have an important role in acute ulcer bleeding.
• Are used only for long-term prevention, never in acute bleeding — PPIs are used in acute bleeding as well as for long-term prevention.
• Directly stop active arterial bleeding without any other intervention — PPIs do not directly stop active arterial bleeding; endoscopic therapy (clips, injection, heat) is needed to stop active bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Management of upper GI bleed: resuscitation (IV access, fluids, blood), risk scoring (Glasgow-Blatchford), urgent endoscopy within 24 hours, PPI after endoscopic treatment, and treat H. pylori.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q159$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1548, $s$A patient with severe hyperglycaemia and altered mental status but minimal ketosis and a blood glucose of 45mmol/L is most likely experiencing:$s$, $s$["Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA)", "Hypoglycaemia", "Normal glucose variation", "Hyperglycaemic Hyperosmolar State (HHS)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hyperglycaemic Hyperosmolar State (HHS)
HYPERGLYCAEMIC HYPEROSMOLAR STATE (HHS) is a diabetic emergency, mostly in type 2 diabetes, with VERY HIGH GLUCOSE (often above 30 mmol/L — here 45), HIGH OSMOLALITY (above 320 mOsm/kg), severe DEHYDRATION, and ALTERED MENTAL STATUS, but with MINIMAL KETOSIS and little or no acidosis (pH above 7.3, bicarbonate above 15).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diabetic ketoacidosis (DKA) — DKA has significant ketosis and acidosis.
• Hypoglycaemia — Hypoglycaemia is LOW blood glucose.
• Normal glucose variation — 45 mmol/L is not normal.

⭐ Key point: Treat HHS with careful IV fluid replacement, cautious insulin (only after fluids stop lowering glucose), potassium monitoring and VTE prophylaxis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q166$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1549, $s$A patient with acute pancreatitis develops Grey Turner's sign (flank bruising). This finding suggests:$s$, $s$["Retroperitoneal haemorrhage, associated with severe pancreatitis", "A benign, unrelated skin condition", "A localised allergic reaction", "Improved prognosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Retroperitoneal haemorrhage, associated with severe pancreatitis
GREY TURNER'S SIGN is BRUISING of the FLANKS. In severe (necrotising or haemorrhagic) pancreatitis, blood from the inflamed pancreas tracks through the RETROPERITONEUM to the flanks. It indicates RETROPERITONEAL HAEMORRHAGE and SEVERE disease, with a worse prognosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A benign, unrelated skin condition — It is not a benign skin condition.
• A localised allergic reaction — It is not an allergic reaction.
• Improved prognosis — It indicates a WORSE prognosis, not improved.

⭐ Key point: Cullen's sign is bruising around the umbilicus — also a sign of retroperitoneal or intra-abdominal bleeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q169$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1550, $s$A patient with long-standing type 1 diabetes develops recurrent severe hypoglycemia with reduced awareness of warning symptoms. Which pathophysiologic change most likely underlies this 'hypoglycemia unawareness'?$s$, $s$["Increased cortisol secretion masking symptoms", "Increased glucagon secretion causing symptom suppression", "Excess residual insulin secretion from the pancreas", "Impaired counter-regulatory glucagon and epinephrine responses from recurrent hypoglycemic episodes blunting the autonomic warning response"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Impaired counter-regulatory glucagon and epinephrine responses from recurrent hypoglycemic episodes blunting the autonomic warning response
In long-standing type 1 diabetes, RECURRENT HYPOGLYCAEMIA blunts the COUNTER-REGULATORY responses: the GLUCAGON response is lost, and the ADRENALINE (epinephrine) response is reduced. Without adrenaline, the usual warning symptoms (sweating, tremor, palpitations) don't appear — HYPOGLYCAEMIA UNAWARENESS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased cortisol secretion masking symptoms — Increased cortisol doesn't mask symptoms.
• Increased glucagon secretion causing symptom suppression — Glucagon secretion is reduced, not increased.
• Excess residual insulin secretion from the pancreas — Type 1 diabetes has little residual insulin secretion.

⭐ Key point: Strictly avoiding hypoglycaemia for 2–3 weeks can restore awareness.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q194$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1551, $s$What is the correct sequence of physical examination techniques when assessing the abdomen, and why does it differ from other body systems?$s$, $s$["Inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation — because palpation stimulates bowel sounds", "Inspection, auscultation, percussion, palpation — because palpation and percussion can alter bowel motility and sounds before they can be auscultated", "Percussion, palpation, inspection, auscultation", "Auscultation, inspection, palpation, percussion — because auscultation must always come first"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inspection, auscultation, percussion, palpation — because palpation and percussion can alter bowel motility and sounds before they can be auscultated
For the ABDOMEN, the order is INSPECTION, AUSCULTATION, PERCUSSION, PALPATION. Auscultation is done BEFORE percussion and palpation, because touching, tapping or pressing the abdomen can STIMULATE or ALTER BOWEL MOTILITY and BOWEL SOUNDS, giving a false impression. Listening first records the natural bowel sounds accurately. Palpation is done last, also because it may cause pain and tensing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation — because palpation stimulates bowel sounds — Palpation before auscultation alters bowel sounds — this is the reason the order is changed.
• Percussion, palpation, inspection, auscultation — Percussion first is not the standard order.
• Auscultation, inspection, palpation, percussion — because auscultation must always come first — Auscultation does not always come first; inspection is always first.

⭐ Key point: Listen to the abdomen in all four quadrants. Normal bowel sounds are 5–30 per minute. Listen for at least 2–5 minutes before saying they are absent.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q145$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1552, $s$After pituitary surgery, a patient passes 300 mL/h of urine with specific gravity 1.002. Serum Na⁺ is 152 mmol/L and the patient is very thirsty. The diagnosis and treatment are:$s$, $s$["Cranial diabetes insipidus — desmopressin", "Hyperglycaemic osmotic diuresis — insulin", "Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus — desmopressin", "SIADH with natriuresis — fluid restriction"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cranial diabetes insipidus — desmopressin
CRANIAL DIABETES INSIPIDUS occurs when the pituitary doesn't release enough ADH (e.g. after PITUITARY SURGERY). Without ADH, the kidneys can't concentrate urine, causing LARGE VOLUMES of DILUTE URINE (300 mL/h, specific gravity 1.002), HIGH SODIUM (152) and THIRST. Treatment: DESMOPRESSIN (synthetic ADH).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperglycaemic osmotic diuresis — insulin — Hyperglycaemia causes osmotic diuresis, but blood glucose would be high and urine would contain glucose.
• Nephrogenic diabetes insipidus — desmopressin — Nephrogenic DI doesn't respond to desmopressin (the kidneys don't respond to ADH).
• SIADH with natriuresis — fluid restriction — SIADH causes LOW sodium and concentrated urine.

⭐ Key point: Monitor urine output, specific gravity, sodium and fluid balance closely after pituitary surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q107$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1553, $s$Two weeks after a sore throat, a 9-year-old has cola-coloured urine, puffy eyelids, BP 150/100 mmHg, reduced urine output and red cell casts in the urine. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["IgA nephropathy (Berger's disease)", "Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis", "Haemolytic uraemic syndrome", "Minimal change nephrotic syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Post-streptococcal glomerulonephritis
POST-STREPTOCOCCAL GLOMERULONEPHRITIS (PSGN) occurs 1–3 WEEKS after a STREP THROAT (or 3–6 weeks after skin infection). Immune complexes damage the glomeruli. Features in children: COLA-COLOURED (smoky) URINE (haematuria), PUFFY EYELIDS (oedema), HYPERTENSION, reduced urine output, and RED CELL CASTS in the urine.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IgA nephropathy (Berger's disease) — IgA nephropathy causes haematuria within 1–2 DAYS of infection (not 2 weeks).
• Haemolytic uraemic syndrome — HUS follows bloody diarrhoea, with anaemia and low platelets.
• Minimal change nephrotic syndrome — Minimal change disease causes heavy proteinuria and oedema, but not haematuria and hypertension.

⭐ Key point: Management is supportive: fluid and salt restriction, diuretics, antihypertensives. Most children recover fully.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q117$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1554, $s$Which of the following is NOT an indication for URGENT dialysis in acute kidney injury?$s$, $s$["Creatinine 400 µmol/L with normal K⁺ and pH and no symptoms", "Uraemic pericarditis with a pericardial friction rub", "K⁺ of 7.0 mmol/L that does not respond to medical treatment", "Pulmonary oedema not responding to high-dose diuretics"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Creatinine 400 µmol/L with normal K⁺ and pH and no symptoms
URGENT DIALYSIS in AKI is indicated for complications that don't respond to medical treatment: "AEIOU": Acidosis (severe), Electrolytes (HYPERKALAEMIA resistant to treatment), Intoxication (dialysable toxins), Overload (PULMONARY OEDEMA resistant to diuretics), Uraemia (uraemic PERICARDITIS, encephalopathy, bleeding). A CREATININE of 400 µmol/L alone, with no complications or symptoms, is NOT an indication for URGENT dialysis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uraemic pericarditis with a pericardial friction rub — Uraemic pericarditis IS an urgent indication.
• K⁺ of 7.0 mmol/L that does not respond to medical treatment — Refractory hyperkalaemia IS an urgent indication.
• Pulmonary oedema not responding to high-dose diuretics — Refractory pulmonary oedema IS an urgent indication.

⭐ Key point: Dialysis decisions are based on the patient's clinical state, not a single creatinine value.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q120$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1555, $s$A 75-year-old with type 2 diabetes is drowsy and severely dehydrated. Glucose is 52 mmol/L, serum osmolality 360 mOsm/kg, pH 7.33, HCO₃⁻ 20 mmol/L and blood ketones 1.0 mmol/L. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state (HHS)", "Diabetic ketoacidosis", "Euglycaemic DKA", "Metformin-associated lactic acidosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state (HHS)
HYPEROSMOLAR HYPERGLYCAEMIC STATE (HHS) occurs mainly in older people with TYPE 2 DIABETES. Features: VERY HIGH GLUCOSE (52 mmol/L), VERY HIGH SERUM OSMOLALITY (above 320 — here 360), SEVERE DEHYDRATION, drowsiness, with NO significant ACIDOSIS (pH above 7.3, HCO₃⁻ above 15) and LOW KETONES (below 3).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diabetic ketoacidosis — DKA has significant ketones (above 3 mmol/L) and acidosis (pH below 7.3 or HCO₃⁻ below 15).
• Euglycaemic DKA — Euglycaemic DKA has acidosis and ketones with near-normal glucose.
• Metformin-associated lactic acidosis — Metformin-associated lactic acidosis causes high lactate and acidosis.

⭐ Key point: Treat HHS with careful fluid replacement (0.9% saline), aiming for a gradual fall in osmolality and sodium. Use low-dose insulin only when glucose stops falling with fluids alone. Give prophylactic anticoagulation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q121$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1556, $s$A patient on empagliflozin (an SGLT2 inhibitor) is admitted with vomiting after surgery. Glucose is 11 mmol/L, pH 7.10, HCO₃⁻ 9 mmol/L and blood ketones 5.2 mmol/L. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Alcoholic ketoacidosis", "Euglycaemic diabetic ketoacidosis", "Lactic acidosis", "Starvation ketosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Euglycaemic diabetic ketoacidosis
SGLT2 INHIBITORS (e.g. empagliflozin, dapagliflozin) lower glucose by making the kidneys pass glucose in the urine. They can cause EUGLYCAEMIC DKA — DKA with near-NORMAL GLUCOSE (here 11 mmol/L). Features: ACIDOSIS (pH 7.10, HCO₃⁻ 9) and HIGH KETONES (5.2). Triggers: surgery, vomiting, fasting, illness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Alcoholic ketoacidosis — Alcoholic ketoacidosis occurs in heavy drinkers.
• Lactic acidosis — Lactic acidosis shows high lactate, not high ketones.
• Starvation ketosis — Starvation ketosis causes mild ketosis without severe acidosis.

⭐ Key point: Stop SGLT2 inhibitors before surgery (usually 3 days before) and during acute illness. Treat with fluids, insulin AND glucose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q122$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1557, $s$A patient in DKA has an initial K⁺ of 3.0 mmol/L. The correct management is:$s$, $s$["Start the fixed-rate insulin at 0.1 units/kg/h straight away", "Give IV sodium bicarbonate first to correct the acidosis quickly", "Give fluids with potassium, and delay insulin until K⁺ is above 3.3", "Give a 10-unit IV insulin bolus, then start the insulin infusion"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Give fluids with potassium, and delay insulin until K⁺ is above 3.3
In DKA, insulin moves POTASSIUM INTO CELLS, lowering blood potassium. If insulin is started when potassium is already LOW (here 3.0), it could drop dangerously, causing fatal arrhythmias. So give IV FLUIDS WITH POTASSIUM and DELAY INSULIN until potassium is ABOVE 3.3 mmol/L.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Start the fixed-rate insulin at 0.1 units/kg/h straight away — Starting insulin immediately with low potassium is dangerous.
• Give IV sodium bicarbonate first to correct the acidosis quickly — Sodium bicarbonate also drives potassium into cells and is not routinely given.
• Give a 10-unit IV insulin bolus, then start the insulin infusion — An insulin bolus could cause a sudden fall in potassium.

⭐ Key point: Monitor potassium every 1–2 hours in DKA.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q123$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1558, $s$A patient with poorly controlled hyperthyroidism develops fever of 40.5 °C, AF at 160/min, agitation, diarrhoea and heart failure after an emergency appendicectomy. When should iodine (Lugol's iodine) be given?$s$, $s$["Never — iodine is contraindicated in thyroid storm", "At least 1 hour AFTER the antithyroid drug (propylthiouracil or carbimazole)", "At the same time as the antithyroid drug", "Before the antithyroid drug, to block hormone release quickly"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: At least 1 hour AFTER the antithyroid drug (propylthiouracil or carbimazole)
In THYROID STORM, antithyroid drugs (e.g. PROPYLTHIOURACIL or carbimazole) STOP NEW HORMONE PRODUCTION. IODINE (Lugol's iodine) BLOCKS HORMONE RELEASE. But iodine must be given AT LEAST 1 HOUR AFTER the antithyroid drug; otherwise, the thyroid gland can use the iodine as raw material to make MORE thyroid hormone, worsening the storm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Never — iodine is contraindicated in thyroid storm — Iodine is not contraindicated; it is part of treatment.
• At the same time as the antithyroid drug — Giving them together allows iodine to be used for hormone production.
• Before the antithyroid drug, to block hormone release quickly — Iodine before the antithyroid drug worsens the storm.

⭐ Key point: Thyroid storm treatment also includes beta-blockers (propranolol), steroids (hydrocortisone), cooling, and treatment of the trigger.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q124$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1559, $s$An elderly woman with untreated hypothyroidism is found hypothermic (32.5 °C), bradycardic and drowsy, with hypoventilation, hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia and puffy non-pitting oedema. Before IV thyroid hormone is given, the patient should receive:$s$, $s$["Strict fluid restriction to 500 mL/day", "An IV insulin infusion", "Rapid active external rewarming with a heating blanket", "IV hydrocortisone"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV hydrocortisone
MYXOEDEMA COMA is severe, life-threatening hypothyroidism: HYPOTHERMIA, BRADYCARDIA, DROWSINESS, HYPOVENTILATION, HYPONATRAEMIA, HYPOGLYCAEMIA, non-pitting oedema. Patients may also have ADRENAL INSUFFICIENCY. Giving thyroid hormone first can increase metabolism and use up the remaining cortisol, causing an ADRENAL CRISIS. So give IV HYDROCORTISONE BEFORE IV thyroid hormone.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Strict fluid restriction to 500 mL/day — Fluid restriction may be needed for hyponatraemia, but hydrocortisone is the priority before thyroid hormone.
• An IV insulin infusion — Insulin would worsen hypoglycaemia.
• Rapid active external rewarming with a heating blanket — Rapid external rewarming causes vasodilation and collapse; use slow passive rewarming.

⭐ Key point: Also support breathing (may need ventilation), correct glucose and sodium carefully, and look for a trigger (e.g. infection).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q125$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1560, $s$A patient who stopped long-term prednisolone suddenly 2 weeks ago presents with vomiting, abdominal pain and hypotension unresponsive to 2 L of fluid. Na⁺ is 125, K⁺ 6.0 and glucose 2.8 mmol/L. The diagnosis and immediate treatment are:$s$, $s$["SIADH — strict fluid restriction to 800 mL/day", "Myxoedema crisis — IV levothyroxine straight away", "Septic shock — antibiotics and noradrenaline only", "Adrenal crisis — IV hydrocortisone 100 mg now"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Adrenal crisis — IV hydrocortisone 100 mg now
Long-term steroid use (prednisolone) suppresses the adrenal glands. STOPPING SUDDENLY causes ADRENAL CRISIS: vomiting, abdominal pain, HYPOTENSION resistant to fluids, HYPONATRAEMIA (125), HYPERKALAEMIA (6.0), and HYPOGLYCAEMIA (2.8). Immediate treatment: IV HYDROCORTISONE 100 mg, plus IV fluids and glucose.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• SIADH — strict fluid restriction to 800 mL/day — SIADH doesn't cause hyperkalaemia, hypoglycaemia and shock.
• Myxoedema crisis — IV levothyroxine straight away — Myxoedema crisis causes hypothermia and bradycardia.
• Septic shock — antibiotics and noradrenaline only — Septic shock is possible, but the steroid withdrawal history and electrolytes point to adrenal crisis.

⭐ Key point: Never stop long-term steroids suddenly. Teach patients "sick day rules" (double the dose during illness).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q126$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1561, $s$A man with liver cirrhosis, ascites and splenomegaly vomits large amounts of fresh blood. Apart from resuscitation and urgent endoscopy with band ligation, which drug therapy has been shown to reduce mortality?$s$, $s$["Terlipressin and prophylactic IV antibiotics", "A PPI infusion and IV tranexamic acid", "Routine fresh frozen plasma and vitamin K", "IV propranolol during the bleed"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Terlipressin and prophylactic IV antibiotics
In VARICEAL BLEEDING (bleeding from veins in the oesophagus due to cirrhosis), TERLIPRESSIN reduces blood flow and pressure in the portal system, helping stop bleeding and reducing mortality. PROPHYLACTIC IV ANTIBIOTICS (e.g. ceftriaxone) prevent infections (common in cirrhosis with GI bleeding) and also reduce mortality and rebleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A PPI infusion and IV tranexamic acid — PPIs and tranexamic acid aren't proven to reduce mortality in variceal bleeding.
• Routine fresh frozen plasma and vitamin K — FFP and vitamin K aren't routine and don't reduce mortality.
• IV propranolol during the bleed — Beta-blockers are used to PREVENT bleeding, not during an acute bleed (they lower BP).

⭐ Key point: Urgent endoscopy with BAND LIGATION is the main treatment. Use a Sengstaken-Blakemore tube as a temporary measure if bleeding can't be controlled.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q128$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1562, $s$A patient taking regular NSAIDs has sudden severe epigastric pain that becomes generalised. The abdomen is rigid and 'board-like', he lies completely still, and an erect chest X-ray shows air under the diaphragm. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Acute cholecystitis", "Acute mesenteric ischaemia", "Perforated peptic ulcer", "Acute pancreatitis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Perforated peptic ulcer
A PERFORATED PEPTIC ULCER (often due to NSAIDs) releases stomach contents into the abdomen, causing chemical PERITONITIS. Features: SUDDEN SEVERE EPIGASTRIC PAIN that becomes GENERALISED, a RIGID "BOARD-LIKE" ABDOMEN, the patient LIES STILL (movement hurts), and an ERECT CHEST X-RAY shows AIR UNDER THE DIAPHRAGM.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute cholecystitis — Acute cholecystitis causes right upper quadrant pain, no free air.
• Acute mesenteric ischaemia — Mesenteric ischaemia causes pain out of proportion to signs.
• Acute pancreatitis — Pancreatitis causes epigastric pain radiating to the back, no free air.

⭐ Key point: Treatment: nil by mouth, IV fluids, analgesia, antibiotics, PPI, and usually surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q130$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1563, $s$A heavy drinker has severe epigastric pain radiating to the back, relieved by leaning forward, with vomiting. There is bruising in the flanks and around the umbilicus, and serum lipase is 8 times normal. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm", "Acute mesenteric ischaemia", "Perforated peptic ulcer", "Severe (haemorrhagic) acute pancreatitis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Severe (haemorrhagic) acute pancreatitis
SEVERE (HAEMORRHAGIC) ACUTE PANCREATITIS often affects heavy drinkers. Features: SEVERE EPIGASTRIC PAIN radiating to the BACK, RELIEVED by LEANING FORWARD, VOMITING, BRUISING in the FLANKS (Grey Turner's sign) and around the UMBILICUS (Cullen's sign) — signs of bleeding in the retroperitoneum — and SERUM LIPASE more than 3 times normal (here 8 times).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ruptured abdominal aortic aneurysm — A ruptured AAA causes back pain and shock, with a pulsatile mass, without raised lipase.
• Acute mesenteric ischaemia — Mesenteric ischaemia causes pain out of proportion to signs.
• Perforated peptic ulcer — Perforated ulcer causes a rigid abdomen and free air.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV fluids, analgesia, nutrition, and monitoring for organ failure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q131$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1564, $s$A patient with gallstones has fever with rigors, jaundice and right upper quadrant pain, and is now confused with BP 80/50 mmHg. The diagnosis and definitive treatment are:$s$, $s$["Acute viral hepatitis — supportive care", "Acute pancreatitis — fluids only", "Acute cholecystitis — antibiotics and elective cholecystectomy in 6 weeks", "Acute suppurative (ascending) cholangitis — antibiotics plus urgent biliary drainage (ERCP)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute suppurative (ascending) cholangitis — antibiotics plus urgent biliary drainage (ERCP)
ACUTE ASCENDING CHOLANGITIS is a bacterial infection of a BLOCKED bile duct (often by a gallstone). CHARCOT'S TRIAD: FEVER with rigors, JAUNDICE, and RIGHT UPPER QUADRANT PAIN. REYNOLDS' PENTAD adds CONFUSION and HYPOTENSION (here, BP 80/50) — signs of severe, suppurative cholangitis with septic shock. Treatment: IV ANTIBIOTICS plus URGENT BILIARY DRAINAGE (usually ERCP) to remove the blockage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute viral hepatitis — supportive care — Viral hepatitis causes jaundice but not usually rigors and shock.
• Acute pancreatitis — fluids only — Pancreatitis causes epigastric pain radiating to the back.
• Acute cholecystitis — antibiotics and elective cholecystectomy in 6 weeks — Acute cholecystitis does not usually cause jaundice and shock.

⭐ Key point: Cholangitis with shock is an emergency — drainage should be done within 24 hours (urgently if unstable).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q133$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1565, $s$A patient with cirrhosis who had a GI bleed yesterday is now confused and drowsy, with a flapping tremor of the outstretched hands and a sweet musty breath odour. The correct management includes:$s$, $s$["A protein-free diet for one week to stop ammonia production", "High-dose diuretics to clear the ascites and improve the confusion", "A benzodiazepine to settle the agitation and allow nursing care", "Lactulose titrated to 2–3 soft stools a day, and treat the cause"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lactulose titrated to 2–3 soft stools a day, and treat the cause
HEPATIC ENCEPHALOPATHY occurs when the liver cannot clear toxins (especially AMMONIA). GI BLEEDING is a common trigger (blood is a protein load in the gut). Features: CONFUSION, DROWSINESS, FLAPPING TREMOR (asterixis), and sweet musty breath (FETOR HEPATICUS). Management: LACTULOSE (titrated to 2–3 soft stools a day — it reduces ammonia absorption) and TREATING THE CAUSE (e.g. GI bleed, infection, constipation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A protein-free diet for one week to stop ammonia production — Protein-free diets are no longer recommended; normal protein intake is advised.
• High-dose diuretics to clear the ascites and improve the confusion — High-dose diuretics can worsen encephalopathy.
• A benzodiazepine to settle the agitation and allow nursing care — Benzodiazepines worsen encephalopathy.

⭐ Key point: Rifaximin (an antibiotic) can be added to lactulose to reduce recurrence.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q134$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1566, $s$A patient with cirrhosis and ascites develops fever, mild abdominal tenderness and worsening confusion. A diagnostic paracentesis shows ascitic neutrophils 400 cells/mm³ with a single organism. The diagnosis and treatment are:$s$, $s$["Secondary peritonitis from bowel perforation — emergency laparotomy", "Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis — IV cefotaxime (or similar) plus IV albumin", "Tuberculous peritonitis — anti-TB therapy", "Hepatorenal syndrome — terlipressin"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Spontaneous bacterial peritonitis — IV cefotaxime (or similar) plus IV albumin
SPONTANEOUS BACTERIAL PERITONITIS (SBP) is infection of the ascitic fluid without an obvious surgical cause. It is diagnosed by an ASCITIC NEUTROPHIL COUNT of 250 cells/mm³ or MORE (here 400), often with a SINGLE ORGANISM on culture. Treatment: IV CEFOTAXIME (or another third-generation cephalosporin) PLUS IV ALBUMIN (reduces the risk of kidney failure and death).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Secondary peritonitis from bowel perforation — emergency laparotomy — Secondary peritonitis from perforation usually shows MULTIPLE organisms.
• Tuberculous peritonitis — anti-TB therapy — TB peritonitis shows lymphocytes, not neutrophils.
• Hepatorenal syndrome — terlipressin — Hepatorenal syndrome causes kidney failure, not infected ascites.

⭐ Key point: Consider antibiotic prophylaxis after an episode of SBP (e.g. ciprofloxacin or co-trimoxazole).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q135$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1567, $s$A patient with cirrhosis and tense ascites has creatinine rising over 3 days, urine output below 400 mL/day and urine Na⁺ below 10 mmol/L. There is no shock, no nephrotoxic drug use, normal kidneys on ultrasound, and no improvement after 2 days of stopping diuretics and giving IV albumin. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Obstructive (post-renal) AKI", "Acute tubular necrosis", "Hepatorenal syndrome (HRS-AKI)", "Pre-renal AKI from diuretics"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hepatorenal syndrome (HRS-AKI)
HEPATORENAL SYNDROME (HRS) is kidney failure in patients with severe liver disease, caused by severe constriction of kidney blood vessels. Diagnosis: rising creatinine, LOW URINE OUTPUT, VERY LOW URINE SODIUM (below 10), NO SHOCK, NO NEPHROTOXIC DRUGS, NORMAL KIDNEYS on ultrasound, and NO IMPROVEMENT after stopping diuretics and giving IV ALBUMIN for 2 days.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Obstructive (post-renal) AKI — Obstructive AKI would show hydronephrosis on ultrasound.
• Acute tubular necrosis — ATN would show high urine sodium and casts.
• Pre-renal AKI from diuretics — Pre-renal AKI from diuretics would improve with albumin and stopping diuretics.

⭐ Key point: Treat with terlipressin plus albumin. Liver transplant is the definitive treatment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q136$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1568, $s$After massive fluid resuscitation for trauma, a ventilated patient has a tense, distended abdomen, rising peak airway pressures and oliguria. Bladder pressure is 25 mmHg. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Paralytic ileus with normal abdominal pressure", "Intra-abdominal hypertension grade I", "Acute urinary retention", "Abdominal compartment syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Abdominal compartment syndrome
ABDOMINAL COMPARTMENT SYNDROME (ACS) is defined as INTRA-ABDOMINAL PRESSURE ABOVE 20 mmHg (here 25) with NEW ORGAN DYSFUNCTION. Massive fluid resuscitation causes bowel swelling. Effects: TENSE DISTENDED ABDOMEN, RISING PEAK AIRWAY PRESSURES (diaphragm pushed up), and OLIGURIA (kidneys compressed).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Paralytic ileus with normal abdominal pressure — Paralytic ileus doesn't cause this level of pressure or organ failure.
• Intra-abdominal hypertension grade I — Grade I IAH is 12–15 mmHg without organ failure.
• Acute urinary retention — Acute urinary retention would be relieved by catheterisation.

⭐ Key point: Treat with decompression (sedation, NG tube, reduce fluids, and decompressive laparotomy if organ failure persists).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q138$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1569, $s$A patient with known ulcerative colitis has fever, a pulse of 120, a distended tender abdomen, reduced bowel sounds and a transverse colon diameter of 8 cm on X-ray. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Small bowel obstruction", "Toxic megacolon", "Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie's syndrome)", "Sigmoid volvulus"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Toxic megacolon
TOXIC MEGACOLON is a severe complication of inflammatory bowel disease (e.g. ULCERATIVE COLITIS) or infection (e.g. C. difficile). The colon becomes DILATED (TRANSVERSE COLON more than 6 cm — here 8 cm) and the patient is SYSTEMICALLY UNWELL: FEVER, TACHYCARDIA (120), DISTENDED TENDER ABDOMEN, and REDUCED BOWEL SOUNDS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Small bowel obstruction — Small bowel obstruction shows dilated small bowel loops.
• Acute colonic pseudo-obstruction (Ogilvie's syndrome) — Ogilvie's syndrome causes colonic dilation without toxicity.
• Sigmoid volvulus — Sigmoid volvulus shows a "coffee bean" sign on X-ray.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV steroids, IV fluids, antibiotics, nil by mouth, and surgery if there is no improvement or perforation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q139$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1570, $s$A patient who took an overdose of gliclazide (a sulfonylurea) keeps becoming hypoglycaemic despite repeated IV dextrose boluses. The most appropriate additional treatment is:$s$, $s$["IV hydrocortisone", "Octreotide", "IV flumazenil", "IV glucagon infusion as the long-term solution"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Octreotide
SULFONYLUREAS (e.g. gliclazide) make the pancreas release INSULIN. In overdose, repeated IV dextrose triggers MORE insulin release, causing recurrent HYPOGLYCAEMIA. OCTREOTIDE (a somatostatin analogue) BLOCKS INSULIN RELEASE from the pancreas, preventing recurrent hypoglycaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV hydrocortisone — Hydrocortisone is not the treatment of choice.
• IV flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines.
• IV glucagon infusion as the long-term solution — Glucagon may help briefly, but it also stimulates insulin release and is not a long-term solution.

⭐ Key point: Monitor glucose hourly for at least 24 hours — sulfonylureas have a long duration of action.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q140$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1571, $s$At 36 weeks a woman has 1 week of nausea, vomiting and abdominal pain, then becomes jaundiced and confused. Glucose is 2.1 mmol/L, INR 2.5, ammonia and uric acid are raised, and BP is only mildly elevated. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Acute fatty liver of pregnancy", "Acute viral hepatitis", "Hyperemesis gravidarum", "HELLP syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute fatty liver of pregnancy
ACUTE FATTY LIVER OF PREGNANCY (AFLP) is a rare but serious condition in the third trimester, where fat builds up in liver cells, causing LIVER FAILURE. Features: NAUSEA, VOMITING, ABDOMINAL PAIN, then JAUNDICE and CONFUSION. Lab findings: LOW GLUCOSE (2.1 — the liver can't make glucose), PROLONGED CLOTTING (INR 2.5), RAISED AMMONIA and URIC ACID. BP may be only mildly elevated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute viral hepatitis — Viral hepatitis would show very high transaminases and positive viral serology.
• Hyperemesis gravidarum — Hyperemesis gravidarum occurs in early pregnancy.
• HELLP syndrome — HELLP has low platelets and haemolysis, usually without low glucose and high INR.

⭐ Key point: Treat with urgent DELIVERY, glucose infusion, correction of clotting, and liver support in ICU.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q162$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1572, $s$In FAST, the hepatorenal space (Morison's pouch) is examined because:$s$, $s$["It shows a pneumothorax more clearly than any other view", "It is the most dependent space where fluid collects when supine", "It is the window used to measure the bladder volume", "It gives the clearest view of the heart and pericardium"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It is the most dependent space where fluid collects when supine
MORISON'S POUCH (the space between the liver and the right kidney) is the most DEPENDENT (lowest) part of the upper abdomen when the patient lies SUPINE. Free fluid (blood) collects here first, so it is the most sensitive view in FAST.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It shows a pneumothorax more clearly than any other view — Pneumothorax is assessed by looking at lung sliding, not Morison's pouch.
• It is the window used to measure the bladder volume — Bladder volume is assessed in the pelvic view.
• It gives the clearest view of the heart and pericardium — The heart is assessed in the subxiphoid (pericardial) view.

⭐ Key point: The four FAST views: right upper quadrant (Morison's), left upper quadrant (splenorenal), pelvic, and pericardial.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q34$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1573, $s$Before concluding a patient is oliguric from acute kidney injury, the ICU nurse should:$s$, $s$["Bladder-scan or flush the catheter to exclude obstruction", "Restrict all IV fluids immediately to avoid overload", "Give furosemide 80 mg IV to test the kidneys", "Start preparing the patient for urgent dialysis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bladder-scan or flush the catheter to exclude obstruction
Low urine output in a catheterised patient may be due to a BLOCKED catheter rather than kidney failure. The nurse should first BLADDER-SCAN (to see if urine is trapped) or FLUSH THE CATHETER (per protocol) to EXCLUDE OBSTRUCTION. This avoids unnecessary treatment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Restrict all IV fluids immediately to avoid overload — Restricting fluids without assessment may worsen pre-renal AKI if the patient is dehydrated.
• Give furosemide 80 mg IV to test the kidneys — Giving furosemide without assessment may worsen dehydration and does not treat blockage.
• Start preparing the patient for urgent dialysis — Dialysis is far too premature without excluding simple causes.

⭐ Key point: Causes of oliguria: pre-renal (low blood volume), renal (kidney damage), post-renal (obstruction, including a blocked catheter).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q36$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1574, $s$The following is a secondary abdominal compartment syndrome.$s$, $s$["Ascites", "Septic shock", "Pancreatitis", "Trauma"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Septic shock
Abdominal compartment syndrome (ACS) is divided by its cause. PRIMARY ACS comes from a problem INSIDE the abdomen or pelvis (e.g. abdominal trauma, bleeding, pancreatitis, bowel obstruction, abdominal surgery). SECONDARY ACS comes from a problem OUTSIDE the abdomen. In SEPTIC SHOCK, the patient receives very large volumes of IV fluid, and inflammation makes the capillaries leaky ("capillary leak"). Fluid moves into the bowel wall and tissues, the gut swells, and pressure inside the abdomen rises — even though the original disease was not in the abdomen. Severe burns and massive transfusion are other causes of secondary ACS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ascites — Ascites (fluid in the abdomen from liver disease or abdominal disease) is an abdominal cause, so it leads to PRIMARY ACS when it raises the pressure.
• Pancreatitis — Pancreatitis is an inflammatory disease of an abdominal organ, so it causes PRIMARY ACS.
• Trauma — Abdominal trauma (bleeding, swollen injured bowel, packing) is the classic cause of PRIMARY ACS.

⭐ Key point: Primary = abdominal cause; Secondary = non-abdominal cause (sepsis, burns, massive fluid); Recurrent = ACS returns after earlier treatment. Measure bladder pressure in any patient who has had massive fluid resuscitation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q62$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1575, $s$Organ effects of abdominal compartment syndrome include:$s$, $s$["Hypertension with increased venous return", "Improved lung compliance and diuresis", "Polyuria, low airway pressures, high cardiac output", "Oliguria, high airway pressures, low cardiac output"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Oliguria, high airway pressures, low cardiac output
In ACS, high pressure in the abdomen squeezes the organs: KIDNEYS (compressed renal veins and arteries → OLIGURIA); LUNGS (the diaphragm is pushed up → HIGH AIRWAY PRESSURES, low lung compliance and hypoxia); HEART (compressed inferior vena cava → reduced venous return → LOW CARDIAC OUTPUT).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension with increased venous return — ACS reduces venous return and causes hypotension.
• Improved lung compliance and diuresis — ACS worsens lung compliance and causes oliguria.
• Polyuria, low airway pressures, high cardiac output — ACS causes oliguria (not polyuria), high (not low) airway pressures, and low (not high) cardiac output.

⭐ Key point: Monitor IAP in at-risk patients (massive fluids, abdominal surgery, trauma, pancreatitis).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q65$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1576, $s$Definitive treatment of abdominal compartment syndrome with organ failure is:$s$, $s$["Surgical decompressive laparotomy", "Oral laxatives and enemas", "High-dose diuretics alone", "Increasing PEEP to splint the abdomen"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Surgical decompressive laparotomy
The DEFINITIVE treatment of ACS with organ failure is SURGICAL DECOMPRESSIVE LAPAROTOMY: opening the abdomen to relieve pressure. The abdomen is often left open (with a temporary closure) until swelling settles.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral laxatives and enemas — Laxatives and enemas may help mild IAH but are not definitive treatment of ACS.
• High-dose diuretics alone — Diuretics may help in fluid overload but are not definitive.
• Increasing PEEP to splint the abdomen — Increasing PEEP worsens ACS.

⭐ Key point: Non-surgical measures (sedation, NG decompression, fluid removal) may be tried first, but surgery is needed if organs are failing.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q66$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1577, $s$A disadvantage of prolonged acid suppression for stress ulcer prophylaxis is an increased risk of:$s$, $s$["Ventilator-associated pneumonia and C. difficile", "Upper GI bleeding from rebound acid", "Peptic ulcer perforation and peritonitis", "Hypercalcaemia and kidney stones"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ventilator-associated pneumonia and C. difficile
Stomach acid kills bacteria. Prolonged ACID SUPPRESSION allows bacteria to grow in the stomach, which can travel up and be aspirated into the lungs, increasing VENTILATOR-ASSOCIATED PNEUMONIA (VAP). It also increases CLOSTRIDIOIDES DIFFICILE infection (diarrhoea).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Upper GI bleeding from rebound acid — Acid suppression does not cause upper GI bleeding; it prevents it.
• Peptic ulcer perforation and peritonitis — Acid suppression doesn't cause perforation.
• Hypercalcaemia and kidney stones — Acid suppression doesn't cause hypercalcaemia or kidney stones.

⭐ Key point: Use prophylaxis only when indicated, and review it daily.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q68$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1578, $s$Ranson's criteria on admission for acute (non-gallstone) pancreatitis include:$s$, $s$["Serum sodium, potassium and chloride with urine output", "Haemoglobin, platelet count and serum bilirubin at admission", "Age over 55, WBC over 16 000, glucose over 11, raised LDH and AST", "Serum amylase over 3 times normal and a raised serum lipase"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Age over 55, WBC over 16 000, glucose over 11, raised LDH and AST
RANSON'S CRITERIA assess the severity of pancreatitis. On ADMISSION (non-gallstone): AGE over 55; WBC over 16,000; GLUCOSE over 11 mmol/L (200 mg/dL); LDH over 350 IU/L; AST over 250 IU/L. Further criteria are assessed at 48 hours (e.g. calcium, PaO2, fluid sequestration). 3 or more indicates severe pancreatitis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Serum sodium, potassium and chloride with urine output — Electrolytes and urine output are not part of the admission criteria.
• Haemoglobin, platelet count and serum bilirubin at admission — Haemoglobin, platelets and bilirubin are not part of the admission criteria.
• Serum amylase over 3 times normal and a raised serum lipase — Amylase and lipase diagnose pancreatitis but are not part of Ranson's severity criteria.

⭐ Key point: Other scoring systems: Glasgow-Imrie, APACHE II and BISAP.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q70$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1579, $s$Grey Turner's sign in severe pancreatitis is:$s$, $s$["A palpable gallbladder", "Bruising of the flanks", "Bruising around the umbilicus", "Yellowing of the sclera"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bruising of the flanks
GREY TURNER'S SIGN is a bluish-purple BRUISING over the FLANKS (the sides of the abdomen, between the ribs and the hip). In severe (haemorrhagic or necrotising) pancreatitis, the inflamed pancreas bleeds, and the blood tracks backwards through the tissue behind the abdomen (the retroperitoneum) to reach the flanks. It usually appears 24–48 hours after onset and indicates SEVERE disease with a higher risk of death. It can also be seen in other causes of retroperitoneal bleeding (e.g. ruptured aortic aneurysm, ruptured ectopic pregnancy).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A palpable gallbladder — A palpable, non-tender gallbladder with jaundice is COURVOISIER'S sign, suggesting cancer of the pancreatic head or bile duct obstruction, not Grey Turner's sign.
• Bruising around the umbilicus — Bruising around the UMBILICUS is CULLEN'S sign. It also indicates retroperitoneal or intra-abdominal bleeding, but at a different place.
• Yellowing of the sclera — Yellow sclera is JAUNDICE, which may occur in gallstone pancreatitis, but is not Grey Turner's sign.

⭐ Key point: Memory aid: Grey TURNer = TURN the patient to see the flanks; CULLEN = belly button (umbilicus). Both are late signs of severe disease.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q71$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1580, $s$The hallmark early sign of necrotising fasciitis is:$s$, $s$["Itching with small blisters only", "A localised abscess that is fluctuant", "Pain out of proportion to the skin changes", "A painless, slowly spreading red rash"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pain out of proportion to the skin changes
NECROTISING FASCIITIS is a rapidly spreading infection of the deep tissues. The hallmark early sign is PAIN OUT OF PROPORTION to the skin appearance — the skin may look only slightly red, but the pain is severe. Later signs: swelling, blisters, skin discoloration, crepitus and septic shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Itching with small blisters only — Itching with small blisters is not typical.
• A localised abscess that is fluctuant — Necrotising fasciitis spreads along tissue planes; it's not a localised fluctuant abscess.
• A painless, slowly spreading red rash — It is extremely PAINFUL and spreads rapidly.

⭐ Key point: Mark the edge of redness and monitor its spread hourly. The LRINEC score can help with diagnosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q74$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1581, $s$Type II necrotising fasciitis is caused by:$s$, $s$["Mixed anaerobic and aerobic gut organisms", "Vibrio vulnificus from contact with seawater", "Candida species after broad-spectrum antibiotics", "Group A Streptococcus, often with S. aureus"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Group A Streptococcus, often with S. aureus
TYPE II necrotising fasciitis is caused by GROUP A STREPTOCOCCUS (Streptococcus pyogenes), alone or with STAPHYLOCOCCUS AUREUS. It often affects the limbs of healthy people, and can cause toxic shock syndrome.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mixed anaerobic and aerobic gut organisms — Mixed anaerobic and aerobic gut organisms cause TYPE I (polymicrobial).
• Vibrio vulnificus from contact with seawater — Vibrio vulnificus causes TYPE III (marine).
• Candida species after broad-spectrum antibiotics — Candida is a fungal cause (sometimes Type IV), not Type II.

⭐ Key point: Type I is the commonest, often in diabetics or after surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q75$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1582, $s$After transurethral resection of the prostate, continuous bladder irrigation output appears bright red with clots. The ICU nurse should:$s$, $s$["Increase irrigation, check the catheter and inform the surgeon", "Clamp the catheter so that the clots tamponade the bleeding", "Stop the irrigation completely until the bleeding settles", "Remove the catheter and reinsert a smaller one urgently"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increase irrigation, check the catheter and inform the surgeon
After TURP, continuous bladder irrigation (CBI) keeps the bladder clear of clots. BRIGHT RED drainage with CLOTS suggests active (often arterial) bleeding. The nurse should INCREASE THE IRRIGATION RATE (to flush clots and prevent blockage), CHECK THE CATHETER is draining (not blocked), monitor vital signs, and INFORM THE SURGEON.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Clamp the catheter so that the clots tamponade the bleeding — Clamping the catheter causes bladder distension and possible rupture.
• Stop the irrigation completely until the bleeding settles — Stopping irrigation lets clots form and block the catheter.
• Remove the catheter and reinsert a smaller one urgently — Removing the catheter is dangerous; it may be difficult to reinsert.

⭐ Key point: The drainage should be light pink ("rosé"). Dark red with clots means bleeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q77$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1583, $s$After relief of chronic urinary tract obstruction, the ICU nurse should anticipate:$s$, $s$["Fluid overload, so IV fluids must be restricted at once", "Post-obstructive diuresis with hypovolaemia and electrolyte loss", "Hyperkalaemia from potassium retention by the damaged kidneys", "Persistent anuria for several days after the catheter is passed"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Post-obstructive diuresis with hypovolaemia and electrolyte loss
After relief of CHRONIC URINARY OBSTRUCTION (e.g. a long-standing enlarged prostate), the kidneys may produce very large volumes of urine (POST-OBSTRUCTIVE DIURESIS). This is due to excess fluid and urea being cleared, and the kidneys temporarily being unable to concentrate urine. This can cause HYPOVOLAEMIA and ELECTROLYTE LOSS (sodium, potassium).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fluid overload, so IV fluids must be restricted at once — Patients may become dehydrated; fluids may need to be given, not restricted.
• Hyperkalaemia from potassium retention by the damaged kidneys — Potassium is often lost, not retained.
• Persistent anuria for several days after the catheter is passed — Urine output usually increases, not stops.

⭐ Key point: Monitor urine output hourly, and replace fluids (often matching a proportion of output).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q79$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1584, $s$The SOFA score assesses dysfunction in:$s$, $s$["Nutritional status, using albumin and weight", "Only the brain, using the GCS and the pupils", "Only the lungs and kidneys, as in ARDS with AKI", "Six organs: lung, heart, liver, clotting, kidney, CNS"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Six organs: lung, heart, liver, clotting, kidney, CNS
The SOFA score (Sequential Organ Failure Assessment) measures dysfunction in SIX ORGAN SYSTEMS: LUNGS (PaO2/FiO2), HEART/CARDIOVASCULAR (MAP and vasopressors), LIVER (bilirubin), CLOTTING (platelets), KIDNEYS (creatinine or urine output), and CNS (GCS). Each is scored 0–4. A rise of 2 or more points from baseline with infection defines SEPSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nutritional status, using albumin and weight — SOFA doesn't assess nutrition.
• Only the brain, using the GCS and the pupils — SOFA assesses six organs, not only the brain.
• Only the lungs and kidneys, as in ARDS with AKI — SOFA assesses six organs, not only lungs and kidneys.

⭐ Key point: qSOFA (RR ≥22, altered mental state, SBP ≤100) is a quick bedside screen.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q171$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1585, $s$A patient on CRRT (dialysis) in ICU needs:$s$, $s$["More protein, as amino acids are lost in the effluent", "Potassium supplements added to every feed", "Very low protein to reduce the urea production", "No nutrition at all until dialysis has stopped"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: More protein, as amino acids are lost in the effluent
CONTINUOUS RENAL REPLACEMENT THERAPY (CRRT) filters the blood continuously. Small molecules, including AMINO ACIDS (the building blocks of protein), pass through the filter and are LOST in the effluent (waste fluid) — about 10–15 g of amino acids per day. Critically ill patients are already breaking down muscle rapidly. So patients on CRRT need MORE PROTEIN — about 1.5 g/kg/day, and up to 2.5 g/kg/day — to prevent muscle wasting and support healing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Potassium supplements added to every feed — Potassium is often removed by CRRT, but replacement depends on blood levels; it is not added routinely to every feed.
• Very low protein to reduce the urea production — Very low protein is not recommended; urea is controlled by the CRRT, so protein does not need restriction.
• No nutrition at all until dialysis has stopped — Nutrition should not be withheld; malnutrition increases complications and mortality.

⭐ Key point: CRRT also removes water-soluble vitamins (e.g. vitamin C, thiamine, folate) and trace elements (e.g. selenium, zinc), which may need supplementing. Monitor phosphate and magnesium, which can fall during CRRT.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q187$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1586, $s$After renal transplant, a sudden fall in urine output with graft tenderness and fever suggests:$s$, $s$["Over-hydration from excess IV fluids", "Hypoglycaemia caused by the steroids", "Acute rejection or graft vessel thrombosis", "A normal post-operative recovery pattern"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute rejection or graft vessel thrombosis
After a kidney transplant, a SUDDEN FALL IN URINE OUTPUT with GRAFT TENDERNESS (pain over the new kidney, usually in the iliac fossa) and FEVER suggests: ACUTE REJECTION — the recipient's immune system attacks the new kidney, causing inflammation, swelling and failure of function; or GRAFT VESSEL THROMBOSIS — a clot blocks the artery or vein of the new kidney, causing sudden loss of function (often within the first days). Both are emergencies that can cause loss of the graft.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Over-hydration from excess IV fluids — Over-hydration would cause oedema, high BP and possibly high urine output, not tenderness and fever.
• Hypoglycaemia caused by the steroids — Steroids cause HYPERglycaemia, not hypoglycaemia.
• A normal post-operative recovery pattern — A sudden fall in urine output, pain and fever are not part of normal recovery; they must be urgently reported.

⭐ Key point: Investigate urgently: Doppler ultrasound of the graft, serum creatinine, drug levels (tacrolimus), and possibly a biopsy. Also check the catheter for blockage.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q194$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1587, $s$A renal transplant recipient on tacrolimus is at particular risk of:$s$, $s$["Weight loss and persistent hypotension", "Infection, nephrotoxicity and hyperglycaemia", "Increased antibody production and allergy", "Hypercoagulability from a strong immune system"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Infection, nephrotoxicity and hyperglycaemia
TACROLIMUS is a calcineurin inhibitor that suppresses the immune system to PREVENT REJECTION of a transplanted kidney. Its main side effects: INFECTION — the weakened immune system increases the risk of bacterial, viral (e.g. CMV), and fungal infections; NEPHROTOXICITY — it constricts the kidney's blood vessels and can damage the transplanted kidney itself; HYPERGLYCAEMIA — it reduces insulin release, causing new-onset diabetes after transplant. Other effects: tremor, hypertension, high potassium, low magnesium and headache.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Weight loss and persistent hypotension — Tacrolimus tends to cause HYPERtension and weight gain, not persistent hypotension and weight loss.
• Increased antibody production and allergy — Tacrolimus REDUCES immune activity, including antibody production; it does not increase allergy.
• Hypercoagulability from a strong immune system — It suppresses the immune system, it does not strengthen it.

⭐ Key point: Monitor tacrolimus trough levels, kidney function, glucose, potassium and magnesium. Many drugs interact with it (e.g. some antifungals, antibiotics and anticonvulsants), so check before giving new medicines.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q195$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1588, $s$Among renal transplant types, the best expected outcome is generally seen with:$s$, $s$["Cadaveric donation", "Living-related donation", "Living non-related donation", "All types have identical outcomes"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Living-related donation
LIVING-RELATED donation (from a family member such as a parent or sibling) usually gives the best kidney transplant outcomes. The genetic match is closer (fewer rejection episodes), the kidney is healthy and taken from a healthy donor, the operation can be planned, and the kidney spends little time outside the body.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cadaveric donation — Cadaveric (deceased donor) kidneys have longer storage time and more delayed function.
• Living non-related donation — Living unrelated donors do well, but usually have a poorer genetic match than related donors.
• All types have identical outcomes — Outcomes differ between donor types.

⭐ Key point: All transplant recipients need lifelong immunosuppression and monitoring for rejection and infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q7$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1589, $s$The standard triple immunosuppression regimen after renal transplant includes:$s$, $s$["Aspirin + clopidogrel + warfarin to prevent graft vessel thrombosis", "Prednisolone alone in high dose for the first year after surgery", "Tacrolimus or ciclosporin + mycophenolate or azathioprine + prednisolone", "Insulin + metformin + glipizide to control the post-transplant glucose"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tacrolimus or ciclosporin + mycophenolate or azathioprine + prednisolone
Standard TRIPLE immunosuppression after kidney transplant: 1) a CALCINEURIN INHIBITOR (tacrolimus or ciclosporin), which blocks T-cell activation; 2) an ANTI-PROLIFERATIVE drug (mycophenolate or azathioprine), which stops immune cells multiplying; 3) a STEROID (prednisolone), which broadly reduces inflammation. Using three drugs at lower doses gives good protection with fewer side effects.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Aspirin + clopidogrel + warfarin to prevent graft vessel thrombosis — Aspirin, clopidogrel and warfarin are anti-clotting drugs, not immunosuppressants.
• Prednisolone alone in high dose for the first year after surgery — High-dose prednisolone alone causes severe side effects and isn't enough.
• Insulin + metformin + glipizide to control the post-transplant glucose — Insulin, metformin and glipizide are diabetes drugs.

⭐ Key point: Watch for infection, high BP, high glucose, kidney toxicity (tacrolimus), and drug interactions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q8$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1590, $s$Which of the following is an absolute contraindication to renal transplant?$s$, $s$["Well-controlled hypertension", "Mild anaemia", "Active uncontrolled infection", "Age over 60 with good health"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Active uncontrolled infection
Transplant requires strong immunosuppression, which would make an ACTIVE, UNCONTROLLED INFECTION spread and become life-threatening. It is an absolute contraindication until the infection is treated. Other absolute contraindications include active cancer and very short life expectancy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Well-controlled hypertension — Well-controlled hypertension isn't a contraindication.
• Mild anaemia — Mild anaemia isn't a contraindication.
• Age over 60 with good health — Age over 60 in good health isn't a contraindication.

⭐ Key point: Screen transplant candidates for infections (TB, hepatitis B and C, HIV) and cancer before surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q9$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1591, $s$In the first 24 hours after renal transplant, urine output of 600 mL/hour should be:$s$, $s$["Restricted by stopping all IV fluids", "Replaced hour-by-hour with IV fluid", "Treated with IV furosemide to promote diuresis", "Ignored, as it will settle by itself"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Replaced hour-by-hour with IV fluid
After kidney transplant, the new kidney may pass very large volumes of urine (post-transplant diuresis), sometimes 500–1000 mL per hour. If not replaced, the patient becomes dehydrated, and the new kidney is under-perfused and damaged. The usual practice is to REPLACE the urine output hour by hour with IV fluid (such as "urine output plus 30 mL/hour").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Restricted by stopping all IV fluids — Restricting fluids would cause dehydration and harm the graft.
• Treated with IV furosemide to promote diuresis — Furosemide would worsen fluid loss.
• Ignored, as it will settle by itself — Ignoring it risks dehydration and electrolyte problems.

⭐ Key point: Monitor urine output hourly, and check potassium, sodium, magnesium and glucose frequently.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q10$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1592, $s$Following nephrectomy, an ICU patient must be monitored especially for:$s$, $s$["Raised intracranial pressure and seizures", "Hypothyroidism with bradycardia and cold skin", "Hyperglycaemia and diabetic ketoacidosis", "Bleeding, remaining kidney function and pneumothorax"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bleeding, remaining kidney function and pneumothorax
After nephrectomy (kidney removal), monitor for: BLEEDING (the kidney has a large blood supply; watch BP, pulse, drains and haemoglobin), the function of the REMAINING KIDNEY (urine output, creatinine), and PNEUMOTHORAX (the flank approach is close to the pleura, which can be injured).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Raised intracranial pressure and seizures — Raised ICP isn't a typical complication.
• Hypothyroidism with bradycardia and cold skin — Hypothyroidism isn't related.
• Hyperglycaemia and diabetic ketoacidosis — Hyperglycaemia and DKA aren't typical complications.

⭐ Key point: Urine output must be at least 0.5 mL/kg/hour. The remaining kidney must do all the work.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q16$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1593, $s$A primary cause of abdominal compartment syndrome is:$s$, $s$["Septic shock from pneumonia", "Abdominal trauma with intra-abdominal bleeding", "Massive fluid resuscitation for burns", "Large-volume ascites from cirrhosis only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Abdominal trauma with intra-abdominal bleeding
PRIMARY abdominal compartment syndrome comes from a problem IN the abdomen or pelvis: ABDOMINAL TRAUMA with intra-abdominal BLEEDING, pancreatitis, abdominal surgery, or bowel obstruction. SECONDARY ACS comes from problems OUTSIDE the abdomen, such as massive fluid resuscitation (in sepsis or burns) causing gut and abdominal wall swelling.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Septic shock from pneumonia — Septic shock from pneumonia causes SECONDARY ACS (via fluid resuscitation).
• Massive fluid resuscitation for burns — Massive fluid resuscitation for burns causes SECONDARY ACS.
• Large-volume ascites from cirrhosis only — Ascites can raise IAP but is listed here as not the primary cause.

⭐ Key point: Recurrent ACS happens after treatment (such as after decompression).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q62$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1594, $s$Bleeding from a stress ulcer in a ventilated patient is suggested by:$s$, $s$["Fresh blood suctioned from the endotracheal tube", "Coffee-ground NG aspirate and a falling haemoglobin", "Bright red blood in the urinary catheter bag", "Bruising around the umbilicus and flanks"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Coffee-ground NG aspirate and a falling haemoglobin
STRESS ULCER bleeding in ventilated patients shows as COFFEE-GROUND NG aspirate (blood partly digested by stomach acid) and a FALLING HAEMOGLOBIN. Melaena and haemodynamic instability may follow if bleeding continues.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fresh blood suctioned from the endotracheal tube — Fresh blood from the ETT suggests airway or lung bleeding.
• Bright red blood in the urinary catheter bag — Blood in the urinary catheter suggests bladder or kidney bleeding.
• Bruising around the umbilicus and flanks — Bruising around the umbilicus and flanks suggests retroperitoneal bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Prevent stress ulcers in high-risk patients with PPIs or H2 blockers and early enteral feeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q64$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1595, $s$A patient with severe acute pancreatitis develops oliguria, hypoxia and hypotension on day 2. This represents:$s$, $s$["Simple dehydration that needs oral fluids", "A systemic inflammatory response with organ failure", "Gallstones passing into the duodenum", "Normal recovery from an attack of pancreatitis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A systemic inflammatory response with organ failure
In SEVERE acute pancreatitis, inflammation spreads throughout the body (systemic inflammatory response, SIRS), causing ORGAN FAILURE: oliguria (kidneys), hypoxia (lungs, ARDS), and hypotension (circulation). Organ failure lasting more than 48 hours defines severe pancreatitis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple dehydration that needs oral fluids — This isn't simple dehydration treatable with oral fluids.
• Gallstones passing into the duodenum — Gallstones passing don't explain organ failure.
• Normal recovery from an attack of pancreatitis — Organ failure isn't normal recovery.

⭐ Key point: Treat with ICU support: fluids, organ support, early enteral feeding, and treatment of complications.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q65$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1596, $s$Cullen's sign in acute pancreatitis is:$s$, $s$["Periumbilical bruising from retroperitoneal bleeding", "Shoulder tip pain from diaphragm irritation", "Flank bruising from retroperitoneal bleeding", "Jaundice from compression of the bile duct"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Periumbilical bruising from retroperitoneal bleeding
CULLEN'S SIGN is bluish bruising AROUND the UMBILICUS (belly button), caused by RETROPERITONEAL bleeding tracking to the skin. It is seen in severe (haemorrhagic) pancreatitis, ruptured ectopic pregnancy and retroperitoneal haemorrhage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shoulder tip pain from diaphragm irritation — Shoulder tip pain is Kehr's sign.
• Flank bruising from retroperitoneal bleeding — FLANK bruising is GREY TURNER'S sign.
• Jaundice from compression of the bile duct — Jaundice is from bile duct obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Cullen = umbilicus ("C" for centre). Grey Turner = flanks.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q66$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1597, $s$Fournier's gangrene is:$s$, $s$["Clostridial gas gangrene of the thigh", "Necrotising fasciitis of the perineum", "Ischaemic colitis affecting the rectum", "Dry gangrene of the toes in diabetes"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Necrotising fasciitis of the perineum
FOURNIER'S GANGRENE is NECROTISING FASCIITIS of the PERINEUM, genitals and perianal area. It spreads rapidly along the fascial planes, destroying tissue. Risk factors: diabetes, alcoholism and immunosuppression. It is a surgical emergency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Clostridial gas gangrene of the thigh — Clostridial gas gangrene of the thigh is different.
• Ischaemic colitis affecting the rectum — Ischaemic colitis is bowel ischaemia.
• Dry gangrene of the toes in diabetes — Dry gangrene of the toes is from poor blood supply.

⭐ Key point: Treatment: urgent surgical debridement, broad-spectrum antibiotics, and sepsis resuscitation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q68$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1598, $s$The LRINEC score is used to:$s$, $s$["Grade the depth of pressure ulcers", "Assess delirium in ventilated ICU patients", "Predict post-operative nausea and vomiting", "Help identify necrotising fasciitis from blood tests"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Help identify necrotising fasciitis from blood tests
The LRINEC score (Laboratory Risk Indicator for Necrotising Fasciitis) uses blood tests: CRP, white cell count, haemoglobin, sodium, creatinine and glucose. A higher score suggests NECROTISING FASCIITIS. It supports, but never replaces, clinical judgement and surgical exploration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Grade the depth of pressure ulcers — Pressure ulcers are graded by categories (1–4).
• Assess delirium in ventilated ICU patients — Delirium is assessed with CAM-ICU.
• Predict post-operative nausea and vomiting — PONV risk uses the Apfel score.

⭐ Key point: If necrotising fasciitis is clinically suspected, don't wait for scores; get urgent surgical review.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q69$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1599, $s$After TURP, a three-way catheter blocks with clots and the patient has bladder spasm. The nurse should:$s$, $s$["Clamp the drainage tubing for one hour", "Give a diuretic to flush the bladder through", "Stop irrigation, wash out per protocol, inform the doctor", "Increase the irrigation rate to force the clot out"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stop irrigation, wash out per protocol, inform the doctor
After TURP, a three-way catheter with continuous bladder irrigation prevents clots. If the catheter BLOCKS with clots (no outflow, painful bladder spasm, distended bladder), STOP the IRRIGATION (fluid going in with nothing coming out can overdistend or rupture the bladder), do a WASHOUT (bladder irrigation by syringe) as per protocol, and inform the doctor.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Clamp the drainage tubing for one hour — Clamping drainage would worsen the problem.
• Give a diuretic to flush the bladder through — A diuretic wouldn't clear clots and would add fluid.
• Increase the irrigation rate to force the clot out — Increasing irrigation into a blocked bladder risks rupture.

⭐ Key point: Monitor irrigation input and output closely; the output must equal input plus urine.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q70$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1600, $s$Acute urinary retention from benign prostatic hyperplasia is relieved by:$s$, $s$["Oral analgesics and a warm bath only", "Restricting oral fluids until it settles", "Urethral catheter, or suprapubic if urethral fails", "IV fluids with a dose of IV furosemide"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Urethral catheter, or suprapubic if urethral fails
Acute urinary retention from an enlarged prostate is relieved by inserting a URETHRAL CATHETER. If urethral catheterisation fails (an obstructing prostate, stricture), a SUPRAPUBIC catheter is inserted through the lower abdomen into the bladder.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral analgesics and a warm bath only — Analgesics and a warm bath don't relieve true acute retention.
• Restricting oral fluids until it settles — Restricting fluids doesn't relieve retention.
• IV fluids with a dose of IV furosemide — Furosemide increases urine production, worsening the distension.

⭐ Key point: Record the residual volume drained. Watch for post-obstructive diuresis (very high urine output) and replace losses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q71$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1601, $s$Polyneuropathy in a diabetic ICU patient most increases the risk of:$s$, $s$["Hyperglycaemia only, without skin risk", "Raised intracranial pressure", "Pressure injury and silent foot ulceration", "Severe hypertension and stroke"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pressure injury and silent foot ulceration
Diabetic polyneuropathy causes LOSS of SENSATION, especially in the feet. The patient can't feel pressure, pain or injury, so PRESSURE INJURIES and SILENT FOOT ULCERS develop without warning. ICU immobility increases the risk further.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperglycaemia only, without skin risk — Neuropathy increases skin risk; it isn't just about glucose.
• Raised intracranial pressure — Neuropathy doesn't cause raised ICP.
• Severe hypertension and stroke — Neuropathy doesn't directly cause hypertension and stroke.

⭐ Key point: Inspect the feet and heels every shift, offload the heels, and use pressure-relieving devices.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q72$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1602, $s$The cosyntropin (synacthen) stimulation test assesses:$s$, $s$["Thyroid hormone production", "Adrenal cortisol response to ACTH", "Growth hormone release", "Insulin secretion by the pancreas"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Adrenal cortisol response to ACTH
The cosyntropin (Synacthen) test checks how well the ADRENAL glands produce CORTISOL in response to ACTH. Synthetic ACTH (cosyntropin) is injected, and cortisol is measured before and after 30–60 minutes. A poor rise in cortisol suggests ADRENAL INSUFFICIENCY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Thyroid hormone production — Thyroid function is assessed by TSH and T4.
• Growth hormone release — Growth hormone has other tests.
• Insulin secretion by the pancreas — Insulin secretion has other tests.

⭐ Key point: In critical illness, don't wait for test results to treat suspected adrenal crisis; give hydrocortisone.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q75$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1603, $s$Physiological jaundice differs from pathological jaundice because physiological jaundice:$s$, $s$["Appears after 24 hours and resolves by 2 weeks", "Requires exchange transfusion in all cases", "Is associated with a conjugated bilirubin rise", "Appears within the first 24 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Appears after 24 hours and resolves by 2 weeks
PHYSIOLOGICAL jaundice is common and harmless. It APPEARS AFTER 24 HOURS (usually day 2–3), peaks around day 3–5, and RESOLVES by about 2 WEEKS (3 weeks in preterm babies). It is unconjugated bilirubin from the normal breakdown of fetal red cells and an immature liver.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Requires exchange transfusion in all cases — Most physiological jaundice doesn't need any treatment, let alone exchange transfusion.
• Is associated with a conjugated bilirubin rise — A CONJUGATED bilirubin rise is always pathological (liver or biliary disease).
• Appears within the first 24 hours — Jaundice appearing within the FIRST 24 hours is always PATHOLOGICAL.

⭐ Key point: Jaundice lasting more than 2 weeks (prolonged jaundice) needs investigation, including conjugated bilirubin.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q85$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1604, $s$A sickle cell patient's Hb electrophoresis showing HbF elevated after hydroxyurea means:$s$, $s$["The patient has beta thalassaemia major as well", "The drug has failed and should be stopped now", "Hydroxyurea is raising fetal Hb, which reduces sickling", "The patient is dehydrated and needs IV fluids"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hydroxyurea is raising fetal Hb, which reduces sickling
HYDROXYUREA (hydroxycarbamide) increases the production of FETAL HAEMOGLOBIN (HbF). HbF does not sickle and dilutes the sickle haemoglobin (HbS) in red cells, so cells are less likely to sickle. A RISING HbF after starting hydroxyurea shows that the DRUG IS WORKING, which reduces painful crises, acute chest syndrome, transfusion needs and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient has beta thalassaemia major as well — A raised HbF in this context is due to the drug, not a sign of thalassaemia major.
• The drug has failed and should be stopped now — A rising HbF is the desired response, not drug failure.
• The patient is dehydrated and needs IV fluids — HbF level is not related to dehydration.

⭐ Key point: Hydroxyurea side effects: bone marrow suppression (monitor FBC), and it should be avoided in pregnancy. It is recommended for many children with sickle cell anaemia from 9 months of age.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q193$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1605, $s$A patient in DKA presents with deep, rapid breathing (Kussmaul respirations) and a fruity breath odour. This reflects the body's attempt to:$s$, $s$["Increase oxygen delivery with no relation to acid-base status", "Reduce blood glucose directly through breathing", "Compensate for respiratory alkalosis", "Compensate for metabolic acidosis by blowing off CO2"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Compensate for metabolic acidosis by blowing off CO2
In DKA, ketones (acids) build up, causing metabolic acidosis. The body tries to raise the pH by breathing faster and deeper (Kussmaul breathing) to blow off CO₂, which is a respiratory acid. This is respiratory COMPENSATION for metabolic acidosis. The "fruity" or pear-drop smell on the breath is acetone, a ketone breathed out.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase oxygen delivery with no relation to acid-base status — The breathing pattern is about acid, not oxygen delivery.
• Reduce blood glucose directly through breathing — Breathing does not lower blood glucose.
• Compensate for respiratory alkalosis — The problem is ACIDOSIS, not alkalosis; Kussmaul breathing compensates for acidosis.

⭐ Key point: Kussmaul breathing = deep, sighing, rapid breaths in metabolic acidosis (DKA, kidney failure, lactic acidosis). It slows as the acidosis is corrected.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q21$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1606, $s$A patient with diabetic ketoacidosis has K⁺ 5.2 mEq/L before insulin therapy. After starting insulin, which K⁺ level indicates effective treatment?$s$, $s$["4.8 mEq/L", "3.9 mEq/L", "5.0 mEq/L", "3.2 mEq/L"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3.2 mEq/L
Insulin drives potassium (with glucose) into the cells. In DKA, total body potassium is low even if the blood level looks normal or high, because acidosis pushes potassium out of the cells. When insulin starts, potassium moves back in and the blood level FALLS. A drop from 5.2 to 3.2 shows insulin is working (potassium has shifted into cells). But 3.2 is LOW and must be replaced to prevent arrhythmias.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 4.8 mEq/L — 4.8 shows almost no shift.
• 3.9 mEq/L — 3.9 is a smaller fall, and the question's key looks for the biggest shift that shows the insulin effect.
• 5.0 mEq/L — 5.0 is almost unchanged.

⭐ Key point: In DKA, add potassium to the IV fluids once K⁺ is below 5.5 and urine is flowing. Check K⁺ every 1–2 hours at first. Hold insulin if K⁺ is below 3.3.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q22$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1607, $s$In DKA, insulin infusion is started at a fixed rate of:$s$, $s$["10 units/hour for all", "0.1 units/kg/hour", "1 unit/kg/hour", "0.01 units/kg/hour"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 0.1 units/kg/hour
Current DKA guidance uses a FIXED-RATE IV insulin infusion of 0.1 units/kg/hour (about 7 units/hour for a 70 kg adult), using soluble insulin (50 units in 50 mL of 0.9% saline). It is fixed by weight, not adjusted by glucose level. The aim is to clear ketones (falling by at least 0.5 mmol/L per hour). When glucose falls below 14 mmol/L, add 10% dextrose and keep the insulin going until the ketones clear.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 units/hour for all — 10 units/hour for everyone ignores body weight and is too much for most patients.
• 1 unit/kg/hour — 1 unit/kg/hour is ten times too much.
• 0.01 units/kg/hour — 0.01 units/kg/hour is ten times too little.

⭐ Key point: Continue the patient's usual long-acting insulin during DKA treatment. Don't stop the IV insulin until ketones are below 0.6 and the patient is eating.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q24$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1608, $s$Insulin should not be started in DKA until the potassium is at least:$s$, $s$["2.0 mmol/L", "6.5 mmol/L", "3.3 mmol/L", "5.5 mmol/L"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3.3 mmol/L
Insulin pushes potassium into the cells and lowers the blood level quickly. If potassium is already LOW (below 3.3 mmol/L) when insulin starts, it can drop to a dangerous level and cause fatal arrhythmias (VT, VF) and muscle weakness, including of the breathing muscles. So if K⁺ is below 3.3, give potassium first (and fluids), then start insulin once K⁺ reaches 3.3 or more.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2.0 mmol/L — 2.0 is dangerously low; insulin must not be started at this level.
• 6.5 mmol/L — 6.5 is HIGH; insulin can be started (and will help lower it).
• 5.5 mmol/L — 5.5 is above the safe threshold; the minimum is 3.3, not 5.5.

⭐ Key point: Potassium in DKA: above 5.5, give no K⁺ in the fluids; 3.5–5.5, add 40 mmol/L; below 3.3, hold insulin and replace K⁺ first.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q25$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1609, $s$A major risk of correcting DKA too rapidly in a child is:$s$, $s$["Hyperkalaemia", "Hypernatraemia", "Cerebral oedema", "Pulmonary embolism"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cerebral oedema
In DKA, the brain cells adapt to the high glucose and osmolality. If fluids are given too fast or glucose falls too quickly, water rushes into the brain cells, causing CEREBRAL OEDEMA. Children are at the highest risk; it is the main cause of death in childhood DKA. Fluids are therefore given more slowly in children (over 48 hours), with no insulin bolus.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperkalaemia — Hyperkalaemia is not the main risk of rapid correction; potassium usually falls with treatment.
• Hypernatraemia — Sodium does rise as glucose falls, but hypernatraemia is not the classic danger.
• Pulmonary embolism — PE can occur in HHS (thick blood) but isn't the main risk of rapid DKA correction in a child.

⭐ Key point: Warning signs of cerebral oedema: headache, irritability, falling GCS, slow pulse and rising BP. Treat with mannitol or 3% saline and reduce the fluid rate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q26$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1610, $s$Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state differs from DKA because HHS has:$s$, $s$["Low serum osmolality with a metabolic alkalosis", "Hypoglycaemia with confusion and sweating", "Very high glucose and osmolality with little ketosis", "Severe ketoacidosis with a low or normal glucose"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Very high glucose and osmolality with little ketosis
HHS (hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state) occurs mainly in older type 2 diabetics. They still make a little insulin, enough to stop ketone production but not enough to control glucose. Glucose climbs very high (often above 30–33 mmol/L), the blood becomes very concentrated (osmolality above 320 mOsm/kg), and the patient becomes severely dehydrated and confused, with little or no ketosis or acidosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low serum osmolality with a metabolic alkalosis — HHS has HIGH osmolality, not low, and no metabolic alkalosis.
• Hypoglycaemia with confusion and sweating — Hypoglycaemia is the opposite problem.
• Severe ketoacidosis with a low or normal glucose — Severe ketoacidosis with low or normal glucose fits euglycaemic DKA (for example with SGLT2 inhibitors), not HHS.

⭐ Key point: HHS develops over days, and the fluid loss is huge (10 L or more). The death rate is higher than in DKA. Watch for clots and give prophylactic LMWH.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q27$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1611, $s$In HHS, the first-line treatment is:$s$, $s$["Hypotonic fluid rapidly", "Sodium bicarbonate infusion", "High-dose insulin bolus", "Slow IV 0.9% saline rehydration"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Slow IV 0.9% saline rehydration
In HHS, the main problem is extreme dehydration and very high osmolality. The first treatment is IV 0.9% saline given steadily (about 1 L in the first hour, then guided by response), which itself lowers glucose and osmolality. Correction must be SLOW (osmolality falling by no more than 3–8 mOsm/kg per hour) to avoid cerebral oedema and central pontine myelinolysis. Low-dose insulin (0.05 units/kg/hour) is added only when glucose stops falling with fluids.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypotonic fluid rapidly — Fast hypotonic fluid lowers osmolality too quickly and risks brain swelling.
• Sodium bicarbonate infusion — Bicarbonate isn't needed; there is little acidosis.
• High-dose insulin bolus — A high-dose insulin bolus lowers glucose too fast and pushes water into the cells.

⭐ Key point: In HHS, fluids alone lower glucose. Monitor osmolality, sodium, glucose and fluid balance closely, and watch for heart failure in older patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q28$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1612, $s$Thyroid storm presents with:$s$, $s$["Hyperpyrexia, tachyarrhythmia, agitation, heart failure", "Hypothermia, bradycardia and a slow decline into coma", "Hypoglycaemia with low sodium and high potassium", "Weight gain, cold intolerance and slowed thinking"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hyperpyrexia, tachyarrhythmia, agitation, heart failure
Thyroid storm is life-threatening hyperthyroidism, usually triggered by surgery, infection, labour, or stopping anti-thyroid drugs. Too much thyroid hormone speeds up everything: very high fever (hyperpyrexia, often above 40 °C), very fast heart rate or AF, heart failure, agitation, confusion, delirium, vomiting and diarrhoea, and finally coma.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypothermia, bradycardia and a slow decline into coma — Hypothermia, bradycardia and coma describe myxoedema coma (low thyroid).
• Hypoglycaemia with low sodium and high potassium — Low glucose, low sodium and high potassium describe adrenal crisis.
• Weight gain, cold intolerance and slowed thinking — Weight gain, cold intolerance and slow thinking describe hypothyroidism.

⭐ Key point: Treatment order: beta-blocker (propranolol) for the heart, PTU or carbimazole to stop hormone production, Lugol's iodine 1 hour AFTER PTU, hydrocortisone, and cooling with paracetamol (not aspirin).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q29$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1613, $s$Myxoedema coma is characterised by:$s$, $s$["Hypothermia, bradycardia, hypoventilation, coma", "Hyperthermia, tachycardia and agitation", "Severe hypertension with a headache", "Diarrhoea, weight loss and tremor"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypothermia, bradycardia, hypoventilation, coma
Myxoedema coma is the most severe form of hypothyroidism. Lack of thyroid hormone slows everything down: low body temperature (hypothermia), slow pulse (bradycardia), slow breathing with CO₂ retention (hypoventilation), low BP, low sodium, low glucose, and reduced consciousness leading to coma. The face is puffy and the skin dry. It is often triggered by cold, infection or sedatives in elderly women.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperthermia, tachycardia and agitation — High temperature, fast pulse and agitation describe thyroid STORM (too much hormone).
• Severe hypertension with a headache — Severe hypertension with headache doesn't fit myxoedema coma.
• Diarrhoea, weight loss and tremor — Diarrhoea, weight loss and tremor are signs of HYPERthyroidism.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV thyroxine or T3, plus IV hydrocortisone (adrenal insufficiency may coexist), slow passive rewarming, ventilation, and treatment of the trigger.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q30$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1614, $s$Addisonian (adrenal) crisis typically presents with:$s$, $s$["Hypertension with low potassium and alkalosis", "Hypotension, low Na⁺, high K⁺ and low glucose", "High glucose with weight gain and striae", "High sodium with polyuria and thirst"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypotension, low Na⁺, high K⁺ and low glucose
The adrenal glands make cortisol (which keeps BP and glucose up) and aldosterone (which keeps sodium in and pushes potassium out). In adrenal crisis, both are lacking. The patient has low BP or shock not responding to fluids, LOW sodium, HIGH potassium, LOW glucose, vomiting and abdominal pain. It is often triggered by stopping long-term steroids suddenly, or by infection or surgery in someone with Addison's disease.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension with low potassium and alkalosis — High BP with low potassium describes HYPERaldosteronism (Conn's syndrome).
• High glucose with weight gain and striae — High glucose with weight gain and striae describes Cushing's syndrome (too much cortisol).
• High sodium with polyuria and thirst — High sodium with polyuria and thirst suggests diabetes insipidus.

⭐ Key point: Give IV hydrocortisone 100 mg immediately (don't wait for results), IV 0.9% saline, and dextrose for low glucose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q31$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1615, $s$After anaphylaxis is treated, the patient is observed for at least 6–12 hours because of:$s$, $s$["Steroid side effects", "Biphasic (late) reactions", "Delayed drug absorption", "Hypoglycaemia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Biphasic (late) reactions
About 5–20% of patients have a BIPHASIC reaction: symptoms come back hours after the first reaction settles (usually within 12 hours, but up to 72 hours), without re-exposure to the trigger. Patients are observed for at least 6–12 hours (longer if the reaction was severe or needed more than one adrenaline dose) so a second reaction can be treated quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Steroid side effects — Steroid side effects are not the reason for observation.
• Delayed drug absorption — Delayed drug absorption is not the main reason; the risk is a return of the reaction itself.
• Hypoglycaemia — Hypoglycaemia is not a feature of anaphylaxis.

⭐ Key point: Before discharge: two adrenaline auto-injectors, teaching on how to use them, an allergy referral, and a clear record of the allergy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q33$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1616, $s$Severe hypoglycaemia in an unconscious ICU patient is treated with:$s$, $s$["IV insulin 10 units to reset the glucose control", "Oral glucose gel placed into the patient's mouth", "IM glucagon only, repeated every 10 minutes", "IV 10% dextrose 150–200 mL (or 20% dextrose 75–100 mL)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV 10% dextrose 150–200 mL (or 20% dextrose 75–100 mL)
An unconscious patient cannot swallow, so oral glucose is dangerous (aspiration). The fastest safe treatment is IV dextrose: 150–200 mL of 10% dextrose (or 75–100 mL of 20%) over about 15 minutes. Recheck glucose after 10–15 minutes and repeat if still below 4 mmol/L. Then find the cause (insulin infusion rate, feeds stopped, liver failure, sepsis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV insulin 10 units to reset the glucose control — Insulin would make hypoglycaemia worse and could kill.
• Oral glucose gel placed into the patient's mouth — Oral gel in an unconscious patient risks aspiration.
• IM glucagon only, repeated every 10 minutes — IM glucagon is used when there is NO IV access; it works poorly in liver disease or starvation and is not repeated every 10 minutes.

⭐ Key point: 50% dextrose is no longer preferred because it damages veins if it leaks. Once the patient wakes, give a longer-acting carbohydrate or feed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q34$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1617, $s$Syndrome of inappropriate ADH (SIADH) causes:$s$, $s$["High sodium with very dilute urine and thirst", "Low sodium with concentrated urine, euvolaemic", "High potassium with a metabolic acidosis", "Hypovolaemia with polyuria and low urine sodium"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Low sodium with concentrated urine, euvolaemic
In SIADH, too much ADH makes the kidneys keep too much water. The extra water dilutes the blood, giving LOW sodium (hyponatraemia), while the urine is CONCENTRATED (inappropriately, given the low sodium). The patient is euvolaemic (no obvious swelling or dehydration), because the extra water spreads throughout all body compartments. Causes: head injury, meningitis, pneumonia, lung cancer, drugs (carbamazepine, SSRIs), and surgery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High sodium with very dilute urine and thirst — High sodium with dilute urine and thirst describes DIABETES INSIPIDUS (too little ADH).
• High potassium with a metabolic acidosis — High potassium with metabolic acidosis suggests adrenal insufficiency or kidney failure.
• Hypovolaemia with polyuria and low urine sodium — Hypovolaemia with lots of urine suggests diabetes insipidus or cerebral salt wasting, not SIADH.

⭐ Key point: Treat SIADH with fluid restriction (about 800–1000 mL/day). Raise sodium slowly: no more than 8–10 mmol/L in 24 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q36$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1618, $s$Peripheral polyneuritis causing glove-and-stocking sensory loss is commonly due to:$s$, $s$["Hypothyroidism on its own", "Excess intake of vitamin C", "Diabetes and vitamin B deficiency", "Hypertension and bronchial asthma"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Diabetes and vitamin B deficiency
Peripheral polyneuritis (polyneuropathy) damages the longest nerves first, so symptoms start in the feet and hands: a "GLOVE-AND-STOCKING" pattern of numbness, tingling and burning pain. The most common causes are DIABETES and VITAMIN B deficiency (B1 thiamine, B6, B12), often linked to alcohol misuse, poor diet, or drugs like isoniazid.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypothyroidism on its own — Hypothyroidism can cause neuropathy, but less commonly.
• Excess intake of vitamin C — Excess vitamin C doesn't cause neuropathy (excess B6 can).
• Hypertension and bronchial asthma — Hypertension and asthma are not causes of neuropathy.

⭐ Key point: Patients with sensory neuropathy can't feel injuries. Check and protect the feet daily, especially in diabetes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q93$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1619, $s$A child with diabetic ketoacidosis should have fluids replaced:$s$, $s$["Only by mouth, with no IV fluids", "Slowly over 48 hours to reduce cerebral oedema", "Rapidly over 2 hours to correct shock", "With hypotonic fluid to lower sodium"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Slowly over 48 hours to reduce cerebral oedema
In children with DKA, the brain is at risk of CEREBRAL OEDEMA if fluids are given too quickly or glucose and osmolality fall too fast. Fluid deficits are therefore replaced SLOWLY and evenly over 48 HOURS. Boluses are given only if the child is in shock (10 mL/kg, repeated carefully).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only by mouth, with no IV fluids — DKA children are too dehydrated and vomiting for oral fluids alone.
• Rapidly over 2 hours to correct shock — Rapid replacement over 2 hours increases the risk of cerebral oedema.
• With hypotonic fluid to lower sodium — Hypotonic fluids increase the risk of cerebral oedema; 0.9% saline is used.

⭐ Key point: In children's DKA: no insulin bolus, start insulin (0.05–0.1 units/kg/hour) 1–2 hours AFTER fluids begin, and watch for headache, slow pulse and falling GCS.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q117$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1620, $s$A growth-restricted baby at delivery requires preparation for:$s$, $s$["Fluid overload and generalised oedema", "Hyperglycaemia and polyuria at birth", "Resuscitation, hypoglycaemia and hypothermia", "Macrosomia and shoulder dystocia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Resuscitation, hypoglycaemia and hypothermia
A growth-restricted (IUGR) baby has limited energy stores (low glycogen and fat). After birth, the baby is at high risk of HYPOGLYCAEMIA (low sugar) and HYPOTHERMIA (cold). These babies may have been short of oxygen in the womb, so they may need RESUSCITATION at birth. Prepare a warm environment, resuscitation equipment and early feeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fluid overload and generalised oedema — Fluid overload and oedema are not typical of growth-restricted babies.
• Hyperglycaemia and polyuria at birth — They have LOW glucose, not high, and don't usually have polyuria.
• Macrosomia and shoulder dystocia — Macrosomia (a large baby) and shoulder dystocia are the opposite of growth restriction.

⭐ Key point: Check blood glucose regularly in IUGR babies, feed early (within the first hour), and keep them warm (skin-to-skin contact, a hat).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q134$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1621, $s$Nutrition in acute kidney injury without dialysis aims for:$s$, $s$["Enough calories, moderate protein, electrolyte control", "Fasting until the kidney function improves", "No protein at all until the kidneys recover", "Unlimited potassium in the feeds"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Enough calories, moderate protein, electrolyte control
In acute kidney injury without dialysis, nutrition aims to provide ENOUGH CALORIES (25–30 kcal/kg/day) to prevent muscle breakdown, MODERATE protein (0.8–1.0 g/kg/day) to limit urea build-up, and careful control of ELECTROLYTES (potassium, phosphate, sodium) and fluid. If dialysis starts, protein needs rise (1.0–1.5 g/kg/day or more), because dialysis removes amino acids.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fasting until the kidney function improves — Fasting causes muscle breakdown and worsens outcomes.
• No protein at all until the kidneys recover — No protein at all causes muscle wasting and poor healing.
• Unlimited potassium in the feeds — Unlimited potassium is dangerous in AKI (hyperkalaemia).

⭐ Key point: Critically ill patients should not have protein restricted just to delay dialysis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q190$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1622, $s$Feeding a diabetic ICU patient enterally requires:$s$, $s$["Adding sugar to the feeds to prevent lows", "No glucose checks while on enteral feeds", "Glucose checks and insulin matched to the feed", "Stopping all feeds to control the glucose"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Glucose checks and insulin matched to the feed
Enteral feeds contain carbohydrates that raise blood glucose. In a diabetic ICU patient, check GLUCOSE regularly (every 1–4 hours) and match INSULIN to the feed (insulin infusion, or regular insulin while feeds run). If feeds stop suddenly (vomiting, tube blocked, procedure), insulin must be reduced or stopped, or hypoglycaemia can occur.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adding sugar to the feeds to prevent lows — Adding sugar to feeds will cause high glucose.
• No glucose checks while on enteral feeds — Glucose checks are essential in diabetics on feeds.
• Stopping all feeds to control the glucose — Stopping all feeds causes malnutrition; glucose should be managed with insulin.

⭐ Key point: ICU glucose target is usually 6–10 mmol/L. Avoid both high and low glucose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q191$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1623, $s$In peritoneal dialysis, the dialysate is$s$, $s$["Normosmolar", "Hyposmolar", "Hyperosmosis", "Hyperosmolar"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hyperosmolar
In peritoneal dialysis, the dialysis fluid is HYPEROSMOLAR (contains high glucose, which makes it more concentrated than blood). Water moves by OSMOSIS from the blood (lower concentration) into the dialysate in the peritoneal cavity, removing excess fluid (ultrafiltration). Waste products move by diffusion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normosmolar — Normosmolar fluid wouldn't pull fluid out.
• Hyposmolar — Hyposmolar fluid would move water INTO the blood, the wrong direction.
• Hyperosmosis — "Hyperosmosis" isn't the correct term; the dialysate is hyperosmolar.

⭐ Key point: Higher glucose concentrations remove more fluid. Watch for peritonitis (cloudy fluid, abdominal pain, fever).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q81$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1624, $s$A 53-year-old patient who is receiving ibuprofen 400 mg twice a day, for chronic, low back pain develops lower-extremity oedema. The pain management nurse suspects that the oedema is caused by:$s$, $s$["An increase in glomerular filtration rate", "A decrease in renal function", "A low creatinine level.", "An increase in plasma proteins."]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A decrease in renal function
NSAIDs like ibuprofen block prostaglandins, which normally keep the kidney arteries open. Without them, blood flow to the kidneys falls, especially in older people or with dehydration. This REDUCES KIDNEY FUNCTION, causing salt and water retention and OEDEMA, high BP, and sometimes acute kidney injury.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An increase in glomerular filtration rate — NSAIDs DECREASE, not increase, the GFR.
• A low creatinine level. — Kidney damage RAISES creatinine, not lowers it.
• An increase in plasma proteins. — NSAIDs don't increase plasma proteins.

⭐ Key point: Avoid NSAIDs in kidney disease, heart failure, dehydration and the elderly. Monitor creatinine in long-term use.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q82$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1625, $s$KDIGO stage 3 acute kidney injury includes urine output below:$s$, $s$["Under 0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 consecutive hours", "Under 2 mL/kg/h for 1 hour after surgery", "Under 0.3 mL/kg/h for 24 h, or anuria for 12 h", "Under 1 mL/kg/h for 2 consecutive hours"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Under 0.3 mL/kg/h for 24 h, or anuria for 12 h
KDIGO stages AKI by creatinine and urine output. STAGE 3 (most severe) is: urine output below 0.3 mL/kg/hour for 24 HOURS or more, or NO urine (anuria) for 12 hours or more; OR creatinine 3 times baseline (or 354 µmol/L or above); OR starting dialysis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Under 0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 consecutive hours — Below 0.5 mL/kg/hour for 6 hours is STAGE 1.
• Under 2 mL/kg/h for 1 hour after surgery — 2 mL/kg/hour is a normal or good urine output.
• Under 1 mL/kg/h for 2 consecutive hours — 1 mL/kg/hour is a normal urine output.

⭐ Key point: Stage 1: below 0.5 for 6–12 hours; Stage 2: below 0.5 for 12 hours or more; Stage 3: below 0.3 for 24 hours or more, or anuria for 12 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q83$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1626, $s$A urinary sodium below 20 mmol/L in oliguria suggests:$s$, $s$["Established acute tubular necrosis", "Recent loop diuretic administration", "Post-renal obstruction of the outflow", "Pre-renal AKI with intact tubules"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pre-renal AKI with intact tubules
In PRE-RENAL AKI (from dehydration or low BP), the kidney tubules still WORK, so they hold on to as much SODIUM (and water) as possible to restore blood volume. The urine sodium is LOW (below 20 mmol/L), the urine is concentrated, and the fractional excretion of sodium (FeNa) is below 1%.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Established acute tubular necrosis — In established ATN, the damaged tubules CAN'T hold sodium, so urine sodium is HIGH (above 40).
• Recent loop diuretic administration — Loop diuretics RAISE urine sodium (they block sodium reabsorption), making the test unreliable.
• Post-renal obstruction of the outflow — Post-renal obstruction doesn't usually show this pattern.

⭐ Key point: Pre-renal AKI usually improves quickly with fluid. If untreated, it can progress to ATN.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q84$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1627, $s$CRRT is preferred to intermittent haemodialysis in:$s$, $s$["Haemodynamically unstable patients", "Patients who can walk", "Stable outpatients", "Patients with no kidney disease"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Haemodynamically unstable patients
Continuous renal replacement therapy (CRRT) removes fluid and waste SLOWLY over 24 hours, so the blood pressure stays more stable. Intermittent haemodialysis (IHD) removes fluid quickly over 3–4 hours, which can cause low BP. CRRT is therefore preferred in HAEMODYNAMICALLY UNSTABLE patients (shock, on vasopressors) and in patients with raised ICP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Patients who can walk — Patients who can walk are usually stable; IHD is fine.
• Stable outpatients — Stable outpatients have IHD.
• Patients with no kidney disease — Patients without kidney disease don't usually need dialysis.

⭐ Key point: CRRT also allows better fluid control and continuous correction of acidosis and electrolytes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q86$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1628, $s$Dehydration is suggested by:$s$, $s$["Polyuria with very dilute urine and low urea", "Bradycardia with peripheral oedema and weight gain", "Raised JVP with basal crackles and a gallop", "Tachycardia, dry mucosa, oliguria, raised urea"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tachycardia, dry mucosa, oliguria, raised urea
Dehydration (hypovolaemia) shows: TACHYCARDIA (the heart compensates for low volume), dry MUCOUS membranes (mouth, tongue), OLIGURIA (low urine output, concentrated urine), poor skin turgor, low BP (late), and RAISED UREA (urea rises more than creatinine in pre-renal states, a urea-to-creatinine ratio above 100:1 in µmol units).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Polyuria with very dilute urine and low urea — Polyuria with dilute urine and low urea doesn't fit dehydration (it suggests diabetes insipidus or diuresis).
• Bradycardia with peripheral oedema and weight gain — Bradycardia with oedema and weight gain suggests fluid overload.
• Raised JVP with basal crackles and a gallop — Raised JVP with crackles suggests fluid overload or heart failure.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV fluid boluses (and find the cause), reassessing after each bolus.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q97$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1629, $s$Dilated small bowel loops with multiple air-fluid levels suggest:$s$, $s$["Small bowel obstruction", "A normal gas pattern", "Gross ascites", "Perforation only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Small bowel obstruction
In small bowel obstruction, gas and fluid build up above the blockage. The X-ray shows DILATED loops of small bowel (more than 3 cm) in the centre of the abdomen, with VALVULAE CONNIVENTES (folds crossing the whole width) and, on an erect or decubitus film, multiple AIR–FLUID LEVELS (flat lines where gas sits on fluid).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal gas pattern — A normal gas pattern doesn't show dilated loops with multiple fluid levels.
• Gross ascites — Ascites gives a hazy, ground-glass abdomen.
• Perforation only — Perforation shows free air under the diaphragm.

⭐ Key point: Treat with "drip and suck" (IV fluids, NG tube on free drainage), and surgery if there are signs of strangulation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q129$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1630, $s$Before IV contrast, the nurse checks:$s$, $s$["Hearing and the use of hearing aids", "Renal function, allergy history, metformin use", "Visual acuity and the use of spectacles", "Hair colour and the use of hair dye"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Renal function, allergy history, metformin use
Before giving IV contrast, check: KIDNEY FUNCTION (creatinine or eGFR), because contrast can damage the kidneys; ALLERGY history (previous contrast reactions, asthma, severe allergies); and METFORMIN use, because metformin with kidney injury can cause lactic acidosis, so it may be stopped. Make sure the patient is well hydrated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hearing and the use of hearing aids — Hearing isn't relevant to contrast.
• Visual acuity and the use of spectacles — Visual acuity isn't relevant.
• Hair colour and the use of hair dye — Hair dye isn't relevant.

⭐ Key point: Have resuscitation equipment ready during contrast injection, in case of anaphylaxis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q131$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1631, $s$An intravenous urogram (IVU/IVP) shows:$s$, $s$["Kidneys, ureters and bladder after contrast", "The lungs and the pleural spaces", "The brain ventricles and basal cisterns", "The heart chambers and the heart valves"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Kidneys, ureters and bladder after contrast
An intravenous urogram (IVU or IVP) injects iodine contrast into a vein, then takes X-rays as the kidneys filter the contrast into the urine. It shows the KIDNEYS, URETERS and BLADDER, revealing stones, blockages, tumours and abnormal anatomy. It has mostly been replaced by CT urography.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The lungs and the pleural spaces — The lungs and pleura are seen on a chest X-ray or CT chest.
• The brain ventricles and basal cisterns — The brain is seen on CT or MRI head.
• The heart chambers and the heart valves — The heart is seen on an echocardiogram.

⭐ Key point: Check kidney function and allergy history before an IVU, and keep the patient well hydrated.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q132$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1632, $s$Paediatric poisoning with iron tablets can cause:$s$, $s$["No symptoms at any dose", "Hypoglycaemia only, with sweating", "Hypertension only, with headache", "GI bleeding, shock and liver failure"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: GI bleeding, shock and liver failure
Iron is toxic in overdose. It irritates the gut (vomiting, diarrhoea, GI BLEEDING), then damages cells, causing SHOCK and metabolic acidosis, then LIVER FAILURE (1–4 days later), and later gut scarring. Children are at risk because iron tablets look like sweets.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No symptoms at any dose — Iron overdose is dangerous, especially above 60 mg/kg of elemental iron.
• Hypoglycaemia only, with sweating — Low glucose isn't the main problem.
• Hypertension only, with headache — High BP isn't typical; shock with low BP is.

⭐ Key point: The antidote is DESFERRIOXAMINE. Activated charcoal does NOT bind iron.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q197$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1633, $s$Hyperacute transplant rejection occurs within:$s$, $s$["Months to years", "1–4 days", "Days to weeks", "Minutes to hours"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Minutes to hours
HYPERACUTE rejection occurs within MINUTES to HOURS after the transplanted organ's blood supply is connected. It is caused by PRE-FORMED antibodies in the recipient (from ABO incompatibility, or previous transfusions, pregnancies or transplants) that immediately attack the organ's blood vessels, causing clotting and organ death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Months to years — Chronic rejection develops over months to years.
• 1–4 days — 1–4 days isn't the timing for hyperacute rejection.
• Days to weeks — Acute rejection develops over days to weeks.

⭐ Key point: Hyperacute rejection is prevented by cross-matching and ABO matching before transplant.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q82$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1634, $s$Subacute (subfulminant) hepatic failure develops over a timeframe of:$s$, $s$["<2 weeks", "Months to years", "2–12 weeks", "Decades"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2–12 weeks
Liver failure is classified by how quickly encephalopathy develops after jaundice: HYPERACUTE (under 1 week), ACUTE (1–4 weeks), and SUBACUTE (subfulminant), developing over about 2–12 WEEKS. Subacute failure has a worse prognosis without transplant, despite a slower onset.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• <2 weeks — Under 2 weeks is the fulminant (hyperacute or acute) range.
• Months to years — Months to years describes chronic liver disease.
• Decades — Decades describes long-term chronic disease.

⭐ Key point: In acute liver failure, watch for low glucose, cerebral oedema (raised ICP), bleeding and infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q87$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1635, $s$Hyperglycaemia is linked to ICU-AW because it:$s$, $s$["Has no effect on nerves or muscle", "Only affects the kidneys", "Prevents muscle wasting", "Damages muscle and nerve tissue"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Damages muscle and nerve tissue
HIGH BLOOD GLUCOSE in critical illness damages the small blood vessels and the nerve and muscle cells (through oxidative stress, inflammation and impaired energy use). This contributes to critical illness polyneuropathy and myopathy, which together cause ICU-acquired weakness (ICU-AW). Good glucose control helps reduce it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Has no effect on nerves or muscle — Hyperglycaemia DOES affect nerves and muscles.
• Only affects the kidneys — It affects more than the kidneys.
• Prevents muscle wasting — It doesn't prevent wasting; it contributes to weakness.

⭐ Key point: ICU glucose target: about 6–10 mmol/L. Avoid both very high and very low glucose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q88$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1636, $s$Which vascular access is generally preferred for long-term hemodialysis due to lower infection and thrombosis rates compared with other options?$s$, $s$["Peripheral IV line", "Arteriovenous fistula", "Temporary femoral catheter", "Tunneled central venous catheter"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Arteriovenous fistula
An ARTERIOVENOUS FISTULA (AVF) is a surgical connection between an artery and a vein (usually in the forearm). It is the PREFERRED long-term access for haemodialysis, because it uses the patient's own vessels, so it has the LOWEST rates of infection and thrombosis and lasts longest.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Peripheral IV line — A peripheral IV line can't support dialysis.
• Temporary femoral catheter — A temporary femoral catheter has a HIGH infection risk.
• Tunneled central venous catheter — A tunnelled central catheter has higher infection and clotting risk than an AVF.

⭐ Key point: Protect the fistula arm: no BP measurements, no cannulas or blood tests, and check the thrill and bruit daily.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q90$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1637, $s$A 36 year old male patient is diagnosed with acute kidney injury. The patient is voiding 4 L/day of urine. What complication can arise based on the stage of AKI this patient is in? Select all that apply:$s$, $s$["High urine specific gravity", "Hypotension", "Water intoxication", "Hyperkalemia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Water intoxication
This patient passes 4 L of urine a day: the POLYURIC (DIURETIC) PHASE of AKI. The recovering tubules can't yet concentrate urine, so large volumes of dilute urine are lost, along with sodium and potassium. If these losses are replaced with too much plain water or hypotonic fluid, WATER INTOXICATION (dilutional hyponatraemia) can result, which is the keyed answer. Dehydration and low potassium are also risks.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High urine specific gravity — Urine specific gravity is LOW (dilute), not high.
• Hypotension — Hypotension can occur from dehydration, but it isn't the keyed answer.
• Hyperkalemia — Potassium tends to FALL (lost in urine), not rise, in the polyuric phase.

⭐ Key point: In the polyuric phase, monitor weight, fluid balance and electrolytes closely, and replace losses with the right fluid.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q91$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1638, $s$Uremic pericarditis, a potential complication of untreated or inadequately dialyzed end-stage renal disease, is classically treated by:$s$, $s$["Fluid restriction only, with no change to dialysis", "Beta-blockers alone", "Intensified or urgent dialysis", "High-dose diuretics"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intensified or urgent dialysis
Uraemic pericarditis is inflammation of the pericardium caused by toxins (urea and others) that build up when the kidneys fail and dialysis is inadequate. It causes chest pain, a pericardial friction rub, fever, and possibly an effusion or tamponade. The treatment is INTENSIFIED or URGENT DIALYSIS, which removes the toxins.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fluid restriction only, with no change to dialysis — Fluid restriction alone doesn't remove toxins.
• Beta-blockers alone — Beta-blockers don't treat uraemic pericarditis.
• High-dose diuretics — Diuretics don't work well in end-stage kidney disease and don't remove the toxins.

⭐ Key point: Watch for tamponade (low BP, raised JVP, muffled heart sounds). Heparin during dialysis may increase bleeding into the pericardium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q94$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1639, $s$In adult DKA, the recommended fixed-rate IV insulin infusion is:$s$, $s$["0.01 unit/kg/hour of long-acting insulin", "10 units/hour for every patient", "0.1 unit/kg/hour of soluble insulin", "1 unit/kg/hour of soluble insulin"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 0.1 unit/kg/hour of soluble insulin
Adult DKA is treated with a FIXED-RATE IV insulin infusion of 0.1 units/kg/hour using SOLUBLE (short-acting) insulin. It suppresses ketone production and lowers glucose steadily. Continue the patient's usual long-acting insulin as well.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.01 unit/kg/hour of long-acting insulin — Long-acting insulin is not used in the IV infusion.
• 10 units/hour for every patient — 10 units/hour for everyone ignores weight.
• 1 unit/kg/hour of soluble insulin — 1 unit/kg/hour is ten times too much.

⭐ Key point: Targets: ketones fall at least 0.5 mmol/L per hour, and glucose falls about 3 mmol/L per hour. Add potassium to fluids once K⁺ is below 5.5 and urine is passed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q96$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1640, $s$In DKA, when blood glucose falls below 14 mmol/L, the nurse should:$s$, $s$["Stop insulin, as the DKA has resolved", "Add 10% dextrose and continue the insulin infusion", "Give a 50% dextrose bolus and stop the fluids", "Switch to subcutaneous insulin at once"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Add 10% dextrose and continue the insulin infusion
In DKA, glucose usually falls faster than ketones clear. When glucose drops below 14 mmol/L, ADD 10% DEXTROSE to prevent hypoglycaemia, and CONTINUE the insulin infusion, because insulin is still needed to clear the ketones and correct the acidosis. Continue until ketones are below 0.6 mmol/L and pH is above 7.3.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stop insulin, as the DKA has resolved — DKA hasn't resolved just because glucose is lower; ketones are still present.
• Give a 50% dextrose bolus and stop the fluids — A 50% dextrose bolus isn't needed and stopping fluids would be wrong.
• Switch to subcutaneous insulin at once — Switching to subcutaneous insulin is done only once DKA has resolved and the patient is eating.

⭐ Key point: When stopping the IV insulin, give subcutaneous insulin first and overlap by 30–60 minutes to prevent rebound DKA.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q97$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1641, $s$The feature that best separates hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state (HHS) from DKA is:$s$, $s$["pH below 7.0 with a very high anion gap", "Rapid onset over 1–2 hours in young people", "Blood glucose below 11 mmol/L with high ketones", "Osmolality above 320 mOsm/kg with little or no ketosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Osmolality above 320 mOsm/kg with little or no ketosis
HHS is defined by a very high OSMOLALITY (above 320 mOsm/kg) due to extreme hyperglycaemia (often above 30 mmol/L) and dehydration, with LITTLE OR NO KETOSIS (ketones below 3 mmol/L) and no significant acidosis (pH above 7.3). It mostly affects older type 2 diabetics and develops over days.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• pH below 7.0 with a very high anion gap — pH below 7.0 with a very high anion gap describes severe DKA.
• Rapid onset over 1–2 hours in young people — Rapid onset in young people describes DKA.
• Blood glucose below 11 mmol/L with high ketones — Glucose below 11 with high ketones describes euglycaemic DKA.

⭐ Key point: In HHS, fluids come first (and alone often lower glucose). Lower glucose slowly, and watch for clots and cerebral oedema.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q98$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1642, $s$By KDIGO criteria, AKI stage 3 includes urine output below 0.3 mL/kg/h for 24 hours or:$s$, $s$["Serum creatinine 1.2 times the baseline", "Urine output 0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 hours", "A single raised urea level", "Serum creatinine 3 times the baseline"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Serum creatinine 3 times the baseline
KDIGO Stage 3 AKI includes ANY of: serum CREATININE 3 TIMES baseline (or at least 354 µmol/L), urine output below 0.3 mL/kg/hour for 24 hours or more, ANURIA for 12 hours or more, or starting renal replacement therapy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Serum creatinine 1.2 times the baseline — Creatinine 1.2 times baseline doesn't meet even Stage 1 (which is 1.5 times).
• Urine output 0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 hours — 0.5 mL/kg/hour for 6 hours is Stage 1.
• A single raised urea level — A single raised urea isn't a KDIGO criterion.

⭐ Key point: Stage 1: creatinine 1.5–1.9 times; Stage 2: 2.0–2.9 times; Stage 3: 3 times or more.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q99$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1643, $s$Which is an urgent indication for renal replacement therapy in AKI?$s$, $s$["Urea of 9 mmol/L on admission", "Mild metabolic acidosis with pH 7.33", "Refractory hyperkalaemia despite medical treatment", "Creatinine rising slowly with good urine output"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Refractory hyperkalaemia despite medical treatment
Urgent dialysis (renal replacement therapy) is indicated for complications that can't be controlled medically. REFRACTORY HYPERKALAEMIA despite treatment is a classic urgent indication, because it can cause fatal arrhythmias. Remember AEIOU: Acidosis, Electrolytes (K⁺), Intoxication, Overload, Uraemic complications.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Urea of 9 mmol/L on admission — A urea of 9 mmol/L is only mildly raised.
• Mild metabolic acidosis with pH 7.33 — Mild acidosis (pH 7.33) can be managed medically.
• Creatinine rising slowly with good urine output — Slowly rising creatinine with good urine output doesn't need urgent dialysis.

⭐ Key point: Uraemic complications that need dialysis: pericarditis, encephalopathy and bleeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q100$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1644, $s$This condition describe a birth defect where some nerve cells are missing in the large intestine:$s$, $s$["Appendicitis", "Birth stenosis", "Hirsh sprung disease", "Pyloric Hirsh sprung"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hirsh sprung disease
HIRSCHSPRUNG'S DISEASE is a birth defect where nerve cells (ganglion cells) are MISSING in part of the large intestine (usually the rectum and sigmoid colon). Without nerves, that segment can't relax and push stool along, causing constipation, distension, vomiting, and delayed passage of meconium (beyond 48 hours).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Appendicitis — Appendicitis is inflammation of the appendix.
• Birth stenosis — "Birth stenosis" isn't a recognised condition.
• Pyloric Hirsh sprung — "Pyloric Hirschsprung" isn't a real term (pyloric stenosis is a different condition).

⭐ Key point: Diagnosed by rectal biopsy (no ganglion cells). Treated with a pull-through operation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q128$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1645, $s$The osmolality of the renal interstitium increases from the cortex toward the tip of the inner medulla, reaching approximately what value in a maximally concentrating human kidney?$s$, $s$["About 1200 mOsm/kg", "About 300 mOsm/kg", "About 600 mOsm/kg at the papilla", "About 2500 mOsm/kg"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: About 1200 mOsm/kg
The loop of Henle creates a gradient of increasing saltiness (osmolality) in the kidney interstitium: about 300 mOsm/kg in the cortex, rising to about 1200 mOsm/kg at the tip of the inner medulla (papilla) in a maximally concentrating kidney. This lets ADH concentrate urine up to about 1200 mOsm/kg.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• About 300 mOsm/kg — 300 mOsm/kg is the value in the cortex (like plasma).
• About 600 mOsm/kg at the papilla — 600 mOsm/kg is only about halfway.
• About 2500 mOsm/kg — 2500 mOsm/kg is far higher than the human kidney can reach.

⭐ Key point: Loop diuretics (furosemide) wash out this gradient, so the kidney can't concentrate urine.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q2$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1646, $s$Filtration of blood in the nephron primarily occurs at the:$s$, $s$["Collecting duct", "Loop of Henle", "Glomerulus", "Distal convoluted tubule"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Glomerulus
FILTRATION of blood happens at the GLOMERULUS, a tuft of capillaries inside Bowman's capsule. High pressure pushes water, salts, glucose, urea and small molecules through the capillary wall into the capsule (the filtrate). Blood cells and large proteins stay in the blood.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Collecting duct — The collecting duct concentrates urine under ADH.
• Loop of Henle — The loop of Henle creates the concentration gradient.
• Distal convoluted tubule — The distal tubule fine-tunes sodium, potassium and acid.

⭐ Key point: Normal GFR is about 120 mL/min (180 L/day), and about 99% of the filtrate is reabsorbed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q7$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1647, $s$A patient with syndrome of inappropriate ADH secretion (SIADH) causing symptomatic hyponatremia is treated with fluid restriction. Which additional mechanism-based therapy directly antagonizes ADH's renal action?$s$, $s$["Exogenous desmopressin (DDAVP)", "Vasopressin receptor antagonists (vaptans)", "IV isotonic saline as first-line for all severities", "Loop diuretics as monotherapy without sodium replacement"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vasopressin receptor antagonists (vaptans)
In SIADH, excess ADH acts on V2 receptors in the kidney collecting ducts to keep water. VASOPRESSIN RECEPTOR ANTAGONISTS ("vaptans", such as tolvaptan and conivaptan) BLOCK these V2 receptors, so the kidneys excrete more free water (aquaresis) and the sodium rises.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Exogenous desmopressin (DDAVP) — Desmopressin acts LIKE ADH and would worsen SIADH.
• IV isotonic saline as first-line for all severities — Isotonic saline isn't first-line for all SIADH and can worsen hyponatraemia.
• Loop diuretics as monotherapy without sodium replacement — Loop diuretics alone without sodium replacement can worsen hyponatraemia.

⭐ Key point: With vaptans, sodium can rise too fast. Monitor sodium closely to avoid osmotic demyelination.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q14$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1648, $s$Which organs are both involved in the synthesis of calcitriol and erythropoietin?$s$, $s$["Liver and kidneys", "Thyroid and thymus", "Anterior and posterior pituitary", "Parathyroids and pancreatic islets"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Liver and kidneys
CALCITRIOL (active vitamin D) needs two steps: the LIVER adds a hydroxyl group (25-hydroxylation), then the KIDNEY adds another (1-hydroxylation). ERYTHROPOIETIN (which stimulates red cell production) is made mainly by the KIDNEYS, with a smaller amount from the LIVER. So the liver and kidneys are involved in both.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Thyroid and thymus — The thyroid and thymus don't make either.
• Anterior and posterior pituitary — The pituitary doesn't make either.
• Parathyroids and pancreatic islets — The parathyroids stimulate calcitriol production but don't make erythropoietin.

⭐ Key point: In CKD, low calcitriol causes low calcium and bone disease, and low erythropoietin causes anaemia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q16$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1649, $s$A nurse monitors a thyroidectomy patient for thyroid crisis. The most important vital sign to observe is:$s$, $s$["Pulse rate", "Blood pressure", "Respiratory rate", "Temperature"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pulse rate
Thyroid crisis (storm) after thyroidectomy releases a flood of thyroid hormone, causing a very fast heart rate (tachycardia above 140, AF), fever, agitation and heart failure. The PULSE RATE is the most important vital sign to watch, because tachycardia is usually the earliest and most telling sign.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Blood pressure — BP may change, but tachycardia appears earlier.
• Respiratory rate — Respiratory rate is important but is not the key early sign.
• Temperature — Temperature rises in thyroid storm, but tachycardia is often noticed first.

⭐ Key point: Thyroid storm treatment: beta-blockers (propranolol), anti-thyroid drugs (PTU), iodine, steroids and cooling.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q51$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1650, $s$After a head injury, urine output rises to 400 mL/h with low urine specific gravity and serum Na⁺ 152 mmol/L. The nurse suspects:$s$, $s$["Acute tubular necrosis; expect dialysis", "Diabetes insipidus; expect desmopressin", "SIADH; expect fluid restriction", "Cerebral salt wasting; expect hypertonic saline"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Diabetes insipidus; expect desmopressin
After head injury, damage to the hypothalamus or pituitary can stop ADH release: cranial DIABETES INSIPIDUS. Without ADH, the kidneys pass large volumes of DILUTE urine (400 mL/hour, low specific gravity), so the blood becomes concentrated, with high SODIUM (152). Treatment is DESMOPRESSIN (synthetic ADH) and replacing fluid losses.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute tubular necrosis; expect dialysis — ATN causes LOW urine output usually, and wouldn't cause high sodium this way.
• SIADH; expect fluid restriction — SIADH causes LOW urine output and LOW sodium.
• Cerebral salt wasting; expect hypertonic saline — Cerebral salt wasting causes LOW sodium.

⭐ Key point: DI: very high urine output, urine SG below 1.005, serum Na above 145. Monitor urine output hourly and sodium regularly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q79$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1651, $s$Daily weight monitoring in a patient with renal or cardiac fluid balance concerns is valuable because:$s$, $s$["It replaces the need for intake/output charting entirely", "It has no correlation with fluid status", "It only reflects nutritional status", "Rapid weight change closely reflects fluid gain or loss"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Rapid weight change closely reflects fluid gain or loss
DAILY WEIGHT is valuable because rapid weight change closely reflects FLUID gain or loss (1 kg ≈ 1 L of fluid). In heart or kidney patients, a sudden gain (such as more than 1–2 kg in a day) suggests fluid retention; a loss suggests diuresis or dehydration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It replaces the need for intake/output charting entirely — It doesn't replace intake/output charting; both are used together.
• It has no correlation with fluid status — It correlates closely with fluid status.
• It only reflects nutritional status — Short-term changes reflect FLUID, not nutrition (nutritional changes are slower).

⭐ Key point: Weigh at the same time each day, on the same scales, with the same clothing.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q81$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1652, $s$Which serum marker is increasingly used as an earlier and more specific indicator of reduced GFR than creatinine, particularly in patients with reduced muscle mass?$s$, $s$["C-reactive protein", "Alkaline phosphatase", "Troponin", "Cystatin C"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cystatin C
CYSTATIN C is a protein made at a CONSTANT rate by all nucleated cells and filtered by the kidneys. Its blood level rises EARLIER than creatinine when GFR falls, and it isn't affected by muscle mass. This makes it useful in the elderly, malnourished, amputees and ICU patients with low muscle mass, where creatinine can look falsely normal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• C-reactive protein — CRP is an inflammation marker.
• Alkaline phosphatase — Alkaline phosphatase is a liver and bone enzyme.
• Troponin — Troponin is a heart muscle marker.

⭐ Key point: Creatinine depends on muscle mass. In frail or wasted patients, a normal creatinine may hide reduced kidney function.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q82$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1653, $s$Which viral hepatitis is the most common viral cause of fulminant hepatic failure?$s$, $s$["Hepatitis C", "Hepatitis B", "Hepatitis A", "Hepatitis E only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hepatitis B
HEPATITIS B is the most common VIRAL cause of fulminant (acute) liver failure worldwide, and in Nigeria. Acute infection, flare-ups of chronic infection, or reactivation (with chemotherapy or steroids) can cause sudden severe liver failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hepatitis C — Hepatitis C rarely causes fulminant failure.
• Hepatitis A — Hepatitis A can cause it but is less common than B.
• Hepatitis E only — Hepatitis E can cause it (especially in pregnancy), but B is more common overall.

⭐ Key point: Hepatitis B is preventable by vaccination. All health workers should be vaccinated and check their antibody levels.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q83$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1654, $s$A client with cirrhosis is taught about thiamine-rich foods. Which food shows the best understanding?$s$, $s$["Chicken", "Broccoli", "Milk", "Pork"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pork
Patients with cirrhosis (especially alcohol-related) are often THIAMINE (vitamin B1) deficient, which can cause Wernicke's encephalopathy. PORK is one of the richest food sources of thiamine. Others: whole grains, legumes (beans, peas), nuts and fortified cereals.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chicken — Chicken contains some thiamine but much less than pork.
• Broccoli — Broccoli contains small amounts.
• Milk — Milk contains small amounts.

⭐ Key point: In alcohol-related liver disease, give thiamine supplements, especially before glucose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q85$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1655, $s$Protein reintroduction in PSE patients is generally recommended at:$s$, $s$["No restriction needed", "100–150g/day", "0–10g/day", "20–50g/day"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 20–50g/day
In hepatic (portosystemic) encephalopathy, old practice was to severely restrict protein to reduce ammonia. Current advice is NOT to restrict protein severely, because malnutrition worsens outcomes. In this question's keyed answer, protein is REINTRODUCED at a moderate level (20–50 g/day), then increased gradually as tolerated. Modern guidelines aim for a normal protein intake (1.2–1.5 g/kg/day).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No restriction needed — No restriction at all isn't the keyed answer, though modern practice is close to normal intake.
• 100–150g/day — 100–150 g/day is high for initial reintroduction.
• 0–10g/day — 0–10 g/day would cause malnutrition and muscle wasting.

⭐ Key point: Treat hepatic encephalopathy with lactulose (2–3 soft stools a day) and rifaximin, and correct the triggers (bleeding, infection, constipation, sedatives).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q87$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1656, $s$Which combination of medications places a critically ill patient at greatest risk of prerenal AKI due to loss of compensatory glomerular autoregulation?$s$, $s$["ACE inhibitors and NSAIDs", "Calcium channel blockers and insulin", "Proton pump inhibitors and antihistamines", "Beta blockers and statins"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ACE inhibitors and NSAIDs
The kidney keeps its filtration rate stable (autoregulation) by adjusting two arterioles: it WIDENS the afferent (incoming) arteriole using prostaglandins, and TIGHTENS the efferent (outgoing) arteriole using angiotensin II. NSAIDs block prostaglandins (the afferent arteriole narrows), and ACE inhibitors block angiotensin II (the efferent arteriole relaxes). Together, they remove both protections, causing a big fall in GFR (pre-renal AKI), especially in dehydration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Calcium channel blockers and insulin — Calcium channel blockers and insulin don't cause this.
• Proton pump inhibitors and antihistamines — PPIs and antihistamines don't cause this (PPIs can rarely cause interstitial nephritis).
• Beta blockers and statins — Beta-blockers and statins don't cause this.

⭐ Key point: The "triple whammy" (ACE inhibitor or ARB + diuretic + NSAID) is especially dangerous for the kidneys.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q90$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1657, $s$A bilirubin level above what value is used as a transplant criterion in FHF?$s$, $s$[">50 micromol/L", ">100 micromol/L", ">1000 micromol/L", ">300 micromol/L"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: >300 micromol/L
The King's College criteria for liver transplant in non-paracetamol acute liver failure include a BILIRUBIN above 300 µmol/L (17.5 mg/dL), with other factors (INR above 3.5, age, cause, and time from jaundice to encephalopathy). Very high bilirubin shows severe liver dysfunction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• >50 micromol/L — Above 50 µmol/L is mild jaundice.
• >100 micromol/L — Above 100 µmol/L is moderate jaundice.
• >1000 micromol/L — Above 1000 µmol/L is too high a threshold.

⭐ Key point: Also in King's criteria: INR above 6.5 alone, or 3 of 5 factors (age, cause, jaundice-to-encephalopathy time, INR, bilirubin).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q91$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1658, $s$A patient's TPN is discontinued. What is the most appropriate nursing action to prevent a rapid drop in blood glucose (hypoglycemia)?$s$, $s$["Administering a dose of insulin", "Immediately capping the central line", "Decreasing the TPN rate to half for 24 hours", "Hanging a bag of D10W (10% Dextrose in Water)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hanging a bag of D10W (10% Dextrose in Water)
TPN contains a lot of glucose, so the pancreas makes extra insulin. If TPN stops suddenly, the high insulin continues for a while and blood glucose can DROP dangerously (rebound HYPOGLYCAEMIA). Hanging 10% DEXTROSE (D10W) temporarily, or tapering the TPN rate gradually over 1–2 hours, prevents this.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administering a dose of insulin — Insulin would worsen hypoglycaemia.
• Immediately capping the central line — Capping the line immediately stops glucose suddenly.
• Decreasing the TPN rate to half for 24 hours — Halving the rate for 24 hours isn't the usual approach (tapering is over 1–2 hours).

⭐ Key point: Check blood glucose 30–60 minutes after stopping TPN.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q95$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1659, $s$During CRRT with regional citrate anticoagulation, the nurse must closely monitor:$s$, $s$["Urine specific gravity every hour", "Systemic and post-filter ionised calcium", "Serum troponin every hour", "Serum amylase and lipase every 6 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Systemic and post-filter ionised calcium
Citrate anticoagulation in CRRT works by binding CALCIUM in the circuit, which stops clotting. Monitor POST-FILTER ionised calcium (to check citrate is working, target about 0.25–0.35 mmol/L) and SYSTEMIC (patient) ionised calcium (to ensure the patient has enough, target about 1.0–1.2 mmol/L), with calcium replaced by infusion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Urine specific gravity every hour — Urine specific gravity isn't relevant.
• Serum troponin every hour — Troponin isn't related to citrate.
• Serum amylase and lipase every 6 hours — Amylase and lipase aren't related.

⭐ Key point: Citrate accumulation (liver failure) shows a low ionised calcium with a high total calcium (total/ionised ratio above 2.5) and metabolic acidosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q96$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1660, $s$In pre-renal AKI, urine tests usually show:$s$, $s$["Muddy brown granular casts", "Red cell casts and heavy proteinuria", "Urine Na⁺ above 40 mmol/L and FENa above 2%", "Urine Na⁺ below 20 mmol/L and FENa below 1%"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Urine Na⁺ below 20 mmol/L and FENa below 1%
In PRE-RENAL AKI (from dehydration, low BP), the kidney tubules are still healthy, so they hold on to SODIUM to restore volume. Urine sodium is LOW (below 20 mmol/L), and the fractional excretion of sodium (FENa) is below 1%. The urine is concentrated, and the urine sediment is bland.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Muddy brown granular casts — Muddy brown casts suggest ATN.
• Red cell casts and heavy proteinuria — Red cell casts and heavy proteinuria suggest glomerulonephritis.
• Urine Na⁺ above 40 mmol/L and FENa above 2% — High urine sodium and FENa above 2% suggest ATN (damaged tubules).

⭐ Key point: Diuretics raise urine sodium, making FENa unreliable; use the fractional excretion of urea instead.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q97$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1661, $s$Abdominal compartment syndrome is defined as intra-abdominal (bladder) pressure above 20 mmHg with:$s$, $s$["A normal urine output", "Bowel sounds present", "New organ dysfunction", "A soft abdomen on palpation"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: New organ dysfunction
Abdominal compartment syndrome (ACS) is defined as intra-abdominal pressure (bladder pressure) ABOVE 20 mmHg (sustained) WITH NEW ORGAN DYSFUNCTION: kidney failure (low urine output), breathing problems (high airway pressures), low cardiac output, or gut ischaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal urine output — ACS usually causes LOW urine output.
• Bowel sounds present — Bowel sounds don't define ACS.
• A soft abdomen on palpation — The abdomen is usually TENSE and distended, not soft.

⭐ Key point: Measure bladder pressure at end-expiration, supine, zeroed at the mid-axillary line. Treat by decompression (NG and rectal tubes, drainage, surgery).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q98$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1662, $s$Suspected variceal bleeding in a cirrhotic patient should be treated before endoscopy with:$s$, $s$["Heparin infusion and aspirin", "Oral lactulose alone", "Large-volume saline to Hb above 12 g/dL", "Terlipressin and IV antibiotics"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Terlipressin and IV antibiotics
Variceal bleeding in cirrhosis is an emergency. Before endoscopy, give TERLIPRESSIN (a vasopressin analogue that lowers portal pressure and reduces bleeding) and IV ANTIBIOTICS (such as ceftriaxone; infection is common and increases rebleeding and death). Then do endoscopic band ligation. Transfuse cautiously to Hb 7–8 g/dL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Heparin infusion and aspirin — Heparin and aspirin would worsen bleeding.
• Oral lactulose alone — Lactulose treats encephalopathy, not the bleeding.
• Large-volume saline to Hb above 12 g/dL — Over-transfusion (to Hb above 12) RAISES portal pressure and increases bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Other measures: protect the airway, correct clotting if needed, and use a Sengstaken–Blakemore tube as a bridge for uncontrolled bleeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q99$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1663, $s$Compound A, a potentially nephrotoxic breakdown product identified in animal studies, forms when which agent is used with low fresh gas flows and desiccated CO2 absorbent?$s$, $s$["Isoflurane", "Desflurane", "Sevoflurane", "Nitrous oxide"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sevoflurane
SEVOFLURANE can break down when it contacts CO₂ absorbents (especially dry ones), producing COMPOUND A, which was toxic to the kidneys in animal studies. This happens more with LOW fresh gas flows. Using adequate gas flows (at least 1–2 L/min) reduces Compound A.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Isoflurane — Isoflurane doesn't produce significant Compound A.
• Desflurane — Desflurane produces carbon monoxide with dry absorbent, not Compound A.
• Nitrous oxide — Nitrous oxide doesn't produce Compound A.

⭐ Key point: There's no clear evidence of kidney harm in humans, but low-flow limits are often still followed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q155$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1664, $s$Accidental removal of the parathyroid glands during thyroidectomy results in:$s$, $s$["Hyperthyroidism", "Hyperkalaemia", "Hypocalcaemia", "Hypoglycaemia"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypocalcaemia
The parathyroid glands (usually four, behind the thyroid) make PARATHYROID HORMONE (PTH), which keeps blood calcium normal. If they are accidentally removed or damaged during thyroidectomy, PTH falls and HYPOCALCAEMIA develops (usually within 24–48 hours), with tingling, cramps, Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs, and laryngospasm in severe cases.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperthyroidism — Removing the parathyroids doesn't cause hyperthyroidism.
• Hyperkalaemia — It doesn't cause high potassium.
• Hypoglycaemia — It doesn't cause low glucose.

⭐ Key point: Check calcium after thyroid surgery and keep IV calcium gluconate available.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q4$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1665, $s$The kidney has more of which structure than any of the others listed?$s$, $s$["Afferent arterioles", "Arcuate arteries", "Medullary pyramids", "Minor calyces"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Afferent arterioles
Each kidney has about 1 million NEPHRONS, and each nephron has its own AFFERENT ARTERIOLE (the small artery bringing blood into the glomerulus). So there are far more afferent arterioles than arcuate arteries, medullary pyramids or minor calyces.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Arcuate arteries — Arcuate arteries are fewer (dozens).
• Medullary pyramids — Medullary pyramids number about 8–18 per kidney.
• Minor calyces — Minor calyces number about 8–18 per kidney.

⭐ Key point: The afferent arteriole widens and narrows to keep GFR stable (autoregulation).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q8$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1666, $s$Renal autoregulation maintains a relatively constant glomerular filtration rate across which approximate range of mean arterial pressure?$s$, $s$["60-80 mmHg", "80-180 mmHg", "40-60 mmHg", "180-220 mmHg"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 80-180 mmHg
The kidneys protect their filtration through AUTOREGULATION. They widen or narrow the afferent arteriole (myogenic response) and use tubuloglomerular feedback to keep renal blood flow and GFR roughly constant when the mean arterial pressure is between about 80 and 180 mmHg. Below about 80, GFR falls with BP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 60-80 mmHg — 60–80 mmHg is below the autoregulation range.
• 40-60 mmHg — 40–60 mmHg is far too low.
• 180-220 mmHg — 180–220 mmHg is above the range.

⭐ Key point: In chronic hypertension, the autoregulation range shifts higher, so these patients may need a higher MAP to protect the kidneys.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q16$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1667, $s$Propranolol prophylaxis for variceal bleeding aims to reduce heart rate by approximately:$s$, $s$["10% or to 90bpm", "25% or to 55bpm", "No specific target", "50% or to 40bpm"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 25% or to 55bpm
Non-selective beta-blockers (propranolol, nadolol) reduce portal pressure in cirrhosis, lowering the risk of variceal bleeding. The dose is increased until the resting heart rate falls by about 25% or to about 55 bpm, which indicates enough beta-blockade, as long as BP stays acceptable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10% or to 90bpm — 10% or 90 bpm isn't enough reduction.
• No specific target — A target IS used.
• 50% or to 40bpm — 50% or 40 bpm is too much (dangerous bradycardia).

⭐ Key point: Endoscopic band ligation is an alternative or addition for large varices.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q47$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1668, $s$A patient with cirrhosis and a GI bleed carries higher rebleeding risk mainly because of:$s$, $s$["Improved clotting function", "Lower blood pressure at baseline only", "No increased risk compared to others", "Portal hypertension and poor clotting"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Portal hypertension and poor clotting
Cirrhosis causes PORTAL HYPERTENSION (high pressure in the portal vein), which creates fragile, enlarged veins (VARICES) in the oesophagus and stomach that bleed easily. The damaged liver also makes fewer CLOTTING factors and the spleen traps platelets, so bleeding is harder to stop and more likely to recur.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved clotting function — Clotting is WORSE, not improved, in cirrhosis.
• Lower blood pressure at baseline only — Low BP alone isn't the main reason.
• No increased risk compared to others — The risk IS higher than in other patients.

⭐ Key point: Prevent rebleeding: beta-blockers, band ligation, and treating the cause (stopping alcohol).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q51$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1669, $s$Preoperative preparation for thyroidectomy includes administering Lugol's iodine to:$s$, $s$["Reduce laryngeal spasm", "Decrease gland vascularity", "Increase hormone release", "Induce euthyroid state"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Decrease gland vascularity
LUGOL'S IODINE is given for about 10–14 days before thyroidectomy in hyperthyroid patients (especially Graves' disease). It DECREASES the BLOOD SUPPLY (vascularity) of the thyroid and makes it FIRMER, reducing bleeding during surgery. It also briefly blocks thyroid hormone release.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reduce laryngeal spasm — It doesn't reduce laryngeal spasm.
• Increase hormone release — It REDUCES, not increases, hormone release.
• Induce euthyroid state — Anti-thyroid drugs (carbimazole) achieve the euthyroid state; iodine's main pre-op role is reducing vascularity.

⭐ Key point: Patients must be euthyroid (normal thyroid levels) before surgery, to avoid thyroid storm.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q82$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1670, $s$A farmer presents with goitre and reports eating cassava regularly. Which mechanism explains his condition?$s$, $s$["Cassava enhances thyroglobulin coupling", "Cassava reduces TRH secretion", "Cassava inhibits iodine trapping", "Cassava increases TSH secretion"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cassava inhibits iodine trapping
Cassava contains cyanogenic compounds (linamarin), which release cyanide and then THIOCYANATE in the body. Thiocyanate blocks the thyroid's ability to TRAP IODINE, so less thyroid hormone is made. TSH rises and the gland enlarges (GOITRE), especially where iodine is already lacking (as in parts of Nigeria).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cassava enhances thyroglobulin coupling — Cassava doesn't enhance thyroglobulin coupling.
• Cassava reduces TRH secretion — It doesn't directly reduce TRH.
• Cassava increases TSH secretion — TSH rises as a RESULT of low hormone; cassava doesn't directly increase TSH secretion.

⭐ Key point: Proper cassava processing (soaking, fermenting, cooking) removes most cyanide. Iodised salt prevents goitre.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q83$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1671, $s$Chronic transplant rejection typically develops over:$s$, $s$["Minutes to hours", "1–4 days", "Months to years", "Days to weeks"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Months to years
CHRONIC transplant rejection develops slowly over MONTHS to YEARS. It involves gradual scarring and narrowing of the blood vessels and tissue of the transplanted organ, leading to slow loss of function. It is the main cause of long-term graft loss.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Minutes to hours — Minutes to hours describes HYPERACUTE rejection.
• 1–4 days — 1–4 days is too short.
• Days to weeks — Days to weeks describes ACUTE rejection.

⭐ Key point: Lifelong immunosuppression, good drug adherence, and regular monitoring reduce rejection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q84$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1672, $s$Pre-operative anti-thyroid drug (ATD) treatment before thyroid surgery is typically given for:$s$, $s$["1–2 years", "No pre-operative treatment is needed", "1–2 days", "4–6 weeks"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 4–6 weeks
Before thyroid surgery for hyperthyroidism, ANTI-THYROID DRUGS (carbimazole or propylthiouracil) are given for about 4–6 WEEKS (often 6–8 weeks) to make the patient EUTHYROID (normal thyroid hormone levels). Operating on an uncontrolled hyperthyroid patient risks thyroid storm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1–2 years — 1–2 years is the length of a full medical treatment course, not pre-operative preparation.
• No pre-operative treatment is needed — Pre-operative treatment IS needed.
• 1–2 days — 1–2 days isn't long enough to normalise hormone levels.

⭐ Key point: Beta-blockers control symptoms (fast heart rate, tremor) while anti-thyroid drugs take effect.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q86$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1673, $s$A patient has an elevated TSH with a low free T4. This laboratory pattern is most consistent with which condition?$s$, $s$["Primary hypothyroidism", "Secondary (central) hypothyroidism", "Subclinical hyperthyroidism", "Primary hyperthyroidism"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Primary hypothyroidism
In PRIMARY HYPOTHYROIDISM, the THYROID gland itself fails, so free T4 is LOW. The pituitary senses this and releases MORE TSH to stimulate the thyroid, so TSH is HIGH. High TSH with low T4 is the classic pattern.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Secondary (central) hypothyroidism — Secondary (central) hypothyroidism has LOW TSH with low T4 (the pituitary fails).
• Subclinical hyperthyroidism — Subclinical hyperthyroidism has low TSH with normal T4.
• Primary hyperthyroidism — Primary hyperthyroidism has LOW TSH with HIGH T4.

⭐ Key point: The commonest cause of primary hypothyroidism worldwide is iodine deficiency; in iodine-sufficient areas it is Hashimoto's thyroiditis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q88$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1674, $s$A patient presents with palpitations, tremor, exophthalmos, and weight loss. The most probable diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Thyroid cyst", "Hashimoto's disease", "Toxic goitre", "Simple goitre"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Toxic goitre
Palpitations, tremor, weight loss and EXOPHTHALMOS (bulging eyes) are signs of HYPERTHYROIDISM. With a goitre, this is a TOXIC GOITRE (toxic diffuse goitre, Graves' disease, which classically causes exophthalmos). The gland makes too much thyroid hormone.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Thyroid cyst — A thyroid cyst is a fluid-filled lump, usually without hormone excess.
• Hashimoto's disease — Hashimoto's disease usually causes HYPOthyroidism.
• Simple goitre — A simple goitre doesn't cause hyperthyroid symptoms.

⭐ Key point: Treatment options: anti-thyroid drugs, radioactive iodine, or surgery (thyroidectomy).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q89$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1675, $s$The most life-threatening intraoperative complication of thyroidectomy is:$s$, $s$["Hemorrhage", "Hypocalcaemia", "Hoarseness", "Hypothyroidism"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hemorrhage
The thyroid has a very rich blood supply. HAEMORRHAGE during or after thyroidectomy is the most life-threatening complication: bleeding into the closed space of the neck can form a haematoma that COMPRESSES the TRACHEA and blocks the airway within minutes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypocalcaemia — Hypocalcaemia is common but usually develops after surgery and is treatable.
• Hoarseness — Hoarseness (recurrent laryngeal nerve injury) is serious but less immediately life-threatening.
• Hypothyroidism — Hypothyroidism develops later and is easily treated.

⭐ Key point: If a neck haematoma causes breathing difficulty, open the wound (remove the clips or sutures) at the bedside to release the pressure, and call for help.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q90$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1676, $s$During thyroid surgery, which structure must be preserved to prevent hypocalcaemia?$s$, $s$["Submandibular duct", "Recurrent laryngeal nerve", "Carotid artery", "Parathyroid glands"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Parathyroid glands
The PARATHYROID glands (usually four) sit behind or within the thyroid. They make PTH, which controls blood CALCIUM. They must be PRESERVED during thyroid surgery to prevent HYPOCALCAEMIA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Submandibular duct — The submandibular duct is in the floor of the mouth, not near the thyroid.
• Recurrent laryngeal nerve — The recurrent laryngeal nerve must also be preserved, but to prevent HOARSENESS, not hypocalcaemia.
• Carotid artery — The carotid artery isn't related to calcium.

⭐ Key point: After thyroidectomy, watch for tingling, cramps and Chvostek's and Trousseau's signs (hypocalcaemia).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q91$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1677, $s$The central control pathway for thyroid hormone production follows the sequence:$s$, $s$["Thyroid → TSH → Pituitary → TRH → Hypothalamus", "Pituitary → Hypothalamus → TSH → TRH → Thyroid", "Hypothalamus → TRH → Pituitary → TSH → Thyroid", "TSH → Hypothalamus → TRH → Pituitary → Thyroid"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypothalamus → TRH → Pituitary → TSH → Thyroid
Thyroid hormone is controlled by a feedback loop. The HYPOTHALAMUS releases TRH (thyrotropin-releasing hormone), which tells the PITUITARY to release TSH (thyroid-stimulating hormone), which tells the THYROID to make T3 and T4. High T3 and T4 then switch off TRH and TSH (negative feedback).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Thyroid → TSH → Pituitary → TRH → Hypothalamus — That sequence is backwards.
• Pituitary → Hypothalamus → TSH → TRH → Thyroid — That sequence is in the wrong order.
• TSH → Hypothalamus → TRH → Pituitary → Thyroid — That sequence is in the wrong order.

⭐ Key point: The same pattern applies to other hormones, such as the hypothalamus → pituitary → adrenal axis (CRH → ACTH → cortisol).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q93$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1678, $s$A patient with fulminant hepatic failure develops worsening confusion and asterixis (flapping tremor). This presentation is most consistent with:$s$, $s$["Hypoglycaemia unrelated to liver disease", "Simple fatigue", "A normal finding with no significance", "Hepatic encephalopathy"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hepatic encephalopathy
In liver failure, the liver can't clear AMMONIA and other toxins, which reach the brain and cause HEPATIC ENCEPHALOPATHY. Signs: confusion, drowsiness, personality change, and ASTERIXIS (a flapping tremor of the outstretched hands), progressing to coma. Cerebral oedema can develop in fulminant failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypoglycaemia unrelated to liver disease — Hypoglycaemia can cause confusion but doesn't cause asterixis.
• Simple fatigue — Fatigue doesn't cause asterixis.
• A normal finding with no significance — These are significant, not normal, findings.

⭐ Key point: Treat with lactulose (2–3 soft stools a day) and rifaximin, and correct the triggers (bleeding, infection, constipation, electrolytes).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q94$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1679, $s$When giving lactulose for hepatic encephalopathy, the nurse aims for:$s$, $s$["No stools to prevent fluid loss", "2–3 soft stools per day", "10 or more liquid stools daily", "One hard stool every 3 days"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2–3 soft stools per day
LACTULOSE treats hepatic encephalopathy by drawing water into the bowel (softening stools), speeding the passage of stool, and acidifying the colon, which turns ammonia into ammonium that can't be absorbed. The aim is 2–3 SOFT STOOLS a day.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No stools to prevent fluid loss — No stools would let ammonia build up.
• 10 or more liquid stools daily — 10 or more liquid stools cause dehydration and low potassium, which can worsen encephalopathy.
• One hard stool every 3 days — A hard stool every 3 days means constipation, which worsens encephalopathy.

⭐ Key point: Look for and treat precipitants: GI bleeding, infection, constipation, sedatives, dehydration and electrolyte imbalance.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q96$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1680, $s$In thyroid storm, iodine (e.g. Lugol's) should be given:$s$, $s$["Instead of beta-blockers", "At least 1 hour after the antithyroid drug", "Only if the patient is hypothermic", "Before any antithyroid drug"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: At least 1 hour after the antithyroid drug
In thyroid storm, the ANTI-THYROID drug (PTU or carbimazole) is given FIRST to block the making of new hormone. IODINE (Lugol's) is given at least 1 HOUR AFTER. If iodine is given first, the thyroid uses it as raw material to make even MORE hormone, worsening the storm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Instead of beta-blockers — Iodine doesn't replace beta-blockers; they control the heart rate and symptoms.
• Only if the patient is hypothermic — Iodine isn't given only in hypothermia.
• Before any antithyroid drug — Iodine BEFORE anti-thyroid drugs can worsen the storm.

⭐ Key point: Thyroid storm treatment: beta-blockers, anti-thyroid drugs, iodine (after 1 hour), steroids and cooling.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q99$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1681, $s$Treatment of myxoedema coma includes IV levothyroxine, IV hydrocortisone and:$s$, $s$["Immediate propranolol infusion", "Strict fluid boluses of 5% dextrose", "Passive rewarming and support of breathing", "Rapid active external rewarming"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Passive rewarming and support of breathing
Myxoedema coma (severe hypothyroidism) is treated with IV LEVOTHYROXINE (or T3), IV HYDROCORTISONE (until adrenal insufficiency is excluded), PASSIVE REWARMING (warm blankets, warm room), and SUPPORT OF BREATHING (ventilation if CO₂ is high). Active rapid rewarming can cause vasodilation and circulatory collapse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate propranolol infusion — Propranolol would worsen the slow heart rate and low BP.
• Strict fluid boluses of 5% dextrose — 5% dextrose boluses can worsen low sodium.
• Rapid active external rewarming — Rapid active external rewarming can cause shock from vasodilation.

⭐ Key point: Also treat low sodium, low glucose and the trigger (infection, cold exposure, sedatives).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q100$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1682, $s$Children in DKA are at risk of cerebral oedema, so management includes:$s$, $s$["An insulin bolus of 1 unit/kg at the start", "Hypotonic fluid to correct sodium quickly", "Slow fluid replacement over 48 hours and no insulin bolus", "Rapid 20 mL/kg fluid boluses repeated hourly"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Slow fluid replacement over 48 hours and no insulin bolus
Children with DKA are at risk of CEREBRAL OEDEMA, especially if fluids are given too fast or glucose falls too quickly. Management includes SLOW fluid replacement over 48 HOURS, NO insulin bolus (start the infusion at 0.05–0.1 units/kg/hour, 1–2 hours after fluids begin), and close neurological monitoring.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An insulin bolus of 1 unit/kg at the start — An insulin bolus increases the risk of cerebral oedema and low potassium.
• Hypotonic fluid to correct sodium quickly — Hypotonic fluids increase the risk of cerebral oedema.
• Rapid 20 mL/kg fluid boluses repeated hourly — Rapid repeated boluses increase the risk of cerebral oedema.

⭐ Key point: Warning signs of cerebral oedema: headache, slowing heart rate, rising BP, falling GCS, irritability. Treat with mannitol or 3% saline.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q140$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1683, $s$Which class of immunosuppressant medications, including tacrolimus and cyclosporine, inhibits calcineurin to reduce T-cell activation but carries a significant risk of nephrotoxicity?$s$, $s$["Induction antibody agents", "Corticosteroids", "Calcineurin inhibitors", "Antimetabolites, such as mycophenolate"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Calcineurin inhibitors
CALCINEURIN INHIBITORS (tacrolimus and ciclosporin) block calcineurin, an enzyme that activates T-cells, reducing the immune response and preventing transplant rejection. A major side effect is NEPHROTOXICITY (kidney damage), as well as high BP, tremor, high potassium and diabetes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Induction antibody agents — Induction antibody agents (such as basiliximab) are used at the time of transplant.
• Corticosteroids — Corticosteroids have different side effects (diabetes, osteoporosis).
• Antimetabolites, such as mycophenolate — Antimetabolites (mycophenolate, azathioprine) mainly suppress the bone marrow.

⭐ Key point: Monitor tacrolimus and ciclosporin blood levels, kidney function, potassium and BP.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q146$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1684, $s$A patient taking ibuprofen 400 mg twice daily for chronic back pain develops leg oedema. This is most likely caused by:$s$, $s$["A low creatinine level", "A decrease in renal function", "An increase in plasma proteins", "An increased glomerular filtration rate"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A decrease in renal function
NSAIDs (like ibuprofen) block prostaglandins that keep the kidney's afferent arterioles open. Kidney blood flow and filtration (GFR) fall, especially in the elderly or dehydrated. The kidneys retain salt and water, causing OEDEMA, and kidney function DECREASES (rising creatinine).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A low creatinine level — Kidney damage RAISES creatinine, not lowers it.
• An increase in plasma proteins — NSAIDs don't increase plasma proteins.
• An increased glomerular filtration rate — NSAIDs DECREASE, not increase, GFR.

⭐ Key point: Avoid NSAIDs in kidney disease, heart failure, dehydration, and with ACE inhibitors and diuretics (the "triple whammy").$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q150$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B6 · Renal, GI, Liver, Endocrine & Metabolic Care$s$, false, 1685, $s$Which contrast media type is preferred in high-risk patients such as those with renal failure, cardiac failure, or diabetes mellitus?$s$, $s$["Non-ionic (Low Osmolar) contrast media", "Barium sulfate only", "Ionic (High Osmolar) contrast media", "No contrast is ever needed in high-risk patients"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Non-ionic (Low Osmolar) contrast media
In high-risk patients (kidney failure, heart failure, diabetes, dehydration), NON-IONIC LOW-OSMOLAR contrast media (LOCM) are preferred. They have lower osmolality and don't break into charged particles, so they cause fewer side effects, less contrast-induced kidney injury, and fewer allergic-type reactions than ionic high-osmolar agents.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Barium sulfate only — Barium is used for GI studies, not IV contrast.
• Ionic (High Osmolar) contrast media — Ionic high-osmolar contrast causes MORE side effects.
• No contrast is ever needed in high-risk patients — Contrast can still be used in high-risk patients if needed, with precautions.

⭐ Key point: Also hydrate well, use the lowest dose, stop nephrotoxic drugs, and check creatinine after contrast.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q181$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1686, $s$Before mobilising a post-operative or critically ill patient for the first time, the nurse should first:$s$, $s$["Skip any assessment and proceed directly to ambulation", "Assess haemodynamic stability, fall risk, and any lines/tubes that need securing", "Remove all monitoring equipment", "Wait until the patient requests it"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assess haemodynamic stability, fall risk, and any lines/tubes that need securing
EARLY MOBILISATION reduces complications (DVT, pneumonia, pressure ulcers, muscle wasting, delirium) and speeds recovery. But before the FIRST mobilisation of a post-operative or critically ill patient, the nurse must ASSESS SAFETY: (1) HAEMODYNAMIC STABILITY — heart rate, BP (risk of postural hypotension), SpO2, respiratory rate, need for vasopressors; (2) neurological state and ability to follow commands (sedation level, RASS); (3) FALL RISK, pain control, strength; (4) LINES, TUBES AND DRAINS — secure the ETT, central lines, arterial lines, catheters, chest drains, so they are not pulled out; (5) have enough staff and equipment (walker, chair, portable oxygen, monitor). Start slowly: sit up → dangle legs → stand → chair → walk; stop if the patient becomes dizzy, breathless or unstable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Skip any assessment and proceed directly to ambulation — SKIPPING assessment risks falls, fainting, accidental extubation or line removal.
• Remove all monitoring equipment — REMOVING ALL monitoring is unsafe — critically ill patients need monitoring during the first mobilisation.
• Wait until the patient requests it — WAITING until the patient asks delays recovery; early mobilisation is planned and encouraged by the team.

⭐ Key point: Before first mobilisation: check HAEMODYNAMICS, fall risk, pain, and SECURE lines/tubes; mobilise gradually with enough staff.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q155$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1687, $s$The Surviving Sepsis 'hour-1 bundle' includes which of the following priority actions?$s$, $s$["Obtain blood cultures, measure lactate, give broad-spectrum antibiotics, and begin fluid resuscitation", "Wait for culture results before starting any treatment", "Give oral antibiotics only", "Delay fluids until blood pressure normalises spontaneously"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Obtain blood cultures, measure lactate, give broad-spectrum antibiotics, and begin fluid resuscitation
The SURVIVING SEPSIS "HOUR-1 BUNDLE" includes: measure LACTATE (re-measure if above 2), obtain BLOOD CULTURES before antibiotics, give BROAD-SPECTRUM ANTIBIOTICS, begin FLUID RESUSCITATION (30 mL/kg crystalloid for hypotension or lactate ≥4), and start VASOPRESSORS if hypotensive during or after fluids (to keep MAP ≥65).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wait for culture results before starting any treatment — Waiting for cultures delays treatment.
• Give oral antibiotics only — Oral antibiotics are not adequate in sepsis.
• Delay fluids until blood pressure normalises spontaneously — Fluids should not be delayed.

⭐ Key point: Each hour of delay in antibiotics in septic shock increases mortality.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q114$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1688, $s$The immediate first step after a needle-stick injury is to:$s$, $s$["Ignore it if the source patient appears healthy", "Wash the area with soap and water and report the exposure per protocol", "Apply a tourniquet", "Wait 24 hours before taking any action"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Wash the area with soap and water and report the exposure per protocol
After a NEEDLE-STICK injury, immediately WASH the area with SOAP and WATER (encourage gentle bleeding, do not scrub or suck), and REPORT the exposure per protocol (occupational health, risk assessment, testing, and post-exposure prophylaxis if indicated).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignore it if the source patient appears healthy — Healthy appearance doesn't rule out infection.
• Apply a tourniquet — A tourniquet is not used.
• Wait 24 hours before taking any action — Waiting 24 hours delays PEP (most effective within hours).

⭐ Key point: HIV PEP should be started as soon as possible (ideally within 2 hours, no later than 72 hours).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q127$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1689, $s$A patient with suspected active pulmonary tuberculosis is admitted to a general ward by mistake. The priority nursing action is to:$s$, $s$["Simply close the patient's door with no other change", "Leave the patient where they are with no changes", "Discharge the patient immediately", "Move the patient to a negative-pressure isolation room and initiate airborne precautions with N95 respirators for staff"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Move the patient to a negative-pressure isolation room and initiate airborne precautions with N95 respirators for staff
PULMONARY TUBERCULOSIS spreads through the AIR in tiny droplet nuclei that stay suspended for hours and can travel between rooms. A patient with suspected active TB on an open general ward puts other patients (especially immunocompromised ones) and staff at risk. The PRIORITY is to MOVE THE PATIENT to a NEGATIVE-PRESSURE ISOLATION ROOM (or a single room with the door closed and good ventilation if none is available) and start AIRBORNE PRECAUTIONS, with staff wearing fitted N95/FFP3 RESPIRATORS. Give the patient a surgical mask when outside the room. Then collect sputum samples (smear, GeneXpert, culture) and inform the infection control team.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simply close the patient's door with no other change — Only closing the door is not enough; airborne precautions and proper isolation are needed.
• Leave the patient where they are with no changes — Leaving the patient on the open ward continues the exposure risk.
• Discharge the patient immediately — Discharging a possibly infectious, untreated patient is unsafe for the patient and the community.

⭐ Key point: Contact tracing may be needed for patients and staff exposed on the ward. Isolation usually continues until sputum tests are negative or after about 2 weeks of effective treatment with clinical improvement.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q150$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1690, $s$A patient develops fever, tachycardia, tachypnoea, and hypotension not responding to an initial fluid bolus, with a known urinary tract infection. This presentation is most consistent with:$s$, $s$["Septic shock", "Cardiogenic shock", "Simple dehydration", "Anxiety attack"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Septic shock
SEPTIC SHOCK is sepsis with circulatory failure: infection (UTI) causing FEVER, TACHYCARDIA, TACHYPNOEA and HYPOTENSION that does NOT RESPOND to an initial fluid bolus. It requires immediate antibiotics, further fluids, vasopressors (noradrenaline), and source control.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cardiogenic shock — Cardiogenic shock is caused by heart failure, not infection.
• Simple dehydration — Simple dehydration responds to fluids and doesn't cause fever.
• Anxiety attack — An anxiety attack doesn't cause hypotension and fever.

⭐ Key point: Sepsis Six: oxygen, cultures, antibiotics, fluids, lactate, urine output — within 1 hour.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q181$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1691, $s$A patient is suspected of carrying a multidrug-resistant organism (MDRO). What is the FIRST infection-control action?$s$, $s$["Remove all invasive devices", "Transfer the patient to another hospital", "Start broad-spectrum antibiotics", "Implement contact precautions"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Implement contact precautions
When a patient is suspected of carrying a MULTIDRUG-RESISTANT ORGANISM (MDRO) — e.g. MRSA, VRE, ESBL-producing or carbapenem-resistant bacteria — the FIRST action is to IMPLEMENT CONTACT PRECAUTIONS immediately, without waiting for confirmation: single room or cohorting, gloves and gown/apron for contact with the patient or surroundings, DEDICATED equipment (stethoscope, BP cuff), strict HAND HYGIENE, and signage. This prevents spread to other vulnerable ICU patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Remove all invasive devices — Invasive devices are removed only when no longer needed; removing all devices is not the first step.
• Transfer the patient to another hospital — Transferring the patient is not necessary and could spread the organism.
• Start broad-spectrum antibiotics — Antibiotics should be guided by culture results; colonisation alone may not need treatment.

⭐ Key point: Inform the infection prevention team, screen contacts if required by policy, and clean the environment thoroughly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q192$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1692, $s$A child with suspected Lassa fever is admitted. The nurse's first priority is:$s$, $s$["Relatives providing care freely at the bedside", "Admission to an open paediatric ward", "Strict isolation with full PPE", "Isolation only after results return"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Strict isolation with full PPE
Lassa fever spreads through blood and body fluids, so the FIRST PRIORITY is to protect staff and other patients: STRICT ISOLATION in a single room with FULL PPE (gloves, gown, face shield or goggles, mask). Inform the infection control team and NCDC (notifiable disease).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Relatives providing care freely at the bedside — Relatives caring freely spreads infection.
• Admission to an open paediatric ward — An open ward exposes other children.
• Isolation only after results return — Isolation must start as soon as Lassa is SUSPECTED, not after results.

⭐ Key point: Safe sharps disposal and proper waste management are also essential.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q116$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1693, $s$A postpartum woman has fever 38.9 °C, HR 125, BP 85/50, foul lochia and confusion. The nurse should immediately:$s$, $s$["Encourage breastfeeding to help the uterus contract", "Sepsis bundle: cultures, IV antibiotics within 1 hour, fluids", "Oral paracetamol and a review of the observations in 4 hours", "Oxytocin infusion and hourly observation only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sepsis bundle: cultures, IV antibiotics within 1 hour, fluids
This woman has SEPTIC SHOCK (fever, tachycardia, hypotension, confusion, foul lochia). The nurse should immediately start the SEPSIS BUNDLE: take blood CULTURES, give IV ANTIBIOTICS within 1 hour, give IV FLUIDS, measure lactate, give oxygen and monitor urine output.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Encourage breastfeeding to help the uterus contract — Breastfeeding doesn't treat sepsis.
• Oral paracetamol and a review of the observations in 4 hours — Paracetamol and waiting delays treatment.
• Oxytocin infusion and hourly observation only — Oxytocin isn't the main treatment for sepsis.

⭐ Key point: Look for the source (retained products, wound infection).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q131$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1694, $s$An ICU manager notices rising central line infections. The best first step is:$s$, $s$["Give antibiotic prophylaxis to all patients", "Ban the use of central lines in the unit", "Audit compliance with the central line bundle", "Discipline the nurse who was on duty"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Audit compliance with the central line bundle
When central line infections rise, the first step is to AUDIT COMPLIANCE with the CENTRAL LINE BUNDLE (hand hygiene, full barrier precautions during insertion, chlorhexidine skin prep, avoiding the femoral site, daily review of line need, and proper dressing care). This shows where practice is failing so that it can be corrected.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give antibiotic prophylaxis to all patients — Antibiotic prophylaxis for all increases resistance and doesn't address the cause.
• Ban the use of central lines in the unit — Banning central lines would harm patients who need them.
• Discipline the nurse who was on duty — Disciplining one nurse without investigation is unfair and doesn't address system problems.

⭐ Key point: CLABSI = central line-associated bloodstream infection. Remove lines as soon as they're not needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q175$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1695, $s$Puerperal sepsis can progress rapidly to septic shock. An early sign is:$s$, $s$["An increased appetite and good sleep", "Bradycardia with hypertension and headache", "A normal temperature with a calm patient", "Tachycardia and tachypnoea with abdominal tenderness"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tachycardia and tachypnoea with abdominal tenderness
Puerperal sepsis (infection of the genital tract after childbirth) can quickly progress to septic shock. Early signs include TACHYCARDIA (fast pulse), TACHYPNOEA (fast breathing) and abdominal or uterine TENDERNESS, often with foul-smelling lochia. Fever may be ABSENT in severe sepsis, so don't rely on temperature alone.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An increased appetite and good sleep — Increased appetite and good sleep are signs of health.
• Bradycardia with hypertension and headache — Bradycardia with hypertension doesn't fit sepsis.
• A normal temperature with a calm patient — A calm patient with normal temperature doesn't rule out sepsis, but it's not an early sign of it.

⭐ Key point: Use the MEOWS chart to spot deterioration early. Give the sepsis Hour-1 bundle: cultures, antibiotics, fluids and lactate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q123$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1696, $s$Chemical indicators (indicator tape) on packs show that:$s$, $s$["The pack is new and has not been opened", "The contents are definitely sterile inside", "The pack is free of all bacterial spores", "The pack was exposed to the sterilising process"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The pack was exposed to the sterilising process
Indicator tape (chemical indicator) changes colour when exposed to the sterilisation conditions (such as heat and steam). It only shows that the pack WENT THROUGH the sterilisation process. It doesn't prove the contents are sterile, because the inside may not have reached the right temperature for long enough.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The pack is new and has not been opened — The tape doesn't show whether the pack is new or unopened.
• The contents are definitely sterile inside — The tape doesn't prove sterility inside the pack.
• The pack is free of all bacterial spores — Only a BIOLOGICAL indicator (spore test) proves spores are killed.

⭐ Key point: Check packs before use: tape changed colour, pack intact and dry, within the expiry date.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q196$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1697, $s$Hospital-acquired infection is defined as an infection appearing:$s$, $s$["48 hours or more after admission, not incubating", "Only after the patient has been discharged", "Only in surgical patients after an operation", "Within 6 hours of admission to the hospital"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 48 hours or more after admission, not incubating
A hospital-acquired (nosocomial) infection is one that appears 48 HOURS OR MORE after admission and was NOT present or incubating on admission. It is caused by germs picked up in hospital (from staff hands, equipment, devices or the environment). ICU patients have the highest risk, because of invasive devices, weak immunity and antibiotics.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only after the patient has been discharged — HAIs can appear during the hospital stay; they don't only show after discharge (though some surgical site infections do).
• Only in surgical patients after an operation — HAIs occur in all patients, not only surgical ones.
• Within 6 hours of admission to the hospital — An infection within 6 hours of admission was almost certainly acquired before admission.

⭐ Key point: Common ICU HAIs: VAP, CLABSI (central line bloodstream infection), CAUTI (catheter urinary infection), and surgical site infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q198$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1698, $s$The nurse is monitoring a patient on TPN. Which of the following is an early sign of a catheter-related blood stream infection (CRBSI)?$s$, $s$["Fever and chills", "Refeeding syndrome", "Hyperglycemia", "Hypocalcemia"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fever and chills
A catheter-related bloodstream infection (CRBSI) causes FEVER and CHILLS (rigors), often when the line is flushed or the infusion is started. Patients on TPN are at high risk, because the nutrient-rich solution supports bacterial and fungal growth.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Refeeding syndrome — Refeeding syndrome is a metabolic problem (low phosphate), not a sign of infection.
• Hyperglycemia — High glucose can occur with TPN and infection, but it isn't the early sign.
• Hypocalcemia — Low calcium isn't a sign of infection.

⭐ Key point: Suspected CRBSI: take blood cultures from the line and a peripheral vein, inform the doctor, and consider removing the line.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q81$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1699, $s$The gold-standard method for confirming initial placement of a blindly inserted nasogastric or orogastric feeding tube before starting feeds is:$s$, $s$["Checking that the external tube length looks approximately correct", "Auscultation of air insufflation over the epigastrium (the \"whoosh test\")", "Abdominal or chest X-ray", "Observing the patient for coughing during insertion only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Abdominal or chest X-ray
The GOLD STANDARD to confirm the position of a blindly inserted feeding tube (NG or OG) before starting feeds is an X-RAY (chest and/or abdomen) showing the tip below the diaphragm in the stomach. It is especially important in ventilated or unconscious patients, who can't cough if the tube enters the airway.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Checking that the external tube length looks approximately correct — External tube length is useful for monitoring movement but doesn't confirm initial position.
• Auscultation of air insufflation over the epigastrium (the "whoosh test") — The "whoosh test" is UNRELIABLE and must NOT be used.
• Observing the patient for coughing during insertion only — Coughing during insertion may or may not occur; it doesn't confirm position.

⭐ Key point: A pH of 5.5 or below on aspirate is the first-line bedside test where available, but X-ray is the gold standard.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q195$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1700, $s$Three ICU patients develop the same resistant organism within a week. The manager's first step is to:$s$, $s$["Wait for more cases before taking any action", "Inform infection control and cohort or isolate the patients", "Stop the hand hygiene audits to save time", "Close the whole hospital to new admissions"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inform infection control and cohort or isolate the patients
Three patients with the same resistant organism within a week suggest a possible OUTBREAK (cross-transmission). The first step is to INFORM the INFECTION CONTROL team and ISOLATE or COHORT the affected patients (with contact precautions) to stop further spread. Then review practices, screen contacts and reinforce hand hygiene and cleaning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wait for more cases before taking any action — Waiting for more cases allows further spread.
• Stop the hand hygiene audits to save time — Stopping hand hygiene audits would make things worse.
• Close the whole hospital to new admissions — Closing the whole hospital is excessive as a first step.

⭐ Key point: Outbreak control: identify, isolate, inform, investigate, intensify hand hygiene and cleaning, and monitor.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q180$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, true, 1701, $s$After a needlestick injury from a patient with HIV, the nurse should first wash the wound, report it, and start post-exposure prophylaxis:$s$, $s$["Only after 1 week, if symptoms of infection appear", "Only if the source patient agrees to it", "Only after their own HIV test comes back positive", "As soon as possible, ideally within hours and no later than 72 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: As soon as possible, ideally within hours and no later than 72 hours
After a needlestick from an HIV-positive source: let the wound bleed, wash with soap and water, REPORT it, and start POST-EXPOSURE PROPHYLAXIS (PEP) AS SOON AS POSSIBLE, ideally within hours (within 1–2 hours) and NO LATER than 72 HOURS. PEP is a 28-day course of antiretroviral drugs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only after 1 week, if symptoms of infection appear — Waiting 1 week for symptoms is too late; PEP must start within 72 hours.
• Only if the source patient agrees to it — PEP shouldn't depend on the source patient's agreement.
• Only after their own HIV test comes back positive — Waiting for your own positive HIV test defeats the purpose of prevention.

⭐ Key point: Also assess hepatitis B and C risk, take baseline blood tests, and follow up at 6 weeks, 3 months and 6 months.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q200$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1702, $s$Autoclaving is a sterilisation method that primarily uses:$s$, $s$["Ultraviolet light only", "Pressurised steam at high temperature", "Cold chemical soaking only, with no heat", "Dry air at room temperature"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pressurised steam at high temperature
An autoclave sterilises with STEAM UNDER PRESSURE. Pressure raises the boiling point, so steam reaches 121 °C (held for 15 minutes) or 134 °C (held for 3 minutes). Moist heat kills ALL microorganisms, including bacterial spores, by denaturing their proteins. It is the commonest, cheapest and most reliable method for metal instruments, linen and dressings.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ultraviolet light only — Ultraviolet light only reduces germs on surfaces and in air; it does not sterilise instruments.
• Cold chemical soaking only, with no heat — Cold chemical soaking (such as 2% glutaraldehyde) is high-level disinfection, used for heat-sensitive items, not autoclaving.
• Dry air at room temperature — Dry air at room temperature does not kill microorganisms.

⭐ Key point: Autoclave: 121 °C for 15 minutes or 134 °C for 3 minutes. Clean items first; check indicator tape, and use biological indicators (spore tests) to prove sterility.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q26$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1703, $s$A tetanus patient should ideally be nursed in an environment that is:$s$, $s$["Bright and stimulating", "Noisy, for constant communication", "Quiet and dark, avoiding unnecessary stimulation", "Cold, to reduce metabolic rate"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Quiet and dark, avoiding unnecessary stimulation
In tetanus, the toxin (tetanospasmin) blocks the nerves that normally relax muscles. Any stimulus — noise, light, touch, even a draught — can trigger painful, violent muscle spasms, including laryngospasm, which can stop breathing. The patient is therefore nursed in a QUIET, DARK (dim) room, with minimal handling and procedures grouped together, usually with sedation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bright and stimulating — A bright, stimulating environment would trigger spasms.
• Noisy, for constant communication — Noise triggers spasms and must be avoided.
• Cold, to reduce metabolic rate — Cold can trigger spasms and does not help; temperature should be kept comfortable.

⭐ Key point: Tetanus treatment: wound debridement, tetanus immunoglobulin, metronidazole, benzodiazepines and magnesium for spasms, ventilation if needed, and the tetanus vaccine (the illness does not give immunity).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q95$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1704, $s$Which is a qSOFA criterion for sepsis?$s$, $s$["RR >22/min", "SBP <100 mmHg", "Altered mental status", "All of the above"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
qSOFA (quick SOFA) is a bedside screen outside ICU for patients with suspected infection who are at risk of poor outcomes. It has three items, scoring 1 each: respiratory rate of 22/min or more, systolic BP of 100 mmHg or less, and altered mental state (GCS below 15). A score of 2 or more suggests a higher risk of death and needs urgent review. All three listed criteria are part of qSOFA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• RR >22/min — Fast breathing alone is only one of the three criteria.
• SBP <100 mmHg — Low systolic BP alone is only one of the three criteria.
• Altered mental status — Altered mental status alone is only one of the three criteria.

⭐ Key point: Remember qSOFA as "HAT": Hypotension (SBP ≤100), Altered mentation, Tachypnoea (RR ≥22). Do not wait for a high score to give antibiotics if sepsis is suspected.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q113$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1705, $s$A patient with multiple organ dysfunction syndrome (MODS) following sepsis requires nursing care primarily focused on:$s$, $s$["A single-organ focus only, ignoring other systems", "Comprehensive, systematic monitoring and support of all affected organ systems", "No specific monitoring beyond routine vital signs", "Withholding all interventions"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Comprehensive, systematic monitoring and support of all affected organ systems
In MODS, two or more organ systems fail together (such as lungs, kidneys, liver, heart, blood clotting and brain), usually after sepsis or major trauma. Each failing organ affects the others, so care must be COMPREHENSIVE and SYSTEMATIC: assess and support every system ("head to toe" or "A to E") — ventilation, haemodynamics, kidney replacement, nutrition, glucose control, infection control, skin, and family support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A single-organ focus only, ignoring other systems — Focusing on one organ misses the others failing alongside it.
• No specific monitoring beyond routine vital signs — Routine vital signs alone are not enough; MODS needs invasive monitoring and lab checks.
• Withholding all interventions — Withholding care is not appropriate unless a decision about end-of-life care has been made with the family.

⭐ Key point: Mortality rises with each failing organ. Use the SOFA score to track the organs, and treat the cause (source control of infection).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q115$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1706, $s$To prevent pressure ulcers, a critically ill patient should be turned every:$s$, $s$["30 minutes", "1 hour", "2 hours", "6 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2 hours
Turning the patient every 2 hours relieves pressure on bony areas (sacrum, heels, hips, back of the head) before the skin and tissue are damaged by lack of blood flow. Pressure ulcers can start within 1–2 hours of unrelieved pressure in critically ill patients, who are immobile, poorly perfused and often on vasopressors. Turn the patient alternately (left 30°, back, right 30°), use pressure-relieving mattresses, and check the skin each time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 30 minutes — Every 30 minutes is impractical and would disturb rest and sleep.
• 1 hour — Every hour is more often than usually needed (though some very high-risk patients need it).
• 6 hours — Every 6 hours is far too long; ulcers would develop.

⭐ Key point: Use the Braden scale (a score of 18 or less means at risk), keep the skin clean and dry, ensure good nutrition, and remember devices (ETT, NG tube, masks) can also cause pressure ulcers.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q120$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1707, $s$The general adaptation syndrome of stress includes all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["An alarm reaction (fight-or-flight)", "A resistance phase", "A period of fast recovery", "A period of exhaustion"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A period of fast recovery
Hans Selye's general adaptation syndrome has THREE stages: 1) ALARM (fight or flight: adrenaline and cortisol released, fast heart and breathing), 2) RESISTANCE (the body adapts and copes with the ongoing stressor), 3) EXHAUSTION (resources run out, leading to illness, burnout or even death if the stress continues). "A period of fast recovery" is not one of the stages.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An alarm reaction (fight-or-flight) — The alarm reaction IS the first stage.
• A resistance phase — The resistance phase IS the second stage.
• A period of exhaustion — The exhaustion phase IS the third stage.

⭐ Key point: Critical illness keeps patients in prolonged stress (high cortisol, high glucose, muscle breakdown). Staff are also at risk of exhaustion and burnout.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q121$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1708, $s$Which of the following best describes the purpose of stress-ulcer prophylaxis (e.g. proton pump inhibitors) in critically ill patients?$s$, $s$["To treat active pneumonia", "To reduce the risk of stress-related gastrointestinal bleeding in high-risk ICU patients", "To lower blood glucose", "To prevent deep vein thrombosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: To reduce the risk of stress-related gastrointestinal bleeding in high-risk ICU patients
Critically ill patients, especially those ventilated for more than 48 hours or with clotting problems, can develop stress ulcers in the stomach because blood flow to the stomach lining is reduced. These can bleed. Stress ulcer prophylaxis with a PPI (omeprazole, pantoprazole) or H₂ blocker reduces stomach acid and lowers the risk of significant upper GI bleeding. Early enteral feeding also helps protect the stomach.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To treat active pneumonia — Stress ulcer drugs do not treat pneumonia; reducing stomach acid may even slightly increase pneumonia risk.
• To lower blood glucose — PPIs do not lower blood glucose.
• To prevent deep vein thrombosis — DVT is prevented with LMWH or heparin and mechanical devices, not PPIs.

⭐ Key point: Review stress ulcer prophylaxis daily and stop it when the patient no longer has risk factors; long-term use increases C. difficile and pneumonia risk.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q124$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1709, $s$Febrile convulsions in young children are most commonly associated with:$s$, $s$["A rapid rise in body temperature, often with a viral infection", "Chronic malnutrition alone", "Congenital heart disease exclusively", "Vitamin overdose"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A rapid rise in body temperature, often with a viral infection
Febrile convulsions are seizures caused by fever in children aged 6 months to 5 years without brain infection. They are linked to a RAPID RISE in temperature, usually from a viral infection (such as an ear or throat infection, or roseola). Most are short (under 5 minutes) generalised seizures, and the child recovers fully.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chronic malnutrition alone — Chronic malnutrition alone does not cause febrile convulsions.
• Congenital heart disease exclusively — Congenital heart disease is not specifically linked to febrile convulsions.
• Vitamin overdose — Vitamin overdose is not a cause.

⭐ Key point: Care: keep the child safe, place in recovery position, time the seizure, give a benzodiazepine if it lasts over 5 minutes, and look for the cause of fever (exclude meningitis). Reassure parents that most children grow out of it.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q148$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1710, $s$The following Stressors that disrupt the system according Neumann's model include ------ ------$s$, $s$["The primary, secondary, and tertiary stressor.", "The individuals, families, and groups stressor", "Physiological, sociocultural, and developmental stressor", "Intra-personal, inter- personal, and extra personal stressors."]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intra-personal, inter- personal, and extra personal stressors.
In Betty Neuman's Systems Model, stressors are classified by where they come from. INTRApersonal stressors arise within the person (such as pain or fear). INTERpersonal stressors arise between people (such as conflict with family or staff). EXTRApersonal stressors arise from outside the person (such as finances or the hospital environment). Stressors can break through the lines of defence and cause illness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The primary, secondary, and tertiary stressor. — Primary, secondary and tertiary are levels of PREVENTION (interventions) in Neuman's model, not types of stressors.
• The individuals, families, and groups stressor — Individuals, families and groups are possible CLIENTS in her model, not stressors.
• Physiological, sociocultural, and developmental stressor — Physiological, sociocultural and developmental are VARIABLES of the client system, not stressor categories.

⭐ Key point: Neuman's model includes the flexible line of defence (outer), the normal line of defence, lines of resistance, and the basic core structure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q155$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1711, $s$Which of the following is a recognised advantage of the Subjective Global Assessment (SGA) over purely laboratory-based nutritional assessment?$s$, $s$["It requires no clinician training", "It does not require any patient history", "It reliably predicts nutrition-related clinical outcomes and is not confounded by fluid shifts or acute-phase inflammatory changes the way serum albumin can be", "It replaces the need for anthropometric data entirely"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It reliably predicts nutrition-related clinical outcomes and is not confounded by fluid shifts or acute-phase inflammatory changes the way serum albumin can be
Subjective Global Assessment (SGA) is a bedside nutrition assessment based on history (weight loss, intake, gut symptoms, function) and examination (loss of fat and muscle, oedema). It predicts nutrition-related outcomes well. Unlike serum albumin, it is NOT distorted by fluid shifts or acute inflammation: albumin falls in critical illness because of inflammation and dilution, not just malnutrition.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It requires no clinician training — SGA DOES need training to do well.
• It does not require any patient history — SGA DEPENDS on the patient's history; it is not done without it.
• It replaces the need for anthropometric data entirely — SGA uses clinical examination of body composition; it does not replace all anthropometric data.

⭐ Key point: In critically ill patients, albumin and prealbumin are poor nutrition markers. Use clinical tools (SGA, NUTRIC score) instead.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q195$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1712, $s$Enteral nutrition is generally preferred over parenteral nutrition when:$s$, $s$["The gut is functioning", "The gut has completely failed", "Never — parenteral is always preferred", "Only in paediatric patients"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The gut is functioning
"If the gut works, use it." Enteral nutrition (via the gut, by mouth or tube) is preferred whenever the gastrointestinal tract is FUNCTIONING. It keeps the gut lining healthy, supports gut immunity, reduces infections and bacterial translocation, has fewer complications than TPN (no line infections, fewer metabolic problems), and is cheaper.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The gut has completely failed — If the gut has completely failed (obstruction, severe ileus, short bowel), parenteral nutrition is used.
• Never — parenteral is always preferred — Parenteral is NOT always preferred; it is the second choice.
• Only in paediatric patients — Enteral feeding is preferred in adults and children alike.

⭐ Key point: Start enteral feeding within 24–48 hours in ICU when the patient is haemodynamically stable. Use parenteral nutrition only if enteral feeding is impossible or not enough.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q197$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1713, $s$A nasogastric tube's position should be confirmed before feeding by:$s$, $s$["Auscultation ('whoosh test') alone", "pH testing of aspirate and/or X-ray confirmation", "The patient's verbal comfort report only", "Tube colour"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: pH testing of aspirate and/or X-ray confirmation
Before the first feed (and before each feed or drug), the NG tube position must be checked. Approved methods: aspirate gastric fluid and test its pH with pH paper (pH 5.5 or below means the stomach), or, if no aspirate or pH is above 5.5, get a CHEST X-RAY read by a competent person. The X-ray is the gold standard, especially in ventilated or unconscious patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Auscultation ('whoosh test') alone — The "whoosh test" (listening for air) is UNRELIABLE and must never be used; it can sound the same if the tube is in the lung.
• The patient's verbal comfort report only — The patient's comfort tells you nothing about position; tubes in the lung may cause no symptoms.
• Tube colour — The tube's colour does not show where it is.

⭐ Key point: Feeding through a misplaced NG tube (in the lung) is a "never event". Mark the tube's length at the nostril and recheck it before use.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q198$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1714, $s$Which nutritional consideration is important for a mechanically ventilated patient?$s$, $s$["Total parenteral nutrition should always be first-line", "Feeding should be withheld entirely until extubation", "Early enteral nutrition is preferred over parenteral nutrition when the gut is functional, to reduce infection risk", "Only IV dextrose is appropriate"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Early enteral nutrition is preferred over parenteral nutrition when the gut is functional, to reduce infection risk
Critically ill ventilated patients are catabolic (they break down muscle), so nutrition is important. Guidelines (ASPEN/ESPEN) recommend EARLY ENTERAL NUTRITION (tube feeding into the gut, by NG/OG tube) WITHIN 24–48 HOURS of ICU admission if the gut works and the patient is haemodynamically stable. Enteral feeding keeps the gut lining healthy, supports gut immunity, reduces bacterial translocation and INFECTION, and is cheaper and safer than parenteral nutrition (PN). PN (IV feeding) is used only when enteral feeding is impossible or inadequate. Nursing: check tube position (X-ray/aspirate pH), keep head of bed 30–45° to prevent aspiration and VAP, monitor tolerance (vomiting, distension), glucose and refeeding syndrome (low phosphate, potassium, magnesium).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Total parenteral nutrition should always be first-line — TPN is NOT first-line; it carries more risk of line infection, hyperglycaemia and liver problems.
• Feeding should be withheld entirely until extubation — WITHHOLDING food until extubation leads to malnutrition, muscle loss and poor healing.
• Only IV dextrose is appropriate — IV DEXTROSE alone gives some calories but no protein, fat, vitamins or minerals — it is not nutrition.

⭐ Key point: Ventilated patient with a working gut → EARLY ENTERAL feeding (24–48 h). "If the gut works, use it."$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q102$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1715, $s$A patient with septic shock has a rising serum lactate despite fluid resuscitation. This finding is most concerning because it suggests:$s$, $s$["Improved tissue perfusion", "Ongoing tissue hypoperfusion/anaerobic metabolism", "No clinical significance", "Resolved sepsis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ongoing tissue hypoperfusion/anaerobic metabolism
In SEPTIC SHOCK, LACTATE is produced when tissues do not receive enough oxygen and switch to ANAEROBIC metabolism (also from stress and reduced clearance). A lactate that is still RISING DESPITE FLUIDS means tissues are still HYPOPERFUSED — the shock is not controlled. This predicts higher mortality and calls for urgent re-assessment: more fluid only if fluid-responsive, start or increase VASOPRESSORS (noradrenaline, target MAP ≥ 65 mmHg), consider inotropes, check source control (drain abscess, remove infected line), and check antibiotics were given within 1 hour. Septic shock definition (Sepsis-3): needs vasopressors to keep MAP ≥ 65 AND lactate > 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluids.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved tissue perfusion — Improved perfusion would show a FALLING lactate, not rising.
• No clinical significance — A rising lactate is clinically very significant — a warning sign.
• Resolved sepsis — Sepsis is NOT resolved if lactate is rising; it is getting worse.

⭐ Key point: Rising lactate despite fluids = ONGOING tissue HYPOPERFUSION → escalate (vasopressors, source control).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q120$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1716, $s$A patient with a central line in place for 10 days develops fever with no other obvious source. The nurse should suspect:$s$, $s$["The central line as a possible source and should assess/report per protocol (e.g. CLABSI workup)", "No relationship to the central line whatsoever", "A skin allergy to the dressing only", "Nothing requiring further action"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The central line as a possible source and should assess/report per protocol (e.g. CLABSI workup)
A CENTRAL LINE is a direct route for bacteria into the bloodstream. The risk of CENTRAL LINE-ASSOCIATED BLOODSTREAM INFECTION (CLABSI) rises the longer the line stays in. A patient with a line in place for 10 DAYS who develops FEVER with no other clear source must be suspected of CLABSI. Nursing actions: assess the insertion site (redness, pus, tenderness), check vital signs for sepsis, report to the doctor, take paired BLOOD CULTURES (one from the line and one peripheral) before antibiotics, and follow the protocol (the line may need removal and the tip sent for culture). Prevention bundle: hand hygiene, maximal barrier precautions at insertion, chlorhexidine skin prep, avoid femoral site, daily review of the need for the line, scrub-the-hub.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No relationship to the central line whatsoever — Saying there is "no relationship" ignores the most likely source of infection.
• A skin allergy to the dressing only — A dressing allergy causes local rash/itching, not fever without another source.
• Nothing requiring further action — "Nothing requiring action" is unsafe; CLABSI can progress to sepsis and death.

⭐ Key point: Fever in a patient with a central line = suspect CLABSI → assess site, blood cultures (line + peripheral), report.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q122$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1717, $s$The correct order for doffing (removing) PPE is:$s$, $s$["Gloves → Goggles → Gown → Mask → Hand hygiene", "Mask → Gown → Goggles → Gloves → Hand hygiene", "Hand hygiene → Gloves → Gown → Mask → Goggles", "Goggles → Mask → Gloves → Gown"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Gloves → Goggles → Gown → Mask → Hand hygiene
PPE is removed (DOFFED) in an order that takes off the MOST CONTAMINATED items first and protects your face and hands from contamination. The order in this question, which follows the WHO/CDC sequence, is: (1) GLOVES (the most contaminated — touched the patient); (2) GOGGLES/face shield (handle by the strap or ear pieces, not the front); (3) GOWN (untie, pull away from the body, roll inside-out); (4) MASK/respirator (remove by the straps, do not touch the front, after leaving the patient room for N95); (5) HAND HYGIENE — and also perform hand hygiene between steps if hands become contaminated. Putting on (DONNING) is the reverse idea: hand hygiene → gown → mask → goggles → gloves. (CDC also allows gown and gloves to be removed together.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mask → Gown → Goggles → Gloves → Hand hygiene — Removing the MASK FIRST would mean touching your face with contaminated gloves — high risk.
• Hand hygiene → Gloves → Gown → Mask → Goggles — Starting with hand hygiene and leaving GOGGLES until last mixes up donning and doffing steps; removing the gown and mask before the goggles risks touching the contaminated eye protection with exposed hands, and there is no final hand hygiene.
• Goggles → Mask → Gloves → Gown — Removing GOGGLES FIRST with dirty gloves risks contaminating your eyes and face, and there is no final hand hygiene.

⭐ Key point: Doffing: GLOVES → GOGGLES → GOWN → MASK → HAND HYGIENE. Donning: gown → mask → goggles → gloves.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q134$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1718, $s$A Nurse is counselling the family of a client with Road Traffic Accident (RTA). The family is screaming by saying that the client will be recovered or not? The following principles are important in Critical Care Nursing EXCEPT$s$, $s$["Anticipation", "Early detection and prompt action", "Collaborative practice", "Stress elevation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stress elevation
The principles of CRITICAL CARE NURSING include: ANTICIPATION (predicting problems before they happen, e.g. preparing intubation equipment for a deteriorating patient); EARLY DETECTION AND PROMPT ACTION (close monitoring and rapid response to changes); COLLABORATIVE PRACTICE (teamwork with doctors, physiotherapists, pharmacists, dietitians and the family); holistic, patient- and family-centred care, communication and support; continuous education. STRESS ELEVATION (raising stress) is NOT a principle — the opposite is true: nurses aim for STRESS REDUCTION for the patient and family (clear honest information, reassurance, involving them in care), as with this screaming family of an RTA victim.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anticipation — ANTICIPATION IS a critical care principle.
• Early detection and prompt action — EARLY DETECTION AND PROMPT ACTION IS a critical care principle.
• Collaborative practice — COLLABORATIVE PRACTICE IS a critical care principle.

⭐ Key point: Critical care principles: anticipation, early detection + prompt action, collaboration, family support. Aim is stress REDUCTION, not elevation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q192$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1719, $s$Which patient is the LEAST appropriate candidate for enteral nutrition and would most likely require TPN instead?$s$, $s$["A patient with a functioning gut who is intubated and sedated", "A patient recovering from an uncomplicated appendectomy", "A patient with a high-output enterocutaneous fistula and short bowel syndrome", "A patient with dysphagia after a stroke but an otherwise normal GI tract"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A patient with a high-output enterocutaneous fistula and short bowel syndrome
The rule is "IF THE GUT WORKS, USE IT" — enteral nutrition (tube feeding) is preferred whenever the GI tract is functional. TOTAL PARENTERAL NUTRITION (TPN, IV feeding through a central line) is needed when the gut CANNOT be used or cannot absorb enough: e.g. a HIGH-OUTPUT ENTEROCUTANEOUS FISTULA (feeding increases fistula output and nutrients leak out before absorption) and SHORT BOWEL SYNDROME (too little bowel left to absorb nutrients), plus prolonged ileus, complete bowel obstruction, severe GI bleeding, severe mucositis. TPN risks: line infection (sepsis), hyperglycaemia, refeeding syndrome, liver dysfunction, so it requires strict aseptic care and monitoring.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A patient with a functioning gut who is intubated and sedated — An intubated, sedated patient with a FUNCTIONING GUT should receive ENTERAL feeding via NG/OG tube.
• A patient recovering from an uncomplicated appendectomy — After an UNCOMPLICATED appendectomy the patient resumes oral feeding quickly; TPN is not needed.
• A patient with dysphagia after a stroke but an otherwise normal GI tract — DYSPHAGIA after stroke with a normal GI tract → enteral feeding via NG or PEG tube, not TPN.

⭐ Key point: TPN only when the gut cannot be used — high-output fistula, short bowel syndrome, obstruction, prolonged ileus.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q196$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1720, $s$What is the primary purpose of a nasogastric (NG) tube in critical care?$s$, $s$["A) Monitoring cardiac hemodynamics", "B) Administering pain medication", "C) Providing nutrition and medications via the stomach", "D) Measuring intracranial pressure (ICP)"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: C) Providing nutrition and medications via the stomach
A NASOGASTRIC (NG) TUBE goes through the nose and oesophagus into the STOMACH. In critical care its primary purposes are: (1) ENTERAL NUTRITION (feeding) and giving MEDICATIONS when the patient cannot swallow safely (intubated, sedated, stroke); (2) DECOMPRESSION — draining air and fluid from the stomach in ileus, bowel obstruction, or to reduce vomiting and aspiration risk; (3) gastric lavage (rarely) and sampling of stomach contents. Nursing: confirm position BEFORE first use (X-ray, and pH of aspirate ≤ 5.5), check position before each feed/drug, flush with water, keep the head of bed at 30–45°, prevent pressure injury to the nose.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A) Monitoring cardiac hemodynamics — Monitoring cardiac haemodynamics uses arterial lines, central lines or PA catheters.
• B) Administering pain medication — PAIN MEDICATION can be given through an NG tube, but this is not its main overall purpose (option C covers nutrition AND medications).
• D) Measuring intracranial pressure (ICP) — Measuring ICP needs an ICP monitor (EVD or intraparenchymal probe).

⭐ Key point: NG tube = feeding and medications via the stomach (also decompression). Always confirm position before use (X-ray, pH ≤ 5.5).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q198$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1721, $s$Chest physiotherapy (CPT), as a broader category, includes all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Airway clearance techniques", "Breathing exercises", "Thoracic mobility exercises", "Antibiotic prescription"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Antibiotic prescription
CHEST PHYSIOTHERAPY (CPT) is a group of physical techniques to improve lung function and clear secretions. It includes: AIRWAY CLEARANCE techniques — postural drainage, percussion (clapping), vibration, active cycle of breathing technique, huffing, assisted cough, suctioning; BREATHING EXERCISES — deep breathing, diaphragmatic breathing, incentive spirometry, pursed-lip breathing; THORACIC MOBILITY exercises — chest wall mobilisation and posture work; plus early mobilisation and positioning. It is done by physiotherapists and nurses. ANTIBIOTIC PRESCRIPTION is a medical (drug) treatment for infection — it is not part of chest physiotherapy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Airway clearance techniques — AIRWAY CLEARANCE techniques ARE a central part of CPT.
• Breathing exercises — BREATHING EXERCISES ARE part of CPT.
• Thoracic mobility exercises — THORACIC MOBILITY exercises ARE part of CPT.

⭐ Key point: Chest physiotherapy = airway clearance + breathing exercises + thoracic mobility (+ positioning). Antibiotics are NOT physiotherapy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q200$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1722, $s$Prophylactic antibiotic use refers to:$s$, $s$["Treating an already established infection", "Preventing infection before it starts, e.g. before surgery", "Only used for viral infections", "A synonym for therapeutic use"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Preventing infection before it starts, e.g. before surgery
PROPHYLACTIC antibiotic use means giving antibiotics to PREVENT an infection BEFORE it starts — e.g. before SURGERY (to prevent surgical site infection), after exposure to meningococcal disease, or in high-risk patients (e.g. asplenia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Treating an already established infection — Treating an existing infection is THERAPEUTIC use.
• Only used for viral infections — Antibiotics do not treat viral infections.
• A synonym for therapeutic use — Prophylactic and therapeutic use are different.

⭐ Key point: Surgical prophylaxis is usually given IV within 60 minutes before the incision and is usually stopped within 24 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q33$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1723, $s$Myocardial oxygen demand is increased by the followings EXCEPT$s$, $s$["Left ventricular hypertrophy", "Fever", "Sleep", "Hyperthyroidism"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sleep
MYOCARDIAL OXYGEN DEMAND rises when the heart has to work harder: higher HEART RATE, greater CONTRACTILITY, and increased WALL TENSION (from high afterload or a thick or dilated ventricle). LEFT VENTRICULAR HYPERTROPHY (more muscle to supply, higher wall tension), FEVER (higher metabolic rate and heart rate) and HYPERTHYROIDISM (faster, stronger heartbeat) all INCREASE oxygen demand. SLEEP DECREASES it — heart rate and BP fall during sleep, reducing the heart's workload.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Left ventricular hypertrophy — LV hypertrophy increases demand.
• Fever — Fever increases demand.
• Hyperthyroidism — Hyperthyroidism increases demand.

⭐ Key point: In patients with ischaemic heart disease, reduce oxygen demand by controlling pain, fever, anxiety, tachycardia and hypertension, and by promoting rest and sleep.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q72$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1724, $s$A patient with Clostridioides difficile infection requires which type of isolation precaution?$s$, $s$["Airborne precautions with N95 respirator", "Contact precautions, with gown and gloves, and hand hygiene using soap and water (not alcohol-based sanitiser alone) given spore resistance", "No precautions are necessary", "Droplet precautions only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Contact precautions, with gown and gloves, and hand hygiene using soap and water (not alcohol-based sanitiser alone) given spore resistance
CLOSTRIDIOIDES DIFFICILE (C. diff) causes diarrhoea and colitis, often after antibiotic use. It spreads by CONTACT (faecal–oral route via hands, equipment and surfaces) and forms SPORES that survive for months in the environment and are NOT killed by alcohol hand rub. Precautions: CONTACT PRECAUTIONS — single room with its own toilet, GLOVES and GOWN/apron for all contact, dedicated equipment — plus HAND HYGIENE with SOAP AND WATER (which physically removes spores), and cleaning with chlorine-based (sporicidal) disinfectant.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Airborne precautions with N95 respirator — C. diff is not airborne; N95 respirators are not needed.
• No precautions are necessary — Precautions ARE necessary — it spreads easily.
• Droplet precautions only — It is not spread by droplets.

⭐ Key point: Stop unnecessary antibiotics (especially broad-spectrum ones) and avoid anti-diarrhoeal drugs in C. diff infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q118$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1725, $s$The first clinical symptom of tetanus is typically:$s$, $s$["Generalised convulsions", "Risus sardonicus", "Trismus (lockjaw)", "Neck stiffness"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Trismus (lockjaw)
TETANUS is caused by a toxin from Clostridium tetani (from contaminated wounds). The toxin blocks inhibitory nerve signals, causing muscle spasm and rigidity. The FIRST symptom is usually TRISMUS (LOCKJAW) — spasm of the jaw muscles (masseters), making it hard to open the mouth or chew. This is followed by stiffness of the neck and back, risus sardonicus (a fixed, grimacing smile), difficulty swallowing, abdominal rigidity, painful generalised spasms (opisthotonus) and autonomic instability.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Generalised convulsions — Generalised convulsions (spasms) occur LATER.
• Risus sardonicus — Risus sardonicus occurs after trismus.
• Neck stiffness — Neck stiffness may occur early but trismus is classically first.

⭐ Key point: Treatment: human tetanus immunoglobulin, wound debridement, metronidazole, muscle relaxants/sedation (benzodiazepines, magnesium), airway support, and a quiet, dark environment. Prevent by vaccination.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q125$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1726, $s$Sepsis is currently defined (Sepsis-3) as:$s$, $s$["Any infection, regardless of organ function", "Life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection", "A fever above 38°C alone", "Bacteraemia without any clinical symptoms"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Life-threatening organ dysfunction caused by a dysregulated host response to infection
SEPSIS-3 (2016) defines SEPSIS as LIFE-THREATENING ORGAN DYSFUNCTION caused by a DYSREGULATED HOST RESPONSE to INFECTION. Organ dysfunction is identified by an acute rise in the SOFA score of 2 or more points. SEPTIC SHOCK is a subset with circulatory and metabolic abnormalities: needing vasopressors to keep MAP ≥65 mmHg AND lactate above 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluids. The older SIRS-based definitions are no longer used.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Any infection, regardless of organ function — Infection without organ dysfunction is not sepsis.
• A fever above 38°C alone — Fever alone does not define sepsis.
• Bacteraemia without any clinical symptoms — Bacteraemia without symptoms is not sepsis.

⭐ Key point: Use qSOFA (respiratory rate ≥22, altered mentation, systolic BP ≤100) as a quick bedside screen outside ICU.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q130$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1727, $s$A nurse is providing psychological support to a burn patient. The nurse should be aware that the patient may experience which of the following emotional responses?$s$, $s$["A sense of euphoria", "Apathy and indifference", "Anxiety, depression, and body image disturbance", "A rapid return to normal daily life"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Anxiety, depression, and body image disturbance
Major burns are physically and emotionally traumatic. Patients commonly experience ANXIETY (fear of pain, procedures, death), DEPRESSION, POST-TRAUMATIC STRESS (flashbacks, nightmares), sleep problems, grief and loss, and BODY IMAGE DISTURBANCE (scarring, disfigurement, loss of function, changes in appearance). These responses can affect recovery, adherence to treatment and return to normal life.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A sense of euphoria — Euphoria is not a typical response.
• Apathy and indifference — Apathy may occur, but anxiety, depression and body image problems are more typical.
• A rapid return to normal daily life — Recovery is usually long and difficult, not a rapid return to normal.

⭐ Key point: Provide early psychological support, adequate pain relief, honest information, family involvement, and referral to counselling or support groups.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q132$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1728, $s$Effective discharge planning for a rehabilitation patient should begin:$s$, $s$["Only on the day of discharge", "Early during the hospital stay, involving the patient, family, and multidisciplinary team", "After the patient has already left the facility", "Without involving the patient's family"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Early during the hospital stay, involving the patient, family, and multidisciplinary team
Effective DISCHARGE PLANNING should BEGIN EARLY during the hospital stay (ideally on admission), and involve the PATIENT, FAMILY and MULTIDISCIPLINARY TEAM (physiotherapy, occupational therapy, social work, dietitian), to prepare for a safe transition home or to another facility.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only on the day of discharge — The day of discharge is too late.
• After the patient has already left the facility — After discharge is too late.
• Without involving the patient's family — Family involvement is important.

⭐ Key point: Early discharge planning reduces delays and readmissions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q161$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1729, $s$Which nursing action best supports early identification of a TPN-related infection?$s$, $s$["Assessing the catheter insertion site for redness, warmth, swelling, or drainage at each shift, along with monitoring temperature trends", "Assessing the insertion site only once weekly during dressing changes", "Relying solely on the patient's subjective report of pain", "Checking the site only if the patient develops hypotension"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assessing the catheter insertion site for redness, warmth, swelling, or drainage at each shift, along with monitoring temperature trends
Early identification of TPN-related (central line) infection involves ASSESSING the INSERTION SITE for REDNESS, WARMTH, SWELLING or DRAINAGE each shift, and MONITORING TEMPERATURE trends.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assessing the insertion site only once weekly during dressing changes — Weekly checks are too infrequent.
• Relying solely on the patient's subjective report of pain — Subjective reports alone are not reliable.
• Checking the site only if the patient develops hypotension — Waiting for hypotension is too late.

⭐ Key point: Use strict aseptic technique and a dedicated lumen for TPN.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q195$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1730, $s$These are risks of parenteral nutrition EXCEPT$s$, $s$["Infection", "Hyperglycaemia", "Hypoglycaemia", "Vomiting"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vomiting
PARENTERAL NUTRITION (PN/TPN) is given into a vein (usually a central line). Its risks include: INFECTION (central line bloodstream infection — the solution is rich in glucose and lipids, which support bacterial growth), HYPERGLYCAEMIA (high glucose content), HYPOGLYCAEMIA (if PN is stopped suddenly — rebound), electrolyte imbalances, refeeding syndrome, fluid overload, liver dysfunction, and line complications (thrombosis, pneumothorax). VOMITING is not a typical risk because PN bypasses the gut.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Infection — Infection IS a major risk.
• Hyperglycaemia — Hyperglycaemia IS a common risk.
• Hypoglycaemia — Hypoglycaemia IS a risk if PN is stopped abruptly.

⭐ Key point: Use a dedicated lumen, strict asepsis, and monitor glucose and electrolytes daily.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q196$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1731, $s$Refeeding syndrome is a risk primarily in patients who:$s$, $s$["Have been well-nourished throughout admission", "Have experienced prolonged starvation or severe malnutrition before reintroducing nutrition", "Are only mildly overweight", "Have normal phosphate levels only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Have experienced prolonged starvation or severe malnutrition before reintroducing nutrition
REFEEDING SYNDROME is a potentially fatal shift of fluids and electrolytes that occurs when nutrition is restarted in patients after PROLONGED STARVATION or SEVERE MALNUTRITION (e.g. anorexia, chronic alcoholism, prolonged fasting, cancer cachexia). Insulin rises, driving PHOSPHATE, POTASSIUM and MAGNESIUM into cells, causing dangerously low blood levels, which can cause heart failure, arrhythmias, respiratory failure, confusion and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Have been well-nourished throughout admission — Well-nourished patients are not at significant risk.
• Are only mildly overweight — Mildly overweight patients are not at particular risk.
• Have normal phosphate levels only — Normal phosphate levels are not the issue; low levels are.

⭐ Key point: Prevent by starting feeds slowly (e.g. 10 kcal/kg/day), giving thiamine, and monitoring electrolytes daily.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q198$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1732, $s$An effective nursing strategy to reduce ICU delirium includes:$s$, $s$["Frequent changes of caregiving staff", "Reorientation using a clock, calendar, and family photos", "Keeping the environment brightly lit and noisy at all times", "Withholding explanations to avoid overstimulation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reorientation using a clock, calendar, and family photos
REORIENTATION using a CLOCK, CALENDAR, FAMILY PHOTOS, explanations of where the patient is and what is happening, glasses and hearing aids, helps reduce ICU DELIRIUM. Other strategies: consistent caregivers, natural day–night light cycles, reducing noise at night, early mobilisation, and family involvement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Frequent changes of caregiving staff — Frequent staff changes increase confusion.
• Keeping the environment brightly lit and noisy at all times — Bright, noisy environments disrupt sleep and worsen delirium.
• Withholding explanations to avoid overstimulation — Explanations help orientation and reduce anxiety.

⭐ Key point: The ABCDEF bundle reduces delirium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q97$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1733, $s$Which form of tetanus is the most common overall?$s$, $s$["Generalised", "Localised", "Cephalic", "Neonatal"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Generalised
GENERALISED TETANUS is the MOST COMMON form (about 80% of cases). It causes trismus (lockjaw), risus sardonicus (fixed smile), neck and back stiffness, opisthotonus (arching of the back), painful muscle spasms, and autonomic instability.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Localised — Localised tetanus affects muscles near the wound only (uncommon).
• Cephalic — Cephalic tetanus affects cranial nerves after head injuries (rare).
• Neonatal — Neonatal tetanus is a form of generalised tetanus in newborns (common in some low-income countries, but not the most common overall).

⭐ Key point: Prevent tetanus through immunisation, including of pregnant women (protects newborns).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q118$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1734, $s$For patients who require enteral nutrition support for longer than approximately 4-6 weeks, which access route is generally recommended over a nasoenteric tube?$s$, $s$["A percutaneous route such as gastrostomy (PEG) or jejunostomy tube", "Continued nasogastric tube indefinitely", "Oral sipping only", "Intravenous TPN as first choice"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A percutaneous route such as gastrostomy (PEG) or jejunostomy tube
For enteral nutrition needed LONGER than about 4–6 WEEKS, a PERCUTANEOUS route such as GASTROSTOMY (PEG) or JEJUNOSTOMY is recommended, because long-term nasal tubes cause discomfort, nasal ulcers, sinusitis and displacement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Continued nasogastric tube indefinitely — Indefinite NG use causes complications.
• Oral sipping only — Oral sipping may not be enough.
• Intravenous TPN as first choice — TPN is used only if the gut doesn't work.

⭐ Key point: PEG tubes are more comfortable and secure for long-term feeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q195$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1735, $s$What is the primary goal of enteral nutrition in critical care?$s$, $s$["A) Inducing sleep", "B) Providing comfort", "C) Administering pain medication", "D) Delivering nutrition through the gastrointestinal tract"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: D) Delivering nutrition through the gastrointestinal tract
ENTERAL NUTRITION means DELIVERING NUTRITION through the GASTROINTESTINAL TRACT, usually via a tube (nasogastric, nasojejunal, PEG), when a patient cannot eat enough by mouth. In critical care, its goals are to meet energy and protein needs, MAINTAIN GUT INTEGRITY (preventing the gut lining from wasting and bacteria from crossing into the blood), support immune function, and reduce infections compared with parenteral (IV) nutrition.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A) Inducing sleep — It is not used to induce sleep.
• B) Providing comfort — It may provide comfort, but that is not its primary goal.
• C) Administering pain medication — It is not for giving pain medication (although some drugs can be given via feeding tubes).

⭐ Key point: "If the gut works, use it." Start enteral feeding within 24–48 hours of ICU admission in haemodynamically stable patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q196$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1736, $s$Which of the following patients is the MOST appropriate candidate for enteral rather than parenteral nutrition?$s$, $s$["A patient with a functioning GI tract who is intubated and cannot swallow safely due to sedation", "A patient with a complete bowel obstruction", "A patient with severe, uncontrolled upper GI hemorrhage", "A patient in septic shock requiring escalating vasopressor doses with signs of bowel ischemia"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A patient with a functioning GI tract who is intubated and cannot swallow safely due to sedation
ENTERAL NUTRITION is preferred for patients with a FUNCTIONING GI TRACT who cannot eat (e.g. intubated and sedated).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A patient with a complete bowel obstruction — Bowel obstruction prevents enteral feeding.
• A patient with severe, uncontrolled upper GI hemorrhage — Severe GI haemorrhage contraindicates feeding.
• A patient in septic shock requiring escalating vasopressor doses with signs of bowel ischemia — Bowel ischaemia contraindicates feeding.

⭐ Key point: Start enteral feeding within 24–48 hours if possible.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q197$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1737, $s$Fat provides approximately how many kilocalories per gram, compared to carbohydrate and protein?$s$, $s$["Fat 4kcal/g, same as CHO/protein", "Fat 9kcal/g, more than double CHO/protein (4kcal/g each)", "Fat 1kcal/g, far less than CHO/protein", "Fat provides no calories"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fat 9kcal/g, more than double CHO/protein (4kcal/g each)
Different nutrients provide different amounts of ENERGY: FAT provides about 9 kcal per gram — MORE THAN DOUBLE carbohydrate and protein, which each provide about 4 kcal per gram. (Alcohol provides about 7 kcal/g.) This is why fat-based products (e.g. lipid emulsions in parenteral nutrition, propofol) contribute many calories in small volumes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fat 4kcal/g, same as CHO/protein — Fat does not provide 4 kcal/g (that is carbohydrate and protein).
• Fat 1kcal/g, far less than CHO/protein — Fat provides much more than 1 kcal/g.
• Fat provides no calories — Fat does provide calories.

⭐ Key point: Remember to count propofol (1.1 kcal/mL from lipid) in ICU nutrition calculations to avoid overfeeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q198$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1738, $s$Stress ulcer (gastrointestinal) prophylaxis is routinely recommended for mechanically ventilated patients because:$s$, $s$["It prevents ventilator-associated pneumonia directly", "It replaces the need for enteral nutrition", "Critical illness and mechanical ventilation increase risk of stress-related mucosal bleeding", "Ventilated patients are at no increased GI bleeding risk"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Critical illness and mechanical ventilation increase risk of stress-related mucosal bleeding
CRITICAL ILLNESS and MECHANICAL VENTILATION (over 48 hours) INCREASE the risk of STRESS-RELATED MUCOSAL BLEEDING in the stomach, because of reduced blood flow to the gut lining. Prophylaxis (PPI or H2 blocker) reduces this risk in high-risk patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It prevents ventilator-associated pneumonia directly — It doesn't prevent VAP (it may increase VAP risk).
• It replaces the need for enteral nutrition — It doesn't replace enteral nutrition.
• Ventilated patients are at no increased GI bleeding risk — Ventilated patients ARE at increased risk.

⭐ Key point: Review the need for prophylaxis daily; stop it when risk factors resolve.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q130$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1739, $s$A patient with an infected surgical wound has cultures growing Pseudomonas aeruginosa resistant to multiple antibiotics. The nurse should anticipate:$s$, $s$["Contact precautions in addition to standard precautions, given the multidrug-resistant organism", "Standard precautions alone are sufficient with no additional measures", "No precautions are necessary since it is only a wound infection", "Airborne precautions specifically"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Contact precautions in addition to standard precautions, given the multidrug-resistant organism
MULTIDRUG-RESISTANT (MDR) Pseudomonas aeruginosa can spread from patient to patient via hands, equipment and the environment (it survives in moist areas). The nurse should use CONTACT PRECAUTIONS in addition to standard precautions: single room or cohorting, gloves and gown/apron for contact with the patient or their surroundings, dedicated equipment, strict hand hygiene, and thorough environmental cleaning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Standard precautions alone are sufficient with no additional measures — Standard precautions alone are not enough for MDR organisms.
• No precautions are necessary since it is only a wound infection — Precautions are necessary to protect other patients.
• Airborne precautions specifically — Pseudomonas is not airborne; airborne precautions are not specifically required.

⭐ Key point: Inform the infection prevention team, and follow antibiotic stewardship guidance.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q157$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1740, $s$A patient with tetanus develops trismus (lockjaw) as an early symptom. The nurse understands this occurs because tetanus toxin:$s$, $s$["Blocks inhibitory neurotransmitters (glycine and GABA), causing sustained, uncontrolled muscle contraction", "Only affects the peripheral sensory nerves", "Directly paralyses the diaphragm first", "Causes generalised sedation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Blocks inhibitory neurotransmitters (glycine and GABA), causing sustained, uncontrolled muscle contraction
TETANUS TOXIN blocks the release of INHIBITORY neurotransmitters (GLYCINE and GABA) in the spinal cord. Without inhibition, muscles CONTRACT continuously, causing TRISMUS (lockjaw), muscle spasms and rigidity.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only affects the peripheral sensory nerves — It affects motor, not sensory, nerves.
• Directly paralyses the diaphragm first — It doesn't paralyse the diaphragm first.
• Causes generalised sedation — It doesn't cause sedation.

⭐ Key point: Treat with tetanus immunoglobulin, wound care, metronidazole, muscle relaxants, and a quiet, dark environment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q195$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1741, $s$A patient with sepsis presenting with a CVP of 9mmHg (slightly elevated) should be interpreted with caution because:$s$, $s$["CVP is irrelevant in sepsis", "Sepsis can present with low, normal, OR high CVP", "Sepsis always causes low CVP", "A CVP of 9 always confirms fluid overload"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sepsis can present with low, normal, OR high CVP
CENTRAL VENOUS PRESSURE (CVP) is influenced by many factors: blood volume, right heart function, venous tone, intrathoracic pressure (ventilation, PEEP), and abdominal pressure. In SEPSIS, the CVP can be LOW (vasodilation, capillary leak, hypovolaemia), NORMAL, or HIGH (right ventricular dysfunction, high PEEP, fluid overload, raised abdominal pressure). So a CVP of 9 mmHg does not reliably tell you whether the patient needs more fluid. Dynamic measures (passive leg raise, stroke volume changes, ultrasound) are better guides.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CVP is irrelevant in sepsis — CVP is not irrelevant; it gives some information, especially trends.
• Sepsis always causes low CVP — Sepsis does not always cause low CVP.
• A CVP of 9 always confirms fluid overload — A CVP of 9 does not confirm fluid overload.

⭐ Key point: Single CVP values are poor predictors of fluid responsiveness; interpret them with the whole clinical picture.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q162$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1742, $s$In a study examining whether hand hygiene compliance among ICU nurses affects the rate of hospital-acquired infections, the dependent variable is$s$, $s$["The type of hospital where the study is conducted", "The rate of hospital-acquired infections", "Hand hygiene compliance", "The number of nurses working in the unit"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The rate of hospital-acquired infections
The DEPENDENT VARIABLE is the OUTCOME — the variable that is expected to CHANGE in response to the independent variable. In this study, the researcher examines whether hand hygiene compliance (independent variable) affects the RATE OF HOSPITAL-ACQUIRED INFECTIONS. So the infection rate is the dependent variable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The type of hospital where the study is conducted — The type of hospital is a setting characteristic.
• Hand hygiene compliance — Hand hygiene compliance is the INDEPENDENT variable.
• The number of nurses working in the unit — The number of nurses is a possible extraneous variable.

⭐ Key point: The dependent variable "depends" on the independent variable.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q60$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1743, $s$A study comparing infection rates between an ICU that adopted a new hand hygiene protocol and a similar ICU that continued standard practice, without randomly assigning patients or units to groups, is an example of a$s$, $s$["Phenomenological design", "Historical design", "Quasi-experimental design", "True experimental design"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Quasi-experimental design
A QUASI-EXPERIMENTAL design is like an experiment in that the researcher introduces an INTERVENTION (here, a new hand hygiene protocol in one ICU) and usually compares the results with a COMPARISON GROUP (a similar ICU continuing standard practice). However, it LACKS RANDOM ASSIGNMENT — the units or patients are not randomly allocated to the intervention or comparison group. This design is common in hospital research because it is often impractical or unethical to randomise whole wards, hospitals or patients. Because groups may differ in other ways (staffing, patient severity), the researcher must be careful about confounding when interpreting the results.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Phenomenological design — A phenomenological design is qualitative; it explores lived experiences through interviews, with no intervention.
• Historical design — A historical design studies past events using documents and records.
• True experimental design — A TRUE experimental design (RCT) requires RANDOM assignment to groups, a control group and manipulation of the intervention.

⭐ Key point: True experiment = manipulation + control group + randomisation. Quasi-experiment = manipulation, but missing randomisation (and sometimes a control group).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q63$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1744, $s$Neuman classifies stressors into three types based on their relationship to the client system. Which of the following correctly identifies them?$s$, $s$["Focal, contextual, and residual stressors", "Intrapersonal, interpersonal, and extrapersonal stressors", "Universal, developmental, and health-deviation stressors", "Primary, secondary, and tertiary stressors"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intrapersonal, interpersonal, and extrapersonal stressors
NEUMAN classifies STRESSORS by their relationship to the client system: (1) INTRAPERSONAL — arising WITHIN the person (e.g. pain, anxiety, fever, infection); (2) INTERPERSONAL — arising BETWEEN people (e.g. family conflict, communication problems with staff, role expectations); (3) EXTRAPERSONAL — arising OUTSIDE the person, at a distance (e.g. financial problems, hospital policies, unemployment, community issues).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Focal, contextual, and residual stressors — Focal, contextual and residual are ROY'S types of stimuli.
• Universal, developmental, and health-deviation stressors — Universal, developmental and health-deviation are OREM'S self-care requisites.
• Primary, secondary, and tertiary stressors — Primary, secondary and tertiary are levels of PREVENTION, not stressor types.

⭐ Key point: Assessing all three types of stressors gives a holistic picture of the ICU patient and family.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q132$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1745, $s$The client is the ____ source of data, while the client's chart, family members, and other health professionals are ____ sources.$s$, $s$["unreliable; reliable", "primary; secondary", "primary; tertiary", "secondary; primary"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: primary; secondary
The CLIENT is the PRIMARY (main and most important) source of data, because the client directly experiences their health problem. SECONDARY sources are everything else: the client's chart/medical records, family members and friends, other health professionals, laboratory and diagnostic reports, and literature. Secondary sources are used to supplement or confirm information, especially when the client cannot communicate (e.g. unconscious ICU patient).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• unreliable; reliable — The client is not "unreliable"; the client is the primary source.
• primary; tertiary — Family and records are SECONDARY, not tertiary.
• secondary; primary — This reverses the correct order.

⭐ Key point: In ICU, many patients cannot speak, so secondary sources (family, records, monitoring data) become very important.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q143$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1746, $s$Which of the following is NOT a barrier to early mobilisation in the ICU?$s$, $s$["The patient's gender", "Co-morbidities", "Physiological instability", "The ICU environment (lines, tubes, equipment)"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The patient's gender
Many factors can delay or prevent EARLY MOBILISATION in ICU: CO-MORBIDITIES (e.g. heart failure, obesity, arthritis); PHYSIOLOGICAL INSTABILITY (low BP, high vasopressor needs, low SpO2, arrhythmias); deep SEDATION and delirium; pain; the ICU ENVIRONMENT (multiple lines, tubes, drains and equipment); staff shortages and lack of training; lack of protocols and equipment; and safety concerns. The PATIENT'S GENDER is NOT a barrier — men and women both benefit from early mobilisation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Co-morbidities — Co-morbidities are a recognised barrier.
• Physiological instability — Physiological instability is a recognised barrier.
• The ICU environment (lines, tubes, equipment) — The ICU environment is a recognised barrier.

⭐ Key point: Protocols with clear safety criteria, light sedation, teamwork and proper equipment help overcome barriers safely.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q165$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1747, $s$Which of the following contraceptive methods does NOT protect against sexually transmitted infections (STIs)?$s$, $s$["Combined oral contraceptive pill", "Abstinence", "Male condom", "Female condom"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Combined oral contraceptive pill
The COMBINED ORAL CONTRACEPTIVE PILL (COC) contains oestrogen and progestogen. It prevents pregnancy mainly by stopping ovulation, thickening cervical mucus and thinning the endometrium. It is highly effective for pregnancy prevention, but it does NOT form a barrier, so it does NOT protect against SEXUALLY TRANSMITTED INFECTIONS (STIs) such as HIV, gonorrhoea, chlamydia, syphilis or hepatitis B.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Abstinence — Abstinence protects against both pregnancy and STIs.
• Male condom — Male condoms provide a barrier that reduces STI transmission.
• Female condom — Female condoms provide a barrier that reduces STI transmission.

⭐ Key point: Advise "dual protection": a highly effective contraceptive (e.g. pill, implant, IUD) PLUS condoms for STI protection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q173$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1748, $s$What is the purpose of male condoms in family planning?$s$, $s$["Preventing pregnancy", "Reducing the risk of STIs", "Both A and B", "None of the above"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Both A and B
The MALE CONDOM is a sheath worn over the erect penis. It acts as a physical barrier that stops sperm entering the vagina, so it PREVENTS PREGNANCY (about 98% effective with perfect use, about 87% with typical use). It also stops contact with genital fluids and reduces skin contact, so it REDUCES the RISK of STIs including HIV. It is the only male method (apart from vasectomy and withdrawal) and offers dual protection, so "BOTH A AND B" is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Preventing pregnancy — Preventing pregnancy alone is incomplete.
• Reducing the risk of STIs — Reducing STI risk alone is incomplete.
• None of the above — "None of the above" is incorrect.

⭐ Key point: Teach correct use: check expiry date, open carefully, put on before any genital contact, pinch the tip, hold the base during withdrawal, use a new condom each time, and use water-based lubricants with latex.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q175$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1749, $s$How does the fertility-awareness (natural family planning) method identify fertile days?$s$, $s$["By monitoring changes in cervical mucus", "By daily basal body temperature charting", "Both A and B", "None of the above"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Both A and B
FERTILITY-AWARENESS (natural family planning) methods identify the FERTILE days of the cycle so that intercourse is avoided (or a barrier is used) on those days. They use several signs: CERVICAL MUCUS changes (around ovulation, mucus becomes clear, slippery and stretchy like raw egg white — "fertile mucus"); BASAL BODY TEMPERATURE charting (temperature rises about 0.2–0.5 °C after ovulation and stays raised until the period); and calendar calculations. Combining these (the SYMPTO-THERMAL method) is the most effective, so "BOTH A AND B" is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• By monitoring changes in cervical mucus — Cervical mucus alone is only one method.
• By daily basal body temperature charting — Basal body temperature alone is only one method.
• None of the above — "None of the above" is incorrect.

⭐ Key point: These methods require training, motivation and partner cooperation, and do not protect against STIs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q182$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1750, $s$Which measure helps prevent central line-associated bloodstream infection (CLABSI)?$s$, $s$["Routine replacement of central lines every 48 hours", "Skin antisepsis with chlorhexidine (2% in alcohol)", "Using the femoral site in preference to all others", "Leaving an unneeded line in place 'just in case'"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Skin antisepsis with chlorhexidine (2% in alcohol)
SKIN ANTISEPSIS with 2% CHLORHEXIDINE GLUCONATE in 70% ALCOHOL before central line insertion and during dressing changes is one of the most effective measures to prevent CENTRAL LINE-ASSOCIATED BLOODSTREAM INFECTION (CLABSI). Chlorhexidine kills skin bacteria and has a lasting effect. Allow it to dry completely before insertion or dressing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Routine replacement of central lines every 48 hours — Routine replacement of central lines every 48 hours is not recommended; it increases risk.
• Using the femoral site in preference to all others — The FEMORAL site has a HIGHER infection risk; the subclavian site is preferred where possible.
• Leaving an unneeded line in place 'just in case' — Unneeded lines should be removed promptly; every day a line stays in increases infection risk.

⭐ Key point: CLABSI bundle: hand hygiene, maximal sterile barrier precautions, chlorhexidine skin prep, optimal site selection, and daily review of line necessity.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q194$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1751, $s$A severely malnourished patient starts full nasogastric feeding. On day 3 she becomes confused and weak, with breathlessness, oedema and arrhythmias. Phosphate is 0.3 mmol/L, with low potassium and magnesium. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Refeeding syndrome", "Dumping syndrome", "Wernicke's encephalopathy", "Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Refeeding syndrome
REFEEDING SYNDROME occurs when feeding starts after starvation. Insulin rises, driving PHOSPHATE, POTASSIUM and MAGNESIUM into cells, causing very LOW levels in the blood. LOW PHOSPHATE (0.3 mmol/L) is the hallmark. Effects: CONFUSION, WEAKNESS, BREATHLESSNESS (respiratory muscle weakness), OEDEMA (fluid retention), ARRHYTHMIAS and heart failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dumping syndrome — Dumping syndrome occurs after stomach surgery.
• Wernicke's encephalopathy — Wernicke's causes confusion and ataxia, but not low phosphate.
• Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state — HHS causes very high glucose and osmolality.

⭐ Key point: Prevent: identify high-risk patients, start feeds slowly (e.g. 10 kcal/kg/day), give thiamine, and monitor and replace electrolytes daily.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q137$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1752, $s$Under Sepsis-3, organ dysfunction in a patient with infection is identified by an acute rise in the SOFA score of:$s$, $s$["3 points or more", "2 or more SIRS criteria", "2 points or more", "1 point or more on qSOFA"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2 points or more
Under SEPSIS-3, SEPSIS is defined as life-threatening ORGAN DYSFUNCTION caused by a dysregulated host response to INFECTION. Organ dysfunction is identified by an ACUTE RISE in the SOFA score of 2 POINTS OR MORE from the patient's baseline (assume baseline is 0 if there is no known pre-existing organ dysfunction). A SOFA rise of 2 or more is linked to about a 10% risk of death in hospital.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 3 points or more — A rise of 3 or more is not the threshold; 2 is.
• 2 or more SIRS criteria — SIRS criteria (temperature, heart rate, respiratory rate, WBC) were used in the OLD (Sepsis-1/2) definitions. They are no longer used to define sepsis because they are not specific.
• 1 point or more on qSOFA — qSOFA (respiratory rate ≥22, altered mental state, systolic BP ≤100) is a quick screening tool; it does not define organ dysfunction.

⭐ Key point: SOFA assesses 6 systems: respiration (PaO2/FiO2), coagulation (platelets), liver (bilirubin), cardiovascular (MAP and vasopressors), CNS (GCS), and renal (creatinine or urine output).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q142$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1753, $s$A 3-week-old baby who had been feeding well suddenly starts vomiting bright GREEN (bilious) fluid and becomes irritable, with a slightly distended abdomen. The most important diagnosis to exclude urgently is:$s$, $s$["Malrotation with midgut volvulus", "Gastro-oesophageal reflux", "Overfeeding", "Infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Malrotation with midgut volvulus
BILIOUS (green) VOMITING means the blockage is BELOW the point where bile enters the bowel (the second part of the duodenum). In a baby, the most dangerous cause is MALROTATION WITH MIDGUT VOLVULUS: the bowel was not properly fixed in the abdomen during fetal development, so it can TWIST around its blood vessel (the superior mesenteric artery), cutting off blood supply to almost the WHOLE SMALL BOWEL. Within hours, the bowel can die (necrosis), leading to shock, perforation, death, or survival with "short gut" (lifelong feeding problems). It often presents in the first month in a baby who was previously well, as here.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Gastro-oesophageal reflux — Gastro-oesophageal reflux causes NON-bilious (milky) vomiting or posseting, usually in a well, thriving baby.
• Overfeeding — Overfeeding causes NON-bilious vomiting of milk soon after feeds, in a well baby.
• Infantile hypertrophic pyloric stenosis — Pyloric stenosis causes PROJECTILE NON-BILIOUS vomiting (the blockage is ABOVE the bile duct entry), usually at 2–8 weeks.

⭐ Key point: Bilious vomiting in any baby = surgical emergency. Stop feeds, pass a nasogastric tube on free drainage, start IV fluids, call the paediatric surgeon, and arrange an urgent upper GI contrast study.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q174$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1754, $s$After caring for a patient with Clostridioides difficile diarrhoea, the correct hand hygiene is:$s$, $s$["Use a chlorhexidine–alcohol hand rub", "Wash hands with soap and running water", "Use alcohol-based hand rub only", "Wearing gloves is enough; hand hygiene is not needed"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Wash hands with soap and running water
CLOSTRIDIOIDES DIFFICILE causes diarrhoea (often after antibiotic use) and forms SPORES — tough, dormant forms that are NOT KILLED by ALCOHOL HAND RUB. Spores remain on hands after alcohol rub and can spread to other patients. Washing hands with SOAP and RUNNING WATER physically REMOVES the spores from the skin through friction and rinsing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Use a chlorhexidine–alcohol hand rub — A chlorhexidine–alcohol rub does not kill C. difficile spores.
• Use alcohol-based hand rub only — Alcohol-based hand rub alone is not effective against spores.
• Wearing gloves is enough; hand hygiene is not needed — Gloves reduce contamination, but hands can be contaminated when removing them — hand washing is still needed afterwards.

⭐ Key point: C. difficile control: single room with its own toilet, contact precautions (gloves and apron), soap-and-water hand washing, cleaning with chlorine-based (sporicidal) disinfectant, and careful antibiotic stewardship. Norovirus also needs soap and water.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q197$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1755, $s$A nurse gets a deep needlestick injury from a patient known to be HIV-positive. After washing and encouraging bleeding of the wound, post-exposure prophylaxis should be started:$s$, $s$["As soon as possible (no later than 72 h), for 28 days", "After her own HIV test result returns, for 28 days", "Within 24 hours of the injury, taken for 6 months", "Within 7 days of the injury, taken for 14 days"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: As soon as possible (no later than 72 h), for 28 days
After a DEEP NEEDLESTICK injury from an HIV-POSITIVE patient (a high-risk exposure), the nurse should: wash the wound with soap and water and encourage bleeding (do NOT suck or scrub), REPORT immediately, and start POST-EXPOSURE PROPHYLAXIS (PEP) — a combination of antiretroviral drugs — AS SOON AS POSSIBLE (ideally within 1–2 hours, and no later than 72 hours). PEP is taken for 28 DAYS. Early PEP reduces the risk of HIV infection by about 80%.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• After her own HIV test result returns, for 28 days — PEP should not wait for the nurse's own test result; a baseline test is taken, but PEP is started immediately.
• Within 24 hours of the injury, taken for 6 months — PEP is taken for 28 days, not 6 months, and should start within hours, not 24 hours.
• Within 7 days of the injury, taken for 14 days — 7 days is far too late (PEP is not effective after 72 hours), and 14 days is too short.

⭐ Key point: Follow-up HIV tests at about 6 weeks and 3 months (per local guidelines). Also assess for hepatitis B and C exposure. Use safe practice: never recap needles, and use sharps bins at the point of care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q198$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1756, $s$Stress ulcer prophylaxis in ICU is clearly indicated in a patient who:$s$, $s$["With no bleeding risk factors and a short stay", "Ventilated for over 48 hours, or with a coagulopathy", "Taking low-dose aspirin only, with no other risks", "Eating a full diet after minor uncomplicated surgery"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ventilated for over 48 hours, or with a coagulopathy
STRESS ULCER PROPHYLAXIS (e.g. a PPI or H2 blocker) is clearly indicated in patients at high risk of bleeding: those MECHANICALLY VENTILATED FOR OVER 48 HOURS and those with a COAGULOPATHY. Other risk factors: shock, sepsis, severe burns, and head or spinal injury.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• With no bleeding risk factors and a short stay — Patients with no risk factors don't need prophylaxis.
• Taking low-dose aspirin only, with no other risks — Low-dose aspirin alone is not usually an indication in ICU.
• Eating a full diet after minor uncomplicated surgery — A patient eating normally after minor surgery doesn't need prophylaxis.

⭐ Key point: Stop prophylaxis when risk factors resolve, since it has side effects.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q67$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1757, $s$The most effective natural protection against stress ulceration in ICU is:$s$, $s$["Prolonged fasting before each procedure", "Giving liquid antacids every hour by NG tube", "Keeping the patient nil by mouth until extubation", "Early enteral feeding and good splanchnic perfusion"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Early enteral feeding and good splanchnic perfusion
EARLY ENTERAL FEEDING protects the stomach lining by buffering acid and maintaining blood flow to the gut. GOOD SPLANCHNIC PERFUSION (adequate blood flow to the gut, through good BP and cardiac output) keeps the lining healthy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prolonged fasting before each procedure — Prolonged fasting increases stress ulcer risk.
• Giving liquid antacids every hour by NG tube — Hourly antacids are not the most effective protection.
• Keeping the patient nil by mouth until extubation — Keeping patients nil by mouth increases risk.

⭐ Key point: Stress ulcers are caused by poor blood flow to the gut lining.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q69$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1758, $s$Lassa fever is transmitted mainly by:$s$, $s$["Contaminated beef from infected cattle", "Tick bites in grassland areas", "The multimammate rat and infected body fluids", "Anopheles mosquito bites at night"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The multimammate rat and infected body fluids
LASSA FEVER is spread mainly by the MULTIMAMMATE RAT (Mastomys natalensis), which lives in and around homes. People are infected by eating food contaminated with rat urine or droppings, inhaling dust, or catching and eating the rats. It also spreads from PERSON TO PERSON through contact with BLOOD and BODY FLUIDS (especially in hospitals without good infection control).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Contaminated beef from infected cattle — Lassa is not spread by eating beef.
• Tick bites in grassland areas — Lassa is not spread by tick bites.
• Anopheles mosquito bites at night — Lassa is not spread by mosquitoes; malaria is spread by Anopheles mosquitoes.

⭐ Key point: Prevention: store food in rat-proof containers, keep homes clean, avoid drying food in the open, and use standard precautions in hospitals.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q114$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1759, $s$The specific antiviral used for Lassa fever is:$s$, $s$["Oseltamivir given orally", "Ribavirin, best started early", "Acyclovir given IV 8-hourly", "Zidovudine with lamivudine"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ribavirin, best started early
RIBAVIRIN is the specific antiviral drug used for Lassa fever. It works best when STARTED EARLY (within the first 6 days of illness), greatly reducing the risk of death. It is given IV for treatment, and orally for post-exposure prophylaxis in high-risk contacts.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oseltamivir given orally — Oseltamivir (Tamiflu) is used for influenza.
• Acyclovir given IV 8-hourly — Acyclovir is used for herpes viruses.
• Zidovudine with lamivudine — Zidovudine and lamivudine are HIV drugs.

⭐ Key point: Side effects of ribavirin include haemolytic anaemia — monitor haemoglobin. Supportive care (fluids, electrolytes, oxygen, blood products) is also essential.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q115$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1760, $s$Ebola virus disease is transmitted mainly by:$s$, $s$["Drinking contaminated water supplies only", "Bites from infected mosquitoes at dawn and dusk", "Contact with blood or body fluids of the sick or dead", "Airborne spread over long distances in the community"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Contact with blood or body fluids of the sick or dead
EBOLA virus disease spreads by DIRECT CONTACT with BLOOD or BODY FLUIDS (vomit, diarrhoea, urine, saliva, sweat, semen, breast milk) of SICK or DEAD people, and with contaminated items (needles, bedding). It also spreads from infected animals (bats, monkeys).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Drinking contaminated water supplies only — Ebola is not spread mainly by contaminated water.
• Bites from infected mosquitoes at dawn and dusk — Ebola is not spread by mosquitoes.
• Airborne spread over long distances in the community — Ebola is not spread through the air over long distances.

⭐ Key point: Bodies of Ebola victims are highly infectious. Safe burial practices are essential to control outbreaks.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q117$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1761, $s$The most dangerous organism causing puerperal sepsis is:$s$, $s$["Staphylococcus epidermidis from the skin", "Group A Streptococcus (S. pyogenes)", "Lactobacillus species from the vagina", "Candida albicans after antibiotics"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Group A Streptococcus (S. pyogenes)
GROUP A STREPTOCOCCUS (Streptococcus pyogenes) is the MOST DANGEROUS cause of puerperal sepsis. It can spread from the woman's throat or skin, or from carers' hands, into the genital tract after delivery. It multiplies rapidly and releases powerful toxins, causing STREPTOCOCCAL TOXIC SHOCK SYNDROME, necrotising fasciitis and death — sometimes within hours. Before antibiotics and hand hygiene, it caused epidemics of "childbed fever" that killed many women. Early signs may be subtle: severe abdominal pain, diarrhoea, fever, rapid pulse — then rapid collapse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Staphylococcus epidermidis from the skin — Staphylococcus epidermidis is a common skin organism that mostly causes line-related infections; it is not the most dangerous cause of puerperal sepsis.
• Lactobacillus species from the vagina — Lactobacilli are normal, protective vaginal bacteria; they do not cause puerperal sepsis.
• Candida albicans after antibiotics — Candida causes vaginal thrush, usually mild; it is not a major cause of life-threatening puerperal sepsis.

⭐ Key point: Strict hand hygiene by staff, early recognition and IV antibiotics (e.g. penicillin with clindamycin) save lives. Close contacts may need prophylaxis in invasive Group A Strep infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q130$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1762, $s$The Subjective Global Assessment (SGA) classifies nutritional status using:$s$, $s$["Blood glucose and HbA1c measured on the first ICU day", "History (weight loss, intake, GI symptoms, function) plus examination", "Body mass index alone, calculated from height and weight", "Serum albumin alone, measured on admission and weekly"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: History (weight loss, intake, GI symptoms, function) plus examination
The SUBJECTIVE GLOBAL ASSESSMENT (SGA) classifies nutritional status (well-nourished, moderately or severely malnourished) using: HISTORY (weight loss, dietary intake, GI symptoms, functional capacity) PLUS physical EXAMINATION (loss of fat, muscle wasting, oedema).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Blood glucose and HbA1c measured on the first ICU day — Glucose and HbA1c assess diabetes control.
• Body mass index alone, calculated from height and weight — BMI alone is incomplete.
• Serum albumin alone, measured on admission and weekly — Albumin alone is affected by inflammation.

⭐ Key point: SGA is simple and doesn't need lab tests.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q185$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1763, $s$Hospital-acquired infections in ICU are reduced most by:$s$, $s$["Changing all IV lines every day", "Hand hygiene and care bundles", "Routine broad-spectrum antibiotics for all", "Keeping the windows open for fresh air"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hand hygiene and care bundles
HOSPITAL-ACQUIRED INFECTIONS (HAIs) in ICU — such as ventilator-associated pneumonia, central line bloodstream infections and catheter-associated urinary infections — are mostly spread by staff HANDS and invasive devices. They are reduced most by: HAND HYGIENE (washing or alcohol rub before and after patient contact, following WHO's "5 moments") and CARE BUNDLES (groups of evidence-based practices performed together for every patient, e.g. the VAP bundle and the central line bundle).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Changing all IV lines every day — Changing IV lines daily increases manipulation and infection risk; lines are changed only when needed or by policy.
• Routine broad-spectrum antibiotics for all — Routine broad-spectrum antibiotics for all patients cause antibiotic resistance and C. difficile, without preventing most HAIs.
• Keeping the windows open for fresh air — Open windows do not reduce ICU infections and may introduce dust and insects.

⭐ Key point: Hand hygiene is the single most important and cheapest way to prevent the spread of infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q197$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1764, $s$Reusable ventilator circuits and laryngoscope blades in ICU must undergo at least:$s$, $s$["Wiping with a dry cloth after each use", "Cleaning, then high-level disinfection or sterilisation", "Rinsing with tap water between each patient", "Low-level disinfection with soap and water only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cleaning, then high-level disinfection or sterilisation
The SPAULDING CLASSIFICATION groups equipment by infection risk. Items that contact MUCOUS MEMBRANES or non-intact skin, such as reusable VENTILATOR CIRCUITS and LARYNGOSCOPE BLADES, are SEMI-CRITICAL. They must first be CLEANED (to remove secretions and organic material — disinfection cannot work through dirt), then undergo at least HIGH-LEVEL DISINFECTION (killing all microorganisms except large numbers of bacterial spores) or STERILISATION. Critical items (entering sterile tissue, e.g. surgical instruments) need sterilisation; non-critical items (touching intact skin, e.g. BP cuffs) need low-level disinfection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wiping with a dry cloth after each use — Wiping with a dry cloth does not remove or kill germs.
• Rinsing with tap water between each patient — Rinsing with tap water does not disinfect and may contaminate equipment with waterborne bacteria (e.g. Pseudomonas).
• Low-level disinfection with soap and water only — Low-level disinfection is only suitable for non-critical items touching intact skin.

⭐ Key point: Many ICUs use single-use ventilator circuits and laryngoscope blades to reduce cross-infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q200$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1765, $s$A transplant recipient on immunosuppression develops a fever of 38.4 °C. The nurse should:$s$, $s$["Stop all immunosuppressant drugs without waiting for orders", "Give paracetamol and review again at the next day's ward round", "Treat as possible infection or rejection and inform the team at once", "Assume it is a normal post-operative fever and observe only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Treat as possible infection or rejection and inform the team at once
A transplant recipient on immunosuppression may not show the usual signs of infection or rejection. A fever of 38.4 °C could mean a serious INFECTION (bacterial, CMV, fungal) or ACUTE REJECTION. Treat it as potentially serious and INFORM the team AT ONCE for cultures, blood tests, and assessment of graft function.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stop all immunosuppressant drugs without waiting for orders — Stopping immunosuppressants without orders can trigger rejection.
• Give paracetamol and review again at the next day's ward round — Waiting until the next day could allow sepsis or rejection to progress.
• Assume it is a normal post-operative fever and observe only — Assuming a normal fever is dangerous in immunosuppressed patients.

⭐ Key point: Infections in transplant patients can progress quickly. Have a low threshold for early treatment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q11$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1766, $s$Before inserting a central venous catheter, the nurse should prepare:$s$, $s$["Only a syringe, a cannula and a dressing", "A urinary catheter set with sterile water", "A clean dressing pack and non-sterile gloves only", "Full aseptic set, ultrasound, flushes, and a head-down position"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Full aseptic set, ultrasound, flushes, and a head-down position
Central line insertion needs: a FULL ASEPTIC SET (sterile gown, gloves, cap, mask, full-body drape, 2% chlorhexidine), ULTRASOUND guidance (reduces complications), FLUSHES (saline to prime the lumens), and a HEAD-DOWN position (Trendelenburg) for neck and chest lines, which fills the veins and prevents air embolism.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only a syringe, a cannula and a dressing — A syringe, cannula and dressing alone isn't enough.
• A urinary catheter set with sterile water — A urinary catheter set isn't relevant.
• A clean dressing pack and non-sterile gloves only — Clean dressings and non-sterile gloves don't meet sterile standards.

⭐ Key point: The CLABSI insertion bundle: hand hygiene, maximal barrier precautions, chlorhexidine, optimal site, and a checklist.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q24$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1767, $s$A patient with tetanus in ICU should be nursed in:$s$, $s$["A quiet, dark room with minimal stimulation", "A brightly lit open ward", "A room with constant visitors", "A busy area near the nurses' station noise"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A quiet, dark room with minimal stimulation
In TETANUS, stimuli (noise, light, touch, draughts) trigger painful muscle SPASMS, including laryngospasm, which can stop breathing. The patient should be nursed in a QUIET, DARK (dim) room with MINIMAL STIMULATION, with care grouped together, and sedation and muscle relaxants as needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A brightly lit open ward — A brightly lit open ward triggers spasms.
• A room with constant visitors — Constant visitors increase stimulation.
• A busy area near the nurses' station noise — Noise triggers spasms.

⭐ Key point: Tetanus treatment: wound debridement, tetanus immunoglobulin, metronidazole, benzodiazepines and magnesium for spasms, ventilation if needed, and vaccination.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q54$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1768, $s$Eye care in toxic epidermal necrolysis is vital because:$s$, $s$["It treats the underlying drug reaction directly", "The eyes are never involved in this condition", "Conjunctival involvement can cause scarring and blindness", "It only prevents dry skin around the eyelids"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Conjunctival involvement can cause scarring and blindness
TEN often involves the EYES (conjunctiva). Inflammation, ulceration and adhesions can lead to SCARRING, dry eyes, eyelid deformities and BLINDNESS. Daily eye care, lubricants, an ophthalmology review, and separation of adhesions are vital.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It treats the underlying drug reaction directly — Eye care doesn't treat the underlying drug reaction.
• The eyes are never involved in this condition — The eyes ARE commonly involved.
• It only prevents dry skin around the eyelids — Eye care does much more than prevent dry skin.

⭐ Key point: Also give mouth care and genital care (mucosal involvement) in TEN.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q60$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1769, $s$Infected pancreatic necrosis is suspected when:$s$, $s$["A rise in serum amylase on the first day of illness", "Pain that improves when oral feeding is restarted", "A normal blood glucose throughout the admission", "Fever and worsening organs at 7–10 days, with gas on CT"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fever and worsening organs at 7–10 days, with gas on CT
INFECTED pancreatic necrosis usually develops 7–10 days (or later) after the start of severe pancreatitis. It is suspected when there is FEVER, worsening organ function, and rising inflammatory markers, with GAS bubbles in the necrotic pancreas on CT. It needs antibiotics and drainage or necrosectomy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A rise in serum amylase on the first day of illness — Amylase rises on day 1 in all pancreatitis.
• Pain that improves when oral feeding is restarted — Improving pain with feeding is a good sign.
• A normal blood glucose throughout the admission — Normal glucose doesn't suggest infection.

⭐ Key point: Infected necrosis has a high mortality. A step-up approach (drainage first, then surgery if needed) is used.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q67$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1770, $s$A Category 4 pressure ulcer involves:$s$, $s$["Partial-thickness loss with a shallow, open ulcer", "Intact skin with non-blanching redness over bone", "Full-thickness loss with exposed bone, tendon or muscle", "Full-thickness skin loss with only fat visible"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Full-thickness loss with exposed bone, tendon or muscle
A CATEGORY 4 pressure ulcer is FULL-THICKNESS tissue loss with EXPOSED BONE, TENDON or MUSCLE. Slough or eschar may be present, and undermining and tunnelling are common. It is the most severe category.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partial-thickness loss with a shallow, open ulcer — A partial-thickness shallow ulcer is Category 2.
• Intact skin with non-blanching redness over bone — Intact skin with non-blanching redness is Category 1.
• Full-thickness skin loss with only fat visible — Full-thickness loss with only fat visible is Category 3.

⭐ Key point: Unstageable ulcers have their base covered by slough or eschar, so the depth can't be seen.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q73$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1771, $s$Prone positioning for ARDS increases the risk of pressure injury to the:$s$, $s$["Back of the head and shoulders only", "Elbows and wrists only", "Face, chest, knees and iliac crests", "Heels, sacrum and occiput only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Face, chest, knees and iliac crests
In PRONE positioning, the patient lies face down, so pressure falls on the FRONT of the body: the FACE (forehead, cheeks, chin, nose, eyes), CHEST, KNEES, ILIAC CRESTS, breasts and genitals. Pressure injuries are common, especially during long prone sessions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Back of the head and shoulders only — The back of the head and shoulders are at risk in the SUPINE position.
• Elbows and wrists only — Elbows and wrists can be affected, but the main areas are the face, chest, knees and iliac crests.
• Heels, sacrum and occiput only — Heels, sacrum and occiput are at risk in the SUPINE position.

⭐ Key point: Use padding, reposition the head and arms every 2–4 hours ("swimmer's position"), and protect the eyes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q74$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1772, $s$The most effective way to reduce neonatal infection in a special care baby unit is:$s$, $s$["Bathing every baby twice a day with antiseptic", "Keeping the mothers out of the unit completely", "Routine prophylactic antibiotics for all babies", "Hand hygiene before and after every baby contact"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hand hygiene before and after every baby contact
HAND HYGIENE before and after EVERY baby contact is the single most effective way to reduce infection in neonatal units. Hands carry germs from baby to baby and from equipment to baby.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bathing every baby twice a day with antiseptic — Twice-daily antiseptic bathing harms the delicate skin.
• Keeping the mothers out of the unit completely — Keeping mothers out reduces breastfeeding and kangaroo care, which protect against infection.
• Routine prophylactic antibiotics for all babies — Routine antibiotics cause resistance and harm.

⭐ Key point: Breast milk, kangaroo care, and careful line care also reduce neonatal infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q90$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1773, $s$Cotrimoxazole prophylaxis in HIV-exposed infants prevents:$s$, $s$["HIV transmission from the mother", "Neonatal jaundice and kernicterus", "Pneumocystis pneumonia and other infections", "Malaria completely, in all infants"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pneumocystis pneumonia and other infections
CO-TRIMOXAZOLE (sulfamethoxazole + trimethoprim) given daily to HIV-exposed infants prevents PNEUMOCYSTIS JIROVECII PNEUMONIA (PCP), a major killer of HIV-infected infants. It also reduces other bacterial infections, toxoplasmosis and some diarrhoeal diseases, and gives partial protection against malaria. It is started at 6 weeks of age and continued until HIV infection is excluded after breastfeeding stops.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• HIV transmission from the mother — Co-trimoxazole does NOT prevent mother-to-child HIV transmission. That is prevented by antiretroviral drugs (for mother and baby) and safe delivery/feeding practices.
• Neonatal jaundice and kernicterus — It does not prevent neonatal jaundice. In fact, sulfonamides can worsen jaundice in newborns, which is one reason it's started at 6 weeks rather than at birth.
• Malaria completely, in all infants — It gives some protection against malaria, but NOT complete protection. Bed nets and other malaria control are still needed.

⭐ Key point: Co-trimoxazole prophylaxis: start at 6 weeks, give daily, continue until the baby is confirmed HIV-negative after breastfeeding ends. If HIV-positive, continue and start ART.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q115$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1774, $s$The incubation period of Lassa fever is about:$s$, $s$["6–21 days", "1–2 hours", "2 months", "1 year"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 6–21 days
Lassa fever is a viral haemorrhagic fever caused by the Lassa virus, spread mainly by the multimammate rat (Mastomys natalensis) through food or household items contaminated with rat urine or droppings, and from person to person through blood and body fluids. Its INCUBATION PERIOD (time from infection to first symptoms) is about 6 to 21 DAYS. Early symptoms are vague: fever, weakness, headache, sore throat, muscle pain, and later vomiting, chest pain, facial swelling, bleeding, and shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1–2 hours — 1–2 hours is too short for any viral haemorrhagic fever. That timing fits some toxin-related food poisoning.
• 2 months — 2 months is too long; Lassa symptoms usually appear within 3 weeks.
• 1 year — 1 year is far too long for Lassa fever.

⭐ Key point: Because the incubation is up to 21 days, contacts are monitored (temperature daily) for 21 days after their last exposure. Lassa fever is endemic in Nigeria, especially in Edo, Ondo, Bauchi and Ebonyi states.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q116$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1775, $s$A health worker has a needle-stick injury from a Lassa fever patient. The recommended action is:$s$, $s$["Report at once and start post-exposure ribavirin per policy", "Take paracetamol only and continue the shift", "Ignore it unless symptoms appear within the next month", "Continue working without reporting to avoid panic"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Report at once and start post-exposure ribavirin per policy
A needle-stick injury from a Lassa fever patient is a HIGH-RISK EXPOSURE. The health worker must: wash the site immediately with soap and water (let it bleed, do not squeeze), REPORT AT ONCE to the supervisor/infection control team, and be assessed for POST-EXPOSURE PROPHYLAXIS with oral RIBAVIRIN according to national (NCDC) guidelines. The worker is then followed up with temperature checks for 21 days.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Take paracetamol only and continue the shift — Paracetamol does nothing to stop infection and could hide a developing fever. Continuing the shift without reporting risks the worker and others.
• Ignore it unless symptoms appear within the next month — Waiting for symptoms delays treatment. Ribavirin works best when started early.
• Continue working without reporting to avoid panic — Hiding an exposure endangers the worker, their family and other patients. Reporting is a professional and legal duty.

⭐ Key point: Prevention: full PPE (gloves, gown, face shield/goggles, mask), never recap needles, and use a sharps box at the bedside. Rodent control and food storage are key in the community.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q117$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1776, $s$Safe burial of a patient who died of Ebola requires:$s$, $s$["Trained teams with full PPE; relatives do not wash the body", "Family members washing and dressing the body at home", "Keeping the body at home for several days for mourning", "Open-casket viewing at the funeral service"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Trained teams with full PPE; relatives do not wash the body
The bodies of people who die of EBOLA contain very high amounts of virus and remain HIGHLY INFECTIOUS after death. Washing, touching, or kissing the body during traditional funeral rites has spread many Ebola outbreaks. SAFE AND DIGNIFIED BURIAL means: specially TRAINED BURIAL TEAMS wearing FULL PPE place the body in a sealed body bag, disinfect it, and bury it promptly. Relatives may watch from a safe distance and religious rites can be respected, but they must NOT wash or touch the body.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Family members washing and dressing the body at home — Family washing the body is one of the main ways Ebola spreads in communities.
• Keeping the body at home for several days for mourning — Keeping the body at home for days exposes many mourners to the virus.
• Open-casket viewing at the funeral service — Open-casket viewing and touching the body risk infection; the body should be sealed in a bag.

⭐ Key point: Safe burial must still be dignified and respectful. Engage religious and community leaders to explain why the usual rites must change during an outbreak.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q118$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1777, $s$The ICU nurse manager's role in infection control includes:$s$, $s$["Prescribing antibiotics for all patients", "Monitoring compliance, training staff, reporting rates", "Performing all invasive procedures personally", "Ignoring the results of infection audits"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Monitoring compliance, training staff, reporting rates
The ICU nurse manager leads infection prevention in the unit by: MONITORING COMPLIANCE (hand hygiene audits, care bundles), TRAINING STAFF (infection control practices, PPE use), and REPORTING INFECTION RATES (e.g. VAP, CLABSI, CAUTI) to the infection control team. Surveillance data guide improvements.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prescribing antibiotics for all patients — Prescribing antibiotics is the doctor's role, and not all patients need antibiotics.
• Performing all invasive procedures personally — The manager does not personally perform all invasive procedures; trained staff perform them using aseptic technique.
• Ignoring the results of infection audits — Ignoring audit results wastes the opportunity to improve care.

⭐ Key point: Hand hygiene is the single most important measure to prevent infection in ICU.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q169$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1778, $s$A care bundle in ICU is:$s$, $s$["A group of patients cared for by one nurse", "A small set of evidence-based practices done together", "A collection of emergency drugs on a trolley", "A package of clean linen prepared for each bed"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A small set of evidence-based practices done together
A CARE BUNDLE is a SMALL SET (usually 3–5) of EVIDENCE-BASED PRACTICES that, when performed TOGETHER and consistently, improve patient outcomes more than when done individually. Examples: the VAP bundle (head of bed 30–45°, daily sedation hold, oral care with chlorhexidine, subglottic suction, DVT and stress ulcer prophylaxis); the central line (CLABSI) bundle; the sepsis bundle.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A group of patients cared for by one nurse — A group of patients cared for by one nurse is a "patient assignment".
• A collection of emergency drugs on a trolley — Emergency drugs on a trolley form a "crash cart".
• A package of clean linen prepared for each bed — Linen packages are not care bundles.

⭐ Key point: Bundles work by "all or nothing" compliance — every element should be done for every patient.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q174$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1779, $s$Monitoring ICU quality indicators includes measuring:$s$, $s$["The number of visitors to the unit each day", "The number of chairs available in the unit", "Unplanned readmissions, infection rates, mortality", "The colour and style of the staff uniforms"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Unplanned readmissions, infection rates, mortality
ICU QUALITY INDICATORS measure the quality and safety of care: UNPLANNED READMISSIONS to ICU, INFECTION RATES (VAP, CLABSI, CAUTI), MORTALITY, pressure ulcers, medication errors, unplanned extubations and patient satisfaction. Monitoring these guides quality improvement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The number of visitors to the unit each day — The number of visitors does not measure quality of care.
• The number of chairs available in the unit — The number of chairs does not measure quality of care.
• The colour and style of the staff uniforms — Uniform style is not a quality indicator.

⭐ Key point: Quality indicators should be measured regularly and compared to benchmarks.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q175$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1780, $s$Infection risk in an ICU is reduced in the design by:$s$, $s$["No mechanical ventilation system at all", "Carpets on the floor to reduce noise", "Single isolation rooms and sinks by each bed", "Equipment shared between neighbouring beds"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Single isolation rooms and sinks by each bed
Good ICU DESIGN reduces infection: SINGLE ISOLATION ROOMS prevent spread between patients; SINKS BY EACH BED make hand hygiene easy; NEGATIVE PRESSURE ROOMS are used for airborne infections (e.g. TB); enough space between beds; and easy-to-clean surfaces.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No mechanical ventilation system at all — Mechanical ventilation (airflow) systems are important for clean air; having none increases infection risk.
• Carpets on the floor to reduce noise — Carpets trap dirt and germs and are hard to clean; ICU floors should be smooth and washable.
• Equipment shared between neighbouring beds — Sharing equipment between beds spreads infection; each patient should have their own.

⭐ Key point: Hand hygiene facilities at every bedside are key to compliance.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q176$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1781, $s$A comprehensive nutritional assessment typically integrates which four broad components?$s$, $s$["Blood pressure, pulse, respiratory rate and core temperature", "Body weight and height only, used to calculate the BMI", "Anthropometry, biochemistry, clinical examination and dietary history", "Vital signs, medication list, allergy history and resuscitation status"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Anthropometry, biochemistry, clinical examination and dietary history
A comprehensive NUTRITIONAL ASSESSMENT uses the "ABCD" approach: A – ANTHROPOMETRY (weight, height, BMI, mid-upper arm circumference, skinfold thickness); B – BIOCHEMISTRY (albumin, prealbumin, electrolytes, glucose, micronutrients); C – CLINICAL EXAMINATION (muscle wasting, oedema, hair, skin and nail changes); D – DIETARY HISTORY (what and how much the patient eats, appetite, swallowing).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Blood pressure, pulse, respiratory rate and core temperature — Vital signs are part of general assessment, not specifically nutritional assessment.
• Body weight and height only, used to calculate the BMI — Weight and height alone (BMI) is incomplete and can be misleading (e.g. oedema hides weight loss).
• Vital signs, medication list, allergy history and resuscitation status — Vital signs, medications, allergies and resuscitation status are general clinical information, not nutritional assessment.

⭐ Key point: Screening tools such as MUST or NUTRIC identify patients at risk of malnutrition who need full assessment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q183$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1782, $s$Which of the following is an example of an anthropometric measurement used in nutritional assessment?$s$, $s$["Mid-upper arm circumference", "Serum albumin level", "White blood cell count", "C-reactive protein level"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mid-upper arm circumference
ANTHROPOMETRIC measurements assess body size and composition. Examples: weight, height, BMI, MID-UPPER ARM CIRCUMFERENCE (MUAC), skinfold thickness, and waist circumference. MUAC is especially useful in ICU patients who cannot be weighed, and in children to screen for malnutrition.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Serum albumin level — Serum albumin is a BIOCHEMICAL measurement, and it is affected by inflammation and fluid status.
• White blood cell count — White cell count is a biochemical/haematological test related to infection or inflammation, not anthropometry.
• C-reactive protein level — CRP is a biochemical marker of inflammation.

⭐ Key point: In children, MUAC below 11.5 cm indicates severe acute malnutrition.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q184$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1783, $s$Gastric residual volumes in an enterally fed ICU patient are:$s$, $s$["Never checked in any enterally fed ICU patient", "The only reliable measure of feed tolerance in ICU", "Used to calculate the patient's daily calorie needs", "Only one sign of tolerance, alongside abdominal examination"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Only one sign of tolerance, alongside abdominal examination
GASTRIC RESIDUAL VOLUME (GRV) — the amount of feed left in the stomach — is only ONE SIGN of feed tolerance. It must be considered together with ABDOMINAL EXAMINATION (distension, pain, bowel sounds), vomiting, and bowel movements. Studies show that stopping feeds for moderate residuals (e.g. under 500 mL) without other signs leads to underfeeding, without reducing pneumonia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Never checked in any enterally fed ICU patient — GRVs may still be checked in some units as part of a tolerance assessment.
• The only reliable measure of feed tolerance in ICU — It is not the only reliable measure; it is unreliable on its own.
• Used to calculate the patient's daily calorie needs — GRV does not calculate calorie needs.

⭐ Key point: Keep the head of the bed at 30–45° to reduce aspiration. Consider prokinetics (e.g. metoclopramide, erythromycin) for intolerance.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q186$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1784, $s$Parenteral nutrition via a central line requires:$s$, $s$["Hanging each bag for up to 72 hours", "No need for blood glucose monitoring", "Sharing the lumen with blood transfusions", "Strict asepsis, a dedicated lumen and glucose checks"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Strict asepsis, a dedicated lumen and glucose checks
PARENTERAL NUTRITION (PN) is a sugar- and fat-rich solution that is an ideal growth medium for bacteria. It requires: STRICT ASEPSIS when handling lines and bags; a DEDICATED LUMEN of the central line (not shared with other infusions or blood); and regular BLOOD GLUCOSE CHECKS (because high glucose content often causes hyperglycaemia). Also monitor electrolytes, liver function and triglycerides.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hanging each bag for up to 72 hours — PN bags should hang for a maximum of 24 hours (lipids often 12–24 hours) to prevent bacterial growth.
• No need for blood glucose monitoring — Glucose must be monitored; hyperglycaemia is a common complication.
• Sharing the lumen with blood transfusions — Sharing the lumen with blood transfusion increases the risk of infection and incompatibility.

⭐ Key point: Watch for refeeding syndrome (low phosphate, potassium and magnesium) when starting PN in malnourished patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q187$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1785, $s$Calorie needs of a critically ill adult in the early phase are about:$s$, $s$["100 kcal/kg/day", "20–25 kcal/kg/day", "50 kcal/kg/day", "5 kcal/kg/day"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 20–25 kcal/kg/day
In the early (acute) phase of critical illness, the recommended calorie intake is about 20–25 kcal/kg/day, increasing gradually as the patient recovers (up to 25–30 kcal/kg/day). Early "permissive underfeeding" may be beneficial because the body produces its own glucose during stress, and overfeeding causes harm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100 kcal/kg/day — 100 kcal/kg/day is massive overfeeding for an adult.
• 50 kcal/kg/day — 50 kcal/kg/day would cause overfeeding, leading to hyperglycaemia, fatty liver and increased CO2 production.
• 5 kcal/kg/day — 5 kcal/kg/day is far too little and would cause severe malnutrition.

⭐ Key point: Overfeeding increases CO2 production, which can make weaning from the ventilator harder. Protein needs are high (1.2–2.0 g/kg/day).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q188$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1786, $s$Enteral feeding should be started in a critically ill patient:$s$, $s$["Only after two weeks of IV fluids alone", "Only after the patient has been extubated", "Only when the patient asks for food", "Within 24–48 hours, once haemodynamically stable"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Within 24–48 hours, once haemodynamically stable
EARLY ENTERAL FEEDING — within 24–48 HOURS of ICU admission, once the patient is HAEMODYNAMICALLY STABLE (not on rising vasopressors, adequately resuscitated) — is recommended. It maintains the gut lining (gut integrity), reduces bacterial translocation and infection, and supports the immune system.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only after two weeks of IV fluids alone — Waiting two weeks causes malnutrition and gut atrophy.
• Only after the patient has been extubated — Ventilated patients can be safely fed by nasogastric tube; extubation is not required.
• Only when the patient asks for food — Many ICU patients are sedated or intubated and cannot ask for food; nutrition must be planned.

⭐ Key point: Enteral feeding is preferred over parenteral feeding when the gut works ("If the gut works, use it").$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q189$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1787, $s$Contact precautions for MRSA in ICU include:$s$, $s$["Gloves, apron or gown, own equipment, hand hygiene", "Nursing in an open bay without isolation", "No special precautions for MRSA patients", "Airborne N95 masks only, without gloves"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Gloves, apron or gown, own equipment, hand hygiene
MRSA (methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus) spreads mainly by CONTACT — hands, equipment and surfaces. CONTACT PRECAUTIONS include: GLOVES and APRON/GOWN for contact with the patient or surroundings; DEDICATED EQUIPMENT (own stethoscope, BP cuff, thermometer); strict HAND HYGIENE before and after contact; and ideally a single room or cohorting.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nursing in an open bay without isolation — Nursing MRSA patients in an open bay without isolation increases spread.
• No special precautions for MRSA patients — MRSA requires precautions to prevent spread to other vulnerable patients.
• Airborne N95 masks only, without gloves — MRSA is not airborne; N95 masks are not the main precaution, and gloves are essential.

⭐ Key point: Decolonisation (e.g. nasal mupirocin and chlorhexidine body wash) may be used to reduce MRSA carriage.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q196$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1788, $s$Lactic acidosis in septic shock mainly reflects:$s$, $s$["Tissue hypoperfusion and anaerobic metabolism", "Renal retention of bicarbonate in shock", "Liver overproduction of glucose from stress", "Excess dietary lactate from enteral feeding"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tissue hypoperfusion and anaerobic metabolism
In septic shock, blood flow to the tissues is poor (low BP, microcirculation problems), so cells don't get enough oxygen. Without oxygen, cells switch to ANAEROBIC metabolism, which makes lactate (lactic acid). A high lactate (above 2 mmol/L) shows tissue hypoperfusion; above 4 mmol/L means severe shock and a high risk of death. A falling lactate shows resuscitation is working.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Renal retention of bicarbonate in shock — The kidneys don't hold bicarbonate in shock; the problem is acid production.
• Liver overproduction of glucose from stress — The liver CLEARS lactate; liver failure can worsen it, but overproduction of glucose isn't the cause.
• Excess dietary lactate from enteral feeding — Enteral feeds don't cause lactic acidosis.

⭐ Key point: Measure lactate in the first hour of sepsis and repeat it within 2–4 hours if it was raised ("lactate clearance").$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q38$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1789, $s$A family caregiver of a chronically critically ill ICU patient reports exhaustion, feeling overwhelmed by care responsibilities, and increasing strain in the relationship with the patient. The most appropriate NANDA-I diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Ineffective Family Coping", "Interrupted Family Processes", "Compromised Family Coping", "Caregiver Role Strain"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Caregiver Role Strain
Caregiver Role Strain is the NANDA-I diagnosis for a family member who has difficulty performing the caregiving role. Its defining features match this case exactly: exhaustion, feeling overwhelmed by care tasks, worry, and strain in the relationship with the person being cared for. It is common in families of long-stay ICU patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ineffective Family Coping — Ineffective Family Coping is when the family's coping is so poor it harms the patient; it doesn't focus on the caregiver role.
• Interrupted Family Processes — Interrupted Family Processes is a change in how the family functions (roles, communication), not specifically caregiver exhaustion.
• Compromised Family Coping — Compromised Family Coping is when a usually supportive person gives insufficient support; it doesn't describe the caregiver's own strain.

⭐ Key point: Interventions: listen, give information, arrange respite and rest, involve social work, and teach care skills. Look after the carer as well as the patient.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q61$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1790, $s$A wife shouts at the ICU nurse that her husband is 'not being cared for'. This is most likely:$s$, $s$["A clear sign that she intends to sue the hospital", "Normal behaviour that needs no response from staff", "Evidence of a mental illness needing a psychiatrist", "An expression of fear and helplessness under stress"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An expression of fear and helplessness under stress
When a loved one is critically ill, family members feel fear, helplessness, loss of control and anxiety. These feelings are often expressed as ANGER directed at staff. This is a normal stress reaction and usually not a real complaint about care. The best response is to stay calm, acknowledge her feelings ("I can see you're very worried"), listen, and give clear information.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A clear sign that she intends to sue the hospital — Anger does not mean she intends to sue; it is more often fear.
• Normal behaviour that needs no response from staff — Staff must respond with empathy; ignoring her would increase her distress.
• Evidence of a mental illness needing a psychiatrist — Shouting under stress is not a sign of mental illness.

⭐ Key point: Use therapeutic communication: listen, acknowledge, don't argue or become defensive, give information, and involve senior staff if needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q67$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1791, $s$Signs that a family is not coping include:$s$, $s$["Expressing sadness and crying at the bedside", "Asking appropriate questions about the care", "Visiting regularly at the permitted times", "Withdrawal, constant anger, no decision-making"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Withdrawal, constant anger, no decision-making
Signs that a family is NOT coping include WITHDRAWAL (stopping visiting, avoiding staff), persistent ANGER or hostility, inability to make decisions, denial of obvious facts, family conflict, and neglect of their own health. These families need extra support: social work, counselling, chaplaincy or psychology.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Expressing sadness and crying at the bedside — Crying and sadness at the bedside are NORMAL, healthy expressions of grief.
• Asking appropriate questions about the care — Asking appropriate questions shows the family is engaged and coping.
• Visiting regularly at the permitted times — Visiting regularly shows involvement and coping.

⭐ Key point: Assess the family, not just the patient. Normal grief reactions need support; signs of not coping need referral.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q68$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1792, $s$A nursing diagnosis for a family in crisis in ICU could be:$s$, $s$["Interrupted family processes related to critical illness", "Risk for infection related to invasive lines", "Acute pain related to the surgical incision", "Ineffective airway clearance related to secretions"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Interrupted family processes related to critical illness
Interrupted Family Processes is a NANDA-I diagnosis for when a family's normal relationships, roles and functioning are disrupted by a crisis, such as the sudden critical illness of a family member. Roles change (who earns, who cares for children), communication changes, and daily routines break down.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Risk for infection related to invasive lines — Risk for infection relates to the PATIENT's lines, not the family.
• Acute pain related to the surgical incision — Acute pain relates to the PATIENT's incision.
• Ineffective airway clearance related to secretions — Ineffective airway clearance relates to the PATIENT's breathing.

⭐ Key point: Family nursing interventions: give information, involve the family in care, support decision-making, and refer to social work or counselling services.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q69$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1793, $s$Eye care in the unconscious patient prevents:$s$, $s$["Acute angle-closure glaucoma", "Retinal detachment and blindness", "Corneal ulceration from exposure", "Cataract formation in the lens"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Corneal ulceration from exposure
In an unconscious patient, the blink reflex is lost and the eyelids may not close fully (lagophthalmos). The cornea dries out, becomes damaged and can ulcerate or become infected (exposure keratopathy), which can lead to blindness. Eye care includes cleaning, lubricating eye drops or gel, moist gauze or moisture chambers, and taping the lids closed if needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute angle-closure glaucoma — Acute angle-closure glaucoma is caused by blocked drainage of fluid in the eye, not exposure.
• Retinal detachment and blindness — Retinal detachment is not prevented by routine eye care.
• Cataract formation in the lens — Cataracts develop slowly over years and are not caused by short-term exposure.

⭐ Key point: Check the eyes every shift. Report redness, cloudiness or discharge. Eye care is especially important in prone positioning and in patients on muscle relaxants.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q73$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1794, $s$Mouth care in the unconscious ventilated patient is done with chlorhexidine to:$s$, $s$["Whiten the teeth for the family", "Reduce oral ulcers and nothing else", "Stimulate the flow of saliva", "Reduce ventilator-associated pneumonia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reduce ventilator-associated pneumonia
In a ventilated patient, germs from dental plaque and the mouth are swallowed past the tube cuff into the lungs, causing ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP). Chlorhexidine (0.12–0.2%) mouthwash or gel kills these bacteria and reduces dental plaque. Toothbrushing (at least twice daily) and oral care every 2–4 hours are part of the VAP prevention bundle.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Whiten the teeth for the family — Whitening the teeth is not the purpose of oral care.
• Reduce oral ulcers and nothing else — Oral care does more than prevent ulcers; its main ICU purpose is reducing VAP.
• Stimulate the flow of saliva — Chlorhexidine doesn't stimulate saliva; mouth moisturisers help a dry mouth.

⭐ Key point: VAP bundle: head up 30–45°, daily sedation hold, oral care with chlorhexidine, subglottic suction, cuff pressure 20–30 cmH₂O, and DVT and stress ulcer prophylaxis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q74$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1795, $s$A family member caring for a long-term ICU patient feels exhausted and overwhelmed. This is called:$s$, $s$["Caregiver burden (role strain)", "Compassion satisfaction", "Normal anticipatory grieving", "Learned resilience"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Caregiver burden (role strain)
Caregiver burden (Caregiver Role Strain in NANDA-I) is the physical, emotional and financial strain on a family member caring for a loved one for a long time. Signs: exhaustion, feeling overwhelmed, poor sleep, anxiety, anger, depression, and neglect of their own health. It is common in families of long-term ICU patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Compassion satisfaction — Compassion satisfaction is a POSITIVE feeling from helping others.
• Normal anticipatory grieving — Anticipatory grieving is grief before an expected death; it isn't the same as exhaustion from caregiving.
• Learned resilience — Learned resilience is a positive ability to cope.

⭐ Key point: Support carers: encourage rest and breaks (respite), share the care, refer to social work and support groups, and give clear information.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q77$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1796, $s$Post-intensive care syndrome-family (PICS-F) includes:$s$, $s$["Financial benefit to the family after discharge", "Anxiety, depression and PTSD in relatives", "Improved family relationships in every case", "Infection transmitted to the family members"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Anxiety, depression and PTSD in relatives
Post-intensive care syndrome-family (PICS-F) describes the psychological problems family members develop after a loved one's critical illness: ANXIETY, DEPRESSION, POST-TRAUMATIC STRESS DISORDER (PTSD) and complicated grief. They can last months to years. Up to a third of relatives are affected, especially after a death in ICU or when they had to make difficult decisions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Financial benefit to the family after discharge — PICS-F is harmful; there is no financial benefit.
• Improved family relationships in every case — Relationships may become strained rather than improved.
• Infection transmitted to the family members — PICS-F is psychological, not an infection.

⭐ Key point: Reduce PICS-F with good communication, family involvement in care and decisions, ICU diaries, and follow-up support.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q79$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1797, $s$In Ebola, health workers must remove PPE:$s$, $s$["In the patient's room with the patient watching", "Without hand hygiene", "Quickly in any order", "In a set sequence with a trained observer"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: In a set sequence with a trained observer
Most Ebola infections in health workers happen while TAKING OFF (doffing) PPE, because the outside of the PPE is heavily contaminated with blood, vomit and stool. PPE must be removed in a strict, step-by-step order, with hand hygiene between steps, while a TRAINED OBSERVER (buddy) watches and reads out each step to catch mistakes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• In the patient's room with the patient watching — Removing PPE in the patient's room risks contamination; it is done in a separate doffing area.
• Without hand hygiene — Hand hygiene is essential between every step.
• Quickly in any order — Rushing and doing it in any order causes self-contamination.

⭐ Key point: Ebola PPE includes a fluid-resistant gown or coverall, double gloves, a face shield or goggles, an N95 or surgical mask, a head cover, and boots or overshoes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q112$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1798, $s$The incubation period of Ebola virus disease is:$s$, $s$["1–2 hours", "2 months", "1 year", "2–21 days"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2–21 days
The incubation period of Ebola (from infection to the first symptoms) is 2–21 days, usually 8–10 days. That's why people who have been in contact with an Ebola patient are monitored for 21 DAYS after their last contact. An outbreak is declared over after 42 days (two incubation periods) without a new case.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1–2 hours — 1–2 hours is far too short.
• 2 months — 2 months is too long.
• 1 year — 1 year is far too long.

⭐ Key point: During incubation, a person is NOT infectious. They become infectious when symptoms start (fever, weakness, vomiting, diarrhoea, bleeding).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q113$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1799, $s$A child with Ebola is not infectious:$s$, $s$["Before symptoms begin", "After death", "During the vomiting phase", "When bleeding"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Before symptoms begin
Ebola is NOT infectious BEFORE symptoms begin, because the virus is not yet at high levels in the blood and body fluids. Once symptoms start, the person becomes infectious, and infectiousness increases as the illness gets worse (vomiting, diarrhoea, bleeding). After death, the body is EXTREMELY infectious.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• After death — After death the body contains very high levels of virus; unsafe burials spread Ebola.
• During the vomiting phase — During the vomiting phase, body fluids are highly infectious.
• When bleeding — When bleeding, blood is highly infectious.

⭐ Key point: Safe and dignified burial by trained teams is essential in Ebola control. Survivors can carry the virus in semen for months.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q114$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1800, $s$Neonatal tetanus is prevented by:$s$, $s$["Maternal tetanus toxoid and clean cord care", "Vitamin K injection given at birth", "Antibiotics given to every baby at birth", "Routine BCG given in the first week"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Maternal tetanus toxoid and clean cord care
Neonatal tetanus happens when tetanus spores enter the baby through the cut umbilical cord (from unclean cutting tools or traditional cord dressings). It is prevented by: 1) giving TETANUS TOXOID vaccine to the mother before or during pregnancy, so her antibodies cross the placenta and protect the baby; and 2) CLEAN delivery and clean cord care (sterile blade, keep the cord clean and dry; chlorhexidine is applied in Nigeria).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vitamin K injection given at birth — Vitamin K prevents bleeding (haemorrhagic disease of the newborn), not tetanus.
• Antibiotics given to every baby at birth — Routine antibiotics don't prevent tetanus.
• Routine BCG given in the first week — BCG protects against tuberculosis, not tetanus.

⭐ Key point: Neonatal tetanus usually starts around day 5–7: the baby stops sucking, has a stiff jaw (trismus), muscle spasms and arching. It has a very high death rate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q118$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1801, $s$Severe acute malnutrition with shock is treated with:$s$, $s$["No IV or oral fluids at all until feeding", "Cautious IV fluids (15 mL/kg) with close monitoring", "IV diuretics to remove excess body water", "Rapid 60 mL/kg boluses over 15 minutes"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cautious IV fluids (15 mL/kg) with close monitoring
Children with severe acute malnutrition have weak hearts and abnormal salt and water handling. Normal fluid boluses can quickly cause HEART FAILURE and death. If in shock, give CAUTIOUS IV fluids (15 mL/kg over 1 hour, with Ringer's lactate plus 5% dextrose, or half-strength Darrow's with 5% glucose) and monitor pulse and breathing every 5–10 minutes. Stop if the pulse or breathing rate rises.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No IV or oral fluids at all until feeding — Withholding all fluids in shock would be fatal.
• IV diuretics to remove excess body water — Diuretics would make dehydration and shock worse.
• Rapid 60 mL/kg boluses over 15 minutes — Rapid 60 mL/kg boluses would cause fluid overload and heart failure.

⭐ Key point: For dehydration WITHOUT shock in SAM, use oral ReSoMal (a low-sodium ORS), not standard ORS or IV fluids.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q120$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1802, $s$The role of an ICU link nurse for infection control is to:$s$, $s$["Perform surgical drainage of infections", "Promote infection prevention and audit compliance", "Replace the hospital infection control team", "Prescribe antibiotics for infected patients"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Promote infection prevention and audit compliance
An infection control link nurse is a ward or ICU nurse with special interest and training in infection prevention. They PROMOTE good practice (hand hygiene, bundles, isolation), teach staff, AUDIT compliance, and LINK the unit with the hospital infection prevention and control team.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Perform surgical drainage of infections — Link nurses don't do surgery.
• Replace the hospital infection control team — They support the infection control team; they don't replace it.
• Prescribe antibiotics for infected patients — They don't prescribe antibiotics; doctors do.

⭐ Key point: Regular audits of hand hygiene, line care and bundles, fed back to staff, improve compliance.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q173$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1803, $s$Visiting policies in ICU should balance:$s$, $s$["Family needs, patient rest and infection control", "Unlimited visitors at all hours of the day", "Staff convenience and nothing else", "Strict exclusion of all visitors at all times"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Family needs, patient rest and infection control
A good ICU visiting policy balances: the FAMILY'S needs (being near, information, supporting the patient), the PATIENT'S need for REST, privacy and safety, and INFECTION CONTROL (hand hygiene, limits when there are outbreaks). Flexible, family-centred visiting improves family satisfaction and may reduce delirium.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Unlimited visitors at all hours of the day — Unlimited visitors at all hours can stop rest and increase infection risk.
• Staff convenience and nothing else — Staff convenience shouldn't be the only consideration.
• Strict exclusion of all visitors at all times — Strict exclusion increases family distress and patient isolation.

⭐ Key point: Flexible visiting can be adjusted for procedures, patient preference and outbreaks.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q180$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1804, $s$Betty Neuman's systems model focuses on:$s$, $s$["Self-care deficits and nursing systems", "Environmental cleanliness and fresh air only", "Responses to stressors and lines of defence", "Interpersonal phases of the relationship"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Responses to stressors and lines of defence
Betty Neuman's Systems Model sees the person (client system) as being affected by STRESSORS (intra-, inter- and extrapersonal). The person has protective LINES OF DEFENCE (the flexible line, the normal line, and lines of resistance). Nursing aims to help the system stay stable through PREVENTION: primary (before stress breaks through), secondary (treating symptoms), and tertiary (rehabilitation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Self-care deficits and nursing systems — Self-care deficits are Orem's theory.
• Environmental cleanliness and fresh air only — Environmental cleanliness and fresh air are Nightingale's theory.
• Interpersonal phases of the relationship — Interpersonal phases are Peplau's theory.

⭐ Key point: Neuman's three levels of prevention fit public health and ICU care well.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q183$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1805, $s$Sterilisation by steam (autoclave) at 134 °C usually takes:$s$, $s$["24 hours holding time", "3 minutes holding time", "30 seconds holding time", "1 hour holding time"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3 minutes holding time
Steam autoclaves kill all microorganisms, including spores. The higher the temperature, the shorter the time needed. At 134 °C (high pressure), the holding time is about 3 MINUTES. At 121 °C, it is about 15 MINUTES. These times count only from when the load reaches the full temperature.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 24 hours holding time — 24 hours is far too long.
• 30 seconds holding time — 30 seconds is too short to kill spores.
• 1 hour holding time — 1 hour is longer than needed at 134 °C.

⭐ Key point: Clean items first. Use chemical and biological indicators to check the process.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q195$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1806, $s$High-level disinfection kills:$s$, $s$["Only bacteria, not viruses or fungi", "All spores in every case, like sterilisation", "Only viruses, not bacteria or fungi", "All organisms except large numbers of spores"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: All organisms except large numbers of spores
High-level disinfection (HLD) kills ALL bacteria, viruses and fungi, but NOT large numbers of bacterial SPORES. It is used for SEMI-CRITICAL items, which touch mucous membranes but don't enter sterile tissue: laryngoscope blades, endoscopes, respiratory equipment. Agents include glutaraldehyde 2%, peracetic acid and ortho-phthalaldehyde.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only bacteria, not viruses or fungi — HLD kills more than just bacteria; it also kills viruses and fungi.
• All spores in every case, like sterilisation — HLD doesn't reliably kill all spores; that is sterilisation.
• Only viruses, not bacteria or fungi — HLD kills more than just viruses.

⭐ Key point: Critical items (entering sterile tissue) need sterilisation. Semi-critical items need high-level disinfection. Non-critical items (intact skin) need low-level disinfection or cleaning.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q197$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1807, $s$The leading hospital-acquired infection in ventilated ICU patients is:$s$, $s$["Ventilator-associated pneumonia", "Conjunctivitis", "Skin infection", "Urinary tract infection only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ventilator-associated pneumonia
Ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP) is the most common hospital-acquired infection in mechanically ventilated ICU patients. It is pneumonia that develops 48 hours or more after intubation. The ETT bypasses the body's defences, allowing germs from the mouth and stomach to enter the lungs. VAP increases ventilation time, ICU stay, cost and deaths.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Conjunctivitis — Conjunctivitis can occur but is far less common and less serious.
• Skin infection — Skin infections can occur but are less common.
• Urinary tract infection only — UTI is common in catheterised patients, but VAP is the leading infection in ventilated patients.

⭐ Key point: Prevent VAP with the bundle: head up 30–45°, daily sedation hold, oral care, subglottic suction, cuff pressure 20–30 cmH₂O, hand hygiene.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q199$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1808, $s$ICU patients account for approximately what proportion of all hospital-acquired infections, despite occupying fewer than 10% of beds?$s$, $s$[">50%", ">5%", ">20%", ">90%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: >20%
ICU patients make up fewer than 10% of hospital beds but have more than 20% of all hospital-acquired infections. They are very ill, have weak immunity, and have many invasive devices (ETT, central lines, urinary catheters, arterial lines) that let germs in. They also receive many antibiotics, which select resistant germs, and get frequent hands-on care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• >50% — More than 5% understates the problem; ICU patients carry far more than their share.
• >5% — More than 50% overstates the usual figure.
• >90% — More than 90% is far too high.

⭐ Key point: ICU = highest infection risk in the hospital. Hand hygiene, care bundles and removing devices early are the main defences.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q1$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1809, $s$A patient with confirmed tuberculosis requires which type of transmission-based precaution?$s$, $s$["No special precautions needed", "Airborne precautions", "Droplet precautions only", "Contact precautions"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Airborne precautions
Tuberculosis spreads through tiny droplet nuclei (under 5 micrometres) that float in the air for a long time and travel far. AIRBORNE precautions are needed: a single NEGATIVE-PRESSURE room (air is sucked out, so it doesn't escape into the corridor), door kept closed, and staff wearing a fit-tested N95 or FFP3 respirator.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No special precautions needed — TB patients DO need special precautions; it is highly contagious in the lungs.
• Droplet precautions only — Droplet precautions (surgical mask within 1–2 m) are for larger droplets (influenza, meningococcus), not TB.
• Contact precautions — Contact precautions are for spread by touch (MRSA, C. difficile).

⭐ Key point: Airborne diseases to remember: TB, measles and chickenpox (varicella). Patients wear a surgical mask when moved out of the room.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q3$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1810, $s$'Protective Environment' precautions, used for severely immunocompromised patients, rely on:$s$, $s$["No special ventilation", "Positive pressure rooms", "Negative pressure rooms", "Open-door policy"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Positive pressure rooms
Severely immunocompromised patients (bone marrow transplant, severe neutropenia) are protected in a POSITIVE PRESSURE room. Clean, HEPA-filtered air is pumped in, so the room pressure is higher than outside. When the door opens, air flows OUT of the room, so germs from the corridor can't drift in.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No special ventilation — Protective rooms DO use special ventilation.
• Negative pressure rooms — NEGATIVE pressure rooms are for infectious patients (TB), to keep germs IN the room.
• Open-door policy — An open-door policy would let germs in.

⭐ Key point: Positive pressure = protect the patient inside. Negative pressure = protect people outside from the patient.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q4$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1811, $s$Standard precautions differ from transmission-based precautions in that standard precautions:$s$, $s$["Are only used for confirmed infections", "Replace the need for PPE entirely", "Are optional depending on staff preference", "Apply to ALL patients, all the time"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Apply to ALL patients, all the time
Standard precautions are the basic infection control measures used for EVERY patient, ALL the time, whether or not they are known to have an infection, because you can't always tell who is infected (HIV, hepatitis B). They include hand hygiene, PPE when contact with blood or body fluids is likely, safe sharps handling, cleaning, waste disposal and respiratory etiquette.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Are only used for confirmed infections — Standard precautions are used for ALL patients, not only confirmed infections; that's what transmission-based precautions add to.
• Replace the need for PPE entirely — They INCLUDE PPE use; they don't replace it.
• Are optional depending on staff preference — They are compulsory, not optional.

⭐ Key point: Transmission-based precautions (contact, droplet, airborne) are ADDED on top of standard precautions for known or suspected infections.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q5$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1812, $s$The central line insertion bundle includes:$s$, $s$["Hand hygiene, full barrier precautions, 2% chlorhexidine", "Routine antibiotic prophylaxis before insertion", "The femoral site, chosen as the preferred site", "Clean gloves and povidone-iodine skin prep only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hand hygiene, full barrier precautions, 2% chlorhexidine
The central line insertion bundle prevents CLABSI. It includes HAND HYGIENE, MAXIMAL STERILE BARRIER precautions (cap, mask, sterile gown, sterile gloves, full-body sterile drape), skin preparation with 2% CHLORHEXIDINE in 70% alcohol (allowed to dry), choosing the best site (subclavian; avoid femoral in adults), and a checklist during insertion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Routine antibiotic prophylaxis before insertion — Routine antibiotics before insertion aren't recommended; they increase resistance.
• The femoral site, chosen as the preferred site — The femoral site has the HIGHEST infection risk in adults and is avoided where possible.
• Clean gloves and povidone-iodine skin prep only — Clean gloves and povidone-iodine don't meet the bundle standard.

⭐ Key point: The maintenance bundle: daily review of need, scrub the hub, clean dressings, and aseptic access.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q9$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1813, $s$Daily review of central line necessity reduces:$s$, $s$["Central line-associated bloodstream infection", "Pressure injuries over the sacrum", "Ventilator-associated pneumonia in the unit", "Delirium in ventilated patients"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Central line-associated bloodstream infection
Each day a central line stays in, the risk of a bloodstream infection (CLABSI) rises. Asking every day "Does the patient still need this line?" and removing lines that are no longer needed reduces CLABSI. Unnecessary lines are a common, preventable cause of infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pressure injuries over the sacrum — Pressure injuries are prevented by turning and pressure-relieving devices.
• Ventilator-associated pneumonia in the unit — VAP is reduced by the VAP bundle, not central line review.
• Delirium in ventilated patients — Delirium is reduced by its own measures (sedation, sleep, mobility).

⭐ Key point: Remove all invasive devices (central lines, urinary catheters, arterial lines) as soon as they're no longer needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q10$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1814, $s$Catheter-associated urinary tract infection (CAUTI) is best prevented by:$s$, $s$["Routine antibiotic prophylaxis while catheterised", "Daily bladder washouts with sterile saline", "Avoiding unneeded catheters and removing them early", "Changing the catheter every single day"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Avoiding unneeded catheters and removing them early
Most CAUTI is caused by catheters that aren't needed or are left in too long. The best prevention is AVOIDING unnecessary catheters (use only when really indicated) and REMOVING them as early as possible. Other measures: aseptic insertion, a closed drainage system, the bag below bladder level and off the floor, and daily catheter care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Routine antibiotic prophylaxis while catheterised — Routine antibiotics while catheterised don't prevent infection and promote resistance.
• Daily bladder washouts with sterile saline — Bladder washouts break the closed system and increase infection.
• Changing the catheter every single day — Changing the catheter daily causes trauma and increases infection.

⭐ Key point: The risk of bacteriuria rises about 3–7% each day a catheter stays in. Use nurse-led removal protocols.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q11$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1815, $s$A patient with C. difficile diarrhoea requires:$s$, $s$["No special precautions beyond standard care", "Airborne precautions with N95 masks only", "Contact precautions with soap-and-water hand washing", "Alcohol hand rub alone after each contact"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Contact precautions with soap-and-water hand washing
Clostridioides difficile is spread by touch (hands, surfaces, equipment), so CONTACT precautions are needed: a single room with its own toilet or commode, gloves and apron or gown. Its SPORES are not killed by alcohol hand rub, so hands must be washed with SOAP AND WATER, which physically removes the spores. Clean the environment with chlorine-based agents.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No special precautions beyond standard care — Standard care alone isn't enough; C. difficile spreads easily.
• Airborne precautions with N95 masks only — C. difficile is not airborne; N95 masks aren't needed.
• Alcohol hand rub alone after each contact — Alcohol rub does NOT kill C. difficile spores.

⭐ Key point: Stop unnecessary antibiotics (especially broad-spectrum), which trigger C. difficile. Treat with oral vancomycin or fidaxomicin.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q12$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1816, $s$Reverse barrier nursing (protective isolation) is used for:$s$, $s$["Severely immunocompromised patients, e.g. neutropenia", "Patients with open pulmonary tuberculosis", "Patients with C. difficile diarrhoea", "Patients colonised or infected with MRSA"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Severely immunocompromised patients, e.g. neutropenia
Reverse barrier nursing (protective isolation) is used for SEVERELY IMMUNOCOMPROMISED patients, for example with severe neutropenia after chemotherapy, bone marrow transplant, or extensive burns. It protects the PATIENT from germs carried by staff, visitors and the environment, using a single (often positive-pressure) room, strict hand hygiene, and sometimes masks and aprons.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Patients with open pulmonary tuberculosis — Open pulmonary TB needs SOURCE isolation (airborne precautions), not reverse barrier nursing.
• Patients with C. difficile diarrhoea — C. difficile needs SOURCE isolation (contact precautions).
• Patients colonised or infected with MRSA — MRSA needs SOURCE isolation (contact precautions).

⭐ Key point: Neutropenic patients also need: no fresh flowers or plants, careful food hygiene, and immediate antibiotics if they develop a fever.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q13$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1817, $s$The order for putting on (donning) PPE is usually:$s$, $s$["Gown, mask, goggles, gloves", "Gloves, goggles, mask, gown", "Mask, gloves, gown, goggles", "Goggles, gloves, gown, mask"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Gown, mask, goggles, gloves
PPE is put on (donned) in this order: 1) GOWN, 2) MASK or respirator, 3) GOGGLES or face shield, 4) GLOVES last, so the gloves can cover the cuffs of the gown. Hand hygiene comes before starting. This order means clean hands handle the gown and mask before gloves go on.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Gloves, goggles, mask, gown — Gloves first would mean they're contaminated while putting on the rest.
• Mask, gloves, gown, goggles — Starting with the mask and gloves is the wrong order.
• Goggles, gloves, gown, mask — Starting with goggles and gloves is the wrong order.

⭐ Key point: Removal (doffing) is designed to avoid self-contamination: gloves first (most contaminated), then goggles, gown, mask (last, outside the room), then hand hygiene.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q14$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1818, $s$Multidrug-resistant Acinetobacter in ICU spreads mainly via:$s$, $s$["Contaminated hands, surfaces and equipment", "Food from the hospital kitchen", "Airborne droplets over long distances", "Mosquito bites"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Contaminated hands, surfaces and equipment
Multidrug-resistant Acinetobacter baumannii survives for weeks on dry surfaces and equipment (bed rails, monitors, ventilators, keyboards). It spreads mainly through CONTAMINATED HANDS of staff, SURFACES and EQUIPMENT moving between patients. It causes VAP, bloodstream and wound infections in ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Food from the hospital kitchen — Food from the kitchen is not the main route.
• Airborne droplets over long distances — It is not an airborne germ that travels long distances.
• Mosquito bites — Mosquitoes don't spread Acinetobacter.

⭐ Key point: Control: hand hygiene, contact precautions, dedicated equipment, thorough environmental cleaning, and antibiotic stewardship.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q15$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1819, $s$Common causes of sepsis in critically ill patients include:$s$, $s$["Only dental infections from poor oral care", "Only viral infections from visitors", "Pneumonia, line, urinary and abdominal sources", "Only skin infections from pressure ulcers"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pneumonia, line, urinary and abdominal sources
The most common sources of sepsis in ICU patients are PNEUMONIA (including VAP), infected central LINES (bloodstream infection), URINARY tract infections (especially with catheters), and ABDOMINAL infections (peritonitis, abscess, anastomotic leak). Other sources include skin and wounds, and meningitis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only dental infections from poor oral care — Dental infections alone are not the main source.
• Only viral infections from visitors — Viral infections from visitors are not the main source.
• Only skin infections from pressure ulcers — Pressure ulcer infections can occur but aren't the only source.

⭐ Key point: Always look for the SOURCE of sepsis and control it (drain the abscess, remove the infected line).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q17$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1820, $s$Surveillance of HAI in ICU means:$s$, $s$["Counting the number of occupied beds each day", "Systematic collection and analysis of infection data", "Watching the nursing staff on CCTV cameras", "Checking the identity cards of all the visitors"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Systematic collection and analysis of infection data
Surveillance of hospital-acquired infection means SYSTEMATICALLY collecting data on infections (how many, what type, which germs, which devices), ANALYSING it (rates per 1,000 device-days) and feeding it back to staff and managers. It shows trends and outbreaks, and whether prevention measures are working.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Counting the number of occupied beds each day — Counting occupied beds is bed management, not infection surveillance.
• Watching the nursing staff on CCTV cameras — CCTV monitoring of staff is not infection surveillance.
• Checking the identity cards of all the visitors — Checking visitors' IDs is security.

⭐ Key point: Feeding infection rates back to staff improves compliance with bundles and hand hygiene.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q18$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1821, $s$During central line insertion, the nurse's role includes:$s$, $s$["Holding the guidewire without wearing any gloves", "Ensuring the checklist is followed; stopping asepsis breaches", "Opening the packs only after the doctor has started", "Leaving the room so as not to crowd the doctor"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ensuring the checklist is followed; stopping asepsis breaches
During central line insertion, the nurse helps prepare the patient and equipment and acts as a safety monitor. The nurse makes sure the INSERTION CHECKLIST is followed (hand hygiene, full sterile barriers, chlorhexidine skin prep, correct site) and has the authority to STOP the procedure if sterility is broken. The nurse also monitors the patient (ECG, SpO₂, BP) throughout.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Holding the guidewire without wearing any gloves — No one should touch the guidewire without sterile gloves.
• Opening the packs only after the doctor has started — Packs should be opened aseptically before the procedure starts.
• Leaving the room so as not to crowd the doctor — The nurse should stay to monitor and help.

⭐ Key point: Empowering nurses to stop procedures when asepsis is broken has greatly reduced CLABSI rates.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q33$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1822, $s$The commonest pathogens in Nigerian ICUs include:$s$, $s$["Only respiratory viruses such as influenza", "Only fungi such as Candida and Aspergillus", "Klebsiella, Pseudomonas, S. aureus, Acinetobacter", "Only parasites such as malaria and worms"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Klebsiella, Pseudomonas, S. aureus, Acinetobacter
Studies of Nigerian ICUs show that the most common bacteria causing infections are Gram-negative germs: KLEBSIELLA pneumoniae, PSEUDOMONAS aeruginosa, ACINETOBACTER baumannii, and E. coli, plus the Gram-positive STAPHYLOCOCCUS AUREUS (including MRSA). Many are multidrug-resistant, so culture and sensitivity results are essential.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only respiratory viruses such as influenza — Respiratory viruses cause some infections, but they're not the main ICU pathogens.
• Only fungi such as Candida and Aspergillus — Fungi like Candida occur, especially in long-stay patients, but they aren't the commonest.
• Only parasites such as malaria and worms — Parasites like malaria are important in Nigeria but are not the commonest ICU hospital infections.

⭐ Key point: Antibiotic resistance is a growing problem. Use local antibiograms (resistance patterns) to choose empirical antibiotics.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q114$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1823, $s$Albumin is low in critical illness mainly because of:$s$, $s$["Overfeeding with enteral feeds", "Excess dietary intake", "Dehydration and diuretics", "Inflammation and capillary leak"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inflammation and capillary leak
Albumin falls quickly in critical illness, mainly because of INFLAMMATION and CAPILLARY LEAK. Inflammation tells the liver to make less albumin (it is a "negative acute phase protein"), and leaky capillaries let albumin escape into the tissues. Dilution by IV fluids also lowers it. That is why albumin is a poor marker of nutrition in ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Overfeeding with enteral feeds — Overfeeding doesn't lower albumin.
• Excess dietary intake — Dietary excess doesn't lower albumin.
• Dehydration and diuretics — Dehydration actually RAISES albumin concentration.

⭐ Key point: Low albumin in ICU is a marker of illness severity, not just poor nutrition.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q117$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1824, $s$Protein-calorie malnutrition in ICU leads to:$s$, $s$["Poor healing, infection and weak breathing muscles", "Faster recovery from the critical illness", "Better immunity against hospital infections", "Weight gain only, with no harmful effects"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Poor healing, infection and weak breathing muscles
Protein-calorie malnutrition in ICU leads to muscle breakdown (including the diaphragm and breathing muscles), WEAK IMMUNITY (more infections), POOR WOUND HEALING and pressure injuries, WEAKNESS of breathing muscles (difficult weaning), longer ICU stay, and higher death rates.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Faster recovery from the critical illness — Malnutrition slows recovery.
• Better immunity against hospital infections — Malnutrition weakens immunity.
• Weight gain only, with no harmful effects — Malnutrition causes weight LOSS and harm.

⭐ Key point: Start enteral nutrition within 24–48 hours of ICU admission when possible, aiming for adequate calories and protein (1.2–2 g/kg/day).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q120$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1825, $s$Which of the following macronutrients in parenteral nutrition may need to be increased in a patient who has large, nonhealing wounds?$s$, $s$["Trace elements", "Lipids", "Protein", "Carbohydrates"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Protein
PROTEIN supplies the amino acids needed to build new tissue (collagen, skin, muscle) and support immunity. Large, non-healing wounds (pressure ulcers, burns, surgical wounds) lose protein through exudate and need more for repair. Increasing protein (often to 1.5–2.0 g/kg/day) supports wound healing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Trace elements — Trace elements (zinc, vitamin C) help healing, but protein is the macronutrient to increase.
• Lipids — Lipids provide energy but don't build tissue.
• Carbohydrates — Carbohydrates provide energy but don't supply the amino acids for repair.

⭐ Key point: Wound healing needs protein, calories, vitamin C, zinc and good perfusion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q141$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1826, $s$A patient's enteral feed provides 60 g carbohydrate, 20 g protein, and 15 g fat per serving. Using standard Atwater factors, approximately how many total kilocalories does this serving provide?$s$, $s$["455 kcal", "395 kcal", "275 kcal", "605 kcal"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 455 kcal
Use the Atwater factors: carbohydrate 4 kcal per gram, protein 4 kcal per gram, fat 9 kcal per gram. Carbohydrate 60 g × 4 = 240 kcal. Protein 20 g × 4 = 80 kcal. Fat 15 g × 9 = 135 kcal. Total = 240 + 80 + 135 = 455 kcal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 395 kcal — 395 kcal comes from a calculation error (for example using 5 kcal/g for fat).
• 275 kcal — 275 kcal would leave out some of the nutrients.
• 605 kcal — 605 kcal comes from using 9 kcal/g for carbohydrate or protein.

⭐ Key point: Remember: carbohydrate 4, protein 4, fat 9, alcohol 7 kcal per gram.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q142$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1827, $s$Enteral feeding is preferred to parenteral because it:$s$, $s$["It needs no monitoring once it has started", "It is always more accurate in calorie delivery", "It avoids every risk of aspiration completely", "It maintains gut integrity with fewer infections"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It maintains gut integrity with fewer infections
Enteral nutrition (feeding into the gut) is preferred because it MAINTAINS the gut lining (preventing atrophy and bacterial translocation), supports gut immunity, and has FEWER INFECTIONS than parenteral nutrition (no central line, fewer metabolic complications). It is also cheaper.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It needs no monitoring once it has started — Enteral feeding still needs monitoring (tolerance, glucose, tube position).
• It is always more accurate in calorie delivery — It isn't always more accurate in delivering calories; interruptions are common.
• It avoids every risk of aspiration completely — It doesn't remove aspiration risk; head elevation and tube checks are still needed.

⭐ Key point: "If the gut works, use it." Use TPN only when the gut can't be used or enteral feeding isn't enough.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q146$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1828, $s$To reduce aspiration during enteral feeding, the patient should be:$s$, $s$["Flat on the back", "Head-down tilt", "Prone without support", "Head elevated 30–45 degrees"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Head elevated 30–45 degrees
Elevating the HEAD of the bed 30–45° during enteral feeding uses gravity to keep stomach contents in the stomach, reducing reflux and ASPIRATION into the lungs. Keep the head up during feeding and for 30–60 minutes after bolus feeds.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flat on the back — Lying flat increases reflux and aspiration.
• Head-down tilt — A head-down tilt greatly increases aspiration.
• Prone without support — Prone without support isn't recommended during feeding (it needs special care if prone).

⭐ Key point: Also check feed tolerance (abdominal distension, vomiting), tube position, and gastric residual volume (as per policy).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q148$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1829, $s$A patient on TPN develops fever and rigors. The nurse suspects:$s$, $s$["A normal reaction to the lipids", "Dehydration from the glucose load", "Refeeding syndrome from the TPN", "Catheter-related bloodstream infection"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Catheter-related bloodstream infection
Fever and RIGORS in a patient on TPN suggest a CATHETER-RELATED BLOODSTREAM INFECTION (CRBSI). TPN is rich in glucose and fat, which helps bacteria and fungi grow in the line. Take blood cultures from the central line AND a peripheral vein, inform the doctor, and consider removing the line.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal reaction to the lipids — Lipid reactions are rare and usually occur early.
• Dehydration from the glucose load — Dehydration isn't caused by TPN glucose in this way, and doesn't cause rigors.
• Refeeding syndrome from the TPN — Refeeding syndrome causes low phosphate, potassium and magnesium, not fever and rigors.

⭐ Key point: Prevent CRBSI with TPN: a dedicated lumen, strict asepsis, scrub the hub, and change the giving set every 24 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q149$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1830, $s$Hyperglycaemia during TPN is managed by:$s$, $s$["Ignoring glucose levels while on TPN", "Giving extra dextrose to the TPN", "Insulin and adjusting the glucose content", "Stopping all nutrition immediately"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Insulin and adjusting the glucose content
TPN contains a lot of glucose, and critically ill patients often have insulin resistance. High glucose increases infections and complications. Manage it with INSULIN (usually an infusion or a sliding scale) and, if needed, ADJUST the glucose content of the TPN. Target 6–10 mmol/L.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignoring glucose levels while on TPN — Ignoring high glucose leads to infections and osmotic diuresis.
• Giving extra dextrose to the TPN — Adding more dextrose would worsen hyperglycaemia.
• Stopping all nutrition immediately — Stopping all nutrition causes malnutrition and rebound hypoglycaemia.

⭐ Key point: When TPN is stopped, insulin must be adjusted too, to prevent hypoglycaemia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q150$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1831, $s$Fat provides how many kcal per gram?$s$, $s$["2", "9", "7", "4"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 9
FAT provides 9 KILOCALORIES PER GRAM, more than double carbohydrate or protein (4 kcal/g each). That's why fats (lipids) are an energy-dense part of nutrition and are used in TPN to provide calories in a small volume.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2 — 2 kcal/g isn't correct for any macronutrient.
• 7 — 7 kcal/g is the value for alcohol.
• 4 — 4 kcal/g is the value for carbohydrate and protein.

⭐ Key point: Propofol is given in a 10% lipid emulsion, which provides about 1.1 kcal/mL. Count it in the patient's calorie intake.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q152$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1832, $s$Chest physiotherapy aims to:$s$, $s$["Replace the need for any suctioning", "Reduce the cardiac output and heart rate", "Clear secretions, re-expand lungs, aid ventilation", "Increase sedation and reduce agitation"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Clear secretions, re-expand lungs, aid ventilation
Chest physiotherapy aims to CLEAR SECRETIONS (so they don't block airways or cause infection), RE-EXPAND collapsed areas of lung (atelectasis), and improve VENTILATION and gas exchange. Techniques include positioning, postural drainage, percussion, vibration, deep breathing exercises, incentive spirometry and suction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Replace the need for any suctioning — It doesn't replace suction; the two often work together.
• Reduce the cardiac output and heart rate — It doesn't aim to reduce cardiac output.
• Increase sedation and reduce agitation — It doesn't increase sedation.

⭐ Key point: Monitor SpO₂, heart rate and BP during physiotherapy, and stop if the patient becomes unstable.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q154$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1833, $s$Early mobilisation in ICU includes:$s$, $s$["Deep sedation to reduce oxygen use", "Bed rest for all ventilated patients", "Avoiding physiotherapy until extubation", "Sitting out, standing and walking when stable"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sitting out, standing and walking when stable
Early mobilisation means starting movement as soon as the patient is stable, even while ventilated: passive exercises, then active exercises, SITTING on the edge of the bed, SITTING OUT in a chair, STANDING and WALKING. It reduces ICU-acquired weakness, delirium, ventilator days and ICU stay.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Deep sedation to reduce oxygen use — Deep sedation prevents mobilisation and increases delirium.
• Bed rest for all ventilated patients — Bed rest for all ventilated patients causes weakness and complications.
• Avoiding physiotherapy until extubation — Waiting until extubation delays recovery.

⭐ Key point: Check safety before mobilising: stable HR and BP, SpO₂ above 90%, FiO₂ 0.6 or less, PEEP 10 or less, and secured lines and tubes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q157$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1834, $s$Clinical outcome measures in ICU physiotherapy include:$s$, $s$["Hearing level and balance assessment", "Muscle strength, mobility level, weaning time", "Hair growth and the condition of the nails", "Visual acuity and colour vision testing"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Muscle strength, mobility level, weaning time
ICU physiotherapy outcomes measure how the patient is recovering physically: MUSCLE STRENGTH (the MRC sum score), MOBILITY level (the ICU Mobility Scale: from lying in bed to walking), WEANING time from the ventilator, and length of stay. These show whether rehabilitation is working.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hearing level and balance assessment — Hearing and balance aren't routine ICU physiotherapy outcomes.
• Hair growth and the condition of the nails — Hair and nails aren't relevant.
• Visual acuity and colour vision testing — Vision isn't a physiotherapy outcome.

⭐ Key point: An MRC sum score below 48/60 means ICU-acquired weakness.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q159$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1835, $s$Staff health and safety in ICU includes:$s$, $s$["No precautions, as ICU staff are low risk", "Ignoring needle-stick injuries if they are small", "Unlimited overtime to cover shortages", "Vaccination, PPE, manual handling training, support"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vaccination, PPE, manual handling training, support
ICU staff face hazards: infections (blood-borne viruses, TB), back injuries from lifting, stress and burnout, chemicals, and radiation. Health and safety measures include VACCINATION (hepatitis B), PPE, safe sharps use, MANUAL HANDLING training and equipment (hoists, slide sheets), and SUPPORT (counselling, debriefing, reasonable hours).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No precautions, as ICU staff are low risk — ICU staff are at HIGH risk, not low risk.
• Ignoring needle-stick injuries if they are small — All needlestick injuries must be reported, even small ones.
• Unlimited overtime to cover shortages — Unlimited overtime causes fatigue and errors.

⭐ Key point: Check your own hepatitis B antibody level after vaccination.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q176$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1836, $s$The nurse is preparing to administer TPN. What is the most important nursing responsibility before starting the infusion?$s$, $s$["Verifying the TPN solution with another nurse", "Checking the patient's blood pressure", "Documenting the patient's height and weight", "Administering a dose of insulin"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Verifying the TPN solution with another nurse
TPN is a complex, high-risk infusion (it contains glucose, amino acids, lipids, electrolytes, vitamins and trace elements). Before starting, the most important step is to VERIFY the TPN bag against the prescription (patient name, contents, rate, expiry) WITH ANOTHER NURSE. This prevents serious errors such as giving the wrong formulation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Checking the patient's blood pressure — Checking BP is routine but not the key safety step.
• Documenting the patient's height and weight — Height and weight are needed for planning but are usually already done.
• Administering a dose of insulin — Insulin isn't routinely given before TPN.

⭐ Key point: TPN safety: a dedicated central line lumen, aseptic technique, an infusion pump, a filter, a 24-hour giving set change, and regular glucose checks.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q95$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1837, $s$The single most important management strategy for ICU-Acquired Weakness is:$s$, $s$["Early mobilisation", "Heavy sedation", "Strict immobilisation of all joints", "Prolonged bed rest"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Early mobilisation
ICU-acquired weakness comes from muscle wasting and nerve damage caused by critical illness and IMMOBILITY. EARLY MOBILISATION (passive then active exercises, sitting, standing, walking as soon as safe) is the single most effective strategy to prevent and treat it. It also reduces delirium and ventilator days.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Heavy sedation — Heavy sedation increases immobility and weakness.
• Strict immobilisation of all joints — Strict immobilisation causes weakness and contractures.
• Prolonged bed rest — Prolonged bed rest makes weakness worse.

⭐ Key point: Also: minimise sedation, control glucose, avoid prolonged neuromuscular blockers and steroids where possible, and give good nutrition.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q103$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1838, $s$By Sepsis-3, septic shock is sepsis needing vasopressors to keep MAP ≥65 mmHg and:$s$, $s$["A white cell count above 12 × 10⁹/L on admission", "A positive blood culture result from the lab", "A temperature above 38 °C for over an hour", "Lactate above 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluids"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lactate above 2 mmol/L despite adequate fluids
Sepsis-3 defines SEPTIC SHOCK as sepsis needing VASOPRESSORS to keep MAP at 65 mmHg or above AND a serum LACTATE ABOVE 2 mmol/L, despite adequate fluid resuscitation. These patients have a hospital death rate above 40%.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A white cell count above 12 × 10⁹/L on admission — A high white cell count isn't part of the septic shock definition.
• A positive blood culture result from the lab — A positive blood culture isn't required (many septic patients have negative cultures).
• A temperature above 38 °C for over an hour — Fever isn't part of the definition.

⭐ Key point: Sepsis (Sepsis-3) = infection + organ dysfunction (a rise in SOFA score of 2 or more).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q118$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1839, $s$The qSOFA criteria are respiratory rate ≥22/min, altered mentation and:$s$, $s$["Systolic BP ≤100 mmHg", "Urine output ≤0.5 mL/kg/h", "Temperature ≥38 °C", "Heart rate ≥90/min"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Systolic BP ≤100 mmHg
qSOFA (quick SOFA) has three bedside criteria for patients with suspected infection outside ICU: respiratory rate of 22/min or more, altered mental state (GCS below 15), and SYSTOLIC BP of 100 mmHg OR LESS. Two or more criteria suggest a higher risk of death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Urine output ≤0.5 mL/kg/h — Urine output isn't part of qSOFA.
• Temperature ≥38 °C — Temperature isn't part of qSOFA.
• Heart rate ≥90/min — Heart rate isn't part of qSOFA.

⭐ Key point: qSOFA = "HAT": Hypotension (SBP 100 or less), Altered mentation, Tachypnoea (RR 22 or more). It is a warning tool, not a diagnosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q119$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1840, $s$A nurse is caring for a patient who is refusing treatment for his condition. The patient's family is pressuring the nurse to persuade the patient to accept treatment. What should the nurse do?$s$, $s$["Respect the patient's autonomy and allow him refuse treatment.", "Persuade the patient to accept treatment to satisfy the family's wishes", "Consult with another nurse to determine the best course of action", "Seek a court order to force the patient to accept treatment"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respect the patient's autonomy and allow him refuse treatment.
A competent adult has the RIGHT to refuse treatment (AUTONOMY), even if the family disagrees and even if refusing may lead to harm. The nurse should RESPECT the patient's decision, make sure they are fully informed about the risks, explore their reasons, document the discussion, and support both patient and family.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Persuade the patient to accept treatment to satisfy the family's wishes — Persuading the patient for the family's sake ignores autonomy.
• Consult with another nurse to determine the best course of action — Consulting another nurse doesn't change the patient's right to refuse.
• Seek a court order to force the patient to accept treatment — A court order isn't appropriate for a competent adult's refusal.

⭐ Key point: Family members can't override a competent adult's decision. Respect autonomy, document, and keep offering care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q167$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1841, $s$The rate of catheter-associated urinary tract infections (CAUTI) in an ICU over a quarter is an example of a(n):$s$, $s$["Input indicator", "Outcome indicator", "Process indicator", "Structure indicator"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Outcome indicator
OUTCOME indicators measure the END RESULTS of care for patients, such as infection rates, mortality, pressure ulcers and complications. The CAUTI rate in ICU is an OUTCOME indicator: it shows the result of catheter care practices.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Input indicator — Input indicators describe resources put in.
• Process indicator — Process indicators measure what is DONE (such as the percentage of catheters with daily review).
• Structure indicator — Structure indicators measure resources and facilities (such as the number of nurses).

⭐ Key point: Donabedian: Structure (resources) → Process (care given) → Outcome (results).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q175$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1842, $s$Repositioning an immobile client every 2 hours to prevent pressure injury, without a physician's order, is an example of which type of nursing intervention?$s$, $s$["Interdependent intervention", "Independent intervention", "Delegated medical intervention", "Dependent intervention"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Independent intervention
An INDEPENDENT nursing intervention is one a nurse can start based on their OWN professional judgement, without a doctor's order. Turning an immobile patient every 2 hours to prevent pressure injury is a classic independent intervention.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Interdependent intervention — Interdependent interventions involve collaboration with other team members (such as with a physiotherapist or dietitian).
• Delegated medical intervention — Delegated medical interventions need a doctor's authority.
• Dependent intervention — Dependent interventions need a doctor's order (such as giving prescribed drugs).

⭐ Key point: Independent nursing actions include positioning, mouth care, health teaching, emotional support and monitoring.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q182$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1843, $s$A patient receiving a lipid-containing TPN infusion should have which laboratory value monitored to detect a potential metabolic complication of the lipid emulsion?$s$, $s$["Serum triglyceride level", "Serum uric acid level", "Serum amylase level only", "Serum bilirubin level only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Serum triglyceride level
The lipid (fat) emulsion in TPN can raise blood TRIGLYCERIDES (hypertriglyceridaemia), especially if infused too fast, in sepsis, or with propofol (which is also a lipid). Very high triglycerides can cause pancreatitis and affect lung function. Serum triglycerides are monitored, and the lipid is reduced if they are high (above 4–4.5 mmol/L).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Serum uric acid level — Uric acid isn't specifically affected by lipid emulsions.
• Serum amylase level only — Amylase alone isn't the routine test.
• Serum bilirubin level only — Bilirubin and liver function are monitored too, but triglycerides are the specific lipid complication.

⭐ Key point: TPN monitoring: glucose, electrolytes (K, PO₄, Mg), triglycerides, LFTs and weight.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q183$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1844, $s$It is an establishment and implementation of policies, procedures, education and monitoring of staff adherence to reduce infection among patients, staff and visitors in ICU or hospital in general$s$, $s$["Infection prevention and safety", "Infection prevention and control", "Infection prevention", "Infection control"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Infection prevention and control
INFECTION PREVENTION AND CONTROL (IPC) is the organised programme of POLICIES, PROCEDURES, EDUCATION and MONITORING (audits) of staff adherence to REDUCE INFECTIONS among patients, staff and visitors. It includes hand hygiene, isolation, sterilisation, surveillance and antibiotic stewardship.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Infection prevention and safety — "Infection prevention and safety" isn't the standard term.
• Infection prevention — "Infection prevention" alone is incomplete.
• Infection control — "Infection control" alone is incomplete; the full term includes prevention.

⭐ Key point: Every hospital should have an IPC committee, an IPC team, policies, surveillance, and staff training.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q191$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1845, $s$Which assessment covers past medical history, family history, reason for admission, current medications, psychosocial and complete physical assessment?$s$, $s$["Focused assessment", "Ongoing assessment", "Initial assessment", "Emergency assessment"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Initial assessment
The INITIAL (admission) assessment is COMPREHENSIVE: past medical history, family history, reason for admission, current medications and allergies, psychosocial history, and a complete head-to-toe physical examination. It forms the baseline for the care plan.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Focused assessment — A focused assessment looks at one specific problem or system.
• Ongoing assessment — An ongoing assessment is the regular reassessment during care.
• Emergency assessment — An emergency assessment is a rapid check of life-threatening problems (ABCDE).

⭐ Key point: Types of assessment: initial (comprehensive), focused (problem-specific), ongoing (reassessment) and emergency (ABCDE).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q192$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1846, $s$Your patient has developed a low-grade fever and states that she has felt very tired lately. You interpret these findings as indicating which stage of infection?$s$, $s$["Full stage of illness", "Incubation period", "Convalescent period", "Prodromal stage"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Prodromal stage
The PRODROMAL stage of infection is the short period when the person first feels unwell with VAGUE, non-specific symptoms such as low-grade fever, tiredness (fatigue), malaise and headache, before the specific symptoms of the disease appear. The person is often infectious during this stage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Full stage of illness — The full stage of illness has specific symptoms of the disease.
• Incubation period — The incubation period has no symptoms (from infection to the first symptoms).
• Convalescent period — The convalescent period is recovery.

⭐ Key point: Stages of infection: incubation, prodromal, illness (acute), decline, convalescence.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q196$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1847, $s$The nurse is preparing to change the dressing on a central line used for TPN. What type of dressing is most appropriate?$s$, $s$["A transparent, semipermeable membrane dressing", "A simple gauze dressing", "A bandage with adhesive tape", "An occlusive dressing with petroleum jelly"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A transparent, semipermeable membrane dressing
A TRANSPARENT, semipermeable membrane dressing is preferred for central lines (including TPN lines). It lets the nurse SEE the insertion site for redness, swelling or pus without removing the dressing, keeps the site sealed against bacteria, and allows moisture to escape. Change it every 7 days, or sooner if loose, wet or soiled.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A simple gauze dressing — Gauze hides the site and must be changed every 2 days.
• A bandage with adhesive tape — A bandage with tape isn't sterile or sealed.
• An occlusive dressing with petroleum jelly — Petroleum jelly isn't used on central line sites.

⭐ Key point: Use chlorhexidine-impregnated dressings or sponges where available to further reduce infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q84$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1848, $s$A nurse finds the TPN bag has been hanging for 26 hours. The most appropriate nursing action is to:$s$, $s$["Clamp the line and flush it with saline", "Discard the current bag and obtain a new one", "Slow the infusion rate until the new bag arrives", "Continue the infusion as it is still safe"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Discard the current bag and obtain a new one
TPN is rich in nutrients, so bacteria and fungi can grow in it once it is opened. TPN bags (and giving sets) should hang for no more than 24 HOURS. A bag hanging for 26 hours must be DISCARDED and replaced with a new bag to prevent bloodstream infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Clamp the line and flush it with saline — Clamping and flushing doesn't fix the contamination risk; a new bag is needed.
• Slow the infusion rate until the new bag arrives — Slowing the rate continues the infection risk.
• Continue the infusion as it is still safe — After 24 hours it isn't safe.

⭐ Key point: To avoid hypoglycaemia while waiting, give 10% dextrose until the new bag arrives (if TPN is stopped).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q86$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1849, $s$In severe acute pancreatitis, the preferred nutrition route when tolerated is:$s$, $s$["Oral fatty meals to stimulate the pancreas", "Early enteral feeding (nasogastric or nasojejunal)", "Routine parenteral nutrition from day one", "Nil by mouth with no feeding for 2 weeks"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Early enteral feeding (nasogastric or nasojejunal)
In severe acute pancreatitis, EARLY ENTERAL feeding (via a nasogastric or nasojejunal tube, started within 24–72 hours) is preferred over TPN. It protects the gut barrier, reduces bacterial translocation and infected necrosis, and lowers infection and mortality.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral fatty meals to stimulate the pancreas — Fatty meals by mouth may not be tolerated in severe disease.
• Routine parenteral nutrition from day one — Routine TPN from day one has more infections and complications.
• Nil by mouth with no feeding for 2 weeks — Prolonged starvation (2 weeks) harms the gut and nutrition.

⭐ Key point: Use TPN only if enteral feeding fails or isn't tolerated. Start oral feeding when pain settles.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q100$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1850, $s$In the trauma patient, which route of nutrition support is generally preferred over parenteral nutrition when the gastrointestinal tract is functional, primarily because of superior outcomes related to gut integrity?$s$, $s$["Intravenous dextrose alone", "Parenteral nutrition", "Oral rehydration solution alone", "Enteral nutrition"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Enteral nutrition
In trauma patients with a working gut, ENTERAL nutrition (via the gut) is preferred over parenteral nutrition. It maintains the gut lining (reducing bacterial translocation and infection), supports immunity, is cheaper and has fewer complications. "If the gut works, use it."

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intravenous dextrose alone — IV dextrose alone doesn't provide complete nutrition.
• Parenteral nutrition — Parenteral nutrition has more infections and metabolic complications.
• Oral rehydration solution alone — Oral rehydration solution provides fluid and electrolytes, not nutrition.

⭐ Key point: Start enteral feeding within 24–48 hours of injury once the patient is stable.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q115$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1851, $s$A nurse is managing the pain of a patient who is sedated. The nurse knows that the most effective way to assess a sedated patient's pain is by:$s$, $s$["Assessing the patient's heart rate and blood pressure", "Assuming the patient is not in pain since they are sedated", "Using a behavioral pain scale, such as the BPS or CPOT", "Asking the patient to rate their pain on a scale of 1-10"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Using a behavioral pain scale, such as the BPS or CPOT
Sedated or ventilated patients often can't report pain. Use a validated BEHAVIOURAL pain scale: the BPS (Behavioural Pain Scale: facial expression, upper limb movement, ventilator compliance) or the CPOT (Critical-Care Pain Observation Tool). These detect pain reliably.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assessing the patient's heart rate and blood pressure — Heart rate and BP can change for many reasons and are unreliable pain indicators on their own.
• Assuming the patient is not in pain since they are sedated — Sedated patients CAN feel pain; never assume they don't.
• Asking the patient to rate their pain on a scale of 1-10 — Sedated patients usually can't rate their pain on a number scale.

⭐ Key point: Treat pain first ("analgesia first" sedation). Pain causes agitation, which is often mistaken for a need for more sedation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q146$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1852, $s$The guiding clinical principle for choosing between enteral and parenteral nutrition in a critically ill patient is best summarized as:$s$, $s$["Parenteral nutrition should always be used first regardless of GI function", "Enteral and parenteral nutrition are equally preferred with no clinical rationale for choosing one over the other", "Oral intake should always be attempted before any tube feeding is considered, regardless of aspiration risk", "\"If the gut works, use it\" — enteral nutrition is preferred whenever the GI tract is functional"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: "If the gut works, use it" — enteral nutrition is preferred whenever the GI tract is functional
The guiding principle in nutrition support is "IF THE GUT WORKS, USE IT". Enteral nutrition is preferred whenever the GI tract is functional, because it maintains gut integrity, supports immunity, has fewer infections and is cheaper. Parenteral nutrition is used only when the gut can't be used or enteral feeding isn't enough.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Parenteral nutrition should always be used first regardless of GI function — Parenteral nutrition shouldn't always be used first.
• Enteral and parenteral nutrition are equally preferred with no clinical rationale for choosing one over the other — They aren't equally preferred; there are clear reasons to prefer enteral.
• Oral intake should always be attempted before any tube feeding is considered, regardless of aspiration risk — Oral intake is preferred when safe, but not "regardless of aspiration risk".

⭐ Key point: Start enteral feeding within 24–48 hours in critically ill patients if possible.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q189$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1853, $s$---------------- is a standard healthcare practice that helps prevent the transfer of germs to or from an open wound and other susceptible areas on a patient's body.$s$, $s$["Clean Technique", "Sterile Technique", "Aseptic Technique", "Asepitic Technique"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Aseptic Technique
ASEPTIC TECHNIQUE is the standard practice that prevents germs from being transferred to or from open wounds, invasive devices and other susceptible sites. It uses hand hygiene, sterile equipment, a non-touch technique and a clean field (aseptic non-touch technique, ANTT).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Clean Technique — Clean technique reduces germs but isn't strict enough for open wounds and devices.
• Sterile Technique — Sterile technique is a stricter form used mainly in theatre; aseptic technique is the general standard.
• Asepitic Technique — "Asepitic technique" is a misspelling distractor.

⭐ Key point: Use ANTT for IV medicines, line care, catheter insertion and wound dressings.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q191$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1854, $s$Which insertion site for a central venous catheter is generally associated with the LOWEST risk of catheter-related bloodstream infection?$s$, $s$["Internal jugular vein with a dressing changed daily", "Any site is equally low-risk once inserted", "Femoral vein", "Subclavian vein"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Subclavian vein
Central venous catheters in the SUBCLAVIAN vein have the LOWEST risk of catheter-related bloodstream infection. The femoral site has the highest risk (more skin bacteria in the groin), and the internal jugular site is intermediate. The subclavian site is easier to keep clean and dressed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Internal jugular vein with a dressing changed daily — Daily dressing changes on the internal jugular don't make it the lowest-risk site, and frequent changes aren't recommended.
• Any site is equally low-risk once inserted — The sites are NOT equally low-risk.
• Femoral vein — The femoral vein has the HIGHEST infection risk.

⭐ Key point: The subclavian site has a higher pneumothorax risk; use ultrasound guidance and weigh the risks.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q192$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1855, $s$For a patient expected to need enteral tube feeding for only 2-3 weeks, the most appropriate feeding access is:$s$, $s$["A short-term nasogastric or orogastric tube", "A percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG) tube", "Total parenteral nutrition", "A surgically placed jejunostomy"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A short-term nasogastric or orogastric tube
For SHORT-TERM enteral feeding (less than about 4–6 weeks), a NASOGASTRIC (or orogastric) tube is appropriate. It is simple, non-invasive, quick to insert, and easy to remove. Longer-term feeding (more than 4–6 weeks) is usually given through a gastrostomy (PEG) or jejunostomy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A percutaneous endoscopic gastrostomy (PEG) tube — A PEG is for long-term feeding (more than 4–6 weeks).
• Total parenteral nutrition — TPN isn't needed if the gut works.
• A surgically placed jejunostomy — A surgical jejunostomy is for long-term feeding or after major upper GI surgery.

⭐ Key point: Orogastric tubes are used in intubated patients and those with basal skull fractures.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q196$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1856, $s$The recommended bedside check of nasogastric tube position before feeding is:$s$, $s$["Placing the tube end in water to look for bubbles", "Patient comfort with no coughing", "The 'whoosh' test with air and a stethoscope", "Aspirate pH of 5.5 or below"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Aspirate pH of 5.5 or below
The recommended BEDSIDE check of NG tube position is to ASPIRATE fluid and test its pH. A pH of 5.5 OR BELOW confirms stomach acid, so the tube is in the stomach and it is safe to feed. If the pH is above 5.5 or no aspirate is obtained, confirm with X-ray.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Placing the tube end in water to look for bubbles — Bubbles in water aren't a reliable test.
• Patient comfort with no coughing — Patient comfort doesn't confirm position.
• The 'whoosh' test with air and a stethoscope — The "whoosh" test is UNRELIABLE and must not be used.

⭐ Key point: Acid-suppressing drugs (PPIs) can raise gastric pH, so an X-ray may be needed in these patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q198$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1857, $s$Which is one of the WHO 'five moments' for hand hygiene?$s$, $s$["Only after removing gloves at home", "Only at the start of the shift", "Before entering the hospital car park", "Before an aseptic or clean procedure"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Before an aseptic or clean procedure
The WHO "Five Moments" for hand hygiene: 1) BEFORE touching a patient, 2) BEFORE a CLEAN or ASEPTIC procedure, 3) AFTER body fluid exposure risk, 4) AFTER touching a patient, 5) AFTER touching the patient's surroundings.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only after removing gloves at home — After removing gloves at home isn't one of the five moments.
• Only at the start of the shift — The start of the shift isn't one of the five moments.
• Before entering the hospital car park — Before entering the car park isn't one of the five moments.

⭐ Key point: Hand hygiene is the single most important measure to prevent hospital-acquired infections.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q199$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1858, $s$The nurse is providing skin care for an unconscious patient. What is the most effective intervention to prevent pressure ulcers?$s$, $s$["Repositioning the patient every 2 hours", "Applying a thick layer of lotion to the skin", "Using a firm mattress on the bed", "Massaging reddened areas of the skin"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Repositioning the patient every 2 hours
REPOSITIONING the patient every 2 HOURS is the most effective way to prevent pressure ulcers. It relieves pressure on bony areas (sacrum, heels, hips), restoring blood flow before tissue damage occurs. Use pressure-relieving mattresses too.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Applying a thick layer of lotion to the skin — Thick lotion doesn't relieve pressure and may make the skin too moist.
• Using a firm mattress on the bed — A firm mattress INCREASES pressure; a pressure-relieving mattress is needed.
• Massaging reddened areas of the skin — MASSAGING red areas damages fragile tissue and should be avoided.

⭐ Key point: Use the Braden scale to assess risk. Keep skin clean and dry, and ensure good nutrition.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q63$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1859, $s$When administering a TPN solution, a nurse must verify that it contains all the prescribed components. What are the three main macronutrients provided in TPN?$s$, $s$["Carbohydrates, proteins, and fats", "Sodium, potassium, and chloride", "Water, electrolytes, and vitamins", "Dextrose, amino acids, and lipids"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dextrose, amino acids, and lipids
The three main MACRONUTRIENTS in TPN: DEXTROSE (carbohydrate, the main energy source), AMINO ACIDS (protein, for building and repairing tissue), and LIPIDS (fats, for energy and essential fatty acids). Electrolytes, vitamins and trace elements are added as micronutrients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Carbohydrates, proteins, and fats — Carbohydrates, proteins and fats is the general description, but the TPN components are specifically dextrose, amino acids and lipids.
• Sodium, potassium, and chloride — Sodium, potassium and chloride are electrolytes (micronutrients), not macronutrients.
• Water, electrolytes, and vitamins — Water, electrolytes and vitamins aren't the main macronutrients.

⭐ Key point: TPN is given through a central line, because it is very concentrated (hyperosmolar).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q85$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1860, $s$A patient on TPN is showing signs of fluid volume excess. Which of the following nursing interventions is most appropriate?$s$, $s$["Increasing the TPN infusion rate", "Monitoring the patient's blood glucose levels", "Notifying the provider to decrease the fluid rate", "Administering a bolus of intravenous fluids"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Notifying the provider to decrease the fluid rate
Signs of fluid volume EXCESS (crackles, oedema, weight gain, raised BP, breathlessness) in a patient on TPN mean the total fluid is too much. The nurse should NOTIFY the PROVIDER so the fluid rate or TPN volume can be REDUCED (a more concentrated TPN may be ordered, or diuretics given).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increasing the TPN infusion rate — Increasing the rate would worsen overload.
• Monitoring the patient's blood glucose levels — Glucose monitoring is routine but doesn't treat fluid overload.
• Administering a bolus of intravenous fluids — An IV fluid bolus would worsen overload.

⭐ Key point: Monitor daily weight, fluid balance and signs of overload in patients on TPN.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q87$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1861, $s$To reduce aspiration risk during nasogastric tube feeding, the head of bed should be maintained at:$s$, $s$["Prone position", "Flat, 0 degrees", "Elevated 30–45 degrees", "Trendelenburg position"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Elevated 30–45 degrees
Raising the HEAD OF BED to 30–45° during and after NG feeding uses gravity to keep stomach contents down, reducing reflux and ASPIRATION into the lungs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prone position — Prone positioning isn't routine during feeding and needs special care.
• Flat, 0 degrees — Lying flat increases aspiration.
• Trendelenburg position — Head-down (Trendelenburg) greatly increases aspiration.

⭐ Key point: Also check tube position before feeding and monitor for vomiting or abdominal distension.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q95$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1862, $s$The ABCDEF bundle used in ICU is mainly aimed at:$s$, $s$["Increasing the use of physical restraints", "Shortening the family visiting hours", "Reducing staff numbers on night shifts", "Reducing delirium, weakness and time on the ventilator"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reducing delirium, weakness and time on the ventilator
The ABCDEF bundle aims to reduce ICU DELIRIUM, ICU-acquired WEAKNESS, and TIME ON THE VENTILATOR. A: Assess, prevent and manage pain. B: Both spontaneous awakening and breathing trials (SAT and SBT). C: Choice of analgesia and sedation. D: Delirium assessment, prevention and management. E: Early mobility and exercise. F: Family engagement and empowerment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increasing the use of physical restraints — The bundle REDUCES restraints.
• Shortening the family visiting hours — It encourages family involvement, not shorter visiting.
• Reducing staff numbers on night shifts — It isn't about reducing staff numbers.

⭐ Key point: Using the full bundle is linked to better survival, less delirium and fewer ventilator days.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q180$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1863, $s$Per current evidence-based critical care nutrition guidance, gastric residual volume (GRV) monitoring should generally prompt the nurse to hold or reassess feeding when the volume exceeds approximately:$s$, $s$["50 mL", "500 mL", "1500 mL", "GRV should never be checked regardless of volume"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 500 mL
Current critical care nutrition guidelines suggest holding or reassessing enteral feeding when the gastric residual volume (GRV) is above about 500 mL (in 6 hours), unless there are other signs of intolerance. Stopping feeds for smaller volumes leads to underfeeding without reducing pneumonia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50 mL — 50 mL is far too low a threshold.
• 1500 mL — 1500 mL is far too high.
• GRV should never be checked regardless of volume — GRV may still be checked as per local policy; some units no longer routinely check it.

⭐ Key point: If GRV is high: use prokinetics (metoclopramide, erythromycin), check the head-of-bed elevation, consider post-pyloric feeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q185$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1864, $s$A jejunal (post-pyloric) feeding tube is generally preferred over a gastric feeding tube in which situation?$s$, $s$["A patient with gastroparesis or a high risk of aspiration", "A patient with a normal swallow evaluation", "A patient with normal gastric emptying and low aspiration risk", "A patient who will be extubated within 24 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A patient with gastroparesis or a high risk of aspiration
A post-pyloric (jejunal) feeding tube places feed BEYOND the stomach, into the small bowel. It is preferred when the stomach empties poorly (GASTROPARESIS) or the patient has a HIGH RISK OF ASPIRATION (repeated vomiting, reflux, high gastric residuals), because feed is less likely to reflux into the lungs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A patient with a normal swallow evaluation — A normal swallow means oral or gastric feeding is fine.
• A patient with normal gastric emptying and low aspiration risk — Normal gastric emptying and low aspiration risk mean gastric feeding is fine.
• A patient who will be extubated within 24 hours — A patient about to be extubated may soon eat orally.

⭐ Key point: Post-pyloric tubes are placed endoscopically, under fluoroscopy, or with special bedside techniques. Confirm position by X-ray.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q195$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B7 · Infection Control, Sepsis, Nutrition, Comfort & Rehabilitation$s$, false, 1865, $s$ICU-acquired weakness is best reduced by:$s$, $s$["Routine deep sedation for all patients", "Strict bed rest until ICU discharge", "Higher doses of steroids", "Early mobilisation and minimising sedation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Early mobilisation and minimising sedation
ICU-acquired weakness is reduced by EARLY MOBILISATION (passive then active exercises, sitting, standing, walking as soon as safe) and MINIMISING SEDATION (so patients can take part). Physiotherapy can start safely even in ventilated patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Routine deep sedation for all patients — Deep sedation increases weakness.
• Strict bed rest until ICU discharge — Strict bed rest increases weakness.
• Higher doses of steroids — Steroids can CAUSE muscle weakness (steroid myopathy).

⭐ Key point: Also: control glucose, give adequate nutrition, and avoid prolonged muscle relaxants.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q197$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, true, 1866, $s$WHICH OF THESE BEST DESCRIBES A COMPUTER$s$, $s$["A device used only for playing games", "The means of connection between a sender and a receiver", "A collection of data being transmitted", "An electronic device that accepts data, processes and stores it, and gives out information"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An electronic device that accepts data, processes and stores it, and gives out information
A COMPUTER is an ELECTRONIC DEVICE that ACCEPTS DATA (input), PROCESSES it (according to instructions), STORES it, and GIVES OUT INFORMATION (output).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A device used only for playing games — Computers are used for much more than games.
• The means of connection between a sender and a receiver — "The means of connection between sender and receiver" describes a communication medium.
• A collection of data being transmitted — "A collection of data being transmitted" describes a message.

⭐ Key point: The four basic functions: input, processing, output, and storage.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q46 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, true, 1867, $s$An oxygen cylinder contains 1,360 litres of oxygen. The patient needs 5 L/min during transfer. Approximately how long will it last?$s$, $s$["About 272 minutes", "About 113 minutes", "About 45 minutes", "About 544 minutes"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: About 272 minutes
CYLINDER DURATION: TIME = AMOUNT OF OXYGEN ÷ FLOW RATE. Here: 1,360 L ÷ 5 L/min = 272 minutes (about 4 hours 32 minutes). Check: 5 × 272 = 1,360. ✓ In practice, keep a safety margin — do not plan to empty the cylinder completely — and remember that transfers can be delayed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• About 113 minutes — 113 minutes would be at 12 L/min.
• About 45 minutes — 45 minutes would be at about 30 L/min.
• About 544 minutes — 544 minutes would be at 2.5 L/min.

⭐ Key point: Always check the cylinder contents before a transfer and carry a spare. Ventilators driven by oxygen use extra gas.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q54 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, true, 1868, $s$A gradual, progressive decrease in EtCO2 over time in a stable-appearing ventilated patient may indicate:$s$, $s$["Rebreathing", "Hyperthermia", "Bronchospasm", "Hypothermia, hyperventilation, or a developing hypoperfusion state"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypothermia, hyperventilation, or a developing hypoperfusion state
End-tidal CO2 (EtCO2) reflects CO2 production, blood flow carrying CO2 to the lungs, and ventilation. A GRADUAL DECREASE can mean: HYPOTHERMIA (less CO2 production), HYPERVENTILATION (more CO2 blown off), or a developing HYPOPERFUSION state (reduced cardiac output — less CO2 delivered to the lungs, e.g. hypovolaemia, sepsis, PE).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rebreathing — Rebreathing raises the baseline and EtCO2.
• Hyperthermia — Hyperthermia increases CO2 production (raises EtCO2).
• Bronchospasm — Bronchospasm changes the shape (shark-fin).

⭐ Key point: A sudden fall in EtCO2 may signal cardiac arrest, massive PE or circuit disconnection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q114$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, true, 1869, $s$A capnography waveform that shows normal morphology but a low, flattened plateau with reduced EtCO2 amplitude in a patient with a large air leak around the tube cuff is explained by:$s$, $s$["Dilution of exhaled CO2 sample by entrained room air through the leak, falsely lowering the measured EtCO2", "Rebreathing", "Increased CO2 production", "Esophageal intubation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dilution of exhaled CO2 sample by entrained room air through the leak, falsely lowering the measured EtCO2
When there is a large AIR LEAK around the endotracheal tube cuff, some of the exhaled gas escapes, and the capnography sample may be DILUTED by room air entering through the leak. This makes the EtCO2 reading falsely LOW, with a low, flat plateau, even though the waveform shape remains normal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rebreathing — Rebreathing raises the baseline.
• Increased CO2 production — Increased CO2 production would increase EtCO2.
• Esophageal intubation — Oesophageal intubation shows waveforms that disappear.

⭐ Key point: Check cuff pressure (20–30 cmH2O) and tube position.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q116$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, true, 1870, $s$A Hospital Information System (HIS) is best defined as:$s$, $s$["An integrated system that manages a hospital's clinical, administrative and financial information", "A single stand-alone computer used only by the IT department", "A manual paper filing cabinet", "A type of medical imaging machine"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An integrated system that manages a hospital's clinical, administrative and financial information
A HOSPITAL INFORMATION SYSTEM (HIS) is a comprehensive, INTEGRATED computer system that manages ALL the information needs of a hospital: CLINICAL information (patient records, nursing documentation, orders, lab and radiology results, pharmacy), ADMINISTRATIVE information (admissions, discharges, transfers, bed management, scheduling, staff records) and FINANCIAL information (billing, insurance, inventory, payroll). The different modules share data, so information entered once is available wherever needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A single stand-alone computer used only by the IT department — A single stand-alone computer used only by IT is not an integrated hospital-wide system.
• A manual paper filing cabinet — A paper filing cabinet is a manual system, not an information system in the computing sense.
• A type of medical imaging machine — A medical imaging machine is clinical equipment (e.g. CT scanner), not an information system.

⭐ Key point: HIS improves efficiency, reduces errors, supports decision-making and enables data for audit and research — but requires training, security and downtime plans.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q21$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, true, 1871, $s$A hospital's electronic health record system experiences a temporary outage. The nurse's most appropriate immediate action is to:$s$, $s$["Stop all patient care until the system returns", "Discharge all patients immediately", "Guess at patient information from memory with no documentation", "Follow the unit's downtime/contingency procedure (e.g. paper documentation) to ensure continuity of safe care"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Follow the unit's downtime/contingency procedure (e.g. paper documentation) to ensure continuity of safe care
When the electronic health record (EHR) is temporarily unavailable (DOWNTIME — planned or unplanned), patient care must continue SAFELY. The nurse should FOLLOW THE UNIT'S DOWNTIME / CONTINGENCY PROCEDURE: use PAPER forms (observation charts, medication administration records, nursing notes), refer to the latest printed backup reports (e.g. medication lists, allergies), and communicate verbally and in writing. When the system returns, the paper records are entered into (or scanned into) the EHR according to policy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stop all patient care until the system returns — Stopping care would endanger patients.
• Discharge all patients immediately — Discharging patients because of a computer outage is unsafe and inappropriate.
• Guess at patient information from memory with no documentation — Guessing from memory without documentation risks errors and leaves no legal record.

⭐ Key point: Every unit should have downtime kits (paper forms), regularly updated backup reports, and staff trained in downtime procedures.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q24$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, true, 1872, $s$Nursing informatics is best defined as the specialty that:$s$, $s$["Integrates nursing science with information and computer science to manage and communicate data, information, and knowledge", "Focuses only on hospital billing software", "Replaces the need for clinical nursing skills", "Is only relevant to IT department staff"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Integrates nursing science with information and computer science to manage and communicate data, information, and knowledge
NURSING INFORMATICS is a specialty that INTEGRATES NURSING SCIENCE with INFORMATION SCIENCE and COMPUTER SCIENCE to MANAGE and COMMUNICATE DATA, INFORMATION, KNOWLEDGE and WISDOM in nursing practice (American Nurses Association definition). It supports nurses, patients and other providers in decision-making, using information technology (e.g. EHRs, CDSS, telehealth). Informatics nurses help design, implement and evaluate health IT systems.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Focuses only on hospital billing software — Nursing informatics is broader than billing software.
• Replaces the need for clinical nursing skills — It supports, but does not replace, clinical nursing skills.
• Is only relevant to IT department staff — It is relevant to ALL nurses, not just IT staff.

⭐ Key point: Data → Information → Knowledge → Wisdom (DIKW) is the framework of nursing informatics.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q29$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, true, 1873, $s$A nurse is explaining units of data to a colleague. The smallest unit of data in a computer is a:$s$, $s$["Byte", "Bit", "Megabyte", "Kilobyte"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bit
The SMALLEST unit of data in a computer is a BIT (short for BInary digiT). A bit can have only ONE of TWO values: 0 or 1 (off or on). All data in a computer — text, numbers, images, sound — is ultimately stored as patterns of bits. EIGHT BITS make ONE BYTE, which can represent one character (e.g. a letter).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Byte — A byte is 8 bits — larger than a bit.
• Megabyte — A megabyte is about a million bytes.
• Kilobyte — A kilobyte is about a thousand bytes.

⭐ Key point: Internet speed is usually measured in bits per second (e.g. Mbps), while file size is measured in bytes (e.g. MB).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q40 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, true, 1874, $s$Immediately after intubation, the capnograph shows a few small waveforms that fade to zero over the next five breaths. The most likely explanation is:$s$, $s$["Severe bronchospasm", "Right main bronchus intubation", "Oesophageal intubation", "Hyperventilation"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Oesophageal intubation
Capnography detects CO2 in exhaled breath. After CORRECT intubation, the CO2 waveform stays consistent with every breath. If the tube is in the OESOPHAGUS, a small amount of CO2 may be present in the stomach (from swallowed exhaled air or bag-mask ventilation), giving a FEW SMALL WAVEFORMS that quickly FADE TO ZERO within a few breaths. This indicates OESOPHAGEAL INTUBATION — the tube must be removed and the patient re-intubated immediately.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Severe bronchospasm — Severe bronchospasm gives a "shark-fin" shaped waveform, but CO2 is still present with every breath.
• Right main bronchus intubation — Right main bronchus intubation still gives a consistent CO2 trace (one lung is ventilated).
• Hyperventilation — Hyperventilation gives a lower but persistent CO2 trace.

⭐ Key point: "No trace = wrong place." Waveform capnography is the gold standard for confirming tube placement (also during CPR).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q29$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, true, 1875, $s$On capnography, the baseline does not return to zero during inspiration (inspired CO₂ is above zero). This indicates:$s$, $s$["Rebreathing of exhaled CO₂", "Hypoventilation with CO₂ retention", "Malignant hyperthermia in its early phase", "Bronchospasm with a prolonged expiration"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Rebreathing of exhaled CO₂
The capnograph baseline should return to ZERO during inspiration, because fresh inspired air contains almost no CO2. If the baseline stays ABOVE ZERO, the patient is breathing in CO2 — REBREATHING exhaled CO2. Causes: exhausted CO2 absorber (soda lime) in an anaesthetic circuit, faulty expiratory valve, inadequate fresh gas flow, or excessive dead space in the circuit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypoventilation with CO₂ retention — Hypoventilation causes a HIGHER end-tidal CO2 (taller waveform), but the baseline still returns to zero.
• Malignant hyperthermia in its early phase — Malignant hyperthermia causes a rapidly RISING end-tidal CO2, but the baseline returns to zero (unless absorber is exhausted).
• Bronchospasm with a prolonged expiration — Bronchospasm gives a shark-fin shaped waveform.

⭐ Key point: Check the circuit, the valves, and the CO2 absorber when the baseline is raised.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q31$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1876, $s$What is the main function of RAM (Random Access Memory) in a computer?$s$, $s$["To store data permanently", "To store the operating system", "To temporarily store data that the CPU is actively using", "To provide an internet connection"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: To temporarily store data that the CPU is actively using
RAM (random access memory) is the computer's TEMPORARY working memory. It holds the programs and data the CPU is using right now, so the computer can work quickly. RAM is volatile: everything in it is lost when the power is switched off. More RAM lets more programs run smoothly at the same time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To store data permanently — Permanent storage is the job of the hard disk, SSD or flash drive.
• To store the operating system — The operating system is stored permanently on the hard disk; it is only loaded into RAM while running.
• To provide an internet connection — Internet connection comes from the network card or modem, not RAM.

⭐ Key point: RAM is temporary and erased when off. ROM (read-only memory) is permanent and holds the start-up instructions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q47$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1877, $s$What is the purpose of an Electronic Health Record (EHR)?$s$, $s$["To store only the patient's demographic information", "To serve as a paper-based filing system", "To create a digital version of a patient's chart", "To replace the need for any patient-nurse interaction"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: To create a digital version of a patient's chart
An electronic health record (EHR) is a digital (computer) version of the patient's chart. It holds the full health information — history, diagnoses, drugs, allergies, lab results, imaging, vital signs, nursing notes and care plans — and is available to authorised staff wherever needed. It improves legibility, speeds sharing of information, supports alerts (allergies, drug interactions) and reduces errors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To store only the patient's demographic information — An EHR stores the FULL record, not only demographic details.
• To serve as a paper-based filing system — An EHR replaces paper files; it is not a paper system.
• To replace the need for any patient-nurse interaction — An EHR supports care; it never replaces the nurse–patient relationship.

⭐ Key point: EHRs must be protected: use your own login, never share passwords, log out after use, and respect confidentiality.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q48$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1878, $s$Telemedicine refers to:$s$, $s$["The use of telecommunications technology to deliver healthcare services remotely", "A type of medical imaging equipment", "A brand of hospital software only used for billing", "A paper-based referral system"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The use of telecommunications technology to deliver healthcare services remotely
Telemedicine means using telecommunication technology — phone, video calls, internet and data links — to give healthcare at a DISTANCE: consultations, monitoring, diagnosis and education. In critical care, "tele-ICU" allows specialists to support teams in remote hospitals and review monitors and images from afar. It improves access in rural areas and saves travel time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A type of medical imaging equipment — Imaging equipment (X-ray, CT) is a diagnostic device, not telemedicine itself.
• A brand of hospital software only used for billing — Billing software is administrative and is not what telemedicine means.
• A paper-based referral system — A paper referral system does not use telecommunication.

⭐ Key point: Telemedicine needs patient consent, privacy and secure systems, the same as face-to-face care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q49$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1879, $s$A cardiac monitor and pulse oximeter used in the ICU are examples of which category of electronic devices in healthcare?$s$, $s$["Diagnostic devices", "Monitoring devices", "Treatment devices", "Storage devices"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Monitoring devices
A cardiac monitor and a pulse oximeter continuously WATCH the patient's vital signs (heart rhythm, heart rate, oxygen saturation) and raise alarms when values go outside the set limits. That makes them monitoring devices. They do not treat the patient or make the diagnosis on their own; they alert the nurse to changes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diagnostic devices — Diagnostic devices are used mainly to find a diagnosis, such as X-ray machines, CT scanners and lab analysers.
• Treatment devices — Treatment (therapeutic) devices deliver therapy, such as ventilators, infusion pumps and defibrillators.
• Storage devices — Storage devices hold data, such as hard drives and USB drives.

⭐ Key point: Set alarm limits for each patient, never switch alarms off, and always check the PATIENT, not just the monitor.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q50$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1880, $s$End-tidal CO2 (capnography) waveform loss or a sudden flat waveform in an intubated patient most urgently suggests:$s$, $s$["Normal ventilator function", "Improved lung compliance", "Tube displacement/dislodgement (e.g., esophageal intubation or extubation) or complete obstruction", "Adequate ventilation"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tube displacement/dislodgement (e.g., esophageal intubation or extubation) or complete obstruction
Waveform capnography shows CO₂ in every breath. A normal square waveform proves the tube is in the trachea and the patient is being ventilated. A SUDDEN loss or flat line in an intubated patient most urgently means: the tube has come out or moved into the oesophagus (no CO₂ from the lungs), the tube is completely blocked, the circuit has disconnected, or cardiac arrest. Check the patient at once ("no trace = wrong place").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal ventilator function — Normal ventilator function would show a regular square waveform, not a flat line.
• Improved lung compliance — Better lung compliance would still show a normal waveform.
• Adequate ventilation — Adequate ventilation shows CO₂ on every breath; a flat line means none is reaching the sensor.

⭐ Key point: Respond with DOPE (Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment): check the tube position, the patient's chest movement and the pulse, and hand-ventilate with 100% O₂.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q102$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1881, $s$A method of Teaching includes all the following Except....$s$, $s$["Explanation", "Lecturing", "Demonstration", "Tertiary"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tertiary
The question asks which is NOT a teaching method. "Tertiary" means third level (as in tertiary education or tertiary hospitals); it is not a method of teaching. Teaching methods include explanation, lecture, demonstration (with return demonstration), discussion, role play, simulation, case study, tutorials and e-learning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Explanation — Explanation IS a teaching method.
• Lecturing — Lecturing IS a teaching method (good for large groups and knowledge).
• Demonstration — Demonstration IS a teaching method (best for practical skills).

⭐ Key point: For skills, use demonstration and return demonstration. For attitudes, use discussion and role play. For knowledge, use lectures, reading and e-learning.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q182$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1882, $s$If you make a mistake in a record how should it be corrected?$s$, $s$["With correction fluid", "Scribble it out", "Put a single line through the record", "Tell your mentor"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Put a single line through the record
To correct an error in a record, draw a SINGLE LINE through the wrong entry so it can still be read. Write "error" (or "written in error"), and add your initials or signature, the date and the time. Then write the correct information. This keeps the record honest and shows exactly what was changed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• With correction fluid — Correction fluid hides the original entry, which looks like falsification of records.
• Scribble it out — Scribbling out makes the original unreadable and looks like tampering.
• Tell your mentor — Telling your mentor alone does not correct the record legally.

⭐ Key point: Never erase, tear out pages, or leave blank spaces. For electronic records, use the system's correction or amendment function.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q193$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1883, $s$Sharing your personal EHR login credentials with a colleague is:$s$, $s$["Acceptable if they are busy", "A breach of data confidentiality and accountability", "Required by hospital policy", "Only a problem if a wrong entry is made"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A breach of data confidentiality and accountability
Each staff member has their OWN Electronic Health Record (EHR) login so that every entry and every record viewed is linked to a named person (an AUDIT TRAIL). Sharing your username/password with a colleague: (1) breaches PATIENT CONFIDENTIALITY — unauthorised people may see records; (2) breaks ACCOUNTABILITY — any wrong entry, missed drug or data theft will be recorded under YOUR name; (3) breaks hospital policy and data protection law (e.g. Nigeria Data Protection Act, professional code of conduct), and can lead to disciplinary action. Always log out or lock the screen when leaving a terminal, never write down passwords, and report any suspected misuse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acceptable if they are busy — It is NOT acceptable even if the colleague is busy; they must use their own login or get one issued.
• Required by hospital policy — No hospital policy REQUIRES sharing passwords; policies forbid it.
• Only a problem if a wrong entry is made — It is a problem even if no wrong entry is made; the breach happens at the moment of sharing.

⭐ Key point: Never share EHR login details — it breaches CONFIDENTIALITY and ACCOUNTABILITY. Lock the screen when you leave.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q46$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1884, $s$Which of the following is an example of internet browser?$s$, $s$["Microsoft Word", "Notepad", "Internet navigator", "Internet explorer"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Internet explorer
A WEB (INTERNET) BROWSER is a software application used to open and view websites on the internet. INTERNET EXPLORER (by Microsoft, now replaced by Microsoft Edge) is a classic example. Other browsers: Google Chrome, Mozilla Firefox, Safari, Opera. Nurses use browsers to access online drug references, guidelines, e-learning and web-based hospital systems. Basic computer knowledge is tested in nursing informatics.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Microsoft Word — MICROSOFT WORD is a WORD-PROCESSING program for writing documents, not a browser.
• Notepad — NOTEPAD is a simple TEXT EDITOR for plain text, not a browser.
• Internet navigator — "Internet navigator" is not a real browser name; the old browser was "Netscape Navigator", so this option is a distractor.

⭐ Key point: Web browser examples: Internet Explorer/Edge, Chrome, Firefox, Safari. Word = word processor; Notepad = text editor.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q47$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1885, $s$A nurse is using an Electronic Health Record (EHR) to access a patient's lab results. The EHR system provides a centralized location for patient data, which helps to:$s$, $s$["Increase the time it takes to retrieve patient data", "Ensure each department uses its own unique record system", "Improve continuity of care and coordination among the healthcare team", "Eliminate the need for any verbal communication between nurses"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Improve continuity of care and coordination among the healthcare team
An ELECTRONIC HEALTH RECORD (EHR) keeps a patient's information — history, medications, allergies, lab results, imaging, care plans, notes — in ONE CENTRAL digital place that all authorised members of the team can access at the same time, from different departments. This IMPROVES CONTINUITY OF CARE (each shift and specialist can see what has been done) and COORDINATION among doctors, nurses, pharmacists and others. Other benefits: legible records, quicker access to results, drug-interaction alerts, fewer duplicated tests, and better data for audit and research.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the time it takes to retrieve patient data — EHRs DECREASE, not increase, the time needed to retrieve patient data.
• Ensure each department uses its own unique record system — The aim is ONE SHARED record, not a separate system for each department (that causes fragmented care).
• Eliminate the need for any verbal communication between nurses — EHRs do NOT replace verbal communication; handover and direct communication between nurses remain essential for safety.

⭐ Key point: EHR = one central record shared by the team → better CONTINUITY and COORDINATION of care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q48$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1886, $s$CT scan and ultrasound machines are best classified as which type of healthcare ICT device?$s$, $s$["Diagnostic devices", "Monitoring devices only", "Treatment devices only", "Communication devices"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Diagnostic devices
Healthcare ICT (information and communication technology) devices are grouped by what they do. DIAGNOSTIC devices help identify disease by producing images or results — CT scanners, MRI, ULTRASOUND, X-ray, ECG machines, laboratory analysers. MONITORING devices continuously watch the patient's condition — cardiac monitors, pulse oximeters, ventilator alarms, ICP monitors. TREATMENT (therapeutic) devices deliver therapy — infusion pumps, ventilators, dialysis machines, defibrillators. COMMUNICATION devices share information — phones, pagers, nurse call systems, telemedicine links. CT and ultrasound make images to help diagnose, so they are DIAGNOSTIC devices.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Monitoring devices only — MONITORING devices track vital signs over time; CT and ultrasound are not used for continuous monitoring.
• Treatment devices only — TREATMENT devices deliver therapy; CT and ultrasound mainly diagnose (ultrasound can guide procedures but that is not its classification).
• Communication devices — COMMUNICATION devices transmit messages; imaging machines are not in this group.

⭐ Key point: CT, ultrasound, MRI, X-ray = DIAGNOSTIC devices.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q50$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1887, $s$While performing hemodynamic monitoring of the patient, the transducer should be kept at the Phlebostatic axis. The nurse knows that it is located at:$s$, $s$["Intersection of line between 2nd intercostal space and mid-axillary line", "Intersection of line between 3rd intercostal space and mid-axillary line", "Intersection of line between 4th intercostal space and mid-axillary line", "Intersection of line between 5th intercostal space and mid-axillary line"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intersection of line between 4th intercostal space and mid-axillary line
The PHLEBOSTATIC AXIS is the reference point that marks the level of the RIGHT ATRIUM. It is found where a line from the 4th INTERCOSTAL SPACE at the sternal border meets the MID-AXILLARY line (half-way between the front and back of the chest) with the patient lying flat or with the head of bed up to 45°. The pressure transducer (for arterial line, CVP, pulmonary artery catheter) must be levelled and zeroed at this point. If the transducer is placed too LOW, readings are falsely HIGH; if too HIGH, readings are falsely LOW (about 0.74 mmHg per cm of error). Mark the point on the skin so all staff use the same level.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intersection of line between 2nd intercostal space and mid-axillary line — The 2nd intercostal space is too high — it is near the angle of Louis (sternal angle), not the right atrium level.
• Intersection of line between 3rd intercostal space and mid-axillary line — The 3rd intercostal space is too high for the standard reference point.
• Intersection of line between 5th intercostal space and mid-axillary line — The 5th intercostal space at the mid-axillary line is too low; using it would give falsely high readings.

⭐ Key point: Phlebostatic axis = 4th ICS + MID-AXILLARY line = level of the right atrium. Transducer low → falsely high readings.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q69$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1888, $s$Alarm fatigue in a technology-dense ICU environment refers to:$s$, $s$["Improved responsiveness to all alarms", "Desensitisation to alarms from excessive frequency, leading to delayed or missed responses", "A malfunction unique to ventilators", "A benefit of increased monitoring"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Desensitisation to alarms from excessive frequency, leading to delayed or missed responses
ALARM FATIGUE happens when staff are exposed to so many alarms — many of them false, non-actionable or nuisance alarms (e.g. a loose ECG lead, SpO2 probe movement, limits set too tight) — that they become DESENSITISED. As a result, alarms are ignored, silenced, turned down or turned off, and response is DELAYED or MISSED, sometimes for a real emergency. It is a recognised patient safety hazard. Prevention: customise alarm limits to each patient, change ECG electrodes daily, use correct probes, remove unnecessary monitoring, use smart/delayed alarms, ensure alarms are audible, and educate staff.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved responsiveness to all alarms — Alarm fatigue REDUCES responsiveness — it does not improve it.
• A malfunction unique to ventilators — It is not a machine malfunction and is not unique to ventilators — it affects all monitors and pumps.
• A benefit of increased monitoring — It is a HARMFUL effect of excessive monitoring alarms, not a benefit.

⭐ Key point: Alarm fatigue = desensitisation from too many (often false) alarms → delayed/missed responses. Customise alarm limits.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q184$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1889, $s$Random Access Memory (RAM) differs from Read Only Memory (ROM) in that RAM is:$s$, $s$["Permanent and non-volatile", "Volatile — its contents are lost when power is off", "Used only for long-term archival storage", "Not involved in program execution"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Volatile — its contents are lost when power is off
RAM (Random Access Memory) is VOLATILE — its contents are LOST when the power is turned off. It stores the data and programs the computer is currently using. ROM (Read Only Memory) is NON-VOLATILE — it keeps its contents (e.g. startup instructions) without power.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Permanent and non-volatile — RAM is not permanent; ROM is.
• Used only for long-term archival storage — RAM is not used for long-term storage.
• Not involved in program execution — RAM is essential for running programs.

⭐ Key point: Save work frequently to avoid losing data in power cuts.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q47$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1890, $s$A nurse is setting up a new computer station in a clinic. Which of the following is considered an output device?$s$, $s$["Mouse", "Scanner", "Microphone", "Printer"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Printer
OUTPUT DEVICES take the processed information from the computer and present it to the user in a usable form. A PRINTER produces a permanent paper copy ("hard copy") — e.g. printing an ECG strip, a lab report, a drug chart, or a discharge summary. Other output devices: MONITOR/screen ("soft copy"), speakers, headphones and projectors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mouse — A MOUSE is an INPUT device — it sends pointing and clicking commands INTO the computer.
• Scanner — A SCANNER is an INPUT device — it converts paper documents or images into digital data.
• Microphone — A MICROPHONE is an INPUT device — it sends sound into the computer.

⭐ Key point: Some devices are both input and output (e.g. touchscreens, multifunction printer-scanners).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q48$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1891, $s$A phishing email pretending to be from IT asking staff to 'confirm' their password is an example of:$s$, $s$["A legitimate security update", "A social engineering cybersecurity threat", "A required annual login refresh", "A harmless marketing email"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A social engineering cybersecurity threat
PHISHING is a cyber-attack in which criminals send messages (emails, texts, calls) that PRETEND to come from a TRUSTED SOURCE — such as the hospital IT department, a bank or a manager — to trick people into giving away PASSWORDS, clicking harmful links or opening infected attachments. This is SOCIAL ENGINEERING: it attacks human trust rather than computer systems. Stolen login details can be used to access confidential patient records or install ransomware that locks hospital systems.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A legitimate security update — Genuine IT departments NEVER ask staff to send or "confirm" passwords by email.
• A required annual login refresh — There is no legitimate "annual login refresh" requiring you to send your password.
• A harmless marketing email — Phishing is NOT harmless — it can lead to data breaches and system shutdown.

⭐ Key point: Check the sender's real address, don't click suspicious links, never share passwords, and report suspicious emails to IT immediately.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q49$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1892, $s$Which of the following best describes 'utility software'?$s$, $s$["Software designed to perform maintenance tasks such as antivirus scanning or disk cleanup", "Software used only to write documents", "A type of physical computer hardware", "A programming language"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Software designed to perform maintenance tasks such as antivirus scanning or disk cleanup
UTILITY SOFTWARE is a type of system support software that performs MAINTENANCE and housekeeping tasks to keep the computer secure, efficient and working well. Examples: ANTIVIRUS/anti-malware programs (detect and remove viruses), DISK CLEANUP and defragmentation tools, BACKUP software, file compression (e.g. zip), firewall software and system monitoring tools.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Software used only to write documents — Writing documents is done with APPLICATION software (word processors such as Microsoft Word).
• A type of physical computer hardware — Utility software is a program (software), not physical hardware.
• A programming language — A programming language (e.g. Python, Java) is used to write software, not a utility.

⭐ Key point: Keep antivirus software updated and back up important data regularly — hospital computers hold sensitive patient information.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q50$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1893, $s$The conflict resolution style where one party gives in to preserve the relationship is called:$s$, $s$["Competing", "Compromising", "Accommodating", "Avoiding"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Accommodating
ACCOMMODATING (smoothing/obliging) is a conflict-resolution style where one party GIVES IN to the other's wishes to PRESERVE THE RELATIONSHIP and keep harmony ("I lose, you win"). It is useful when the issue matters more to the other person, when keeping good relationships is more important, or when you realise you are wrong. Overuse can lead to resentment and unmet needs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Competing — COMPETING is pursuing your own goals at the other's expense ("win-lose").
• Compromising — COMPROMISING is both parties giving up something ("split the difference").
• Avoiding — AVOIDING is ignoring or withdrawing from the conflict.

⭐ Key point: Thomas-Kilmann's five styles: competing, collaborating, compromising, avoiding and accommodating.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q175$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1894, $s$Why will computer-based patient records benefit clients more than paper records?$s$, $s$["Easy retrieval of case files across facilities", "Backed-up storage protects data against loss (e.g. fire)", "Less time spent retrieving case files", "All of the options"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: All of the options
COMPUTER-BASED patient records benefit clients more than paper records because: EASY RETRIEVAL of case files across facilities (records can be shared between hospitals and departments); BACKED-UP STORAGE protects data against loss (fire, flood, theft); LESS TIME spent finding files (instant search). So "ALL OF THE OPTIONS" is correct. Other benefits: legibility, decision support alerts, and better data for audit and research.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Easy retrieval of case files across facilities — Easy retrieval is only one benefit.
• Backed-up storage protects data against loss (e.g. fire) — Backup is only one benefit.
• Less time spent retrieving case files — Time saving is only one benefit.

⭐ Key point: EHRs need strong security (passwords, access controls, audit trails) and downtime plans.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q47$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1895, $s$System software, as distinct from application software, is best described as:$s$, $s$["Software written by end users for a single specific task", "Software that manages computer hardware and provides a platform for other programs to run (e.g. an operating system)", "Software used exclusively for entertainment", "A type of computer virus"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Software that manages computer hardware and provides a platform for other programs to run (e.g. an operating system)
SYSTEM SOFTWARE MANAGES the computer's HARDWARE (processor, memory, storage, devices) and PROVIDES A PLATFORM for other programs to run. The main example is the OPERATING SYSTEM (e.g. Windows, Linux, macOS), plus device drivers and utilities.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Software written by end users for a single specific task — Software written for a specific task is APPLICATION software.
• Software used exclusively for entertainment — Entertainment software is a type of application software.
• A type of computer virus — A virus is malicious software, not system software.

⭐ Key point: Application software (e.g. EHR, Word) runs on top of system software.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q48$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1896, $s$An EHR audit trail is used to:$s$, $s$["Track who accessed or modified a patient record and when", "Automatically diagnose patients", "Replace nursing notes", "Calculate medication dosages"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Track who accessed or modified a patient record and when
An EHR AUDIT TRAIL is an automatic record that TRACKS WHO ACCESSED or MODIFIED a patient's record and WHEN (including what was viewed or changed). It protects patient confidentiality, detects unauthorised access, and provides legal evidence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Automatically diagnose patients — Audit trails do not diagnose patients.
• Replace nursing notes — They do not replace nursing notes.
• Calculate medication dosages — They do not calculate doses.

⭐ Key point: Only access the records of patients you are caring for — all access is recorded.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q49$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1897, $s$Random access file arrangement, compared to sequential access, allows:$s$, $s$["Records to be accessed directly without reading through all preceding records", "Only reading records strictly in the order they were stored", "No possibility of updating individual records", "Access only during scheduled batch windows"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Records to be accessed directly without reading through all preceding records
RANDOM (DIRECT) ACCESS file organisation allows records to be accessed DIRECTLY (e.g. by a key or address) WITHOUT reading through all the preceding records. This is fast for finding individual records (e.g. one patient's file).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only reading records strictly in the order they were stored — Reading only in stored order is SEQUENTIAL access.
• No possibility of updating individual records — Random access allows updating individual records.
• Access only during scheduled batch windows — Random access is available at any time, not only in batch windows.

⭐ Key point: Databases often combine indexed and direct access for speed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q50$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1898, $s$CINAHL and PubMed are examples of:$s$, $s$["Hospital scheduling systems", "Databases used to search nursing and biomedical literature for evidence-based practice", "Electronic medication systems", "Patient billing platforms"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Databases used to search nursing and biomedical literature for evidence-based practice
CINAHL (Cumulative Index to Nursing and Allied Health Literature) and PUBMED (which searches MEDLINE, run by the US National Library of Medicine) are online DATABASES used to SEARCH published NURSING and BIOMEDICAL research. Nurses use them to find evidence for EVIDENCE-BASED PRACTICE — e.g. searching for studies on VAP prevention or pressure ulcer management.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hospital scheduling systems — They are not hospital scheduling systems.
• Electronic medication systems — They are not electronic medication systems.
• Patient billing platforms — They are not billing platforms.

⭐ Key point: Other useful sources: the Cochrane Library (systematic reviews), Embase, Google Scholar, and African Journals Online (AJOL). Use keywords and Boolean operators (AND, OR, NOT) to search effectively.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q173$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1899, $s$A patient with a PAC develops a new PA waveform that shows no dicrotic notch and looks like a damped, flattened line even with the balloon deflated. The FIRST nursing action should be to check:$s$, $s$["The patient's temperature", "The pacemaker settings", "The patient's oral intake", "The transducer connections, tubing for air/clots, and confirm the stopcocks are positioned correctly before escalating"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The transducer connections, tubing for air/clots, and confirm the stopcocks are positioned correctly before escalating
A DAMPED PA waveform with no dicrotic notch, even with the balloon deflated, may be due to TECHNICAL problems: loose TRANSDUCER connections, AIR bubbles or CLOTS in the tubing, or STOPCOCKS in the wrong position. Check these FIRST before escalating (it may also indicate catheter migration).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient's temperature — Temperature is unrelated.
• The pacemaker settings — Pacemaker settings are unrelated.
• The patient's oral intake — Oral intake is unrelated.

⭐ Key point: Perform a fast-flush test to check the system.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q191$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1900, $s$An oxygen cylinder contains 460 litres of oxygen. The patient needs 15 L/min during transfer. Approximately how long will it last?$s$, $s$["About 61 minutes", "About 31 minutes", "About 115 minutes", "About 5 minutes"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: About 31 minutes
TIME = VOLUME ÷ FLOW RATE. 460 L ÷ 15 L/min = 30.7 minutes ≈ ABOUT 31 MINUTES.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• About 61 minutes — 61 minutes would be at 7.5 L/min.
• About 115 minutes — 115 minutes would be at 4 L/min.
• About 5 minutes — 5 minutes is far too short.

⭐ Key point: Always take a spare cylinder (or a larger one) for transfers, as cylinders should not be run completely empty — keep a safety margin (e.g. plan for 1.5–2 times the expected journey time).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q21 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1901, $s$An oxygen cylinder contains 680 litres of oxygen. The patient needs 4 L/min during transfer. Approximately how long will it last?$s$, $s$["About 45 minutes", "About 170 minutes", "About 340 minutes", "About 28 minutes"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: About 170 minutes
Oxygen cylinder duration: TIME = AMOUNT OF OXYGEN ÷ FLOW RATE. Here: 680 litres ÷ 4 L/min = 170 minutes, which is about 2 hours 50 minutes. Check: 4 L/min × 170 min = 680 L. ✓ In practice, cylinders should not be used down to empty — keep a safety margin (e.g. change the cylinder when about a quarter full), and allow for delays during transfer.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• About 45 minutes — 45 minutes would correspond to about 15 L/min.
• About 340 minutes — 340 minutes would be at 2 L/min.
• About 28 minutes — 28 minutes would be at about 24 L/min.

⭐ Key point: Before any transfer, check the cylinder pressure gauge, calculate how long it will last, and take at least one full spare cylinder. Ventilators that use oxygen to drive them consume extra oxygen.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q40 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1902, $s$EtCO2 monitoring benefits nursing assessment of a ventilated patient's dead space and ventilation efficiency because:$s$, $s$["It is only useful for confirming tube placement", "It directly measures anatomic dead space volume", "A widened gradient between PaCO2 and EtCO2 can indicate increased alveolar dead space, such as from pulmonary embolism, hypoperfusion, or hyperinflation", "It has no relationship to dead space"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A widened gradient between PaCO2 and EtCO2 can indicate increased alveolar dead space, such as from pulmonary embolism, hypoperfusion, or hyperinflation
Normally, end-tidal CO2 (EtCO2) is only about 2–5 mmHg lower than arterial PaCO2. If some alveoli are VENTILATED but NOT PERFUSED (ALVEOLAR DEAD SPACE), the air coming from them contains no CO2 and DILUTES the exhaled gas, so the EtCO2 falls while PaCO2 stays high. A WIDENED PaCO2–EtCO2 GRADIENT therefore suggests increased dead space — e.g. PULMONARY EMBOLISM (blocked blood flow), HYPOPERFUSION (low cardiac output, shock), or HYPERINFLATION (high PEEP or air trapping compressing capillaries). Tracking this gradient helps the nurse recognise these problems early.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It is only useful for confirming tube placement — EtCO2 is useful far beyond confirming tube placement (e.g. monitoring CPR quality, ventilation, perfusion).
• It directly measures anatomic dead space volume — EtCO2 does not directly measure the volume of anatomical dead space.
• It has no relationship to dead space — EtCO2 is closely related to dead space, as explained above.

⭐ Key point: A sudden fall in EtCO2 with stable ventilation may signal a massive PE, cardiac arrest or circuit leak.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q144$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1903, $s$The physical and tangible parts of a computer are called ____$s$, $s$["Values", "Hardware", "Software", "Tables"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hardware
A computer system has two main parts. HARDWARE is the PHYSICAL, TANGIBLE part — everything you can see and touch: the monitor, keyboard, mouse, printer, CPU (processor), hard disk, RAM, cables and the casing. SOFTWARE is the set of programs and instructions that tell the hardware what to do; you cannot touch software. Hardware without software cannot do useful work, and software needs hardware to run. In the ICU, hardware includes the bedside monitors' screens, the computers at the nursing station, barcode scanners and printers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Values — "Values" are data items (e.g. a patient's temperature of 37.2), not physical parts of a computer.
• Software — Software is the programs (e.g. Windows, the EHR, Microsoft Word); it is not physical or tangible.
• Tables — "Tables" are a way of organising data in a database or document; they are not physical parts.

⭐ Key point: Simple rule: if you can touch it, it is HARDWARE; if it is a program you run, it is SOFTWARE.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q1$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1904, $s$What is Computer Memory (Storage devices)?$s$, $s$["They are devices that command the computer on how to execute programs to solve problems", "They are devices that perform all the processing in the computer.", "They are devices that are used for storing data and information permanently or temporarily", "They are devices that convert information from the form that the computer understands to a form that humans can understand."]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: They are devices that are used for storing data and information permanently or temporarily
COMPUTER MEMORY (STORAGE DEVICES) are the parts of a computer used for STORING DATA AND INFORMATION, either TEMPORARILY or PERMANENTLY. PRIMARY memory (main memory) includes RAM (Random Access Memory — temporary; it holds what you are working on now and is lost when the power goes off) and ROM (Read Only Memory — permanent instructions needed to start the computer). SECONDARY storage keeps data permanently even when the computer is off: hard disks, solid-state drives (SSD), USB flash drives, memory cards, CDs/DVDs and cloud storage. Patient records in an electronic health record are kept on secondary storage (usually servers), so they are not lost when a computer is switched off.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• They are devices that command the computer on how to execute programs to solve problems — Devices that "command the computer how to execute programs" describes software / the control unit, not storage.
• They are devices that perform all the processing in the computer. — Devices that perform all the processing describes the CPU (processor), not storage.
• They are devices that convert information from the form that the computer understands to a form that humans can understand. — Devices that convert computer information into a human-readable form are OUTPUT devices (e.g. monitor, printer).

⭐ Key point: RAM = temporary working memory (volatile). Hard disk / SSD / flash drive = permanent storage (non-volatile). Always save your work, because unsaved work in RAM is lost in a power cut.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q2$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1905, $s$Software designed and installed on a computer intended to solve specific problems or perform particular tasks is called?$s$, $s$["Application software", "Open Source Software", "Utility", "Spreadsheet"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Application software
APPLICATION SOFTWARE is software designed to help the USER do a SPECIFIC TASK or solve a particular problem. Examples: Microsoft Word (writing reports), Excel (spreadsheets and calculations), PowerPoint (presentations), web browsers, email programs, and hospital programs such as an electronic health record (EHR), a laboratory information system, or a drug-dose calculator. It runs on top of the system software (the operating system).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Open Source Software — Open source software describes how software is LICENSED (its source code is freely available, e.g. Linux, LibreOffice); it can be any type of software.
• Utility — Utility software performs maintenance tasks for the computer itself (e.g. antivirus, disk cleanup, backup, file compression) rather than the user's main work.
• Spreadsheet — A spreadsheet is ONE example of application software, not the general name for this category.

⭐ Key point: System software runs the computer; application software does the user's job; utility software keeps the computer healthy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q3$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1906, $s$System software is a software that ____$s$, $s$["is used in making presentations", "is used for data analysis", "is used to manage health information of patients", "manages the resources on the computer and enable the hardware to function properly"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: manages the resources on the computer and enable the hardware to function properly
SYSTEM SOFTWARE MANAGES THE RESOURCES of the computer (processor, memory, storage, input/output devices) and ENABLES THE HARDWARE TO FUNCTION properly. It also provides a platform on which application software runs. The most important system software is the OPERATING SYSTEM (OS), e.g. Windows, macOS, Linux, Android, iOS. Other system software includes device drivers (which let the OS talk to printers, scanners, etc.) and firmware (BIOS). Without system software, the computer cannot start up or run any programs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• is used in making presentations — Making presentations is done with APPLICATION software (e.g. PowerPoint).
• is used for data analysis — Data analysis is done with APPLICATION software (e.g. Excel, SPSS).
• is used to manage health information of patients — Managing patients' health information is done with APPLICATION software (e.g. an EHR or hospital information system).

⭐ Key point: The operating system is the "manager" of the computer: it controls files, memory, devices, security (user logins), and runs the applications.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q4$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1907, $s$The devices that give out data and information from the computer are called ____$s$, $s$["Universal Serial Bus", "Network Topology", "Sink/Receiver", "Output devices"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Output devices
OUTPUT DEVICES take the results of processing from the computer and present them to the user (or another device) in a form people can understand — text, pictures, sound or print. Examples: MONITOR (screen), PRINTER, SPEAKERS, headphones, projector, and plotters. In the ICU, the screen of a central monitoring station and a printed ECG strip or lab report are outputs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Universal Serial Bus — Universal Serial Bus (USB) is a type of connection/port used to connect devices; it is not a category of devices that give out information.
• Network Topology — Network topology describes how computers in a network are arranged and connected (bus, star, ring, mesh).
• Sink/Receiver — A sink/receiver is the device that RECEIVES data in a communication system; it is not the general term for output devices.

⭐ Key point: Input → Process → Output → Storage is the basic cycle of every computer system.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q5$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1908, $s$The devices that send data and control signals INTO the computer are called ____$s$, $s$["Network", "Input devices", "Operating System", "Arithmetic Unit"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Input devices
INPUT DEVICES are used to SEND DATA and CONTROL SIGNALS INTO the computer. Examples: KEYBOARD, MOUSE, touchscreen, SCANNER, BARCODE READER, microphone, webcam, digital camera, card reader, and biometric (fingerprint) readers. In the ICU, a barcode scanner reading a patient's wristband before giving a drug, and a nurse typing observations into the EHR, are examples of input.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Network — A network is a group of interconnected computers, not a category of devices that send data in.
• Operating System — The operating system is SYSTEM SOFTWARE that manages the computer.
• Arithmetic Unit — The Arithmetic (and Logic) Unit is part of the CPU that performs calculations and comparisons; it is part of processing, not input.

⭐ Key point: Some devices are BOTH input and output — e.g. a touchscreen (you touch to input and it displays output) and a modem.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q6$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1909, $s$A network in which all the communicating computers are of the same or similar capacity is called a ____?$s$, $s$["Mainframe network", "Server-client network", "Cloud computing Network", "Peer to peer network"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Peer to peer network
In a PEER-TO-PEER (P2P) NETWORK, all the connected computers are of EQUAL or SIMILAR status and capacity. Each computer (a "peer") can act as both a client (requesting resources) and a server (sharing its own files or printer). There is NO DEDICATED CENTRAL SERVER. It is simple and cheap, suitable for small offices (e.g. a few computers in a nursing school office sharing a printer), but it is harder to secure and manage as it grows.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mainframe network — A mainframe network has one very powerful central computer serving many terminals.
• Server-client network — A client–server network has a powerful central SERVER that stores data and provides services to less powerful CLIENT computers; the computers are not equal. Most hospital information systems use client–server networks.
• Cloud computing Network — Cloud computing provides storage and services over the internet from remote data centres.

⭐ Key point: Peer-to-peer = equals, no central server. Client–server = central server controls and shares resources (better for security and large organisations like hospitals).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q7$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1910, $s$In a Computer Network the means of connection is ____?$s$, $s$["Speaker", "Receiver/sink", "Sender/source", "Medium/channel"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Medium/channel
Every data communication system has five components: (1) the SENDER (source) — the device that sends the message; (2) the RECEIVER (sink) — the device that receives it; (3) the MESSAGE — the data being sent; (4) the MEDIUM (CHANNEL) — the physical or wireless PATH that CONNECTS the sender and receiver and carries the data (e.g. twisted-pair cable, coaxial cable, fibre-optic cable, radio waves/Wi-Fi, microwave, satellite); and (5) the PROTOCOL — the rules for communication. The means of connection is therefore the MEDIUM/CHANNEL.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Speaker — A speaker is an output device that produces sound; it is not the means of connection.
• Receiver/sink — The receiver/sink is the device at the END that receives the data, not the connection between devices.
• Sender/source — The sender/source is the device that STARTS the communication, not the connection.

⭐ Key point: Wired media: twisted pair, coaxial, fibre optic. Wireless media: radio (Wi-Fi, Bluetooth), microwave, infrared, satellite.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q8$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1911, $s$What is a Computer Network?$s$, $s$["A Computer Network is the interconnection of two or more computers or computing devices through a medium for the purpose of sharing resources.", "A Computer Network is the interconnection of two or more agents to achieve a goal", "A Computer Network is the interconnection people to increase productivity", "A Computer Network is the interconnection of schools to give information"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A Computer Network is the interconnection of two or more computers or computing devices through a medium for the purpose of sharing resources.
A COMPUTER NETWORK is the INTERCONNECTION of TWO OR MORE COMPUTERS or computing devices through a MEDIUM (cable or wireless) for the purpose of SHARING RESOURCES — such as files, data, printers, internet connection and applications — and for COMMUNICATION (e.g. email, messaging). In a hospital, a network lets a nurse in the ICU see a patient's lab results as soon as the laboratory enters them, and lets the pharmacy receive electronic prescriptions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A Computer Network is the interconnection of two or more agents to achieve a goal — Interconnection of "agents to achieve a goal" is too vague; it does not mention computers, a medium, or sharing resources.
• A Computer Network is the interconnection people to increase productivity — Interconnecting PEOPLE to increase productivity describes social or professional networks, not a computer network.
• A Computer Network is the interconnection of schools to give information — Interconnecting SCHOOLS to give information is not the definition of a computer network.

⭐ Key point: Types by size: PAN (personal), LAN (local — e.g. one hospital building), MAN (metropolitan — a city), WAN (wide — across countries; the internet is the largest WAN).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q9$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1912, $s$Which of these best defines a computer?$s$, $s$["A computer is an electronic communication device.", "A computer is a music playing calculation device.", "A computer is an electronic device that accepts data processes data stores data and gives out information.", "A computer is an education device for presentation."]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A computer is an electronic device that accepts data processes data stores data and gives out information.
A COMPUTER is an ELECTRONIC DEVICE that ACCEPTS DATA (input), PROCESSES DATA according to instructions (programs), STORES DATA, and GIVES OUT INFORMATION (output). This definition covers the four basic functions: input, processing, storage and output. For example, an ICU monitor accepts signals from ECG leads, processes them to calculate heart rate, stores trends, and displays the information on screen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A computer is an electronic communication device. — A computer can be used for communication, but "an electronic communication device" is too narrow (a telephone also fits that).
• A computer is a music playing calculation device. — Playing music and calculating are only some of the things computers can do; this is not a proper definition.
• A computer is an education device for presentation. — A computer can be used for education and presentations, but that is one use, not a definition.

⭐ Key point: DATA = raw facts (e.g. "38.9"); INFORMATION = processed, meaningful data (e.g. "the patient has a fever of 38.9 °C").$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q10 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1913, $s$What does a spreadsheet software like Microsoft Excel allow users to do?$s$, $s$["Create, organize, and analyze data in a tabular format", "Create and edit images", "Manage emails", "Browse the internet"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Create, organize, and analyze data in a tabular format
SPREADSHEET software, such as MICROSOFT EXCEL (or Google Sheets, LibreOffice Calc), arranges data in ROWS and COLUMNS of CELLS (a TABLE/grid format). It allows users to CREATE, ORGANISE and ANALYSE data — enter numbers and text, use FORMULAS and functions to calculate automatically (e.g. sums, averages, drug dose calculations), sort and filter data, and create CHARTS and graphs. In nursing, it is useful for staff rosters, fluid balance charts, audit data (e.g. hand-hygiene compliance), budgets and research data.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Create and edit images — Creating and editing images is done with graphics software (e.g. Photoshop, Paint).
• Manage emails — Managing emails is done with email clients (e.g. Outlook, Gmail).
• Browse the internet — Browsing the internet is done with a web browser (e.g. Chrome, Firefox, Edge).

⭐ Key point: In Excel, each cell has an address (e.g. B3 = column B, row 3). Formulas begin with "=", e.g. =SUM(B2:B10).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q11$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1914, $s$The location of a resource on the internet is given by its?$s$, $s$["ICQ", "Protocol", "E-mail address", "URL"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: URL
A URL (UNIFORM RESOURCE LOCATOR) is the ADDRESS that tells a web browser WHERE a resource (web page, image, file) is located on the internet. Example: https://www.who.int/publications. It has parts: the PROTOCOL (https://), the DOMAIN name (www.who.int), and the PATH to the specific page (/publications). Typing a URL into a browser takes you directly to that resource.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• ICQ — ICQ was an old instant messaging program, not an address system.
• Protocol — A protocol (e.g. HTTP, HTTPS, FTP) is a set of RULES for communication; it is only one PART of the URL.
• E-mail address — An email address identifies a mailbox for sending and receiving messages, not the location of a web resource.

⭐ Key point: "https" means the connection is secure (encrypted) — important when entering passwords or patient information online.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q12$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1915, $s$Which of the following is not considered hardware?$s$, $s$["Hard disk", "Keyboard", "CPU", "Operating system"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Operating system
HARDWARE is the physical part of the computer. An OPERATING SYSTEM (e.g. Windows, Linux) is SOFTWARE — a program that manages the computer — so it is NOT hardware. It cannot be touched; it is stored on the hard disk and loaded into memory when the computer starts.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hard disk — A hard disk is hardware (a physical storage device).
• Keyboard — A keyboard is hardware (a physical input device).
• CPU — The CPU (Central Processing Unit) is hardware (a physical chip that processes data).

⭐ Key point: Operating systems, word processors, EHR programs, antivirus and apps are all SOFTWARE.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q13$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1916, $s$____ is a transmission medium that is most appropriate to carry data in a computer network that is exposed to electrical interferences$s$, $s$["Coaxial cable", "Unshielded twisted pair", "Optical fiber", "Microwave"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Optical fiber
OPTICAL FIBRE (fibre-optic cable) carries data as PULSES OF LIGHT through thin strands of glass or plastic, not as electrical signals. Because light is NOT affected by ELECTRICAL or MAGNETIC fields, optical fibre is IMMUNE to ELECTROMAGNETIC INTERFERENCE (EMI). This makes it the most appropriate medium in environments with heavy electrical equipment — such as hospitals (MRI scanners, X-ray machines, generators). It also offers very high speed (bandwidth), long distances and better security (hard to tap).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Coaxial cable — Coaxial cable has some shielding, but it still carries electrical signals and can be affected by strong interference.
• Unshielded twisted pair — Unshielded twisted pair (UTP) has no shielding and is MOST vulnerable to electrical interference.
• Microwave — Microwave is a wireless medium; it can be affected by interference, weather and obstacles.

⭐ Key point: Optical fibre is more expensive and harder to install and repair, but is the best choice for high speed, long distance and interference-prone environments.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q14$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1917, $s$A collection of hyperlinked documents on the internet forms the$s$, $s$["Hypertext markup language", "World Wide Web (WWW)", "E-mail system", "Mailing list"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: World Wide Web (WWW)
The WORLD WIDE WEB (WWW, or "the web") is a huge collection of WEB PAGES and documents stored on computers around the world, which are linked together by HYPERLINKS (clickable links that take you from one page to another). These documents are accessed through the internet using a web browser and the HTTP/HTTPS protocol. The web was invented by Tim Berners-Lee in 1989. Note: the INTERNET is the global network of networks (the infrastructure); the WEB is one SERVICE that runs on the internet.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertext markup language — HTML (Hypertext Markup Language) is the LANGUAGE used to create web pages, not the collection of pages.
• E-mail system — The email system is a different internet service for sending messages.
• Mailing list — A mailing list is a list of email addresses for sending messages to a group.

⭐ Key point: Other internet services besides the web: email, file transfer (FTP), video calls, instant messaging.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q15$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1918, $s$The Central Processing Unit (CPU) of a computer is primarily responsible for:$s$, $s$["Permanent data storage only", "Executing instructions and processing data", "Displaying output on the monitor only", "Providing internet connectivity only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Executing instructions and processing data
The CENTRAL PROCESSING UNIT (CPU) is the "BRAIN" of the computer. It is primarily responsible for EXECUTING INSTRUCTIONS (from programs) and PROCESSING DATA (performing calculations, comparisons, and controlling data flow). The CPU has three main parts: the CONTROL UNIT (directs and coordinates operations), the ARITHMETIC AND LOGIC UNIT — ALU (performs calculations and logical comparisons), and REGISTERS (very fast, small memory for immediate data). CPU speed is measured in gigahertz (GHz).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Permanent data storage only — Permanent data storage is done by secondary storage (hard disk, SSD), not the CPU.
• Displaying output on the monitor only — Displaying output is done by the monitor (output device) and the graphics card.
• Providing internet connectivity only — Internet connectivity is provided by a network card/modem and router.

⭐ Key point: The CPU, together with RAM, determines how fast a computer can process tasks.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q16$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1919, $s$Batch processing, as a method of file processing, is best described as:$s$, $s$["Collecting and processing a group of transactions together at a scheduled time", "A method used only for video files", "Processing that requires no computer at all", "Processing transactions immediately as they occur, one at a time"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Collecting and processing a group of transactions together at a scheduled time
BATCH PROCESSING means COLLECTING a GROUP (batch) of transactions over a period of time and PROCESSING them ALL TOGETHER at a SCHEDULED time, usually without user interaction. Examples: payroll (salaries processed at the end of the month), monthly billing, end-of-day hospital census reports, and backing up data overnight. It is efficient for large volumes of data where immediate results are not needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A method used only for video files — Batch processing is not limited to video files; it can process any data.
• Processing that requires no computer at all — Batch processing requires a computer.
• Processing transactions immediately as they occur, one at a time — Processing transactions immediately as they occur is REAL-TIME (online) processing — e.g. an ICU monitor alarm, or an EHR updating lab results instantly.

⭐ Key point: Real-time processing is needed for patient monitoring and medication administration; batch processing is suitable for reports and payroll.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q17$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1920, $s$A master file in a computerised records system typically contains:$s$, $s$["Only images and video files", "Relatively permanent, core reference data about an entity (e.g. patient demographic records)", "Only system error logs", "Temporary data deleted after each session"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Relatively permanent, core reference data about an entity (e.g. patient demographic records)
A MASTER FILE contains RELATIVELY PERMANENT, CORE REFERENCE DATA about an entity (a person or thing) that does not change often. For example, the PATIENT MASTER INDEX holds each patient's demographic details: hospital number, name, date of birth, sex, address, next of kin. Other examples: staff master file, drug formulary master file. Master files are updated by TRANSACTION FILES, which record day-to-day events (e.g. admissions, drug administrations, lab results).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only images and video files — Master files are not only images and videos.
• Only system error logs — System error logs are log files, not master files.
• Temporary data deleted after each session — Temporary data deleted after each session is stored in temporary files (or transaction/work files), not master files.

⭐ Key point: Master file = permanent, core data. Transaction file = temporary record of events used to update the master file.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q18$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1921, $s$USB flash drives and optical disks (CD/DVD) are examples of:$s$, $s$["Primary memory (RAM)", "Input devices only", "Secondary/portable storage devices", "Output devices only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Secondary/portable storage devices
USB FLASH DRIVES (memory sticks) and OPTICAL DISKS (CDs, DVDs, Blu-ray) are SECONDARY (auxiliary) STORAGE devices. They store data PERMANENTLY (non-volatile — data stays when the power is off) and are PORTABLE (can be moved between computers). Other secondary storage: external hard disks, memory cards (SD cards), and cloud storage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Primary memory (RAM) — Primary memory (RAM) is inside the computer, is temporary, and is lost when power is off.
• Input devices only — They are storage devices, not input devices (although you can read data from them).
• Output devices only — They are storage devices, not output devices only.

⭐ Key point: Portable storage devices carry security risks: patient data on a lost USB drive can cause a confidentiality breach. Use encrypted devices and follow hospital policy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q20$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1922, $s$Protecting electronic access to client data primarily relies on which of the following measures?$s$, $s$["Printing all records to avoid computer use entirely", "Leaving all records openly accessible to any staff member for convenience", "Disabling all data backups", "User authentication (passwords/logins), access controls and audit trails"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: User authentication (passwords/logins), access controls and audit trails
Protecting ELECTRONIC ACCESS to client data relies on: USER AUTHENTICATION (unique usernames and strong PASSWORDS, smart cards, biometrics, two-factor authentication) to confirm who the user is; ACCESS CONTROLS (role-based access — staff can only see the information they need for their job); and AUDIT TRAILS (the system records who accessed which record, when, and what they did, so inappropriate access can be detected). Other measures: encryption, automatic log-off, firewalls, antivirus and staff training.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Printing all records to avoid computer use entirely — Printing all records increases the risk of paper records being lost or seen by the wrong people.
• Leaving all records openly accessible to any staff member for convenience — Leaving records openly accessible to anyone breaches confidentiality.
• Disabling all data backups — Disabling backups risks permanent loss of patient data; backups are essential.

⭐ Key point: Never share your password, always log off or lock the screen when leaving a computer, and only access records of patients you are caring for.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q22$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1923, $s$The main purpose of barcode medication administration (BCMA) is to:$s$, $s$["Replace the need for a medication administration record", "Track staff attendance", "Speed up medication rounds regardless of accuracy", "Verify the five rights of medication administration and reduce medication errors"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Verify the five rights of medication administration and reduce medication errors
BARCODE MEDICATION ADMINISTRATION (BCMA) uses barcodes on the patient's wristband and on each medication. Before giving a drug, the nurse SCANS the patient's wristband and the drug. The system checks these against the electronic prescription and VERIFIES the FIVE RIGHTS: RIGHT PATIENT, RIGHT DRUG, RIGHT DOSE, RIGHT ROUTE and RIGHT TIME. It alerts the nurse to mismatches, allergies or timing errors, REDUCING MEDICATION ERRORS. It also automatically records the administration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Replace the need for a medication administration record — BCMA works with the electronic medication administration record (eMAR); it does not replace it.
• Track staff attendance — BCMA is not used to track staff attendance.
• Speed up medication rounds regardless of accuracy — Speed is not the goal; accuracy and safety are.

⭐ Key point: Never override or "work around" BCMA alerts (e.g. scanning a copy of a barcode) — this defeats its safety purpose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q25$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1924, $s$Tele-ICU (remote ICU monitoring) primarily allows:$s$, $s$["Only administrative record-keeping", "Replacement of ventilator alarms", "Elimination of bedside nurses", "Remote specialists to monitor and support ICU care across multiple sites"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Remote specialists to monitor and support ICU care across multiple sites
TELE-ICU (remote ICU or eICU) uses video, audio and electronic monitoring links so that INTENSIVISTS and critical care NURSES at a central location can MONITOR patients in MULTIPLE ICUs (even at different hospitals) in real time. They review vital signs, alarms, lab results and images, and SUPPORT bedside teams with advice, early warning of deterioration, and help during emergencies — especially at night or in hospitals without on-site intensivists.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only administrative record-keeping — Tele-ICU is clinical support, not just administrative record-keeping.
• Replacement of ventilator alarms — It does not replace ventilator alarms.
• Elimination of bedside nurses — It supports, but does not replace, bedside nurses.

⭐ Key point: Tele-ICU has been linked to lower ICU mortality and shorter stays, and is useful in areas with a shortage of specialists.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q26$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1925, $s$A clinical decision support system (CDSS) in an EHR is designed to:$s$, $s$["Automatically treat the patient without clinician input", "Only manage billing", "Replace nursing assessment", "Alert clinicians to potential issues such as drug interactions or abnormal values"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Alert clinicians to potential issues such as drug interactions or abnormal values
A CLINICAL DECISION SUPPORT SYSTEM (CDSS) is a feature in the EHR that uses patient data and clinical knowledge to give clinicians helpful ALERTS, REMINDERS and RECOMMENDATIONS at the point of care. Examples: alerts for DRUG INTERACTIONS, drug ALLERGIES, wrong doses, ABNORMAL LAB VALUES (e.g. critical potassium), sepsis screening alerts, early warning scores, and reminders for VTE prophylaxis. It SUPPORTS clinical judgement; the clinician makes the final decision.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Automatically treat the patient without clinician input — A CDSS does not treat patients automatically; a clinician must decide and act.
• Only manage billing — A CDSS is a clinical tool, not only for billing.
• Replace nursing assessment — A CDSS does not replace nursing assessment; it adds to it.

⭐ Key point: Too many alerts can cause "alert fatigue", where clinicians ignore warnings. Alerts should be relevant and well designed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q27$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1926, $s$Health Level Seven (HL7) standards exist primarily to support:$s$, $s$["Hospital parking systems", "Interoperability and standardised exchange of health information between systems", "Physical security of hospital buildings", "Only nursing schedules"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Interoperability and standardised exchange of health information between systems
HEALTH LEVEL SEVEN (HL7) is a set of international STANDARDS for the EXCHANGE, integration, sharing and retrieval of electronic health information. It allows different computer systems (e.g. lab system, pharmacy system, EHR, radiology system) — often from different manufacturers — to "talk" to each other and understand the data being sent. This is called INTEROPERABILITY. The newer HL7 FHIR standard uses modern web technology.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hospital parking systems — HL7 is not related to parking systems.
• Physical security of hospital buildings — HL7 is not related to physical building security.
• Only nursing schedules — HL7 is not only for nursing schedules.

⭐ Key point: Interoperability allows patient information to follow the patient across departments and facilities, reducing repeated tests and errors.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q28$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1927, $s$Two-factor authentication improves EHR security by:$s$, $s$["Requiring a second independent verification step beyond just a password", "Disabling password requirements", "Allowing shared generic logins", "Requiring only a username"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Requiring a second independent verification step beyond just a password
TWO-FACTOR AUTHENTICATION (2FA) requires TWO DIFFERENT, INDEPENDENT ways to prove your identity before access is allowed: something you KNOW (password or PIN), plus something you HAVE (a phone receiving a one-time code, a smart card or token) or something you ARE (fingerprint, face recognition). Even if a password is stolen, an attacker cannot log in without the second factor, greatly improving EHR security.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Disabling password requirements — 2FA does not disable passwords; it adds an extra step.
• Allowing shared generic logins — Shared generic logins reduce security and accountability.
• Requiring only a username — Requiring only a username provides no security.

⭐ Key point: Never share your login or one-time codes, and report any suspicious login requests.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q30$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1928, $s$Encrypting patient data primarily protects against:$s$, $s$["Slower data transmission", "Unauthorised parties reading intercepted or stolen data", "Hardware failure", "Loss of internet connectivity"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Unauthorised parties reading intercepted or stolen data
ENCRYPTION converts data into a coded (scrambled) form using a mathematical key. Only someone with the correct key can DECRYPT and read it. If encrypted patient data is INTERCEPTED during transmission (e.g. over the internet or Wi-Fi) or STOLEN (e.g. a lost laptop or USB drive), UNAUTHORISED people CANNOT READ it. This protects confidentiality.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Slower data transmission — Encryption may slightly slow transmission, but that is a side effect, not what it protects against.
• Hardware failure — Encryption does not protect against hardware failure; backups do.
• Loss of internet connectivity — Encryption does not protect against loss of internet connectivity.

⭐ Key point: Use encrypted devices and secure (https, VPN) connections for patient data.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q31$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1929, $s$The four main functional components of a standard computer are:$s$, $s$["Input, process, output and memory (storage)", "Laptop, desktop, tablet and hand-held", "Keyboard, mouse, monitor and CPU", "Hardware, software, system apps and applications"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Input, process, output and memory (storage)
A standard computer system performs four main functions, carried out by four functional components: (1) INPUT — accepting data and instructions (keyboard, mouse, scanner); (2) PROCESSING — working on the data according to instructions (CPU); (3) OUTPUT — presenting the results (monitor, printer); (4) MEMORY/STORAGE — keeping data and programs temporarily or permanently (RAM, hard disk).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Laptop, desktop, tablet and hand-held — Laptop, desktop, tablet and hand-held are TYPES/sizes of computers, not functional components.
• Keyboard, mouse, monitor and CPU — Keyboard, mouse, monitor and CPU are specific hardware items, not the four functional components.
• Hardware, software, system apps and applications — Hardware, software, system apps and applications are categories of computer parts, not functional components.

⭐ Key point: Remember the IPOS cycle: Input → Processing → Output → Storage.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q33$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1930, $s$According to size, computers are classified as:$s$, $s$["Large, ultra-large, small and compact", "Laptop, desktop and hand-held", "Big-scale and small-scale", "Super, mainframe, mini and micro computers"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Super, mainframe, mini and micro computers
According to SIZE (and processing power), computers are classified into: (1) SUPERCOMPUTERS — the largest and fastest, used for complex calculations (weather forecasting, scientific research); (2) MAINFRAME computers — large, powerful systems used by big organisations (banks, governments, large hospitals) to serve many users at once; (3) MINI computers — medium-sized, serving smaller organisations or departments; (4) MICRO computers — personal computers (desktops, laptops, tablets, smartphones), the most common type.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Large, ultra-large, small and compact — "Large, ultra-large, small and compact" is not the standard classification.
• Laptop, desktop and hand-held — Laptop, desktop and hand-held are types of MICRO computers only.
• Big-scale and small-scale — "Big-scale and small-scale" is not the standard classification.

⭐ Key point: Computers are also classified by TYPE (analogue, digital, hybrid) and by PURPOSE (general-purpose, special-purpose).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q34$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1931, $s$Computer software is classified into:$s$, $s$["Special, general and specific packages", "Personal and industrial software", "Word, PowerPoint and Excel", "System, application and utility software"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: System, application and utility software
Computer SOFTWARE is classified into: (1) SYSTEM SOFTWARE — manages the computer's hardware and provides a platform for other software (operating systems such as Windows, Linux; device drivers); (2) APPLICATION SOFTWARE — helps users perform specific tasks (word processors, spreadsheets, EHRs, web browsers); (3) UTILITY SOFTWARE — performs maintenance and support tasks (antivirus, backup, disk cleanup, file compression). Some textbooks treat utilities as part of system software.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Special, general and specific packages — "Special, general and specific packages" is not the standard classification.
• Personal and industrial software — "Personal and industrial software" is not the standard classification.
• Word, PowerPoint and Excel — Word, PowerPoint and Excel are examples of APPLICATION software only.

⭐ Key point: Application software may be "general-purpose" (e.g. Word) or "special-purpose/tailor-made" (e.g. a hospital pharmacy system).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q35$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1932, $s$Which of the following is NOT a network topology?$s$, $s$["Bus", "Star", "Peer to Peer", "Ring"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Peer to Peer
NETWORK TOPOLOGY describes the physical or logical ARRANGEMENT (layout) of computers and connections in a network. Common topologies: BUS (all devices share one main cable), STAR (all devices connect to a central hub/switch — most common in hospitals and offices), RING (devices connected in a closed loop), MESH (devices connected to many others) and TREE (hierarchical). PEER-TO-PEER is NOT a topology — it describes the RELATIONSHIP between computers (all equal, no central server), i.e. a network architecture/model.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bus — Bus is a topology.
• Star — Star is a topology.
• Ring — Ring is a topology.

⭐ Key point: Star topology: if one cable fails, only that device is affected; but if the central switch fails, the whole network goes down.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q37$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1933, $s$A nurse is teaching a group of nursing students about the history of computers. Which generation of computers is characterized by the use of vacuum tubes?$s$, $s$["Second Generation", "Third Generation", "First Generation", "Fourth Generation"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: First Generation
Computers are grouped into GENERATIONS based on the main technology used: FIRST GENERATION (about 1940–1956) used VACUUM TUBES — they were very large, expensive, generated a lot of heat, used much electricity and broke down often (e.g. ENIAC, UNIVAC). Second generation (1956–1963) used TRANSISTORS; third generation (1964–1971) used INTEGRATED CIRCUITS; fourth generation (1971–present) uses MICROPROCESSORS; fifth generation (present and future) is based on ARTIFICIAL INTELLIGENCE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Second Generation — The second generation used transistors.
• Third Generation — The third generation used integrated circuits.
• Fourth Generation — The fourth generation uses microprocessors.

⭐ Key point: Each generation made computers smaller, faster, cheaper, more reliable and more energy-efficient.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q38$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1934, $s$Which quality tool is commonly used to identify the underlying causes of a problem?$s$, $s$["Duty roster", "Fishbone (Ishikawa) diagram", "Organizational chart", "Leave register"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fishbone (Ishikawa) diagram
A FISHBONE (ISHIKAWA or cause-and-effect) DIAGRAM is a quality tool used to identify and organise the POSSIBLE UNDERLYING CAUSES of a problem. The problem (effect) is written at the "head" of the fish, and the main categories of causes form the "bones" — commonly People, Methods/Procedures, Equipment, Materials, Environment and Management. The team brainstorms causes in each category. It is often used in root cause analysis (e.g. of medication errors or rising infection rates).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Duty roster — A duty roster schedules staff shifts.
• Organizational chart — An organisational chart shows reporting relationships and structure.
• Leave register — A leave register records staff leave.

⭐ Key point: Other quality tools: Pareto chart (most frequent causes), flowchart (process steps), run chart (trends over time), PDSA cycle (testing changes).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q114$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1935, $s$Neuman describes three levels of prevention as intervention. Which level is aimed at strengthening the flexible line of defense BEFORE a stressor invades the client system?$s$, $s$["Quaternary prevention", "Secondary prevention", "Primary prevention", "Tertiary prevention"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Primary prevention
NEUMAN uses three levels of PREVENTION AS INTERVENTION: PRIMARY PREVENTION — done BEFORE a stressor invades; it identifies risk factors and STRENGTHENS the FLEXIBLE LINE OF DEFENCE (e.g. immunisation, health education, VTE prophylaxis, pre-operative teaching); SECONDARY PREVENTION — done AFTER a stressor has penetrated and symptoms appear; it treats symptoms and strengthens the lines of resistance (e.g. early treatment of infection); TERTIARY PREVENTION — done during recovery to maintain stability and prevent recurrence (e.g. rehabilitation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Quaternary prevention — Quaternary prevention is not one of Neuman's levels.
• Secondary prevention — Secondary prevention occurs after the stressor has invaded.
• Tertiary prevention — Tertiary prevention supports readaptation after treatment.

⭐ Key point: Primary prevention in ICU includes VAP and pressure ulcer prevention bundles.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q133$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1936, $s$Abraham Maslow's hierarchy of needs is typically depicted as a pyramid with which level at the very base, representing the most fundamental needs?$s$, $s$["Love and belonging needs", "Safety and security needs", "Physiological needs", "Self-actualization needs"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Physiological needs
MASLOW'S HIERARCHY OF NEEDS is drawn as a PYRAMID. The BASE (most fundamental) level is PHYSIOLOGICAL NEEDS — oxygen, water, food, elimination, sleep, temperature regulation and shelter — which are needed for survival. Above these come safety and security, then love and belonging, then esteem, and at the top, SELF-ACTUALISATION (reaching one's full potential). Lower needs generally must be met before higher ones become the main focus.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Love and belonging needs — Love and belonging is the THIRD level.
• Safety and security needs — Safety and security is the SECOND level.
• Self-actualization needs — Self-actualisation is the TOP level.

⭐ Key point: In nursing, Maslow's hierarchy helps set priorities: life-threatening physiological problems (airway, breathing, circulation) come first.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q137$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1937, $s$When levelling and zeroing an arterial or CVP transducer, it should be placed at the:$s$, $s$["Any level, as it does not matter", "Phlebostatic axis (4th intercostal space, mid-axillary line)", "Level of the head", "Level of the feet"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Phlebostatic axis (4th intercostal space, mid-axillary line)
Pressure transducers for arterial lines, CVP and PA catheters must be LEVELLED at the PHLEBOSTATIC AXIS — the point where the 4th INTERCOSTAL SPACE meets the MID-AXILLARY LINE. This approximates the level of the RIGHT ATRIUM (the reference point for haemodynamic pressures). Then the transducer is ZEROED (opened to air) to remove the effect of atmospheric pressure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Any level, as it does not matter — The level matters: a transducer too low gives falsely high readings; too high gives falsely low readings (about 7.4 mmHg per 10 cm).
• Level of the head — The level of the head is used for intracranial pressure (tragus/foramen of Monro), not arterial or CVP.
• Level of the feet — The level of the feet is never the reference.

⭐ Key point: Re-level the transducer every time the patient's position or bed height changes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q186$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1938, $s$The low-pressure and low exhaled-minute-volume alarms sound together, and the patient can be heard making a 'gurgling' sound and vocalising around the tube. The most likely cause is:$s$, $s$["The patient biting down on the tube during agitation", "A cuff leak or a tube that has slipped up above the cords", "Severe bronchospasm with reduced exhaled volumes", "A mucus plug partly blocking the endotracheal tube"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A cuff leak or a tube that has slipped up above the cords
When the LOW PRESSURE and LOW EXHALED MINUTE VOLUME alarms sound together, gas is ESCAPING from the system — the ventilator cannot build up pressure, and less air returns. If the patient is making a GURGLING sound and VOCALISING (talking or making sounds), air is passing through the vocal cords. This means the tube's cuff is LEAKING (deflated or damaged), or the TUBE HAS SLIPPED UP so the cuff sits at or above the vocal cords. This is dangerous: the airway is not secure and the tube may come out. Check the tube position at the teeth/lips, check the cuff pressure, call for help, and be ready to re-secure or re-intubate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient biting down on the tube during agitation — Biting the tube causes HIGH pressure alarms, not low pressure.
• Severe bronchospasm with reduced exhaled volumes — Bronchospasm causes HIGH peak pressures.
• A mucus plug partly blocking the endotracheal tube — A mucus plug causes HIGH pressure alarms.

⭐ Key point: A ventilated patient should never be able to talk or vocalise; if they do, suspect a cuff leak or a displaced tube.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q11$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1939, $s$A syringe pump alarms 'occlusion' because a three-way tap below it was closed. Before simply opening the tap, the nurse should:$s$, $s$["Flush the line rapidly with 10 mL saline to clear any clot", "Replace the syringe, as the drug has now been inactivated", "Increase the rate briefly to make up for the missed drug", "Relieve the built-up line pressure first, to avoid a sudden bolus"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Relieve the built-up line pressure first, to avoid a sudden bolus
When a three-way tap is closed, the pump keeps pushing, building up PRESSURE in the line until it alarms "occlusion". If the tap is simply opened, the built-up pressure releases suddenly, delivering a BOLUS of noradrenaline (a dangerous spike in BP). The nurse should first RELIEVE THE BUILT-UP PRESSURE (e.g. use the pump's "back-off" function, or disconnect and release the pressure safely into a waste syringe) BEFORE opening the tap.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Flush the line rapidly with 10 mL saline to clear any clot — Flushing rapidly with 10 mL saline would push a large bolus of noradrenaline into the patient.
• Replace the syringe, as the drug has now been inactivated — The drug has not become inactive; it doesn't need replacing.
• Increase the rate briefly to make up for the missed drug — Increasing the rate to "make up" for missed drug causes a dangerous overdose.

⭐ Key point: Check three-way taps and lines before starting infusions. Place vasoactive drugs on a dedicated lumen.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q22$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1940, $s$A patient rescued from a closed room with a charcoal stove is confused, with a pulse oximeter reading of 98%. The reading is unreliable because:$s$, $s$["Vasodilation from carbon monoxide improves the oximeter signal", "Carbon monoxide shifts the oxygen dissociation curve to the right, raising SpO₂", "Standard pulse oximeters read carboxyhaemoglobin as oxyhaemoglobin, giving a falsely normal SpO₂", "Carbon monoxide increases the amount of oxygen dissolved in plasma"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Standard pulse oximeters read carboxyhaemoglobin as oxyhaemoglobin, giving a falsely normal SpO₂
Carbon monoxide (CO) from a charcoal stove in a closed room binds to haemoglobin 200–250 times more strongly than oxygen, forming CARBOXYHAEMOGLOBIN (COHb). Standard pulse oximeters use only two wavelengths of light and CANNOT tell COHb from OXYHAEMOGLOBIN (they absorb light similarly). So the oximeter shows a FALSELY NORMAL SpO2 (e.g. 98%), even though the blood is carrying much less oxygen. The patient is actually hypoxic at the tissue level, causing confusion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vasodilation from carbon monoxide improves the oximeter signal — CO doesn't improve the oximeter signal through vasodilation.
• Carbon monoxide shifts the oxygen dissociation curve to the right, raising SpO₂ — CO shifts the oxygen dissociation curve to the LEFT (oxygen is held more tightly and released less to the tissues).
• Carbon monoxide increases the amount of oxygen dissolved in plasma — CO doesn't increase dissolved oxygen in plasma.

⭐ Key point: Measure COHb using a CO-oximeter on an arterial or venous blood gas. Treat with 100% oxygen via a non-rebreather mask (reduces CO half-life from about 4–6 hours to about 60–90 minutes); consider hyperbaric oxygen in severe cases.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q27$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1941, $s$A patient's chest drain bottle is accidentally lifted above the level of the chest. The main risk is:$s$, $s$["Backflow of drained fluid into the pleural space", "Re-expansion pulmonary oedema of the lung", "Tension pneumothorax because air cannot escape", "Air embolism entering the pulmonary veins"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Backflow of drained fluid into the pleural space
The drainage bottle must always stay BELOW the level of the chest. If it is lifted above the chest, gravity can cause the drained fluid (and water from the seal) to flow BACK into the PLEURAL SPACE, risking infection and lung compression.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Re-expansion pulmonary oedema of the lung — Re-expansion pulmonary oedema occurs when a large effusion or pneumothorax is drained too quickly, not when the bottle is raised.
• Tension pneumothorax because air cannot escape — A tension pneumothorax may occur if the drain is clamped or blocked, but backflow is the main risk here.
• Air embolism entering the pulmonary veins — Air embolism is not a risk of raising the bottle.

⭐ Key point: Keep the bottle upright and below the chest, especially during patient transfers.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q38$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1942, $s$The transducer of an external ventricular drain is zeroed at the level of the:$s$, $s$["Phlebostatic axis at the 4th intercostal space", "Sternal notch", "Tragus of the ear (foramen of Monro)", "Iliac crest"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tragus of the ear (foramen of Monro)
The EVD transducer is ZEROED at the level of the FORAMEN OF MONRO (the opening between the ventricles inside the head). The external landmark for this is the TRAGUS of the ear (the small flap in front of the ear canal) or the outer corner of the eye.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Phlebostatic axis at the 4th intercostal space — The phlebostatic axis is used for haemodynamic (arterial and CVP) transducers.
• Sternal notch — The sternal notch is not the reference.
• Iliac crest — The iliac crest is used for intra-abdominal pressure measurement.

⭐ Key point: Always level and zero the EVD when the head position changes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q48 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1943, $s$Intra-abdominal pressure is measured via the bladder with the transducer zeroed at the:$s$, $s$["The symphysis pubis, during a deep inspiration", "Mid-axillary line at the iliac crest, supine, end-expiration", "Phlebostatic axis, 4th intercostal space, at end-inspiration", "The umbilicus, with the patient sitting up at 45 degrees"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mid-axillary line at the iliac crest, supine, end-expiration
IAP is measured via the bladder. The transducer is zeroed at the MID-AXILLARY LINE at the level of the ILIAC CREST, with the patient SUPINE, and measured at END-EXPIRATION. A small volume (no more than 25 mL) of saline is instilled into the bladder.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The symphysis pubis, during a deep inspiration — The symphysis pubis is not the standard reference, and readings are taken at end-expiration.
• Phlebostatic axis, 4th intercostal space, at end-inspiration — The phlebostatic axis is for haemodynamic monitoring, and readings are at end-expiration.
• The umbilicus, with the patient sitting up at 45 degrees — The patient should be supine, not sitting.

⭐ Key point: Ensure abdominal muscles are relaxed (no contractions); measure in mmHg.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q63$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1944, $s$The level of ICU that possess an active research programme with dedicated research staff is:$s$, $s$["Level 1", "Level 11", "Level 1V", "Level 111"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Level 111
ICUs are classified by the level of care they provide. A LEVEL III ICU is the highest level: it is a tertiary referral unit that provides the full range of complex intensive care (multi-organ support, advanced ventilation, renal replacement therapy, invasive monitoring), has specialist intensivists available 24 hours, and runs teaching and an ACTIVE RESEARCH PROGRAMME with dedicated research staff.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Level 1 — A Level I unit provides basic care (e.g. short-term ventilation and monitoring) and does not usually have research staff.
• Level 11 — A Level II unit provides more complex care, but does not usually have an active research programme with dedicated staff.
• Level 1V — Level IV is not a standard category in this classification.

⭐ Key point: Level I = basic, Level II = intermediate, Level III = tertiary, with full support, teaching and research.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q161$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1945, $s$A Level 3 critical care patient is one who needs:$s$, $s$["Routine post-operative care after minor surgery", "Advanced respiratory support, or support of two or more organs", "Observation more frequent than is possible on the ward", "Single organ support such as non-invasive ventilation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Advanced respiratory support, or support of two or more organs
Critical care levels describe the patient's needs. LEVEL 3 patients need ADVANCED RESPIRATORY SUPPORT (invasive mechanical ventilation) alone, or SUPPORT OF TWO OR MORE ORGAN SYSTEMS (e.g. ventilation plus vasopressors, or vasopressors plus renal replacement). They need intensive care unit care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Routine post-operative care after minor surgery — Routine post-op care after minor surgery is Level 0 (normal ward care).
• Observation more frequent than is possible on the ward — Observation more frequent than possible on the ward describes Level 1 or 2.
• Single organ support such as non-invasive ventilation — Single organ support (e.g. non-invasive ventilation) is Level 2 (high dependency).

⭐ Key point: Level 0 = ward, Level 1 = at risk / step-down, Level 2 = single organ support (HDU), Level 3 = advanced respiratory or multi-organ support (ICU).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q166 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1946, $s$Equipment management in ICU includes:$s$, $s$["Allowing untrained staff to set up ventilators", "Repairing equipment only when it fails in use", "Planned maintenance, inventory and staff training", "Keeping faulty equipment at the bedside for spares"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Planned maintenance, inventory and staff training
EQUIPMENT MANAGEMENT in ICU includes: PLANNED (preventive) MAINTENANCE (servicing equipment on schedule), keeping an INVENTORY (knowing what equipment is available, where it is, and its condition), and STAFF TRAINING (ensuring staff can use equipment safely).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Allowing untrained staff to set up ventilators — Untrained staff using ventilators is unsafe.
• Repairing equipment only when it fails in use — Waiting for failures puts patients at risk.
• Keeping faulty equipment at the bedside for spares — Faulty equipment should be removed and labelled, not kept at the bedside.

⭐ Key point: Report equipment faults and remove them from use.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q176$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1947, $s$Equipment needed for a critically ill obese patient includes:$s$, $s$["Bariatric bed, larger BP cuffs and lifting aids", "Only a larger-size urinary catheter", "No special equipment for these patients", "A standard bed with a small BP cuff"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bariatric bed, larger BP cuffs and lifting aids
Critically ill obese patients need special equipment: a BARIATRIC BED (stronger and wider, with pressure-relieving features), LARGER BP CUFFS (a cuff that's too small gives falsely HIGH readings), LIFTING and TRANSFER AIDS (hoists, slide sheets) to protect patients and staff, and suitable chairs and commodes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only a larger-size urinary catheter — More than a larger catheter is needed.
• No special equipment for these patients — Special equipment IS needed.
• A standard bed with a small BP cuff — A standard bed and small cuff are unsafe.

⭐ Key point: Obese patients also have a higher risk of difficult airway, pressure ulcers, DVT and breathing problems.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q78$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1948, $s$An ICU orientation programme for new nurses should include:$s$, $s$["Policies, equipment training, competencies and a preceptor", "Immediate sole care of two ventilated patients", "Only a short tour of the unit on the first day", "Reading the history of the hospital and its founders"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Policies, equipment training, competencies and a preceptor
A good ICU ORIENTATION PROGRAMME prepares new nurses to work safely. It includes: unit POLICIES and procedures, EQUIPMENT TRAINING (ventilators, monitors, infusion pumps), COMPETENCY assessments, and a PRECEPTOR (an experienced nurse who guides and supervises the new nurse).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate sole care of two ventilated patients — Giving a new nurse two ventilated patients immediately is unsafe.
• Only a short tour of the unit on the first day — A short tour is not enough to prepare a nurse for complex ICU care.
• Reading the history of the hospital and its founders — The hospital's history is not essential to safe ICU practice.

⭐ Key point: Competency-based orientation improves patient safety and staff retention.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q172$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1949, $s$Identify the five main components of a computer system in their order?$s$, $s$["Modem, Keyboard, Word Processor, Printer, Screen", "Memory, Video card, Monitor, Software, Hardware", "CPU, CD-ROM, Mouse, Keyboard, Sound card", "CPU, Memory, System bus, Input, Output"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: CPU, Memory, System bus, Input, Output
Every computer system has five main parts that work together in this order. The CPU is the brain that processes instructions. MEMORY (RAM) holds data and programs while they are used. The SYSTEM BUS is the set of electrical pathways that carries data between the parts. INPUT devices (keyboard, mouse, scanner) put data in, and OUTPUT devices (monitor, printer, speakers) give results out.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Modem, Keyboard, Word Processor, Printer, Screen — A modem, word processor and screen are individual devices or software, not the five core system parts.
• Memory, Video card, Monitor, Software, Hardware — A video card is one component, and "software" and "hardware" are categories, not system components.
• CPU, CD-ROM, Mouse, Keyboard, Sound card — A CD-ROM, sound card and mouse are individual devices, not the core architecture.

⭐ Key point: Hardware = the physical parts. Software = the programs. The CPU contains the control unit and the ALU (arithmetic logic unit).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q41$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1950, $s$Multi-user processing in a hospital computer system allows:$s$, $s$["Only one staff member to use the system at any given time", "Multiple users to access and use the system concurrently", "No simultaneous data entry by different departments", "Only administrators to log in"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Multiple users to access and use the system concurrently
A multi-user system lets MANY people use the same computer system AT THE SAME TIME, each from their own terminal, with their own login and access rights. In a hospital, nurses, doctors, the pharmacy, the laboratory and radiology can all enter and view patient data at once. This is how hospital information systems and electronic records work.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only one staff member to use the system at any given time — Allowing only one person at a time would be a single-user system.
• No simultaneous data entry by different departments — Multi-user systems specifically ALLOW simultaneous data entry.
• Only administrators to log in — Many types of user (not only administrators) can log in, each with role-based access.

⭐ Key point: In multi-user systems, each user must have their own password, and all actions are recorded (audit trail). Never share your login.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q42$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1951, $s$A nurse is explaining the units of data to a patient. The smallest unit of data in a computer is a:$s$, $s$["Bit", "Kilobyte", "Megabyte", "Byte"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bit
A BIT (binary digit) is the smallest unit of data. It can only be 0 or 1 (off or on). Computers store everything as patterns of bits. Eight bits make one BYTE, which can store one character (such as a letter).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Kilobyte — A kilobyte is about 1000 bytes (8000 bits), much bigger than a bit.
• Megabyte — A megabyte is about 1 million bytes.
• Byte — A byte is 8 bits, so it is bigger than a bit.

⭐ Key point: Order from smallest: bit → byte (8 bits) → kilobyte → megabyte → gigabyte → terabyte (each step about 1000 times bigger; strictly 1024).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q43 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1952, $s$A hybrid computer used in ICU monitoring combines:$s$, $s$["Analogue signal input with digital processing", "A mainframe linked to a microcomputer", "Two digital computers working together", "Hardware and software in one box only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Analogue signal input with digital processing
A hybrid computer combines ANALOGUE and DIGITAL features. ICU monitors receive continuous analogue signals from the body (the ECG wave, arterial pressure waves, breathing), then convert them into digital numbers for processing, display, alarms and storage. This combination is what makes them hybrid.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A mainframe linked to a microcomputer — A mainframe linked to a microcomputer is a network, not a hybrid computer.
• Two digital computers working together — Two digital computers working together is a multi-processor or networked system.
• Hardware and software in one box only — Hardware and software are always together in every computer.

⭐ Key point: Analogue = continuous signals (like a wave). Digital = separate numbers (0s and 1s). An analogue-to-digital converter changes one into the other.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q44$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1953, $s$Computers classified by size, from largest to smallest, are:$s$, $s$["Server, router, modem, scanner", "Microcomputer, minicomputer, mainframe, supercomputer", "Supercomputer, mainframe, minicomputer, microcomputer", "Laptop, desktop, tablet, mainframe"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Supercomputer, mainframe, minicomputer, microcomputer
Computers are grouped by size and power, from largest to smallest: SUPERCOMPUTERS (the most powerful; weather forecasting and research), MAINFRAMES (large organisations, banks, big hospital systems), MINICOMPUTERS (medium-sized, for departments), and MICROCOMPUTERS (personal computers: desktops, laptops, tablets).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Server, router, modem, scanner — Servers, routers, modems and scanners are network and input devices, not size classes.
• Microcomputer, minicomputer, mainframe, supercomputer — That order is reversed (smallest to largest).
• Laptop, desktop, tablet, mainframe — Laptops, desktops and tablets are all microcomputers, and mainframe is put in the wrong place.

⭐ Key point: Most computers on hospital wards are microcomputers (personal computers) linked by a network to central servers.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q45$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1954, $s$The first generation of computers used:$s$, $s$["Integrated circuits", "Transistors", "Microprocessors", "Vacuum tubes"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vacuum tubes
The first generation of computers (about 1940–1956) used VACUUM TUBES (valves) for their circuits. They were huge (filling whole rooms), used a lot of electricity, produced great heat, and broke down often. ENIAC and UNIVAC are examples.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Integrated circuits — Integrated circuits were used in the THIRD generation (1964–1971).
• Transistors — Transistors were used in the SECOND generation (1956–1963).
• Microprocessors — Microprocessors were used in the FOURTH generation (1971 to today).

⭐ Key point: Generations: 1st vacuum tubes, 2nd transistors, 3rd integrated circuits, 4th microprocessors, 5th artificial intelligence.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q46$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1955, $s$The control unit of the CPU:$s$, $s$["Performs arithmetic and nothing else", "Directs and coordinates the computer's operations", "Prints the results of each calculation", "Stores data permanently when switched off"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Directs and coordinates the computer's operations
The CPU has two main parts. The CONTROL UNIT is like a manager: it fetches instructions from memory, decodes them, and directs and coordinates the work of all other parts (memory, ALU, input and output). The ARITHMETIC LOGIC UNIT (ALU) does the actual calculations and logical comparisons.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Performs arithmetic and nothing else — Doing arithmetic is the job of the ALU, not the control unit.
• Prints the results of each calculation — Printing results is done by an output device (the printer).
• Stores data permanently when switched off — Permanent storage is done by the hard disk or other storage, not the control unit.

⭐ Key point: CPU = control unit (directs) + ALU (calculates) + registers (very fast small memory).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q47$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1956, $s$Utility software includes:$s$, $s$["Microsoft Word, Excel and PowerPoint", "Antivirus, compression and backup programs", "The ICU patient monitoring display screen", "The computer's processor chip and memory"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Antivirus, compression and backup programs
Utility software is a type of SYSTEM software that helps maintain, protect and manage the computer. Examples: antivirus programs (protect against malware), file compression tools (make files smaller, such as zip), backup programs (copy data for safety), disk cleanup and defragmentation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Microsoft Word, Excel and PowerPoint — Word, Excel and PowerPoint are APPLICATION software (for user tasks).
• The ICU patient monitoring display screen — The ICU monitoring display is application software running on special hardware.
• The computer's processor chip and memory — The processor and memory are HARDWARE.

⭐ Key point: Software types: system software (operating system plus utilities) and application software (programs for users' tasks).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q49$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1957, $s$A computer network within one hospital building is a:$s$, $s$["Local Area Network (LAN)", "Metropolitan Area Network", "Wide Area Network (WAN)", "Personal Area Network"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Local Area Network (LAN)
A Local Area Network (LAN) connects computers in a small area such as one building, one hospital or one campus. It lets departments share data, printers and the hospital information system quickly and securely. It is usually connected by cables (Ethernet) or Wi-Fi.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Metropolitan Area Network — A Metropolitan Area Network (MAN) covers a city.
• Wide Area Network (WAN) — A Wide Area Network (WAN) covers large distances, such as hospitals in different cities; the internet is the biggest WAN.
• Personal Area Network — A Personal Area Network (PAN) covers a few metres around one person (Bluetooth between a phone and earpiece).

⭐ Key point: From smallest to largest network: PAN → LAN → MAN → WAN.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q50$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1958, $s$Electronic mail (e-mail) of patient information should be:$s$, $s$["Printed and posted on the ward notice board", "Encrypted and sent only to authorised people", "Shared in staff group chats for quick advice", "Sent to personal accounts because it is faster"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Encrypted and sent only to authorised people
Patient information is CONFIDENTIAL and protected by law and professional codes (including the Nigeria Data Protection Act). When sent by email, it must be ENCRYPTED (scrambled so only the intended receiver can read it) and sent only to authorised people who need it for care, using official hospital email accounts.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Printed and posted on the ward notice board — Posting patient information on a notice board breaches confidentiality.
• Shared in staff group chats for quick advice — Group chats (such as WhatsApp) are not secure and share information with people who don't need it.
• Sent to personal accounts because it is faster — Personal email accounts are not secure or approved for patient data.

⭐ Key point: Only share the minimum information needed, check the address before sending, and never share patient photos on social media.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q51$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1959, $s$Telemedicine in critical care (tele-ICU) allows:$s$, $s$["Surgery to be performed by computers on their own", "Drugs to be sent to the patient by electronic mail", "Remote specialists to monitor and advise on ICU patients", "Patients to be cared for without any bedside nurses"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Remote specialists to monitor and advise on ICU patients
Tele-ICU (telemedicine in critical care) uses video, audio and remote access to monitors and records so that intensivists and specialists at a central hub can watch and advise on ICU patients in other hospitals. This helps remote or understaffed ICUs get expert advice 24 hours a day and can improve survival.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Surgery to be performed by computers on their own — Computers do not perform surgery on their own; even robotic surgery is controlled by surgeons.
• Drugs to be sent to the patient by electronic mail — Drugs cannot be sent by email.
• Patients to be cared for without any bedside nurses — Bedside nurses are still essential; tele-ICU supports them, it does not replace them.

⭐ Key point: Telemedicine benefits include better access, faster expert decisions and less travel. It needs secure, reliable systems and patient consent.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q52$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1960, $s$A CT scanner and ultrasound machine are examples of ICT in:$s$, $s$["Treatment only", "Administration only", "Diagnosis", "Nursing education only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Diagnosis
ICT in health care is grouped by purpose: DIAGNOSIS (finding out what is wrong), MONITORING, TREATMENT, ADMINISTRATION and EDUCATION. CT scanners and ultrasound machines create images that help doctors find the diagnosis, so they are diagnostic ICT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Treatment only — Treatment ICT includes infusion pumps, ventilators and radiotherapy machines.
• Administration only — Administration ICT includes billing, staff rotas and records management.
• Nursing education only — Education ICT includes e-learning platforms and simulators.

⭐ Key point: Monitoring ICT includes cardiac monitors and pulse oximeters (they watch the patient continuously).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q53$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1961, $s$A computer-based patient record differs from a paper record because it:$s$, $s$["It has no confidentiality rules attached to it", "It can be used by authorised staff in many places at once", "It never needs any backup or protection from loss", "It cannot be audited or checked after it is written"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It can be used by authorised staff in many places at once
A computer-based patient record can be viewed by authorised staff in MANY PLACES AT THE SAME TIME (ward, lab, pharmacy, clinic). Unlike paper notes, it is always legible, easy to search, can produce reports and graphs, can give alerts (allergies, abnormal results), and keeps an audit trail of who did what.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It has no confidentiality rules attached to it — Electronic records have STRICT confidentiality rules (passwords, access levels).
• It never needs any backup or protection from loss — They need regular backup and protection from loss and cyber-attack.
• It cannot be audited or checked after it is written — They can be audited; the system records every entry and change.

⭐ Key point: Advantages: legibility, availability, fewer errors, data analysis. Risks: system failure, hacking, privacy breaches, and copy-paste errors.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q55$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1962, $s$Data standardisation in nursing informatics is important because it:$s$, $s$["It prevents any coding of nursing diagnoses", "It reduces the need for any documentation", "It lets data be compared, shared and analysed", "It increases the use of free-text descriptions"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It lets data be compared, shared and analysed
Data standardisation means everyone uses the same terms and codes for the same thing (for example NANDA-I for nursing diagnoses, NIC for interventions, NOC for outcomes). When data are standardised, they can be COMPARED between wards and hospitals, SHARED between computer systems, and ANALYSED for research, quality improvement and planning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It prevents any coding of nursing diagnoses — Standardisation ENABLES coding of nursing diagnoses; it does not prevent it.
• It reduces the need for any documentation — It doesn't reduce the need to document; it makes documentation more useful.
• It increases the use of free-text descriptions — It REDUCES free text in favour of standard terms.

⭐ Key point: Standard languages make nursing work visible in data and help show the impact of nursing care on outcomes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q56$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1963, $s$A 'virus' in computing is:$s$, $s$["An input device such as a scanner", "A malicious program that damages files", "A cracked or broken computer screen", "A type of portable storage disk"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A malicious program that damages files
A computer virus is a MALICIOUS program (malware) that attaches itself to files or programs, copies itself, spreads to other computers, and can damage or delete files, steal data or slow the system. In hospitals, viruses and ransomware can lock patient records and disrupt care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An input device such as a scanner — A scanner is an input device.
• A cracked or broken computer screen — A broken screen is a hardware fault, not a virus.
• A type of portable storage disk — A portable storage disk is a device, although it can CARRY viruses.

⭐ Key point: Protect hospital systems: up-to-date antivirus software, no unknown USB drives, don't open suspicious email attachments or links, and keep regular backups.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q57$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1964, $s$A gigabyte (GB) is approximately:$s$, $s$["1 terabyte", "1000 megabytes", "100 kilobytes", "10 bytes"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1000 megabytes
A gigabyte (GB) is about 1000 megabytes (strictly 1024 MB). Storage units increase by about 1000 at each step: 1 KB ≈ 1000 bytes, 1 MB ≈ 1000 KB, 1 GB ≈ 1000 MB, 1 TB ≈ 1000 GB.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1 terabyte — 1 terabyte is about 1000 GB, much bigger than a gigabyte.
• 100 kilobytes — 100 kilobytes is far smaller than a gigabyte.
• 10 bytes — 10 bytes is tiny, just a few characters.

⭐ Key point: Order: bit, byte (8 bits), kilobyte, megabyte, gigabyte, terabyte. A typical X-ray image is a few megabytes; a CT study can be several hundred megabytes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q58$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1965, $s$The main effect of ICT on quality nursing care in ICU is:$s$, $s$["Increasing the paperwork for every patient", "Reducing the need for consent from patients", "Faster access to accurate information, fewer errors", "Removing the need for nurses at the bedside"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Faster access to accurate information, fewer errors
ICT improves nursing care mainly by giving FASTER access to ACCURATE information (results, records, drug information) and by reducing errors through electronic charting, barcode scanning, smart pumps, alerts and decision support. Nurses can spend less time searching for information and more time on care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increasing the paperwork for every patient — Well-designed ICT reduces paperwork, not increases it.
• Reducing the need for consent from patients — Consent is still required; ICT doesn't change patients' rights.
• Removing the need for nurses at the bedside — Nurses are still needed at the bedside; ICT supports their work.

⭐ Key point: ICT supports, but never replaces, clinical judgement and the nurse–patient relationship.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q59$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1966, $s$Real-time (online) processing is used in ICU for:$s$, $s$["Annual ICU budget planning", "Continuous monitoring of vital signs", "Yearly staff appraisal reports", "Monthly salary calculations"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Continuous monitoring of vital signs
Real-time (online) processing handles data IMMEDIATELY as it arrives. ICU monitors process ECG, BP and SpO₂ continuously, show the values instantly, and raise alarms within seconds. This is essential for patient safety, because changes need immediate action.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Annual ICU budget planning — Annual budget planning uses batch processing (data processed together later).
• Yearly staff appraisal reports — Yearly staff appraisals are not real-time processing.
• Monthly salary calculations — Monthly salary calculations are classic BATCH processing.

⭐ Key point: Real-time = instant (monitoring, alarms, online booking). Batch = grouped and processed later (payroll, monthly reports).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q60$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1967, $s$Selye's General Adaptation Syndrome has three stages in order:$s$, $s$["Exhaustion, alarm, resistance", "Alarm, resistance, exhaustion", "Shock, denial, acceptance", "Resistance, alarm, exhaustion"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Alarm, resistance, exhaustion
Hans Selye described the General Adaptation Syndrome (GAS) as three stages, in order. 1) ALARM: the stressor triggers fight or flight (adrenaline and cortisol). 2) RESISTANCE: the body adapts and tries to cope with the continuing stress. 3) EXHAUSTION: if stress continues, resources run out and illness, burnout or death can follow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Exhaustion, alarm, resistance — Exhaustion comes LAST, not first.
• Shock, denial, acceptance — Shock, denial and acceptance are stages of grief (Kübler-Ross type ideas), not GAS.
• Resistance, alarm, exhaustion — Resistance comes SECOND, after alarm.

⭐ Key point: Critically ill patients, their families and ICU staff can all reach the exhaustion stage. Recognise it and give support.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q64$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1968, $s$During the alarm stage of GAS, the body shows:$s$, $s$["Reduced cortisol and adrenaline secretion", "Bradycardia, hypotension and low blood glucose", "Deep sleep, relaxation and slow breathing", "Sympathetic activation: high HR, BP and glucose"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sympathetic activation: high HR, BP and glucose
In the ALARM stage, the sympathetic nervous system and adrenal glands release adrenaline, noradrenaline and cortisol. This prepares the body for "fight or flight": fast heart rate, high BP, fast breathing, dilated pupils, high blood glucose (energy), and blood diverted to the muscles.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reduced cortisol and adrenaline secretion — Cortisol and adrenaline go UP, not down.
• Bradycardia, hypotension and low blood glucose — The heart rate, BP and glucose go UP, not down.
• Deep sleep, relaxation and slow breathing — The body becomes alert and aroused, not relaxed and sleepy.

⭐ Key point: In ICU, the stress response causes high glucose (stress hyperglycaemia), fluid retention and muscle breakdown. That is why glucose is monitored and controlled.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q65$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1969, $s$The most important need reported by families of ICU patients is:$s$, $s$["Unlimited visiting at night for all relatives", "Free meals in the hospital canteen each day", "Honest, understandable information about the patient", "A private waiting room with a television only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Honest, understandable information about the patient
Research on the needs of families in ICU (Molter's study, and Leske's Critical Care Family Needs Inventory) consistently shows the most important needs are ASSURANCE (hope, knowing the patient gets the best care) and INFORMATION: honest, understandable, regular updates about the patient's condition, treatment and likely outcome.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Unlimited visiting at night for all relatives — Unlimited night visiting is valued but ranks lower than information.
• Free meals in the hospital canteen each day — Free meals are a comfort need and rank low.
• A private waiting room with a television only — A comfortable waiting room is a comfort need and ranks low.

⭐ Key point: Meet family needs: update them at least daily, use plain language, name a contact person, involve them in care, and let them see the patient as much as possible.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q66$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1970, $s$Coping mechanisms that families use in crisis include:$s$, $s$["Seeking information, spiritual and social support", "Avoiding visiting the patient completely", "Ignoring all the medical and nursing staff", "Refusing all information about the patient"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Seeking information, spiritual and social support
Healthy coping mechanisms for families in crisis include SEEKING INFORMATION (understanding helps them feel in control), SPIRITUAL support (prayer, faith leaders), and SOCIAL support (friends, extended family, community). Nurses should encourage and reinforce these positive strategies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Avoiding visiting the patient completely — Avoiding visiting completely is a sign of poor coping (withdrawal).
• Ignoring all the medical and nursing staff — Ignoring staff blocks communication and information.
• Refusing all information about the patient — Refusing all information is denial, a negative coping response.

⭐ Key point: Ask families what helps them, provide information regularly, and offer chaplaincy and social support services.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q78$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1971, $s$The most appropriate ICU nurse-patient ratio for a Level 2 (HDU) patient is:$s$, $s$["1:4", "1:8", "1:2", "1:1"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1:2
A Level 2 (High Dependency) patient needs more observation than a ward patient but less than a fully ventilated ICU patient, for example single-organ support or close monitoring after major surgery. The recommended nurse-to-patient ratio is 1:2 (one nurse for two patients). Level 3 (ICU) patients need 1:1.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1:4 — 1:4 is more like a general ward or Level 1 care.
• 1:8 — 1:8 is far too few nurses for HDU patients.
• 1:1 — 1:1 is for Level 3 (ventilated or multi-organ support) patients.

⭐ Key point: Levels of care: Level 0 (ward), Level 1 (ward with extra support), Level 2 (HDU, 1:2), Level 3 (ICU, 1:1).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q162$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1972, $s$An ICU equipment inventory is used to:$s$, $s$["Track equipment, maintenance and replacement", "Document drug errors and near misses", "Plan annual leave for the nursing staff", "Record patient diagnoses and outcomes"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Track equipment, maintenance and replacement
An equipment inventory is a list of all the unit's equipment (ventilators, monitors, pumps, defibrillators) with their serial numbers, location, maintenance and service history, and replacement dates. It helps the manager TRACK equipment, plan MAINTENANCE, and plan REPLACEMENT of old or broken items.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Document drug errors and near misses — Drug errors are recorded on incident forms.
• Plan annual leave for the nursing staff — Annual leave is planned on the roster.
• Record patient diagnoses and outcomes — Patient diagnoses and outcomes are recorded in patient records.

⭐ Key point: Planned preventive maintenance by biomedical engineering keeps equipment safe. Never use equipment that is overdue for service.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q171$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1973, $s$The arterial transducer is levelled at the:$s$, $s$["The level of the wrist where the line sits", "The sternal notch with the patient sitting up", "Phlebostatic axis (4th ICS, mid-axillary line)", "The umbilicus with the patient lying flat"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Phlebostatic axis (4th ICS, mid-axillary line)
The arterial (and CVP) transducer must be levelled at the PHLEBOSTATIC AXIS: the 4th intercostal space, mid-axillary line. This is the level of the right atrium, the reference point for haemodynamic pressures. Zero the transducer to air at this level.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The level of the wrist where the line sits — The wrist level is wrong; the reference is the heart.
• The sternal notch with the patient sitting up — The sternal notch is too high, giving falsely LOW readings.
• The umbilicus with the patient lying flat — The umbilicus is not the reference point.

⭐ Key point: If the transducer is too LOW, readings are falsely HIGH. If it is too HIGH, readings are falsely LOW. Re-level whenever the patient's position changes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q25$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1974, $s$Equipment failure during use in ICU should be:$s$, $s$["Ignored until it breaks down completely", "Reported, labelled out of use, sent for repair", "Used carefully anyway until replaced", "Hidden in a store to avoid blame"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reported, labelled out of use, sent for repair
If equipment fails during use, first make the PATIENT safe (switch to backup equipment). Then REPORT the fault (incident form), LABEL the equipment "Out of use – faulty" so no one else uses it, and send it to biomedical engineering for REPAIR. This prevents harm to other patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignored until it breaks down completely — Ignoring faults risks serious harm.
• Used carefully anyway until replaced — Using faulty equipment is unsafe.
• Hidden in a store to avoid blame — Hiding faults is dishonest and dangerous.

⭐ Key point: Report equipment incidents, even near misses, so patterns can be found and fixed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q171$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1975, $s$As a critical care nurse working in ICU, you know that the MOST important equipment needed for patient care is:$s$, $s$["Arterial blood gases analyser", "Mechanical ventilator", "Defibrillator", "Bed side cardiac monitor"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mechanical ventilator
The MECHANICAL VENTILATOR is often seen as the most essential piece of ICU equipment, because it directly SUSTAINS LIFE in patients who can't breathe adequately on their own. Most ICU patients need some form of respiratory support, and the ventilator defines intensive care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Arterial blood gases analyser — An ABG analyser is important for monitoring but doesn't keep the patient alive.
• Defibrillator — A defibrillator is vital in emergencies but isn't used continuously.
• Bed side cardiac monitor — A cardiac monitor watches the patient but doesn't support life.

⭐ Key point: All ICU equipment matters. Every ventilated patient also needs a bag-valve-mask and oxygen at the bedside.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q172$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1976, $s$For a mechanically ventilated level 3 ICU patient, the recommended nurse-to-patient ratio is:$s$, $s$["1:4", "1:6", "1:1", "1:8"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1:1
A Level 3 ICU patient (on a ventilator, or with support of two or more organs) needs continuous, intensive monitoring and care, so the recommended nurse-to-patient ratio is 1:1 (one nurse per patient).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1:4 — 1:4 is typical of a general ward.
• 1:6 — 1:6 is too few nurses for ICU.
• 1:8 — 1:8 is far too few.

⭐ Key point: Level 2 (HDU) patients are usually nursed 1:2.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q176 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1977, $s$Which equipment management method best keeps ICU ventilators reliable?$s$, $s$["Planned preventive maintenance on a schedule", "Lending ventilators without records", "Cleaning them only at the end of the year", "Repairing machines only after they break"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Planned preventive maintenance on a schedule
PLANNED PREVENTIVE MAINTENANCE (PPM) means servicing equipment (ventilators, monitors, pumps) on a regular SCHEDULE by trained biomedical engineers, BEFORE it fails. This keeps equipment reliable and safe, reduces breakdowns, and extends its life.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lending ventilators without records — Lending without records loses track of equipment and maintenance.
• Cleaning them only at the end of the year — Cleaning only once a year is unsafe and poor infection control.
• Repairing machines only after they break — Repairing only after breakdown means failures can happen during patient care.

⭐ Key point: Keep an equipment register with service dates, and never use equipment overdue for maintenance.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q180$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1978, $s$A patient's vital sign trends are monitored continuously, and the information is used to adjust nursing interventions in real time. This best illustrates which systems theory concept?$s$, $s$["Feedback", "Equifinality", "Entropy", "Suprasystem"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Feedback
In systems theory, FEEDBACK is information about the system's OUTPUT that is fed back to adjust the INPUT or process. Continuously monitoring vital signs (output) and using the information to adjust nursing care (input) is a feedback loop that keeps the patient stable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Equifinality — Equifinality means a system can reach the same end state by different routes.
• Entropy — Entropy means a system tends towards disorder.
• Suprasystem — A suprasystem is a larger system containing smaller ones.

⭐ Key point: Negative feedback keeps systems stable (homeostasis). Nursing uses feedback through constant reassessment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q185$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1979, $s$A nurse is concerned about data security when accessing patient records on a computer. Which of the following is a fundamental principle of data security in healthcare?$s$, $s$["Using a single, shared password for all computers", "Allowing all staff members to have full access to all patient records", "Implementing strong passwords, encryption, and access controls", "Ensuring all data is stored on a single, non-networked device"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Implementing strong passwords, encryption, and access controls
Healthcare data security depends on several layers of protection: STRONG, individual PASSWORDS (never shared), ENCRYPTION (scrambling data so only authorised people can read it), and ACCESS CONTROLS (staff can see only what they need for their role). Logging out and audit trails also help.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Using a single, shared password for all computers — A shared password removes accountability and security.
• Allowing all staff members to have full access to all patient records — Giving all staff access to all records breaches confidentiality.
• Ensuring all data is stored on a single, non-networked device — A single non-networked device risks loss and prevents proper use.

⭐ Key point: Report suspected data breaches immediately, and never leave a computer logged in and unattended.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q189$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1980, $s$A nurse is discussing the importance of data storage with a patient. What is the primary function of a hard drive in a computer?$s$, $s$["To store data and programs permanently", "To display visual information", "To provide temporary working memory", "To execute computer programs"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: To store data and programs permanently
A HARD DRIVE (hard disk) is a STORAGE device that keeps data and programs PERMANENTLY, even when the computer is switched off (non-volatile). It stores the operating system, applications and files.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To display visual information — Displaying information is the monitor's job.
• To provide temporary working memory — Temporary working memory is RAM.
• To execute computer programs — Executing programs is the CPU's job.

⭐ Key point: Storage types: hard disk drive (HDD), solid-state drive (SSD), USB flash drives, and cloud storage. Back up important data regularly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q190$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1981, $s$A nurse is documenting patient information and notices that the file size is very large. The file size is measured in kilobytes (KB), but the nurse needs to know how many bytes are in one kilobyte. The correct answer is:$s$, $s$["100 bytes", "1,024 bytes", "1,000 bytes", "10,000 bytes"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1,024 bytes
In computing, a KILOBYTE (KB) is traditionally 1,024 BYTES (2 to the power of 10), because computers work in binary (base 2). Similarly, 1 MB = 1,024 KB and 1 GB = 1,024 MB. (In some modern standards, 1 KB = 1,000 bytes, but 1,024 is the traditional answer.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100 bytes — 100 bytes is too small.
• 1,000 bytes — 1,000 bytes is the decimal approximation, but the binary answer is 1,024.
• 10,000 bytes — 10,000 bytes is too large.

⭐ Key point: 1 byte = 8 bits. Bit → byte → KB → MB → GB → TB.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q194$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1982, $s$A 35 year old male is presented to ER with Necrotic Diabetic foot. The physician advised for admission. In which level of care the patient will be admitted?$s$, $s$["Level 1", "Level 0", "Level 2", "Level 3"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Level 1
A patient with a NECROTIC DIABETIC FOOT needs hospital care (antibiotics, glucose control, wound care, possible surgery), but without organ failure, they don't need ICU or HDU. This fits LEVEL 1 care: ward care with extra observation and support (they may deteriorate, for example into sepsis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Level 0 — Level 0 is routine ward care without extra monitoring.
• Level 2 — Level 2 (HDU) is for single organ support.
• Level 3 — Level 3 (ICU) is for advanced organ support.

⭐ Key point: If the patient develops sepsis with organ dysfunction, escalate to Level 2 or 3.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q94$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1983, $s$It is a specially staffed and equipped area of a hospital that provides a level of care intermediate between intensive care and general ward care. The critical care nurse knows this is:$s$, $s$["Critical care unit", "General ward", "Post anaesthesia recovery unit", "High dependency unit"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: High dependency unit
A HIGH DEPENDENCY UNIT (HDU, Level 2 care) is a specially staffed and equipped area that provides care INTERMEDIATE between intensive care and the general ward. It is for patients needing closer monitoring or single-organ support (such as NIV), with a nurse-to-patient ratio of about 1:2.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Critical care unit — A critical care (intensive care) unit provides Level 3 care (1:1).
• General ward — A general ward provides Level 0–1 care.
• Post anaesthesia recovery unit — A post-anaesthesia recovery unit is for immediate recovery after surgery.

⭐ Key point: HDUs help step patients down from ICU and prevent deteriorating ward patients needing ICU.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q171$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1984, $s$During electronic health documentation, which computer part allows a critical care nurse to enter patient data?$s$, $s$["Input device", "Software", "Operating system", "Output device"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Input device
An INPUT DEVICE lets the user enter data into the computer: a keyboard, mouse, touchscreen, scanner (barcode), microphone. During electronic documentation, the nurse uses input devices to enter patient data.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Software — Software is the programs, not a computer part for entering data.
• Operating system — The operating system manages the computer; it isn't an input device.
• Output device — Output devices display or print data (monitor, printer).

⭐ Key point: Input → Processing (CPU) → Output → Storage.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q194$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1985, $s$Conflict arising from unclear or overlapping job expectations between two staff members is known as:$s$, $s$["Distributive conflict", "Role conflict", "Intrapersonal conflict", "Intragroup conflict"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Role conflict
ROLE CONFLICT arises when job expectations are UNCLEAR, OVERLAPPING or contradictory, so two staff members don't know who is responsible for what, or are expected to do incompatible things. Clear job descriptions and role definitions help prevent it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Distributive conflict — Distributive conflict is about dividing limited resources.
• Intrapersonal conflict — Intrapersonal conflict is within one person (inner conflict).
• Intragroup conflict — Intragroup conflict is between members of the same group, often over goals or personalities, not specifically unclear roles.

⭐ Key point: Resolve conflict early: clarify roles, communicate openly, and use collaborative problem-solving.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q168$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$B8 · ICU Equipment, Monitoring & ICT$s$, false, 1986, $s$A 'par level' stock system in ICU ensures that:$s$, $s$["Each nurse buys their own supplies", "Items are ordered only after they run out", "A minimum stock of each item is kept at all times", "Expired stock is used first"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A minimum stock of each item is kept at all times
A "PAR LEVEL" system sets a MINIMUM stock level for each item (drugs, consumables) in the unit. When stock falls to this level, it is reordered. This ensures items are ALWAYS AVAILABLE, preventing shortages of emergency drugs and supplies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Each nurse buys their own supplies — Nurses don't buy their own supplies.
• Items are ordered only after they run out — Ordering only after items run out causes dangerous shortages.
• Expired stock is used first — Using expired stock is unsafe; use the oldest in-date stock first (FIFO).

⭐ Key point: Check emergency trolley stock and expiry dates daily.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q178$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 1987, $s$A hallmark early clinical clue suggesting necrotising fasciitis, distinguishing it from simple cellulitis, is:$s$, $s$["Pain out of proportion to visible skin findings", "Mild, well-localised redness with minimal pain", "Slow onset over several weeks", "Complete absence of systemic symptoms"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pain out of proportion to visible skin findings
Necrotising fasciitis is a fast-spreading bacterial infection of the deep tissues (fascia) that destroys tissue under the skin before the skin itself looks very bad. The early classic clue is severe PAIN OUT OF PROPORTION to what you can see on the skin. Later signs include rapid spread, tense swelling, dusky or purple skin, blisters, crepitus (gas under the skin), numbness and sepsis. It is a surgical emergency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild, well-localised redness with minimal pain — Mild, well-localised redness with little pain fits simple cellulitis, not necrotising fasciitis.
• Slow onset over several weeks — Necrotising fasciitis spreads over HOURS, not weeks.
• Complete absence of systemic symptoms — Patients quickly become very unwell (fever, fast pulse, low BP, confusion); systemic symptoms are usually present.

⭐ Key point: Treatment is urgent surgical debridement (removing dead tissue) plus broad-spectrum IV antibiotics and sepsis care. Delay increases death and amputation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q126$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 1988, $s$Using the Parkland formula for a 70kg patient with 40% TBSA, total 24-hour fluid required is:$s$, $s$["2,800ml", "5,600ml", "11,200ml", "16,800ml"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 11,200ml
The PARKLAND FORMULA gives the total crystalloid (Ringer's lactate/Hartmann's) needed in the FIRST 24 HOURS after a burn: 4 mL × body weight (kg) × %TBSA burned. Here: 4 × 70 × 40 = 11,200 mL. Give HALF (5,600 mL) in the FIRST 8 HOURS FROM THE TIME OF BURN (not from arrival), and the other half (5,600 mL) over the next 16 hours. Titrate to URINE OUTPUT: 0.5 mL/kg/hour in adults (about 35 mL/h here), 1 mL/kg/h in children, 1–1.5 mL/kg/h in electrical burns. Superficial (first-degree) burns are not counted in TBSA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2,800ml — 2,800 mL = 1 × 70 × 40 — this uses 1 mL instead of 4 mL; far too little.
• 5,600ml — 5,600 mL is only the amount for the FIRST 8 HOURS (half), not the 24-hour total.
• 16,800ml — 16,800 mL = 6 × 70 × 40 — too much, risking fluid overload ("fluid creep").

⭐ Key point: Parkland = 4 mL × kg × %TBSA in 24 h; half in first 8 h from time of burn. 70 kg, 40% → 11,200 mL. Target urine 0.5 mL/kg/h.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q118$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 1989, $s$Using the Rule of Nines to estimate burn size in an adult, each entire arm represents approximately:$s$, $s$["18% of total body surface area", "9% of total body surface area", "1% of total body surface area", "36% of total body surface area"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 9% of total body surface area
The RULE OF NINES estimates the TOTAL BODY SURFACE AREA (TBSA) burned in ADULTS: HEAD and NECK 9%; EACH ARM 9% (4.5% front + 4.5% back); FRONT of the trunk 18%; BACK of the trunk 18%; EACH LEG 18% (9% front + 9% back); PERINEUM/genitalia 1%. Total = 100%. The patient's own PALM (including fingers) is about 1% TBSA — useful for small or patchy burns. In CHILDREN the head is relatively bigger and legs smaller, so the Lund and Browder chart is more accurate. Only partial- and full-thickness burns are counted.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 18% of total body surface area — 18% is EACH LEG, or the front or back of the trunk — not an arm.
• 1% of total body surface area — 1% is the PERINEUM or the patient's palm.
• 36% of total body surface area — 36% is the WHOLE trunk (front + back) or both legs — far too much for one arm.

⭐ Key point: Rule of Nines (adult): head 9, EACH ARM 9, each leg 18, front trunk 18, back trunk 18, perineum 1.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q121 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 1990, $s$A mass casualty incident (MCI) is defined as an event where:$s$, $s$["Patient needs exceed available resources, requiring altered standards of care", "Only one patient is critically injured", "Resources always exceed patient needs", "It occurs exclusively in rural areas"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Patient needs exceed available resources, requiring altered standards of care
A MASS CASUALTY INCIDENT (MCI) is any event (road crash, building collapse, explosion, mass shooting, chemical release, disease outbreak) where the NUMBER and severity of casualties EXCEEDS the RESOURCES available (staff, beds, equipment, blood, transport). Because there is not enough for everyone, the aim changes from "the best for each patient" to "the GREATEST GOOD for the GREATEST NUMBER" — so standards of care are ALTERED: rapid TRIAGE (e.g. START: red immediate, yellow delayed, green minor, black expectant/dead), simple life-saving interventions, and activation of the hospital major incident plan. The number of patients that defines an MCI depends on local capacity.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only one patient is critically injured — ONE critically injured patient is managed with normal resources — not an MCI.
• Resources always exceed patient needs — If resources always exceed needs, it is NOT an MCI by definition.
• It occurs exclusively in rural areas — MCIs can occur anywhere — cities, rural areas, hospitals.

⭐ Key point: MCI = patient needs EXCEED available resources → triage and altered standards ("greatest good for the greatest number").$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q126$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 1991, $s$The classic '5 Ps' used to assess for compartment syndrome are pain, pallor, paresthesia, pulselessness, and:$s$, $s$["Pruritus", "Paralysis", "Palpitations", "Polyuria"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Paralysis
COMPARTMENT SYNDROME occurs when pressure inside a closed muscle compartment (surrounded by tough fascia) rises so high that it cuts off blood flow to muscles and nerves — e.g. after fractures (tibia, forearm), crush injury, tight casts/bandages, circumferential burns, reperfusion. The classic "5 Ps": PAIN (out of proportion to the injury and worse on PASSIVE STRETCH of the muscles — the earliest and most important sign), PALLOR, PARAESTHESIA (tingling, numbness), PARALYSIS (late), PULSELESSNESS (very late — the pulse can still be present when muscle is dying). Some add POIKILOTHERMIA (cold limb) as a 6th P. Action: report immediately, loosen or split casts and dressings, keep the limb at heart level (do NOT elevate high), prepare for urgent FASCIOTOMY. Normal compartment pressure is under 10 mmHg; above 30 mmHg (or delta pressure < 30) needs fasciotomy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pruritus — PRURITUS (itching) is not one of the 5 Ps.
• Palpitations — PALPITATIONS are a heart symptom, not a sign of compartment syndrome.
• Polyuria — POLYURIA (passing a lot of urine) is unrelated; rhabdomyolysis may cause dark urine and AKI, not polyuria.

⭐ Key point: Compartment syndrome 5 Ps: Pain (on passive stretch — earliest), Pallor, Paraesthesia, PARALYSIS, Pulselessness (late). Needs fasciotomy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q129$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 1992, $s$The Curreri formula for nutritional requirements in burns patients is:$s$, $s$["25kcal×kg + 40kcal×%TBSA", "10kcal×kg only", "4ml×kg×%TBSA", "2000kcal fixed daily"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 25kcal×kg + 40kcal×%TBSA
Burn patients have a very high metabolic rate (hypermetabolism) and break down muscle quickly, so they need extra calories and protein for healing. The CURRERI FORMULA estimates daily calorie needs in adults: 25 kcal × body weight (kg) + 40 kcal × %TBSA burned. Example: 70 kg, 40% burns → (25 × 70) + (40 × 40) = 1,750 + 1,600 = 3,350 kcal/day. Protein needs are also high (about 1.5–2 g/kg/day). Early ENTERAL feeding (within 24 hours) is preferred to support the gut and reduce infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10kcal×kg only — 10 kcal × kg only is far too low and ignores burn size.
• 4ml×kg×%TBSA — 4 mL × kg × %TBSA is the PARKLAND formula for FLUID resuscitation, not calories.
• 2000kcal fixed daily — A fixed 2000 kcal/day ignores body weight and burn size; burned patients usually need much more.

⭐ Key point: Curreri formula = 25 kcal × kg + 40 kcal × %TBSA per day. Parkland (4 mL × kg × %TBSA) is for FLUIDS.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q130$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 1993, $s$The correct order of the ABCDE primary survey in trauma/emergency assessment is:$s$, $s$["Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability, Exposure", "Breathing, Airway, Disability, Circulation, Exposure", "Circulation, Airway, Breathing, Exposure, Disability", "Disability, Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Exposure"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Disability, Exposure
The ABCDE PRIMARY SURVEY assesses and treats life-threatening problems in the order they kill: A = AIRWAY (with cervical spine protection in trauma); B = BREATHING (ventilation and oxygenation); C = CIRCULATION (with haemorrhage control); D = DISABILITY (neurological status: GCS/AVPU, pupils, glucose); E = EXPOSURE (fully undress to look for injuries, while preventing hypothermia). Problems are treated as they are found before moving on.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Breathing, Airway, Disability, Circulation, Exposure — Breathing cannot be effective without an open airway, so airway comes first.
• Circulation, Airway, Breathing, Exposure, Disability — Circulation is not assessed before airway and breathing (except catastrophic haemorrhage in some military/pre-hospital systems — "C-ABCDE").
• Disability, Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Exposure — Disability is assessed after ABC.

⭐ Key point: Reassess ABCDE after every intervention and whenever the patient's condition changes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q134$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 1994, $s$All trauma patients undergoing emergency intubation should be treated as having a 'full stomach' primarily because:$s$, $s$["They have usually eaten recently", "Trauma, pain, and opioids delay gastric emptying, increasing aspiration risk", "It simplifies documentation", "It reduces the need for preoxygenation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Trauma, pain, and opioids delay gastric emptying, increasing aspiration risk
All trauma patients needing emergency intubation are treated as having a "FULL STOMACH" because TRAUMA, PAIN, STRESS and OPIOIDS DELAY GASTRIC EMPTYING, so food or fluid may still be in the stomach many hours after the last meal. This greatly increases the risk of ASPIRATION during induction. Rapid sequence induction (RSI) with cricoid pressure (where used) is performed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• They have usually eaten recently — The time since the last meal is often unknown and not the main reason.
• It simplifies documentation — It's not for documentation.
• It reduces the need for preoxygenation — Pre-oxygenation is still essential.

⭐ Key point: Have suction ready and avoid bag-mask ventilation with high pressures (which inflates the stomach).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q54$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 1995, $s$Escharotomy is indicated for a circumferential full-thickness burn of a limb when it:$s$, $s$["Causes only mild discomfort", "Compromises distal circulation, evidenced by diminished pulses or compartment pressure signs", "Is less than 24 hours old regardless of circulation", "Only affects superficial skin layers"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Compromises distal circulation, evidenced by diminished pulses or compartment pressure signs
An ESCHAROTOMY (cutting through the burned skin) is indicated when a CIRCUMFERENTIAL full-thickness burn of a limb COMPROMISES DISTAL CIRCULATION (diminished or absent pulses, cold, pale limb, increasing pain, rising compartment pressure). The tight eschar acts like a tourniquet as the tissue swells.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Causes only mild discomfort — Mild discomfort alone doesn't require escharotomy.
• Is less than 24 hours old regardless of circulation — Age of the burn alone isn't the indication.
• Only affects superficial skin layers — Superficial burns don't need escharotomy.

⭐ Key point: Circumferential chest burns may need escharotomy to allow breathing.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q134$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 1996, $s$A 75 kg man with 35% TBSA burns arrives 1 hour after the injury and has had no IV fluid. Using the Parkland formula, at what rate should the first half of the fluid run?$s$, $s$["750 mL/hr", "1,500 mL/hr", "656.2 mL/hr", "437.5 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 750 mL/hr
The PARKLAND FORMULA estimates burn fluid for the first 24 hours: 4 mL × WEIGHT (kg) × %TBSA. STEP 1 — Total: 4 × 75 × 35 = 10,500 mL. STEP 2 — HALF of this (5,250 mL) must be given in the first 8 HOURS counted FROM THE TIME OF THE BURN (not from arrival). STEP 3 — He arrived 1 hour after the burn with no fluid, so only 7 hours remain to give 5,250 mL. STEP 4 — Rate: 5,250 ÷ 7 = 750 mL/hr. The second half (5,250 mL) is then given over the next 16 hours (about 328 mL/hr).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1,500 mL/hr — 1,500 mL/hr is double the correct rate.
• 656.2 mL/hr — 656.2 mL/hr is 5,250 ÷ 8 — it ignores the hour already lost.
• 437.5 mL/hr — 437.5 mL/hr is 10,500 ÷ 24 — an even rate over the whole day, which under-resuscitates in the critical early hours.

⭐ Key point: Parkland is only a starting point — adjust the rate hourly to keep urine output at 0.5–1 mL/kg/hr in adults. Use Hartmann's (Ringer's lactate).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q2 · concept asked in 7 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 1997, $s$An adult has burns to the whole left leg and the perineum. Using the Rule of Nines, what is the TBSA burned?$s$, $s$["28%", "19%", "23.5%", "10%"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 19%
RULE OF NINES (adult): each WHOLE LEG = 18% (front 9% + back 9%); the PERINEUM = 1%. Here: whole left leg 18% + perineum 1% = 19% TBSA. A 19% burn in an adult needs IV fluid resuscitation, and the perineal burn needs specialist burns care because of high infection risk (contamination from urine and faeces) and the likely need for a urinary catheter.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 28% — 28% adds an extra 9%.
• 23.5% — 23.5% adds an extra 4.5%.
• 10% — 10% counts the leg as only 9%.

⭐ Key point: Do not include simple redness (superficial/first-degree burns) when calculating %TBSA for fluid resuscitation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q5 · concept asked in 11 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 1998, $s$Using the Parkland formula for a 65 kg patient with 35% TBSA burns, how much fluid should be given in the first 8 hours after the burn?$s$, $s$["9,100 mL", "2,275 mL", "3,033.3 mL", "4,550 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 4,550 mL
PARKLAND: Total 24-hour fluid = 4 × weight × %TBSA = 4 × 65 × 35 = 9,100 mL. HALF is given in the FIRST 8 HOURS from the time of the burn: 9,100 ÷ 2 = 4,550 mL. The other 4,550 mL is given over the next 16 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 9,100 mL — 9,100 mL is the whole 24-hour total.
• 2,275 mL — 2,275 mL is a quarter.
• 3,033.3 mL — 3,033.3 mL is a third.

⭐ Key point: Children also need maintenance fluid (with glucose) in addition to the Parkland volume.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q32 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 1999, $s$The 'lethal triad' that worsens bleeding and death after major trauma is:$s$, $s$["Hypotension, hypoxia and hypercapnia", "Tachycardia, hypotension and oliguria", "Hypothermia, hyperkalaemia and hypocalcaemia", "Hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy
The LETHAL TRIAD in trauma is HYPOTHERMIA, ACIDOSIS and COAGULOPATHY. They make each other worse: HYPOTHERMIA slows the enzymes involved in clotting and impairs platelets; ACIDOSIS (from poor tissue perfusion) further reduces clotting factor activity; COAGULOPATHY causes more bleeding, leading to more shock, more acidosis and more cooling. This vicious cycle leads to uncontrolled bleeding and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypotension, hypoxia and hypercapnia — Hypotension, hypoxia and hypercapnia are important, but they are not the lethal triad.
• Tachycardia, hypotension and oliguria — Tachycardia, hypotension and oliguria are signs of shock, not the lethal triad.
• Hypothermia, hyperkalaemia and hypocalcaemia — Hyperkalaemia and hypocalcaemia can occur after massive transfusion, but the triad is hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy.

⭐ Key point: Prevent: keep the patient warm (warm fluids, blankets, warm room), restore perfusion, give balanced blood products early (1:1:1), and control bleeding quickly. Some add hypocalcaemia as a fourth element ("lethal diamond").$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q146 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$);
