($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 2000, $s$A patient rescued from a fire involving burning foam mattresses and plastics is comatose and hypotensive, with lactate 14 mmol/L and a central venous oxygen saturation of 90% (very high). The most likely additional poisoning and antidote are:$s$, $s$["Cyanide — IV hydroxocobalamin", "Carbon monoxide — hyperbaric oxygen", "Methaemoglobinaemia — methylene blue", "Organophosphate — atropine"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cyanide — IV hydroxocobalamin
Burning plastics, foam and synthetic materials release HYDROGEN CYANIDE gas. CYANIDE stops cells from using oxygen (it blocks the mitochondrial enzyme cytochrome oxidase). Features: COMA, HYPOTENSION, very HIGH LACTATE (cells switch to anaerobic metabolism), and HIGH CENTRAL VENOUS OXYGEN SATURATION (oxygen is delivered but not used, so venous blood stays oxygen-rich). Antidote: IV HYDROXOCOBALAMIN (5 g), which binds cyanide to form harmless cyanocobalamin (vitamin B12).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Carbon monoxide — hyperbaric oxygen — CO poisoning is also likely in fires, but the very high ScvO2 and lactate point to cyanide.
• Methaemoglobinaemia — methylene blue — Methaemoglobinaemia causes chocolate-brown blood and low SpO2.
• Organophosphate — atropine — Organophosphate poisoning causes cholinergic symptoms (secretions, small pupils, bradycardia).

⭐ Key point: In smoke inhalation with high lactate (above 8–10 mmol/L), suspect cyanide and give hydroxocobalamin early. It turns urine and skin red.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q154$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 2001, $s$After a chest drain is inserted for haemothorax, the initial drainage is 1200 mL and the patient stabilises. The nurse should next:$s$, $s$["Clamp the drain for one hour to allow the blood to clot", "Remove the drain once the BP has been stable for 1 hour", "Record hourly drainage; report over 200 mL/hour for 2–4 hours", "Milk and strip the tube vigorously every 15 minutes"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Record hourly drainage; report over 200 mL/hour for 2–4 hours
After the first drainage of 1200 mL (below the 1500 mL massive threshold) and the patient is now stable, the nurse must watch for ONGOING BLEEDING. Record the drain output HOURLY and report if it is more than about 200 mL/hour for 2–4 hours (a sign of continued bleeding that may need thoracotomy). Also watch the vital signs, the colour of the drainage and the haemoglobin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Clamp the drain for one hour to allow the blood to clot — Clamping a drain in haemothorax lets blood build up in the chest, which can compress the lung and heart; the drain is not clamped to "let blood clot".
• Remove the drain once the BP has been stable for 1 hour — One hour of stable BP is far too soon; drains are removed when drainage is low (e.g. under 100–200 mL/day), there is no air leak and the X-ray shows the lung is expanded.
• Milk and strip the tube vigorously every 15 minutes — Vigorous milking and stripping create very high negative pressure that can damage the lung tissue; it is not routine practice. Gentle hand-over-hand squeezing may be used to clear clots if allowed by local policy.

⭐ Key point: Keep the drainage bottle below chest level, the system sealed, and the tubing free of kinks and loops.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q6$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 2002, $s$Tranexamic acid in major trauma is most effective when given:$s$, $s$["Only once the INR exceeds 3", "After 8 hours of injury", "Only after surgery is completed", "Within 3 hours of injury"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Within 3 hours of injury
TRANEXAMIC ACID (TXA) reduces clot breakdown. The CRASH-2 trial showed it reduces deaths from bleeding in trauma when given WITHIN 3 HOURS of injury — ideally within 1 hour. If given after 3 hours, it may be harmful.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only once the INR exceeds 3 — TXA is not dependent on INR; it is given early.
• After 8 hours of injury — After 8 hours is too late and possibly harmful.
• Only after surgery is completed — TXA is given as early as possible, not after surgery.

⭐ Key point: TXA dose: 1 g IV over 10 minutes, then 1 g over 8 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q30 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 2003, $s$A nurse is performing initial emergency care for a burn patient. The first priority is to:$s$, $s$["Cover the burned area with a dry sterile dressing", "Stop the burning process", "Administer pain medication", "Start fluid resuscitation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stop the burning process
The FIRST priority in burn care is to STOP THE BURNING PROCESS: remove the person from the source, put out flames ("stop, drop and roll"), remove burned or hot clothing and jewellery, switch off the electricity for electrical burns, and COOL the burn with cool running water for 20 minutes (within 3 hours of injury). This stops further tissue damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cover the burned area with a dry sterile dressing — Covering the wound comes after stopping the burn and cooling.
• Administer pain medication — Pain relief is important but comes after stopping the burn.
• Start fluid resuscitation — Fluid resuscitation comes after first aid and assessment.

⭐ Key point: After stopping the burn: ABCDE assessment, oxygen (smoke inhalation), estimate TBSA, start fluids, give analgesia, and cover with cling film.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q114$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 2004, $s$Systemic carbon monoxide poisoning in burns patients is best treated with:$s$, $s$["Room air only", "Humidified 100% oxygen", "Bronchodilators alone", "Antibiotics"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Humidified 100% oxygen
Carbon monoxide (CO) binds haemoglobin about 240 times more strongly than oxygen, forming carboxyhaemoglobin (COHb), which can't carry oxygen. Breathing 100% OXYGEN (humidified, via a non-rebreather mask or ETT) speeds CO removal: its half-life falls from about 4–5 hours on room air to about 1 hour on 100% oxygen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Room air only — Room air takes much longer to remove CO.
• Bronchodilators alone — Bronchodilators don't remove CO.
• Antibiotics — Antibiotics don't treat CO poisoning.

⭐ Key point: Pulse oximeters give FALSELY normal SpO₂ in CO poisoning. Measure COHb on a co-oximetry blood gas. Hyperbaric oxygen is used if available.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q21 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 2005, $s$The classic triad of fat embolism syndrome is:$s$, $s$["Hypotension, tachycardia, pallor", "Confusion, dyspnoea, petechiae", "Fever, cough, chest pain", "Jaundice, ascites, confusion"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Confusion, dyspnoea, petechiae
Fat embolism syndrome happens 24–72 hours after long bone or pelvic fractures. Fat droplets from the marrow travel to small vessels. The classic TRIAD: 1) DYSPNOEA (breathlessness and hypoxia, as fat blocks lung capillaries), 2) CONFUSION (neurological: drowsiness, agitation), and 3) PETECHIAE (a pinpoint rash on the chest, armpits and conjunctiva).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypotension, tachycardia, pallor — Low BP, fast pulse and pallor describe shock.
• Fever, cough, chest pain — Fever, cough and chest pain describe pneumonia.
• Jaundice, ascites, confusion — Jaundice, ascites and confusion describe liver failure.

⭐ Key point: Prevent with early fracture fixation. Treatment is supportive (oxygen, ventilation if needed).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q49$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, true, 2006, $s$A widened mediastinum after chest trauma raises suspicion of:$s$, $s$["Simple rib fracture", "Normal anatomical variant", "Minor bruising only", "Thoracic aortic disruption"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Thoracic aortic disruption
A WIDENED MEDIASTINUM (more than 8 cm on a chest X-ray) after chest trauma, especially a high-speed deceleration injury (car crash, fall), is the most important X-ray sign of THORACIC AORTIC DISRUPTION (a tear of the aorta). It is life-threatening and needs urgent CT angiography and surgical or endovascular repair.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple rib fracture — A simple rib fracture doesn't widen the mediastinum.
• Normal anatomical variant — After trauma, a wide mediastinum shouldn't be dismissed as normal.
• Minor bruising only — Minor bruising doesn't cause mediastinal widening.

⭐ Key point: Other signs: loss of the aortic knuckle, a deviated trachea or NG tube to the right, and left haemothorax. Keep SBP 100–120 until repair.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q102 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2007, $s$When intubating a patient with suspected cervical spine injury, the recommended technique to protect the spine is:$s$, $s$["Full neck extension for optimal glottic view", "Manual in-line stabilization with the anterior portion of the cervical collar removed during the procedure", "Flexion of the neck", "Leaving the patient fully upright"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Manual in-line stabilization with the anterior portion of the cervical collar removed during the procedure
In suspected cervical spine injury, the head and neck must not move during intubation. An assistant holds the head in line with the body, using both hands on each side (manual in-line stabilisation, MILS). The FRONT of the hard collar is opened or removed so the mouth can open wide enough for the laryngoscope. Video laryngoscopes help get a good view with less neck movement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Full neck extension for optimal glottic view — Full neck extension could worsen a spinal injury and cause paralysis.
• Flexion of the neck — Flexing the neck could also move an unstable fracture.
• Leaving the patient fully upright — Sitting the patient upright does not protect the spine and makes intubation harder.

⭐ Key point: Treat every major trauma patient as having a C-spine injury until it is cleared. Use jaw thrust (not head tilt) to open the airway.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q103$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2008, $s$A patient with pelvic fracture is at risk for:$s$, $s$["Fat embolism", "Massive hemorrhage", "Neurogenic shock", "A and B"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A and B
Pelvic fractures cause both. MASSIVE HAEMORRHAGE: the pelvis has large veins and arteries, and the pelvic space can hold several litres of blood. Unstable fractures (open book) can cause rapid shock and death. FAT EMBOLISM: fat from the bone marrow of large fractured bones (pelvis, femur) can enter the bloodstream and travel to the lungs, brain and skin, usually 24–72 hours later.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fat embolism — Fat embolism alone is only part of the answer.
• Massive hemorrhage — Massive haemorrhage alone is only part of the answer.
• Neurogenic shock — Neurogenic shock comes from spinal cord injury, not pelvic fracture.

⭐ Key point: Fat embolism triad: breathing difficulty (hypoxia), confusion, and a petechial rash (armpits, chest, conjunctiva). Early fixation of fractures reduces the risk.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q116$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2009, $s$A patient with multiple trauma has a pelvic fracture suspected on examination. The nurse should avoid which action that could worsen bleeding?$s$, $s$["Applying a pelvic binder as indicated", "Repeatedly 'springing' or manually testing pelvic stability, which can dislodge clots and worsen haemorrhage", "Monitoring vital signs closely", "Preparing for possible blood transfusion"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Repeatedly 'springing' or manually testing pelvic stability, which can dislodge clots and worsen haemorrhage
Repeatedly pressing or "springing" the pelvis to check if it is stable can move the broken bone ends, tear vessels and dislodge clots that are slowing the bleeding. This can turn a controlled bleed into a fatal one. If a pelvic fracture is suspected (from the mechanism or signs), apply a pelvic binder and get imaging; avoid repeated examinations.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Applying a pelvic binder as indicated — Applying a pelvic binder at the level of the greater trochanters is CORRECT; it reduces pelvic volume and bleeding.
• Monitoring vital signs closely — Monitoring vital signs is correct and essential.
• Preparing for possible blood transfusion — Preparing blood products is correct, because large blood loss is expected.

⭐ Key point: Also avoid log-rolling patients with a suspected pelvic fracture more than needed, and do not insert a urinary catheter if there is blood at the urethral meatus (urethral injury) until it is checked.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q117$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2010, $s$A patient with facial burns, singed nasal hair, and hoarseness of voice after a house fire should be closely monitored for:$s$, $s$["No specific concern beyond the visible burns", "Inhalational airway injury, with high risk of rapid airway obstruction", "Simple anxiety", "Only skin infection risk"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inhalational airway injury, with high risk of rapid airway obstruction
Facial burns, singed nasal hairs, soot in the mouth, a hoarse voice, cough with black sputum, and a history of a fire in an ENCLOSED space all suggest INHALATION INJURY. Heat and smoke cause swelling of the upper airway that can rapidly block it within hours, especially once IV fluids start. These patients need early assessment by an anaesthetist and often EARLY intubation before the airway closes. Also check for carbon monoxide and cyanide poisoning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No specific concern beyond the visible burns — There IS a specific concern: the airway can be lost quickly.
• Simple anxiety — Anxiety does not explain singed nasal hair and hoarseness.
• Only skin infection risk — Infection is a later risk; the immediate danger is airway obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Give 100% oxygen (also treats CO poisoning; SpO₂ is falsely normal in CO poisoning). Watch for stridor, which is a late, dangerous sign.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q119$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2011, $s$The following are indication of monitoring intra-abdominal pressure except:$s$, $s$["Shock requiring resuscitation.", "Damage control laparotomy", "Major burns \\> 25%", "Laparotomy closed with normal tension"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Laparotomy closed with normal tension
The question asks which is NOT an indication for monitoring intra-abdominal pressure. Measuring IAP (via the bladder) is recommended for patients at risk of intra-abdominal hypertension: shock needing large-volume resuscitation, damage control laparotomy, major burns (above 25% TBSA), severe pancreatitis, massive transfusion, and abdominal surgery closed under tension. A laparotomy closed with NORMAL tension is low risk, so it is not an indication.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shock requiring resuscitation. — Shock needing large fluid resuscitation IS an indication (the gut swells).
• Damage control laparotomy — Damage control laparotomy IS an indication (packs, swollen bowel).
• Major burns \> 25% — Major burns above 25% IS an indication (huge fluid volumes cause gut and wall oedema).

⭐ Key point: Normal IAP is 5–7 mmHg. IAH is 12 mmHg or more. Abdominal compartment syndrome is above 20 mmHg with new organ failure, which needs decompression.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q123$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2012, $s$The 'Golden Hour' concept in trauma care refers to:$s$, $s$["The first hour after hospital discharge", "The first 60 minutes after major trauma, during which rapid assessment and definitive care most improve survival", "The hour before surgery begins", "A 60-minute waiting period before treatment can start"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The first 60 minutes after major trauma, during which rapid assessment and definitive care most improve survival
The "golden hour" is the first 60 minutes after a major injury. During this time, rapid assessment (primary survey ABCDE), resuscitation, stopping bleeding and getting the patient to definitive care (surgery, CT, ICU) give the best chance of survival. Many trauma deaths from bleeding and airway problems are preventable if treated quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The first hour after hospital discharge — The time after hospital discharge has nothing to do with the golden hour.
• The hour before surgery begins — The hour before surgery is not the definition; the clock starts at the time of INJURY.
• A 60-minute waiting period before treatment can start — The golden hour is about acting FAST, not waiting.

⭐ Key point: Trauma deaths come in three peaks: immediate (unsurvivable), early (minutes to hours, the golden hour, preventable), and late (days to weeks, from sepsis and multi-organ failure).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q127$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2013, $s$A common complication of severe hypothermia is:$s$, $s$["Hyperactivity", "Increased body temperature", "Cardiac arrest (arrhythmias)", "Rapid breathing"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cardiac arrest (arrhythmias)
In severe hypothermia (core temperature below 28–30 °C), the heart becomes cold and very irritable. The heart rate slows, and the risk of arrhythmias rises sharply: atrial fibrillation, then VF, then asystole. Even moving the patient roughly can trigger VF. The ECG may show J (Osborn) waves. Cardiac arrest is the most serious complication.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperactivity — Hypothermia causes slowing and drowsiness, not hyperactivity.
• Increased body temperature — Hypothermia means LOW body temperature, by definition.
• Rapid breathing — Breathing becomes slow and shallow in severe hypothermia, not rapid.

⭐ Key point: Handle hypothermic patients gently, rewarm (warm fluids, forced-air warming, ECMO in severe cases), and remember "no one is dead until warm and dead". Defibrillation and drugs work poorly below 30 °C.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q128$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2014, $s$The Good Samaritan act covers:$s$, $s$["Residents of long-term care facilities", "Passerby in car accidents", "Protection from civil liability when emergency care is provided in good faith", "Nurses who choose not to provide care in an emergency"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Protection from civil liability when emergency care is provided in good faith
Good Samaritan laws protect people who give EMERGENCY care in GOOD FAITH, outside their job (such as at a roadside accident), from civil liability (being sued), as long as they act within their training, without payment, and without gross negligence. These laws encourage health workers and the public to help in emergencies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Residents of long-term care facilities — Residents of long-term care facilities are covered by care standards, not specifically this law.
• Passerby in car accidents — Passersby are covered only when they help in good faith; the law protects helpers, not people simply present.
• Nurses who choose not to provide care in an emergency — Refusing to help in an emergency is not protected, and it may raise ethical issues.

⭐ Key point: When helping at the roadside, stay within your skills, get consent if possible, hand over to emergency services, and document what you did afterwards.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q192$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2015, $s$Electrotherapy equipment includes:$s$, $s$["MRI", "Mammogram", "Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS)", "Spirometer"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS)
Electrotherapy uses electrical energy to treat patients, mainly for pain relief and muscle stimulation. TENS (transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation) sends mild electrical pulses through skin pads. These block pain signals (the "gate control" theory) and may release endorphins. It is used for pain after surgery, in labour and in chronic pain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• MRI — MRI is a diagnostic imaging scanner using magnets, not a therapy.
• Mammogram — A mammogram is an X-ray of the breast for diagnosis.
• Spirometer — A spirometer measures lung volumes and flow (a diagnostic or monitoring tool).

⭐ Key point: Do not use TENS over a pacemaker, the front of the neck, broken skin or the pregnant abdomen (except approved labour use), or in patients with epilepsy (over the head).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q200$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2016, $s$Gastric lavage is contraindicated in which of the following situations?$s$, $s$["General sedative overdose within 1 hour", "Corrosive/acid ingestion", "Barbiturate overdose while still conscious", "Within 2 hours of a large tablet overdose"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Corrosive/acid ingestion
GASTRIC LAVAGE (stomach washout through a large tube) is now rarely used. It is CONTRAINDICATED in CORROSIVE (acid or alkali) ingestion because: (1) the oesophagus and stomach walls are already burned and weak, so the tube can PERFORATE them; (2) washing can cause the patient to vomit, so the corrosive passes over the oesophagus again, causing more burns, and may be aspirated into the lungs. Other contraindications: hydrocarbon/petroleum ingestion (aspiration pneumonitis), unprotected airway (reduced consciousness without intubation), and bleeding risk. For corrosives: keep NPO, protect the airway, do NOT induce vomiting or neutralise, and arrange early endoscopy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• General sedative overdose within 1 hour — A sedative overdose within 1 hour was the traditional (though now limited) indication for lavage, not a contraindication, if the airway is protected.
• Barbiturate overdose while still conscious — Barbiturate overdose in a conscious patient within the time window is also an older indication, not a contraindication.
• Within 2 hours of a large tablet overdose — Within 2 hours of a large tablet overdose may still be a situation where lavage was considered; time alone is not the main contraindication.

⭐ Key point: Gastric lavage — NEVER in CORROSIVE (acid/alkali) or hydrocarbon ingestion, or without a protected airway.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q32$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2017, $s$A patient develops serotonin syndrome after being started on two serotonergic medications simultaneously. The nurse should recognise this presentation includes:$s$, $s$["Bradycardia and hypotension with no other findings", "Agitation, hyperthermia, tremor, and autonomic instability", "No clinical significance requiring only observation", "Improved mental status"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Agitation, hyperthermia, tremor, and autonomic instability
SEROTONIN SYNDROME is caused by too much serotonin activity in the nervous system, often from COMBINING serotonergic drugs: SSRIs/SNRIs, MAO inhibitors, tramadol, pethidine, linezolid, triptans, ondansetron, St John's wort. It usually starts within 24 hours of a new drug or dose change. Classic TRIAD: (1) MENTAL STATUS changes — agitation, confusion, restlessness; (2) AUTONOMIC instability — HYPERTHERMIA, tachycardia, high BP, sweating, dilated pupils, diarrhoea; (3) NEUROMUSCULAR signs — TREMOR, CLONUS (especially inducible ankle clonus), hyperreflexia, muscle rigidity. Treatment: STOP the drugs, supportive care, cooling, benzodiazepines, and cyproheptadine (serotonin antagonist); severe cases need ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bradycardia and hypotension with no other findings — BRADYCARDIA and HYPOTENSION are not typical; serotonin syndrome usually causes TACHYCARDIA and hypertension.
• No clinical significance requiring only observation — It is NOT harmless — severe cases cause very high fever, seizures, rhabdomyolysis and death, so it needs urgent action.
• Improved mental status — It causes WORSENING mental status (agitation, confusion), not improvement.

⭐ Key point: Serotonin syndrome = AGITATION + HYPERTHERMIA + TREMOR/CLONUS + autonomic instability. Stop the drugs; give cyproheptadine.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q33$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2018, $s$What percentage of chest injuries are managed WITHOUT surgery?$s$, $s$["20%", "50%", "80%", "100%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 80%
Most CHEST INJURIES — about 80–85% (often quoted as "over 80%" or "85–90%") — are managed WITHOUT major surgery (thoracotomy). The usual treatments are simple: oxygen, good PAIN RELIEF (so the patient can breathe deeply and cough), physiotherapy, and CHEST DRAIN (intercostal tube) insertion for pneumothorax or haemothorax. Only about 10–20% need thoracotomy — for example, massive haemothorax (over 1500 mL immediately or over 200 mL/hour), cardiac tamponade not relieved, major airway or great vessel injury.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20% — 20% is far too low; it would mean most chest injuries need surgery, which is not true.
• 50% — 50% is also too low.
• 100% — 100% is impossible — some chest injuries (e.g. massive bleeding, great vessel injury) definitely need surgery.

⭐ Key point: About 80% of chest injuries need NO thoracotomy — just oxygen, analgesia, physiotherapy and chest drains.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q53$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2019, $s$Special areas of the body that require CCU-level attention in burns include all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Face", "Hands and feet", "Perineum and genitalia", "Upper back"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Upper back
Burns to certain SPECIAL AREAS need specialist (burns unit/critical care) referral even if they are small, because they carry high risk of disability, infection or airway problems: the FACE (airway swelling, eyes, appearance), HANDS and FEET (function, contractures), PERINEUM and GENITALIA (infection, urinary function), and MAJOR JOINTS (contractures). Also circumferential burns of limbs or chest (compartment syndrome, restricted breathing), inhalation injury, electrical and chemical burns. The UPPER BACK has no special functional or airway importance — burns there are assessed by size and depth in the normal way.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Face — FACE burns ARE special — risk of airway oedema and eye injury.
• Hands and feet — HANDS and FEET burns ARE special — risk of loss of function and contractures.
• Perineum and genitalia — PERINEUM and GENITALIA burns ARE special — infection risk and difficult care.

⭐ Key point: Special burn areas: FACE, HANDS, FEET, PERINEUM/GENITALIA, MAJOR JOINTS (plus circumferential, inhalation, electrical, chemical).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q117$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2020, $s$In multidisciplinary management of bladder trauma, which specialist is usually NOT involved?$s$, $s$["Orthopaedic surgeon", "Radiologist", "Urologist", "Oncologist"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Oncologist
BLADDER TRAUMA usually happens with PELVIC FRACTURES (blunt trauma, e.g. road accidents) or penetrating injuries. Management is multidisciplinary: the UROLOGIST repairs the bladder (intraperitoneal rupture needs surgery; extraperitoneal is often managed with a catheter); the RADIOLOGIST performs and interprets CT cystography/retrograde cystogram to diagnose the injury; the ORTHOPAEDIC surgeon stabilises the PELVIC FRACTURE; plus trauma/general surgeons, ICU and nurses. An ONCOLOGIST treats CANCER and has no role in acute bladder trauma.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Orthopaedic surgeon — The ORTHOPAEDIC surgeon IS involved because most bladder injuries come with pelvic fractures.
• Radiologist — The RADIOLOGIST IS involved — imaging confirms the diagnosis and type of rupture.
• Urologist — The UROLOGIST IS the main specialist who repairs the bladder.

⭐ Key point: Bladder trauma team = urologist, radiologist, orthopaedic (pelvic fracture), trauma surgeon. Oncologist = cancer, not trauma.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q124$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2021, $s$In a mass casualty incident involving burns, triage prioritisation typically considers:$s$, $s$["Burn size alone, ignoring airway status", "Total body surface area burned together with airway involvement and other injuries", "Only the patient's age", "Cosmetic appearance of the burn"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Total body surface area burned together with airway involvement and other injuries
In a MASS CASUALTY BURN incident, triage must consider: (1) the SIZE of the burn (% TBSA) — larger burns need more fluid, staff and beds; (2) AIRWAY/INHALATION INJURY — singed nasal hair, soot in mouth, hoarse voice, stridor, burns in an enclosed space; these patients can lose their airway within hours and need early intubation; (3) OTHER INJURIES (fractures, head injury, blast injury) and (4) AGE and comorbidities — together these predict survival (e.g. Baux score = age + %TBSA). Patients with survivable burns but airway risk get high priority; those with very large burns plus inhalation injury and very low survival chance may be triaged as "expectant" when resources are limited.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Burn size alone, ignoring airway status — Using burn SIZE ALONE and ignoring the airway is dangerous; airway burns kill quickly.
• Only the patient's age — Age ALONE is not enough; size, airway and injuries matter too.
• Cosmetic appearance of the burn — COSMETIC appearance has no role in emergency triage.

⭐ Key point: Burn triage = %TBSA + AIRWAY/inhalation injury + other injuries + age (Baux score).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q127$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2022, $s$A casualty who is walking and talking at a disaster scene, with only minor injuries, is typically triaged as:$s$, $s$["Immediate (red)", "Delayed (yellow)", "Minor (green)", "Deceased (black)"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Minor (green)
In disaster triage (e.g. START triage), the first step is: "Everyone who can WALK, move over here." Casualties who are WALKING and TALKING with only MINOR injuries are tagged GREEN — MINOR ("walking wounded"). They can safely wait for treatment and may even help others. Categories: RED — IMMEDIATE (life-threatening but survivable with quick treatment — e.g. airway problem, respiratory rate over 30, capillary refill over 2 seconds/no radial pulse, cannot follow commands); YELLOW — DELAYED (serious injuries but stable, cannot walk, can wait a few hours); GREEN — MINOR; BLACK — DECEASED or EXPECTANT (not breathing after airway opening, or unsurvivable injuries with limited resources).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate (red) — IMMEDIATE (red) is for life-threatening, time-critical injuries — not walking patients with minor injuries.
• Delayed (yellow) — DELAYED (yellow) is for serious but stable injuries in patients who usually cannot walk.
• Deceased (black) — DECEASED (black) is for patients who are dead or not expected to survive.

⭐ Key point: START triage: walking + talking + minor injuries = GREEN (minor). Red immediate, yellow delayed, black deceased/expectant.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q131$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2023, $s$The secondary survey includes:$s$, $s$["AMPLE history", "Head-to-toe assessment", "Vital signs", "All of the above"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
In trauma care, the PRIMARY SURVEY (ABCDE) finds and treats immediate life threats. Once the patient is stabilised, the SECONDARY SURVEY is done — a complete re-assessment that includes: (1) AMPLE HISTORY — Allergies, Medications, Past medical history/Pregnancy, Last meal, Events/Environment of injury; (2) a full HEAD-TO-TOE examination (head, face, neck, chest, abdomen, pelvis, perineum, limbs, back with log-roll, neurological exam) to find all injuries; (3) repeated VITAL SIGNS to detect deterioration; plus investigations (X-rays, CT, bloods). If the patient deteriorates at any time, go back to the primary survey.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• AMPLE history — AMPLE history alone is only one part of the secondary survey.
• Head-to-toe assessment — Head-to-toe assessment alone is only one part.
• Vital signs — Vital signs alone are only one part.

⭐ Key point: Secondary survey = AMPLE history + HEAD-TO-TOE exam + repeated VITAL SIGNS (all of the above).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q132$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2024, $s$Which trace element is a cofactor essential for wound healing, immune function, and taste perception, and is often supplemented in patients with large wounds or burns?$s$, $s$["Zinc", "Chromium", "Selenium", "Molybdenum"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Zinc
ZINC is a trace element essential for WOUND HEALING: it is a cofactor for many enzymes involved in cell division, protein and COLLAGEN synthesis, and tissue repair. It also supports IMMUNE FUNCTION (T-cell function), and TASTE and smell perception (zinc deficiency causes loss of taste — hypogeusia). Patients with large WOUNDS, BURNS, pressure ulcers, diarrhoea/fistulas, or malnutrition lose zinc and often receive supplements (e.g. zinc sulphate). Signs of deficiency: poor wound healing, hair loss, skin rash (around the mouth and anus), diarrhoea, poor appetite and taste, frequent infections. Children with diarrhoea are also given zinc (WHO: ORS + zinc for 10–14 days).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chromium — CHROMIUM helps insulin action (glucose metabolism), not mainly wound healing and taste.
• Selenium — SELENIUM is an ANTIOXIDANT (glutathione peroxidase) and thyroid hormone cofactor; it does not mainly affect taste.
• Molybdenum — MOLYBDENUM is a cofactor for a few enzymes (e.g. xanthine oxidase); deficiency is very rare.

⭐ Key point: ZINC = wound healing, immunity, TASTE. Supplement in burns, large wounds, diarrhoea.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q195$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2025, $s$Keto-adaptation, which spares muscle during starvation, fails to protect muscle in:$s$, $s$["Simple fasting", "Sepsis and trauma", "Healthy individuals on a diet", "It never fails"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sepsis and trauma
In SIMPLE STARVATION (fasting), the body adapts: insulin falls, glycogen is used up in about 24 hours, and then the liver makes KETONES from fat. After a few days the BRAIN switches to using ketones for fuel (KETO-ADAPTATION), so the body needs less glucose from protein breakdown — this SPARES MUSCLE. In SEPSIS, TRAUMA, burns and major surgery (STRESS/HYPERMETABOLIC states), stress hormones (cortisol, glucagon, catecholamines) and inflammatory cytokines drive continuous muscle protein breakdown, insulin resistance, and blocked ketone production — so keto-adaptation FAILS and MUSCLE WASTING continues rapidly. This is why critically ill patients lose muscle quickly and need early nutrition with adequate protein (1.2–2 g/kg/day).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple fasting — In SIMPLE FASTING keto-adaptation WORKS and protects muscle.
• Healthy individuals on a diet — In HEALTHY people on a diet, keto-adaptation also works normally.
• It never fails — It DOES fail — in sepsis and trauma — so "never fails" is wrong.

⭐ Key point: Keto-adaptation spares muscle in simple starvation but FAILS in SEPSIS and TRAUMA (stress hypermetabolism) → rapid muscle loss.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q197$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2026, $s$A widened mediastinum on chest X-ray after trauma is most suggestive of:$s$, $s$["Simple rib fracture", "Thoracic aortic disruption", "Pleural effusion", "Diaphragmatic hernia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Thoracic aortic disruption
After major blunt trauma (especially HIGH-SPEED DECELERATION — head-on collisions or falls from height), a WIDENED MEDIASTINUM on chest X-ray (more than about 8 cm on a supine AP film) suggests bleeding into the mediastinum from a THORACIC AORTIC DISRUPTION (tear), most commonly at the aortic isthmus just beyond the left subclavian artery. Other X-ray clues: loss of the aortic knob, deviation of the trachea or NG tube to the right, left apical "cap", and fractures of the first/second ribs. Most patients with complete transection die at the scene; survivors have a contained tear that can rupture at any moment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple rib fracture — A simple rib fracture does not widen the mediastinum.
• Pleural effusion — A pleural effusion causes fluid in the pleural space, not mediastinal widening.
• Diaphragmatic hernia — A diaphragmatic hernia shows bowel or stomach in the chest.

⭐ Key point: Confirm with CT angiography; keep heart rate under 100 and systolic BP about 100–120 mmHg (beta-blocker), give analgesia, and arrange urgent surgical or endovascular repair.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q45 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2027, $s$A CSF leak is suspected from a patient's nose after head trauma. Which bedside test helps confirm CSF rather than mucus?$s$, $s$["Kernig's sign", "Doll's eye test", "Halo (ring) sign on gauze", "Babinski test"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Halo (ring) sign on gauze
After head trauma, clear fluid from the nose (rhinorrhoea) or ear (otorrhoea) may be CSF leaking through a skull base fracture. The HALO (RING) SIGN is a simple bedside test: a drop of the fluid is placed on gauze or filter paper. If CSF is present (mixed with blood), it separates into a central red/pink spot surrounded by a clear or yellowish "halo" ring, because CSF spreads further than blood. Confirmation is by testing for GLUCOSE (CSF contains glucose; mucus does not) or, more accurately, BETA-2 TRANSFERRIN.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Kernig's sign — Kernig's sign tests for meningeal irritation.
• Doll's eye test — The doll's eye test (oculocephalic reflex) assesses brainstem function.
• Babinski test — The Babinski test assesses upper motor neurone damage.

⭐ Key point: With a suspected CSF leak: avoid nasal tubes and nasal suction, tell the patient not to blow the nose, keep head elevated, and watch for meningitis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q94$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2028, $s$What musculoskeletal complication is common with chest compressions during CPR?$s$, $s$["Skull fracture", "Pelvic fracture", "Rib fractures and/or sternal fracture", "Femur fracture"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Rib fractures and/or sternal fracture
Effective CHEST COMPRESSIONS in adults must be 5–6 cm deep at 100–120 per minute. This force commonly causes RIB FRACTURES and/or STERNAL FRACTURES, especially in older people with brittle bones. Other possible injuries: costochondral separation, lung contusion, and (rarely) liver or spleen injury. These injuries are ACCEPTABLE because effective CPR is life-saving — never reduce compression depth because of fear of fractures.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Skull fracture — Skull fractures are not caused by chest compressions.
• Pelvic fracture — Pelvic fractures are not caused by chest compressions.
• Femur fracture — Femur fractures are not caused by chest compressions.

⭐ Key point: Correct hand position (centre of the chest, lower half of the sternum) reduces the risk of injury to abdominal organs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q104$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2029, $s$Infraglottic inhalational injury is mainly caused by:$s$, $s$["Direct heat", "Noxious chemical combustion products in smoke", "Blunt trauma", "Radiation exposure"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Noxious chemical combustion products in smoke
In INHALATION INJURY, the UPPER airway (above the vocal cords — supraglottic) absorbs most of the HEAT from hot gases (it is very efficient at cooling air), so direct thermal burns are usually limited to the upper airway. INFRAGLOTTIC (below the vocal cords) injury is mainly caused by NOXIOUS CHEMICAL COMBUSTION PRODUCTS in SMOKE — e.g. aldehydes, ammonia, sulphur dioxide, chlorine, and acids from burning plastics — which irritate and damage the lining of the trachea, bronchi and alveoli, causing bronchospasm, oedema, sloughing of the mucosa, and ARDS. (Steam is an exception — it carries heat lower.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Direct heat — Direct heat mainly injures the UPPER (supraglottic) airway.
• Blunt trauma — Blunt trauma is not the mechanism.
• Radiation exposure — Radiation exposure is not the mechanism.

⭐ Key point: Suspect inhalation injury with burns in an enclosed space, soot in the mouth or sputum, hoarseness, and facial burns.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q114$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2030, $s$Which of the following is not a cause of polytrauma?$s$, $s$["Bomb blast", "Fall from height", "Accident", "Infection"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Infection
POLYTRAUMA means multiple significant injuries to different body regions (e.g. head, chest, abdomen, limbs), usually from high-energy mechanisms: BOMB BLASTS (blast, shrapnel, burns, crush), FALLS FROM HEIGHT, road traffic ACCIDENTS (cars, motorcycles — very common in Nigeria), assaults, gunshots, and building collapses. INFECTION is a disease process caused by microorganisms, not a physical force, so it is NOT a cause of trauma (though infection can be a complication of trauma).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bomb blast — Bomb blasts ARE a cause of polytrauma.
• Fall from height — Falls from height ARE a cause.
• Accident — Accidents ARE a cause.

⭐ Key point: Manage polytrauma with the ATLS approach: primary survey (ABCDE), resuscitation, then secondary survey.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q120$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2031, $s$During a multi-vehicle road traffic accident with 15 casualties and limited ambulances, the triage officer applies START triage. A casualty who is breathing at 35/min after airway repositioning is categorised as:$s$, $s$["Minor (green) — can wait", "Immediate (red) — respiratory rate outside the normal range signals urgent priority", "Deceased (black)", "Delayed (yellow)"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Immediate (red) — respiratory rate outside the normal range signals urgent priority
START TRIAGE (Simple Triage and Rapid Treatment) is used in mass casualties. Steps: (1) Can the patient WALK? If yes → GREEN (minor). (2) Is the patient BREATHING? If no after airway repositioning → BLACK. (3) Respiratory rate above 30/min → RED (IMMEDIATE). (4) Check perfusion (capillary refill over 2 s or no radial pulse) → RED. (5) Can't follow simple commands → RED. Otherwise → YELLOW (delayed). A casualty breathing at 35/min is categorised as IMMEDIATE (RED).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Minor (green) — can wait — Green is for the walking wounded.
• Deceased (black) — Black is for those not breathing after airway repositioning.
• Delayed (yellow) — Yellow is for those who can wait but are not walking.

⭐ Key point: START aims to do the greatest good for the greatest number with limited resources.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q122$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2032, $s$A patient with severe burns and multisystem trauma in a resource-limited disaster is triaged as black because:$s$, $s$["Survival is unlikely", "The patient is deceased", "Resources are needed elsewhere", "A and C"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A and C
In DISASTER TRIAGE (mass casualties with limited resources), a BLACK (EXPECTANT) category is used for patients whose injuries are so severe that SURVIVAL is UNLIKELY even with treatment, AND treating them would use up SCARCE RESOURCES (staff, blood, theatre time) needed to save more people with survivable injuries. So "A AND C" is correct. These patients still receive comfort care (pain relief) and are reassessed if resources become available.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Survival is unlikely — Unlikely survival alone is only part of the reason.
• The patient is deceased — Being deceased is also categorised as black, but this patient is described as alive.
• Resources are needed elsewhere — Resource needs alone is only part of the reason.

⭐ Key point: Disaster triage shifts from "best for each patient" to "greatest good for the greatest number".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q124$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2033, $s$In mass casualty triage systems such as START, a casualty who is not breathing even after repositioning the airway is typically categorised as:$s$, $s$["Immediate (red)", "Delayed (yellow)", "Minor (green)", "Deceased/expectant (black)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Deceased/expectant (black)
In START TRIAGE, a casualty who is NOT BREATHING even AFTER REPOSITIONING THE AIRWAY (head tilt/jaw thrust) is categorised as DECEASED/EXPECTANT (BLACK). In a mass casualty situation, resources are directed to people with survivable injuries; CPR is not usually started in this setting because it uses up many responders for one patient with a very low chance of survival. If the patient starts breathing after airway repositioning, they are categorised as IMMEDIATE (RED).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate (red) — Immediate (red) is for those who breathe after airway repositioning or have other serious signs.
• Delayed (yellow) — Delayed (yellow) is for those who can wait.
• Minor (green) — Minor (green) is for the walking wounded.

⭐ Key point: Triage categories are reassessed as the situation and resources change.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q128$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2034, $s$A patient with an electrical burn injury passes dark, tea-coloured urine. The nurse should suspect:$s$, $s$["A normal finding requiring no action", "Myoglobinuria from muscle breakdown, requiring aggressive fluid therapy and monitoring for acute kidney injury", "A urinary tract infection only", "Dehydration alone with no other significance"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Myoglobinuria from muscle breakdown, requiring aggressive fluid therapy and monitoring for acute kidney injury
High-voltage ELECTRICAL injuries cause extensive DEEP MUSCLE DAMAGE along the path of the current (even when skin burns look small). Damaged muscle releases MYOGLOBIN into the blood (RHABDOMYOLYSIS), which turns urine DARK, TEA- or COLA-COLOURED (MYOGLOBINURIA). Myoglobin can block the kidney tubules and cause ACUTE KIDNEY INJURY. Treatment: AGGRESSIVE IV FLUIDS (aim for urine output 1–2 mL/kg/hr until the urine clears), monitoring of creatinine, CK and potassium, and possibly mannitol and bicarbonate per protocol.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal finding requiring no action — Dark urine after electrical injury is NOT normal.
• A urinary tract infection only — A urinary tract infection would not explain this picture.
• Dehydration alone with no other significance — Dehydration may concentrate urine, but tea-coloured urine after electrical injury suggests myoglobinuria.

⭐ Key point: Also watch for hyperkalaemia, cardiac arrhythmias (continuous ECG), and compartment syndrome.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q133$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2035, $s$Which of the following is a key responsibility of a nurse manager during disaster preparedness?$s$, $s$["Develop, communicate, and regularly evaluate disaster response plans", "Close the hospital permanently", "Cancel emergency drills", "Delegate all planning to external agencies"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Develop, communicate, and regularly evaluate disaster response plans
DISASTER PREPAREDNESS is a key nurse manager responsibility. The manager should help DEVELOP disaster response PLANS (roles, surge capacity, triage areas, staff call-in lists, supplies and equipment, communication systems, evacuation routes), COMMUNICATE them to all staff (training, briefings), and REGULARLY EVALUATE them through drills and exercises, updating the plan based on lessons learned.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Close the hospital permanently — Closing the hospital permanently is not disaster preparedness.
• Cancel emergency drills — Cancelling drills prevents staff from being ready.
• Delegate all planning to external agencies — Planning should be done internally with input from external agencies, not fully delegated.

⭐ Key point: Regular drills reveal weaknesses before a real disaster occurs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q176$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2036, $s$Which does NOT need ruled out before assessing for brain death?$s$, $s$["Fractures", "Electrolyte imbalances", "Hypothermia", "Drug intoxication"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fractures
Before testing for BRAIN DEATH, REVERSIBLE causes of coma must be EXCLUDED, because they can mimic brain death: ELECTROLYTE and METABOLIC imbalances (e.g. severe hyponatraemia, hypoglycaemia), HYPOTHERMIA (core temperature must be above about 34–36 °C), DRUG INTOXICATION (sedatives, opioids, muscle relaxants), and endocrine disorders. FRACTURES do not affect brainstem function and do not need to be ruled out.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Electrolyte imbalances — Electrolyte imbalances must be ruled out.
• Hypothermia — Hypothermia must be ruled out.
• Drug intoxication — Drug intoxication must be ruled out.

⭐ Key point: Brain death testing is done by two senior doctors and includes brainstem reflexes and an apnoea test.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q88$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2037, $s$A disaster drill evaluates:$s$, $s$["Communication systems", "Resource allocation", "Staff response", "All of the above"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
A DISASTER DRILL tests how well a hospital or unit would cope with a real disaster. It evaluates COMMUNICATION SYSTEMS (alerting staff, call-in lists, phones, radios), RESOURCE ALLOCATION (beds, ventilators, blood, supplies), and STAFF RESPONSE (roles, triage, teamwork). So "ALL OF THE ABOVE" is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Communication systems — Communication is only one part.
• Resource allocation — Resource allocation is only one part.
• Staff response — Staff response is only one part.

⭐ Key point: After each drill, debrief and update the disaster plan.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q116$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2038, $s$An SpO₂ below what value is generally used as an ICU admission threshold?$s$, $s$["95%", "93%", "90%", "85%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 90%
An SpO2 BELOW 90% (despite oxygen) generally indicates significant HYPOXAEMIA and is used as a threshold for considering ICU admission or escalation, because it corresponds to a PaO2 of about 60 mmHg — the steep part of the oxygen dissociation curve, where small changes cause rapid drops in oxygen content.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 95% — 95% is normal.
• 93% — 93% is mildly low but usually manageable on the ward.
• 85% — 85% is severe; waiting until then delays care.

⭐ Key point: Consider the trend and the oxygen requirement, not just one value.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q117$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2039, $s$Ventilation strategy for a patient with significant inhalational injury favours:$s$, $s$["High tidal volumes to maximise oxygenation", "Low tidal volumes with permissive hypercapnia", "No ventilatory support is needed", "Maximum PEEP at all times"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Low tidal volumes with permissive hypercapnia
Patients with INHALATION INJURY often develop ARDS-like lung damage. The recommended ventilation strategy is LUNG-PROTECTIVE: LOW TIDAL VOLUMES (6 mL/kg predicted body weight), plateau pressure ≤30 cmH2O, adequate PEEP, and PERMISSIVE HYPERCAPNIA (accepting higher CO2 to avoid lung injury).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High tidal volumes to maximise oxygenation — High tidal volumes cause further lung injury.
• No ventilatory support is needed — Many need ventilatory support.
• Maximum PEEP at all times — Maximum PEEP at all times can cause barotrauma and hypotension.

⭐ Key point: Bronchoscopy may be needed to assess and clear soot and debris from the airway.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q119$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2040, $s$Operative management of extraperitoneal bladder rupture may be indicated in:$s$, $s$["Concomitant rectal or vaginal injury", "Foreign body in the bladder wall", "Extensive bladder wall tear", "All of the above"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
EXTRAPERITONEAL bladder rupture (urine leaks outside the peritoneum) is usually managed CONSERVATIVELY with catheter drainage. SURGERY is indicated when there is: concomitant RECTAL or VAGINAL injury, a FOREIGN BODY (e.g. bone fragment) in the bladder wall, an EXTENSIVE bladder wall tear, bladder neck injury, or when the patient is having laparotomy for other injuries. So "ALL OF THE ABOVE" is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Concomitant rectal or vaginal injury — Rectal/vaginal injury is one indication.
• Foreign body in the bladder wall — Foreign body is one indication.
• Extensive bladder wall tear — Extensive tear is one indication.

⭐ Key point: Intraperitoneal bladder rupture always requires surgical repair.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q120$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2041, $s$A patient with 40% total body surface area burns is being resuscitated using the Parkland formula. If the patient weighs 70kg, the total fluid requirement for the first 24 hours is calculated as:$s$, $s$["4 x 70 x 40 = 11,200ml", "4 x 40 = 160ml", "70 x 40 = 2,800ml only", "No formula is used for burns"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 4 x 70 x 40 = 11,200ml
PARKLAND FORMULA: 4 mL × WEIGHT (kg) × %TBSA = 4 × 70 × 40 = 11,200 mL in 24 hours. Half (5,600 mL) in the first 8 hours from the burn, and half over the next 16 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 4 x 40 = 160ml — 160 mL omits the weight.
• 70 x 40 = 2,800ml only — 2,800 mL omits the 4 mL factor.
• No formula is used for burns — A formula IS used for burns.

⭐ Key point: Adjust fluids according to urine output (0.5–1 mL/kg/hr in adults).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q126$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2042, $s$Which one of the following is included in man-made disaster?$s$, $s$["Earth quake", "Floods", "Tsunami", "Wars"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Wars
DISASTERS are classified by cause. MAN-MADE (human-caused) disasters result from human actions or failures: WARS and armed conflict, terrorism and bomb blasts, industrial explosions, chemical or radiation leaks, building collapses, major fires, and transport accidents (e.g. tanker explosions, plane crashes). NATURAL disasters are caused by natural forces: earthquakes, floods, tsunamis, cyclones, droughts and epidemics.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Earth quake — An earthquake is a NATURAL disaster (movement of the earth's crust).
• Floods — Floods are usually NATURAL (heavy rainfall, rivers overflowing), though poor drainage and construction can worsen them.
• Tsunami — A tsunami is a NATURAL disaster (giant waves, often caused by undersea earthquakes).

⭐ Key point: Hospitals need major incident plans covering both natural and man-made disasters, including triage, surge capacity and communication systems.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q128$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2043, $s$A burns patient's wound care plan includes silver sulfadiazine cream. The nurse understands this agent is used primarily for its:$s$, $s$["Analgesic properties only", "Broad-spectrum antimicrobial effect, helping prevent burn wound infection", "Ability to promote scar-free healing with no infection benefit", "Cooling effect only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Broad-spectrum antimicrobial effect, helping prevent burn wound infection
SILVER SULFADIAZINE (SSD) cream combines silver (which kills microbes) with a sulfonamide antibiotic. It has a BROAD-SPECTRUM ANTIMICROBIAL effect against gram-positive and gram-negative bacteria (including Pseudomonas and Staphylococcus aureus) and some fungi (Candida). It is applied to burn wounds to PREVENT and treat BURN WOUND INFECTION, which is a major cause of death in burns patients (burns destroy the skin barrier).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Analgesic properties only — It is not mainly a painkiller (it has a soothing effect but is not an analgesic).
• Ability to promote scar-free healing with no infection benefit — It does not guarantee scar-free healing; it may slightly delay healing.
• Cooling effect only — It is not used mainly for cooling.

⭐ Key point: Side effects: transient leukopenia, skin discoloration; avoid in sulfa allergy, near-term pregnancy and newborns (kernicterus risk). Newer silver dressings reduce the need for daily dressing changes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q129$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2044, $s$Which of the following statements regarding blunt injury are false?$s$, $s$["The mechanisms are direct or indirect.", "In indirect injury, associated injuries may be present and should be sought.", "Overt injury should lead the clinician to look for a covert injury as well.", "In chest injuries, damage to abdominal organs is rare."]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: In chest injuries, damage to abdominal organs is rare.
In BLUNT CHEST injury, damage to ABDOMINAL ORGANS (liver, spleen, kidneys) is COMMON, because the lower ribs cover these organs. So the statement "damage to abdominal organs is rare" is FALSE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The mechanisms are direct or indirect. — Blunt injury can be direct or indirect — true.
• In indirect injury, associated injuries may be present and should be sought. — In indirect injury, associated injuries should be sought — true.
• Overt injury should lead the clinician to look for a covert injury as well. — Overt injury should prompt a search for covert injury — true.

⭐ Key point: Lower rib fractures should raise suspicion of liver (right) or spleen (left) injury.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q132$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2045, $s$Treatment for myoglobinuria after electrical injury includes double fluids plus:$s$, $s$["Mannitol and sodium bicarbonate", "Insulin and glucose", "Furosemide alone", "No additional treatment needed"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mannitol and sodium bicarbonate
High-voltage ELECTRICAL injuries cause extensive deep MUSCLE damage (even when skin burns look small). Damaged muscle releases MYOGLOBIN, which can block the kidney tubules and cause ACUTE KIDNEY INJURY. Treatment: GIVE MORE IV FLUIDS than standard burn formulas (often double), aiming for a URINE OUTPUT of 1–2 mL/kg/hr (about 100 mL/hr in adults) until the urine clears; plus MANNITOL (an osmotic diuretic that flushes the tubules) and SODIUM BICARBONATE (alkalinises the urine, which reduces myoglobin precipitation in the tubules), per local protocol.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Insulin and glucose — Insulin and glucose are used for hyperkalaemia, not specifically for myoglobinuria.
• Furosemide alone — Furosemide alone is not the standard treatment and may worsen dehydration.
• No additional treatment needed — Additional treatment IS needed beyond standard fluids.

⭐ Key point: Also monitor for hyperkalaemia, cardiac arrhythmias (continuous ECG for 24 hours), and compartment syndrome needing fasciotomy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q133$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2046, $s$An adult has burns to the back of the trunk, the head and neck and the perineum. Using the Rule of Nines, what is the TBSA burned?$s$, $s$["32.5%", "19%", "28%", "37%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 28%
RULE OF NINES (adult): BACK of the trunk = 18%; HEAD and NECK = 9%; PERINEUM = 1%. Total: 18 + 9 + 1 = 28% TBSA. Head and neck burns are dangerous because of the risk of airway swelling (especially with inhalation injury) — check for hoarseness, stridor, singed nasal hair and soot in the mouth; early intubation may be needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 32.5% — 32.5% adds an extra 4.5%.
• 19% — 19% misses the head and neck (or counts the back as 9%).
• 37% — 37% adds an extra 9%.

⭐ Key point: Facial, perineal and circumferential burns need referral to a burns centre regardless of their size.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q14 · concept asked in 11 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2047, $s$A 65 kg man with 60% TBSA burns arrives 2 hours after the injury and has had no IV fluid. Using the Parkland formula, at what rate should the first half of the fluid run?$s$, $s$["975 mL/hr", "1,300 mL/hr", "2,600 mL/hr", "650 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1,300 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Total (Parkland): 4 × 65 × 60 = 15,600 mL in 24 hours. STEP 2 — First half: 7,800 mL in the first 8 hours from the burn. STEP 3 — Arrived 2 hours after the burn with no fluid, so 6 hours remain. STEP 4 — Rate: 7,800 ÷ 6 = 1,300 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 975 mL/hr — 975 mL/hr is 7,800 ÷ 8 — it ignores the 2 hours lost.
• 2,600 mL/hr — 2,600 mL/hr is double.
• 650 mL/hr — 650 mL/hr is the rate for the second half over 12 hours.

⭐ Key point: A 60% burn is massive; very high fluid volumes are needed, through large-bore cannulas or a central line, with an arterial line and urinary catheter for monitoring.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q28 · concept asked in 7 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2048, $s$An adult has burns to the perineum, the whole left arm and the whole right leg. Using the Rule of Nines, what is the TBSA burned?$s$, $s$["32.5%", "37%", "19%", "28%"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 28%
RULE OF NINES (adult): PERINEUM = 1%; each WHOLE ARM = 9%; each WHOLE LEG = 18%. Here: perineum 1% + whole left arm 9% + whole right leg 18% = 28% TBSA. A 28% burn in an adult is a MAJOR burn: start IV fluid resuscitation (Parkland), insert a urinary catheter to monitor urine output hourly, give strong analgesia, keep the patient warm, and refer to a burns unit. Perineal involvement adds a high infection risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 32.5% — 32.5% adds an extra 4.5% (half an arm).
• 37% — 37% adds an extra 9%.
• 19% — 19% leaves out the arm.

⭐ Key point: Re-assess the burn size after the wounds have been cleaned — soot and dirt can make areas look burned when they are not, and blisters may hide deeper burns.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q34 · concept asked in 11 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2049, $s$A 70 kg patient with 50% TBSA burns requires fluid resuscitation. The Parkland formula calculates the 24-hour fluid as:$s$, $s$["14,000 mL", "3,500 mL", "28,000 mL", "7,000 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 14,000 mL
PARKLAND FORMULA: 24-hour fluid = 4 mL × weight (kg) × %TBSA = 4 × 70 × 50 = 14,000 mL (14 litres). Half (7,000 mL) is given in the first 8 hours after the burn, and the other half over the next 16 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 3,500 mL — 3,500 mL is a quarter.
• 28,000 mL — 28,000 mL is double (8 mL formula).
• 7,000 mL — 7,000 mL is only the first-8-hour half.

⭐ Key point: Over-resuscitation ("fluid creep") causes oedema, abdominal compartment syndrome and lung problems — titrate to urine output.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q36$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2050, $s$An adult has burns to the whole left leg, the whole right arm, the front of the trunk (chest and abdomen) and the perineum. Using the Rule of Nines, what is the TBSA burned?$s$, $s$["46%", "37%", "55%", "50.5%"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 46%
RULE OF NINES (adult): WHOLE LEFT LEG = 18%; WHOLE RIGHT ARM = 9%; FRONT of the TRUNK (chest and abdomen) = 18%; PERINEUM = 1%. Total: 18 + 9 + 18 + 1 = 46% TBSA. A burn of nearly half the body surface is life-threatening: massive fluid shifts, shock, hypothermia, infection and a very high metabolic rate. These patients need ICU or burns-unit care, large-bore IV access, high-volume Parkland resuscitation, airway assessment, and early nutrition.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 37% — 37% misses 9% (e.g. the arm).
• 55% — 55% adds an extra 9%.
• 50.5% — 50.5% adds an extra 4.5%.

⭐ Key point: Mortality rises with burn size, age and inhalation injury. Circumferential chest burns can restrict breathing and may need escharotomy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q40 · concept asked in 11 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2051, $s$A 50 kg man with 40% TBSA burns arrives 4 hours after the injury and has had no IV fluid. Using the Parkland formula, at what rate should the first half of the fluid run?$s$, $s$["333.3 mL/hr", "1,000 mL/hr", "500 mL/hr", "2,000 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1,000 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Total (Parkland): 4 × 50 × 40 = 8,000 mL. STEP 2 — First half: 4,000 mL in the first 8 hours from the burn. STEP 3 — Arrived 4 hours after the burn with no fluid, so 4 hours remain. STEP 4 — Rate: 4,000 ÷ 4 = 1,000 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 333.3 mL/hr — 333.3 mL/hr is the second-half rate (4,000 ÷ 12).
• 500 mL/hr — 500 mL/hr is 4,000 ÷ 8 — ignores the 4 hours lost.
• 2,000 mL/hr — 2,000 mL/hr is double.

⭐ Key point: Delayed resuscitation increases the risk of kidney injury and shock; start fluids as soon as possible after a major burn.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q45 · concept asked in 7 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2052, $s$A 60 kg man with 20% TBSA burns arrives 1 hour after the injury and has had no IV fluid. Using the Parkland formula, at what rate should the first half of the fluid run?$s$, $s$["342.9 mL/hr", "685.7 mL/hr", "300 mL/hr", "200 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 342.9 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Total (Parkland): 4 × 60 × 20 = 4,800 mL. STEP 2 — First half: 2,400 mL in the first 8 hours. STEP 3 — Arrived 1 hour after the burn, so 7 hours remain. STEP 4 — Rate: 2,400 ÷ 7 = 342.9 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 685.7 mL/hr — 685.7 mL/hr is double.
• 300 mL/hr — 300 mL/hr is 2,400 ÷ 8 — ignores the lost hour.
• 200 mL/hr — 200 mL/hr is the 24-hour average.

⭐ Key point: In adults, formal fluid resuscitation is usually needed for burns above 15–20% TBSA.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q47 · concept asked in 7 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2053, $s$When preoxygenating a trauma patient with facial injuries before intubation, the nurse should be cautious with bag-valve-mask ventilation because:$s$, $s$["It improves cervical spine stability", "Excessive ventilation pressure can cause gastric insufflation and increase aspiration risk, and may worsen facial injuries", "It has no risks", "It always causes hypoxemia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Excessive ventilation pressure can cause gastric insufflation and increase aspiration risk, and may worsen facial injuries
When BAG-VALVE-MASK ventilating a trauma patient, EXCESSIVE PRESSURE or volume can force air into the STOMACH (GASTRIC INSUFFLATION), increasing the risk of VOMITING and ASPIRATION. In patients with FACIAL INJURIES, mask pressure may worsen injuries, and fractures may prevent a good seal. Use gentle, slow breaths only as needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It improves cervical spine stability — BVM doesn't improve cervical spine stability; in-line stabilisation is needed.
• It has no risks — BVM does carry risks.
• It always causes hypoxemia — BVM doesn't always cause hypoxaemia; it can prevent it.

⭐ Key point: Use the minimum pressure needed for chest rise; consider cricoid pressure and early intubation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q109$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2054, $s$A patient presents with dry flushed skin, dilated pupils, urinary retention, hyperthermia, tachycardia, and confusion after ingesting an unknown substance. This presentation is most consistent with which toxidrome?$s$, $s$["Anticholinergic toxidrome", "Cholinergic toxidrome", "Sedative-hypnotic toxidrome", "Opioid toxidrome"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Anticholinergic toxidrome
The ANTICHOLINERGIC TOXIDROME (e.g. from antihistamines, tricyclic antidepressants, atropine, datura plants) causes: DRY FLUSHED SKIN ("dry as a bone, red as a beet"), DILATED PUPILS ("blind as a bat"), HYPERTHERMIA ("hot as a hare"), CONFUSION ("mad as a hatter"), URINARY RETENTION ("full as a flask"), and TACHYCARDIA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cholinergic toxidrome — Cholinergic toxidrome causes the OPPOSITE: secretions, small pupils, bradycardia.
• Sedative-hypnotic toxidrome — Sedative-hypnotic toxidrome causes CNS and respiratory depression.
• Opioid toxidrome — Opioid toxidrome causes pinpoint pupils and respiratory depression.

⭐ Key point: The key difference from the sympathomimetic toxidrome is DRY skin (no sweating).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q163$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2055, $s$A patient with a severe brain injury is noted to have a rising temperature unrelated to infection. The nurse understands this can occur due to:$s$, $s$["Simple dehydration only", "No relationship between brain injury and temperature regulation", "Always indicates infection with no other cause possible", "Hypothalamic dysfunction from the injury itself, sometimes called 'central fever'"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypothalamic dysfunction from the injury itself, sometimes called 'central fever'
The HYPOTHALAMUS controls body temperature. In severe brain injury, damage to the hypothalamus can cause FEVER without infection — "CENTRAL" (neurogenic) FEVER. However, infection must still be ruled out.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple dehydration only — Dehydration may contribute but is not the only cause.
• No relationship between brain injury and temperature regulation — Brain injury can affect temperature regulation.
• Always indicates infection with no other cause possible — Fever does not always indicate infection.

⭐ Key point: Fever worsens brain injury — keep temperature normal (cooling, antipyretics).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q172$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2056, $s$A patient with clear fluid draining from the nose after head trauma should be assessed for:$s$, $s$["No clinical significance", "Cerebrospinal fluid rhinorrhoea, a sign of possible skull base fracture with dural tear", "A common cold unrelated to the trauma", "Simple allergic rhinitis with no further concern"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cerebrospinal fluid rhinorrhoea, a sign of possible skull base fracture with dural tear
CLEAR FLUID from the nose after head trauma may be CEREBROSPINAL FLUID (CSF RHINORRHOEA), indicating a SKULL BASE FRACTURE with a tear in the dura. This increases the risk of MENINGITIS. Test the fluid for glucose or beta-2 transferrin, and avoid nasal tubes and suctioning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No clinical significance — It is clinically significant.
• A common cold unrelated to the trauma — It is unlikely to be a cold.
• Simple allergic rhinitis with no further concern — Allergic rhinitis is not the cause.

⭐ Key point: Other signs of skull base fracture: raccoon eyes, Battle's sign, haemotympanum, CSF otorrhoea.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q174$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2057, $s$A farm worker presents with excessive salivation, lacrimation, urination, diarrhoea, GI cramps, emesis (SLUDGE), miosis, bradycardia, and muscle fasciculations after exposure to a crop spray. Which class of toxin is most likely responsible?$s$, $s$["Anticholinergic poisoning", "Organophosphate (cholinergic) poisoning", "Opioid overdose", "Sympathomimetic overdose"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Organophosphate (cholinergic) poisoning
ORGANOPHOSPHATE pesticides (crop sprays) block the enzyme ACETYLCHOLINESTERASE, so ACETYLCHOLINE builds up at nerve endings. This causes CHOLINERGIC overstimulation: muscarinic effects — SLUDGE (Salivation, Lacrimation, Urination, Diarrhoea, GI cramps, Emesis), MIOSIS (pinpoint pupils), BRADYCARDIA, bronchorrhoea and bronchospasm ("killer Bs"); nicotinic effects — MUSCLE FASCICULATIONS, then weakness and paralysis; CNS effects — confusion, seizures, coma.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anticholinergic poisoning — Anticholinergic poisoning causes the opposite: dry skin, dilated pupils, tachycardia.
• Opioid overdose — Opioids cause pinpoint pupils and drowsiness, but not SLUDGE or fasciculations.
• Sympathomimetic overdose — Sympathomimetics cause dilated pupils, tachycardia and hypertension.

⭐ Key point: Treat with ATROPINE (in doubling doses until the chest is clear) and PRALIDOXIME; remove contaminated clothing and wash the skin; staff wear PPE.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q189$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2058, $s$Using the Parkland formula for a 70 kg patient with 40% TBSA burns, how much fluid should be given in the first 8 hours after the burn?$s$, $s$["3,733.3 mL", "5,600 mL", "2,800 mL", "11,200 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 5,600 mL
PARKLAND FORMULA: Total fluid in 24 hours = 4 mL × weight (kg) × %TBSA burned. Here: 4 × 70 × 40 = 11,200 mL. HALF of this is given in the FIRST 8 HOURS FROM THE TIME OF THE BURN: 11,200 ÷ 2 = 5,600 mL. The other half (5,600 mL) is given over the next 16 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 3,733.3 mL — 3,733.3 mL is one-third of 11,200 — not the Parkland split.
• 2,800 mL — 2,800 mL is one-quarter.
• 11,200 mL — 11,200 mL is the full 24-hour total, not the first 8 hours.

⭐ Key point: Use Ringer's lactate/Hartmann's. Adjust the rate according to urine output (target 0.5–1 mL/kg/hr in adults; 1 mL/kg/hr in children).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q5 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2059, $s$A 50 year old patient weighing 60kg with 36% secondary degree burns needs fluid replacement therapy in ICU. Using Parkland formula, calculate the total fluid he will need in 24 hours?$s$, $s$["4320ml", "8640ml", "2160ml", "12000ml"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 8640ml
PARKLAND FORMULA: Total fluid in 24 hours = 4 mL × weight (kg) × %TBSA. Here: 4 × 60 × 36 = 8,640 mL. Half (4,320 mL) is given in the first 8 hours, and the rest (4,320 mL) over the next 16 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 4320ml — 4,320 mL is only the first-8-hour half.
• 2160ml — 2,160 mL is a quarter of the total (2 mL formula with an error).
• 12000ml — 12,000 mL does not match the formula.

⭐ Key point: Only partial-thickness (second-degree) and full-thickness (third-degree) burns are counted in the TBSA; superficial (first-degree) burns are not counted.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q13$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2060, $s$A patient has burns to the anterior trunk, entire right arm, and anterior left leg. Using the Rule of Nines, the TBSA burned is:$s$, $s$["27%", "54%", "36%", "45%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 36%
RULE OF NINES (adult): head and neck 9%; each arm 9%; front of trunk 18%; back of trunk 18%; each leg 18% (front 9%, back 9%); perineum 1%. Here: anterior trunk = 18%; entire right arm = 9%; anterior left leg = 9%. Total: 18 + 9 + 9 = 36%.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 27% — 27% misses one 9% area.
• 54% — 54% counts the whole left leg (18%) and the whole trunk front and back.
• 45% — 45% overcounts.

⭐ Key point: The rule of nines is less accurate in children (larger head); use the Lund and Browder chart for children. The patient's palm (including fingers) is about 1% TBSA for small or scattered burns.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q14 · concept asked in 11 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2061, $s$An adult has burns to the whole left leg, the whole left arm and the head and neck. Using the Rule of Nines, what is the TBSA burned?$s$, $s$["36%", "40.5%", "45%", "27%"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 36%
The RULE OF NINES divides an adult's body surface into areas of 9% (or multiples of 9): head and neck 9%; each whole arm 9%; front of trunk 18%; back of trunk 18%; each whole leg 18%; perineum 1% — total 100%. Here: WHOLE LEFT LEG = 18%; WHOLE LEFT ARM = 9%; HEAD AND NECK = 9%. Total = 18 + 9 + 9 = 36% TBSA. Only partial-thickness and full-thickness burns are counted; simple redness (superficial burns) is not. Burns above 15–20% TBSA in adults need formal IV fluid resuscitation (e.g. Parkland formula).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 40.5% — 40.5% adds an extra 4.5% (half an arm) that was not burned.
• 45% — 45% adds an extra 9%.
• 27% — 27% wrongly counts the leg as 9% instead of 18%.

⭐ Key point: For children, use the Lund and Browder chart, because a child's head is a larger part of the body (up to about 18% in infants) and the legs are smaller.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q18 · concept asked in 11 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2062, $s$A 90 kg man with 45% TBSA burns arrives 2 hours after the injury and has had no IV fluid. Using the Parkland formula, at what rate should the first half of the fluid run?$s$, $s$["2,700 mL/hr", "675 mL/hr", "1,350 mL/hr", "1,012.5 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1,350 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Total 24-hour fluid (Parkland): 4 × 90 × 45 = 16,200 mL. STEP 2 — First half: 8,100 mL, to be given in the first 8 hours FROM THE TIME OF THE BURN. STEP 3 — He arrived 2 hours after the burn with no fluid, so 8,100 mL must be given in the remaining 6 hours. STEP 4 — Rate: 8,100 ÷ 6 = 1,350 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2,700 mL/hr — 2,700 mL/hr would give 8,100 mL in 3 hours.
• 675 mL/hr — 675 mL/hr is the rate for the second half over 12 hours.
• 1,012.5 mL/hr — 1,012.5 mL/hr is 8,100 ÷ 8 — it ignores the 2 hours already lost.

⭐ Key point: The 8-hour clock starts at the time of the burn, not arrival. Very high rates need large-bore access and close monitoring for over-resuscitation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q19 · concept asked in 7 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2063, $s$An adult has burns to the whole right leg and the perineum. Using the Rule of Nines, what is the TBSA burned?$s$, $s$["28%", "23.5%", "19%", "10%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 19%
RULE OF NINES (adult): each WHOLE LEG = 18% (front 9% + back 9%); the PERINEUM (genital area) = 1%. Here: whole right leg 18% + perineum 1% = 19% TBSA. A burn of 19% in an adult usually needs IV fluid resuscitation. PERINEAL burns are also important because of the high risk of infection (contamination with urine and stool) — they are a reason for referral to a burns centre regardless of size.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 28% — 28% adds an extra 9% that was not burned.
• 23.5% — 23.5% adds an extra 4.5%.
• 10% — 10% counts the leg as only 9%.

⭐ Key point: Burns that should be referred to a specialist burns unit include those affecting the face, hands, feet, genitalia/perineum, major joints, circumferential burns, inhalation injury, electrical and chemical burns, and large TBSA burns.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q35 · concept asked in 11 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2064, $s$An adult has burns to the perineum and the whole left arm. Using the Rule of Nines, what is the TBSA burned?$s$, $s$["14.5%", "10%", "1%", "19%"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10%
RULE OF NINES (adult): the PERINEUM = 1%; each WHOLE ARM = 9% (front 4.5% + back 4.5%). Here: perineum 1% + whole left arm 9% = 10% TBSA. Although 10% is a moderate burn by size, the PERINEAL involvement makes it a burn needing specialist care (high infection risk, possible need for urinary catheter).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 14.5% — 14.5% adds an extra 4.5%.
• 1% — 1% counts only the perineum and forgets the arm.
• 19% — 19% wrongly counts the arm as 18% (a leg value).

⭐ Key point: The patient's PALM (including fingers) is about 1% of their TBSA — useful for estimating small or patchy burns.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q43 · concept asked in 11 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2065, $s$An adult has burns to the whole left arm and the whole right leg. Using the Rule of Nines, what is the TBSA burned?$s$, $s$["31.5%", "36%", "27%", "18%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 27%
RULE OF NINES (adult): each WHOLE ARM = 9%; each WHOLE LEG = 18%. Here: whole left arm 9% + whole right leg 18% = 27% TBSA. A 27% burn in an adult is a MAJOR burn requiring IV fluid resuscitation (Parkland formula), urinary catheter, close monitoring and referral to a burns centre.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 31.5% — 31.5% adds an extra 4.5%.
• 36% — 36% adds an extra 9%.
• 18% — 18% counts only the leg.

⭐ Key point: Circumferential burns of a limb can cause compartment syndrome — check pulses and sensation, and escharotomy may be needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q45 · concept asked in 11 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2066, $s$An adult has burns to the front of the right arm and the front of the left leg. Using the Rule of Nines, what is the TBSA burned?$s$, $s$["18%", "22.5%", "13.5%", "4.5%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 13.5%
RULE OF NINES: when only the FRONT or BACK of a limb is burned, use HALF of that limb's value. Each arm = 9%, so the FRONT of the right arm = 4.5%. Each leg = 18%, so the FRONT of the left leg = 9%. Total: 4.5 + 9 = 13.5% TBSA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 18% — 18% counts both whole limbs (wrongly) — or adds 9 + 9.
• 22.5% — 22.5% counts the whole leg (18%) plus the front of the arm (4.5%).
• 4.5% — 4.5% counts only the front of the arm.

⭐ Key point: Accurate burn size matters because fluid needs are calculated from it — an overestimate causes fluid overload, an underestimate causes shock and kidney injury.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q47 · concept asked in 11 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2067, $s$An adult has burns to the front of the right leg, the front of the trunk (chest and abdomen) and the back of the trunk. Using the Rule of Nines, what is the TBSA burned?$s$, $s$["45%", "36%", "54%", "49.5%"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 45%
RULE OF NINES: FRONT of the right leg = 9% (half of 18%); FRONT of the trunk (chest and abdomen) = 18%; BACK of the trunk = 18%. Total: 9 + 18 + 18 = 45% TBSA. This is a very large burn needing urgent fluid resuscitation, airway assessment, analgesia, warmth and transfer to a burns unit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 36% — 36% misses the front of the leg (9%).
• 54% — 54% wrongly counts the whole leg (18%).
• 49.5% — 49.5% adds an extra 4.5%.

⭐ Key point: Large burns cause massive fluid shifts, hypothermia, infection risk and a high metabolic rate — they need ICU-level care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q48 · concept asked in 11 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2068, $s$A 55 kg man with 30% TBSA burns arrives 3 hours after the injury and has had no IV fluid. Using the Parkland formula, at what rate should the first half of the fluid run?$s$, $s$["660 mL/hr", "412.5 mL/hr", "275 mL/hr", "1,320 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 660 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Total (Parkland): 4 × 55 × 30 = 6,600 mL. STEP 2 — First half: 3,300 mL, due in the first 8 hours from the burn. STEP 3 — Arrived 3 hours after the burn with no fluid, so 3,300 mL must run over the remaining 5 hours. STEP 4 — Rate: 3,300 ÷ 5 = 660 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 412.5 mL/hr — 412.5 mL/hr is 3,300 ÷ 8 (ignores lost time).
• 275 mL/hr — 275 mL/hr is the second-half rate (3,300 ÷ 12).
• 1,320 mL/hr — 1,320 mL/hr is double.

⭐ Key point: Late arrival means the first-half fluid must be given faster.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q55 · concept asked in 7 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2069, $s$A 90 kg man with 30% TBSA burns arrives 1 hour after the injury and has had no IV fluid. Using the Parkland formula, at what rate should the first half of the fluid run?$s$, $s$["771.4 mL/hr", "450 mL/hr", "675 mL/hr", "1,542.9 mL/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 771.4 mL/hr
STEP 1 — Total (Parkland): 4 × 90 × 30 = 10,800 mL. STEP 2 — First half: 5,400 mL in the first 8 hours from the burn. STEP 3 — Arrived 1 hour after the burn, so 7 hours remain. STEP 4 — Rate: 5,400 ÷ 7 = 771.4 mL/hr.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 450 mL/hr — 450 mL/hr is the second-half rate (5,400 ÷ 12).
• 675 mL/hr — 675 mL/hr is 5,400 ÷ 8 (ignores the lost hour).
• 1,542.9 mL/hr — 1,542.9 mL/hr is double.

⭐ Key point: Recheck urine output hourly and adjust.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q58 · concept asked in 7 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2070, $s$A patient presents with agitation, dilated pupils, tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, and diaphoresis after suspected cocaine or amphetamine use. Which toxidrome is this?$s$, $s$["Sympathomimetic toxidrome", "Opioid toxidrome", "Anticholinergic toxidrome", "Cholinergic toxidrome"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sympathomimetic toxidrome
A TOXIDROME is a group of signs typical of a type of poisoning. The SYMPATHOMIMETIC toxidrome is caused by drugs that stimulate the sympathetic ("fight or flight") nervous system — COCAINE, AMPHETAMINES (including methamphetamine, MDMA/ecstasy) and some others. Features: AGITATION, anxiety, paranoia, DILATED PUPILS (mydriasis), TACHYCARDIA, HYPERTENSION, HYPERTHERMIA, and SWEATING (diaphoresis). Complications: seizures, arrhythmias, MI, stroke and rhabdomyolysis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Opioid toxidrome — Opioid toxidrome: pinpoint pupils, drowsiness and respiratory depression.
• Anticholinergic toxidrome — Anticholinergic toxidrome: also dilated pupils, tachycardia and hyperthermia — but the skin is DRY and flushed (no sweating), with urinary retention and absent bowel sounds.
• Cholinergic toxidrome — Cholinergic toxidrome: small pupils, salivation, sweating, bradycardia, diarrhoea and bronchorrhoea (e.g. organophosphates).

⭐ Key point: The key difference between sympathomimetic and anticholinergic toxidromes is SWEATING (wet skin = sympathomimetic, dry skin = anticholinergic). Treat with benzodiazepines, cooling and supportive care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q187$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2071, $s$A nurse rapidly assesses an unresponsive trauma patient's airway, breathing, and circulation. This is an example of what type of assessment?$s$, $s$["Emergency assessment", "Focused assessment", "Time-lapsed reassessment", "Initial comprehensive assessment"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Emergency assessment
An EMERGENCY ASSESSMENT is a RAPID assessment performed during a crisis to identify LIFE-THREATENING problems and act immediately. Checking the AIRWAY, BREATHING and CIRCULATION (ABC) of an unresponsive trauma patient is an emergency assessment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Focused assessment — A focused assessment examines a specific problem in detail (e.g. assessing a wound or pain).
• Time-lapsed reassessment — A time-lapsed reassessment compares current status with earlier data after some time.
• Initial comprehensive assessment — An initial comprehensive assessment is a complete, detailed assessment on admission.

⭐ Key point: Types of assessment: initial comprehensive, focused (problem-oriented), emergency, and time-lapsed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q146$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2072, $s$What is the purpose of emergency contraception?$s$, $s$["To treat infertility", "To terminate an established pregnancy", "To regulate menstrual cycles", "To prevent pregnancy after unprotected intercourse"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: To prevent pregnancy after unprotected intercourse
EMERGENCY CONTRACEPTION (EC) is used to PREVENT PREGNANCY after UNPROTECTED INTERCOURSE, CONTRACEPTIVE FAILURE (e.g. a burst condom, missed pills) or sexual assault. Options: emergency contraceptive PILLS (levonorgestrel within 72 hours, or ulipristal acetate within 120 hours) and the COPPER IUD (within 5 days). They work mainly by delaying ovulation (pills) or preventing fertilisation (copper IUD). EC does NOT end an established pregnancy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To treat infertility — It does not treat infertility.
• To terminate an established pregnancy — It does not terminate an established pregnancy (it is not an abortion pill).
• To regulate menstrual cycles — It is not used to regulate menstrual cycles.

⭐ Key point: The sooner EC is taken, the more effective it is. Offer ongoing contraception and STI screening.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q174$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2073, $s$What is the main mechanism of action of emergency contraceptive pills (e.g. levonorgestrel)?$s$, $s$["Killing sperm in the vagina", "Delaying or preventing ovulation", "Inducing an abortion", "Destroying an implanted embryo"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Delaying or preventing ovulation
LEVONORGESTREL emergency contraceptive pills (e.g. Postinor) work mainly by DELAYING or PREVENTING OVULATION — they stop the release of an egg, so sperm in the reproductive tract cannot fertilise one. If ovulation has already happened, the pill is much less effective. They do NOT cause abortion and do not harm an existing pregnancy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Killing sperm in the vagina — They do not kill sperm in the vagina (that is how spermicides work).
• Inducing an abortion — They do not induce abortion.
• Destroying an implanted embryo — They do not destroy an implanted embryo.

⭐ Key point: Most effective within 72 hours (best within 24 hours). Less effective in women with higher body weight; the copper IUD is more effective.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q177$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2074, $s$What is considered the most effective form of emergency contraception?$s$, $s$["Copper IUD", "Diaphragm", "Condoms", "Levonorgestrel emergency pill"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Copper IUD
The COPPER IUD is the MOST EFFECTIVE form of emergency contraception — over 99% effective when inserted within 5 days (120 hours) of unprotected intercourse. Its effectiveness is not affected by body weight or the timing of ovulation, and it can be left in place to provide LONG-TERM contraception for up to 10–12 years.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diaphragm — A diaphragm is a barrier method used before intercourse, not emergency contraception.
• Condoms — Condoms are used during intercourse; they are not emergency contraception.
• Levonorgestrel emergency pill — Levonorgestrel pills are less effective (about 85–89% reduction in pregnancy risk), especially if ovulation is near or body weight is high.

⭐ Key point: Offer the copper IUD as the first-line emergency option where trained providers are available.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q178$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2075, $s$A patient with a T4 spinal cord injury suddenly has a pounding headache, BP 210/110 mmHg, pulse 50, a flushed and sweaty face above the injury, and pale, cool skin below it. The FIRST nursing action is to:$s$, $s$["Give IV atropine first to treat the slow heart rate", "Sit the patient up and check the bladder and catheter", "Give IV labetalol first, then look for the cause later", "Lay the patient flat with the legs raised to restore flow"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sit the patient up and check the bladder and catheter
AUTONOMIC DYSREFLEXIA occurs in spinal cord injuries at or above T6. A stimulus below the injury (most commonly a FULL BLADDER or BLOCKED CATHETER, or constipation) triggers a massive sympathetic response below the injury, causing SEVERE HYPERTENSION. The body tries to lower the BP with a slow heart rate (BRADYCARDIA) and vasodilation above the injury (FLUSHED, SWEATY FACE, POUNDING HEADACHE), but the signals cannot get through the damaged cord, so the skin below stays PALE and cool. The FIRST nursing action is to SIT THE PATIENT UP (to lower BP through gravity) and CHECK THE BLADDER and CATHETER (for blockage or kinks) to remove the trigger.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give IV atropine first to treat the slow heart rate — Atropine does not treat the cause; the bradycardia is a response to the high BP.
• Give IV labetalol first, then look for the cause later — Antihypertensives may be needed if BP stays high, but the first step is to sit up and remove the cause.
• Lay the patient flat with the legs raised to restore flow — Lying flat with legs raised would further increase BP.

⭐ Key point: Other triggers: constipation, tight clothing, pressure sores, ingrown toenails. Educate the patient and family to recognise and treat it.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q85$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2076, $s$After a road traffic collision, a man has HR 132, BP 85/50 mmHg, RR 34, is anxious and confused, and passes 10 mL/h of urine. By ATLS, the estimated blood loss class is:$s$, $s$["Class III (30–40% of blood volume)", "Class II (15–30%)", "Class IV (more than 40%)", "Class I (less than 15%)"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Class III (30–40% of blood volume)
ATLS classifies haemorrhage by estimated blood loss and clinical signs. CLASS III (30–40% blood volume, 1500–2000 mL in a 70 kg adult): HEART RATE 120–140 (here 132), SYSTOLIC BP DECREASED (here 85/50), RESPIRATORY RATE 30–40 (here 34), URINE OUTPUT 5–15 mL/h (here 10), and the patient is ANXIOUS and CONFUSED.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Class II (15–30%) — Class II (15–30%): HR 100–120, normal systolic BP, narrowed pulse pressure, urine 20–30 mL/h, mildly anxious.
• Class IV (more than 40%) — Class IV (over 40%): HR above 140, very low BP, negligible urine output, lethargic or unconscious.
• Class I (less than 15%) — Class I (less than 15%): near-normal vital signs.

⭐ Key point: Class III and IV need urgent blood transfusion and haemorrhage control (surgery or interventional radiology). Activate the major haemorrhage protocol.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q144$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2077, $s$A trauma patient with major haemorrhage arrives 1 hour after injury. Tranexamic acid should be given as:$s$, $s$["2 g as a single IV bolus — within 6 hours of injury", "1 g over 10 min — only if fibrinogen is below 1 g/L", "1 g over 10 min, then 1 g over 8 h — after 3 hours only", "1 g over 10 min, then 1 g over 8 h — within 3 h of injury"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1 g over 10 min, then 1 g over 8 h — within 3 h of injury
TRANEXAMIC ACID (TXA) stops clots from breaking down. The CRASH-2 trial showed that giving TXA to bleeding trauma patients WITHIN 3 HOURS of injury reduces deaths from bleeding. The dose is 1 g IV over 10 MINUTES, followed by 1 g IV over 8 HOURS. The earlier it is given, the greater the benefit. This patient arrived 1 hour after injury, so TXA should be given now.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2 g as a single IV bolus — within 6 hours of injury — 2 g as a single bolus within 6 hours is not the standard regimen, and giving after 3 hours is not beneficial.
• 1 g over 10 min — only if fibrinogen is below 1 g/L — TXA should be given based on the time from injury, not on a fibrinogen level.
• 1 g over 10 min, then 1 g over 8 h — after 3 hours only — Giving TXA AFTER 3 hours may INCREASE the risk of death from bleeding.

⭐ Key point: TXA is part of the major haemorrhage protocol. Record the time of injury and the time TXA is given.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q145$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2078, $s$A 70 kg man has 40% total body surface area partial- and full-thickness burns and arrives 2 hours after the burn. Using the Parkland formula, the IV fluid rate for the next 6 hours should be about:$s$, $s$["1,400 mL/h", "467 mL/h", "933 mL/h", "700 mL/h"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 933 mL/h
The PARKLAND FORMULA calculates IV fluid for the first 24 hours after a burn: 4 mL × body weight (kg) × % TBSA burned. Here: 4 × 70 × 40 = 11,200 mL in 24 hours. HALF (5,600 mL) is given in the FIRST 8 HOURS FROM THE TIME OF THE BURN (not from arrival). The other half is given over the next 16 hours. The patient arrived 2 hours after the burn, so 5,600 mL must be given in the remaining 6 hours: 5,600 ÷ 6 = about 933 mL/h.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1,400 mL/h — 1,400 mL/h is too fast; it would give 8,400 mL in 6 hours.
• 467 mL/h — 467 mL/h is the rate for the second half (5,600 mL over 12 hours — wrong).
• 700 mL/h — 700 mL/h is 5,600 ÷ 8, which ignores the 2 hours already passed.

⭐ Key point: Use Hartmann's (Ringer's lactate). Adjust fluids based on urine output (target 0.5–1 mL/kg/h in adults). Avoid over-resuscitation ("fluid creep").$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q151$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2079, $s$After a tricyclic antidepressant overdose, a patient is drowsy with dilated pupils, dry flushed skin, urinary retention, HR 130 and a QRS duration of 140 ms, and then has a seizure. The first-line treatment for the cardiotoxicity is:$s$, $s$["IV sodium bicarbonate 8.4%", "IV flumazenil", "IV physostigmine", "IV amiodarone"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV sodium bicarbonate 8.4%
TRICYCLIC ANTIDEPRESSANT (TCA) overdose causes: ANTICHOLINERGIC effects (DROWSINESS, DILATED PUPILS, DRY FLUSHED SKIN, URINARY RETENTION, TACHYCARDIA), SEIZURES, and CARDIOTOXICITY (TCAs block sodium channels, WIDENING the QRS — here 140 ms — and causing arrhythmias). First-line treatment for cardiotoxicity: IV SODIUM BICARBONATE 8.4% (provides sodium to overcome the channel block, and alkalinises the blood, reducing TCA binding to heart cells).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV flumazenil — Flumazenil can cause seizures in TCA overdose.
• IV physostigmine — Physostigmine can cause asystole in TCA overdose.
• IV amiodarone — Amiodarone can worsen QRS widening and arrhythmias.

⭐ Key point: Aim for a blood pH of 7.45–7.55. Treat seizures with benzodiazepines.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q157$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2080, $s$A farmer in northern Nigeria was bitten on the foot by a snake 12 hours ago. He now has bleeding gums, blood in the urine and oozing from a cannula site; blood in a dry glass tube does not clot after 20 minutes. The most likely snake and treatment are:$s$, $s$["Dog bite with rabies — rabies vaccine", "Cobra envenoming with neurotoxic paralysis — neostigmine alone", "Scorpion sting — analgesia only", "Carpet viper (Echis ocellatus) envenoming — specific antivenom"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Carpet viper (Echis ocellatus) envenoming — specific antivenom
In northern Nigeria, the CARPET VIPER (Echis ocellatus) causes most snakebite deaths. Its venom destroys clotting factors, causing BLEEDING (gums, urine, cannula sites) and INCOAGULABLE BLOOD (the 20-MINUTE WHOLE BLOOD CLOTTING TEST shows no clot). Treatment: SPECIFIC ANTIVENOM (e.g. EchiTAb), given IV, repeated until the blood clots.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dog bite with rabies — rabies vaccine — Dog bites cause rabies risk, not bleeding.
• Cobra envenoming with neurotoxic paralysis — neostigmine alone — Cobra venom causes paralysis (ptosis, breathing difficulty), not bleeding.
• Scorpion sting — analgesia only — Scorpion stings cause pain and autonomic effects, not bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Repeat the 20WBCT every 6 hours; give more antivenom if blood still doesn't clot. Avoid tourniquets and cutting the wound.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q158$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2081, $s$Six hours after a tibial fracture is placed in a cast, a patient has severe pain not relieved by morphine, pain on passive stretching of the toes, and a tense swollen calf. Foot pulses are still palpable. The most likely diagnosis and action are:$s$, $s$["Cellulitis under the cast — start IV flucloxacillin and elevate the leg", "Deep vein thrombosis — elevate the limb and start therapeutic heparin", "Arterial occlusion — excluded, because the foot pulses are present", "Compartment syndrome — split the cast and arrange urgent fasciotomy"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Compartment syndrome — split the cast and arrange urgent fasciotomy
COMPARTMENT SYNDROME occurs when pressure builds up in a closed muscle compartment (e.g. after a tibial fracture with a tight cast), cutting off blood flow to the muscles and nerves. Early signs: SEVERE PAIN NOT RELIEVED by strong painkillers, PAIN ON PASSIVE STRETCHING of the muscles (e.g. moving the toes), and a TENSE, SWOLLEN limb. PULSES ARE OFTEN STILL PRESENT (loss of pulse is a very late sign). Action: SPLIT THE CAST (down to skin) and arrange URGENT FASCIOTOMY (surgical opening of the compartment).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cellulitis under the cast — start IV flucloxacillin and elevate the leg — Cellulitis causes redness, warmth and fever, but not pain on passive stretch within hours.
• Deep vein thrombosis — elevate the limb and start therapeutic heparin — DVT causes calf swelling and tenderness, but not severe pain on passive stretch.
• Arterial occlusion — excluded, because the foot pulses are present — Palpable pulses do NOT exclude compartment syndrome.

⭐ Key point: Elevate the limb only to heart level (not higher), as elevation above the heart reduces perfusion. Untreated compartment syndrome leads to muscle death, contractures and amputation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q160$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2082, $s$In a negligence claim against a nurse, the element proved by showing that the patient's injury would NOT have happened 'but for' the nurse's failure is:$s$, $s$["Damage (harm)", "Breach of duty", "Duty of care", "Causation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Causation
To succeed in a NEGLIGENCE claim, the patient must prove four elements: (1) DUTY OF CARE — the nurse had a professional duty to the patient; (2) BREACH OF DUTY — the nurse failed to meet the expected standard of care; (3) CAUSATION — the breach actually CAUSED the injury; and (4) DAMAGE — the patient suffered real harm. CAUSATION is tested with the "BUT FOR" test: would the injury have happened "but for" (without) the nurse's failure? If the injury would NOT have happened without the failure, causation is proven.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Damage (harm) — DAMAGE is the harm itself (injury, loss), not the link between the failure and the harm.
• Breach of duty — BREACH OF DUTY is the failure to meet the standard of care.
• Duty of care — DUTY OF CARE is the relationship that creates the obligation to care.

⭐ Key point: If the injury would have happened anyway (e.g. from the disease itself), causation fails, and the negligence claim fails even if there was a breach of duty.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q195$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2083, $s$Which chest X-ray finding most strongly suggests blunt traumatic aortic injury?$s$, $s$["A widened mediastinum with loss of the aortic knob", "A small apical pneumothorax on the left side", "Fractured lower ribs on the right side only", "Blunting of one costophrenic angle with no shift"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A widened mediastinum with loss of the aortic knob
Blunt traumatic aortic injury (tear of the aorta) causes bleeding into the mediastinum (the middle of the chest). On X-ray this appears as a WIDENED MEDIASTINUM (more than 8 cm on a supine AP film) and LOSS OF THE AORTIC KNOB (the normal bulge of the aortic arch becomes blurred). Other signs include deviation of the trachea or nasogastric tube to the right, depression of the left main bronchus, a left apical cap, and fractures of the first or second rib.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A small apical pneumothorax on the left side — A small apical pneumothorax does not specifically suggest aortic injury.
• Fractured lower ribs on the right side only — Fractured lower right ribs suggest liver injury, not aortic injury.
• Blunting of one costophrenic angle with no shift — A blunted costophrenic angle suggests a small effusion or haemothorax, not specifically aortic injury.

⭐ Key point: CT angiography confirms the diagnosis. Most patients with aortic transection die at the scene; survivors have a contained tear that can rupture at any time.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q10$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2084, $s$For a patient with suspected traumatic aortic injury awaiting repair, the ICU nurse should aim to keep:$s$, $s$["Large fluid boluses running continuously to prevent any hypotension", "HR under 100 and systolic BP about 100–120 using beta-blockade", "Heart rate above 120 per minute to keep the cardiac output high", "Systolic BP above 160 mmHg to maintain cerebral and renal flow"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: HR under 100 and systolic BP about 100–120 using beta-blockade
In suspected aortic injury, the aortic wall is torn and held together only by its thin outer layer. High heart rate and high BP increase the force (shear stress) on the tear, risking complete RUPTURE. The goal is to keep the HEART RATE UNDER 100 and the SYSTOLIC BP around 100–120 mmHg, usually using a short-acting IV BETA-BLOCKER (e.g. esmolol, labetalol), with pain relief, until surgical or endovascular repair.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Large fluid boluses running continuously to prevent any hypotension — Large fluid boluses raise BP and may cause rupture. Fluids are given carefully.
• Heart rate above 120 per minute to keep the cardiac output high — A heart rate over 120 increases shear force on the aorta and risks rupture.
• Systolic BP above 160 mmHg to maintain cerebral and renal flow — A systolic BP over 160 increases the risk of rupture.

⭐ Key point: Give adequate analgesia — pain causes tachycardia and hypertension. Beta-blockers are given BEFORE vasodilators to prevent reflex tachycardia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q11$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2085, $s$The most common site of blunt traumatic aortic injury is the:$s$, $s$["Abdominal aorta, at the level of the renal arteries", "Aortic root, just above the aortic valve", "Aortic arch, at the origin of the brachiocephalic trunk", "Aortic isthmus, just beyond the left subclavian"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Aortic isthmus, just beyond the left subclavian
The AORTIC ISTHMUS is the part of the aorta just beyond the origin of the LEFT SUBCLAVIAN ARTERY, where the ligamentum arteriosum holds it. In a sudden deceleration (e.g. head-on crash), the mobile arch keeps moving forward while the isthmus is held in place. The shearing force tears the aorta at this point. About 80–90% of blunt aortic injuries happen here.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Abdominal aorta, at the level of the renal arteries — The abdominal aorta is less commonly injured in blunt trauma.
• Aortic root, just above the aortic valve — The aortic root may be injured, but these patients usually die at the scene; it is not the most common site in survivors.
• Aortic arch, at the origin of the brachiocephalic trunk — The arch at the brachiocephalic origin is a less common site.

⭐ Key point: Isthmus = the area of the ligamentum arteriosum, just distal to the left subclavian artery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q12$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2086, $s$Which mechanism of injury should make the nurse suspect blunt aortic trauma?$s$, $s$["A minor rear-end collision at about 10 km/h", "A stab wound to the thigh with brisk bleeding", "A low-speed fall from standing height in an elderly patient", "High-speed deceleration, e.g. head-on crash or fall from height"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: High-speed deceleration, e.g. head-on crash or fall from height
Blunt aortic injury is caused by HIGH-SPEED DECELERATION — when the body stops suddenly but the heart and aortic arch keep moving forward, tearing the aorta at its fixed point (the isthmus). Typical mechanisms: HEAD-ON or SIDE-IMPACT car crashes at high speed, ejection from a vehicle, and FALLS FROM A GREAT HEIGHT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A minor rear-end collision at about 10 km/h — A low-speed (10 km/h) rear-end crash is unlikely to produce enough deceleration force.
• A stab wound to the thigh with brisk bleeding — A stab wound to the thigh is penetrating limb trauma, not blunt chest trauma.
• A low-speed fall from standing height in an elderly patient — A fall from standing height usually causes hip fractures or head injuries, but not aortic tears.

⭐ Key point: "Mechanism of injury" helps predict hidden injuries. With high-speed deceleration, always think of the aorta.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q13$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2087, $s$On a supine trauma chest X-ray, a pneumothorax may be suggested by:$s$, $s$["Kerley B lines", "A curved meniscus", "A deep sulcus sign", "A clear apical lung edge"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A deep sulcus sign
In a SUPINE patient, air in the pleural space rises to the FRONT and the bottom of the chest (the anterior costophrenic angle). On the X-ray, this makes the costophrenic angle look abnormally DEEP and dark: the DEEP SULCUS SIGN. It is an important sign because the usual apical lung edge may not be visible in supine films.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Kerley B lines — Kerley B lines are short horizontal lines at the lung edges, seen in pulmonary oedema.
• A curved meniscus — A curved meniscus indicates fluid without air (on an erect film).
• A clear apical lung edge — A clear lung edge at the apex is the classic sign on an ERECT film, often not seen when supine.

⭐ Key point: Supine films can miss pneumothoraces. Ultrasound (absence of "lung sliding") or CT is more sensitive.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q17$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2088, $s$Which sign appears earliest in Class II haemorrhage?$s$, $s$["A narrowed pulse pressure with tachycardia", "Anuria for more than 2 hours", "A systolic BP below 90 mmHg", "Loss of consciousness and confusion"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A narrowed pulse pressure with tachycardia
In CLASS II haemorrhage (15–30% loss), the body compensates by releasing adrenaline. This causes TACHYCARDIA (100–120) and VASOCONSTRICTION, which raises the DIASTOLIC pressure. Because the systolic stays normal while the diastolic rises, the PULSE PRESSURE (systolic minus diastolic) NARROWS. This is one of the earliest signs of significant blood loss.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anuria for more than 2 hours — Anuria is a late sign (Class IV).
• A systolic BP below 90 mmHg — A systolic BP below 90 is a late sign (Class III–IV).
• Loss of consciousness and confusion — Loss of consciousness is a late sign (Class IV).

⭐ Key point: A narrowing pulse pressure with rising heart rate is an early warning sign — act before the BP falls.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q22$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2089, $s$A trauma patient on beta-blockers loses 1.5 L of blood. Why may the class of haemorrhage be underestimated?$s$, $s$["They cause a diuresis that masks oliguria", "They blunt the tachycardic response", "They keep the patient alert despite shock", "They raise the BP and so hide hypotension"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: They blunt the tachycardic response
BETA-BLOCKERS block the effect of adrenaline on the heart, so the heart CANNOT SPEED UP (tachycardia is blunted) in response to blood loss. Because tachycardia is a key sign used to grade haemorrhage, the patient may appear less shocked than they really are, and the blood loss is UNDERESTIMATED.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• They cause a diuresis that masks oliguria — Beta-blockers do not cause diuresis.
• They keep the patient alert despite shock — They don't keep patients alert; they can worsen shock.
• They raise the BP and so hide hypotension — Beta-blockers LOWER the BP; they don't raise it.

⭐ Key point: Other patients who may not show tachycardia: elderly people, athletes, patients with pacemakers. Look at other signs (lactate, base deficit, urine output, mental state).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q23$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2090, $s$The 'golden hour' in trauma emphasises:$s$, $s$["Delaying surgery until the patient is fully stable", "Waiting one hour before any IV fluid is given", "A full secondary survey before any treatment", "Early assessment, resuscitation and definitive care"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Early assessment, resuscitation and definitive care
The "GOLDEN HOUR" is the critical period after injury when rapid ASSESSMENT, RESUSCITATION and DEFINITIVE CARE (e.g. surgery to stop bleeding) give the best chance of survival. Many trauma deaths in this period (from bleeding, airway problems) are preventable if treated quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delaying surgery until the patient is fully stable — Delaying surgery until "fully stable" is wrong for bleeding patients; surgery IS the way to stabilise them.
• Waiting one hour before any IV fluid is given — Waiting before IV fluids delays resuscitation.
• A full secondary survey before any treatment — The secondary survey comes AFTER the primary survey and resuscitation of life-threatening problems.

⭐ Key point: Rapid transport, early resuscitation and early surgery save lives in trauma.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q24$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2091, $s$In the trimodal distribution of trauma deaths, the second peak (minutes to hours) is mainly due to:$s$, $s$["Pulmonary embolism occurring after hospital discharge", "Haemorrhage, airway and chest injuries that are treatable", "Sepsis and multi-organ failure several weeks later", "Immediate brain stem and aortic transection injuries"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Haemorrhage, airway and chest injuries that are treatable
Trauma deaths occur in three peaks (trimodal distribution): FIRST PEAK (seconds to minutes) — immediately fatal injuries (e.g. brainstem damage, aortic transection), often at the scene; SECOND PEAK (minutes to hours) — HAEMORRHAGE, AIRWAY and CHEST INJURIES, which are often TREATABLE if dealt with quickly (this is the target of ATLS); THIRD PEAK (days to weeks) — sepsis and multi-organ failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pulmonary embolism occurring after hospital discharge — Pulmonary embolism after discharge is a late complication, not the second peak.
• Sepsis and multi-organ failure several weeks later — Sepsis and MOF weeks later are the THIRD peak.
• Immediate brain stem and aortic transection injuries — Immediate brainstem and aortic injuries cause the FIRST peak.

⭐ Key point: Rapid ATLS-style care targets the second peak, where most preventable deaths occur.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q25$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2092, $s$The six phases of pre-hospital trauma care begin with:$s$, $s$["Rehabilitation and follow-up", "Detection and reporting of the incident", "The secondary survey in the ED", "Hospital admission and surgery"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Detection and reporting of the incident
Pre-hospital trauma care follows a chain of phases. It starts with DETECTION of the incident and REPORTING it (calling emergency services), followed by response, on-scene care, care during transport, and transfer to definitive care (hospital).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rehabilitation and follow-up — Rehabilitation is a late phase, after hospital treatment.
• The secondary survey in the ED — The secondary survey happens in the emergency department, not first.
• Hospital admission and surgery — Hospital admission is a later phase.

⭐ Key point: Fast detection and reporting reduce delays; teach communities how to call for help.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q26$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2093, $s$Permissive hypotension in penetrating torso trauma means:$s$, $s$["Allowing the BP to fall to any level without any treatment", "Large crystalloid boluses to bring the BP back to normal", "Vasopressors as first-line treatment instead of giving blood", "A lower BP target (systolic 80–90) until bleeding is controlled"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A lower BP target (systolic 80–90) until bleeding is controlled
PERMISSIVE HYPOTENSION means deliberately allowing a LOWER BP (systolic about 80–90 mmHg, or a palpable radial pulse) in bleeding penetrating trauma UNTIL the bleeding is surgically CONTROLLED. A normal or high BP can "pop the clot" and increase bleeding, while too much crystalloid dilutes clotting factors. The BP target is enough to perfuse vital organs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Allowing the BP to fall to any level without any treatment — The BP is not allowed to fall to any level; there is a target to maintain organ perfusion.
• Large crystalloid boluses to bring the BP back to normal — Large crystalloid boluses dilute clotting factors and worsen bleeding.
• Vasopressors as first-line treatment instead of giving blood — Blood products are the main treatment; vasopressors are not first-line in haemorrhage.

⭐ Key point: Permissive hypotension is NOT used in traumatic brain injury, where a higher BP is needed to perfuse the brain.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q27$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2094, $s$Damage control resuscitation in massive haemorrhage from polytrauma emphasises:$s$, $s$["Avoiding surgical bleeding control until the patient is fully stabilised", "Early balanced blood products, permissive hypotension and bleeding control", "Aggressive large-volume crystalloid infusion as the sole resuscitation strategy", "Delaying all blood products until the haemoglobin falls below 5 g/dL"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Early balanced blood products, permissive hypotension and bleeding control
DAMAGE CONTROL RESUSCITATION aims to prevent the "lethal triad" in massive haemorrhage. It emphasises: EARLY BALANCED BLOOD PRODUCTS (red cells, plasma and platelets in roughly 1:1:1 ratio), PERMISSIVE HYPOTENSION, minimal crystalloid, tranexamic acid, keeping the patient warm, and EARLY BLEEDING CONTROL (damage control surgery or interventional radiology).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Avoiding surgical bleeding control until the patient is fully stabilised — Bleeding control should happen as early as possible; delaying it worsens the patient's condition.
• Aggressive large-volume crystalloid infusion as the sole resuscitation strategy — Large-volume crystalloid worsens coagulopathy, acidosis and hypothermia.
• Delaying all blood products until the haemoglobin falls below 5 g/dL — Waiting until Hb is below 5 is far too late; blood is given early.

⭐ Key point: Damage control surgery: stop the bleeding and contamination quickly, then stabilise in ICU before definitive surgery later.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q28$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2095, $s$The 'lethal triad' in polytrauma refers to:$s$, $s$["Anaemia, hypokalaemia and hyponatraemia", "Hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy", "Tachycardia, tachypnoea and fever", "Hypoxia, hypercapnia and hypotension"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy
The LETHAL TRIAD in trauma is HYPOTHERMIA, ACIDOSIS and COAGULOPATHY. Each worsens the others: cold slows clotting enzymes; acidosis (from poor perfusion) impairs clotting; coagulopathy causes more bleeding, more shock, more acidosis and more cooling. This vicious cycle is deadly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anaemia, hypokalaemia and hyponatraemia — Anaemia, hypokalaemia and hyponatraemia are not the lethal triad.
• Tachycardia, tachypnoea and fever — Tachycardia, tachypnoea and fever are not the lethal triad.
• Hypoxia, hypercapnia and hypotension — Hypoxia, hypercapnia and hypotension are not the lethal triad.

⭐ Key point: Prevent the triad: warm fluids and blankets, stop bleeding early, blood products, avoid excessive crystalloid.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q29 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2096, $s$Diagnostic peritoneal lavage is considered positive in blunt trauma when the aspirate shows:$s$, $s$["A lavage amylase within the normal laboratory range", "White cells below 500/mm³ with no bacteria seen", "10 mL or more of frank blood, or over 100 000 RBC/mm³", "Clear lavage fluid with a few red cells on microscopy"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10 mL or more of frank blood, or over 100 000 RBC/mm³
DIAGNOSTIC PERITONEAL LAVAGE (DPL) involves putting a catheter into the abdomen to look for blood. It is POSITIVE (suggesting significant injury) if: 10 mL OR MORE of FRANK BLOOD is aspirated immediately, or after washing with fluid, the lavage shows OVER 100,000 RED CELLS/mm³, over 500 white cells/mm³, bacteria, food particles or bile.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A lavage amylase within the normal laboratory range — A normal amylase does not indicate a positive result.
• White cells below 500/mm³ with no bacteria seen — White cells below 500 with no bacteria is negative.
• Clear lavage fluid with a few red cells on microscopy — Clear fluid with few red cells is negative.

⭐ Key point: DPL is now mostly replaced by FAST ultrasound and CT, but may be used when these are not available.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q31$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2097, $s$Before diagnostic peritoneal lavage, the nurse should ensure:$s$, $s$["A full bladder is maintained to lift the bowel up", "The patient has eaten recently to stimulate the bowel", "The patient is placed prone for the procedure", "The bladder is catheterised and the stomach decompressed"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The bladder is catheterised and the stomach decompressed
Before DPL, the nurse should: CATHETERISE THE BLADDER (to empty it so the needle doesn't puncture it) and DECOMPRESS THE STOMACH with a nasogastric tube (to prevent stomach puncture). This makes the procedure safer.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A full bladder is maintained to lift the bowel up — A full bladder risks being punctured by the needle.
• The patient has eaten recently to stimulate the bowel — The patient should not eat; recent food increases aspiration risk.
• The patient is placed prone for the procedure — DPL is performed with the patient supine, not prone.

⭐ Key point: Pregnancy and previous abdominal surgery are relative contraindications for DPL.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q32$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2098, $s$FAST has largely replaced DPL in unstable trauma patients because FAST is:$s$, $s$["Rapid, non-invasive, repeatable and done at the bedside", "Free of operator dependence", "Able to show solid organ grading in detail", "More sensitive for hollow viscus injury than any test"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Rapid, non-invasive, repeatable and done at the bedside
FAST (Focused Assessment with Sonography in Trauma) is a bedside ultrasound to look for free fluid (blood) in the abdomen and around the heart. It has largely replaced DPL because it is RAPID, NON-INVASIVE (no needle), REPEATABLE (can be repeated if the patient changes), and done at the BEDSIDE, which is ideal for unstable patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Free of operator dependence — FAST depends on the skill of the operator.
• Able to show solid organ grading in detail — FAST does not grade solid organ injuries well; CT is better.
• More sensitive for hollow viscus injury than any test — FAST is poor at detecting hollow organ (bowel) injury.

⭐ Key point: eFAST also checks for pneumothorax and haemothorax. A positive FAST in an unstable patient usually means surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q33$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2099, $s$A bladder scan in an ICU patient with a urinary catheter showing 450 mL suggests:$s$, $s$["Acute kidney injury with oliguria", "Normal residual volume in a catheterised patient", "Catheter blockage or malposition", "Over-hydration requiring diuretics"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Catheter blockage or malposition
A working catheter drains the bladder continuously, so a bladder scan should show very little urine. A scan showing 450 mL in a catheterised patient means urine is NOT draining: the CATHETER IS BLOCKED (kinked, clot, sediment) or MALPOSITIONED (e.g. balloon in the urethra).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute kidney injury with oliguria — AKI with oliguria means the kidneys aren't making urine, so the bladder would be empty.
• Normal residual volume in a catheterised patient — A normal residual in a catheterised patient should be close to zero.
• Over-hydration requiring diuretics — Overhydration would increase urine output; this doesn't explain a full bladder with a catheter.

⭐ Key point: Before treating "oliguria", always check the catheter: look for kinks, flush it, or bladder scan.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q35$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2100, $s$During central venous catheter insertion via the subclavian route, the patient is positioned:$s$, $s$["Prone, with the head turned away from the site", "Left lateral, with the knees flexed to the chest", "Head-down, to distend the vein and prevent air embolism", "Sitting upright at 90 degrees with the arms by the side"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Head-down, to distend the vein and prevent air embolism
For subclavian or internal jugular central line insertion, the patient is placed HEAD-DOWN (TRENDELENBURG position). This DISTENDS (fills) the vein, making it easier to puncture, and raises the venous pressure above atmospheric pressure, PREVENTING AIR from being sucked into the vein (AIR EMBOLISM).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prone, with the head turned away from the site — Prone positioning prevents access to the subclavian area.
• Left lateral, with the knees flexed to the chest — Left lateral with knees flexed is used for lumbar puncture.
• Sitting upright at 90 degrees with the arms by the side — Sitting upright reduces venous filling and increases air embolism risk.

⭐ Key point: Always confirm line position with a chest X-ray and check for pneumothorax after subclavian insertion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q37$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2101, $s$A polytrauma patient develops confusion, hypoxia and a petechial rash over the axillae 48 hours after femoral fracture. The likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Transfusion-related lung injury", "Pulmonary thromboembolism", "Septic shock from wound infection", "Fat embolism syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fat embolism syndrome
FAT EMBOLISM SYNDROME occurs 24–72 hours after long bone (especially femur) or pelvic fractures. Fat droplets enter the blood and block small vessels. The classic triad: RESPIRATORY problems (hypoxia, breathlessness), NEUROLOGICAL changes (confusion), and a PETECHIAL RASH (especially on the axillae, chest, conjunctiva and mouth).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Transfusion-related lung injury — TRALI occurs within 6 hours of transfusion and doesn't cause a petechial rash.
• Pulmonary thromboembolism — Pulmonary embolism doesn't cause a petechial rash.
• Septic shock from wound infection — Septic shock from wound infection doesn't typically cause a petechial rash in the axillae.

⭐ Key point: Prevention: early fracture stabilisation. Treatment is supportive (oxygen, ventilation if needed).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q40$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2102, $s$A pelvic fracture with blood at the urethral meatus suggests:$s$, $s$["Bladder infection; urgent urethral catheter is needed", "Urethral injury; avoid urethral catheterisation until imaged", "Renal injury that will need an urgent nephrectomy", "A normal finding after any pelvic fracture"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Urethral injury; avoid urethral catheterisation until imaged
A pelvic fracture with BLOOD AT THE URETHRAL MEATUS suggests a URETHRAL INJURY. Do NOT insert a urethral catheter until a RETROGRADE URETHROGRAM (imaging) confirms the urethra is intact. A catheter could turn a partial tear into a complete one.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bladder infection; urgent urethral catheter is needed — Blood at the meatus does not mean infection.
• Renal injury that will need an urgent nephrectomy — Kidney injury causes haematuria, not usually meatal blood.
• A normal finding after any pelvic fracture — Blood at the meatus is never a normal finding.

⭐ Key point: Other signs of urethral injury: inability to pass urine, a high-riding prostate, and perineal bruising.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q80$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2103, $s$A patient bitten by a dog of unknown vaccination status arrives in the emergency unit. The nurse's legal and clinical duty includes:$s$, $s$["Waiting for the dog to die before giving any treatment", "Wound care, rabies PEP and reporting the bite", "Treating only if the dog's owner agrees to pay", "Discharge without treatment if the wound is small"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Wound care, rabies PEP and reporting the bite
A dog bite from an animal whose vaccination status is UNKNOWN is a possible RABIES exposure. Rabies is almost 100% fatal once symptoms start, but it is PREVENTABLE if treatment is given promptly. The nurse's duty includes: WOUND CARE (wash immediately and thoroughly with soap and running water for at least 15 minutes, then apply an antiseptic such as povidone-iodine; avoid suturing deep bites immediately if possible), RABIES POST-EXPOSURE PROPHYLAXIS (PEP — rabies vaccine, plus rabies immunoglobulin for severe bites), tetanus prophylaxis and antibiotics if needed, and REPORTING the bite to the public health / veterinary authorities so the dog can be traced and observed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Waiting for the dog to die before giving any treatment — Waiting for the dog to die wastes vital time. PEP should start immediately; it can be stopped if the dog stays healthy for 10 days.
• Treating only if the dog's owner agrees to pay — Treatment cannot depend on payment from the dog owner. Emergency care is a professional and ethical duty.
• Discharge without treatment if the wound is small — Even small wounds can transmit rabies, especially on the face, head and hands. Size does not decide the need for PEP.

⭐ Key point: Rabies PEP must never be delayed. Document the exposure, the wound category, the doses given and the follow-up dates for the vaccine schedule.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q151$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2104, $s$Rabies post-exposure prophylaxis for a severe (category III) bite includes:$s$, $s$["Wound washing, rabies vaccine and immunoglobulin", "Tetanus toxoid only, with a booster", "Oral antibiotics only for seven days", "Observation of the dog for 10 days only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Wound washing, rabies vaccine and immunoglobulin
WHO classifies animal bites into categories. CATEGORY III (severe) exposures include single or multiple bites or scratches that break the skin, licks on broken skin, or contamination of mucous membranes (eyes, mouth) with saliva, and any exposure to bats. These need the FULL post-exposure prophylaxis: (1) immediate and thorough WOUND WASHING with soap and water for 15 minutes, (2) RABIES VACCINE (a full course), and (3) RABIES IMMUNOGLOBULIN (RIG), infiltrated into and around the wound to neutralise the virus immediately, before the vaccine has had time to produce antibodies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tetanus toxoid only, with a booster — Tetanus toxoid is important for all wounds, but it does not protect against rabies.
• Oral antibiotics only for seven days — Antibiotics may prevent bacterial wound infection, but they have no effect on the rabies virus.
• Observation of the dog for 10 days only — Observing the dog for 10 days alone is not enough. PEP must start immediately; observation can only guide stopping the vaccine later.

⭐ Key point: Category I (touching or feeding animals, licks on intact skin): no PEP. Category II (minor scratches without bleeding, nibbling of uncovered skin): wound washing and vaccine. Category III: washing, vaccine AND immunoglobulin.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q152$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2105, $s$Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation (TENS) in critical care is used to:$s$, $s$["Defibrillate the heart in ventricular fibrillation", "Pace the phrenic nerve to replace ventilation", "Measure muscle strength in ICU-acquired weakness", "Relieve pain by stimulating large nerve fibres"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Relieve pain by stimulating large nerve fibres
TENS (Transcutaneous Electrical Nerve Stimulation) uses a small battery device with electrodes on the skin that delivers mild electrical impulses. It RELIEVES PAIN by stimulating LARGE NERVE FIBRES (A-beta fibres) that carry touch sensation. According to the GATE CONTROL THEORY, these signals "close the gate" in the spinal cord, blocking pain signals carried by small fibres. It may also stimulate the release of natural painkillers (endorphins). It is used for post-operative pain, musculoskeletal pain and labour pain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Defibrillate the heart in ventricular fibrillation — Defibrillation requires a high-energy shock delivered by a defibrillator; TENS currents are far too weak.
• Pace the phrenic nerve to replace ventilation — Phrenic nerve pacing requires implanted electrodes; TENS cannot replace ventilation.
• Measure muscle strength in ICU-acquired weakness — Muscle strength in ICU-acquired weakness is measured using the MRC score, not TENS.

⭐ Key point: Avoid TENS over the front of the neck, over broken skin, and in patients with pacemakers (unless approved), and avoid placing electrodes over the uterus during pregnancy (except for labour use as directed).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q191$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2106, $s$In toxic epidermal necrolysis, more than 30% of the body surface is detached. ICU care is similar to:$s$, $s$["Major burns: fluids, warmth, wound care, infection control, nutrition", "Cellulitis: oral antibiotics and elevation of the limbs", "A simple allergy: antihistamines and observation on the ward", "Eczema: emollients and topical steroids at home"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Major burns: fluids, warmth, wound care, infection control, nutrition
TOXIC EPIDERMAL NECROLYSIS (TEN) is a severe, life-threatening skin reaction (usually to a drug, e.g. sulfonamides including co-trimoxazole, nevirapine, anticonvulsants, allopurinol). The top layer of skin (epidermis) separates from the body over large areas (more than 30% of body surface area). This causes problems very similar to MAJOR BURNS: loss of fluid and protein through the raw skin (needs IV FLUIDS), loss of heat (keep the room WARM), raw areas needing careful WOUND CARE (non-adherent dressings), high INFECTION risk (strict INFECTION CONTROL), and a high metabolic rate (high-calorie NUTRITION). Mucous membranes (eyes, mouth, genitals) are also affected.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cellulitis: oral antibiotics and elevation of the limbs — Cellulitis care (oral antibiotics and elevation) would be inadequate; TEN is not a simple skin infection.
• A simple allergy: antihistamines and observation on the ward — Antihistamines and ward observation are inadequate for a life-threatening condition.
• Eczema: emollients and topical steroids at home — Emollients and topical steroids are for eczema; TEN needs intensive or burns unit care.

⭐ Key point: STOP the causative drug immediately. Get eye care early (to prevent blindness) and treat in a burns unit or ICU. Mortality can be high, especially in older patients or those with large areas affected.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q196$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2107, $s$The nurse notes a 'seatbelt sign' across the abdomen of a crash victim. This should raise suspicion of:$s$, $s$["Cerebral contusion from a whiplash movement", "Hollow viscus and lumbar (Chance) spine injuries", "Isolated skin bruising without deeper injury", "Pulmonary embolism from the lower limb veins"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hollow viscus and lumbar (Chance) spine injuries
A "seatbelt sign" is bruising across the abdomen from a lap belt in a car crash. The belt compresses abdominal organs against the spine at high speed, causing HOLLOW VISCUS injuries (bowel perforation, mesenteric tears) and a CHANCE FRACTURE (a flexion-distraction fracture of the lumbar spine).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cerebral contusion from a whiplash movement — A whiplash cerebral contusion isn't linked to the seatbelt sign.
• Isolated skin bruising without deeper injury — Assuming only bruising could miss serious injuries.
• Pulmonary embolism from the lower limb veins — PE isn't an immediate concern from a seatbelt sign.

⭐ Key point: Bowel injuries can be missed early. Repeat abdominal examination and CT are needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q22$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2108, $s$The main limitation of FAST compared with DPL or CT is that FAST:$s$, $s$["It cannot detect any free fluid in the abdomen", "It requires the patient to be moved to the CT room", "It can miss hollow viscus and retroperitoneal injuries", "It uses ionising radiation like plain X-rays"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It can miss hollow viscus and retroperitoneal injuries
FAST ultrasound is good at detecting FREE FLUID (blood) in the abdomen and around the heart, but it can MISS HOLLOW VISCUS injuries (bowel perforation, which may have little free fluid at first) and RETROPERITONEAL injuries (kidneys, pancreas, duodenum, major vessels), which are behind the peritoneum.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It cannot detect any free fluid in the abdomen — FAST CAN detect free fluid.
• It requires the patient to be moved to the CT room — FAST is done at the bedside; no CT room is needed.
• It uses ionising radiation like plain X-rays — FAST uses ultrasound, not ionising radiation.

⭐ Key point: A negative FAST doesn't rule out serious injury. Use CT in stable patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q23$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2109, $s$In a polytrauma patient with a head injury and hypotension, the target systolic BP is usually:$s$, $s$["At least 100–110 mmHg to protect cerebral perfusion", "Above 180 mmHg to force blood into the brain", "Any value, as BP does not affect head injury", "70–80 mmHg, as permissive hypotension in trauma"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: At least 100–110 mmHg to protect cerebral perfusion
In a polytrauma patient WITH a HEAD INJURY, low BP is very harmful: a single episode of hypotension DOUBLES mortality in traumatic brain injury, because it reduces blood flow to the injured brain. The target systolic BP is at least 100–110 mmHg (higher in older patients) to protect cerebral perfusion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Above 180 mmHg to force blood into the brain — Above 180 is too high and may worsen bleeding.
• Any value, as BP does not affect head injury — BP DOES affect head injury outcomes.
• 70–80 mmHg, as permissive hypotension in trauma — Permissive hypotension (70–80) is NOT used in head injury.

⭐ Key point: Avoid hypotension and hypoxia in brain injury; both worsen outcomes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q31$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2110, $s$Massive transfusion in trauma is generally defined as:$s$, $s$["One unit every 24 hours for a period of one week", "One blood volume in 24 hours (about 10 units of red cells)", "Any transfusion given in the emergency department", "Two units of red cells transfused within 24 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: One blood volume in 24 hours (about 10 units of red cells)
MASSIVE TRANSFUSION is generally defined as replacing ONE whole BLOOD VOLUME (about 10 units of red cells in an adult) within 24 HOURS. Other definitions include 50% of blood volume in 3 hours, or 4 units or more in 1 hour.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• One unit every 24 hours for a period of one week — One unit a day for a week is a small, slow transfusion.
• Any transfusion given in the emergency department — Any transfusion in the emergency department isn't massive by definition.
• Two units of red cells transfused within 24 hours — Two units in 24 hours is a small transfusion.

⭐ Key point: Massive transfusion protocols give red cells, FFP and platelets in a fixed ratio (about 1:1:1).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q32$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2111, $s$A trauma patient arrives with an unstable pelvic fracture and hypotension. The immediate step is:$s$, $s$["Elevate both legs on pillows to raise the BP", "Log-roll repeatedly to examine the back fully", "Apply a pelvic binder at the greater trochanters", "Insert a urethral catheter before anything else"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Apply a pelvic binder at the greater trochanters
An UNSTABLE pelvic fracture can cause massive bleeding into the pelvis (from veins and bone). A PELVIC BINDER applied at the level of the GREATER TROCHANTERS (hips), not the iliac crests, closes the pelvic ring, reduces the pelvic volume, and slows venous bleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Elevate both legs on pillows to raise the BP — Elevating the legs doesn't stop pelvic bleeding.
• Log-roll repeatedly to examine the back fully — Repeated log rolling disturbs clots and worsens bleeding.
• Insert a urethral catheter before anything else — Urethral catheterisation may be unsafe (urethral injury) and isn't the immediate priority.

⭐ Key point: Pelvic fractures with ongoing shock may need angiographic embolisation or surgical packing.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q34$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2112, $s$The secondary survey in trauma is performed:$s$, $s$["Before any assessment of the airway or breathing", "Only after the patient is discharged from hospital", "After the primary survey and resuscitation are under way", "Instead of the primary survey if the patient is stable"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: After the primary survey and resuscitation are under way
The SECONDARY SURVEY is a full head-to-toe examination with an AMPLE history. It is done AFTER the PRIMARY SURVEY (ABCDE) and resuscitation are under way, and the patient is stable enough. Its aim is to find all injuries not found in the primary survey.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Before any assessment of the airway or breathing — Airway and breathing always come FIRST (primary survey).
• Only after the patient is discharged from hospital — It is done in hospital early, not after discharge.
• Instead of the primary survey if the patient is stable — It doesn't replace the primary survey.

⭐ Key point: If the patient deteriorates during the secondary survey, go back to the primary survey (ABCDE).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q35$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2113, $s$The AMPLE history in trauma stands for:$s$, $s$["Alcohol, Mental state, Pupils, Limbs and the ECG", "Age, Mechanism, Pain, Location and Effects of injury", "Allergies, Medications, Past history, Last meal, Events", "Airway, Mouth, Pulse, Lungs and Eyes of the patient"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Allergies, Medications, Past history, Last meal, Events
AMPLE is a quick trauma history: A = Allergies, M = Medications (such as anticoagulants or beta-blockers), P = Past medical history (and pregnancy), L = Last meal (aspiration risk during anaesthesia), E = Events and environment related to the injury (mechanism).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Alcohol, Mental state, Pupils, Limbs and the ECG — Alcohol, mental state, pupils, limbs and ECG isn't AMPLE.
• Age, Mechanism, Pain, Location and Effects of injury — Age, mechanism, pain, location and effects isn't AMPLE.
• Airway, Mouth, Pulse, Lungs and Eyes of the patient — Airway, mouth, pulse, lungs and eyes isn't AMPLE.

⭐ Key point: Anticoagulants and beta-blockers change how trauma patients present and bleed. Always ask about medicines.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q36$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2114, $s$A urinary catheter should not be inserted in a trauma patient with:$s$, $s$["Blood at the meatus, perineal bruising or high-riding prostate", "A full bladder after an uncomplicated pelvic operation", "A stable femoral shaft fracture held in traction", "A head injury with a GCS of 13 and confusion"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Blood at the meatus, perineal bruising or high-riding prostate
Don't insert a urethral catheter in a trauma patient with signs of URETHRAL INJURY: BLOOD at the urethral MEATUS, PERINEAL or scrotal BRUISING, or a HIGH-RIDING PROSTATE on rectal examination (in men with pelvic fractures). Catheterisation could convert a partial tear into a complete one. A urologist may place a suprapubic catheter instead.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A full bladder after an uncomplicated pelvic operation — A full bladder after uncomplicated pelvic surgery can be catheterised.
• A stable femoral shaft fracture held in traction — A stable femoral fracture isn't a contraindication.
• A head injury with a GCS of 13 and confusion — A head injury isn't a contraindication.

⭐ Key point: A retrograde urethrogram can confirm urethral injury.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q37$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2115, $s$Damage control surgery in polytrauma means:$s$, $s$["Complete repair of every injury in one long single operation", "A short operation to control bleeding and contamination; repair later", "Delaying any surgery until the patient's temperature is normal", "Only conservative treatment, with surgery avoided altogether"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A short operation to control bleeding and contamination; repair later
DAMAGE CONTROL SURGERY is used in critically injured patients who are cold, acidotic and coagulopathic (the lethal triad). A SHORT operation CONTROLS BLEEDING and CONTAMINATION (packing, clamping, closing bowel ends), then the patient goes to ICU for warming and resuscitation, and DEFINITIVE repair happens later (24–48 hours).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Complete repair of every injury in one long single operation — A long single operation in an unstable patient worsens the lethal triad.
• Delaying any surgery until the patient's temperature is normal — Surgery can't be delayed until the temperature is normal; bleeding must be controlled now.
• Only conservative treatment, with surgery avoided altogether — Avoiding surgery isn't an option when there is surgical bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Damage control resuscitation: permissive hypotension (no head injury), early blood products, TXA, warming, and damage control surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q38$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2116, $s$Fluid resuscitation in a severe burns patient arriving at 4 hours post-burn requires that:$s$, $s$["No fluid at all until the burns have been cleaned and dressed", "Half of the volume over the next 8 hours from the arrival time", "The full 24-hour volume calculated from the time of arrival", "The first half of the Parkland volume over the remaining 4 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The first half of the Parkland volume over the remaining 4 hours
The Parkland formula calculates 24-hour fluid from the TIME OF INJURY. Half is given in the FIRST 8 HOURS AFTER THE BURN. If the patient arrives 4 hours after the burn (with no fluid given yet), the first half must be given over the REMAINING 4 HOURS of that 8-hour period, to catch up.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No fluid at all until the burns have been cleaned and dressed — Withholding fluid until wounds are dressed causes burn shock.
• Half of the volume over the next 8 hours from the arrival time — Starting the 8-hour clock from arrival would under-resuscitate.
• The full 24-hour volume calculated from the time of arrival — The full 24-hour volume from arrival isn't correct; timing is from injury.

⭐ Key point: Subtract any fluid already given before arrival, and adjust the rate to urine output (0.5–1 mL/kg/hour).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q40$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2117, $s$Hypothermia in trauma (accidental hypothermia) is considered severe below:$s$, $s$["34 °C", "28 °C", "35 °C", "32 °C"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 28 °C
Hypothermia severity: MILD 32–35 °C, MODERATE 28–32 °C, SEVERE BELOW 28 °C. Severe hypothermia causes coma, a very slow pulse, low BP, and a high risk of VF and cardiac arrest.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 34 °C — Below 34 °C is mild to moderate.
• 35 °C — Below 35 °C is the threshold for any hypothermia.
• 32 °C — Below 32 °C is moderate hypothermia.

⭐ Key point: Handle severely hypothermic patients gently; rough handling can trigger VF.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q79$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2118, $s$Brain stem death testing can be performed only when:$s$, $s$["The patient has been in the ICU for a full 24 hours on the ventilator", "The family has formally requested that the tests should be performed", "The patient is still receiving sedative infusions at a reduced rate", "Reversible causes such as drugs, hypothermia and metabolic disorders are excluded"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reversible causes such as drugs, hypothermia and metabolic disorders are excluded
BRAIN STEM DEATH testing can only be performed when the cause of coma is a known, irreversible brain injury AND REVERSIBLE CAUSES have been EXCLUDED. These include: DRUGS (sedatives, opioids, muscle relaxants) — allowing time for them to clear; HYPOTHERMIA (temperature should be above 34 °C); METABOLIC/ENDOCRINE disturbances (very abnormal sodium, glucose, etc.); and circulatory problems (very low BP). Only then can brainstem reflexes be tested reliably.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient has been in the ICU for a full 24 hours on the ventilator — There is no fixed rule that the patient must be ventilated for 24 hours; what matters is that preconditions are met.
• The family has formally requested that the tests should be performed — Testing is a medical decision based on criteria, not something done because the family requests it.
• The patient is still receiving sedative infusions at a reduced rate — Sedative drugs must be stopped and have cleared from the body. Testing while sedatives are running may give false results.

⭐ Key point: Tests are performed by two senior, experienced doctors, done twice. They check pupils, corneal reflex, gag and cough reflexes, eye movement responses and an apnoea test. The nurse supports the family and documents carefully.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q141$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2119, $s$A patient with animal bite and hydrophobia, agitation and spasms most likely has:$s$, $s$["Clinical rabies, which is almost always fatal", "Simple anxiety after the dog bite", "Bacterial meningitis from the wound", "Tetanus with lockjaw from a wound"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Clinical rabies, which is almost always fatal
RABIES is a viral infection of the brain spread by the bite or scratch of an infected animal (usually dogs in Nigeria). After an incubation period (often 1–3 months), it causes HYDROPHOBIA (fear of water — painful throat spasms when trying to drink), AEROPHOBIA (spasms triggered by air on the face), AGITATION, confusion, hallucinations, excessive salivation and muscle spasms. Once these symptoms appear, rabies is ALMOST ALWAYS FATAL. Care is supportive and comfort-focused: a quiet, dark room, sedation and pain relief.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple anxiety after the dog bite — Anxiety does not cause hydrophobia and spasms. These signs are specific to rabies.
• Bacterial meningitis from the wound — Meningitis causes fever, headache and neck stiffness, but not hydrophobia.
• Tetanus with lockjaw from a wound — Tetanus causes lockjaw and muscle spasms, but not hydrophobia, and the patient is usually mentally clear.

⭐ Key point: Rabies is PREVENTABLE: wash the bite wound with soap and running water for 15 minutes, then give post-exposure rabies vaccine (and rabies immunoglobulin for severe bites) promptly. Staff caring for patients use barrier precautions (gloves, gown, mask, eye protection).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q155$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2120, $s$Disaster planning for an ICU includes:$s$, $s$["No plans at all, because disasters are very rare", "Surge plans, staff call-in lists and equipment reserves", "Closing the unit completely during an emergency", "Leaving the decisions to visitors and relatives"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Surge plans, staff call-in lists and equipment reserves
DISASTER PLANNING ensures the ICU can cope with a sudden large number of critically ill patients (e.g. bombing, building collapse, epidemic). It includes: SURGE PLANS (extra beds and areas), STAFF CALL-IN LISTS, EQUIPMENT RESERVES (ventilators, oxygen, drugs), clear roles, communication systems, and regular DRILLS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No plans at all, because disasters are very rare — Disasters are unpredictable; planning is essential.
• Closing the unit completely during an emergency — Closing the unit in an emergency would deny critical care when it is most needed.
• Leaving the decisions to visitors and relatives — Visitors and relatives cannot make professional decisions.

⭐ Key point: Regular drills test readiness and identify weaknesses before a real disaster.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q178$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2121, $s$Chest physiotherapy with percussion is contraindicated in:$s$, $s$["Retained secretions in the lower lobes", "Cystic fibrosis with thick sputum", "Lobar atelectasis on the chest X-ray", "Unstable rib fractures and active bleeding"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Unstable rib fractures and active bleeding
Chest physiotherapy with PERCUSSION (clapping on the chest) is CONTRAINDICATED in UNSTABLE RIB FRACTURES (it could displace fractures and injure the lungs) and ACTIVE BLEEDING (e.g. haemoptysis, coagulopathy, low platelets), because it could worsen injury and bleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Retained secretions in the lower lobes — Retained secretions are an indication for chest physiotherapy.
• Cystic fibrosis with thick sputum — Cystic fibrosis with thick sputum is a classic indication.
• Lobar atelectasis on the chest X-ray — Lobar atelectasis (collapse) is an indication; physiotherapy helps re-expand the lung.

⭐ Key point: Other contraindications: unstable cardiovascular status, raised intracranial pressure, recent spinal injury, osteoporosis and untreated pneumothorax.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q190$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2122, $s$Early mobilisation in ICU is safe when:$s$, $s$["The patient's SpO₂ is below 85% on high FiO₂", "The patient's intracranial pressure is uncontrolled", "The patient is on increasing doses of vasopressors", "The patient is cardiovascularly and respiratorily stable"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The patient is cardiovascularly and respiratorily stable
EARLY MOBILISATION (sitting up, standing, walking) in ICU reduces muscle weakness, delirium, ventilator days and length of stay. It is safe when the patient is CARDIOVASCULARLY STABLE (stable BP and heart rate, not on rising vasopressors) and RESPIRATORY STABLE (adequate oxygenation on reasonable ventilator settings).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient's SpO₂ is below 85% on high FiO₂ — SpO2 below 85% on high FiO2 is severe respiratory instability; mobilisation is unsafe.
• The patient's intracranial pressure is uncontrolled — Uncontrolled intracranial pressure is a contraindication.
• The patient is on increasing doses of vasopressors — Increasing vasopressor doses show cardiovascular instability; mobilisation is unsafe.

⭐ Key point: Mobilisation progresses step by step: sitting in bed → sitting on the edge → standing → marching on the spot → walking.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q192$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2123, $s$Passive range-of-motion exercises in the unconscious patient prevent:$s$, $s$["Hypoglycaemia", "Urinary infection", "Contractures and muscle wasting", "Pressure ulcers on the heels"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Contractures and muscle wasting
When a patient can't move, joints stiffen and muscles shorten (contractures), and muscles weaken and shrink (wasting) within days. Passive range-of-motion exercises, where the nurse or physiotherapist moves each joint through its full range at least twice a day, keep joints flexible and maintain blood flow to muscles. Splints keep the ankles at 90° to prevent foot drop.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypoglycaemia — ROM exercises don't affect blood sugar.
• Urinary infection — ROM doesn't prevent urinary infection; catheter care does.
• Pressure ulcers on the heels — Heel pressure ulcers are prevented by turning and offloading the heels, not by ROM exercises alone.

⭐ Key point: Early mobilisation (passive, then active, then sitting out and walking) reduces ICU-acquired weakness and shortens recovery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q75$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2124, $s$In a trauma patient needing intubation, the airway is secured with:$s$, $s$["Awake nasal intubation in every trauma case", "RSI with manual in-line stabilisation", "Blind finger sweep and bag ventilation only", "Head tilt and chin lift without neck support"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: RSI with manual in-line stabilisation
A trauma patient needing intubation may have a cervical spine injury and a full stomach. RAPID SEQUENCE INDUCTION (RSI) means pre-oxygenation, then a sedative and a fast-acting muscle relaxant given together, then intubation without bag ventilation where possible. This reduces the risk of aspiration. MANUAL IN-LINE STABILISATION (an assistant holds the head and neck still) protects the spine, with the front of the collar opened.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Awake nasal intubation in every trauma case — Awake nasal intubation is not routine in trauma; it is contraindicated in basal skull fracture.
• Blind finger sweep and bag ventilation only — A blind finger sweep can push objects further in and is not a definitive airway.
• Head tilt and chin lift without neck support — Head tilt and chin lift move the neck and risk spinal cord injury; use jaw thrust instead.

⭐ Key point: Always confirm tube placement with waveform capnography (EtCO₂) after intubation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q84$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2125, $s$Nasal intubation is avoided in trauma with:$s$, $s$["Suspected basal skull fracture", "Isolated limb fractures", "Mandibular fracture only", "Simple scalp lacerations"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Suspected basal skull fracture
A basal skull fracture (signs: raccoon eyes, Battle's sign behind the ear, CSF leak from the nose or ear) can break the thin bone between the nose and the brain (the cribriform plate). A nasal tube (nasal ETT or NG tube) could pass through the fracture INTO THE BRAIN. Use the mouth instead: oral intubation and an orogastric tube.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Isolated limb fractures — Isolated limb fractures do not affect the nasal route.
• Mandibular fracture only — A mandibular fracture may make oral intubation harder; nasal intubation may even be used, but it isn't the classic contraindication.
• Simple scalp lacerations — Scalp lacerations don't affect the nasal route.

⭐ Key point: CSF leak test: clear fluid from the nose that tests positive for glucose, or a "halo sign" (a clear ring around blood on gauze).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q85$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2126, $s$Spinal shock after cord injury is characterised by:$s$, $s$["Normal reflexes with weakness", "Hypertension and tachycardia", "Spastic paralysis with hyperreflexia at once", "Flaccid paralysis and areflexia below the injury"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Flaccid paralysis and areflexia below the injury
Spinal shock happens right after a spinal cord injury. All function below the injury temporarily stops: FLACCID (floppy) paralysis, loss of all reflexes (areflexia), loss of sensation, and loss of bladder and bowel control. It usually lasts days to weeks. As it resolves, reflexes return and may become brisk, with spasticity (if it is an upper motor neurone injury).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal reflexes with weakness — Reflexes are LOST, not normal.
• Hypertension and tachycardia — High BP and fast pulse are not typical of spinal shock.
• Spastic paralysis with hyperreflexia at once — Spastic paralysis with brisk reflexes comes LATER, after spinal shock resolves, not at once.

⭐ Key point: Spinal shock is a neurological state (no reflexes). Neurogenic shock is a circulatory state (low BP and slow pulse). They can occur together.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q96$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2127, $s$Malrotation with midgut volvulus is a surgical emergency because:$s$, $s$["It causes only mild constipation in the infant", "It affects only the stomach and duodenum", "The twisted mesenteric artery causes gut ischaemia", "It resolves without treatment in most babies"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The twisted mesenteric artery causes gut ischaemia
In malrotation, the bowel didn't rotate and fix properly during fetal development. The small bowel can TWIST around its blood supply (volvulus), twisting the superior mesenteric artery. This cuts off the blood supply to most of the small bowel, causing ischaemia and death of the gut within hours. It presents with BILIOUS (green) vomiting in a baby and needs emergency surgery (Ladd's procedure).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It causes only mild constipation in the infant — It causes severe illness, not mild constipation.
• It affects only the stomach and duodenum — It affects the whole midgut (most of the small bowel and part of the colon), not only the stomach and duodenum.
• It resolves without treatment in most babies — Without surgery, the bowel dies and the baby can die.

⭐ Key point: Bilious (green) vomiting in a newborn or infant is a surgical emergency until proven otherwise. Keep nil by mouth, place an NG tube on free drainage, give IV fluids and refer urgently.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q105$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2128, $s$Felony-related cases in ICU (e.g. gunshot wounds) require the nurse to:$s$, $s$["Refuse to treat the patient until police arrive", "Discharge the patient quickly to avoid trouble", "Give care and notify police as the law requires", "Investigate the crime personally at the bedside"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Give care and notify police as the law requires
Felony-related injuries (gunshot wounds, stab wounds, assault) are medico-legal cases. The nurse's first duty is to GIVE CARE: emergency treatment is never delayed or refused. At the same time, the nurse must NOTIFY the police as required by law, preserve evidence properly (bag clothing, keep bullets, maintain the chain of custody), and document carefully.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Refuse to treat the patient until police arrive — Refusing treatment until police arrive is illegal and unethical (the right to emergency care).
• Discharge the patient quickly to avoid trouble — Early discharge to avoid trouble is unsafe and unethical.
• Investigate the crime personally at the bedside — Investigating the crime is the police's job, not the nurse's.

⭐ Key point: In Nigeria, the Compulsory Treatment and Care for Victims of Gunshots Act (2017) requires hospitals to treat gunshot victims immediately, without waiting for a police report.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q156$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2129, $s$For diagnostic peritoneal lavage, the nurse instils:$s$, $s$["50 mL of sterile water, given cold", "200 mL of X-ray contrast medium", "5 litres of 5% dextrose over one hour", "1 litre of warm saline (10 mL/kg in children)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1 litre of warm saline (10 mL/kg in children)
In diagnostic peritoneal lavage (DPL), a catheter is placed into the abdomen, and 1 LITRE of WARM normal saline (10 mL/kg in children) is run in, then drained back out by gravity. The fluid is sent to the lab for red cells, white cells, bacteria and food particles, to detect bleeding or bowel injury after trauma.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50 mL of sterile water, given cold — 50 mL is too little, and cold fluid causes hypothermia.
• 200 mL of X-ray contrast medium — X-ray contrast isn't used for DPL.
• 5 litres of 5% dextrose over one hour — 5 litres of dextrose is far too much and the wrong fluid.

⭐ Key point: DPL has mostly been replaced by FAST ultrasound and CT, but may still be used where they aren't available.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q30$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2130, $s$A bladder scan is used in ICU to:$s$, $s$["Replace the need for urine cultures", "Estimate bladder volume non-invasively", "Diagnose kidney and ureteric stones", "Measure renal blood flow and GFR"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Estimate bladder volume non-invasively
A bladder scan uses portable ultrasound to ESTIMATE the volume of urine in the bladder without inserting a catheter. In ICU it helps check for urinary retention (after a catheter is removed), measure post-void residual, and avoid unnecessary catheterisation (which reduces CAUTI risk).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Replace the need for urine cultures — It doesn't replace urine cultures for infection.
• Diagnose kidney and ureteric stones — It doesn't diagnose kidney stones.
• Measure renal blood flow and GFR — It doesn't measure renal blood flow or GFR.

⭐ Key point: If the scan shows more than 400–500 mL with the patient unable to void, consider in-and-out (intermittent) catheterisation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q31$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2131, $s$After removing a central line, the patient is asked to:$s$, $s$["Take a deep breath in as the line is pulled out", "Sit upright with the legs down beside the bed", "Cough repeatedly to clear the chest during removal", "Hold the breath (Valsalva) during removal; seal the site"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hold the breath (Valsalva) during removal; seal the site
When a central line is removed, there is a risk that AIR enters the vein through the opening (air embolism), especially if the patient breathes in. Lie the patient flat or slightly head-down and ask them to HOLD THEIR BREATH or perform a VALSALVA manoeuvre (bear down) while the line is pulled out. This raises chest pressure and prevents air being sucked in. Then apply pressure and an airtight dressing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Take a deep breath in as the line is pulled out — Taking a deep breath IN lowers chest pressure and can suck air into the vein.
• Sit upright with the legs down beside the bed — Sitting upright increases air embolism risk.
• Cough repeatedly to clear the chest during removal — Coughing during removal is unpredictable and unsafe.

⭐ Key point: Apply firm pressure for at least 5 minutes, then an occlusive (airtight) dressing for 24 hours. Check the catheter tip is intact.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q34$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2132, $s$Volutrauma refers to lung injury from:$s$, $s$["Infection of the lower airways", "Over-distension by large tidal volumes", "Low PEEP causing alveolar collapse", "Oxygen toxicity from high FiO₂"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Over-distension by large tidal volumes
VOLUTRAUMA is lung injury caused by OVER-DISTENSION of the alveoli from large tidal volumes, which stretches and tears the alveolar walls, causing inflammation and leaking. It is the main reason for low tidal volume (6 mL/kg) ventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Infection of the lower airways — Airway infection is VAP or tracheobronchitis.
• Low PEEP causing alveolar collapse — Repeated opening and closing of alveoli (from low PEEP) is ATELECTRAUMA.
• Oxygen toxicity from high FiO₂ — Injury from high oxygen is oxygen toxicity.

⭐ Key point: Types of ventilator lung injury: barotrauma (high pressure, causing air leaks), volutrauma (over-stretching), atelectrauma (collapse–reopening), and biotrauma (inflammation).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q74$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2133, $s$A burn patient's protein requirement is:$s$, $s$["Increased, about 1.5–2 g/kg/day", "Normal at 0.8 g/kg/day", "Reduced to 0.5 g/kg/day", "Nil until healing begins"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Increased, about 1.5–2 g/kg/day
Major burns cause a HYPERMETABOLIC state: energy and protein use can almost double. Protein is lost through the burn wound exudate and used for tissue repair and immunity. Protein needs rise to about 1.5–2.0 g/kg/day (sometimes more in large burns), with early enteral feeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal at 0.8 g/kg/day — 0.8 g/kg/day is a normal healthy adult's need.
• Reduced to 0.5 g/kg/day — 0.5 g/kg/day is too low and causes muscle wasting.
• Nil until healing begins — Nil protein would stop healing completely.

⭐ Key point: Start enteral feeding early (within 24 hours) in major burns. It reduces the stress response and infections.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q151$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2134, $s$A contraindication to chest percussion is:$s$, $s$["Sputum retention in the lower lobes", "Pneumonia with purulent secretions", "Rib fractures or severe osteoporosis", "Atelectasis of the left lower lobe"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Rib fractures or severe osteoporosis
Chest percussion involves repeated impacts on the chest wall. With RIB FRACTURES, it causes severe pain and can move the broken ends, injuring the lung. With severe OSTEOPOROSIS, it can CAUSE fractures. These are contraindications.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sputum retention in the lower lobes — Sputum retention is an INDICATION.
• Pneumonia with purulent secretions — Pneumonia with secretions is an INDICATION.
• Atelectasis of the left lower lobe — Atelectasis is an INDICATION.

⭐ Key point: Other contraindications: low platelets, haemoptysis, untreated pneumothorax, unstable haemodynamics, raised ICP and chest wall burns or wounds.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q158$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2135, $s$Paradoxical chest wall movement, where the flail segment moves inward on inspiration, is characteristic of:$s$, $s$["Pericardial tamponade", "Flail chest", "Pulmonary contusion", "Simple pneumothorax"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Flail chest
A FLAIL CHEST happens when 2 or more adjacent ribs are broken in 2 or more places, so a segment of chest wall is "floating" free. On breathing IN, the negative pressure in the chest sucks the flail segment INWARD while the rest of the chest moves out. On breathing out, it moves outward. This PARADOXICAL movement reduces ventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pericardial tamponade — Pericardial tamponade affects the heart (low BP, high JVP, muffled heart sounds), not chest wall movement.
• Pulmonary contusion — Pulmonary contusion is bruising of the lung; it often occurs WITH flail chest but doesn't cause paradoxical movement itself.
• Simple pneumothorax — A simple pneumothorax reduces chest movement on that side; it doesn't make a segment move inward.

⭐ Key point: Flail chest management: oxygen, strong pain relief (epidural, nerve blocks), physiotherapy, and ventilation if needed. The underlying lung contusion causes most of the hypoxia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q21$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2136, $s$A 24 year old male patient is brought to the Emergency by his family with head injury. Initial survey was done by the Nurse in Triage. The Nurse informed physician that he has no respiratory efforts. Which mode of the ventilation will be used for this patient?$s$, $s$["Volume controlled", "Assist Control Ventilation", "Synchronized Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation (SIMV)", "Pressure controlled"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assist Control Ventilation
This patient has NO respiratory effort, so the ventilator must provide every breath. ASSIST-CONTROL (AC) ventilation guarantees a set number of full breaths per minute (set rate and tidal volume), and if the patient later starts to breathe, each effort is fully supported. This ensures adequate ventilation in an apnoeic head-injured patient.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Volume controlled — Volume control is part of AC, but the question is about the mode that guarantees breaths whatever the patient's effort.
• Synchronized Intermittent Mandatory Ventilation (SIMV) — SIMV relies on spontaneous breathing between mandatory breaths; this patient has none.
• Pressure controlled — Pressure control is possible, but AC is the keyed answer for full support.

⭐ Key point: In head injury, ventilate to normal CO₂ (PaCO₂ 35–40 mmHg); avoid hyperventilation, which narrows brain vessels.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q27$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2137, $s$A 35-year-old woman from the Jos Plateau presents with an anterior neck swelling that moves with swallowing. She has no systemic symptoms. What is the most likely diagnosis?$s$, $s$["Thyrotoxicosis", "Simple non-toxic goitre", "Follicular carcinoma", "Hashimoto's thyroiditis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Simple non-toxic goitre
A painless swelling in the front of the neck that MOVES WITH SWALLOWING is a THYROID swelling. Without systemic symptoms (no weight loss, palpitations, heat intolerance), the most likely diagnosis is a SIMPLE NON-TOXIC GOITRE. In Nigeria, especially the Jos Plateau and other iodine-deficient areas, IODINE DEFICIENCY is a common cause.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Thyrotoxicosis — Thyrotoxicosis causes symptoms (weight loss, tremor, palpitations).
• Follicular carcinoma — Follicular carcinoma is less likely and usually presents as a nodule.
• Hashimoto's thyroiditis — Hashimoto's thyroiditis often causes hypothyroid symptoms.

⭐ Key point: Iodised salt has greatly reduced iodine-deficiency goitre. Large goitres can compress the trachea, so assess the airway before surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q85$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2138, $s$The basal metabolic rate (BMR) in a major burns patient can increase by:$s$, $s$["100–200%", "500–600%", "No change", "10–20%"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 100–200%
A major burn triggers a massive stress response (catecholamines, cortisol, inflammation), and heat and fluid are lost through the burn wound. The basal metabolic rate rises by 100–200% (the body may need double or triple its normal energy), and this can last for months. Muscle protein is broken down rapidly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 500–600% — 500–600% is far higher than seen in burns.
• No change — There IS a large change.
• 10–20% — 10–20% is the rise seen after minor surgery, not major burns.

⭐ Key point: Burns patients need early enteral feeding, high protein (1.5–2 g/kg/day), a warm room (28–32 °C) and sometimes drugs (propranolol, oxandrolone) to reduce hypermetabolism.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q101$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2139, $s$Effective disaster response coordination between a hospital and emergency medical services primarily requires:$s$, $s$["Each agency working entirely independently with no communication", "Clear communication, pre-established protocols, and a defined command structure", "No advance planning, since disasters are unpredictable", "Only the hospital's decisions being followed, regardless of on-scene conditions"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Clear communication, pre-established protocols, and a defined command structure
In a disaster, many agencies (hospital, ambulance service, fire service, police) must work together. This needs CLEAR COMMUNICATION channels, PRE-ESTABLISHED PROTOCOLS (a hospital major incident plan, practised in drills), and a defined COMMAND STRUCTURE (an incident commander, clear roles). Without these, there is chaos, duplication and lost patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Each agency working entirely independently with no communication — Agencies working alone without communication leads to confusion.
• No advance planning, since disasters are unpredictable — Disasters ARE planned for in advance (risk assessment, drills).
• Only the hospital's decisions being followed, regardless of on-scene conditions — Hospital decisions must take account of on-scene conditions reported by ambulance teams.

⭐ Key point: Hospital major incident plan: activation, command and control, communication, triage, surge capacity (extra beds and staff), and recovery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q102$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2140, $s$Supraglottic inhalational injury is primarily caused by:$s$, $s$["Heat", "Carbon monoxide only", "Chemical irritants only", "Bacterial infection"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Heat
Supraglottic (upper airway, above the vocal cords) inhalation injury is caused mainly by HEAT (hot air, steam, flames). The heat burns the mouth, pharynx and larynx, causing rapid SWELLING that can block the airway within hours. Lower airway injury (below the cords) is mainly caused by chemicals in smoke, because the upper airway cools hot air before it reaches the lungs (except steam).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Carbon monoxide only — Carbon monoxide causes poisoning of the blood (it binds haemoglobin), not upper airway burns.
• Chemical irritants only — Chemical irritants mainly injure the LOWER airways.
• Bacterial infection — Infection is a later complication, not the cause.

⭐ Key point: Signs of upper airway burn: facial burns, singed nasal hairs, soot in the mouth, hoarseness and stridor. Intubate early before swelling closes the airway.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q104$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2141, $s$Compartment syndrome following an electrical burn is treated with:$s$, $s$["Compression bandaging", "Amputation immediately", "Ice packs only", "Fasciotomy"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fasciotomy
Electrical burns cause deep damage to muscles along the current's path, with severe swelling inside the tight muscle compartments (fascia). This raises the pressure, cutting off blood flow (compartment syndrome). The treatment is FASCIOTOMY: surgically cutting the fascia to release the pressure and save the muscle and limb.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Compression bandaging — Compression bandaging INCREASES compartment pressure.
• Amputation immediately — Amputation is a last resort for dead limbs, not the first treatment.
• Ice packs only — Ice packs don't relieve compartment pressure.

⭐ Key point: Signs of compartment syndrome: pain out of proportion, pain on passive stretch, tense swelling, numbness, and late loss of pulses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q106$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2142, $s$Using the Rule of Nines, each entire upper limb of an adult represents approximately what percentage of total body surface area?$s$, $s$["9%", "18%", "36%", "4.5%"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 9%
In the adult Rule of Nines, each ENTIRE upper limb (arm) is 9% of total body surface area. Other areas: head and neck 9%, front of trunk 18%, back of trunk 18%, each leg 18%, perineum 1%. Total = 100%.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 18% — 18% is the value for each whole leg, or the front or back of the trunk.
• 36% — 36% is the value for both legs, or the whole trunk.
• 4.5% — 4.5% is the value for the front OR back of one arm.

⭐ Key point: For small or scattered burns, the patient's PALM (with fingers) is about 1% of their TBSA. Children have relatively bigger heads (use the Lund–Browder chart).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q107 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2143, $s$A nurse is teaching a family about the classification of burns. A third-degree burn is also known as a:$s$, $s$["Superficial partial-thickness burn", "Partial-thickness burn", "Full-thickness burn", "Superficial burn"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Full-thickness burn
A THIRD-DEGREE burn is a FULL-THICKNESS burn: it destroys ALL layers of the skin (epidermis and dermis) and may reach the fat below. The skin looks white, leathery, brown or charred, and is PAINLESS (nerve endings are destroyed). It usually needs skin grafting.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Superficial partial-thickness burn — A superficial partial-thickness burn is second-degree (painful, blistered).
• Partial-thickness burn — A partial-thickness burn is second-degree.
• Superficial burn — A superficial burn is first-degree.

⭐ Key point: Burn depth: first-degree (superficial, red, painful), second-degree (partial thickness, blisters), third-degree (full thickness, painless, leathery), fourth-degree (into muscle and bone).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q108$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2144, $s$A nurse is assessing the depth of a burn. A burn that is characterized by redness and pain but no blisters is a:$s$, $s$["Fourth-degree burn", "Third-degree burn", "Second-degree burn", "First-degree burn"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: First-degree burn
A FIRST-DEGREE (superficial) burn involves ONLY the epidermis. The skin is RED and PAINFUL but has NO BLISTERS (like mild sunburn). It blanches when pressed and heals in 3–7 days without scarring. It is NOT counted in TBSA for fluid resuscitation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fourth-degree burn — A fourth-degree burn extends to muscle and bone.
• Third-degree burn — A third-degree burn is full thickness, white or charred, and painless.
• Second-degree burn — A second-degree burn has BLISTERS.

⭐ Key point: Only partial and full-thickness burns are counted in TBSA for the Parkland formula.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q109$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2145, $s$In a disaster triage system, a casualty who is breathing normally, has a normal pulse, and can follow simple commands, but has a significant injury that will need treatment, is typically categorised as:$s$, $s$["Immediate (red)", "Delayed (yellow)", "Deceased (black)", "Minor (green)"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Delayed (yellow)
In disaster triage, DELAYED (YELLOW) patients have serious injuries that need treatment but are STABLE enough to wait a while: breathing normally, a normal pulse and able to follow commands. Example: a fractured femur with stable vital signs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate (red) — IMMEDIATE (RED) patients have life-threatening problems needing treatment now (airway compromise, shock).
• Deceased (black) — BLACK means dead or expected to die (unsurvivable injuries).
• Minor (green) — MINOR (GREEN) patients are the "walking wounded" with small injuries.

⭐ Key point: START triage: can walk → green; not breathing after the airway is opened → black; breathing over 30/min, capillary refill over 2 s, or can't obey commands → red; otherwise → yellow.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q111$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2146, $s$A patient has total burns to the groin, anterior trunk and the the left upper limb. Using the Wallace rules of nine, the TBSAB is?$s$, $s$["36%", "29%", "27%", "28%"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 28%
Add up the areas using the Rule of Nines. GROIN (perineum and genitalia) = 1%. ANTERIOR (front) TRUNK = 18%. LEFT UPPER LIMB (whole arm) = 9%. Total = 1 + 18 + 9 = 28% TBSA. (The old answer key said 36%, which does not match the Rule of Nines; it has been corrected to 28%.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 36% — 36% would be the whole trunk (front and back) or both legs, not this combination.
• 29% — 29% comes from a miscount (such as counting the groin as 2%).
• 27% — 27% leaves out the groin (1%).

⭐ Key point: Rule of Nines: head 9, each arm 9, front trunk 18, back trunk 18, each leg 18, perineum 1.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q112 · answer key corrected$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2147, $s$In ICU monitoring, which principle is emphasised as more important than a single reading?$s$, $s$["Only the lowest recorded value", "The most recent value alone", "Trends over time", "Only the highest recorded value"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Trends over time
In ICU, the TREND of a value over time (rising, falling, stable) is more important than a single reading. A single number can be affected by errors (a loose probe, patient movement, wrong levelling) or short-term changes. A gradual rise in heart rate or fall in urine output over hours may show early deterioration before any single value looks alarming.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only the lowest recorded value — The lowest value alone doesn't show the pattern.
• The most recent value alone — The most recent value alone may be misleading.
• Only the highest recorded value — The highest value alone doesn't show the pattern.

⭐ Key point: Chart vital signs on flowcharts or graphs to see trends, and use early warning scores (NEWS2).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q113$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2148, $s$A patient with a severe burn is at high risk for which of the following complications in the first 24 hours?$s$, $s$["Hypertension", "Hypovolemic shock", "Fluid volume excess", "Hyperglycemia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypovolemic shock
In the first 24–48 hours after a major burn, damaged capillaries become LEAKY, and large amounts of fluid and protein move from the blood vessels into the tissues (burn oedema). Fluid is also lost through the burn wound. The blood volume falls, causing HYPOVOLAEMIC (burn) SHOCK unless fluids are replaced quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension — Hypertension isn't typical early on; BP tends to FALL.
• Fluid volume excess — Fluid excess comes later (when fluid moves back into the vessels after 48–72 hours), or from over-resuscitation.
• Hyperglycemia — Hyperglycaemia can occur (stress) but isn't the main early risk.

⭐ Key point: Parkland formula: 4 mL × weight (kg) × % TBSA of Hartmann's in 24 hours; half in the first 8 hours from the time of the burn. Target urine output of 0.5 mL/kg/hour.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q115$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2149, $s$Front of trunk (chest + abdomen) accounts for what percentage of TBSA under the Rule of Nines?$s$, $s$["18%", "27%", "9%", "36%"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 18%
In the Rule of Nines, the FRONT of the TRUNK (chest and abdomen) is 18% of TBSA (2 × 9%). The BACK of the trunk is also 18%. So the whole trunk is 36%.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 27% — 27% isn't a standard Rule of Nines area.
• 9% — 9% is the head, or one arm.
• 36% — 36% is the WHOLE trunk (front and back).

⭐ Key point: Rule of Nines adds to 100%: head 9, arms 9 + 9, front trunk 18, back trunk 18, legs 18 + 18, perineum 1.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q116$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2150, $s$In a major haemorrhage protocol, tranexamic acid works best when given:$s$, $s$["Only to children", "After bleeding has stopped", "Within 3 hours of injury", "Only after 24 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Within 3 hours of injury
Tranexamic acid (TXA) stops clots being broken down too early (an antifibrinolytic). In trauma and PPH, it reduces deaths from bleeding when given EARLY, within 3 HOURS of injury (1 g IV over 10 minutes, then 1 g over 8 hours). Given after 3 hours, it doesn't help and may cause harm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only to children — TXA is used in adults and children.
• After bleeding has stopped — After the bleeding has stopped, it isn't needed.
• Only after 24 hours — After 24 hours, it is too late.

⭐ Key point: Major haemorrhage protocol: TXA early, blood products in balanced ratios (red cells : FFP : platelets ≈ 1:1:1), warming, and stopping the bleeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q120 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2151, $s$Severe subcutaneous emphysema after chest trauma raises suspicion of:$s$, $s$["Pericardial tamponade", "Normal healing", "Tracheobronchial injury", "Simple rib fracture"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tracheobronchial injury
Subcutaneous (surgical) emphysema is air under the skin, which feels like crackling (crepitus) when pressed. SEVERE or rapidly spreading subcutaneous emphysema after chest trauma suggests a large air leak from the AIRWAY: a TRACHEOBRONCHIAL INJURY (a torn trachea or bronchus), or a pneumothorax with a lung tear.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pericardial tamponade — Tamponade causes low BP, raised JVP and muffled heart sounds.
• Normal healing — Severe emphysema isn't part of normal healing.
• Simple rib fracture — A simple rib fracture may cause mild local emphysema, but severe emphysema suggests airway injury.

⭐ Key point: Tracheobronchial injury can cause a persistent large air leak despite a chest drain. It needs bronchoscopy and surgical repair.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q25$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2152, $s$According to the Parkland formula, how much fluid is a nurse required to administer to a patient weighing 70 kg with 30% total body surface area (TBSA) burns in the first 8 hours?$s$, $s$["2100 mL", "4200 mL", "8400 mL", "2800 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 4200 mL
Parkland formula: 4 mL × weight (kg) × %TBSA = total fluid for the FIRST 24 hours. 4 × 70 × 30 = 8400 mL. HALF of this is given in the FIRST 8 HOURS (counted from the time of the burn, not arrival): 8400 ÷ 2 = 4200 mL. The other half (4200 mL) is given over the next 16 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2100 mL — 2100 mL is a quarter of the total (an error).
• 8400 mL — 8400 mL is the TOTAL for 24 hours, not the first 8 hours.
• 2800 mL — 2800 mL comes from a calculation error.

⭐ Key point: Use Hartmann's (Ringer's lactate). Adjust the rate to keep urine output at 0.5–1 mL/kg/hour in adults.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q101$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2153, $s$Inhalational injury is responsible for approximately what percentage of burns deaths?$s$, $s$["50%", "90%", "10%", "100%"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 90%
Inhalation injury (smoke and heat damage to the airways and lungs, plus carbon monoxide and cyanide poisoning) is the main cause of death in burns and house fires. In this question's key it accounts for about 90% of burn deaths, especially in fires in enclosed spaces. It greatly increases mortality compared with a burn of the same size without inhalation injury.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50% — 50% underestimates it in this question.
• 10% — 10% underestimates it.
• 100% — 100% is incorrect; other causes (shock, sepsis, organ failure) also cause deaths.

⭐ Key point: Suspect inhalation injury with enclosed-space fires, facial burns, soot in the mouth, a hoarse voice and stridor. Give 100% oxygen and consider early intubation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q102$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2154, $s$Rubbing frozen tissue during frostbite management is:$s$, $s$["Never done — it worsens tissue damage", "Only done on the hands", "Recommended to restore circulation", "Only done if pain-free"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Never done — it worsens tissue damage
In frostbite, ice crystals form in and around the cells, making the tissue very fragile. RUBBING or massaging the frozen part damages the tissue further (and snow rubbing adds more cold injury). It is NEVER done. Instead, rewarm rapidly in warm water (37–39 °C) for 15–30 minutes, give analgesia, and protect the area.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only done on the hands — It isn't done on any area, including the hands.
• Recommended to restore circulation — Rubbing worsens damage; it doesn't restore circulation.
• Only done if pain-free — It isn't done even if pain-free.

⭐ Key point: Don't rewarm if there is a risk of refreezing (rewarming then refreezing causes more damage).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q103$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2155, $s$A patient with a large burn is being monitored for infection. The nurse should be aware that the primary source of infection in a burn patient is:$s$, $s$["The hospital environment", "The patient's visitors", "The patient's own skin flora", "The burn wound itself"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The burn wound itself
A burn wound is the PRIMARY source of infection in burn patients. The skin barrier is destroyed, and the dead tissue (eschar) and protein-rich fluid are an ideal environment for bacteria, which come from the patient's own skin and gut, and from the environment. The wound becomes colonised within days, and invasive wound infection can lead to sepsis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The hospital environment — The hospital environment contributes but isn't the primary source.
• The patient's visitors — Visitors can bring germs but aren't the main source.
• The patient's own skin flora — The patient's own skin flora colonises the wound, but the wound itself is the main site.

⭐ Key point: Burn wound care: aseptic dressings, topical antimicrobials (such as silver sulfadiazine), early excision and grafting, and monitoring for sepsis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q104$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2156, $s$The target urine output in an adult burns patient during fluid resuscitation is:$s$, $s$["5ml/kg/hr", "0.5–1.0ml/kg/hr", "2–3ml/kg/hr", "0.1–0.2ml/kg/hr"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 0.5–1.0ml/kg/hr
Urine output is the best bedside guide to whether burn fluid resuscitation is adequate. The target in adults is 0.5–1.0 mL/kg/hour (about 30–50 mL/hour for 70 kg). Children: 1 mL/kg/hour. In electrical burns with myoglobinuria: 1–2 mL/kg/hour (dark urine) to protect the kidneys.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5ml/kg/hr — 5 mL/kg/hour is too high and means over-resuscitation.
• 2–3ml/kg/hr — 2–3 mL/kg/hour is too high for routine adult burns.
• 0.1–0.2ml/kg/hr — 0.1–0.2 mL/kg/hour is far too low (kidney failure risk).

⭐ Key point: Insert a urinary catheter in major burns and measure output hourly. Too much fluid causes "fluid creep" (oedema, compartment syndromes).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q106$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2157, $s$A nurse is providing wound care for a patient with a burn. The main goal of burn wound care is to:$s$, $s$["Promote the formation of eschar", "Keep the wound wet to prevent infection", "Prevent infection and promote healing", "Only use topical antibiotics"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Prevent infection and promote healing
The main GOALS of burn wound care are to PREVENT INFECTION (the main cause of death after the first days) and PROMOTE HEALING (moist wound environment, removing dead tissue, grafting deep burns). Care also aims to reduce pain, maintain function, and limit scarring.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Promote the formation of eschar — Eschar (dead tissue) is removed, not encouraged, because it harbours bacteria.
• Keep the wound wet to prevent infection — A moist environment helps healing, but a "wet" wound doesn't prevent infection.
• Only use topical antibiotics — Topical antibiotics are one tool, not the only goal.

⭐ Key point: Change dressings with aseptic technique, give analgesia before dressing changes, and assess the wound for signs of infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q107$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2158, $s$A poly-trauma patient with multiple long bone fractures develops sudden dyspnoea, a petechial rash, and confusion 24-48 hours after injury. This presentation should raise suspicion for:$s$, $s$["Anaesthetic death", "Chronic bronchitis exacerbation", "A simple wound infection", "Fat embolism syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fat embolism syndrome
Sudden breathlessness, a PETECHIAL rash (pinpoint spots on the chest, armpits and conjunctiva) and CONFUSION 24–72 hours after MULTIPLE LONG BONE fractures is the classic picture of FAT EMBOLISM SYNDROME. Fat droplets from the bone marrow travel to the lungs, brain and skin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anaesthetic death — "Anaesthetic death" isn't a diagnosis that fits these signs.
• Chronic bronchitis exacerbation — Chronic bronchitis doesn't present this way after trauma.
• A simple wound infection — A wound infection doesn't cause a petechial rash and confusion this quickly.

⭐ Key point: Treat fat embolism with supportive care: oxygen, ventilation if needed, fluids. Early fixation of fractures reduces the risk.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q108$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2159, $s$A 23 year old young adult presented to ER Road Traffic accident (RTA).He has poly traumas and massive bleeding. The characteristic of the blood is dark red in color and steady in flow. Which type of hemorrhage is this?$s$, $s$["Venous Hemorrhage", "Arterial Hemorrhage", "Internal Hemorrhage", "External Hemorrhage"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Venous Hemorrhage
VENOUS bleeding is DARK RED (low oxygen) and flows STEADILY, because veins are under lower pressure. ARTERIAL bleeding is BRIGHT RED (oxygenated) and SPURTS with each heartbeat, because arteries are under high pressure. Capillary bleeding oozes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Arterial Hemorrhage — Arterial bleeding is bright red and spurting.
• Internal Hemorrhage — "Internal" describes where the bleeding is, not its type; here it is visible.
• External Hemorrhage — "External" describes location; the colour and flow tell you it is venous.

⭐ Key point: Control external bleeding with direct pressure first. Use a tourniquet for life-threatening limb bleeding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q109$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2160, $s$After a burn, a client has BP 70/40, HR 140, RR 25, pallor and weak pedal pulses. The nurse's first action is to:$s$, $s$["Check pulses with a Doppler", "Obtain a complete blood count", "Start intravenous fluids", "Obtain an ECG"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Start intravenous fluids
After a burn, low BP (70/40), fast heart rate (140), fast breathing, pallor and weak pulses mean HYPOVOLAEMIC (burn) SHOCK from fluid leaking out of the vessels. The FIRST action is to START IV FLUIDS (large-bore cannulas, Hartmann's) to restore circulating volume.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Check pulses with a Doppler — A Doppler pulse check doesn't treat shock.
• Obtain a complete blood count — A blood count can wait; treatment comes first.
• Obtain an ECG — An ECG can wait; fluid resuscitation is the priority.

⭐ Key point: Follow ABCDE: secure the airway (watch for inhalation injury), give oxygen, then start fluids using the Parkland formula.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q111$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2161, $s$Diaphragmatic injury should be suspected with penetrating trauma at or below the:$s$, $s$["5th intercostal space", "Diaphragmatic injury is never related to wound location", "2nd intercostal space", "10th intercostal space"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 5th intercostal space
The diaphragm rises as high as the 4th–5th intercostal space during expiration. Any penetrating wound (stab, gunshot) at or below the 5TH INTERCOSTAL SPACE (or the nipple line in front, the scapular tip behind) can injure the diaphragm and the abdominal organs below it (liver, spleen, stomach).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diaphragmatic injury is never related to wound location — Wound location DOES matter for diaphragm injury.
• 2nd intercostal space — The 2nd intercostal space is too high to reach the diaphragm.
• 10th intercostal space — The 10th intercostal space is below most of the diaphragm's range.

⭐ Key point: Diaphragmatic injuries may be missed initially. Bowel can herniate into the chest later. Laparoscopy or CT helps diagnose them.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q112$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2162, $s$The 'lethal triad' in major trauma is:$s$, $s$["Hypertension, bradycardia and apnoea", "Hypoxia, hypercapnia and hypotension", "Fever, tachycardia and leucocytosis", "Hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy
The "LETHAL TRIAD" in major trauma is HYPOTHERMIA, ACIDOSIS and COAGULOPATHY. Each worsens the others: cold slows clotting enzymes and platelets, acidosis (from shock) impairs clotting, and ongoing bleeding worsens shock, acidosis and cooling. Breaking the cycle is key to survival.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension, bradycardia and apnoea — Hypertension, bradycardia and apnoea is Cushing's triad.
• Hypoxia, hypercapnia and hypotension — Hypoxia, hypercapnia and hypotension isn't the trauma triad.
• Fever, tachycardia and leucocytosis — Fever, tachycardia and leucocytosis are signs of inflammation or infection.

⭐ Key point: Prevent the triad: warm the patient, fluids and blood, stop bleeding early (damage control surgery), and give blood products in balanced ratios.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q117 · concept asked in 3 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2163, $s$In penetrating torso trauma without head injury, permissive hypotension aims for a systolic BP of about:$s$, $s$["140–160 mmHg using vasopressors", "50 mmHg using no fluids at all", "80–90 mmHg until bleeding is controlled", "120 mmHg using large crystalloid volumes"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 80–90 mmHg until bleeding is controlled
In PERMISSIVE HYPOTENSION, BP is kept lower than normal (systolic about 80–90 mmHg, enough to perfuse the brain and heart) until the bleeding is surgically controlled. Raising BP too high with lots of fluid can dislodge clots, dilute clotting factors, and increase bleeding. It is NOT used in head injury, where higher BP is needed to protect the brain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 140–160 mmHg using vasopressors — 140–160 mmHg is too high and would increase bleeding.
• 50 mmHg using no fluids at all — 50 mmHg is too low and risks organ damage.
• 120 mmHg using large crystalloid volumes — 120 mmHg with large crystalloid volumes increases bleeding.

⭐ Key point: In TBI, keep systolic BP at 110 mmHg or above (higher in older patients).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q118$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2164, $s$A trauma patient has HR 130, falling systolic BP, RR 35 and confusion. The estimated blood loss class is:$s$, $s$["No loss; this is anxiety", "Class III (30–40% of blood volume)", "Class II (15–30% with normal BP)", "Class I (up to 15% of blood volume)"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Class III (30–40% of blood volume)
ATLS haemorrhage Class III (30–40% blood loss, about 1500–2000 mL): heart rate above 120 (here 130), FALLING systolic BP, respiratory rate 30–40 (here 35), and anxious or confused mental state. Class III is the first class where BP falls, and it usually needs blood transfusion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No loss; this is anxiety — These signs show significant bleeding, not anxiety.
• Class II (15–30% with normal BP) — Class II has a NORMAL systolic BP.
• Class I (up to 15% of blood volume) — Class I has nearly normal signs.

⭐ Key point: Falling BP is a late sign of blood loss. Act on tachycardia and narrowing pulse pressure early.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q119$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2165, $s$A ward patient's NEWS2 score is 7. The ICU outreach nurse expects:$s$, $s$["Routine 12-hourly observations on the ward", "No change, as this score is in the normal range", "An emergency response by a critical care team", "Discharge home later that same evening"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An emergency response by a critical care team
NEWS2 (National Early Warning Score) adds up points for RR, SpO₂, BP, heart rate, consciousness and temperature. A total of 7 OR MORE means HIGH clinical risk: an EMERGENCY response is needed, with urgent review by a critical care outreach team, continuous monitoring, and consideration of ICU transfer.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Routine 12-hourly observations on the ward — 12-hourly observations are for a score of 0.
• No change, as this score is in the normal range — A score of 7 isn't normal; it is high risk.
• Discharge home later that same evening — Discharge would be unsafe.

⭐ Key point: NEWS2 responses: 0 = routine; 1–4 = ward review; 5–6 (or 3 in one parameter) = urgent review; 7 or more = emergency.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q176$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2166, $s$On room air at sea level a patient has PaO₂ 60 mmHg and PaCO₂ 40 mmHg. The A–a gradient is about:$s$, $s$["40 mmHg, raised, suggesting V/Q mismatch or shunt", "10 mmHg, normal for a young adult", "0 mmHg, normal, showing pure hypoventilation", "150 mmHg, equal to the inspired oxygen pressure"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 40 mmHg, raised, suggesting V/Q mismatch or shunt
Alveolar oxygen (PAO₂) = FiO₂ × (barometric pressure − water vapour pressure) − PaCO₂ ÷ 0.8. Here: 0.21 × (760 − 47) − 40 ÷ 0.8 = 150 − 50 = about 100 mmHg. A–a gradient = PAO₂ − PaO₂ = 100 − 60 = 40 mmHg. This is RAISED (normal is about 5–15 in young adults), suggesting a problem with gas transfer: V/Q mismatch or shunt (pneumonia, PE, oedema).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 mmHg, normal for a young adult — 10 mmHg would be normal, but the calculation gives 40.
• 0 mmHg, normal, showing pure hypoventilation — 0 mmHg would mean perfect gas exchange (pure hypoventilation gives a normal gradient, not zero).
• 150 mmHg, equal to the inspired oxygen pressure — 150 mmHg is the inspired oxygen pressure, not the gradient.

⭐ Key point: Normal A–a gradient increases with age: roughly (age ÷ 4) + 4. A normal gradient with hypoxia suggests hypoventilation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q17$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2167, $s$ARDS in the context of orthopaedic trauma most often develops as a complication of:$s$, $s$["Fat embolism or severe polytrauma", "Simple plaster cast application", "Isolated finger fractures", "Routine elective joint replacement with no complications"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fat embolism or severe polytrauma
In orthopaedic trauma, ARDS most often develops after FAT EMBOLISM (fat from long bone fractures damaging the lung capillaries) or SEVERE POLYTRAUMA (massive bleeding, transfusion, shock and inflammation). These cause widespread lung inflammation and leaky capillaries.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple plaster cast application — A plaster cast doesn't cause ARDS.
• Isolated finger fractures — Isolated finger fractures don't cause ARDS.
• Routine elective joint replacement with no complications — Uncomplicated elective joint replacement rarely causes ARDS.

⭐ Key point: Early fixation of long bone fractures reduces fat embolism and ARDS.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q31$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2168, $s$A 50-year old man is admitted to the trauma resuscitation unit. He is intubated on arrival. What is the next step in the primary survey?$s$, $s$["Obtain a chest x-ray to determine tube placement", "Initiate large-bore intravenous catheter placement and replace fluid.", "Assess the patient's blood pressure and peripheral pulses.", "Listen to lung sounds to assess for ventilation."]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Listen to lung sounds to assess for ventilation.
In the primary survey (ABCDE), after the airway is secured by intubation, the next step is BREATHING: confirm the tube is working by LISTENING to LUNG SOUNDS on both sides (and checking chest rise and capnography). This checks for correct tube placement and problems like pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Obtain a chest x-ray to determine tube placement — A chest X-ray is useful, but comes after the clinical check.
• Initiate large-bore intravenous catheter placement and replace fluid. — Circulation (IV lines, fluids) comes after breathing.
• Assess the patient's blood pressure and peripheral pulses. — Assessing BP and pulses is circulation, which follows breathing.

⭐ Key point: Confirm ETT placement with waveform capnography (the gold standard) plus clinical signs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q55$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2169, $s$Which does NOT need to be excluded before testing for brain death?$s$, $s$["Limb fractures", "Electrolyte imbalances", "Hypothermia", "Drug intoxication"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Limb fractures
Before brainstem death testing, conditions that can mimic brainstem death must be EXCLUDED: DRUGS (sedatives, opioids, neuromuscular blockers), HYPOTHERMIA (below 34 °C), and severe METABOLIC and ELECTROLYTE disturbances (sodium, glucose, potassium) or endocrine problems. LIMB FRACTURES don't affect brainstem function, so they don't need to be excluded.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Electrolyte imbalances — Electrolyte imbalances MUST be excluded.
• Hypothermia — Hypothermia MUST be excluded.
• Drug intoxication — Drug intoxication MUST be excluded.

⭐ Key point: Brainstem death tests are done by two experienced doctors, on two occasions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q61$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2170, $s$An epidural hematoma classically results from injury to which structure, located in the potential space between the skull and the dura mater?$s$, $s$["The superior sagittal sinus", "The internal carotid artery", "The middle meningeal artery", "A bridging vein"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The middle meningeal artery
An EPIDURAL (extradural) haematoma is usually caused by a skull fracture (often the temporal bone at the pterion) that tears the MIDDLE MENINGEAL ARTERY. Arterial blood collects between the skull and the dura, forming a lens-shaped (biconvex) clot on CT. A lucid interval is common.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The superior sagittal sinus — The superior sagittal sinus is a large vein; damage causes venous bleeding.
• The internal carotid artery — The internal carotid artery isn't the classic cause.
• A bridging vein — Bridging veins cause SUBDURAL haematomas.

⭐ Key point: Extradural haematoma needs urgent surgical evacuation. Outcomes are good if treated quickly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q76$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2171, $s$A patient with a spinal cord injury above T6 develops sudden severe hypertension, a pounding headache, bradycardia, and flushing above the level of injury after the bladder becomes distended. Which NANDA-I diagnosis best matches this presentation?$s$, $s$["Autonomic Dysreflexia", "Risk for Autonomic Dysreflexia", "Ineffective Cerebral Tissue Perfusion", "Decreased Cardiac Output"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Autonomic Dysreflexia
The patient ALREADY has the signs (sudden severe hypertension, pounding headache, bradycardia, flushing above the injury) after a trigger (a distended bladder), with a spinal injury above T6. This is the actual NANDA-I diagnosis AUTONOMIC DYSREFLEXIA. Act at once: sit the patient up, empty the bladder, and check for other triggers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Risk for Autonomic Dysreflexia — "Risk for Autonomic Dysreflexia" is used BEFORE it happens; here it is already happening.
• Ineffective Cerebral Tissue Perfusion — Ineffective cerebral tissue perfusion doesn't describe the full syndrome.
• Decreased Cardiac Output — Decreased cardiac output doesn't describe this problem.

⭐ Key point: Common triggers: a full bladder (a blocked catheter), constipation, pressure ulcers and tight clothing.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q103 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2172, $s$For small or patchy burns, a quick estimate of surface area can be made using:$s$, $s$["No estimation method exists", "The patient's own palm ≈ 1% TBSA", "Lund and Browder chart only in adults", "The Rule of Nines only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The patient's own palm ≈ 1% TBSA
For small or patchy (scattered) burns, the PALM METHOD gives a quick estimate. The patient's own palm (including the fingers) is about 1% of their total body surface area. Count how many "palms" the burns cover.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No estimation method exists — Estimation methods DO exist.
• Lund and Browder chart only in adults — The Lund–Browder chart is accurate for all ages, but the question asks for a quick method for small burns.
• The Rule of Nines only — The Rule of Nines is used for large burns; it is less practical for small, patchy ones.

⭐ Key point: Use the Lund–Browder chart for children (their heads are bigger and their legs smaller).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q104$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2173, $s$Compartment syndrome causing pain on passive stretch of the affected muscle is significant because it is:$s$, $s$["Only seen after the compartment has already been decompressed", "Never associated with compartment syndrome", "A late, unreliable sign", "Often one of the earliest warning signs"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Often one of the earliest warning signs
In compartment syndrome, the swollen muscles are squeezed inside their tight fascial compartments, cutting off blood flow. PAIN OUT OF PROPORTION to the injury, especially pain on PASSIVE STRETCH of the muscles (moving the fingers or toes), is often one of the EARLIEST and most reliable warning signs. Pulses are often still present until late.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only seen after the compartment has already been decompressed — It occurs before decompression, not only after.
• Never associated with compartment syndrome — It IS associated with compartment syndrome.
• A late, unreliable sign — It is an EARLY sign, not a late one (pulselessness and paralysis are late).

⭐ Key point: The 6 Ps: Pain, Pain on passive stretch, Pallor, Paraesthesia, Paralysis, Pulselessness (late). Treat with fasciotomy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q106$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2174, $s$The overarching principle guiding resource allocation in a mass casualty disaster response is:$s$, $s$["Achieve the greatest good for the greatest number of casualties", "Treat the most severely injured patient first no matter what", "Treat only patients who can pay for care", "Treat whoever arrived first, regardless of severity"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Achieve the greatest good for the greatest number of casualties
In a mass casualty disaster, resources (staff, beds, blood, operating theatres) are limited. The guiding principle is to do THE GREATEST GOOD FOR THE GREATEST NUMBER of casualties. Triage prioritises patients who are most likely to survive with treatment, rather than spending all resources on one patient who is unlikely to survive.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Treat the most severely injured patient first no matter what — Treating the most severely injured first, no matter what, can waste resources on unsurvivable injuries.
• Treat only patients who can pay for care — Ability to pay is not an ethical basis for disaster care.
• Treat whoever arrived first, regardless of severity — First-come, first-served doesn't consider severity.

⭐ Key point: Triage categories: red (immediate), yellow (delayed), green (minor), black (dead or expectant).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q107$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2175, $s$The critical care nurse knows simultaneous injury of different regions of the body of which one or the combination of these injuries is life threatening, is popularly known as:$s$, $s$["Very severe trauma", "Polytrauma", "Poly head injuries", "Severe multiple injuries"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Polytrauma
POLYTRAUMA means simultaneous injuries to two or more body regions or systems (such as the head, chest, abdomen, pelvis and limbs), where at least one injury, or the combination, is life-threatening. These patients need a coordinated trauma team approach (ATLS).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Very severe trauma — "Very severe trauma" isn't the recognised term.
• Poly head injuries — "Poly head injuries" isn't a recognised term.
• Severe multiple injuries — "Severe multiple injuries" is descriptive, but the recognised term is polytrauma.

⭐ Key point: Polytrauma patients need a primary survey (ABCDE), resuscitation, then a secondary survey (head to toe).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q108$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2176, $s$The correct first aid for a chemical burn is:$s$, $s$["Apply a neutralising agent immediately", "Apply ice directly", "Cover with a dry dressing only", "Copious irrigation with water"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Copious irrigation with water
For chemical burns, the first aid is COPIOUS IRRIGATION with large volumes of running WATER (for at least 20–30 minutes, longer for alkalis), after brushing off any dry chemical powder and removing contaminated clothing. Water dilutes and washes away the chemical, stopping the burning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Apply a neutralising agent immediately — Neutralising agents can cause a heat-producing reaction, worsening the burn.
• Apply ice directly — Ice can cause cold injury.
• Cover with a dry dressing only — A dry dressing alone leaves the chemical on the skin.

⭐ Key point: Alkali burns (cement, lye) are often more damaging than acids and need longer irrigation. Protect yourself with PPE.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q109$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2177, $s$Voltage above what threshold is classified as 'high voltage' in electrical burns?$s$, $s$["10,000V", "100V", "1000V", "100,000V"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1000V
Electrical injuries are classified by voltage. HIGH VOLTAGE is above 1000 VOLTS (1 kV), as in power lines and industrial sources. High voltage causes deep tissue damage along the path of the current, cardiac arrhythmias, compartment syndrome, rhabdomyolysis and kidney injury, even if the skin burns look small.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10,000V — 10,000 V is high voltage, but the threshold is 1000 V.
• 100V — 100 V is low voltage.
• 100,000V — 100,000 V is far above the threshold.

⭐ Key point: All electrical injuries need an ECG, and high-voltage injuries need cardiac monitoring, CK and urine checks.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q110$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2178, $s$Because of extensive skin loss, TENS patients are managed most similarly to which other critical care population?$s$, $s$["Uncomplicated cellulitis patients", "Major burn patients", "Stable post-operative orthopedic patients", "Chronic wound outpatients"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Major burn patients
In TEN (toxic epidermal necrolysis), large areas of skin peel away, causing massive FLUID loss, heat loss, protein loss, pain and a high risk of INFECTION, just like a major burn. These patients are therefore managed like MAJOR BURN patients, ideally in a burns unit or ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uncomplicated cellulitis patients — Uncomplicated cellulitis is a localised skin infection.
• Stable post-operative orthopedic patients — Stable post-operative orthopaedic patients don't have widespread skin loss.
• Chronic wound outpatients — Chronic wound outpatients don't have acute, widespread skin loss.

⭐ Key point: TEN care: stop the causing drug, warm the room, fluids, non-adherent dressings, eye and mouth care, nutrition and pain relief.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q112$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2179, $s$A 30-year-old man with a stab wound to the neck is alert but bleeding actively in the resuscitation bay. You should first:$s$, $s$["Locally explore the wound to find the bleeding point", "Clamp the bleeding vessels blindly", "Place two large-bore IV cannulae and give O-negative blood", "Apply direct pressure to the site"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Apply direct pressure to the site
Active bleeding from a neck wound needs immediate DIRECT PRESSURE on the bleeding site. This is the quickest and most effective way to control external haemorrhage, while help is called and resuscitation is prepared. Don't probe or explore the wound in the resuscitation bay.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Locally explore the wound to find the bleeding point — Locally exploring the wound can dislodge clots and cause catastrophic bleeding.
• Clamp the bleeding vessels blindly — Blind clamping can damage vessels and nerves (and airway structures).
• Place two large-bore IV cannulae and give O-negative blood — IV access and blood are needed, but stopping the bleeding with pressure comes first.

⭐ Key point: Neck wounds can also threaten the airway (expanding haematoma). Watch for stridor and voice change.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q114$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2180, $s$A patient with carbon monoxide poisoning from smoke inhalation should be treated with:$s$, $s$["IV antibiotics as the primary treatment", "Room air only", "No oxygen therapy is needed", "Humidified 100% oxygen"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Humidified 100% oxygen
Carbon monoxide binds haemoglobin about 240 times more strongly than oxygen. HUMIDIFIED 100% OXYGEN (via a non-rebreather mask or ETT) speeds up its removal from haemoglobin (its half-life falls from about 4–5 hours on room air to about 1 hour on 100% oxygen) and treats tissue hypoxia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV antibiotics as the primary treatment — Antibiotics don't treat CO poisoning.
• Room air only — Room air removes CO slowly.
• No oxygen therapy is needed — Oxygen IS needed.

⭐ Key point: Pulse oximeters read FALSELY normal in CO poisoning. Measure COHb on co-oximetry.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q116 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2181, $s$After a high-voltage electrical burn with dark red urine, the target urine output is about:$s$, $s$["5 mL/kg/h using diuretics only", "1–1.5 mL/kg/h until the urine clears", "0.1 mL/kg/h to protect the kidneys", "0.3 mL/kg/h with fluid restriction"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1–1.5 mL/kg/h until the urine clears
High-voltage electrical burns damage muscles, releasing MYOGLOBIN, which turns the urine dark red or brown. Myoglobin can block the kidney tubules and cause AKI. Fluids are given to keep the urine output higher than in ordinary burns, about 1–1.5 mL/kg/hour (or 100 mL/hour in adults), until the urine clears.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5 mL/kg/h using diuretics only — 5 mL/kg/hour with diuretics only is excessive and doesn't replace fluid.
• 0.1 mL/kg/h to protect the kidneys — 0.1 mL/kg/hour is far too low and would harm the kidneys.
• 0.3 mL/kg/h with fluid restriction — 0.3 mL/kg/hour with fluid restriction would harm the kidneys.

⭐ Key point: Also monitor the ECG (arrhythmias), CK, potassium, and limbs for compartment syndrome.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q117$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2182, $s$The current ATLS site for needle decompression of tension pneumothorax in adults is:$s$, $s$["4th–5th intercostal space, anterior to mid-axillary line", "Just above the umbilicus", "2nd intercostal space in the lateral chest", "7th intercostal space, posterior axillary line"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 4th–5th intercostal space, anterior to mid-axillary line
Current ATLS guidance for needle decompression in ADULTS recommends the 4th–5th INTERCOSTAL SPACE, just ANTERIOR to the MID-AXILLARY line. The chest wall is thinner here than at the 2nd ICS mid-clavicular line, so the needle is more likely to reach the pleural space. (The 2nd ICS mid-clavicular line is still used in children.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Just above the umbilicus — Above the umbilicus is in the abdomen.
• 2nd intercostal space in the lateral chest — The 2nd ICS in the lateral chest isn't the recommended site.
• 7th intercostal space, posterior axillary line — The 7th ICS posterior axillary line risks injuring abdominal organs.

⭐ Key point: Follow needle decompression with a chest drain.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q119$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2183, $s$A FAST ultrasound scan in trauma looks for free fluid in the pericardium, right and left upper quadrants and:$s$, $s$["Thyroid", "Pelvis", "Spinal canal", "Brain"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pelvis
FAST (Focused Assessment with Sonography for Trauma) is a quick bedside ultrasound looking for FREE FLUID (usually blood) in four areas: the PERICARDIUM, the RIGHT upper quadrant (Morison's pouch, between the liver and kidney), the LEFT upper quadrant (around the spleen), and the PELVIS (behind the bladder).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Thyroid — The thyroid isn't part of FAST.
• Spinal canal — The spinal canal isn't part of FAST.
• Brain — The brain isn't part of FAST.

⭐ Key point: Extended FAST (eFAST) also checks both chest sides for pneumothorax and haemothorax.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q120$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2184, $s$A patient presents with pinpoint pupils, respiratory rate of 6 breaths/min, decreased level of consciousness, and bradycardia. Which toxidrome does this most likely represent?$s$, $s$["Cholinergic toxidrome", "Anticholinergic toxidrome", "Opioid toxidrome", "Sympathomimetic toxidrome"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Opioid toxidrome
The OPIOID toxidrome (the classic pattern of opioid overdose): PINPOINT pupils (miosis), RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION (here RR 6), decreased level of CONSCIOUSNESS, and often bradycardia and hypotension. Treat with naloxone and support breathing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cholinergic toxidrome — The cholinergic toxidrome also causes small pupils, but with SLUDGE signs (salivation, diarrhoea, sweating).
• Anticholinergic toxidrome — The anticholinergic toxidrome causes DILATED pupils, a fast pulse, hot dry skin and confusion.
• Sympathomimetic toxidrome — The sympathomimetic toxidrome (cocaine, amphetamines) causes dilated pupils, a fast pulse, high BP and agitation.

⭐ Key point: Toxidromes help identify a poison quickly when the cause is unknown.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q143$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C1 · Trauma, Burns, Poisoning, Emergencies & Disaster$s$, false, 2185, $s$A literature review that is organized by grouping studies according to recurring topics or findings, rather than simply listing them chronologically, is following a$s$, $s$["Random and unstructured approach", "Purely alphabetical listing with no analysis", "Approach that ignores all study findings", "Thematic organization approach"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Thematic organization approach
A THEMATIC organisation groups studies by recurring THEMES or topics (such as "barriers to pain assessment" or "nurse education"), comparing and contrasting their findings. It gives a clearer, more analytical literature review than listing studies one by one in date order.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Random and unstructured approach — A random, unstructured approach lacks clarity.
• Purely alphabetical listing with no analysis — An alphabetical list with no analysis doesn't synthesise the literature.
• Approach that ignores all study findings — Ignoring study findings defeats the purpose of a review.

⭐ Key point: A good literature review summarises, compares, critiques, and identifies gaps that the new study will address.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q192$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, true, 2186, $s$A patient develops rapidly rising temperature, severe muscle rigidity, tachycardia, and hypercarbia shortly after receiving succinylcholine. The priority treatment is:$s$, $s$["Dantrolene, which reduces calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum", "Naloxone", "Neostigmine", "Flumazenil"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dantrolene, which reduces calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum
These signs (rapidly rising temperature, muscle rigidity including the jaw, fast heart rate, rising EtCO₂ and acidosis) after suxamethonium or a volatile gas are MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA. It is an inherited muscle disorder in which calcium floods out of the muscle stores, causing uncontrolled contraction and heat production. DANTROLENE 2.5 mg/kg IV (repeated as needed) is the specific treatment; it stops the calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids and has no effect here.
• Neostigmine — Neostigmine reverses non-depolarising muscle relaxants and does not treat MH.
• Flumazenil — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines and has no effect here.

⭐ Key point: MH management: stop the trigger, call for help, give 100% oxygen at high flow, give dantrolene, cool the patient, treat high potassium and acidosis, and monitor urine (rhabdomyolysis). Warn the family about genetic testing.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q107$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, true, 2187, $s$Succinylcholine is contraindicated in a patient with a personal or family history of:$s$, $s$["Malignant hyperthermia", "Seasonal allergic rhinitis", "Migraine headaches", "Mild intermittent asthma"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Malignant hyperthermia
SUCCINYLCHOLINE (suxamethonium) is a fast-acting depolarising muscle relaxant used for rapid sequence intubation. It is a strong TRIGGER of MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA (MH) — an inherited muscle disorder (often in the RYR1 gene) in which triggering drugs (succinylcholine and volatile anaesthetic gases like sevoflurane) cause uncontrolled calcium release in muscle. Signs: rising end-tidal CO2 (often the first sign), masseter/jaw rigidity, tachycardia, very high temperature, hyperkalaemia, rhabdomyolysis. Treatment: stop triggers, give DANTROLENE, cool the patient. So a personal or FAMILY history of MH is an absolute contraindication. Other contraindications: HYPERKALAEMIA risk (burns or crush injury after 24–48 h, spinal cord injury, prolonged immobility), and pseudocholinesterase deficiency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Seasonal allergic rhinitis — SEASONAL ALLERGIC RHINITIS does not affect safety of succinylcholine.
• Migraine headaches — MIGRAINE history is not a contraindication.
• Mild intermittent asthma — MILD INTERMITTENT ASTHMA is not a contraindication to succinylcholine (it does not cause much histamine release).

⭐ Key point: Succinylcholine is contraindicated in MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA history (treat MH with DANTROLENE) and in hyperkalaemia risk.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q34$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, true, 2188, $s$Early signs of local anaesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST) include:$s$, $s$["Perioral numbness, tinnitus, and metallic taste", "Bradycardia only", "Hyperthermia", "Improved level of consciousness"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Perioral numbness, tinnitus, and metallic taste
LOCAL ANAESTHETIC SYSTEMIC TOXICITY (LAST) happens when local anaesthetic (especially bupivacaine) reaches high levels in the blood. EARLY signs are usually neurological: PERIORAL NUMBNESS (tingling around the mouth and tongue), TINNITUS (ringing in the ears), METALLIC TASTE, light-headedness, dizziness, visual disturbance, agitation and confusion. These can progress quickly to SEIZURES, then cardiovascular toxicity (arrhythmias, hypotension, cardiac arrest).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bradycardia only — Bradycardia alone is a later cardiovascular sign, not an early sign.
• Hyperthermia — Hyperthermia is not typical of LAST.
• Improved level of consciousness — Consciousness WORSENS, not improves.

⭐ Key point: If LAST is suspected: stop the injection, call for help, give oxygen, treat seizures with benzodiazepines, and give 20% lipid emulsion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q110$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, true, 2189, $s$A patient shivering on emergence from general anaesthesia most commonly has this symptom because of:$s$, $s$["Hypoglycaemia", "Anxiety", "Local anaesthetic toxicity", "Perioperative hypothermia and altered thermoregulatory set point"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Perioperative hypothermia and altered thermoregulatory set point
General anaesthesia IMPAIRS THERMOREGULATION — it widens the temperature range the body tolerates and causes vasodilation, so heat moves from the core to the skin and is lost. Patients also lose heat in cold operating theatres, from exposed skin and open body cavities, and from cold IV fluids. This causes PERIOPERATIVE HYPOTHERMIA. On waking, the brain's normal temperature control returns and triggers SHIVERING to generate heat. Some anaesthetic drugs (e.g. volatile agents) can also cause shivering directly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypoglycaemia — Hypoglycaemia is not the usual cause of post-anaesthetic shivering.
• Anxiety — Anxiety may contribute but is not the main cause.
• Local anaesthetic toxicity — Local anaesthetic toxicity causes metallic taste, tinnitus, seizures and arrhythmias — not shivering.

⭐ Key point: Shivering greatly INCREASES OXYGEN CONSUMPTION and heart workload (dangerous in heart disease). Prevent and treat with active warming (forced-air blankets, warmed fluids), oxygen, and drugs such as pethidine if needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q200$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, true, 2190, $s$Prevention of Mendelson's syndrome in an emergency caesarean section includes:$s$, $s$["Antacids, H2 blockers, RSI and cricoid pressure", "A large meal two hours before surgery", "Inhalational induction with a face mask", "Avoiding pre-oxygenation to save time"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Antacids, H2 blockers, RSI and cricoid pressure
Prevention of Mendelson's in emergency caesarean: ANTACIDS (e.g. 30 mL sodium citrate) to neutralise acid; H2 BLOCKERS (e.g. ranitidine) or PPIs to reduce acid production; RAPID SEQUENCE INDUCTION (RSI) to secure the airway quickly; and CRICOID PRESSURE (to compress the oesophagus). Regional anaesthesia is preferred where possible.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A large meal two hours before surgery — Eating before surgery increases risk.
• Inhalational induction with a face mask — Inhalational induction with a face mask is slow and increases risk.
• Avoiding pre-oxygenation to save time — Pre-oxygenation is essential, especially in pregnancy.

⭐ Key point: Metoclopramide may be given to speed stomach emptying.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q183$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, true, 2191, $s$Which group of patients is classically at highest risk of Mendelson's syndrome?$s$, $s$["Patients who fasted appropriately before elective day-case surgery", "Well-controlled type 2 diabetics undergoing outpatient dental extraction", "Patients undergoing local anaesthesia for minor skin procedures", "Pregnant women undergoing emergency general anaesthesia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pregnant women undergoing emergency general anaesthesia
MENDELSON'S SYNDROME is a chemical pneumonitis caused by inhaling acidic stomach contents during anaesthesia. PREGNANT WOMEN undergoing EMERGENCY GENERAL ANAESTHESIA (e.g. emergency caesarean) are at highest risk because: progesterone relaxes the lower oesophageal sphincter; gastric emptying is delayed (especially in labour and with opioids); the enlarged uterus raises intra-abdominal pressure; and emergency patients may not have fasted. Mendelson first described it in obstetric patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Patients who fasted appropriately before elective day-case surgery — Patients who have fasted properly before elective surgery have a low risk.
• Well-controlled type 2 diabetics undergoing outpatient dental extraction — Well-controlled diabetics having dental extraction (usually under local anaesthesia) have a low risk.
• Patients undergoing local anaesthesia for minor skin procedures — Local anaesthesia keeps protective airway reflexes intact, so the risk is very low.

⭐ Key point: Prevention: fasting where possible, antacids (sodium citrate), H2 blockers (ranitidine) or PPIs, metoclopramide, regional anaesthesia instead of GA, and rapid sequence induction with cricoid pressure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q181$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, true, 2192, $s$Rapid sequence induction is primarily indicated for patients:$s$, $s$["At high risk of pulmonary aspiration", "Who are fasted for over 12 hours", "Undergoing elective minor surgery", "With no comorbidities"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: At high risk of pulmonary aspiration
Rapid sequence induction (RSI) is used when the patient is at HIGH RISK of ASPIRATION of stomach contents: not fasted ("full stomach"), trauma, bowel obstruction, pregnancy, emergency surgery, or reflux. Drugs are given quickly together, and the tube is placed fast, to shorten the time between loss of airway reflexes and securing the airway.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Who are fasted for over 12 hours — Patients fasted for more than 12 hours are low risk.
• Undergoing elective minor surgery — Elective minor surgery usually doesn't need RSI.
• With no comorbidities — Having no comorbidities isn't an indication.

⭐ Key point: RSI steps: preparation, pre-oxygenation (3–5 minutes), induction and paralysis together, intubation, and confirmation with capnography.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q150$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, true, 2193, $s$Regional anaesthesia is contraindicated in$s$, $s$["Diagnosis of placenta previa", "Thrombocytopenia and elevated bleeding time", "Abrutio placenta", "Eclampsia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Thrombocytopenia and elevated bleeding time
Regional (spinal or epidural) anaesthesia is CONTRAINDICATED in THROMBOCYTOPENIA and a prolonged BLEEDING TIME, because inserting a needle near the spinal cord can cause a spinal or epidural HAEMATOMA, which can compress the cord and cause permanent paralysis. (The old answer key said "eclampsia"; eclampsia itself isn't a contraindication if platelets and clotting are normal, so the key has been corrected.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diagnosis of placenta previa — Placenta praevia isn't an absolute contraindication; neuraxial anaesthesia is often used for caesarean if the mother is stable.
• Abrutio placenta — Placental abruption is only a contraindication if there is DIC or shock.
• Eclampsia — Eclampsia isn't itself a contraindication; check platelets and clotting first. Neuraxial anaesthesia is often preferred in pre-eclampsia.

⭐ Key point: Check platelets (usually above 75–80 × 10⁹/L) and clotting before spinal or epidural anaesthesia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q151 · answer key corrected$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, true, 2194, $s$An early sign of malignant hyperthermia under anaesthesia is:$s$, $s$["A fall in heart rate and blood pressure", "An unexplained rise in end-tidal CO₂", "Pinpoint pupils", "A falling body temperature"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An unexplained rise in end-tidal CO₂
In MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA (triggered by volatile anaesthetics or suxamethonium), the muscles' metabolism becomes uncontrolled, producing large amounts of CO₂ and heat. An UNEXPLAINED RISE in END-TIDAL CO₂ (despite increased ventilation) is one of the EARLIEST signs, along with tachycardia and masseter (jaw) rigidity. High temperature is a LATE sign.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A fall in heart rate and blood pressure — Heart rate RISES, not falls.
• Pinpoint pupils — Pupils aren't a typical early sign.
• A falling body temperature — Temperature RISES, not falls.

⭐ Key point: Treatment: stop triggering agents, give 100% oxygen at high flow, give DANTROLENE 2.5 mg/kg IV, cool the patient, and treat high potassium and acidosis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q159$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, true, 2195, $s$A commonly used first-line antiemetic for postoperative nausea and vomiting prophylaxis is:$s$, $s$["Atropine", "Morphine", "Succinylcholine", "Ondansetron"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ondansetron
ONDANSETRON is a 5-HT3 receptor antagonist, a standard first-line drug to prevent and treat postoperative nausea and vomiting (PONV). It blocks serotonin receptors in the gut and the brain's vomiting centre. The usual dose is 4 mg IV.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Atropine — Atropine is an anticholinergic for bradycardia.
• Morphine — Morphine CAUSES nausea and vomiting.
• Succinylcholine — Succinylcholine is a muscle relaxant.

⭐ Key point: Other PONV drugs: dexamethasone, cyclizine, droperidol. Risk factors: female, non-smoker, previous PONV, postoperative opioids.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q154$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, true, 2196, $s$Which of the following should be the first monitor attached to the patient on arrival in the post anaesthesia care area (PACU):$s$, $s$["Pulse oximeter", "Temperature monitoring device", "Non-invasive blood pressure", "Cardiac monitor"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pulse oximeter
On arrival in the PACU (recovery), the PULSE OXIMETER is usually the first monitor attached. Oxygenation and the airway are the top priority after anaesthesia (hypoxia from airway obstruction, residual drugs or hypoventilation is the commonest early danger), and the oximeter also shows the heart rate. ECG and BP monitoring follow immediately. (The old answer key said "cardiac monitor"; airway and oxygenation come first, so the key has been corrected to pulse oximeter.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Temperature monitoring device — Temperature monitoring is important but isn't the first priority.
• Non-invasive blood pressure — Non-invasive BP is important but comes after oxygenation.
• Cardiac monitor — A cardiac monitor is important but follows pulse oximetry in airway-first priority.

⭐ Key point: PACU priorities: airway, breathing (SpO₂, RR), circulation (HR, BP, ECG), consciousness, pain, temperature and the surgical site.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q56 · answer key corrected$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, true, 2197, $s$What is the main target of neuromuscular blockers?$s$, $s$["Motor end plate", "Synoptic vesicles", "Central nervous system (CNS)", "Muscle membrane"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Motor end plate
Neuromuscular blockers (muscle relaxants like rocuronium, vecuronium, suxamethonium) act at the MOTOR END PLATE (the neuromuscular junction), where nerve signals pass to the muscle. They block acetylcholine receptors there, causing paralysis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Synoptic vesicles — Synaptic vesicles store acetylcholine in the nerve ending; blockers don't target them.
• Central nervous system (CNS) — They don't act on the CNS (patients remain conscious if not sedated).
• Muscle membrane — They act at the end plate receptors, not the whole muscle membrane.

⭐ Key point: Neuromuscular blockers cause NO sedation or pain relief. Always give sedation and analgesia with them, to prevent awareness.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q74$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2198, $s$Which patient can safely receive AB-negative blood?$s$, $s$["O-negative", "B-negative", "AB-negative", "A-negative"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: AB-negative
AB-negative red cells carry the A antigen, the B antigen, and no D (Rh) antigen. They can only be given safely to someone whose plasma has NO anti-A and NO anti-B antibodies, which means a group AB recipient. Because the cells are Rh-negative, they can go to an AB-negative (or AB-positive) patient. Among the options, only the AB-negative patient can safely receive them.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• O-negative — An O-negative patient has both anti-A and anti-B, which would destroy AB cells (a haemolytic reaction).
• B-negative — A B-negative patient has anti-A, which would attack the A antigen on AB cells.
• A-negative — An A-negative patient has anti-B, which would attack the B antigen on AB cells.

⭐ Key point: Red cells: O-negative is the universal donor and AB-positive is the universal recipient. Plasma is the reverse: AB plasma is the universal plasma donor.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q5$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2199, $s$Diffusion hypoxia after discontinuing nitrous oxide anaesthesia is best prevented by:$s$, $s$["Rapidly increasing volatile agent concentration", "Administering 100% oxygen for several minutes at the end of the case before returning to room air", "Hyperventilating the patient with room air", "Administering naloxone"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Administering 100% oxygen for several minutes at the end of the case before returning to room air
Nitrous oxide is very soluble in blood. When it is stopped at the end of anaesthesia, large amounts rush OUT of the blood into the alveoli and dilute the oxygen there. If the patient breathes room air at that moment, the alveolar oxygen can drop dangerously, causing hypoxia (diffusion hypoxia, the Fink effect). Giving 100% oxygen for 5–10 minutes after stopping nitrous oxide prevents this.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rapidly increasing volatile agent concentration — Increasing the volatile agent deepens anaesthesia and doesn't solve the oxygen dilution.
• Hyperventilating the patient with room air — Hyperventilating with room air would still give low oxygen.
• Administering naloxone — Naloxone reverses opioids; it doesn't prevent diffusion hypoxia.

⭐ Key point: Nitrous oxide also expands gas-filled spaces, so avoid it in pneumothorax, bowel obstruction, middle-ear surgery and air embolism.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q106$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2200, $s$A pilot study provides the opportunity to:$s$, $s$["Publish the results early", "Test the research subjects", "Understand the research problem", "Test the research instrument"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Test the research instrument
A pilot study is a small trial run done before the main study. Its main purpose is to TEST the research instrument (questionnaire, checklist, equipment) and procedures: are the questions clear, how long does it take, does data collection work, is the tool reliable? Problems are fixed before the full study, saving time and money.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Publish the results early — Pilot results are not meant for early publication; they test feasibility.
• Test the research subjects — Pilot studies test the methods, not the research subjects themselves.
• Understand the research problem — The research problem should be understood before the pilot, from the literature review.

⭐ Key point: Participants in the pilot are usually NOT included in the main study sample.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q172$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2201, $s$To ensure homogenization when diluting powdered medication in a vial, the nurse should:$s$, $s$["Do nothing after adding the solution to the vial.", "Invert the vial and let it stand for 1 minute.", "Shake the vial vigorously.", "Roll the vial gently between the palms."]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Roll the vial gently between the palms.
After adding diluent to a powdered drug, the nurse must mix it evenly (homogenise) without damaging it. The correct method is to ROLL THE VIAL GENTLY BETWEEN THE PALMS until all the powder has dissolved and the solution is clear. Rolling mixes the drug without making froth or air bubbles. Many drugs — especially proteins (insulin suspensions, monoclonal antibodies, some antibiotics, vaccines) — can be DAMAGED (denatured) or form foam by vigorous shaking, which can change the dose drawn up. After mixing, check the solution for particles or discolouration, label the vial with date/time/strength if multi-dose, and follow storage instructions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Do nothing after adding the solution to the vial. — Doing NOTHING after adding diluent may leave undissolved powder, giving the wrong dose.
• Invert the vial and let it stand for 1 minute. — Inverting and leaving it to stand for 1 minute does not reliably dissolve the powder or mix the solution evenly.
• Shake the vial vigorously. — SHAKING VIGOROUSLY creates foam and bubbles and may denature protein drugs, so it is avoided.

⭐ Key point: Reconstituted vials: ROLL GENTLY between the palms — do not shake. Check the solution is clear before drawing up.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q31$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2202, $s$Emergence delirium following general anaesthesia is characterised by:$s$, $s$["Calm, oriented behaviour", "Agitation, confusion, and thrashing shortly after waking", "Prolonged unconsciousness", "Normal cognitive function throughout"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Agitation, confusion, and thrashing shortly after waking
EMERGENCE DELIRIUM (emergence agitation) happens in the first minutes as a patient WAKES from general anaesthesia: they are AGITATED, CONFUSED, crying, restless, THRASHING about, may pull at drains, lines and the airway, and do not recognise surroundings. It usually settles within 10–30 minutes. It is more common in children, young adults, after volatile agents (sevoflurane), with pain, full bladder, hypoxia, and in patients with PTSD or anxiety. Nursing: protect the patient from injury and falls (side rails, stay close), check and treat causes (pain, HYPOXIA, hypercapnia, hypoglycaemia, full bladder), calm reassurance, and small doses of sedatives/opioids as prescribed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Calm, oriented behaviour — Calm, oriented behaviour is NORMAL recovery, not emergence delirium.
• Prolonged unconsciousness — Prolonged unconsciousness is DELAYED EMERGENCE, a different problem.
• Normal cognitive function throughout — Normal cognitive function throughout means no delirium is present.

⭐ Key point: Emergence delirium = agitation, confusion and thrashing right after waking from anaesthesia. Protect from injury; check for hypoxia, pain, full bladder.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q99$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2203, $s$A patient recovering from surgery develops confusion, agitation, and disorientation on post-operative day 2, with no prior history of cognitive impairment. This is most consistent with:$s$, $s$["Baseline dementia", "Postoperative delirium", "Normal anaesthesia recovery expected for weeks", "A permanent neurological condition"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Postoperative delirium
POSTOPERATIVE DELIRIUM is an ACUTE, FLUCTUATING disturbance of attention, awareness and thinking that starts after surgery — typically on POST-OP DAYS 1–3 — in a patient who was previously cognitively normal. Signs: confusion, disorientation, agitation (or quietness), poor attention, sleep–wake disturbance, sometimes hallucinations. Risk factors: older age, major surgery, pain, opioids/benzodiazepines, infection, dehydration, hypoxia, electrolyte imbalance, urinary retention, sleep deprivation, alcohol withdrawal. It is usually reversible when the cause is treated, but it increases complications and length of stay. Screen with CAM, then look for and treat the cause.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Baseline dementia — BASELINE DEMENTIA is a long-standing, slowly progressive problem; this patient had NO previous cognitive impairment.
• Normal anaesthesia recovery expected for weeks — Normal anaesthesia recovery takes minutes to hours, not confusion lasting for weeks; day-2 confusion is NOT normal.
• A permanent neurological condition — A permanent neurological condition is unlikely; delirium is acute and usually reversible (though a stroke must be excluded if there are focal signs).

⭐ Key point: New confusion/agitation on post-op day 1–3 in a previously normal patient = POSTOPERATIVE DELIRIUM. Find and treat the cause.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q100$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2204, $s$The definitive drug treatment for malignant hyperthermia is:$s$, $s$["Dantrolene", "Naloxone", "Flumazenil", "Neostigmine"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dantrolene
MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA (MH) is a rare inherited muscle disorder triggered by volatile anaesthetic gases (sevoflurane, desflurane, isoflurane) and SUCCINYLCHOLINE. Uncontrolled calcium release in muscle causes: rising END-TIDAL CO2 (often the earliest sign), tachycardia, muscle rigidity (masseter spasm), rapidly RISING TEMPERATURE, acidosis, HYPERKALAEMIA, rhabdomyolysis and arrhythmias. The definitive drug is DANTROLENE (2.5 mg/kg IV, repeated until signs settle, up to about 10 mg/kg). It stops calcium release from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, relaxing muscles and stopping heat production. Also: stop triggering agents, hyperventilate with 100% oxygen, active cooling, treat hyperkalaemia and arrhythmias, monitor urine output (myoglobin).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Naloxone — NALOXONE reverses opioids, not MH.
• Flumazenil — FLUMAZENIL reverses benzodiazepines, not MH.
• Neostigmine — NEOSTIGMINE reverses non-depolarising muscle relaxants (e.g. rocuronium), not MH.

⭐ Key point: Malignant hyperthermia → DANTROLENE 2.5 mg/kg IV. Triggers: volatile gases + succinylcholine. Earliest sign: rising EtCO2.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q101$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2205, $s$During Guedel's stages of anaesthesia, surgical procedures are typically performed during:$s$, $s$["Stage I (analgesia)", "Stage II (excitement)", "Stage III (surgical anaesthesia)", "Stage IV (overdose)"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stage III (surgical anaesthesia)
GUEDEL'S STAGES OF ANAESTHESIA (described with ether) are: STAGE I — ANALGESIA: from induction to loss of consciousness; patient is drowsy and pain is reduced. STAGE II — EXCITEMENT (delirium): after loss of consciousness; irregular breathing, struggling, vomiting, breath-holding — dangerous, so modern drugs aim to pass through it quickly. STAGE III — SURGICAL ANAESTHESIA: regular breathing, loss of reflexes, muscle relaxation, eyes central — SURGERY is performed here (it has 4 planes). STAGE IV — OVERDOSE (medullary paralysis): breathing stops, circulatory collapse — death unless reversed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stage I (analgesia) — STAGE I gives analgesia but the patient is still conscious and moving — not suitable for surgery.
• Stage II (excitement) — STAGE II is the excitement stage with uncontrolled movement and risk of vomiting/laryngospasm — never used for surgery.
• Stage IV (overdose) — STAGE IV is overdose with respiratory and circulatory failure — dangerous, not a working stage.

⭐ Key point: Guedel: I analgesia, II excitement, III SURGICAL anaesthesia (operate here), IV overdose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q108$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2206, $s$A patient in the recovery room after general anaesthesia has delayed emergence (remains unrousable well beyond expected time). The nurse should consider all of the following causes EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Residual anaesthetic/opioid/sedative effect", "Hypoglycaemia or metabolic derangement", "Improved cerebral perfusion", "Intraoperative stroke or other neurological event"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Improved cerebral perfusion
DELAYED EMERGENCE means the patient does not wake up within the expected time (often 30–60 minutes) after anaesthesia. Causes to check: (1) DRUGS — residual anaesthetic, OPIOIDS, BENZODIAZEPINES, muscle relaxants (check reversal); (2) METABOLIC — HYPOGLYCAEMIA, hyperglycaemia, hyponatraemia, uraemia, liver failure, hypothyroidism; (3) respiratory — HYPOXIA, high CO2 (CO2 narcosis); (4) HYPOTHERMIA; (5) NEUROLOGICAL — intraoperative STROKE, intracranial bleeding, seizures (non-convulsive), raised ICP. Nursing: ABC, check SpO2, check blood glucose, temperature, review drugs given, consider reversal agents, check pupils and limbs for focal signs, call the anaesthetist. IMPROVED cerebral perfusion would help the patient wake up; it is not a cause of delayed waking.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Residual anaesthetic/opioid/sedative effect — RESIDUAL anaesthetic/opioid/sedative IS the most common cause.
• Hypoglycaemia or metabolic derangement — HYPOGLYCAEMIA and other metabolic problems ARE important causes — always check glucose.
• Intraoperative stroke or other neurological event — STROKE or other neurological events ARE serious causes to consider.

⭐ Key point: Delayed emergence: think DRUGS, METABOLIC (glucose!), hypoxia/CO2, hypothermia, NEURO (stroke). Improved perfusion is not a cause.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q109$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2207, $s$Anaesthetic death investigations typically consider all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Adequacy of pre-anaesthetic assessment", "Appropriateness of drug selection and dosing", "Monitoring during the procedure", "The patient's choice of hospital ward décor"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The patient's choice of hospital ward décor
When a patient dies under or after ANAESTHESIA, a structured investigation (audit, mortality review, confidential enquiry) looks at the whole process of care: PRE-ANAESTHETIC ASSESSMENT (history, airway, ASA grade, fasting, investigations, consent); choice and DOSE of DRUGS (allergies, overdose, wrong drug); MONITORING during the procedure (SpO2, capnography, ECG, BP, temperature) and how alarms were responded to; equipment checks; airway management; staffing, skill and supervision; post-operative recovery care; and documentation. The aim is to learn and prevent future deaths, not only to blame. The patient's choice of ward DÉCOR has no relationship to anaesthetic safety.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adequacy of pre-anaesthetic assessment — PRE-ANAESTHETIC ASSESSMENT IS reviewed — poor assessment is a common cause of avoidable deaths.
• Appropriateness of drug selection and dosing — DRUG SELECTION and DOSING ARE reviewed — drug errors and reactions are key causes.
• Monitoring during the procedure — MONITORING during the procedure IS reviewed — missed hypoxia or oesophageal intubation can be fatal.

⭐ Key point: Anaesthetic death reviews look at assessment, drugs, monitoring, equipment, airway, staff and recovery — not ward décor.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q111$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2208, $s$Neuromuscular blocking agents (e.g. vecuronium) must always be administered together with:$s$, $s$["Nothing else is required", "Adequate sedation and analgesia, since paralytics do not sedate or relieve pain", "A diuretic", "An antihistamine"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Adequate sedation and analgesia, since paralytics do not sedate or relieve pain
NEUROMUSCULAR BLOCKING AGENTS (e.g. vecuronium, rocuronium, cisatracurium) PARALYSE muscles but do NOT provide SEDATION or PAIN RELIEF. A paralysed patient may be fully awake and in pain without being able to show it — a terrifying experience. They must ALWAYS be given with ADEQUATE SEDATION and ANALGESIA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nothing else is required — Sedation and analgesia are essential.
• A diuretic — Diuretics are not required.
• An antihistamine — Antihistamines are not required.

⭐ Key point: Monitor depth of paralysis with train-of-four, provide eye care and pressure care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q39$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2209, $s$After a pneumonectomy, the trachea characteristically shifts:$s$, $s$["Toward the operated (empty) side", "Away from the operated side", "Never shifts after pneumonectomy", "Shifts downward only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Toward the operated (empty) side
After a PNEUMONECTOMY (removal of a whole lung), the empty space fills with fluid over time, and the MEDIASTINUM and TRACHEA shift TOWARDS the OPERATED (empty) side, because there is no lung there to keep them in place. This is expected.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Away from the operated side — Shift AWAY from the operated side would suggest something is pushing (e.g. excessive fluid or bleeding).
• Never shifts after pneumonectomy — The trachea does shift after pneumonectomy.
• Shifts downward only — The trachea doesn't just shift downward.

⭐ Key point: A sudden shift AWAY from the operated side may indicate bleeding or bronchopleural fistula — report urgently.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q42$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2210, $s$Hypercarbia during anaesthesia refers to:$s$, $s$["Elevated blood carbon dioxide levels", "Low blood oxygen levels", "Elevated blood glucose", "Low blood pressure"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Elevated blood carbon dioxide levels
HYPERCARBIA (also called hypercapnia) means an ELEVATED level of CARBON DIOXIDE in the blood (PaCO2 above 45 mmHg). During anaesthesia, causes include: HYPOVENTILATION (opioids, residual muscle relaxants, inadequate ventilator settings), REBREATHING (exhausted CO2 absorber, faulty valves), increased CO2 PRODUCTION (fever, sepsis, MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA), and CO2 absorption during laparoscopy. Effects: acidosis, tachycardia, hypertension, arrhythmias, drowsiness, and raised ICP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Low blood oxygen levels — LOW blood oxygen is HYPOXAEMIA.
• Elevated blood glucose — High blood glucose is HYPERGLYCAEMIA.
• Low blood pressure — Low BP is HYPOTENSION.

⭐ Key point: Capnography (end-tidal CO2) is the main monitor to detect hypercarbia during anaesthesia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q106$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2211, $s$A patient is extubated after general anaesthesia and shortly develops high-pitched inspiratory stridor. The nurse should suspect:$s$, $s$["Normal post-extubation voice change with no concern", "Laryngospasm or airway oedema, requiring urgent assessment and possible intervention", "Improved airway patency", "A sign of adequate recovery"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Laryngospasm or airway oedema, requiring urgent assessment and possible intervention
HIGH-PITCHED INSPIRATORY STRIDOR soon after extubation means the UPPER AIRWAY is NARROWED — usually from LARYNGOSPASM (spasm of the vocal cords) or AIRWAY OEDEMA (swelling of the larynx from the tube). It can quickly progress to complete obstruction. Urgent actions: call for help, sit the patient up, give high-flow oxygen, and consider nebulised adrenaline, IV steroids (dexamethasone), positive pressure (CPAP) for laryngospasm, and preparation for re-intubation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal post-extubation voice change with no concern — Stridor is not a normal voice change; hoarseness may be normal, stridor is not.
• Improved airway patency — Stridor shows a NARROWED airway, not improved patency.
• A sign of adequate recovery — Stridor is a sign of a dangerous problem, not adequate recovery.

⭐ Key point: A "cuff leak test" before extubation can help predict post-extubation airway oedema.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q109$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2212, $s$The nurse is monitoring a sedated patient using the Bispectral Index (BIS) monitoring. The purpose of BIS monitoring is to:$s$, $s$["Monitor the patient's blood pressure", "Assess the patient's heart rate", "Measure the patient's level of consciousness", "Assess the patient's muscle paralysis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Measure the patient's level of consciousness
The BISPECTRAL INDEX (BIS) monitor uses sensors on the forehead to analyse the brain's electrical activity (EEG) and produce a number from 0 to 100 that reflects the LEVEL OF CONSCIOUSNESS (depth of sedation/anaesthesia). 100 = fully awake; 60–80 = light to moderate sedation; 40–60 = general anaesthesia (low risk of awareness); below 40 = deep hypnotic state; 0 = no brain activity. It is especially useful in PARALYSED patients, who cannot show signs of awareness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Monitor the patient's blood pressure — BIS does not measure blood pressure.
• Assess the patient's heart rate — BIS does not measure heart rate.
• Assess the patient's muscle paralysis — Muscle paralysis is assessed with a peripheral nerve stimulator (train-of-four), not BIS.

⭐ Key point: Use BIS together with clinical assessment; muscle activity and some drugs can affect readings.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q112$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2213, $s$A patient recovering from anaesthesia after a lung transplant requires particular nursing vigilance for:$s$, $s$["Signs of rejection and pulmonary infection", "Only cardiac monitoring, with no respiratory focus", "Skin integrity exclusively", "Bowel function exclusively"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Signs of rejection and pulmonary infection
After LUNG TRANSPLANT, patients take strong immunosuppressive drugs to prevent REJECTION, which makes them very vulnerable to INFECTION. The transplanted lung is also directly exposed to inhaled germs and has impaired cough reflex and lymphatic drainage. Nursing vigilance focuses on signs of REJECTION (breathlessness, falling SpO2, fever, new infiltrates, declining lung function) and PULMONARY INFECTION (fever, purulent sputum, new chest X-ray changes), as well as primary graft dysfunction, fluid balance and pain control.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only cardiac monitoring, with no respiratory focus — Respiratory monitoring is essential after lung transplant, not just cardiac.
• Skin integrity exclusively — Skin care is important, but not the main focus.
• Bowel function exclusively — Bowel function is important, but not the main focus.

⭐ Key point: Strict infection control (hand hygiene, protective isolation) and early mobilisation and physiotherapy are essential.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q113$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2214, $s$The heart's four chambers consist of:$s$, $s$["Two atria and two ventricles", "Four ventricles only", "Four atria only", "One atrium and three ventricles"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Two atria and two ventricles
The heart has FOUR CHAMBERS: TWO ATRIA (upper, thin-walled receiving chambers) and TWO VENTRICLES (lower, thick-walled pumping chambers). The right atrium and ventricle handle deoxygenated blood (to the lungs); the left atrium and ventricle handle oxygenated blood (to the body). Valves between them (tricuspid, mitral) and at the exits (pulmonary, aortic) keep blood flowing in one direction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Four ventricles only — Four ventricles only is incorrect.
• Four atria only — Four atria only is incorrect.
• One atrium and three ventricles — One atrium and three ventricles is incorrect.

⭐ Key point: Blood flow order: body → right atrium → right ventricle → lungs → left atrium → left ventricle → aorta → body.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q13$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2215, $s$Water reabsorbed by the collecting duct enters the:$s$, $s$["Nephron loop", "Minor calyx", "Ureter", "Vasa recta"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vasa recta
In the COLLECTING DUCT, ADH (antidiuretic hormone) makes the walls permeable to water. Water moves out of the duct into the very concentrated tissue of the medulla and is then taken up by the VASA RECTA — the long, looping capillaries that run alongside the loops of Henle — which return the water to the general circulation. This allows the body to produce concentrated urine and conserve water.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nephron loop — The nephron loop (loop of Henle) is part of the nephron, not where collecting duct water goes.
• Minor calyx — The minor calyx receives urine, not reabsorbed water.
• Ureter — The ureter carries urine to the bladder.

⭐ Key point: Without ADH (diabetes insipidus), the collecting duct stays impermeable to water, causing large volumes of dilute urine.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q15$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2216, $s$To prevent graft-versus-host disease, blood is:$s$, $s$["Leukocyte-reduced", "Washed", "Irradiated", "Frozen"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Irradiated
TRANSFUSION-ASSOCIATED GRAFT-VERSUS-HOST DISEASE (TA-GvHD) happens when donor T-lymphocytes (white cells) in transfused blood attack the recipient's tissues. It is rare but almost always fatal. IRRADIATING blood (gamma or X-ray) damages the DNA of donor lymphocytes so they cannot multiply, preventing TA-GvHD. Irradiated blood is given to high-risk patients: immunocompromised (e.g. stem cell transplant, Hodgkin lymphoma, certain chemotherapies), intrauterine and neonatal exchange transfusions, and transfusions from relatives.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Leukocyte-reduced — Leukocyte reduction reduces febrile reactions and CMV transmission but does not reliably prevent TA-GvHD.
• Washed — Washing removes plasma proteins (for severe allergic reactions).
• Frozen — Freezing is used for long-term storage of rare blood.

⭐ Key point: Check that "irradiated" is specified on blood products when required.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q19$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2217, $s$The preferred route for surgical antibiotic prophylaxis is:$s$, $s$["Oral", "Systemic (IV)", "Topical only", "Intramuscular only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Systemic (IV)
SURGICAL ANTIBIOTIC PROPHYLAXIS is given by the SYSTEMIC (IV) route, usually within 60 MINUTES BEFORE the skin incision, so that high antibiotic levels are present in the tissues at the time of surgery. It reduces surgical site infections.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral — Oral absorption is unreliable and too slow for timing before surgery.
• Topical only — Topical antibiotics alone are not standard prophylaxis.
• Intramuscular only — IM is rarely used.

⭐ Key point: A repeat dose may be needed for long operations or major blood loss; prophylaxis is usually not continued beyond 24 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q38$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2218, $s$Which of the following is an example of secondary (long-term) storage?$s$, $s$["RAM", "Hard disk drive", "CPU cache", "CPU register"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hard disk drive
Computer memory is divided into PRIMARY memory (RAM, cache, registers — fast but TEMPORARY, lost when the power goes off) and SECONDARY storage (LONG-TERM, PERMANENT, keeps data when the power is off). A HARD DISK DRIVE (HDD) is secondary storage: it stores the operating system, programs and files (e.g. patient records) permanently on spinning magnetic disks. Other secondary storage: solid-state drives (SSD), USB flash drives, memory cards, optical discs (CD/DVD) and cloud storage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• RAM — RAM (Random Access Memory) is PRIMARY memory — it holds what the computer is working on now and is erased when the power is switched off.
• CPU cache — CPU cache is very fast, small, temporary memory inside or near the processor.
• CPU register — CPU registers are tiny storage locations inside the processor that hold data being processed at that instant.

⭐ Key point: Always save work to secondary storage (and back it up) — unsaved data in RAM is lost during power cuts, which are common in many hospitals.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q46$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2219, $s$Which of the following is not a treatment modality for patient with epiglottitis?$s$, $s$["Tracheostomy", "Steroids", "Antibiotics", "Observation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Observation
EPIGLOTTITIS is a life-threatening swelling of the epiglottis that can suddenly block the airway. It always needs ACTIVE treatment, not simple observation. Treatment includes: SECURING THE AIRWAY (calling senior anaesthesia/ENT help; intubation in theatre, with a surgical airway — TRACHEOSTOMY or cricothyroidotomy — if intubation fails), IV ANTIBIOTICS (e.g. ceftriaxone, against Haemophilus influenzae type b and streptococci), STEROIDS (dexamethasone, to reduce swelling), humidified oxygen, and ICU monitoring. "OBSERVATION" ALONE is NOT a treatment modality — waiting without intervention risks sudden, complete airway obstruction and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tracheostomy — Tracheostomy (or cricothyroidotomy) IS used when the airway cannot be secured by intubation.
• Steroids — Steroids ARE used to reduce airway swelling.
• Antibiotics — Antibiotics ARE essential to treat the bacterial infection.

⭐ Key point: Do not lie the patient flat, do not examine the throat with a tongue depressor, and do not cause distress (e.g. cannulation) before airway experts arrive.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q55$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2220, $s$Which volatile anaesthetic agent has the highest blood-gas partition coefficient, giving it the slowest onset and offset among modern agents?$s$, $s$["Desflurane", "Sevoflurane", "Halothane", "Nitrous oxide"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Halothane
The BLOOD-GAS PARTITION COEFFICIENT shows how soluble an anaesthetic gas is in blood. A HIGH coefficient means the gas dissolves easily in blood, so it takes LONGER to build up enough concentration to act on the brain (SLOW onset) and to wash out (SLOW offset). HALOTHANE has the HIGHEST coefficient among the options (about 2.4).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Desflurane — Desflurane has the LOWEST (about 0.42) — fastest onset and offset.
• Sevoflurane — Sevoflurane is low (about 0.65) — fast, used for inhalational induction.
• Nitrous oxide — Nitrous oxide is low (about 0.47) — fast.

⭐ Key point: Halothane is rarely used today because of liver toxicity and arrhythmias.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q104$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2221, $s$The following are the classical hemolytic transfusion reaction in general anesthesia except:$s$, $s$["Hypotension", "Wheezing", "Hemoglobinuria", "bradycardia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: bradycardia
A HAEMOLYTIC TRANSFUSION REACTION (usually ABO incompatibility) destroys the transfused red cells. Under GENERAL ANAESTHESIA, the usual early symptoms (fever, chills, back or chest pain, anxiety) are hidden. Classic signs then include: unexplained HYPOTENSION, TACHYCARDIA, HAEMOGLOBINURIA (red or dark urine in the catheter bag), diffuse oozing from the wound and IV sites (DIC), and sometimes WHEEZING/bronchospasm. BRADYCARDIA is NOT a classic sign — the heart usually speeds up (tachycardia) in response to hypotension.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypotension — Hypotension IS a classic sign under anaesthesia.
• Wheezing — Wheezing (bronchospasm) can occur.
• Hemoglobinuria — Haemoglobinuria IS a classic sign.

⭐ Key point: If suspected: stop the transfusion immediately, keep the vein open with saline through a new set, support BP, maintain urine output, check clotting, and return the blood bag to the lab with samples.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q111$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2222, $s$A patient who has been admitted with a gastric ulcer now develops severe epigastric pain that spreads over the entire abdomen. He is very pale and is lying on his side. What should be the nurse's immediate action?$s$, $s$["Reposition the patient In Semi-Fowler's Position.", "Perform deep palpations on the abdomen.", "Notify the doctor and check the vitals.", "Immediately administer opioids."]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Notify the doctor and check the vitals.
SEVERE EPIGASTRIC PAIN spreading over the WHOLE ABDOMEN in a patient with a GASTRIC ULCER suggests PERFORATION and PERITONITIS. PALLOR suggests shock or bleeding. The immediate action is to NOTIFY THE DOCTOR and CHECK VITAL SIGNS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reposition the patient In Semi-Fowler's Position. — Repositioning is not the priority.
• Perform deep palpations on the abdomen. — Deep palpation would cause pain and is unnecessary.
• Immediately administer opioids. — Opioids without assessment may mask signs.

⭐ Key point: Prepare for IV access, fluids, nil by mouth and surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q185$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2223, $s$A patient's surgical wound shows increasing redness, warmth, and purulent discharge on post-operative day 4, with a fever of 38.6°C. The nurse should suspect:$s$, $s$["Surgical site infection, requiring assessment, wound culture, and physician notification", "A drug reaction", "No clinical significance", "Normal wound healing"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Surgical site infection, requiring assessment, wound culture, and physician notification
A SURGICAL SITE INFECTION (SSI) typically appears 3–7 days after surgery. Signs: increasing REDNESS, WARMTH, SWELLING, PAIN or tenderness, PURULENT (pus) DISCHARGE, wound breakdown, and FEVER (here 38.6 °C). The nurse should ASSESS the wound (size, colour, discharge, odour, edges), take a WOUND SWAB for CULTURE, mark the area of redness, monitor vital signs (sepsis screening), and NOTIFY THE PHYSICIAN (antibiotics, possible opening and drainage of the wound).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A drug reaction — A drug reaction would not cause pus discharge from the wound.
• No clinical significance — These signs are clinically significant.
• Normal wound healing — Normal healing shows decreasing redness, with no pus or fever after day 2–3.

⭐ Key point: Prevent SSI: hand hygiene, aseptic dressing technique, glucose control, nutrition, and keeping dressings clean and dry.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q155$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2224, $s$A patient develops a temperature of 39.5 degrees C with tachycardia and jaw muscle rigidity shortly after induction with succinylcholine. What should the nurse suspect first?$s$, $s$["A routine post-operative fever, so paracetamol alone should be given", "Nothing abnormal; reassess again in 4 hours", "Malignant hyperthermia, and the MH protocol should be initiated immediately", "The jaw rigidity means more succinylcholine is needed to relax the muscle"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Malignant hyperthermia, and the MH protocol should be initiated immediately
MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA (MH) is a rare inherited muscle disorder triggered by SUCCINYLCHOLINE and VOLATILE ANAESTHETICS. Early signs: JAW (MASSETER) MUSCLE RIGIDITY, TACHYCARDIA, a rapidly rising END-TIDAL CO2, muscle rigidity, and then a RISING TEMPERATURE (here 39.5 °C), acidosis and hyperkalaemia. It is life-threatening. The MH PROTOCOL must start IMMEDIATELY: stop triggering agents, call for help, hyperventilate with 100% oxygen, give IV DANTROLENE, cool the patient, and treat complications.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A routine post-operative fever, so paracetamol alone should be given — This is not a routine post-operative fever.
• Nothing abnormal; reassess again in 4 hours — Waiting 4 hours could be fatal.
• The jaw rigidity means more succinylcholine is needed to relax the muscle — More succinylcholine would worsen MH.

⭐ Key point: Every theatre should have an MH kit with dantrolene. Test family members for susceptibility.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q168$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2225, $s$In data transfer process, what does FTP stand for?$s$, $s$["File transmission protocol", "File transfer Protocol", "File transfer program", "File transfer protection"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: File transfer Protocol
FTP stands for FILE TRANSFER PROTOCOL. It is a standard set of rules (protocol) for TRANSFERRING FILES between computers over a network such as the internet — e.g. uploading files to a web server or downloading large files. Secure versions (SFTP, FTPS) encrypt the transfer.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• File transmission protocol — "File transmission protocol" is not the correct expansion.
• File transfer program — "File transfer program" is not correct; FTP is a protocol (set of rules), not a program.
• File transfer protection — "File transfer protection" is not correct.

⭐ Key point: Other protocols: HTTP/HTTPS (web pages), SMTP (sending email), POP3/IMAP (receiving email), TCP/IP (internet communication).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q32$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2226, $s$Which one is NOT an input device?$s$, $s$["Monitor", "Mouse", "Scanner", "Keyboard"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Monitor
A MONITOR is an OUTPUT device — it DISPLAYS information (text, images, video) from the computer to the user. It does NOT send data INTO the computer, so it is NOT an input device (unless it is a touchscreen, which is both input and output).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mouse — A mouse is an input device (pointing and clicking).
• Scanner — A scanner is an input device (converts paper documents or images into digital form).
• Keyboard — A keyboard is an input device (typing text and commands).

⭐ Key point: Common input devices: keyboard, mouse, scanner, microphone, webcam, barcode reader. Common output devices: monitor, printer, speakers, projector.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q36$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2227, $s$During general anaesthesia with sevoflurane and suxamethonium, a patient develops masseter rigidity, a rapidly rising end-tidal CO₂ despite increased ventilation, tachycardia, rising temperature and hyperkalaemia. The diagnosis and specific treatment are:$s$, $s$["Neuroleptic malignant syndrome — bromocriptine", "Thyroid storm — propylthiouracil and iodine", "Malignant hyperthermia — IV dantrolene", "Serotonin syndrome — cyproheptadine"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Malignant hyperthermia — IV dantrolene
MALIGNANT HYPERTHERMIA (MH) is a rare inherited condition triggered by VOLATILE ANAESTHETICS (e.g. sevoflurane) and SUXAMETHONIUM. Muscle calcium regulation fails, causing massive muscle activity. Features: MASSETER RIGIDITY (jaw stiffness), RAPIDLY RISING END-TIDAL CO2 (often the earliest sign), TACHYCARDIA, RISING TEMPERATURE (may be late), HYPERKALAEMIA, acidosis and rhabdomyolysis. Specific treatment: IV DANTROLENE (2.5 mg/kg, repeated as needed), which stops calcium release in muscle.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Neuroleptic malignant syndrome — bromocriptine — NMS follows antipsychotic use, developing over days.
• Thyroid storm — propylthiouracil and iodine — Thyroid storm doesn't cause masseter rigidity and isn't triggered by anaesthetic gases.
• Serotonin syndrome — cyproheptadine — Serotonin syndrome follows serotonergic drugs.

⭐ Key point: Stop the volatile agent, hyperventilate with 100% oxygen, cool the patient, treat hyperkalaemia and arrhythmias. Test family members for susceptibility.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q159$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2228, $s$A patient with 50% burns sustained 3 days ago needs rapid sequence intubation. Which drug should be AVOIDED?$s$, $s$["Suxamethonium", "Fentanyl", "Ketamine", "Rocuronium"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Suxamethonium
SUXAMETHONIUM (succinylcholine) is a depolarising muscle relaxant. It works by stimulating acetylcholine receptors on muscle, and each time it does, POTASSIUM LEAKS OUT of the muscle cells. Normally this raises potassium by only about 0.5 mmol/L. After major BURNS (from about 24–48 hours onwards, and lasting for months), the muscles grow MANY EXTRA acetylcholine receptors spread all over the muscle membrane ("up-regulation"). Suxamethonium then triggers a MASSIVE potassium release, causing severe HYPERKALAEMIA, ventricular arrhythmias and CARDIAC ARREST. This patient is 3 days after a 50% burn, so suxamethonium must be AVOIDED.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fentanyl — Fentanyl (an opioid) is safe after burns; it blunts the stress response to intubation, though doses may need adjusting for BP.
• Ketamine — Ketamine is safe and useful in burns: it keeps the BP up, provides pain relief and maintains airway reflexes.
• Rocuronium — Rocuronium (a non-depolarising relaxant) does not release potassium and is the preferred drug for rapid sequence intubation when suxamethonium is contraindicated (higher doses are often needed in burns).

⭐ Key point: Other situations to avoid suxamethonium (risk of hyperkalaemia): spinal cord injury or stroke after the first few days, prolonged immobility or critical illness, crush injuries, muscular dystrophies, and existing hyperkalaemia. Also avoid in malignant hyperthermia susceptibility.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q181$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2229, $s$Sugammadex rapidly reverses neuromuscular blockade caused by:$s$, $s$["Suxamethonium", "All neuromuscular blocking drugs", "Rocuronium and vecuronium", "Atracurium and cisatracurium"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Rocuronium and vecuronium
SUGAMMADEX is a modified sugar molecule (a cyclodextrin) shaped like a ring. It ENCAPSULATES (wraps tightly around) the STEROIDAL non-depolarising muscle relaxants — mainly ROCURONIUM, and to a lesser extent VECURONIUM — and removes them from the neuromuscular junction. It can reverse even deep blockade within 2–3 minutes. This makes it very useful in a "can't intubate, can't oxygenate" emergency after rocuronium, and for rapid recovery of muscle strength after surgery. The drug-sugammadex complex is excreted by the kidneys.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Suxamethonium — Suxamethonium is a depolarising relaxant, broken down by plasma cholinesterase; sugammadex has no effect on it.
• All neuromuscular blocking drugs — Sugammadex does not work on all relaxants; it is specific to steroidal drugs.
• Atracurium and cisatracurium — Atracurium and cisatracurium are benzylisoquinolinium (non-steroidal) drugs; they are broken down by Hofmann elimination and are reversed by neostigmine (with glycopyrrolate), not sugammadex.

⭐ Key point: Sugammadex can rarely cause anaphylaxis and bradycardia. It reduces the effectiveness of hormonal contraceptives — advise women to use additional contraception for 7 days.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q182$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2230, $s$Minutes after a large dose of bupivacaine for a nerve block, a patient complains of a metallic taste, tingling around the mouth and tinnitus, then has a seizure followed by ventricular arrhythmias. The specific treatment is:$s$, $s$["IV dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg", "IV naloxone 400 micrograms", "IV flumazenil 200 micrograms", "IV 20% lipid emulsion 1.5 mL/kg"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV 20% lipid emulsion 1.5 mL/kg
LOCAL ANAESTHETIC SYSTEMIC TOXICITY (LAST) occurs when a local anaesthetic (especially BUPIVACAINE, which is very cardiotoxic) reaches high levels in the blood — from an overdose or accidental injection into a blood vessel. Early CNS signs: METALLIC TASTE, PERIORAL TINGLING (around the mouth), TINNITUS, light-headedness, agitation; then SEIZURES and loss of consciousness. Cardiovascular signs follow: bradycardia, heart block, VENTRICULAR ARRHYTHMIAS and cardiac arrest. The specific treatment is IV 20% LIPID EMULSION (Intralipid): 1.5 mL/kg bolus over 1 minute, then an infusion of 15 mL/kg/hour; boluses can be repeated. The lipid acts as a "LIPID SINK", pulling the fat-soluble local anaesthetic out of the heart and brain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg — Dantrolene treats malignant hyperthermia, not LAST.
• IV naloxone 400 micrograms — Naloxone reverses opioids; it has no effect on local anaesthetics.
• IV flumazenil 200 micrograms — Flumazenil reverses benzodiazepines and may worsen seizures.

⭐ Key point: Also: stop injecting, call for help, give 100% oxygen and secure the airway, treat seizures with benzodiazepines, and start CPR if needed — CPR may need to continue for a long time (over 1 hour). Always know the maximum safe dose (bupivacaine about 2 mg/kg).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q185$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2231, $s$Shortly after a spinal anaesthetic for caesarean section, a woman becomes profoundly hypotensive and bradycardic, cannot move her arms, says she cannot breathe, then loses consciousness and stops breathing. The most likely cause is:$s$, $s$["Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity", "Amniotic fluid embolism", "Vasovagal reaction", "High (total) spinal block"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: High (total) spinal block
A HIGH (or TOTAL) SPINAL BLOCK happens when the spinal anaesthetic spreads too far UP the spinal cord, sometimes reaching the brainstem. It causes: severe HYPOTENSION (blocking all sympathetic nerves, causing massive vasodilation) and BRADYCARDIA (blocking the cardio-accelerator nerves at T1–T4); WEAKNESS of the ARMS and hands (block reaching cervical nerves C5–T1); DIFFICULTY BREATHING ("I can't breathe") as the intercostal muscles and then the diaphragm (C3–C5) are paralysed; and finally LOSS OF CONSCIOUSNESS and APNOEA when the brainstem is affected. It usually occurs within minutes of the injection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity — LAST causes metallic taste, tinnitus, seizures and arrhythmias, but not progressive ascending arm weakness.
• Amniotic fluid embolism — Amniotic fluid embolism causes sudden collapse, hypoxia and DIC, not arm weakness after the spinal.
• Vasovagal reaction — A vasovagal reaction causes brief fainting with bradycardia and hypotension, but not arm paralysis and apnoea.

⭐ Key point: Treatment: call for help, secure the airway (intubate and ventilate), give vasopressors (ephedrine, phenylephrine, adrenaline if needed), atropine for bradycardia, IV fluids, left uterine displacement, and deliver the baby if the mother arrests. The block wears off in 1–2 hours; reassure the patient, who may be awake but paralysed — give sedation once intubated.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q186$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2232, $s$Mal-rotation with volvulus can be diagnosed through the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["ESR", "History taking", "Abdominal ultrasound", "Abdominal x-ray"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ESR
MALROTATION is when the bowel does not rotate and fix properly in the abdomen during fetal life. The loose small bowel can then twist on its blood supply (VOLVULUS), cutting off blood flow — a surgical emergency. It is diagnosed by: HISTORY (classically a newborn or infant with sudden BILIOUS — green — vomiting, abdominal distension, and later blood in the stool or shock), ABDOMINAL X-RAY (may show a "double bubble" or few gas-filled loops, or can be normal), ABDOMINAL ULTRASOUND (may show the "whirlpool sign" of twisted vessels and abnormal position of the mesenteric vessels), and the gold standard UPPER GI CONTRAST STUDY. The ESR (erythrocyte sedimentation rate) is a non-specific blood marker of inflammation; it does NOT diagnose malrotation, so it is the EXCEPTION.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• History taking — History taking IS useful: bilious vomiting in an infant must be treated as malrotation with volvulus until proven otherwise.
• Abdominal ultrasound — Ultrasound IS useful: it can show the reversed position of the superior mesenteric artery and vein and the "whirlpool" twist.
• Abdominal x-ray — Abdominal X-ray IS useful: it may show obstruction or a double bubble, and rules out perforation (free air).

⭐ Key point: Bilious vomiting in a newborn = surgical emergency. Pass a nasogastric tube, keep nil by mouth, give IV fluids, and call the paediatric surgeon. Delay can cause death of the whole small bowel.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q101$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2233, $s$The Code of Professional Conduct for nurses in Nigeria is issued by:$s$, $s$["The Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria", "The Federal Ministry of Health, Abuja", "The hospital's management board", "The Medical and Dental Council of Nigeria"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria
The NURSING AND MIDWIFERY COUNCIL OF NIGERIA (NMCN) is the statutory (legal) body that regulates nursing and midwifery in Nigeria. It sets standards of education and practice, registers and licenses nurses and midwives, issues the CODE OF PROFESSIONAL CONDUCT, and disciplines nurses who break the code (for misconduct such as negligence, falsifying records, breach of confidentiality).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The Federal Ministry of Health, Abuja — The Federal Ministry of Health makes health policy, but it is not the body that issues the nurses' professional code.
• The hospital's management board — A hospital's management board sets local policies, but it does not issue the national professional code.
• The Medical and Dental Council of Nigeria — The Medical and Dental Council of Nigeria (MDCN) regulates doctors and dentists, not nurses.

⭐ Key point: Every nurse must know and follow the NMCN Code of Professional Conduct, and must renew their licence to practise as required.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q153$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2234, $s$Which of these is an example of a structure standard in a critical care unit QA programme?$s$, $s$["Nurse documents pain score every 2 hours", "Ratio of one nurse to two ventilated patients", "Patient reports satisfaction with pain control", "Reduction in ventilator-associated pneumonia rate"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ratio of one nurse to two ventilated patients
Donabedian's model of quality uses three types of standards: STRUCTURE (the resources and setting — staff numbers, equipment, buildings, policies), PROCESS (what is done — the care activities), and OUTCOME (the results for patients). A NURSE-TO-PATIENT RATIO (e.g. one nurse to two ventilated patients) is about staffing resources, so it is a STRUCTURE standard.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nurse documents pain score every 2 hours — Documenting pain scores every 2 hours is a PROCESS standard (an activity of care).
• Patient reports satisfaction with pain control — Patient satisfaction with pain control is an OUTCOME standard.
• Reduction in ventilator-associated pneumonia rate — A reduction in VAP rate is an OUTCOME standard.

⭐ Key point: Structure = "what we have", Process = "what we do", Outcome = "what we achieve".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q164$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2235, $s$Anaesthetic deaths are reported and reviewed mainly to:$s$, $s$["Avoid informing the family about the cause of the death", "Identify and punish the anaesthetist responsible for the death", "Delay the death certificate until the review is complete", "Learn lessons and prevent deaths, with medico-legal accountability"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Learn lessons and prevent deaths, with medico-legal accountability
When a patient dies in connection with anaesthesia, the death is REPORTED (to the hospital, and where required, to the coroner/police and national bodies) and REVIEWED (e.g. in mortality meetings or national confidential enquiries). The main aim is to LEARN LESSONS — find out what went wrong (equipment, drugs, airway management, staffing, communication) and change systems to PREVENT future DEATHS. Reporting also ensures MEDICO-LEGAL ACCOUNTABILITY: the case is investigated transparently and the family is told honestly what happened.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Avoid informing the family about the cause of the death — Families have a right to an honest explanation (duty of candour); reviews must never be used to hide information.
• Identify and punish the anaesthetist responsible for the death — The aim is learning, not punishment. A blame culture discourages reporting and hides problems.
• Delay the death certificate until the review is complete — A review should not unnecessarily delay the death certificate or burial; legal procedures (e.g. coroner) are followed when required.

⭐ Key point: Many anaesthetic deaths are linked to airway problems, aspiration, drug errors and inadequate monitoring — so learning from reviews has improved safety worldwide.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q184$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2236, $s$A key principle of adult learning (andragogy) when teaching ICU staff is that adults:$s$, $s$["Do not need any feedback on their performance", "Prefer lectures with no participation or discussion", "Learn best when content is relevant and builds on experience", "Learn best by memorising facts without any context"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Learn best when content is relevant and builds on experience
ANDRAGOGY is the theory of how adults learn (Malcolm Knowles). Key principles: adults learn best when the content is RELEVANT to their work and life (they want to know why they need to learn something); learning BUILDS ON their previous EXPERIENCE; they are self-directed; they are problem-centred (prefer solving real problems); and they are motivated mainly by internal factors. For ICU staff, teaching should therefore use real cases, discussion of experiences, simulations and practical problem-solving.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Do not need any feedback on their performance — Adults need regular feedback to improve their performance.
• Prefer lectures with no participation or discussion — Adults usually prefer active participation and discussion, not passive lectures.
• Learn best by memorising facts without any context — Adults learn best when facts are given in context, not memorised without meaning.

⭐ Key point: Use case studies, simulation, reflective discussions and hands-on practice when teaching ICU nurses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q198$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2237, $s$A myasthenic crisis is distinguished from a cholinergic crisis by:$s$, $s$["Strength improves after edrophonium (or neostigmine)", "Pinpoint pupils and bradycardia are present", "Strength worsens after edrophonium is given", "Excess salivation and diarrhoea are present"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Strength improves after edrophonium (or neostigmine)
Both crises cause weakness. A MYASTHENIC crisis (too little anticholinesterase, or disease flare) IMPROVES with edrophonium or neostigmine, which increase acetylcholine. A CHOLINERGIC crisis (too much drug) WORSENS with more anticholinesterase and shows SLUDGE signs (salivation, sweating, diarrhoea), small pupils and bradycardia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pinpoint pupils and bradycardia are present — Pinpoint pupils and bradycardia suggest CHOLINERGIC crisis.
• Strength worsens after edrophonium is given — Worsening strength after edrophonium suggests CHOLINERGIC crisis.
• Excess salivation and diarrhoea are present — Excess salivation and diarrhoea suggest CHOLINERGIC crisis.

⭐ Key point: The edrophonium (Tensilon) test is rarely used now. Secure the airway first in any crisis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q52$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2238, $s$Non-surgical measures to reduce intra-abdominal pressure include:$s$, $s$["Large-volume IV fluid boluses to improve gut perfusion", "A tight abdominal binder to support the abdominal wall", "Prone positioning without any support under the abdomen", "NG decompression, sedation, avoiding a positive fluid balance"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: NG decompression, sedation, avoiding a positive fluid balance
Non-surgical measures to reduce IAP: NG and rectal tube DECOMPRESSION (remove gas and fluid from the gut), SEDATION and analgesia (relax the abdominal wall), AVOIDING a POSITIVE FLUID BALANCE (diuretics, CRRT), draining ascites, and brief neuromuscular blockade. Surgical decompression is needed if these fail.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Large-volume IV fluid boluses to improve gut perfusion — Large IV fluid boluses INCREASE gut swelling and IAP.
• A tight abdominal binder to support the abdominal wall — A tight abdominal binder INCREASES IAP.
• Prone positioning without any support under the abdomen — Prone positioning without abdominal support can increase IAP.

⭐ Key point: Monitor bladder pressure regularly in at-risk patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q63$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2239, $s$A health talk to relatives of ICU patients should be:$s$, $s$["Full of medical terms and abbreviations", "Simple, short and with time for questions", "Given without any preparation", "Very long, without any breaks"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Simple, short and with time for questions
Relatives of ICU patients are often stressed, worried and tired. A health talk should be SIMPLE (plain language, no jargon), SHORT, and allow TIME FOR QUESTIONS. This helps them understand and reduces their anxiety.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Full of medical terms and abbreviations — Medical terms and abbreviations confuse families.
• Given without any preparation — Preparation is needed to make sure the information is accurate and appropriate.
• Very long, without any breaks — Long talks without breaks overwhelm stressed relatives.

⭐ Key point: Use teach-back (ask relatives to explain in their own words) to check understanding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q198$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2240, $s$Reflective practice after a difficult ICU case helps nurses to:$s$, $s$["Avoid learning from the event", "Forget the event as fast as possible", "Learn from experience and improve care", "Find someone else to blame"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Learn from experience and improve care
REFLECTIVE PRACTICE means thinking carefully about an experience — what happened, how you felt, what went well and what didn't, and what you would do differently. It helps nurses LEARN FROM EXPERIENCE and IMPROVE future CARE. It also helps them process emotional stress after difficult cases.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Avoid learning from the event — Reflection is designed to help learning, not avoid it.
• Forget the event as fast as possible — Reflection helps nurses process and learn, not just forget.
• Find someone else to blame — Reflection is not about blaming others.

⭐ Key point: Gibbs' reflective cycle: Description, Feelings, Evaluation, Analysis, Conclusion, Action plan.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q199$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2241, $s$Critical thinking in ICU nursing involves:$s$, $s$["Following every order exactly without any thought", "Relying only on memory for all clinical decisions", "Analysing data, questioning assumptions, reasoned decisions", "Avoiding decisions and waiting for others to act"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Analysing data, questioning assumptions, reasoned decisions
CRITICAL THINKING in ICU nursing means ANALYSING DATA (vital signs, lab results, patient responses), QUESTIONING ASSUMPTIONS (e.g. "Is this really the cause?", "Is this order safe?"), and making REASONED DECISIONS based on evidence. It helps the nurse spot problems early and keep patients safe.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Following every order exactly without any thought — Following every order without thought could lead to harm (e.g. a wrong dose).
• Relying only on memory for all clinical decisions — Memory alone can be wrong; decisions should be based on current assessment and evidence.
• Avoiding decisions and waiting for others to act — Avoiding decisions delays care and may harm the patient.

⭐ Key point: Critical thinking improves patient safety and helps nurses act as the patient's advocate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q200$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2242, $s$A barcode scanner used for patient identification is an example of:$s$, $s$["A processing device", "An input device", "An output device", "A storage device"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An input device
An INPUT device puts data INTO the computer. A barcode scanner reads the barcode on a patient's wristband (or a drug) and sends the data into the system. This is used in barcode medication administration (BCMA) to confirm the right patient and the right drug.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A processing device — Processing is done by the CPU.
• An output device — Output devices give information OUT, such as monitors, printers and speakers.
• A storage device — Storage devices keep data, such as hard drives and flash drives.

⭐ Key point: Input: keyboard, mouse, scanner, microphone, touchscreen, webcam. Output: monitor, printer, speaker. Some devices do both (a touchscreen, a modem).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q48$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2243, $s$ICU diaries written by nurses and families help patients by:$s$, $s$["Increasing the ICU length of stay", "Recording drug doses for legal purposes", "Replacing the official medical record", "Filling memory gaps and reducing PTSD"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Filling memory gaps and reducing PTSD
Many ICU patients have no memory, or only frightening, false memories (from delirium), of their ICU stay. This gap can cause anxiety, nightmares and PTSD after discharge. An ICU diary written in plain language by nurses and family, with dates, events and sometimes photos, helps the patient later understand what happened. This fills memory gaps and reduces PTSD symptoms.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increasing the ICU length of stay — Diaries don't affect the length of stay.
• Recording drug doses for legal purposes — Diaries are not legal drug records; drug doses are in the official chart.
• Replacing the official medical record — They do not replace the medical record; they are a personal account.

⭐ Key point: Write entries in simple, kind language; give the diary to the patient after discharge, ideally with a follow-up visit to explain it.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q80$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2244, $s$Brudzinski's sign is positive when:$s$, $s$["The pupils dilate on neck movement", "Neck flexion causes hip and knee flexion", "The knee cannot be fully extended", "The great toe extends upwards on stroking"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Neck flexion causes hip and knee flexion
Brudzinski's sign is a test for meningeal irritation (meningitis, subarachnoid haemorrhage). With the patient lying flat, the examiner gently bends the patient's neck forward. If the hips and knees automatically BEND (flex), the sign is positive. The legs bend to reduce the stretch on the inflamed meninges.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The pupils dilate on neck movement — Pupils don't dilate with neck movement in Brudzinski's sign.
• The knee cannot be fully extended — Being unable to fully straighten the knee (with the hip flexed) is KERNIG's sign.
• The great toe extends upwards on stroking — The big toe going up on stroking the sole is the BABINSKI sign.

⭐ Key point: Meningitis: neck stiffness, photophobia, headache, fever, a positive Kernig's and Brudzinski's signs, and possibly a purpuric rash. Start antibiotics quickly and use droplet isolation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q100$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2245, $s$Postural drainage uses gravity to:$s$, $s$["Drain secretions from specific lung segments", "Improve the patient's appetite and feeding", "Increase the blood pressure in hypotension", "Reduce the intracranial pressure after injury"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Drain secretions from specific lung segments
Postural drainage uses GRAVITY: the patient is placed in positions that put a specific lung segment ABOVE the main airway, so secretions drain downward into the bigger airways, where they can be coughed up or suctioned. Different positions drain different lung segments. It is often combined with percussion and vibration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improve the patient's appetite and feeding — It doesn't improve appetite.
• Increase the blood pressure in hypotension — It doesn't treat hypotension.
• Reduce the intracranial pressure after injury — Head-down positions RAISE ICP, so they are avoided in raised ICP.

⭐ Key point: Avoid head-down positions in raised ICP, recent eye or brain surgery, heart failure, and after meals or feeds.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q193$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2246, $s$Barrier nursing, as classically defined, aims to:$s$, $s$["Have no protective purpose", "Only apply to surgical patients", "Protect others from the patient", "Protect the patient from others"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Protect others from the patient
Barrier nursing (source isolation) is used when the PATIENT has an infection that can spread. The barriers (single room, gloves, gown, mask, dedicated equipment, hand hygiene) stop germs from leaving the patient and reaching staff, visitors and other patients. Its purpose is to PROTECT OTHERS FROM THE PATIENT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Have no protective purpose — It clearly has a protective purpose.
• Only apply to surgical patients — It is used for any infectious patient, not only surgical ones.
• Protect the patient from others — Protecting the patient FROM others is REVERSE barrier nursing (protective isolation).

⭐ Key point: Source isolation = protect others from an infected patient. Protective (reverse) isolation = protect a vulnerable patient from others.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q2$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2247, $s$A key nursing intervention to prevent line sepsis is:$s$, $s$["Scrubbing the hub before every access", "Changing dressings only when they fall off", "Reusing syringes on the same patient's line", "Leaving three-way taps open and uncapped"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Scrubbing the hub before every access
Germs on the outside of a catheter hub, needleless connector or three-way tap can be pushed into the bloodstream every time the line is accessed. SCRUBBING THE HUB vigorously for 15 seconds with 2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol (and letting it dry) before EVERY access kills these germs and prevents line sepsis (CLABSI).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Changing dressings only when they fall off — Dressings should be changed when wet, loose or soiled, or on schedule, not only when they fall off.
• Reusing syringes on the same patient's line — Reusing syringes on lines spreads infection.
• Leaving three-way taps open and uncapped — Open, uncapped three-way taps let germs in.

⭐ Key point: "Scrub the hub" for 15 seconds and let it dry. Use sterile caps on unused ports.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q16$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2248, $s$Thoracostomy differs from thoracotomy in that it is:$s$, $s$["Identical to thoracotomy", "A large incision for major intrathoracic surgery", "Only used for cardiac surgery", "A small opening for placing a chest drain"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A small opening for placing a chest drain
A thoracostomy is a SMALL surgical opening in the chest wall, usually to insert a CHEST DRAIN (tube thoracostomy) to drain air, blood or fluid from the pleural space. A thoracotomy is a LARGE incision to open the chest for major surgery (lung, heart, great vessels).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Identical to thoracotomy — They are different procedures, not identical.
• A large incision for major intrathoracic surgery — A large incision for major surgery describes a thoracotomy.
• Only used for cardiac surgery — Thoracostomy is used for pleural problems, not only cardiac surgery.

⭐ Key point: "-ostomy" means making an opening; "-otomy" means cutting into.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q21$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2249, $s$Each unit of blood should be completed within:$s$, $s$["24 hours of leaving the fridge", "4 hours of leaving the fridge", "12 hours of leaving the fridge", "30 minutes of leaving the fridge"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 4 hours of leaving the fridge
Each unit of red cells must be COMPLETED within 4 HOURS of leaving the controlled-temperature storage (the blood fridge). After that, bacteria can grow in the warm blood, increasing the risk of a septic transfusion reaction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 24 hours of leaving the fridge — 24 hours is far too long; bacteria would multiply.
• 12 hours of leaving the fridge — 12 hours is too long.
• 30 minutes of leaving the fridge — 30 minutes is too short to give a unit safely (except in an emergency).

⭐ Key point: Start the transfusion within 30 minutes of collecting the blood from the fridge, and finish within 4 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q107$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2250, $s$The modified Allen's test is positive (normal) when:$s$, $s$["The hand stays pale for more than 30 seconds", "The patient feels pain in the fingers", "Colour returns within 5–15 seconds after ulnar release", "The radial pulse disappears on release"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Colour returns within 5–15 seconds after ulnar release
The modified Allen test checks blood flow through the ULNAR artery to the hand before using the radial artery. Both arteries are pressed while the patient clenches the fist (the hand goes pale). Then the ulnar artery is released. If COLOUR returns within 5–15 SECONDS, the ulnar artery can supply the hand: a normal (positive) result. The radial artery can then be used.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The hand stays pale for more than 30 seconds — Paleness lasting more than 30 seconds is ABNORMAL; don't use that radial artery.
• The patient feels pain in the fingers — Pain in the fingers isn't how the test is read.
• The radial pulse disappears on release — The radial pulse isn't released in this test, and its disappearance isn't the result.

⭐ Key point: An abnormal Allen test means the radial artery shouldn't be punctured. Choose another site.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q119$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2251, $s$Which spinal nerves give rise to the cervical plexus?$s$, $s$["T1–T12", "C3–T3", "C5–T1", "C1–C4"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: C1–C4
The cervical plexus is a network of nerves formed by the front branches (anterior rami) of spinal nerves C1–C4. It supplies the skin and muscles of the neck and gives off the PHRENIC nerve (C3, C4, C5), which controls the diaphragm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• T1–T12 — T1–T12 are the thoracic nerves (intercostal nerves).
• C3–T3 — C3–T3 isn't a recognised plexus.
• C5–T1 — C5–T1 form the BRACHIAL plexus, which supplies the arm.

⭐ Key point: "C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive": spinal injuries at or above C3–C5 can stop breathing.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q2$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2252, $s$Which of these is a granulocyte?$s$, $s$["An eosinophil", "An erythrocyte", "A macrophage", "A lymphocyte"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An eosinophil
Granulocytes are white cells with granules in their cytoplasm: NEUTROPHILS, EOSINOPHILS and BASOPHILS. An eosinophil is therefore a granulocyte. Eosinophils fight parasites and are involved in allergic reactions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An erythrocyte — An erythrocyte is a red blood cell, not a white cell.
• A macrophage — A macrophage comes from monocytes (agranulocytes).
• A lymphocyte — A lymphocyte is an agranulocyte.

⭐ Key point: Granulocytes = neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils. Agranulocytes = lymphocytes and monocytes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q3$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2253, $s$Which muscle aids deep (forced) expiration?$s$, $s$["Scalenes", "Rectus abdominis", "Sternocleidomastoid", "External intercostals"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Rectus abdominis
Normal breathing out is passive (the lungs recoil). DEEP or FORCED expiration (coughing, exercise, asthma) uses the ABDOMINAL muscles, such as the RECTUS ABDOMINIS, plus the internal intercostals. They push the abdominal contents up against the diaphragm and pull the ribs down, forcing air out.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Scalenes — The scalenes help INSPIRATION (they lift the upper ribs).
• Sternocleidomastoid — The sternocleidomastoid helps INSPIRATION (an accessory muscle).
• External intercostals — The external intercostals help INSPIRATION.

⭐ Key point: Patients with abdominal surgery or weakness can't cough well, so they need help clearing secretions (pain relief, supported coughing, physiotherapy).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q5$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2254, $s$What percentage of mechanically ventilated patients develop significant weakness?$s$, $s$["25%", "5%", "100%", "75%"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 25%
ICU-acquired weakness (ICU-AW) affects about 25% (roughly a quarter, and up to 50% in sepsis) of patients ventilated for more than a few days. It is caused by critical illness polyneuropathy and myopathy, immobility, inflammation, high glucose, and drugs (steroids, muscle relaxants). It causes difficult weaning and long recovery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5% — 5% underestimates it.
• 100% — 100% is too high; not every patient develops it.
• 75% — 75% overestimates it for ventilated patients overall.

⭐ Key point: Reduce ICU-AW: early mobilisation, glucose control, minimising sedation and muscle relaxants, and good nutrition.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q23$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2255, $s$Atracurium is muscle relaxant which is$s$, $s$["Contraindicated in renal transplant", "Metabolized by Hoffman degradation", "Metabolized by liver", "Metabolized by kidney"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Metabolized by Hoffman degradation
ATRACURIUM is a non-depolarising muscle relaxant broken down mainly by HOFMANN DEGRADATION: a spontaneous chemical breakdown that depends on body temperature and pH, not on the liver or kidneys. Its action stays predictable in liver or kidney failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Contraindicated in renal transplant — It isn't contraindicated in renal transplant; it's actually useful in kidney failure.
• Metabolized by liver — It doesn't rely on liver metabolism.
• Metabolized by kidney — It doesn't rely on kidney excretion.

⭐ Key point: Atracurium can release histamine (flushing, low BP). Cisatracurium releases less histamine and is often preferred in ICU.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q142$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2256, $s$Which inhalational anaesthetic agent has the lowest blood-gas partition coefficient among the following, resulting in the most rapid induction and emergence?$s$, $s$["Desflurane", "Sevoflurane", "Halothane", "Isoflurane"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Desflurane
The BLOOD–GAS PARTITION COEFFICIENT shows how soluble an anaesthetic gas is in blood. A LOW coefficient means the gas doesn't dissolve much, so it quickly reaches the brain and quickly leaves, giving FAST induction and FAST recovery. DESFLURANE has the lowest (about 0.42) of the common volatile agents.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sevoflurane — Sevoflurane is about 0.65 (also fast, but higher than desflurane).
• Halothane — Halothane is about 2.4 (slow).
• Isoflurane — Isoflurane is about 1.4 (slower).

⭐ Key point: Sevoflurane is preferred for gas INDUCTION (pleasant smell, not irritating). Desflurane is irritating to the airway, so it's used for maintenance, not induction.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q144$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2257, $s$A patient on IV nitroglycerin has a headache. Which action is BEST?$s$, $s$["Switch to oral nitrates", "Administer PRN acetaminophen", "Increase the infusion rate", "Hold the next dose"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Administer PRN acetaminophen
HEADACHE is the most common side effect of nitroglycerin (GTN), caused by dilation of blood vessels in the head. It is usually mild and often improves as tolerance develops. The best action is to give a simple painkiller such as PARACETAMOL (acetaminophen) PRN, while continuing the GTN if it's still needed for chest pain or BP.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Switch to oral nitrates — Switching to oral nitrates isn't necessary for a headache.
• Increase the infusion rate — Increasing the rate would worsen the headache and may lower BP.
• Hold the next dose — Holding the dose could allow chest pain or high BP to return.

⭐ Key point: Also monitor BP on GTN; hypotension is the main serious side effect. Avoid it with PDE-5 inhibitors (sildenafil).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q145$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2258, $s$Neostigmine is used to$s$, $s$["Increase the action of muscle relaxant", "To reverse the action of non depolarizing muscle relaxant", "To increase the action of depolarizing muscle relaxant", "Reduce the action of muscle relaxant"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: To reverse the action of non depolarizing muscle relaxant
NEOSTIGMINE blocks acetylcholinesterase, the enzyme that breaks down acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction. More acetylcholine builds up and competes with NON-DEPOLARISING muscle relaxants (rocuronium, vecuronium, atracurium), REVERSING their effect at the end of surgery. It is given with glycopyrrolate or atropine to block its slowing effect on the heart.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the action of muscle relaxant — It doesn't increase the action of muscle relaxants.
• To increase the action of depolarizing muscle relaxant — It PROLONGS (not reverses) the action of DEPOLARISING relaxants (suxamethonium).
• Reduce the action of muscle relaxant — "Reduce the action of muscle relaxant" is vague; the correct answer specifically names non-depolarising relaxants.

⭐ Key point: Sugammadex is another reversal agent; it binds rocuronium and vecuronium directly and works faster.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q147$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2259, $s$Which of the following agents is most likely to cause airway irritation, coughing, and breath-holding, making it unsuitable for inhalational induction?$s$, $s$["Sevoflurane", "Nitrous oxide", "Propofol", "Desflurane"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Desflurane
DESFLURANE is PUNGENT (irritating) to the airway. When breathed in, it causes coughing, breath-holding, laryngospasm and increased secretions. That's why it isn't used for gas (inhalational) induction, but only for maintaining anaesthesia once the patient is asleep and intubated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sevoflurane — Sevoflurane is sweet-smelling and non-irritating, the agent of choice for gas induction.
• Nitrous oxide — Nitrous oxide is not irritating.
• Propofol — Propofol is an IV drug, not an inhaled agent.

⭐ Key point: Desflurane has fast recovery because of its low blood solubility, but it irritates the airway.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q153$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2260, $s$Minimum fasting time for a light meal before elective anaesthesia is generally:$s$, $s$["2 hours", "24 hours", "1 hour", "6 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 6 hours
Before elective anaesthesia, the fasting times are: a LIGHT MEAL, 6 HOURS; a heavy or fatty meal, 8 hours; formula milk, 6 hours; breast milk, 4 hours; clear fluids, 2 hours. This lets the stomach empty and reduces the risk of aspiration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2 hours — 2 hours is the time for clear fluids.
• 24 hours — 24 hours is unnecessarily long and causes dehydration and low glucose.
• 1 hour — 1 hour is too short for food (some centres allow clear fluids up to 1 hour for children).

⭐ Key point: Long fasting causes dehydration, low glucose and discomfort. Encourage clear fluids up to 2 hours before surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q154$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2261, $s$Guedel's classification describes depth of anaesthesia in how many stages, with which stage representing the ideal plane for surgery?$s$, $s$["Four stages; Stage III", "Two stages; Stage I", "Three stages; Stage II", "Five stages; Stage IV"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Four stages; Stage III
Guedel described FOUR stages of anaesthesia (with ether). Stage I: analgesia (from induction to loss of consciousness). Stage II: excitement (delirium, struggling, vomiting, risk of laryngospasm). Stage III: SURGICAL ANAESTHESIA (the ideal level for surgery, divided into 4 planes). Stage IV: medullary paralysis (overdose, with respiratory and cardiac arrest).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Two stages; Stage I — There are four stages, not two.
• Three stages; Stage II — Stage II is the dangerous excitement stage, not ideal for surgery.
• Five stages; Stage IV — There are four stages, not five; Stage IV is overdose.

⭐ Key point: Modern IV induction passes quickly through Stage II, but the excitement stage can still occur during gas induction and emergence.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q156$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2262, $s$Suxamethonium should be avoided in a patient who is 5 days post major burn because of the risk of:$s$, $s$["Hypocalcaemia and tetany", "Severe hyperkalaemia and cardiac arrest", "Prolonged hypertension only", "Severe hypoglycaemia and seizures"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Severe hyperkalaemia and cardiac arrest
After a major burn (from about 24–48 hours onward), the muscle develops many extra acetylcholine receptors. Suxamethonium stimulates all of them, causing a massive release of potassium from the muscles: SEVERE HYPERKALAEMIA, which can cause CARDIAC ARREST. It should be avoided from about 24 hours after the burn until healing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypocalcaemia and tetany — Low calcium and tetany are not the risk.
• Prolonged hypertension only — Prolonged hypertension isn't the risk.
• Severe hypoglycaemia and seizures — Hypoglycaemia isn't the risk.

⭐ Key point: Avoid suxamethonium in burns, crush injuries, spinal cord injury, prolonged immobility, and neuromuscular disease. Use rocuronium instead.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q158$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2263, $s$A test's sensitivity is the proportion of:$s$, $s$["All tests that are correct", "Positive tests that are true positives", "Patients with the disease who test positive", "Patients without the disease who test negative"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Patients with the disease who test positive
SENSITIVITY is the proportion of patients WHO HAVE the disease who TEST POSITIVE (true positives ÷ all people with the disease). A highly sensitive test rarely misses the disease, so a NEGATIVE result helps RULE OUT the disease (SnOut).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• All tests that are correct — "All tests that are correct" describes accuracy.
• Positive tests that are true positives — "Positive tests that are true positives" is the POSITIVE PREDICTIVE VALUE.
• Patients without the disease who test negative — "Patients without the disease who test negative" is SPECIFICITY.

⭐ Key point: Sensitivity rules OUT (SnOut); specificity rules IN (SpIn).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q197$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2264, $s$The approximate total body water content of an adult woman is:$s$, $s$["80%", "70%", "50%", "60%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 50%
Total body water is about 60% of body weight in adult MEN and about 50–55% in adult WOMEN. Women have more body fat, and fat contains less water than muscle. Water content also falls with age and obesity.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 80% — 80% is too high for adults (newborns are about 75–80%).
• 70% — 70% is too high for adult women.
• 60% — 60% is the value for adult men.

⭐ Key point: Two-thirds of body water is inside the cells (ICF) and one-third outside (ECF: interstitial fluid and plasma).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q5$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2265, $s$How many pulmonary veins empty into the right atrium?$s$, $s$["Four", "Two", "One", "None"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: None
There are FOUR pulmonary veins (two from each lung). They carry OXYGENATED blood from the lungs to the LEFT ATRIUM. NONE empty into the right atrium. The right atrium receives deoxygenated blood from the superior and inferior vena cava and the coronary sinus.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Four — Four is the total number, but they drain into the LEFT atrium.
• Two — Two drain from each lung, into the left atrium.
• One — One is incorrect.

⭐ Key point: Pulmonary veins carry oxygenated blood (the exception to "veins carry deoxygenated blood"); pulmonary arteries carry deoxygenated blood.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q10$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2266, $s$Chest tube drainage exceeding approximately 200–300mL per hour after coronary artery bypass surgery should prompt concern for:$s$, $s$["Resolution of the surgical procedure", "Postoperative haemorrhage requiring urgent evaluation", "Chest tube malfunction only, with no clinical significance", "Normal expected postoperative drainage"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Postoperative haemorrhage requiring urgent evaluation
After CABG, some chest drain bleeding is normal, but output of more than about 200–300 mL per HOUR (or 400 mL in the first hour, or persistent output over 100–200 mL/hour) suggests ongoing surgical BLEEDING (POSTOPERATIVE HAEMORRHAGE). It may need correction of clotting, blood transfusion and a return to theatre.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Resolution of the surgical procedure — High drainage doesn't mean the procedure has resolved.
• Chest tube malfunction only, with no clinical significance — High drainage is clinically significant, not just a tube problem.
• Normal expected postoperative drainage — This volume is above normal expected drainage.

⭐ Key point: Also watch for SUDDEN cessation of drainage with low BP and rising CVP, which suggests cardiac tamponade from blocked drains.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q43$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2267, $s$The most common presentation of a pre-eclamptic patient is:$s$, $s$["No symptoms", "Dyspareunia", "Abdominal pain", "Convulsions"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: No symptoms
Most women with pre-eclampsia have NO SYMPTOMS. It is usually found at a routine antenatal check by finding HIGH BP and PROTEIN in the urine. That's why regular antenatal visits are so important. Symptoms (headache, visual changes, epigastric pain, swelling) appear when it becomes severe.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dyspareunia — Dyspareunia (painful intercourse) is unrelated.
• Abdominal pain — Abdominal (epigastric) pain is a sign of SEVERE disease or HELLP.
• Convulsions — Convulsions mean ECLAMPSIA, a late complication.

⭐ Key point: Check BP and urine for protein at every antenatal visit.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q129$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2268, $s$Management of hyperemesis gravidarum involves:$s$, $s$["Vitamin B6 combined with doxylamine", "Mandatory IM analgin", "Nil by mouth until discharge", "Dextrose water as the preferred fluid"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Vitamin B6 combined with doxylamine
For nausea and vomiting in pregnancy and hyperemesis gravidarum, VITAMIN B6 (PYRIDOXINE) combined with DOXYLAMINE (an antihistamine) is a safe and effective first-line treatment. Severe cases need IV fluids (0.9% saline with potassium), thiamine, other antiemetics, and thromboprophylaxis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mandatory IM analgin — IM analgin (metamizole) isn't a treatment for hyperemesis.
• Nil by mouth until discharge — Nil by mouth until discharge isn't recommended; oral intake is reintroduced as tolerated.
• Dextrose water as the preferred fluid — Dextrose solutions without thiamine can trigger Wernicke's encephalopathy; saline is preferred.

⭐ Key point: Give thiamine to women with prolonged vomiting before giving dextrose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q131$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2269, $s$Which action should the nurse take when preparing patient-controlled analgesia for a postoperative patient?$s$, $s$["Ask another nurse to double-check the setup before patient use.", "Caution the patient to limit the number of times he presses the dosing button.", "Provide clear, simple instructions for dosing if the patient is cognitively impaired", "Instruct the patient to administer a dose only when experiencing pain."]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ask another nurse to double-check the setup before patient use.
Patient-controlled analgesia (PCA) delivers opioids, and programming errors can cause fatal overdoses. Having a SECOND NURSE DOUBLE-CHECK the setup (the drug, concentration, bolus dose, lockout time and hourly limit) before use is a key safety step.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Caution the patient to limit the number of times he presses the dosing button. — Patients should press the button when they need pain relief; don't tell them to limit it.
• Provide clear, simple instructions for dosing if the patient is cognitively impaired — PCA isn't suitable for cognitively impaired patients who can't use it correctly.
• Instruct the patient to administer a dose only when experiencing pain. — Patients can also press it before painful activities (such as physiotherapy or coughing), not only when in pain.

⭐ Key point: Only the PATIENT should press the button, never family or staff ("PCA by proxy" causes overdoses).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q147$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2270, $s$A patient on warfarin has an INR of 8.0. Which reversal agent is used for active bleeding?$s$, $s$["Vitamin K IV", "FFP", "Protamine sulfate", "Andexanet alfa"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: FFP
An INR of 8.0 with ACTIVE BLEEDING needs IMMEDIATE replacement of clotting factors. FRESH FROZEN PLASMA (FFP) provides all clotting factors and works immediately; it is the keyed answer here. IV vitamin K (5–10 mg) should also be given, because it takes 6–24 hours but gives lasting reversal. Where available, prothrombin complex concentrate (PCC) is preferred, as it works faster and needs a smaller volume.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vitamin K IV — Vitamin K alone works too slowly for active bleeding (but should also be given).
• Protamine sulfate — Protamine reverses heparin, not warfarin.
• Andexanet alfa — Andexanet alfa reverses factor Xa inhibitors (apixaban, rivaroxaban), not warfarin.

⭐ Key point: Warfarin reversal for major bleeding: PCC (or FFP) plus IV vitamin K.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q149$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2271, $s$Clinically significant carbon monoxide production within an anaesthesia circuit is most associated with which agent reacting with desiccated (dried-out) CO2 absorbent?$s$, $s$["Desflurane", "Sevoflurane", "Propofol", "Oxygen alone"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Desflurane
When CO₂ absorbent (soda lime) in the anaesthetic circuit becomes very DRY (desiccated), it can react with some volatile agents to produce CARBON MONOXIDE. DESFLURANE produces the most (isoflurane less, sevoflurane very little). This can cause CO poisoning in the patient.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sevoflurane — Sevoflurane produces Compound A (not much CO) with dry absorbent.
• Propofol — Propofol is an IV drug, not used in the circuit.
• Oxygen alone — Oxygen alone doesn't produce CO.

⭐ Key point: Prevent CO production: change absorbent regularly, and turn off the fresh gas flow when the machine isn't in use (it dries the absorbent).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q152$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2272, $s$The oxygen flush valve on an anaesthesia machine delivers pure oxygen directly to the common gas outlet, bypassing the vaporizer, at a flow rate of approximately:$s$, $s$["1-2 L/min", "100-150 L/min", "5-10 L/min", "35-75 L/min"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 35-75 L/min
The OXYGEN FLUSH valve on an anaesthesia machine delivers 100% oxygen directly to the common gas outlet, bypassing the flowmeters and vaporiser, at a very HIGH flow of about 35–75 L/min. It quickly fills the circuit or breathing bag in an emergency.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1-2 L/min — 1–2 L/min is a normal low fresh gas flow.
• 100-150 L/min — 100–150 L/min is too high.
• 5-10 L/min — 5–10 L/min is a normal fresh gas flow.

⭐ Key point: Using the flush during inspiration can cause barotrauma; it can also dilute anaesthetic agents and risk awareness.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q153$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2273, $s$Dexmedetomidine is useful in ICU because it:$s$, $s$["Is a long-acting opioid analgesic", "Has no effect on heart rate", "Sedates with little respiratory depression", "Causes deep amnesia and apnoea"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sedates with little respiratory depression
DEXMEDETOMIDINE is an alpha-2 agonist that provides calm, cooperative ("rousable") sedation with LITTLE RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION. Patients can often be woken and can follow commands. It causes less delirium than benzodiazepines and is useful for weaning and for agitated patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Is a long-acting opioid analgesic — It isn't an opioid, though it has some analgesic effect.
• Has no effect on heart rate — It DOES affect heart rate: it causes BRADYCARDIA.
• Causes deep amnesia and apnoea — It doesn't cause deep amnesia or apnoea.

⭐ Key point: Watch for bradycardia and hypotension. It is less suitable when deep sedation is needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q159$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2274, $s$Mr john a post-operative patient in ICU developed a severe fever 4 days after the surgery, this is typical of:$s$, $s$["Primary infection", "Secondary infection", "Malnutrition", "Normal surgical process"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Secondary infection
A new fever appearing several days (3–5 days) after surgery suggests a SECONDARY (hospital-acquired) INFECTION such as a wound infection, pneumonia, UTI or line infection. These are acquired after admission, from surgery, devices or the hospital environment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Primary infection — "Primary infection" isn't the term used for an infection developing days after surgery.
• Malnutrition — Malnutrition doesn't cause a severe fever on day 4.
• Normal surgical process — A mild fever in the first 24–48 hours can be part of the normal response to surgery, but a SEVERE fever on day 4 isn't.

⭐ Key point: Causes of post-op fever by day ("5 Ws"): Wind (lungs, days 1–2), Water (urine, days 3–5), Wound (days 5–7), Walking (DVT, day 5+), Wonder drugs (any time).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q188$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2275, $s$The process by which water and diffusible substances move together across a membrane in response to fluid pressure is:$s$, $s$["Diffusion", "Active transport", "Filtration", "Osmosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Filtration
FILTRATION is the movement of water and small dissolved substances TOGETHER across a membrane, driven by FLUID (hydrostatic) PRESSURE. Example: in the glomerulus, blood pressure pushes water and solutes out into Bowman's capsule. In capillaries, it pushes fluid into the tissues.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diffusion — Diffusion moves solutes from high to low concentration (no pressure needed).
• Active transport — Active transport uses energy (ATP) to move substances against a concentration gradient.
• Osmosis — Osmosis moves WATER across a membrane toward higher solute concentration.

⭐ Key point: Filtration = pressure-driven; diffusion = concentration-driven; osmosis = water moving to concentrated areas.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q12$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2276, $s$On the day of surgery, a patient's regular β-blocker medication should generally be:$s$, $s$["Continued", "Stopped abruptly", "Doubled in dose", "Replaced with a diuretic"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Continued
Regular BETA-BLOCKERS should generally be CONTINUED on the day of surgery (taken with a sip of water). Stopping them suddenly can cause REBOUND effects: fast heart rate, high BP, angina or MI. Continuing them protects the heart during the stress of surgery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stopped abruptly — Stopping abruptly causes rebound tachycardia and ischaemia.
• Doubled in dose — Doubling the dose isn't recommended.
• Replaced with a diuretic — Replacing it with a diuretic isn't appropriate.

⭐ Key point: Other drugs usually continued: statins and most anti-epileptics. Often held: ACE inhibitors, diuretics, oral hypoglycaemics (check local policy).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q48$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2277, $s$On the day of surgery, diuretics are generally:$s$, $s$["Continued as normal", "Usually withheld", "Doubled in dose", "Given intravenously regardless of oral status"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Usually withheld
DIURETICS are usually WITHHELD on the morning of surgery. Patients are already fasting (with less fluid intake), and anaesthesia lowers BP. Diuretics could add to dehydration, low BP, and electrolyte problems (low potassium).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Continued as normal — Continuing as normal risks dehydration and low potassium.
• Doubled in dose — Doubling the dose is never appropriate here.
• Given intravenously regardless of oral status — IV diuretics aren't routinely given before surgery.

⭐ Key point: Restart diuretics after surgery when the patient is eating and drinking and fluid balance is stable.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q49$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2278, $s$Lugol's iodine is used pre-operatively in thyroid surgery at a dose of:$s$, $s$["It is not used pre-operatively", "50 drops for 1 day", "2–5 drops for 10–15 days", "1 drop for 1 year"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2–5 drops for 10–15 days
Before thyroid surgery, LUGOL'S IODINE is given at about 2–5 drops (or 0.1–0.3 mL) THREE times a day for 10–15 DAYS. This short course reduces the gland's blood supply and makes it firmer, making surgery safer.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It is not used pre-operatively — It IS used before surgery.
• 50 drops for 1 day — 50 drops for 1 day is the wrong dose and duration.
• 1 drop for 1 year — 1 drop for 1 year is the wrong regimen.

⭐ Key point: Iodine's effect wears off after about 2 weeks ("escape"), so surgery is timed accordingly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q92$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2279, $s$Which volatile anaesthetic agent is most associated with sensitizing the myocardium to catecholamines, predisposing to arrhythmias, particularly if epinephrine is administered concurrently?$s$, $s$["Halothane", "Sevoflurane", "Nitrous oxide", "Desflurane"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Halothane
HALOTHANE (an older volatile anaesthetic) strongly SENSITISES the heart muscle to CATECHOLAMINES (adrenaline). If adrenaline is given (such as in local anaesthetic to reduce bleeding), dangerous ARRHYTHMIAS (ventricular ectopics, VT, VF) can occur. Halothane is now rarely used in many countries but may still appear in exams.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sevoflurane — Sevoflurane causes much less sensitisation.
• Nitrous oxide — Nitrous oxide doesn't significantly sensitise the heart.
• Desflurane — Desflurane causes less sensitisation than halothane.

⭐ Key point: Halothane can also cause "halothane hepatitis" (liver damage), especially with repeated exposure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q145$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2280, $s$A patient with myasthenia gravis is prescribed neostigmine. The nurse should monitor for:$s$, $s$["Cholinergic crisis", "Neuroleptic malignant syndrome", "Thyroid storm", "Serotonin syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cholinergic crisis
NEOSTIGMINE (an anticholinesterase) increases acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction, improving muscle strength in myasthenia gravis. Too much causes a CHOLINERGIC CRISIS: excessive acetylcholine leads to WEAKNESS (including the breathing muscles), plus SLUDGE signs (salivation, sweating, diarrhoea), small pupils and a slow pulse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Neuroleptic malignant syndrome — Neuroleptic malignant syndrome is caused by antipsychotics.
• Thyroid storm — Thyroid storm is a thyroid emergency.
• Serotonin syndrome — Serotonin syndrome is caused by serotonergic drugs.

⭐ Key point: Myasthenic crisis (too little drug) and cholinergic crisis (too much) both cause weakness. SLUDGE signs suggest cholinergic crisis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q152$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2281, $s$A thyroidectomy patient complains of palpitations and anxiety preoperatively. The nurse should administer:$s$, $s$["Propanolol", "Dopamine", "Adrenaline", "Morphine"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Propanolol
Before thyroid surgery, palpitations and anxiety come from excess thyroid hormone stimulating the sympathetic system (like adrenaline). PROPRANOLOL (a non-selective beta-blocker) controls these symptoms: it slows the heart rate, reduces tremor and anxiety, and also reduces conversion of T4 to active T3.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dopamine — Dopamine would increase heart rate and palpitations.
• Adrenaline — Adrenaline would worsen palpitations and anxiety.
• Morphine — Morphine doesn't treat the cause and could depress breathing.

⭐ Key point: Patients should be euthyroid before thyroid surgery, to prevent thyroid storm.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q153$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2282, $s$Which of the following is not an ideal characteristic of a volatile anaesthetic agent?$s$, $s$["Odourless or pleasant to inhale", "Cheap to manufacture", "Flammable", "Safe for all ages and in pregnancy"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Flammable
An ideal volatile anaesthetic would be pleasant to breathe, cheap, NON-FLAMMABLE, stable, have a fast onset and recovery, cause minimal side effects, and be safe for all ages. FLAMMABILITY is dangerous (fires and explosions in theatre, as with old agents like ether and cyclopropane), so it isn't an ideal characteristic.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Odourless or pleasant to inhale — Being odourless or pleasant to inhale IS ideal (for gas induction).
• Cheap to manufacture — Being cheap IS ideal.
• Safe for all ages and in pregnancy — Being safe for all ages and in pregnancy IS ideal.

⭐ Key point: Modern volatile agents (sevoflurane, desflurane, isoflurane) are non-flammable.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q154$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2283, $s$The concentration of inhalational anaesthetic at which a patient responds purposefully to verbal command during emergence is referred to as:$s$, $s$["MAC95", "MAC-awake", "Blood-gas MAC", "MAC-BAR"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: MAC-awake
MAC-AWAKE is the concentration of inhaled anaesthetic at which 50% of patients open their eyes or respond PURPOSEFULLY to a VERBAL COMMAND during emergence. It is about 0.3–0.5 times the standard MAC. It helps understand awareness and recovery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• MAC95 — MAC95 is the concentration that prevents movement to incision in 95% of patients.
• Blood-gas MAC — "Blood-gas MAC" isn't a standard term.
• MAC-BAR — MAC-BAR is the concentration that blocks the adrenergic (stress) response to incision.

⭐ Key point: Standard MAC = the concentration preventing movement to surgical incision in 50% of patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q155$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2284, $s$Rocuronium-induced neuromuscular block can be rapidly reversed by:$s$, $s$["Neostigmine alone at any depth of block", "Suxamethonium", "Dantrolene", "Sugammadex"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sugammadex
SUGAMMADEX is a modified cyclodextrin that ENCAPSULATES (binds and traps) rocuronium (and vecuronium) molecules in the blood, quickly reversing even DEEP neuromuscular block within minutes. It works much faster and more reliably than neostigmine.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Neostigmine alone at any depth of block — Neostigmine works only for SHALLOW block (when some recovery has begun), not at any depth.
• Suxamethonium — Suxamethonium is a muscle relaxant, not a reversal agent.
• Dantrolene — Dantrolene treats malignant hyperthermia.

⭐ Key point: Sugammadex is also used in "can't intubate, can't oxygenate" situations after rocuronium. It may reduce hormonal contraceptive effectiveness.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q157$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C2 · Anaesthesia & Perioperative Care$s$, false, 2285, $s$A competent patient's living will refuses aggressive cancer treatment, but the physician insists on chemotherapy for the patient's benefit. The physician is exercising:$s$, $s$["Veracity", "Paternalism", "Ideal beneficence", "Respect for autonomy"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Paternalism
PATERNALISM is overriding a competent patient's known wishes "for their own good". The patient's living will REFUSES aggressive cancer treatment, but the physician insists on chemotherapy for the patient's benefit. This overrides the patient's AUTONOMY, which is paternalism.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Veracity — Veracity means truthfulness.
• Ideal beneficence — "Ideal beneficence" isn't a standard term; real beneficence respects the patient's values.
• Respect for autonomy — Respect for autonomy would mean honouring the living will.

⭐ Key point: A competent patient's valid refusal (including an advance directive) must be respected.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q165$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2286, $s$The most common cause of postpartum haemorrhage, among the 4 Ts, is:$s$, $s$["Tone (uterine atony)", "Trauma", "Tissue", "Thrombin"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tone (uterine atony)
After birth, the uterus must contract firmly to squeeze shut the blood vessels where the placenta was attached. If it stays soft and relaxed (uterine ATONY), blood pours out. Poor TONE causes about 70% of PPH. Risk factors include a big baby, twins, polyhydramnios, long or very fast labour, grand multiparity, infection and drugs that relax the uterus.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Trauma — Trauma (tears of the cervix, vagina or perineum, uterine rupture) causes about 20%.
• Tissue — Tissue (retained placenta or membranes) causes about 10%.
• Thrombin — Thrombin (clotting problems such as DIC) causes about 1%.

⭐ Key point: First steps for atonic PPH: call for help, rub the uterus (fundal massage), give oxytocin, empty the bladder, start IV fluids, and give tranexamic acid.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q136$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2287, $s$A nurse is performing neonatal resuscitation. The newborn's heart rate is below 60 beats per minute after 30 seconds of effective positive-pressure ventilation. The next step is to:$s$, $s$["Continue positive-pressure ventilation for another 30 seconds", "Administer epinephrine", "Initiate chest compressions", "Stop resuscitation and call the physician"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Initiate chest compressions
Neonatal resuscitation focuses on ventilation, because most newborns need only effective breaths. If the heart rate is still BELOW 60 beats per minute after 30 seconds of EFFECTIVE ventilation (chest is moving), START CHEST COMPRESSIONS. The ratio is 3 compressions to 1 breath (90 compressions and 30 breaths per minute), using two thumbs on the lower third of the sternum, and increase oxygen to 100%.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Continue positive-pressure ventilation for another 30 seconds — Continuing ventilation alone wastes time once the heart rate is below 60 with good ventilation.
• Administer epinephrine — Adrenaline is given only if the heart rate stays below 60 after about 30 seconds of good compressions and ventilation.
• Stop resuscitation and call the physician — Stopping resuscitation is wrong; call for help while continuing.

⭐ Key point: Newborn steps: dry, warm and stimulate; open the airway; give 5 inflation breaths; ventilate; compressions if HR is below 60; then drugs through an umbilical venous catheter.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q140 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2288, $s$Pyloric stenosis in infants classically presents with:$s$, $s$["Bilious vomiting from birth", "Projectile, non-bilious vomiting typically starting around 3–6 weeks of age", "Chronic diarrhoea", "Bloody stools with fever"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Projectile, non-bilious vomiting typically starting around 3–6 weeks of age
Pyloric stenosis is thickening of the pylorus muscle (the stomach outlet), which blocks food from leaving the stomach. It usually starts at 3–6 weeks of age, often in first-born boys. Vomiting is PROJECTILE (forceful) and NON-BILIOUS (milky, not green), because the blockage is above where bile enters. The baby is hungry after vomiting, and an "olive" mass may be felt in the upper abdomen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bilious vomiting from birth — Bilious (green) vomiting from birth suggests an obstruction BELOW the bile duct (such as duodenal atresia or malrotation).
• Chronic diarrhoea — Chronic diarrhoea is not a feature; babies are usually constipated.
• Bloody stools with fever — Bloody stools with fever suggest infection or intussusception.

⭐ Key point: Vomiting stomach acid causes a hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic METABOLIC ALKALOSIS. Correct fluids and electrolytes FIRST, then do surgery (pyloromyotomy).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q142$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2289, $s$Intussusception in infants classically presents with:$s$, $s$["Painless abdominal distension only", "Episodic colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, and 'redcurrant jelly' stools", "Chronic constipation with no acute symptoms", "A silent, asymptomatic abdominal mass"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Episodic colicky abdominal pain, vomiting, and 'redcurrant jelly' stools
Intussusception is when one part of the bowel telescopes into the next part, usually at the ileocaecal junction, in babies aged 3 months to 3 years. It causes episodes of severe colicky pain (the baby screams and draws up the legs, then is quiet between episodes), vomiting, and later "redcurrant jelly" stools (blood and mucus). A sausage-shaped mass may be felt in the abdomen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Painless abdominal distension only — It is very painful, not painless distension.
• Chronic constipation with no acute symptoms — It is an acute emergency, not chronic constipation.
• A silent, asymptomatic abdominal mass — The baby is clearly unwell, so it is not a silent, asymptomatic mass.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV fluids, an NG tube, and air or contrast enema reduction. Surgery is needed if the enema fails or the bowel perforates.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q143$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2290, $s$Primary postpartum haemorrhage after a vaginal delivery is defined as blood loss of:$s$, $s$["≥200ml", "≥500ml", "≥1000ml", "≥2000ml"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ≥500ml
PRIMARY POSTPARTUM HAEMORRHAGE (PPH) is blood loss of 500 mL OR MORE within 24 HOURS after a VAGINAL delivery (WHO definition), or 1000 mL or more after CAESAREAN section. It can also be defined as any blood loss enough to make the mother haemodynamically unstable. Minor PPH = 500–1000 mL; major PPH = over 1000 mL; severe = over 2000 mL. SECONDARY PPH is excessive bleeding from 24 hours up to 12 weeks after birth (often from retained products or infection). Causes — the "4 Ts": TONE (uterine atony — the commonest, about 70%), TRAUMA (tears, rupture), TISSUE (retained placenta/clots), THROMBIN (clotting problems). PPH is a leading cause of maternal death in Nigeria and worldwide.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• ≥200ml — 200 mL is within normal blood loss for a vaginal birth.
• ≥1000ml — 1000 mL is the threshold for CAESAREAN section or for MAJOR PPH, not the basic vaginal definition.
• ≥2000ml — 2000 mL is SEVERE/massive haemorrhage; waiting for this would delay treatment dangerously.

⭐ Key point: Primary PPH = ≥ 500 mL within 24 h after vaginal birth (≥ 1000 mL after CS). Causes = 4 Ts (Tone commonest).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q136$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2291, $s$A key distinguishing feature between placental abruption and placenta previa is that abruption classically presents with:$s$, $s$["Painless vaginal bleeding", "Painful vaginal bleeding, often with a rigid, tender uterus", "No vaginal bleeding at all", "Bleeding only in the first trimester"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Painful vaginal bleeding, often with a rigid, tender uterus
Both conditions cause bleeding in the second half of pregnancy (antepartum haemorrhage), but they feel different. PLACENTAL ABRUPTION: the normally placed placenta SEPARATES early from the uterine wall. Blood collects behind the placenta and irritates the uterus, causing PAINFUL bleeding, a HARD, RIGID ("woody"), TENDER uterus, and often foetal distress. Bleeding may be hidden (concealed) so shock can be worse than visible blood loss suggests; DIC is a risk. Risk factors: hypertension/pre-eclampsia, trauma, cocaine, smoking. PLACENTA PRAEVIA: the placenta lies low over or near the cervix, causing PAINLESS, bright red bleeding with a SOFT, non-tender uterus. Never do a vaginal examination in suspected praevia until it is excluded by ultrasound.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Painless vaginal bleeding — PAINLESS vaginal bleeding is typical of PLACENTA PRAEVIA, not abruption.
• No vaginal bleeding at all — Abruption usually causes some visible bleeding (though it may be concealed); "no bleeding at all" is not the classic feature.
• Bleeding only in the first trimester — Abruption occurs in the second half of pregnancy (after 20 weeks), not only in the first trimester.

⭐ Key point: ABRUPTION = PAINFUL bleeding + RIGID tender uterus. PRAEVIA = PAINLESS bright red bleeding, soft uterus. No VE in praevia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q137$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2292, $s$HELLP syndrome, a complication of severe pre-eclampsia, stands for:$s$, $s$["Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets", "High Eclampsia, Low Labour Progress", "Hypertension, Elevated Lipids, Low Protein", "Haematoma, Embolism, Liver failure, Pulmonary oedema"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets
HELLP syndrome is a severe form/complication of pre-eclampsia (it can occur even with mild BP rise). HELLP stands for: H — HAEMOLYSIS (red cells broken down; raised LDH, low haptoglobin, schistocytes on blood film, raised bilirubin); EL — ELEVATED LIVER ENZYMES (raised AST/ALT from liver damage; may cause right upper quadrant/epigastric pain, nausea, vomiting); LP — LOW PLATELETS (under 100 × 10⁹/L — bleeding risk). Complications: DIC, placental abruption, liver haematoma/rupture, AKI, pulmonary oedema. Management: stabilise the mother, MAGNESIUM SULPHATE for seizure prevention, control BP, correct clotting/platelets, and DELIVERY of the baby is the definitive treatment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High Eclampsia, Low Labour Progress — "High Eclampsia, Low Labour Progress" is not a medical term.
• Hypertension, Elevated Lipids, Low Protein — "Hypertension, Elevated Lipids, Low Protein" is incorrect; lipids are not part of HELLP.
• Haematoma, Embolism, Liver failure, Pulmonary oedema — "Haematoma, Embolism, Liver failure, Pulmonary oedema" lists possible complications, but not the meaning of HELLP.

⭐ Key point: HELLP = Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets. Definitive treatment = delivery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q138 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2293, $s$Croup in a child is classically characterised by:$s$, $s$["A high-pitched, barking cough with inspiratory stridor", "Silent chest with no cough", "Chronic productive cough over months", "Painless swallowing difficulty"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A high-pitched, barking cough with inspiratory stridor
CROUP (laryngotracheobronchitis) is a viral infection (usually PARAINFLUENZA virus) mainly in children aged 6 months to 3 years. It causes swelling of the larynx and trachea just below the vocal cords. Classic features: a "BARKING" (seal-like) COUGH, HOARSE voice, INSPIRATORY STRIDOR (noisy breathing in), often worse at night, after 1–2 days of cold-like symptoms and low-grade fever. Treatment: keep the child CALM (crying worsens obstruction), give oral DEXAMETHASONE (0.15–0.6 mg/kg) for all cases, NEBULISED ADRENALINE for severe stridor at rest, and oxygen if needed. Warning signs: stridor at rest, chest recession, agitation or drowsiness, cyanosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Silent chest with no cough — A SILENT CHEST with no cough is a sign of life-threatening ASTHMA (no air movement), not croup.
• Chronic productive cough over months — A CHRONIC productive cough over months suggests conditions like TB, bronchiectasis or cystic fibrosis.
• Painless swallowing difficulty — PAINLESS swallowing difficulty is not typical of croup; EPIGLOTTITIS causes PAINFUL swallowing and drooling.

⭐ Key point: Croup = BARKING cough + INSPIRATORY STRIDOR + hoarseness (parainfluenza). Treat with dexamethasone ± nebulised adrenaline.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q147$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2294, $s$In a pregnant patient having eclamptic seizures, the first-line treatment is:$s$, $s$["Lorazepam", "Phenytoin", "Magnesium sulphate", "Diazepam"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Magnesium sulphate
ECLAMPSIA is the occurrence of SEIZURES in a woman with pre-eclampsia. The FIRST-LINE treatment for eclamptic seizures — and for preventing further seizures — is MAGNESIUM SULPHATE: loading dose 4 g IV over 5–15 minutes, then a maintenance infusion of 1 g/hour (Zuspan regimen) or IM regimen (Pritchard). Large trials (Collaborative Eclampsia Trial, Magpie Trial) showed magnesium sulphate is MORE EFFECTIVE than diazepam and phenytoin at preventing recurrent seizures and REDUCES MATERNAL DEATH.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lorazepam — Lorazepam is first-line for most other seizures, but not for eclampsia.
• Phenytoin — Phenytoin is less effective than magnesium in eclampsia.
• Diazepam — Diazepam is less effective in eclampsia and causes more maternal and neonatal sedation.

⭐ Key point: During magnesium therapy, monitor reflexes, respiratory rate and urine output hourly; keep calcium gluconate (antidote) at the bedside. Delivery is the definitive treatment of eclampsia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q85$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2295, $s$A newborn requires resuscitation immediately after birth and is not breathing adequately. The priority initial action is to:$s$, $s$["Wait several minutes before intervening", "Dry, stimulate, and position the airway, then provide positive pressure ventilation if breathing remains inadequate", "Immediately begin chest compressions before assessing breathing", "Give oxygen by face mask only, with no other assessment"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dry, stimulate, and position the airway, then provide positive pressure ventilation if breathing remains inadequate
Most newborns who need help at birth have a BREATHING problem. The first steps (within the first 30–60 seconds) are: DRY the baby (and keep warm), STIMULATE (drying and rubbing), and POSITION THE AIRWAY (head in neutral position, clear the airway if obstructed). If the baby is still NOT BREATHING ADEQUATELY (apnoea, gasping) or the heart rate is below 100/min, give POSITIVE PRESSURE VENTILATION (inflation breaths with a bag-valve-mask or T-piece). Effective ventilation usually improves the heart rate quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wait several minutes before intervening — Waiting several minutes allows hypoxic brain injury.
• Immediately begin chest compressions before assessing breathing — Chest compressions come after ventilation, only if the heart rate stays below 60/min.
• Give oxygen by face mask only, with no other assessment — Face mask oxygen alone does not help a baby who is not breathing.

⭐ Key point: The "golden minute": begin ventilation within the first minute after birth if needed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q137$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2296, $s$Umbilical cord prolapse is an obstetric emergency because it can cause:$s$, $s$["Improved fetal oxygenation", "Cord compression leading to fetal hypoxia", "Maternal hypertension only", "No risk if the mother is asymptomatic"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cord compression leading to fetal hypoxia
UMBILICAL CORD PROLAPSE happens when the cord slips down through the cervix BEFORE (or beside) the baby's presenting part, usually after the membranes rupture. The baby's head or body then presses the cord against the pelvis, COMPRESSING the umbilical vessels, and the cold air and handling cause the cord vessels to spasm. Blood flow to the baby is cut off, causing FETAL HYPOXIA, bradycardia and death or brain damage within minutes unless delivery is rapid. Risk factors: malpresentation (breech, transverse), polyhydramnios, preterm baby, multiple pregnancy, and artificial rupture of membranes with a high head.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved fetal oxygenation — Cord prolapse REDUCES, not improves, fetal oxygenation.
• Maternal hypertension only — It does not cause maternal hypertension.
• No risk if the mother is asymptomatic — The danger is to the baby even if the mother feels well.

⭐ Key point: Emergency actions: call for help, keep the presenting part off the cord (manually or by filling the bladder), knee–chest or exaggerated Sims' position, avoid handling the cord, and arrange immediate delivery (usually caesarean section).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q141$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2297, $s$Oesophageal atresia in a newborn classically presents with:$s$, $s$["Normal feeding with no complications", "Excessive drooling, choking and coughing with feeding attempts", "Delayed passage of meconium only", "No respiratory symptoms at all"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Excessive drooling, choking and coughing with feeding attempts
OESOPHAGEAL ATRESIA means the oesophagus ends in a blind pouch, so swallowed saliva and milk cannot reach the stomach. Classic signs in a newborn: EXCESSIVE DROOLING/frothy saliva from the mouth, CHOKING, COUGHING and CYANOSIS with FEEDING attempts, and respiratory distress (from aspiration — especially if there is a tracheo-oesophageal fistula). A nasogastric tube cannot be passed into the stomach (it coils in the pouch on X-ray). There may be a history of polyhydramnios in pregnancy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal feeding with no complications — Normal feeding is impossible.
• Delayed passage of meconium only — Delayed passage of meconium suggests Hirschsprung's disease or meconium ileus.
• No respiratory symptoms at all — Respiratory symptoms are common because of aspiration.

⭐ Key point: Stop oral feeds, nurse head-up, use continuous suction of the upper pouch (Replogle tube), and arrange urgent surgical repair.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q145$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2298, $s$A nurse is caring for a newborn in the NICU and suspects neonatal sepsis. Which of the following is an early sign of neonatal sepsis?$s$, $s$["A high-grade fever", "A stable blood sugar level", "A sudden increase in activity", "Poor feeding, lethargy, and respiratory distress"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Poor feeding, lethargy, and respiratory distress
NEONATAL SEPSIS often presents with SUBTLE, NON-SPECIFIC signs because newborns have an immature immune system. Early signs: POOR FEEDING, LETHARGY (reduced activity, floppiness), RESPIRATORY DISTRESS (tachypnoea, grunting, apnoea), temperature instability (often HYPOthermia rather than fever), jaundice, vomiting, poor colour or mottling, and abnormal glucose levels.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A high-grade fever — High-grade fever is not typical — newborns often have low or unstable temperatures.
• A stable blood sugar level — Stable blood sugar is reassuring, not a sign of sepsis (sepsis often causes hypo- or hyperglycaemia).
• A sudden increase in activity — Increased activity is not typical; lethargy is more common.

⭐ Key point: Any newborn who is "not right" should be assessed for sepsis; take cultures and start IV antibiotics early.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q147$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2299, $s$The classic triad seen in Amniotic Fluid Embolism is:$s$, $s$["Fever, rash, joint pain", "Hypotension, hypoxia, DIC", "Headache, blurred vision, hypertension", "Jaundice, ascites, confusion"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypotension, hypoxia, DIC
AMNIOTIC FLUID EMBOLISM (AFE) is a rare, catastrophic obstetric emergency, usually during labour, delivery or just after. Amniotic fluid and fetal cells enter the mother's circulation and trigger a massive reaction. The classic TRIAD is: sudden HYPOTENSION/cardiovascular collapse (often cardiac arrest), HYPOXIA (severe breathlessness, cyanosis, respiratory failure), and DIC (severe bleeding from the uterus, wounds and IV sites). Seizures or altered consciousness may also occur.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fever, rash, joint pain — Fever, rash and joint pain suggest infection or rheumatic disease, not AFE.
• Headache, blurred vision, hypertension — Headache, blurred vision and hypertension suggest pre-eclampsia.
• Jaundice, ascites, confusion — Jaundice, ascites and confusion suggest liver failure.

⭐ Key point: Treatment is supportive and urgent: CPR (with perimortem caesarean if needed), oxygen and ventilation, vasopressors and inotropes, and massive transfusion with clotting factors.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q135$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2300, $s$The '4 Ts' framework for causes of postpartum haemorrhage includes tone, tissue, trauma, and:$s$, $s$["Temperature", "Thrombin (coagulopathy)", "Tachycardia", "Transfusion"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Thrombin (coagulopathy)
The "4 Ts" are the four causes of POSTPARTUM HAEMORRHAGE (PPH): TONE — poor uterine contraction (atony), the commonest cause (~70%); TRAUMA — tears of the perineum, vagina or cervix, uterine rupture or inversion; TISSUE — retained placenta, membranes or clots; THROMBIN — clotting problems (coagulopathy, e.g. DIC from abruption, sepsis, HELLP, amniotic fluid embolism, or anticoagulants). This framework helps the team find and treat the cause quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Temperature — Temperature is not one of the 4 Ts.
• Tachycardia — Tachycardia is a sign of blood loss, not a cause.
• Transfusion — Transfusion is a treatment, not a cause.

⭐ Key point: Initial PPH management: call for help, rub up the uterus, give oxytocin, large-bore IV access, send blood for crossmatch and clotting, check the placenta, look for tears, and give tranexamic acid within 3 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q143 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2301, $s$What is the 24-hour maintenance fluid requirement for a child weighing 28 kg (Holliday–Segar method)?$s$, $s$["2,800 mL", "1,400 mL", "1,860 mL", "1,660 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1,660 mL
HOLLIDAY–SEGAR (24-hour): first 10 kg × 100 mL = 1,000 mL; next 10 kg × 50 mL = 500 mL; remaining 8 kg × 20 mL = 160 mL. Total: 1,000 + 500 + 160 = 1,660 mL/day.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2,800 mL — 2,800 mL is 100 mL/kg for all 28 kg.
• 1,400 mL — 1,400 mL is 50 mL/kg for all.
• 1,860 mL — 1,860 mL miscalculates the last part.

⭐ Key point: Equivalent hourly rate by the 4-2-1 rule: 40 + 20 + 8 = 68 mL/hr.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q57 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2302, $s$To reduce deaths from postpartum haemorrhage, tranexamic acid 1 g IV should be given within:$s$, $s$["12 hours of birth", "6 hours of birth", "3 hours of birth", "24 hours of birth"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3 hours of birth
The WOMAN trial showed that TRANEXAMIC ACID (1 g IV over 10 minutes) given WITHIN 3 HOURS OF BIRTH reduces deaths from bleeding in postpartum haemorrhage. After 3 hours, it gives no benefit. A second dose can be given if bleeding continues after 30 minutes or restarts within 24 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 12 hours of birth — 12 hours is too late.
• 6 hours of birth — 6 hours is too late.
• 24 hours of birth — 24 hours is too late.

⭐ Key point: TXA is given in addition to (not instead of) uterotonic drugs and other PPH treatments.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q168 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2303, $s$Severe hypertension in pregnancy (BP 160/110) should be treated urgently with:$s$, $s$["Large doses of IV furosemide every 6 hours", "An ACE inhibitor such as lisinopril by mouth", "IV labetalol, IV hydralazine or oral nifedipine", "No treatment at all until after the delivery"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV labetalol, IV hydralazine or oral nifedipine
BP of 160/110 or more in pregnancy is SEVERE HYPERTENSION and must be lowered urgently to reduce the risk of maternal STROKE (brain bleed), eclampsia and placental abruption. Safe first-line drugs: IV LABETALOL, IV HYDRALAZINE or ORAL NIFEDIPINE (immediate-release). The goal is to lower BP gradually (e.g. to about 140–150/90–100), not too fast, to protect blood flow to the placenta.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Large doses of IV furosemide every 6 hours — Diuretics such as furosemide reduce blood volume, which in pre-eclampsia is already low, worsening placental perfusion. They are used only for pulmonary oedema.
• An ACE inhibitor such as lisinopril by mouth — ACE inhibitors are CONTRAINDICATED in pregnancy: they damage fetal kidneys and cause malformations.
• No treatment at all until after the delivery — Leaving severe hypertension untreated risks stroke and death.

⭐ Key point: Methyldopa is used for long-term control but acts too slowly for an emergency. Also give magnesium sulphate in severe pre-eclampsia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q136 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2304, $s$Among the 4 Ts of PPH, 'Tissue' refers to:$s$, $s$["Uterine muscle tone", "Clotting factor deficiency", "Retained placental tissue", "Genital tract lacerations"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Retained placental tissue
The 4 Ts of PPH: TONE (soft uterus), TRAUMA (tears), TISSUE (retained placenta or membranes), and THROMBIN (clotting problems). "TISSUE" refers to RETAINED PLACENTAL TISSUE or membranes inside the uterus, which stop it from contracting fully and cause bleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uterine muscle tone — Uterine muscle tone is "TONE".
• Clotting factor deficiency — Clotting factor deficiency is "THROMBIN".
• Genital tract lacerations — Genital tract tears are "TRAUMA".

⭐ Key point: Check the placenta and membranes are complete after every delivery. Retained tissue needs manual removal or evacuation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q131 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, true, 2305, $s$Peripartum cardiomyopathy is heart failure that develops:$s$, $s$["Late in pregnancy or within 5 months after delivery", "Only in women over 50 years of age", "Only after a twin or triplet pregnancy", "Only in the first trimester of pregnancy"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Late in pregnancy or within 5 months after delivery
PERIPARTUM CARDIOMYOPATHY is heart failure caused by weak heart muscle (ejection fraction below 45%) that develops in the LAST MONTH of pregnancy or within 5 MONTHS after delivery, with no other cause. It presents with breathlessness, orthopnoea, cough and swelling, often mistaken for normal pregnancy symptoms.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only in women over 50 years of age — It occurs in women of childbearing age, not only over 50.
• Only after a twin or triplet pregnancy — It isn't limited to multiple pregnancies (though twins are a risk factor).
• Only in the first trimester of pregnancy — It occurs late in pregnancy or after delivery, not in the first trimester.

⭐ Key point: Diagnose with echocardiography. Treat with heart failure drugs (some are avoided in pregnancy) and anticoagulation if the EF is very low.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q134 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2306, $s$All are signs of ruptured ectopic pregnancy EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Tachycardia, shock, pallor and collapse", "Hyperglycaemia", "Abdominal pain", "Vaginal bleeding"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hyperglycaemia
The question asks which is NOT a sign of a ruptured ectopic pregnancy. Hyperglycaemia (high blood sugar) has no link with it. A ruptured ectopic causes internal bleeding into the abdomen: sudden lower abdominal pain (often one-sided), vaginal bleeding (usually light), shoulder-tip pain (blood irritating the diaphragm), dizziness, and signs of shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tachycardia, shock, pallor and collapse — Tachycardia, shock, pallor and collapse ARE signs; they come from internal bleeding.
• Abdominal pain — Abdominal pain IS a sign, often the first symptom.
• Vaginal bleeding — Vaginal bleeding IS a sign, though often less than the internal bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Any woman of childbearing age with abdominal pain and shock has a ruptured ectopic until proven otherwise. Do a pregnancy test, resuscitate, cross-match, and arrange emergency surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q131$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2307, $s$A pregnant patient at 32 weeks presents with painless, bright red vaginal bleeding. This presentation is most concerning for:$s$, $s$["Placental abruption", "Placenta praevia", "Normal spotting in pregnancy", "Round ligament pain"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Placenta praevia
Placenta praevia means the placenta lies low in the uterus, over or near the cervix. As the lower uterus stretches in the third trimester, the placenta separates and bleeds. The classic sign is PAINLESS, BRIGHT RED vaginal bleeding, often recurrent, with a soft, non-tender uterus. The baby's head may not be engaged, or the lie may be abnormal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Placental abruption — Placental abruption causes PAINFUL bleeding with a hard, tender ("woody") uterus.
• Normal spotting in pregnancy — Bleeding at 32 weeks is never "normal spotting"; it must be investigated.
• Round ligament pain — Round ligament pain causes sharp groin pain, not bleeding.

⭐ Key point: In placenta praevia NEVER do a vaginal examination (it can cause torrential bleeding). Confirm with ultrasound, resuscitate, and plan a caesarean section.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q132$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2308, $s$What are the symptoms of premature rupture of membranes?$s$, $s$["Feeling a gush of fluid from the vagina", "Slow leak or trickle of fluid", "The fluid is clear and odourless", "All of the options"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: All of the options
Premature rupture of membranes (PROM) is when the amniotic sac breaks before labour starts. Women may notice a sudden GUSH of fluid or a slow, constant leak or trickle that they can't control. Normal amniotic fluid is clear (or pale straw-coloured) and odourless. All the options describe PROM, so "all of the options" is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Feeling a gush of fluid from the vagina — A gush of fluid is one sign, but not the only one listed.
• Slow leak or trickle of fluid — A slow leak or trickle is one sign, but not the only one listed.
• The fluid is clear and odourless — Clear, odourless fluid is one feature, but not the only one listed.

⭐ Key point: Green fluid = meconium (fetal distress). Foul-smelling fluid with fever = chorioamnionitis (infection). After PROM, avoid digital vaginal exams, check for infection, and give antibiotics as per protocol.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q133$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2309, $s$A cervical incompetence diagnosis in a pregnant patient primarily increases the risk of:$s$, $s$["Post-term pregnancy", "Second-trimester pregnancy loss or preterm birth from painless cervical dilation", "Gestational diabetes", "Multiple pregnancy"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Second-trimester pregnancy loss or preterm birth from painless cervical dilation
Cervical incompetence (cervical insufficiency) means the cervix is weak and opens painlessly in the second trimester, without contractions. The pregnancy can be lost suddenly (second-trimester miscarriage) or the baby born very early. Treatment includes cervical cerclage (a stitch around the cervix) or vaginal progesterone, with monitoring of cervical length by ultrasound.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Post-term pregnancy — Cervical incompetence leads to EARLY birth, not post-term pregnancy.
• Gestational diabetes — Gestational diabetes is a metabolic condition, not related to the cervix.
• Multiple pregnancy — Multiple pregnancy is a cause of preterm birth, not a result of cervical incompetence.

⭐ Key point: History is the clue: repeated painless second-trimester losses. The cerclage stitch is removed at about 36–37 weeks or when labour starts.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q134$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2310, $s$A Rhesus-negative mother delivers a Rhesus-positive baby. The nurse anticipates administration of:$s$, $s$["No specific treatment is needed", "Anti-D immunoglobulin to the mother to prevent sensitisation in future pregnancies", "Anti-D immunoglobulin to the baby", "Oxytocin to the mother for this purpose"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Anti-D immunoglobulin to the mother to prevent sensitisation in future pregnancies
When an Rh-negative mother delivers an Rh-positive baby, some of the baby's red cells enter her blood. Her immune system can make anti-D antibodies (sensitisation). In a FUTURE pregnancy with an Rh-positive baby, these antibodies cross the placenta and destroy the baby's red cells (haemolytic disease of the newborn). Giving ANTI-D immunoglobulin to the MOTHER within 72 hours of delivery removes the baby's cells before her body reacts.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No specific treatment is needed — Without treatment, the next Rh-positive baby is at serious risk.
• Anti-D immunoglobulin to the baby — Anti-D is given to the MOTHER, not the baby.
• Oxytocin to the mother for this purpose — Oxytocin contracts the uterus; it does not prevent Rh sensitisation.

⭐ Key point: Also give anti-D after any sensitising event (miscarriage, ectopic, amniocentesis, abdominal trauma, bleeding) and routinely at 28 weeks in many units.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q135$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2311, $s$What important modification should be made when performing CPR on a pregnant patient in later pregnancy?$s$, $s$["Manual left uterine displacement to relieve aortocaval compression and improve venous return", "Place the patient in the prone position", "Avoid performing chest compressions entirely", "Compress the abdomen instead of the chest"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Manual left uterine displacement to relieve aortocaval compression and improve venous return
After about 20 weeks, the pregnant uterus presses on the inferior vena cava and aorta when the woman lies on her back (aortocaval compression). This sharply reduces blood returning to the heart, so CPR would be ineffective. Pushing the uterus to the LEFT by hand (manual left uterine displacement) while doing chest compressions relieves the pressure. A left lateral tilt of 15–30° is an alternative.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Place the patient in the prone position — The prone position (face down) makes CPR impossible.
• Avoid performing chest compressions entirely — Chest compressions are still essential in pregnancy.
• Compress the abdomen instead of the chest — Compressions are always on the CHEST (lower half of the sternum), never the abdomen.

⭐ Key point: In maternal cardiac arrest, if there is no return of circulation within 4 minutes, prepare for a perimortem caesarean section by 5 minutes. This helps both mother and baby.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q137$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2312, $s$Tracheo-oesophageal fistula is often associated with which other congenital anomaly?$s$, $s$["Oesophageal atresia", "Cleft lip only", "Club foot only", "Polydactyly only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Oesophageal atresia
Tracheo-oesophageal fistula (TOF) is an abnormal connection between the trachea and the oesophagus. In about 85% of cases, it occurs together with oesophageal ATRESIA, where the upper oesophagus ends in a blind pouch. The baby has frothy saliva, chokes and coughs or turns blue on feeding, and an NG tube cannot be passed into the stomach.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cleft lip only — Cleft lip can occur with other anomalies, but it is not the classic partner of TOF.
• Club foot only — Club foot is a limb anomaly, not typically linked with TOF.
• Polydactyly only — Polydactyly (extra fingers) is not typically linked with TOF.

⭐ Key point: TOF may be part of VACTERL (Vertebral, Anal, Cardiac, Tracheo-oesophageal, Renal, Limb anomalies). Keep the baby nil by mouth, head up, with continuous suction of the upper pouch until surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q138$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2313, $s$The most widely used effective treatment for neonatal jaundice is:$s$, $s$["Blood transfusion", "Antibiotics", "Phototherapy", "Liver transplant"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Phototherapy
Phototherapy uses blue-green light (wavelength about 460–490 nm) to change bilirubin in the skin into a water-soluble form that the baby can pass in urine and stool without needing the liver. It is safe, non-invasive and effective for most babies with significant jaundice. Exchange transfusion is kept for very high or rapidly rising bilirubin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Blood transfusion — Blood transfusion does not lower bilirubin (exchange transfusion is a different, specialised procedure).
• Antibiotics — Antibiotics treat infection, which is only sometimes a cause of jaundice.
• Liver transplant — A liver transplant is only for rare liver diseases such as biliary atresia that fails surgery.

⭐ Key point: Phototherapy care: cover the eyes, expose as much skin as possible, turn the baby, give extra fluids or feeds, monitor temperature, and check bilirubin regularly. Jaundice within 24 hours of birth is always abnormal.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q141$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2314, $s$Regarding the newborn which of this is correct?$s$, $s$["Preterms are babies delivered before 40 weeks of delivery", "All preterms are low birth weight", "All preterms are small for gestational age", "The term newborn may be small for gestational age"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The term newborn may be small for gestational age
Small for gestational age (SGA) means a baby's weight is below the 10th centile FOR ITS GESTATIONAL AGE. A TERM baby (37–42 weeks) can still be SGA if it did not grow well in the womb (placental problems, maternal smoking, pre-eclampsia, infection). This statement is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Preterms are babies delivered before 40 weeks of delivery — Preterm means born BEFORE 37 completed weeks, not before 40 weeks.
• All preterms are low birth weight — Not all preterm babies are low birth weight; a baby at 36 weeks can weigh more than 2500 g.
• All preterms are small for gestational age — Not all preterm babies are SGA; many preterm babies are an appropriate weight for their age.

⭐ Key point: Low birth weight is below 2500 g, very low is below 1500 g, extremely low is below 1000 g. SGA babies are at risk of low glucose, hypothermia and polycythaemia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q144$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2315, $s$Which is part of pre-operative care for a child with Hirschsprung's disease?$s$, $s$["CT scan", "Shaving the axillary hair", "Giving bowel washouts/enemas", "Obtaining consent from the child himself"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Giving bowel washouts/enemas
In Hirschsprung's disease, part of the bowel (usually the rectum and sigmoid colon) has no nerve cells, so it cannot relax and push stool along. Stool builds up above it, causing distension, constipation and risk of enterocolitis. Before surgery, regular bowel WASHOUTS (saline rectal irrigations) or enemas are given to empty the bowel, relieve distension and reduce infection risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CT scan — A CT scan is not routine before surgery; diagnosis is by rectal biopsy and contrast enema.
• Shaving the axillary hair — Shaving the axillary hair is not relevant to bowel surgery.
• Obtaining consent from the child himself — A child cannot legally give consent; parents or guardians consent.

⭐ Key point: Signs of Hirschsprung's: failure to pass meconium within 48 hours, distended abdomen, bilious vomiting. Watch for enterocolitis (fever, explosive foul diarrhoea), which is dangerous.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q145$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2316, $s$Sudden Infant Death Syndrome (SIDS) risk is reduced by which key recommendation?$s$, $s$["Placing infants to sleep prone (on their stomach)", "Placing infants to sleep supine (on their back)", "Co-sleeping in the same bed as an adult at all times", "Using soft, loose bedding in the crib"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Placing infants to sleep supine (on their back)
Placing babies to sleep on their BACK (supine) is the most important way to reduce SIDS ("Back to Sleep"). Other safe sleep advice: a firm flat mattress, no pillows, toys or loose bedding, the baby's feet at the foot of the cot, room-sharing (not bed-sharing) for the first 6 months, a smoke-free home, breastfeeding, and no overheating.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Placing infants to sleep prone (on their stomach) — Sleeping prone (on the stomach) INCREASES the risk of SIDS.
• Co-sleeping in the same bed as an adult at all times — Bed-sharing with adults increases the risk, especially if the adult smokes, drinks or takes sedatives.
• Using soft, loose bedding in the crib — Soft, loose bedding increases the risk of suffocation and SIDS.

⭐ Key point: SIDS peaks at 2–4 months of age. Teach all parents safe sleep before discharge.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q150$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2317, $s$Pendred syndrome is classically associated with goitre plus:$s$, $s$["Congenital deafness", "Congenital blindness", "Cardiac defects", "Renal failure"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Congenital deafness
PENDRED SYNDROME is an inherited (autosomal recessive) condition caused by a mutation in the SLC26A4 gene, which makes a protein called PENDRIN. Pendrin moves iodide in the THYROID and moves ions in the INNER EAR. So patients have two main features: (1) GOITRE (enlarged thyroid, often with mild hypothyroidism) from a defect in iodide organification; and (2) CONGENITAL SENSORINEURAL DEAFNESS (often with an enlarged vestibular aqueduct/cochlear malformation). It is one of the most common causes of syndromic hearing loss.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Congenital blindness — CONGENITAL BLINDNESS is not a feature of Pendred syndrome.
• Cardiac defects — CARDIAC DEFECTS are not part of Pendred syndrome.
• Renal failure — RENAL FAILURE is not part of Pendred syndrome.

⭐ Key point: Pendred syndrome = GOITRE + congenital sensorineural DEAFNESS (pendrin gene).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q114$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2318, $s$A woman in the third trimester develops sudden severe headache, visual disturbance, and blood pressure of 165/110mmHg with proteinuria. The nurse should recognise this as warning signs of impending:$s$, $s$["Normal pregnancy discomfort", "Eclamptic seizure", "Simple migraine unrelated to pregnancy", "Round ligament strain"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Eclamptic seizure
PRE-ECLAMPSIA is new HYPERTENSION (≥ 140/90) after 20 weeks of pregnancy with PROTEINURIA or organ dysfunction. SEVERE features include BP ≥ 160/110, severe HEADACHE, VISUAL DISTURBANCES (blurred vision, flashing lights), epigastric/right upper quadrant pain, low platelets, raised liver enzymes (HELLP), pulmonary oedema, reduced urine output. Headache and visual disturbance show CEREBRAL IRRITATION, warning that an ECLAMPTIC SEIZURE may happen soon. Actions: call for senior help, left lateral position, seizure precautions, IV MAGNESIUM SULPHATE (loading dose 4 g IV over 5–15 minutes, then 1 g/hour; monitor reflexes, respiratory rate ≥ 12, urine output ≥ 25–30 mL/hour; antidote calcium gluconate), lower BP with labetalol, hydralazine or nifedipine, and plan delivery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal pregnancy discomfort — These are NOT normal pregnancy discomforts; BP 165/110 with proteinuria is a medical emergency.
• Simple migraine unrelated to pregnancy — A simple migraine does not cause BP 165/110 with proteinuria.
• Round ligament strain — ROUND LIGAMENT strain causes groin/lower abdominal pain, not headache, visual changes and hypertension.

⭐ Key point: Pregnancy + BP ≥ 160/110 + proteinuria + headache/visual disturbance = severe pre-eclampsia → imminent ECLAMPSIA. Give MgSO4.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q135$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2319, $s$What is the best thing one can do to prevent premature rupture of membranes?$s$, $s$["Avoid smoking cigarettes", "Maintain a healthy pregnancy", "Attend all prenatal clinic", "All of the options"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: All of the options
PREMATURE RUPTURE OF MEMBRANES (PROM/PPROM) means the amniotic sac breaks before labour starts (and before 37 weeks for PPROM). Risk factors include: SMOKING (weakens membranes), infections (urinary tract infection, bacterial vaginosis, STIs), previous PROM or preterm birth, poor nutrition, multiple pregnancy, polyhydramnios, cervical insufficiency, bleeding in pregnancy and low socio-economic status. Prevention therefore includes ALL the options: AVOID SMOKING, MAINTAIN A HEALTHY PREGNANCY (good nutrition, vitamin C and other nutrients, treat infections, rest when advised), and ATTEND ALL ANTENATAL (PRENATAL) CLINICS so that infections and risk factors are found and treated early.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Avoid smoking cigarettes — Avoiding smoking alone is correct but is only one preventive measure.
• Maintain a healthy pregnancy — Maintaining a healthy pregnancy alone is correct but does not include the other important measures.
• Attend all prenatal clinic — Attending antenatal clinic alone is correct, but it is only one of several preventive actions.

⭐ Key point: Prevent PROM: no smoking + healthy pregnancy (nutrition, treat infections) + regular antenatal care — ALL of the options.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q139$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2320, $s$Magnesium sulfate is administered in severe preeclampsia primarily to:$s$, $s$["Lower blood glucose", "Prevent or treat eclamptic seizures", "Induce labour immediately", "Treat postpartum haemorrhage"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Prevent or treat eclamptic seizures
In SEVERE PRE-ECLAMPSIA, MAGNESIUM SULPHATE (MgSO4) is given mainly to PREVENT ECLAMPTIC SEIZURES — and to TREAT them if they occur. It is a central nervous system depressant and cerebral vasodilator that stabilises nerve membranes. The Magpie trial showed it halves the risk of eclampsia. Regimen (Zuspan/Pritchard): loading dose 4 g IV over 5–15 minutes (or 4 g IV + 10 g IM), then maintenance 1 g/hour IV (or 5 g IM every 4 hours) for 24 hours after delivery or the last fit. MONITOR for MAGNESIUM TOXICITY: loss of PATELLAR REFLEXES (first sign), respiratory rate below 12/min, urine output below 25–30 mL/hour, drowsiness. ANTIDOTE: CALCIUM GLUCONATE 10% 10 mL IV over 10 minutes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lower blood glucose — MgSO4 does NOT lower blood glucose.
• Induce labour immediately — It does not INDUCE labour (oxytocin or prostaglandins do); it may slightly relax the uterus.
• Treat postpartum haemorrhage — It does not treat PPH; uterotonics (oxytocin, ergometrine, misoprostol) do.

⭐ Key point: MgSO4 in pre-eclampsia = PREVENT/TREAT SEIZURES. Toxicity: lost reflexes, RR < 12, low urine. Antidote: calcium gluconate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q141$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2321, $s$A premature infant in the NICU develops abdominal distension, bloody stools, and feeding intolerance. The nurse should suspect:$s$, $s$["Normal prematurity findings requiring no evaluation", "Necrotising enterocolitis (NEC), a serious neonatal GI emergency", "Simple constipation", "Normal digestion in preterm infants"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Necrotising enterocolitis (NEC), a serious neonatal GI emergency
NECROTISING ENTEROCOLITIS (NEC) is a serious bowel emergency, mostly in PREMATURE and low-birth-weight babies, usually in the first 2–3 weeks after starting feeds. Part of the bowel wall becomes inflamed and dies (necrosis), sometimes with perforation. Classic signs: FEEDING INTOLERANCE (increased gastric residuals, bilious vomiting), ABDOMINAL DISTENSION and tenderness, BLOODY STOOLS, plus signs of sepsis (apnoea, bradycardia, temperature instability, lethargy). X-ray: pneumatosis intestinalis (gas in the bowel wall), portal venous gas, free air if perforated. Management: STOP FEEDS (NPO), NG tube on free drainage, IV fluids/TPN, IV antibiotics, serial X-rays, surgical review. Breast milk reduces NEC risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal prematurity findings requiring no evaluation — These are NOT normal prematurity findings; ignoring them can be fatal.
• Simple constipation — SIMPLE CONSTIPATION does not cause bloody stools, bilious aspirates and systemic illness.
• Normal digestion in preterm infants — Normal digestion does not cause distension, bloody stools and feeding intolerance.

⭐ Key point: Preterm + feeding intolerance + abdominal distension + bloody stools = NEC → stop feeds, NG decompression, antibiotics, X-ray.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q145$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2322, $s$The APGAR score evaluates a newborn's condition based on which five components?$s$, $s$["Appearance, pulse, grimace, activity, and respiration", "Airway, pulse, glucose, activity, respiration", "Appearance, position, glucose, activity, reflex", "Alertness, pain, growth, appetite, reflex"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Appearance, pulse, grimace, activity, and respiration
The APGAR score assesses a newborn at 1 and 5 minutes after birth (and every 5 minutes up to 20 minutes if low). Five components, each scored 0, 1 or 2: A — APPEARANCE (skin colour: blue/pale 0, body pink with blue limbs 1, completely pink 2); P — PULSE (heart rate: absent 0, below 100 = 1, 100 or above = 2); G — GRIMACE (reflex irritability: no response 0, grimace 1, cry/cough/sneeze 2); A — ACTIVITY (muscle tone: limp 0, some flexion 1, active movement 2); R — RESPIRATION (absent 0, slow/irregular 1, good strong cry 2). Total 7–10 = normal; 4–6 = moderately depressed (needs some help); 0–3 = severely depressed (needs resuscitation). Resuscitation should not wait for the 1-minute score.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Airway, pulse, glucose, activity, respiration — "Airway, pulse, glucose…" — glucose is not part of APGAR.
• Appearance, position, glucose, activity, reflex — "Appearance, position, glucose, activity, reflex" — position and glucose are not components.
• Alertness, pain, growth, appetite, reflex — "Alertness, pain, growth, appetite, reflex" — none of these, except reflex in part, are APGAR components.

⭐ Key point: APGAR = Appearance, Pulse, Grimace, Activity, Respiration (0–2 each, total 10). Score at 1 and 5 minutes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q149$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2323, $s$Neonates at increased risk for hypoglycaemia after birth include those born to:$s$, $s$["Mothers with well-controlled, non-diabetic pregnancies", "Diabetic mothers, and infants who are preterm or growth-restricted", "Mothers with normal glucose tolerance only", "Only infants born after 42 weeks gestation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Diabetic mothers, and infants who are preterm or growth-restricted
After birth, the baby's glucose supply from the mother stops suddenly, so the newborn must make its own. Babies at HIGH RISK of NEONATAL HYPOGLYCAEMIA: (1) INFANTS OF DIABETIC MOTHERS — high maternal glucose crosses the placenta, so the baby's pancreas makes a lot of INSULIN (hyperinsulinism); after birth the glucose supply stops but insulin stays high, so glucose drops; these babies are often large (macrosomic). (2) PRETERM infants — small glycogen and fat stores. (3) GROWTH-RESTRICTED (SGA/IUGR) infants — poor stores. Also: babies who are cold, septic, asphyxiated, or of mothers on beta-blockers. Signs: jitteriness, poor feeding, lethargy, apnoea, seizures. Management: early and frequent feeding, check blood glucose (treat if below about 2.6 mmol/L/47 mg/dL), oral or IV dextrose (10% dextrose 2 mL/kg).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mothers with well-controlled, non-diabetic pregnancies — Babies of mothers with well-controlled NON-diabetic pregnancies are NOT at increased risk.
• Mothers with normal glucose tolerance only — Mothers with normal glucose tolerance do not raise the baby's risk.
• Only infants born after 42 weeks gestation — Post-term (> 42 weeks) babies can be at some risk (placental insufficiency), but "only" these babies is wrong — diabetic mothers, preterm and SGA babies are the main groups.

⭐ Key point: Neonatal hypoglycaemia risk: infants of DIABETIC mothers (hyperinsulinism), PRETERM, and growth-restricted (SGA) babies.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q150$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2324, $s$G cells, which secrete the hormone gastrin, are located predominantly in the:$s$, $s$["Pyloric antrum", "Fundus", "Cardia", "Duodenum exclusively"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pyloric antrum
GASTRIN is a hormone that stimulates the stomach to secrete acid (via parietal cells) and promotes stomach movement. It is produced mainly by G CELLS in the PYLORIC ANTRUM (the lower part of the stomach near the pylorus), with smaller numbers in the duodenum. G cells release gastrin when food (especially protein) enters the stomach and when the stomach is stretched; release is reduced when the stomach becomes very acidic (negative feedback).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fundus — The fundus mainly contains parietal cells (acid, intrinsic factor) and chief cells (pepsinogen).
• Cardia — The cardia contains mainly mucus-secreting glands.
• Duodenum exclusively — The duodenum does contain some G cells, but not "exclusively" — most are in the antrum.

⭐ Key point: A gastrin-secreting tumour (gastrinoma, Zollinger–Ellison syndrome) causes severe, recurrent peptic ulcers from excess acid.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q2$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2325, $s$Primary postpartum haemorrhage following a caesarean section is defined as blood loss of:$s$, $s$["≥250ml", "≥500ml", "≥1000ml", "≥3000ml"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: ≥1000ml
POSTPARTUM HAEMORRHAGE (PPH) is defined by the amount of blood loss within 24 hours of birth: after VAGINAL birth — 500 mL or more; after CAESAREAN SECTION — 1,000 mL or more (because more blood is normally lost during surgery). Major PPH is often defined as more than 1,000 mL, and severe as more than 2,000 mL. Any blood loss causing signs of shock is PPH regardless of the amount.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• ≥250ml — 250 mL is a normal blood loss.
• ≥500ml — 500 mL is the threshold for vaginal birth.
• ≥3000ml — 3,000 mL is massive haemorrhage, far above the definition threshold.

⭐ Key point: Blood loss is often underestimated — weigh pads and drapes, and watch vital signs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q135$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2326, $s$Clinical features of hyperemesis gravidarum include all EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Vomiting more than 3–4 times a day", "Weight loss of more than 5%", "Dizziness", "Insomnia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Insomnia
HYPEREMESIS GRAVIDARUM causes severe, persistent nausea and vomiting in pregnancy. Typical features: VOMITING many times a day (more than 3–4 episodes), inability to keep food or fluids down, WEIGHT LOSS of more than 5% of pre-pregnancy weight, DEHYDRATION (DIZZINESS, dry mouth, low urine output, tachycardia, postural hypotension), ketonuria, electrolyte imbalance (low potassium, low sodium), and fatigue. INSOMNIA (inability to sleep) is not a typical clinical feature.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vomiting more than 3–4 times a day — Frequent vomiting IS a feature.
• Weight loss of more than 5% — Weight loss of more than 5% IS a feature.
• Dizziness — Dizziness (from dehydration) IS a feature.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV fluids, anti-emetics, electrolyte replacement, thiamine, and thromboprophylaxis if admitted.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q136$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2327, $s$The following are complications of eclampsia except$s$, $s$["Neurological deficit", "Pulmonary oedema", "Acute renal failure", "Sepsis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sepsis
ECLAMPSIA (seizures in pre-eclampsia) can cause serious complications: NEUROLOGICAL DEFICIT (stroke, cerebral haemorrhage, cerebral oedema, blindness), PULMONARY OEDEMA, ACUTE RENAL FAILURE, HELLP syndrome, DIC, liver rupture, placental abruption, aspiration pneumonia, and maternal and fetal death. SEPSIS is an infection and is NOT a direct complication of eclampsia (although it can occur in any hospitalised patient).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Neurological deficit — Neurological deficit IS a complication.
• Pulmonary oedema — Pulmonary oedema IS a complication.
• Acute renal failure — Acute renal failure IS a complication.

⭐ Key point: Eclampsia is a leading cause of maternal death in Nigeria; early antenatal care and BP monitoring help prevent it.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q139$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2328, $s$Which of the following is part of the definition of hyperemesis gravidarum?$s$, $s$["Vomiting that persists for the entire pregnancy", "Vomiting upon arising more than five days per week", "Vomiting that appears after the 20th week of pregnancy", "Severe nausea and vomiting with weight loss greater than 5% of pregpregnacy body weight"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Severe nausea and vomiting with weight loss greater than 5% of pregpregnacy body weight
HYPEREMESIS GRAVIDARUM is defined as SEVERE, persistent NAUSEA and VOMITING in pregnancy causing WEIGHT LOSS of MORE THAN 5% of pre-pregnancy body weight, together with dehydration, ketosis and/or electrolyte imbalance. It usually starts before 20 weeks (often 4–10 weeks) and often improves by 16–20 weeks, although in some women it continues longer.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vomiting that persists for the entire pregnancy — It does not have to persist for the entire pregnancy (although it can in a few women).
• Vomiting upon arising more than five days per week — Vomiting on waking several days a week describes ordinary morning sickness, not the defining criteria.
• Vomiting that appears after the 20th week of pregnancy — Vomiting that starts after 20 weeks suggests another cause (e.g. pre-eclampsia, HELLP, acute fatty liver, infection), not hyperemesis.

⭐ Key point: Assess hydration (urine ketones, electrolytes), give IV fluids, anti-emetics and thiamine, and consider thromboprophylaxis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q142$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2329, $s$A gravid uterus at term weighs about:$s$, $s$["1000 g", "60 g", "100 g", "500 g"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1000 g
The non-pregnant uterus weighs only about 50–70 g. During pregnancy, its muscle cells grow enormously (hypertrophy) and stretch, so that by TERM the uterus (without its contents) weighs about 1,000 g (1 kg) — roughly a 15–20-fold increase. Its capacity also increases from about 10 mL to about 5 litres. After delivery, it shrinks back (involution) over about 6 weeks.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 60 g — 60 g is the weight of the NON-pregnant uterus.
• 100 g — 100 g is far too small for a term uterus.
• 500 g — 500 g is too small for a term uterus (it's about the weight 1 week after delivery).

⭐ Key point: Assess uterine involution after delivery: the fundus should be firm and descend about 1 cm (one finger-breadth) per day.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q143$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2330, $s$Amniotic fluid embolism classically presents with the sudden onset of:$s$, $s$["Gradual, mild shortness of breath over days", "Acute hypoxia, hypotension, and coagulopathy during or shortly after labour", "Isolated mild fever", "Chronic hypertension"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acute hypoxia, hypotension, and coagulopathy during or shortly after labour
AMNIOTIC FLUID EMBOLISM (AFE) is a rare but catastrophic obstetric emergency occurring DURING LABOUR, delivery or SHORTLY AFTER. Amniotic fluid enters the maternal circulation, triggering a massive inflammatory reaction. It classically presents SUDDENLY with: ACUTE HYPOXIA (breathlessness, cyanosis, respiratory arrest), HYPOTENSION/cardiovascular collapse (often cardiac arrest), and COAGULOPATHY (DIC — severe bleeding). Seizures or confusion may also occur.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Gradual, mild shortness of breath over days — AFE is SUDDEN, not gradual over days.
• Isolated mild fever — Isolated mild fever is not a feature.
• Chronic hypertension — Chronic hypertension is unrelated.

⭐ Key point: Management is supportive and urgent: call for help, CPR, oxygen/ventilation, vasopressors, massive transfusion with clotting factors, and delivery of the baby.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q144$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2331, $s$Which action best prevents heat loss by evaporation in a newborn?$s$, $s$["Warming the crib pad", "Closing the doors to the room", "Drying the infant with a warm blanket", "Turning on the overhead radiant warmer"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Drying the infant with a warm blanket
Newborns lose heat in four ways: EVAPORATION (amniotic fluid evaporating from wet skin — the biggest loss immediately after birth), conduction (contact with cold surfaces), convection (cold air currents), and radiation (heat radiating to cold walls/objects). The best way to prevent EVAPORATIVE heat loss is to DRY THE INFANT thoroughly and immediately with a WARM BLANKET/towel (and then replace the wet towel with a dry one), and place the baby skin-to-skin with the mother, with a hat.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Warming the crib pad — Warming the crib pad prevents CONDUCTIVE heat loss.
• Closing the doors to the room — Closing doors prevents CONVECTIVE heat loss (draughts).
• Turning on the overhead radiant warmer — A radiant warmer reduces RADIANT heat loss.

⭐ Key point: Hypothermia in newborns causes hypoglycaemia, acidosis and breathing problems — keep the "warm chain".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q146$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2332, $s$First action in a precipitous delivery?$s$, $s$["Establish an airway for the newborn", "Arrange transport", "Ascertain condition of fundus", "Tie and cut cord"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Establish an airway for the newborn
In a PRECIPITOUS (very rapid, unplanned) DELIVERY, the FIRST action after the baby is born is to ESTABLISH AN AIRWAY for the newborn — clear the mouth and nose if needed (wipe or gentle suction), dry and stimulate the baby, and ensure breathing. Breathing is the most critical need for survival.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Arrange transport — Arranging transport can wait until the mother and baby are stable.
• Ascertain condition of fundus — Checking the fundus is important for the mother after the placenta is delivered, but the newborn's airway comes first.
• Tie and cut cord — Tying and cutting the cord is not urgent (delayed cord clamping is beneficial if the baby is well).

⭐ Key point: Keep the baby warm (skin-to-skin), and watch the mother for postpartum haemorrhage.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q148$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2333, $s$Pulse check location in an infant during CPR assessment is the:$s$, $s$["Carotid artery", "Radial artery", "Brachial artery", "Femoral artery"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Brachial artery
In an INFANT (under 1 year), the BRACHIAL ARTERY (on the inside of the upper arm, between elbow and shoulder) is used to check the pulse during CPR. The infant's neck is short and fat, making the carotid hard to feel.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Carotid artery — The carotid artery is used in children over 1 and adults.
• Radial artery — The radial artery is unreliable in shock and in infants.
• Femoral artery — The femoral artery can be used, but the brachial is standard.

⭐ Key point: Take no more than 10 seconds to check for a pulse; start CPR if not sure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q64$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2334, $s$Among the 4 Ts of PPH, 'Tissue' refers to:$s$, $s$["Retained placental tissue", "Uterine muscle tone", "Genital tract lacerations", "Clotting factor deficiency"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Retained placental tissue
The "4 Ts" of POSTPARTUM HAEMORRHAGE: TONE (uterine atony — the uterus fails to contract, the commonest cause), TRAUMA (tears of the perineum, vagina or cervix, uterine rupture or inversion), TISSUE — RETAINED PLACENTAL TISSUE, membranes or blood clots, which prevent the uterus from contracting properly, and THROMBIN (clotting disorders). Retained tissue is managed by manual removal or evacuation in theatre, along with uterotonics and antibiotics.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uterine muscle tone — Uterine muscle tone is "TONE", not tissue.
• Genital tract lacerations — Genital tract lacerations are "TRAUMA".
• Clotting factor deficiency — Clotting factor deficiency is "THROMBIN".

⭐ Key point: Always inspect the placenta and membranes after delivery to check they are complete — missing pieces suggest retained tissue.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q137 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2335, $s$ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in pregnancy-related hypertension mainly because they can cause:$s$, $s$["Maternal hair loss", "Fetal renal toxicity", "Improved placental blood flow", "No significant risk"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fetal renal toxicity
ACE INHIBITORS (e.g. lisinopril, enalapril, captopril) and ARBs are CONTRAINDICATED in pregnancy. They interfere with the fetal renin-angiotensin system, which the fetal kidneys need to develop and function. Effects (especially in the second and third trimesters): FETAL RENAL TOXICITY (kidney failure), OLIGOHYDRAMNIOS (low amniotic fluid, because fetal urine forms the fluid), fetal growth restriction, skull ossification defects, limb contractures, lung hypoplasia, and fetal or neonatal death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Maternal hair loss — Maternal hair loss is not the concern.
• Improved placental blood flow — ACE inhibitors do not improve placental blood flow.
• No significant risk — There IS significant risk to the fetus.

⭐ Key point: Safe antihypertensives in pregnancy: LABETALOL, NIFEDIPINE, METHYLDOPA and HYDRALAZINE. Women of childbearing age on ACE inhibitors should be advised to switch before or as soon as pregnancy is confirmed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q138$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2336, $s$Which may be life-saving in managing severe pre-eclampsia?$s$, $s$["Paracetamol for frontal headache in moderate disease", "Outpatient care when hyperreflexia appears", "Delivery, even at a very early gestational age", "Conservative management of imminent eclampsia at 29 weeks"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Delivery, even at a very early gestational age
The DEFINITIVE treatment of PRE-ECLAMPSIA is DELIVERY of the baby and placenta. In severe pre-eclampsia, delivery may be life-saving for the mother, even at a very early gestational age.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Paracetamol for frontal headache in moderate disease — Paracetamol for headache doesn't treat severe disease.
• Outpatient care when hyperreflexia appears — Hyperreflexia is a warning sign; outpatient care is unsafe.
• Conservative management of imminent eclampsia at 29 weeks — Imminent eclampsia requires stabilisation and delivery, not conservative management.

⭐ Key point: Give magnesium sulphate, control BP, and give steroids if preterm.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q139$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2337, $s$A patient with severe pre-eclampsia is started on magnesium sulfate. Before each dose/during the infusion, the nurse must assess:$s$, $s$["Only blood glucose", "Deep tendon reflexes, respiratory rate, and urine output ('TRU')", "Only skin colour", "Only pain score"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Deep tendon reflexes, respiratory rate, and urine output ('TRU')
MAGNESIUM SULPHATE prevents eclamptic seizures, but excess magnesium depresses nerves and muscles. Before each dose and hourly during the infusion, the nurse must check "TRU": T = TENDON REFLEXES (deep tendon/patellar reflexes — LOSS of reflexes is the FIRST sign of toxicity); R = RESPIRATORY RATE (stop if below 12/min — respiratory depression); U = URINE OUTPUT (magnesium is excreted by the kidneys, so output below 30 mL/hr allows it to build up). Also monitor SpO2, BP, consciousness and serum magnesium levels if available.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only blood glucose — Blood glucose is not the main safety check for magnesium.
• Only skin colour — Skin colour alone does not detect magnesium toxicity.
• Only pain score — Pain score is not a magnesium safety check.

⭐ Key point: If toxicity is suspected: stop the infusion, call for help, support breathing, and give the antidote — 10% calcium gluconate 10 mL IV over 10 minutes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q141$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2338, $s$Primary difference between morning sickness and hyperemesis gravidarum?$s$, $s$["Hyperemesis typically requires medical treatment", "Hyperemesis resolves after 1st trimester", "Morning sickness is more severe", "Morning sickness occurs only in the evening"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hyperemesis typically requires medical treatment
MORNING SICKNESS (nausea and vomiting of pregnancy) is common (up to 80% of pregnancies), usually mild, and settles by about 12–16 weeks; women can usually keep some food and fluids down. HYPEREMESIS GRAVIDARUM is a SEVERE form (about 1–2% of pregnancies): persistent vomiting causing DEHYDRATION, WEIGHT LOSS of more than 5% of pre-pregnancy weight, ketosis and electrolyte imbalance. It typically REQUIRES MEDICAL TREATMENT — IV fluids, electrolyte replacement, anti-emetics, thiamine, and often hospital admission.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperemesis resolves after 1st trimester — Hyperemesis can continue beyond the first trimester, sometimes throughout pregnancy.
• Morning sickness is more severe — Morning sickness is LESS severe than hyperemesis.
• Morning sickness occurs only in the evening — Morning sickness can occur at ANY time of day, despite its name.

⭐ Key point: Give thiamine before or with IV dextrose to prevent Wernicke's encephalopathy, and consider thromboprophylaxis in admitted women.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q142$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2339, $s$Preeclampsia is generally diagnosed by new-onset hypertension after 20 weeks gestation together with:$s$, $s$["Isolated mild headache only", "Proteinuria or other signs of end-organ dysfunction", "Normal blood pressure readings", "Glucose intolerance alone"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Proteinuria or other signs of end-organ dysfunction
PRE-ECLAMPSIA is defined as NEW-ONSET HYPERTENSION (BP ≥140/90 mmHg) after 20 WEEKS of pregnancy, together with PROTEINURIA (e.g. protein:creatinine ratio ≥30 mg/mmol) OR other signs of END-ORGAN DYSFUNCTION — such as kidney impairment, liver involvement (raised transaminases, right upper quadrant pain), neurological signs (severe headache, visual disturbance, hyperreflexia, clonus), low platelets/haemolysis (HELLP), pulmonary oedema, or placental dysfunction (fetal growth restriction).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Isolated mild headache only — A mild headache alone does not define pre-eclampsia.
• Normal blood pressure readings — Pre-eclampsia requires HIGH BP, not normal readings.
• Glucose intolerance alone — Glucose intolerance alone indicates gestational diabetes.

⭐ Key point: Severe pre-eclampsia: BP ≥160/110 with symptoms or lab abnormalities — needs magnesium sulphate, BP control and planning for delivery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q144$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2340, $s$When investigating a newborn with hyperbilirubinaemia, which bilirubin fractions should be measured?$s$, $s$["Conjugated bilirubin only", "Total bilirubin only", "Total and unconjugated bilirubin", "Total and conjugated bilirubin"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Total and conjugated bilirubin
In a newborn with jaundice, measuring both TOTAL and CONJUGATED (direct) BILIRUBIN is important. Most neonatal jaundice is UNCONJUGATED (e.g. physiological, breast milk, haemolysis). But a raised CONJUGATED bilirubin (more than about 25 µmol/L or more than 20% of total) is ALWAYS ABNORMAL and suggests liver disease or BILIARY ATRESIA, which needs urgent surgery (ideally before 6–8 weeks of age). Unconjugated bilirubin can be calculated as total minus conjugated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Conjugated bilirubin only — Conjugated bilirubin alone does not show the total level needed to decide phototherapy.
• Total bilirubin only — Total bilirubin alone can miss conjugated hyperbilirubinaemia.
• Total and unconjugated bilirubin — Unconjugated bilirubin is usually calculated, and does not identify cholestasis.

⭐ Key point: Pale stools and dark urine with prolonged jaundice (beyond 14 days in term babies) need urgent investigation for biliary atresia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q145$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2341, $s$A newborn is born with poor muscle tone and is not breathing. The nurse's first action, after ensuring warmth and position, should be:$s$, $s$["To administer chest compressions", "To intubate the newborn", "To provide positive-pressure ventilation", "To administer epinephrine"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: To provide positive-pressure ventilation
Most newborns who need resuscitation have a BREATHING problem. If the baby has POOR TONE and is NOT BREATHING after drying, warming and opening the airway (head in neutral position), the next step is POSITIVE-PRESSURE VENTILATION (PPV) — five inflation breaths with a bag-valve-mask or T-piece. Effective lung inflation usually raises the heart rate quickly. Assess chest movement and heart rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To administer chest compressions — Chest compressions are started only if the heart rate stays below 60/min despite 30 seconds of effective ventilation.
• To intubate the newborn — Intubation may be needed later, but mask ventilation is the first step.
• To administer epinephrine — Adrenaline is given only if the heart rate stays below 60/min despite effective ventilation and compressions.

⭐ Key point: "Airway, Breathing, Circulation, Drugs" — most newborns respond to effective ventilation alone.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q146$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2342, $s$Purpose of erythromycin ointment in neonates?$s$, $s$["Ophthalmia neonatorum", "Strabismus", "Cataracts", "Diabetes retinopathy"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ophthalmia neonatorum
ERYTHROMYCIN EYE OINTMENT is applied to newborns' eyes shortly after birth to PREVENT OPHTHALMIA NEONATORUM — a serious eye infection (conjunctivitis) caught from the mother's birth canal, mainly due to Neisseria gonorrhoeae (and Chlamydia). Untreated gonococcal ophthalmia can rapidly damage the cornea and cause BLINDNESS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Strabismus — Strabismus (squint) is a muscle alignment problem, not an infection.
• Cataracts — Cataracts are clouding of the lens.
• Diabetes retinopathy — Diabetic retinopathy affects adults with diabetes, not newborns.

⭐ Key point: Tetracycline ointment or povidone-iodine drops are alternatives. Treat mothers with STIs during pregnancy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q149$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2343, $s$Severe neonatal jaundice is primarily concerning because untreated high bilirubin levels can cause:$s$, $s$["No lasting harm regardless of level", "Kernicterus (bilirubin-induced brain damage)", "Improved neurological development", "Only mild, temporary skin discolouration with no other risk"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Kernicterus (bilirubin-induced brain damage)
SEVERE NEONATAL JAUNDICE (very high UNCONJUGATED bilirubin) is dangerous because bilirubin can cross the immature blood–brain barrier and damage the brain — KERNICTERUS (bilirubin encephalopathy), especially in the basal ganglia. Early signs: lethargy, poor feeding, high-pitched cry, hypotonia; later: arching (opisthotonus), seizures. Survivors may have athetoid cerebral palsy, deafness and learning problems.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No lasting harm regardless of level — High bilirubin can cause permanent harm.
• Improved neurological development — Bilirubin does not improve development.
• Only mild, temporary skin discolouration with no other risk — Severe jaundice is not just a skin colour change.

⭐ Key point: Use bilirubin charts to guide phototherapy and exchange transfusion. Risk is higher in preterm babies and with haemolysis (e.g. G6PD deficiency, ABO/Rh incompatibility).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q150$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2344, $s$A patient in labour has premature rupture of membranes with meconium-stained liquor. This finding is significant because it may indicate:$s$, $s$["Possible fetal distress, requiring closer fetal monitoring", "A sign of maternal infection only", "A completely normal, reassuring finding with no significance", "Definite fetal death, requiring no further action"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Possible fetal distress, requiring closer fetal monitoring
MECONIUM is the baby's first stool. If it passes into the amniotic fluid before birth (MECONIUM-STAINED LIQUOR — green or brown fluid), it can indicate FETAL DISTRESS: low oxygen causes the fetal bowel to contract and the anal sphincter to relax. It is more common in post-term babies. It also carries a risk of MECONIUM ASPIRATION SYNDROME if the baby inhales it. So CLOSER FETAL MONITORING (continuous CTG), assessment of labour progress, and having a neonatal team ready at delivery are needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A sign of maternal infection only — Meconium staining is not mainly a sign of maternal infection (although infection should also be considered with prolonged rupture).
• A completely normal, reassuring finding with no significance — It is not a reassuring finding; it needs attention.
• Definite fetal death, requiring no further action — It does not mean the fetus is dead; the fetal heart should be monitored.

⭐ Key point: Thick meconium is more concerning than thin, light staining. Prolonged rupture of membranes also increases the risk of infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q146$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2345, $s$A woman in active labour develops a sudden onset of severe abdominal pain, vaginal bleeding, and a change in fetal heart rate pattern to bradycardia after a previous caesarean section. This should raise suspicion for:$s$, $s$["Braxton Hicks contractions", "Uterine rupture at the site of the previous caesarean scar", "Simple round ligament pain", "Normal labour progress"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uterine rupture at the site of the previous caesarean scar
UTERINE RUPTURE is a tear in the uterine wall, most commonly through a PREVIOUS CAESAREAN SECTION SCAR during labour (VBAC — vaginal birth after caesarean). Classic features: SUDDEN SEVERE ABDOMINAL PAIN (may persist between contractions), VAGINAL BLEEDING, FETAL HEART RATE ABNORMALITIES (especially sudden BRADYCARDIA — the most common sign), loss of contractions, the presenting part moving back up, and signs of maternal shock.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Braxton Hicks contractions — Braxton Hicks contractions are irregular, painless or mildly uncomfortable "practice" contractions — not this.
• Simple round ligament pain — Round ligament pain is a mild, sharp groin pain in pregnancy, not associated with bleeding and fetal bradycardia.
• Normal labour progress — This is not normal labour progress.

⭐ Key point: Uterine rupture is an emergency: call for help, resuscitate the mother (large-bore IV, blood), and arrange immediate laparotomy to deliver the baby and repair or remove the uterus.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 5 · Q184$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2346, $s$What is the 24-hour maintenance fluid requirement for a child weighing 22 kg (Holliday–Segar method)?$s$, $s$["1,540 mL", "2,200 mL", "1,100 mL", "1,740 mL"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1,540 mL
HOLLIDAY–SEGAR method: 100 mL/kg for the FIRST 10 kg; 50 mL/kg for the NEXT 10 kg (11–20 kg); 20 mL/kg for EACH kg ABOVE 20 kg. For 22 kg: first 10 kg = 1,000 mL; next 10 kg = 500 mL; remaining 2 kg = 2 × 20 = 40 mL. Total: 1,000 + 500 + 40 = 1,540 mL in 24 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2,200 mL — 2,200 mL is 100 mL/kg for all 22 kg.
• 1,100 mL — 1,100 mL is 50 mL/kg for all 22 kg.
• 1,740 mL — 1,740 mL miscalculates the last part (2 × 120).

⭐ Key point: The hourly equivalent is the 4-2-1 rule: 40 + 20 + 2 = 62 mL/hr (≈ 1,488 mL/day — slightly different due to rounding).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q23 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2347, $s$How does a copper intrauterine device (IUD) mainly prevent pregnancy?$s$, $s$["By thinning the endometrium through hormone release", "By causing abortion of the implanted embryo", "By creating a uterine environment that is toxic to sperm, preventing fertilisation", "By inhibiting ovulation"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: By creating a uterine environment that is toxic to sperm, preventing fertilisation
The COPPER INTRAUTERINE DEVICE (IUD) is a small T-shaped device placed in the uterus. It releases COPPER IONS that cause a local inflammatory reaction in the uterus and fallopian tubes. This environment is TOXIC TO SPERM (reducing their movement and survival) and to the egg, so FERTILISATION is PREVENTED. It contains no hormones, so ovulation continues normally. It is over 99% effective and lasts up to 10–12 years.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• By thinning the endometrium through hormone release — Thinning the endometrium with hormones describes the LEVONORGESTREL (hormonal) IUD.
• By causing abortion of the implanted embryo — It does not cause abortion of an implanted embryo; it acts mainly before fertilisation.
• By inhibiting ovulation — It does not inhibit ovulation.

⭐ Key point: Side effects: heavier, more painful periods. Risks: expulsion, perforation (rare), and infection soon after insertion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q176$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2348, $s$If a woman becomes pregnant while using an IUD, which complication should be suspected and excluded?$s$, $s$["Twin pregnancy", "Pre-eclampsia", "Ectopic pregnancy", "Gestational diabetes"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ectopic pregnancy
IUDs prevent pregnancies in the UTERUS very effectively, but are LESS effective at preventing pregnancies in the FALLOPIAN TUBES. So if a woman DOES become pregnant with an IUD in place, there is a HIGHER PROPORTION of ECTOPIC (tubal) pregnancies. Ectopic pregnancy can rupture and cause life-threatening internal bleeding, so it must be suspected and EXCLUDED urgently (pregnancy test, transvaginal ultrasound).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Twin pregnancy — Twin pregnancy is not linked to IUD use.
• Pre-eclampsia — Pre-eclampsia is not linked to IUD use.
• Gestational diabetes — Gestational diabetes is not linked to IUD use.

⭐ Key point: If an intrauterine pregnancy is confirmed and the IUD strings are visible, the IUD should be removed early to reduce the risk of miscarriage and infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q179$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2349, $s$How long does one injection of DMPA (Depo-Provera, 150 mg IM) protect against pregnancy?$s$, $s$["6 months", "1 month", "3 months", "1 year"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 3 months
DMPA (DEPOT MEDROXYPROGESTERONE ACETATE, Depo-Provera), given as 150 mg by DEEP INTRAMUSCULAR injection, protects against pregnancy for 3 MONTHS (12–13 weeks), with a grace period of a few weeks for repeat injections. It works by stopping ovulation and thickening cervical mucus. There is also a subcutaneous version (DMPA-SC, Sayana Press, 104 mg) that women can self-inject, also every 3 months.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 6 months — 6 months is too long; protection would be lost.
• 1 month — 1 month is the duration of the MONTHLY combined injectable (e.g. Cyclofem).
• 1 year — 1 year is far too long.

⭐ Key point: Side effects: irregular bleeding or amenorrhoea, weight gain, delayed return of fertility (up to 10 months after stopping), and reduced bone density with long-term use.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q180$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2350, $s$A woman at 34 weeks has right upper quadrant pain, nausea and BP 150/100 mmHg. Bloods show haemolysis on the film, ALT 400 IU/L, platelets 60 × 10⁹/L, a normal glucose and normal clotting times. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["HELLP syndrome", "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura", "Acute fatty liver of pregnancy", "Obstetric cholestasis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: HELLP syndrome
HELLP SYNDROME is a severe form of pre-eclampsia: Haemolysis (fragmented red cells on blood film), Elevated Liver enzymes (ALT 400), Low Platelets (60). Typical features: RIGHT UPPER QUADRANT PAIN (liver swelling), nausea, and high BP (150/100). Glucose and clotting are usually NORMAL (unless DIC develops).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura — TTP causes neurological symptoms and fever; not linked to pregnancy BP rise.
• Acute fatty liver of pregnancy — Acute fatty liver of pregnancy causes LOW glucose and prolonged clotting.
• Obstetric cholestasis — Obstetric cholestasis causes itching, especially on the palms and soles.

⭐ Key point: The definitive treatment for HELLP is DELIVERY. Also give magnesium sulphate, control BP, and monitor platelets and liver function.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q161$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2351, $s$A woman with severe pre-eclampsia is on a magnesium sulphate infusion. Which finding means the nurse should stop the infusion and call the doctor FIRST?$s$, $s$["Serum magnesium of 2.5 mmol/L", "Urine output of 35 mL/h", "Absent patellar (knee-jerk) reflexes", "Respiratory rate of 14/min"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Absent patellar (knee-jerk) reflexes
MAGNESIUM SULPHATE is used in pre-eclampsia, but too much is toxic. The FIRST sign of toxicity is LOSS OF PATELLAR (KNEE-JERK) REFLEXES (at about 3.5–5 mmol/L). The nurse should STOP THE INFUSION and call the doctor. If toxicity worsens, respiratory depression and cardiac arrest can follow.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Serum magnesium of 2.5 mmol/L — A serum magnesium of 2.5 mmol/L is within the therapeutic range (2–3.5).
• Urine output of 35 mL/h — A urine output of 35 mL/h is acceptable (concern if below 30 mL/h).
• Respiratory rate of 14/min — A respiratory rate of 14/min is acceptable (concern if below 12/min).

⭐ Key point: Monitor reflexes, respiratory rate, SpO2 and urine output hourly. Antidote: 10% calcium gluconate 10 mL IV over 10 minutes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q163$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2352, $s$During labour, shortly after artificial rupture of membranes, a woman suddenly becomes breathless and cyanosed, collapses with severe hypotension and then has a cardiac arrest. Those who survive the first hour develop severe bleeding from DIC. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Amniotic fluid embolism", "Placental abruption", "Eclampsia", "Massive pulmonary thromboembolism"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Amniotic fluid embolism
AMNIOTIC FLUID EMBOLISM (AFE) is a rare, catastrophic emergency during labour or shortly after (often after rupture of membranes). Amniotic fluid enters the mother's blood, triggering a massive inflammatory reaction. Features: SUDDEN BREATHLESSNESS and CYANOSIS, COLLAPSE with SEVERE HYPOTENSION, CARDIAC ARREST, and in survivors, severe BLEEDING from DIC.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Placental abruption — Placental abruption causes abdominal pain and bleeding, but not usually sudden cardiorespiratory collapse.
• Eclampsia — Eclampsia causes seizures, often with high BP.
• Massive pulmonary thromboembolism — Massive PE causes collapse, but not usually DIC.

⭐ Key point: Treatment is supportive: CPR, oxygen, vasopressors, blood products for DIC, and delivery of the baby.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q164$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2353, $s$At 33 weeks a woman with pre-eclampsia has sudden constant abdominal pain and slight dark vaginal bleeding. The uterus is hard, 'woody' and tender, the fetal heart rate is 90/min, and she is in shock out of proportion to the visible blood loss. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Ruptured vasa praevia", "Uterine rupture", "Major placenta praevia", "Concealed placental abruption"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Concealed placental abruption
PLACENTAL ABRUPTION is separation of the placenta from the uterine wall before delivery. In CONCEALED abruption, the blood is trapped behind the placenta, so visible bleeding is SMALL, but the woman is in SHOCK OUT OF PROPORTION to the visible blood loss. Features: SUDDEN CONSTANT ABDOMINAL PAIN, a HARD, "WOODY", TENDER UTERUS, and FETAL DISTRESS (heart rate 90). PRE-ECLAMPSIA is a major risk factor.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ruptured vasa praevia — Vasa praevia causes painless bleeding when the membranes rupture, with fetal distress.
• Uterine rupture — Uterine rupture usually occurs during labour, often with a previous caesarean scar.
• Major placenta praevia — Placenta praevia causes PAINLESS bright red bleeding with a soft uterus.

⭐ Key point: Resuscitate the mother (IV access, blood), monitor for DIC, and deliver urgently.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q165$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2354, $s$A woman attempting a vaginal birth after a previous caesarean section suddenly has severe abdominal pain. Contractions stop, the fetal heart rate drops to 70/min, the presenting part moves back up, and fetal parts are easily felt through the abdomen. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Placenta praevia", "Uterine rupture", "Placental abruption", "Normal second stage of labour"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uterine rupture
UTERINE RUPTURE is a tear in the uterine wall, most commonly through a PREVIOUS CAESAREAN SCAR during labour (VBAC). Features: SUDDEN SEVERE ABDOMINAL PAIN, CONTRACTIONS STOP, FETAL BRADYCARDIA (70), the PRESENTING PART MOVES BACK UP, and FETAL PARTS EASILY FELT through the abdomen (the baby is outside the uterus).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Placenta praevia — Placenta praevia causes painless bleeding, often before labour.
• Placental abruption — Placental abruption causes a hard, woody uterus, with contractions continuing.
• Normal second stage of labour — These are not signs of a normal second stage.

⭐ Key point: Uterine rupture is an emergency: resuscitate, call for help, and perform immediate laparotomy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q166$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2355, $s$A woman has a postpartum haemorrhage from uterine atony. Which uterotonic is contraindicated because she has pre-eclampsia?$s$, $s$["Misoprostol", "Oxytocin", "Ergometrine", "Carboprost"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ergometrine
ERGOMETRINE (an ergot alkaloid, also in the combination drug Syntometrine) makes the uterus contract strongly and for a long time, which helps stop bleeding from uterine atony. However, it also CONSTRICTS BLOOD VESSELS throughout the body (alpha-adrenergic and serotonin effects), which can cause a SUDDEN, SEVERE RISE IN BP. In a woman with PRE-ECLAMPSIA (whose BP is already high and whose blood vessels are already irritable), this can trigger a STROKE (brain haemorrhage), ECLAMPTIC SEIZURES or pulmonary oedema. So ergometrine is CONTRAINDICATED in pre-eclampsia, hypertension and heart disease.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Misoprostol — Misoprostol (a prostaglandin E1) does not raise BP significantly; it can be used in pre-eclampsia (side effects: fever, shivering, diarrhoea).
• Oxytocin — Oxytocin is the FIRST-LINE uterotonic for PPH and is safe in pre-eclampsia (give as a slow IV bolus or infusion; rapid boluses can cause hypotension).
• Carboprost — Carboprost (prostaglandin F2-alpha, given IM) can be used in pre-eclampsia, but must be AVOIDED in ASTHMA, as it causes bronchospasm.

⭐ Key point: PPH drug sequence: oxytocin → ergometrine (if no hypertension) → carboprost / misoprostol, plus tranexamic acid. If drugs fail: balloon tamponade, then surgery (B-Lynch suture, uterine artery ligation, hysterectomy).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q167$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2356, $s$A term baby born by elective caesarean section (no labour) is breathing at 80/min with mild recession and needs little oxygen. Chest X-ray shows fluid in the horizontal fissure and prominent vascular markings. Symptoms settle over 48 hours. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Transient tachypnoea of the newborn", "Respiratory distress syndrome", "Group B streptococcal pneumonia", "Meconium aspiration syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Transient tachypnoea of the newborn
TRANSIENT TACHYPNOEA OF THE NEWBORN (TTN) occurs when FLUID in the baby's lungs is cleared slowly after birth, especially after ELECTIVE CAESAREAN SECTION (no labour — the hormonal and physical squeeze of labour helps clear lung fluid). Features: FAST BREATHING (80/min), mild recession, mild oxygen need. Chest X-ray: FLUID IN THE HORIZONTAL FISSURE and prominent vascular markings. Resolves within 24–72 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Respiratory distress syndrome — RDS occurs in preterm babies and shows a ground-glass appearance.
• Group B streptococcal pneumonia — GBS pneumonia causes sepsis signs and worsening, not improvement.
• Meconium aspiration syndrome — Meconium aspiration occurs with meconium-stained liquor.

⭐ Key point: Management is supportive: oxygen as needed and monitoring.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q172$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2357, $s$A 9-month-old has episodes of screaming with knees drawn up and pallor every 15–20 minutes, sleepy between attacks. He then passes stool mixed with blood and mucus ('redcurrant jelly'), and a sausage-shaped mass is felt in the right upper abdomen. The diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Acute gastroenteritis", "Infantile colic", "Intussusception", "Meckel's diverticulum"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intussusception
INTUSSUSCEPTION is when one part of the bowel telescopes into the next, usually in infants aged 6–18 months. Features: EPISODES OF SCREAMING with KNEES DRAWN UP and pallor every 15–20 minutes, SLEEPY BETWEEN attacks, "REDCURRANT JELLY" stools (blood and mucus), and a SAUSAGE-SHAPED MASS in the right upper abdomen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute gastroenteritis — Gastroenteritis causes diarrhoea and vomiting without a mass.
• Infantile colic — Colic is crying in young babies, without blood or mass.
• Meckel's diverticulum — Meckel's diverticulum causes painless rectal bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Treat with air or contrast enema reduction if no peritonitis; surgery if failed or perforated.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q175$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2358, $s$A 3-year-old was diagnosed with croup 2 days ago but is now much worse, with high fever, a toxic appearance, copious thick purulent secretions from the trachea and stridor that does not improve with nebulised adrenaline. The most likely diagnosis is:$s$, $s$["Viral croup (laryngotracheobronchitis)", "Bacterial tracheitis", "Inhaled foreign body", "Acute epiglottitis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bacterial tracheitis
BACTERIAL TRACHEITIS is a serious bacterial infection of the trachea (often Staphylococcus aureus), usually following a viral illness (e.g. croup). Features: the child WORSENS after initially having croup, with HIGH FEVER, a TOXIC appearance, COPIOUS THICK PURULENT SECRETIONS from the trachea, and STRIDOR that DOES NOT IMPROVE with nebulised adrenaline.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Viral croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) — Viral croup improves with nebulised adrenaline and steroids.
• Inhaled foreign body — An inhaled foreign body causes sudden choking, without high fever.
• Acute epiglottitis — Epiglottitis causes drooling and sitting forward, often without a cough.

⭐ Key point: Treat with IV antibiotics and often intubation with frequent tracheal suctioning.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q176$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2359, $s$A 10 kg, 8-month-old infant has severe dehydration from diarrhoea (lethargic, sunken eyes, drinks poorly, very slow skin pinch). By WHO Plan C, how much Ringer's lactate should be given in the FIRST hour?$s$, $s$["300 mL (30 mL/kg)", "1,000 mL (100 mL/kg)", "700 mL (70 mL/kg)", "200 mL (20 mL/kg)"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 300 mL (30 mL/kg)
WHO PLAN C for SEVERE DEHYDRATION: in INFANTS UNDER 12 MONTHS, give IV Ringer's lactate 30 mL/kg in the FIRST HOUR, then 70 mL/kg over the next 5 HOURS. For a 10 kg infant: 30 × 10 = 300 mL in the first hour.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1,000 mL (100 mL/kg) — 1,000 mL (100 mL/kg) is the total over 6 hours, not the first hour.
• 700 mL (70 mL/kg) — 700 mL (70 mL/kg) is the amount for the next 5 hours.
• 200 mL (20 mL/kg) — 200 mL (20 mL/kg) is the standard shock bolus, not WHO Plan C.

⭐ Key point: For children over 12 months: 30 mL/kg in 30 minutes, then 70 mL/kg over 2.5 hours. Reassess often.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q177$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2360, $s$The main indication for exchange blood transfusion in a newborn is:$s$, $s$["Mild physiological jaundice on day 3", "Severe jaundice despite intensive phototherapy", "Uncomplicated prematurity below 34 weeks", "Neonatal hypoglycaemia not responding to feeds"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Severe jaundice despite intensive phototherapy
EXCHANGE TRANSFUSION is used mainly for SEVERE JAUNDICE (very high unconjugated bilirubin) that does not respond to INTENSIVE PHOTOTHERAPY, or shows signs of brain damage (acute bilirubin encephalopathy). It removes bilirubin and antibodies (e.g. Rh disease) and corrects anaemia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild physiological jaundice on day 3 — Mild physiological jaundice resolves on its own or with phototherapy.
• Uncomplicated prematurity below 34 weeks — Prematurity alone is not an indication.
• Neonatal hypoglycaemia not responding to feeds — Hypoglycaemia is treated with IV dextrose.

⭐ Key point: Use bilirubin charts for the baby's age and risk factors to decide on treatment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q81$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2361, $s$A double-volume exchange transfusion in a neonate uses about:$s$, $s$["40–60 mL/kg of blood", "300–350 mL/kg of blood", "80–90 mL/kg of blood", "160–180 mL/kg of blood"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 160–180 mL/kg of blood
An EXCHANGE TRANSFUSION removes the baby's blood in small amounts and replaces it with donor blood. A term newborn's blood volume is about 80–90 mL per kg. A "DOUBLE-VOLUME" exchange replaces TWICE this volume: 2 × 80–90 = 160–180 mL/kg. For example, for a 3 kg baby: 3 × 170 = about 510 mL in total. A double-volume exchange removes about 85–90% of the baby's red cells (including antibody-coated cells in haemolytic disease) and about 50% of the circulating bilirubin. Bilirubin also moves out of the tissues back into the blood afterwards, so levels may rebound and must be rechecked.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 40–60 mL/kg of blood — 40–60 mL/kg is less than even a single blood volume, so it would remove too little bilirubin and too few antibody-coated cells.
• 300–350 mL/kg of blood — 300–350 mL/kg is almost four times the baby's blood volume; it is unnecessary and increases risks (e.g. electrolyte problems, prolonged procedure).
• 80–90 mL/kg of blood — 80–90 mL/kg is a SINGLE-volume exchange (one blood volume). It is used for some conditions (e.g. polycythaemia uses a partial exchange) but not for a double-volume exchange.

⭐ Key point: Monitor during exchange: heart rate, breathing, temperature, blood glucose, calcium and potassium. Record each aliquot removed and given, so the running total is always known.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q82$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2362, $s$During exchange transfusion, blood is removed and replaced in small aliquots mainly to:$s$, $s$["Allow more bilirubin to be removed", "Avoid sudden swings in blood volume", "Speed up the whole procedure", "Reduce the amount of donor blood used"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Avoid sudden swings in blood volume
Removing and replacing blood in SMALL ALIQUOTS (e.g. 5–10 mL/kg at a time) avoids SUDDEN SWINGS IN BLOOD VOLUME. Large swings can cause changes in BP, heart rate and brain blood flow, risking heart failure or brain bleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Allow more bilirubin to be removed — Small aliquots are mainly for safety.
• Speed up the whole procedure — Small aliquots make the procedure slower.
• Reduce the amount of donor blood used — The amount of donor blood depends on the total volume needed.

⭐ Key point: The procedure takes 1–2 hours. Monitor vital signs, glucose and calcium closely.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q83$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2363, $s$A complication of exchange transfusion that the nurse must monitor for is:$s$, $s$["Polycythaemia from excess donor cells", "Hypercalcaemia from the stored blood", "Persistent hyperglycaemia for days", "Hypocalcaemia from citrate, with jitteriness"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypocalcaemia from citrate, with jitteriness
Stored donor blood contains CITRATE, which is added as an anticoagulant. Citrate works by BINDING CALCIUM in the blood. During an exchange transfusion, a large amount of citrated blood is given to a very small baby, so the baby's ionised calcium can drop: HYPOCALCAEMIA. Signs in a newborn include JITTERINESS, irritability, tremors, twitching, seizures, apnoea, and prolonged QT or arrhythmias on the ECG. Calcium gluconate may be given slowly IV, with ECG monitoring, if hypocalcaemia occurs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Polycythaemia from excess donor cells — Polycythaemia does not occur because the volume taken out equals the volume given in; the aim is to keep the blood volume stable.
• Hypercalcaemia from the stored blood — Citrate LOWERS calcium; it does not cause hypercalcaemia.
• Persistent hyperglycaemia for days — Donor blood contains glucose, which can cause a brief rise in glucose, but the bigger danger after exchange is rebound HYPOglycaemia (the baby's insulin rises). Persistent hyperglycaemia for days is not typical.

⭐ Key point: Other complications of exchange transfusion: hypoglycaemia, hyperkalaemia, thrombocytopenia, infection, air embolism, portal vein thrombosis, necrotising enterocolitis, and cardiac arrhythmias.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q84$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2364, $s$Blood for neonatal exchange transfusion should ideally be:$s$, $s$["Taken from a parent to reduce reactions", "Over 3 weeks old to reduce potassium", "Fresh, irradiated, CMV-negative and warmed", "Cold from the fridge to reduce bilirubin"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fresh, irradiated, CMV-negative and warmed
Blood for neonatal exchange transfusion should be: FRESH (less than 5 days old — lower potassium and better quality), IRRADIATED (prevents graft-versus-host disease), CMV-NEGATIVE (prevents infection), and WARMED (prevents hypothermia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Taken from a parent to reduce reactions — Parental blood may carry antibodies causing the problem (e.g. Rh or ABO incompatibility).
• Over 3 weeks old to reduce potassium — Older blood has more potassium and fewer working cells.
• Cold from the fridge to reduce bilirubin — Cold blood causes hypothermia and arrhythmias.

⭐ Key point: For Rh disease, use O Rh-negative blood cross-matched against the mother.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q85$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2365, $s$Kernicterus is caused by deposition of unconjugated bilirubin in the:$s$, $s$["Bone marrow precursors", "Basal ganglia of the brain", "Proximal renal tubules", "Hepatocytes of the liver"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Basal ganglia of the brain
KERNICTERUS means "yellow nucleus". It occurs when very high levels of UNCONJUGATED (fat-soluble) BILIRUBIN cross the blood–brain barrier and stain and damage parts of the brain, especially the BASAL GANGLIA (particularly the globus pallidus), the brainstem nuclei (including the auditory and eye-movement nuclei), and the cerebellum. The early stage (acute bilirubin encephalopathy) shows lethargy, poor sucking, hypotonia and a high-pitched cry; later, there is arching (opisthotonus), fever and seizures. Survivors may have permanent ATHETOID CEREBRAL PALSY, sensorineural DEAFNESS, upward gaze palsy, and dental enamel problems.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bone marrow precursors — Bilirubin does not damage the bone marrow precursors to cause kernicterus.
• Proximal renal tubules — Bilirubin can be excreted in urine in its conjugated form, but kernicterus is a BRAIN condition, not a kidney one.
• Hepatocytes of the liver — The liver processes bilirubin (conjugation), but kernicterus is defined by BRAIN damage, not liver damage.

⭐ Key point: Risk is higher in preterm babies, sick babies (sepsis, acidosis, hypoxia), and with very rapid bilirubin rises (e.g. haemolysis from Rh or ABO incompatibility or G6PD deficiency — common in Nigeria). Treat early with phototherapy or exchange transfusion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q86$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2366, $s$In neonatal resuscitation, the most important and effective action is:$s$, $s$["Giving sodium bicarbonate", "Immediate chest compressions", "Effective ventilation of the lungs", "Giving adrenaline through a UVC"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Effective ventilation of the lungs
Most newborns needing resuscitation have a breathing problem, not a heart problem. EFFECTIVE VENTILATION of the lungs is the single most important and effective action. It usually brings the heart rate up quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Giving sodium bicarbonate — Sodium bicarbonate is rarely used.
• Immediate chest compressions — Chest compressions are only needed if the heart rate is below 60 despite effective ventilation.
• Giving adrenaline through a UVC — Adrenaline is rarely needed if ventilation is effective.

⭐ Key point: Check chest movement — if the chest doesn't move, ventilation is not effective.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q87$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2367, $s$A nurse is performing neonatal resuscitation. The newborn's heart rate is below 60 beats per minute after 30 seconds of effective positive-pressure ventilation. The next step is to:$s$, $s$["Administer epinephrine", "Continue positive-pressure ventilation for another 30 seconds", "Initiate chest compressions", "Stop resuscitation and call the physician"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Initiate chest compressions
If the heart rate stays BELOW 60 per minute after 30 seconds of EFFECTIVE positive-pressure ventilation, START CHEST COMPRESSIONS, coordinated with ventilation (3:1 ratio). Increase oxygen to 100%.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer epinephrine — Adrenaline is given only if heart rate stays below 60 after compressions and ventilation.
• Continue positive-pressure ventilation for another 30 seconds — Continuing ventilation alone is insufficient.
• Stop resuscitation and call the physician — Stopping resuscitation is wrong; the baby needs active help.

⭐ Key point: Use the two-thumb technique for compressions on the lower third of the sternum.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q88 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2368, $s$Early-onset neonatal sepsis (within 72 hours) is most commonly caused by:$s$, $s$["Pseudomonas from ventilators", "Staphylococcus epidermidis from lines", "Candida albicans", "Group B Streptococcus and E. coli"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Group B Streptococcus and E. coli
EARLY-ONSET NEONATAL SEPSIS occurs within the first 72 hours of life (some definitions use 7 days). The baby is usually infected by organisms from the MOTHER'S birth canal — before or during delivery, especially when the membranes have been ruptured for a long time or the mother has chorioamnionitis. The most common organisms are GROUP B STREPTOCOCCUS (GBS) and ESCHERICHIA COLI. Listeria monocytogenes and Klebsiella are other causes. In many low-income settings, including Nigeria, gram-negative organisms (Klebsiella, E. coli) and Staphylococcus aureus are very important too.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pseudomonas from ventilators — Pseudomonas from ventilators causes LATE-onset (hospital-acquired) infection, after the baby has been in the unit for some time.
• Staphylococcus epidermidis from lines — Staphylococcus epidermidis (coagulase-negative staph) from IV lines is a common cause of LATE-onset sepsis in preterm babies with central lines.
• Candida albicans — Candida albicans causes late-onset fungal sepsis, mainly in very preterm babies on long-term antibiotics and central lines.

⭐ Key point: Late-onset sepsis (after 72 hours) is usually caught from the hospital environment or community. Hand hygiene is the main prevention.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q91$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2369, $s$Maternal risk factors for early-onset neonatal sepsis include:$s$, $s$["Maternal age under 25 years with a first pregnancy", "Planned caesarean section before the onset of labour", "Maternal hypertension that is controlled with drugs", "Membranes ruptured over 18 hours, with maternal fever"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Membranes ruptured over 18 hours, with maternal fever
Maternal risk factors for early-onset sepsis include: PROLONGED RUPTURE OF MEMBRANES (over 18 hours), MATERNAL FEVER (intrapartum fever, chorioamnionitis), maternal GBS carriage, and preterm labour.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Maternal age under 25 years with a first pregnancy — Young maternal age is not a major risk factor.
• Planned caesarean section before the onset of labour — Planned caesarean before labour has a lower risk.
• Maternal hypertension that is controlled with drugs — Controlled hypertension isn't a major risk factor.

⭐ Key point: Give the mother intrapartum antibiotics if she is a GBS carrier.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q92$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2370, $s$A neonate with sepsis may present with:$s$, $s$["Hypertension with bradycardia and a loud cry", "Unstable temperature, apnoea, poor feeding, lethargy", "Localised neck stiffness with a bulging fontanelle", "High-grade fever with rigors and sweating only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Unstable temperature, apnoea, poor feeding, lethargy
Newborns, especially preterm babies, do not show the "typical" signs of infection seen in older children. Their signs of SEPSIS are often VAGUE and NON-SPECIFIC: TEMPERATURE INSTABILITY (fever OR, very commonly, HYPOTHERMIA), APNOEA (pauses in breathing) and breathing difficulty, POOR FEEDING or feed intolerance, LETHARGY or floppiness, irritability, jaundice, vomiting, abdominal distension, poor colour or mottling, tachycardia or bradycardia, and abnormal glucose levels. Any newborn who "is not doing well" should be assessed for sepsis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension with bradycardia and a loud cry — Newborns with sepsis more often show low BP and tachycardia or bradycardia from shock. Hypertension with a loud cry is not typical.
• Localised neck stiffness with a bulging fontanelle — Neck stiffness is uncommon in newborns even with meningitis; a bulging fontanelle is a late sign. These are not the usual presenting signs of neonatal sepsis.
• High-grade fever with rigors and sweating only — Newborns often cannot mount a high fever with rigors and sweating; they are more likely to have a LOW temperature.

⭐ Key point: Have a low threshold for doing a septic screen (blood culture, FBC, CRP, glucose, and lumbar puncture if stable) and starting IV antibiotics in an unwell newborn.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q93$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2371, $s$Daily maintenance fluid for a term neonate on day 1 is about:$s$, $s$["20 mL/kg/day until the baby passes urine", "150 mL/kg/day from the first hour of life", "60 mL/kg/day, increasing through the first week", "200 mL/kg/day to prevent jaundice"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 60 mL/kg/day, increasing through the first week
Immediately after birth, a newborn has extra body water that it needs to lose over the first few days, and its kidneys are immature. So fluid intake starts LOW and increases gradually. A term neonate on DAY 1 needs about 60 mL/kg/day, increasing by about 20–30 mL/kg/day to reach about 150 mL/kg/day by days 5–7. Example: a 3 kg baby on day 1 needs about 180 mL in 24 hours (about 7.5 mL per hour). A normal weight loss of up to about 10% in the first week is expected.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20 mL/kg/day until the baby passes urine — 20 mL/kg/day is far too little and would cause dehydration and hypoglycaemia.
• 150 mL/kg/day from the first hour of life — 150 mL/kg/day from the first hour would overload a newborn with too much fluid (risk of heart failure, patent ductus arteriosus, and NEC).
• 200 mL/kg/day to prevent jaundice — 200 mL/kg/day is excessive and does not prevent jaundice; it would cause fluid overload.

⭐ Key point: Monitor fluid balance by daily weights, urine output (normal 1–3 mL/kg/hr), serum sodium and blood glucose. Preterm babies and those under radiant warmers or phototherapy may need more fluid.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q95$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2372, $s$The usual IV fluid for neonates in the first 24 hours is:$s$, $s$["0.9% sodium chloride", "5% albumin", "10% dextrose", "Hartmann's solution with potassium"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10% dextrose
In the first 24 hours, a newborn's main need is GLUCOSE to prevent hypoglycaemia (the baby has lost the continuous supply from the placenta and has limited glycogen stores). 10% DEXTROSE at 60 mL/kg/day provides about 4 mg/kg/min of glucose — close to the newborn's natural glucose production. Sodium and potassium are usually NOT added in the first 24–48 hours, because the newborn already has extra body water and sodium, and potassium may be high in the first days; they are added once urine output is established and the weight starts falling.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.9% sodium chloride — 0.9% sodium chloride has no glucose and too much sodium for routine newborn maintenance; it is used for fluid boluses in shock.
• 5% albumin — 5% albumin is a colloid used in special cases (e.g. some forms of shock); it is not a maintenance fluid.
• Hartmann's solution with potassium — Hartmann's solution with potassium has no glucose and contains potassium, which may be dangerous in the first day before urine output is established.

⭐ Key point: Check blood glucose regularly; aim to keep it above 2.6 mmol/L (about 47 mg/dL). If high concentrations (above 12.5%) are needed, a central line is used because they damage peripheral veins.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q96$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2373, $s$Trophic (minimal enteral) feeding in a sick preterm neonate is given to:$s$, $s$["Remove the need for any IV fluids at all", "Treat neonatal jaundice by binding bilirubin", "Provide all of the baby's nutritional needs", "Stimulate gut maturation and improve tolerance"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stimulate gut maturation and improve tolerance
TROPHIC FEEDING (also called minimal enteral or "gut priming" feeding) gives very small amounts of milk (usually 10–20 mL/kg/day, ideally breast milk) to a sick or preterm baby whose nutrition mainly comes from IV fluids. These small feeds are not meant to provide nutrition. Instead, they STIMULATE GUT MATURATION — encouraging the release of gut hormones, improving gut movement (motility), maintaining the gut lining, and encouraging healthy gut bacteria. Babies who receive trophic feeds tolerate full feeds sooner and need fewer days on IV nutrition.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Remove the need for any IV fluids at all — Trophic feeds are too small to replace IV fluids; the baby still needs IV fluids or parenteral nutrition.
• Treat neonatal jaundice by binding bilirubin — Trophic feeds do not treat jaundice. (Feeding does help bilirubin excretion in general, but that is not the purpose of trophic feeding.)
• Provide all of the baby's nutritional needs — Trophic feeds provide only a tiny fraction of nutritional needs; full nutrition comes from IV nutrition until feeds are increased.

⭐ Key point: Breast milk (mother's own or donor milk) reduces the risk of necrotising enterocolitis. Watch for feed intolerance: vomiting, abdominal distension, bile-stained aspirates or blood in the stool.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q97$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2374, $s$Sudden infant death syndrome risk is reduced by:$s$, $s$["Bed-sharing with parents who smoke at night", "Sleeping prone to prevent aspiration of vomit", "Back to sleep, no smoke exposure, no overheating", "Soft pillows and heavy blankets in the cot"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Back to sleep, no smoke exposure, no overheating
SUDDEN INFANT DEATH SYNDROME (SIDS) is the sudden, unexplained death of an apparently healthy baby, usually during sleep, mostly in the first 6 months. The most effective prevention measures are: placing the baby on the BACK to sleep ("Back to Sleep") for every sleep; NO SMOKE EXPOSURE (during pregnancy and after birth); avoiding OVERHEATING (no heavy wrapping, hats indoors, or very warm rooms); a firm flat mattress with no pillows, bumpers, toys or loose blankets; and room-sharing (the baby in their own cot in the parents' room) for the first 6 months.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bed-sharing with parents who smoke at night — Bed-sharing, especially with parents who smoke, drink alcohol or take sedating drugs, greatly INCREASES the risk of SIDS and suffocation.
• Sleeping prone to prevent aspiration of vomit — Sleeping prone (on the tummy) INCREASES SIDS risk. Healthy babies on their backs do not choke on vomit; their airway reflexes protect them.
• Soft pillows and heavy blankets in the cot — Soft pillows and heavy blankets INCREASE the risk of suffocation and overheating.

⭐ Key point: Breastfeeding, a dummy (pacifier) at sleep time, and complete immunisation also reduce SIDS risk. Nurses should model safe sleep in hospital so parents copy it at home.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q98$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2375, $s$A premature neonate is at risk of retinopathy of prematurity mainly because of:$s$, $s$["Feeding with expressed breast milk", "Phototherapy for neonatal jaundice", "High or fluctuating oxygen levels", "Repeated low blood glucose levels"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: High or fluctuating oxygen levels
RETINOPATHY OF PREMATURITY (ROP) is abnormal growth of blood vessels in the retina of preterm babies. In the womb the retina develops in a low-oxygen environment. When a very preterm baby is exposed to HIGH oxygen levels, normal vessel growth stops; later, the retina becomes short of oxygen and produces growth factors (VEGF) that cause abnormal, fragile new vessels. These can bleed, scar and pull the retina off (retinal detachment), causing BLINDNESS. HIGH and FLUCTUATING (up-and-down) oxygen levels are the main modifiable risk factors. The risk is highest in babies born before 32 weeks or under 1500 g.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Feeding with expressed breast milk — Breast milk actually REDUCES the risk of ROP; it is not a cause.
• Phototherapy for neonatal jaundice — Phototherapy does not cause ROP.
• Repeated low blood glucose levels — Hypoglycaemia is not the main cause of ROP.

⭐ Key point: Use oxygen like a drug: set SpO2 alarm limits (usually a target around 91–95%), avoid unnecessary high oxygen, and arrange ROP eye screening for at-risk babies at about 4–6 weeks after birth (or 31–32 weeks corrected age).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q99$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2376, $s$Severe neonatal hypothermia increases the risk of:$s$, $s$["Increased surfactant production", "Hyperglycaemia and metabolic alkalosis", "Hypertension and polyuria", "Hypoglycaemia, acidosis and respiratory distress"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypoglycaemia, acidosis and respiratory distress
A cold newborn tries to make heat by burning BROWN FAT (non-shivering thermogenesis — newborns cannot shiver effectively). This uses large amounts of OXYGEN and GLUCOSE. As a result: glucose is used up quickly (HYPOGLYCAEMIA); oxygen demand rises while cold-induced vasoconstriction reduces tissue perfusion, so cells switch to anaerobic metabolism and produce lactic acid (METABOLIC ACIDOSIS); and acidosis with hypoxia narrows the lung blood vessels and reduces surfactant production, leading to RESPIRATORY DISTRESS. Severe hypothermia also increases the risk of infection, bleeding (including in the brain), NEC and death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increased surfactant production — Cold REDUCES surfactant production; it does not increase it.
• Hyperglycaemia and metabolic alkalosis — Hypothermia causes LOW glucose and metabolic ACIDOSIS, not high glucose and alkalosis.
• Hypertension and polyuria — Hypothermia does not typically cause hypertension and polyuria; severe cold causes bradycardia, poor perfusion and reduced urine output.

⭐ Key point: Prevent hypothermia with the "warm chain": warm delivery room, dry the baby immediately, skin-to-skin contact, a cap, delayed bathing, early breastfeeding, and warm transport. For very preterm babies, a plastic wrap/bag at birth helps. Rewarm hypothermic babies gradually while monitoring glucose.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q100$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2377, $s$A 9-month-old has paroxysmal colic, drawing up of the legs, vomiting and 'redcurrant jelly' stools. The first-line treatment if there is no peritonitis is:$s$, $s$["Antibiotics and observation for 48 hours", "Air or contrast enema reduction under imaging", "Immediate laparotomy and resection", "Oral laxatives"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Air or contrast enema reduction under imaging
These are the classic features of INTUSSUSCEPTION (one segment of bowel telescoping into the next) in an infant aged 6–18 months: sudden attacks of colicky pain with drawing up of the legs and crying, vomiting, and "REDCURRANT JELLY" stools (blood and mucus from the swollen bowel lining). If there are NO signs of PERITONITIS or PERFORATION, and the child is resuscitated and stable, the first-line treatment is AIR (pneumatic) or CONTRAST (hydrostatic) ENEMA REDUCTION under X-ray or ultrasound guidance. The pressure pushes the telescoped bowel back into place. It succeeds in about 80% of cases and avoids an operation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Antibiotics and observation for 48 hours — Antibiotics and observation do not unblock the bowel; waiting allows the trapped bowel to die.
• Immediate laparotomy and resection — Laparotomy is needed only if the enema fails, or there is peritonitis, perforation or shock that does not respond to resuscitation.
• Oral laxatives — Laxatives are useless and dangerous in a mechanical obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Before enema reduction: IV access, fluid resuscitation, nasogastric tube, and a surgeon must be available in case of perforation. Watch for recurrence (about 10%) after reduction.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q102$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2378, $s$Hirschsprung's disease is diagnosed definitively by:$s$, $s$["Serum bilirubin and liver function tests", "Stool culture showing enteric organisms", "Plain abdominal X-ray showing dilated loops", "Rectal suction biopsy showing no ganglion cells"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Rectal suction biopsy showing no ganglion cells
In HIRSCHSPRUNG'S DISEASE, the nerve cells (GANGLION CELLS) that make the bowel wall relax are missing from the end of the large bowel. The definitive diagnosis is a RECTAL SUCTION BIOPSY showing NO GANGLION CELLS (and often thickened nerve fibres and increased acetylcholinesterase staining). Other tests support the diagnosis: a contrast enema may show a narrow rectum with a widened bowel above it (transition zone), and anorectal manometry shows the absence of the normal relaxation reflex.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Serum bilirubin and liver function tests — Bilirubin and liver function tests are used for jaundice and liver disease, not Hirschsprung's.
• Stool culture showing enteric organisms — Stool culture identifies infections, not the absence of nerve cells.
• Plain abdominal X-ray showing dilated loops — A plain X-ray may show dilated bowel loops, but this is not specific — many conditions cause it. It cannot confirm the diagnosis.

⭐ Key point: Suspect Hirschsprung's when a term baby does not pass meconium within the first 48 hours, has a swollen abdomen and bilious vomiting, or when a child has long-term constipation since birth.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q103$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2379, $s$Bilious vomiting in a newborn requires an urgent:$s$, $s$["Upper GI contrast study to exclude malrotation", "Stool test for reducing sugars and pH", "Trial of a change of formula milk for 24 hours", "A course of oral antibiotics and review"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Upper GI contrast study to exclude malrotation
BILIOUS (green) VOMITING in a newborn means the blockage is BELOW the opening of the bile duct (in the duodenum or beyond). The most dangerous cause is MALROTATION WITH VOLVULUS, which can kill the whole small bowel within a few hours. An URGENT UPPER GI CONTRAST STUDY is the best test to show the abnormal position of the duodenum (the duodeno-jejunal flexure is not in its normal place) and confirm or exclude malrotation. Meanwhile: stop feeds, pass a nasogastric tube, start IV fluids, and inform the surgeon.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stool test for reducing sugars and pH — Testing the stool for reducing sugars checks for lactose intolerance, which does not cause bilious vomiting.
• Trial of a change of formula milk for 24 hours — Changing formula is dangerous here; it wastes time while the bowel may be dying.
• A course of oral antibiotics and review — Antibiotics and review do not address a surgical blockage.

⭐ Key point: Never treat bilious vomiting in a newborn as "simple reflux". Green vomit = surgical emergency until proven otherwise.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q104$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2380, $s$A child with perforated appendicitis develops fever and abdominal distension 5 days after surgery. The nurse should suspect:$s$, $s$["Pelvic or intra-abdominal abscess", "Urinary tract infection only", "Paralytic ileus without infection", "Normal post-operative course"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pelvic or intra-abdominal abscess
When an appendix PERFORATES, pus and bowel contents spill into the abdomen. Even after surgery, infected fluid can collect in the pelvis or between bowel loops to form an ABSCESS. The typical time for an abscess to show is 5–7 days after the operation, with SPIKING (swinging) FEVER, abdominal pain or distension, poor appetite, diarrhoea (pelvic abscess irritating the rectum) and sometimes urinary symptoms. Ultrasound or CT confirms it; treatment is antibiotics and drainage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Urinary tract infection only — A UTI can cause fever, but it does not explain abdominal distension after perforated appendicitis. An abscess must be excluded first.
• Paralytic ileus without infection — Paralytic ileus causes distension, but by itself does not cause fever. Persistent ileus with fever suggests an underlying abscess.
• Normal post-operative course — Fever and distension on day 5 are NOT normal; most children are improving by then.

⭐ Key point: Monitor temperature, abdominal girth, bowel function and wound after perforated appendicitis. Report fever after day 3–4.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q105$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2381, $s$Hb electrophoresis shows HbS and HbC. This patient has:$s$, $s$["Normal adult haemoglobin", "Sickle cell trait (HbAS) only", "Beta thalassaemia major", "HbSC disease, a sickling disorder"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: HbSC disease, a sickling disorder
Haemoglobin ELECTROPHORESIS separates the different haemoglobin types. Finding HbS (sickle haemoglobin) AND HbC (another abnormal haemoglobin), with NO HbA (normal adult haemoglobin), means the patient inherited HbS from one parent and HbC from the other: HbSC DISEASE. It is a SICKLING DISORDER, usually milder than HbSS (sickle cell anaemia), but patients can still have painful crises, acute chest syndrome, and are at higher risk of eye damage (retinopathy) and avascular necrosis of the hip.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal adult haemoglobin — Normal adult haemoglobin shows mainly HbA, with a little HbA2 and HbF.
• Sickle cell trait (HbAS) only — Sickle cell trait (HbAS) shows both HbA and HbS. HbAS people are usually healthy carriers.
• Beta thalassaemia major — Beta thalassaemia major shows mainly HbF and little or no HbA, with no HbS or HbC.

⭐ Key point: Genetic counselling is important: two HbAS parents have a 1 in 4 chance of having an HbSS child with each pregnancy. In Nigeria, premarital genotype testing helps prevent sickle cell disease.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q108$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2382, $s$A child with acute leukaemia and a WBC of 300 × 10⁹/L is at risk of:$s$, $s$["Secondary polycythaemia and stroke", "Leucostasis and tumour lysis syndrome", "Hypercoagulability only, with DVT", "Iron overload and haemosiderosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Leucostasis and tumour lysis syndrome
A very high white cell count (over 100 × 10⁹/L — called HYPERLEUKOCYTOSIS) makes the blood thick and sticky. The large leukaemic cells clog small blood vessels (LEUCOSTASIS), especially in the lungs (breathlessness, hypoxia) and brain (confusion, stroke). In addition, when treatment starts, the huge number of cancer cells breaks down quickly and releases their contents (TUMOUR LYSIS SYNDROME), causing dangerous electrolyte changes and kidney failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Secondary polycythaemia and stroke — Polycythaemia means too many RED cells; leukaemia usually causes anaemia.
• Hypercoagulability only, with DVT — Hypercoagulability may occur, but leucostasis and tumour lysis are the main dangers.
• Iron overload and haemosiderosis — Iron overload results from repeated transfusions, not directly from a high WBC.

⭐ Key point: Treatment: aggressive IV hydration, allopurinol or rasburicase, close electrolyte monitoring, and sometimes leukapheresis. Avoid red cell transfusion if possible because it increases blood thickness.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q111$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2383, $s$A febrile neutropenic child (neutrophils below 0.5 × 10⁹/L) needs:$s$, $s$["Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics within 1 hour", "Live vaccines to boost immunity", "Antibiotics only after culture results", "Oral paracetamol and observation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Broad-spectrum IV antibiotics within 1 hour
A child with NEUTROPENIA (neutrophils below 0.5 × 10⁹/L) cannot fight bacterial infections. Fever may be the ONLY sign of a life-threatening infection that can kill within hours. The child needs BROAD-SPECTRUM IV ANTIBIOTICS (e.g. piperacillin-tazobactam) WITHIN 1 HOUR of arrival, after taking blood cultures. Do not wait for results.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Live vaccines to boost immunity — Live vaccines are dangerous in immunocompromised children.
• Antibiotics only after culture results — Waiting for culture results (24–48 hours) can be fatal.
• Oral paracetamol and observation — Paracetamol and observation delay life-saving treatment.

⭐ Key point: Avoid rectal temperatures, suppositories and enemas in neutropenic children.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q113$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2384, $s$In a child with Ebola virus disease, the main supportive treatment is:$s$, $s$["Routine whole blood transfusion every day", "High-dose steroids and IV antihistamines", "Broad-spectrum antibiotics alone, given IV", "Aggressive fluid and electrolyte replacement"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Aggressive fluid and electrolyte replacement
Ebola causes massive fluid loss through severe vomiting and diarrhoea, leading to dehydration, electrolyte imbalance and shock. The main supportive treatment is AGGRESSIVE FLUID AND ELECTROLYTE REPLACEMENT (oral rehydration and IV fluids), with correction of potassium, sodium, glucose and acidosis. Specific antibodies (e.g. Inmazeb, Ebanga) are now available for the Zaire strain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Routine whole blood transfusion every day — Routine daily blood transfusion is not standard.
• High-dose steroids and IV antihistamines — Steroids and antihistamines don't treat Ebola.
• Broad-spectrum antibiotics alone, given IV — Antibiotics don't treat viruses, though they may be used for secondary infections.

⭐ Key point: Early supportive care greatly improves survival.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q118$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2385, $s$Polyhydramnios is defined as an amniotic fluid index above:$s$, $s$["24–25 cm (or deepest pool above 8 cm)", "10 cm (or deepest pool above 3 cm)", "5 cm (or deepest pool above 2 cm)", "15 cm (or deepest pool above 5 cm)"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 24–25 cm (or deepest pool above 8 cm)
POLYHYDRAMNIOS is too much amniotic fluid. It is defined as an AMNIOTIC FLUID INDEX (AFI — the sum of the deepest pools in four quadrants) ABOVE 24–25 cm, or a single DEEPEST VERTICAL POOL above 8 cm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 cm (or deepest pool above 3 cm) — An AFI of 10 cm is normal.
• 5 cm (or deepest pool above 2 cm) — An AFI below 5 cm (deepest pool below 2 cm) indicates OLIGOhydramnios.
• 15 cm (or deepest pool above 5 cm) — An AFI of 15 cm is normal.

⭐ Key point: Normal AFI is about 5–24 cm.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q121$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2386, $s$A recognised cause of polyhydramnios is:$s$, $s$["Placental insufficiency with growth restriction", "Fetal oesophageal atresia, preventing swallowing", "Fetal renal agenesis, preventing urine output", "Post-term pregnancy beyond 42 weeks"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fetal oesophageal atresia, preventing swallowing
Amniotic fluid is constantly being made (mainly fetal urine) and removed (mainly by the fetus swallowing it and absorbing it through the gut). POLYHYDRAMNIOS happens when there is too much production or too little removal. In OESOPHAGEAL ATRESIA, the oesophagus ends in a blind pouch, so the fetus CANNOT SWALLOW amniotic fluid into the stomach and gut. Fluid is still produced as fetal urine but not removed, so it builds up. Ultrasound may show polyhydramnios with a small or absent stomach bubble. Other causes: maternal diabetes (fetal polyuria), twin-to-twin transfusion, fetal anaemia, anencephaly (poor swallowing), duodenal atresia, and neuromuscular disorders.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Placental insufficiency with growth restriction — Placental insufficiency reduces blood flow to the fetal kidneys, so urine output falls, causing OLIGOhydramnios.
• Fetal renal agenesis, preventing urine output — Renal agenesis means no fetal kidneys and no urine, causing severe OLIGOhydramnios.
• Post-term pregnancy beyond 42 weeks — Post-term pregnancy is associated with reduced (OLIGO) fluid, as placental function declines.

⭐ Key point: After delivery of a baby from a pregnancy with polyhydramnios, pass a nasogastric tube before the first feed to check for oesophageal atresia. If the tube does not pass, do not feed and refer to surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q122$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2387, $s$A woman with severe polyhydramnios is at risk during labour of:$s$, $s$["Uterine hypertonia and fetal hypoxia", "Cord prolapse and postpartum haemorrhage", "Shoulder dystocia and perineal tears", "Prolonged pregnancy and macrosomia"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cord prolapse and postpartum haemorrhage
In severe polyhydramnios, the uterus is over-stretched. During labour, when the membranes rupture, a gush of fluid can sweep the cord down (CORD PROLAPSE). After delivery, the over-stretched uterus may not contract well, causing POSTPARTUM HAEMORRHAGE. Also: unstable lie, malpresentation and placental abruption.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uterine hypertonia and fetal hypoxia — Uterine hypertonia is not a typical risk.
• Shoulder dystocia and perineal tears — Shoulder dystocia is linked to macrosomia, not polyhydramnios itself.
• Prolonged pregnancy and macrosomia — Polyhydramnios is associated with preterm labour, not prolonged pregnancy.

⭐ Key point: Rupture membranes in a controlled way (with the head engaged) to reduce cord prolapse risk.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q123$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2388, $s$Asymmetrical intra-uterine growth restriction is usually caused by:$s$, $s$["Placental insufficiency late in pregnancy", "Congenital infection in the first trimester", "Genetic small stature", "Chromosomal abnormalities early in pregnancy"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Placental insufficiency late in pregnancy
In ASYMMETRICAL IUGR, the baby's body is small but the HEAD is relatively normal ("head-sparing"). It is usually caused by PLACENTAL INSUFFICIENCY developing LATE in pregnancy (third trimester), for example from pre-eclampsia, hypertension, smoking, or maternal vascular disease. When the placenta fails to supply enough oxygen and nutrients, the fetus redistributes blood to protect the BRAIN, heart and adrenals ("brain-sparing effect"), while reducing blood flow to the liver, gut and kidneys. The liver stores less glycogen, so the abdominal circumference becomes small, while the head circumference stays near normal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Congenital infection in the first trimester — Congenital infection early in pregnancy (e.g. rubella, CMV) limits cell number in all organs, causing SYMMETRICAL IUGR.
• Genetic small stature — Genetically small (constitutionally small) babies are healthy and proportionally small; they are not growth-restricted.
• Chromosomal abnormalities early in pregnancy — Chromosomal abnormalities affect growth from early pregnancy, causing SYMMETRICAL IUGR.

⭐ Key point: Asymmetrical IUGR is the commonest type (about 70–80%). These babies often show catch-up growth after birth if well fed, but are at risk of hypoglycaemia in the first days.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q124$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2389, $s$An IUGR newborn is at particular risk of:$s$, $s$["Hypoglycaemia, hypothermia and polycythaemia", "Hyperglycaemia, hyperthermia and anaemia", "Hyperbilirubinaemia only, from haemolysis", "Macrosomia, birth injury and fractures"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypoglycaemia, hypothermia and polycythaemia
IUGR babies have specific risks after birth: HYPOGLYCAEMIA — they have small glycogen and fat stores, and may have high insulin levels, so their glucose drops quickly; HYPOTHERMIA — they have little body fat for insulation and heat production, and a large surface area compared to their weight; POLYCYTHAEMIA — chronic low oxygen in the womb stimulates the fetus to make more red cells (erythropoietin), so the blood becomes thick, which can cause jaundice, breathing problems and poor circulation. Other risks: perinatal asphyxia, meconium aspiration, hypocalcaemia and infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperglycaemia, hyperthermia and anaemia — IUGR babies have LOW glucose (not high), and are prone to hypothermia (not hyperthermia). They usually have polycythaemia, not anaemia.
• Hyperbilirubinaemia only, from haemolysis — Jaundice can occur (from polycythaemia), but it is not the only risk, and it is not usually caused by haemolysis in IUGR.
• Macrosomia, birth injury and fractures — Macrosomia (a large baby) is the opposite of IUGR; birth injury and fractures are risks of macrosomia.

⭐ Key point: Feed IUGR babies early and frequently, keep them warm (skin-to-skin), check glucose before feeds in the first 24–48 hours, and check haematocrit if the baby looks plethoric (very red).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q125$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2390, $s$Doppler showing absent or reversed end-diastolic flow in the umbilical artery indicates:$s$, $s$["Normal placental function in late pregnancy", "Fetal macrosomia from maternal diabetes", "Polyhydramnios from excess fetal urine", "Severe placental insufficiency with fetal compromise"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Severe placental insufficiency with fetal compromise
The UMBILICAL ARTERY DOPPLER measures blood flow from the fetus to the placenta. Normally, blood keeps flowing forward even during the heart's resting phase (diastole), because the placenta has low resistance. When the placenta is badly damaged, resistance rises. First, flow during diastole falls; then it stops completely (ABSENT END-DIASTOLIC FLOW, AEDF); and in the worst cases it goes backwards (REVERSED END-DIASTOLIC FLOW, REDF). This indicates SEVERE PLACENTAL INSUFFICIENCY and FETAL COMPROMISE — the fetus is at high risk of hypoxia, acidosis and stillbirth (REDF is especially dangerous).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal placental function in late pregnancy — Normal placental function shows forward flow in diastole throughout pregnancy. Absent or reversed flow is never normal.
• Fetal macrosomia from maternal diabetes — Macrosomia (a large baby from diabetes) is associated with normal or high placental flow, not absent flow.
• Polyhydramnios from excess fetal urine — Polyhydramnios is unrelated; placental insufficiency is associated with OLIGOhydramnios.

⭐ Key point: Abnormal Doppler requires urgent obstetric review, close fetal monitoring (CTG, ductus venosus Doppler), antenatal steroids and magnesium sulphate if preterm, and planning for delivery (often by caesarean section).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q126$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2391, $s$Cervical incompetence typically presents as:$s$, $s$["Hypertension with heavy proteinuria", "Painful contractions in the first trimester", "Painless dilatation with second-trimester loss", "Bleeding from placenta praevia at term"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Painless dilatation with second-trimester loss
CERVICAL INCOMPETENCE (cervical insufficiency) means the cervix is too weak to stay closed as the pregnancy grows. It typically presents as PAINLESS DILATATION of the cervix in the SECOND TRIMESTER (about 16–24 weeks), often with bulging membranes and pressure, followed by rupture of membranes and rapid delivery of a baby who is too immature to survive (SECOND-TRIMESTER LOSS). Women often have a history of repeated painless mid-trimester losses. Causes include previous cervical surgery (cone biopsy), repeated dilatation, cervical trauma from previous deliveries, and congenital weakness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension with heavy proteinuria — Hypertension with proteinuria indicates PRE-ECLAMPSIA, not cervical incompetence.
• Painful contractions in the first trimester — Painful contractions suggest labour or miscarriage from other causes. Cervical incompetence is classically PAINLESS.
• Bleeding from placenta praevia at term — Bleeding at term from placenta praevia is a placental problem, not cervical incompetence.

⭐ Key point: Management: cervical cerclage (a stitch around the cervix, usually at 12–14 weeks in women with a history of losses), or progesterone. Ultrasound measurement of cervical length helps identify at-risk women.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q127$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2392, $s$A woman with a cervical cerclage goes into labour at 35 weeks. The nurse should:$s$, $s$["Give tocolytics and keep the stitch", "Leave the suture until delivery", "Arrange urgent removal of the suture", "Start oxytocin infusion"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Arrange urgent removal of the suture
A CERVICAL CERCLAGE is a stitch that holds the cervix closed. When labour starts, the uterus contracts strongly and pushes the baby against the cervix. If the stitch stays in place, it can TEAR THE CERVIX (lacerations that may extend into the uterus), cause heavy bleeding, or even cause uterine rupture. So when a woman with a cerclage goes into established labour (or her membranes rupture), the nurse should arrange URGENT REMOVAL of the suture by the obstetric team. At 35 weeks, the baby is mature enough that labour can usually be allowed to continue after removal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give tocolytics and keep the stitch — Tocolytics (to stop labour) are not appropriate at 35 weeks, and keeping the stitch during contractions risks serious cervical injury.
• Leave the suture until delivery — Leaving the suture until delivery risks cervical tearing and bleeding.
• Start oxytocin infusion — Starting oxytocin with the suture in place would increase contractions against the closed cervix, causing serious injury.

⭐ Key point: Elective removal of a cerclage is usually done at 36–37 weeks, before labour starts. If a caesarean is planned, the stitch may be removed at the same time.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q128$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2393, $s$Puerperal sepsis is infection of the genital tract occurring:$s$, $s$["From membrane rupture or labour to 42 days postpartum", "Only after the 6-week postnatal check", "Only in the first 24 hours after delivery", "Only during the first stage of labour"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: From membrane rupture or labour to 42 days postpartum
PUERPERAL SEPSIS is infection of the genital tract occurring any time from RUPTURE OF MEMBRANES or LABOUR to 42 DAYS after delivery (WHO definition).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only after the 6-week postnatal check — It's not only after 6 weeks.
• Only in the first 24 hours after delivery — It's not only in the first 24 hours.
• Only during the first stage of labour — It's not only during the first stage of labour.

⭐ Key point: Signs include fever, pelvic pain, foul lochia, and delayed uterine involution.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q129$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2394, $s$Risk factors for puerperal sepsis include:$s$, $s$["Multiparity with a quick spontaneous vaginal birth", "A short labour with intact membranes until delivery", "Caesarean section, long labour, retained products, many VEs", "An elective delivery with no vaginal examinations"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Caesarean section, long labour, retained products, many VEs
RISK FACTORS for puerperal sepsis are things that allow bacteria to reach the genital tract or reduce the woman's ability to fight infection. They include: CAESAREAN SECTION (the strongest risk factor — surgery opens the uterus and abdomen), PROLONGED LABOUR, prolonged rupture of membranes, RETAINED PRODUCTS of conception (placenta or membranes that become infected), MANY VAGINAL EXAMINATIONS (each VE can carry bacteria upwards), manual removal of the placenta, perineal tears, anaemia, diabetes, obesity, HIV, and poor hygiene.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Multiparity with a quick spontaneous vaginal birth — Multiparity with a quick spontaneous vaginal birth carries a LOW risk of infection.
• A short labour with intact membranes until delivery — A short labour with intact membranes until delivery reduces the time for bacteria to ascend, so the risk is low.
• An elective delivery with no vaginal examinations — Elective delivery with no vaginal examinations has a low risk of infection.

⭐ Key point: Prevention: limit VEs, aseptic technique during delivery and procedures, prophylactic antibiotics at caesarean section, check the placenta is complete, and treat anaemia during pregnancy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q132$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2395, $s$Supine hypotensive syndrome in late pregnancy is relieved by:$s$, $s$["A steep head-down (Trendelenburg) position", "Raising both legs on pillows while supine", "Left lateral tilt or manual uterine displacement", "Lying flat on the back with arms by the side"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Left lateral tilt or manual uterine displacement
SUPINE HYPOTENSIVE SYNDROME occurs when the heavy uterus compresses the inferior vena cava (and aorta) when lying on the back. This reduces blood return to the heart, causing low BP. It's relieved by LEFT LATERAL TILT or MANUAL UTERINE DISPLACEMENT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A steep head-down (Trendelenburg) position — Head-down position doesn't relieve compression.
• Raising both legs on pillows while supine — Raising legs while supine doesn't relieve compression.
• Lying flat on the back with arms by the side — Lying flat causes the problem.

⭐ Key point: Never let a pregnant woman lie flat on her back in late pregnancy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q133$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2396, $s$During CPR in a woman over 20 weeks pregnant, the team should:$s$, $s$["Avoid defibrillation altogether because of the fetal heart", "Displace the uterus left and prepare for perimortem caesarean by 4–5 min", "Use a slower compression rate to protect the pregnant uterus", "Delay CPR until the baby has been delivered by caesarean section"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Displace the uterus left and prepare for perimortem caesarean by 4–5 min
During CPR in a woman over 20 weeks pregnant: DISPLACE THE UTERUS LEFT (to relieve compression) and PREPARE for PERIMORTEM CAESAREAN by 4–5 minutes if there is no return of circulation. This helps both mother and baby.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Avoid defibrillation altogether because of the fetal heart — Defibrillation is safe and should not be avoided.
• Use a slower compression rate to protect the pregnant uterus — Compression rate should be normal (100–120/min).
• Delay CPR until the baby has been delivered by caesarean section — CPR should not be delayed.

⭐ Key point: Perimortem caesarean should be done in the resuscitation room.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q134$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2397, $s$Hypotension after spinal anaesthesia for caesarean section is best treated with:$s$, $s$["Phenylephrine infusion with fluid co-loading", "Large doses of IV furosemide and fluid restriction", "Withholding all IV fluids to avoid oedema", "Head-up tilt to 45 degrees with the legs down"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Phenylephrine infusion with fluid co-loading
Spinal anaesthesia blocks the SYMPATHETIC nerves that keep the blood vessels tight. The vessels dilate (vasodilation), blood pools in the legs and abdomen, and BP falls. In pregnancy this is worsened by compression of the vena cava by the uterus. Low BP reduces blood flow to the placenta and can cause FETAL DISTRESS, and causes nausea, vomiting and dizziness in the mother. The best treatment is a vasoconstrictor that reverses the vasodilation: PHENYLEPHRINE (an alpha-agonist) given as a prophylactic INFUSION or boluses, together with FLUID CO-LOADING (giving fluid rapidly at the time the spinal is inserted) and LEFT UTERINE DISPLACEMENT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Large doses of IV furosemide and fluid restriction — Furosemide and fluid restriction would make hypotension much worse by reducing blood volume.
• Withholding all IV fluids to avoid oedema — Withholding fluids worsens hypotension.
• Head-up tilt to 45 degrees with the legs down — Head-up tilt with the legs down causes more pooling of blood in the legs and worsens hypotension.

⭐ Key point: Ephedrine can also be used, but phenylephrine is preferred because it causes less fetal acidosis. Aim to keep systolic BP close to the woman's baseline.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q135$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2398, $s$Amniotic fluid embolism classically presents with:$s$, $s$["Sudden collapse, hypoxia, hypotension and DIC", "A slowly rising blood pressure", "Gradual onset of fever and a rash", "Headache and visual disturbance only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sudden collapse, hypoxia, hypotension and DIC
AMNIOTIC FLUID EMBOLISM (AFE) is a rare but often fatal emergency occurring during labour, delivery or shortly after. Amniotic fluid and fetal cells enter the mother's circulation and trigger a massive inflammatory and allergic-like reaction. The classic presentation is SUDDEN COLLAPSE with: HYPOXIA (severe breathlessness, cyanosis — from acute lung injury), HYPOTENSION/cardiovascular collapse or cardiac arrest (acute right then left heart failure), and DISSEMINATED INTRAVASCULAR COAGULATION (DIC) — severe bleeding from the uterus, IV sites and wounds. The woman may also have seizures or agitation, and fetal distress.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A slowly rising blood pressure — A slowly rising BP suggests pre-eclampsia, not AFE, which causes sudden collapse.
• Gradual onset of fever and a rash — A gradual fever with rash suggests infection or allergy, not AFE.
• Headache and visual disturbance only — Headache and visual disturbance suggest pre-eclampsia, not AFE.

⭐ Key point: Treatment is supportive and urgent: call for help, CPR if arrested, high-flow oxygen and intubation, vasopressors and inotropes, massive transfusion with clotting factors, and immediate delivery of the baby if the mother arrests.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q136$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2399, $s$The Modified Early Obstetric Warning Score (MEOWS) is used to:$s$, $s$["Interpret fetal heart rate patterns in labour", "Assess the maturity of the newborn at birth", "Grade perineal tears after vaginal delivery", "Detect deterioration in pregnant and postnatal women"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Detect deterioration in pregnant and postnatal women
The MODIFIED EARLY OBSTETRIC WARNING SCORE (MEOWS) is a chart for PREGNANT and POSTNATAL WOMEN that tracks: respiratory rate, oxygen saturation, heart rate, BP, temperature, level of consciousness, and sometimes pain and lochia. Abnormal values (usually coloured yellow or red zones) trigger escalation. It is modified from the general early warning score because pregnancy changes normal values (e.g. higher heart rate, lower BP). Its purpose is to DETECT DETERIORATION early (e.g. from haemorrhage, sepsis, pre-eclampsia), so that medical review happens before the woman becomes critically ill.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Interpret fetal heart rate patterns in labour — Fetal heart rate patterns are interpreted using CTG (cardiotocography), not MEOWS.
• Assess the maturity of the newborn at birth — Newborn maturity is assessed using the Ballard or Dubowitz score.
• Grade perineal tears after vaginal delivery — Perineal tears are graded first to fourth degree by examination, not MEOWS.

⭐ Key point: Escalate when the score triggers: one red or two yellow scores usually need urgent medical review. Document and repeat observations as the chart indicates.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q137$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2400, $s$In severe pre-eclampsia, magnesium sulphate toxicity is first detected by:$s$, $s$["A rising heart rate", "Loss of the patellar reflex", "An increasing urine output", "A rising blood pressure"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Loss of the patellar reflex
MAGNESIUM SULPHATE prevents eclamptic seizures, but too much causes toxicity. The FIRST sign of toxicity is LOSS OF THE PATELLAR (knee-jerk) REFLEX, which occurs at serum levels of about 3.5–5 mmol/L (7–10 mEq/L). As levels rise further: respiratory depression (breathing rate below 12/min, at about 5–6.5 mmol/L), then cardiac conduction problems and CARDIAC ARREST (above about 7.5 mmol/L). Because reflexes disappear before breathing is affected, checking reflexes regularly gives an early warning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A rising heart rate — Magnesium toxicity causes a SLOWER heart rate and conduction block, not tachycardia.
• An increasing urine output — Magnesium is excreted by the kidneys, so REDUCED urine output (below 30 mL/hr) raises the risk of toxicity. An increasing urine output is not a sign of toxicity.
• A rising blood pressure — Magnesium can LOWER BP slightly by relaxing blood vessels; a rising BP is a sign of pre-eclampsia worsening, not magnesium toxicity.

⭐ Key point: Hourly checks during magnesium therapy: patellar reflexes, respiratory rate, SpO2 and urine output. If reflexes are absent, stop the infusion and inform the doctor. Antidote: calcium gluconate 10%, 10 mL IV over 10 minutes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q139$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2401, $s$Postpartum DIC is most commonly associated with:$s$, $s$["Mild iron-deficiency anaemia during pregnancy", "Uncomplicated breastfeeding in the first week", "Normal vaginal delivery at term without problems", "Placental abruption, amniotic fluid embolism, sepsis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Placental abruption, amniotic fluid embolism, sepsis
DISSEMINATED INTRAVASCULAR COAGULATION (DIC) is a condition where the clotting system is activated throughout the body, using up clotting factors and platelets, leading to both clots and severe BLEEDING. In obstetrics, DIC is most commonly triggered by: PLACENTAL ABRUPTION (the damaged placenta releases tissue factor into the mother's blood), AMNIOTIC FLUID EMBOLISM, SEPSIS, severe pre-eclampsia/HELLP, retained dead fetus, and massive postpartum haemorrhage (with dilution of clotting factors).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild iron-deficiency anaemia during pregnancy — Mild iron-deficiency anaemia does not cause DIC.
• Uncomplicated breastfeeding in the first week — Breastfeeding has no link to DIC.
• Normal vaginal delivery at term without problems — An uncomplicated normal delivery does not usually cause DIC.

⭐ Key point: Signs: bleeding from multiple sites (uterus, IV sites, gums, wounds), low platelets, prolonged PT/APTT, low fibrinogen, raised D-dimers. Treat the cause (e.g. deliver the baby, control bleeding) and replace blood, fresh frozen plasma, platelets and cryoprecipitate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q140$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2402, $s$Inflation breaths for a term newborn are given:$s$, $s$["Five breaths, each held for 2–3 seconds", "Ten breaths at a rate of 60 per minute", "Two quick breaths, each lasting 1 second", "One sustained breath held for 10 seconds"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Five breaths, each held for 2–3 seconds
For a term newborn, give FIVE INFLATION BREATHS, each held for 2–3 SECONDS, at a pressure of about 30 cmH₂O. These long breaths push the fluid out of the lungs and fill them with air for the first time. Then give ventilation breaths at about 30 per minute.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ten breaths at a rate of 60 per minute — Ten breaths at 60 per minute is ventilation rate, not inflation breaths.
• Two quick breaths, each lasting 1 second — Two quick 1-second breaths are too short to inflate fluid-filled lungs.
• One sustained breath held for 10 seconds — One 10-second breath isn't the standard technique.

⭐ Key point: Watch for chest movement. If none, reposition the head (neutral), use jaw thrust, and check for obstruction.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q82$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2403, $s$Room air is used at the start of term newborn resuscitation because:$s$, $s$["It is cheaper than oxygen", "Oxygen is never needed in newborns", "It increases the heart rate faster", "Excess oxygen may harm the newborn"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Excess oxygen may harm the newborn
Term newborn resuscitation starts with ROOM AIR (21% oxygen) because EXCESS OXYGEN can HARM the newborn: it creates free radicals that damage the brain, lungs and eyes, and it is linked to higher mortality. Oxygen is added if needed to meet target saturations (measured on the right hand, pre-ductal).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It is cheaper than oxygen — Cost isn't the reason.
• Oxygen is never needed in newborns — Oxygen IS sometimes needed (if saturations stay low).
• It increases the heart rate faster — Room air doesn't raise the heart rate faster.

⭐ Key point: Target pre-ductal SpO₂: about 60% at 1 minute, 70% at 3 minutes, 80% at 5 minutes, 85–90% at 10 minutes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q83$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2404, $s$A newborn with meconium-stained liquor who is not breathing should first receive:$s$, $s$["IV adrenaline before ventilation is attempted", "Inflation breaths and ventilation; suction only if blocked", "Routine deep tracheal suction before any breaths", "Chest compressions started immediately at birth"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inflation breaths and ventilation; suction only if blocked
For a newborn with meconium-stained liquor who is NOT BREATHING, start INFLATION BREATHS and ventilation without delay. Routine deep tracheal suction before breathing is NO LONGER recommended (it delays ventilation). Suction only if the airway is visibly BLOCKED or ventilation fails.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• IV adrenaline before ventilation is attempted — Adrenaline is given late, after ventilation and compressions.
• Routine deep tracheal suction before any breaths — Routine tracheal suction delays essential ventilation.
• Chest compressions started immediately at birth — Chest compressions start only if the HR stays below 60 despite effective ventilation.

⭐ Key point: The priority in a non-breathing newborn is ventilation, whatever the colour of the liquor.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q84$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2405, $s$During phototherapy, the nurse should:$s$, $s$["Cover the eyes, expose the skin, watch temperature and fluids", "Stop all feeds while the lights are on", "Cover the whole body with a blanket to keep warm", "Avoid turning the baby during phototherapy"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cover the eyes, expose the skin, watch temperature and fluids
During phototherapy: COVER the baby's EYES (to protect the retina), EXPOSE as much SKIN as possible (more light contact), monitor TEMPERATURE (risk of overheating or cooling), and maintain FLUIDS (phototherapy increases water loss through the skin). Turn the baby regularly and continue feeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stop all feeds while the lights are on — Stopping feeds would cause dehydration and increase bilirubin.
• Cover the whole body with a blanket to keep warm — Covering the body blocks the light.
• Avoid turning the baby during phototherapy — The baby should be turned regularly.

⭐ Key point: Check bilirubin regularly. Watch for loose green stools and skin rashes (common, harmless side effects).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q86$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2406, $s$During exchange transfusion, blood glucose is checked because:$s$, $s$["Hypoglycaemia can follow the high glucose in citrated blood", "Glucose has no relevance during an exchange transfusion", "Hyperglycaemia always persists for several days afterwards", "The baby cannot be fed for a week after the procedure"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hypoglycaemia can follow the high glucose in citrated blood
Donor blood for exchange transfusion is stored in a citrate preservative containing high GLUCOSE. During the exchange, the baby's pancreas makes extra insulin. When the high-glucose blood stops, rebound HYPOGLYCAEMIA can occur. Glucose is checked during and after the procedure, along with calcium (citrate lowers it), potassium and blood gases.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Glucose has no relevance during an exchange transfusion — Glucose IS relevant.
• Hyperglycaemia always persists for several days afterwards — Hyperglycaemia doesn't persist for days.
• The baby cannot be fed for a week after the procedure — The baby can be fed soon after (as per protocol).

⭐ Key point: Exchange transfusion risks also include infection, NEC, thrombosis, arrhythmias and electrolyte problems.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q87$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2407, $s$The umbilical venous catheter used for exchange transfusion is inserted:$s$, $s$["Into the radial artery at the wrist", "Into the umbilical vein, aseptically", "Into a scalp vein over the temple", "Into the femoral vein in the groin"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Into the umbilical vein, aseptically
For neonatal exchange transfusion, an UMBILICAL VENOUS CATHETER (UVC) is inserted into the UMBILICAL VEIN using strict ASEPTIC technique. The umbilical vein is large and easily accessible in the first days of life, allowing blood to be removed and replaced in small amounts.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Into the radial artery at the wrist — The radial artery isn't used for exchange in this way.
• Into a scalp vein over the temple — Scalp veins are too small.
• Into the femoral vein in the groin — The femoral vein carries higher risks in neonates.

⭐ Key point: Confirm the UVC position by X-ray and secure it. Complications include infection, thrombosis, air embolism and liver injury.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q88$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2408, $s$Late-onset neonatal sepsis (after 72 hours) is commonly caused by:$s$, $s$["Coagulase-negative staphylococci and Gram-negative bacteria", "Malaria parasites transmitted in the unit", "Rotavirus, in all babies with late sepsis", "Group B Streptococcus from the mother in every case"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Coagulase-negative staphylococci and Gram-negative bacteria
LATE-ONSET neonatal sepsis (after 72 hours of life) is usually HOSPITAL-ACQUIRED. Common causes are COAGULASE-NEGATIVE STAPHYLOCOCCI (from skin, central lines) and GRAM-NEGATIVE bacteria (Klebsiella, E. coli, Pseudomonas). Central lines, ventilation and prolonged admission increase the risk.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Malaria parasites transmitted in the unit — Malaria isn't the common cause of late-onset sepsis.
• Rotavirus, in all babies with late sepsis — Rotavirus causes diarrhoea, not typical late-onset sepsis.
• Group B Streptococcus from the mother in every case — Group B Streptococcus mainly causes EARLY-onset sepsis (from the mother at birth).

⭐ Key point: Hand hygiene, line care bundles, and breast milk reduce late-onset sepsis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q89$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2409, $s$Kangaroo mother care is beneficial for preterm babies because it:$s$, $s$["It is used only for well term babies", "It increases the risk of hypothermia", "Warmth, breastfeeding and less infection", "It replaces the need for any feeding"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Warmth, breastfeeding and less infection
KANGAROO MOTHER CARE (skin-to-skin contact of the baby on the mother's chest) helps preterm babies by keeping them WARM (prevents hypothermia), promoting BREASTFEEDING, reducing INFECTION, stabilising breathing and heart rate, and improving bonding and weight gain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It is used only for well term babies — It is used especially for preterm and low birth weight babies, not only well term babies.
• It increases the risk of hypothermia — It REDUCES the risk of hypothermia.
• It replaces the need for any feeding — It supports feeding; it doesn't replace it.

⭐ Key point: Kangaroo care can reduce mortality in low birth weight babies, especially where incubators are limited.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q91$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2410, $s$Feeding a preterm baby of 30 weeks with no sucking reflex is best done by:$s$, $s$["Orogastric or nasogastric feeds of breast milk", "No feeds until 36 weeks' corrected age", "Spoon feeding of standard infant formula", "Bottle feeding with a large, fast-flow teat"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Orogastric or nasogastric feeds of breast milk
Preterm babies under about 32–34 weeks usually can't coordinate sucking, swallowing and breathing. A 30-week baby with no sucking reflex is fed by OROGASTRIC or NASOGASTRIC tube, ideally with BREAST MILK (expressed). Feeds are increased gradually as tolerated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No feeds until 36 weeks' corrected age — Withholding feeds until 36 weeks would cause malnutrition (IV nutrition may be used meanwhile, but enteral feeds start early).
• Spoon feeding of standard infant formula — Spoon feeding is unsafe without a suck-swallow reflex (aspiration risk).
• Bottle feeding with a large, fast-flow teat — Bottle feeding with a fast teat is unsafe and risks aspiration.

⭐ Key point: Breast milk reduces NEC and infection in preterm babies.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q92$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2411, $s$Necrotising enterocolitis in a preterm baby presents with:$s$, $s$["Constipation only, with normal feeding", "Projectile non-bilious vomiting after feeds", "Distension, bloody stools and bilious aspirates", "Watery stools with a soft, normal abdomen"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Distension, bloody stools and bilious aspirates
Necrotising enterocolitis (NEC) in a preterm baby presents with ABDOMINAL DISTENSION, BLOODY STOOLS, BILIOUS (green) aspirates or vomiting, feed intolerance, and in severe cases, abdominal tenderness, discolouration and shock. X-ray may show gas in the bowel wall (pneumatosis intestinalis).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Constipation only, with normal feeding — Constipation alone with normal feeding doesn't suggest NEC.
• Projectile non-bilious vomiting after feeds — Projectile non-bilious vomiting suggests pyloric stenosis.
• Watery stools with a soft, normal abdomen — Watery stools with a soft abdomen suggest gastroenteritis.

⭐ Key point: NEC: stop feeds, decompress the stomach (NG tube on free drainage), give IV fluids and antibiotics, and refer to surgery if there is perforation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q93$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2412, $s$Respiratory distress syndrome in preterm babies is caused by:$s$, $s$["Excess lung fluid at term", "Meconium aspiration", "Surfactant deficiency", "Pneumothorax only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Surfactant deficiency
Respiratory distress syndrome (RDS) in preterm babies is caused by SURFACTANT DEFICIENCY. Surfactant (made by type II pneumocytes, mostly after 34 weeks) keeps the alveoli open. Without it, the alveoli collapse, causing breathing difficulty soon after birth.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Excess lung fluid at term — Excess lung fluid at term causes transient tachypnoea of the newborn.
• Meconium aspiration — Meconium aspiration affects term or post-term babies.
• Pneumothorax only — Pneumothorax can complicate RDS but isn't its cause.

⭐ Key point: Prevent and treat RDS: antenatal steroids, CPAP, and surfactant given via the ETT.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q94$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2413, $s$Signs of respiratory distress in a newborn include:$s$, $s$["Grunting, flaring, recession, rate over 60/min", "Hiccups and sneezing only", "Normal breathing with a loud cry", "Rate under 20/min with a pink colour"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Grunting, flaring, recession, rate over 60/min
Signs of respiratory distress in a newborn: GRUNTING (a short sound on breathing out, which keeps the alveoli open), NASAL FLARING, RECESSION (chest wall pulling in), a breathing RATE OVER 60 per minute, head bobbing and cyanosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hiccups and sneezing only — Hiccups and sneezing are normal.
• Normal breathing with a loud cry — Normal breathing with a loud cry is healthy.
• Rate under 20/min with a pink colour — A rate below 20 is abnormally slow (apnoea risk), but pink colour doesn't suggest distress; this isn't the distress pattern.

⭐ Key point: Grunting is a sign of serious respiratory distress in newborns. Assess and support breathing urgently.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q95$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2414, $s$The normal blood glucose of a newborn should be maintained above:$s$, $s$["10 mmol/L (about 180 mg/dL)", "0.5 mmol/L (about 9 mg/dL)", "1.0 mmol/L (about 18 mg/dL)", "2.6 mmol/L (about 47 mg/dL)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2.6 mmol/L (about 47 mg/dL)
Newborn blood glucose should be kept ABOVE about 2.6 mmol/L (about 45–47 mg/dL). Low glucose (hypoglycaemia) can cause brain injury, seizures and developmental problems.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 mmol/L (about 180 mg/dL) — 10 mmol/L is high (hyperglycaemia).
• 0.5 mmol/L (about 9 mg/dL) — 0.5 mmol/L is dangerously low.
• 1.0 mmol/L (about 18 mg/dL) — 1.0 mmol/L is dangerously low.

⭐ Key point: At-risk babies (preterm, small, large, infants of diabetic mothers, sick or cold babies) need early feeding and glucose monitoring.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q96$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2415, $s$A 'safe sleep' environment for infants in hospital should include:$s$, $s$["Firm flat mattress, on the back, no loose bedding", "Sharing a bed with an adult for warmth", "Prone position with the head turned to the side", "Soft pillows and toys in the cot for comfort"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Firm flat mattress, on the back, no loose bedding
A safe sleep environment for infants: a FIRM, FLAT MATTRESS, sleeping on the BACK (supine), with NO loose bedding, pillows, bumpers or toys in the cot. This reduces the risk of sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS) and suffocation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sharing a bed with an adult for warmth — Bed sharing with an adult increases the risk of SIDS and suffocation.
• Prone position with the head turned to the side — The prone position increases the risk of SIDS.
• Soft pillows and toys in the cot for comfort — Soft pillows and toys increase the risk of suffocation.

⭐ Key point: In hospital, model safe sleep for parents, so they continue it at home.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q98$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2416, $s$An apnoea monitor alarm sounds on a preterm baby with HR 70 and dusky colour. The nurse's first action is:$s$, $s$["Stimulate gently; if no breathing, start bag-mask ventilation", "Wait 5 minutes to see whether the apnoea resolves alone", "Give a feed by NG tube to stimulate the baby to breathe", "Silence the alarm and continue routine observation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stimulate gently; if no breathing, start bag-mask ventilation
An apnoea alarm with a slow heart rate (70) and dusky colour means the preterm baby has stopped breathing (APNOEA OF PREMATURITY). First, STIMULATE the baby gently (rub the back or feet). If breathing doesn't start, begin BAG-MASK VENTILATION. Call for help.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wait 5 minutes to see whether the apnoea resolves alone — Waiting 5 minutes could cause hypoxic brain injury or cardiac arrest.
• Give a feed by NG tube to stimulate the baby to breathe — Feeding a baby who isn't breathing risks aspiration.
• Silence the alarm and continue routine observation — Silencing the alarm without acting is dangerous.

⭐ Key point: Apnoea of prematurity is treated with caffeine citrate. Also look for other causes (sepsis, hypoglycaemia, anaemia).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q99$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2417, $s$Caffeine citrate is used in preterm babies to:$s$, $s$["Prevent neonatal hypoglycaemia", "Sedate the restless baby", "Treat apnoea of prematurity", "Treat neonatal jaundice"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Treat apnoea of prematurity
CAFFEINE CITRATE stimulates the breathing centre in the brain and improves diaphragm strength. It is used to treat (and prevent) APNOEA OF PREMATURITY, reducing apnoea episodes and the need for ventilation. It is given once daily (orally or IV).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prevent neonatal hypoglycaemia — It doesn't prevent hypoglycaemia.
• Sedate the restless baby — It's a stimulant, not a sedative.
• Treat neonatal jaundice — It doesn't treat jaundice.

⭐ Key point: Side effects of caffeine: tachycardia, feed intolerance and irritability. Monitor heart rate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q100$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2418, $s$A child with severe dehydration (10% or more) needs initial IV fluid of:$s$, $s$["Oral rehydration fluids only, by cup or spoon", "20 mL/kg boluses of 0.9% saline, reassessing each time", "5 mL/kg of 3% hypertonic saline as a single bolus", "50 mL/kg of 5% dextrose over 10 minutes, once"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 20 mL/kg boluses of 0.9% saline, reassessing each time
A child with SEVERE dehydration (10% or more) is in or near shock: very sleepy or unconscious, sunken eyes, very slow skin pinch, weak fast pulse, cold hands, and little or no urine. The first step is to restore circulation fast with IV ISOTONIC fluid — 0.9% saline (or Ringer's lactate) — given as 20 mL/kg BOLUSES. After each bolus, REASSESS (pulse, capillary refill, conscious level, urine output) and repeat if still shocked. After shock is corrected, the remaining fluid deficit plus maintenance is replaced slowly over 24–48 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral rehydration fluids only, by cup or spoon — Oral fluids alone are too slow for a severely dehydrated or shocked child, who may also be too drowsy to drink safely. ORS is used for mild to moderate dehydration.
• 5 mL/kg of 3% hypertonic saline as a single bolus — 3% hypertonic saline is used for raised intracranial pressure or severe symptomatic hyponatraemia, NOT for routine dehydration resuscitation.
• 50 mL/kg of 5% dextrose over 10 minutes, once — 5% dextrose is not suitable for resuscitation: it leaves the blood vessels quickly and can cause dangerous hyponatraemia and brain swelling. 50 mL/kg in 10 minutes is also a dangerous volume and rate.

⭐ Key point: Remember "20 mL/kg, then reassess". In a severely malnourished child, fluids are given more slowly and cautiously because the heart can easily fail.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q103$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2419, $s$For a 15 kg child, the maintenance fluid by the Holliday–Segar method is:$s$, $s$["2000 mL per 24 hours", "750 mL per 24 hours", "1500 mL per 24 hours", "1250 mL per 24 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1250 mL per 24 hours
The Holliday–Segar method calculates daily maintenance fluid by weight: 100 mL/kg for the FIRST 10 kg, 50 mL/kg for the NEXT 10 kg (11–20 kg), and 20 mL/kg for each kg ABOVE 20 kg. For a 15 kg child: first 10 kg = 10 × 100 = 1000 mL; the next 5 kg = 5 × 50 = 250 mL. Total = 1000 + 250 = 1250 mL per 24 hours (about 52 mL per hour).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 2000 mL per 24 hours — 2000 mL is far too much for 15 kg; it is roughly the requirement for a 50 kg child and would cause fluid overload.
• 750 mL per 24 hours — 750 mL is too little; it would be the requirement for only 7.5 kg.
• 1500 mL per 24 hours — 1500 mL is the requirement for a 20 kg child (1000 + 500), not 15 kg.

⭐ Key point: Hourly shortcut, the "4-2-1 rule": 4 mL/kg/hr for the first 10 kg, 2 mL/kg/hr for the next 10 kg, 1 mL/kg/hr after that. For 15 kg: 40 + 10 = 50 mL/hr. Sick children in ICU often need restricted fluids (e.g. 2/3 of maintenance) because of ADH release.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q104$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2420, $s$A child with croup has stridor at rest and recession. The first-line treatment is:$s$, $s$["Dexamethasone, plus nebulised adrenaline if severe", "Sedation with IV diazepam to calm the child", "Examining the throat with a tongue spatula", "Oral antibiotics only for five days"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dexamethasone, plus nebulised adrenaline if severe
Croup (laryngotracheobronchitis) is a viral infection causing swelling below the vocal cords. Stridor AT REST with chest recession means MODERATE to SEVERE croup. DEXAMETHASONE (0.15–0.6 mg/kg orally or IM) reduces airway swelling within a few hours. NEBULISED ADRENALINE gives rapid but short-lived relief (about 2 hours) in severe croup by shrinking the swollen airway lining. Keep the child calm on the parent's lap, give oxygen if needed, and watch closely for rebound after adrenaline.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sedation with IV diazepam to calm the child — Sedating the child is dangerous: it can reduce breathing effort and hide signs of worsening obstruction, leading to respiratory arrest.
• Examining the throat with a tongue spatula — Examining the throat with a spatula can upset the child and trigger complete airway obstruction (especially if it is actually epiglottitis).
• Oral antibiotics only for five days — Croup is viral, so antibiotics do not help, and they would not relieve the airway swelling.

⭐ Key point: Croup: barking "seal-like" cough, hoarse voice, stridor, mild fever, usually ages 6 months–3 years, often worse at night. Crying worsens the obstruction — keep the child calm and do not upset them.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q105$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2421, $s$Epiglottitis differs from croup because the child:$s$, $s$["Has a barking cough with a mild fever at night", "Has a gradual onset of symptoms over a week", "Is toxic, drooling and sitting forward, with little cough", "Improves rapidly with steam inhalation at home"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Is toxic, drooling and sitting forward, with little cough
EPIGLOTTITIS is a bacterial infection (classically Haemophilus influenzae type b) that causes the epiglottis to swell rapidly and can completely block the airway. The child looks TOXIC (very ill, high fever), DROOLS because swallowing is too painful, SITS FORWARD in a "tripod" position with the neck extended to keep the airway open, has a muffled "hot potato" voice and LITTLE OR NO COUGH. Croup, by contrast, has a barking cough, a gradual onset, and the child is less toxic.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Has a barking cough with a mild fever at night — A barking cough with mild night fever describes CROUP, not epiglottitis.
• Has a gradual onset of symptoms over a week — Epiglottitis has a RAPID onset (hours). A gradual onset over days is more typical of croup or other infections.
• Improves rapidly with steam inhalation at home — Epiglottitis does not get better with steam. It is a life-threatening emergency needing an airway expert.

⭐ Key point: In suspected epiglottitis: do NOT examine the throat, do NOT lie the child down, do NOT do painful procedures. Keep the child calm with the parent, give oxygen, and call anaesthesia/ENT to secure the airway in theatre. The Hib vaccine has made it rare.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q106$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2422, $s$Intussusception that has caused peritonitis or perforation is treated by:$s$, $s$["Oral laxatives and a high-fibre diet", "Observation for 24 hours with IV fluids", "Air enema reduction under X-ray screening", "Surgical laparotomy with reduction or resection"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Surgical laparotomy with reduction or resection
Intussusception is when one part of the bowel "telescopes" into the next, usually in infants aged 6–18 months. If the child has PERITONITIS (rigid tender abdomen) or PERFORATION (air under the diaphragm), the bowel is already dead or torn. A non-surgical enema would be dangerous. The child needs resuscitation, antibiotics and SURGICAL LAPAROTOMY — to manually reduce the bowel, or RESECT (cut out) any dead bowel.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral laxatives and a high-fibre diet — Laxatives do nothing for a mechanical blockage and may worsen it. A high-fibre diet is irrelevant in this emergency.
• Observation for 24 hours with IV fluids — Observation alone allows the dead bowel to worsen, leading to sepsis and death.
• Air enema reduction under X-ray screening — Air or contrast enema reduction is the first-line treatment ONLY if there is NO peritonitis or perforation. With perforation, the enema would push air/contrast into the abdomen and worsen the situation.

⭐ Key point: Classic signs of intussusception: sudden episodes of severe colicky pain with drawing up of the legs, vomiting, "redcurrant jelly" stools (blood and mucus), and a sausage-shaped mass in the abdomen.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q109$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2423, $s$In a child with suspected appendicitis, the ICU nurse should anticipate perforation if:$s$, $s$["There is mild nausea only, without vomiting", "The child becomes hungry and asks for food", "Pain becomes generalised with high fever and rigidity", "The pain moves from the umbilicus to the right iliac fossa"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pain becomes generalised with high fever and rigidity
When an inflamed appendix PERFORATES (bursts), pus and bowel contents spread across the abdomen, causing GENERALISED PERITONITIS. The pain spreads from the right lower area to the whole abdomen, a HIGH FEVER develops, and the abdomen becomes RIGID (board-like) and very tender. The child looks toxic and may become shocked. There may be a brief period of relief just as the appendix bursts, followed by worse pain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• There is mild nausea only, without vomiting — Mild nausea without vomiting is an early or mild feature, not a sign of perforation.
• The child becomes hungry and asks for food — A child who becomes hungry and wants to eat is usually improving. Appendicitis typically causes loss of appetite.
• The pain moves from the umbilicus to the right iliac fossa — Pain moving from the umbilicus to the right iliac fossa is the CLASSIC early pattern of appendicitis. It shows inflammation, not perforation.

⭐ Key point: Young children perforate early because they cannot describe their pain well, and their omentum (the fatty tissue that "walls off" infection) is small. Nurse NPO, IV fluids, IV antibiotics, analgesia, and prepare for urgent surgery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q110$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2424, $s$In an infant with Hirschsprung-associated enterocolitis, the priority is:$s$, $s$["Oral laxatives only, given twice daily", "Fluid resuscitation, antibiotics and rectal washouts", "A high-fibre diet and extra oral fluids", "Immediate discharge with outpatient follow-up"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fluid resuscitation, antibiotics and rectal washouts
Hirschsprung's disease is the absence of nerve cells in part of the large bowel, so that part cannot relax. Stool and bacteria build up behind it. HIRSCHSPRUNG-ASSOCIATED ENTEROCOLITIS is a serious infection and inflammation of the bowel causing fever, swollen abdomen, explosive foul-smelling diarrhoea, and quickly sepsis and shock. Priorities are: FLUID RESUSCITATION (IV fluids for dehydration/shock), IV ANTIBIOTICS (covering gut bacteria including anaerobes, e.g. metronidazole), and RECTAL WASHOUTS (to decompress the bowel and remove infected stool). The baby is kept nil by mouth with a nasogastric tube.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral laxatives only, given twice daily — Oral laxatives do not treat the infection or the blocked segment, and the baby may be too ill to take anything by mouth.
• A high-fibre diet and extra oral fluids — Diet changes do not treat this life-threatening infection.
• Immediate discharge with outpatient follow-up — Enterocolitis can kill quickly; the infant needs hospital admission, not discharge.

⭐ Key point: Hirschsprung's: delayed passing of meconium (more than 48 hours after birth), swollen abdomen, bilious vomiting, chronic constipation. Diagnosis is by rectal biopsy (no ganglion cells). Enterocolitis is the main cause of death.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q111$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2425, $s$Children with HIV in ICU commonly present with:$s$, $s$["Hypertension and haemorrhagic stroke", "Severe pneumonia, including Pneumocystis", "Kidney stones and renal colic", "Gallstones and acute cholecystitis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Severe pneumonia, including Pneumocystis
Children with HIV have weak immunity, so their most common reason for ICU admission is SEVERE PNEUMONIA. This includes ordinary bacterial pneumonia, TB, and especially PNEUMOCYSTIS JIROVECII PNEUMONIA (PCP). PCP is common in HIV-infected infants (especially 2–6 months old). It causes rapid breathing, severe hypoxia, and often little on chest examination. Treatment: high-dose co-trimoxazole and steroids (prednisolone) if hypoxic, plus oxygen and respiratory support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypertension and haemorrhagic stroke — Hypertension and haemorrhagic stroke are not typical of paediatric HIV in ICU.
• Kidney stones and renal colic — Kidney stones are not a common presentation of paediatric HIV.
• Gallstones and acute cholecystitis — Gallstones and cholecystitis are rare in children and not a typical HIV presentation.

⭐ Key point: Other common ICU problems in children with HIV: sepsis, severe malnutrition, diarrhoea with dehydration, meningitis (including cryptococcal) and TB. Always check HIV status in children with severe or recurrent pneumonia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q114$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2426, $s$Leukaemia in children most commonly presents with:$s$, $s$["Jaundice and generalised itching", "Hypertension and persistent headaches", "Weight gain and generalised oedema", "Pallor, fever, bruising and bone pain"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pallor, fever, bruising and bone pain
LEUKAEMIA is a cancer of white blood cell precursors in the bone marrow. The abnormal cells crowd out normal blood cell production. This causes: PALLOR and tiredness (anaemia – too few red cells), FEVER and infections (too few working white cells), BRUISING, petechiae and bleeding (too few platelets), and BONE PAIN (the marrow is packed with cancer cells). Other signs: enlarged lymph nodes, liver and spleen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Jaundice and generalised itching — Jaundice and itching are mainly features of liver or bile duct disease, not the typical presentation of childhood leukaemia.
• Hypertension and persistent headaches — Hypertension and headache are not the classic presentation.
• Weight gain and generalised oedema — Weight gain and oedema are features of kidney disease (e.g. nephrotic syndrome). Leukaemia usually causes weight LOSS.

⭐ Key point: Acute lymphoblastic leukaemia (ALL) is the commonest childhood cancer. A full blood count with blood film is the first test; diagnosis is confirmed by bone marrow examination.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q119$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2427, $s$A child receiving chemotherapy has fever 38.5 °C. The nurse should first:$s$, $s$["Send the child home with oral antibiotics", "Give paracetamol and wait to see if the fever settles", "Give a live vaccine to boost the immune system", "Take blood cultures and give IV antibiotics within 1 hour"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Take blood cultures and give IV antibiotics within 1 hour
A child on chemotherapy usually has a very low white cell count (NEUTROPENIA), so they cannot fight infection properly. Fever of 38 °C or above may be the ONLY sign of a severe infection (NEUTROPENIC SEPSIS), which can kill within hours. The nurse should immediately TAKE BLOOD CULTURES (from the central line and peripherally) and START BROAD-SPECTRUM IV ANTIBIOTICS WITHIN 1 HOUR of arrival ("door-to-needle" time). Do not wait for the blood count result.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Send the child home with oral antibiotics — Sending the child home is unsafe; neutropenic sepsis needs urgent IV antibiotics and close monitoring in hospital.
• Give paracetamol and wait to see if the fever settles — Waiting with paracetamol delays treatment and may hide the fever, while sepsis progresses.
• Give a live vaccine to boost the immune system — Live vaccines are DANGEROUS in immunocompromised children because the vaccine organism can cause serious infection.

⭐ Key point: Avoid rectal temperatures and rectal medicines in neutropenic children (risk of bleeding and infection). Use strict hand hygiene and protective isolation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q120$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2428, $s$Therapeutic amnioreduction may be performed for polyhydramnios when:$s$, $s$["There is a placenta praevia on scan", "The fetus is growth-restricted on scan", "The mother has severe breathlessness or discomfort", "The amniotic fluid index measures 10 cm"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The mother has severe breathlessness or discomfort
POLYHYDRAMNIOS is too much amniotic fluid. It stretches the uterus, which pushes up on the diaphragm and causes maternal BREATHLESSNESS, pain and discomfort, and can trigger preterm labour. THERAPEUTIC AMNIOREDUCTION — removing amniotic fluid with a needle through the abdomen — is done mainly to relieve SEVERE MATERNAL SYMPTOMS (breathlessness, discomfort) or to reduce the risk of preterm labour. The fluid is removed slowly to avoid a sudden drop in uterine pressure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• There is a placenta praevia on scan — Placenta praevia is not an indication for amnioreduction. A needle procedure near a low placenta may cause bleeding.
• The fetus is growth-restricted on scan — Fetal growth restriction is usually linked to REDUCED fluid (oligohydramnios), not polyhydramnios.
• The amniotic fluid index measures 10 cm — An AFI of 10 cm is NORMAL (normal range about 5–24 cm). There is no excess fluid to remove.

⭐ Key point: Rapid removal of large volumes can cause placental abruption. Polyhydramnios causes include maternal diabetes, fetal swallowing problems (oesophageal atresia, anencephaly), twin-twin transfusion, and fetal anaemia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q121$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2429, $s$Indomethacin may reduce polyhydramnios by:$s$, $s$["Increasing maternal urine output", "Increasing fetal swallowing", "Causing uterine contractions", "Reducing fetal urine production"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reducing fetal urine production
Most amniotic fluid in later pregnancy is FETAL URINE. INDOMETHACIN (an NSAID) reduces blood flow to the fetal kidneys and increases ADH effects, so the fetus produces LESS URINE. This reduces the amount of amniotic fluid. It is sometimes used to treat polyhydramnios.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increasing maternal urine output — Indomethacin acts on the FETAL kidneys, not by increasing the mother's urine output.
• Increasing fetal swallowing — It does not increase fetal swallowing; it works by reducing fetal urine production.
• Causing uterine contractions — Indomethacin actually REDUCES uterine contractions (it is also used as a tocolytic). It does not cause them.

⭐ Key point: Indomethacin can cause premature closure of the fetal DUCTUS ARTERIOSUS (fetal heart problems) and oligohydramnios, so it is usually avoided after 32 weeks and fluid levels are monitored closely.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q122$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2430, $s$Oligohydramnios is associated with:$s$, $s$["Renal agenesis, placental insufficiency, ruptured membranes", "Fetal oesophageal atresia with absent stomach bubble", "Poorly controlled maternal diabetes with macrosomia", "Fetal anencephaly with failure of swallowing"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Renal agenesis, placental insufficiency, ruptured membranes
OLIGOHYDRAMNIOS (too little amniotic fluid) happens when the fetus makes too little urine or fluid leaks out. The main causes are: (1) RENAL AGENESIS or blocked urinary tract (no fetal urine); (2) PLACENTAL INSUFFICIENCY (poor blood flow to the fetus, so the fetus diverts blood away from the kidneys); (3) RUPTURED MEMBRANES (fluid leaks out). It can also occur in post-term pregnancy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fetal oesophageal atresia with absent stomach bubble — Oesophageal atresia stops the fetus from swallowing fluid, so fluid builds up — causing POLYhydramnios, not oligohydramnios.
• Poorly controlled maternal diabetes with macrosomia — Poorly controlled diabetes causes fetal polyuria (more urine), so it causes POLYhydramnios.
• Fetal anencephaly with failure of swallowing — Anencephaly impairs swallowing, causing POLYhydramnios.

⭐ Key point: Oligohydramnios can cause pulmonary hypoplasia (underdeveloped lungs, because lungs need fluid to grow), limb deformities, cord compression and fetal distress. Potter sequence is seen with renal agenesis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q123$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2431, $s$Symmetrical IUGR is more commonly due to:$s$, $s$["A post-term pregnancy beyond 42 completed weeks", "Early insults, e.g. chromosomal anomaly or congenital infection", "Late placental insufficiency in the third trimester", "Maternal pre-eclampsia developing after 34 weeks"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Early insults, e.g. chromosomal anomaly or congenital infection
In SYMMETRICAL IUGR (intrauterine growth restriction), the head, abdomen and limbs are ALL proportionally small. It usually results from an EARLY insult (first or early second trimester) that limits the total number of cells formed, such as CHROMOSOMAL ABNORMALITIES (e.g. trisomy), CONGENITAL INFECTIONS (TORCH: toxoplasmosis, rubella, CMV, herpes) or exposure to toxins (alcohol, smoking, drugs).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A post-term pregnancy beyond 42 completed weeks — Post-term pregnancy affects growth late, usually causing asymmetrical growth problems, not symmetrical IUGR.
• Late placental insufficiency in the third trimester — Late placental insufficiency causes ASYMMETRICAL IUGR: the fetus protects ("spares") the brain, so the head is relatively normal but the abdomen is small.
• Maternal pre-eclampsia developing after 34 weeks — Pre-eclampsia developing late usually causes asymmetrical IUGR through placental insufficiency.

⭐ Key point: Symmetrical = early cause, everything small. Asymmetrical = late placental cause, "head-sparing" with thin abdomen. Asymmetrical is more common.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q124$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2432, $s$Fetal surveillance in IUGR includes:$s$, $s$["Urine dipstick for protein at each visit only", "No monitoring until delivery is planned", "Maternal weight at each antenatal visit only", "Serial growth scans, umbilical Doppler and CTG"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Serial growth scans, umbilical Doppler and CTG
A growth-restricted fetus may not be getting enough oxygen and nutrients. Monitoring checks fetal growth and wellbeing: SERIAL GROWTH SCANS (every 2 weeks) show whether the fetus is still growing; UMBILICAL ARTERY DOPPLER shows how well the placenta is working — absent or reversed end-diastolic flow is a serious warning sign; CTG checks the fetal heart rate pattern for signs of distress. Amniotic fluid volume is also measured. These results guide the timing of delivery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Urine dipstick for protein at each visit only — Urine dipstick checks the mother for pre-eclampsia, but does not monitor fetal growth or wellbeing.
• No monitoring until delivery is planned — No monitoring is dangerous; the fetus could die in the womb without warning.
• Maternal weight at each antenatal visit only — Maternal weight is a poor guide to fetal growth and does not show placental function.

⭐ Key point: Abnormal Doppler (absent or reversed end-diastolic flow) means the fetus is at high risk; delivery is often planned early, with antenatal steroids and magnesium sulphate if preterm.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q125$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2433, $s$Antenatal corticosteroids before preterm delivery are given mainly to:$s$, $s$["Mature the fetal lungs and reduce RDS", "Prevent maternal infection after delivery", "Stop uterine contractions in preterm labour", "Treat maternal hypertension in pre-eclampsia"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mature the fetal lungs and reduce RDS
ANTENATAL CORTICOSTEROIDS (betamethasone or dexamethasone given IM to the mother) cross the placenta and speed up FETAL LUNG MATURITY by stimulating SURFACTANT production. This reduces RESPIRATORY DISTRESS SYNDROME (RDS), as well as brain bleeding (intraventricular haemorrhage), necrotising enterocolitis and newborn death. They work best when delivery occurs between 24 hours and 7 days after the first dose.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prevent maternal infection after delivery — Steroids do not prevent maternal infection; they can slightly increase infection risk.
• Stop uterine contractions in preterm labour — Steroids do not stop contractions. Tocolytics (e.g. nifedipine) are used to delay labour so the steroids have time to work.
• Treat maternal hypertension in pre-eclampsia — Steroids do not treat hypertension. Pre-eclampsia is treated with antihypertensives and magnesium sulphate.

⭐ Key point: Typical regimen: dexamethasone 6 mg IM every 12 hours × 4 doses, or betamethasone 12 mg IM every 24 hours × 2 doses. Usually given between 24 and 34 weeks. Monitor blood glucose in diabetic mothers.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q126$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2434, $s$Magnesium sulphate given before very preterm birth (under 32 weeks) protects the baby against:$s$, $s$["Neonatal sepsis", "Respiratory distress syndrome", "Neonatal jaundice", "Cerebral palsy"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cerebral palsy
MAGNESIUM SULPHATE given to the mother shortly before VERY PRETERM birth (under 32 weeks) protects the baby's developing brain (NEUROPROTECTION). Studies show it significantly reduces the risk of CEREBRAL PALSY. It is thought to stabilise blood vessels in the brain, reduce inflammation, and block harmful brain cell damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Neonatal sepsis — Magnesium does not prevent neonatal sepsis; antibiotics are used for that.
• Respiratory distress syndrome — RDS is prevented by antenatal corticosteroids, not magnesium.
• Neonatal jaundice — Neonatal jaundice is not prevented by magnesium.

⭐ Key point: Monitor the mother on magnesium for toxicity: loss of knee reflexes, breathing rate below 12/min, urine output below 30 mL/hour. Antidote: calcium gluconate 10% IV.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q127$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2435, $s$Signs of uterine rupture during labour include:$s$, $s$["A rising blood pressure with headache and visual change", "A painless vaginal discharge with normal CTG", "Sudden pain, contractions stop, maternal shock, fetal distress", "A gradual increase in contraction strength only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sudden pain, contractions stop, maternal shock, fetal distress
UTERINE RUPTURE is a tear in the uterine wall, usually through a previous caesarean scar or in obstructed labour or with excessive oxytocin. Signs: SUDDEN severe abdominal pain (sometimes a "tearing" sensation), CONTRACTIONS STOP suddenly, MATERNAL SHOCK (fast pulse, low BP, pallor) from internal bleeding, FETAL DISTRESS or loss of fetal heart, fetal parts felt easily under the abdominal skin, the presenting part moving back up, and vaginal bleeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A rising blood pressure with headache and visual change — Rising BP with headache and visual changes suggests PRE-ECLAMPSIA, not uterine rupture.
• A painless vaginal discharge with normal CTG — Painless discharge with normal CTG is not consistent with rupture, which causes pain, shock and fetal distress.
• A gradual increase in contraction strength only — Contractions STOP or weaken with rupture; a gradual increase in strength is normal labour.

⭐ Key point: Uterine rupture is an obstetric emergency: resuscitate (two large IV lines, blood), call for help and prepare for immediate laparotomy. Risk factors: previous caesarean, obstructed labour, grand multiparity and oxytocin misuse.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q128$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2436, $s$Septic shock in a postnatal woman with a retained placenta requires:$s$, $s$["An oxytocin infusion alone, with observation", "Resuscitation, antibiotics and evacuation of retained products", "Waiting for spontaneous passage of the products", "Antibiotics alone, without any surgical procedure"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Resuscitation, antibiotics and evacuation of retained products
Retained placental tissue in the uterus becomes infected and acts as a SOURCE of sepsis. The woman is in SEPTIC SHOCK. Treatment has three parts: (1) RESUSCITATION — oxygen, IV fluids, and vasopressors if BP stays low; (2) broad-spectrum IV ANTIBIOTICS within one hour; (3) SOURCE CONTROL — EVACUATION OF RETAINED PRODUCTS of conception (surgical removal), once she is stabilised. Without removing the infected tissue, the infection will not clear.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An oxytocin infusion alone, with observation — Oxytocin may help the uterus contract, but alone it does not treat septic shock or reliably remove infected tissue.
• Waiting for spontaneous passage of the products — Waiting allows the infection and shock to worsen, and may be fatal.
• Antibiotics alone, without any surgical procedure — Antibiotics alone cannot clear an infection that has a source of dead/infected tissue still present.

⭐ Key point: Sepsis Six: give oxygen, take blood cultures, give IV antibiotics, give IV fluids, measure lactate, and monitor urine output — all within 1 hour.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q129$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2437, $s$Breast abscess in a postnatal woman is managed by:$s$, $s$["Observation without any treatment for a week", "Stopping breastfeeding on both sides permanently", "Cold compresses only, without antibiotics", "Drainage, antibiotics and continued milk removal"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Drainage, antibiotics and continued milk removal
A BREAST ABSCESS is a collection of pus in the breast, usually caused by Staphylococcus aureus entering through cracked nipples. Treatment: DRAINAGE of the pus (needle aspiration under ultrasound, or incision and drainage), ANTIBIOTICS (e.g. flucloxacillin), pain relief, and CONTINUED MILK REMOVAL. Breastfeeding can continue from the unaffected breast, and milk is expressed from the affected side (or the baby may feed from it if the drainage site is away from the nipple). Stopping milk removal causes milk stasis, which worsens the infection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Observation without any treatment for a week — An abscess will not go away by observation; untreated it can spread and cause sepsis.
• Stopping breastfeeding on both sides permanently — Stopping breastfeeding on both sides is not necessary and causes milk stasis, making things worse.
• Cold compresses only, without antibiotics — Cold compresses can ease pain, but an abscess needs drainage and antibiotics.

⭐ Key point: Prevent mastitis and abscess: good latch, frequent feeding, emptying the breasts, and early treatment of mastitis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q130$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2438, $s$The '4 Ts' of postpartum haemorrhage are:$s$, $s$["Tests, Transfusion, Treatment, Transfer", "Tachycardia, Tachypnoea, Temperature, Thirst", "Tone, Trauma, Tissue, Thrombin", "Temperature, Time, Tachycardia, Tension"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Tone, Trauma, Tissue, Thrombin
The 4 Ts are the causes of POSTPARTUM HAEMORRHAGE (PPH): TONE — the uterus does not contract well (uterine atony), the commonest cause (about 70%); TRAUMA — tears of the cervix, vagina or perineum, or uterine rupture; TISSUE — retained placenta or membranes; THROMBIN — clotting problems (e.g. DIC, HELLP, anticoagulants). This helps the team find and treat the cause quickly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tests, Transfusion, Treatment, Transfer — Tests, transfusion, treatment and transfer describe management steps, not the causes of PPH.
• Tachycardia, Tachypnoea, Temperature, Thirst — Tachycardia, tachypnoea, temperature and thirst are signs, not the causes.
• Temperature, Time, Tachycardia, Tension — Temperature, time, tachycardia and tension are not the recognised framework.

⭐ Key point: First response to PPH: call for help, rub up the uterus (fundal massage), give oxytocin, IV access with blood samples, fluids, empty the bladder, check the placenta is complete, and look for tears.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q131 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2439, $s$Tranexamic acid in postpartum haemorrhage should be given:$s$, $s$["Within 3 hours of birth", "Only after 24 hours", "Only if the patient is not bleeding", "Only after hysterectomy"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Within 3 hours of birth
TRANEXAMIC ACID (TXA) stops clots from breaking down (antifibrinolytic). The WOMAN trial showed that giving 1 g IV TXA WITHIN 3 HOURS OF BIRTH in PPH reduces deaths from bleeding. If given later than 3 hours, it gives no benefit. A second dose can be given if bleeding continues after 30 minutes or restarts within 24 hours.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only after 24 hours — After 24 hours is too late; the benefit is only within the first 3 hours.
• Only if the patient is not bleeding — TXA is given TO STOP bleeding; it is not used when there is no bleeding (in PPH treatment).
• Only after hysterectomy — TXA is given early to try to AVOID needing a hysterectomy, not only afterwards.

⭐ Key point: TXA dose: 1 g IV over 10 minutes. The earlier it is given, the better. It is added to — not instead of — oxytocin and other PPH treatments.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q132 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2440, $s$A ruptured ectopic pregnancy with shock requires:$s$, $s$["Observation with a repeat scan in 48 hours", "Methotrexate injection and outpatient review", "Oral analgesia and discharge home", "Resuscitation with blood and urgent laparotomy"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Resuscitation with blood and urgent laparotomy
A RUPTURED ECTOPIC PREGNANCY means the pregnancy has burst through the fallopian tube, causing major internal bleeding into the abdomen. With SHOCK (fast pulse, low BP, pallor, collapse), the woman can bleed to death. She needs immediate RESUSCITATION (two large IV lines, cross-match and give BLOOD) and URGENT LAPAROTOMY (or laparoscopy) to stop the bleeding, usually by removing the tube (salpingectomy).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Observation with a repeat scan in 48 hours — Waiting 48 hours for a repeat scan would allow fatal bleeding.
• Methotrexate injection and outpatient review — Methotrexate is only for a stable, UNRUPTURED, small ectopic pregnancy. It does not stop active bleeding.
• Oral analgesia and discharge home — Discharging a woman in shock with internal bleeding would be fatal.

⭐ Key point: Suspect ectopic in any woman of reproductive age with lower abdominal pain, missed period, vaginal bleeding, shoulder tip pain (blood irritating the diaphragm) or collapse. Do a pregnancy test early.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q133$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2441, $s$HELLP syndrome stands for:$s$, $s$["Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets", "Haemorrhage, Electrolytes, Lactate, Pressure", "High Enzymes, Low Lipids, Proteinuria", "Hypertension, Eclampsia, Liver failure, Pain"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets
HELLP syndrome stands for: H – HAEMOLYSIS (red cells breaking down), EL – ELEVATED LIVER enzymes (liver damage), LP – LOW PLATELETS. It is a severe complication of pre-eclampsia. Symptoms: pain in the upper right abdomen or epigastrium, nausea and vomiting, malaise and headache. It can cause liver rupture, DIC, kidney failure and maternal death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Haemorrhage, Electrolytes, Lactate, Pressure — Haemorrhage, electrolytes, lactate and pressure is not the correct meaning.
• High Enzymes, Low Lipids, Proteinuria — High enzymes, low lipids and proteinuria is incorrect.
• Hypertension, Eclampsia, Liver failure, Pain — Hypertension, eclampsia, liver failure and pain is incorrect.

⭐ Key point: The definitive treatment of HELLP is DELIVERY of the baby. Also give magnesium sulphate (seizure prevention), control BP, and correct clotting problems.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q134 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2442, $s$A woman in the third trimester with sudden breathlessness, chest pain and tachycardia most likely has:$s$, $s$["Pulmonary embolism", "Normal pregnancy breathlessness", "Mild asthma", "Heartburn"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pulmonary embolism
Pregnancy is a HYPERCOAGULABLE state (the blood clots more easily). The growing uterus also slows blood flow from the legs. A deep vein thrombosis can break off and travel to the lungs, causing PULMONARY EMBOLISM (PE). Sudden breathlessness, pleuritic chest pain, tachycardia, low oxygen saturation and sometimes collapse or coughing blood strongly suggest PE. It is a leading cause of maternal death. Treat with LMWH (low-molecular-weight heparin) immediately if suspected, while confirming the diagnosis (CTPA or V/Q scan, leg Doppler).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal pregnancy breathlessness — Normal pregnancy breathlessness comes on gradually, without chest pain, tachycardia or low oxygen.
• Mild asthma — Mild asthma would cause wheeze, but not usually sudden chest pain with tachycardia in this setting.
• Heartburn — Heartburn causes burning chest discomfort after meals but does not cause sudden breathlessness or tachycardia.

⭐ Key point: Warfarin is avoided in pregnancy (it damages the fetus); LMWH is safe. Risk factors include caesarean section, obesity, immobility, age over 35, and previous clots.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q137$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2443, $s$A pregnant woman collapses. The nurse should immediately:$s$, $s$["Give oral fluids and raise the legs", "Call for help, start CPR, displace the uterus to the left", "Lie her flat on her back without any tilt", "Wait for the obstetrician before starting CPR"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Call for help, start CPR, displace the uterus to the left
If a pregnant woman collapses: CALL FOR HELP (including obstetric and neonatal teams), START CPR immediately, and MANUALLY DISPLACE THE UTERUS TO THE LEFT. After about 20 weeks, the uterus presses on the inferior vena cava (aortocaval compression) when the woman lies flat, which reduces blood returning to the heart and makes CPR ineffective. Pushing the uterus to the left relieves this compression. Hand position for compressions is the same as for other adults.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Give oral fluids and raise the legs — Oral fluids are dangerous in an unconscious patient (aspiration). Raising the legs alone is not resuscitation.
• Lie her flat on her back without any tilt — Lying flat without tilt or displacement allows the uterus to compress the vena cava, reducing the effectiveness of CPR.
• Wait for the obstetrician before starting CPR — Waiting for the obstetrician wastes vital minutes; CPR must start at once.

⭐ Key point: If no return of circulation within 4 minutes of arrest, perform a PERIMORTEM CAESAREAN (resuscitative hysterotomy) so the baby is delivered within 5 minutes. This also improves the mother's chances of survival.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q138$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2444, $s$The main cause of maternal death in Nigeria is:$s$, $s$["Diabetes in pregnancy", "Postpartum haemorrhage", "Migraine with aura", "Acute severe asthma"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Postpartum haemorrhage
POSTPARTUM HAEMORRHAGE (PPH) — heavy bleeding after childbirth — is the LEADING CAUSE of maternal death in Nigeria and many low-resource countries. Many deliveries happen without skilled attendants, and blood, oxytocin, and emergency surgery may not be readily available. Other major causes include pre-eclampsia/eclampsia, sepsis, obstructed labour and unsafe abortion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diabetes in pregnancy — Diabetes is an important risk but is not the main cause of maternal death in Nigeria.
• Migraine with aura — Migraine is not a significant cause of maternal death.
• Acute severe asthma — Severe asthma is rare as a cause of maternal death compared with PPH.

⭐ Key point: Prevention of PPH: active management of the third stage of labour (oxytocin after birth, controlled cord traction, uterine massage), skilled birth attendants, and treating anaemia during pregnancy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q139$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2445, $s$Puerperal pyrexia is defined as a temperature of 38 °C or above:$s$, $s$["Only after the 6-week postnatal check", "Only during the first stage of labour", "Within 14 days after childbirth or miscarriage", "Only in the first hour after delivery"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Within 14 days after childbirth or miscarriage
PUERPERAL PYREXIA is a temperature of 38 °C or above occurring WITHIN 14 DAYS after childbirth or miscarriage (classically on any two days, or a single high reading). It must be investigated as a possible infection: genital tract (endometritis — the commonest serious cause), urinary tract, breasts (mastitis), wound (caesarean or episiotomy), chest, and DVT. Puerperal sepsis is a major cause of maternal death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only after the 6-week postnatal check — The definition covers the first 14 days, not only after the 6-week postnatal check.
• Only during the first stage of labour — Fever during labour is "intrapartum fever", not puerperal pyrexia.
• Only in the first hour after delivery — The definition is not limited to the first hour; it covers 14 days.

⭐ Key point: Investigations: blood cultures, high vaginal swab, urine culture, wound swab, FBC, CRP. Start antibiotics promptly if sepsis is suspected.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q140$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2446, $s$For a child of Jehovah's Witness parents who needs life-saving blood, the team usually:$s$, $s$["Seeks a court order, or acts in the child's best interests in an emergency", "Accepts the parents' refusal even though the child will probably die", "Asks the young child to make the final decision about the blood alone", "Gives no further treatment of any kind to the child until discharge"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Seeks a court order, or acts in the child's best interests in an emergency
A child cannot make this decision alone, and parents cannot refuse life-saving treatment that is in the child's best interests. In an EMERGENCY where delay would cause death or serious harm, doctors may give the blood in the child's BEST INTERESTS. When there is time, the hospital usually SEEKS A COURT ORDER to authorise treatment. The child's welfare is paramount. Bloodless alternatives are still used wherever possible, and the family's beliefs are treated with respect.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Accepts the parents' refusal even though the child will probably die — Accepting a refusal that leads to the child's death goes against the duty to protect the child.
• Asks the young child to make the final decision about the blood alone — A young child is usually not able to make such a serious decision alone.
• Gives no further treatment of any kind to the child until discharge — Stopping all treatment would harm the child; care must continue.

⭐ Key point: Parents' rights are limited by the child's best interests. Document everything clearly and involve senior staff and the hospital legal team.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q144$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2447, $s$A 2-year-old with intussusception becomes lethargic and the abdomen is rigid. The nurse should prepare for:$s$, $s$["Air enema reduction under imaging", "Discharge home with oral antibiotics", "Urgent laparotomy, not enema reduction", "Oral rehydration solution only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Urgent laparotomy, not enema reduction
Intussusception can cut off the blood supply to the trapped bowel. If the bowel dies (gangrene) or perforates, the child develops PERITONITIS: a rigid, tender abdomen, lethargy, fever and shock. An air or contrast enema is then CONTRAINDICATED, because it would push air or contrast through the hole. The child needs resuscitation and an urgent LAPAROTOMY (open surgery).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Air enema reduction under imaging — Air enema reduction is for stable children WITHOUT signs of perforation or peritonitis.
• Discharge home with oral antibiotics — This child is critically ill, so discharge would be fatal.
• Oral rehydration solution only — Oral fluids are dangerous; the child needs nil by mouth, IV fluids and surgery.

⭐ Key point: Contraindications to enema reduction: peritonitis, perforation (free air on X-ray), shock that doesn't respond to fluids.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q101$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2448, $s$A classic triad of intussusception is:$s$, $s$["Bilious vomiting, jaundice and pale, chalky stools", "Projectile vomiting, an olive mass and a metabolic alkalosis", "Colicky pain, sausage-shaped mass, redcurrant jelly stool", "Fever, rash, conjunctivitis and peeling of the fingertips"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Colicky pain, sausage-shaped mass, redcurrant jelly stool
The classic triad of intussusception: 1) COLICKY abdominal pain (the child screams and draws up the legs in episodes, quiet between), 2) a SAUSAGE-SHAPED mass in the abdomen (the telescoped bowel), and 3) REDCURRANT JELLY stools (blood and mucus, a late sign). Vomiting is also common. Not every child has all three.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bilious vomiting, jaundice and pale, chalky stools — Bilious vomiting, jaundice and pale stools suggest biliary problems (such as biliary atresia).
• Projectile vomiting, an olive mass and a metabolic alkalosis — Projectile vomiting, an olive mass and metabolic alkalosis describe PYLORIC STENOSIS.
• Fever, rash, conjunctivitis and peeling of the fingertips — Fever, rash, conjunctivitis and peeling fingertips describe KAWASAKI disease.

⭐ Key point: Intussusception usually affects children aged 3 months to 3 years, often after a viral illness (swollen lymph tissue in the bowel acts as the lead point).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q102$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2449, $s$Appendicitis in a child under 5 is more dangerous because:$s$, $s$["Early perforation; the omentum is poorly developed", "Young children always present very early", "The pain is always well localised in them", "Appendicitis never occurs below this age"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Early perforation; the omentum is poorly developed
In young children (under 5), appendicitis is often diagnosed LATE, because the child can't describe the pain clearly and the symptoms are vague (vomiting, irritability, fever). Their OMENTUM (the fatty apron that normally wraps around and contains an infected appendix) is short and poorly developed, so it can't wall off the infection. Perforation happens early and spreads, causing generalised peritonitis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Young children always present very early — Young children usually present LATE, not early.
• The pain is always well localised in them — Pain in young children is often POORLY localised.
• Appendicitis never occurs below this age — Appendicitis does occur in young children, though less often.

⭐ Key point: Perforation rates are very high in children under 5. Have a high suspicion and get a surgical review early.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q103$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2450, $s$Hirschsprung's disease is treated definitively by:$s$, $s$["Long-term laxatives and a high-fibre diet", "Appendicectomy and a temporary ileostomy", "A pull-through operation removing the aganglionic bowel", "Rectal irrigation for life, without surgery"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A pull-through operation removing the aganglionic bowel
In Hirschsprung's disease, a segment of the bowel (usually the rectum and part of the colon) has NO nerve cells (aganglionic), so it can't relax and push stool along. The definitive treatment is a PULL-THROUGH operation: the abnormal bowel is removed and normal bowel (with nerve cells) is pulled down and joined to the anus. Sometimes a temporary colostomy is done first.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Long-term laxatives and a high-fibre diet — Laxatives and fibre don't fix the missing nerve cells.
• Appendicectomy and a temporary ileostomy — Appendicectomy has no role; an ileostomy may be done in some cases but is not the definitive treatment.
• Rectal irrigation for life, without surgery — Rectal irrigation is a temporary measure before surgery, not lifelong treatment.

⭐ Key point: Watch for Hirschsprung's enterocolitis (fever, distension, explosive foul diarrhoea) before and after surgery. It can be life-threatening.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q104$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2451, $s$Epiglottitis has become less common because of vaccination against:$s$, $s$["Tuberculosis (BCG)", "Measles (rubeola virus)", "Rotavirus infection", "Haemophilus influenzae type b"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Haemophilus influenzae type b
Epiglottitis was mostly caused by Haemophilus influenzae type b (Hib). The Hib vaccine, given as part of the pentavalent vaccine in the Nigerian childhood immunisation schedule (at 6, 10 and 14 weeks), has made epiglottitis much rarer in children.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tuberculosis (BCG) — BCG protects against severe forms of TB.
• Measles (rubeola virus) — Measles vaccine protects against measles.
• Rotavirus infection — Rotavirus vaccine protects against rotavirus diarrhoea.

⭐ Key point: Pentavalent vaccine covers diphtheria, pertussis, tetanus, hepatitis B and Hib. Epiglottitis can still occur in unvaccinated children and adults.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q106$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2452, $s$A child with suspected epiglottitis should:$s$, $s$["Lie flat on a trolley for a lateral neck X-ray", "Be kept calm with a parent, with airway expert present", "Have an IV cannula inserted immediately", "Have the throat examined with a spatula at once"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Be kept calm with a parent, with airway expert present
In epiglottitis, the swollen epiglottis can block the airway completely at any moment. Upsetting the child (crying, lying down, examining the throat, inserting a cannula) can trigger complete obstruction and cardiac arrest. Keep the child CALM, sitting up on a parent's lap, with oxygen given gently if tolerated, and call the most experienced airway experts (anaesthetist, ENT) to secure the airway in theatre.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lie flat on a trolley for a lateral neck X-ray — Lying flat can cause the epiglottis to fall back and block the airway.
• Have an IV cannula inserted immediately — Painful procedures like cannulation can cause panic and obstruction; delay until the airway is safe.
• Have the throat examined with a spatula at once — Examining the throat with a spatula can trigger laryngospasm and complete obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Signs: toxic-looking child, high fever, drooling, tripod sitting, muffled voice, soft stridor, no cough. Don't upset the child.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q107$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2453, $s$Sudden infant death syndrome is most common at age:$s$, $s$["1–2 years", "5–6 years", "Newborn first week", "2–4 months"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2–4 months
Sudden infant death syndrome (SIDS) is the sudden, unexplained death of an apparently healthy baby during sleep. It is most common at 2–4 MONTHS of age, and 90% of cases occur before 6 months. Risk factors: sleeping on the front (prone), parental smoking, overheating, soft bedding, bed-sharing, and prematurity.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1–2 years — SIDS is rare after 1 year.
• 5–6 years — SIDS doesn't occur at 5–6 years.
• Newborn first week — The first week of life is not the peak period.

⭐ Key point: Prevent SIDS: sleep on the back, in the parents' room but in their own cot, firm mattress, no pillows or loose bedding, no smoking, avoid overheating, and breastfeed.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q108$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2454, $s$Severe pneumonia in a child is suggested by:$s$, $s$["A normal breathing rate for the child's age", "Chest indrawing, grunting or SpO₂ below 90%", "A mild dry cough lasting for a few days", "A runny nose with sneezing and mild fever"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Chest indrawing, grunting or SpO₂ below 90%
WHO signs of SEVERE pneumonia in children include CHEST INDRAWING (the lower chest wall pulls in when breathing in), GRUNTING (a short sound when breathing out, keeping the alveoli open), oxygen saturation BELOW 90%, central cyanosis, inability to drink or breastfeed, convulsions, lethargy and vomiting everything. These children need hospital admission, oxygen and IV antibiotics.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal breathing rate for the child's age — A normal breathing rate is not a sign of pneumonia.
• A mild dry cough lasting for a few days — A mild dry cough for a few days suggests a simple cold or viral illness.
• A runny nose with sneezing and mild fever — A runny nose with sneezing is a cold (upper respiratory infection).

⭐ Key point: Pneumonia is a leading cause of death in children under 5 in Nigeria. Give oxygen if SpO₂ is below 90% (some guidelines use 92%).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q115$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2455, $s$The respiratory rate considered fast breathing in a child aged 2–12 months is:$s$, $s$["20 breaths/min or more", "80 breaths/min or more", "50 breaths/min or more", "30 breaths/min or more"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 50 breaths/min or more
WHO fast breathing thresholds (counted for a full minute while the child is calm): under 2 months, 60 breaths/min or more; 2–12 months, 50 breaths/min or more; 1–5 years, 40 breaths/min or more. A child aged 2–12 months breathing 50 or more per minute has fast breathing, which suggests pneumonia.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 20 breaths/min or more — 20 breaths/min is normal for an older child, not fast for an infant.
• 80 breaths/min or more — 80 breaths/min is very fast, but the threshold is 50.
• 30 breaths/min or more — 30 breaths/min is normal for an infant.

⭐ Key point: Count breaths for a FULL minute when the child is calm (not crying or feeding), because the rate varies.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q116$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2456, $s$Kwashiorkor in a critically ill child presents with:$s$, $s$["Oedema, skin changes and low albumin", "High albumin with polycythaemia", "Obesity with hypertension", "Severe wasting without any oedema"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Oedema, skin changes and low albumin
Kwashiorkor is a form of severe acute malnutrition with PROTEIN deficiency. Low blood albumin lets fluid leak into the tissues, causing OEDEMA (swelling of the feet, legs and face). Other features: skin changes (peeling, "flaky paint" skin), thin, pale hair, an enlarged fatty liver, apathy, and misery. Muscle wasting is hidden under the oedema.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High albumin with polycythaemia — Albumin is LOW in kwashiorkor, and polycythaemia is not a feature.
• Obesity with hypertension — Obesity with hypertension is the opposite of malnutrition.
• Severe wasting without any oedema — Severe wasting WITHOUT oedema describes MARASMUS.

⭐ Key point: Danger in SAM: hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, infection and dehydration. Feed slowly (F-75 formula first) to avoid refeeding syndrome.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q119$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2457, $s$Cervical cerclage is usually inserted at:$s$, $s$["12–14 weeks of gestation", "4–6 weeks of gestation", "28–30 weeks of gestation", "36–38 weeks of gestation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 12–14 weeks of gestation
Cervical cerclage is a stitch placed around the cervix to keep it closed in women with cervical weakness (insufficiency). A planned (history-indicated) cerclage is usually placed at 12–14 WEEKS, after the first-trimester scan confirms a healthy pregnancy and after most miscarriages for other reasons would have occurred.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 4–6 weeks of gestation — At 4–6 weeks it's too early; the pregnancy may not yet be confirmed as viable.
• 28–30 weeks of gestation — At 28–30 weeks it's too late for routine placement (an emergency rescue cerclage may be placed up to about 24 weeks).
• 36–38 weeks of gestation — At 36–38 weeks the stitch is REMOVED, not inserted.

⭐ Key point: The stitch is removed at about 36–37 weeks, or immediately if labour starts or the membranes rupture, to prevent cervical tearing.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q122$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2458, $s$The '3 delays' model for maternal death refers to delays in:$s$, $s$["Deciding to seek care, reaching care, receiving care", "Diagnosis, treatment and payment of bills", "Labour, delivery and discharge from hospital", "Booking, scanning and laboratory testing"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Deciding to seek care, reaching care, receiving care
The "three delays" model explains why women die in pregnancy and childbirth. 1) DELAY IN DECIDING to seek care (not recognising danger signs, cultural barriers, cost). 2) DELAY IN REACHING care (distance, transport, roads). 3) DELAY IN RECEIVING care at the facility (staff shortages, lack of blood, drugs or equipment). Reducing each delay saves lives.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diagnosis, treatment and payment of bills — Diagnosis, treatment and payment are not the three delays.
• Labour, delivery and discharge from hospital — Labour, delivery and discharge are not the three delays.
• Booking, scanning and laboratory testing — Booking, scanning and testing are not the three delays.

⭐ Key point: Nigeria has one of the highest maternal mortality rates in the world. Educate about danger signs, plan transport, and make sure facilities are ready with blood and drugs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q124$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2459, $s$Obstetric haemorrhage of 1500 mL with ongoing bleeding needs:$s$, $s$["Activation of the massive haemorrhage protocol", "Oral iron tablets and review next day", "IV diuretics to reduce fluid overload", "Observation of vital signs for 4 hours"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Activation of the massive haemorrhage protocol
Losing 1500 mL with ongoing bleeding is a MAJOR obstetric haemorrhage that can quickly become life-threatening. The MASSIVE HAEMORRHAGE PROTOCOL must be activated: call senior obstetric and anaesthetic help, alert the blood bank, give blood products (red cells, FFP, platelets, cryoprecipitate), tranexamic acid 1 g IV, warm all fluids, and treat the cause (the 4 Ts).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral iron tablets and review next day — Oral iron is for long-term anaemia, not active bleeding.
• IV diuretics to reduce fluid overload — Diuretics would worsen shock.
• Observation of vital signs for 4 hours — Waiting 4 hours would be fatal.

⭐ Key point: Major PPH: more than 1000 mL. Massive PPH: more than 1500–2000 mL or ongoing bleeding with shock. Monitor blood loss by weighing pads.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q125$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2460, $s$In placenta accreta spectrum, the placenta:$s$, $s$["Separates completely before the baby is born", "Lies over the cervix and bleeds painlessly", "Is much smaller than normal for gestation", "Invades the uterine wall and fails to separate"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Invades the uterine wall and fails to separate
In placenta accreta spectrum, the placenta grows INTO the uterine wall: accreta into the muscle surface, increta deeper into the muscle, percreta through the wall to other organs such as the bladder. The placenta can't separate normally after birth, causing massive haemorrhage. The main risk factor is a PREVIOUS CAESAREAN SECTION, especially with a low-lying placenta (placenta praevia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Separates completely before the baby is born — In accreta, the placenta does NOT separate; separating before birth describes abruption.
• Lies over the cervix and bleeds painlessly — Lying over the cervix and bleeding painlessly describes placenta praevia.
• Is much smaller than normal for gestation — The size of the placenta isn't the problem in accreta.

⭐ Key point: Planned delivery in a specialist centre, with blood available and often a planned caesarean hysterectomy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q126$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2461, $s$Ergometrine is contraindicated in:$s$, $s$["Pre-eclampsia and heart disease", "Mild iron-deficiency anaemia", "Primary postpartum haemorrhage", "Uterine atony after delivery"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pre-eclampsia and heart disease
Ergometrine strongly contracts the uterus, but it also tightens blood vessels, causing severe HYPERTENSION and coronary artery spasm. In PRE-ECLAMPSIA it can cause a stroke or eclampsia. In HEART DISEASE it can cause ischaemia, MI or heart failure. It is therefore contraindicated in both.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mild iron-deficiency anaemia — Mild iron-deficiency anaemia is not a contraindication.
• Primary postpartum haemorrhage — Primary PPH is an INDICATION for ergometrine (if no contraindications).
• Uterine atony after delivery — Uterine atony is an INDICATION for ergometrine.

⭐ Key point: In pre-eclampsia or heart disease, use oxytocin (and carboprost or misoprostol if needed) instead of ergometrine or Syntometrine.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q129$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2462, $s$Sickle cell disease in pregnancy increases the risk of:$s$, $s$["Crises, pre-eclampsia, infection and growth restriction", "Post-term pregnancy continuing beyond 42 weeks", "Polycythaemia and hyperviscosity in the mother", "Fetal macrosomia and shoulder dystocia at delivery"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Crises, pre-eclampsia, infection and growth restriction
Pregnancy is dangerous for women with sickle cell disease. It increases the risk of: sickle CRISES (pain and acute chest syndrome), PRE-ECLAMPSIA, INFECTIONS (urinary, chest, malaria), clots, and a small, growth-restricted baby (IUGR), preterm birth and miscarriage. These women need care from a specialist team.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Post-term pregnancy continuing beyond 42 weeks — Sickle cell disease is linked to PRETERM birth, not post-term.
• Polycythaemia and hyperviscosity in the mother — Sickle cell disease causes ANAEMIA, not polycythaemia.
• Fetal macrosomia and shoulder dystocia at delivery — Babies tend to be SMALL (growth-restricted), not large.

⭐ Key point: Care in pregnancy: daily folic acid (5 mg), malaria prophylaxis, low-dose aspirin, hydration, avoid cold, early treatment of crises and infection, and growth scans for the baby.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q131$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2463, $s$Severe malaria in pregnancy is treated with:$s$, $s$["Oral metronidazole", "IV artesunate", "IM penicillin", "Oral chloroquine only"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV artesunate
IV ARTESUNATE is the first-line treatment for severe malaria in all patients, including pregnant women in ANY trimester. It clears parasites faster and saves more lives than quinine. Give 2.4 mg/kg IV at 0, 12 and 24 hours, then daily until the patient can take oral treatment, followed by a full course of oral artemisinin combination therapy (ACT).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Oral metronidazole — Metronidazole is an antibiotic for anaerobic and protozoal infections, not malaria.
• IM penicillin — Penicillin is an antibiotic and doesn't treat malaria.
• Oral chloroquine only — Chloroquine is no longer effective in Nigeria due to resistance, and oral treatment is not suitable for severe malaria.

⭐ Key point: Severe malaria in pregnancy: watch for hypoglycaemia (check glucose often, especially with quinine), pulmonary oedema, anaemia and preterm labour.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q132$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2464, $s$Women with a history of second-trimester loss from cervical weakness may be offered:$s$, $s$["Routine caesarean section at 20 weeks", "Strict bed rest only for the whole pregnancy", "No treatment, as recurrence is very rare", "Cervical length scans with cerclage or progesterone"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Cervical length scans with cerclage or progesterone
Women who have lost a pregnancy in the second trimester because of a weak cervix are at risk of it happening again. They are offered CERVICAL LENGTH SCANS (transvaginal ultrasound) from about 14–16 weeks. If the cervix shortens, a CERCLAGE (stitch) or vaginal PROGESTERONE can be offered to prevent another loss.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Routine caesarean section at 20 weeks — A caesarean at 20 weeks would deliver a baby too premature to survive.
• Strict bed rest only for the whole pregnancy — Strict bed rest alone hasn't been shown to work and increases clot risk.
• No treatment, as recurrence is very rare — Recurrence is NOT rare, so treatment is offered.

⭐ Key point: Cervical length below 25 mm before 24 weeks means a high risk of preterm birth.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q135$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2465, $s$Pulmonary oedema in severe pre-eclampsia is worsened by:$s$, $s$["Magnesium sulphate", "Fluid restriction", "Excessive IV fluids", "Left lateral position"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Excessive IV fluids
In severe pre-eclampsia, the blood vessels are leaky (damaged endothelium), and the blood albumin is low. Extra IV fluid easily leaks into the lungs, causing PULMONARY OEDEMA. That's why fluids are RESTRICTED (usually to about 80 mL/hour total, or 1 mL/kg/hour), with strict fluid balance, even if urine output is low.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Magnesium sulphate — Magnesium sulphate doesn't cause pulmonary oedema; it prevents fits.
• Fluid restriction — Fluid restriction PREVENTS pulmonary oedema.
• Left lateral position — The left lateral position improves blood flow and doesn't worsen pulmonary oedema.

⭐ Key point: Low urine output in pre-eclampsia is usually NOT treated with fluid boluses. Monitor fluid balance hourly and SpO₂ to detect oedema early.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q136$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2466, $s$After delivery, a woman on magnesium sulphate has respiratory rate 8/min. The nurse should:$s$, $s$["Increase the magnesium dose to prevent fits", "Encourage breastfeeding to help recovery", "Stop the infusion and give IV calcium gluconate", "Give a sedative to calm the patient"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Stop the infusion and give IV calcium gluconate
Magnesium sulphate prevents seizures in pre-eclampsia, but too much is toxic. Toxicity signs appear in order: loss of knee reflexes, then RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION (RR below 12), then cardiac arrest. A respiratory rate of 8/min is a sign of serious toxicity. STOP the infusion immediately and give the antidote, IV CALCIUM GLUCONATE 10% (10 mL over 10 minutes). Give oxygen and support breathing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the magnesium dose to prevent fits — Increasing the dose would worsen toxicity and could cause respiratory arrest.
• Encourage breastfeeding to help recovery — Breastfeeding doesn't treat respiratory depression.
• Give a sedative to calm the patient — A sedative would further depress breathing.

⭐ Key point: Monitor hourly on magnesium: respiratory rate (above 12), knee reflexes (present), urine output (above 30 mL/hour) and SpO₂. Keep calcium gluconate at the bedside.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q137$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2467, $s$Retained products of conception after delivery cause:$s$, $s$["Secondary PPH and infection", "Hyperemesis gravidarum", "Hypertension and seizures", "Polyhydramnios in the next pregnancy"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Secondary PPH and infection
Retained products of conception (pieces of placenta or membranes left in the uterus) stop the uterus from contracting properly and become infected. This causes SECONDARY PPH (bleeding from 24 hours to 12 weeks after birth) and INFECTION (endometritis, puerperal sepsis): fever, offensive lochia and a tender, bulky uterus.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hyperemesis gravidarum — Hyperemesis gravidarum is severe vomiting in early pregnancy.
• Hypertension and seizures — Hypertension and seizures are caused by pre-eclampsia and eclampsia.
• Polyhydramnios in the next pregnancy — Retained products don't cause polyhydramnios in the next pregnancy.

⭐ Key point: Treatment: antibiotics, then evacuation of retained products (ERPC or manual vacuum aspiration) under antibiotic cover.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q138$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2468, $s$The nurse should monitor a woman after caesarean section with general anaesthesia for:$s$, $s$["Hypoglycaemia only, every hour", "Airway, bleeding, uterine tone and pain", "Visual acuity only, every hour", "Polyuria only, every hour"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Airway, bleeding, uterine tone and pain
After a caesarean section under general anaesthesia, monitor: AIRWAY and breathing (the patient is recovering from anaesthesia and is at risk of airway obstruction and aspiration), BLEEDING (wound, vaginal loss, PPH risk), UTERINE TONE (a soft uterus means atony and bleeding), and PAIN (good pain relief helps breathing and early mobility). Also check vital signs, urine output and the wound. Record on the MEOWS chart.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hypoglycaemia only, every hour — Glucose monitoring alone is not enough.
• Visual acuity only, every hour — Visual acuity is not a routine post-op check.
• Polyuria only, every hour — Urine output alone is not enough.

⭐ Key point: Most post-caesarean complications in the first hours are airway problems, bleeding and low BP. Check the uterus (fundus) and lochia regularly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q139$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2469, $s$Readmission to ICU within 48 hours of discharge is a marker of:$s$, $s$["Good ward staffing and early review", "Possible premature discharge or poor ward care", "Low patient acuity across the hospital", "Excellent ICU care and good outcomes"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Possible premature discharge or poor ward care
Readmission to ICU within 48 hours of discharge suggests the patient may have been DISCHARGED TOO EARLY (still unstable) or the WARD could not meet their needs (staffing, skills, monitoring). It is used as a QUALITY INDICATOR for ICU discharge decisions and ward care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Good ward staffing and early review — It suggests problems, not good ward staffing.
• Low patient acuity across the hospital — It doesn't indicate low patient acuity.
• Excellent ICU care and good outcomes — It is a warning sign, not a sign of excellent care.

⭐ Key point: Reduce readmissions: discharge during the day (not at night), good handover to the ward, outreach team follow-up, and clear step-down plans.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q176$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2470, $s$When the neonates heart rate falls or fails to respond after five inflation breath next action is$s$, $s$["Commence chest compression", "Insert umbilical catheter", "Insert endotracheal tube", "Recheck airway"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Commence chest compression
In newborn resuscitation, the first 5 INFLATION BREATHS aim to fill the fluid-filled lungs with air. If the heart rate doesn't rise, first make sure the breaths are moving the chest (reposition, jaw thrust, suction). If the heart rate stays below 60 despite effective ventilation (chest moving), START CHEST COMPRESSIONS at a ratio of 3:1 with ventilation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Insert umbilical catheter — An umbilical catheter is placed later if drugs are needed.
• Insert endotracheal tube — Intubation may help but isn't the immediate next step for a low heart rate.
• Recheck airway — Rechecking the airway is important, but the key answer is compressions if the HR stays low despite good ventilation.

⭐ Key point: Newborn resuscitation: dry and warm, airway, 5 inflation breaths, ventilation breaths, compressions (HR below 60), then drugs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q181$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2471, $s$Blue colour, Moderate tone, Inadequate breath and Slow heart rate in a neonate should be followed by which action$s$, $s$["Wrap baby and handover to mother", "Commence breastfeeding", "Commence active resuscitation", "Keep baby in the cot"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Commence active resuscitation
A newborn who is BLUE, has only MODERATE (reduced) tone, INADEQUATE breathing and a SLOW heart rate is not coping after birth and needs ACTIVE RESUSCITATION: open the airway, give inflation breaths with a bag-mask, and reassess the heart rate. Delay can cause hypoxic brain injury or death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wrap baby and handover to mother — Wrapping the baby and handing over ignores a baby in trouble.
• Commence breastfeeding — Breastfeeding is for a well baby; this baby needs help breathing.
• Keep baby in the cot — Leaving the baby in the cot could be fatal.

⭐ Key point: Assess every newborn quickly: colour, tone, breathing, heart rate. Most babies who need help need only breaths.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q182$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2472, $s$One of the following neonates does not need resuscitation$s$, $s$["Baby whose mother is on sedatives", "Baby with hypothermia", "Baby with congenital heart disease", "Baby with heart rate of 100bpm"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Baby with heart rate of 100bpm
A newborn heart rate of 100 bpm or more (normal 110–160) shows the baby is coping. Resuscitation is guided by heart rate and breathing: a heart rate below 100 suggests the need for ventilation, and below 60 needs compressions. A baby with a heart rate of 100 and good breathing doesn't need resuscitation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Baby whose mother is on sedatives — A baby whose mother had sedatives may be born sleepy, with depressed breathing, and may need help.
• Baby with hypothermia — A hypothermic baby is at risk and needs warming and support.
• Baby with congenital heart disease — A baby with congenital heart disease may need resuscitation or special care.

⭐ Key point: Heart rate is the most important sign of whether resuscitation is working.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q183$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2473, $s$The APGAR score is assessed at:$s$, $s$["Only once, at 24 hours after birth", "1 and 5 minutes after birth", "Before delivery, using the CTG", "30 minutes and 1 hour after birth"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1 and 5 minutes after birth
The APGAR score is done at 1 MINUTE and 5 MINUTES after birth (and every 5 minutes after if the score is low). It scores 5 signs from 0 to 2: Appearance (colour), Pulse (heart rate), Grimace (reflex irritability), Activity (tone) and Respiration. A score of 7–10 is normal, 4–6 moderately low, and 0–3 very low.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only once, at 24 hours after birth — One assessment at 24 hours is too late.
• Before delivery, using the CTG — APGAR is done after birth, not before.
• 30 minutes and 1 hour after birth — 30 minutes and 1 hour aren't the standard times.

⭐ Key point: Don't wait for the 1-minute APGAR to start resuscitation. Start as soon as the baby isn't breathing well.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q184$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2474, $s$A newborn with HR 80, gasping breaths and floppy tone needs:$s$, $s$["Adrenaline first", "Chest compressions immediately", "Bag-mask ventilation", "Routine care with the mother"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bag-mask ventilation
A newborn with HR 80 (below 100), GASPING breaths and FLOPPY tone needs effective BAG-MASK VENTILATION. Most newborns in trouble have a breathing problem, and the heart rate usually rises once the lungs are ventilated. If the HR stays below 60 despite good ventilation, then start compressions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adrenaline first — Adrenaline is only given if the HR stays below 60 after effective ventilation and compressions.
• Chest compressions immediately — Compressions start only if the HR is below 60 despite effective ventilation.
• Routine care with the mother — Routine care is wrong; the baby is in trouble.

⭐ Key point: Ventilation is the most important step in newborn resuscitation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q185$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2475, $s$Thermal care of the newborn includes:$s$, $s$["Bathing the baby immediately after birth", "Drying, removing wet towels, skin-to-skin or warmer", "Leaving the baby wet until the cord is cut", "Nursing the baby in a cool, airy room"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Drying, removing wet towels, skin-to-skin or warmer
Newborns lose heat quickly (large surface area, little fat, wet skin). Hypothermia increases death, low glucose, breathing problems and acidosis. Thermal care: DRY the baby immediately, REMOVE WET TOWELS, put the baby SKIN-TO-SKIN with the mother (or under a radiant warmer), cover the head with a hat, and keep the room warm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bathing the baby immediately after birth — Bathing immediately causes heat loss; delay bathing for at least 24 hours.
• Leaving the baby wet until the cord is cut — Leaving the baby wet causes heat loss by evaporation.
• Nursing the baby in a cool, airy room — A cool, airy room causes heat loss by convection.

⭐ Key point: The "warm chain": warm delivery room, immediate drying, skin-to-skin, breastfeeding, delayed bathing, warm transport.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q186$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2476, $s$Phototherapy works by:$s$, $s$["Blocking the liver enzymes temporarily", "Increasing the production of bilirubin", "Destroying the baby's red cells faster", "Converting bilirubin to water-soluble isomers"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Converting bilirubin to water-soluble isomers
Bilirubin in the skin absorbs BLUE LIGHT (wavelength about 430–490 nm). The light changes it into WATER-SOLUBLE forms (isomers such as lumirubin) that can be excreted in bile and URINE without the liver having to process them first. This lowers the bilirubin level.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Blocking the liver enzymes temporarily — Phototherapy doesn't block liver enzymes.
• Increasing the production of bilirubin — It doesn't increase bilirubin production.
• Destroying the baby's red cells faster — It doesn't destroy red cells.

⭐ Key point: During phototherapy: cover the eyes, expose as much skin as possible, turn the baby, monitor temperature and hydration, and check bilirubin regularly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q187$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2477, $s$Exchange transfusion is indicated when bilirubin:$s$, $s$["Is within the normal range for age", "Is falling steadily on phototherapy", "Is just above the phototherapy line only", "Reaches exchange levels or shows encephalopathy"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reaches exchange levels or shows encephalopathy
Exchange transfusion replaces the baby's blood with donor blood, quickly removing bilirubin and antibodies (in haemolytic disease). It is indicated when the bilirubin REACHES the EXCHANGE level (on charts that depend on the baby's age in hours and gestation) despite phototherapy, or if there are signs of BILIRUBIN ENCEPHALOPATHY (lethargy, poor feeding, high-pitched cry, arching).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Is within the normal range for age — Normal bilirubin doesn't need treatment.
• Is falling steadily on phototherapy — Bilirubin falling on phototherapy is responding.
• Is just above the phototherapy line only — Just above the phototherapy line needs only phototherapy.

⭐ Key point: Kernicterus (bilirubin brain damage) causes permanent deafness, cerebral palsy and learning disability. Treat high bilirubin promptly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q188$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2478, $s$First-line drug for neonatal seizures is:$s$, $s$["Valproate", "Phenytoin", "Carbamazepine", "Phenobarbital"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Phenobarbital
PHENOBARBITAL is the first-line anticonvulsant for neonatal seizures. It is given IV as a loading dose of 20 mg/kg, with further doses if seizures continue. It acts on GABA receptors. Monitor breathing, as it can depress respiration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Valproate — Valproate is avoided in neonates (liver toxicity).
• Phenytoin — Phenytoin is a second-line drug.
• Carbamazepine — Carbamazepine is oral and not used for neonatal seizures.

⭐ Key point: Always check glucose and calcium first. Neonatal seizures can be subtle: lip smacking, cycling movements, eye deviation, apnoea.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q190$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2479, $s$Feeding intolerance in a preterm infant may indicate:$s$, $s$["Necrotising enterocolitis or sepsis", "Good bowel function and tolerance", "Overhydration from IV fluids", "Normal growth and gut maturation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Necrotising enterocolitis or sepsis
In a preterm baby, feeding intolerance (vomiting, especially green bile; large residuals; a swollen, tender abdomen; blood in the stool) can be an early sign of NECROTISING ENTEROCOLITIS (NEC), a serious inflammation and death of the bowel. It can also be a sign of SEPSIS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Good bowel function and tolerance — Feeding intolerance is not a sign of good bowel function.
• Overhydration from IV fluids — Overhydration isn't the typical cause.
• Normal growth and gut maturation — It is not a sign of normal growth.

⭐ Key point: NEC: stop feeds (nil by mouth), place an NG tube on free drainage, give IV fluids and antibiotics, take an abdominal X-ray, and get a surgical review.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q191$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2480, $s$In neonates, fluid requirements increase with:$s$, $s$["Feeding by NG tube only", "Phototherapy and radiant warmers", "A lower room temperature", "Fluid restriction orders"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Phototherapy and radiant warmers
Newborns lose water through the skin (insensible losses). PHOTOTHERAPY and RADIANT WARMERS increase water loss through evaporation from the skin, so the baby needs MORE FLUID (often 10–20% extra) to avoid dehydration.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Feeding by NG tube only — NG feeding doesn't increase fluid needs.
• A lower room temperature — A cooler room reduces insensible loss.
• Fluid restriction orders — Fluid restriction orders reduce intake, not needs.

⭐ Key point: Monitor weight, urine output and sodium in babies under phototherapy or warmers.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q192$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2481, $s$Fluid for a child in shock is given as:$s$, $s$["50 mL/kg of 10% dextrose as a single bolus", "10–20 mL/kg isotonic bolus, reassessing each time", "Oral fluids only, by cup and spoon", "Hypotonic fluid given rapidly over 10 minutes"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10–20 mL/kg isotonic bolus, reassessing each time
In a shocked child, give a BOLUS of ISOTONIC fluid (0.9% saline or balanced crystalloid) of 10–20 mL/kg, then REASSESS heart rate, capillary refill, BP, consciousness and liver size after each bolus. Repeat if still shocked, watching for signs of overload (crackles, enlarged liver).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50 mL/kg of 10% dextrose as a single bolus — 50 mL/kg of 10% dextrose would cause severe hyperglycaemia and isn't a resuscitation fluid.
• Oral fluids only, by cup and spoon — Oral fluids are too slow for shock.
• Hypotonic fluid given rapidly over 10 minutes — Hypotonic fluid given rapidly can cause low sodium and brain swelling.

⭐ Key point: Use smaller boluses (10 mL/kg) in children with severe malnutrition, malaria with anaemia, or heart disease.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q193$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2482, $s$Signs of raised intracranial pressure in infants include:$s$, $s$["Bulging fontanelle, vomiting, sunset eyes", "Normal head growth on the chart", "Sunken fontanelle with a dry mouth", "Hyperactivity with increased hunger"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Bulging fontanelle, vomiting, sunset eyes
In infants, the skull sutures are not fused and the fontanelle is open. Raised ICP shows as a BULGING (tense) FONTANELLE, VOMITING, irritability, a high-pitched cry, "SUNSET EYES" (eyes deviated downward), increasing head size (hydrocephalus), and later poor feeding, seizures and reduced consciousness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal head growth on the chart — Normal head growth doesn't suggest raised ICP.
• Sunken fontanelle with a dry mouth — A sunken fontanelle with a dry mouth suggests DEHYDRATION.
• Hyperactivity with increased hunger — Hyperactivity with hunger doesn't suggest raised ICP.

⭐ Key point: Measure head circumference regularly in at-risk infants. A rapidly increasing head size suggests hydrocephalus.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q194$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2483, $s$Bronchiolitis in infants is most commonly caused by:$s$, $s$["Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)", "Streptococcus pneumoniae", "Haemophilus influenzae type b", "Candida albicans"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respiratory syncytial virus (RSV)
Bronchiolitis is a viral infection of the small airways (bronchioles) in infants, most commonly caused by RESPIRATORY SYNCYTIAL VIRUS (RSV). It causes cough, fast breathing, wheeze and crackles, poor feeding, and sometimes apnoea in young babies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Streptococcus pneumoniae — Streptococcus pneumoniae causes bacterial pneumonia.
• Haemophilus influenzae type b — Haemophilus influenzae type b causes epiglottitis and meningitis.
• Candida albicans — Candida is a fungus, not the cause of bronchiolitis.

⭐ Key point: Bronchiolitis treatment is supportive: oxygen if SpO₂ is low, nasal suction, fluids (NG or IV if feeding poorly), and CPAP if severe. Antibiotics and bronchodilators don't usually help.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q196$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2484, $s$Kerosene ingestion in a child mainly causes:$s$, $s$["Acute liver failure", "Kidney stones", "Hypoglycaemia", "Aspiration pneumonitis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Aspiration pneumonitis
Kerosene (paraffin) is a hydrocarbon with low viscosity. If swallowed, it is easily inhaled into the lungs, especially if the child coughs or vomits, causing ASPIRATION PNEUMONITIS (chemical lung inflammation): coughing, choking, fast breathing, hypoxia and fever. The lungs are the main danger, not the gut.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Acute liver failure — Liver failure isn't the main effect.
• Kidney stones — Kidney stones aren't related.
• Hypoglycaemia — Hypoglycaemia isn't the main effect.

⭐ Key point: Do NOT induce vomiting or give charcoal. Give oxygen and monitor for respiratory distress for at least 6 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q198$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2485, $s$Meningococcal sepsis in a child presents with:$s$, $s$["Slow weight gain over several months", "Fever, non-blanching purpuric rash, shock", "Painless jaundice with pale stools", "Itchy urticaria after an injection"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fever, non-blanching purpuric rash, shock
Meningococcal sepsis (Neisseria meningitidis) progresses very quickly. It presents with FEVER, a NON-BLANCHING PURPURIC (or petechial) RASH (it doesn't fade when pressed with a glass), and SHOCK (cold hands and feet, mottled skin, fast heart rate, slow capillary refill). Meningitis signs may or may not be present.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Slow weight gain over several months — Slow weight gain over months isn't acute sepsis.
• Painless jaundice with pale stools — Painless jaundice with pale stools suggests biliary obstruction.
• Itchy urticaria after an injection — Itchy urticaria after an injection suggests an allergic reaction.

⭐ Key point: Give IV or IO CEFTRIAXONE IMMEDIATELY (or IM benzylpenicillin before transfer), treat shock with fluids, and give prophylaxis to close contacts.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q200$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2486, $s$A pregnant patient with severe hyperemesis gravidarum is at risk of thiamine deficiency, which can cause:$s$, $s$["No neurological consequences", "Wernicke's encephalopathy", "Only mild fatigue with no other risk", "Improved neurological function"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Wernicke's encephalopathy
In hyperemesis gravidarum, prolonged vomiting and poor intake use up thiamine (vitamin B1). This can cause WERNICKE'S ENCEPHALOPATHY: confusion, ataxia (unsteady walking) and eye movement problems (nystagmus). Giving IV glucose without thiamine can trigger or worsen it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No neurological consequences — Thiamine deficiency DOES have neurological effects.
• Only mild fatigue with no other risk — It's more than mild fatigue; it can cause permanent brain damage.
• Improved neurological function — It worsens, not improves, neurological function.

⭐ Key point: Give thiamine before or with IV dextrose in women with prolonged vomiting.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q121$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2487, $s$What proportion of maternal deaths are estimated to occur in the postpartum period?$s$, $s$["100%", "5%", "40%", "75%"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 40%
About 40% (and in some estimates more than half) of maternal deaths occur in the POSTPARTUM period, especially the first 24 hours after birth (PPH, pre-eclampsia, infection, embolism). This highlights the need for close monitoring after delivery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100% — 100% is incorrect; deaths also occur in pregnancy and labour.
• 5% — 5% underestimates the postpartum risk.
• 75% — 75% overestimates the figure used in this question.

⭐ Key point: Monitor closely after birth: vital signs, uterine tone, blood loss, and BP (pre-eclampsia can appear postpartum). Use MEOWS.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q124$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2488, $s$Oxygen saturation of 95-100% in room air Conscious and easily arausable good muscle tone, respiration 30-60 is average 40 describes$s$, $s$["A sick neonate", "An apnoeic baby", "A healthy baby", "An asphyxiated baby"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A healthy baby
A healthy newborn has SpO₂ of 95–100% in room air (after the first minutes), is CONSCIOUS and easily rousable, has GOOD MUSCLE TONE, and breathes 30–60 times per minute (average about 40). These findings describe a HEALTHY baby.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A sick neonate — A sick neonate shows poor tone, abnormal breathing, low SpO₂ or lethargy.
• An apnoeic baby — An apnoeic baby isn't breathing.
• An asphyxiated baby — An asphyxiated baby is floppy, blue or pale, with poor breathing.

⭐ Key point: Newborn normal values: HR 110–160, RR 30–60 (some use 40–60), temperature 36.5–37.5 °C, SpO₂ 95% or above after 10 minutes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q125$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2489, $s$A 25 years old para 4, was seen in the clinic with the diagnosis of mastitis. what feeding advice will nurse Iyabo give?$s$, $s$["Wean the baby", "Feed the baby with express breast milk", "Give infant formula", "Continue breastfeeding"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Continue breastfeeding
Mastitis is inflammation (sometimes infection) of the breast, often from milk stasis (a blocked duct or poor drainage). The best advice is to CONTINUE BREASTFEEDING (or expressing) from the affected breast. Emptying the breast removes the stagnant milk and speeds recovery. The milk is safe for the baby. Antibiotics may be needed if it doesn't improve.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wean the baby — Weaning the baby causes more milk stasis and can lead to an abscess.
• Feed the baby with express breast milk — Expressed breast milk works, but feeding directly is preferred.
• Give infant formula — Infant formula is unnecessary and reduces milk supply.

⭐ Key point: Also advise: rest, fluids, warm compresses before feeds, a good latch, pain relief (paracetamol, ibuprofen), and antibiotics if not better in 12–24 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q126$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2490, $s$Using the Holliday-Segar method, what is the daily maintenance IV fluid requirement for a 22 kg child?$s$, $s$["1000 mL/day", "1100 mL/day", "2200 mL/day", "1540 mL/day"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1540 mL/day
Holliday–Segar method for daily maintenance fluid: first 10 kg = 100 mL/kg (1000 mL); next 10 kg = 50 mL/kg (500 mL); each kg above 20 = 20 mL/kg. For 22 kg: 1000 + 500 + (2 × 20 = 40) = 1540 mL/day.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1000 mL/day — 1000 mL/day covers only the first 10 kg.
• 1100 mL/day — 1100 mL/day is a miscalculation.
• 2200 mL/day — 2200 mL/day comes from using 100 mL/kg for the whole weight.

⭐ Key point: Hourly version (4-2-1 rule): 4 mL/kg/hour for the first 10 kg, 2 mL/kg/hour for the next 10 kg, 1 mL/kg/hour above 20 kg. For 22 kg: 40 + 20 + 2 = 62 mL/hour.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q129$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2491, $s$Vaginal bleeding after 28 weeks of pregnancy which is sudden in onset, painless, causeless and recurrent is known as.$s$, $s$["Abortion", "Vasa praevia", "Placenta praevia", "Abruption placentae"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Placenta praevia
PLACENTA PRAEVIA is when the placenta lies low in the uterus, over or near the cervix. In the third trimester, as the lower uterus stretches, the placenta separates and bleeds. The classic bleeding is SUDDEN, PAINLESS, CAUSELESS (no trigger), and RECURRENT, usually bright red, after 28 weeks.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Abortion — Abortion (miscarriage) occurs before 28 weeks (before viability in many definitions).
• Vasa praevia — Vasa praevia causes bleeding when the membranes rupture, with fetal distress.
• Abruption placentae — Abruptio placentae causes PAINFUL bleeding with a tense, tender uterus.

⭐ Key point: In suspected placenta praevia, NEVER do a vaginal examination. Confirm with ultrasound.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q130$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2492, $s$Foetal complications occurs in Eclampsia as a result of$s$, $s$["Placenta pigmentation", "Placenta hypo-perfusion", "Placental bleeding", "Placenta hyper perfusion"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Placenta hypo-perfusion
In eclampsia (and pre-eclampsia), the blood vessels of the PLACENTA are narrowed and damaged, reducing blood flow to the placenta (PLACENTAL HYPOPERFUSION). Less oxygen and nutrients reach the baby, causing growth restriction (IUGR), fetal distress, placental abruption, preterm birth and stillbirth.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Placenta pigmentation — "Placental pigmentation" isn't the mechanism.
• Placental bleeding — Placental bleeding (abruption) can occur, but the main cause of fetal problems is poor perfusion.
• Placenta hyper perfusion — Placental HYPER-perfusion doesn't occur in eclampsia.

⭐ Key point: Monitor the fetus with CTG and growth scans in pre-eclampsia. Delivery is the definitive treatment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q132$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2493, $s$In eclampsia, the Zuspan regimen of magnesium sulfate is:$s$, $s$["0.5 g orally every 8 hours", "10 g IV push in one minute, then nothing", "4 g IV loading dose over 5–20 min, then 1 g/hour", "1 g IM once daily for 3 days"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 4 g IV loading dose over 5–20 min, then 1 g/hour
The ZUSPAN regimen of magnesium sulphate (IV): a LOADING DOSE of 4 g IV over 5–20 minutes, then a MAINTENANCE infusion of 1 g per hour, continued for 24 hours after delivery or the last seizure. The Pritchard regimen uses IM injections instead.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 0.5 g orally every 8 hours — 0.5 g orally isn't used for eclampsia.
• 10 g IV push in one minute, then nothing — A 10 g IV push in one minute would cause dangerous toxicity.
• 1 g IM once daily for 3 days — 1 g IM once daily is far too little.

⭐ Key point: Pritchard regimen: 4 g IV plus 10 g IM (5 g in each buttock), then 5 g IM every 4 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q133$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2494, $s$The first-line drug for preventing and treating postpartum haemorrhage is:$s$, $s$["Ergometrine in pre-eclampsia", "Nifedipine", "Oxytocin", "Salbutamol"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Oxytocin
OXYTOCIN (10 units IM or IV) is the FIRST-LINE drug to prevent and treat PPH. It makes the uterus contract strongly, squeezing the blood vessels at the placental site. It is safe in hypertension and pre-eclampsia, unlike ergometrine.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ergometrine in pre-eclampsia — Ergometrine causes severe hypertension and is avoided in pre-eclampsia.
• Nifedipine — Nifedipine relaxes the uterus (a tocolytic), so it would worsen bleeding.
• Salbutamol — Salbutamol relaxes the uterus and would worsen bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Other uterotonics for PPH: ergometrine (avoid in high BP), carboprost (avoid in asthma), misoprostol. Also give tranexamic acid.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q137$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2495, $s$In cardiac arrest in a woman more than 20 weeks pregnant, CPR should include:$s$, $s$["Lying the woman on her right side for compressions", "Manual left uterine displacement and perimortem caesarean by 5 minutes", "Delaying CPR until the baby has been delivered", "Using much lower defibrillation energy than in adults"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Manual left uterine displacement and perimortem caesarean by 5 minutes
In maternal cardiac arrest after 20 weeks, the heavy uterus compresses the vena cava and aorta (aortocaval compression), so CPR is ineffective. Push the uterus to the LEFT by hand (MANUAL LEFT UTERINE DISPLACEMENT) during compressions. If there is no return of circulation by 4 minutes, perform a PERIMORTEM CAESAREAN SECTION by 5 minutes; this helps resuscitate the mother and may save the baby.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lying the woman on her right side for compressions — Lying her on her right side increases aortocaval compression.
• Delaying CPR until the baby has been delivered — CPR must never be delayed.
• Using much lower defibrillation energy than in adults — Defibrillation energy is the SAME as for other adults.

⭐ Key point: Remember the 4 and 5 minute rule: decide by 4 minutes, deliver by 5 minutes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q138$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2496, $s$Sudden dyspnoea, hypoxia, hypotension and bleeding from IV sites during labour suggest:$s$, $s$["A simple vasovagal faint", "Normal anxiety of labour", "Mild pre-eclampsia only", "Amniotic fluid embolism with DIC"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Amniotic fluid embolism with DIC
AMNIOTIC FLUID EMBOLISM (AFE) happens when amniotic fluid enters the mother's circulation (usually in labour or just after), triggering a severe reaction: SUDDEN BREATHLESSNESS, HYPOXIA, HYPOTENSION, collapse or cardiac arrest, and then DIC (bleeding from IV sites, the uterus and wounds). It is rare but often fatal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A simple vasovagal faint — A vasovagal faint doesn't cause hypoxia and bleeding.
• Normal anxiety of labour — Anxiety doesn't cause hypoxia, low BP and bleeding.
• Mild pre-eclampsia only — Mild pre-eclampsia doesn't cause sudden collapse and DIC.

⭐ Key point: Treat AFE: CPR if needed, oxygen and ventilation, circulatory support, massive transfusion (including cryoprecipitate), and delivery of the baby.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q139$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2497, $s$A normal arterial PaCO₂ in late pregnancy is about:$s$, $s$["8 kPa (60 mmHg), due to progesterone", "5.3 kPa (40 mmHg), the same as non-pregnant women", "4 kPa (30 mmHg), due to increased minute ventilation", "6.5 kPa (49 mmHg), due to splinting of the diaphragm"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 4 kPa (30 mmHg), due to increased minute ventilation
In pregnancy, progesterone stimulates breathing, increasing minute ventilation by about 40%. This lowers the PaCO₂ to about 4 kPa (30 mmHg), with the kidneys compensating by lowering bicarbonate. So a "normal" PaCO₂ of 5.3 kPa (40 mmHg) in a pregnant woman suggests she is TIRING and may be in respiratory failure.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 8 kPa (60 mmHg), due to progesterone — 8 kPa (60 mmHg) is HIGH (respiratory failure).
• 5.3 kPa (40 mmHg), the same as non-pregnant women — 5.3 kPa is normal for NON-pregnant women.
• 6.5 kPa (49 mmHg), due to splinting of the diaphragm — 6.5 kPa is high; the diaphragm doesn't cause CO₂ retention in normal pregnancy.

⭐ Key point: Interpret ABGs in pregnancy with pregnancy normal values: PaCO₂ about 4 kPa (30 mmHg), HCO₃⁻ about 18–21 mmol/L.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q140$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2498, $s$Concerning convulsions in eclampsia, which is correct?$s$, $s$["Diazepam is the first-line anticonvulsant", "The total MgSO4 loading dose is 15 g", "After MgSO4 at 34 weeks the pregnancy should continue to 40 weeks", "Strict monitoring of respiratory rate and urine output is essential"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Strict monitoring of respiratory rate and urine output is essential
Magnesium sulphate is cleared by the KIDNEYS. If urine output falls, magnesium builds up, causing toxicity: loss of reflexes, RESPIRATORY DEPRESSION and cardiac arrest. Strict monitoring of RESPIRATORY RATE, URINE OUTPUT and reflexes (hourly) is essential.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diazepam is the first-line anticonvulsant — MAGNESIUM SULPHATE (not diazepam) is the first-line anticonvulsant in eclampsia.
• The total MgSO4 loading dose is 15 g — The usual loading dose is 4 g IV (Pritchard total initial dose is 14 g), not 15 g.
• After MgSO4 at 34 weeks the pregnancy should continue to 40 weeks — After eclampsia, delivery is planned once the mother is stable; continuing to 40 weeks isn't appropriate.

⭐ Key point: Hold magnesium if RR is below 12, urine output below 30 mL/hour, or reflexes are absent. Antidote: calcium gluconate 10%.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q121$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2499, $s$A nurse is providing family-centered NICU care. The primary goal of family-centered care is to:$s$, $s$["Keep the parents separated from the newborn to prevent infection", "Involve the parents as partners in the newborn's care", "Allow the parents to make all of the medical decisions", "Only communicate with the parents when they ask a question"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Involve the parents as partners in the newborn's care
Family-centred care in the NICU sees the PARENTS as PARTNERS in their baby's care. They are given information, involved in decisions and care (feeding, skin-to-skin contact, kangaroo care), and supported emotionally. This improves bonding, breastfeeding, and parental confidence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Keep the parents separated from the newborn to prevent infection — Separating parents harms bonding and isn't necessary with good infection control.
• Allow the parents to make all of the medical decisions — Parents share decisions with the team; they don't make all medical decisions alone.
• Only communicate with the parents when they ask a question — Communication should be proactive and regular, not only on request.

⭐ Key point: Kangaroo mother care (skin-to-skin) improves temperature control, breastfeeding and survival in low birth weight babies.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q122$s$);
