($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2500, $s$Which statement about intussusception is FALSE?$s$, $s$["The proximal bowel telescopes into the distal bowel", "It usually occurs in healthy babies without a demonstrable cause", "It is mostly found in adolescents", "It most commonly occurs at the ileocolic junction"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It is mostly found in adolescents
The question asks for the FALSE statement. Intussusception is MOSTLY found in INFANTS and toddlers (3 months to 3 years, peak 6–18 months), NOT adolescents. In adolescents and adults it is rare and usually has a lead point (a polyp or tumour).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The proximal bowel telescopes into the distal bowel — It is TRUE that the proximal bowel telescopes into the distal bowel.
• It usually occurs in healthy babies without a demonstrable cause — It is TRUE that it usually occurs in healthy babies without a clear cause (often after a viral illness).
• It most commonly occurs at the ileocolic junction — It is TRUE that it most commonly occurs at the ileocolic junction.

⭐ Key point: Classic triad: colicky pain, a sausage-shaped mass, and redcurrant jelly stools.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q123$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2501, $s$Amniotic fluid embolism is definitively confirmed by:$s$, $s$["Autopsy", "Clinical symptoms alone", "Ultrasound", "Routine blood test"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Autopsy
Amniotic fluid embolism (AFE) is a CLINICAL diagnosis during life (sudden collapse, hypoxia, hypotension and DIC in labour, after excluding other causes). There is no reliable test to confirm it while the patient is alive. Definitive confirmation is at AUTOPSY, finding fetal cells and debris (squames) in the mother's lung blood vessels.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Clinical symptoms alone — Clinical symptoms suggest AFE but don't definitively confirm it.
• Ultrasound — Ultrasound doesn't diagnose AFE.
• Routine blood test — No routine blood test confirms AFE.

⭐ Key point: AFE is rare but has a high mortality. Treat with resuscitation, ventilation, circulatory support, and massive transfusion for DIC.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q124$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2502, $s$The most critical period of the puerperium is:$s$, $s$["Active management of the third stage", "The postnatal family-planning visit", "The whole 6 weeks equally", "The early (immediate) period"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The early (immediate) period
The puerperium is the 6 weeks after birth. The most CRITICAL period is the EARLY (immediate) period, especially the first 24 hours, when the risks of PPH, eclampsia, shock and amniotic fluid embolism are highest. Close monitoring (vital signs, uterine tone, blood loss) is essential.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Active management of the third stage — Active management of the third stage is a delivery procedure, not a puerperium period.
• The postnatal family-planning visit — The postnatal family-planning visit is later and lower risk.
• The whole 6 weeks equally — The risk is NOT equal throughout; it is highest early.

⭐ Key point: Monitor the mother closely in the first hours: pulse, BP, uterine tone, lochia and bladder.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q125$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2503, $s$HELLP syndrome is:$s$, $s$["A form of gestational hypertension only", "A type of fetal growth restriction", "An inherited maternal coagulopathy", "A complication of severe pre-eclampsia"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A complication of severe pre-eclampsia
HELLP syndrome (Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets) is a severe complication of PRE-ECLAMPSIA (though it can occur without high BP). It causes RUQ or epigastric pain, nausea, and can lead to DIC, liver rupture and death. Delivery is the main treatment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A form of gestational hypertension only — It isn't just gestational hypertension; it involves blood and liver damage.
• A type of fetal growth restriction — It isn't a type of fetal growth restriction (though IUGR may occur).
• An inherited maternal coagulopathy — It isn't inherited; it is a complication of pregnancy.

⭐ Key point: Check platelets and liver enzymes in pre-eclampsia. Platelets below 100 × 10⁹/L are a warning sign.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q126$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2504, $s$Which statement about hypertensive disorders of pregnancy is true?$s$, $s$["Gestational hypertension means any BP ≥140/90 at any time in pregnancy", "Prolonged barrier contraception is protective", "Placental pathology is implicated in the aetiology of pre-eclampsia", "The Korotkoff V sound marks systolic pressure"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Placental pathology is implicated in the aetiology of pre-eclampsia
Pre-eclampsia is caused mainly by ABNORMAL PLACENTATION: the spiral arteries of the uterus don't remodel properly, so the placenta gets poor blood flow. The stressed placenta releases factors that damage the mother's blood vessel lining throughout the body, causing high BP, protein in the urine and organ damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Gestational hypertension means any BP ≥140/90 at any time in pregnancy — Gestational hypertension is NEW high BP after 20 weeks WITHOUT protein or organ damage, not "any BP at any time".
• Prolonged barrier contraception is protective — Barrier contraception may actually increase risk (limited exposure to partner's sperm antigens); it isn't protective.
• The Korotkoff V sound marks systolic pressure — The Korotkoff V sound (disappearance) marks DIASTOLIC pressure, not systolic.

⭐ Key point: Low-dose aspirin from 12 weeks reduces pre-eclampsia in high-risk women.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q127$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2505, $s$This describe the premature separation of a normally situated placenta occurring after the 28th week of pregnancy.$s$, $s$["Placenta praevia", "Placenta accrete", "Abruption placentae", "Placenta circumventer"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Abruption placentae
ABRUPTIO PLACENTAE (placental abruption) is the PREMATURE SEPARATION of a NORMALLY SITED placenta from the uterine wall after 28 weeks (or after 20 weeks in some definitions). It causes painful bleeding (which may be concealed), a tense, tender uterus, fetal distress and possibly DIC.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Placenta praevia — Placenta praevia is an ABNORMALLY low-lying placenta.
• Placenta accrete — Placenta accreta is abnormal invasion into the uterine wall.
• Placenta circumventer — Placenta circumvallata is an abnormal shape of the placental edge.

⭐ Key point: Risk factors for abruption: hypertension, trauma, cocaine, smoking, previous abruption.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q128$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2506, $s$A nurse is assessing a newborn for signs of birth asphyxia. Which of the following is a key sign?$s$, $s$["A vigorous kick and a respiratory rate of 40 breaths per minute", "Poor muscle tone and a low Apgar score", "A pink color and a strong cry", "A heart rate of 120 beats per minute"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Poor muscle tone and a low Apgar score
Birth asphyxia (lack of oxygen around birth) causes a baby that is FLOPPY (poor muscle tone), with poor breathing, a slow heart rate, and a LOW APGAR score. The baby may need resuscitation and may develop hypoxic-ischaemic encephalopathy (HIE).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A vigorous kick and a respiratory rate of 40 breaths per minute — A vigorous kick and normal breathing describe a healthy baby.
• A pink color and a strong cry — A pink colour and strong cry describe a healthy baby.
• A heart rate of 120 beats per minute — A heart rate of 120 is normal.

⭐ Key point: Severe birth asphyxia may be treated with therapeutic cooling (if available) to protect the brain.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q130$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2507, $s$A nurse is providing care for a newborn who is at risk for hypothermia. The most effective nursing intervention is to:$s$, $s$["Place the newborn under a radiant warmer", "Keep the newborn's crib away from any heat sources", "Bathe the newborn immediately after birth", "Wrap the newborn in a cold towel"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Place the newborn under a radiant warmer
A RADIANT WARMER provides direct heat to the baby (especially preterm, low birth weight or sick babies), preventing heat loss while allowing access for care. It is a highly effective way to prevent hypothermia, along with drying, skin-to-skin contact and a hat.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Keep the newborn's crib away from any heat sources — Keeping the crib away from heat sources isn't a warming measure.
• Bathe the newborn immediately after birth — Immediate bathing causes heat loss.
• Wrap the newborn in a cold towel — A cold towel causes heat loss.

⭐ Key point: Newborns lose heat by evaporation, conduction, convection and radiation. Keep the delivery room warm (25–28 °C).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q132$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2508, $s$Severe hypertension in pre-eclampsia (≥160/110 mmHg) is treated urgently with:$s$, $s$["No treatment until after the delivery", "IV furosemide alone, repeated hourly", "IV labetalol or hydralazine, or oral nifedipine", "ACE inhibitors such as oral lisinopril"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: IV labetalol or hydralazine, or oral nifedipine
Severe hypertension in pre-eclampsia (160/110 mmHg or above) must be treated URGENTLY (within 30–60 minutes) to prevent STROKE. First-line drugs: IV LABETALOL, IV HYDRALAZINE, or ORAL NIFEDIPINE (immediate-release). The target is about 140–150/90–100.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No treatment until after the delivery — Waiting until after delivery risks maternal stroke.
• IV furosemide alone, repeated hourly — Furosemide doesn't treat pre-eclampsia hypertension (it may worsen low blood volume).
• ACE inhibitors such as oral lisinopril — ACE inhibitors are contraindicated in pregnancy (harm to the fetus).

⭐ Key point: Avoid dropping BP too quickly; it can reduce placental blood flow and harm the baby.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q133 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2509, $s$The usual threshold for treating neonatal hypoglycaemia is blood glucose below:$s$, $s$["1.0 mmol/L only if the baby has seizures", "5.5 mmol/L (about 100 mg/dL)", "2.6 mmol/L (about 45 mg/dL)", "8.0 mmol/L (about 145 mg/dL)"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 2.6 mmol/L (about 45 mg/dL)
The usual threshold for treating neonatal hypoglycaemia is a blood glucose below 2.6 mmol/L (about 45 mg/dL). Babies at risk: preterm, small or large for gestational age, infants of diabetic mothers, those who are cold or sick, and those with poor feeding.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1.0 mmol/L only if the baby has seizures — 1.0 mmol/L is dangerously low; treatment should start well before this.
• 5.5 mmol/L (about 100 mg/dL) — 5.5 mmol/L is normal.
• 8.0 mmol/L (about 145 mg/dL) — 8.0 mmol/L is high.

⭐ Key point: Treat with feeding first if the baby is well. If glucose is very low or the baby is symptomatic, give 2 mL/kg of 10% dextrose IV and an infusion.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q136$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2510, $s$Newborn resuscitation begins with:$s$, $s$["Immediate chest compressions at 120/min", "Five inflation breaths of 2–3 seconds each", "IV adrenaline before any breaths", "Deep suction of the stomach for one minute"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Five inflation breaths of 2–3 seconds each
Newborn resuscitation begins (after drying, warming and opening the airway) with FIVE INFLATION BREATHS, each lasting 2–3 SECONDS at 30 cmH₂O (25 in preterm babies). These long breaths push fluid out of the lungs and fill them with air for the first time. Then reassess heart rate and chest movement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate chest compressions at 120/min — Chest compressions start only if the heart rate stays below 60 despite effective ventilation.
• IV adrenaline before any breaths — Adrenaline is given late, after ventilation and compressions.
• Deep suction of the stomach for one minute — Deep stomach suction isn't part of initial resuscitation (it can cause bradycardia).

⭐ Key point: Most babies respond to effective ventilation alone. Check that the chest is moving.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q137$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2511, $s$Oxygen saturation targets of about 91–95% in preterm infants help prevent:$s$, $s$["Retinopathy of prematurity", "Umbilical cord infection", "Neonatal hypoglycaemia", "Physiological neonatal jaundice"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Retinopathy of prematurity
Preterm babies' retinas are still developing. TOO MUCH oxygen damages the growing blood vessels in the retina, causing RETINOPATHY OF PREMATURITY (ROP), which can lead to blindness. Keeping SpO₂ at about 91–95% reduces ROP while still preventing harm from too little oxygen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Umbilical cord infection — Umbilical infection isn't related to oxygen targets.
• Neonatal hypoglycaemia — Hypoglycaemia isn't related to oxygen targets.
• Physiological neonatal jaundice — Jaundice isn't related to oxygen targets.

⭐ Key point: Preterm babies need eye screening for ROP. Avoid both hyperoxia and hypoxia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q138$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2512, $s$The estimated weight of a 5-year-old child using (age + 4) × 2 is:$s$, $s$["18 kg", "25 kg", "10 kg", "30 kg"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 18 kg
The formula weight (kg) = (age + 4) × 2 estimates the weight of children aged 1–10 years in emergencies. For a 5-year-old: (5 + 4) × 2 = 9 × 2 = 18 kg.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 25 kg — 25 kg comes from a calculation error.
• 10 kg — 10 kg is too low (more like a 1-year-old).
• 30 kg — 30 kg is too high.

⭐ Key point: Use the estimate for emergency drug and fluid doses only when the child can't be weighed. Use the actual weight when possible.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q140$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2513, $s$A pregnant patient presents with severe, constant abdominal pain, a tense/rigid uterus, and dark vaginal bleeding. This presentation is most concerning for:$s$, $s$["Normal labour onset", "Placenta praevia", "Braxton Hicks contractions", "Placental abruption"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Placental abruption
Severe, constant abdominal pain, a TENSE, RIGID ("woody"), tender uterus, and DARK vaginal bleeding in late pregnancy are classic signs of PLACENTAL ABRUPTION (premature separation of a normally sited placenta). The bleeding may be partly hidden behind the placenta, so the shock may be worse than the visible blood loss suggests.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal labour onset — Normal labour has contractions that come and go, not constant pain and a rigid uterus.
• Placenta praevia — Placenta praevia causes PAINLESS, bright red bleeding with a soft uterus.
• Braxton Hicks contractions — Braxton Hicks contractions are painless and irregular, without bleeding.

⭐ Key point: Abruption is an emergency: risk of fetal death, DIC and haemorrhage. Resuscitate and deliver.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q121$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2514, $s$MEOWS stands for:$s$, $s$["Maternal Early Warning Signs", "Modified Early Obstetric Warning Score", "Modified Early Obstetric Warning Signs", "Moment Evaluation of Obstetric Syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Modified Early Obstetric Warning Score
MEOWS stands for MODIFIED EARLY OBSTETRIC WARNING SCORE. It is a chart that scores vital signs (BP, pulse, RR, SpO₂, temperature, consciousness) adjusted for pregnancy, to detect early deterioration in pregnant and postnatal women and trigger urgent review.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Maternal Early Warning Signs — Maternal Early Warning Signs isn't the correct term.
• Modified Early Obstetric Warning Signs — "Signs" isn't correct; it is a "Score".
• Moment Evaluation of Obstetric Syndrome — "Moment Evaluation of Obstetric Syndrome" isn't a real term.

⭐ Key point: Pregnancy changes normal vital signs, so obstetric-specific warning charts are used.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q124$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2515, $s$Which of the following is not an obstetric cause of disseminated intravascular coagulopathy;$s$, $s$["Pre-eclampsia/eclampsia", "Amniotic fluid embolism", "Abruptio placentae", "Uterine inversion"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Uterine inversion
Obstetric causes of DIC include PLACENTAL ABRUPTION, AMNIOTIC FLUID EMBOLISM, PRE-ECLAMPSIA, ECLAMPSIA and HELLP, severe PPH, sepsis, and retained dead fetus. UTERINE INVERSION causes shock and haemorrhage, but it is not typically listed as a direct cause of DIC.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pre-eclampsia/eclampsia — Pre-eclampsia and eclampsia ARE causes of DIC.
• Amniotic fluid embolism — Amniotic fluid embolism IS a major cause of DIC.
• Abruptio placentae — Abruptio placentae IS a major cause of DIC.

⭐ Key point: Uterine inversion is an emergency: replace the uterus immediately and treat shock.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q125$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2516, $s$An acute event involving the cardiorespiratory and/or central nervous system that causes reduced or absent consciousness in pregnancy is:$s$, $s$["Ectopic pregnancy", "Placenta praevia", "Maternal collapse", "Hyperemesis gravidarum"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Maternal collapse
MATERNAL COLLAPSE is an acute event involving the cardiorespiratory and/or central nervous systems, causing reduced or absent consciousness (and potentially death) at any stage of pregnancy and up to 6 weeks after delivery. Causes include haemorrhage, PE, AFE, eclampsia, sepsis and cardiac disease.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ectopic pregnancy — Ectopic pregnancy is a cause of collapse, not the definition.
• Placenta praevia — Placenta praevia causes bleeding, not the definition of collapse.
• Hyperemesis gravidarum — Hyperemesis gravidarum is severe vomiting.

⭐ Key point: Maternal collapse: call for help, left lateral tilt or manual uterine displacement, ABCDE, CPR if needed, and perimortem caesarean by 5 minutes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q126$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2517, $s$A 35year old para 7+2 [6alive] ,just had a spontaneous vaginal delivery of a live baby boy, she is lying on bed not talking, on examination her pulse rate is 100b/m, blood pressure 50/?, the possible cause is: ___________$s$, $s$["Inadequate history during antenatal booking", "Inadequate history of onset of labor", "Not regular in antenatal visit prenatally", "Thromboembolism"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Thromboembolism
This grand multiparous woman (para 7) collapsed just after delivery with a fast pulse and very LOW BP (50 systolic). The commonest cause in a grand multipara is POSTPARTUM HAEMORRHAGE (uterine atony), which must be checked first (uterus, blood loss). Among the options given, the keyed answer is THROMBOEMBOLISM (such as amniotic fluid embolism or pulmonary embolism), which causes sudden collapse after delivery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Inadequate history during antenatal booking — Inadequate antenatal booking history doesn't cause collapse.
• Inadequate history of onset of labor — Inadequate history of labour onset doesn't cause collapse.
• Not regular in antenatal visit prenatally — Irregular antenatal visits don't directly cause collapse.

⭐ Key point: Postpartum collapse: check for PPH first (rub the uterus, check blood loss), then think of AFE, PE, sepsis and eclampsia.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q127$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2518, $s$The commonest surgical procedure used to treat ruptured tubal pregnancy is$s$, $s$["Salpingectomy", "Salpingo-oophorectomy", "Total hysterectomy", "oophorectomy"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Salpingectomy
For a RUPTURED tubal (ectopic) pregnancy with bleeding, the standard surgery is SALPINGECTOMY: removal of the affected fallopian tube. It stops the bleeding quickly and removes the pregnancy. It can be done by laparotomy (if unstable) or laparoscopy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Salpingo-oophorectomy — Salpingo-oophorectomy removes the ovary too, which is usually unnecessary.
• Total hysterectomy — Total hysterectomy is far too extensive.
• oophorectomy — Oophorectomy (removing the ovary) doesn't treat a tubal pregnancy.

⭐ Key point: Ruptured ectopic with shock: resuscitate, cross-match blood, and take to theatre urgently.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q128$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2519, $s$Which drug is contraindicated for controlling postpartum bleeding in a woman with eclampsia?$s$, $s$["Carboprost", "Ergometrine", "Misoprostol", "Oxytocin"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ergometrine
ERGOMETRINE strongly tightens blood vessels (vasoconstriction), causing a sharp rise in BP. In a woman with ECLAMPSIA (already very high BP), this can cause stroke, worsening seizures, or heart problems. It is CONTRAINDICATED in eclampsia, pre-eclampsia, hypertension and heart disease.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Carboprost — Carboprost is avoided in asthma, but is fine in eclampsia.
• Misoprostol — Misoprostol is safe in eclampsia.
• Oxytocin — Oxytocin is the FIRST-LINE drug and safe in eclampsia.

⭐ Key point: Syntometrine (oxytocin plus ergometrine) also contains ergometrine, so avoid it in hypertension.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q129$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2520, $s$Which statement about pre-eclampsia is correct?$s$, $s$["It is mainly a disease of primigravidae", "BP >140/110 defines severe disease", "MgSO4 is used to lower blood pressure in emergencies", "Oedema is required for diagnosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It is mainly a disease of primigravidae
PRE-ECLAMPSIA is mainly a disease of FIRST pregnancies (PRIMIGRAVIDAE). Other risk factors: a new partner, previous pre-eclampsia, multiple pregnancy, age over 40, obesity, chronic hypertension, diabetes and kidney disease.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• BP >140/110 defines severe disease — Severe pre-eclampsia is BP of 160/110 or above, not 140/110.
• MgSO4 is used to lower blood pressure in emergencies — Magnesium sulphate PREVENTS SEIZURES; it isn't used to lower BP (labetalol, hydralazine and nifedipine are used).
• Oedema is required for diagnosis — Oedema is NOT required for diagnosis (it is common in normal pregnancy).

⭐ Key point: Pre-eclampsia = new hypertension after 20 weeks plus proteinuria or organ dysfunction.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q131$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2521, $s$Common causes of antepartum haemorrhage from 24 weeks' gestation include which of the following?$s$, $s$["Uterine rupture", "placenta praevia", "Placenta abruption", "Normal period"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: placenta praevia
Antepartum haemorrhage (bleeding from 24 weeks until birth) is commonly caused by PLACENTA PRAEVIA (a low-lying placenta, causing painless bleeding) and PLACENTAL ABRUPTION (painful bleeding). In this question, the keyed answer is placenta praevia, a leading recognised cause.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uterine rupture — Uterine rupture is a rare cause, usually during labour.
• Placenta abruption — Abruption is also a common cause; the question accepts placenta praevia as its answer.
• Normal period — A normal period doesn't occur in pregnancy.

⭐ Key point: Never do a vaginal examination in antepartum haemorrhage until placenta praevia has been excluded by ultrasound.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q132$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2522, $s$A child in septic shock needs a fluid bolus of:$s$, $s$["100 mL/kg of 5% dextrose quickly", "1 mL/kg of blood only", "50 mL/kg of 0.18% saline", "10 mL/kg of crystalloid, then reassess"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 10 mL/kg of crystalloid, then reassess
For a child in septic shock, give a fluid BOLUS of 10 mL/kg of isotonic CRYSTALLOID (0.9% saline or balanced solution), then REASSESS (heart rate, capillary refill, BP, liver size, crackles) before giving more. Repeat as needed (up to 40–60 mL/kg), watching for fluid overload.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100 mL/kg of 5% dextrose quickly — 100 mL/kg of 5% dextrose is dangerous (hyperglycaemia, low sodium, overload).
• 1 mL/kg of blood only — 1 mL/kg of blood only is far too little.
• 50 mL/kg of 0.18% saline — 0.18% saline is hypotonic and unsafe for resuscitation.

⭐ Key point: Give antibiotics within 1 hour, and start inotropes if the child stays shocked after 40–60 mL/kg.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q133$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2523, $s$In children aged 1–10 years, hypotension is a systolic BP below:$s$, $s$["100 + (age in years) mmHg", "70 + (2 × age in years) mmHg", "50 mmHg at any age", "90 mmHg at any age"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 70 + (2 × age in years) mmHg
In children aged 1–10 years, HYPOTENSION is a systolic BP below 70 + (2 × age in years) mmHg. Example: a 5-year-old is hypotensive below 70 + 10 = 80 mmHg. Hypotension is a LATE sign of shock in children; tachycardia and poor perfusion appear first.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 100 + (age in years) mmHg — 100 + age is closer to the expected normal systolic BP, not the hypotension threshold.
• 50 mmHg at any age — 50 mmHg is too low as a general threshold.
• 90 mmHg at any age — 90 mmHg is the adult threshold.

⭐ Key point: Children compensate for shock well. When BP falls, they are close to cardiac arrest.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q137$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2524, $s$A child with croup has stridor at rest. The nurse expects:$s$, $s$["Throat swab and oral antibiotics only", "Immediate chest physiotherapy", "Nebulised salbutamol alone", "Oral dexamethasone and nebulised adrenaline"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Oral dexamethasone and nebulised adrenaline
Croup with STRIDOR AT REST is moderate to severe. The nurse expects ORAL DEXAMETHASONE (0.15–0.6 mg/kg), which reduces airway swelling over hours, and NEBULISED ADRENALINE (which shrinks the swelling quickly, within 10–30 minutes, but wears off after about 2 hours). Keep the child calm on the parent's lap.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Throat swab and oral antibiotics only — Croup is viral; antibiotics don't help.
• Immediate chest physiotherapy — Chest physiotherapy isn't useful and may upset the child.
• Nebulised salbutamol alone — Salbutamol doesn't help croup (the problem is upper airway swelling, not bronchospasm).

⭐ Key point: Observe for at least 2–4 hours after nebulised adrenaline, as the stridor may return.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q138$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$C3 · Obstetric, Neonatal & Paediatric Critical Care$s$, false, 2525, $s$A drooling, toxic-looking child sitting forward with stridor may have epiglottitis. The nurse should:$s$, $s$["Keep the child calm and upright and not examine the throat", "Lie the child flat and inspect the throat", "Take all blood samples first, then act", "Give a sedative to stop the child crying"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Keep the child calm and upright and not examine the throat
EPIGLOTTITIS is a life-threatening swelling of the epiglottis. The child is toxic, drooling, sitting forward (tripod position) with stridor. KEEP THE CHILD CALM and UPRIGHT (on the parent's lap), and DON'T examine the throat or do painful procedures. Upsetting the child can cause sudden complete airway obstruction. Call senior anaesthetic and ENT help to secure the airway in theatre.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lie the child flat and inspect the throat — Lying flat and inspecting the throat can cause complete obstruction.
• Take all blood samples first, then act — Blood tests can upset the child; secure the airway first.
• Give a sedative to stop the child crying — Sedation can depress breathing and cause obstruction.

⭐ Key point: Hib vaccination has made epiglottitis rare in children.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q139$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, true, 2526, $s$The Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria (NMCN), as a professional regulatory body, is primarily responsible for:$s$, $s$["Setting nursing education standards, registering/licensing nurses, and enforcing professional discipline", "Only organising nursing conferences", "Managing hospital construction budgets", "Setting national drug prices"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Setting nursing education standards, registering/licensing nurses, and enforcing professional discipline
The Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria (NMCN) is the government body that regulates nursing and midwifery in Nigeria. Its roles: setting and monitoring standards of nursing and midwifery education, accrediting schools, conducting licensing examinations (including post-basic council exams), registering and licensing nurses and midwives, renewing licences, and DISCIPLINING professional misconduct to protect the public.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only organising nursing conferences — Organising conferences is not its main legal function.
• Managing hospital construction budgets — Hospital construction budgets are not its responsibility.
• Setting national drug prices — Drug prices are not set by the NMCN.

⭐ Key point: The professional association and union for nurses is NANNM (National Association of Nigerian Nurses and Midwives), which is different from the regulator, the NMCN.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q185$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, true, 2527, $s$The doctrine of 'Res Ipsa Loquitur' ('the thing speaks for itself') applies when:$s$, $s$["A patient explicitly consents to a risky procedure", "An injury is of a type that would not normally occur without someone's negligence (e.g. a retained surgical instrument)", "A nurse documents care accurately", "A patient refuses treatment"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An injury is of a type that would not normally occur without someone's negligence (e.g. a retained surgical instrument)
Res ipsa loquitur ("the thing speaks for itself") is a legal rule used when the injury is of a kind that would NOT normally happen without negligence, the thing that caused it was under the professional's control, and the patient did not contribute. A swab or instrument left inside after surgery is the classic example. The patient doesn't have to prove exactly how the negligence happened.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A patient explicitly consents to a risky procedure — A patient consenting to a risky procedure doesn't apply here; consent relates to known risks, not negligence.
• A nurse documents care accurately — Accurate documentation is good practice and not related to this doctrine.
• A patient refuses treatment — A patient's refusal of treatment is about autonomy and consent.

⭐ Key point: Prevent such events with surgical safety checklists, swab and instrument counts, and correct-site marking.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q186$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, true, 2528, $s$'Battery' in a legal/nursing context refers to:$s$, $s$["A device that powers medical equipment", "Unlawful physical contact with a patient without consent", "A type of medication error", "A category of documentation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Unlawful physical contact with a patient without consent
Battery is unlawful, intentional TOUCHING of a person without their consent, even if no harm is done and even if the touching was meant to help. Examples: giving an injection, inserting a catheter or doing a procedure when the patient has refused or not consented. Assault is threatening to touch, causing fear of being touched.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A device that powers medical equipment — A battery that powers equipment is the everyday meaning, not the legal one.
• A type of medication error — A medication error is a different issue (possible negligence).
• A category of documentation — Battery is not a documentation category.

⭐ Key point: Always get valid consent before touching or treating a patient, and respect refusal. In emergencies with no capacity, act in the patient's best interests.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q188$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, true, 2529, $s$For informed consent to be legally valid, it must include all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Adequate information given to the patient", "Patient capacity to understand the information", "Voluntary agreement, free from coercion", "Approval from a family member instead of the patient, when the patient has full capacity"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Approval from a family member instead of the patient, when the patient has full capacity
The question asks which is NOT needed for valid consent. If the adult patient has full capacity, only THEY can consent. A family member's approval cannot replace the patient's own decision. Valid consent needs adequate INFORMATION (risks, benefits, alternatives), CAPACITY (able to understand, retain, weigh and communicate) and VOLUNTARY agreement (no pressure).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adequate information given to the patient — Adequate information IS required.
• Patient capacity to understand the information — Patient capacity IS required.
• Voluntary agreement, free from coercion — Voluntary agreement IS required.

⭐ Key point: Family can be involved and consulted, but they cannot consent for or overrule a competent adult. Consent for children is given by parents or guardians.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q189$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, true, 2530, $s$'Veracity' as an ethical principle in nursing means:$s$, $s$["Loyalty and faithfulness to commitments", "Truthfulness and honesty with patients", "Fairness in resource distribution", "Avoiding harm"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Truthfulness and honesty with patients
VERACITY means TRUTHFULNESS: being honest with patients, not lying or deceiving them, and giving information they need to make decisions. It builds trust and supports autonomy (informed consent). It also means being honest when errors occur (duty of candour).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Loyalty and faithfulness to commitments — Loyalty and faithfulness to commitments is FIDELITY.
• Fairness in resource distribution — Fairness in distributing resources is JUSTICE.
• Avoiding harm — Avoiding harm is NON-MALEFICENCE.

⭐ Key point: Ethical principles: autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice, veracity (truth), fidelity (keeping promises) and confidentiality.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q190$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, true, 2531, $s$A conscious, competent adult patient refuses a blood transfusion on religious grounds despite life-threatening anaemia. The nurse's ethically and legally appropriate response is to:$s$, $s$["Administer the transfusion anyway, since the situation is life-threatening", "Respect the patient's autonomous refusal after ensuring they are fully informed, and document thoroughly", "Have a family member override the patient's decision", "Discharge the patient immediately with no further care"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respect the patient's autonomous refusal after ensuring they are fully informed, and document thoroughly
A conscious adult with capacity has the legal and ethical right to refuse ANY treatment, even life-saving treatment, including a blood transfusion for religious reasons (such as Jehovah's Witnesses). This respects AUTONOMY. The nurse makes sure the patient is fully informed of the risks, explores alternatives (iron, erythropoietin, cell salvage, tranexamic acid), and DOCUMENTS the refusal carefully. Care continues.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Administer the transfusion anyway, since the situation is life-threatening — Giving blood anyway would be battery and a violation of autonomy.
• Have a family member override the patient's decision — Family members cannot override a competent adult's decision.
• Discharge the patient immediately with no further care — Discharging the patient without care is abandonment; other care continues.

⭐ Key point: An advance decision (such as a "no blood" card) must also be respected. For children, the courts can authorise transfusion in an emergency.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q194$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, true, 2532, $s$The Belmont Report's 3 core ethical principles are:$s$, $s$["Autonomy, Beneficence, Justice", "Consent, Privacy, Fairness", "Honesty, Accuracy, Transparency", "Safety, Dignity, Respect"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Autonomy, Beneficence, Justice
The BELMONT REPORT (USA, 1979) set out three core ethical principles for research with human subjects: (1) RESPECT FOR PERSONS (AUTONOMY) — treat people as autonomous, obtain informed consent, and protect those with reduced autonomy; (2) BENEFICENCE — maximise benefits and minimise harm; (3) JUSTICE — fair selection of participants and fair distribution of research burdens and benefits.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Consent, Privacy, Fairness — Consent, privacy and fairness are applications of the principles, not the three principles themselves.
• Honesty, Accuracy, Transparency — Honesty, accuracy and transparency relate to research integrity.
• Safety, Dignity, Respect — Safety, dignity and respect are general values.

⭐ Key point: Research ethics committees use these principles when reviewing study proposals.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q170 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, true, 2533, $s$A nurse floats to an unfamiliar ICU due to staffing shortages and is assigned a complex ventilated patient. The most appropriate first action is to:$s$, $s$["Refuse the assignment entirely with no discussion", "Communicate honestly about their competency level with this patient type, request orientation/support as needed, and advocate for patient safety", "Accept the assignment silently despite feeling unprepared, saying nothing", "Provide care without seeking any support or clarification"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Communicate honestly about their competency level with this patient type, request orientation/support as needed, and advocate for patient safety
When floated to an unfamiliar ICU, the nurse should COMMUNICATE HONESTLY about their COMPETENCY, REQUEST ORIENTATION or SUPPORT, and ADVOCATE for PATIENT SAFETY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Refuse the assignment entirely with no discussion — Refusing without discussion is unprofessional.
• Accept the assignment silently despite feeling unprepared, saying nothing — Accepting silently despite being unprepared risks patient safety.
• Provide care without seeking any support or clarification — Providing care without support may be unsafe.

⭐ Key point: Nurses must practise within their competence.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q177$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, true, 2534, $s$Which ethical principle refers to a patient's right to make their own healthcare decisions?$s$, $s$["Beneficence", "Autonomy", "Non-maleficence", "Justice"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Autonomy
AUTONOMY is the ethical principle that recognises a competent patient's RIGHT to MAKE THEIR OWN HEALTHCARE DECISIONS — including accepting or refusing treatment — based on adequate information and free from coercion. Nurses support autonomy through informed consent, honest communication, respecting choices and advance directives, and advocating for patients' wishes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Beneficence — Beneficence means acting for the patient's good.
• Non-maleficence — Non-maleficence means avoiding harm.
• Justice — Justice means fairness.

⭐ Key point: When patients lack capacity, decisions are made in their best interests, considering their known wishes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q191 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, true, 2535, $s$The primary purpose of incident (adverse event) reporting in healthcare is to:$s$, $s$["Assign blame and discipline individual staff", "Identify system issues and improve patient safety through learning", "Satisfy insurance requirements only", "Reduce documentation workload"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Identify system issues and improve patient safety through learning
The primary purpose of INCIDENT REPORTING is to IDENTIFY SYSTEM ISSUES and IMPROVE PATIENT SAFETY through LEARNING. Reports are analysed for trends and root causes, leading to changes (e.g. new protocols, equipment, training).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assign blame and discipline individual staff — It is not mainly to assign blame.
• Satisfy insurance requirements only — It is not only for insurance.
• Reduce documentation workload — It increases, not reduces, documentation.

⭐ Key point: Report all incidents and near misses promptly and factually.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q160$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, true, 2536, $s$An unconscious trauma patient with no relatives present needs an emergency life-saving procedure. Treatment can proceed legally on the basis of:$s$, $s$["Written informed consent signed by the nurse in charge", "A court order, which must be obtained before any treatment", "Proxy consent from any staff member on duty", "The doctrine of necessity (implied consent) in an emergency"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The doctrine of necessity (implied consent) in an emergency
Normally, treatment requires the patient's CONSENT. In an EMERGENCY, when the patient is UNCONSCIOUS (lacks capacity), and no one with legal authority is available, treatment that is IMMEDIATELY NECESSARY to save life or prevent serious harm can proceed under the DOCTRINE OF NECESSITY (also called IMPLIED CONSENT) — it is assumed that a reasonable person would want life-saving treatment. Only treatment that cannot wait until the patient regains capacity should be given, and it must be in the patient's best interests.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Written informed consent signed by the nurse in charge — A nurse (or any staff member) cannot sign consent on behalf of an adult patient.
• A court order, which must be obtained before any treatment — A court order is not required for emergency life-saving treatment; delay would cause harm.
• Proxy consent from any staff member on duty — Staff members cannot give "proxy consent" for patients.

⭐ Key point: Document clearly why treatment was needed urgently, that the patient lacked capacity, and that no valid refusal (e.g. an advance directive) was known. Inform relatives as soon as possible.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q196$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, true, 2537, $s$Telephone orders in an emergency should be:$s$, $s$["Read back, documented and countersigned", "Ignored until a doctor attends in person", "Accepted from any caller on the phone", "Accepted without writing anything down"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Read back, documented and countersigned
In an emergency, a doctor may give a verbal or telephone order. To prevent errors, the nurse must READ BACK the order (repeat it to the doctor to confirm the drug, dose, route and time), DOCUMENT it immediately (date, time, doctor's name, "telephone order, read back"), and ensure the doctor COUNTERSIGNS it as soon as possible (usually within 24 hours).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignored until a doctor attends in person — Ignoring an emergency order could harm the patient.
• Accepted from any caller on the phone — Orders should only be accepted from known, authorised prescribers.
• Accepted without writing anything down — Unwritten orders cannot be checked and are a legal risk.

⭐ Key point: Avoid telephone orders except in emergencies. Never accept them for high-risk drugs if avoidable, and always have a second nurse listen if possible.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q157$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2538, $s$According to adult learning principles (andragogy), adults learn best when the content is:$s$, $s$["Abstract, theoretical, and disconnected from real experience", "Relevant, problem-centred, and builds on their prior experience", "Delivered purely through passive lecture with no interaction", "Identical regardless of the learner's background"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Relevant, problem-centred, and builds on their prior experience
Malcolm Knowles' principles of adult learning (andragogy) say adults learn best when learning is RELEVANT to their work or life, PROBLEM-CENTRED (solving real problems) and builds on their EXPERIENCE. Adults are self-directed, motivated by internal factors, and want to know WHY they need to learn something. Use case studies, discussion, simulation and hands-on practice.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Abstract, theoretical, and disconnected from real experience — Abstract theory disconnected from experience is less effective for adults.
• Delivered purely through passive lecture with no interaction — Passive lectures with no interaction ignore adults' experience and self-direction.
• Identical regardless of the learner's background — Adults differ; teaching should consider each learner's background.

⭐ Key point: Pedagogy = teaching children (teacher-directed). Andragogy = teaching adults (self-directed, experience-based).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q181$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2539, $s$An incident report should be completed when:$s$, $s$["A medication error occurs", "A patient is discharged", "A nurse resumes duty", "The ward is cleaned"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A medication error occurs
An incident report is completed for any event that caused, or could have caused, harm to a patient, visitor or staff member, or that is not consistent with routine care. A MEDICATION ERROR is a classic example. Others: falls, needlestick injuries, equipment failures, wrong-patient procedures and near misses. The report is used to learn and improve the system, not to blame.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A patient is discharged — Discharge is routine and needs no incident report.
• A nurse resumes duty — Resuming duty is routine.
• The ward is cleaned — Cleaning the ward is routine.

⭐ Key point: The incident report is NOT placed in the patient's notes, and the notes don't say "incident form completed"; record the facts of what happened and the care given.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q184$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2540, $s$The four elements that must be proven to establish nursing negligence are:$s$, $s$["Consent, capacity, competence, and cost", "Duty, breach of duty, causation, and damages", "Autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, and justice", "Assessment, diagnosis, planning, and evaluation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Duty, breach of duty, causation, and damages
To prove negligence, all FOUR elements must be shown. 1) DUTY of care existed (a nurse–patient relationship). 2) BREACH of that duty (care fell below the expected standard). 3) CAUSATION (the breach directly caused the harm). 4) DAMAGES (actual harm or loss occurred). If any element is missing, negligence is not proven.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Consent, capacity, competence, and cost — Consent, capacity, competence and cost relate to consent and resources, not negligence.
• Autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, and justice — Autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence and justice are ETHICAL principles.
• Assessment, diagnosis, planning, and evaluation — Assessment, diagnosis, planning and evaluation are steps of the NURSING PROCESS.

⭐ Key point: Good documentation, following policies, staying within your scope and keeping up to date are the nurse's best protection against negligence claims.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q187$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2541, $s$A Do-Not-Resuscitate (DNR) order should be:$s$, $s$["Assumed automatically for all elderly patients", "Clearly documented in the medical record and communicated to the entire care team", "Kept verbal only, never written", "Decided solely by the nurse without physician or patient/family involvement"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Clearly documented in the medical record and communicated to the entire care team
A DNR (DNACPR) order must be CLEARLY DOCUMENTED in the patient's record (on the proper form, signed and dated by the senior doctor responsible) and COMMUNICATED to the whole team at handover, so everyone knows not to start CPR. It should be discussed with the patient (if they have capacity) and/or the family, and reviewed regularly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assumed automatically for all elderly patients — A DNR is never automatic for elderly patients; age alone is not a reason.
• Kept verbal only, never written — A verbal-only DNR is unsafe and legally risky.
• Decided solely by the nurse without physician or patient/family involvement — The nurse cannot decide alone; a senior doctor makes the decision with the patient or family.

⭐ Key point: DNR means no CPR only. All other care (pain relief, oxygen, feeding, antibiotics, dignity) continues unless separately decided.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q191$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2542, $s$Vulnerable research subjects include all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Children", "Prisoners", "Healthy working adults", "The physically/mentally challenged"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Healthy working adults
VULNERABLE research subjects are people who may have a reduced ability to give free, informed CONSENT or who are at higher risk of being exploited or harmed. They need extra ethical protection (e.g. parental consent plus child assent, independent advocates, extra committee review). Groups include: CHILDREN (minors), PRISONERS (may feel pressured, limited freedom), people with PHYSICAL or MENTAL disabilities, pregnant women and foetuses, the critically ill or unconscious, the elderly with dementia, the very poor or uneducated, students and employees of the researcher. HEALTHY WORKING ADULTS with full capacity can give free informed consent, so they are NOT considered a vulnerable group.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Children — CHILDREN ARE vulnerable — they cannot legally consent for themselves.
• Prisoners — PRISONERS ARE vulnerable — their freedom of choice may be limited.
• The physically/mentally challenged — The physically/mentally CHALLENGED ARE vulnerable — capacity to consent or ability to refuse may be reduced.

⭐ Key point: Vulnerable subjects: children, prisoners, mentally/physically challenged, pregnant women, critically ill. Healthy adults are NOT.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q167$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2543, $s$If a nurse witnesses another staff member committing a serious, unsafe practice violation, the appropriate ethical action is to:$s$, $s$["Ignore it to avoid conflict", "Report it through appropriate institutional and/or regulatory channels", "Only discuss it informally with friends", "Confront the colleague publicly in front of patients"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Report it through appropriate institutional and/or regulatory channels
Nurses have an ethical and professional duty to protect patients (beneficence, non-maleficence; NMCN Code of Professional Conduct). If a nurse WITNESSES a colleague doing something seriously UNSAFE (e.g. working while impaired, falsifying records, giving wrong drugs on purpose, abusing patients), they must first ensure the PATIENT IS SAFE, then REPORT it through the proper INSTITUTIONAL channels (nurse manager, chain of command, incident reporting system) and, when needed, to the REGULATORY body. Reporting should be factual, documented, and made in good faith; many institutions have whistle-blowing policies to protect the reporter.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignore it to avoid conflict — IGNORING it to avoid conflict allows patients to be harmed and makes the witness partly responsible.
• Only discuss it informally with friends — Only discussing it INFORMALLY with friends does not protect patients and may be gossip/breach of confidentiality.
• Confront the colleague publicly in front of patients — Confronting the colleague PUBLICLY in front of patients is unprofessional, causes distress and does not follow proper process.

⭐ Key point: Serious unsafe practice → keep the patient safe, then REPORT through institutional and/or regulatory channels.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q177$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2544, $s$Which ethical framework emphasizes caring as both an emotion and an action that guides moral decision making?$s$, $s$["Deontology", "Utilitarianism", "Virtue ethics", "Care ethics"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Care ethics
CARE ETHICS (ethics of care; Carol Gilligan, Nel Noddings) focuses on RELATIONSHIPS, compassion and responsibility to others. It sees CARING as both an EMOTION (concern, empathy) and an ACTION (doing things to meet the needs of the cared-for person), and uses this caring relationship to guide moral decisions — paying attention to the particular person and situation rather than only to abstract rules. It fits naturally with nursing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Deontology — DEONTOLOGY (Kant) bases decisions on DUTIES and RULES — some acts are right or wrong regardless of consequences.
• Utilitarianism — UTILITARIANISM bases decisions on CONSEQUENCES — the greatest good for the greatest number.
• Virtue ethics — VIRTUE ETHICS (Aristotle) focuses on good CHARACTER traits (honesty, courage, compassion) of the moral agent, not specifically caring as emotion and action.

⭐ Key point: CARE ETHICS = caring (emotion + action) and relationships guide moral decisions. Deontology = duty; utilitarianism = consequences.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q185$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2545, $s$The role of a national nursing regulatory council (e.g. NMCN) primarily includes:$s$, $s$["Setting and enforcing standards of practice, registering/licensing nurses, and handling professional misconduct", "Only organising social events for nurses", "Setting hospital drug prices", "Managing hospital construction projects"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Setting and enforcing standards of practice, registering/licensing nurses, and handling professional misconduct
The NURSING AND MIDWIFERY COUNCIL OF NIGERIA (NMCN), like other national nursing regulatory bodies, exists to PROTECT THE PUBLIC by regulating the profession. Its main roles: setting STANDARDS of nursing and midwifery education and PRACTICE; approving and accrediting training schools and curricula; conducting licensing examinations; REGISTERING and LICENSING nurses and midwives (and renewal of licences with continuing education); maintaining the register; publishing the Code of Professional Conduct; and investigating and disciplining PROFESSIONAL MISCONDUCT (through tribunals that can suspend or remove nurses from the register).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only organising social events for nurses — Organising SOCIAL events is not the council's role (professional associations like NANNM may organise events).
• Setting hospital drug prices — Setting hospital DRUG PRICES is not done by the nursing council.
• Managing hospital construction projects — Managing hospital CONSTRUCTION is not the council's role.

⭐ Key point: NMCN = set and enforce standards, accredit schools, register/license nurses, handle MISCONDUCT — to protect the public.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q186$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2546, $s$A nurse experiences moral distress when a terminally ill patient receives aggressive CPR. This arises from:$s$, $s$["Ethical dilemma", "Legal obligation", "Personal values conflict", "Inadequate staffing"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Personal values conflict
MORAL DISTRESS occurs when a nurse KNOWS what they believe is the ethically right action but feels unable to carry it out because of constraints (hospital policy, family demands, doctor's orders, hierarchy). It arises from a CONFLICT between the nurse's PERSONAL/professional VALUES and what they are required to do. Example: giving aggressive CPR to a terminally ill patient whom the nurse believes should be allowed to die peacefully. Effects: frustration, guilt, burnout and leaving the profession. Help: ethics consultation, clear DNACPR and end-of-life discussions, debriefing and team support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ethical dilemma — An ETHICAL DILEMMA is a situation with two or more morally acceptable options where the right choice is unclear; in moral distress the nurse knows what is right but is prevented from doing it.
• Legal obligation — A LEGAL OBLIGATION (e.g. to perform CPR without a DNACPR order) may be the constraint, but the distress itself arises from values conflict.
• Inadequate staffing — INADEQUATE STAFFING may cause stress, but it is not the source of distress in this scenario.

⭐ Key point: Moral distress = knowing the right action but being constrained → conflict with PERSONAL VALUES. Dilemma = unclear right choice.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q188$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2547, $s$Distributive Justice, as a concept, is most associated with the philosopher:$s$, $s$["John Rawls", "Aristotle", "Sigmund Freud", "Florence Nightingale"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: John Rawls
DISTRIBUTIVE JUSTICE concerns how society's benefits and burdens (including health care resources) are SHARED fairly. It is most associated with the American philosopher JOHN RAWLS (A Theory of Justice, 1971). Rawls proposed the "veil of ignorance" — choosing rules without knowing your own position in society — and the "difference principle": inequalities are only fair if they benefit the LEAST ADVANTAGED. In health care, this guides fair allocation of scarce resources (ICU beds, organs, ventilators, drugs) and equal access to care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Aristotle — ARISTOTLE wrote about justice (treat equals equally) and virtue ethics, but distributive justice as a modern concept is linked most with Rawls.
• Sigmund Freud — SIGMUND FREUD was a psychoanalyst, not a philosopher of justice.
• Florence Nightingale — FLORENCE NIGHTINGALE was the founder of modern nursing, not a justice philosopher.

⭐ Key point: Distributive justice = fair sharing of resources — JOHN RAWLS (veil of ignorance, difference principle).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q189$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2548, $s$During a critical patient handover between shifts, using the SBAR (Situation-Background-Assessment-Recommendation) framework primarily helps to:$s$, $s$["Make handover longer and more complicated", "Structure communication clearly and concisely, reducing the risk of missed or miscommunicated critical information", "Replace the need for any written documentation", "Only apply to nurse-to-nurse communication, never nurse-to-physician"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Structure communication clearly and concisely, reducing the risk of missed or miscommunicated critical information
SBAR is a structured communication tool: S — SITUATION (what is happening now: "Mr A, bed 4, BP has fallen to 80/50"); B — BACKGROUND (relevant history, diagnosis, treatment); A — ASSESSMENT (what the nurse thinks the problem is: "I think he is going into septic shock"); R — RECOMMENDATION/request (what action is needed: "Please come and review him in the next 10 minutes; should I start fluids?"). It makes handover and escalation CLEAR, CONCISE and complete, reduces the chance of MISSED or MISCOMMUNICATED information, and helps junior staff speak up with confidence. It is used for nurse-to-nurse handovers, nurse-to-doctor calls and inter-department transfers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Make handover longer and more complicated — SBAR makes handover SHORTER and simpler, not longer and more complicated.
• Replace the need for any written documentation — It does NOT replace written documentation; both are needed.
• Only apply to nurse-to-nurse communication, never nurse-to-physician — It is used across ALL professions, including nurse-to-physician communication (it was designed partly for this).

⭐ Key point: SBAR = Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation → clear, concise handover and escalation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q190$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2549, $s$The ethical principle of 'justice' in healthcare primarily concerns:$s$, $s$["Being truthful with patients", "Fair and equitable distribution of healthcare resources and treatment", "Avoiding harm to patients", "Keeping promises made to patients"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fair and equitable distribution of healthcare resources and treatment
The four main principles of healthcare ethics: AUTONOMY (respect the patient's right to decide), BENEFICENCE (do good), NON-MALEFICENCE (do no harm) and JUSTICE. JUSTICE means FAIRNESS — treating people fairly and distributing health care RESOURCES and treatment EQUITABLY, based on need and not on wealth, tribe, religion, gender or status. Examples: triage by clinical urgency, fair allocation of ICU beds, equal-quality care for all patients. Other related principles: VERACITY (truth-telling), FIDELITY (keeping promises), confidentiality.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Being truthful with patients — Being TRUTHFUL with patients is VERACITY.
• Avoiding harm to patients — Avoiding harm is NON-MALEFICENCE.
• Keeping promises made to patients — Keeping promises is FIDELITY.

⭐ Key point: JUSTICE = fair, equitable distribution of care and resources. Veracity = truth; non-maleficence = no harm; fidelity = promises.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q191$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2550, $s$A patient's family requests that the patient not be told their terminal diagnosis, though the patient (who has full decision-making capacity) has asked directly about their prognosis. The nurse's most appropriate approach is to:$s$, $s$["Automatically follow the family's wishes over the patient's own request", "Respect the competent patient's autonomy and right to information, while sensitively working with the family to understand their concerns", "Refuse to discuss the situation with either party", "Tell the patient nothing and avoid the topic indefinitely"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respect the competent patient's autonomy and right to information, while sensitively working with the family to understand their concerns
A competent adult patient has the RIGHT TO KNOW about their own diagnosis and prognosis (AUTONOMY and VERACITY/truth-telling). Here the patient has full decision-making capacity and has ASKED DIRECTLY. The nurse (with the doctor responsible for breaking bad news) should RESPECT the patient's request for information, while working SENSITIVELY with the family: listen to their fears and cultural reasons, explain the patient's rights and why honesty helps (planning, saying goodbye, spiritual preparation, consent for treatment), and include them in a supportive conversation if the patient agrees. Information should be given gently, at the patient's pace, using a structured approach such as SPIKES.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Automatically follow the family's wishes over the patient's own request — Automatically following the FAMILY over a competent patient violates the patient's autonomy.
• Refuse to discuss the situation with either party — REFUSING to discuss with either party abandons both the patient and family.
• Tell the patient nothing and avoid the topic indefinitely — Avoiding the topic indefinitely denies the patient's right to information and is dishonest.

⭐ Key point: Competent patient asks → respect AUTONOMY and right to the truth, while supporting the family sensitively (e.g. SPIKES).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q193$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2551, $s$Increasing opioids to relieve a dying patient's pain, even if it may hasten death, can be justified under the rule of double effect only if:$s$, $s$["The act itself is good or neutral", "The intention is to relieve suffering, not to kill", "The good effect outweighs the bad effect, which is not the means to the good", "All of these conditions are met"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: All of these conditions are met
The PRINCIPLE (RULE) OF DOUBLE EFFECT allows an action with both a GOOD effect and a possible BAD effect (e.g. high-dose opioids relieve pain but MAY hasten death) if ALL FOUR conditions are met: (1) the ACT ITSELF is good or morally neutral (giving pain relief); (2) the INTENTION is only the good effect — to relieve suffering — not to cause death (the bad effect is foreseen but not intended); (3) the bad effect is NOT the MEANS of achieving the good effect (pain is relieved by the drug's analgesic action, not by the patient dying); (4) there is PROPORTIONALITY — the good effect outweighs the bad one (severe suffering at the end of life). If any condition is missing, the act is not justified (e.g. giving a dose intended to kill is euthanasia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The act itself is good or neutral — "The act itself is good or neutral" is only ONE condition.
• The intention is to relieve suffering, not to kill — "The intention is to relieve suffering" is only ONE condition.
• The good effect outweighs the bad effect, which is not the means to the good — "The good outweighs the bad, which is not the means" covers proportionality and means, but not intention or the nature of the act.

⭐ Key point: Double effect requires ALL: good/neutral act, good intention, bad effect not the means, and good outweighs bad.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q194$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2552, $s$Copy-pasting a previous nursing note into today's entry without verifying current accuracy risks:$s$, $s$["Improved efficiency with no downside", "Documentation errors and propagation of outdated or inaccurate information ('note bloat')", "Better legal protection", "Faster patient discharge"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Documentation errors and propagation of outdated or inaccurate information ('note bloat')
COPY-PASTING previous notes without checking accuracy risks DOCUMENTATION ERRORS: outdated or wrong information is repeated ("note bloat"), real changes may be missed, and the record may misrepresent the patient's current condition. This is unsafe and can have legal consequences.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Improved efficiency with no downside — It may seem efficient, but has serious downsides.
• Better legal protection — It weakens, not improves, legal protection.
• Faster patient discharge — It doesn't speed up discharge.

⭐ Key point: Document only what you have personally assessed or done, at the time.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q157$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2553, $s$A core ethical requirement in human subjects research is that participation must be:$s$, $s$["Mandatory for all eligible patients", "Voluntary, based on informed consent, without coercion", "Compensated financially regardless of consent", "Determined solely by the researcher"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Voluntary, based on informed consent, without coercion
A core ethical requirement of research with human participants is that participation must be VOLUNTARY, based on INFORMED CONSENT, and WITHOUT COERCION. Participants must be told the purpose, procedures, risks, benefits, confidentiality arrangements, and their RIGHT to REFUSE or WITHDRAW at any time without penalty (e.g. their care will not be affected). Consent should be documented (usually signed).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mandatory for all eligible patients — Participation can never be mandatory.
• Compensated financially regardless of consent — Payment does not replace consent; excessive payment can be coercive.
• Determined solely by the researcher — The researcher cannot decide for participants; they decide for themselves.

⭐ Key point: Extra protection is needed for vulnerable groups (children, unconscious ICU patients, prisoners), e.g. consent from legal representatives.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q169$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2554, $s$A hospital's 'departmental risk management programme' typically includes all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Identification of common risk categories", "Incident reporting mechanisms", "Monitoring and evaluation of interventions", "Concealing incidents to protect the department's reputation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Concealing incidents to protect the department's reputation
A departmental RISK MANAGEMENT PROGRAMME aims to identify, prevent and reduce harm. It includes: IDENTIFICATION of common RISK categories (falls, medication errors, infections, pressure ulcers, equipment failure), INCIDENT REPORTING mechanisms, analysis of incidents (e.g. root cause analysis), corrective actions, staff education, and MONITORING and EVALUATION of interventions. CONCEALING INCIDENTS to protect the department's reputation is unethical and dangerous — it prevents learning, repeats harm, and may be illegal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Identification of common risk categories — Identifying risk categories IS part of the programme.
• Incident reporting mechanisms — Incident reporting IS part of the programme.
• Monitoring and evaluation of interventions — Monitoring and evaluation IS part of the programme.

⭐ Key point: Openness (duty of candour) with patients and families after incidents builds trust.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q174$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2555, $s$Below are the Patient and family-focused interventions EXCEPT$s$, $s$["Relieve anxiety and provide a sense of security", "Allow family to stay with patient, if possible, to alleviate anxiety", "Provide explanations and information", "Documentation of consent"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Documentation of consent
PATIENT- and FAMILY-FOCUSED interventions address emotional and psychological needs: RELIEVING ANXIETY and providing a SENSE OF SECURITY, ALLOWING FAMILY to stay with the patient where possible, providing clear EXPLANATIONS and INFORMATION, involving family in care and decisions, and respecting cultural and spiritual needs. DOCUMENTATION OF CONSENT is an important LEGAL/administrative task, but it is not a patient- or family-focused supportive intervention.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Relieve anxiety and provide a sense of security — Relieving anxiety IS a patient-focused intervention.
• Allow family to stay with patient, if possible, to alleviate anxiety — Allowing family presence IS family-focused.
• Provide explanations and information — Providing explanations IS patient- and family-focused.

⭐ Key point: Family-centred care in ICU reduces anxiety and improves satisfaction and outcomes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q185$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2556, $s$When triaging multiple patients, a nurse practising justice should prioritise based on:$s$, $s$["Personal characteristics such as wealth", "Clinical need", "Order of arrival only, regardless of severity", "Insurance status"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Clinical need
The ethical principle of JUSTICE requires fair treatment of all patients. In TRIAGE, a nurse practising justice prioritises patients based on CLINICAL NEED — the most seriously ill or injured, who will benefit most from immediate care, are seen first — not on personal characteristics, wealth, social status, or insurance.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Personal characteristics such as wealth — Wealth and personal characteristics should never determine care priority.
• Order of arrival only, regardless of severity — Order of arrival alone ignores severity and could let critically ill patients deteriorate.
• Insurance status — Insurance status should not decide clinical priority.

⭐ Key point: Use validated triage tools (e.g. South African Triage Scale, ESI) for fair, consistent prioritisation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q186$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2557, $s$The key distinction between Justice and Fairness is that:$s$, $s$["Justice = rightness/what's deserved; Fairness = impartiality/equal treatment", "They mean exactly the same thing with no difference", "Justice only applies to law, never to nursing", "Fairness only applies to financial matters"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Justice = rightness/what's deserved; Fairness = impartiality/equal treatment
JUSTICE and FAIRNESS are related but slightly different: JUSTICE refers to RIGHTNESS — giving people what they DESERVE or are entitled to, and distributing benefits and burdens appropriately. FAIRNESS refers to IMPARTIALITY — treating people EQUALLY and without bias or favouritism. In nursing, both guide ethical decisions about care and resources.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• They mean exactly the same thing with no difference — They are closely related but not exactly the same.
• Justice only applies to law, never to nursing — Justice applies to nursing ethics (e.g. resource allocation), not only to law.
• Fairness only applies to financial matters — Fairness applies to all aspects of care, not just finances.

⭐ Key point: Equity (giving people what they need) is often what justice requires, beyond simple equal treatment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q187$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2558, $s$Applying the exact same rule to everyone regardless of their different starting points is:$s$, $s$["Always guaranteed to be fair", "Justice, but not necessarily truly fair to everyone", "Never a valid ethical approach", "Only relevant in legal settings"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Justice, but not necessarily truly fair to everyone
Applying the SAME RULE to everyone regardless of DIFFERENT STARTING POINTS may be just (consistent), but NOT NECESSARILY FAIR (equitable) — some people need more support to achieve the same outcome.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Always guaranteed to be fair — Equal treatment is not always fair.
• Never a valid ethical approach — It can be a valid approach in some cases.
• Only relevant in legal settings — It applies beyond legal settings.

⭐ Key point: Equity means giving people what they need to achieve fair outcomes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q188$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2559, $s$The core legal/professional principle behind nursing documentation is best summarised as:$s$, $s$["'If it wasn't documented, it wasn't done'", "Documentation is optional if care was given correctly", "Only negative events need to be documented", "Documentation can be completed weeks later without issue"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 'If it wasn't documented, it wasn't done'
The legal and professional principle "IF IT WASN'T DOCUMENTED, IT WASN'T DONE" means that in legal proceedings, audits or investigations, care that is not recorded is assumed not to have been given. Documentation is evidence of care, communicates information to the team, supports continuity of care, and protects both patient and nurse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Documentation is optional if care was given correctly — Documentation is never optional, even if care was correct.
• Only negative events need to be documented — All care, observations and events should be documented, not only negative events.
• Documentation can be completed weeks later without issue — Documentation must be timely (contemporaneous); late entries must be clearly marked.

⭐ Key point: Write accurate, clear, factual, timely and signed records.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q189$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2560, $s$The ethical principle of justice, as applied to resource allocation in critical care, refers to:$s$, $s$["Treating all patients identically regardless of need", "Fair and equitable distribution of limited healthcare resources", "Prioritising the wealthiest patients", "Ignoring resource limitations entirely"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fair and equitable distribution of limited healthcare resources
JUSTICE in critical care means FAIR and EQUITABLE DISTRIBUTION of LIMITED resources (ICU beds, ventilators, dialysis machines, staff time, expensive drugs) based on clinical need and likely benefit, using transparent and consistent criteria.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Treating all patients identically regardless of need — Treating everyone identically ignores different needs.
• Prioritising the wealthiest patients — Prioritising wealthy patients is unjust.
• Ignoring resource limitations entirely — Ignoring resource limits is unrealistic and can harm patients.

⭐ Key point: Triage and admission policies, made openly by senior clinicians, support just allocation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q190$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2561, $s$Which is one of the twin Latin maxims applied in the principles of fair hearing?$s$, $s$["Nemo dat quod non habet", "Ubi jus ibi remedium", "Nemo judex in causa sua", "Res ipsa loquitur"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Nemo judex in causa sua
The two Latin maxims of FAIR HEARING (natural justice) are: "NEMO JUDEX IN CAUSA SUA" (no one should be a judge in their own case — no bias) and "AUDI ALTERAM PARTEM" (hear the other side).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nemo dat quod non habet — "Nemo dat quod non habet" relates to property law.
• Ubi jus ibi remedium — "Ubi jus ibi remedium" means where there is a right, there is a remedy.
• Res ipsa loquitur — "Res ipsa loquitur" means the thing speaks for itself.

⭐ Key point: Fair hearing applies in disciplinary proceedings.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q192$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2562, $s$An advance directive is a document that:$s$, $s$["Is only valid after the patient's death", "Expresses a patient's wishes for future medical care if they become unable to decide for themselves", "Can only be created by a physician", "Overrides all patient autonomy while the patient is conscious"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Expresses a patient's wishes for future medical care if they become unable to decide for themselves
An ADVANCE DIRECTIVE expresses a patient's wishes for FUTURE MEDICAL CARE if they become UNABLE TO DECIDE (e.g. refusing resuscitation or ventilation).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Is only valid after the patient's death — It is valid while the patient is alive.
• Can only be created by a physician — Patients create it, not physicians.
• Overrides all patient autonomy while the patient is conscious — It supports autonomy.

⭐ Key point: Check for advance directives on admission.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q193$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2563, $s$A dying patient has Cheyne-Stokes respirations. The nurse educates the family that this is:$s$, $s$["A sign of pain", "Indicative of fluid overload", "Normal at end-of-life", "Reversible with oxygen"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Normal at end-of-life
CHEYNE-STOKES respiration is a cyclical breathing pattern: breaths gradually get deeper and faster, then shallower and slower, followed by a period of APNOEA (no breathing), then the cycle repeats. It is caused by changes in the brain's control of breathing and is common and NORMAL near the END OF LIFE. Educating the family that this is an expected part of dying (and not a sign of suffering) helps reduce their distress.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A sign of pain — It is not a sign of pain.
• Indicative of fluid overload — It does not indicate fluid overload.
• Reversible with oxygen — It is not reversible with oxygen at end of life.

⭐ Key point: Focus on comfort: positioning, mouth care, calm environment, and supporting the family.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q194$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2564, $s$A core principle of modern risk management and incident reporting culture is to:$s$, $s$["Foster a blame-free, just culture that encourages open reporting of errors and near misses to enable learning and system improvement", "Punish every staff member who reports an incident", "Discourage any reporting to keep statistics low", "Hold only the most junior staff accountable for systemic errors"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Foster a blame-free, just culture that encourages open reporting of errors and near misses to enable learning and system improvement
Modern RISK MANAGEMENT uses a BLAME-FREE / JUST CULTURE that ENCOURAGES staff to openly REPORT errors and near misses without fear of unfair punishment. Reports are analysed to find SYSTEM problems (e.g. look-alike drugs, staffing, equipment design) so changes can prevent future harm. A just culture still holds people accountable for reckless behaviour.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Punish every staff member who reports an incident — Punishing every reporter discourages reporting.
• Discourage any reporting to keep statistics low — Discouraging reporting hides risks.
• Hold only the most junior staff accountable for systemic errors — Blaming junior staff ignores system causes.

⭐ Key point: Most errors result from system weaknesses, not careless individuals.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q154$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2565, $s$The ethical principle of beneficence in research means:$s$, $s$["Maintain confidentiality", "Keep anonymity and privacy", "Above all, do no harm", "Avoid plagiarism"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Above all, do no harm
BENEFICENCE in research means acting for the participants' good — maximising possible BENEFITS and, ABOVE ALL, DOING NO HARM (minimising risks of physical, psychological, social, legal and financial harm). Researchers must ensure that the expected benefits of the study outweigh its risks, stop the study if harm occurs, and protect vulnerable participants. (Some texts describe "non-maleficence" — do no harm — as part of beneficence.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Maintain confidentiality — Maintaining confidentiality relates to the principle of respect for persons and privacy.
• Keep anonymity and privacy — Anonymity and privacy are separate protections.
• Avoid plagiarism — Avoiding plagiarism relates to research integrity and honesty.

⭐ Key point: The Belmont Report's three principles: respect for persons (autonomy, informed consent), beneficence, and justice (fair selection of participants).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q168$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2566, $s$Leadership is based on a superior’s$s$, $s$["authority.", "responsibility.", "accountability.", "persuasive communication."]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: persuasive communication.
LEADERSHIP is the ability to INFLUENCE people to work willingly towards goals. It is based mainly on PERSUASIVE COMMUNICATION — inspiring, motivating, convincing and building trust — rather than giving orders. A leader can lead without a formal position (e.g. an experienced staff nurse others follow). MANAGEMENT, in contrast, is based on formal AUTHORITY given by the organisation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• authority. — Authority is the formal right to give orders — the basis of management, not leadership.
• responsibility. — Responsibility is the duty to carry out assigned tasks.
• accountability. — Accountability is being answerable for the outcomes of one's actions.

⭐ Key point: The best nurse managers combine management authority with good leadership (communication, vision and support).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q176$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2567, $s$The process of interaction between two or more people in an organisation is:$s$, $s$["Adaptation", "Interaction", "Induction", "Communication"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Communication
COMMUNICATION is the PROCESS of INTERACTION between two or more people in which information, ideas, feelings and meanings are exchanged — through verbal, written and non-verbal means. In organisations, communication flows downward (instructions), upward (feedback, reports), and horizontally (between colleagues). Good communication is essential for teamwork and patient safety.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Adaptation — Adaptation is the process of adjusting to change or the environment.
• Interaction — Interaction is a general term, but the organisational process described here is communication.
• Induction — Induction is the orientation of new staff.

⭐ Key point: Structured communication tools (e.g. SBAR for handover and escalation) reduce misunderstandings and errors.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q178$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2568, $s$Which four elements must all be proven for a nurse to be found legally negligent?$s$, $s$["Duty of care, breach of duty, harm, and breach causing the harm", "Intent, motive, opportunity, and consequence", "Consent, capacity, communication, and confidentiality", "Only that harm occurred, regardless of cause"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Duty of care, breach of duty, harm, and breach causing the harm
To prove a nurse NEGLIGENT, the claimant must establish ALL FOUR elements: (1) DUTY OF CARE — the nurse had a professional responsibility to the patient; (2) BREACH OF DUTY — the nurse failed to meet the expected standard of care (what a reasonable, competent nurse would do); (3) HARM (damage) — the patient suffered actual injury or loss; (4) CAUSATION — the breach directly CAUSED the harm. If any one element is missing, negligence is not proven.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intent, motive, opportunity, and consequence — Intent, motive, opportunity and consequence relate to criminal law (crimes), not negligence.
• Consent, capacity, communication, and confidentiality — Consent, capacity, communication and confidentiality are important ethical/legal principles, but not the elements of negligence.
• Only that harm occurred, regardless of cause — Harm alone is not enough — the other elements must also be proven.

⭐ Key point: Accurate, timely documentation is the nurse's best evidence that the standard of care was met.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q185$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2569, $s$A family disagrees with a DNR order for a patient with metastatic lung cancer. The nurse involves:$s$, $s$["Ethics committee", "Hospital security", "Risk management", "Social services"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ethics committee
When a FAMILY DISAGREES with a DO-NOT-RESUSCITATE (DNR) order — for example, for a patient with metastatic lung cancer — the nurse should involve the ETHICS COMMITTEE (along with senior medical staff). The ethics committee is a multidisciplinary group (doctors, nurses, ethicists, legal advisers, chaplains) that helps MEDIATE conflicts, clarifies ethical principles (patient autonomy, best interests, futility), reviews the patient's wishes and advance directives, and supports a fair, transparent decision.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hospital security — Hospital security deals with safety threats, not ethical disagreements.
• Risk management — Risk management focuses on legal and financial risk, not ethical mediation.
• Social services — Social services help with social needs (housing, finances, support), not ethical conflict resolution.

⭐ Key point: Early, honest, compassionate communication with families about prognosis and goals of care helps prevent such conflicts.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q191$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2570, $s$Which of the following best describes moral uncertainty?$s$, $s$["A situation where nurses have no ethical guidance or rules to follow", "A situation where nurses have conflicting ethical guidance or rules to follow", "A situation where nurses have doubts or uncertainties about the ethical course of action", "A situation where nurses are unable to make ethical decisions due to lack of knowledge or experience"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A situation where nurses have doubts or uncertainties about the ethical course of action
MORAL UNCERTAINTY occurs when a nurse has DOUBTS about which ethical course of action is right, or whether a moral problem even exists.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A situation where nurses have no ethical guidance or rules to follow — Having no guidance is not the definition.
• A situation where nurses have conflicting ethical guidance or rules to follow — Conflicting guidance describes a moral dilemma.
• A situation where nurses are unable to make ethical decisions due to lack of knowledge or experience — Lack of knowledge isn't the core definition.

⭐ Key point: Moral distress: knowing what is right but being unable to act.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q192$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2571, $s$Patient confidentiality may be ethically breached when:$s$, $s$["A colleague is simply curious", "There is a credible risk of serious harm to the patient or identifiable third parties", "A family member requests information without consent", "It is more convenient for staff"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: There is a credible risk of serious harm to the patient or identifiable third parties
CONFIDENTIALITY may be breached when there is a CREDIBLE RISK of SERIOUS HARM to the patient or IDENTIFIABLE THIRD PARTIES (e.g. threat of violence, child abuse, notifiable diseases).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A colleague is simply curious — Curiosity is not a valid reason.
• A family member requests information without consent — Family members need consent.
• It is more convenient for staff — Convenience is not a valid reason.

⭐ Key point: Share only the minimum necessary information.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q193$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2572, $s$A nursing instructor tells students that the most important principle when caring for patients is to do no harm. This principle is:$s$, $s$["Beneficence", "Justice", "Non-maleficence", "Veracity"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Non-maleficence
NON-MALEFICENCE means "FIRST, DO NO HARM" (primum non nocere). Nurses must avoid causing harm — intentionally or through carelessness — and must weigh the risks and benefits of every intervention. Examples: preventing falls, pressure ulcers and medication errors; avoiding unnecessary invasive procedures; using safe practices.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Beneficence — BENEFICENCE means actively DOING GOOD for the patient.
• Justice — JUSTICE means FAIRNESS in care and distribution of resources.
• Veracity — VERACITY means TRUTHFULNESS.

⭐ Key point: Patient safety initiatives (e.g. checklists, bundles, incident reporting) put non-maleficence into practice.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q194$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2573, $s$A patient's blood culture grows a common skin organism (coagulase-negative Staphylococcus) from only one of two culture sets drawn. The nurse understands this pattern often suggests:$s$, $s$["Always indicates central line infection", "A laboratory error with no clinical relevance", "Possible contamination, requiring clinical correlation rather than automatic treatment", "Definite true bacteraemia requiring immediate aggressive treatment with no further consideration"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Possible contamination, requiring clinical correlation rather than automatic treatment
COAGULASE-NEGATIVE STAPHYLOCOCCUS is a common SKIN organism. When it grows in only ONE of TWO blood culture sets, it often represents CONTAMINATION. The result needs CLINICAL CORRELATION (e.g. presence of a central line, prosthetic material, signs of infection).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Always indicates central line infection — It doesn't always indicate line infection.
• A laboratory error with no clinical relevance — It's not necessarily a lab error.
• Definite true bacteraemia requiring immediate aggressive treatment with no further consideration — It's not definite bacteraemia.

⭐ Key point: Proper skin disinfection reduces contamination.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 6 · Q197$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2574, $s$A nurse who designs and leads a new discharge-teaching service inside the hospital where she is employed is practising:$s$, $s$["Outsourcing", "Sole proprietorship", "Intrapreneurship", "Franchising"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Intrapreneurship
INTRAPRENEURSHIP is ENTREPRENEURIAL behaviour WITHIN an EXISTING ORGANISATION. An employee (an "intrapreneur") creates and leads a new product, service or process inside the organisation where she works, using its resources. Designing and leading a new discharge-teaching service in her own hospital is intrapreneurship.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Outsourcing — Outsourcing is contracting work to an outside company.
• Sole proprietorship — Sole proprietorship is a business owned by one person.
• Franchising — Franchising is operating a business under another company's brand and system.

⭐ Key point: Intrapreneurs share the creativity of entrepreneurs, but the organisation carries most of the financial risk.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q83$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2575, $s$A business owned and managed by one person who bears unlimited liability for its debts is a:$s$, $s$["Cooperative society", "Sole proprietorship", "Limited liability company", "Partnership"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sole proprietorship
A SOLE PROPRIETORSHIP is a business OWNED and MANAGED by ONE PERSON, who receives all the profits but bears UNLIMITED LIABILITY — meaning the owner's personal assets (house, car, savings) can be used to pay business debts. It is the simplest and most common form of small business.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Cooperative society — A cooperative is owned by members.
• Limited liability company — A limited liability company has limited liability.
• Partnership — A partnership is owned by two or more people.

⭐ Key point: Advantages: easy to start, full control. Disadvantages: unlimited liability, limited capital.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q90$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2576, $s$The main advantage of a limited liability company over a sole proprietorship is that:$s$, $s$["Profits never have to be shared", "The owners' personal assets are protected; their liability is limited to what they invested", "It cannot be sued", "It does not need to be registered"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The owners' personal assets are protected; their liability is limited to what they invested
In a LIMITED LIABILITY COMPANY, the owners' (shareholders') PERSONAL ASSETS are PROTECTED. Their liability for business debts is LIMITED to the amount they INVESTED in the company. This is the main advantage over a sole proprietorship, where the owner has unlimited liability.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Profits never have to be shared — Profits may be shared among shareholders.
• It cannot be sued — A company can be sued as a legal entity.
• It does not need to be registered — Companies must be registered.

⭐ Key point: A limited company is a separate legal entity from its owners.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q91$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2577, $s$Choosing a specific group of clients for a service (for example, families of patients discharged on home oxygen) is called:$s$, $s$["Diversification", "Liquidation", "Auditing", "Market segmentation and targeting"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Market segmentation and targeting
MARKET SEGMENTATION is dividing the whole market into smaller GROUPS (segments) of people with similar NEEDS or characteristics — for example, by age, health condition, location or income. TARGETING is then choosing which segment(s) to focus on and serve. Choosing FAMILIES of patients DISCHARGED ON HOME OXYGEN as clients is segmentation and targeting: the nurse can design services (oxygen management, education, equipment checks) and marketing specifically for their needs, which is more effective than trying to serve "everyone".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diversification — DIVERSIFICATION is expanding into new products, services or markets to spread risk.
• Liquidation — LIQUIDATION is closing a business and selling its assets to pay debts.
• Auditing — AUDITING is examining financial or clinical records to check accuracy and compliance.

⭐ Key point: After targeting comes POSITIONING — how you want the target clients to see your service compared with competitors (e.g. "reliable, expert respiratory home care").$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q100$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2578, $s$Which source of start-up finance does NOT have to be repaid with interest?$s$, $s$["A bank overdraft", "The owner's personal savings (owner's equity)", "A microfinance loan", "A bank loan"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The owner's personal savings (owner's equity)
The OWNER'S PERSONAL SAVINGS put into the business are called OWNER'S EQUITY. This money belongs to the owner, so it does NOT have to be REPAID, and NO INTEREST is charged. It also shows lenders and partners that the owner is committed. The disadvantage is that the owner risks losing their own savings if the business fails, and savings may be limited.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A bank overdraft — A bank OVERDRAFT is borrowed money that must be repaid, usually with high interest.
• A microfinance loan — A MICROFINANCE LOAN must be repaid with interest, often within a short period.
• A bank loan — A BANK LOAN must be repaid with interest and may require collateral.

⭐ Key point: Other non-repayable sources include grants (e.g. government or NGO entrepreneurship grants) and gifts from family. Investors provide equity but take a share of ownership and profits.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q101$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2579, $s$When a nurse entrepreneur advertises her services, which practice is MOST consistent with professional ethics?$s$, $s$["Truthful advertising with no false claims of cure or guaranteed results", "Using clients' photographs without consent", "Criticising named colleagues to win their clients", "Promising guaranteed cures to attract clients"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Truthful advertising with no false claims of cure or guaranteed results
Nurse entrepreneurs must follow the NMCN CODE OF PROFESSIONAL CONDUCT when advertising. Advertising must be TRUTHFUL and accurate, with NO FALSE CLAIMS of cure or GUARANTEED RESULTS, and must not mislead the public or exploit vulnerable people. Advertising should describe the services offered, qualifications, location and contact details honestly. This protects the public and the reputation of the nursing profession.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Using clients' photographs without consent — Using clients' photographs without consent breaches confidentiality and privacy.
• Criticising named colleagues to win their clients — Criticising named colleagues to win clients is unprofessional and may be defamatory.
• Promising guaranteed cures to attract clients — Promising guaranteed cures is dishonest and unethical; no health professional can guarantee outcomes.

⭐ Key point: Ethical marketing builds long-term trust; satisfied clients and professional referrals are often the best advertisement.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q105$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2580, $s$The theory that individuals compare their job inputs and outcomes with others' to remove inequities is:$s$, $s$["Reinforcement theory", "Efficacy theory", "Maslow's hierarchy", "Equity theory"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Equity theory
EQUITY THEORY (J. Stacy Adams, 1963) states that employees COMPARE the ratio of their own job INPUTS (effort, skills, experience, time) to OUTCOMES (salary, recognition, promotion) with the ratio of OTHER people in similar roles. If they perceive INEQUITY (unfairness) — e.g. a colleague doing less work gets the same pay — they feel demotivated and try to REMOVE the inequity: by working less, asking for a raise, changing their comparison, or leaving.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reinforcement theory — Reinforcement theory (Skinner) states behaviour is shaped by rewards and punishments.
• Efficacy theory — Efficacy (self-efficacy) theory (Bandura) is about a person's belief in their ability to succeed.
• Maslow's hierarchy — Maslow's hierarchy describes levels of human needs, not comparison with others.

⭐ Key point: Managers should ensure fair, transparent allocation of workload, pay, recognition and opportunities to maintain motivation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q108$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2581, $s$FIFO is mainly used to:$s$, $s$["Increase waste", "Delay procurement", "Increase stock", "Prevent expiry of supplies"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Prevent expiry of supplies
FIFO (FIRST IN, FIRST OUT) is a stock rotation method in which the OLDEST STOCK (the items received first) is USED FIRST. New stock is placed behind or below older stock. This PREVENTS items from passing their EXPIRY DATE on the shelf, reducing WASTE and cost, and ensuring patients receive safe, in-date drugs and supplies. It is especially important for drugs, IV fluids, sterile supplies and blood products.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase waste — FIFO reduces, not increases, waste.
• Delay procurement — FIFO does not delay procurement; good stock control helps order the right amounts at the right time.
• Increase stock — FIFO is about rotation, not increasing stock levels.

⭐ Key point: Check expiry dates regularly, remove expired items, and use a stock register or inventory system.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q112$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2582, $s$A medication error is detected and corrected before it reaches the patient. This event is classified as a:$s$, $s$["Near miss", "Never event", "Adverse event", "Sentinel event"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Near miss
A NEAR MISS is an error or unsafe event that is DETECTED and CORRECTED BEFORE it REACHES the PATIENT, so no harm occurs. Example: a nurse notices a wrong drug dose during a double-check and corrects it before administration. Near misses must be REPORTED because they show weaknesses in the system that could cause harm next time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Never event — A NEVER EVENT is a serious, largely preventable incident that should never happen if proper safety measures are followed (e.g. wrong-site surgery, retained swab).
• Adverse event — An ADVERSE EVENT is an incident that DOES reach the patient and causes harm.
• Sentinel event — A SENTINEL EVENT is an unexpected event causing death or serious physical or psychological injury.

⭐ Key point: A culture that encourages reporting near misses helps prevent serious incidents.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q113$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2583, $s$A nurse manager observes that staff members are reluctant to report medication errors due to fear of punishment. Which culture should be promoted?$s$, $s$["Just culture", "Competitive culture", "Blame culture", "Punitive culture"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Just culture
A JUST CULTURE encourages staff to REPORT errors and near misses WITHOUT FEAR of unfair PUNISHMENT, so the organisation can LEARN and fix SYSTEM problems. It distinguishes between: HUMAN ERROR (an honest mistake — console the person and fix the system), AT-RISK BEHAVIOUR (taking shortcuts — coach the person), and RECKLESS BEHAVIOUR (conscious disregard of serious risk — disciplinary action). This balances learning with accountability.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Competitive culture — A competitive culture may discourage openness about mistakes.
• Blame culture — A blame culture punishes individuals, so staff hide errors.
• Punitive culture — A punitive culture punishes errors, discouraging reporting.

⭐ Key point: Most errors are caused by system weaknesses (e.g. poor labelling, high workload); a just culture helps identify and correct them.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q115$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2584, $s$____ involves harmony and team spirit among employees.$s$, $s$["Standardisation", "Esprit de corps", "Order", "Discipline"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Esprit de corps
ESPRIT DE CORPS (French for "spirit of the group") is one of FAYOL'S 14 principles. It means promoting HARMONY, UNITY and TEAM SPIRIT among employees. Managers should encourage teamwork, cooperation and good communication, and avoid dividing staff. Strong team spirit increases morale, job satisfaction and performance — vital in the ICU, where care depends on teamwork.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Standardisation — STANDARDISATION refers to uniform methods and procedures (associated with Taylor).
• Order — ORDER (Fayol's principle) means "a place for everything and everything in its place" — for materials and people.
• Discipline — DISCIPLINE means respect for rules and agreements.

⭐ Key point: Other Fayol principles: division of work, authority and responsibility, unity of command, unity of direction, remuneration, centralisation, scalar chain, equity, stability of tenure, initiative.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q119$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2585, $s$Which of the following diagnostic statements contains an error because the etiology names something that cannot legally or ethically be documented as a "cause" by a nurse?$s$, $s$["\"Impaired Skin Integrity related to inadequate nursing care\"", "\"Ineffective Coping related to situational crisis\"", "\"Risk for Falls related to unsteady gait\"", "\"Acute Pain related to surgical incision\""]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: "Impaired Skin Integrity related to inadequate nursing care"
The ETIOLOGY of a nursing diagnosis must never be written in a way that is LEGALLY INADVISABLE or blames staff. "Impaired Skin Integrity related to INADEQUATE NURSING CARE" admits negligence in the legal record and could be used against the nurse and hospital in court. It should be written with the real cause, e.g. "related to immobility and pressure over bony prominences".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• "Ineffective Coping related to situational crisis" — "Ineffective Coping related to situational crisis" is correctly written.
• "Risk for Falls related to unsteady gait" — "Risk for Falls related to unsteady gait" is correctly written.
• "Acute Pain related to surgical incision" — "Acute Pain related to surgical incision" is correctly written.

⭐ Key point: Other errors to avoid: using medical diagnoses as the problem, writing circular statements, value judgements, and nursing interventions as diagnoses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q151$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2586, $s$An ICU nurse promises a frightened patient that she will be the one to update his wife after the procedure, and makes sure she does so even though the shift is busy. Which ethical principle does this show?$s$, $s$["Veracity", "Non-maleficence", "Fidelity", "Justice"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fidelity
FIDELITY means being FAITHFUL to one's PROMISES and COMMITMENTS. The nurse promised a frightened patient that she would personally update his wife after the procedure, and she KEPT THAT PROMISE even though the shift was busy. This builds TRUST, which is essential in the nurse–patient relationship, especially for anxious ICU patients and families.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Veracity — VERACITY means telling the truth; this scenario is about keeping a promise.
• Non-maleficence — NON-MALEFICENCE means avoiding harm.
• Justice — JUSTICE means fairness in treating patients and distributing resources.

⭐ Key point: Avoid making promises you cannot keep. If you cannot fulfil a promise, explain honestly and arrange for someone else to do it.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q194$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2587, $s$Active euthanasia differs from withdrawal of futile treatment because euthanasia:$s$, $s$["It must always be requested by the family", "It involves stopping a ventilator after brain death", "It allows the disease to take its natural course", "It intentionally ends life by a direct act, which is illegal"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It intentionally ends life by a direct act, which is illegal
ACTIVE EUTHANASIA means INTENTIONALLY ENDING LIFE by a DIRECT ACT (e.g. a lethal injection). It is ILLEGAL in Nigeria. WITHDRAWAL OF FUTILE TREATMENT means stopping treatment that no longer benefits the patient, allowing natural death — it is legal and ethical.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It must always be requested by the family — Euthanasia doesn't depend on family request.
• It involves stopping a ventilator after brain death — Stopping a ventilator after brain death is not euthanasia; the patient is already dead.
• It allows the disease to take its natural course — Allowing natural death describes withdrawal of treatment, not euthanasia.

⭐ Key point: The intention and the act are what distinguish euthanasia from withdrawal.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q141$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2588, $s$A dying declaration is:$s$, $s$["A statement by someone who believes death is imminent about the cause", "A written will made by a healthy person before any illness", "A request by the patient to withdraw life support", "A certificate issued by a doctor confirming the death"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A statement by someone who believes death is imminent about the cause
A DYING DECLARATION is a statement made by someone who BELIEVES THEIR DEATH IS IMMINENT about the CAUSE or circumstances of their death. It can be used as evidence in court.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A written will made by a healthy person before any illness — A will is made in advance.
• A request by the patient to withdraw life support — A request to withdraw support isn't a dying declaration.
• A certificate issued by a doctor confirming the death — A death certificate confirms death.

⭐ Key point: Record it accurately.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q143$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2589, $s$When an assault victim in ICU makes a dying declaration, the nurse should:$s$, $s$["Ignore it because only the police may record it", "Record the exact words, the time and witnesses, and sign", "Ask leading questions to obtain more details", "Summarise it in her own words at the end of the shift"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Record the exact words, the time and witnesses, and sign
A DYING DECLARATION can be used as evidence in court, so it must be recorded accurately. The nurse should: RECORD THE EXACT WORDS of the patient (verbatim — not her own interpretation), note the DATE and TIME, note the patient's mental state (e.g. alert, oriented), list the WITNESSES present, and SIGN the record (with witnesses also signing if possible). If possible, a magistrate or police officer should record it, but if death is imminent and they are not available, the doctor or nurse may record it. The patient should not be pressured.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignore it because only the police may record it — Ignoring it would lose important evidence. Health workers may record a dying declaration if the police or a magistrate are not available.
• Ask leading questions to obtain more details — Leading questions (suggesting answers) can make the statement unreliable and inadmissible in court.
• Summarise it in her own words at the end of the shift — Summarising in her own words could change the meaning; the exact words must be recorded immediately, not at the end of the shift.

⭐ Key point: Keep the record in a secure place and hand a copy to the appropriate authority following hospital policy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q144$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2590, $s$The Patient's Bill of Rights includes the right to:$s$, $s$["Demanding any treatment regardless of clinical need", "Information, consent or refusal, privacy and respect", "Choosing the nurses' shift patterns and rotas", "Reading other patients' records on request"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Information, consent or refusal, privacy and respect
A PATIENT'S BILL OF RIGHTS lists what patients can expect from health services. Key rights include: the right to INFORMATION about their condition and treatment in understandable language; the right to give INFORMED CONSENT or REFUSE treatment; the right to PRIVACY and confidentiality; the right to be treated with RESPECT and dignity without discrimination; the right to safe, quality care; the right to complain; and the right to access their own records.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Demanding any treatment regardless of clinical need — Patients cannot demand any treatment regardless of need; clinicians must not provide treatment that is not clinically appropriate.
• Choosing the nurses' shift patterns and rotas — Patients do not choose nurses' shift patterns; staffing is a management responsibility.
• Reading other patients' records on request — Patients cannot read other patients' records; this would breach confidentiality.

⭐ Key point: Nurses act as patient advocates: they explain rights, protect privacy and dignity, and speak up when rights are not respected.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q147$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2591, $s$The following are among the patients' bill of rights EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Right to dignity and personality", "Right to prompt attention and attendance", "Right to get married before discharge", "Right to access care when and as due"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Right to get married before discharge
A PATIENT'S BILL OF RIGHTS covers rights related to HEALTHCARE: RIGHT TO DIGNITY and respect of personality, RIGHT TO PROMPT ATTENTION and attendance (timely care, especially in emergencies), RIGHT TO ACCESS CARE when and as due, as well as rights to information, consent, privacy, confidentiality and complaint. The "RIGHT TO GET MARRIED BEFORE DISCHARGE" is NOT a healthcare right and is not in the bill of rights, so it is the EXCEPTION.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right to dignity and personality — Dignity and personality are central patient rights.
• Right to prompt attention and attendance — Prompt attention and attendance are patient rights, especially in emergencies.
• Right to access care when and as due — Access to care when due is a core patient right.

⭐ Key point: Know the main patient rights so you can respect and protect them.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q148$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2592, $s$Res ipsa loquitur applies when:$s$, $s$["The injury was caused only by the natural progression of the patient's disease", "The injury would not occur without negligence and the defendant controlled the cause", "The nurse followed all the written protocols and the injury was unavoidable", "The patient signed a written consent form before the procedure was carried out"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The injury would not occur without negligence and the defendant controlled the cause
RES IPSA LOQUITUR ("the thing speaks for itself") applies when an injury would NOT normally occur WITHOUT NEGLIGENCE, and the defendant had CONTROL of the cause (e.g. a swab left inside a patient).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The injury was caused only by the natural progression of the patient's disease — Natural disease progression is not negligence.
• The nurse followed all the written protocols and the injury was unavoidable — Following protocols suggests no negligence.
• The patient signed a written consent form before the procedure was carried out — Consent doesn't cover negligence.

⭐ Key point: The burden of proof shifts to the defendant.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q149$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2593, $s$A nurse who helps at a road accident while off duty is protected under Good Samaritan principles if she:$s$, $s$["Performs procedures she has never been trained to do", "Acts in good faith, within competence, without gross negligence", "Leaves the casualty halfway through care to go home", "Charges a reasonable fee for the help given at the scene"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acts in good faith, within competence, without gross negligence
GOOD SAMARITAN principles protect people who help at an emergency voluntarily. A nurse helping at a road accident while off duty is protected if she ACTS IN GOOD FAITH (genuinely trying to help), WITHIN HER COMPETENCE (doing only what she is trained to do), and WITHOUT GROSS NEGLIGENCE (no reckless or seriously careless actions). Examples of reasonable help: making the scene safe, calling emergency services, opening the airway, controlling bleeding, CPR and staying with the casualty until help arrives.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Performs procedures she has never been trained to do — Performing procedures she has never been trained to do (e.g. an emergency surgical airway) is outside her competence and is NOT protected.
• Leaves the casualty halfway through care to go home — Abandoning a casualty partway through care may cause harm and is NOT protected.
• Charges a reasonable fee for the help given at the scene — Charging a fee changes the help from voluntary to professional service, so Good Samaritan protection does not apply.

⭐ Key point: At any emergency: safety first, call for help, give care within your competence, and hand over clearly to the ambulance team.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q150$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2594, $s$In a suspected criminal case, a bullet removed from a patient in ICU should be:$s$, $s$["Labelled, sealed and given to police with chain of custody", "Washed and handed to the relatives on request", "Discarded with the clinical waste in theatre", "Kept by the surgeon in a pot as a souvenir"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Labelled, sealed and given to police with chain of custody
A bullet removed from a patient is FORENSIC EVIDENCE in a criminal case. To be valid in court, its handling must be documented from removal to handing over — this is the CHAIN OF CUSTODY. The bullet should be handled carefully (avoid metal instruments that scratch it if possible, as scratches can affect ballistic matching), placed in a clean container, LABELLED (patient name, date, time, description, who removed it), SEALED and signed, and handed directly to the POLICE, with each transfer recorded and signed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Washed and handed to the relatives on request — Washing it may destroy evidence (e.g. fibres, blood), and relatives have no right to it — it belongs to the legal investigation.
• Discarded with the clinical waste in theatre — Discarding it destroys important evidence and could obstruct justice.
• Kept by the surgeon in a pot as a souvenir — Keeping it as a souvenir is unprofessional, unlawful and breaks the chain of custody.

⭐ Key point: The same chain-of-custody principles apply to other evidence: clothing (place in paper, not plastic, bags), weapons, and samples in poisoning or assault cases.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q154$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2595, $s$Fidelity in nursing ethics means:$s$, $s$["Distributing resources fairly", "Telling the truth at all times", "Keeping promises and commitments", "Avoiding harm to the patient"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Keeping promises and commitments
FIDELITY means being FAITHFUL to one's COMMITMENTS and PROMISES — keeping your word to patients, being loyal to the patient's interests, and fulfilling professional duties. Example: if a nurse promises to return with pain medication in 10 minutes, fidelity means doing so, or explaining a delay. It builds trust between nurse and patient.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Distributing resources fairly — Distributing resources fairly is JUSTICE.
• Telling the truth at all times — Telling the truth is VERACITY.
• Avoiding harm to the patient — Avoiding harm is NON-MALEFICENCE.

⭐ Key point: The key ethical principles: autonomy (self-determination), beneficence (doing good), non-maleficence (avoiding harm), justice (fairness), veracity (truthfulness), fidelity (keeping promises), and confidentiality.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q155$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2596, $s$A nurse documents care after the patient's death that was not actually given. This is:$s$, $s$["Good documentation practice in ICU", "Required by law to complete the chart", "Falsification of records and misconduct", "Acceptable if the care had been planned"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Falsification of records and misconduct
Documenting care that was NOT ACTUALLY GIVEN is FALSIFICATION OF RECORDS. It is dishonest, misleads other health workers and the court, and hides possible omissions in care. It is PROFESSIONAL MISCONDUCT that can lead to disciplinary action by the NMCN (suspension or removal from the register) and even criminal charges.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Good documentation practice in ICU — This is the opposite of good documentation. Records must reflect only what was actually done.
• Required by law to complete the chart — The law requires TRUTHFUL records, not completed charts with false information.
• Acceptable if the care had been planned — Planned care that was not given must never be recorded as given. Record what was done and, if appropriate, why planned care was missed.

⭐ Key point: Records are legal documents. "If it wasn't documented, it wasn't done" — but equally, "if it wasn't done, it must not be documented".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q156$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2597, $s$Informed consent for an ICU procedure is not valid if:$s$, $s$["The patient was coerced or not told material risks", "A witness was present at the signing", "The patient asked questions before signing", "The doctor explained the alternatives"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The patient was coerced or not told material risks
For consent to be VALID, it must be: (1) given VOLUNTARILY (free from COERCION, pressure, threats or manipulation); (2) INFORMED (the patient was told the nature of the procedure, its benefits, MATERIAL RISKS and alternatives); and (3) given by a patient with CAPACITY (able to understand, retain, weigh and communicate the information). If the patient was COERCED or NOT TOLD the MATERIAL RISKS, the consent is NOT valid, even if a form was signed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A witness was present at the signing — A witness being present supports validity; it does not make consent invalid.
• The patient asked questions before signing — A patient asking questions shows engagement and understanding; it does not make consent invalid.
• The doctor explained the alternatives — The doctor explaining alternatives is a requirement for valid consent, not a reason for invalidity.

⭐ Key point: A signature on a form is only evidence of consent. What makes consent valid is the process of explanation, understanding and free choice.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q157$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2598, $s$When a non-responsive on-call doctor fails to attend a deteriorating patient, the nurse should:$s$, $s$["Escalate through the chain of command, documenting calls", "Transfer the patient to another hospital herself", "Wait until the consultant's morning ward round", "Treat the patient beyond her scope without orders"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Escalate through the chain of command, documenting calls
When a patient is deteriorating and the on-call doctor does not respond, the nurse must not wait. She should ESCALATE THROUGH THE CHAIN OF COMMAND: repeat the call, then contact the next senior doctor (registrar, consultant), the nursing supervisor or the rapid response team, following the hospital escalation policy. She must DOCUMENT each call (time, person called, information given, response) and continue to monitor and support the patient within her scope of practice (e.g. oxygen, positioning, emergency protocols).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Transfer the patient to another hospital herself — Transferring a patient to another hospital requires medical decisions and arrangements; it is not a nurse's independent action.
• Wait until the consultant's morning ward round — Waiting until the morning round could allow the patient to die; delay is negligence.
• Treat the patient beyond her scope without orders — Acting beyond her scope without orders exposes the patient to risk and the nurse to legal liability. She acts within approved protocols only.

⭐ Key point: Use SBAR (Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation) to communicate clearly and convincingly when escalating.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q158$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2599, $s$Advance directives made by a competent patient should be:$s$, $s$["Respected when valid and applicable", "Overruled by the relatives' wishes", "Ignored once the patient is unconscious", "Followed only if the doctor agrees"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respected when valid and applicable
An ADVANCE DIRECTIVE is a statement made by a competent person about the treatment they would or would not want if they later lose capacity (e.g. "no mechanical ventilation" or "no blood transfusion"). If it is VALID (made voluntarily by a competent adult, not withdrawn) and APPLICABLE (it covers the current situation and treatment), it must be RESPECTED, as it represents the patient's own autonomous decision.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Overruled by the relatives' wishes — Relatives cannot overrule a valid advance directive, because the decision belongs to the patient.
• Ignored once the patient is unconscious — The directive is designed exactly for when the patient becomes unconscious; it must not be ignored then.
• Followed only if the doctor agrees — The doctor's agreement is not required; a valid and applicable refusal must be followed even if the doctor disagrees.

⭐ Key point: Check that the directive is valid and applicable, document it clearly, and involve the senior team and legal advice if in doubt.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q159$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2600, $s$Vicarious liability means that:$s$, $s$["The employer may be liable for staff negligence at work", "The patient is liable for the errors made by staff", "Nurses are never personally accountable for errors", "Only the employer can ever be sued for negligence"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The employer may be liable for staff negligence at work
VICARIOUS LIABILITY is a legal principle where an EMPLOYER (e.g. the hospital) can be held LIABLE for the NEGLIGENCE of its employees, if the negligence occurred while they were doing their job ("in the course of employment"). This allows patients to claim compensation from the hospital. The nurse is still personally accountable professionally.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The patient is liable for the errors made by staff — The patient is not liable for staff errors.
• Nurses are never personally accountable for errors — Nurses remain personally and professionally accountable; vicarious liability does not remove this.
• Only the employer can ever be sued for negligence — Individual staff can also be sued; it is not only the employer.

⭐ Key point: Nurses should practise within their scope and follow policies, as acting outside them may remove the employer's cover. Personal indemnity insurance is advisable.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q160$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2601, $s$A QA audit reveals that although the ICU has adequate equipment and staff (structure) and nurses are documenting care appropriately (process), patient outcomes remain poor. This scenario illustrates that:$s$, $s$["Structure and process alone are insufficient; outcomes must be measured", "The audit should be stopped because the standards are met", "Outcome measures are irrelevant when the structure is adequate", "Good structure and process always guarantee good patient outcomes"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Structure and process alone are insufficient; outcomes must be measured
Good STRUCTURE (adequate equipment and staff) and good PROCESS (proper documentation) are necessary, but they do NOT GUARANTEE good OUTCOMES. Outcomes may still be poor because of other factors: the wrong processes being measured, poor quality of care despite documentation, case mix, or system problems. So OUTCOMES MUST ALSO BE MEASURED to judge real quality of care, and the reasons for poor outcomes must be investigated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The audit should be stopped because the standards are met — The audit should not stop; poor outcomes show there is still a problem.
• Outcome measures are irrelevant when the structure is adequate — Outcome measures are the most important indicator of whether care is effective.
• Good structure and process always guarantee good patient outcomes — This scenario shows the opposite: good structure and process do not always guarantee good outcomes.

⭐ Key point: A complete quality programme measures all three (structure, process, outcome) and looks for links between them.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q165$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2602, $s$An ICU outreach (rapid response) team mainly aims to:$s$, $s$["Replace ward nursing staff during staff shortages", "Discharge ICU patients faster to free beds", "Perform elective procedures on the wards", "Identify and treat deteriorating ward patients early"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Identify and treat deteriorating ward patients early
An ICU OUTREACH or RAPID RESPONSE TEAM is a group of critical care staff who go to the wards to IDENTIFY and TREAT DETERIORATING PATIENTS EARLY (triggered by early warning scores like NEWS2). This can prevent cardiac arrest and unplanned ICU admission, and support ward staff. They also follow up patients discharged from ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Replace ward nursing staff during staff shortages — They do not replace ward nurses during shortages.
• Discharge ICU patients faster to free beds — Their aim is not to speed up discharges but to support safe care.
• Perform elective procedures on the wards — They do not perform elective procedures on the wards.

⭐ Key point: Early recognition and response to deterioration saves lives.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q169$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2603, $s$The APACHE II score is used in ICU to:$s$, $s$["Calculate nursing workload for the duty roster", "Assess the depth of sedation in ventilated patients", "Measure pain in patients who cannot self-report", "Estimate illness severity and predicted mortality"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Estimate illness severity and predicted mortality
The APACHE II score (Acute Physiology and Chronic Health Evaluation) uses the worst values of physiological measurements in the first 24 hours (e.g. temperature, BP, heart rate, oxygenation, pH, sodium, potassium, creatinine, haematocrit, WBC, GCS), plus age and chronic health conditions, to ESTIMATE ILLNESS SEVERITY and PREDICT MORTALITY. A higher score means a higher risk of death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Calculate nursing workload for the duty roster — Nursing workload is measured using tools like TISS or NAS.
• Assess the depth of sedation in ventilated patients — Sedation depth is assessed using RASS or Ramsay scores.
• Measure pain in patients who cannot self-report — Pain in non-communicative patients is assessed using CPOT or BPS.

⭐ Key point: APACHE II is used for comparing ICU performance and research, not to decide individual treatment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q170$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2604, $s$The best method to distribute ICU nurses on a shift is according to:$s$, $s$["Patient acuity and nurse competence", "Personal preference of nurses", "Alphabetical order of names", "Seniority of the nurses only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Patient acuity and nurse competence
ICU nurses should be allocated according to PATIENT ACUITY (how sick and complex the patient is) and NURSE COMPETENCE (skills and experience). The sickest patients get the most experienced nurses, and new nurses get supervision. This ensures safe, effective care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Personal preference of nurses — Personal preference doesn't ensure safe care.
• Alphabetical order of names — Alphabetical order ignores patient needs and nurse skills.
• Seniority of the nurses only — Seniority alone ignores patient acuity and specific skills.

⭐ Key point: Review allocation during the shift as patient conditions change.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q172$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2605, $s$An ICU discharge to the ward should ideally occur:$s$, $s$["During the day with a structured handover", "Without notes to save time", "At night to free beds quickly", "Before the ward has accepted the patient"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: During the day with a structured handover
ICU discharge should ideally happen DURING THE DAY, with a STRUCTURED HANDOVER (written and verbal) to the ward team. Night-time discharges are linked to higher readmission and death rates, because ward staffing is lower and fewer senior doctors are available.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Without notes to save time — Notes are essential for continuity of care.
• At night to free beds quickly — Night discharges increase risk.
• Before the ward has accepted the patient — The ward must accept the patient and be prepared to receive them.

⭐ Key point: Outreach teams should follow up patients after ICU discharge.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q174$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2606, $s$An open ICU differs from a closed ICU in that, in an open ICU:$s$, $s$["Patients can walk in freely without any referral or review", "Only the intensivists may write prescriptions for patients", "There are no doors or walls separating the bed spaces", "Patients stay under their admitting consultants, not an intensivist"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Patients stay under their admitting consultants, not an intensivist
In an OPEN ICU, patients remain under the care of their ADMITTING CONSULTANTS (e.g. the surgeon or physician), who make the main decisions, sometimes with input from an intensivist. In a CLOSED ICU, an INTENSIVIST team takes over the patient's care while in ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Patients can walk in freely without any referral or review — Open ICUs still require proper referral and admission.
• Only the intensivists may write prescriptions for patients — In a closed (not open) ICU, intensivists write prescriptions.
• There are no doors or walls separating the bed spaces — "Open" refers to management, not physical layout.

⭐ Key point: Closed ICUs led by intensivists are associated with better outcomes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q179$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2607, $s$Withdrawal of life-sustaining treatment in ICU should involve:$s$, $s$["Team consensus, family discussion and a documented comfort plan", "No written record, to protect the staff involved", "Stopping all care, including analgesia and sedation", "A single nurse deciding alone during the night shift"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Team consensus, family discussion and a documented comfort plan
WITHDRAWAL OF LIFE-SUSTAINING TREATMENT (e.g. stopping ventilation or inotropes when further treatment cannot help) is a serious decision. It must involve: TEAM CONSENSUS (doctors and nurses agree it is in the patient's best interests); FAMILY DISCUSSION (explaining the situation, listening to their views and the patient's wishes); and a DOCUMENTED COMFORT/END-OF-LIFE CARE PLAN (pain relief, sedation, mouth care, privacy, spiritual support).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No written record, to protect the staff involved — Decisions must be clearly written in the notes. Hiding them is unprofessional and unlawful.
• Stopping all care, including analgesia and sedation — Comfort care continues: pain relief and sedation must be given to prevent suffering.
• A single nurse deciding alone during the night shift — No nurse should make this decision alone; it is a team decision led by the senior doctor.

⭐ Key point: "Withdrawal of treatment" never means "withdrawal of care". Dignity, comfort, family presence and spiritual/religious needs remain priorities.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q142$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2608, $s$A competent Jehovah's Witness refuses blood transfusion while bleeding in ICU. The nurse should:$s$, $s$["Discharge the patient from ICU to the ward", "Transfuse the blood secretly while the patient sleeps", "Sedate the patient and then give the transfusion", "Respect the refusal, document it and support alternatives"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Respect the refusal, document it and support alternatives
A COMPETENT ADULT has the legal and ethical right to REFUSE any treatment, including blood transfusion, even if refusing may lead to death (the principle of AUTONOMY). The nurse should RESPECT the refusal, make sure the patient understands the risks, DOCUMENT the discussion clearly (an advance directive or signed refusal form may exist), and SUPPORT ALTERNATIVES: cell salvage (if acceptable), iron, erythropoietin, tranexamic acid, volume expanders, minimising blood tests, and control of the bleeding source.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Discharge the patient from ICU to the ward — Discharging the patient from ICU because they refuse blood is abandonment. The patient still deserves full care.
• Transfuse the blood secretly while the patient sleeps — Transfusing secretly is assault (battery) and a serious ethical and legal violation.
• Sedate the patient and then give the transfusion — Sedating a patient in order to give a refused transfusion is assault and a gross ethical breach.

⭐ Key point: Check the patient's competence (understands, retains, weighs and communicates the decision) and that the decision is made freely, without pressure. Many Jehovah's Witnesses accept certain alternatives; ask each patient individually.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q143$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2609, $s$The legal age of consent for medical treatment in Nigeria is generally:$s$, $s$["18 years", "14 years", "12 years", "21 years"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 18 years
In Nigeria, a person aged 18 YEARS or older is generally treated as an ADULT who can give legal consent for medical treatment. Below 18, consent is usually obtained from a parent or legal guardian. In emergencies, treatment needed to save a minor's life can proceed in the child's best interests without waiting for consent.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 14 years — 14 years is below the general age of consent.
• 12 years — 12 years is below the general age of consent.
• 21 years — 21 years is higher than the general legal age of adulthood in Nigeria.

⭐ Key point: Older children should still be involved in decisions and their assent sought, even if a parent gives formal consent.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q145$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2610, $s$A nurse gives the wrong drug to an ICU patient without harm. The correct action is to:$s$, $s$["Say nothing, because the patient came to no harm", "Inform the doctor, monitor, document and report the incident", "Change the drug chart to hide the administration error", "Ask a colleague to sign the chart for the wrong drug"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inform the doctor, monitor, document and report the incident
Even if no harm occurred, a medication error must be handled openly. The nurse should: INFORM THE DOCTOR immediately (so the patient can be assessed for possible effects), MONITOR THE PATIENT closely, DOCUMENT accurately what was given and actions taken, and REPORT THE INCIDENT through the hospital's incident reporting system. Open disclosure to the patient/family is also part of the "duty of candour".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Say nothing, because the patient came to no harm — Hiding the error is dishonest; effects may appear later, and the chance to learn and prevent future errors is lost.
• Change the drug chart to hide the administration error — Changing the chart to hide the error is falsification of records — serious misconduct that can lead to losing your licence.
• Ask a colleague to sign the chart for the wrong drug — Asking someone to sign for a drug they didn't give is falsifying records and dishonest.

⭐ Key point: Reporting errors is not about blame; it helps the system find causes (e.g. similar drug names, poor labelling) and prevent future harm. Use the "rights" of medication administration to prevent errors.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q146$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2611, $s$Patient information may be shared without consent when:$s$, $s$["A journalist requests it for a story", "The patient is a well-known public figure", "A friend of the patient calls the unit", "Required by law, e.g. notifiable diseases"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Required by law, e.g. notifiable diseases
Patient information is CONFIDENTIAL. It may be shared WITHOUT consent only in limited situations: when REQUIRED BY LAW (e.g. NOTIFIABLE DISEASES such as Lassa fever, cholera, TB must be reported to public health authorities; a court order), or when there is a serious risk to the public or others (public interest), or in safeguarding cases. Only the necessary information is shared, with the right people.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A journalist requests it for a story — Journalists have no right to patient information; sharing it is a serious breach.
• The patient is a well-known public figure — Being famous does not remove a patient's right to confidentiality.
• A friend of the patient calls the unit — A friend calling has no right to information without the patient's consent.

⭐ Key point: In ICU, be careful on the phone: confirm the caller's identity and the patient's consent (or use a password system agreed with the family).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q147$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2612, $s$Taking photographs of an ICU patient for social media is:$s$, $s$["A breach of confidentiality and misconduct", "Encouraged to support health education", "Acceptable if the patient's face is covered", "Acceptable with a relative's verbal permission"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A breach of confidentiality and misconduct
Taking and posting photographs of an ICU patient on social media is a BREACH OF CONFIDENTIALITY and PROFESSIONAL MISCONDUCT. Patients in ICU are often unconscious, vulnerable and unable to consent. Even partially hidden images can identify a patient (tattoos, location, equipment, timing). The Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria (NMCN) can discipline nurses for this, including suspension or removal from the register.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Encouraged to support health education — Health education must never use identifiable patient images without proper, formal consent and approval.
• Acceptable if the patient's face is covered — Covering the face does not remove the breach; other details can identify the patient, and consent is still missing.
• Acceptable with a relative's verbal permission — A relative's verbal permission is not valid consent for social media publication.

⭐ Key point: Never take photos of patients on personal phones. Clinical photographs (for the record) need formal consent and must be stored securely in the hospital system.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q148$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2613, $s$Documentation in the ICU chart should be:$s$, $s$["Accurate, timely, legible, signed, never erased", "Written up at the end of the working week", "Written by any staff member for others", "Corrected neatly using correction fluid"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Accurate, timely, legible, signed, never erased
Good ICU documentation is ACCURATE (true and correct), TIMELY (written as soon as possible after care), LEGIBLE (easy to read), SIGNED with name, designation, date and time, and NEVER ERASED or obliterated. Errors are corrected by drawing a SINGLE LINE through the mistake (so it can still be read), writing "error" or the correct entry, and signing and dating it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Written up at the end of the working week — Writing notes at the end of the week leads to errors and omissions; records must be written at the time of care.
• Written by any staff member for others — Each person must document only the care they personally gave or witnessed.
• Corrected neatly using correction fluid — Correction fluid hides the original entry; this is not allowed because it looks like falsification.

⭐ Key point: "If it's not documented, it was not done." Records are legal documents and can be used in court.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q149$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2614, $s$A late entry in the patient record should be:$s$, $s$["Backdated to the time the event actually happened", "Squeezed in between the earlier lines of the notes", "Written in pencil so it can be changed later", "Marked as a late entry, with the current date and time"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Marked as a late entry, with the current date and time
If something was not recorded at the time it happened, a LATE ENTRY can be made. It must be clearly MARKED AS A "LATE ENTRY", written with the CURRENT DATE AND TIME (when you are actually writing it), and should state the date and time the event happened. It is signed as usual. This keeps the record honest and shows exactly when information was added.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Backdated to the time the event actually happened — Backdating makes it look like the entry was written earlier — this is falsification of records.
• Squeezed in between the earlier lines of the notes — Squeezing an entry between earlier lines is misleading and looks like tampering.
• Written in pencil so it can be changed later — Pencil can be erased or altered; records must be in permanent ink.

⭐ Key point: Records must show a true timeline; never alter or rewrite past entries.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q150$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2615, $s$The doctrine of informed consent requires disclosure of:$s$, $s$["Nature, benefits, material risks and alternatives", "Only the time and place of the procedure", "Only the cost of the procedure to the family", "Only the name of the doctor doing the procedure"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Nature, benefits, material risks and alternatives
INFORMED CONSENT means the patient agrees to a procedure after being given enough information to make a real choice. The patient must be told: the NATURE of the procedure (what will be done and why), the expected BENEFITS, the MATERIAL RISKS (risks that a reasonable patient would want to know about — including serious but rare risks such as death or paralysis, and common minor risks), and the ALTERNATIVES (including the option of doing nothing). The patient must be competent, the consent must be voluntary (no pressure), and the information must be given in language they understand.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only the time and place of the procedure — The time and place are practical details only; they do not allow the patient to weigh risks and benefits.
• Only the cost of the procedure to the family — Cost may be useful to know, but cost alone is not what makes consent "informed".
• Only the name of the doctor doing the procedure — Knowing the doctor's name alone does not explain what will happen or its risks.

⭐ Key point: Consent is a process, not just a signature. The patient can withdraw consent at any time. The nurse can witness the signature and should alert the doctor if the patient does not seem to understand.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q151$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2616, $s$Restraining an agitated ICU patient without assessment or orders may constitute:$s$, $s$["An example of beneficence", "Battery and false imprisonment", "Good, safe nursing practice", "An example of fidelity"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Battery and false imprisonment
Restraining a patient (physically tying them down or using chemical sedation to control behaviour) without proper ASSESSMENT, a valid reason and an ORDER/protocol can be unlawful. BATTERY is touching or applying force to a person without their consent or lawful justification. FALSE IMPRISONMENT is restricting someone's freedom of movement without legal justification. Restraints are only allowed as a last resort, to prevent serious harm (e.g. pulling out an endotracheal tube), with documentation and regular review.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An example of beneficence — Beneficence means doing good. Unnecessary or unassessed restraint can cause injury, distress and delirium — it is not beneficence.
• Good, safe nursing practice — Restraint without assessment is not good practice. First look for causes of agitation (pain, hypoxia, full bladder, delirium, drug withdrawal).
• An example of fidelity — Fidelity means keeping promises and being faithful to duties, not restraint.

⭐ Key point: If restraint is needed: assess the cause, try alternatives first, follow policy, use the least restrictive method, check circulation and skin regularly, document, and review often.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q152$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2617, $s$When a patient dies in ICU after an assault, the case must be reported to:$s$, $s$["No one, as the patient has died in hospital", "Only the family, who decide what happens", "The police or coroner, as a medico-legal case", "The hospital chaplain and the mortuary only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The police or coroner, as a medico-legal case
A death that follows an ASSAULT (violence), accident, suspected poisoning, or other unnatural or suspicious causes is a MEDICO-LEGAL (coroner's) case. It must be REPORTED TO THE POLICE and/or the CORONER. The coroner decides whether a post-mortem examination (autopsy) is needed to establish the cause of death. The body must not be released to the family until the legal authorities allow it, and lines, tubes and evidence are left in place per policy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No one, as the patient has died in hospital — Dying in hospital does not remove the legal duty to report; the death resulted from a crime.
• Only the family, who decide what happens — The family cannot decide this; the law requires reporting.
• The hospital chaplain and the mortuary only — The chaplain and mortuary are informed for other reasons, but legal reporting is still required.

⭐ Key point: Other reportable deaths: deaths within 24 hours of admission, during or soon after surgery/anaesthesia, unknown cause, and deaths in police custody.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q153$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2618, $s$An ICU nurse is asked to give evidence in court. She should:$s$, $s$["Refuse to attend court without any reason", "Use her contemporaneous notes and state facts honestly", "Give expert opinions outside her area of competence", "Rewrite the notes before attending court"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Use her contemporaneous notes and state facts honestly
When called to court, the nurse must ATTEND and act as a truthful witness of FACT. She may refer to her CONTEMPORANEOUS NOTES (records written at the time of the events), which help her recall details accurately. She should answer questions HONESTLY, clearly and only about what she saw and did — never guessing or exaggerating.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Refuse to attend court without any reason — A nurse called as a witness must attend court; refusing without reason can be contempt of court.
• Give expert opinions outside her area of competence — She should stay within her own knowledge and competence; giving opinions outside her expertise is unprofessional and may mislead the court.
• Rewrite the notes before attending court — Rewriting notes is falsifying records — a crime and serious misconduct.

⭐ Key point: Good, accurate, timely documentation is the nurse's best legal defence. Seek advice from the hospital legal department or your professional association before attending court.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q154$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2619, $s$Respect for autonomy is best shown in ICU by:$s$, $s$["Following the family's wishes over the patient's", "Involving competent patients in decisions", "Making all the decisions for the patient", "Withholding information to avoid worry"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Involving competent patients in decisions
AUTONOMY is the patient's right to make decisions about their own body and care. In ICU this is best shown by INVOLVING COMPETENT PATIENTS IN DECISIONS: giving them honest information in a way they understand, answering questions, asking their wishes, and respecting their choices (including refusal). For patients who cannot decide (e.g. sedated), the team considers any advance wishes and what the patient would have wanted.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Following the family's wishes over the patient's — Following the family's wishes over a competent patient's ignores the patient's autonomy.
• Making all the decisions for the patient — Making all decisions for the patient is paternalism and does not respect autonomy.
• Withholding information to avoid worry — Withholding information prevents the patient from making informed choices.

⭐ Key point: The four main ethical principles: autonomy, beneficence (do good), non-maleficence (do no harm), and justice (fairness).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q156$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2620, $s$Justice in ICU resource allocation means:$s$, $s$["First come, first served, whatever the need", "Allocating beds by need and likely benefit", "Admitting only patients who can pay in advance", "Admitting friends and relatives of staff first"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Allocating beds by need and likely benefit
JUSTICE in resource allocation means distributing scarce ICU beds and treatment FAIRLY, based on clinical NEED and LIKELY BENEFIT (who is sick enough to need ICU and is likely to benefit from it), not on wealth, status or relationships. Triage policies and admission criteria help make fair and consistent decisions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• First come, first served, whatever the need — First come, first served ignores severity of illness; a sicker patient arriving later may need the bed more.
• Admitting only patients who can pay in advance — Admitting only those who can pay discriminates against the poor and is unjust.
• Admitting friends and relatives of staff first — Favouring friends and relatives of staff is unfair and unethical.

⭐ Key point: Fair triage decisions should be transparent, consistent, documented and made by senior clinicians.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q157$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2621, $s$The Good Samaritan principle does NOT protect a nurse who:$s$, $s$["Provides first aid in good faith at the scene", "Calls for emergency help and ambulance services", "Stays with the casualty until help arrives", "Acts with gross negligence or beyond her training"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Acts with gross negligence or beyond her training
The GOOD SAMARITAN principle protects people who give emergency help voluntarily and IN GOOD FAITH, within their level of training, without expecting payment. It does NOT protect a nurse who acts with GROSS NEGLIGENCE (seriously careless, reckless actions) or who performs procedures BEYOND HER TRAINING and competence, causing harm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Provides first aid in good faith at the scene — Giving first aid in good faith is exactly what the principle protects.
• Calls for emergency help and ambulance services — Calling for help is a reasonable and protected action.
• Stays with the casualty until help arrives — Staying with the casualty until help arrives is good practice and protected.

⭐ Key point: At an emergency scene: ensure safety, call for help, give care within your competence, and hand over clearly to the emergency team.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q158$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2622, $s$Handing over a patient at the end of the shift should include:$s$, $s$["Personal opinions and gossip about the family", "Diagnosis, status, treatments, risks and pending tasks", "Only the bed number and the consultant's name", "Only the patient's name and the hospital number"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Diagnosis, status, treatments, risks and pending tasks
A good HANDOVER passes on all the information the next nurse needs to care safely: patient identity, DIAGNOSIS, current clinical STATUS (vital signs, level of consciousness, ventilator settings, infusions), TREATMENTS given and planned, RISKS (falls, pressure ulcers, allergies, infection status), and PENDING TASKS (tests, results to chase, drugs due). Structured tools such as SBAR (Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation) reduce errors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Personal opinions and gossip about the family — Gossip and personal opinions are unprofessional and do not help patient care.
• Only the bed number and the consultant's name — Bed number and consultant name alone are not enough to provide safe care.
• Only the patient's name and the hospital number — Name and hospital number identify the patient but say nothing about their care needs.

⭐ Key point: Handover is a high-risk time for errors. Do it at the bedside where possible, check the patient, lines, alarms and charts together.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q159$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2623, $s$Dispensing medications by a nurse without prescription in ICU is:$s$, $s$["Outside her legal scope except under approved protocols", "Allowed for all drugs during the night shift", "Allowed whenever the patient asks for a drug", "Always allowed in any emergency situation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Outside her legal scope except under approved protocols
Prescribing and dispensing medicines is generally the legal role of doctors and pharmacists. A nurse may only give medicines WITHOUT AN INDIVIDUAL PRESCRIPTION under APPROVED PROTOCOLS or standing orders (e.g. an emergency protocol for hypoglycaemia or cardiac arrest drugs) that the hospital has formally authorised. Otherwise, it is OUTSIDE HER LEGAL SCOPE of practice.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Allowed for all drugs during the night shift — Night shift does not change the law; all medicines still need a prescription or approved protocol.
• Allowed whenever the patient asks for a drug — A patient's request does not authorise a nurse to give medicines.
• Always allowed in any emergency situation — Even in emergencies, drugs are given according to approved protocols or verbal orders from a doctor, which must be documented.

⭐ Key point: Practising outside your scope can lead to disciplinary action by the NMCN and legal liability.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q160$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2624, $s$A root cause analysis after a serious incident in ICU aims to:$s$, $s$["Close the case as quickly as possible", "Identify system failures behind the event", "Find the individual who should be blamed", "Avoid informing the family of the event"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Identify system failures behind the event
ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS (RCA) is a structured investigation after a serious incident to find out WHAT happened, WHY it happened, and HOW to prevent it happening again. It focuses on SYSTEM FAILURES (processes, equipment, staffing, communication) rather than blaming individuals.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Close the case as quickly as possible — Closing the case quickly misses the chance to learn and prevent future harm.
• Find the individual who should be blamed — RCA is not about blaming individuals; blame discourages reporting.
• Avoid informing the family of the event — The family should be informed honestly (duty of candour), not kept in the dark.

⭐ Key point: A "just culture" balances accountability with learning; staff are encouraged to report errors and near misses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q167$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2625, $s$A 'near miss' in ICU should be:$s$, $s$["Hidden from the manager to avoid blame", "Reported, because it shows a risk of harm", "Ignored, because no harm occurred", "Reported only if the patient complains"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reported, because it shows a risk of harm
A NEAR MISS is an event that could have caused harm but was caught before it reached the patient (or reached the patient without harm). It should be REPORTED because it shows a RISK in the system. Learning from near misses helps prevent actual harm in the future.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hidden from the manager to avoid blame — Hiding near misses prevents learning and allows the risk to remain.
• Ignored, because no harm occurred — Even without harm, the same error could injure the next patient.
• Reported only if the patient complains — Reporting should not depend on patient complaints.

⭐ Key point: Many serious incidents are preceded by many near misses. Reporting them is a sign of a good safety culture.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q168$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2626, $s$Staff appraisal in ICU is mainly used to:$s$, $s$["Replace formal training and education", "Reduce the salaries of underperforming staff", "Punish staff for errors made in the year", "Review performance and plan development"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Review performance and plan development
STAFF APPRAISAL (performance review) is a regular meeting between the manager and the staff member to REVIEW PERFORMANCE (strengths and areas to improve) and PLAN DEVELOPMENT (training, goals, career progression). It provides feedback and motivation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Replace formal training and education — Appraisal identifies training needs; it does not replace training.
• Reduce the salaries of underperforming staff — It is not used to reduce salaries.
• Punish staff for errors made in the year — It is not for punishment; errors are handled through supportive systems.

⭐ Key point: Appraisal should be fair, two-way, documented and lead to a personal development plan.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q173$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2627, $s$Moral distress among ICU nurses often arises when:$s$, $s$["They have adequate staffing on every shift", "They receive good training and support", "They are well paid for their work in ICU", "They cannot act on what they believe is right"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: They cannot act on what they believe is right
MORAL DISTRESS occurs when a nurse KNOWS WHAT IS ETHICALLY RIGHT but CANNOT ACT on it because of constraints (e.g. continuing aggressive treatment the nurse believes is futile, lack of resources, hierarchy, or institutional policies). It can lead to burnout and staff leaving.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• They have adequate staffing on every shift — Adequate staffing reduces moral distress.
• They receive good training and support — Good training and support reduce moral distress.
• They are well paid for their work in ICU — Good pay does not cause moral distress.

⭐ Key point: Ethics committees, open team discussions, debriefing and staff support help reduce moral distress.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q180$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2628, $s$The Code of Professional Conduct for nurses mainly sets out:$s$, $s$["Standards of behaviour and duties nurses must follow", "The prescribing rules that apply to doctors", "The salaries that nurses should receive each grade", "The building requirements for hospitals and clinics"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Standards of behaviour and duties nurses must follow
The Code of Professional Conduct (issued in Nigeria by the NMCN) sets out the STANDARDS of behaviour and DUTIES every nurse must follow: respect for patients, confidentiality, competence, honesty, working within the scope of practice, accountability, and protecting patients from harm. It guides practice and is used to judge misconduct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The prescribing rules that apply to doctors — Prescribing rules for doctors are set by the Medical and Dental Council, not the nursing code.
• The salaries that nurses should receive each grade — Salaries are set by employers and government, not by the code of conduct.
• The building requirements for hospitals and clinics — Building standards are set by health and building regulators.

⭐ Key point: Breaking the code can lead to NMCN disciplinary action: a warning, suspension, or removal from the register.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q141$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2629, $s$A Do-Not-Resuscitate decision should be:$s$, $s$["Clearly documented, discussed and reviewed", "Made by the bedside nurse alone at night", "Applied automatically to all elderly patients", "Kept secret from the rest of the team"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Clearly documented, discussed and reviewed
A Do-Not-Resuscitate (DNACPR) decision is a serious decision about withholding CPR. It must be made by the senior doctor in charge, DISCUSSED with the patient (if they have capacity) and/or the family, clearly DOCUMENTED on the proper form, COMMUNICATED to the whole team, and REVIEWED regularly or when the patient's condition changes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Made by the bedside nurse alone at night — A bedside nurse alone cannot make a DNR decision.
• Applied automatically to all elderly patients — Age alone is never a reason for DNR; each patient is assessed individually.
• Kept secret from the rest of the team — The decision must be shared with the team, or CPR might be started wrongly.

⭐ Key point: DNR means no CPR only. All other appropriate care continues (oxygen, fluids, antibiotics, pain relief, nursing care).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q142$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2630, $s$Passive euthanasia is sometimes confused with:$s$, $s$["Starting CPR in an unexpected cardiac arrest", "Giving adequate pain relief at the end of life", "Feeding the patient by nasogastric tube", "Withdrawing or withholding futile treatment"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Withdrawing or withholding futile treatment
Some people wrongly call withholding or withdrawing futile treatment "passive euthanasia". But stopping treatment that cannot help (such as a ventilator in a patient who cannot recover), after proper discussion and documentation, is lawful and ethical. The patient dies from the underlying disease, not from an act intended to kill. Active euthanasia (giving something to deliberately end life) is illegal in Nigeria.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Starting CPR in an unexpected cardiac arrest — Starting CPR is an attempt to save life, not euthanasia.
• Giving adequate pain relief at the end of life — Good pain relief at the end of life is good care (principle of double effect).
• Feeding the patient by nasogastric tube — Tube feeding is a form of treatment and nutrition, not euthanasia.

⭐ Key point: Futile treatment is treatment that won't achieve the goals of care. Decisions are made by the senior team with the family, and comfort care continues.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q143$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2631, $s$A dying declaration recorded by a nurse is valuable in court because:$s$, $s$["It replaces the need for a police investigation", "It is always written down by a lawyer", "A person facing death is presumed to be truthful", "It counts as a formal medical diagnosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A person facing death is presumed to be truthful
A dying declaration is a statement made by a person who believes they are about to die, about the cause or circumstances of their death (for example, who attacked them). Courts accept it as evidence because the law presumes a person facing death has no reason to lie. The nurse should record it word for word, with the date, time, the patient's mental state and the names of witnesses.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It replaces the need for a police investigation — It doesn't replace a police investigation; it is one piece of evidence.
• It is always written down by a lawyer — It can be recorded by a nurse, doctor or magistrate; a lawyer isn't required.
• It counts as a formal medical diagnosis — It is legal evidence, not a medical diagnosis.

⭐ Key point: Record the exact words in quotation marks, note whether the patient was conscious and orientated, and get witnesses to sign.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q144$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2632, $s$A swab left inside a patient after surgery would allow the doctrine of:$s$, $s$["Double effect", "Vicarious immunity", "Good Samaritan", "Res ipsa loquitur"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Res ipsa loquitur
Res ipsa loquitur means "the thing speaks for itself". It applies when the harm would not normally happen without negligence, and the thing that caused it was under the professional's control. A surgical swab left inside a patient is the classic example: the swab itself proves something went wrong, so negligence is presumed without the patient needing to prove exactly how it happened.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Double effect — Double effect is an ethical principle about actions with good and bad effects (such as end-of-life pain relief).
• Vicarious immunity — "Vicarious immunity" is not a real doctrine; vicarious LIABILITY makes employers responsible for employees.
• Good Samaritan — Good Samaritan laws protect people who give emergency help outside work.

⭐ Key point: Prevent retained swabs and instruments with careful counting (before, during and after surgery) and the WHO surgical safety checklist.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q145$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2633, $s$The Patient's Bill of Rights includes the right to:$s$, $s$["Choose any staff member's salary", "Read the notes of other patients", "Know who is providing their care", "Refuse to pay all hospital bills"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Know who is providing their care
The Patient's Bill of Rights lists rights patients have in health care, including: the right to KNOW WHO is providing their care (names and roles of staff), to respectful care, to information about their condition and treatment, to give or refuse consent, to privacy and confidentiality, and to complain.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Choose any staff member's salary — Patients don't choose staff salaries.
• Read the notes of other patients — Patients cannot read other patients' notes; that would breach confidentiality.
• Refuse to pay all hospital bills — Patients have the responsibility to pay for services as agreed; refusing all bills is not a right.

⭐ Key point: Patients also have responsibilities: give accurate information, follow agreed treatment, respect staff and other patients.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q146$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2634, $s$A patient bitten by a dog should have the wound:$s$, $s$["Covered with a dressing without cleaning", "Left untreated if the wound is small", "Washed with soap and water for 15 minutes", "Sutured tightly at once to stop bleeding"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Washed with soap and water for 15 minutes
After a dog bite, the most important first step is to WASH the wound thoroughly with SOAP and running WATER for at least 15 minutes. This removes saliva and much of the rabies virus and bacteria, and greatly reduces the risk of rabies. Then apply an antiseptic (povidone-iodine or alcohol), give tetanus prophylaxis, and start rabies vaccine (and rabies immunoglobulin for severe bites) as indicated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Covered with a dressing without cleaning — Covering without cleaning leaves the virus and bacteria in the wound.
• Left untreated if the wound is small — Even a small bite can transmit rabies; it must be treated.
• Sutured tightly at once to stop bleeding — Bite wounds are usually NOT sutured tightly at first (high infection risk), except on the face.

⭐ Key point: Rabies is almost 100% fatal once symptoms start, but it is preventable with prompt wound care and vaccination after exposure.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q147$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2635, $s$Good Samaritan laws do not apply when the nurse:$s$, $s$["Gives first aid at a church", "Is on duty and has a duty of care", "Helps at a road crash off duty", "Assists at a market collapse"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Is on duty and has a duty of care
Good Samaritan laws protect people who give emergency help VOLUNTARILY, in good faith, OUTSIDE their job (off duty, without payment). When a nurse is ON DUTY, they have a professional DUTY OF CARE to the patients, and the usual professional standards and liability apply. Good Samaritan protection doesn't cover on-duty work.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Gives first aid at a church — First aid at a church (off duty, voluntary) is covered by Good Samaritan principles.
• Helps at a road crash off duty — Helping at a road crash off duty is the classic Good Samaritan situation.
• Assists at a market collapse — Helping at a market collapse off duty is covered.

⭐ Key point: Even as a Good Samaritan, stay within your skills, get consent if possible, hand over properly to emergency services, and document what you did.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q148$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2636, $s$The legal term for a person who brings a negligence claim is:$s$, $s$["Defendant", "Witness", "Judge", "Plaintiff"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Plaintiff
In a negligence (civil) case, the PLAINTIFF (also called the claimant) is the person who brings the claim, usually the patient or their family. The DEFENDANT is the person or organisation being sued (the nurse, doctor or hospital).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Defendant — The defendant is the one being sued, not the one bringing the claim.
• Witness — A witness gives evidence but is not a party to the case.
• Judge — The judge hears the case and decides the outcome.

⭐ Key point: In criminal cases, the state (prosecution) brings the case against the accused.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q149$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2637, $s$Malpractice differs from negligence in that malpractice:$s$, $s$["It is negligence by a professional in their role", "It is always intentional and criminal", "It applies only to doctors, not nurses", "It has no legal consequence at all"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: It is negligence by a professional in their role
MALPRACTICE is professional negligence: negligence by a professional (nurse, doctor, midwife) while acting in their professional role. It is judged against the standard of a reasonably competent professional. Like ordinary negligence, it needs a duty of care, a breach of that duty, causation, and damage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• It is always intentional and criminal — Malpractice is usually NOT intentional; it is a failure to meet the standard of care.
• It applies only to doctors, not nurses — It applies to ALL professionals, including nurses.
• It has no legal consequence at all — It has serious legal consequences: compensation, disciplinary action, and sometimes criminal charges.

⭐ Key point: Negligence is the general legal concept; malpractice is negligence in professional practice.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q150$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2638, $s$An ICU nurse who leaves her shift without handover may be liable for:$s$, $s$["Abandonment of patients", "Res ipsa loquitur", "Good professional practice", "Vicarious immunity"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Abandonment of patients
Patient ABANDONMENT happens when a nurse leaves patients in their care without a proper handover to another qualified nurse, so the patients are left without care. In ICU, this is very dangerous, because patients need continuous monitoring. Abandonment can lead to disciplinary action and legal liability if harm results.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Res ipsa loquitur — Res ipsa loquitur is a legal doctrine for obvious negligence, not specifically abandonment.
• Good professional practice — Leaving without handover is NOT good practice.
• Vicarious immunity — "Vicarious immunity" doesn't exist.

⭐ Key point: Always give a full handover (using SBAR) before leaving, and never leave the unit unless your patients are handed over to someone competent.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q151$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2639, $s$Assault means:$s$, $s$["A threat of unlawful touching", "Actual unlawful touching", "Lawful physical restraint", "Valid consent to treatment"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A threat of unlawful touching
In law, ASSAULT is a THREAT or attempt to touch someone unlawfully, which makes them fear harm (even if no touching happens). For example, threatening to give an injection against the patient's will. BATTERY is the actual unlawful touching without consent.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Actual unlawful touching — Actual unlawful touching is BATTERY, not assault.
• Lawful physical restraint — Lawful restraint (with proper legal authority and procedure) is not assault.
• Valid consent to treatment — Valid consent makes touching LAWFUL.

⭐ Key point: Always get consent before procedures, and never threaten a patient to make them comply.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q152$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2640, $s$When a patient wants to leave ICU against medical advice, the nurse should:$s$, $s$["Lock the doors so that the patient cannot leave", "Sedate the patient to keep them in the unit", "Ignore the request and continue the care", "Explain the risks, inform the doctor, document"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Explain the risks, inform the doctor, document
A competent adult has the right to refuse treatment, including leaving hospital against medical advice (AMA). The nurse should explain the RISKS of leaving clearly, try to understand and address the reasons, INFORM the doctor, ask the patient to sign a discharge-against-advice form, and DOCUMENT everything carefully. The patient cannot be held against their will if they have capacity.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Lock the doors so that the patient cannot leave — Locking the doors is false imprisonment, which is illegal.
• Sedate the patient to keep them in the unit — Sedating a patient to keep them in is assault and battery.
• Ignore the request and continue the care — Ignoring the request disrespects autonomy and could lead to harm.

⭐ Key point: If the patient lacks capacity (confused, delirious), they may be kept for their safety under the appropriate legal framework. Assess capacity carefully.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q153$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2641, $s$A nurse who discloses a patient's HIV status to the patient's employer without consent has committed:$s$, $s$["An act of beneficence", "Good professional practice", "An example of fidelity", "Breach of confidentiality"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Breach of confidentiality
Patient information, including HIV status, is CONFIDENTIAL. Disclosing it to an employer (or anyone not involved in care) WITHOUT the patient's CONSENT is a breach of confidentiality. It can lead to discrimination and harm, and the nurse can face disciplinary and legal action. Disclosure is only allowed with consent, or when required by law.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An act of beneficence — Beneficence means doing good; this disclosure harms the patient.
• Good professional practice — This is not good professional practice; it breaks the code of conduct.
• An example of fidelity — Fidelity is about keeping promises, including the promise of confidentiality; this breaks it.

⭐ Key point: Exceptions to confidentiality: patient consent, a court order, notifiable diseases (public health), or a serious risk to others (decided by senior staff and recorded).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q154$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2642, $s$Ethical dilemmas in ICU are best resolved by:$s$, $s$["Ignoring the problem until it goes away", "Following whoever argues the loudest", "The most junior nurse deciding on behalf of all", "Team discussion using ethics and the patient's values"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Team discussion using ethics and the patient's values
Ethical dilemmas, such as whether to continue treatment, have no easy right answer. They are best resolved by the multidisciplinary TEAM discussing together, using ETHICAL principles (autonomy, beneficence, non-maleficence, justice) and the PATIENT'S VALUES and wishes (from the patient or family). A hospital ethics committee can help with difficult cases.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignoring the problem until it goes away — Ignoring the problem can lead to harm and distress.
• Following whoever argues the loudest — The loudest voice isn't necessarily right; decisions should be based on ethics and evidence.
• The most junior nurse deciding on behalf of all — A junior nurse shouldn't make major decisions alone.

⭐ Key point: Steps: gather facts, identify the ethical issue, consider options and consequences, involve the patient and family, decide, document, and review.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q155$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2643, $s$Professional accountability means the nurse:$s$, $s$["She avoids making any decisions", "She is never responsible for any outcome", "She shifts all blame to the doctors", "She answers for her actions and omissions"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: She answers for her actions and omissions
ACCOUNTABILITY means the nurse takes responsibility for, and can explain and justify, their own ACTIONS and OMISSIONS (things they failed to do). Nurses are accountable to the patient, the employer, the profession (NMCN) and the law. It means being competent, following standards, documenting care, and admitting mistakes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• She avoids making any decisions — Avoiding decisions is not accountable practice; nurses must make decisions within their role.
• She is never responsible for any outcome — Nurses ARE responsible for the outcomes of their own actions.
• She shifts all blame to the doctors — Shifting blame to doctors avoids accountability.

⭐ Key point: You are accountable for what you do and for what you delegate. You cannot transfer accountability to someone else.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q158$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2644, $s$Disciplinary action in an ICU should be:$s$, $s$["Immediate and in front of colleagues", "Fair, documented and following due process", "Without hearing the staff member", "Based on rumours from other staff"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fair, documented and following due process
Disciplinary action must be FAIR, DOCUMENTED, and follow DUE PROCESS (proper procedure): hear the staff member's side, gather facts, give written warnings, and offer support and improvement plans. Progressive discipline starts with counselling, then verbal warning, written warning, suspension, and finally dismissal if needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate and in front of colleagues — Disciplining in front of colleagues humiliates the staff member and lowers morale.
• Without hearing the staff member — Not hearing the staff member is unfair and against natural justice.
• Based on rumours from other staff — Rumours are not evidence; decisions must be based on facts.

⭐ Key point: The aim of discipline is to correct behaviour and improve performance, not to punish.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q168$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2645, $s$Interdepartmental communication between ICU and laboratory improves:$s$, $s$["The length of the family visiting hours", "The spread of rumours among the staff", "Speed and accuracy of critical results", "The quality of catering for the patients"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Speed and accuracy of critical results
Good communication between the ICU and the laboratory speeds up the reporting of CRITICAL RESULTS (dangerously abnormal values such as K⁺ above 6.5, glucose below 3, or a positive blood culture). The lab phones critical results directly to the nurse or doctor, who READS BACK the result and DOCUMENTS it. This ensures fast, accurate action.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The length of the family visiting hours — Visiting hours are not related to lab communication.
• The spread of rumours among the staff — Good communication reduces rumours, not spreads them.
• The quality of catering for the patients — Catering is unrelated.

⭐ Key point: Always read back critical results received by phone and inform the doctor immediately.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q172$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2646, $s$Nursing workload in ICU can be measured with tools such as:$s$, $s$["The Braden pressure ulcer scale", "TISS or NAS (Nursing Activities Score)", "The Bishop score for labour", "The APGAR score at birth"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: TISS or NAS (Nursing Activities Score)
Nursing workload tools measure how much nursing time and work patients need. TISS (Therapeutic Intervention Scoring System) scores the treatments and procedures a patient receives. NAS (Nursing Activities Score) measures nursing activities and time (100 points ≈ one nurse's full time). These help plan staffing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The Braden pressure ulcer scale — The Braden scale assesses pressure ulcer risk.
• The Bishop score for labour — The Bishop score assesses the cervix before induction of labour.
• The APGAR score at birth — The APGAR score assesses newborns at birth.

⭐ Key point: Workload tools help managers show the need for staff and plan safe staffing.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q177$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2647, $s$A sentinel event in ICU is:$s$, $s$["A normal shift change with handover", "A routine planned admission from theatre", "A planned discharge to the ward in the day", "An unexpected event causing death or serious harm"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An unexpected event causing death or serious harm
A SENTINEL EVENT is an UNEXPECTED event that causes DEATH or SERIOUS harm (physical or psychological), not related to the natural course of the illness. Examples: wrong-site surgery, a fatal drug error, an infant abduction, a retained instrument. It requires immediate reporting and a ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS (RCA) to find and fix system failures.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A normal shift change with handover — Shift handover is normal routine.
• A routine planned admission from theatre — A planned admission from theatre is routine.
• A planned discharge to the ward in the day — A planned discharge is routine.

⭐ Key point: RCA asks "why?" repeatedly (5 whys, fishbone diagram), focuses on systems, not blame, and results in an action plan.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q179$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2648, $s$ICU bed numbers in a general hospital are often planned at about:$s$, $s$["50% of total hospital beds", "1–4% of total hospital beds", "Less than 0.01% of beds", "25% of total hospital beds"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1–4% of total hospital beds
ICU beds are usually planned at about 1–4% of total hospital beds in a general hospital. Larger teaching or tertiary hospitals with major surgery, trauma and specialist services need a HIGHER proportion (sometimes 5–10%).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 50% of total hospital beds — 50% would mean half the hospital is ICU, which is unrealistic.
• Less than 0.01% of beds — Less than 0.01% would be almost no ICU beds.
• 25% of total hospital beds — 25% is far too high for a general hospital.

⭐ Key point: Planning must also consider high dependency (Level 2) beds and the referral population.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q162$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2649, $s$A clear nursing hierarchy in ICU helps by:$s$, $s$["Removing accountability from leaders", "Preventing teamwork on the unit", "Defining responsibility and communication", "Increasing confusion among staff"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Defining responsibility and communication
A clear nursing hierarchy (unit manager, charge nurse, staff nurses, juniors) DEFINES who is RESPONSIBLE for what and who reports to whom. This makes COMMUNICATION clear, avoids confusion in emergencies, ensures accountability, and helps supervision and decision-making.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Removing accountability from leaders — Hierarchy CREATES accountability; it doesn't remove it.
• Preventing teamwork on the unit — Hierarchy supports teamwork when roles are clear.
• Increasing confusion among staff — Clear structure reduces confusion.

⭐ Key point: Good hierarchy works best combined with open communication: juniors must feel able to speak up about safety.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q163$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2650, $s$Effective handover in ICU uses:$s$, $s$["A structured tool such as SBAR, at the bedside", "No handover, as notes are enough", "An unstructured chat in the corridor", "Notes left on the bed for the next nurse"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A structured tool such as SBAR, at the bedside
Effective handover uses a STRUCTURED tool such as SBAR (Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation) so nothing important is missed. In ICU, handover is best done at the BEDSIDE, so the incoming nurse can see the patient and check lines, pumps, drains, alarms and the ventilator together with the outgoing nurse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No handover, as notes are enough — Notes alone miss important details and plans.
• An unstructured chat in the corridor — An unstructured corridor chat misses information and breaches confidentiality.
• Notes left on the bed for the next nurse — Notes left on the bed are unsafe and unprofessional.

⭐ Key point: Poor handover is a major cause of errors. Use a checklist and allow time for questions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q166$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2651, $s$Communication with relatives in ICU should be:$s$, $s$["Regular, honest and coordinated by the team", "Given in technical jargon to be accurate", "Done only by porters and cleaners", "Avoided to save time for clinical care"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Regular, honest and coordinated by the team
Communication with relatives should be REGULAR (at least daily updates), HONEST (truthful about the condition and prognosis, in plain language) and COORDINATED by the team (consistent messages from doctors and nurses, with family meetings for big decisions). This builds trust and reduces anxiety.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Given in technical jargon to be accurate — Technical jargon confuses relatives.
• Done only by porters and cleaners — Porters and cleaners shouldn't give clinical information.
• Avoided to save time for clinical care — Avoiding communication increases anxiety and conflict.

⭐ Key point: Document family meetings: who was present, what was discussed, and what was decided.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q173$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2652, $s$Teamwork in ICU is improved by:$s$, $s$["Competition between staff members", "Strict silence during every shift", "Avoiding team meetings and briefings", "Clear roles, communication training, respect"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Clear roles, communication training, respect
Good ICU teamwork depends on CLEAR ROLES (everyone knows their job), COMMUNICATION training (closed-loop communication, speaking up about safety), and mutual RESPECT. Crew resource management principles from aviation (briefings, situational awareness, debriefs) improve safety.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Competition between staff members — Competition between staff harms teamwork.
• Strict silence during every shift — Strict silence stops the communication needed for safety.
• Avoiding team meetings and briefings — Team meetings and briefings IMPROVE teamwork.

⭐ Key point: Simulation training for emergencies (arrests, airway emergencies) builds team skills.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q179$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2653, $s$The unit in which comprehensive care of a critically ill patient which is deemed to recoverable stage is carried out.$s$, $s$["Critical Care", "Cardiac ICU", "Critical Care Unit", "Coronary Care Unit"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Critical Care Unit
A CRITICAL CARE UNIT (Intensive Care Unit) is a special unit where comprehensive, continuous care is given to critically ill patients with life-threatening but potentially RECOVERABLE conditions. It provides advanced monitoring, organ support (ventilation, dialysis, vasopressors) and a high nurse-to-patient ratio.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Critical Care — "Critical care" is the type of care, not the unit.
• Cardiac ICU — A cardiac ICU is specialised for heart patients.
• Coronary Care Unit — A coronary care unit is specialised for acute heart attacks and heart rhythm problems.

⭐ Key point: ICU admission is for patients who can benefit from intensive treatment; those who can't recover may be better served by palliative care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q161$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2654, $s$A document that lists the medical treatment a person chooses to refuse if unable to make decisions is the:$s$, $s$["Living will", "Durable power of attorney", "Informed consent", "Advance directives"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Living will
A LIVING WILL is a type of advance directive: a written document in which a person lists the medical treatments they would REFUSE (such as CPR, ventilation or tube feeding) if they lose the ability to make decisions in the future. It lets their wishes be respected.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Durable power of attorney — A durable power of attorney names a PERSON to make decisions for the patient.
• Informed consent — Informed consent is agreement to a specific treatment at the time.
• Advance directives — "Advance directives" is the general term; the specific document listing refused treatments is the living will.

⭐ Key point: Advance directives include living wills and the appointment of a health care proxy (power of attorney).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q162$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2655, $s$A manager who encourages staff to report errors without fear of unfair punishment is promoting a:$s$, $s$["Blame culture", "Just culture", "Competitive culture", "Punitive culture"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Just culture
A JUST CULTURE encourages staff to REPORT errors and near misses openly, without fear of unfair punishment. It focuses on fixing SYSTEM problems, while still holding people accountable for reckless behaviour. More reporting means more learning and safer care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Blame culture — A blame culture punishes individuals, so errors are hidden.
• Competitive culture — A competitive culture doesn't promote error reporting.
• Punitive culture — A punitive culture punishes errors, so staff hide mistakes.

⭐ Key point: Just culture: human error is consoled, at-risk behaviour is coached, reckless behaviour is disciplined.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q163$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2656, $s$Risk/Benefit Ratio is a consideration under which ethical principle?$s$, $s$["Beneficence", "Confidentiality", "Justice", "Plagiarism avoidance"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Beneficence
BENEFICENCE means doing good and acting in the patient's best interests. Considering the RISK/BENEFIT ratio (weighing possible harms against expected benefits) is part of beneficence: an intervention should do more good than harm. It is especially important in research and in deciding on treatments.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Confidentiality — Confidentiality is about keeping information private.
• Justice — Justice is about fairness.
• Plagiarism avoidance — Avoiding plagiarism is about academic honesty.

⭐ Key point: Beneficence (do good) and non-maleficence (do no harm) are closely linked. Both involve weighing risks and benefits.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q166$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2657, $s$A patient experiences a medication error with no resulting harm. This event is classified as a:$s$, $s$["Sentinel event", "Adverse event", "Near miss", "Never event"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Near miss
A medication error that reaches the patient but causes NO harm is classified here as a NEAR MISS (also called a "no harm" incident). It must still be reported, so the cause can be found and fixed before a similar error causes harm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sentinel event — A sentinel event causes death or serious harm.
• Adverse event — An adverse event causes harm.
• Never event — A never event is a serious, preventable event that should never happen (such as wrong-site surgery).

⭐ Key point: Strictly, many systems define a near miss as an error caught BEFORE reaching the patient. Either way, report every error.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q171$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2658, $s$A health worker is working in a community which is spread out over a large rural area. She is too lazy to go to the farthest homes to invite them to participate in an environmental sanitation to campaign. This led to the lack of:$s$, $s$["The 2 categories of justice.", "Equality and fairness", "Distributive justice", "Justice and fairness"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Distributive justice
DISTRIBUTIVE JUSTICE means sharing resources and services FAIRLY among everyone, according to need. By not visiting the farthest homes (because of laziness), the health worker denies those families a fair share of the health service, a failure of distributive justice.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The 2 categories of justice. — "The 2 categories of justice" is vague.
• Equality and fairness — Equality and fairness are related ideas, but the specific principle is distributive justice.
• Justice and fairness — "Justice and fairness" is general; the specific type is distributive justice.

⭐ Key point: Justice in health care: treat people fairly and distribute resources (time, beds, services) fairly, especially to vulnerable and hard-to-reach groups.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q174$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2659, $s$SBAR, used for safe communication between ICU staff, stands for:$s$, $s$["Staff, Budget, Audit, Reporting", "Safety, Bedside, Airway, Resuscitation", "Signs, Blood pressure, Alertness, Respiration", "Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation
SBAR is a structured communication tool. S = SITUATION (what is happening now). B = BACKGROUND (relevant history). A = ASSESSMENT (what you think the problem is). R = RECOMMENDATION (what you want done). It makes handovers and calls to doctors clear and concise, reducing errors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Staff, Budget, Audit, Reporting — Staff, Budget, Audit, Reporting is not SBAR.
• Safety, Bedside, Airway, Resuscitation — Safety, Bedside, Airway, Resuscitation is not SBAR.
• Signs, Blood pressure, Alertness, Respiration — Signs, BP, Alertness, Respiration is not SBAR.

⭐ Key point: Use SBAR when calling a doctor about a deteriorating patient. Have the vital signs and chart ready.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q178$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2660, $s$After a serious medication incident in ICU, a root cause analysis aims to:$s$, $s$["Find system causes and prevent recurrence", "Hide the event from the family", "Calculate the cost to the hospital only", "Find one person to discipline"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Find system causes and prevent recurrence
A root cause analysis (RCA) after a serious incident looks beyond individual blame to find the underlying SYSTEM CAUSES (such as poor labelling, staffing, equipment design, communication or policies). The aim is to PREVENT the incident from happening AGAIN, through changes to processes and systems.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hide the event from the family — Hiding the event is dishonest; there is a duty of candour to the family.
• Calculate the cost to the hospital only — Cost is not the main focus.
• Find one person to discipline — Finding one person to blame misses system causes and discourages reporting.

⭐ Key point: RCA tools: the "5 whys", fishbone (cause and effect) diagrams, and timelines. Results lead to an action plan.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q179$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2661, $s$Which of the following is a commonly recognized high-risk category for nurses in clinical practice?$s$, $s$["Correctly documenting a normal vital sign", "Medication administration errors", "Attending a staff meeting", "Taking an approved lunch break"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Medication administration errors
MEDICATION ADMINISTRATION ERRORS are one of the most common high-risk areas in nursing. In ICU, many high-alert drugs (insulin, potassium, opioids, vasopressors, heparin), complex calculations and frequent changes increase the risk. Errors can cause serious harm or death.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Correctly documenting a normal vital sign — Correctly documenting a normal vital sign isn't a risk category.
• Attending a staff meeting — Attending a staff meeting isn't a clinical risk.
• Taking an approved lunch break — An approved break isn't a risk category.

⭐ Key point: Prevent drug errors: the rights of medication administration, double checks, smart pumps, barcode scanning and clear labelling.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q161$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2662, $s$A critically-ill patient has a "Living Will" in the chart. The patient's condition has deteriorated, but the spouse wants "everything done" regardless of the patient's wishes. Which ethical principle is the spouse violating?$s$, $s$["Autonomy", "Beneficence", "Non-maleficence", "Justice"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Autonomy
A LIVING WILL documents the patient's own wishes about treatment if they can't decide for themselves. When the spouse wants "everything done" against these written wishes, the spouse is ignoring the patient's right to decide: a violation of the patient's AUTONOMY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Beneficence — Beneficence means doing good.
• Non-maleficence — Non-maleficence means avoiding harm.
• Justice — Justice means fairness.

⭐ Key point: A valid advance directive must be respected. Support the family through the decision with clear communication.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q162$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2663, $s$Which ethical principle refers to a patient's right to make their own healthcare decisions?$s$, $s$["Non-maleficence", "Autonomy", "Justice", "Beneficence"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Autonomy
AUTONOMY is the ethical principle that a competent patient has the RIGHT to make their own healthcare decisions, including accepting or refusing treatment, based on adequate information. It underpins informed consent.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Non-maleficence — Non-maleficence means doing no harm.
• Justice — Justice means fairness.
• Beneficence — Beneficence means doing good.

⭐ Key point: Respect autonomy by giving full information, checking capacity, and accepting the patient's decision, even if you disagree.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q165 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2664, $s$Which of the following organizations requires a mechanism for addressing ethical issues?$s$, $s$["The Joint Commission", "American Hospital Association", "American Association of Critical-Care Nurses", "Society of Critical Care Medicine"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The Joint Commission
The JOINT COMMISSION (the US accreditation body for hospitals) requires healthcare organisations to have a MECHANISM for addressing ETHICAL issues, such as an ethics committee or consultation service. This supports staff, patients and families with ethical dilemmas.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• American Hospital Association — The American Hospital Association is a trade association; it doesn't set this requirement.
• American Association of Critical-Care Nurses — AACN is a professional nursing association; it supports ethics, but doesn't require it of hospitals.
• Society of Critical Care Medicine — SCCM is a professional society, not an accreditation body.

⭐ Key point: In Nigeria, hospital ethics committees serve a similar role, especially for research and difficult clinical decisions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q166$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2665, $s$An effective handover should be:$s$, $s$["Conducted without documentation", "Brief but comprehensive", "Based on assumptions", "Done only when convenient"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Brief but comprehensive
An effective handover is BRIEF but COMPREHENSIVE: it conveys all the important information (diagnosis, current condition, treatments, risks, plans and pending tasks) clearly and concisely, often using a structured tool like SBAR. Too long wastes time; too short misses important details.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Conducted without documentation — Handover should be documented and supported by records.
• Based on assumptions — It should be based on facts, not assumptions.
• Done only when convenient — It must happen at every shift change, not only when convenient.

⭐ Key point: Bedside handover lets the incoming nurse see the patient and check equipment, lines and drugs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q167$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2666, $s$Which ethical principle is demonstrated by respecting a competent patient's informed refusal of treatment?$s$, $s$["Justice", "Fidelity", "Autonomy", "Beneficence"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Autonomy
Respecting a competent patient's informed REFUSAL of treatment demonstrates AUTONOMY: the patient's right to make decisions about their own body and care, even if others disagree.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Justice — Justice is fairness in care and resources.
• Fidelity — Fidelity is keeping promises and being loyal.
• Beneficence — Beneficence is doing good; it may conflict with the patient's refusal, but autonomy takes priority for a competent patient.

⭐ Key point: Make sure the refusal is informed (the patient understands the risks), document it, and continue other care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q170$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2667, $s$A family that does not know the patient's end-of-life wishes assumes they know what is best for the patient. This reflects:$s$, $s$["Veracity", "Pragmatism", "Justice", "Paternalism"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Paternalism
PATERNALISM happens when others (family, staff) decide what is "best" for a patient WITHOUT knowing or respecting the patient's own wishes, overriding the patient's autonomy. A family that doesn't know the patient's end-of-life wishes but assumes it knows best is acting paternalistically.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Veracity — Veracity is truthfulness.
• Pragmatism — Pragmatism is a practical approach, not this ethical concept.
• Justice — Justice is fairness.

⭐ Key point: Encourage patients to discuss and document their wishes (advance directives) before they lose capacity.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q172$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2668, $s$In functional nursing, care is organized according to:$s$, $s$["Bed numbers", "Nursing tasks", "Family preference", "Patient diagnosis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Nursing tasks
In FUNCTIONAL nursing, care is organised by TASKS: one nurse gives all the medications, another does all the dressings, another takes all the vital signs. It is efficient with few staff, but care is fragmented, and no one nurse has the full picture of the patient.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bed numbers — Bed numbers describe patient allocation, not functional nursing.
• Family preference — Family preference doesn't define a nursing care model.
• Patient diagnosis — Diagnosis-based allocation isn't functional nursing.

⭐ Key point: Other models: team nursing, primary nursing (one nurse responsible for a patient's full care), and patient allocation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q173$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2669, $s$A nursing audit reveals that many nurses are not completing the required pain assessment documentation. The next step in quality assurance should be to:$s$, $s$["Punish the nurses who failed to document", "Change the pain assessment policy", "Tell the patients to complain if their pain is not assessed", "Provide a refresher course on pain assessment and documentation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Provide a refresher course on pain assessment and documentation
If an audit shows poor documentation of pain assessment, the likely cause is a KNOWLEDGE or SKILL gap, or a system issue. The next step in quality improvement is to address the cause: provide a REFRESHER COURSE (education) on pain assessment and documentation, then RE-AUDIT to check improvement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Punish the nurses who failed to document — Punishing staff discourages honesty and doesn't fix the cause.
• Change the pain assessment policy — The policy may be fine; the problem is compliance.
• Tell the patients to complain if their pain is not assessed — Asking patients to complain isn't a quality improvement method.

⭐ Key point: The audit cycle: standard → measure → compare → change (such as education) → re-audit.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q175$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2670, $s$When one ICU bed is free and two patients need it, the bed should go to the patient who:$s$, $s$["Is most likely to benefit from ICU care", "Can pay for the ICU bed in cash today", "Has the highest social status in the town", "Arrived at the hospital first that day"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Is most likely to benefit from ICU care
When ICU beds are limited, they should go to the patient MOST LIKELY to BENEFIT from intensive care: the one whose condition is reversible and who is likely to survive with ICU treatment. This follows the principle of JUSTICE (fair use of scarce resources) and clinical need.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Can pay for the ICU bed in cash today — Ability to pay isn't an ethical basis for allocation.
• Has the highest social status in the town — Social status isn't an ethical basis.
• Arrived at the hospital first that day — First-come, first-served doesn't consider clinical need and benefit.

⭐ Key point: Use clear, written ICU admission criteria and document the decision-making process.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q177$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2671, $s$Which practice best reduces burnout and moral distress in ICU nurses?$s$, $s$["Removing all staff rotation", "Banning staff from discussing deaths", "Regular team debriefing after critical events", "Longer shifts without breaks"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Regular team debriefing after critical events
Regular TEAM DEBRIEFING after critical events (deaths, cardiac arrests, difficult cases) helps ICU nurses process emotions, learn from the event, feel supported, and reduce burnout and moral distress. Other helpful measures: adequate staffing, manageable workload, breaks, clinical supervision, and psychological support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Removing all staff rotation — Removing staff rotation doesn't reduce burnout.
• Banning staff from discussing deaths — Banning discussion of deaths increases distress.
• Longer shifts without breaks — Longer shifts without breaks increase burnout.

⭐ Key point: Signs of burnout: emotional exhaustion, cynicism, and reduced sense of achievement. Seek support early.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q178$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2672, $s$Discharging patients from ICU at night is discouraged mainly because it is linked to:$s$, $s$["Better sleep for the patient", "A lower nursing workload overnight", "Lower overall hospital costs", "Higher readmission and mortality"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Higher readmission and mortality
Discharging patients from ICU at NIGHT is linked to HIGHER READMISSION rates and HIGHER MORTALITY. At night, wards have fewer staff, fewer senior doctors, and handovers may be rushed, so deteriorating patients may be missed. Discharges should be planned in the DAYTIME, with a full handover.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Better sleep for the patient — Night discharge disturbs sleep rather than improving it.
• A lower nursing workload overnight — It doesn't reduce overall workload; it shifts risk to the ward.
• Lower overall hospital costs — It doesn't save costs if patients are readmitted.

⭐ Key point: Night discharge is often caused by bed pressure. Track it as a quality indicator.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q179$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2673, $s$Which of the following is a risk associated with the use of portable tablets for documentation in a busy clinical setting?$s$, $s$["They require constant access to a wired internet connection", "The tablets are too heavy for nurses to carry", "The tablet screen is too small to view information", "They are susceptible to being lost, stolen, or damaged"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: They are susceptible to being lost, stolen, or damaged
Portable tablets and mobile devices are easily LOST, STOLEN or DAMAGED in a busy clinical setting. If they contain patient information, this risks a DATA BREACH. Devices need password protection, encryption, remote-wipe ability, and secure storage.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• They require constant access to a wired internet connection — Tablets usually use Wi-Fi, not a wired connection.
• The tablets are too heavy for nurses to carry — Tablets aren't too heavy.
• The tablet screen is too small to view information — Screen size may be a minor issue but isn't the main risk.

⭐ Key point: Never store patient data on personal devices. Log out and lock devices when not in use.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q181$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2674, $s$The last function in the sequence, which culminates in the attainment of organization objectives, is:$s$, $s$["coordinating", "controlling", "organizing", "planning"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: controlling
The functions of management: PLANNING, ORGANISING, (staffing), DIRECTING (leading, coordinating), and CONTROLLING. CONTROLLING is the LAST function: it measures performance, compares it with standards and objectives, and corrects deviations, so the organisation attains its objectives. It then feeds back into new planning.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• coordinating — Coordinating comes before controlling.
• organizing — Organising is the second function.
• planning — Planning is the FIRST function.

⭐ Key point: Controlling tools include audits, budgets, performance appraisals and quality indicators.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q172$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2675, $s$The Belmont Report's 3 core ethical principles are:$s$, $s$["Honesty, Accuracy, Transparency", "Consent, Privacy, Fairness", "Safety, Dignity, Respect", "Autonomy, Beneficence, Justice"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Autonomy, Beneficence, Justice
The BELMONT REPORT (1979, USA) set out three core ethical principles for research involving people: 1) RESPECT FOR PERSONS (AUTONOMY: informed consent, protecting vulnerable people), 2) BENEFICENCE (maximise benefits, minimise harm), 3) JUSTICE (fair selection of participants, fair sharing of the burdens and benefits of research).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Honesty, Accuracy, Transparency — Honesty, accuracy and transparency are research integrity values, not the Belmont principles.
• Consent, Privacy, Fairness — Consent, privacy and fairness are related ideas, but not the three principles.
• Safety, Dignity, Respect — Safety, dignity and respect are general values.

⭐ Key point: Ethical research needs informed consent, an ethics committee approval, confidentiality, and the right to withdraw.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q173 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2676, $s$Which document outlines the duties and responsibilities of a staff nurse?$s$, $s$["Job description", "Nursing care plan", "Ward report", "Duty roster"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Job description
A JOB DESCRIPTION is a formal document that outlines the DUTIES, RESPONSIBILITIES, qualifications, reporting relationships and working conditions of a specific job, such as a staff nurse. It guides practice, recruitment and performance appraisal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nursing care plan — A nursing care plan outlines care for a specific patient.
• Ward report — A ward report communicates the state of the ward.
• Duty roster — A duty roster shows staff work schedules.

⭐ Key point: Job descriptions should be reviewed regularly and given to new staff at induction.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q175$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D1 · Ethics, Law, Documentation & Communication$s$, false, 2677, $s$Which handover practice is most likely to improve safety in ICU?$s$, $s$["Skipping handover when the unit is busy", "A structured bedside handover with a checklist", "Handing over by text message only", "A quick corridor chat with no notes"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A structured bedside handover with a checklist
A STRUCTURED BEDSIDE handover with a CHECKLIST improves safety. Using a structured format (such as SBAR) makes sure important information isn't missed. At the bedside, both nurses can see the patient and check lines, pumps, drains, the ventilator and alarms together.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Skipping handover when the unit is busy — Skipping handover leads to errors and missed information.
• Handing over by text message only — Text messages are incomplete, insecure and risk confidentiality breaches.
• A quick corridor chat with no notes — A quick corridor chat misses details and breaches confidentiality.

⭐ Key point: Poor communication at handover is a leading cause of adverse events.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q177$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, true, 2678, $s$Which leadership style is most appropriate during a mass casualty emergency?$s$, $s$["Democratic", "Laissez-faire", "Autocratic", "Affiliative"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Autocratic
In a mass casualty emergency there is no time for discussion. Quick, clear decisions and firm direction are needed to save lives. The AUTOCRATIC (authoritarian) style, where the leader makes decisions and gives direct orders, is most effective in emergencies, cardiac arrests and situations with inexperienced staff.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Democratic — The democratic style involves the team in decisions; it is good for planning but too slow in a crisis.
• Laissez-faire — The laissez-faire style gives little direction; it would be dangerous in an emergency.
• Affiliative — The affiliative style focuses on harmony and feelings, not quick decisions.

⭐ Key point: No leadership style is best for everything. Choose based on the situation (situational leadership). Transformational leadership is best for long-term improvement.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q178$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, true, 2679, $s$A new nurse on the unit repeatedly makes documentation errors despite initial teaching. The nurse manager's most constructive approach is to:$s$, $s$["Publicly criticise the nurse in front of colleagues", "Provide targeted, private feedback, additional focused training, and follow institutional progressive improvement processes", "Terminate employment immediately without any improvement plan", "Ignore the errors to avoid conflict"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Provide targeted, private feedback, additional focused training, and follow institutional progressive improvement processes
When a new nurse keeps making DOCUMENTATION ERRORS despite initial teaching, the manager should take a SUPPORTIVE, CONSTRUCTIVE approach: meet PRIVATELY, give specific, factual FEEDBACK (what the errors are and why they matter for patient safety and legal records), find the cause (understanding, workload, the EHR system, stress), give FOCUSED TRAINING or a preceptor/mentor, set clear expectations and a time-bound improvement plan, monitor progress, and document the meetings. If problems continue, follow the institution's PROGRESSIVE (graduated) discipline/improvement process. This respects the nurse, protects patients, and gives a fair chance to improve.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Publicly criticise the nurse in front of colleagues — PUBLIC criticism humiliates the nurse, damages teamwork and morale, and is unprofessional.
• Terminate employment immediately without any improvement plan — IMMEDIATE termination with no improvement plan is unfair and usually breaks institutional policy and labour law.
• Ignore the errors to avoid conflict — IGNORING the errors is unsafe — poor documentation harms patient care and creates legal risk.

⭐ Key point: Repeated errors → PRIVATE feedback + focused training + improvement plan + follow progressive policy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q181$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, true, 2680, $s$Who was the first to systematically describe management behaviour and pioneer the identification of the principles and skills that underlie effective management?$s$, $s$["Henri Fayol", "Max Weber", "Frederick Taylor", "Peter Drucker"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Henri Fayol
HENRI FAYOL (1841–1925), a French mining engineer and director, is called the "FATHER OF MODERN MANAGEMENT" (administrative/classical management theory). He was the first to SYSTEMATICALLY describe management behaviour and to identify the PRINCIPLES and SKILLS of effective management. He described the five functions of management (planning, organising, commanding, coordinating, controlling — POCCC) and the 14 principles of management (division of work, authority, discipline, unity of command, scalar chain, etc.), in his book "General and Industrial Management" (1916).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Max Weber — MAX WEBER described BUREAUCRACY (rules, hierarchy, impersonal authority).
• Frederick Taylor — FREDERICK TAYLOR is the father of SCIENTIFIC MANAGEMENT (time-and-motion studies, efficiency of workers' tasks).
• Peter Drucker — PETER DRUCKER is a later management writer (management by objectives), not the first.

⭐ Key point: FAYOL = father of modern management (14 principles, POCCC). Taylor = scientific management. Weber = bureaucracy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q182 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, true, 2681, $s$Which management level is responsible for policy formulation?$s$, $s$["First-line managers", "Middle managers", "Top management", "Charge nurses"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Top management
TOP MANAGEMENT (e.g. the board, chief executive, director of nursing services) is responsible for POLICY FORMULATION — setting the organisation's vision, mission, long-term goals, strategies and major policies, and allocating resources. MIDDLE managers (e.g. nursing officers in charge of departments) translate policies into plans and implement them. FIRST-LINE managers (e.g. ward managers, charge nurses) supervise day-to-day operations and staff.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• First-line managers — First-line managers handle daily operations, not policy formulation.
• Middle managers — Middle managers implement policies and coordinate departments.
• Charge nurses — Charge nurses manage shifts and direct care.

⭐ Key point: Policies made at the top should involve input from frontline staff to be practical and effective.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q179$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, true, 2682, $s$The five classic functions of management are best listed as:$s$, $s$["Planning, Organising, Staffing, Directing, Controlling", "Diagnosing, Prescribing, Treating, Evaluating, Discharging", "Assessing, Planning, Implementing, Evaluating, Documenting", "Hiring, Firing, Paying, Training, Promoting"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Planning, Organising, Staffing, Directing, Controlling
The five classic FUNCTIONS OF MANAGEMENT (based on Fayol and later writers) are: PLANNING (deciding goals and how to achieve them), ORGANISING (arranging resources and structure), STAFFING (recruiting, selecting, training and allocating staff), DIRECTING/leading (guiding, motivating and communicating with staff), and CONTROLLING (monitoring performance and correcting deviations). These apply to managing a ward or ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diagnosing, Prescribing, Treating, Evaluating, Discharging — Diagnosing, prescribing, treating, evaluating and discharging are clinical functions.
• Assessing, Planning, Implementing, Evaluating, Documenting — Assessing, planning, implementing, evaluating and documenting relate to the nursing process.
• Hiring, Firing, Paying, Training, Promoting — Hiring, firing, paying, training and promoting are human resource tasks, not the full set of management functions.

⭐ Key point: Gulick's POSDCORB: Planning, Organising, Staffing, Directing, Coordinating, Reporting, Budgeting.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q182$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, true, 2683, $s$Entrepreneurship is best defined as:$s$, $s$["Saving money in a fixed deposit account", "Inheriting a family business without taking part in managing it", "Working as a salaried employee in a government hospital", "The process of identifying an opportunity and organising resources to create a new venture, accepting the risks in return for profit or social value"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The process of identifying an opportunity and organising resources to create a new venture, accepting the risks in return for profit or social value
ENTREPRENEURSHIP is the PROCESS of IDENTIFYING an OPPORTUNITY (an unmet need) and ORGANISING RESOURCES (money, people, equipment, ideas) to CREATE a NEW VENTURE (business or service), while ACCEPTING the RISKS involved, in return for PROFIT or SOCIAL VALUE. Nurses can become entrepreneurs by starting home-care services, private clinics, health education businesses, consultancy, or medical supply businesses.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Saving money in a fixed deposit account — Saving money in a fixed deposit is investment, not creating a venture.
• Inheriting a family business without taking part in managing it — Inheriting a business without managing it is not entrepreneurship.
• Working as a salaried employee in a government hospital — Working as a salaried employee is employment, not entrepreneurship.

⭐ Key point: Key elements: innovation, opportunity, risk-taking, resource organisation and value creation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q81$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, true, 2684, $s$A written document that describes a new venture's goals, target market, operations, management and financial projections is called a:$s$, $s$["Memorandum of understanding", "Duty roster", "Business plan", "Job description"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Business plan
A BUSINESS PLAN is a written document that describes a new venture's GOALS, TARGET MARKET, OPERATIONS, MANAGEMENT and FINANCIAL PROJECTIONS (start-up costs, expected income and expenses, break-even point). It guides the business and is needed to obtain loans or investors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Memorandum of understanding — A memorandum of understanding is an agreement between parties.
• Duty roster — A duty roster is a staff schedule.
• Job description — A job description outlines a job's duties.

⭐ Key point: Main sections: executive summary, business description, market analysis, marketing plan, operations, management, and financial plan.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q87$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, true, 2685, $s$The FIRST step in starting a new business is usually to:$s$, $s$["Buy equipment", "Print business cards", "Identify a business idea/opportunity and assess its feasibility", "Employ staff"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Identify a business idea/opportunity and assess its feasibility
The FIRST step in starting a business is to IDENTIFY a BUSINESS IDEA or OPPORTUNITY — an unmet need that people will pay for (e.g. families needing nursing care at home after discharge) — and ASSESS its FEASIBILITY: Is there enough demand? Who are the competitors? How much will it cost to start and run? Is it legal and practical? Will it make a profit? Only after the idea is shown to be feasible should the entrepreneur write a business plan, raise capital, register the business with the CAC (and obtain professional licences), find premises, buy equipment and recruit staff.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Buy equipment — Buying equipment before confirming the idea is feasible risks wasting money.
• Print business cards — Business cards are part of promotion, which comes much later.
• Employ staff — Employing staff comes after the business is planned, funded and registered.

⭐ Key point: Typical sequence: idea → feasibility study → business plan → finance → registration/licensing → set-up → launch → monitor and evaluate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q88$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, true, 2686, $s$The recommended nurse-to-patient ratio for a ventilated Level 3 patient is:$s$, $s$["1:2", "1:4", "1:1", "1:6"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1:1
A ventilated LEVEL 3 patient is critically ill: they depend on a machine for breathing, often need multiple infusions (sedation, vasopressors), and can deteriorate within seconds (e.g. tube blockage or displacement, sudden hypotension, arrhythmia). They need a NURSE-TO-PATIENT RATIO of 1:1 — one nurse continuously at the bedside — for close monitoring, rapid response to alarms, titration of drugs, suctioning, positioning and family support. Some very complex patients (e.g. ECMO) may need even more than one nurse.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1:2 — 1:2 is the usual ratio for LEVEL 2 (high dependency) patients who need single-organ support, not for ventilated Level 3 patients.
• 1:4 — 1:4 is a general ward ratio and would be unsafe for a ventilated patient.
• 1:6 — 1:6 is far too low and would put a ventilated patient in serious danger.

⭐ Key point: Studies show that inadequate ICU nurse staffing increases errors, hospital infections, unplanned extubations and deaths.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q167 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, true, 2687, $s$A patient needing advanced respiratory support, or support of two or more organs, needs which level of care?$s$, $s$["Level 0 (normal ward)", "Level 1 (ward with outreach)", "Level 3 (intensive care)", "Level 2 (high dependency)"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Level 3 (intensive care)
A patient needing ADVANCED RESPIRATORY SUPPORT (invasive ventilation) alone, OR support of TWO OR MORE organ systems (for example ventilation plus vasopressors, or dialysis plus inotropes), needs LEVEL 3 care: intensive care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Level 0 (normal ward) — Level 0 is normal ward care.
• Level 1 (ward with outreach) — Level 1 is ward care with extra monitoring or outreach support.
• Level 2 (high dependency) — Level 2 (HDU) is for single organ support or close monitoring.

⭐ Key point: Levels of care guide staffing, admission and discharge decisions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q177 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2688, $s$Which site is commonly used for chest tube insertion?$s$, $s$["Midline of the abdomen", "5th intercostal space, mid-axillary line", "Clavicular notch", "4th intercostal space, midclavicular line"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 5th intercostal space, mid-axillary line
The usual chest drain site is the "safe triangle": 4th–5th intercostal space in the MID-AXILLARY line (lateral edge of pectoralis major in front, lateral edge of latissimus dorsi behind, the level of the nipple below). This avoids the internal mammary artery, the heart, the liver and spleen, and large muscles. The drain goes over the TOP of the rib, because the intercostal vessels and nerve run along the bottom edge.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Midline of the abdomen — The midline of the abdomen is for abdominal procedures (such as a laparotomy), not chest drains.
• Clavicular notch — The clavicular notch is not a chest drain site; it is near large vessels.
• 4th intercostal space, midclavicular line — The 4th ICS mid-clavicular line is too close to the heart and breast tissue; the 2nd ICS mid-clavicular line is used for NEEDLE decompression.

⭐ Key point: The patient should sit up with the arm raised behind the head. After insertion, check for swinging and bubbling and get a chest X-ray.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q58$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2689, $s$A retrospective nursing audit involves reviewing records:$s$, $s$["During the care of the patient", "After the patient has been discharged", "Before the patient is admitted to the hospital", "To plan the patient's care before it begins"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: After the patient has been discharged
A RETROSPECTIVE audit looks BACK at care after it has finished, by reviewing the patient's records after discharge. It checks whether care met the set standards (for example, whether every patient had a VTE assessment). It is easy to do but can't change care for that patient.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• During the care of the patient — Reviewing during the patient's care is a CONCURRENT audit.
• Before the patient is admitted to the hospital — Before admission there is no care to audit.
• To plan the patient's care before it begins — Planning care before it begins is care planning, not an audit.

⭐ Key point: Concurrent audit = during care (can correct problems as they happen). Retrospective audit = after discharge (looks for patterns to improve future care).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q162$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2690, $s$The first and most important function of a manager is:$s$, $s$["Delegation", "Funding", "Planning", "Supervision"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Planning
PLANNING is the first and most basic function of management. It means deciding in advance what to do, how, when and by whom: setting goals and objectives, and choosing the best methods. All the other functions (organising, staffing, directing, controlling) depend on the plan. Without planning, work is reactive and disorganised.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delegation — Delegation is a part of directing and organising; it follows planning.
• Funding — Funding (budgeting) is part of planning, not the first function itself.
• Supervision — Supervision is part of directing and controlling, which come after planning.

⭐ Key point: Fayol's functions of management, in order: Planning, Organising, Commanding (Directing), Coordinating, Controlling.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q174$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2691, $s$Henri Fayol's 14 principles include the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Division of labour", "Discipline", "Unity of command", "Strategic management"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Strategic management
The question asks which is NOT one of Fayol's 14 principles. "Strategic management" is a modern management concept, not one of Henri Fayol's 14 principles (1916). His principles include division of labour, authority and responsibility, discipline, unity of command, unity of direction, subordination of individual interest, remuneration, centralisation, scalar chain, order, equity, stability of tenure, initiative, and esprit de corps.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Division of labour — Division of labour IS one of his principles (specialisation improves efficiency).
• Discipline — Discipline IS one of his principles (respect for rules and agreements).
• Unity of command — Unity of command IS one of his principles (each worker has only one boss).

⭐ Key point: Fayol is the father of administrative (modern) management; Taylor is the father of scientific management.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q175$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2692, $s$The ‘father’ of Scientific Management is:$s$, $s$["Henry Gantt", "W. Edwards Deming", "Henry Ford", "Frederick W. Taylor"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Frederick W. Taylor
Frederick Winslow Taylor is the "father of scientific management" (Principles of Scientific Management, 1911). He used time-and-motion studies to find the "one best way" to do each task, selected and trained workers scientifically, and linked pay to output (piece-rate). His ideas aimed to increase efficiency and productivity.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Henry Gantt — Henry Gantt was Taylor's associate and created the Gantt chart for scheduling.
• W. Edwards Deming — W. Edwards Deming is linked with Total Quality Management and the PDSA cycle.
• Henry Ford — Henry Ford introduced the moving assembly line in car manufacturing.

⭐ Key point: Classical management thinkers: Taylor (scientific management), Fayol (administrative management), Weber (bureaucracy).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q176$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2693, $s$In Herzberg's theory, the following are motivators EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Recognition", "Achievement", "Money (salary)", "Challenging work"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Money (salary)
Herzberg's two-factor theory separates MOTIVATORS (which create satisfaction and drive people to work harder: achievement, recognition, responsibility, the work itself, advancement and growth) from HYGIENE factors (which prevent dissatisfaction but don't motivate: salary/money, working conditions, company policy, supervision, job security, relationships). Money is a HYGIENE factor, so it is the exception.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Recognition — Recognition IS a motivator.
• Achievement — Achievement IS a motivator.
• Challenging work — Challenging work (the work itself) IS a motivator.

⭐ Key point: Poor pay causes dissatisfaction, but good pay alone does not motivate long-term. Recognition and growth do.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q177$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2694, $s$A nurse delegates the administration of intravenous medication to a nursing assistant. This action is:$s$, $s$["Appropriate", "Acceptable if supervised", "Inappropriate because it exceeds the assistant's scope of practice", "Recommended during emergencies"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inappropriate because it exceeds the assistant's scope of practice
Giving IV medication requires knowledge, judgement and skills (drug calculations, checking compatibility, monitoring for reactions) that are within the scope of a registered nurse, not a nursing assistant. Delegating it is INAPPROPRIATE and unsafe. If harm occurs, the nurse who delegated is accountable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Appropriate — It is not appropriate, because the task is outside the assistant's role.
• Acceptable if supervised — Supervision cannot make a task acceptable if it is outside the person's legal scope.
• Recommended during emergencies — Emergencies do not allow staff to work outside their scope of practice.

⭐ Key point: The 5 rights of delegation: right task, right circumstance, right person, right direction or communication, right supervision. You can delegate tasks, but not accountability.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q179$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2695, $s$Which of this budget is not part types of budget?$s$, $s$["Personnel budget", "Operational budget", "Capital budget", "Supplementary budget"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Supplementary budget
The main types of budgets in health care management are the OPERATIONAL (operating) budget for day-to-day running costs, the CAPITAL budget for large, long-lasting items like equipment and buildings, and the PERSONNEL budget for staff salaries and benefits (often part of the operating budget). A "supplementary budget" is a government term for extra funds added during the year, not a standard type of nursing unit budget.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Personnel budget — The personnel budget IS a type (salaries, overtime, benefits).
• Operational budget — The operational budget IS a type (supplies, utilities, daily running).
• Capital budget — The capital budget IS a type (ventilators, monitors, building works).

⭐ Key point: Other budget approaches: zero-based budgeting (justify every item from zero), incremental budgeting (add a percentage to last year's budget), and flexible budgeting.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q180$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2696, $s$If a nurse suspects a patient data breach has occurred, the appropriate first action is to:$s$, $s$["Ignore it if no harm seems obvious", "Report it immediately according to institutional policy", "Delete the affected records", "Discuss it publicly with colleagues on social media"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Report it immediately according to institutional policy
A DATA BREACH is any unauthorised access, loss or sharing of patient information (e.g. a lost laptop, records seen by someone with no reason, a misdirected email). The nurse's first action is to REPORT IT IMMEDIATELY through the proper channel — usually the nurse in charge/manager and the hospital's information governance or data protection officer — following institutional policy. Early reporting lets the hospital contain the breach, protect the patient, and meet legal duties to inform regulators and affected people within set times. Document the facts and do not try to cover it up.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignore it if no harm seems obvious — IGNORING it is wrong; harm may not be obvious yet, and hiding a breach is itself a disciplinary and legal offence.
• Delete the affected records — DELETING records destroys evidence and the patient's clinical record — this is illegal and unsafe.
• Discuss it publicly with colleagues on social media — Discussing it on SOCIAL MEDIA spreads the breach further and is unprofessional.

⭐ Key point: Suspected data breach → REPORT IMMEDIATELY per policy. Never ignore, delete or post about it.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q49$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2697, $s$Which of the following dietary recommendations are effective in the management of hyperemesis gravidarum?$s$, $s$["Eating high-fat foods", "Drinking fluids with meals", "Removing salt from the diet", "Eating small, frequent meals"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Eating small, frequent meals
HYPEREMESIS GRAVIDARUM is severe, persistent vomiting in pregnancy causing dehydration, weight loss (> 5%), ketosis and electrolyte imbalance (low potassium and sodium). Dietary advice: eat SMALL, FREQUENT MEALS (every 2–3 hours) so the stomach is never empty or overloaded; choose dry, bland, high-carbohydrate, low-fat foods (crackers, toast, rice); drink fluids BETWEEN meals rather than with meals; avoid spicy, greasy or strong-smelling foods; ginger may help. Severe cases need hospital: IV fluids (normal saline with potassium), antiemetics, THIAMINE (to prevent Wernicke's encephalopathy), and monitoring of electrolytes and ketones.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Eating high-fat foods — HIGH-FAT foods are digested slowly and make nausea worse.
• Drinking fluids with meals — Drinking fluids WITH meals fills the stomach and makes vomiting more likely; fluids should be taken between meals.
• Removing salt from the diet — REMOVING SALT is not helpful; these women often lose sodium through vomiting and need it replaced.

⭐ Key point: Hyperemesis: SMALL, FREQUENT, dry, low-fat meals; fluids BETWEEN meals; IV fluids + thiamine if severe.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q143$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2698, $s$In the PDSA cycle, the Study phase involves:$s$, $s$["Developing the objective and predicting results", "Carrying out the planned change on a small/trial scale", "Analyzing the results/data collected and comparing them to predictions", "Implementing the change hospital-wide permanently"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Analyzing the results/data collected and comparing them to predictions
The PDSA cycle (Plan–Do–Study–Act; Deming/Shewhart) is a quality improvement method for testing changes on a small scale. PLAN — state the objective, PREDICT what will happen, and plan who, what, where, when, and what data to collect. DO — carry out the change on a SMALL/TRIAL scale and collect the data, noting problems. STUDY — ANALYSE the data/results, COMPARE them with the PREDICTIONS, and summarise what was learned. ACT — decide: adopt the change, adapt (modify) it, or abandon it, then plan the next cycle. Many small cycles lead to reliable improvement before wider rollout.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Developing the objective and predicting results — Developing the objective and predicting results is the PLAN phase.
• Carrying out the planned change on a small/trial scale — Carrying out the change on a small scale is the DO phase.
• Implementing the change hospital-wide permanently — Implementing hospital-wide is part of ACT (after successful testing), not Study.

⭐ Key point: PDSA: Plan (objective, prediction) → Do (small test) → STUDY (analyse and compare with prediction) → Act (adopt, adapt, abandon).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q153$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2699, $s$A nursing audit can help to identify:$s$, $s$["The nurse who is the \"best\" at their job", "The single cause of all patient complications", "Trends in patient care that may need to be addressed", "The hospital's financial status"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Trends in patient care that may need to be addressed
A NURSING AUDIT is a systematic review of nursing care, usually by examining records (retrospective audit) or observing care as it happens (concurrent audit), and comparing it against set STANDARDS. It helps to: identify TRENDS and PATTERNS in patient care (e.g. rising pressure ulcer rates, missed drug doses, poor documentation of pain), find areas of good practice and areas needing improvement, guide staff education, improve quality and safety, and check compliance with policies. It is a QUALITY IMPROVEMENT tool, not a way to judge individuals or finances.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The nurse who is the "best" at their job — An audit does not rank nurses to find the "best" one; it looks at the quality of care in the system.
• The single cause of all patient complications — Complications usually have several causes; an audit identifies patterns and contributing factors, not a "single cause of all" complications.
• The hospital's financial status — Hospital FINANCIAL status is assessed by financial audits/accounts, not nursing audit.

⭐ Key point: Nursing audit = compare care with standards → identify TRENDS and areas for improvement in patient care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q162$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2700, $s$The '5 Rights of Delegation' include right task, right circumstance, right person, right direction/communication, and:$s$, $s$["Right documentation only", "Right supervision/evaluation", "Right payment", "Right uniform"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Right supervision/evaluation
The FIVE RIGHTS OF DELEGATION (NCSBN/ANA) help the RN delegate safely: (1) RIGHT TASK — a task that can be delegated (routine, stable, predictable outcome; not assessment, planning, evaluation or teaching); (2) RIGHT CIRCUMSTANCE — the patient is stable and the setting and resources are appropriate; (3) RIGHT PERSON — the delegate has the training, competence and legal scope to perform it; (4) RIGHT DIRECTION/COMMUNICATION — clear instructions: what to do, when, limits, what to report back; (5) RIGHT SUPERVISION/EVALUATION — the RN monitors, gives feedback, evaluates the outcome and intervenes if needed. The RN remains ACCOUNTABLE for the delegated task.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Right documentation only — RIGHT DOCUMENTATION is important but is not one of the five rights.
• Right payment — RIGHT PAYMENT has nothing to do with delegation.
• Right uniform — RIGHT UNIFORM is not a delegation principle.

⭐ Key point: 5 Rights of Delegation: Task, Circumstance, Person, Direction/Communication, SUPERVISION/EVALUATION. RN stays accountable.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q174$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2701, $s$According to the Thomas-Kilmann model, the conflict resolution style with both high assertiveness and high cooperation is:$s$, $s$["Competing", "Avoiding", "Collaborating", "Accommodating"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Collaborating
The THOMAS–KILMANN model describes five conflict-handling styles using two dimensions: ASSERTIVENESS (concern for your own needs) and COOPERATIVENESS (concern for the other person's needs). COMPETING — high assertive, low cooperative ("win–lose"). ACCOMMODATING — low assertive, high cooperative (giving in, "lose–win"). AVOIDING — low assertive, low cooperative (withdrawing, "lose–lose"). COMPROMISING — medium on both (each gives something). COLLABORATING — HIGH assertive AND HIGH cooperative: both parties work together to find a solution that fully meets everyone's needs ("WIN–WIN"). Collaboration takes more time but gives the best long-term outcome and team relationships.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Competing — COMPETING is high assertiveness but LOW cooperation.
• Avoiding — AVOIDING is LOW on both assertiveness and cooperation.
• Accommodating — ACCOMMODATING is high cooperation but LOW assertiveness.

⭐ Key point: Thomas–Kilmann: COLLABORATING = high assertiveness + high cooperation = WIN–WIN.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q175$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2702, $s$The optimal approach to leadership (Managerial Grid) shows:$s$, $s$["High concern for production only", "Low concern for production", "High concern for employees only", "High concern for both production and employees"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: High concern for both production and employees
The BLAKE AND MOUTON MANAGERIAL (LEADERSHIP) GRID plots leadership on two axes from 1 to 9: CONCERN FOR PRODUCTION (results, tasks) and CONCERN FOR PEOPLE (employees). Styles: 1,1 IMPOVERISHED (low on both — does the minimum); 9,1 AUTHORITY-COMPLIANCE/task (high production, low people); 1,9 COUNTRY CLUB (high people, low production — friendly but little work done); 5,5 MIDDLE-OF-THE-ROAD (balance, average results); 9,9 TEAM MANAGEMENT (HIGH concern for BOTH production AND people). Blake and Mouton considered 9,9 TEAM management the OPTIMAL style: committed staff, trust and respect, and high performance.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• High concern for production only — High concern for production ONLY is the authority-compliance (9,1) style — results with poor morale.
• Low concern for production — LOW concern for production describes impoverished or country club styles.
• High concern for employees only — High concern for employees ONLY is the country club (1,9) style.

⭐ Key point: Managerial Grid optimal = 9,9 TEAM MANAGEMENT — high concern for BOTH production AND people.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q176$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2703, $s$The following are part of Fayol's 14 principles EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Division of labour", "Scalar chain", "Order", "Planning"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Planning
HENRI FAYOL (father of modern management) described 14 PRINCIPLES of management: (1) DIVISION OF WORK/labour, (2) authority and responsibility, (3) discipline, (4) unity of command, (5) unity of direction, (6) subordination of individual interest to the general interest, (7) remuneration, (8) centralisation, (9) SCALAR CHAIN (line of authority from top to bottom), (10) ORDER (right person and material in the right place), (11) equity, (12) stability of tenure of personnel, (13) initiative, (14) esprit de corps (team spirit). PLANNING is one of Fayol's five FUNCTIONS of management (POCCC: Planning, Organising, Commanding, Coordinating, Controlling) — not one of the 14 principles.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Division of labour — DIVISION OF LABOUR IS one of the 14 principles.
• Scalar chain — SCALAR CHAIN IS one of the 14 principles.
• Order — ORDER IS one of the 14 principles.

⭐ Key point: Fayol's 14 PRINCIPLES include division of work, scalar chain, order, unity of command. PLANNING is a FUNCTION (POCCC).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q178$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2704, $s$Which task should NOT be delegated to a nursing assistant?$s$, $s$["Bed making", "Feeding a stable patient", "Assessment of pain", "Transporting stable patients"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assessment of pain
Registered nurses can delegate ROUTINE, predictable tasks for STABLE patients to nursing assistants/unregulated care staff — e.g. BED MAKING, FEEDING a stable patient with no swallowing problem, TRANSPORTING stable patients, bathing, measuring and recording vital signs, ambulating. But the RN must NOT delegate the core nursing process steps that need professional judgement: ASSESSMENT (including PAIN ASSESSMENT), planning, nursing diagnosis, evaluation, health teaching, and care of unstable patients. An assistant can REPORT that a patient says they have pain, but the RN must ASSESS the pain (location, severity, character, response to treatment) and decide on action.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bed making — BED MAKING is a routine task and CAN be delegated.
• Feeding a stable patient — FEEDING a STABLE patient CAN be delegated (not one with dysphagia or aspiration risk).
• Transporting stable patients — TRANSPORTING STABLE patients CAN be delegated.

⭐ Key point: Never delegate ASSESSMENT (including pain), planning, evaluation, teaching, or unstable patients. Routine tasks can be delegated.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q179$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2705, $s$A Chief Nurse Executive has a charismatic leadership style. Which of the following behaviors best describes this style?$s$, $s$["Possesses inspirational quality that makes followers get attracted to him and regard him with reverence.", "Uses visioning as the core of his leadership.", "Acts as he does because he expects that his harshness will yield positive results.", "Acts as he does because he expects that his behavior will yield positive results."]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Possesses inspirational quality that makes followers get attracted to him and regard him with reverence.
CHARISMATIC LEADERSHIP is based on the leader's personal qualities — charm, confidence, energy and an INSPIRING personality. Followers are emotionally ATTRACTED to the leader, admire and trust them, often with deep respect or REVERENCE, and are willing to follow because of who the leader is (personal power), not because of position or rewards. Strengths: inspires commitment during change or crisis. Risks: the team may depend too much on one person, and followers may not question poor decisions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Uses visioning as the core of his leadership. — Using VISIONING as the core of leadership describes TRANSFORMATIONAL leadership (creating and sharing a vision).
• Acts as he does because he expects that his harshness will yield positive results. — Being harsh because harshness is expected to give results describes an AUTOCRATIC/authoritarian approach, not charismatic.
• Acts as he does because he expects that his behavior will yield positive results. — Acting because the behaviour is expected to yield positive results is a general/expectancy-type description, not specific to charisma.

⭐ Key point: CHARISMATIC leader = inspiring personal qualities that attract followers and earn their reverence. Visioning = transformational.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q180$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2706, $s$The following are examples of motivation theories EXCEPT$s$, $s$["Maslow theory of basic human need", "Expectancy theory", "System theory", "Hertzberg theory"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: System theory
MOTIVATION THEORIES explain what drives people to act and work. Examples: MASLOW'S HIERARCHY OF NEEDS (physiological → safety → love/belonging → esteem → self-actualisation); HERZBERG'S TWO-FACTOR (motivation–hygiene) theory (hygiene factors like salary and working conditions prevent dissatisfaction; motivators like achievement and recognition increase satisfaction); VROOM'S EXPECTANCY THEORY (people are motivated if they believe effort → performance → valued reward); McClelland's needs theory; McGregor's Theory X and Y; equity theory. SYSTEMS THEORY (von Bertalanffy) is a general theory that sees an organisation as a system of interrelated parts with inputs, throughput, outputs and feedback — it is not a motivation theory.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Maslow theory of basic human need — MASLOW's theory IS a motivation theory (needs hierarchy).
• Expectancy theory — EXPECTANCY theory IS a motivation theory (Vroom).
• Hertzberg theory — HERZBERG's theory IS a motivation theory (two-factor).

⭐ Key point: Motivation theories: Maslow, Herzberg, Vroom (expectancy), McClelland, McGregor. SYSTEMS theory is NOT one.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q183$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2707, $s$Which of the following is NOT a performance appraisal factor?$s$, $s$["Attendance", "Attitude", "Quality of work", "Number of social media handles"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Number of social media handles
PERFORMANCE APPRAISAL is the formal evaluation of how well an employee does their job, compared with job descriptions and set standards. Common factors assessed: ATTENDANCE and punctuality, ATTITUDE (towards patients, colleagues, work), QUALITY of work, quantity/productivity, clinical knowledge and skills, communication, teamwork, initiative, reliability, adherence to policies, professional development. The NUMBER OF SOCIAL MEDIA HANDLES (accounts) a nurse has has nothing to do with job performance and would be irrelevant and unfair to assess.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Attendance — ATTENDANCE IS a performance appraisal factor.
• Attitude — ATTITUDE IS a performance appraisal factor.
• Quality of work — QUALITY OF WORK IS a core performance appraisal factor.

⭐ Key point: Appraisal factors: attendance, attitude, quality of work, skills, teamwork, communication — not social media handles.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q187$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2708, $s$The nurse assessing a ventilated patient should evaluate for signs of inadequate oxygenation or ventilation including all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Restlessness and confusion", "Tachycardia", "Bradycardia and hypotension as late signs", "Polyuria"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Polyuria
Signs of INADEQUATE OXYGENATION or VENTILATION include: RESTLESSNESS and CONFUSION (early sign of brain hypoxia or high CO2), TACHYCARDIA (early compensation), tachypnoea, sweating, cyanosis, and LATE signs such as BRADYCARDIA and HYPOTENSION (pre-arrest). POLYURIA (excessive urine) is NOT a sign of inadequate oxygenation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Restlessness and confusion — Restlessness and confusion ARE signs.
• Tachycardia — Tachycardia IS a sign.
• Bradycardia and hypotension as late signs — Bradycardia and hypotension ARE late signs.

⭐ Key point: Restlessness in a ventilated patient may be hypoxia — check the patient before increasing sedation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q51$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2709, $s$An open (sucking) chest wound should be managed with:$s$, $s$["A fully sealed 4-sided occlusive dressing", "A 3-sided occlusive dressing", "No dressing at all", "Direct pressure only, no dressing"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A 3-sided occlusive dressing
An OPEN (SUCKING) CHEST WOUND lets air into the chest during breathing. It is managed with a 3-SIDED OCCLUSIVE DRESSING (taped on three sides). This acts as a flutter valve: it seals during inspiration, preventing air entry, and lets air escape during expiration, preventing a tension pneumothorax.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A fully sealed 4-sided occlusive dressing — A 4-sided sealed dressing could trap air and cause a tension pneumothorax.
• No dressing at all — No dressing allows air in.
• Direct pressure only, no dressing — Direct pressure alone is not adequate.

⭐ Key point: Commercial vented chest seals are also used. A chest drain is the definitive treatment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q58$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2710, $s$Phaneuf's nursing audit tool evaluates nursing care under seven functions, which include all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Application and execution of physician's legal orders", "Observation of symptoms and reactions", "Supervision of the patient", "Approval of hospital annual budget"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Approval of hospital annual budget
PHANEUF'S NURSING AUDIT evaluates nursing care through records against SEVEN FUNCTIONS of nursing: (1) application and execution of physician's legal orders; (2) observation of symptoms and reactions; (3) supervision of the patient; (4) supervision of those participating in care; (5) reporting and recording; (6) application and execution of nursing procedures and techniques; (7) promotion of physical and emotional health by direction and teaching. "APPROVAL OF HOSPITAL ANNUAL BUDGET" is NOT one of the functions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Application and execution of physician's legal orders — Execution of physician's orders IS a function.
• Observation of symptoms and reactions — Observation of symptoms IS a function.
• Supervision of the patient — Supervision of the patient IS a function.

⭐ Key point: Nursing audits help evaluate and improve the quality of nursing care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q153$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2711, $s$A charge nurse asked why a particular protocol works considers several possible explanations and weighs evidence for and against each rather than accepting the first explanation offered. This reflects:$s$, $s$["Open-mindedness combined with analysis", "Overconfidence", "Avoidance of the question", "Conformity to peer opinion"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Open-mindedness combined with analysis
Considering SEVERAL POSSIBLE EXPLANATIONS and WEIGHING EVIDENCE for and against each, rather than accepting the first answer, shows OPEN-MINDEDNESS (willingness to consider different views) combined with ANALYSIS (systematically evaluating information). These are key critical thinking attitudes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Overconfidence — Overconfidence would mean accepting the first answer without checking.
• Avoidance of the question — Avoidance would mean not answering at all.
• Conformity to peer opinion — Conformity would mean accepting what others think without evaluating.

⭐ Key point: Critical thinking improves clinical decision-making and patient safety.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q154$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2712, $s$How can nurses contribute to quality assurance efforts?$s$, $s$["By ignoring feedback from patients", "By working independently of their colleagues", "By participating in quality improvement initiatives and adhering to best practices", "By prioritizing speed over accuracy"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: By participating in quality improvement initiatives and adhering to best practices
QUALITY ASSURANCE (QA) and quality improvement aim to make care safe, effective and patient-centred. Nurses contribute by: PARTICIPATING in QUALITY IMPROVEMENT INITIATIVES (audits, data collection, quality committees, PDSA cycles, care bundles), ADHERING to BEST PRACTICES and evidence-based guidelines, reporting incidents and near misses, listening to patient feedback, keeping accurate documentation, and continuing professional development.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• By ignoring feedback from patients — Ignoring patient feedback misses opportunities to improve care.
• By working independently of their colleagues — Quality improvement requires teamwork and collaboration, not working alone.
• By prioritizing speed over accuracy — Prioritising speed over accuracy causes errors and harms patients.

⭐ Key point: Every nurse is responsible for quality — not just managers.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q158$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2713, $s$The goal of quality assurance in nursing is to:$s$, $s$["Reduce the cost of care", "Meet the minimum legal standards of care", "Continuously improve patient care and outcomes", "Ensure nurses follow all orders without question"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Continuously improve patient care and outcomes
The main goal of QUALITY ASSURANCE (QA) in nursing is to CONTINUOUSLY IMPROVE PATIENT CARE and OUTCOMES. It involves setting standards, measuring performance (structure, process and outcome), comparing results with standards, identifying gaps, and making improvements, then re-measuring. QA aims for excellence, not just the minimum.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reduce the cost of care — Reducing costs may be a benefit, but it is not the main goal.
• Meet the minimum legal standards of care — Meeting only minimum legal standards is not enough; QA aims higher.
• Ensure nurses follow all orders without question — Following orders without question is unsafe; nurses must use professional judgement.

⭐ Key point: Quality tools include audits, incident reporting, PDSA cycles, care bundles and patient satisfaction surveys.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q162$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2714, $s$Which scholar introduced the functional type of organisation?$s$, $s$["F.W. Taylor", "Chester Bernard", "Allen", "Max Weber"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: F.W. Taylor
Frederick Winslow TAYLOR, the father of scientific management, introduced the FUNCTIONAL TYPE OF ORGANISATION ("functional foremanship"). Instead of one supervisor controlling everything, each worker received instructions from several SPECIALIST supervisors, each responsible for a particular function (e.g. planning, speed, quality, maintenance). This improved specialisation and efficiency, but could confuse workers with multiple bosses (breaking unity of command).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chester Bernard — Chester Barnard wrote about the functions of the executive and acceptance of authority.
• Allen — "Allen" is not associated with the functional organisation.
• Max Weber — Max Weber developed the theory of BUREAUCRACY.

⭐ Key point: In hospitals, nurses often report to both a ward manager and specialist leads — a form of functional organisation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q178$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2715, $s$The major concern of an effective leader is:$s$, $s$["Concern for production only", "Concern for self", "Concern for both production and employees", "Concern for employees only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Concern for both production and employees
An effective leader shows CONCERN for BOTH PRODUCTION (achieving goals — e.g. safe, high-quality patient care, efficient use of resources) AND EMPLOYEES (staff wellbeing, development, motivation and relationships). In Blake and Mouton's MANAGERIAL GRID, this is the "TEAM MANAGEMENT" (9,9) style — considered the most effective, because committed, supported staff achieve better results.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Concern for production only — Concern for production only ("task management") can burn out staff.
• Concern for self — Concern for self is selfish and ineffective.
• Concern for employees only — Concern for employees only ("country club management") may neglect goals.

⭐ Key point: Effective nurse leaders balance task demands with staff support.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q179$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2716, $s$During a mass casualty event, the charge nurse must delegate tasks rapidly to a team with mixed experience levels. The most appropriate delegation approach is to:$s$, $s$["Assign tasks randomly with no regard for skill level", "Match tasks to each team member's competency level, ensuring appropriate supervision for less experienced staff", "Assign only the most complex tasks to the least experienced staff", "Refuse to delegate anything and do all tasks personally"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Match tasks to each team member's competency level, ensuring appropriate supervision for less experienced staff
During a MASS CASUALTY event, the charge nurse should MATCH TASKS to each team member's COMPETENCY level, with APPROPRIATE SUPERVISION for less experienced staff.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assign tasks randomly with no regard for skill level — Random assignment is unsafe.
• Assign only the most complex tasks to the least experienced staff — Complex tasks for inexperienced staff is unsafe.
• Refuse to delegate anything and do all tasks personally — Refusing to delegate is impossible in mass casualties.

⭐ Key point: Use the five rights of delegation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q180$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2717, $s$The following are good qualities of a leader EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Visionary", "Knowledgeable", "Rich", "Integrity"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Rich
Good leaders are VISIONARY (have a clear picture of the future and inspire others towards it), KNOWLEDGEABLE (clinically and managerially competent), and have INTEGRITY (honesty, fairness, consistency, ethical behaviour). Other qualities: good communication, empathy, decisiveness, accountability and humility. Being RICH (wealthy) is NOT a leadership quality — leadership depends on character, skills and behaviour, not money.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Visionary — Visionary IS a leadership quality.
• Knowledgeable — Knowledgeable IS a leadership quality.
• Integrity — Integrity IS a leadership quality.

⭐ Key point: Leadership can be developed through training, mentoring and experience.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q181 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2718, $s$Who advocated the fourteen principles of management?$s$, $s$["Henri Fayol", "Frederick Taylor", "Max Weber", "Karl Marx"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Henri Fayol
Henri FAYOL (1841–1925), a French mining engineer, developed the 14 PRINCIPLES OF MANAGEMENT in his book "General and Industrial Management" (1916): division of work, authority and responsibility, discipline, unity of command, unity of direction, subordination of individual interests to the general interest, remuneration, centralisation, scalar chain, order, equity, stability of tenure, initiative, and esprit de corps.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Frederick Taylor — Frederick Taylor developed SCIENTIFIC MANAGEMENT.
• Max Weber — Max Weber developed the theory of BUREAUCRACY.
• Karl Marx — Karl Marx was a philosopher and economist, not a management theorist.

⭐ Key point: Fayol also described the five functions of management: planning, organising, commanding, coordinating and controlling.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q182$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2719, $s$The nurse manager uses the results of a nursing audit to make changes to the unit's policies. This is an example of the nurse's role in:$s$, $s$["Punishing staff for poor performance", "Maintaining the status quo", "Quality improvement", "Disregarding the documentation process"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Quality improvement
A NURSING AUDIT systematically reviews nursing care (often through records) against agreed standards. When the nurse manager USES AUDIT RESULTS to CHANGE unit POLICIES and practices, this is QUALITY IMPROVEMENT — using data to identify gaps and make changes that improve care. The audit cycle: set standards → measure practice → compare with standards → implement changes → re-audit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Punishing staff for poor performance — Audits are not for punishing staff; they identify system issues.
• Maintaining the status quo — Changing policies based on audit is the opposite of maintaining the status quo.
• Disregarding the documentation process — Audits depend on, rather than disregard, documentation.

⭐ Key point: Involve staff in audit and change — they are more likely to support improvements they helped design.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q183$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2720, $s$Which of the following is a function of a ICU manager?$s$, $s$["Bureaucracy", "Motivation", "Humility", "Oppression"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Motivation
MOTIVATION is a key FUNCTION of an ICU manager (part of directing/leading): inspiring and encouraging staff to work well and develop professionally, through recognition, fair treatment, good working conditions, opportunities for training and promotion, involvement in decisions, and support during stressful situations. Motivated staff provide safer, better-quality patient care and are less likely to leave.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bureaucracy — Bureaucracy is an organisational structure with rigid rules, not a manager's function.
• Humility — Humility is a personal quality, not a function.
• Oppression — Oppression is never an appropriate management behaviour.

⭐ Key point: Herzberg: recognition, responsibility and achievement motivate; pay and conditions prevent dissatisfaction.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q184$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2721, $s$Which of the following best describes the concept of "never events" in quality assurance?$s$, $s$["Adverse events that should never happen in healthcare settings", "Routine occurrences in healthcare settings", "Errors that are insignificant and do not impact patient care", "Events that occur frequently but are easily preventable"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Adverse events that should never happen in healthcare settings
"NEVER EVENTS" are serious, largely PREVENTABLE patient safety incidents that should NEVER happen if proper safety measures are in place. Examples: wrong-site surgery, retained surgical instruments, wrong-route drug administration (e.g. IV drug given intrathecally), and misplaced nasogastric tubes not detected before use.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Routine occurrences in healthcare settings — They are not routine occurrences.
• Errors that are insignificant and do not impact patient care — They are serious, not insignificant.
• Events that occur frequently but are easily preventable — They should be rare, not frequent.

⭐ Key point: Never events must be reported and investigated.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q158$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2722, $s$A nurse manager reviewing a department's risk management programme should ensure it includes:$s$, $s$["Common risk categories, elements of risk management, and a functioning incident reporting system", "Only a list of staff names", "No formal documentation, to save time", "A focus solely on financial risk, excluding patient safety risk"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Common risk categories, elements of risk management, and a functioning incident reporting system
A RISK MANAGEMENT PROGRAMME should include: identification of COMMON RISK CATEGORIES (falls, medication errors, infections, pressure ulcers), ELEMENTS OF RISK MANAGEMENT (identify, analyse, control, monitor risks), and a functioning INCIDENT REPORTING SYSTEM.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only a list of staff names — A list of staff names is not a risk programme.
• No formal documentation, to save time — Documentation is essential.
• A focus solely on financial risk, excluding patient safety risk — Patient safety must be included.

⭐ Key point: Regular risk assessments and audits help prevent harm.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q174$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2723, $s$The stage of conflict where the disagreement becomes visible through behaviour such as arguing or withdrawal is the:$s$, $s$["Latent stage", "Perceived stage", "Manifest stage", "Aftermath stage"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Manifest stage
Pondy's model describes the STAGES of CONFLICT: (1) LATENT — conditions for conflict exist (e.g. scarce resources, unclear roles) but no one is aware yet; (2) PERCEIVED — people become aware that a conflict exists; (3) FELT — emotions develop (anger, anxiety, tension); (4) MANIFEST — the conflict becomes VISIBLE through BEHAVIOUR, such as arguing, open disagreement, sabotage, withdrawal or avoidance; (5) AFTERMATH — the result after the conflict is resolved or suppressed (which may leave lasting effects).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Latent stage — Latent: conditions exist, but there is no awareness.
• Perceived stage — Perceived: awareness, but no visible action yet.
• Aftermath stage — Aftermath: the outcome after the conflict has been handled.

⭐ Key point: Managers should identify and address conflict early (latent or perceived stage) before it becomes manifest and harmful to teamwork and patient care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q175$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2724, $s$A unit is experiencing chronic understaffing, affecting patient safety. As part of the nurse manager's risk management responsibilities, the most appropriate action is to:$s$, $s$["Ignore the issue since staffing is outside nursing's control", "Formally document and escalate the staffing concern through appropriate channels, using incident reports where patient safety is affected", "Accept the situation permanently with no advocacy", "Blame individual staff members for the workload"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Formally document and escalate the staffing concern through appropriate channels, using incident reports where patient safety is affected
When CHRONIC UNDERSTAFFING affects patient safety, the nurse manager should FORMALLY DOCUMENT and ESCALATE the concern through appropriate channels (senior management), using INCIDENT REPORTS where patient safety is affected. This creates a record and supports advocacy for safe staffing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ignore the issue since staffing is outside nursing's control — Ignoring it endangers patients.
• Accept the situation permanently with no advocacy — Accepting it permanently fails to advocate.
• Blame individual staff members for the workload — Blaming individuals ignores the system problem.

⭐ Key point: Safe staffing is a key factor in patient outcomes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q177$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2725, $s$Which is NOT one of Fayol's 14 principles of management?$s$, $s$["Subordination of individual interests", "Remuneration", "Decentralisation", "Scalar chain (clear line of authority)"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Decentralisation
FAYOL'S 14 PRINCIPLES include: division of work, authority, discipline, unity of command, unity of direction, SUBORDINATION OF INDIVIDUAL INTERESTS, REMUNERATION, CENTRALISATION (not decentralisation), SCALAR CHAIN, order, equity, stability of tenure, initiative, and esprit de corps. "DECENTRALISATION" is not one of the principles (Fayol's principle is "centralisation", meaning finding the right balance).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Subordination of individual interests — Subordination of individual interests IS a principle.
• Remuneration — Remuneration IS a principle.
• Scalar chain (clear line of authority) — Scalar chain IS a principle.

⭐ Key point: Fayol is called the "father of modern management".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q180$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2726, $s$When mediating conflict between staff, a nurse manager should ideally:$s$, $s$["Take sides with the more senior staff member", "Act as a neutral mediator", "Resolve the conflict publicly in front of patients", "Avoid addressing it entirely"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Act as a neutral mediator
When MEDIATING CONFLICT between staff, the nurse manager should act as a NEUTRAL MEDIATOR: meet the parties privately, listen to both sides without judgement, focus on facts and behaviour (not personalities), keep the discussion centred on shared goals (safe patient care), help the staff explore solutions, and agree on an action plan with follow-up. Neutrality builds trust and encourages a fair, lasting resolution.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Take sides with the more senior staff member — Taking sides (e.g. with the more senior staff member) is unfair and damages trust.
• Resolve the conflict publicly in front of patients — Resolving conflict publicly in front of patients is unprofessional and embarrassing.
• Avoid addressing it entirely — Avoiding the conflict allows it to grow and harm teamwork.

⭐ Key point: Collaboration ("win-win") is the best long-term conflict resolution approach when time allows.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q181$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2727, $s$Which conflict resolution style involves each party giving up something to reach a middle-ground solution?$s$, $s$["Collaborating", "Compromising", "Competing", "Avoiding"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Compromising
COMPROMISING is a conflict-resolution style where each party GIVES UP SOMETHING to reach a MIDDLE-GROUND solution that is partly acceptable to both ("split the difference"). It is useful when time is limited, both sides have equal power, or a temporary solution is needed. However, neither side is fully satisfied.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Collaborating — COLLABORATING aims for a "win-win" solution that fully meets both parties' needs.
• Competing — COMPETING aims to win at the other party's expense ("win-lose").
• Avoiding — AVOIDING ignores or postpones the conflict ("lose-lose").

⭐ Key point: Thomas–Kilmann styles: competing, collaborating, compromising, avoiding, and accommodating (giving in to the other side).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q183$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2728, $s$Shared governance is practised in some wards. Which describes this style of leadership?$s$, $s$["Leaders rely heavily on visioning and inspire members to achieve results", "Leaders believe people are basically good and need not be closely controlled", "Leadership is shared at the point of care", "The leader makes all decisions alone"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Leadership is shared at the point of care
SHARED GOVERNANCE is a model where LEADERSHIP and decision-making are SHARED with staff nurses at the POINT OF CARE (e.g. through councils on practice, quality and education).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Leaders rely heavily on visioning and inspire members to achieve results — Visioning and inspiring describes transformational leadership.
• Leaders believe people are basically good and need not be closely controlled — Believing people need little control describes Theory Y.
• The leader makes all decisions alone — One leader deciding alone is autocratic.

⭐ Key point: Shared governance improves staff engagement and retention.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q184$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2729, $s$Justice in resource allocation involves ensuring that:$s$, $s$["No patient is unfairly disadvantaged in access to medication, treatment, or staff time", "Resources always go to the first patient who asks", "Resource allocation is kept secret from patients", "Only wealthy patients receive full resources"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: No patient is unfairly disadvantaged in access to medication, treatment, or staff time
JUSTICE in resource allocation means FAIRNESS — ensuring NO PATIENT is UNFAIRLY DISADVANTAGED in access to medication, treatment, or staff time, based on clinical need.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Resources always go to the first patient who asks — First come, first served ignores need.
• Resource allocation is kept secret from patients — Allocation should be transparent.
• Only wealthy patients receive full resources — Wealth should not decide care.

⭐ Key point: Triage policies help allocate scarce resources fairly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q187$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2730, $s$The nurse is obligated to follow a physician's order unless:$s$, $s$["The order is a verbal order", "The physicians order is illegible", "The order has not been transcribed", "The order is an error, violates hospital policy, or would be detrimental to the client."]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The order is an error, violates hospital policy, or would be detrimental to the client.
A nurse must follow a physician's order UNLESS it is an ERROR, VIOLATES HOSPITAL POLICY, or would be HARMFUL (detrimental) to the client. In such cases, the nurse must question the order and not carry it out until clarified.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The order is a verbal order — Verbal orders can be followed per policy.
• The physicians order is illegible — Illegible orders should be clarified, but this is part of the broader rule.
• The order has not been transcribed — Untranscribed orders should be checked, but this isn't the main exception.

⭐ Key point: The nurse is legally accountable for following an unsafe order.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q188$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2731, $s$Index-sequential file organisation allows records to be accessed:$s$, $s$["Only by physically searching every record one by one with no index", "Both sequentially and directly via an index, combining the benefits of both methods", "Only in the exact order they were originally entered, with no shortcuts", "Never — it is a theoretical method with no real use"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Both sequentially and directly via an index, combining the benefits of both methods
INDEX-SEQUENTIAL file organisation stores records in a SEQUENCE (e.g. by hospital number) AND maintains an INDEX (like the index of a book) that points to where groups of records are located. This allows records to be accessed BOTH SEQUENTIALLY (one after another, useful for producing reports of all patients) AND DIRECTLY (jumping straight to a specific record using the index, useful for retrieving one patient's record quickly). It combines the benefits of both methods.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only by physically searching every record one by one with no index — Searching every record one by one without an index describes SERIAL/sequential access only.
• Only in the exact order they were originally entered, with no shortcuts — Accessing only in the order entered with no shortcuts describes SEQUENTIAL access.
• Never — it is a theoretical method with no real use — Index-sequential organisation is widely used in real systems (e.g. databases).

⭐ Key point: File organisation types: serial, sequential, direct (random) and index-sequential.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q23$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2732, $s$A nurse who starts her own private home-care service for elderly patients discharged from hospital is an example of:$s$, $s$["Delegation", "Intrapreneurship", "Moonlighting", "Nurse entrepreneurship"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Nurse entrepreneurship
NURSE ENTREPRENEURSHIP is when a nurse starts and OWNS her OWN BUSINESS that provides nursing or health-related services, taking the financial risk. Starting a private HOME-CARE service for elderly patients discharged from hospital is a clear example.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delegation — Delegation is assigning tasks to others.
• Intrapreneurship — Intrapreneurship is innovation WITHIN an organisation where one is employed.
• Moonlighting — Moonlighting is working a second job in addition to a main job.

⭐ Key point: Nurse entrepreneurs must be registered and licensed, follow the NMCN code, and register their business with the CAC.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q82$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2733, $s$Which characteristic is MOST typical of a successful entrepreneur?$s$, $s$["Avoiding every form of risk", "Willingness to take calculated risks", "Depending on others for every decision", "Resistance to change"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Willingness to take calculated risks
Successful entrepreneurs are WILLING to TAKE CALCULATED RISKS — they assess risks carefully, plan for them, and take them when the potential reward justifies it. Other typical characteristics: creativity and innovation, self-confidence, determination and persistence, vision, initiative, leadership, flexibility, and good decision-making.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Avoiding every form of risk — Avoiding every risk prevents starting or growing a business.
• Depending on others for every decision — Entrepreneurs are independent and make decisions themselves.
• Resistance to change — Entrepreneurs embrace and drive change.

⭐ Key point: "Calculated" risk means informed risk — not reckless gambling.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q84$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2734, $s$In a SWOT analysis, 'Strengths' and 'Weaknesses' refer to:$s$, $s$["Internal factors within the business", "External factors in the market", "Government policies only", "Competitors' future plans"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Internal factors within the business
SWOT analysis is a planning tool. STRENGTHS and WEAKNESSES are INTERNAL factors — things WITHIN the business that it can control (e.g. skilled staff, good location, reputation — or lack of capital, limited equipment). OPPORTUNITIES and THREATS are EXTERNAL factors in the environment (market, government policy, competitors, economy).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• External factors in the market — External factors are opportunities and threats.
• Government policies only — Government policies are external factors.
• Competitors' future plans — Competitors' plans are external factors (threats).

⭐ Key point: SWOT helps a business build on strengths, fix weaknesses, use opportunities and prepare for threats.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q85$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2735, $s$In a SWOT analysis for a private nursing clinic, a new government policy expanding health-insurance coverage to more families would be classified as:$s$, $s$["An opportunity", "A strength", "A weakness", "A threat"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An opportunity
In a SWOT analysis, OPPORTUNITIES are favourable EXTERNAL conditions — things OUTSIDE the business that it can take advantage of to grow. A NEW GOVERNMENT POLICY that EXPANDS HEALTH-INSURANCE coverage (e.g. NHIA/state insurance schemes) means more families will be able to pay for health services through insurance. A private nursing clinic could register as a provider and attract many new clients. Because the policy comes from outside and could benefit the clinic, it is an OPPORTUNITY.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A strength — A STRENGTH is an INTERNAL advantage of the clinic itself (e.g. experienced nurses, good reputation, convenient location).
• A weakness — A WEAKNESS is an INTERNAL disadvantage (e.g. limited capital, no ambulance, few staff).
• A threat — A THREAT is an EXTERNAL factor that could HARM the clinic (e.g. a large new hospital opening nearby, rising costs, unfavourable regulations).

⭐ Key point: Internal = Strengths and Weaknesses (you can control them). External = Opportunities and Threats (you must respond to them).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q86$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2736, $s$A feasibility study is carried out mainly to:$s$, $s$["Calculate staff salaries only", "Determine whether a proposed business idea is practical and likely to succeed financially", "Advertise the business", "Recruit employees"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Determine whether a proposed business idea is practical and likely to succeed financially
A FEASIBILITY STUDY is a careful investigation carried out BEFORE starting a business, to determine whether the proposed idea is PRACTICAL and LIKELY TO SUCCEED, especially FINANCIALLY. It examines: market demand (are there enough clients?), competition, location, technical requirements (equipment, skills), legal and regulatory requirements (licences, registration), start-up and running costs, expected income, and the break-even point. The results help the entrepreneur decide whether to go ahead, change the idea, or abandon it — saving time and money.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Calculate staff salaries only — Calculating salaries is only a small part of the financial analysis, not the main purpose.
• Advertise the business — Advertising is a marketing activity that comes after the business is established.
• Recruit employees — Recruiting employees comes after the decision to start the business.

⭐ Key point: A feasibility study answers the question "Should we do this?"; a business plan answers "How will we do it?"$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q89$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2737, $s$In Nigeria, business names and companies are registered with the:$s$, $s$["Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria", "Central Bank of Nigeria", "Corporate Affairs Commission (CAC)", "NAFDAC"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Corporate Affairs Commission (CAC)
In Nigeria, the CORPORATE AFFAIRS COMMISSION (CAC) is the government agency responsible for REGISTERING BUSINESS NAMES, COMPANIES (e.g. limited liability companies) and incorporated trustees (NGOs), under the Companies and Allied Matters Act (CAMA 2020). Registration gives the business a legal identity, protects its name, allows it to open a corporate bank account, and is needed for contracts, loans and tax purposes. Registration can now be done online through the CAC portal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria — The Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria (NMCN) registers and licenses nurses and midwives, and regulates nursing practice — not businesses.
• Central Bank of Nigeria — The Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) regulates banks and monetary policy.
• NAFDAC — NAFDAC regulates food, drugs, cosmetics and medical devices.

⭐ Key point: A nurse-owned health business also needs a valid nursing licence (NMCN), approval from the State Ministry of Health for health facilities, and tax registration (TIN).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q92$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2738, $s$Money and other resources used to start and run a business are called:$s$, $s$["Turnover", "Profit", "Dividend", "Capital"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Capital
CAPITAL is the MONEY and other RESOURCES (equipment, premises, vehicles, stock) that are used to START and RUN a business. START-UP capital pays for initial costs (registration, rent deposit, equipment, first supplies), while WORKING capital pays day-to-day expenses (salaries, consumables, utilities) until income comes in. Sources of capital include personal savings, family and friends, cooperative societies, microfinance or bank loans, grants, and investors.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Turnover — TURNOVER is the total income from sales in a period, before costs are subtracted.
• Profit — PROFIT is what remains after subtracting all costs from revenue.
• Dividend — A DIVIDEND is the part of a company's profit paid out to shareholders.

⭐ Key point: Many small businesses fail because they run out of working capital — plan for several months of expenses before the business becomes profitable.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q93$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2739, $s$Profit is calculated as:$s$, $s$["Capital plus total revenue", "Total revenue plus total costs", "Total revenue minus total costs", "Total costs minus total revenue"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Total revenue minus total costs
PROFIT = TOTAL REVENUE − TOTAL COSTS. Revenue is all the money received from selling goods or services (e.g. fees from home visits). Costs are all the expenses of running the business — fixed costs (rent, salaries) and variable costs (supplies, transport). If revenue is GREATER than costs, the result is a PROFIT; if costs are greater than revenue, the result is a LOSS. Example: a clinic earns ₦500,000 in a month and spends ₦380,000; profit = ₦120,000.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Capital plus total revenue — Capital plus revenue is not a meaningful measure of profit; capital is the money invested, not earned.
• Total revenue plus total costs — Adding revenue and costs makes no sense — costs reduce profit.
• Total costs minus total revenue — Costs minus revenue gives the LOSS (a negative profit) when costs are higher.

⭐ Key point: Gross profit = revenue − direct costs; net profit = revenue − all costs (including tax). Keep records to calculate profit accurately each month.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q94$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2740, $s$The rent for a clinic's premises, which stays the same whether 5 or 50 clients are seen in a month, is an example of a:$s$, $s$["Variable cost", "Revenue", "Fixed cost", "Dividend"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fixed cost
FIXED COSTS are expenses that STAY THE SAME regardless of how many clients are seen or how much is sold — at least in the short term. RENT for the clinic premises must be paid whether 5 or 50 clients are seen. Other fixed costs: permanent staff salaries, insurance, loan repayments, licence fees, security and internet subscriptions. Because fixed costs must be paid even when business is slow, the business needs enough clients to cover them (the break-even point).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Variable cost — VARIABLE costs change with the level of activity (e.g. gloves, dressings).
• Revenue — REVENUE is income, not a cost.
• Dividend — A DIVIDEND is profit paid to shareholders, not a cost of running the clinic.

⭐ Key point: Total cost = fixed costs + variable costs. Keeping fixed costs low reduces the risk of a new business.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q95$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2741, $s$Disposable gloves and dressings, whose use rises with the number of clients seen, are examples of:$s$, $s$["Variable costs", "Fixed costs", "Owner's equity", "Capital assets"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Variable costs
VARIABLE COSTS RISE and FALL in direct proportion to the level of activity. Each client seen uses DISPOSABLE GLOVES, DRESSINGS, syringes, transport fuel and other consumables, so the more clients seen, the higher these costs; if no clients are seen, these costs are almost zero. Knowing the variable cost per client helps set prices: the price must be higher than the variable cost so that each visit contributes to fixed costs and profit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fixed costs — FIXED costs stay the same regardless of activity (e.g. rent).
• Owner's equity — OWNER'S EQUITY is the owner's investment in the business, not a cost.
• Capital assets — CAPITAL ASSETS are long-term items like equipment and vehicles, not consumables used up per client.

⭐ Key point: Contribution per client = price − variable cost per client. This contribution pays fixed costs first, then becomes profit.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q96$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2742, $s$The break-even point is the level of sales at which:$s$, $s$["Total revenue equals total costs, so there is neither profit nor loss", "Tax becomes payable", "The business makes its maximum profit", "The business must close down"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Total revenue equals total costs, so there is neither profit nor loss
The BREAK-EVEN POINT is the level of sales at which TOTAL REVENUE EQUALS TOTAL COSTS, so there is NEITHER PROFIT NOR LOSS. Sales above this point make a profit; below it, a loss.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tax becomes payable — Tax relates to profits.
• The business makes its maximum profit — Maximum profit is not the break-even point.
• The business must close down — Break-even doesn't mean closure.

⭐ Key point: Break-even point (units) = Fixed costs ÷ (Price per unit − Variable cost per unit).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q97$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2743, $s$A home-care nurse has fixed costs of ₦60,000 a month. Each visit costs ₦2,000 in supplies and transport, and she charges ₦5,000 per visit. How many visits a month does she need to break even?$s$, $s$["12 visits", "40 visits", "30 visits", "20 visits"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 20 visits
BREAK-EVEN = FIXED COSTS ÷ (PRICE per visit − VARIABLE COST per visit). Here: ₦60,000 ÷ (₦5,000 − ₦2,000) = ₦60,000 ÷ ₦3,000 = 20 VISITS per month. Each visit contributes ₦3,000 towards fixed costs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 12 visits — 12 visits would give ₦36,000 contribution — not enough.
• 40 visits — 40 visits would make a profit of ₦60,000.
• 30 visits — 30 visits would make a profit of ₦30,000.

⭐ Key point: The "contribution margin" (price − variable cost) shows how much each sale contributes to fixed costs and profit.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q98$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2744, $s$The '4 Ps' of the marketing mix are:$s$, $s$["Purpose, Process, Product, Profit", "Plan, People, Profit, Policy", "Patients, Providers, Payment, Premises", "Product, Price, Place, Promotion"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Product, Price, Place, Promotion
The MARKETING MIX, or "4 Ps", are the four key elements a business controls to attract clients: PRODUCT — the service or goods offered (e.g. home nursing, wound care, health checks), including quality and features; PRICE — what clients pay, considering costs, competitors and what clients can afford; PLACE — where and how the service is delivered (clinic location, home visits, opening hours, accessibility); PROMOTION — how clients learn about the service (referrals from doctors, social media, flyers, health talks, word of mouth). Getting the right mix helps the business reach and satisfy its target clients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Purpose, Process, Product, Profit — "Purpose, Process, Product, Profit" is not the marketing mix.
• Plan, People, Profit, Policy — "Plan, People, Profit, Policy" is not the marketing mix.
• Patients, Providers, Payment, Premises — "Patients, Providers, Payment, Premises" is not the marketing mix.

⭐ Key point: For service businesses like nursing, the "7 Ps" add PEOPLE (staff skills and attitude), PROCESS (how the service is delivered) and PHYSICAL EVIDENCE (cleanliness, uniforms, documents).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q99$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2745, $s$Keeping accurate records of all income and expenses in a small nursing business is important mainly to:$s$, $s$["Know the financial position, guide decisions and meet tax and legal obligations", "Avoid paying staff", "Impress clients", "Replace the business plan"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Know the financial position, guide decisions and meet tax and legal obligations
ACCURATE RECORDS of all INCOME and EXPENSES (cash book, receipts, invoices, bank statements) allow the nurse entrepreneur to: know the FINANCIAL POSITION (Is the business making a profit or loss? How much cash is available?); GUIDE DECISIONS (pricing, controlling costs, planning expansion or buying equipment); MEET TAX and LEGAL OBLIGATIONS (filing tax returns, audits, regulatory requirements); and support applications for loans or grants. Without good records, a business can fail without the owner realising there is a problem.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Avoid paying staff — Records are not kept to avoid paying staff; staff must be paid fairly and on time.
• Impress clients — Impressing clients is not the main purpose of financial records.
• Replace the business plan — Records support the business plan by showing actual performance; they do not replace it.

⭐ Key point: Keep business money separate from personal money (separate bank account), record every transaction promptly, and review accounts monthly.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q102$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2746, $s$An entrepreneur's ability to recognise an unmet need in the community that could become a business is called:$s$, $s$["Opportunity recognition", "Bureaucracy", "Stagnation", "Risk aversion"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Opportunity recognition
OPPORTUNITY RECOGNITION is the entrepreneur's ability to NOTICE an UNMET NEED or problem in the community and see that it could become a viable business. Nurses are well placed to recognise opportunities because they see gaps in care every day — e.g. no home-care services for elderly people after discharge, lack of wound-care clinics, poor access to health education, or the need for trained caregivers. Recognising the opportunity is the first step in the entrepreneurial process.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bureaucracy — BUREAUCRACY refers to rigid rules and procedures in organisations.
• Stagnation — STAGNATION means lack of growth or development.
• Risk aversion — RISK AVERSION means avoiding risk, which tends to prevent entrepreneurship.

⭐ Key point: Opportunity recognition is followed by evaluating the opportunity (feasibility), then organising resources to exploit it.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q103$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2747, $s$Social entrepreneurship differs from purely commercial entrepreneurship mainly because its main goal is to:$s$, $s$["Maximise profit for the owner only", "Avoid paying taxes", "Compete only with government hospitals", "Solve a social or health problem, with any profit reinvested in the mission"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Solve a social or health problem, with any profit reinvested in the mission
SOCIAL ENTREPRENEURSHIP uses business methods to SOLVE a SOCIAL or HEALTH PROBLEM (e.g. maternal deaths in rural areas, lack of affordable care for people with disabilities, poor sanitation). Its main goal is SOCIAL IMPACT rather than personal wealth. Any PROFIT is mainly REINVESTED in the MISSION (to expand or improve services). Commercial entrepreneurship, in contrast, mainly aims to make profit for the owners.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Maximise profit for the owner only — Maximising profit for the owner only is the goal of purely commercial entrepreneurship.
• Avoid paying taxes — Avoiding taxes is not the purpose; social enterprises must still follow tax laws (though some may qualify for exemptions as registered non-profits).
• Compete only with government hospitals — Competing only with government hospitals is not the definition.

⭐ Key point: Examples of nurse social entrepreneurship: community health insurance schemes, mobile clinics, affordable home-care for the elderly, health education programmes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q104$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2748, $s$The following are qualities of a good leader EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Pride", "Vision", "Integrity", "Knowledge"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pride
Good leaders have qualities that inspire trust and help teams achieve goals: VISION (a clear picture of the future and direction), INTEGRITY (honesty, consistency and strong ethics), KNOWLEDGE and competence, good communication, empathy, decisiveness, fairness, humility and the ability to motivate others. PRIDE (arrogance, excessive self-importance) is NOT a quality of a good leader — a proud leader may refuse feedback, take credit for others' work, refuse to admit mistakes and damage teamwork and morale.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Vision — Vision is a key leadership quality.
• Integrity — Integrity is a key leadership quality.
• Knowledge — Knowledge is a key leadership quality.

⭐ Key point: Effective ICU leaders combine clinical expertise with emotional intelligence, listen to staff, and model the behaviour they expect.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q106 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2749, $s$"The organisation is a complex whole with parts working together like the human body." Which theory fits this statement?$s$, $s$["Human relations theory", "Classical theory", "Humanist theory", "Systems theory"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Systems theory
SYSTEMS THEORY (from Ludwig von Bertalanffy) views an ORGANISATION as a COMPLEX WHOLE made up of interrelated PARTS (subsystems — e.g. departments, units, staff) that WORK TOGETHER to achieve common goals, LIKE THE HUMAN BODY, where organs work together. A change in one part affects the whole system. Organisations take in INPUTS (staff, money, patients, equipment), process them (THROUGHPUT — care delivery), produce OUTPUTS (recovered patients, services) and use FEEDBACK to adjust.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Human relations theory — Human relations theory (Elton Mayo, Hawthorne studies) focuses on social relationships, morale and the human side of work.
• Classical theory — Classical theory (Taylor, Fayol, Weber) focuses on structure, efficiency, rules and hierarchy.
• Humanist theory — Humanist theory (e.g. Maslow, McGregor) focuses on human needs, motivation and potential.

⭐ Key point: Systems thinking in ICU: a staffing problem (input) affects care quality (throughput) and patient outcomes (output).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q107$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2750, $s$A leadership style that allows the opinion of the majority of group members is:$s$, $s$["Democratic", "Bureaucratic", "Autocratic", "Laissez-faire"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Democratic
DEMOCRATIC (PARTICIPATIVE) LEADERSHIP involves team members in DECISION-MAKING. The leader shares information, invites ideas and discussion, and decisions often reflect the MAJORITY OPINION of the group, although the leader remains responsible. It improves staff motivation, creativity, commitment and job satisfaction. It works well for planning, policy development and routine unit management, but can be slow in emergencies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bureaucratic — BUREAUCRATIC leadership relies strictly on rules, procedures and hierarchy.
• Autocratic — AUTOCRATIC leadership makes decisions alone without consulting the team; useful in emergencies (e.g. cardiac arrest).
• Laissez-faire — LAISSEZ-FAIRE leadership gives little or no direction, leaving the team to make decisions on their own.

⭐ Key point: Good leaders adapt their style to the situation (situational leadership): autocratic in emergencies, democratic for routine decisions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q109$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2751, $s$The unwritten personal expectations an employee brings to an organisation are called the:$s$, $s$["Official contract", "Job description", "Psychological contract", "Personal ambition"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Psychological contract
The PSYCHOLOGICAL CONTRACT is the set of UNWRITTEN, informal beliefs and EXPECTATIONS between an employee and the organisation about what each will give and receive. Examples: the employee expects fair treatment, respect, support, training, career development and job security; the organisation expects loyalty, effort and commitment. When these unwritten expectations are not met ("breach of the psychological contract"), staff may lose trust, become demotivated, or leave.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Official contract — The official (employment) contract is a WRITTEN legal document with terms such as salary and hours.
• Job description — The job description is a WRITTEN document listing duties and responsibilities.
• Personal ambition — Personal ambition refers to an individual's career goals, not mutual expectations.

⭐ Key point: Managers can protect the psychological contract by being fair, keeping promises, communicating openly and supporting staff development.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q110$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2752, $s$The purchase of a new ventilator should be included in the:$s$, $s$["Operating budget", "Salary budget", "Capital budget", "Revenue budget"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Capital budget
A CAPITAL BUDGET plans for the purchase of MAJOR, EXPENSIVE, LONG-LASTING items (usually lasting more than one year and costing above a set amount) — such as a NEW VENTILATOR, monitors, dialysis machines, beds, vehicles, or building renovation. Because these purchases are large, they are planned separately, often years ahead, with justification of need and cost.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Operating budget — The OPERATING budget covers day-to-day running expenses (consumables, drugs, utilities, maintenance).
• Salary budget — The SALARY (personnel) budget covers staff wages, allowances and overtime — usually the largest part of the operating budget.
• Revenue budget — The REVENUE budget estimates expected income (e.g. fees, government funding).

⭐ Key point: The ICU manager justifies capital requests with data (e.g. occupancy, equipment age, breakdown rates, patient needs).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q111$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2753, $s$Frederick Winslow Taylor's mechanism of scientific management includes:$s$, $s$["Scientific task setting", "Planning the task", "Standardisation of tools and equipment", "All of the above"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: All of the above
Frederick Winslow TAYLOR, the "father of SCIENTIFIC MANAGEMENT", used scientific methods to find the most efficient way to do each job. His mechanisms included: SCIENTIFIC TASK SETTING (determining a fair day's work through time and motion studies), PLANNING THE TASK (separating planning from doing — managers plan, workers execute), STANDARDISATION OF TOOLS AND EQUIPMENT (and working conditions and methods), scientific selection and training of workers, differential piece-rate pay, and functional foremanship. So "ALL OF THE ABOVE" is correct.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Scientific task setting — Scientific task setting is only one of Taylor's mechanisms.
• Planning the task — Planning the task is only one of Taylor's mechanisms.
• Standardisation of tools and equipment — Standardisation is only one of Taylor's mechanisms.

⭐ Key point: In nursing, standard procedures, protocols and time studies for workload reflect Taylor's ideas, but too much focus on tasks can ignore human needs.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q116$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2754, $s$Henri Fayol was a:$s$, $s$["Social scientist", "Accountant", "Mining engineer", "Production engineer"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mining engineer
Henri FAYOL (1841–1925) was a French MINING ENGINEER and later managing director of a large mining company. From his experience, he developed the classical "ADMINISTRATIVE MANAGEMENT" theory, describing the FIVE FUNCTIONS of management (planning, organising, commanding, coordinating and controlling — often summarised today as POSDCORB or planning, organising, leading, controlling) and the 14 PRINCIPLES OF MANAGEMENT (e.g. division of work, authority, discipline, unity of command, unity of direction, scalar chain, order, equity, esprit de corps).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Social scientist — He was not a social scientist (e.g. Elton Mayo studied human relations).
• Accountant — He was not an accountant.
• Production engineer — He was not a production engineer (Taylor was associated with production engineering).

⭐ Key point: Fayol is called the "father of modern management".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q117$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2755, $s$What is the full form of MBO?$s$, $s$["Method By Objective", "Method By opportunity", "Management By Objectives", "Management By Opportunity"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Management By Objectives
MBO stands for MANAGEMENT BY OBJECTIVES, introduced by Peter DRUCKER (1954). In MBO, managers and staff TOGETHER agree on clear, measurable OBJECTIVES (goals) for a period, plan how to achieve them, MONITOR progress, and EVALUATE performance against the agreed objectives. It improves motivation, clarity of roles, and links individual goals to organisational goals.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Method By Objective — "Method By Objective" is not the correct expansion.
• Method By opportunity — "Method By Opportunity" is not the correct expansion.
• Management By Opportunity — "Management By Opportunity" is not the correct expansion.

⭐ Key point: MBO objectives should be SMART: Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Relevant and Time-bound. Example: "Reduce CLABSI rate by 50% within 6 months."$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q118$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2756, $s$According to Kurt Lewin, which of the following is not a stage in the change process?$s$, $s$["Unfreezing", "Restraining", "Changing", "Refreezing"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Restraining
Kurt LEWIN'S change model has THREE STAGES: (1) UNFREEZING — preparing people for change by explaining why the current practice must change and reducing resistance; (2) CHANGING (MOVING) — introducing the new practice, with training and support; (3) REFREEZING — stabilising the change so it becomes the new normal (through policies, reinforcement, monitoring). "RESTRAINING" is NOT a stage. Restraining forces (forces resisting change, e.g. fear, habit, lack of resources) are part of Lewin's FORCE-FIELD ANALYSIS, along with driving forces (forces pushing for change).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Unfreezing — Unfreezing is a stage.
• Changing — Changing is a stage.
• Refreezing — Refreezing is a stage.

⭐ Key point: To make change succeed, strengthen driving forces and reduce restraining forces.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q120$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2757, $s$A nurse is caring for four clients simultaneously. Which client's need should the nurse prioritize FIRST according to Maslow's hierarchy of needs?$s$, $s$["A client expressing embarrassment about needing help with bathing", "A client requesting a chaplain visit for spiritual support", "A client worried about being discharged without family support", "A client with an oxygen saturation of 84% on room air"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A client with an oxygen saturation of 84% on room air
According to MASLOW, PHYSIOLOGICAL needs come FIRST, and OXYGEN is the most urgent of all physiological needs — without it, brain damage and death occur within minutes. A client with an OXYGEN SATURATION of 84% on room air is HYPOXIC (normal is about 95–100%), so this need must be addressed FIRST (e.g. position upright, give oxygen, assess the airway and breathing, call for help). The other clients' needs are important but not immediately life-threatening.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A client expressing embarrassment about needing help with bathing — Embarrassment about bathing relates to ESTEEM needs (higher level).
• A client requesting a chaplain visit for spiritual support — A chaplain visit relates to spiritual/self-actualisation needs (higher level).
• A client worried about being discharged without family support — Worry about discharge without family support relates to SAFETY and LOVE/BELONGING needs (higher level).

⭐ Key point: Use the ABC (Airway, Breathing, Circulation) approach together with Maslow to prioritise care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q138$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2758, $s$A nurse delegates the task of ambulating a stable postoperative client to a nursing assistant. Which principle of delegation is MOST important for the registered nurse to apply?$s$, $s$["Delegating without providing any instructions", "Avoiding all delegation regardless of task complexity", "Ensuring the task is within the delegatee's scope of practice/competency and does not require nursing judgment", "Delegating tasks that require independent nursing judgment"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ensuring the task is within the delegatee's scope of practice/competency and does not require nursing judgment
When delegating, the registered nurse must ensure the task is WITHIN the delegatee's SCOPE OF PRACTICE and COMPETENCY, and does NOT REQUIRE NURSING JUDGEMENT. Ambulating a stable post-operative client is suitable for a nursing assistant. The nurse remains accountable and must give clear instructions and supervise.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delegating without providing any instructions — Delegating without instructions is unsafe.
• Avoiding all delegation regardless of task complexity — Avoiding all delegation is inefficient.
• Delegating tasks that require independent nursing judgment — Tasks requiring nursing judgement (assessment, evaluation, teaching) must not be delegated to unlicensed staff.

⭐ Key point: Use the five rights of delegation: right task, circumstance, person, direction, supervision.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q160$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2759, $s$Which of the following is NOT one of the 'five rights' of delegation?$s$, $s$["The right task", "The right person", "The right supervision", "The right cost"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The right cost
The FIVE RIGHTS OF DELEGATION help nurses delegate tasks safely: (1) RIGHT TASK — can the task be delegated?; (2) RIGHT CIRCUMSTANCE — is the patient stable and the setting appropriate?; (3) RIGHT PERSON — does the person have the skills and competence?; (4) RIGHT DIRECTION/COMMUNICATION — clear instructions; (5) RIGHT SUPERVISION/EVALUATION — monitoring and feedback. "RIGHT COST" is NOT one of the five rights.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The right task — Right task is one of the five rights.
• The right person — Right person is one of the five rights.
• The right supervision — Right supervision is one of the five rights.

⭐ Key point: The registered nurse REMAINS ACCOUNTABLE for the delegated task. Do not delegate assessment, care planning, evaluation, or tasks requiring nursing judgement to unlicensed staff.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q199$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2760, $s$The ICU manager knows that some of her staffs prefers to be directed rather than to be allowed to use initiative or to exercise responsibility. This statement implies:$s$, $s$["Theory x", "Theory A", "Theory b", "Theory y"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Theory x
Douglas McGREGOR'S THEORY X assumes that workers DISLIKE work, AVOID RESPONSIBILITY, lack ambition and PREFER TO BE DIRECTED. Managers holding this view use close supervision, control and rules. The statement that staff "prefer to be directed rather than use initiative or exercise responsibility" matches Theory X exactly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Theory A — "Theory A" is not part of McGregor's model.
• Theory b — "Theory B" is not part of McGregor's model.
• Theory y — Theory Y assumes staff enjoy work, seek responsibility and are self-directed — the opposite of the statement.

⭐ Key point: Some staff may need more direction (e.g. new nurses), so effective managers adapt their style to staff needs (situational leadership).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q162$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2761, $s$An ICU team designs a new sedation-weaning protocol, trials it on one shift for a week, then reviews compliance and outcome data before deciding whether to adopt it unit-wide. This is an example of:$s$, $s$["A retrospective nursing audit", "A root cause analysis of an incident", "A PDSA quality improvement cycle", "A risk financing strategy"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A PDSA quality improvement cycle
A PDSA cycle (PLAN–DO–STUDY–ACT) is a quality improvement method for testing a change on a small scale before wider use. Here: PLAN — design the sedation-weaning protocol; DO — trial it on one shift for a week; STUDY — review compliance and outcome data; ACT — decide whether to adopt, adapt or abandon it unit-wide. This exactly matches the scenario.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A retrospective nursing audit — A retrospective audit looks back at past practice against standards; it does not test a new change.
• A root cause analysis of an incident — Root cause analysis investigates WHY an incident happened; it does not test a new protocol.
• A risk financing strategy — Risk financing deals with funding the costs of risks (e.g. insurance), not testing practice changes.

⭐ Key point: PDSA cycles are small, rapid and repeated — each cycle builds on what was learned from the last.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q163$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2762, $s$The most appropriate use of an ICU admission policy is to:$s$, $s$["Admit only post-operative surgical patients", "Keep some beds empty for private patients", "Admit patients strictly in order of arrival", "Give beds to patients most likely to benefit"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Give beds to patients most likely to benefit
An ICU ADMISSION POLICY helps make fair, consistent decisions about who should receive scarce ICU beds. Its main purpose is to give beds to patients MOST LIKELY TO BENEFIT — those who are critically ill with a reversible condition, rather than those who are too well (could be managed on the ward) or too sick to benefit.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Admit only post-operative surgical patients — ICUs serve all types of critically ill patients, not just surgical ones.
• Keep some beds empty for private patients — Keeping beds empty for private patients is unjust.
• Admit patients strictly in order of arrival — Admission strictly by arrival order ignores clinical need and benefit.

⭐ Key point: Admission decisions should be made by senior clinicians and documented.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q168$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2763, $s$Burnout among ICU nurses is best reduced by:$s$, $s$["Removing teaching sessions to reduce workload", "Ignoring signs of distress to keep morale up", "Adequate staffing, breaks, debriefing and support", "Longer shifts with fewer days off each month"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Adequate staffing, breaks, debriefing and support
BURNOUT (emotional exhaustion, cynicism, reduced sense of achievement) is common in ICU nurses because of high stress and moral distress. It is reduced by: ADEQUATE STAFFING, regular BREAKS, DEBRIEFING after difficult events, and SUPPORT (counselling, supervision, recognition).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Removing teaching sessions to reduce workload — Removing teaching reduces development and doesn't reduce burnout.
• Ignoring signs of distress to keep morale up — Ignoring distress worsens burnout.
• Longer shifts with fewer days off each month — Longer shifts and fewer days off increase burnout.

⭐ Key point: Burnout affects patient safety; it's a management responsibility.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q177$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2764, $s$The ICU budget is mainly used to plan for:$s$, $s$["Only the salaries of the ICU nursing staff", "Only food and catering for the patients", "Only buying new buildings and furniture", "Staffing, consumables, drugs and equipment upkeep"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Staffing, consumables, drugs and equipment upkeep
The ICU budget plans for the main costs of running the unit: STAFFING (the largest cost), CONSUMABLES (e.g. syringes, catheters, dressings), DRUGS, and EQUIPMENT UPKEEP (maintenance and replacement).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only the salaries of the ICU nursing staff — The budget covers more than salaries.
• Only food and catering for the patients — Catering isn't the main ICU budget item.
• Only buying new buildings and furniture — Buildings and furniture are capital costs, not the main operating budget.

⭐ Key point: Good budgeting helps maintain safe staffing and equipment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q178$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2765, $s$The 'politics of health care' in ICU management refers to:$s$, $s$["Elections held to choose the ward manager", "Nurses joining and campaigning for political parties", "How power, resources and decisions are negotiated", "Government salary scales for health workers only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: How power, resources and decisions are negotiated
The POLITICS OF HEALTH CARE refers to how POWER, RESOURCES and DECISIONS are NEGOTIATED among different groups (government, hospital management, doctors, nurses, patients, unions, funders). In ICU management, this includes negotiating for budget, staff, equipment, beds and policies, and influencing decisions that affect patient care. The nurse manager needs political skills: building relationships, using evidence, negotiating, and advocating for patients and staff.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Elections held to choose the ward manager — It does not refer to elections for the ward manager position.
• Nurses joining and campaigning for political parties — It is not about nurses joining or campaigning for political parties.
• Government salary scales for health workers only — It is broader than government salary scales; it covers how all resources and decisions are negotiated.

⭐ Key point: Effective nurse leaders understand organisational politics to gain resources for safe patient care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q199$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2766, $s$The 'safe triangle' for chest drain insertion is bounded by:$s$, $s$["Pectoralis major, latissimus dorsi and the 5th intercostal space", "Scapula, vertebral column and the 12th rib at the back", "Sternum, clavicle and the 2nd rib at the front of the chest", "Umbilicus, iliac crest and the costal margin below"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pectoralis major, latissimus dorsi and the 5th intercostal space
The SAFE TRIANGLE for chest drain insertion is bounded by the lateral edge of PECTORALIS MAJOR (in front), the lateral edge of LATISSIMUS DORSI (behind), and a line at the 5TH INTERCOSTAL SPACE (nipple level) below, with the apex at the armpit. This avoids major vessels, the heart, and the liver and spleen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Scapula, vertebral column and the 12th rib at the back — The scapula and vertebral column area is at the back and isn't the safe triangle.
• Sternum, clavicle and the 2nd rib at the front of the chest — The sternum, clavicle and 2nd rib area risks the internal mammary vessels and great vessels.
• Umbilicus, iliac crest and the costal margin below — The umbilicus and iliac crest are in the abdomen.

⭐ Key point: Insert the drain over the top of the rib (the intercostal vessels and nerve run along the bottom of each rib).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q6$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2767, $s$The MOST appropriate leadership approach during a sudden cardiac arrest in the ICU is:$s$, $s$["Democratic leadership by voting on interventions", "Autocratic leadership with clear, direct instructions", "Laissez-faire leadership", "Bureaucratic leadership without communication"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Autocratic leadership with clear, direct instructions
A cardiac arrest is an emergency where every second matters. The team needs ONE CLEAR LEADER who gives DIRECT, SPECIFIC INSTRUCTIONS ("You do compressions", "Give adrenaline 1 mg now"). This is AUTOCRATIC (directive) leadership, which is the most suitable style in a crisis because it reduces confusion and delay. Good arrest leaders still use closed-loop communication and listen to team members' input.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Democratic leadership by voting on interventions — Voting wastes precious time; decisions must be made quickly.
• Laissez-faire leadership — Laissez-faire (hands-off) leadership in an arrest would cause chaos.
• Bureaucratic leadership without communication — Bureaucratic leadership without communication would cause delays and confusion.

⭐ Key point: Democratic or transformational leadership is better for routine unit management, but autocratic leadership is best in emergencies.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q161$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2768, $s$A newly employed ICU nurse is unable to operate a mechanical ventilator safely. The nurse manager should FIRST:$s$, $s$["Assign the nurse to independent ventilator care", "Arrange supervised training and assess competency", "Issue a written warning", "Transfer the nurse to another hospital"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Arrange supervised training and assess competency
The nurse is not yet competent with the ventilator, so it is unsafe for them to care for ventilated patients alone. The manager should FIRST arrange SUPERVISED TRAINING (by a senior nurse or clinical educator) and then ASSESS COMPETENCY before allowing independent practice. This protects patients and supports the nurse's development.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assign the nurse to independent ventilator care — Assigning independent ventilator care to an untrained nurse puts patients at serious risk.
• Issue a written warning — A written warning is a disciplinary step; a knowledge gap in a new nurse needs training, not punishment.
• Transfer the nurse to another hospital — Transfer avoids the problem and wastes the chance to train the nurse.

⭐ Key point: ICU orientation should include competency checklists, preceptorship and a gradual increase in patient complexity.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q162$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2769, $s$The ICU manager notices an increase in medication errors during the night shift. The priority action is to:$s$, $s$["Investigate root causes before corrective measures", "Report all of the nurses to the Nursing Council", "Suspend all of the night-duty nurses at once", "Reduce the number of patients admitted at night"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Investigate root causes before corrective measures
When errors increase, the manager must first find out WHY. A ROOT CAUSE ANALYSIS looks for system problems behind the errors: low staffing, fatigue, poor lighting, similar drug packaging, interruptions, lack of double-checking, or poor training. Only after understanding the causes can effective CORRECTIVE MEASURES be planned. This supports a "just culture" where staff report errors without fear.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Report all of the nurses to the Nursing Council — Reporting everyone to the Council without investigation is unfair and does not fix the cause.
• Suspend all of the night-duty nurses at once — Suspending all night nurses punishes staff without evidence and leaves the unit unsafe.
• Reduce the number of patients admitted at night — Reducing admissions may not address the real cause and could harm patients who need ICU.

⭐ Key point: Most errors come from system failures, not just individual carelessness.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q163$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2770, $s$Theory Y managers believe that staff:$s$, $s$["Enjoy work, seek responsibility and self-direct", "Work only for money and fear punishment", "Cannot be trusted to make any decisions", "Dislike work and need close supervision"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Enjoy work, seek responsibility and self-direct
McGregor's THEORY Y assumes that staff naturally ENJOY WORK, SEEK RESPONSIBILITY, are creative and can SELF-DIRECT when committed to goals. Theory Y managers delegate, involve staff in decisions and encourage growth.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Work only for money and fear punishment — Working only for money and fearing punishment describes THEORY X.
• Cannot be trusted to make any decisions — Not trusting staff to make decisions is a Theory X belief.
• Dislike work and need close supervision — Disliking work and needing close supervision is Theory X.

⭐ Key point: Theory X = staff are lazy and need control. Theory Y = staff are motivated and responsible. Most modern ICU management uses Theory Y ideas.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q164$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2771, $s$Herzberg's motivation-hygiene theory states that salary and working conditions are:$s$, $s$["Factors that directly increase achievement and growth", "Completely irrelevant to job satisfaction in nursing", "Hygiene factors: they prevent dissatisfaction but do not motivate", "The main motivators that drive nurses to perform better"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hygiene factors: they prevent dissatisfaction but do not motivate
Herzberg's theory divides work factors into two groups. HYGIENE FACTORS (salary, working conditions, job security, policies, supervision, relationships): when poor, they cause DISSATISFACTION; when good, they PREVENT dissatisfaction, but do NOT truly motivate. MOTIVATORS (achievement, recognition, responsibility, advancement, growth, the work itself) truly increase motivation and satisfaction.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Factors that directly increase achievement and growth — Achievement and growth are motivators; salary and conditions are hygiene factors.
• Completely irrelevant to job satisfaction in nursing — Hygiene factors are not irrelevant; if they are poor, staff become dissatisfied.
• The main motivators that drive nurses to perform better — In Herzberg's theory, salary is NOT the main motivator; motivation comes from recognition, responsibility and achievement.

⭐ Key point: To motivate ICU nurses: fair pay and good conditions (to prevent dissatisfaction) PLUS recognition, responsibility and career development (to motivate).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q165$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2772, $s$Transformational leadership in ICU is characterised by:$s$, $s$["Leaving staff to make all decisions on their own", "Focusing only on tasks, targets and rewards", "Strict rules with punishment for every mistake", "Inspiring a shared vision and encouraging growth"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Inspiring a shared vision and encouraging growth
TRANSFORMATIONAL LEADERS INSPIRE staff with a SHARED VISION, act as role models, encourage innovation, support professional GROWTH and give individual attention. This style improves staff satisfaction, retention and patient outcomes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Leaving staff to make all decisions on their own — Leaving staff to decide everything alone is laissez-faire leadership.
• Focusing only on tasks, targets and rewards — Focusing only on tasks, targets and rewards is transactional leadership.
• Strict rules with punishment for every mistake — Strict rules with punishment describes an autocratic, punitive style.

⭐ Key point: Transformational leadership is linked to "Magnet" hospitals and a positive safety culture.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q166$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2773, $s$When ICU beds are full and a critically ill patient needs admission, the manager should:$s$, $s$["Refuse all communication until a bed becomes free", "Review patients for step-down and agree triage with the team", "Discharge the sickest patient to make room", "Admit the patient to a corridor without monitoring"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Review patients for step-down and agree triage with the team
When ICU is full, the manager should work with the team to find a SAFE SOLUTION: REVIEW current ICU patients to identify those stable enough to STEP DOWN to a high-dependency unit or ward, and agree on TRIAGE with the senior doctors (who needs ICU most). If no bed can be made, follow the escalation policy: transfer to another ICU, or provide temporary critical care outreach.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Refuse all communication until a bed becomes free — Refusing communication leaves a critically ill patient without care.
• Discharge the sickest patient to make room — Discharging the sickest patient is dangerous; it is the most stable patient who should step down.
• Admit the patient to a corridor without monitoring — Placing a critically ill patient in a corridor without monitoring is unsafe.

⭐ Key point: Escalation policies, bed management meetings and outreach teams help manage ICU capacity.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q170$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2774, $s$Conflict between ICU nurses and doctors is best managed by:$s$, $s$["Writing formal complaints without any discussion", "Avoiding each other for the rest of the shift", "Shouting at each other in front of the patients", "Open discussion focused on patient goals, with respect"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Open discussion focused on patient goals, with respect
Conflict between nurses and doctors is best managed by OPEN, RESPECTFUL DISCUSSION FOCUSED ON PATIENT GOALS. This collaborative approach finds the best solution for the patient and improves teamwork. If needed, involve senior staff or use a structured approach (e.g. SBAR).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Writing formal complaints without any discussion — Formal complaints without discussion escalate the conflict without trying to resolve it.
• Avoiding each other for the rest of the shift — Avoidance leaves the conflict unresolved and may harm patient care.
• Shouting at each other in front of the patients — Shouting in front of patients is unprofessional and distressing.

⭐ Key point: Collaboration ("win-win") is the best long-term conflict resolution strategy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q177$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2775, $s$The ICU manager's report on monthly activity should include:$s$, $s$["Only the names of the patients admitted", "Only the birthdays of the staff that month", "Only the number of beds in the unit", "Admissions, outcomes, incidents, staffing, resources"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Admissions, outcomes, incidents, staffing, resources
A MONTHLY ACTIVITY REPORT gives a full picture of the unit's work: ADMISSIONS (numbers and types of patients), OUTCOMES (discharges, deaths), INCIDENTS (errors, falls, infections), STAFFING levels, and RESOURCES (equipment, supplies, budget). This information guides planning and budgeting.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only the names of the patients admitted — Patients' names alone give no useful management information and risk confidentiality.
• Only the birthdays of the staff that month — Staff birthdays are not relevant to unit management.
• Only the number of beds in the unit — The number of beds alone is insufficient.

⭐ Key point: Reports inform decisions about staffing, equipment purchase and quality improvement.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 10 · Q179$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2776, $s$ICU organisation in the curriculum includes the role of the nurse in:$s$, $s$["Hospital finance, billing and insurance claims only", "Surgery and anaesthesia in the main theatre only", "Unit organisation, staffing, equipment and quality", "Pharmacy dispensing and stock control only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Unit organisation, staffing, equipment and quality
The CCN curriculum includes ICU organisation and management: how the unit is ORGANISED (design, levels of care), STAFFING (numbers, skill mix, rosters), EQUIPMENT (buying, maintaining, checking), and QUALITY (audit, standards, safety). The ICU nurse manager coordinates people, resources and standards to provide safe care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Hospital finance, billing and insurance claims only — Hospital finance, billing and insurance are mainly administration's job, not the core ICU nurse role.
• Surgery and anaesthesia in the main theatre only — Surgery and anaesthesia in theatre are other specialities.
• Pharmacy dispensing and stock control only — Pharmacy dispensing and stock control are the pharmacist's job.

⭐ Key point: ICU management covers planning, organising, staffing, directing (leading) and controlling (quality and audit).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q161$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2777, $s$An ICU duty roster should be planned:$s$, $s$["In advance, balancing skill mix, leave, continuity", "Without considering skill mix, using staff names only", "Daily at the start of each shift by whoever is in charge", "By the patients' relatives at the weekly family meeting"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: In advance, balancing skill mix, leave, continuity
A good ICU roster is planned IN ADVANCE (usually 4–6 weeks), and balances: SKILL MIX (experienced and junior nurses on every shift), annual LEAVE and study days, CONTINUITY of care (the same nurses for the same patients where possible), and fairness (equal distribution of nights and weekends). Good rosters reduce stress, sickness and absenteeism.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Without considering skill mix, using staff names only — Ignoring skill mix could leave a shift with only junior staff, which is unsafe.
• Daily at the start of each shift by whoever is in charge — Daily planning at the start of each shift is too late and causes chaos.
• By the patients' relatives at the weekly family meeting — Relatives are not involved in planning staff rosters.

⭐ Key point: Every ICU shift should have an experienced ICU-trained nurse in charge.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q163$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2778, $s$Span of control in ICU management refers to:$s$, $s$["Staff one leader can supervise effectively", "The number of ventilators in the unit", "The total budget of the ICU each year", "The size of the ICU in number of beds"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Staff one leader can supervise effectively
SPAN OF CONTROL is the NUMBER OF STAFF one manager or leader can effectively supervise. A narrow span (few staff per leader) allows closer supervision and support, which is often needed in ICU, where care is complex. A wide span (many staff per leader) suits routine, experienced teams.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The number of ventilators in the unit — The number of ventilators is equipment, not span of control.
• The total budget of the ICU each year — The budget is a financial measure.
• The size of the ICU in number of beds — The number of beds is unit size, not span of control.

⭐ Key point: A related principle is unity of command: each staff member should report to one boss.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q164$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2779, $s$Decision-making in ICU management follows the steps:$s$, $s$["Decide first, and then look for the problem", "Ask only the patient's relatives to decide", "Identify, gather data, weigh options, decide, evaluate", "Choose an option at random and hope"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Identify, gather data, weigh options, decide, evaluate
Good decision-making follows logical steps. 1) IDENTIFY the problem clearly. 2) GATHER information and data. 3) Generate and WEIGH the options (pros and cons). 4) DECIDE (choose the best option). 5) Implement it. 6) EVALUATE the outcome and learn from it.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Decide first, and then look for the problem — Deciding before identifying the problem leads to poor decisions.
• Ask only the patient's relatives to decide — Relatives can contribute to patient care decisions, but they don't make ICU management decisions.
• Choose an option at random and hope — Random choice is not systematic decision-making.

⭐ Key point: The decision-making process is similar to the nursing process (assess, diagnose, plan, implement, evaluate).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q165$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2780, $s$Delegation of a task to a junior nurse requires:$s$, $s$["Complete transfer of all accountability", "Right task, person, communication and supervision", "No explanation to the person delegated", "Only the absence of the senior nurse"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Right task, person, communication and supervision
Delegation is giving a task to someone else while still being responsible for the outcome. Safe delegation follows the rights: the RIGHT TASK (suitable to delegate), the RIGHT PERSON (competent and allowed to do it), the RIGHT COMMUNICATION (clear instructions and expected results), the RIGHT SUPERVISION (checking and support), and the right circumstances.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Complete transfer of all accountability — Accountability is NOT fully transferred; the person who delegates remains accountable for the decision to delegate.
• No explanation to the person delegated — Clear explanation is essential for safe delegation.
• Only the absence of the senior nurse — Delegation is not just about absence; it is planned and appropriate.

⭐ Key point: Never delegate tasks that need professional judgement (assessment, care planning, evaluation) to unqualified staff.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q166$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2781, $s$Staff motivation in ICU is improved by:$s$, $s$["Recognition, training and involvement", "Constant criticism of mistakes", "Withholding unit information", "Unequal and unfair workloads"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Recognition, training and involvement
Staff motivation is improved by RECOGNITION (praise, rewards for good work), TRAINING and development (learning new skills, career growth), and INVOLVEMENT in decisions (feeling valued and listened to). Motivated staff are more productive and give better patient care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Constant criticism of mistakes — Constant criticism lowers morale.
• Withholding unit information — Withholding information makes staff feel excluded.
• Unequal and unfair workloads — Unfair workloads cause resentment and burnout.

⭐ Key point: Herzberg's motivators: achievement, recognition, responsibility, the work itself, and growth.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q167$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2782, $s$Accounting and budgeting in ICU management help to:$s$, $s$["Increase waste of disposable items in the unit", "Avoid planning so that money can be used freely", "Hide the true costs from the hospital management", "Plan and control spending on staff, drugs and equipment"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Plan and control spending on staff, drugs and equipment
Accounting and budgeting help the ICU manager to PLAN how much will be spent on staff, drugs, equipment and supplies, and to CONTROL spending by comparing actual costs with the budget (variance analysis). This ensures resources are used efficiently and that money is available for priorities.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase waste of disposable items in the unit — Budgeting REDUCES waste.
• Avoid planning so that money can be used freely — Budgeting IS planning; it doesn't avoid it.
• Hide the true costs from the hospital management — Budgeting makes costs transparent, not hidden.

⭐ Key point: Budget types: operating budget (day-to-day running costs), capital budget (large items like ventilators), and personnel budget (staff costs).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q169$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2783, $s$Clinical governance in ICU includes:$s$, $s$["Audit, risk management, training, patient involvement", "Only the recruitment of new staff", "Only the financial management of the unit", "Only the maintenance of the buildings"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Audit, risk management, training, patient involvement
Clinical governance is the system through which health services make sure they provide high-quality, safe care and stay accountable. It includes clinical AUDIT, RISK MANAGEMENT (incident reporting), staff TRAINING and education, PATIENT INVOLVEMENT, evidence-based practice, and clinical effectiveness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only the recruitment of new staff — Recruitment is only one small part of management, not clinical governance.
• Only the financial management of the unit — Finance is not the focus of clinical governance.
• Only the maintenance of the buildings — Building maintenance is not clinical governance.

⭐ Key point: The pillars of clinical governance: audit, risk management, education and training, evidence-based practice, patient involvement, staff management, and openness.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q174$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2784, $s$Martha Rogers' Science of Unitary Human Beings views the person as:$s$, $s$["A set of separate, independent organ systems", "A passive recipient of nursing care", "An energy field interacting with the environment", "A machine that needs to be repaired"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An energy field interacting with the environment
Martha Rogers' Science of Unitary Human Beings sees each person as a unified ENERGY FIELD, constantly interacting with the ENVIRONMENTAL energy field. The person can't be divided into separate parts; nursing aims to promote harmony between the person and the environment (for example through therapeutic touch).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A set of separate, independent organ systems — Separate organ systems is a medical (biomedical) view, the opposite of Rogers' holistic view.
• A passive recipient of nursing care — Rogers sees the person as an active energy field, not a passive recipient.
• A machine that needs to be repaired — A machine to repair is a mechanistic view, not Rogers'.

⭐ Key point: Rogers' principles (homeodynamics): resonancy, helicy and integrality.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q181$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2785, $s$Gordon's functional health patterns are used mainly for:$s$, $s$["Organising assessment into 11 patterns", "Planning the ICU's annual budget", "Classifying surgical wounds", "Calculating drug doses in children"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Organising assessment into 11 patterns
Marjory Gordon's 11 functional health patterns help nurses ORGANISE ASSESSMENT holistically. The patterns: health perception and management, nutrition and metabolism, elimination, activity and exercise, sleep and rest, cognition and perception, self-perception and self-concept, roles and relationships, sexuality and reproduction, coping and stress tolerance, and values and beliefs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Planning the ICU's annual budget — Budgets aren't related to Gordon's patterns.
• Classifying surgical wounds — Wound classification uses other systems.
• Calculating drug doses in children — Drug calculations aren't part of assessment frameworks.

⭐ Key point: Gordon's patterns are often used in admission forms and link well with NANDA-I nursing diagnoses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q182$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2786, $s$Quality assurance in ICU uses Donabedian's framework of:$s$, $s$["Planning, organising and controlling", "Input, output and feedback only", "Assessment, diagnosis and planning", "Structure, process and outcome"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Structure, process and outcome
Avedis Donabedian's model judges quality in three parts. STRUCTURE: the resources (staff, equipment, buildings, policies). PROCESS: what is done (how care is given, such as hand hygiene compliance, bundle use). OUTCOME: the results (VAP rate, mortality, patient satisfaction). Good structure supports good process, which leads to good outcomes.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Planning, organising and controlling — Planning, organising and controlling are management functions.
• Input, output and feedback only — Input, output and feedback describe a general systems model.
• Assessment, diagnosis and planning — Assessment, diagnosis and planning are steps of the nursing process.

⭐ Key point: Example in ICU: Structure = enough trained nurses; Process = the VAP bundle is followed; Outcome = low VAP rate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q184$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2787, $s$Critical thinking in the ICU nurse is shown by:$s$, $s$["Following routine without any question", "Noticing subtle changes, questioning and acting", "Recording observations without analysis", "Waiting for doctors to notice any change"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Noticing subtle changes, questioning and acting
Critical thinking means actively analysing information to make sound decisions. In ICU, it is shown when a nurse NOTICES subtle changes (a slowly rising heart rate, slightly lower urine output), QUESTIONS what they mean ("Is this early sepsis?"), and ACTS appropriately (escalates, assesses further). It underpins clinical judgement and patient safety.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Following routine without any question — Following routine without question is the opposite of critical thinking.
• Recording observations without analysis — Recording without analysis misses the meaning of changes.
• Waiting for doctors to notice any change — Waiting for doctors delays care; nurses should notice and escalate.

⭐ Key point: Critical thinking skills: assessing, analysing, questioning, interpreting, evaluating and reflecting.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q185$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2788, $s$A health talk to a group of ICU relatives should start with:$s$, $s$["Assessing their learning needs", "A long lecture using jargon", "Handing out leaflets only", "Testing their knowledge first"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assessing their learning needs
Before teaching, ASSESS the LEARNING NEEDS of the group: what they already know, what they want to know, their concerns, language and literacy level. Teaching is then planned around their needs. This makes the session relevant and effective, and respects adult learners.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A long lecture using jargon — A long lecture with jargon won't be understood by most relatives.
• Handing out leaflets only — Leaflets alone don't address individual needs or questions.
• Testing their knowledge first — Testing first can feel intimidating; assessing needs is more appropriate.

⭐ Key point: Teaching steps: assess needs, plan objectives, implement (simple language, visual aids), and evaluate (ask them to explain back).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q187$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2789, $s$The 'politics of health care' affects ICU nursing through:$s$, $s$["Nurses' personal voting choices", "Individual patient diagnoses", "Funding, staffing decisions and policy", "The pharmacology of ICU drugs"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Funding, staffing decisions and policy
Health care politics affects ICU nursing through government and hospital decisions on FUNDING (money for ICU beds and equipment), STAFFING (number of nurses employed, salaries), and POLICY (laws, guidelines, health insurance). Nurse leaders need to advocate for adequate resources for safe critical care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nurses' personal voting choices — Personal voting is a private matter, not how politics affects nursing practice.
• Individual patient diagnoses — Individual diagnoses are clinical, not political.
• The pharmacology of ICU drugs — Drug pharmacology is science, not politics.

⭐ Key point: Nurses can influence health policy through professional bodies (NANNM, NMCN), research, and leadership.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q188$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2790, $s$Subjective Global Assessment grade C means the patient is:$s$, $s$["Severely malnourished", "Obese", "Well nourished", "Mildly malnourished"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Severely malnourished
Subjective Global Assessment (SGA) grades nutritional status as A, B or C, using history (weight loss, food intake, gut symptoms, function) and examination (loss of fat and muscle, oedema). Grade A = well nourished. Grade B = mildly to moderately malnourished. Grade C = SEVERELY malnourished.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Obese — SGA doesn't grade obesity.
• Well nourished — Well nourished is grade A.
• Mildly malnourished — Mild malnutrition is grade B.

⭐ Key point: Severely malnourished patients (SGA C) are at high risk of complications and refeeding syndrome. Start nutrition support early but carefully.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q189$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2791, $s$The charge nurse on an ICU shift is responsible for:$s$, $s$["Allocating patients, coordinating care, solving problems", "Prescribing drugs and fluids for all the patients", "Performing emergency surgery at the bedside", "Doing all the patient care alone without any help"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Allocating patients, coordinating care, solving problems
The charge nurse (shift leader) manages the unit during the shift: ALLOCATES patients to nurses (matching skills to patient needs), COORDINATES care (admissions, discharges, transfers), SOLVES problems (staffing, equipment, conflicts), supports junior staff, and communicates with doctors, managers and families.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Prescribing drugs and fluids for all the patients — Nurses don't prescribe drugs and fluids (except trained nurse prescribers in some places).
• Performing emergency surgery at the bedside — Nurses don't perform emergency surgery.
• Doing all the patient care alone without any help — The charge nurse leads the team; they don't work alone.

⭐ Key point: Good shift leadership includes a safety briefing ("huddle") at the start of each shift.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q164$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2792, $s$Supervision of ICU students and juniors aims to:$s$, $s$["Replace formal teaching sessions", "Increase the workload of the juniors", "Punish errors made by students", "Ensure safe care and support learning"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ensure safe care and support learning
Supervision of students and junior nurses aims to ENSURE SAFE patient care (by checking their work and correcting errors) and to SUPPORT their LEARNING (teaching, feedback, building confidence and competence). Preceptors and mentors guide them through the ICU's complex care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Replace formal teaching sessions — Supervision complements formal teaching; it doesn't replace it.
• Increase the workload of the juniors — Supervision isn't meant to increase workload.
• Punish errors made by students — Supervision should support learning, not punish.

⭐ Key point: Give constructive feedback: what went well, what to improve, and how.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q165$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2793, $s$Leadership style that involves staff in decisions is:$s$, $s$["Bureaucratic (rule-bound)", "Laissez-faire (hands-off)", "Autocratic (directive)", "Democratic (participative)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Democratic (participative)
In the DEMOCRATIC (participative) leadership style, the leader involves STAFF in DECISIONS: asking for ideas, discussing options, and reaching agreement together. Staff feel valued and committed, which improves morale and teamwork. However, decisions can be slow, so it is less suitable in emergencies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Bureaucratic (rule-bound) — Bureaucratic leadership follows strict rules and procedures.
• Laissez-faire (hands-off) — Laissez-faire leadership gives staff freedom with little direction.
• Autocratic (directive) — Autocratic leadership makes decisions alone, without involving staff.

⭐ Key point: Use democratic leadership for planning and change; switch to autocratic in emergencies.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q167$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2794, $s$Laissez-faire leadership in ICU may lead to:$s$, $s$["Quicker decisions in emergencies", "Poor coordination and unsafe care", "Clear accountability", "Better teamwork always"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Poor coordination and unsafe care
Laissez-faire ("let it be") leadership gives staff almost total freedom with little direction or supervision. In a complex, high-risk unit like ICU, this can lead to POOR COORDINATION, unclear roles, missed tasks, and UNSAFE CARE, unless the team is highly skilled and motivated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Quicker decisions in emergencies — It doesn't speed up decisions in emergencies; there may be no decision at all.
• Clear accountability — Accountability becomes unclear.
• Better teamwork always — Teamwork often suffers without direction.

⭐ Key point: Laissez-faire can work with expert, self-motivated teams on long-term projects, but not in emergencies.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q168$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2795, $s$Authority in management means:$s$, $s$["Seniority in age within the team", "Personal popularity among the staff", "The number of qualifications held", "The legitimate right to give instructions"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The legitimate right to give instructions
AUTHORITY is the LEGITIMATE (official) RIGHT to give instructions, make decisions and use resources, given by a person's POSITION in the organisation (such as unit manager or charge nurse). It always comes with responsibility (duties) and accountability (answering for results).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Seniority in age within the team — Age doesn't give authority.
• Personal popularity among the staff — Popularity gives influence but not formal authority.
• The number of qualifications held — Qualifications support competence but don't give authority by themselves.

⭐ Key point: Authority = the right to act. Responsibility = the duty to act. Accountability = answering for the results.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q169$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2796, $s$A policy on ICU admission and discharge criteria improves:$s$, $s$["Shorter waiting times only", "Better catering services", "Longer visiting hours", "Fair and consistent use of beds"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fair and consistent use of beds
Clear ICU admission and discharge criteria help the team decide fairly and CONSISTENTLY who needs an ICU bed (and who can be discharged), especially when beds are limited. This ensures the sickest patients who can benefit get ICU care, reduces conflict, and supports triage decisions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Shorter waiting times only — Waiting times may improve, but that isn't the main purpose.
• Better catering services — Catering isn't affected by admission policies.
• Longer visiting hours — Visiting hours have their own policy.

⭐ Key point: Criteria are based on need for organ support, reversibility of illness, and the likely benefit from ICU care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q170$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2797, $s$In-service training in ICU should be:$s$, $s$["Random and offered only occasionally", "Given only to the medical doctors", "Given only after a serious error", "Planned around identified learning needs"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Planned around identified learning needs
In-service training (training at work) should be PLANNED around identified LEARNING NEEDS: new equipment, new policies, audit results, incident trends and staff appraisal. Regular, relevant training maintains COMPETENCE and safe practice.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Random and offered only occasionally — Random, occasional training doesn't build consistent skills.
• Given only to the medical doctors — Nurses need training too, not only doctors.
• Given only after a serious error — Training shouldn't only follow errors; it should prevent them.

⭐ Key point: Keep training records and use competency checklists for key ICU skills.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q174$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2798, $s$The ICU unit manager reports mainly to:$s$, $s$["The catering department head", "The hospital pharmacist only", "The nursing service and hospital management", "The patients' relatives and visitors"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The nursing service and hospital management
The ICU unit manager (nurse manager) reports mainly to the NURSING SERVICE (Director of Nursing Services) and HOSPITAL MANAGEMENT. Reports cover unit activity (admissions, occupancy), QUALITY (infection rates, incidents), STAFFING and RESOURCES (budget, equipment needs).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The catering department head — Catering is a separate department.
• The hospital pharmacist only — The pharmacist is a colleague, not the manager's line manager.
• The patients' relatives and visitors — Relatives aren't the reporting line.

⭐ Key point: Clear reporting lines support accountability and help the manager advocate for ICU resources.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q175$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2799, $s$A needle-stick injury in ICU should be:$s$, $s$["Washed, reported at once, assessed for prophylaxis", "Sucked to remove the blood from the wound", "Reported at the end of the month to the manager", "Ignored if the injury is small and not bleeding"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Washed, reported at once, assessed for prophylaxis
After a needlestick injury: encourage the wound to BLEED gently, WASH it with soap and running water (don't scrub or suck), REPORT it immediately to the supervisor and occupational health, and get a RISK ASSESSMENT for post-exposure prophylaxis (HIV PEP within 1–2 hours, hepatitis B vaccine or immunoglobulin), plus baseline blood tests.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sucked to remove the blood from the wound — Sucking the wound is not recommended.
• Reported at the end of the month to the manager — Reporting at the end of the month delays PEP, which works best within hours.
• Ignored if the injury is small and not bleeding — All needlesticks must be reported, regardless of size.

⭐ Key point: HIV PEP should start as soon as possible, ideally within 1–2 hours, and no later than 72 hours.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q177$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2800, $s$An ICU policy manual should be:$s$, $s$["Written once and never reviewed again", "Available only to managers and seniors", "Kept locked in the manager's office", "Up to date, accessible and known to staff"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Up to date, accessible and known to staff
An ICU policy manual guides safe, consistent practice. It must be UP TO DATE (reviewed regularly, usually every 2–3 years or when evidence changes), ACCESSIBLE (on the unit and online) and KNOWN to all staff (through induction and training).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Written once and never reviewed again — Policies must be reviewed regularly.
• Available only to managers and seniors — All staff must have access, not only managers.
• Kept locked in the manager's office — Locking it away means staff can't use it.

⭐ Key point: Policies are only useful if staff know them and follow them. Include them in orientation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q178$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2801, $s$The role of the nurse in ICU unit organisation includes:$s$, $s$["Planning care, managing resources, keeping standards", "Only completing the nursing documentation", "Only following orders from doctors without question", "Only cleaning and tidying the unit each shift"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Planning care, managing resources, keeping standards
ICU nurses play a central role in unit organisation: PLANNING patient care, MANAGING RESOURCES (equipment, drugs, supplies, staff time), and MAINTAINING STANDARDS (policies, infection control, quality). They coordinate the multidisciplinary team and are central to ICU safety.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only completing the nursing documentation — Documentation is only one part of the nurse's role.
• Only following orders from doctors without question — Nurses use professional judgement and question unsafe orders.
• Only cleaning and tidying the unit each shift — Cleaning is done by support staff, though nurses oversee standards.

⭐ Key point: ICU nurses are the constant presence at the bedside, so they are key to spotting problems early.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q180$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2802, $s$Which document should be reviewed FIRST during a nursing quality audit?$s$, $s$["Staff attendance book", "Patient care records", "Hospital inventory", "Payroll register"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Patient care records
In a nursing quality audit, PATIENT CARE RECORDS are reviewed FIRST. They show what care was actually given, documented and evaluated (assessments, care plans, observations, drug charts). They are the main source for checking practice against standards.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Staff attendance book — The staff attendance book relates to staffing, not care quality.
• Hospital inventory — The hospital inventory relates to supplies.
• Payroll register — The payroll register relates to salaries.

⭐ Key point: Audit cycle: set a standard, measure practice, compare with the standard, make changes, and re-audit.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q164$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2803, $s$The first step in an effective risk management cycle is typically to:$s$, $s$["Identify the risk", "Report the incident to the media", "Terminate the staff member involved", "Implement a control plan immediately without analysis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Identify the risk
The risk management cycle starts with IDENTIFYING RISKS (what could go wrong?), using incident reports, audits, complaints and risk assessments. Then: analyse (likelihood and severity), control (put measures in place), monitor and review.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Report the incident to the media — Reporting to the media isn't part of risk management.
• Terminate the staff member involved — Terminating staff isn't part of the cycle; the focus is on systems.
• Implement a control plan immediately without analysis — Implementing a control plan without analysis may not target the real risk.

⭐ Key point: The risk register lists identified risks, their ratings, the actions taken, and who is responsible.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q165$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2804, $s$In a critical care unit, an audit reveals that pain assessment scores were documented for only 40% of eligible patients over the audit period, against a set standard of 100%. This finding represents:$s$, $s$["A process criterion deficiency", "A structure standard being met", "A satisfactory outcome", "An incident report"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A process criterion deficiency
Donabedian's model judges care by STRUCTURE (resources), PROCESS (what is done) and OUTCOME (results). Pain ASSESSMENT and DOCUMENTATION is a nursing ACTIVITY, so it is a PROCESS criterion. Doing it for only 40% of patients against a 100% standard is a PROCESS DEFICIENCY that needs improvement.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A structure standard being met — Structure refers to resources; this finding is about what nurses do.
• A satisfactory outcome — It isn't satisfactory; 40% is far below the standard.
• An incident report — It's an audit finding, not an individual incident.

⭐ Key point: Fix process gaps with training, reminders, documentation prompts and re-audit.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q168$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2805, $s$The Critical Care Nurse manager knowns that, the art of getting an organizational activities done through other people using available resources is called.$s$, $s$["Delegation", "Management", "Leadership", "Governance"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Management
MANAGEMENT is the process of getting organisational work done THROUGH OTHER PEOPLE, using the available RESOURCES (staff, money, equipment, time) efficiently and effectively. It involves planning, organising, staffing, directing and controlling.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Delegation — Delegation is one management skill (giving a task to someone), not the whole process.
• Leadership — Leadership is about influencing and motivating people towards a vision.
• Governance — Governance is about oversight, accountability and standards.

⭐ Key point: Management focuses on systems and resources; leadership focuses on people and direction. Good nurse managers need both.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q169$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2806, $s$A hospital has many unreported needle-stick injuries and incidents of workplace violence, with no reporting or compensation system for staff. The victims of maleficence here are the:$s$, $s$["Patients", "Families", "Communities", "Employees"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Employees
Maleficence means causing harm. The hospital owes its EMPLOYEES a safe workplace. Unreported needlestick injuries (risk of HIV, hepatitis B and C) and workplace violence, with no reporting or compensation systems, mean staff are being harmed without support or protection. The EMPLOYEES are the victims.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Patients — The patients aren't the main victims in this scenario.
• Families — The families aren't the main victims.
• Communities — The communities aren't the direct victims.

⭐ Key point: Every hospital needs needlestick and violence reporting systems, post-exposure prophylaxis, and staff support.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q170$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2807, $s$A production manager contacting the division manager to discuss the schedule of product delivery and quality is an example of:$s$, $s$["Upward communication", "Downward communication", "Grapevine communication", "Lateral communication"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lateral communication
LATERAL (horizontal) communication happens between people at the SAME LEVEL in an organisation, or between different departments, to coordinate work. A production manager and a division manager discussing schedules and quality is lateral communication.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Upward communication — Upward communication goes from junior staff to managers.
• Downward communication — Downward communication goes from managers to staff.
• Grapevine communication — The grapevine is informal, unofficial communication (rumours).

⭐ Key point: In ICU, lateral communication between departments (lab, pharmacy, radiology) is vital for coordinated care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q173$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2808, $s$For severe choking in a conscious adult, management alternates between:$s$, $s$["10 chest compressions and rescue breaths", "5 back slaps and 5 abdominal thrusts", "Head-tilt chin-lift and jaw thrust", "Recovery position and CPR"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 5 back slaps and 5 abdominal thrusts
For severe choking (the person can't speak, cough or breathe) in a conscious ADULT, alternate 5 BACK BLOWS (between the shoulder blades with the heel of the hand) with 5 ABDOMINAL THRUSTS (Heimlich manoeuvre). Each method uses a different force to dislodge the object. Continue until it clears or the person becomes unconscious (then start CPR).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 10 chest compressions and rescue breaths — Chest compressions with rescue breaths are for UNCONSCIOUS patients (CPR).
• Head-tilt chin-lift and jaw thrust — Head-tilt chin-lift and jaw thrust open the airway; they don't clear a foreign body.
• Recovery position and CPR — The recovery position and CPR are for unconscious patients.

⭐ Key point: For infants under 1 year, use 5 back blows and 5 CHEST thrusts (not abdominal thrusts). If the person becomes unconscious, start CPR.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q31$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2809, $s$The nurse manager wants to assess the compliance of staff with a new hand hygiene protocol. Which type of audit would be most effective for this purpose?$s$, $s$["Retrospective audit", "Outcome audit", "Chart audit", "Concurrent audit"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Concurrent audit
A CONCURRENT audit happens IN REAL TIME, while care is being given. To check hand hygiene compliance, the manager directly OBSERVES staff during their work and records whether they clean their hands at the right moments. This is the most accurate way to assess current practice.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Retrospective audit — A retrospective audit looks back at records; hand hygiene isn't usually documented.
• Outcome audit — An outcome audit measures results (such as infection rates), not compliance.
• Chart audit — A chart audit reviews records, which don't capture hand hygiene.

⭐ Key point: Use the WHO "5 Moments" observation tool for hand hygiene audits, and feed results back to staff.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q163$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2810, $s$Discipline, as part of the Controlling function of management, should ideally be:$s$, $s$["Used only as a first response before any coaching or feedback", "Fair, consistent, and applied according to established policy", "Avoided entirely regardless of the situation", "Applied inconsistently depending on personal relationships"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Fair, consistent, and applied according to established policy
Discipline is part of the CONTROLLING function of management. It should be FAIR, CONSISTENT (the same rules for everyone), and follow ESTABLISHED POLICY and due process, usually step by step: counselling, verbal warning, written warning, suspension, dismissal.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Used only as a first response before any coaching or feedback — Discipline should usually come AFTER coaching and feedback, not before.
• Avoided entirely regardless of the situation — Avoiding discipline entirely allows poor practice to continue.
• Applied inconsistently depending on personal relationships — Inconsistent discipline based on relationships is unfair and damages morale.

⭐ Key point: The aim of discipline is to correct behaviour, not to punish. Document every step.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q174$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2811, $s$A nursing manager is using a concurrent audit to identify potential issues with patient care. The advantage of a concurrent audit is that it allows for:$s$, $s$["The collection of a large amount of data from many patients at once", "Immediate intervention to correct a problem", "A review of care after the patient has been discharged", "A historical analysis of care over a long period"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Immediate intervention to correct a problem
A CONCURRENT audit is done WHILE the patient is still receiving care. Its main advantage is that problems can be found and CORRECTED IMMEDIATELY, before the patient is harmed or discharged.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The collection of a large amount of data from many patients at once — Collecting lots of data from many patients at once suits a retrospective audit.
• A review of care after the patient has been discharged — Reviewing after discharge is a RETROSPECTIVE audit.
• A historical analysis of care over a long period — Historical analysis over a long period is retrospective.

⭐ Key point: Concurrent audits help real-time quality improvement; retrospective audits show long-term patterns.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q161$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2812, $s$A ward sister reviews a currently admitted patient's chart mid-treatment to check whether nursing care so far meets set standards, allowing immediate correction if needed. This exemplifies:$s$, $s$["Retrospective audit", "Risk transfer", "Sentinel event review", "Concurrent audit"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Concurrent audit
Reviewing a CURRENTLY ADMITTED patient's chart DURING treatment to check whether care meets standards, so corrections can be made IMMEDIATELY, is a CONCURRENT AUDIT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Retrospective audit — A retrospective audit reviews care AFTER discharge.
• Risk transfer — Risk transfer is a risk management strategy (such as insurance).
• Sentinel event review — A sentinel event review investigates a serious incident.

⭐ Key point: Audit types: concurrent (during care), retrospective (after care), prospective (before care, policy review).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q162$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2813, $s$He was the first to systematically describe management behaviour and also pioneered the identification of the principles and skills that underlie effective management.$s$, $s$["Henri Fayol", "F. Taylor", "Max waber", "Gashinge et all."]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Henri Fayol
HENRI FAYOL (a French mining engineer, 1841–1925) was the first to systematically describe management behaviour. He identified the FUNCTIONS of management (planning, organising, commanding, coordinating, controlling) and 14 PRINCIPLES of management (division of labour, authority, discipline, unity of command and others). He is called the father of modern (administrative) management.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• F. Taylor — F. Taylor is the father of SCIENTIFIC management (efficiency, time-and-motion studies).
• Max waber — Max Weber developed the theory of BUREAUCRACY.
• Gashinge et all. — "Gashinge et al." isn't a recognised management theorist.

⭐ Key point: Classical management theorists: Taylor (scientific), Fayol (administrative), Weber (bureaucratic).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q163 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2814, $s$Which leadership style is most appropriate during cardiopulmonary resuscitation?$s$, $s$["Laissez-faire", "Transformational", "Democratic", "Autocratic"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Autocratic
During CPR, decisions must be made quickly and tasks coordinated precisely. The AUTOCRATIC (directive) leadership style, with one team leader giving clear, direct instructions, is most effective in this emergency. Team members follow instructions while giving feedback (closed-loop communication).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Laissez-faire — Laissez-faire gives little direction, which is dangerous in an emergency.
• Transformational — Transformational leadership is best for long-term change and motivation, not emergencies.
• Democratic — Democratic leadership takes time for discussion, which isn't possible in CPR.

⭐ Key point: Good resuscitation leaders stay "hands off", watch the whole picture, and assign clear roles.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q164$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2815, $s$The Critical care unit manager as an overall seer of the unit activities can employed one of the following as a principle of organizing the unit to achieve the organizational objectives.$s$, $s$["Planning", "Directing", "Balance", "Motivation"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Balance
The unit manager organises the ICU to meet its objectives using principles of organising. BALANCE is one such principle: keeping activities, staff, workload and resources in proportion (for example, balancing skill mix, patient acuity and staff numbers across shifts) so the unit runs smoothly.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Planning — Planning is a management FUNCTION, not an organising principle.
• Directing — Directing is a management FUNCTION.
• Motivation — Motivation is part of directing, not an organising principle.

⭐ Key point: Other organising principles: unity of command, span of control, division of work, and delegation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q166$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2816, $s$Which level of management is responsible for implementing programs that are intended to carry out the broader objectives of an organization set by executives?$s$, $s$["chief financial managers", "middle managers", "first level managers", "supervisory managers"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: middle managers
MIDDLE MANAGERS (such as departmental heads or nursing service managers) translate the broad goals set by top executives into specific PROGRAMMES and PLANS, and oversee first-line managers who carry them out.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• chief financial managers — Chief financial managers are part of top management.
• first level managers — First-level managers (such as ward sisters) supervise day-to-day operations.
• supervisory managers — Supervisory managers are first-line managers.

⭐ Key point: Levels of management: top (strategic), middle (tactical, implementing programmes), first-line (operational, day-to-day).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q167$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2817, $s$A hospital's quality assurance team is performing a prospective audit. This audit would involve:$s$, $s$["Reviewing and establishing new policies and procedures before they are implemented", "Reviewing the outcomes of a patient's treatment after they leave the hospital", "Examining the care provided to a patient in real-time", "Analyzing a patient's chart after a critical event has occurred"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reviewing and establishing new policies and procedures before they are implemented
A PROSPECTIVE audit looks FORWARD: it reviews and evaluates new policies, procedures and plans BEFORE they are implemented, to make sure they are sound and will meet standards. It helps prevent problems before they happen.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reviewing the outcomes of a patient's treatment after they leave the hospital — Reviewing outcomes after discharge is a retrospective audit.
• Examining the care provided to a patient in real-time — Real-time review during care is a concurrent audit.
• Analyzing a patient's chart after a critical event has occurred — Analysing a chart after a critical event is a retrospective review.

⭐ Key point: Prospective = before; concurrent = during; retrospective = after.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q169$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2818, $s$As an ICU manager you are aware that a formal document that most supervisors or managers keep for future reference is called$s$, $s$["Incident reporting", "Report writing", "Incident writing", "Incident report"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Incident report
An INCIDENT REPORT is a formal document recording an unexpected event (an error, fall, near miss, equipment failure) with the facts, time, people involved, and actions taken. Supervisors and managers keep it for FUTURE REFERENCE: analysis, learning, risk management and possible legal use.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Incident reporting — "Incident reporting" is the process, not the document itself.
• Report writing — Report writing is a general skill.
• Incident writing — "Incident writing" isn't a recognised term.

⭐ Key point: Incident reports are NOT kept in the patient's notes. Record the facts of the event and care given in the notes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q170$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2819, $s$The type of audit that involves reviewing a patient's chart while they are still in the hospital is called a:$s$, $s$["Outcome audit", "Prospective audit", "Retrospective audit", "Concurrent audit"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Concurrent audit
A CONCURRENT audit reviews a patient's chart and care WHILE the patient is STILL IN HOSPITAL receiving care. This allows problems to be identified and corrected in real time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Outcome audit — An outcome audit focuses on results of care.
• Prospective audit — A prospective audit evaluates policies before implementation.
• Retrospective audit — A retrospective audit reviews charts after discharge.

⭐ Key point: Concurrent auditing can include direct observation, interviews and chart review.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q171$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2820, $s$Which management principle promotes employee participation in decision-making?$s$, $s$["Discipline", "Centralization", "Unity of command", "Decentralization"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Decentralization
DECENTRALISATION spreads decision-making authority DOWN to lower levels of the organisation (unit managers, staff nurses). This promotes employee PARTICIPATION in decisions, faster local decisions, and staff motivation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Discipline — Discipline means respect for rules and agreements.
• Centralization — Centralisation keeps decision-making at the TOP.
• Unity of command — Unity of command means each employee has one boss.

⭐ Key point: Shared governance in nursing is a form of decentralised decision-making.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q174$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2821, $s$Clinical audit in the ICU is an important part of:$s$, $s$["Building maintenance", "Payroll management", "Clinical governance", "Hospital marketing"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Clinical governance
CLINICAL AUDIT is a key part of CLINICAL GOVERNANCE, the framework through which health services ensure high-quality, safe care and remain accountable. Other parts include risk management, education and training, research and evidence-based practice, patient involvement, and staff management.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Building maintenance — Building maintenance isn't clinical governance.
• Payroll management — Payroll management isn't clinical governance.
• Hospital marketing — Hospital marketing isn't clinical governance.

⭐ Key point: The audit cycle: set a standard, measure practice, compare, make changes, and re-audit.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q176$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D2 · Management, Leadership, Quality & Entrepreneurship$s$, false, 2822, $s$Buying a new ventilator for the ICU falls under the:$s$, $s$["Petty cash budget", "Operating budget", "Capital budget", "Personnel budget"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Capital budget
A new ventilator is an expensive, long-lasting piece of equipment (lasting many years). It falls under the CAPITAL BUDGET, which covers major purchases (equipment, buildings, renovations) that cost a lot and last a long time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Petty cash budget — Petty cash covers small, everyday expenses.
• Operating budget — The operating budget covers recurring costs (consumables, utilities, drugs).
• Personnel budget — The personnel budget covers staff salaries and benefits.

⭐ Key point: Capital purchases usually need a business case justifying the need and benefits.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q179$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, true, 2823, $s$A 'null hypothesis' (H0) states that$s$, $s$["There is no statistically significant relationship or difference between the variables being studied", "There is definitely a significant relationship between the variables", "The researcher's prediction is automatically correct", "Data collection is unnecessary"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: There is no statistically significant relationship or difference between the variables being studied
The null hypothesis (H₀) states that there is NO relationship or difference between the variables being studied. For example: "There is no difference in VAP rates between patients who receive chlorhexidine mouth care and those who don't." Statistical tests check whether the data are strong enough to REJECT the null hypothesis (usually p below 0.05).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• There is definitely a significant relationship between the variables — Stating there IS a significant relationship describes the alternative (research) hypothesis.
• The researcher's prediction is automatically correct — No hypothesis is "automatically correct"; it must be tested with data.
• Data collection is unnecessary — Data are always needed to test a hypothesis.

⭐ Key point: H₀ = no difference. H₁ (alternative) = there is a difference. Rejecting H₀ wrongly is a Type I error; failing to reject a false H₀ is a Type II error.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q163 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, true, 2824, $s$A nurse formulates the nursing diagnosis 'Risk for infection related to invasive lines' for a critically ill patient. This is an example of which type of nursing diagnosis?$s$, $s$["An actual nursing diagnosis, describing a current problem", "A risk nursing diagnosis, describing vulnerability to a potential problem rather than a current one", "A wellness diagnosis", "Not a valid nursing diagnosis format"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A risk nursing diagnosis, describing vulnerability to a potential problem rather than a current one
NANDA-I nursing diagnoses come in several types: ACTUAL (problem-focused) — a problem that EXISTS NOW, with defining characteristics present (e.g. "Impaired Gas Exchange related to… as evidenced by SpO2 85%"); RISK — the patient is VULNERABLE to developing a problem but it has NOT happened yet; written with "Risk for…" and RISK FACTORS only (no "as evidenced by", because no signs exist yet) — e.g. "Risk for infection related to invasive lines"; HEALTH PROMOTION/WELLNESS — readiness to improve health (e.g. "Readiness for enhanced nutrition"); SYNDROME — a cluster of diagnoses. ICU patients with central lines, urinary catheters and ETTs are at risk of infection, so this is a RISK diagnosis.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• An actual nursing diagnosis, describing a current problem — An ACTUAL diagnosis describes a current problem with signs present — infection has not occurred here.
• A wellness diagnosis — A WELLNESS diagnosis describes readiness to improve health, not vulnerability to a problem.
• Not a valid nursing diagnosis format — It IS a valid format — "Risk for [problem] related to [risk factor]" is the standard risk diagnosis.

⭐ Key point: "Risk for…" = RISK diagnosis (vulnerability, no signs yet). Actual = problem present with defining characteristics.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q161$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, true, 2825, $s$Which of the following is the most accurate definition of research?$s$, $s$["A systematic, rigorous and logical process of inquiry that seeks to answer questions and generate new knowledge", "The act of reading widely around a clinical topic before writing a report", "Any opinion a nurse forms after observing a patient once", "A collection of anecdotes gathered from colleagues about a clinical practice"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A systematic, rigorous and logical process of inquiry that seeks to answer questions and generate new knowledge
RESEARCH is a SYSTEMATIC, RIGOROUS and LOGICAL process of inquiry that seeks to ANSWER QUESTIONS, solve problems and GENERATE NEW KNOWLEDGE (or validate existing knowledge). "Systematic" means it follows organised steps: identify a problem → review the literature → state objectives/hypotheses → choose a design → sample → collect data → analyse → interpret → report/disseminate. "Rigorous" means it uses careful, valid and reliable methods to reduce bias. In nursing, research provides the evidence for evidence-based practice (EBP).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The act of reading widely around a clinical topic before writing a report — Reading widely before writing a report is a LITERATURE REVIEW or study — only one step of research, not research itself.
• Any opinion a nurse forms after observing a patient once — An OPINION formed after observing a patient once is not systematic and is open to bias.
• A collection of anecdotes gathered from colleagues about a clinical practice — ANECDOTES from colleagues are informal stories, not systematic inquiry.

⭐ Key point: Research = SYSTEMATIC, RIGOROUS, LOGICAL inquiry to answer questions and generate new knowledge.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q163$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, true, 2826, $s$A nurse wants to implement a new evidence-based practice change on the unit based on a research finding. The most appropriate first step is to:$s$, $s$["Implement the change immediately unit-wide with no evaluation", "Critically appraise the evidence, involve relevant stakeholders, and consider a structured pilot with evaluation before wider rollout", "Ignore the research since current practice has always worked", "Implement changes based on personal opinion alone, without reference to evidence"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Critically appraise the evidence, involve relevant stakeholders, and consider a structured pilot with evaluation before wider rollout
EVIDENCE-BASED PRACTICE (EBP) combines the BEST research evidence, CLINICAL EXPERTISE and PATIENT PREFERENCES. Before changing practice, the nurse should follow a structured process (e.g. Iowa model, ACE Star): ask a clear question (PICO), search for evidence, CRITICALLY APPRAISE it (is it valid, reliable, relevant to our patients?), involve STAKEHOLDERS (nurse manager, doctors, pharmacists, infection control, staff who will use it), get approval, run a small PILOT with clear outcome measures, EVALUATE the results, and then roll out more widely with training and ongoing audit. This makes the change safe, accepted and sustainable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Implement the change immediately unit-wide with no evaluation — Implementing immediately unit-wide with no evaluation risks harm if the evidence does not fit your setting, and staff may resist.
• Ignore the research since current practice has always worked — Ignoring research because "it has always worked" is the opposite of EBP and blocks improvement.
• Implement changes based on personal opinion alone, without reference to evidence — Changes based on PERSONAL OPINION alone, without evidence, are not evidence-based and may be unsafe.

⭐ Key point: EBP change: appraise evidence → involve stakeholders → PILOT and evaluate → then roll out.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q169$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, true, 2827, $s$The first page of the research report is$s$, $s$["Appendix", "Bibliography", "Index", "Title Page"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Title Page
A research report is arranged in three parts. FRONT (preliminary) MATTER — TITLE PAGE (FIRST page: title, author, institution, date/degree), certification/approval page, dedication, acknowledgements, ABSTRACT, table of contents, lists of tables and figures, abbreviations. MAIN BODY — Chapter 1 Introduction, Chapter 2 Literature review, Chapter 3 Methodology, Chapter 4 Results, Chapter 5 Discussion, Conclusion and Recommendations. BACK MATTER — REFERENCES/BIBLIOGRAPHY, APPENDICES (questionnaire, consent form, ethical approval letter), and sometimes an INDEX. So the first page is the TITLE PAGE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Appendix — The APPENDIX is in the back matter, at the end.
• Bibliography — The BIBLIOGRAPHY/references are in the back matter, at the end.
• Index — An INDEX, if present, is at the very end of a report or book.

⭐ Key point: Research report: FRONT matter (TITLE PAGE first, abstract, contents) → main body → BACK matter (references, appendices).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q171$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, true, 2828, $s$Which measure of central tendency is appropriate for nominal (categorical) data, such as the most common blood group among patients in a ward?$s$, $s$["Mean", "Median", "Mode", "Standard deviation"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Mode
NOMINAL data are CATEGORIES with no order (e.g. blood groups A, B, AB, O; sex; ward type). You cannot calculate an average of "blood group A" and "blood group O", and there is no "middle" category. The only meaningful measure of central tendency is the MODE — the MOST FREQUENTLY occurring category (e.g. "blood group O is the most common").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Mean — The MEAN requires numerical (interval/ratio) data.
• Median — The MEDIAN requires data that can be ordered (ordinal or higher).
• Standard deviation — STANDARD DEVIATION is a measure of spread, not central tendency, and requires numerical data.

⭐ Key point: Nominal data are described using frequencies, percentages, and bar or pie charts.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q171$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, true, 2829, $s$A client who was previously the primary breadwinner expresses distress about being unable to return to work after a spinal cord injury and struggles with the loss of that role within the family. This is best classified under which Roy adaptive mode?$s$, $s$["Physiological-physical mode", "Self-concept/group identity mode", "Role function mode", "Interdependence mode"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Role function mode
In ROY'S ADAPTATION MODEL, the ROLE FUNCTION MODE relates to the ROLES a person plays in society (e.g. parent, spouse, worker, breadwinner) and how well they perform them. A client who was the PRIMARY BREADWINNER and is distressed about being unable to return to work and losing that ROLE in the family has a problem in the ROLE FUNCTION mode.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Physiological-physical mode — The physiological mode covers basic body needs (oxygen, nutrition, elimination, activity).
• Self-concept/group identity mode — The self-concept mode covers beliefs and feelings about oneself (body image, self-esteem).
• Interdependence mode — The interdependence mode covers relationships, love and support.

⭐ Key point: The nurse helps the client adapt — e.g. exploring new roles, vocational rehabilitation, and family support.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q152$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, true, 2830, $s$'Operational definition' of a variable refers to$s$, $s$["The name of the statistical test applied to the variable", "A dictionary definition of the term unrelated to the study context", "A precise description of how a variable will be measured or manipulated in a specific study", "The theoretical meaning of a concept found in textbooks only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A precise description of how a variable will be measured or manipulated in a specific study
An OPERATIONAL DEFINITION describes PRECISELY HOW a variable will be MEASURED or MANIPULATED in a particular study. Example: "pain" may be operationally defined as "the score on a 0–10 Numeric Rating Scale recorded 1 hour after surgery". This makes the study clear, measurable and repeatable by other researchers.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The name of the statistical test applied to the variable — The statistical test is chosen based on the data type and design.
• A dictionary definition of the term unrelated to the study context — A dictionary definition is a general meaning unrelated to the study.
• The theoretical meaning of a concept found in textbooks only — The theoretical (conceptual) definition describes the meaning of the concept from theory, not how it is measured.

⭐ Key point: Every variable in a quantitative study should have both a conceptual and an operational definition.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q55$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, true, 2831, $s$A primary source in a literature review refers to$s$, $s$["An original report of a study written by the researcher(s) who actually conducted it", "A newspaper article about a general health topic", "A textbook summarizing several other people's research", "An encyclopedia entry with no citations"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An original report of a study written by the researcher(s) who actually conducted it
A PRIMARY SOURCE is an ORIGINAL REPORT of a study written by the RESEARCHER(S) who ACTUALLY CONDUCTED IT — usually a research article in a peer-reviewed journal, a thesis, or a conference paper presenting original findings. It gives the most accurate, first-hand information about the study's methods and results.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A newspaper article about a general health topic — A newspaper article about health is a secondary (or popular) source.
• A textbook summarizing several other people's research — A textbook summarising others' research is a SECONDARY source.
• An encyclopedia entry with no citations — An encyclopedia entry is a tertiary/secondary source.

⭐ Key point: Literature reviews should rely mainly on primary sources, which can be critically appraised.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q57$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, true, 2832, $s$A researcher studying illicit drug users, a population without an available formal list or registry, asks initial participants to refer other drug users they know. This sampling technique is called$s$, $s$["Systematic sampling", "Stratified random sampling", "Quota sampling", "Snowball sampling"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Snowball sampling
The researcher is studying ILLICIT DRUG USERS — a HIDDEN, hard-to-reach population that has no list or registry (no sampling frame), and whose members may avoid researchers because of stigma or fear of the law. The researcher asks the first participants to REFER other drug users they know, who then refer others, so the sample grows like a SNOWBALL rolling downhill. This is SNOWBALL (chain-referral) SAMPLING, a NON-PROBABILITY method. It also helps build trust, as new participants are introduced by people they know.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Systematic sampling — Systematic sampling needs a complete list of the population to select every kth person; no such list exists here.
• Stratified random sampling — Stratified random sampling needs a list divided into subgroups (strata) from which random samples are taken.
• Quota sampling — Quota sampling fills fixed numbers in chosen subgroups (e.g. 20 men, 20 women) using convenient participants, not referrals.

⭐ Key point: Snowball samples are not representative (people refer others like themselves), so findings cannot be generalised widely. Confidentiality and anonymity are especially important in studies of illegal or stigmatised behaviour.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q70$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, true, 2833, $s$A "Type I error" in hypothesis testing occurs when:$s$, $s$["The wrong statistical test is applied to the data", "A false null hypothesis is not rejected", "The sample size chosen is too large", "A true null hypothesis is incorrectly rejected (a false positive)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A true null hypothesis is incorrectly rejected (a false positive)
A TYPE I ERROR (ALPHA ERROR) occurs when the researcher REJECTS a NULL HYPOTHESIS that is actually TRUE — concluding there IS a difference or effect when there really is NOT (a FALSE POSITIVE). The probability of a Type I error equals the significance level (alpha), usually set at 0.05 (5%). Example: concluding a new drug works when it really doesn't.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The wrong statistical test is applied to the data — Using the wrong test is a methodological error, not a Type I error.
• A false null hypothesis is not rejected — Failing to reject a FALSE null hypothesis is a TYPE II error.
• The sample size chosen is too large — A large sample size reduces errors; it is not a Type I error.

⭐ Key point: Reduce Type I error by using a smaller alpha (e.g. 0.01), but this increases the risk of Type II error.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q77 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, true, 2834, $s$In Roy's model, the stimulus that is immediately confronting the person and requires the most immediate attention is called the:$s$, $s$["Compensatory stimulus", "Residual stimulus", "Contextual stimulus", "Focal stimulus"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Focal stimulus
In Sister Callista ROY'S Adaptation Model, people respond to three types of STIMULI: the FOCAL STIMULUS — the stimulus IMMEDIATELY CONFRONTING the person, demanding the most attention (e.g. acute pain after surgery, difficulty breathing); CONTEXTUAL STIMULI — other factors present that influence the situation (e.g. fear, noisy environment, lack of sleep); RESIDUAL STIMULI — factors whose effects are unclear (e.g. past experiences, beliefs, cultural attitudes).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Compensatory stimulus — "Compensatory stimulus" is not a term in Roy's model.
• Residual stimulus — Residual stimuli have uncertain or unclear effects.
• Contextual stimulus — Contextual stimuli are other factors in the situation that contribute.

⭐ Key point: Nursing interventions in Roy's model aim to manage stimuli to promote adaptation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q134$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2835, $s$Florence Nightingale's Environmental Theory, published in 'Notes on Nursing,' primarily focuses on which idea?$s$, $s$["Nursing should focus on manipulating the client's environment to place the body in the best condition for nature to act and for healing to occur", "Nursing should focus exclusively on administering medications prescribed by physicians", "Illness is caused solely by genetic predisposition and cannot be influenced by environment", "The nurse's primary role is to perform invasive procedures independent of physicians"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Nursing should focus on manipulating the client's environment to place the body in the best condition for nature to act and for healing to occur
Florence Nightingale's Environmental Theory says the nurse's main job is to manage the patient's ENVIRONMENT — fresh air, light, warmth, cleanliness, quiet, clean water, drainage and good food — so that nature can heal the body. She believed disease was helped or hindered by surroundings. Her ideas began modern infection control and patient comfort.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nursing should focus exclusively on administering medications prescribed by physicians — Giving medications is part of nursing, but Nightingale's theory is about the environment, not drug administration.
• Illness is caused solely by genetic predisposition and cannot be influenced by environment — Nightingale believed the environment strongly affects health, so illness is not "solely genetic".
• The nurse's primary role is to perform invasive procedures independent of physicians — Performing invasive procedures independently is not her theory.

⭐ Key point: Nightingale is called the founder of modern nursing; she wrote "Notes on Nursing" (1859) and improved care in the Crimean War through hygiene.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q151$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2836, $s$Which of the following is one of Henderson's 14 basic needs related to physiological function?$s$, $s$["Breathe normally", "Achieve self-actualization", "Maintain the flexible line of defense", "Engage in role-function adaptation"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Breathe normally
Virginia Henderson described 14 basic needs that the nurse helps patients meet when they cannot meet them alone. The FIRST is "Breathe normally". Others include: eat and drink adequately, eliminate, move and keep a good posture, sleep and rest, dress, keep body temperature normal, keep clean, avoid danger, communicate, worship, work, play and learn.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Achieve self-actualization — Self-actualisation is the top of Maslow's hierarchy, not one of Henderson's needs.
• Maintain the flexible line of defense — The flexible line of defence comes from Neuman's Systems Model.
• Engage in role-function adaptation — Role-function adaptation comes from Roy's Adaptation Model.

⭐ Key point: Henderson's definition of nursing: helping the person "perform those activities contributing to health or its recovery that he would perform unaided if he had the necessary strength, will or knowledge".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q152$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2837, $s$Orem identified three types of nursing systems. One of them is:$s$, $s$["Team nursing", "Primary nursing", "Individualised client care", "Wholly compensatory"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Wholly compensatory
Dorothea Orem's self-care deficit theory describes three nursing systems depending on how much the patient can do for themselves. 1) WHOLLY COMPENSATORY: the nurse does everything (an unconscious, ventilated ICU patient). 2) Partly compensatory: the nurse and patient share care. 3) Supportive-educative: the patient can do the care with teaching and support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Team nursing — Team nursing is a way of organising staff, not one of Orem's systems.
• Primary nursing — Primary nursing is a care delivery model, not one of Orem's systems.
• Individualised client care — Individualised client care is a general concept, not one of Orem's three systems.

⭐ Key point: Most ICU patients start in the wholly compensatory system and move toward supportive-educative as they recover.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q153$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2838, $s$In Sister Callista Roy's Adaptation Model, the person is viewed as:$s$, $s$["A biopsychosocial adaptive system that receives input (stimuli) and produces output in the form of adaptive or ineffective responses", "A passive recipient of environmental changes with no internal processing mechanisms", "A system defined solely by 14 basic needs", "An energy field indistinguishable from the environment"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A biopsychosocial adaptive system that receives input (stimuli) and produces output in the form of adaptive or ineffective responses
In Callista Roy's Adaptation Model, the person is an ADAPTIVE SYSTEM. Stimuli (focal, contextual and residual) are the input. The person's coping processes (the regulator, which is physiological, and the cognator, which is mental and emotional) process these stimuli. The output is behaviour, which is either ADAPTIVE (healthy) or INEFFECTIVE. Adaptation happens in four modes: physiological, self-concept, role function, and interdependence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A passive recipient of environmental changes with no internal processing mechanisms — Roy sees the person as active, not a passive recipient.
• A system defined solely by 14 basic needs — The 14 basic needs belong to Henderson, not Roy.
• An energy field indistinguishable from the environment — An energy field indistinguishable from the environment is Martha Rogers' view.

⭐ Key point: The goal of nursing in Roy's model is to promote adaptation in the four modes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q154$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2839, $s$A nurse using Rogers' Science of Unitary Human Beings as a guiding framework would most likely incorporate which of the following into practice?$s$, $s$["Therapeutic touch or other modalities intended to promote harmonious patterning of the human and environmental energy fields", "Strict enforcement of the 14 basic needs checklist", "Ranking patient needs using Maslow's pyramid exclusively", "Assessment limited only to the 11 functional health patterns"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Therapeutic touch or other modalities intended to promote harmonious patterning of the human and environmental energy fields
Martha Rogers' Science of Unitary Human Beings sees each person as an energy field that is constantly exchanging energy with the environmental energy field. The nurse aims to promote harmony between the two fields. Practices like THERAPEUTIC TOUCH, relaxation, music and imagery come from this view.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Strict enforcement of the 14 basic needs checklist — The 14 basic needs checklist belongs to Henderson.
• Ranking patient needs using Maslow's pyramid exclusively — Maslow's pyramid is a psychology theory, not Rogers' framework.
• Assessment limited only to the 11 functional health patterns — The 11 functional health patterns belong to Marjory Gordon.

⭐ Key point: Key Rogers concepts are energy fields, openness, pattern and pandimensionality (the principles of homeodynamics).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q156$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2840, $s$Marjory Gordon's Functional Health Patterns framework is primarily used by nurses to:$s$, $s$["Diagnose medical conditions independently of physicians", "Organize a systematic, holistic nursing assessment across defined categories of client behavior over time", "Replace the nursing process entirely with a single-step evaluation", "Calculate a client's position on the health-illness continuum numerically"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Organize a systematic, holistic nursing assessment across defined categories of client behavior over time
Marjory Gordon's 11 Functional Health Patterns give nurses a structured way to do a HOLISTIC nursing assessment. They cover health perception and management, nutrition and metabolism, elimination, activity and exercise, sleep and rest, cognition and perception, self-perception, role and relationships, sexuality and reproduction, coping and stress, and values and beliefs. The data then guide nursing diagnoses.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Diagnose medical conditions independently of physicians — Nurses do not diagnose medical conditions with this framework; they make NURSING diagnoses.
• Replace the nursing process entirely with a single-step evaluation — It supports the nursing process; it does not replace it.
• Calculate a client's position on the health-illness continuum numerically — It does not calculate a numerical position on a health–illness scale.

⭐ Key point: Gordon's patterns are widely used to organise admission assessments and link well with NANDA nursing diagnoses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q157$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2841, $s$The standard format for writing a 'problem-focused' (actual) NANDA-I nursing diagnosis is often referred to as the PES format, meaning:$s$, $s$["Problem, Etiology, Signs and symptoms (defining characteristics)", "Plan, Evaluate, Solve", "Patient, Environment, Standard", "Priority, Evidence, Solution"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Problem, Etiology, Signs and symptoms (defining characteristics)
A problem-focused (actual) NANDA-I nursing diagnosis is written in PES format. P is the PROBLEM (the NANDA label, such as "Ineffective airway clearance"). E is the ETIOLOGY ("related to" thick secretions and weak cough). S is the SIGNS and SYMPTOMS, the defining characteristics ("as evidenced by" coarse crackles and SpO₂ 88%).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Plan, Evaluate, Solve — Plan, Evaluate, Solve is not the PES format.
• Patient, Environment, Standard — Patient, Environment, Standard is not the PES format.
• Priority, Evidence, Solution — Priority, Evidence, Solution is not the PES format.

⭐ Key point: A RISK nursing diagnosis has no "S" part (the problem hasn't happened yet), for example "Risk for infection related to central venous catheter".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q159$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2842, $s$Standardised nursing terminologies such as NANDA, NIC, and NOC exist to:$s$, $s$["Make documentation more variable between institutions", "Provide a consistent language for nursing diagnoses, interventions, and outcomes", "Replace physician orders", "Track hospital finances"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Provide a consistent language for nursing diagnoses, interventions, and outcomes
Standardised nursing languages give nurses a common, consistent vocabulary. NANDA-I covers nursing DIAGNOSES, NIC (Nursing Interventions Classification) covers nursing INTERVENTIONS, and NOC (Nursing Outcomes Classification) covers patient OUTCOMES. Using them improves communication, documentation, electronic records, research and showing the value of nursing care.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Make documentation more variable between institutions — They make documentation MORE consistent, not more variable.
• Replace physician orders — They describe nursing care; they don't replace physician orders.
• Track hospital finances — They are not tools for tracking hospital finances.

⭐ Key point: Remember the trio: NANDA = diagnosis, NIC = intervention, NOC = outcome.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q160$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2843, $s$Which of the following goal statements is correctly written in SMART format?$s$, $s$["\"Client will feel better soon\"", "\"Client will ambulate the length of the hallway (20 meters) without shortness of breath by the end of the 3-day shift\"", "\"Nurse will help the client walk\"", "\"Client's pain will improve\""]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: "Client will ambulate the length of the hallway (20 meters) without shortness of breath by the end of the 3-day shift"
A SMART goal is Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Realistic and Time-bound. "Client will ambulate the length of the hallway (20 metres) without shortness of breath by the end of the 3-day shift" says who (the client), what (walk), how much (20 metres), under what condition (without shortness of breath) and by when (end of 3 days). It is patient-centred and measurable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• "Client will feel better soon" — "Feel better soon" is vague and not measurable or time-bound.
• "Nurse will help the client walk" — "Nurse will help the client walk" is a nursing action (intervention), not a patient goal.
• "Client's pain will improve" — "Pain will improve" has no measure (such as a pain score) and no time frame.

⭐ Key point: Write goals about the PATIENT, with an action verb, a measurable criterion and a time frame. Another useful format is RUMBA.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q161$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2844, $s$Which variable is NOT manipulated by the researcher but measured as the outcome?$s$, $s$["Extraneous variable", "Independent variable", "Dependent variable", "Internal variable"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Dependent variable
The DEPENDENT variable is the outcome that is MEASURED. Its value "depends" on the independent variable. The researcher doesn't change it directly. Example: in a study of whether early mobilisation shortens ICU stay, the length of stay is the dependent variable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Extraneous variable — An extraneous variable is an outside factor that might affect the result and needs controlling.
• Independent variable — The independent variable is the one the researcher manipulates or changes (the cause), such as early mobilisation.
• Internal variable — "Internal variable" is not a standard research term.

⭐ Key point: Independent = cause (what you change). Dependent = effect (what you measure).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q164$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2845, $s$Probability sampling gives every member of the population:$s$, $s$["Equal representation", "Guaranteed selection", "An equal chance of being selected", "No chance of selection"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An equal chance of being selected
In PROBABILITY (random) sampling, every member of the population has a known, usually EQUAL, chance of being chosen, as in drawing names from a hat or using random number tables. This reduces selection bias and allows the results to be generalised to the whole population. Types include simple random, stratified random, systematic and cluster sampling.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Equal representation — Equal representation isn't guaranteed; random sampling gives an equal CHANCE, but the sample may not match the population perfectly.
• Guaranteed selection — No one is guaranteed to be chosen in random sampling.
• No chance of selection — With no chance of selection, there would be no sampling at all.

⭐ Key point: Non-probability sampling (convenience, purposive, snowball, quota) is common in qualitative research but cannot be generalised as confidently.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q165$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2846, $s$A patient's body weight measured in kilograms is an example of which scale of measurement?$s$, $s$["Nominal", "Ordinal", "Interval", "Ratio"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ratio
Weight in kilograms is RATIO data. It has equal intervals between values and a TRUE ZERO (0 kg means no weight), so you can say "80 kg is twice as heavy as 40 kg". Other ratio examples: height, age, urine output, heart rate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nominal — Nominal data are names or categories without order (blood group, gender).
• Ordinal — Ordinal data have an order but unequal gaps (pain scored mild, moderate, severe; GCS).
• Interval — Interval data have equal gaps but NO true zero (temperature in °C, where 0 °C is not "no temperature").

⭐ Key point: Remember NOIR: Nominal, Ordinal, Interval, Ratio, from least to most information.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q166$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2847, $s$The following are Sources of literature review EXCEPT$s$, $s$["dissertations", "existing theory", "Internet", "Social medias"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Social medias
The question asks which is NOT a proper source for a literature review. Social media (Facebook, WhatsApp, Instagram, TikTok) is not a reliable academic source: posts are not peer-reviewed and often contain opinions or misinformation. Good sources include journal articles, dissertations and theses, books, existing theories, reports and reputable internet databases (PubMed, CINAHL, Cochrane).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• dissertations — Dissertations ARE a valid source (they are reviewed academic work).
• existing theory — Existing theory IS a valid source and often frames the study.
• Internet — The internet can be a valid source when using reputable databases and websites (journals, WHO, government sites).

⭐ Key point: Prefer primary sources (original research reports) and recent, peer-reviewed literature (usually within the last 5–10 years).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q167$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2848, $s$The "mean" of a data set is:$s$, $s$["The most frequently occurring value", "The middle value when data are arranged in order", "The sum of all values divided by the number of values", "The difference between the highest and lowest values"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The sum of all values divided by the number of values
The MEAN is the arithmetic average: add up all the values and divide by how many values there are. Example: heart rates 70, 80 and 90 give (70 + 80 + 90) ÷ 3 = 80. The mean uses every value but is pulled by extreme values (outliers).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The most frequently occurring value — The most frequently occurring value is the MODE.
• The middle value when data are arranged in order — The middle value when the data are in order is the MEDIAN.
• The difference between the highest and lowest values — The difference between the highest and lowest values is the RANGE.

⭐ Key point: For skewed data (such as length of hospital stay), the median describes the middle better than the mean.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q168$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2849, $s$In most nursing and health research, a result is typically considered "statistically significant" when the p-value is:$s$, $s$["Greater than 0.05", "Less than or equal to 0.05", "Equal to 1.0", "Greater than 0.5"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Less than or equal to 0.05
A p-value of 0.05 or less (p ≤ 0.05) is the usual cut-off for statistical significance in health research. It means there is a 5% or smaller probability that the result happened by chance if the null hypothesis were true. So the researcher rejects the null hypothesis and accepts that there is likely a real difference or relationship.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Greater than 0.05 — A p-value above 0.05 is NOT significant; the result could easily be chance.
• Equal to 1.0 — p = 1.0 means no evidence at all against the null hypothesis.
• Greater than 0.5 — p above 0.5 is far from significant.

⭐ Key point: Statistical significance doesn't always mean clinical importance. Also look at the size of the effect and the confidence interval.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q169$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2850, $s$The Chi-square test is most appropriately used to analyse:$s$, $s$["The relationship or association between two categorical (nominal) variables", "The correlation between two continuous variables", "The mean difference between two groups", "The standard deviation of a single sample"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The relationship or association between two categorical (nominal) variables
The chi-square (χ²) test analyses the relationship between CATEGORICAL (nominal or ordinal) variables, using counts or frequencies in a table. Example: is there an association between gender (male/female) and developing VAP (yes/no)? It compares the observed numbers with the numbers expected if there were no relationship.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The correlation between two continuous variables — The correlation between two continuous variables uses Pearson's r (or Spearman's).
• The mean difference between two groups — The mean difference between two groups uses a t-test (or ANOVA for three or more groups).
• The standard deviation of a single sample — The standard deviation describes the spread of one sample; it is not a chi-square test.

⭐ Key point: Choose the test by data type: categories → chi-square; means of 2 groups → t-test; means of 3+ groups → ANOVA; relationship between 2 continuous variables → correlation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q170$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2851, $s$A quasi-experimental design differs from a true experimental design mainly because it$s$, $s$["Lacks random assignment of subjects to groups, though it may still involve manipulation of the independent variable", "Never involves any form of intervention", "Always has a larger sample size than true experiments", "Cannot be used to study cause-and-effect relationships at all"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lacks random assignment of subjects to groups, though it may still involve manipulation of the independent variable
A true experiment has three features: manipulation of the independent variable, a control group, and RANDOM assignment of participants to groups. A quasi-experimental design still tests an intervention but LACKS random assignment (and sometimes a control group), often because randomising is not possible or ethical in real wards. This makes it weaker for proving cause and effect.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Never involves any form of intervention — Quasi-experiments DO involve an intervention.
• Always has a larger sample size than true experiments — Sample size is not what defines the design.
• Cannot be used to study cause-and-effect relationships at all — They can suggest cause and effect, though less strongly than RCTs.

⭐ Key point: The randomised controlled trial (RCT) is the strongest single study design for testing treatments. A systematic review of RCTs sits at the top of the evidence hierarchy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q171$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2852, $s$'Reliability' of a research instrument refers to$s$, $s$["The consistency and stability with which the instrument produces the same results on repeated administration", "The degree to which the instrument measures the intended concept accurately", "The cost of producing and distributing the instrument", "The number of items contained in a questionnaire"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The consistency and stability with which the instrument produces the same results on repeated administration
RELIABILITY is how CONSISTENT and STABLE an instrument is: it gives the same result when used again under the same conditions, or by different people. Example: a reliable pain scale gives similar scores if the patient's pain hasn't changed. Types include test–retest, inter-rater reliability and internal consistency (Cronbach's alpha, aim for 0.7 or above).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The degree to which the instrument measures the intended concept accurately — Measuring the intended concept accurately is VALIDITY, not reliability.
• The cost of producing and distributing the instrument — The cost of an instrument is unrelated to reliability.
• The number of items contained in a questionnaire — The number of items does not define reliability.

⭐ Key point: Reliability = consistency. Validity = accuracy (measures what it should). A tool can be reliable but not valid, but it cannot be valid without being reliable.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q173$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2853, $s$Evaluation that occurs continuously throughout the implementation of care (e.g., during each shift) is called:$s$, $s$["Summative evaluation", "Formative (ongoing) evaluation", "Structure evaluation", "Terminal evaluation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Formative (ongoing) evaluation
FORMATIVE evaluation happens CONTINUOUSLY during care, for example each shift: checking the patient's response and adjusting the plan as you go. It "forms" the care while it is happening. In nursing, ongoing reassessment (such as pain scores after analgesia) is formative evaluation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Summative evaluation — Summative evaluation happens at the END (for example at discharge) to judge the final outcome.
• Structure evaluation — Structure evaluation looks at resources (staff, equipment, buildings), not ongoing patient care.
• Terminal evaluation — Terminal evaluation is another name for evaluation at the end, which is summative.

⭐ Key point: Formative = during (to improve). Summative = at the end (to judge the result).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 1 · Q183$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2854, $s$Which of the following is NOT one of the environmental components Nightingale identified as essential to a healthy healing environment?$s$, $s$["Pure/fresh air and proper ventilation", "Adequate light, especially direct sunlight", "Noise control and quiet", "Strict bedrest for all patients regardless of condition"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Strict bedrest for all patients regardless of condition
FLORENCE NIGHTINGALE's Environmental Theory says the nurse's role is to manage the ENVIRONMENT so that nature can heal the patient. Her key components: PURE/FRESH AIR and VENTILATION, PURE WATER, efficient DRAINAGE, CLEANLINESS, LIGHT (especially direct SUNLIGHT), QUIET (control of NOISE), WARMTH, proper DIET and nutrition, a clean bed and bedding, personal cleanliness, variety (to prevent boredom), and observation of the patient. She did NOT recommend strict bedrest for all patients regardless of condition — care was to be individualised, and she valued activity, variety and attention to each patient's needs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pure/fresh air and proper ventilation — PURE/FRESH AIR and ventilation IS one of her key components (often listed first).
• Adequate light, especially direct sunlight — Adequate LIGHT, especially sunlight, IS a key component.
• Noise control and quiet — NOISE control/quiet IS a key component; she warned that unnecessary noise harms patients.

⭐ Key point: Nightingale's environment: fresh air, pure water, drainage, cleanliness, LIGHT, QUIET, warmth, diet. Not "strict bedrest for all".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q151$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2855, $s$When selecting nursing interventions, the nurse should consider all of the following EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["The desired client outcome", "Evidence-based practice and research support", "The nurse's personal convenience over client needs", "The client's preferences and resources"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The nurse's personal convenience over client needs
When choosing NURSING INTERVENTIONS (the planning stage of the nursing process), the nurse should consider: (1) the DESIRED CLIENT OUTCOME/goal — interventions must help reach it; (2) EVIDENCE-BASED practice and research — use interventions proven to work; (3) the CLIENT'S PREFERENCES, values, culture and RESOURCES (money, family support, ability); (4) safety and the nurse's competence; (5) the nursing diagnosis and its related factors; (6) institutional policies and available resources. The NURSE'S PERSONAL CONVENIENCE must never be placed above the client's needs — care is patient-centred.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The desired client outcome — The DESIRED client outcome IS considered — interventions are chosen to achieve it.
• Evidence-based practice and research support — EVIDENCE-BASED practice IS considered — it makes interventions safer and more effective.
• The client's preferences and resources — The client's PREFERENCES and RESOURCES ARE considered — care must be acceptable and realistic for the patient.

⭐ Key point: Choose interventions by outcome, evidence, client preferences/resources, safety — never the nurse's personal convenience.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q152$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2856, $s$For a family experiencing acute grief after a patient's death in the ICU, which NIC intervention is most appropriate?$s$, $s$["Grief Work Facilitation", "Airway Suctioning", "Electrolyte Management", "Positioning"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Grief Work Facilitation
NIC (Nursing Interventions Classification) is a standard list of nursing interventions. For a FAMILY in ACUTE GRIEF after a death in ICU, the appropriate NIC intervention is GRIEF WORK FACILITATION — assisting people to resolve a significant loss. Activities include: being present and listening, encouraging expression of feelings, allowing the family time with the body, explaining what happened in simple language, respecting cultural and religious practices (e.g. Islamic burial within 24 hours, Christian prayers), offering chaplain/imam support, identifying support systems, and giving information about bereavement services. Other related NIC interventions: family support, emotional support, spiritual support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Airway Suctioning — AIRWAY SUCTIONING is a physical intervention for a patient's airway; it does nothing for the bereaved family.
• Electrolyte Management — ELECTROLYTE MANAGEMENT is a physiological intervention for a living patient.
• Positioning — POSITIONING is for a patient's comfort/physiology, not grief support for a family.

⭐ Key point: Grieving family → NIC "GRIEF WORK FACILITATION": presence, listening, cultural/spiritual support, time with the body.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q154$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2857, $s$When two nursing diagnoses appear closely related, what should the nurse do first to determine which diagnosis most accurately reflects the needs of a patient?$s$, $s$["Reassess the patient", "Examine the related to factors", "Analyze the secondary to factors", "Review the defining characteristics"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Review the defining characteristics
Each NANDA-I nursing diagnosis has DEFINING CHARACTERISTICS — the signs and symptoms (cues) that must be present for that diagnosis. When two diagnoses look similar (e.g. "Ineffective Airway Clearance" vs "Ineffective Breathing Pattern", or "Anxiety" vs "Fear"), the nurse should first REVIEW THE DEFINING CHARACTERISTICS of each and compare them with the patient's data. The diagnosis whose defining characteristics best match the patient's actual cues is the most accurate. Example: Fear has an identifiable source; Anxiety has a vague, non-specific source.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reassess the patient — REASSESSING the patient may be needed if data are missing, but the first step in choosing between two diagnoses is comparing defining characteristics with the data already collected.
• Examine the related to factors — "Related to" factors describe the CAUSE (aetiology); they help write the statement but do not best distinguish which diagnosis is present.
• Analyze the secondary to factors — "Secondary to" usually refers to a medical condition; nursing diagnoses are not chosen based on medical diagnoses.

⭐ Key point: To choose between similar nursing diagnoses → compare DEFINING CHARACTERISTICS (signs/symptoms) with the patient's cues.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q156$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2858, $s$If a goal states a patient will gain 2kg in 4 weeks, and the patient gains only 1kg, the goal is classified as:$s$, $s$["Met", "Partially Met", "Not Met", "Cannot be evaluated"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Partially Met
In the EVALUATION stage of the nursing process, the nurse compares the patient's actual response with the expected outcome (goal). There are three results: GOAL MET — the patient achieved the full outcome by the target date; GOAL PARTIALLY MET — the patient made SOME progress but did not reach the full outcome; GOAL NOT MET — no progress, or got worse. Here the goal was 2 kg weight gain in 4 weeks; the patient gained 1 kg — progress, but only half the target, so the goal is PARTIALLY MET. The nurse should then find out why (intake, illness, plan problems), and revise the plan: continue, modify interventions or change the time frame.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Met — MET would require the full 2 kg gain.
• Not Met — NOT MET would mean no gain at all (or weight loss).
• Cannot be evaluated — It CAN be evaluated — the goal is measurable (kg) and time-bound (4 weeks).

⭐ Key point: Evaluation: Met (full), PARTIALLY MET (some progress), Not met (none). 1 kg of a 2 kg goal = partially met.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q157$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2859, $s$A subtle but important principle of re-assessment is that:$s$, $s$["A goal that appears met always means the problem is fully solved", "A goal may be met on paper while the underlying problem remains unsolved", "Re-assessment is unnecessary once a goal is achieved", "Only unmet goals require re-assessment"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A goal may be met on paper while the underlying problem remains unsolved
RE-ASSESSMENT is an ongoing part of the nursing process. A goal written in a care plan can appear MET "ON PAPER" (e.g. "patient will report pain less than 3/10") while the UNDERLYING PROBLEM is still there (e.g. the patient reports less pain only because they are afraid of injections, or pain falls only when sedated, or a wound has not healed). Good nurses look beyond the goal statement: they check the actual condition of the patient, the cause of the problem, and whether new problems have developed, then update the care plan. Goals should therefore be measurable, realistic and linked to the real problem.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A goal that appears met always means the problem is fully solved — A met goal does NOT always mean the problem is fully solved — that is the subtle point of this question.
• Re-assessment is unnecessary once a goal is achieved — Re-assessment is NOT unnecessary once a goal is met; the patient's condition can change and new problems may appear.
• Only unmet goals require re-assessment — ALL goals, met and unmet, need re-assessment — not only unmet ones.

⭐ Key point: A goal can be met on paper while the real problem remains — ALWAYS re-assess the patient, not just the goal statement.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q158$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2860, $s$A client with well-controlled type 2 diabetes who exercises regularly, follows a prescribed diet, and reports high energy and life satisfaction would most likely be placed toward which end of the health-illness continuum?$s$, $s$["Toward the high-level wellness end", "Toward the death end", "Exactly at the neutral, disease-free point only", "Outside the continuum, since a chronic disease disqualifies wellness placement"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Toward the high-level wellness end
The HEALTH–ILLNESS CONTINUUM (Dunn/Travis) shows health as a range from HIGH-LEVEL WELLNESS at one end to PREMATURE DEATH at the other, not as simply "sick or not sick". A person's position depends on their whole state — physical, mental, social and emotional — and their ability to function and achieve their potential. A person with a CHRONIC disease (e.g. well-controlled type 2 diabetes) can still be near the HIGH-LEVEL WELLNESS end if they manage the condition well: regular exercise, following the diet, taking medications, feeling energetic and satisfied with life. Nurses use this idea to promote wellness in every patient.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Toward the death end — Toward the DEATH end would mean severe illness and poor function — not this active, satisfied person.
• Exactly at the neutral, disease-free point only — The continuum is not about a single "neutral, disease-free" point; this patient has a disease but high wellness.
• Outside the continuum, since a chronic disease disqualifies wellness placement — A chronic disease does NOT exclude a person from the continuum; everyone is somewhere on it.

⭐ Key point: Health–illness continuum: a person with a well-managed chronic disease can be near HIGH-LEVEL WELLNESS.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q159$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2861, $s$A client newly diagnosed with heart failure must learn to monitor daily weights, restrict sodium, and recognize signs of fluid overload. This need is best classified as which type of self-care requisite?$s$, $s$["Universal self-care requisite", "Developmental self-care requisite", "Health-deviation self-care requisite", "Environmental self-care requisite"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Health-deviation self-care requisite
OREM'S SELF-CARE DEFICIT THEORY describes three types of SELF-CARE REQUISITES (needs): (1) UNIVERSAL — needs everyone has: air, water, food, elimination, activity and rest, solitude and social interaction, safety, normality; (2) DEVELOPMENTAL — needs linked to life stages and events (pregnancy, adolescence, ageing, bereavement); (3) HEALTH-DEVIATION — needs that arise because of ILLNESS, injury, disability or medical treatment: learning to live with and manage a disease. A patient NEWLY DIAGNOSED with HEART FAILURE who must learn DAILY WEIGHTS, SODIUM RESTRICTION and signs of FLUID OVERLOAD has HEALTH-DEVIATION self-care requisites.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Universal self-care requisite — UNIVERSAL requisites are basic needs of all people, not disease-specific learning.
• Developmental self-care requisite — DEVELOPMENTAL requisites relate to life stages and maturation, not a new illness.
• Environmental self-care requisite — "Environmental self-care requisite" is not one of Orem's three categories.

⭐ Key point: Orem: universal (everyone), developmental (life stages), HEALTH-DEVIATION (needs caused by illness/treatment).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q160$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2862, $s$Serum potassium level measured in mmol/L is an example of which scale of measurement?$s$, $s$["Nominal", "Ordinal", "Interval", "Ratio"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ratio
There are four LEVELS (scales) OF MEASUREMENT — memory aid "NOIR": NOMINAL — named categories with no order (blood group, gender, tribe); ORDINAL — ordered categories but unequal gaps (pain mild/moderate/severe, GCS, education level, Likert scale); INTERVAL — equal intervals but NO TRUE ZERO (temperature in °C or °F — 0 °C does not mean "no temperature"; you cannot say 40 °C is twice as hot as 20 °C); RATIO — equal intervals AND a TRUE ZERO (zero means none of it), so ratios make sense: weight, height, age, pulse, BP, urine output, SERUM POTASSIUM in mmol/L (0 mmol/L = no potassium; 6 mmol/L is twice 3 mmol/L). Ratio is the highest level and allows all statistics.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nominal — NOMINAL is only labels/categories — potassium is a measured number.
• Ordinal — ORDINAL shows rank order only — potassium has equal, exact intervals.
• Interval — INTERVAL has no true zero — potassium has a true zero.

⭐ Key point: NOIR: Nominal (names), Ordinal (order), Interval (no true zero, e.g. °C), RATIO (true zero — weight, K⁺ in mmol/L).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q164$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2863, $s$The Latin term 'Op cit' is used when:$s$, $s$["A source already cited is referenced again, with other citations in between", "Citing a source for the very first time", "No source is being cited at all", "Citing an unpublished thesis only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A source already cited is referenced again, with other citations in between
Latin referencing terms (used in footnote/endnote styles like Oxford/Chicago): IBID. (ibidem, "in the same place") — the source is the SAME as the IMMEDIATELY preceding citation, with nothing in between. OP. CIT. (opere citato, "in the work cited") — refers to a source ALREADY CITED earlier, but with OTHER CITATIONS IN BETWEEN; you give the author's surname plus "op. cit." and the page number. LOC. CIT. (loco citato, "in the place cited") — the same source AND the same page as an earlier citation, with others in between. Et al. — "and others" (multiple authors).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Citing a source for the very first time — Citing a source for the FIRST time requires the FULL reference, not op. cit.
• No source is being cited at all — If no source is cited, no Latin abbreviation is used at all.
• Citing an unpublished thesis only — Op. cit. is not specific to unpublished theses; it can refer to any previously cited work.

⭐ Key point: IBID = same source as the one just before. OP. CIT. = source cited earlier with OTHER citations in between. Loc. cit. = same page.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q165$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2864, $s$A researcher wants to test whether a new positioning technique reduces pressure ulcer incidence. The dependent variable in this study would be:$s$, $s$["The positioning technique used", "Pressure ulcer incidence, since it is the outcome being measured", "The patient's age", "The researcher's experience level"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pressure ulcer incidence, since it is the outcome being measured
In an experiment, the INDEPENDENT VARIABLE (IV) is what the researcher CHANGES or applies — the "cause" or intervention (here, the new POSITIONING TECHNIQUE). The DEPENDENT VARIABLE (DV) is the OUTCOME the researcher MEASURES to see if it changes because of the IV — the "effect". Here the outcome being measured is PRESSURE ULCER INCIDENCE, so it is the dependent variable (it "depends on" the positioning technique). Memory aid: "The DV Depends on the IV." Other factors that might affect the outcome (age, nutrition, mobility, the researcher's experience) are EXTRANEOUS/CONFOUNDING variables to be controlled.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The positioning technique used — The POSITIONING TECHNIQUE is the INDEPENDENT variable (the intervention).
• The patient's age — The patient's AGE is a possible confounding/extraneous variable, not the outcome.
• The researcher's experience level — The researcher's EXPERIENCE is an extraneous variable that could bias results, not the dependent variable.

⭐ Key point: Independent variable = intervention (positioning). DEPENDENT variable = measured OUTCOME (pressure ulcer incidence).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q166$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2865, $s$Which is NOT a probability sampling method?$s$, $s$["Simple random sampling", "Cluster sampling", "Stratified random sampling", "Convenience sampling"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Convenience sampling
PROBABILITY sampling means every member of the population has a KNOWN, non-zero chance of being selected (random selection), so results can be generalised. Types: SIMPLE RANDOM (lottery, random number table), SYSTEMATIC (every k-th person from a list), STRATIFIED RANDOM (divide into groups/strata, then random sample from each), CLUSTER (randomly select whole groups, e.g. wards or villages), multistage. NON-PROBABILITY sampling does not use random selection: CONVENIENCE (accidental — whoever is easily available), purposive (judgement), quota, snowball. Convenience sampling is common in nursing research but has a high risk of selection bias.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple random sampling — SIMPLE RANDOM sampling IS probability sampling — everyone has an equal chance.
• Cluster sampling — CLUSTER sampling IS probability sampling — clusters are randomly selected.
• Stratified random sampling — STRATIFIED RANDOM sampling IS probability sampling — random selection within strata.

⭐ Key point: Probability = random (simple, systematic, stratified, cluster). CONVENIENCE, purposive, quota, snowball = NON-probability.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q168$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2866, $s$In the PICO framework for developing a clinical research question, the letters stand for:$s$, $s$["Population, Intervention, Comparison, Outcome", "Problem, Investigation, Cost, Objective", "Patient, Illness, Cure, Observation", "Plan, Implement, Coordinate, Overview"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Population, Intervention, Comparison, Outcome
PICO is a framework to write a focused, answerable CLINICAL QUESTION and guide the literature search. P — POPULATION/Patient/Problem (who? e.g. ventilated adults in ICU); I — INTERVENTION (what is being done? e.g. chlorhexidine oral care); C — COMPARISON (compared with what? e.g. saline oral care, or usual care); O — OUTCOME (what result? e.g. ventilator-associated pneumonia rate). Example: "In ventilated ICU adults (P), does chlorhexidine oral care (I) compared with saline (C) reduce VAP (O)?" Some versions add T — TIME (PICOT).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Problem, Investigation, Cost, Objective — "Problem, Investigation, Cost, Objective" is not the PICO framework.
• Patient, Illness, Cure, Observation — "Patient, Illness, Cure, Observation" is not correct — I = Intervention, C = Comparison, O = Outcome.
• Plan, Implement, Coordinate, Overview — "Plan, Implement, Coordinate, Overview" are management words, not PICO.

⭐ Key point: PICO = Population, Intervention, Comparison, Outcome (+ Time = PICOT).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q170$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2867, $s$The science of collectiong, organizing, analyzing, interpreting and presenting data to asist in making more effective decisions is known as:$s$, $s$["Data", "Inference", "Statistics.", "Sample"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Statistics.
STATISTICS is the science of COLLECTING, ORGANISING, ANALYSING, INTERPRETING and PRESENTING DATA to help make better decisions. It has two main branches: DESCRIPTIVE statistics — summarise data (mean, median, mode, standard deviation, percentages, tables, charts); INFERENTIAL statistics — use data from a SAMPLE to draw conclusions about a POPULATION (t-test, chi-square, correlation, p-values, confidence intervals). In nursing, statistics is used in research, audit, infection rates, and evidence-based decision-making.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Data — DATA are the raw facts and figures that statistics works on — not the science itself.
• Inference — INFERENCE is the process of drawing conclusions about a population from a sample — one part of statistics.
• Sample — A SAMPLE is a subset of the population chosen for study — not the science of analysing data.

⭐ Key point: STATISTICS = collecting, organising, analysing, interpreting and presenting data. Descriptive + inferential.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q172$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2868, $s$The 'back matter' of a research report typically includes:$s$, $s$["Reference, Bibliography, and Appendix", "Only the title page", "The introduction and literature review", "The abstract only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reference, Bibliography, and Appendix
The BACK MATTER of a research report is the material that comes AFTER the main text (after the conclusion and recommendations). It typically includes: REFERENCES (works cited in the text), BIBLIOGRAPHY (works consulted, whether cited or not), and APPENDICES/APPENDIX (supporting documents such as the questionnaire, consent forms, ethical approval letter, raw data tables, maps), and sometimes a glossary or index. The FRONT matter is the title page, certification, dedication, acknowledgements, abstract and table of contents. The MAIN BODY holds introduction, literature review, methodology, results and discussion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only the title page — The TITLE PAGE is FRONT matter (the first page).
• The introduction and literature review — The INTRODUCTION and LITERATURE REVIEW are part of the MAIN BODY (chapters 1 and 2).
• The abstract only — The ABSTRACT is FRONT matter, placed near the beginning.

⭐ Key point: Back matter = REFERENCES, BIBLIOGRAPHY, APPENDIX. Front matter = title page, abstract, contents.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 2 · Q173$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2869, $s$On the Travis illness-wellness continuum, the neutral point at the center of the scale represents what?$s$, $s$["Death", "Peak, high-level wellness", "An absence of discernible signs or symptoms of disease, without the presence of positive wellness behaviors", "The point of maximum disability"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An absence of discernible signs or symptoms of disease, without the presence of positive wellness behaviors
On TRAVIS'S ILLNESS-WELLNESS CONTINUUM, the NEUTRAL POINT in the middle represents an ABSENCE of discernible signs or symptoms of DISEASE, WITHOUT POSITIVE WELLNESS BEHAVIOURS. Moving left leads to illness, disability and premature death; moving right (through awareness, education and growth) leads to high-level wellness.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Death — Death is at the extreme left end.
• Peak, high-level wellness — Peak wellness is at the extreme right end.
• The point of maximum disability — Maximum disability is towards the left end.

⭐ Key point: Health promotion aims to move people towards high-level wellness, not just the absence of disease.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q151$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2870, $s$A nurse writes the goal: "Client will ambulate 30 meters with a walker by discharge on 07/28/2026." Which SMART element is reflected by the phrase "by discharge on 07/28/2026"?$s$, $s$["Specific", "Measurable", "Achievable", "Time-bound"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Time-bound
SMART goals make outcomes clear and measurable: S = SPECIFIC (clearly states what will be done — "ambulate 30 metres with a walker"); M = MEASURABLE (can be counted — "30 metres"); A = ACHIEVABLE (realistic for this client); R = RELEVANT/REALISTIC (related to the client's problem); T = TIME-BOUND (has a clear DEADLINE). The phrase "BY DISCHARGE ON 07/28/2026" sets the deadline, so it reflects the TIME-BOUND element.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Specific — "Specific" is reflected by the clear description of the activity.
• Measurable — "Measurable" is reflected by "30 metres".
• Achievable — "Achievable" is about whether the goal is realistic for this patient.

⭐ Key point: Without a time frame, it is impossible to evaluate whether a goal has been met on time.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q152$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2871, $s$Evaluation in the nursing process is best described as:$s$, $s$["A one-time final step only", "An ongoing procedure of re-assessment", "Something done only by physicians", "Unrelated to the original care plan"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: An ongoing procedure of re-assessment
EVALUATION in the nursing process is an ONGOING process of RE-ASSESSMENT, checking whether the patient is achieving the expected outcomes, and modifying the plan as needed. It occurs continuously, not just at the end.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A one-time final step only — It is not a one-time final step.
• Something done only by physicians — Nurses carry out evaluation, not only physicians.
• Unrelated to the original care plan — It is directly related to the original care plan's goals.

⭐ Key point: Evaluation outcomes: goal met, partially met, or not met.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q155$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2872, $s$Which activity best describes the evaluation phase?$s$, $s$["Interpret data and identify client problems", "Establish short- and long-term goals", "Reassess and compare outcomes with the set objectives", "Carry out nursing procedures and document"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Reassess and compare outcomes with the set objectives
The EVALUATION phase is the final step of the nursing process (ADPIE), but it is ongoing. The nurse REASSESSES the patient (collects new data) and COMPARES the actual OUTCOMES with the expected outcomes (objectives/goals) set during planning. Based on this, the nurse decides whether the goal is MET, PARTIALLY MET or NOT MET, and whether to continue, modify or end the care plan.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Interpret data and identify client problems — Interpreting data and identifying client problems is the DIAGNOSIS phase.
• Establish short- and long-term goals — Establishing short- and long-term goals is the PLANNING phase.
• Carry out nursing procedures and document — Carrying out nursing procedures and documenting them is the IMPLEMENTATION phase.

⭐ Key point: If a goal is not met, ask why: was the diagnosis wrong, the goal unrealistic, or the interventions ineffective?$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q156$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2873, $s$A nurse repositions a patient who complains of difficulty breathing. This is which phase of the nursing process?$s$, $s$["Implementation", "Assessment", "Evaluation", "Outcome identification"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Implementation
The IMPLEMENTATION phase of the nursing process is when the nurse CARRIES OUT the planned nursing interventions. REPOSITIONING a patient who has DIFFICULTY BREATHING (e.g. sitting them upright or in high Fowler's position to improve lung expansion) is a nursing ACTION — so it is IMPLEMENTATION. After this, the nurse would EVALUATE whether breathing has improved (respiratory rate, SpO2, work of breathing).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assessment — Assessment is collecting data (e.g. listening to the patient's complaint, measuring SpO2).
• Evaluation — Evaluation is checking the response after the action.
• Outcome identification — Outcome identification is part of planning (setting goals).

⭐ Key point: Implementation includes independent, dependent and collaborative nursing actions, followed by documentation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q159$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2874, $s$A Nurse at HMC asked a patient to verbalise if he understood the information given during nursing care to the client according to a devised nursing care plan. During which step of the nursing process does such action take place?$s$, $s$["Assessment", "Planning", "Implementation", "Evaluation"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Evaluation
Asking the patient to VERBALISE (explain in their own words) whether they UNDERSTOOD the information given during care checks whether the planned TEACHING OUTCOME has been achieved. This is EVALUATION — comparing the patient's response with the expected outcome (e.g. "The patient will verbalise understanding of their medication regimen").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assessment — Assessment is collecting initial data about the patient's needs.
• Planning — Planning is setting goals and choosing interventions.
• Implementation — Implementation is giving the teaching itself.

⭐ Key point: The "teach-back" method is an effective way to evaluate understanding.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q160$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2875, $s$The final step of the research process is typically considered to be$s$, $s$["Disseminating or reporting the research findings to relevant audiences", "Formulating the research hypothesis", "Reviewing related literature", "Selecting the sample"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Disseminating or reporting the research findings to relevant audiences
The RESEARCH PROCESS typically follows these steps: identify the problem, review the literature, develop a framework, formulate objectives/hypotheses, choose a design, select the sample, collect data, analyse data, interpret findings, and finally DISSEMINATE (communicate) the FINDINGS through reports, journal articles, conference presentations or workshops, so that others can use them to improve practice.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Formulating the research hypothesis — Formulating hypotheses is an early step.
• Reviewing related literature — Reviewing literature is an early step (though it continues throughout).
• Selecting the sample — Selecting the sample is a middle step.

⭐ Key point: Research is only useful if its findings are shared and applied in practice (research utilisation).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q163$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2876, $s$Number of ICU admissions per month at a hospital is an example of which scale of measurement?$s$, $s$["Nominal", "Ordinal", "Interval", "Ratio"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ratio
Data are measured on four scales: NOMINAL (categories without order), ORDINAL (ranked categories), INTERVAL (equal intervals, no true zero), and RATIO (equal intervals AND a TRUE ZERO). The NUMBER OF ICU ADMISSIONS per month is a count: zero admissions means none at all (true zero), and 20 admissions is twice as many as 10. So it is RATIO data.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nominal — Nominal data are categories (e.g. blood group, sex).
• Ordinal — Ordinal data are ranks (e.g. pain mild/moderate/severe).
• Interval — Interval data have no true zero (e.g. temperature in °C).

⭐ Key point: Ratio data allow all statistical calculations, including ratios ("twice as many").$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q164$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2877, $s$A study finds a statistically significant but very small difference between two treatments (e.g. a 1% difference in outcome) in a very large sample. This illustrates the important distinction between:$s$, $s$["Statistical significance and clinical significance/relevance", "Reliability and validity, which are unrelated concepts here", "Qualitative and quantitative research", "Ethics and methodology"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Statistical significance and clinical significance/relevance
STATISTICAL SIGNIFICANCE (p < 0.05) means the result is unlikely due to chance. In a VERY LARGE sample, even TINY differences can be statistically significant. CLINICAL SIGNIFICANCE asks whether the difference is LARGE ENOUGH to MATTER to patients. A 1% difference may be statistically significant but not clinically important.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reliability and validity, which are unrelated concepts here — Reliability and validity are about measurement.
• Qualitative and quantitative research — This isn't about qualitative vs quantitative.
• Ethics and methodology — This isn't about ethics vs methodology.

⭐ Key point: Always ask: "Is this difference meaningful for patients?"$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q165$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2878, $s$The Latin term 'Ibid' is used when citing:$s$, $s$["The same work, the same page as just cited", "The same work, a different page from the last citation", "A completely different, unrelated work", "Only works published this year"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The same work, a different page from the last citation
"IBID." (from Latin ibidem, "in the same place") is used in footnote referencing to refer to the SAME WORK cited in the IMMEDIATELY PRECEDING note. In the teaching used for this exam, "Ibid" is used for the same work with a DIFFERENT PAGE (with the new page number given), while "loc. cit." is used for the same work and same page, and "op. cit." for a work cited earlier but not immediately before. (Many modern styles, such as APA, do not use these terms.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The same work, the same page as just cited — In this teaching, "same work, same page" is "loc. cit." rather than ibid.
• A completely different, unrelated work — A completely different work would be cited in full.
• Only works published this year — Ibid is not limited by publication year.

⭐ Key point: Follow the referencing style required by your institution.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q166$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2879, $s$In defining the scope of a study, which is NOT necessarily indicated?$s$, $s$["The period of study", "The population of study", "The setting of the study", "The significance of the study"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The significance of the study
The SCOPE of a study defines its boundaries: the PERIOD (time frame), the POPULATION (who), and the SETTING (where), and sometimes the variables studied. The SIGNIFICANCE of the study (why it is important) is a separate section, not part of the scope.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The period of study — The period IS part of the scope.
• The population of study — The population IS part of the scope.
• The setting of the study — The setting IS part of the scope.

⭐ Key point: Scope and limitations are usually stated in Chapter One of a research project.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q167$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2880, $s$Process of research means the following EXCEPT$s$, $s$["Identifying a problem", "Defining the problem in precise terms in unambiguous terms", "Making policies", "Designing the research"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Making policies
The RESEARCH PROCESS involves systematic steps to generate knowledge: IDENTIFYING a PROBLEM, DEFINING it precisely and unambiguously, reviewing literature, formulating objectives and hypotheses, DESIGNING the research, selecting a sample, collecting and analysing data, interpreting results, and disseminating findings. MAKING POLICIES is NOT part of the research process — policies are made by managers or governments, who may USE research findings as evidence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Identifying a problem — Identifying a problem IS part of the research process.
• Defining the problem in precise terms in unambiguous terms — Defining the problem precisely IS part of the process.
• Designing the research — Designing the research IS part of the process.

⭐ Key point: Evidence-based policy uses research findings, but policy-making itself is a separate process.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q168$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2881, $s$Common electronic databases for nurse researchers include all EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["CINAHL", "PubMed", "MEDLINE", "American Nurses Association"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: American Nurses Association
Nurse researchers use ELECTRONIC DATABASES to search for published literature: CINAHL (nursing and allied health), PUBMED (free search interface for biomedical literature), MEDLINE (the main biomedical database, accessed via PubMed), Cochrane Library, Embase and PsycINFO. The AMERICAN NURSES ASSOCIATION (ANA) is a PROFESSIONAL ORGANISATION, not a literature database (although it publishes standards and journals).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• CINAHL — CINAHL IS a database.
• PubMed — PubMed IS a database search engine.
• MEDLINE — MEDLINE IS a database.

⭐ Key point: Use clear search terms and Boolean operators (AND, OR, NOT) to find relevant studies efficiently.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q172$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2882, $s$A nurse researcher wants to study the lived experience of ICU patients after discharge. The most appropriate research approach for this question is:$s$, $s$["A strictly quantitative, numerical survey with no narrative data", "A qualitative approach (e.g. interviews) to capture rich, descriptive experiential data", "No research method is appropriate for this topic", "A laboratory experiment"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A qualitative approach (e.g. interviews) to capture rich, descriptive experiential data
To study the LIVED EXPERIENCE of ICU patients after discharge, a QUALITATIVE approach (e.g. in-depth INTERVIEWS, phenomenology) is most appropriate, because it captures rich, descriptive data about people's experiences and meanings.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A strictly quantitative, numerical survey with no narrative data — A purely numerical survey can't capture lived experience in depth.
• No research method is appropriate for this topic — Research is possible on this topic.
• A laboratory experiment — A laboratory experiment is not appropriate.

⭐ Key point: Qualitative findings help nurses understand patients' perspectives and improve care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 3 · Q173$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2883, $s$What is the primary goal of a ventricular assist device (VAD) in critical care?$s$, $s$["To monitor blood glucose levels", "To provide mechanical ventilation", "To support heart function and circulation", "To administer antibiotics"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: To support heart function and circulation
A VENTRICULAR ASSIST DEVICE (VAD) is a mechanical pump that SUPPORTS HEART FUNCTION and CIRCULATION when the heart is too weak to pump enough blood. It can be a bridge to recovery, to heart transplant, or long-term therapy.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• To monitor blood glucose levels — It doesn't monitor glucose.
• To provide mechanical ventilation — It doesn't provide mechanical ventilation.
• To administer antibiotics — It doesn't administer antibiotics.

⭐ Key point: VAD patients need anticoagulation and careful monitoring for bleeding, clots and infection.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q67$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2884, $s$Which of the following gestational hormones is hypothesized to be correlated with hyperemesis gravidarum?$s$, $s$["Estrogen", "Testosterone", "Human placental lactogen (HPL)", "Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Human chorionic gonadotropin (hCG
HYPEREMESIS GRAVIDARUM is severe vomiting in pregnancy. It is strongly linked to HUMAN CHORIONIC GONADOTROPIN (hCG): hCG levels peak at about 9–12 weeks, the same time symptoms are worst, and conditions with very high hCG — MULTIPLE PREGNANCY (twins) and MOLAR PREGNANCY — are associated with more severe vomiting. hCG may stimulate the vomiting centre and also the thyroid (causing transient hyperthyroidism). Recent research also links a hormone called GDF15.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Estrogen — Oestrogen may contribute, but hCG is the most strongly linked hormone.
• Testosterone — Testosterone is not linked.
• Human placental lactogen (HPL) — Human placental lactogen (HPL) is linked to insulin resistance, not hyperemesis.

⭐ Key point: Manage with IV fluids, electrolyte replacement, anti-emetics, thiamine, and an ultrasound to check for twins or molar pregnancy.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q140$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2885, $s$In what year did Yura and Walsh formally describe the nursing process as consisting of four steps?$s$, $s$["1955", "1961", "1967", "1973"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 1967
In 1967, YURA and WALSH published a book describing the NURSING PROCESS as having FOUR STEPS: ASSESSING, PLANNING, IMPLEMENTING and EVALUATING. Later (after NANDA was formed in 1973), "DIAGNOSIS" was added as a separate step, giving the five-step process (ADPIE).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1955 — 1955 is when Lydia Hall first used the term "nursing process".
• 1961 — 1961 is when Ida Jean Orlando described the deliberative nursing process.
• 1973 — 1973 is when the first national conference on nursing diagnosis (later NANDA) was held.

⭐ Key point: The nursing process is systematic, client-centred, goal-oriented and cyclical.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q153$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2886, $s$Formative evaluation is best described as:$s$, $s$["A one-time review done only at discharge", "Ongoing judgment of the intervention as it is being implemented", "Only relevant to research studies", "The same thing as summative evaluation"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ongoing judgment of the intervention as it is being implemented
FORMATIVE EVALUATION is the ONGOING assessment of an intervention or care plan WHILE it is being IMPLEMENTED. It allows the nurse to make changes as care progresses (e.g. adjusting pain relief based on hourly pain scores).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A one-time review done only at discharge — A one-time review at discharge is SUMMATIVE evaluation.
• Only relevant to research studies — Formative evaluation is used in clinical practice, education and research.
• The same thing as summative evaluation — Summative evaluation is a final judgement at the end.

⭐ Key point: Formative = during (to improve); summative = at the end (to judge the outcome).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q155$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2887, $s$At which step of the nursing process is the PES statement written?$s$, $s$["Planning", "Implementation", "Assessment", "Diagnosis"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Diagnosis
The PES statement is the standard format for an ACTUAL NURSING DIAGNOSIS: P = PROBLEM (the NANDA diagnostic label, e.g. "Impaired gas exchange"), E = ETIOLOGY (related factors — "related to…"), S = SIGNS and SYMPTOMS (defining characteristics — "as evidenced by…"). It is written during the DIAGNOSIS step of the nursing process, after the nurse has collected and analysed assessment data. Example: "Acute pain related to surgical incision as evidenced by pain score 8/10, grimacing and guarding."

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Planning — Planning comes after diagnosis: setting goals and choosing interventions.
• Implementation — Implementation is carrying out the planned interventions.
• Assessment — Assessment is collecting data — it comes before diagnosis.

⭐ Key point: Risk diagnoses use a two-part format (problem + risk factors), because there are no signs or symptoms yet.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q156$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2888, $s$A nurse develops a care plan with the goal 'Patient will ambulate 50 metres independently by discharge.' At reassessment, the patient can walk 30 metres with assistance. This goal should be documented as:$s$, $s$["Fully met", "Partially met, requiring plan revision", "Not applicable", "Completely unmet with no progress"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Partially met, requiring plan revision
The goal was "walk 50 metres INDEPENDENTLY by discharge". The patient now walks 30 METRES WITH ASSISTANCE — some progress has been made, but the goal is not fully achieved (both the distance and independence are not met). This should be documented as PARTIALLY MET, and the PLAN REVISED (e.g. more physiotherapy, extended time frame, walking aids).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Fully met — The goal is not fully met.
• Not applicable — The goal is still applicable.
• Completely unmet with no progress — There has been progress, so it is not completely unmet.

⭐ Key point: Evaluation compares outcomes with goals and leads to continuing, modifying or ending the plan.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q157$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2889, $s$Nursing diagnoses differ from medical diagnoses mainly in that they are:$s$, $s$["Dependent on the medical diagnosis for direction", "Concerned with caring while medical diagnoses are concerned with curing", "Concerned with human responses while medical diagnoses are concerned with pathology", "Concerned with psychosocial parameters only"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Concerned with human responses while medical diagnoses are concerned with pathology
NURSING DIAGNOSES describe the patient's HUMAN RESPONSES to health problems or life processes (e.g. "Acute pain", "Impaired mobility", "Anxiety"). MEDICAL DIAGNOSES describe the DISEASE or PATHOLOGY (e.g. "Pneumonia", "Myocardial infarction"). Nursing diagnoses can change as the patient's responses change, while medical diagnoses usually remain until the disease resolves.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dependent on the medical diagnosis for direction — Nursing diagnoses are independent of medical diagnoses.
• Concerned with caring while medical diagnoses are concerned with curing — Both disciplines care and cure; this is not the main distinction.
• Concerned with psychosocial parameters only — Nursing diagnoses cover physical, psychological and social responses.

⭐ Key point: One medical diagnosis (e.g. stroke) can lead to many nursing diagnoses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q159$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2890, $s$A nurse using the health-illness continuum framework would assess a client holistically by including which of the following, in addition to physical signs and symptoms?$s$, $s$["Only the client's vital signs and laboratory values", "Only the admitting medical diagnosis", "The client's emotional, social, spiritual, and functional status", "Only the client's insurance status"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The client's emotional, social, spiritual, and functional status
The HEALTH-ILLNESS CONTINUUM sees health as a dynamic state ranging from high-level wellness to severe illness. HOLISTIC assessment includes the client's PHYSICAL, EMOTIONAL, SOCIAL, SPIRITUAL and FUNCTIONAL STATUS — all of which affect health and recovery.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only the client's vital signs and laboratory values — Vital signs and lab values are only part of the picture.
• Only the admitting medical diagnosis — The medical diagnosis alone is incomplete.
• Only the client's insurance status — Insurance status alone is irrelevant to holistic assessment.

⭐ Key point: Holistic care treats the whole person, not just the disease.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q161$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2891, $s$One of the stated advantages of the nursing process is that it:$s$, $s$["Promotes decisions based on routine, unquestioned habits", "Promotes individualised care and evidence-based decisions", "Removes the need for documentation", "Eliminates the need for reassessment"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Promotes individualised care and evidence-based decisions
The NURSING PROCESS (Assessment, Diagnosis, Planning, Implementation, Evaluation) is a systematic, problem-solving approach. Its advantages include: it PROMOTES INDIVIDUALISED CARE (care is based on each patient's own assessment and needs, not routine), and EVIDENCE-BASED DECISIONS (choices are based on data and research). It also improves continuity of care, communication between staff, documentation, patient involvement, and professional accountability.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Promotes decisions based on routine, unquestioned habits — The nursing process discourages routine, unquestioned habits — it requires thinking and justification.
• Removes the need for documentation — It requires documentation at each step.
• Eliminates the need for reassessment — It is cyclical and requires ongoing reassessment.

⭐ Key point: Evaluation feeds back into assessment, so the plan changes as the patient's condition changes.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q162$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2892, $s$Which scale of measurement has a true (absolute) zero point, allowing statements such as "twice as much"?$s$, $s$["Nominal", "Ordinal", "Interval", "Ratio"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ratio
The RATIO SCALE has ordered values, EQUAL INTERVALS and a TRUE (ABSOLUTE) ZERO (zero means none of the quantity). This allows statements like "twice as much". Examples: weight, height, age, pulse rate, urine output, temperature in Kelvin.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Nominal — Nominal data are categories with no order (e.g. blood group).
• Ordinal — Ordinal data have order but unequal intervals (e.g. pain mild/moderate/severe).
• Interval — Interval data have equal intervals but no true zero (e.g. temperature in °C).

⭐ Key point: Ratio and interval data allow means and standard deviations to be calculated.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q163$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2893, $s$The main purpose of including a theoretical or conceptual framework in a research study is to$s$, $s$["Provide a logical structure that explains the relationships among the study's key concepts and guides interpretation of findings", "Increase the length of the proposal without adding any value", "Replace the need for a literature review entirely", "Serve only as a decorative diagram with no functional role"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Provide a logical structure that explains the relationships among the study's key concepts and guides interpretation of findings
A THEORETICAL or CONCEPTUAL FRAMEWORK provides a LOGICAL STRUCTURE explaining the RELATIONSHIPS among the study's key CONCEPTS. It guides the research questions, design, choice of variables and instruments, and helps INTERPRET the FINDINGS.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase the length of the proposal without adding any value — It adds value; it is not just length.
• Replace the need for a literature review entirely — It does not replace the literature review.
• Serve only as a decorative diagram with no functional role — It has a functional role.

⭐ Key point: Many nursing studies use nursing theories (e.g. Orem, Roy) as frameworks.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q164$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2894, $s$Which is a characteristic of quantitative research?$s$, $s$["There are multiple subjective realities", "Data are reported in a literary style", "No generalisation is made from the findings", "A large sample is emphasised"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A large sample is emphasised
QUANTITATIVE RESEARCH measures variables with NUMBERS and uses statistics to describe, test relationships, or establish cause and effect. It usually emphasises a LARGE SAMPLE (often randomly selected), because larger samples are more representative of the population, reduce random error, and give enough STATISTICAL POWER to detect real differences. This allows findings to be GENERALISED to the wider population.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• There are multiple subjective realities — "Multiple subjective realities" is a QUALITATIVE assumption (each person's reality is valid).
• Data are reported in a literary style — Reporting in a literary, narrative style is typical of QUALITATIVE research.
• No generalisation is made from the findings — Quantitative research DOES aim to generalise findings.

⭐ Key point: Sample size for quantitative studies is often calculated in advance using a power analysis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q165$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2895, $s$In a bibliography, the correct group order for arranging sources is:$s$, $s$["Journals → Books → Magazines/Newspapers → Other publications", "Books → Journals → Other publications → Magazines/Newspapers", "Random order within each letter of the alphabet", "Newest to oldest publication date"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Journals → Books → Magazines/Newspapers → Other publications
In some referencing guidelines used in nursing schools, sources in a bibliography are grouped by TYPE, often in the order: JOURNALS → BOOKS → MAGAZINES/NEWSPAPERS → OTHER PUBLICATIONS, and arranged alphabetically within each group. (Note: many modern styles, such as APA, list all references together alphabetically — always follow the required style.)

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Books → Journals → Other publications → Magazines/Newspapers — The order Books → Journals → Other → Magazines is not the stated convention.
• Random order within each letter of the alphabet — Random order is never correct.
• Newest to oldest publication date — Newest to oldest is not standard.

⭐ Key point: Follow the referencing style required by your institution consistently.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q166$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2896, $s$The population to be sampled is divided into units which are known as:$s$, $s$["Sampling frame", "Sampling errors", "Sampling gap", "Sampling units."]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Sampling units.
Before sampling, the population is divided into SAMPLING UNITS — the individual elements or groups that can be selected for the study. A sampling unit may be a person (e.g. an ICU nurse), a group (e.g. a ward), or an organisation (e.g. a hospital). In cluster sampling, for example, the sampling units are clusters such as hospitals. The complete list of sampling units is the SAMPLING FRAME.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Sampling frame — The SAMPLING FRAME is the LIST of all units, not the units themselves.
• Sampling errors — SAMPLING ERRORS are differences between sample results and the true population values.
• Sampling gap — "Sampling gap" is not a standard research term.

⭐ Key point: Clearly defining sampling units and the sampling frame helps ensure the sample represents the population.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q167$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2897, $s$The right to withdraw from a research study at any time is part of the subject's right to:$s$, $s$["Self-determination", "Anonymity only", "Compensation", "Data ownership"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Self-determination
The RIGHT TO SELF-DETERMINATION means participants are AUTONOMOUS — they can freely DECIDE whether to take part in a study, without coercion, and can WITHDRAW at ANY TIME without giving a reason and WITHOUT PENALTY (e.g. their care must not be affected). This right is protected through informed consent, which must clearly explain the right to withdraw.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Anonymity only — Anonymity relates to protecting participants' identity.
• Compensation — Compensation relates to payment for participation or injury.
• Data ownership — Data ownership is a separate legal and ethical issue.

⭐ Key point: Vulnerable participants (e.g. ICU patients, children) need extra protection, such as consent from a legal representative and assent where possible.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q169$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2898, $s$Qualitative research methods are primarily used to:$s$, $s$["Generate statistically generalisable numerical data", "Explore and understand experiences, meanings, and phenomena in depth", "Test drug efficacy through randomised trials", "Calculate p-values"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Explore and understand experiences, meanings, and phenomena in depth
QUALITATIVE RESEARCH aims to EXPLORE and UNDERSTAND people's EXPERIENCES, feelings, beliefs and the MEANINGS they give to events, IN DEPTH. Data are collected as words, through in-depth interviews, focus groups, observation and document analysis, from small, purposively chosen samples. Example: "What is it like for families to have a relative on a ventilator in ICU?"

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Generate statistically generalisable numerical data — Generating statistically generalisable numerical data is QUANTITATIVE research.
• Test drug efficacy through randomised trials — Testing drug efficacy through randomised trials is QUANTITATIVE (experimental) research.
• Calculate p-values — Calculating p-values is part of QUANTITATIVE statistical analysis.

⭐ Key point: Qualitative designs: phenomenology (lived experience), grounded theory (develop theory), ethnography (culture), and historical research.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q170$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2899, $s$The research method is a part of:$s$, $s$["The problem", "The experiment", "Research techniques", "Research methodology"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Research methodology
RESEARCH METHODOLOGY is the overall plan and philosophy of how a study is conducted — including the research design, population and sampling, instruments, data collection procedures, data analysis, and the justification for each choice. The RESEARCH METHOD (the specific procedure or tool used, e.g. questionnaire survey, interview, experiment) is PART of the methodology.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The problem — The research problem is the issue being studied, not where the method belongs.
• The experiment — An experiment is one type of research design/method.
• Research techniques — Research techniques are specific tools within methods.

⭐ Key point: In a research report, the "Methodology" chapter (often Chapter 3 in Nigerian theses) describes design, setting, population, sample, instrument, validity, reliability, data collection and analysis.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 4 · Q172$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2900, $s$Data standardization and classification in nursing informatics (e.g. using NANDA, NIC, NOC) primarily helps to:$s$, $s$["Increase paperwork with no benefit", "Enable consistent communication, data comparison and research across different settings", "Make nursing documentation ambiguous and inconsistent", "Replace the need for any nursing assessment"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Enable consistent communication, data comparison and research across different settings
STANDARDISED NURSING LANGUAGES and CLASSIFICATION systems — such as NANDA-I (nursing diagnoses), NIC (Nursing Interventions Classification) and NOC (Nursing Outcomes Classification) — give nurses COMMON, AGREED TERMS and codes for describing patient problems, nursing actions and outcomes. This enables CONSISTENT COMMUNICATION between nurses and settings, allows DATA to be COMPARED across units, hospitals and countries, supports electronic health records, and makes nursing RESEARCH, quality improvement and evidence-based practice possible. It also makes nursing's contribution to patient outcomes visible.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Increase paperwork with no benefit — Standardisation REDUCES unnecessary paperwork and makes data more useful.
• Make nursing documentation ambiguous and inconsistent — Standardisation makes documentation CLEARER and more consistent, not ambiguous.
• Replace the need for any nursing assessment — Standard languages support documentation of assessment; they do not replace the need to assess patients.

⭐ Key point: Other standardised terminologies: SNOMED CT, ICNP (International Classification for Nursing Practice), ICD (diagnoses), LOINC (lab tests).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q19$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2901, $s$Research usually starts with a problem and ends with:$s$, $s$["Conclusions and recommendations (dissemination of findings)", "A literature review", "A hypothesis", "Data collection"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Conclusions and recommendations (dissemination of findings)
The RESEARCH PROCESS is a series of steps: it starts with IDENTIFYING A PROBLEM, followed by literature review, formulating objectives/hypotheses, designing the study, sampling, data collection, data analysis and interpretation, and ENDS with drawing CONCLUSIONS, making RECOMMENDATIONS, and DISSEMINATING (communicating) the FINDINGS — through reports, publications, conference presentations — so they can be used to improve practice.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A literature review — The literature review is an early step, not the end.
• A hypothesis — A hypothesis is formulated early in the process.
• Data collection — Data collection happens in the middle of the process.

⭐ Key point: Research that is not disseminated cannot improve practice. Nurses should share findings and apply evidence in care.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q41$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2902, $s$In research, the term "universe" refers to the:$s$, $s$["Study setting", "Entire population", "Research instrument", "Sample"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Entire population
In research, the "UNIVERSE" (also called the TARGET POPULATION) refers to the ENTIRE POPULATION — ALL the people, objects or events that meet the criteria for the study and to which the researcher wants to apply (generalise) the results. For example, "all critically ill patients on mechanical ventilation in Nigerian teaching hospitals". A SAMPLE is a smaller group selected from this population.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Study setting — The study setting is the PLACE where the study is conducted.
• Research instrument — A research instrument is the TOOL used to collect data (e.g. questionnaire, checklist).
• Sample — A sample is a subset of the population, not the whole universe.

⭐ Key point: Target population → accessible population → sampling frame → sample.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q42$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2903, $s$All of the following are methods (sources) of problem-solving knowledge EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Tradition", "Assessment", "Voices of authority", "Logical reasoning"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Assessment
Sources of knowledge for solving problems in nursing include: TRADITION (practices passed down — "we have always done it this way"), AUTHORITY (VOICES OF AUTHORITY — experts, senior staff, textbooks), personal EXPERIENCE, TRIAL AND ERROR, intuition, LOGICAL REASONING (inductive and deductive), and RESEARCH (scientific method). ASSESSMENT is a step of the NURSING PROCESS (collecting patient data); it is NOT a source/method of problem-solving knowledge, so it is the EXCEPTION.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Tradition — Tradition IS a source of knowledge (though it may be outdated or wrong).
• Voices of authority — Voices of authority IS a source of knowledge.
• Logical reasoning — Logical reasoning IS a source of knowledge.

⭐ Key point: Research is the most reliable source of knowledge for evidence-based practice, because it is systematic and tested.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q43$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2904, $s$Which step of the research process cuts across all the other steps?$s$, $s$["Literature review", "Research variables", "Problem identification", "Hypothesis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Literature review
The LITERATURE REVIEW is the step that CUTS ACROSS (runs through) ALL other steps of the research process. It helps to: identify and refine the research PROBLEM; develop the theoretical framework; formulate OBJECTIVES and HYPOTHESES; choose an appropriate DESIGN, sample and instruments; and DISCUSS and compare the FINDINGS with previous studies. The researcher continues reviewing literature from the beginning to the end of the study.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Research variables — Research variables are identified at a specific step; they do not cut across all steps.
• Problem identification — Problem identification is the first step only.
• Hypothesis — The hypothesis is formulated at a specific step.

⭐ Key point: Use primary sources (original research reports) whenever possible, and critically appraise each study.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q44$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2905, $s$All the following are probability sampling methods EXCEPT:$s$, $s$["Stratified sampling", "Simple random sampling", "Quota sampling", "Cluster sampling"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Quota sampling
PROBABILITY sampling methods give EVERY member of the population a KNOWN, non-zero CHANCE of being selected, usually through RANDOM selection. Examples: SIMPLE RANDOM, STRATIFIED random, CLUSTER, and SYSTEMATIC sampling. QUOTA SAMPLING is a NON-PROBABILITY method: the researcher fills fixed numbers (quotas) for each subgroup (e.g. 20 males, 20 females) by selecting conveniently available people, not randomly. So it is the EXCEPTION.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stratified sampling — Stratified sampling IS probability: the population is divided into strata, then random samples are taken from each.
• Simple random sampling — Simple random sampling IS probability: everyone has an equal chance (e.g. lottery or random numbers).
• Cluster sampling — Cluster sampling IS probability: groups (clusters, e.g. hospitals) are randomly selected.

⭐ Key point: Non-probability methods: convenience, quota, purposive, and snowball sampling. They are cheaper and easier but less representative.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q45$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2906, $s$The measure of location that divides a data set into two equal parts is the:$s$, $s$["Range", "Mode", "Mean", "Median"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Median
The MEDIAN is the MIDDLE value when data are arranged in order (from smallest to largest). It DIVIDES the data set into TWO EQUAL PARTS — half the values are below it and half are above. Example: for 3, 5, 7, 9, 11, the median is 7. If there is an even number of values, the median is the average of the two middle values. It is useful when data are skewed (e.g. length of ICU stay), because extreme values do not affect it much.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Range — The range is the difference between the highest and lowest values; it is a measure of spread.
• Mode — The mode is the most frequent value.
• Mean — The mean is the arithmetic average (sum ÷ number of values); it is affected by extreme values.

⭐ Key point: Measures of central tendency: mean, median, mode. Measures of dispersion: range, variance, standard deviation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q46$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2907, $s$Plagiarism means:$s$, $s$["Literary appreciation", "Literary reference", "Literary competence", "Literary theft"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Literary theft
PLAGIARISM is "LITERARY THEFT" — presenting someone else's WORDS, IDEAS, DATA or work as your own WITHOUT proper ACKNOWLEDGEMENT (citation and referencing). It includes copying text word-for-word without quotation marks, paraphrasing without citing the source, and submitting another person's work. It is a serious breach of academic and research ethics and can lead to failing an assignment, withdrawal of a degree, or retraction of a publication.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Literary appreciation — Literary appreciation means understanding and enjoying literature.
• Literary reference — Literary reference means citing a source — the opposite of plagiarism.
• Literary competence — Literary competence means skill in reading and writing.

⭐ Key point: Avoid plagiarism by citing sources properly (e.g. APA or Vancouver style), using quotation marks for direct quotes, and using plagiarism-checking software.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q47$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2908, $s$An acceptable reliability coefficient is:$s$, $s$["1.01 to 1.05", "Greater than 0.6–0.7", "1.05 to 1.09", "Greater than 2.0"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Greater than 0.6–0.7
RELIABILITY refers to the CONSISTENCY of a research instrument — whether it gives the same results when used repeatedly under the same conditions. It is measured by a RELIABILITY COEFFICIENT (e.g. Cronbach's alpha, test-retest correlation), which ranges from 0 (no reliability) to 1.0 (perfect reliability). A coefficient GREATER THAN about 0.6–0.7 is generally considered ACCEPTABLE (0.8 or above is good, especially for clinical tools).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 1.01 to 1.05 — Values of 1.01–1.05 are impossible; the coefficient cannot exceed 1.0.
• 1.05 to 1.09 — Values of 1.05–1.09 are impossible for the same reason.
• Greater than 2.0 — A value greater than 2.0 is impossible.

⭐ Key point: Reliability = consistency. Validity = accuracy (measuring what it is supposed to measure). An instrument can be reliable without being valid.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q48$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2909, $s$Quantitative research is characterized by$s$, $s$["In-depth exploration of lived experiences using words and narratives without numbers", "Avoidance of any form of measurement", "Collection and statistical analysis of numerical data to test hypotheses or describe variables objectively", "Reliance solely on the researcher's personal opinion"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Collection and statistical analysis of numerical data to test hypotheses or describe variables objectively
QUANTITATIVE RESEARCH collects NUMERICAL data (numbers, measurements, counts) and uses STATISTICAL ANALYSIS to DESCRIBE variables, examine relationships, TEST HYPOTHESES and establish cause and effect OBJECTIVELY. It uses structured instruments (questionnaires, scales, physiological measurements) and often large samples. Example: measuring whether chlorhexidine mouth care reduces VAP rates in ICU.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• In-depth exploration of lived experiences using words and narratives without numbers — In-depth exploration of lived experiences using words describes QUALITATIVE research.
• Avoidance of any form of measurement — Quantitative research is based on measurement.
• Reliance solely on the researcher's personal opinion — It relies on objective data, not personal opinion.

⭐ Key point: Quantitative designs: descriptive, correlational, quasi-experimental and experimental (RCT).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q49$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2910, $s$Qualitative research is best suited to studies that aim to$s$, $s$["Test a hypothesis using a large randomly selected sample and inferential statistics", "Measure the precise incidence rate of a disease in a population", "Determine the statistical correlation between two numerically measured variables", "Explore and understand the meaning, experiences, and perspectives of individuals in depth"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Explore and understand the meaning, experiences, and perspectives of individuals in depth
QUALITATIVE RESEARCH aims to EXPLORE and UNDERSTAND the MEANING, EXPERIENCES and PERSPECTIVES of people IN DEPTH. It collects data in WORDS (interviews, focus groups, observation), uses small, purposively chosen samples, and analyses themes. Example: exploring the experiences of families of patients in ICU, or nurses' experiences of caring for dying patients.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Test a hypothesis using a large randomly selected sample and inferential statistics — Testing hypotheses with large random samples and inferential statistics is QUANTITATIVE research.
• Measure the precise incidence rate of a disease in a population — Measuring incidence rates is QUANTITATIVE (epidemiological) research.
• Determine the statistical correlation between two numerically measured variables — Determining statistical correlation between numerical variables is QUANTITATIVE research.

⭐ Key point: Qualitative designs include phenomenology, grounded theory, ethnography and historical research.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q50$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2911, $s$A study that describes the characteristics of ventilator-associated pneumonia rates in an ICU without manipulating any variable is an example of$s$, $s$["Quasi-experimental research", "True experimental research", "Descriptive research", "Historical research"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Descriptive research
DESCRIPTIVE RESEARCH aims to DESCRIBE the CHARACTERISTICS of a population, situation or phenomenon AS IT EXISTS, WITHOUT MANIPULATING any variable. Describing VAP rates in an ICU (how often it occurs, in which patients, which organisms) is descriptive research. It answers "what is happening?" and often provides the basis for further studies.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Quasi-experimental research — Quasi-experimental research manipulates an intervention but lacks random assignment.
• True experimental research — True experimental research manipulates an intervention with random assignment and a control group.
• Historical research — Historical research studies PAST events.

⭐ Key point: Descriptive studies may use surveys, observation or record reviews. They do not prove cause and effect.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q51$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2912, $s$Historical research in nursing is primarily concerned with$s$, $s$["Describing current staffing patterns in an ICU", "Predicting future patient outcomes using statistical models", "Systematically collecting and evaluating data related to past events to understand their influence on the present", "Manipulating an intervention to observe its immediate effect"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Systematically collecting and evaluating data related to past events to understand their influence on the present
HISTORICAL RESEARCH SYSTEMATICALLY COLLECTS and critically EVALUATES DATA about PAST EVENTS (from documents, records, letters, interviews with people who were present) to understand how they developed and how they INFLUENCE the PRESENT. Example: studying the development of critical care nursing in Nigeria, or Florence Nightingale's contribution to nursing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Describing current staffing patterns in an ICU — Describing current staffing is descriptive research.
• Predicting future patient outcomes using statistical models — Predicting future outcomes using statistical models is predictive/correlational research.
• Manipulating an intervention to observe its immediate effect — Manipulating an intervention is experimental research.

⭐ Key point: Historical research uses primary sources (original documents) and secondary sources, and evaluates their authenticity (external criticism) and accuracy (internal criticism).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q52$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2913, $s$A research purpose focused on determining whether an increase in nurse-to-patient ratio causes a reduction in medication errors is best described as$s$, $s$["Exploratory research with no measurable variables", "Purely descriptive research", "Explanatory (causal) research", "Historical research"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Explanatory (causal) research
EXPLANATORY (CAUSAL) research aims to EXPLAIN WHY something happens — to determine whether one variable CAUSES a change in another. Determining whether an INCREASE in nurse-to-patient ratio CAUSES a REDUCTION in medication errors is explanatory/causal research. It usually uses experimental or quasi-experimental designs.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Exploratory research with no measurable variables — Exploratory research investigates a new area with little existing knowledge; this question has clear measurable variables.
• Purely descriptive research — Purely descriptive research only describes what exists without examining causes.
• Historical research — Historical research studies past events.

⭐ Key point: Research purposes: exploratory (what?), descriptive (what is?), explanatory (why?), predictive (what will happen?).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q53$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2914, $s$'Validity' in research refers to$s$, $s$["The extent to which an instrument accurately measures what it is intended to measure", "The statistical significance level chosen by the researcher", "The extent to which an instrument gives consistent results on repeated use", "The total number of subjects included in a study"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The extent to which an instrument accurately measures what it is intended to measure
VALIDITY refers to how ACCURATELY an instrument MEASURES WHAT IT IS INTENDED TO MEASURE. For example, a pain scale is valid if it really measures pain (not anxiety). Types of validity: CONTENT validity (covers all aspects of the concept), CRITERION validity (agrees with a "gold standard"), and CONSTRUCT validity (measures the theoretical concept).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The statistical significance level chosen by the researcher — The significance level (e.g. p<0.05) is a statistical decision, not validity.
• The extent to which an instrument gives consistent results on repeated use — Consistency on repeated use is RELIABILITY.
• The total number of subjects included in a study — The number of subjects is the sample size.

⭐ Key point: A valid instrument must be reliable, but a reliable instrument may not be valid.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q54$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2915, $s$The term 'extraneous variable' refers to a variable that$s$, $s$["Always improves the accuracy of the study findings", "Refers only to variables measured using qualitative methods", "Is not the focus of the study but may influence the dependent variable and confound the results if not controlled", "Is deliberately manipulated by the researcher as the main variable of interest"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Is not the focus of the study but may influence the dependent variable and confound the results if not controlled
An EXTRANEOUS VARIABLE (confounding variable) is NOT the focus of the study, but it can INFLUENCE the DEPENDENT VARIABLE and CONFOUND (distort) the results if it is not controlled. Example: in a study of a new mouth-care protocol on VAP, the patient's age, severity of illness and duration of ventilation are extraneous variables. Researchers control them through randomisation, matching, inclusion/exclusion criteria, or statistical adjustment.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Always improves the accuracy of the study findings — Extraneous variables can reduce the accuracy of findings.
• Refers only to variables measured using qualitative methods — They are not limited to qualitative methods.
• Is deliberately manipulated by the researcher as the main variable of interest — The variable deliberately manipulated is the INDEPENDENT variable.

⭐ Key point: Controlling extraneous variables improves the internal validity of a study.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q56$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2916, $s$A secondary source in a literature review is best exemplified by$s$, $s$["The raw dataset collected by the researcher", "A review article or textbook chapter that summarizes and synthesizes findings from several original studies", "The original journal article reporting a specific randomized controlled trial", "An unpublished doctoral thesis reporting the author's own study"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A review article or textbook chapter that summarizes and synthesizes findings from several original studies
A SECONDARY SOURCE is a description, SUMMARY or analysis of research written by someone OTHER than the original researchers. Examples: a REVIEW ARTICLE or TEXTBOOK CHAPTER that SUMMARISES and SYNTHESISES findings from several original studies. Secondary sources give an overview but may be less detailed or may misinterpret the original.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The raw dataset collected by the researcher — The raw dataset is primary data.
• The original journal article reporting a specific randomized controlled trial — The original journal article reporting an RCT is a PRIMARY source.
• An unpublished doctoral thesis reporting the author's own study — An unpublished thesis reporting the author's own study is a PRIMARY source.

⭐ Key point: Use secondary sources to find primary sources, then read the originals.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q58$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2917, $s$A researcher studies the effect of a structured early mobilization protocol on the length of ICU stay. In this study, the independent variable is$s$, $s$["The name of the hospital", "The structured early mobilization protocol", "The length of ICU stay", "The age of the patients"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The structured early mobilization protocol
The INDEPENDENT VARIABLE is the variable that the researcher MANIPULATES or introduces (the presumed "CAUSE"). In this study, the researcher introduces the STRUCTURED EARLY MOBILISATION PROTOCOL to see its effect on length of ICU stay. So the protocol is the independent variable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The name of the hospital — The hospital's name is not a variable being manipulated.
• The length of ICU stay — The length of ICU stay is the DEPENDENT variable (the outcome).
• The age of the patients — Age is an extraneous (confounding) variable.

⭐ Key point: Independent variable = cause/intervention. Dependent variable = effect/outcome.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q59$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2918, $s$A 'discrete variable' is one that$s$, $s$["Can only take on distinct, separate values, typically whole numbers, such as the number of children a person has", "Always refers to a qualitative characteristic that cannot be counted", "Can take on any value along a continuum, including fractions and decimals", "Cannot be measured under any circumstances"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Can only take on distinct, separate values, typically whole numbers, such as the number of children a person has
A DISCRETE VARIABLE can only take DISTINCT, SEPARATE values — usually WHOLE NUMBERS that are counted — with no values in between. Examples: number of children, number of ICU admissions, number of pressure ulcers, number of falls. You cannot have 2.5 children.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Always refers to a qualitative characteristic that cannot be counted — Qualitative characteristics are categorical variables, which can be counted.
• Can take on any value along a continuum, including fractions and decimals — A variable that can take any value, including fractions, is a CONTINUOUS variable (e.g. weight, temperature, BP).
• Cannot be measured under any circumstances — Discrete variables can be measured (counted).

⭐ Key point: Discrete = counted. Continuous = measured on a scale.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q61$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2919, $s$Research questions are generally used instead of hypotheses when$s$, $s$["The researcher wants to test a precise cause-and-effect relationship with an experimental design", "The study must always involve statistical hypothesis testing", "Qualitative research is strictly prohibited from using questions", "The study is exploratory or descriptive in nature and there is insufficient existing knowledge to predict a specific relationship"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The study is exploratory or descriptive in nature and there is insufficient existing knowledge to predict a specific relationship
RESEARCH QUESTIONS are used instead of hypotheses when the study is EXPLORATORY or DESCRIPTIVE, and there is not enough existing knowledge to PREDICT a specific relationship. For example, "What are the experiences of nurses caring for COVID-19 patients in ICU?" A HYPOTHESIS predicts a relationship and is tested statistically (e.g. "Patients who receive early mobilisation will have a shorter ICU stay").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The researcher wants to test a precise cause-and-effect relationship with an experimental design — Testing precise cause-and-effect relationships with experiments uses hypotheses.
• The study must always involve statistical hypothesis testing — Not all studies require statistical hypothesis testing.
• Qualitative research is strictly prohibited from using questions — Qualitative research commonly uses research questions.

⭐ Key point: Hypotheses are used in experimental, quasi-experimental and correlational studies.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q62$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2920, $s$A cross-sectional study design involves$s$, $s$["Collecting data from a population or sample at a single point in time", "Following the same group of subjects over an extended period with repeated measurements", "Manipulating the independent variable with random assignment", "Reviewing historical documents only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Collecting data from a population or sample at a single point in time
A CROSS-SECTIONAL study collects data from a population or sample at a SINGLE POINT IN TIME (a "snapshot"). Example: surveying all ICU nurses in one month to measure their knowledge of VAP prevention. It is quick and inexpensive, but cannot show cause and effect over time.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Following the same group of subjects over an extended period with repeated measurements — Following the same group over time is a LONGITUDINAL study.
• Manipulating the independent variable with random assignment — Manipulating the independent variable with random assignment is an EXPERIMENTAL design.
• Reviewing historical documents only — Reviewing historical documents is historical research.

⭐ Key point: Prevalence (how common something is at one point in time) is often measured using cross-sectional studies.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q64$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2921, $s$A phenomenological research design, a type of qualitative design, is primarily used to$s$, $s$["Explore and describe the lived experiences and meanings that individuals attach to a particular phenomenon", "Manipulate an independent variable under controlled experimental conditions", "Statistically test hypotheses using large, randomly selected samples", "Analyze numerical trends in large population datasets"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Explore and describe the lived experiences and meanings that individuals attach to a particular phenomenon
PHENOMENOLOGY is a qualitative design that EXPLORES and DESCRIBES the LIVED EXPERIENCES of people and the MEANINGS they give to a particular phenomenon. Data are collected through in-depth interviews. Example: "the lived experience of patients recovering from mechanical ventilation".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Manipulate an independent variable under controlled experimental conditions — Manipulating variables is experimental research.
• Statistically test hypotheses using large, randomly selected samples — Testing hypotheses with large samples is quantitative research.
• Analyze numerical trends in large population datasets — Analysing numerical trends is quantitative research.

⭐ Key point: Phenomenology asks: "What is it like to experience this?"$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q65$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2922, $s$An 'ethnographic' research design focuses on$s$, $s$["Studying the culture, beliefs, and practices of a particular group through immersion and observation over time", "Testing the statistical significance of an intervention using a control group", "Randomly assigning subjects to experimental conditions", "Reviewing quantitative survey data collected via mailed questionnaires only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Studying the culture, beliefs, and practices of a particular group through immersion and observation over time
ETHNOGRAPHY is a qualitative design that studies the CULTURE, BELIEFS, VALUES and PRACTICES of a particular group through IMMERSION and OBSERVATION over time (participant observation and interviews). Example: studying the culture of an ICU nursing team, or traditional beliefs about illness in a community.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Testing the statistical significance of an intervention using a control group — Testing statistical significance is quantitative research.
• Randomly assigning subjects to experimental conditions — Random assignment is experimental research.
• Reviewing quantitative survey data collected via mailed questionnaires only — Mailed questionnaires are survey research.

⭐ Key point: Ethnography developed from anthropology.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q66$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2923, $s$A 'sampling frame' is best described as$s$, $s$["A synonym for the research design", "The final report describing the study's results", "The theoretical framework guiding the study", "A complete list of all elements or subjects in the accessible population from which a sample will be drawn"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A complete list of all elements or subjects in the accessible population from which a sample will be drawn
A SAMPLING FRAME is a COMPLETE LIST of all the ELEMENTS or subjects in the ACCESSIBLE POPULATION from which the sample will be drawn. Example: a list of all registered nurses working in a hospital's ICUs. Probability sampling requires a sampling frame.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A synonym for the research design — The research design is the overall plan of the study.
• The final report describing the study's results — The final report describes the results.
• The theoretical framework guiding the study — The theoretical framework guides the study.

⭐ Key point: An incomplete sampling frame can lead to sampling bias.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q67$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2924, $s$Systematic sampling involves$s$, $s$["Choosing subjects purely based on the researcher's personal judgment of who is 'typical'", "Selecting every kth element from a list after a random starting point", "Allowing existing participants to recruit further participants", "Selecting subjects based only on their convenient availability"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Selecting every kth element from a list after a random starting point
SYSTEMATIC SAMPLING selects EVERY kth ELEMENT from a list, after a RANDOM STARTING POINT. The sampling interval k = population size ÷ sample size. Example: from 500 patients, to get 50, select every 10th patient, starting from a randomly chosen number between 1 and 10.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Choosing subjects purely based on the researcher's personal judgment of who is 'typical' — Choosing based on personal judgement is purposive sampling.
• Allowing existing participants to recruit further participants — Participants recruiting others is snowball sampling.
• Selecting subjects based only on their convenient availability — Selecting conveniently available subjects is convenience sampling.

⭐ Key point: Systematic sampling is simple and efficient, but can be biased if the list has a repeating pattern.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q68$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2925, $s$Snowball sampling is a technique especially useful for studying$s$, $s$["Extremely large populations with a complete and accessible sampling frame", "Populations where random selection using computer software is straightforward", "Only populations with fewer than five known members in existence", "Hard-to-reach or hidden populations, where existing participants refer the researcher to other potential participants they know"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Hard-to-reach or hidden populations, where existing participants refer the researcher to other potential participants they know
SNOWBALL SAMPLING asks EXISTING PARTICIPANTS to REFER other people they know who meet the study criteria. The sample grows like a rolling snowball. It is especially useful for HARD-TO-REACH or HIDDEN POPULATIONS (e.g. drug users, people with stigmatised conditions) where there is no list.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Extremely large populations with a complete and accessible sampling frame — For large populations with a complete list, probability sampling is better.
• Populations where random selection using computer software is straightforward — Where random selection is straightforward, probability sampling is better.
• Only populations with fewer than five known members in existence — Snowball sampling isn't limited to very small populations.

⭐ Key point: Snowball sampling is a non-probability method; it may introduce bias since participants refer people similar to themselves.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q69$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2926, $s$The "null hypothesis" (H0) states that:$s$, $s$["There is a significant difference or relationship between the variables studied", "The research design used was invalid", "The sample size used was too small to be valid", "There is no significant difference or relationship between the variables being studied"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: There is no significant difference or relationship between the variables being studied
The NULL HYPOTHESIS (H0) is the statement that there is NO SIGNIFICANT DIFFERENCE or NO RELATIONSHIP between the variables being studied — any difference seen is due to chance. Example: "There is no significant difference in VAP rates between patients who receive chlorhexidine mouth care and those who do not." Statistical tests are used to decide whether to REJECT H0. If the p-value is less than the chosen significance level (usually 0.05), H0 is rejected and the alternative hypothesis (that there IS a difference) is supported.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• There is a significant difference or relationship between the variables studied — A statement that there IS a significant difference or relationship is the ALTERNATIVE (research) hypothesis (H1).
• The research design used was invalid — The null hypothesis is not a statement about whether the design was valid.
• The sample size used was too small to be valid — The null hypothesis is not about sample size.

⭐ Key point: We never "prove" the null hypothesis — we either reject it or fail to reject it.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q71 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2927, $s$Which scale of measurement classifies data into categories with no inherent order, such as blood group (A, B, AB, O)?$s$, $s$["Interval", "Nominal", "Ratio", "Ordinal"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Nominal
The NOMINAL scale is the simplest level of measurement. It places data into CATEGORIES or NAMES with NO INHERENT ORDER or ranking — one category is not "higher" or "better" than another. Examples: BLOOD GROUP (A, B, AB, O), sex (male/female), religion, marital status, ward name, diagnosis. Numbers may be used as labels (e.g. 1 = male, 2 = female), but they have no mathematical meaning. Only counts, percentages and the mode can be calculated.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Interval — Interval data have equal intervals between values but no true zero (e.g. temperature in °C).
• Ratio — Ratio data have equal intervals AND a true zero (e.g. weight, height, heart rate).
• Ordinal — Ordinal data have categories with a meaningful ORDER (e.g. mild, moderate, severe).

⭐ Key point: Remember NOIR (from lowest to highest level): Nominal, Ordinal, Interval, Ratio.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q72$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2928, $s$A patient's pain level rated as "mild, moderate, severe" is an example of which scale of measurement?$s$, $s$["Ratio", "Nominal", "Ordinal", "Interval"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ordinal
The ORDINAL scale places data into CATEGORIES that have a MEANINGFUL ORDER or RANK, but the DISTANCES between categories are NOT necessarily EQUAL. Pain rated as "MILD, MODERATE, SEVERE" is ordinal: severe is worse than moderate, which is worse than mild, but the "gap" between mild and moderate may not equal the gap between moderate and severe. Other examples: levels of education, Likert scales (strongly agree to strongly disagree), pressure ulcer stages, cancer stages.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ratio — Ratio data have equal intervals and a true zero (e.g. weight).
• Nominal — Nominal data have categories with NO order (e.g. blood group).
• Interval — Interval data have equal intervals between values (e.g. temperature in °C).

⭐ Key point: For ordinal data, use the median and mode, and non-parametric tests (e.g. Mann-Whitney U).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q73$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2929, $s$Body temperature measured in degrees Celsius is an example of which scale of measurement?$s$, $s$["Ratio", "Interval", "Ordinal", "Nominal"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Interval
The INTERVAL scale has ordered values with EQUAL INTERVALS between them, but NO TRUE (ABSOLUTE) ZERO. Temperature in DEGREES CELSIUS is interval: the difference between 36 °C and 37 °C is the same as between 38 °C and 39 °C, but 0 °C does NOT mean "no temperature" — it is just the freezing point of water. So you cannot say 40 °C is "twice as hot" as 20 °C.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ratio — Ratio data have a TRUE ZERO (e.g. weight, pulse rate, urine output, temperature in Kelvin).
• Ordinal — Ordinal data have order but unequal intervals.
• Nominal — Nominal data have no order.

⭐ Key point: Interval and ratio data are both "continuous" and allow the mean and standard deviation to be calculated.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q74$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2930, $s$Which measure of central tendency is most appropriate for a data set with extreme outlier values (a highly skewed distribution)?$s$, $s$["Standard deviation", "Mean", "Median", "Mode"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Median
The MEDIAN is the middle value when data are arranged in order. It is NOT strongly affected by EXTREME OUTLIER values, so it is the most appropriate measure of central tendency for SKEWED data. Example: ICU length of stay (most patients stay 2–5 days, but a few stay 60 days) — the mean would be pulled up by the long stays, but the median better represents the "typical" patient.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Standard deviation — Standard deviation measures SPREAD, not central tendency.
• Mean — The mean is pulled towards outliers, so it is misleading in skewed data.
• Mode — The mode is the most frequent value; it may not represent the centre well.

⭐ Key point: Normally distributed data: use mean and standard deviation. Skewed data: use median and interquartile range.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q75$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2931, $s$The "standard deviation" is a measure of:$s$, $s$["The most frequently occurring value", "The central tendency of a data set", "The relationship between two variables", "The average distance or spread of individual values from the mean"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The average distance or spread of individual values from the mean
The STANDARD DEVIATION (SD) is a measure of DISPERSION (spread). It shows the AVERAGE DISTANCE of individual values FROM THE MEAN. A SMALL SD means values are close to the mean (data are tightly grouped); a LARGE SD means values are widely spread out. In a normal distribution, about 68% of values lie within 1 SD of the mean, about 95% within 2 SD, and about 99.7% within 3 SD.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The most frequently occurring value — The most frequent value is the MODE.
• The central tendency of a data set — Central tendency is measured by the mean, median and mode.
• The relationship between two variables — The relationship between two variables is measured by correlation (e.g. Pearson's r).

⭐ Key point: SD is the square root of the variance.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q76$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2932, $s$A "Type II error" in hypothesis testing occurs when:$s$, $s$["A false null hypothesis is incorrectly accepted or not rejected (a false negative)", "The alternative hypothesis is proven true", "The p-value is exactly 0.05", "A true null hypothesis is rejected"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A false null hypothesis is incorrectly accepted or not rejected (a false negative)
A TYPE II ERROR (BETA ERROR) occurs when the researcher FAILS TO REJECT a NULL HYPOTHESIS that is actually FALSE — concluding there is NO difference or effect when there really IS one (a FALSE NEGATIVE). Example: concluding a new treatment doesn't work when it actually does. It is more likely with SMALL SAMPLES (low statistical power).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The alternative hypothesis is proven true — Proving the alternative hypothesis true is not an error.
• The p-value is exactly 0.05 — A p-value of exactly 0.05 is a borderline result, not a Type II error.
• A true null hypothesis is rejected — Rejecting a TRUE null hypothesis is a TYPE I error.

⭐ Key point: Reduce Type II error by increasing the sample size (power analysis). Power = 1 − beta (usually aim for 80%).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q78 · concept asked in 2 mocks$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2933, $s$Which statistical test is commonly used to compare the means of two independent groups, such as two treatment groups?$s$, $s$["Pearson correlation coefficient", "Student's t-test", "Frequency distribution table", "Chi-square test"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Student's t-test
The STUDENT'S t-TEST compares the MEANS of TWO GROUPS to see whether the difference between them is statistically significant. The INDEPENDENT (unpaired) t-test is used for two separate groups (e.g. pain scores in a group given drug A versus a group given drug B). The PAIRED t-test is used for the same group measured twice (e.g. before and after an intervention). It requires interval or ratio data that are approximately normally distributed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Pearson correlation coefficient — Pearson correlation measures the strength of the relationship between two continuous variables.
• Frequency distribution table — A frequency distribution table organises data; it does not compare means.
• Chi-square test — The chi-square test compares categorical (nominal/ordinal) data, e.g. proportions.

⭐ Key point: For non-normal data, use the Mann-Whitney U test instead of the independent t-test.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q79$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2934, $s$When comparing the means of more than two groups simultaneously (e.g., three different ICU sedation protocols), which statistical test is most appropriate?$s$, $s$["Chi-square test", "Analysis of Variance (ANOVA)", "A simple frequency count", "Paired t-test"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Analysis of Variance (ANOVA)
ANALYSIS OF VARIANCE (ANOVA) is used to compare the MEANS of THREE OR MORE GROUPS at the same time (e.g. three ICU sedation protocols). Doing multiple t-tests instead would increase the risk of a Type I error. If ANOVA shows a significant difference, post-hoc tests identify which groups differ.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Chi-square test — Chi-square compares categorical data.
• A simple frequency count — A frequency count doesn't compare means.
• Paired t-test — A paired t-test compares two measurements from the same group.

⭐ Key point: For non-normal data, the Kruskal-Wallis test is the non-parametric alternative to ANOVA.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q80$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2935, $s$General Systems Theory, foundational to many nursing conceptual models, is most closely associated with which theorist?$s$, $s$["Virginia Henderson", "Abraham Maslow", "Ludwig von Bertalanffy", "Florence Nightingale"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ludwig von Bertalanffy
GENERAL SYSTEMS THEORY was developed by LUDWIG VON BERTALANFFY, an Austrian biologist, in the 1930s–1950s. It proposes that all living and social systems (cells, people, families, organisations) are made of interacting parts, and that the whole is more than the sum of its parts. Systems have boundaries, take inputs from the environment, process them and produce outputs, using feedback to maintain balance. It became the foundation for several nursing models, including those of Betty Neuman (Systems Model), Sister Callista Roy (Adaptation Model) and Imogene King.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Virginia Henderson — Virginia Henderson defined nursing and identified 14 basic needs.
• Abraham Maslow — Abraham Maslow developed the hierarchy of human needs.
• Florence Nightingale — Florence Nightingale developed the environmental theory of nursing.

⭐ Key point: Systems thinking helps nurses view the patient holistically — physical, psychological, social and spiritual aspects interact.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q121$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2936, $s$In systems theory, 'entropy' refers to:$s$, $s$["The boundary that separates a system from its environment", "The tendency of a system to gain increasing order and energy over time", "The tendency of a system toward disorder, disorganization, and eventual energy depletion", "The exchange of information between two systems"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The tendency of a system toward disorder, disorganization, and eventual energy depletion
In systems theory, ENTROPY is the natural tendency of a system to move toward DISORDER, DISORGANISATION and eventual ENERGY DEPLETION (breakdown or death) if it does not receive energy from its environment. OPEN systems (like humans and organisations) take in energy and information (inputs) to resist entropy and maintain order. The opposite, NEGENTROPY, is the tendency toward increasing order and organisation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The boundary that separates a system from its environment — The BOUNDARY is the line that separates a system from its environment.
• The tendency of a system to gain increasing order and energy over time — A tendency to gain order and energy is NEGENTROPY.
• The exchange of information between two systems — The exchange of information between systems is communication or feedback.

⭐ Key point: In nursing, illness can be seen as increasing entropy; nursing care provides "inputs" (oxygen, nutrition, support) to restore order.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q122$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2937, $s$A nurse opens the windows of a patient's room to allow fresh air circulation and moves the patient's bed closer to a window for natural sunlight. This action best reflects which nursing theorist's framework?$s$, $s$["Dorothea Orem", "Florence Nightingale", "Sister Callista Roy", "Marjory Gordon"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Florence Nightingale
FLORENCE NIGHTINGALE'S ENVIRONMENTAL THEORY states that the nurse's role is to manipulate the ENVIRONMENT to put the patient in the best condition for nature to heal. She emphasised FRESH AIR (ventilation), LIGHT (especially sunlight), warmth, cleanliness, pure water, efficient drainage, quiet, good nutrition and observation. Opening windows for fresh air and moving the bed nearer to a window for natural sunlight are classic applications of her theory.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Dorothea Orem — Dorothea Orem's theory focuses on self-care deficits.
• Sister Callista Roy — Sister Callista Roy's theory focuses on adaptation to stimuli.
• Marjory Gordon — Marjory Gordon developed the 11 functional health patterns for assessment.

⭐ Key point: Nightingale's principles remain relevant: hand hygiene, ventilation and cleanliness reduce infections, and natural light and quiet reduce ICU delirium.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q123$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2938, $s$Virginia Henderson defined the unique function of the nurse as assisting the individual, sick or well, in the performance of activities contributing to health, recovery, or:$s$, $s$["A peaceful death", "Financial independence", "Employment reintegration", "Permanent institutionalization"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A peaceful death
Virginia HENDERSON (1966) defined the unique function of the nurse as: "to assist the individual, sick or well, in the performance of those activities contributing to health or its recovery (OR TO A PEACEFUL DEATH) that he would perform unaided if he had the necessary strength, will or knowledge, and to do this in such a way as to help him gain independence as rapidly as possible." This recognises that supporting a dignified, peaceful death is part of nursing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Financial independence — Financial independence is not part of Henderson's definition.
• Employment reintegration — Employment reintegration is not part of Henderson's definition.
• Permanent institutionalization — Permanent institutionalisation is the opposite of the independence Henderson emphasised.

⭐ Key point: Henderson's definition was adopted by the International Council of Nurses (ICN) and is still widely quoted.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q124$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2939, $s$According to Henderson, the overarching goal of nursing assistance is to help the client achieve:$s$, $s$["Total reliance on family caregivers", "A prolonged hospital stay to ensure thorough recovery", "Independence as rapidly as possible", "Complete and permanent dependence on the healthcare team"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Independence as rapidly as possible
According to HENDERSON, the overarching goal of nursing assistance is to help the client gain INDEPENDENCE AS RAPIDLY AS POSSIBLE. The nurse temporarily does for the client what they would do for themselves if they had the necessary strength, will or knowledge, and gradually helps them do it themselves. Henderson identified 14 BASIC NEEDS (e.g. breathing normally, eating and drinking, elimination, moving, sleep and rest, dressing, maintaining body temperature, hygiene, avoiding dangers, communication, worship, work, play, and learning).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Total reliance on family caregivers — Total reliance on family is not the goal.
• A prolonged hospital stay to ensure thorough recovery — A prolonged hospital stay is not the goal.
• Complete and permanent dependence on the healthcare team — Complete and permanent dependence on staff is the opposite of Henderson's goal.

⭐ Key point: In ICU, Henderson's ideas guide early mobilisation, weaning, self-care, and patient education.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q125$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2940, $s$According to Orem, a 'self-care deficit' exists when:$s$, $s$["The nurse is unavailable to provide any physical care", "The client's self-care agency (ability) is insufficient to meet the therapeutic self-care demand", "The client has more self-care ability than is needed", "The client's family refuses to participate in care"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The client's self-care agency (ability) is insufficient to meet the therapeutic self-care demand
In OREM'S SELF-CARE DEFICIT THEORY, a SELF-CARE DEFICIT exists when the client's SELF-CARE AGENCY (their ABILITY to perform self-care) is INSUFFICIENT to meet their THERAPEUTIC SELF-CARE DEMAND (all the self-care actions needed to maintain health and well-being). When this happens, nursing is needed to make up the gap. Example: a patient after stroke cannot wash, dress or feed themselves.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The nurse is unavailable to provide any physical care — Nurse unavailability is not part of the definition.
• The client has more self-care ability than is needed — If the client has MORE ability than needed, there is no deficit.
• The client's family refuses to participate in care — Family refusal to participate is not the definition of a self-care deficit.

⭐ Key point: Orem's model has three linked theories: self-care, self-care deficit, and nursing systems.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q126$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2941, $s$Orem categorized self-care requisites into three types. Which of the following correctly lists them?$s$, $s$["Universal, developmental, and health-deviation self-care requisites", "Physiological, psychological, and spiritual self-care requisites", "Primary, secondary, and tertiary self-care requisites", "Focal, contextual, and residual self-care requisites"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Universal, developmental, and health-deviation self-care requisites
OREM identified three types of SELF-CARE REQUISITES (needs): (1) UNIVERSAL requisites — common to all people (air, water, food, elimination, activity and rest, solitude and social interaction, prevention of hazards, normality); (2) DEVELOPMENTAL requisites — related to life stages and events (e.g. pregnancy, childhood, ageing, bereavement); (3) HEALTH-DEVIATION requisites — needs arising from illness, injury, disability or medical treatment (e.g. managing a colostomy, taking medications).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Physiological, psychological, and spiritual self-care requisites — Physiological, psychological and spiritual is not Orem's classification.
• Primary, secondary, and tertiary self-care requisites — Primary, secondary and tertiary relates to levels of prevention.
• Focal, contextual, and residual self-care requisites — Focal, contextual and residual are ROY'S types of stimuli.

⭐ Key point: Orem's model is widely used in rehabilitation, chronic disease management and patient education.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q127$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2942, $s$Orem described three nursing systems used to address self-care deficits. In which system does the nurse perform all of the client's therapeutic self-care because the client is totally unable to do so?$s$, $s$["Wholly compensatory nursing system", "Partly compensatory nursing system", "Supportive-educative nursing system", "Independent nursing system"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Wholly compensatory nursing system
OREM described three NURSING SYSTEMS based on how much the client can do: (1) WHOLLY COMPENSATORY — the nurse performs ALL of the client's self-care because the client is TOTALLY UNABLE (e.g. an unconscious, sedated, ventilated ICU patient); (2) PARTLY COMPENSATORY — the nurse and client share self-care tasks (e.g. a post-operative patient who can feed themselves but needs help with walking and wound care); (3) SUPPORTIVE-EDUCATIVE — the client can perform self-care but needs teaching, guidance and support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Partly compensatory nursing system — Partly compensatory involves shared care, not total care.
• Supportive-educative nursing system — Supportive-educative involves teaching only.
• Independent nursing system — "Independent nursing system" is not one of Orem's systems.

⭐ Key point: As the ICU patient recovers, care moves from wholly compensatory to partly compensatory, then supportive-educative.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q128$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2943, $s$A client who is fully capable of performing self-care but needs teaching and guidance to safely administer insulin at home would be best supported using which of Orem's nursing systems?$s$, $s$["Supportive-educative (supportive-developmental) nursing system", "Wholly compensatory nursing system", "Partly compensatory nursing system", "Restorative nursing system"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Supportive-educative (supportive-developmental) nursing system
The SUPPORTIVE-EDUCATIVE (supportive-developmental) nursing system is used when the client IS ABLE to perform self-care but needs TEACHING, GUIDANCE, SUPPORT or a supportive environment to do so safely. A client who is physically capable but needs to learn how to administer INSULIN at home fits this system — the nurse teaches, demonstrates, observes a return demonstration and provides support.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Wholly compensatory nursing system — Wholly compensatory is for clients who cannot do any self-care.
• Partly compensatory nursing system — Partly compensatory is for clients who can do some but not all self-care.
• Restorative nursing system — "Restorative nursing system" is not one of Orem's three systems.

⭐ Key point: Patient teaching is a core nursing role in discharge planning, especially for chronic conditions.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q129$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2944, $s$Rogers' principle of 'helicy' refers to:$s$, $s$["The exact repetition of identical life patterns over time", "The continuous, innovative, unpredictable, increasingly diverse nature of the mutual human-environment field process, evolving in a probabilistic, non-repeating manner", "The idea that only physical, three-dimensional space governs human experience", "The classification of illness into discrete, static stages"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The continuous, innovative, unpredictable, increasingly diverse nature of the mutual human-environment field process, evolving in a probabilistic, non-repeating manner
Martha ROGERS' "Science of Unitary Human Beings" sees humans and their environment as continuous, inseparable ENERGY FIELDS. Her principle of HELICY describes the nature of change in the human-environment field as CONTINUOUS, INNOVATIVE, UNPREDICTABLE and INCREASINGLY DIVERSE — evolving in a non-repeating, spiral (helical) way rather than returning to the same state. Life is always moving forward and changing.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The exact repetition of identical life patterns over time — Exact repetition of identical patterns contradicts helicy (change is non-repeating).
• The idea that only physical, three-dimensional space governs human experience — Rogers emphasised energy fields and pattern, not only physical, three-dimensional space.
• The classification of illness into discrete, static stages — Rogers did not classify illness into discrete, static stages.

⭐ Key point: Rogers' three principles of homeodynamics: HELICY (continuous, innovative change), RESONANCY (change from lower to higher frequency patterns), and INTEGRALITY (continuous mutual process between human and environment).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q130$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2945, $s$In Neuman's model, the 'basic core structure' refers to:$s$, $s$["The outermost, accordion-like protective buffer against stressors", "The client's coping mechanisms only", "A ranked list of psychosocial needs", "The central, innermost structure containing basic survival factors common to the species, such as genetic features and organ strength"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The central, innermost structure containing basic survival factors common to the species, such as genetic features and organ strength
In Betty NEUMAN'S Systems Model, the client is drawn as a set of concentric circles. At the centre is the BASIC CORE STRUCTURE — the innermost part containing the BASIC SURVIVAL FACTORS common to the species, such as genetic structure, organ strength or weakness, normal temperature range, response patterns and ego structure. If stressors penetrate to the core, the client's survival is threatened. Surrounding the core are the LINES OF RESISTANCE, the NORMAL LINE OF DEFENCE and, on the outside, the FLEXIBLE LINE OF DEFENCE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The outermost, accordion-like protective buffer against stressors — The outermost, accordion-like buffer is the FLEXIBLE LINE OF DEFENCE.
• The client's coping mechanisms only — Coping mechanisms relate to the lines of resistance and defence.
• A ranked list of psychosocial needs — A ranked list of needs relates to Maslow, not Neuman's core.

⭐ Key point: Neuman's goal of nursing is to keep the client system stable by reducing stressors and strengthening the lines of defence.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q131$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2946, $s$Roy's model describes two major internal control processes, or coping subsystems, that a person uses to adapt to stimuli. What are they?$s$, $s$["The wholly compensatory and partly compensatory subsystems", "The self-care agency and therapeutic self-care demand", "The regulator and cognator subsystems", "The core structure and flexible line of defense"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The regulator and cognator subsystems
ROY'S model describes two COPING SUBSYSTEMS (internal control processes): the REGULATOR SUBSYSTEM — physiological coping through the neural, chemical and endocrine systems (e.g. increased heart rate and adrenaline release in response to blood loss); and the COGNATOR SUBSYSTEM — psychological coping through perception and information processing, learning, judgement and emotion (e.g. understanding the illness, problem-solving, seeking support). Their responses are seen in four ADAPTIVE MODES: physiological, self-concept, role function and interdependence.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The wholly compensatory and partly compensatory subsystems — Wholly and partly compensatory are OREM'S nursing systems.
• The self-care agency and therapeutic self-care demand — Self-care agency and therapeutic self-care demand are OREM'S concepts.
• The core structure and flexible line of defense — The core structure and flexible line of defence are NEUMAN'S concepts.

⭐ Key point: The nurse assesses behaviour in the four modes, identifies stimuli, and intervenes to promote adaptive responses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q135$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2947, $s$Gordon's framework organizes assessment data into how many functional health patterns?$s$, $s$["11", "5", "9", "14"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: 11
Marjory GORDON (1987) organised nursing assessment into 11 FUNCTIONAL HEALTH PATTERNS, to give a holistic picture of the client: (1) health perception–health management, (2) nutritional–metabolic, (3) elimination, (4) activity–exercise, (5) sleep–rest, (6) cognitive–perceptual, (7) self-perception–self-concept, (8) role–relationship, (9) sexuality–reproductive, (10) coping–stress tolerance, and (11) value–belief. Data in each pattern are used to identify nursing diagnoses.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• 5 — 5 is too few; Gordon's framework is wider.
• 9 — 9 is not correct.
• 14 — 14 is the number of basic needs in VIRGINIA HENDERSON'S model, not Gordon's patterns.

⭐ Key point: Gordon's patterns fit well with NANDA nursing diagnoses and are widely used in nursing assessment forms.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q136$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2948, $s$Which nurse theorist first introduced the term "nursing process" into nursing literature?$s$, $s$["Virginia Henderson", "Lydia Hall", "Dorothea Orem", "Ida Jean Orlando"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Lydia Hall
LYDIA HALL first used the term "NURSING PROCESS" in 1955, describing nursing as a process (in her "Care, Core and Cure" model). Later, Ida Jean ORLANDO (1961) described the "deliberative nursing process", and the process was developed into the steps used today: Assessment, Diagnosis, Planning, Implementation and Evaluation (ADPIE).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Virginia Henderson — Virginia Henderson defined the unique function of the nurse and the 14 basic needs.
• Dorothea Orem — Dorothea Orem developed the Self-Care Deficit Theory.
• Ida Jean Orlando — Ida Jean Orlando developed the deliberative nursing process (1961), after Hall first used the term.

⭐ Key point: The nursing process is systematic, client-centred, goal-oriented, cyclical and dynamic.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q139$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2949, $s$The addition of "Nursing Diagnosis" as a distinct fifth step of the nursing process is most closely associated with which development?$s$, $s$["Publication of Florence Nightingale's Notes on Nursing", "The founding of the National Conference Group (later NANDA) in 1973", "The Nurse Practice Act of 1938", "The introduction of SMART goal criteria"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The founding of the National Conference Group (later NANDA) in 1973
Originally, the nursing process had FOUR steps (assessment, planning, implementation, evaluation). In 1973, the NATIONAL CONFERENCE GROUP for the Classification of Nursing Diagnoses was formed (it became NANDA — North American Nursing Diagnosis Association — in 1982, now NANDA International). This led to NURSING DIAGNOSIS being recognised as a distinct step, giving the FIVE-STEP process: Assessment, Diagnosis, Planning, Implementation, Evaluation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Publication of Florence Nightingale's Notes on Nursing — Nightingale's "Notes on Nursing" (1859) predates the nursing process.
• The Nurse Practice Act of 1938 — The Nurse Practice Act of 1938 regulated nursing practice, not the nursing process steps.
• The introduction of SMART goal criteria — SMART goals are a tool for writing objectives, not related to adding the diagnosis step.

⭐ Key point: NANDA-I provides standardised nursing diagnosis labels, definitions, defining characteristics and related factors.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q140$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2950, $s$Which characteristic of the nursing process reflects the requirement that care be tailored to each individual rather than applied uniformly?$s$, $s$["Interpersonal nature", "Client-centeredness", "Systematic organization", "Universality"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Client-centeredness
CLIENT-CENTREDNESS means the nursing process is TAILORED to EACH INDIVIDUAL client's unique needs, values, preferences, culture and circumstances, rather than applying the same plan to everyone. The client (and family) are involved in planning care and setting goals.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Interpersonal nature — The interpersonal nature refers to the relationship and communication between nurse and client.
• Systematic organization — Systematic organisation means following an orderly sequence of steps.
• Universality — Universality means the process can be used in all settings and with all clients — it does not mean care is uniform.

⭐ Key point: Characteristics of the nursing process: systematic, dynamic, interpersonal, goal-oriented, client-centred, and universally applicable.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q141$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2951, $s$A client reports, "I have a throbbing headache that is a 7 out of 10." This is an example of which type of data?$s$, $s$["Subjective data", "Validated data", "Objective data", "Secondary data"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Subjective data
SUBJECTIVE DATA are what the CLIENT SAYS — their own feelings, perceptions and SYMPTOMS, which only the client can describe. "I have a throbbing headache that is a 7 out of 10" is the client's own report of pain, so it is subjective. Pain, nausea, anxiety and dizziness are typical subjective data. Record subjective data in the client's own words, using quotation marks.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Validated data — Validated data have been checked or confirmed for accuracy (any type of data can be validated).
• Objective data — OBJECTIVE data are observed or measured by the nurse (e.g. BP, temperature, wound appearance, lab results).
• Secondary data — Secondary data come from sources other than the client (e.g. family, chart).

⭐ Key point: Subjective = what the client SAYS (symptoms). Objective = what the nurse OBSERVES or MEASURES (signs).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q142$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2952, $s$Which physical examination technique uses tapping on the body surface to assess underlying structures based on the sound produced (e.g., dullness vs resonance)?$s$, $s$["Inspection", "Auscultation", "Palpation", "Percussion"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Percussion
PERCUSSION involves TAPPING on the body surface (usually with the fingers) to produce SOUNDS that reveal the density of underlying structures. Sounds include: RESONANCE (normal air-filled lung), HYPER-RESONANCE (too much air — e.g. pneumothorax, emphysema), DULLNESS (solid organs or fluid — e.g. liver, consolidation), STONY DULLNESS (pleural effusion), FLATNESS (bone, muscle), and TYMPANY (air-filled bowel or stomach).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Inspection — INSPECTION is looking (observing) carefully.
• Auscultation — AUSCULTATION is listening with a stethoscope (e.g. heart, breath and bowel sounds).
• Palpation — PALPATION is feeling with the hands (e.g. texture, temperature, tenderness, masses).

⭐ Key point: The usual examination order is inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation — EXCEPT the abdomen.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q144$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2953, $s$A nurse compares a client's current status to data obtained several months ago during a scheduled follow-up visit. This type of assessment is called:$s$, $s$["Initial assessment", "Time-lapsed reassessment", "Focused assessment", "Emergency assessment"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Time-lapsed reassessment
A TIME-LAPSED REASSESSMENT is done after a significant period of time (e.g. weeks or months) to COMPARE the client's CURRENT STATUS with BASELINE data collected earlier. It identifies changes — improvement, stability or deterioration — and helps evaluate whether treatment goals are being met. Example: at a scheduled follow-up clinic visit, the nurse compares the client's current weight, BP, blood glucose control and functional ability with the data recorded months ago.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Initial assessment — An INITIAL (comprehensive) assessment is done when the client first enters care to collect baseline data.
• Focused assessment — A FOCUSED assessment examines a specific problem or body system in detail (e.g. reassessing pain after analgesia).
• Emergency assessment — An EMERGENCY assessment is a rapid check of life-threatening problems (ABC).

⭐ Key point: Comparing data over time is how nurses identify trends, which are often more meaningful than single readings.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q147$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2954, $s$A nurse writes the diagnosis "Risk for Infection related to presence of invasive central venous catheter." Which type of nursing diagnosis is this?$s$, $s$["Possible", "Actual", "Risk", "Syndrome"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Risk
A RISK NURSING DIAGNOSIS describes a problem that is NOT present now, but the client is at HIGH RISK of developing it because of RISK FACTORS. "Risk for Infection related to presence of invasive central venous catheter" is a risk diagnosis: there is no infection now, but the catheter is a risk factor. Risk diagnoses have two parts (problem + risk factors) and are written as "Risk for…".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Possible — A "possible" diagnosis means the nurse suspects a problem but needs more data to confirm it.
• Actual — An ACTUAL diagnosis describes a problem that is present now, with defining characteristics (signs and symptoms).
• Syndrome — A SYNDROME diagnosis describes a cluster of nursing diagnoses that occur together.

⭐ Key point: Nursing interventions for risk diagnoses focus on PREVENTION and monitoring.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q148$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2955, $s$"Rape-Trauma Syndrome" and "Disuse Syndrome" are examples of which type of nursing diagnosis?$s$, $s$["Risk diagnosis", "Actual diagnosis", "Syndrome diagnosis", "Health promotion diagnosis"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Syndrome diagnosis
A SYNDROME DIAGNOSIS describes a CLUSTER of actual or risk NURSING DIAGNOSES that are predicted to occur together because of a certain event or situation. Examples: "RAPE-TRAUMA SYNDROME" (a cluster of psychological and physical responses after rape) and "DISUSE SYNDROME" (risk of complications from immobility, e.g. pressure ulcers, constipation, contractures, pneumonia).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Risk diagnosis — A risk diagnosis is a single potential problem.
• Actual diagnosis — An actual diagnosis is a single present problem.
• Health promotion diagnosis — A health promotion diagnosis describes a client's readiness to improve health (e.g. "Readiness for enhanced nutrition").

⭐ Key point: Types of NANDA nursing diagnoses: actual (problem-focused), risk, health promotion, and syndrome.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q149$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2956, $s$In the standard PES format for writing an actual nursing diagnosis, what does the "E" represent?$s$, $s$["Emergency status", "Evaluation", "Examination findings", "Etiology (related factors)"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Etiology (related factors)
The PES format is used to write ACTUAL nursing diagnoses: P = PROBLEM (the NANDA diagnostic label, e.g. "Impaired Gas Exchange"); E = ETIOLOGY (the RELATED FACTORS or causes, written after "related to"); S = SIGNS AND SYMPTOMS (the defining characteristics, written after "as evidenced by"). Example: "Acute Pain related to surgical incision as evidenced by client reporting pain 8/10 and grimacing."

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Emergency status — "Emergency status" is not part of PES.
• Evaluation — Evaluation is a step of the nursing process.
• Examination findings — Examination findings are part of the S (signs and symptoms).

⭐ Key point: The etiology guides nursing interventions — nurses treat the cause where possible.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q150$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2957, $s$Which of the following best describes a "collaborative problem" as distinguished from a nursing diagnosis?$s$, $s$["A diagnosis exclusively determined by the physician", "A synonym for an actual nursing diagnosis", "A problem that the nurse manages independently using nursing interventions alone", "A potential physiological complication that nurses monitor and manage using interventions prescribed by both nursing and medicine"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A potential physiological complication that nurses monitor and manage using interventions prescribed by both nursing and medicine
A COLLABORATIVE PROBLEM is a potential PHYSIOLOGICAL COMPLICATION (e.g. "Potential Complication: haemorrhage", "PC: hypoglycaemia", "PC: increased intracranial pressure") that nurses MONITOR to detect early, and MANAGE using BOTH NURSING-PRESCRIBED and PHYSICIAN-PRESCRIBED interventions. Nurses cannot treat these independently.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A diagnosis exclusively determined by the physician — It is not determined only by physicians; nurses identify and monitor them.
• A synonym for an actual nursing diagnosis — It is not the same as an actual nursing diagnosis.
• A problem that the nurse manages independently using nursing interventions alone — A problem managed independently with nursing interventions alone is a NURSING DIAGNOSIS.

⭐ Key point: Collaborative problems are usually written as "PC: [complication]" (Carpenito's bifocal model).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q152$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2958, $s$When a nurse writes "Impaired Gas Exchange related to Impaired Gas Exchange," what diagnostic error has occurred?$s$, $s$["This is a \"circular\" statement, in which the etiology restates the problem rather than identifying its cause", "This is an example of a wellness diagnosis", "The statement correctly follows the PES format", "This is an example of a syndrome diagnosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: This is a "circular" statement, in which the etiology restates the problem rather than identifying its cause
"Impaired Gas Exchange related to Impaired Gas Exchange" is a CIRCULAR STATEMENT: the ETIOLOGY simply REPEATS the PROBLEM, and does not identify its cause. This gives no guidance for interventions. A correct statement would identify a cause, e.g. "Impaired Gas Exchange related to alveolar-capillary membrane changes (fluid in alveoli)".

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• This is an example of a wellness diagnosis — A wellness diagnosis describes a healthy state.
• The statement correctly follows the PES format — It does not follow the PES format correctly.
• This is an example of a syndrome diagnosis — A syndrome diagnosis is a cluster of diagnoses.

⭐ Key point: Always make the etiology a different, specific cause that nursing can address.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q153$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2959, $s$What does the "S" in SMART goal criteria stand for?$s$, $s$["Standard", "Specific", "Subjective", "Simple"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Specific
SMART is a framework for writing clear, useful goals/objectives: S = SPECIFIC — clearly states WHAT the client will do (not vague); M = MEASURABLE — can be observed or measured (e.g. distance walked, pain score); A = ACHIEVABLE/ATTAINABLE — realistic for this client; R = RELEVANT/REALISTIC — related to the nursing diagnosis and the client's priorities; T = TIME-BOUND — includes a deadline. Example: "The client will walk 30 metres along the corridor with one assistant by the third post-operative day."

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Standard — "Standard" is not what S stands for.
• Subjective — "Subjective" is not what S stands for; goals should be objective and measurable.
• Simple — "Simple" is not what S stands for.

⭐ Key point: Goals should be client-centred ("The client will…"), written with the client, and evaluated at the stated time.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q154$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2960, $s$In the acronym RUMBA, sometimes used alongside SMART to evaluate outcome statements, what does the "M" represent?$s$, $s$["Multidisciplinary", "Motivating", "Measurable", "Mandatory"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Measurable
RUMBA is a set of criteria used (alongside SMART) to check that outcome statements are well written: R = REAL (realistic and relevant), U = UNDERSTANDABLE (clear to the client, family and team), M = MEASURABLE (progress can be observed or measured — e.g. "SpO2 above 94% on room air", "walks 20 metres"), B = BEHAVIOURAL (describes an observable client behaviour or action), A = ACHIEVABLE (the client can reasonably reach it). The "M" therefore stands for MEASURABLE.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Multidisciplinary — "Multidisciplinary" is not what M stands for.
• Motivating — "Motivating" is not what M stands for.
• Mandatory — "Mandatory" is not what M stands for.

⭐ Key point: If an outcome cannot be measured, the nurse cannot evaluate whether it has been achieved.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q156$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2961, $s$Discharge planning should ideally begin:$s$, $s$["Only after the physician writes the discharge order", "On the day of discharge only", "Only if the client requests it", "At the time of admission and continuing throughout the hospital stay"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: At the time of admission and continuing throughout the hospital stay
DISCHARGE PLANNING should begin AT THE TIME OF ADMISSION and continue THROUGHOUT the hospital stay. Early planning identifies needs (e.g. home care, equipment, education, follow-up appointments), reduces delays in discharge, and helps prevent readmissions.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Only after the physician writes the discharge order — Waiting for the discharge order delays planning.
• On the day of discharge only — Starting on the day of discharge is too late.
• Only if the client requests it — Discharge planning is required for all clients, not just those who request it.

⭐ Key point: Involve the client, family and multidisciplinary team in discharge planning.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q158$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2962, $s$Which of the following is NOT an exercise-prescription variable?$s$, $s$["Intensity", "Progression", "Gender", "Duration"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Gender
EXERCISE PRESCRIPTION describes the "dose" of exercise using variables summarised as FITT-P: FREQUENCY (how often — e.g. sessions per day or week), INTENSITY (how hard — e.g. % of maximum heart rate, perceived exertion, resistance), TIME/DURATION (how long each session lasts), TYPE (mode — e.g. walking, cycling, resistance, range-of-motion exercises), and PROGRESSION (how the programme is gradually increased as the patient improves). GENDER is a personal characteristic that may influence planning, but it is NOT an exercise-prescription variable.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Intensity — Intensity is a core prescription variable.
• Progression — Progression is a core prescription variable.
• Duration — Duration is a core prescription variable.

⭐ Key point: Exercise for ICU survivors should start at low intensity and progress gradually, with monitoring of heart rate, BP, SpO2 and fatigue.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 7 · Q161$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2963, $s$An arterial line trace is flattened, with a slurred upstroke and no dicrotic notch (over-damped). Compared with the true pressure, the monitor shows:$s$, $s$["Falsely high systolic and diastolic, with a falsely high mean", "Accurate systolic and diastolic, but a mean 20 mmHg too low", "Falsely LOW systolic and falsely HIGH diastolic, mean about right", "Falsely HIGH systolic and falsely LOW diastolic, mean about right"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Falsely LOW systolic and falsely HIGH diastolic, mean about right
An OVER-DAMPED arterial line (e.g. from air bubbles, clots, kinks, soft tubing) shows a FLATTENED waveform with a SLURRED UPSTROKE and no dicrotic notch. It UNDERESTIMATES the SYSTOLIC pressure (FALSELY LOW) and OVERESTIMATES the DIASTOLIC pressure (FALSELY HIGH). The MEAN arterial pressure stays about RIGHT.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Falsely high systolic and diastolic, with a falsely high mean — Over-damping does not cause both readings to be high.
• Accurate systolic and diastolic, but a mean 20 mmHg too low — Over-damping affects systolic and diastolic; the mean stays about right.
• Falsely HIGH systolic and falsely LOW diastolic, mean about right — Falsely HIGH systolic and LOW diastolic describes an UNDER-DAMPED system.

⭐ Key point: Look for air bubbles, clots, kinks or loose connections. Use the mean arterial pressure (MAP) for decisions if the trace is damped.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q35$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2964, $s$In the hierarchy of evidence for evidence-based practice, the strongest evidence on the effectiveness of an intervention usually comes from:$s$, $s$["A large prospective cohort study followed for years", "Expert opinion from a respected professional body", "A systematic review and meta-analysis of RCTs", "A single large multicentre randomised controlled trial"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A systematic review and meta-analysis of RCTs
In the HIERARCHY OF EVIDENCE, studies are ranked by how well they reduce bias. The strongest evidence on whether an intervention works usually comes from a SYSTEMATIC REVIEW and META-ANALYSIS of RANDOMISED CONTROLLED TRIALS (RCTs). A systematic review collects all relevant trials using a clear, reproducible method; a meta-analysis combines their results statistically, giving a more precise and reliable estimate than any single study. The hierarchy (strongest to weakest): systematic reviews/meta-analyses → RCTs → cohort studies → case-control studies → case series/reports → expert opinion.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• A large prospective cohort study followed for years — A cohort study is observational, so it is more likely to be affected by bias and confounding than RCTs.
• Expert opinion from a respected professional body — Expert opinion is at the BOTTOM of the hierarchy.
• A single large multicentre randomised controlled trial — A single large RCT is strong, but a systematic review of many RCTs is stronger.

⭐ Key point: Evidence-based practice combines the best research evidence with clinical expertise and the patient's values and preferences.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 8 · Q200$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2965, $s$The Medical Research Council (MRC) sum score is used to:$s$, $s$["Grade muscle strength and diagnose ICU-AW", "Measure the depth of sedation hourly", "Assess delirium in ventilated patients", "Predict ICU mortality on admission"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Grade muscle strength and diagnose ICU-AW
The MRC SUM SCORE grades the strength of 6 muscle groups on both sides (12 in total) from 0 (no movement) to 5 (normal strength). The total is 0–60. A score below 48 suggests ICU-ACQUIRED WEAKNESS (ICU-AW).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Measure the depth of sedation hourly — Sedation depth is measured using RASS.
• Assess delirium in ventilated patients — Delirium is assessed using CAM-ICU.
• Predict ICU mortality on admission — Mortality prediction uses scores like APACHE or SOFA.

⭐ Key point: The patient must be awake and cooperative for the MRC score.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q60$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2966, $s$The most important early treatment priority in severe acute pancreatitis is:$s$, $s$["Early surgery to remove the pancreas", "Strict fasting for 2 weeks", "Goal-directed IV fluid resuscitation", "Prophylactic antibiotics in all cases"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Goal-directed IV fluid resuscitation
In severe acute pancreatitis, inflammation causes massive fluid loss into the tissues (third spacing), leading to hypovolaemia and organ failure. The most important early priority is GOAL-DIRECTED IV FLUID RESUSCITATION (e.g. Ringer's lactate), guided by heart rate, BP, urine output and lactate. Avoid over-resuscitation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Early surgery to remove the pancreas — Early surgery is avoided; it increases mortality.
• Strict fasting for 2 weeks — Early feeding (within 24–72 hours) is encouraged; prolonged fasting isn't.
• Prophylactic antibiotics in all cases — Prophylactic antibiotics are not recommended; they're used only for infected necrosis.

⭐ Key point: Also give analgesia, monitor for organ failure and start early enteral nutrition.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 9 · Q72$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2967, $s$Job performance evaluation should be based on:$s$, $s$["Rumours and complaints from patients", "The manager's personal likes and dislikes", "The age and seniority of the staff", "Clear standards and objective evidence"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Clear standards and objective evidence
Performance evaluation (appraisal) should be based on CLEAR STANDARDS (job description and agreed objectives) and OBJECTIVE EVIDENCE (observed practice, records, audit results, feedback). It should be fair, consistent, and include feedback and a development plan. Regular feedback during the year is better than a single annual review.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Rumours and complaints from patients — Rumours and isolated complaints are not reliable evidence.
• The manager's personal likes and dislikes — Personal likes and dislikes make appraisal unfair and biased.
• The age and seniority of the staff — Age and seniority don't measure performance.

⭐ Key point: Good appraisal is a two-way discussion: review achievements, identify learning needs, and set SMART objectives for next year.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q170$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2968, $s$An ICU admission request for a patient with no chance of recovery should prompt:$s$, $s$["Refusal without any explanation to the team", "Transfer to another hospital without notice", "Senior discussion of goals of care and options", "Immediate admission without any discussion"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Senior discussion of goals of care and options
ICU care is intensive, invasive and limited. If a patient has NO chance of recovery, admission may cause suffering without benefit. The request should prompt a SENIOR discussion (consultant intensivist with the referring team) about the GOALS OF CARE, the patient's wishes, and options, including palliative and comfort care on the ward.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Refusal without any explanation to the team — Refusing without explanation is poor communication and may not be in the patient's best interest.
• Transfer to another hospital without notice — Transferring without notice is unsafe and unethical.
• Immediate admission without any discussion — Admitting without discussion may lead to futile treatment.

⭐ Key point: ICU admission decisions balance benefit, patient wishes and resources. Palliative care is active care, not "giving up".$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q175$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2969, $s$The best approach to new technology introduction in ICU is:$s$, $s$["Training, protocols and evaluation before full use", "Letting one nurse decide how it is used", "Keeping it locked away until it is needed", "Immediate use without any staff training"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Training, protocols and evaluation before full use
New technology (a new ventilator, pump or monitoring system) should be introduced in a planned way: TRAIN all staff, write PROTOCOLS, check COMPETENCY, run a trial or pilot, then EVALUATE before full use. This reduces errors and harm.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Letting one nurse decide how it is used — One nurse deciding alone leads to inconsistent and unsafe use.
• Keeping it locked away until it is needed — Locking it away means staff won't be familiar with it when it is needed.
• Immediate use without any staff training — Using it immediately without training is dangerous.

⭐ Key point: Keep training records and assess competency for all staff using new equipment.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q178$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2970, $s$Reflective practice using Gibbs' cycle includes:$s$, $s$["Assess, diagnose, plan, implement and evaluate care", "Description, feelings, evaluation, analysis, conclusion, action", "Input, process, output and feedback as a system", "Plan, do, study and act, repeated as a quality cycle"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Description, feelings, evaluation, analysis, conclusion, action
Gibbs' reflective cycle (1988) has six stages. DESCRIPTION (what happened?), FEELINGS (what were you thinking and feeling?), EVALUATION (what was good and bad?), ANALYSIS (what sense can you make of it?), CONCLUSION (what else could you have done?), ACTION PLAN (what will you do next time?). It helps nurses learn from experience.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Assess, diagnose, plan, implement and evaluate care — Assess, diagnose, plan, implement and evaluate is the nursing process.
• Input, process, output and feedback as a system — Input, process, output and feedback is a systems model.
• Plan, do, study and act, repeated as a quality cycle — Plan, do, study, act is the PDSA quality improvement cycle.

⭐ Key point: Reflection is required for CPD and helps turn experience into learning.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 11 · Q186$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2971, $s$The first step in planning a new ICU service is:$s$, $s$["Recruiting doctors from abroad", "Painting and decorating the unit", "Needs assessment of the population", "Buying ventilators and monitors first"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Needs assessment of the population
Before opening a new ICU, the first step is a NEEDS ASSESSMENT: How many critically ill patients does the population and hospital produce? What types of illness and surgery? What services are already available? This decides the number of BEDS, STAFF, EQUIPMENT and BUDGET needed.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Recruiting doctors from abroad — Recruiting doctors abroad comes later and depends on the needs assessment.
• Painting and decorating the unit — Painting the unit comes much later.
• Buying ventilators and monitors first — Buying equipment first, without knowing needs, wastes money.

⭐ Key point: Planning steps: assess needs, set objectives, plan resources (beds, staff, equipment, budget), implement, and evaluate.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 12 · Q161$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2972, $s$A nurse researcher numbers all 200 patient files in an ICU register and uses a random number generator to select 40 files for chart review. This is an example of$s$, $s$["Convenience sampling", "Quota sampling", "Simple random sampling", "Purposive sampling"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Simple random sampling
SIMPLE RANDOM SAMPLING gives every member of the population an EQUAL CHANCE of being chosen. Numbering all 200 files and using a random number generator to pick 40 is a classic example. It reduces selection bias.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Convenience sampling — Convenience sampling picks whoever is easiest to reach.
• Quota sampling — Quota sampling picks a set number from different groups, non-randomly.
• Purposive sampling — Purposive sampling picks participants deliberately for a reason.

⭐ Key point: Probability sampling types: simple random, systematic (every nth), stratified (random within groups), and cluster.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q181$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2973, $s$A nurse researcher notices a rising rate of pressure ulcers in immobile ICU patients and decides to investigate the issue. This scenario best illustrates the research process step of$s$, $s$["Problem identification", "Data analysis", "Formulating recommendations", "Reporting research findings"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Problem identification
The first step in the research process is PROBLEM IDENTIFICATION: noticing an issue in practice (rising pressure ulcers in immobile patients) and deciding it is worth investigating. The problem is then defined and refined into a research question.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Data analysis — Data analysis comes after data collection.
• Formulating recommendations — Recommendations come at the end.
• Reporting research findings — Reporting the findings comes at the end.

⭐ Key point: Research steps: identify the problem, review the literature, define the question or hypothesis, design, sample, collect data, analyse, interpret, and report.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q184$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2974, $s$Which of the following is an example of a common ERROR in writing a nursing diagnosis, in which the statement is written as a medical diagnosis rather than a nursing diagnosis?$s$, $s$["\"Activity Intolerance related to fatigue\"", "\"Deficient Fluid Volume related to vomiting\"", "\"Diagnosis: Congestive Heart Failure\"", "\"Acute Pain related to tissue trauma\""]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: "Diagnosis: Congestive Heart Failure"
A NURSING diagnosis describes the patient's RESPONSE to a health problem that NURSES can treat independently (such as Activity Intolerance, Acute Pain). A MEDICAL diagnosis names a DISEASE. "Congestive Heart Failure" is a medical diagnosis, so writing it as a nursing diagnosis is an ERROR.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• "Activity Intolerance related to fatigue" — "Activity Intolerance related to fatigue" is a correctly written nursing diagnosis.
• "Deficient Fluid Volume related to vomiting" — "Deficient Fluid Volume related to vomiting" is a correctly written nursing diagnosis.
• "Acute Pain related to tissue trauma" — "Acute Pain related to tissue trauma" is a correctly written nursing diagnosis.

⭐ Key point: Use NANDA-I labels in PES format (Problem, Etiology, Signs and symptoms) for nursing diagnoses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q186$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2975, $s$A study measuring both nurses' workload and their reported levels of burnout to determine whether the two are related, without manipulating either variable, uses a$s$, $s$["True experimental design", "Historical design", "Case-control design with random assignment", "Correlational design"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Correlational design
A CORRELATIONAL design measures two (or more) variables as they naturally occur, WITHOUT manipulating either, to see whether they are RELATED (correlated). Measuring nurses' workload and burnout to see whether they are linked is correlational research. It shows association, not cause and effect.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• True experimental design — A true experiment manipulates a variable with random assignment.
• Historical design — A historical design studies past events and records.
• Case-control design with random assignment — A case-control design compares people with and without an outcome; random assignment isn't used.

⭐ Key point: Correlation (r) ranges from −1 (perfect negative) to +1 (perfect positive). Correlation does not prove causation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q187$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2976, $s$A researcher selects every 5th patient admitted to the ICU, starting from a randomly chosen first patient, until the desired sample size is reached. This sampling technique is$s$, $s$["Stratified random sampling", "Purposive sampling", "Snowball sampling", "Systematic sampling"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Systematic sampling
SYSTEMATIC SAMPLING selects every kth person (here, every 5th) from an ordered list, after choosing the first one at random. It is easy to use and gives an even spread through the population.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Stratified random sampling — Stratified random sampling divides the population into groups and samples randomly within each.
• Purposive sampling — Purposive sampling deliberately chooses participants for a reason.
• Snowball sampling — Snowball sampling asks participants to recruit others.

⭐ Key point: Systematic sampling can be biased if the list has a hidden repeating pattern that matches the sampling interval.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q193$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2977, $s$A study reports p = 0.03. This means that:$s$, $s$["With no real effect, a result this extreme would occur 3% of the time", "The study lost 3% of its data, so the result is not valid", "3% of the patients in the study had serious side effects", "There is a 97% chance that the new treatment really works"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: With no real effect, a result this extreme would occur 3% of the time
A p-value of 0.03 means that, IF there were truly no effect (the null hypothesis is true), a result this extreme (or more) would happen by chance only 3% of the time. Because 0.03 is below 0.05, the result is called "statistically significant", and the null hypothesis is usually rejected.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• The study lost 3% of its data, so the result is not valid — The p-value isn't about missing data.
• 3% of the patients in the study had serious side effects — It isn't the rate of side effects.
• There is a 97% chance that the new treatment really works — It doesn't mean a 97% chance that the treatment works (a common misunderstanding).

⭐ Key point: Statistical significance doesn't always mean clinical importance. Look at the effect size and confidence interval too.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q198$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2978, $s$A type I error in research is:$s$, $s$["Losing participants during follow-up", "Rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually true", "Accepting a null hypothesis that is false", "Using the wrong sample size"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Rejecting a null hypothesis that is actually true
A TYPE I ERROR is REJECTING the null hypothesis when it is actually TRUE: concluding there IS an effect or difference when there really ISN'T (a false positive). The risk of a type I error is set by alpha (usually 0.05).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Losing participants during follow-up — Losing participants during follow-up is attrition.
• Accepting a null hypothesis that is false — Accepting a false null hypothesis is a TYPE II error (a false negative).
• Using the wrong sample size — Using the wrong sample size can lead to errors, but isn't the definition of a type I error.

⭐ Key point: Type I = false positive (alpha). Type II = false negative (beta), often from too small a sample. Power = 1 − beta.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 13 · Q199$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2979, $s$In research study where the researcher present what he hopes to achieve at the end of study will be expressed under:$s$, $s$["Statement of the problem", "Aims/purpose of the study", "Objectives of the study", "Background of the study"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Aims/purpose of the study
The AIMS or PURPOSE of a study is the broad statement of what the researcher HOPES TO ACHIEVE by the end of the study. For example: "The aim of this study is to improve pain management in ICU patients."

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Statement of the problem — The statement of the problem describes the issue that prompted the study.
• Objectives of the study — Objectives are SPECIFIC, measurable steps to achieve the aim.
• Background of the study — The background gives context and existing knowledge.

⭐ Key point: Aim = the overall goal. Objectives = specific, measurable steps (SMART). Research questions or hypotheses come from the objectives.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q182$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2980, $s$Which of the following is a procedure that enables the researcher to organise, interpret, and communicate numerical information?$s$, $s$["Statistics", "Descriptive", "Inferential", "Means"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Statistics
STATISTICS is the set of procedures for ORGANISING, SUMMARISING, INTERPRETING and COMMUNICATING numerical data. It has two branches: DESCRIPTIVE statistics (summarising data: mean, median, percentages, charts) and INFERENTIAL statistics (drawing conclusions about a population: t-test, chi-square, p-values).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Descriptive — "Descriptive" is one branch of statistics, not the whole procedure.
• Inferential — "Inferential" is one branch of statistics.
• Means — "Means" is just one measure (the average).

⭐ Key point: Descriptive = describe the sample. Inferential = generalise to the population and test hypotheses.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q183$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2981, $s$Which nursing diagnosis format is used when the nurse suspects a problem may exist but needs additional data to confirm or rule it out?$s$, $s$["Possible diagnosis", "Actual diagnosis", "Syndrome diagnosis", "Wellness diagnosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Possible diagnosis
A POSSIBLE nursing diagnosis is used when the nurse SUSPECTS a problem but doesn't yet have ENOUGH DATA to confirm or rule it out. More assessment is needed. For example: "Possible social isolation", which is then confirmed or ruled out.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Actual diagnosis — An actual diagnosis is confirmed by signs and symptoms (defining characteristics).
• Syndrome diagnosis — A syndrome diagnosis groups related diagnoses (such as rape-trauma syndrome).
• Wellness diagnosis — A wellness diagnosis describes readiness to improve a healthy state.

⭐ Key point: Types of nursing diagnosis: actual, risk, possible, syndrome, and health-promotion (wellness).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q184$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2982, $s$A nurse made Mr Kay's care plan on admission on Tuesday, then made another care plan for him on Wednesday's shift. Wednesday's plan is a(n):$s$, $s$["Reviewed plan", "Initial plan", "Ongoing plan", "Discharge plan"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ongoing plan
The INITIAL care plan is made on ADMISSION (Tuesday). Plans made or updated on LATER shifts (Wednesday), as the nurse gathers new information and the patient's condition changes, are ONGOING care plans. Care planning is continuous and dynamic.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Reviewed plan — "Reviewed plan" isn't the standard term used here.
• Initial plan — The initial plan was made on Tuesday.
• Discharge plan — A discharge plan prepares for leaving hospital.

⭐ Key point: Types of planning: initial (on admission), ongoing (during the stay), and discharge (before leaving).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q185$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2983, $s$The New York Heart Association (NYHA) functional classification (Class I-IV) for heart failure severity is an example of which scale of measurement?$s$, $s$["Ordinal scale", "Ratio scale", "Interval scale", "Nominal scale"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Ordinal scale
NYHA classes I–IV rank heart failure severity in ORDER (I = no symptoms, IV = symptoms at rest). The categories have a meaningful order, but the GAPS between classes aren't equal or measurable, so this is an ORDINAL scale.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ratio scale — Ratio scales have equal intervals and a true zero (such as weight).
• Interval scale — Interval scales have equal intervals but no true zero (such as temperature in °C).
• Nominal scale — Nominal scales are categories without order (such as blood group).

⭐ Key point: Other ordinal scales: GCS, pain scores, Likert scales, cancer stages.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q186$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2984, $s$Which step of the research process involves condensing raw data into tables, charts, and summary statistics to make it more understandable?$s$, $s$["Literature review", "Problem identification", "Formulation of research questions", "Organisation and analysis of data"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Organisation and analysis of data
ORGANISATION and ANALYSIS of data condenses raw data into tables, charts, graphs and summary statistics (descriptive and inferential) so the results can be understood and interpreted. This step follows data collection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Literature review — The literature review looks at existing research before the study.
• Problem identification — Problem identification is the first step.
• Formulation of research questions — Formulating research questions happens early in the process.

⭐ Key point: After analysis: interpretation of findings, conclusions, recommendations and dissemination (reporting).$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q187$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2985, $s$Which type of nursing diagnosis describes a problem that is currently present and validated by the existence of defining characteristics (signs and symptoms)?$s$, $s$["Actual nursing diagnosis", "Risk nursing diagnosis", "Health promotion diagnosis", "Possible nursing diagnosis"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Actual nursing diagnosis
An ACTUAL nursing diagnosis describes a problem that is PRESENT now, confirmed by defining characteristics (signs and symptoms). It is written in PES format: Problem, Etiology ("related to"), Signs and symptoms ("as evidenced by").

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Risk nursing diagnosis — A risk diagnosis describes a problem that might develop (no signs yet).
• Health promotion diagnosis — A health promotion diagnosis describes readiness to improve wellbeing.
• Possible nursing diagnosis — A possible diagnosis means the problem is suspected but not confirmed.

⭐ Key point: Example: "Ineffective airway clearance related to thick secretions as evidenced by coarse crackles and a weak cough."$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q193$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2986, $s$The ideal tip position for a central venous catheter is:$s$, $s$["Deep in the right ventricle near the apex", "The lower superior vena cava at the cavo-atrial junction", "In the left atrium across the septum", "In the internal jugular vein near the jaw"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: The lower superior vena cava at the cavo-atrial junction
The ideal tip position for a central venous catheter (jugular or subclavian) is in the LOWER SUPERIOR VENA CAVA at the CAVO-ATRIAL JUNCTION (where the SVC meets the right atrium). Blood flow there dilutes irritant drugs, and pressure readings are accurate.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Deep in the right ventricle near the apex — Deep in the right ventricle risks arrhythmias and perforation.
• In the left atrium across the septum — The left atrium is abnormal (wrong position).
• In the internal jugular vein near the jaw — High in the internal jugular vein is too short and risks thrombosis or dislodgement.

⭐ Key point: Always check the post-insertion X-ray for tip position and pneumothorax before using the line.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 14 · Q197$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2987, $s$For monitoring global perfusion, venous oxygen saturation is NOT reliably measured from a:$s$, $s$["Central venous catheter in the superior vena cava", "Pulmonary artery catheter", "Femoral venous catheter", "Continuous oximetric central catheter"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Femoral venous catheter
Global venous oxygen saturation (ScvO₂ or SvO₂) reflects oxygen use by the whole body. It is measured from a catheter in the SUPERIOR VENA CAVA (ScvO₂) or PULMONARY ARTERY (SvO₂). A FEMORAL venous catheter samples blood from the LEGS only, which doesn't reflect the whole body.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Central venous catheter in the superior vena cava — An SVC catheter is reliable for ScvO₂.
• Pulmonary artery catheter — A PA catheter gives true mixed venous SvO₂.
• Continuous oximetric central catheter — A continuous oximetric central catheter measures ScvO₂ continuously.

⭐ Key point: Normal ScvO₂ is above 70%; SvO₂ is 60–80%. Low values suggest poor oxygen delivery.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q115$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2988, $s$A researcher writes: 'What are the lived experiences of family members of patients admitted to the ICU?' This is best classified as a$s$, $s$["Null hypothesis", "Qualitative research question", "Statistical hypothesis requiring inferential testing", "Directional hypothesis"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Qualitative research question
An open-ended question exploring "LIVED EXPERIENCES" (what it is like for family members) is a QUALITATIVE RESEARCH QUESTION. Qualitative research explores meanings, feelings and experiences using interviews and observation, not numbers. This type of question suits phenomenology.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Null hypothesis — A null hypothesis states there is no difference or relationship.
• Statistical hypothesis requiring inferential testing — A statistical hypothesis needs numbers and testing.
• Directional hypothesis — A directional hypothesis predicts the direction of a relationship.

⭐ Key point: Qualitative research asks "what", "how" and "why" about experiences. Quantitative research measures and tests.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q183$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2989, $s$A researcher wants to explore in depth the coping strategies of five ICU nurses who have personally experienced a patient's cardiac arrest during their shift, deliberately selecting nurses with this specific experience. This is an example of$s$, $s$["Purposive sampling", "Simple random sampling", "Cluster sampling", "Systematic sampling"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Purposive sampling
PURPOSIVE sampling means DELIBERATELY choosing participants who have specific knowledge or experience relevant to the research question (here, nurses who have experienced a patient's cardiac arrest). It is common in qualitative research.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Simple random sampling — Simple random sampling gives everyone an equal chance of selection.
• Cluster sampling — Cluster sampling randomly selects whole groups.
• Systematic sampling — Systematic sampling selects every kth person.

⭐ Key point: Qualitative studies use small, information-rich samples; quantitative studies use larger, random samples to generalise.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q184$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2990, $s$Mr Zee says his heart pounds and he is short of breath on the stairs. The nurse then auscultates the heart and checks the pulse. The nurse is:$s$, $s$["Collecting data", "Processing data into information", "Validating data", "Organising data"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Validating data
Mr Zee REPORTS (subjective data) a pounding heart and breathlessness. The nurse then auscultates the heart and checks the pulse (objective data) to CONFIRM the complaint. Checking that data are accurate by comparing subjective and objective information is VALIDATING DATA.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Collecting data — Collecting data is gathering information; this step goes further to confirm it.
• Processing data into information — Processing data into information is analysis.
• Organising data — Organising data is grouping it into categories.

⭐ Key point: Validation prevents errors in nursing diagnosis, for example confirming a pain complaint with observation.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q186$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2991, $s$A nurse reviewing an electronic health record to identify a client's past surgical history and allergies is using which source and method of data collection?$s$, $s$["Secondary source, record review method", "Secondary source, physical examination method", "Primary source, observation method", "Primary source, interview method"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Secondary source, record review method
The electronic health record is a SECONDARY SOURCE of data (information not obtained directly from the patient). Reviewing it to find past surgical history and allergies is the RECORD REVIEW method of data collection.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Secondary source, physical examination method — Physical examination is a method used with the patient (a primary source), not the record.
• Primary source, observation method — Observation of the patient is a primary source method.
• Primary source, interview method — Interviewing the patient is a primary source method.

⭐ Key point: The patient is the PRIMARY source. Family, records, and other professionals are SECONDARY sources.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q187$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2992, $s$A patient's marital status (single, married, divorced, widowed) recorded in a research questionnaire is measured on which scale?$s$, $s$["Ratio", "Ordinal", "Nominal", "Interval"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Nominal
Marital status (single, married, divorced, widowed) consists of CATEGORIES with NO natural order or numerical value. This is a NOMINAL scale.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Ratio — Ratio scales have equal intervals and a true zero (such as weight).
• Ordinal — Ordinal scales have ordered categories (such as pain mild, moderate, severe).
• Interval — Interval scales have equal intervals without a true zero (such as temperature in °C).

⭐ Key point: Nominal data are summarised with counts and percentages, and analysed with chi-square tests.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q188$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2993, $s$Which of the following data collection methods relies most heavily on the nurse's use of the senses (sight, hearing, smell, touch) without direct questioning of the client?$s$, $s$["Consultation with the physician", "Observation", "Chart review", "Interview"]$s$::jsonb, 1, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Observation
OBSERVATION uses the nurse's SENSES (sight, hearing, smell, touch) to gather data about the patient WITHOUT asking questions, for example noticing skin colour, breathing pattern, facial expressions, smells (ketones, infection) and sounds (wheeze).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Consultation with the physician — Consultation with the physician gathers information from another professional.
• Chart review — Chart review uses written records.
• Interview — An interview involves questioning the patient.

⭐ Key point: Assessment methods: observation, interview, physical examination (inspection, palpation, percussion, auscultation) and record review.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q189$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2994, $s$______ describes the present state of affairs as it exists without having any control over variables.$s$, $s$["Descriptive research", "Distinctive research", "Applied research", "Analytical research"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Descriptive research
DESCRIPTIVE research describes the PRESENT state of affairs, as it exists, WITHOUT CONTROLLING or manipulating any variables. It answers "what is happening?" (such as surveys of nurses' knowledge).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Distinctive research — "Distinctive research" isn't a recognised research type.
• Applied research — Applied research aims to solve practical problems (it can be of any design).
• Analytical research — Analytical research explains relationships and causes, often testing hypotheses.

⭐ Key point: Descriptive studies often come first, identifying problems that later experimental studies can test.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q190$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2995, $s$Before a data collection tool (such as a questionnaire) is used in the main study, it should first undergo:$s$, $s$["Immediate distribution to all participants without review", "Full statistical analysis of anticipated results", "Translation into as many languages as possible regardless of relevance", "Pilot testing to check its clarity, validity, and reliability"]$s$::jsonb, 3, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Pilot testing to check its clarity, validity, and reliability
Before a data collection tool (questionnaire) is used in the main study, it should be PILOT TESTED on a small group similar to the study population. This checks that the questions are CLEAR and understood, that the tool is VALID (measures what it should) and RELIABLE (consistent), and how long it takes. Problems are fixed before the main study.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Immediate distribution to all participants without review — Distributing without review risks poor-quality data.
• Full statistical analysis of anticipated results — Statistical analysis is done after data collection.
• Translation into as many languages as possible regardless of relevance — Translation is only needed where relevant to the population.

⭐ Key point: Pilot study participants are usually not included in the main study.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q193$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2996, $s$A survey of ICU nurses' knowledge and attitudes toward pain assessment in non-verbal patients, without any intervention, best exemplifies a$s$, $s$["Quasi-experimental research design with two groups", "True experimental research design", "Descriptive research design", "Case-control design"]$s$::jsonb, 2, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Descriptive research design
A survey that DESCRIBES nurses' existing knowledge and attitudes WITHOUT any intervention or manipulation is a DESCRIPTIVE research design. It shows the current situation.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Quasi-experimental research design with two groups — A quasi-experimental design involves an intervention without random assignment.
• True experimental research design — A true experiment involves an intervention with random assignment and a control group.
• Case-control design — A case-control design compares people with and without an outcome to find causes.

⭐ Key point: Descriptive surveys are often used to identify education needs before an intervention study.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q194$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2997, $s$Ionic (High Osmolar) contrast media, compared to non-ionic (Low Osmolar) contrast media, are associated with:$s$, $s$["More side effects but lower cost", "No side effects at any dose", "Fewer side effects and higher cost", "Identical side-effect profile and cost"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: More side effects but lower cost
IONIC (high-osmolar) contrast media break into charged particles and are much more concentrated than blood. They cause MORE SIDE EFFECTS (pain, heat, nausea, allergic-type reactions, kidney injury) than non-ionic low-osmolar agents, but are CHEAPER.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• No side effects at any dose — Non-ionic low-osmolar agents have fewer side effects and a higher cost; this option describes those.
• Fewer side effects and higher cost — All contrast can cause side effects.
• Identical side-effect profile and cost — They don't have identical side effects or cost.

⭐ Key point: Most hospitals now use non-ionic low-osmolar contrast for IV studies because it is safer.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q196$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2998, $s$In an electronic health record, an audit trail is used to:$s$, $s$["Record who accessed or changed each record and when", "Speed up the hospital computer system", "Print discharge summaries automatically", "Delete old patient files automatically"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: Record who accessed or changed each record and when
An AUDIT TRAIL in an electronic health record automatically RECORDS WHO accessed or changed each record, WHAT they did, and WHEN. It supports confidentiality (detecting inappropriate access) and accountability (tracking changes).

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Speed up the hospital computer system — It doesn't speed up the computer system.
• Print discharge summaries automatically — It doesn't print discharge summaries.
• Delete old patient files automatically — It doesn't delete files.

⭐ Key point: Only access records of patients you are caring for. Inappropriate access is detected through audit trails.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q198$s$),
($s$bank$s$, $s$D3 · Research, Statistics, Nursing Process & Theories$s$, false, 2999, $s$A potassium result of 6.9 mmol/L comes back from a difficult blood draw in a patient with a normal ECG. The nurse should suspect:$s$, $s$["A haemolysed sample; arrange a repeat sample", "Normal value for an ICU patient", "Definite hyperkalaemia; give insulin at once", "Laboratory error in sodium only"]$s$::jsonb, 0, null, $s$✅ Correct answer: A haemolysed sample; arrange a repeat sample
A very high potassium (6.9) from a DIFFICULT blood draw, with a NORMAL ECG, suggests a HAEMOLYSED sample. When red cells are damaged during collection (a small needle, squeezing, a long tourniquet time, shaking), they release potassium, giving a falsely high result (pseudohyperkalaemia). Arrange a REPEAT sample, while still checking the ECG and the patient.

❌ Why the other options are wrong:
• Normal value for an ICU patient — 6.9 isn't normal for any patient.
• Definite hyperkalaemia; give insulin at once — Giving insulin for a likely false result could cause dangerous hypoglycaemia and hypokalaemia.
• Laboratory error in sodium only — The question is about potassium, not sodium.

⭐ Key point: If the repeat sample confirms high potassium, or the ECG changes, treat urgently.$s$, $s$CCN Mock Day 15 · Q199$s$);
