($s$keynote_cards$s$, $s$9. Acid–Base Balance, pH and ABG Interpretation$s$, false, 587, $s$Respiratory acidosis$s$, null, null, $s$treat the cause; position upright, encourage deep breathing/coughing, physiotherapy, suction; bronchodilators; reverse opioids with naloxone / benzodiazepines with flumazenil (caution); controlled oxygen (COPD 88–92%); NIV (BiPAP) if pH < 7.35 and PaCO₂ > 45–50 in COPD; intubate if failing. On a ventilator: increase minute ventilation (rate first, or tidal volume) to blow off CO₂. Avoid sedatives.$s$, null, $s$9. Acid–Base Balance, pH and ABG Interpretation › 9.9 Nursing management by disorder$s$),
($s$keynote_cards$s$, $s$9. Acid–Base Balance, pH and ABG Interpretation$s$, false, 588, $s$Respiratory alkalosis$s$, null, null, $s$treat the cause (pain relief, reduce anxiety — calm coaching, slow breathing, treat fever/hypoxia); on a ventilator reduce rate/tidal volume or sedate if the patient is over-breathing. Rebreathing into a bag is no longer recommended (risk of hypoxia).$s$, null, $s$9. Acid–Base Balance, pH and ABG Interpretation › 9.9 Nursing management by disorder$s$),
($s$keynote_cards$s$, $s$9. Acid–Base Balance, pH and ABG Interpretation$s$, false, 589, $s$Metabolic acidosis$s$, null, null, $s$treat the cause — fluids/insulin for DKA, restore perfusion in shock (fluids, vasopressors, source control), dialysis for renal failure, antidotes for poisoning. Monitor K⁺ and ECG. Sodium bicarbonate only in specific cases (severe acidosis pH < 7.1 with hyperkalaemia, tricyclic overdose, some renal failure) — it can worsen intracellular acidosis and cause hypernatraemia, fluid overload, hypokalaemia.$s$, null, $s$9. Acid–Base Balance, pH and ABG Interpretation › 9.9 Nursing management by disorder$s$),
($s$keynote_cards$s$, $s$9. Acid–Base Balance, pH and ABG Interpretation$s$, false, 590, $s$Metabolic alkalosis$s$, null, null, $s$stop the cause (antiemetics, review NG suction and diuretics), replace fluid with 0.9% saline and potassium chloride; acetazolamide in some cases; monitor for arrhythmias and hypoventilation.$s$, null, $s$9. Acid–Base Balance, pH and ABG Interpretation › 9.9 Nursing management by disorder$s$),
($s$keynote_cards$s$, $s$10. Oxygen Therapy and Airway Management$s$, false, 591, $s$Targets$s$, null, null, $s$most acutely ill adults 94–98%; patients at risk of hypercapnia (COPD, obesity hypoventilation, neuromuscular/chest wall disease) 88–92%; after cardiac arrest/ROSC 94–98% (avoid hyperoxia); critically ill on ventilators often 92–96%.$s$, null, $s$10. Oxygen Therapy and Airway Management › 10.1 Oxygen therapy$s$),
($s$keynote_cards$s$, $s$10. Oxygen Therapy and Airway Management$s$, false, 592, $s$Signs of hypoxia$s$, null, null, $s$restlessness, anxiety, confusion (early); tachycardia, tachypnoea, hypertension (early) → cyanosis, bradycardia, hypotension, arrhythmias (late).$s$, null, $s$10. Oxygen Therapy and Airway Management › 10.1 Oxygen therapy$s$),
($s$keynote_cards$s$, $s$10. Oxygen Therapy and Airway Management$s$, false, 593, $s$Hazards$s$, null, null, $s$fire (no smoking, no oil/grease on fittings), oxygen toxicity (FiO₂ > 0.6 for > 24–48 h — absorption atelectasis, lung injury), retinopathy of prematurity (neonates), CO₂ retention in at-risk patients, drying of mucosa (humidify above 4 L/min or for long periods).$s$, null, $s$10. Oxygen Therapy and Airway Management › 10.1 Oxygen therapy$s$),
($s$keynote_cards$s$, $s$10. Oxygen Therapy and Airway Management$s$, false, 594, $s$Pulse oximetry pitfalls$s$, null, null, $s$falsely low with poor perfusion/cold peripheries, motion, nail polish, bright light; falsely normal in carbon monoxide poisoning and doesn't show PaCO₂ (a patient on high oxygen can have a normal SpO₂ while CO₂ rises).$s$, null, $s$10. Oxygen Therapy and Airway Management › 10.1 Oxygen therapy$s$),
($s$keynote_cards$s$, $s$10. Oxygen Therapy and Airway Management$s$, false, 595, $s$Look, listen, feel$s$, null, null, $s$see-saw (paradoxical) breathing, noisy breathing (snoring = tongue/partial obstruction; gurgling = fluid; stridor = upper airway narrowing — emergency; wheeze = lower airway), silence = complete obstruction.$s$, null, $s$10. Oxygen Therapy and Airway Management › 10.2 Airway assessment and opening$s$),
