($s$keynote_cards$s$, $s$10. Oxygen Therapy and Airway Management$s$, false, 604, $s$Complications$s$, null, null, $s$hypoxia, oesophageal intubation, right main bronchus intubation (left side silent), dental/airway trauma, aspiration, laryngospasm, bradycardia (vagal), hypotension after intubation (sedation + positive pressure reduces venous return — have fluids and vasopressor ready).$s$, null, $s$10. Oxygen Therapy and Airway Management › 10.3 Endotracheal intubation$s$),
($s$keynote_cards$s$, $s$10. Oxygen Therapy and Airway Management$s$, false, 605, $s$Suctioning (closed or open)$s$, null, null, $s$only when needed (secretions visible/audible, ↑ peak pressure, ↓ SpO₂, saw-tooth flow wave); pre-oxygenate (100% for 30–60 s); catheter no more than half the internal diameter of the tube; pressure 80–150 mmHg (adults); insert without suction until resistance, withdraw 1 cm, then suction while withdrawing; no longer than 10–15 seconds; maximum 2–3 passes; do not instil saline routinely. Watch for hypoxia, bradycardia (vagal), arrhythmias, ↑ICP, bronchospasm, trauma.$s$, null, $s$10. Oxygen Therapy and Airway Management › 10.4 Care of the intubated patient$s$),
