($s$keynote_cards$s$, $s$13. Cardiac Critical Care$s$, false, 758, $s$Intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP)$s$, null, null, $s$balloon in the descending thoracic aorta (tip just below the left subclavian artery) inflates in diastole (at the dicrotic notch → ↑ coronary perfusion) and deflates just before systole (↓ afterload, ↓ myocardial O₂ demand). Used for cardiogenic shock, refractory angina, mechanical MI complications. Nursing: check limb perfusion distal to insertion (cold pulseless leg = limb ischaemia — urgent), left radial pulse/urine output (balloon too high blocks the subclavian; too low blocks renal arteries), keep the leg straight (head of bed ≤ 30°), anticoagulation and bleeding, platelets (thrombocytopenia), timing. Weaning: reduce the ratio 1:1 → 1:2 → 1:3.$s$, null, $s$13. Cardiac Critical Care › 13.6 Pacemakers and IABP$s$),
