($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$1. Normal Values and Ranges$s$, false, 0, $s$1. Normal Values and Ranges$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 1. Normal Values and Ranges

> Values vary slightly between laboratories. Learn the ranges below — they are the ones used in most Nigerian teaching hospitals and in council examinations. Always compare a result with the patient's own trend.

## 1.1 Vital signs (adult)

| Parameter | Normal | Key thresholds |
|---|---|---|
| Temperature | 36.5–37.5 °C | Fever ≥ 38.0 °C; hyperpyrexia ≥ 41 °C; hypothermia < 35 °C (severe < 28 °C) |
| Pulse / heart rate | 60–100 beats/min | Tachycardia > 100; bradycardia < 60 |
| Respiratory rate | 12–20 breaths/min | Tachypnoea > 20; bradypnoea < 12; apnoea = no breathing ≥ 20 s |
| Blood pressure | 90–120 / 60–80 mmHg | Hypertension ≥ 140/90; hypotension SBP < 90 or MAP < 65; crisis ≥ 180/120 |
| Mean arterial pressure (MAP) | 70–100 mmHg | ≥ 65 needed to perfuse organs (≥ 60 minimum) |
| Pulse pressure (SBP − DBP) | 30–50 mmHg | Narrow: shock, tamponade; wide: raised ICP (Cushing's), aortic regurgitation, sepsis |
| SpO₂ | 95–100% | < 94% act; < 90% = hypoxaemia; COPD target 88–92% |
| Capillary refill | < 2 seconds | > 3 s = poor perfusion |
| Pain score | 0/10 | Reassess 30–60 min after analgesia |
| Urine output | ≥ 0.5 mL/kg/hr (≈ 30 mL/hr) | Oliguria < 0.5 mL/kg/hr or < 400 mL/day; anuria < 100 mL/day; child ≥ 1 mL/kg/hr |
| Blood glucose (random) | 4–7.8 mmol/L (72–140 mg/dL) | Hypoglycaemia < 4 mmol/L (< 70 mg/dL); ICU target 6–10 mmol/L |

## 1.2 Vital signs by age (children)

| Age | Heart rate | Resp. rate | Systolic BP |
|---|---|---|---|
| Newborn | 110–160 | 40–60 | 60–90 |
| Infant (1–12 months) | 100–160 | 30–50 | 70–100 |
| Toddler (1–3 years) | 90–150 | 24–40 | 80–110 |
| Pre-school (3–5 years) | 80–140 | 22–34 | 80–110 |
| School age (6–12 years) | 70–120 | 18–30 | 90–120 |
| Adolescent (> 12 years) | 60–100 | 12–20 | 100–120 |

- Minimum acceptable systolic BP in a child (1–10 years) = 70 + (2 × age in years).
- Estimated weight (1–10 years) = (age + 4) × 2 kg.
- Child urine output ≥ 1 mL/kg/hr; infant ≥ 2 mL/kg/hr.

## 1.3 Arterial blood gas (ABG)

| Value | Normal | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| pH | 7.35–7.45 | < 7.35 acidaemia; > 7.45 alkalaemia; life-threatening < 7.1 or > 7.6 |
| PaCO₂ | 35–45 mmHg (4.7–6.0 kPa) | Respiratory (acid) component; ↑ = hypoventilation |
| HCO₃⁻ | 22–26 mmol/L | Metabolic (base) component |
| PaO₂ | 80–100 mmHg (10.7–13.3 kPa) | < 80 hypoxaemia; < 60 (8 kPa) = respiratory failure |
| SaO₂ | 95–100% | |
| Base excess (BE) | −2 to +2 mmol/L | Negative (base deficit) = metabolic acidosis; positive = metabolic alkalosis |
| Lactate | 0.5–2.0 mmol/L | > 2 = hypoperfusion; ≥ 4 = severe (septic shock) |
| Anion gap | 8–12 mmol/L (with K⁺ 12–16) | High in DKA, lactic acidosis, renal failure, poisoning |
| Venous pH / PvCO₂ / ScvO₂ | 7.31–7.41 / 41–51 mmHg / 70–80% | ScvO₂ < 70% = O₂ delivery not meeting demand |

- 1 kPa = 7.5 mmHg. To convert kPa to mmHg multiply by 7.5.
- PaO₂/FiO₂ (P/F) ratio: normal ≥ 400; ≤ 300 mild ARDS, ≤ 200 moderate, ≤ 100 severe.
- Expected PaO₂ on oxygen ≈ FiO₂ (%) × 5 (e.g. on 40% O₂ expect about 200 mmHg).

## 1.4 Electrolytes and renal function

| Test | Normal | Low (hypo-) | High (hyper-) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Sodium (Na⁺) | 135–145 mmol/L | < 135: confusion, seizures (< 120 severe) | > 145: thirst, confusion, seizures |
| Potassium (K⁺) | 3.5–5.0 mmol/L | < 3.5: weakness, U waves, arrhythmias | > 5.5: peaked T, wide QRS; > 6.5 emergency |
| Chloride (Cl⁻) | 98–106 mmol/L | vomiting/NG loss | saline excess |
| Bicarbonate (HCO₃⁻) | 22–26 (venous 22–29) mmol/L | metabolic acidosis | metabolic alkalosis |
| Calcium (total) | 2.2–2.6 mmol/L (8.5–10.5 mg/dL) | tetany, Chvostek, Trousseau, long QT | stones, bones, groans, moans |
| Ionised calcium | 1.1–1.3 mmol/L | | |
| Magnesium | 0.7–1.0 mmol/L (1.7–2.4 mg/dL) | arrhythmias, torsades | loss of reflexes, respiratory depression |
| Phosphate | 0.8–1.5 mmol/L | refeeding syndrome | renal failure |
| Urea | 2.5–7.5 mmol/L (BUN 7–20 mg/dL) | | AKI, dehydration, GI bleed |
| Creatinine | 60–110 µmol/L (0.6–1.2 mg/dL) | | AKI/CKD (best renal marker) |
| eGFR | > 90 mL/min/1.73 m² | CKD stage 3 < 60, stage 4 < 30, stage 5 < 15 | |
| Serum osmolality | 275–295 mOsm/kg | SIADH, water excess | dehydration, DI, HHS (> 320) |
| Urine specific gravity | 1.005–1.030 | DI (< 1.005) | dehydration, SIADH |
| Urine osmolality | 300–900 mOsm/kg | | |

## 1.5 Full blood count and coagulation

| Test | Normal | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Haemoglobin | Male 13–17 g/dL; female 12–16 g/dL | Transfuse usually if < 7 g/dL (< 8 in cardiac disease) |
| Packed cell volume (PCV/Hct) | Male 40–52%; female 36–48% | ≈ Hb × 3 |
| White cell count | 4–11 × 10⁹/L | > 12 or < 4 = SIRS criterion; neutropenia < 1.5 |
| Platelets | 150–400 × 10⁹/L | < 50 bleeding risk; < 20 spontaneous bleeding; < 10 transfuse |
| Prothrombin time (PT) | 11–13.5 seconds | Extrinsic pathway; warfarin, liver disease |
| INR | 0.8–1.2 | Warfarin target 2–3 (mechanical valve 2.5–3.5) |
| aPTT | 25–35 seconds | Intrinsic pathway; heparin target 1.5–2.5 × control (~60–80 s) |
| Fibrinogen | 2–4 g/L | < 1.5 in DIC/massive bleeding — give cryoprecipitate |
| D-dimer | < 0.5 mg/L FEU | ↑ in DVT/PE, DIC (sensitive, not specific) |
| ESR | Male < 15, female < 20 mm/hr | Inflammation |
| CRP | < 5 mg/L | Infection/inflammation |

## 1.6 Liver, cardiac, pancreatic and other tests

| Test | Normal | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Total bilirubin | 3–21 µmol/L (0.3–1.2 mg/dL) | Jaundice visible > 40–50 µmol/L |
| ALT / AST | < 40 U/L | Hepatocellular injury (paracetamol overdose: thousands) |
| ALP | 30–130 U/L | Obstructive jaundice, bone disease |
| Albumin | 35–50 g/L | ↓ in liver disease, malnutrition, nephrotic syndrome → oedema |
| Total protein | 60–80 g/L | |
| Troponin (high-sensitivity) | below the lab's 99th centile (often < 14–20 ng/L) | Rises 3–6 h after MI, peaks 24–48 h, stays up 7–14 days |
| CK-MB | < 5% of total CK | Rises 4–6 h, normal by 2–3 days (useful for re-infarction) |
| BNP / NT-proBNP | BNP < 100 pg/mL; NT-proBNP < 300 pg/mL | ↑ in heart failure |
| Amylase | 30–110 U/L | ≥ 3 × upper limit in acute pancreatitis |
| Lipase | < 60 U/L | More specific for pancreatitis |
| HbA1c | < 6.0% (42 mmol/mol) | Diabetes ≥ 6.5% (48 mmol/mol) |
| Serum ketones (β-hydroxybutyrate) | < 0.6 mmol/L | DKA > 3.0 mmol/L |
| Cholesterol (total) | < 5.2 mmol/L | |
| TSH / free T4 | 0.4–4.0 mU/L / 10–25 pmol/L | |
| Cortisol (9 am) | 140–700 nmol/L | |
| Serum lithium (therapeutic) | 0.4–1.0 mmol/L | Toxic > 1.5 |
| Digoxin (therapeutic) | 0.5–2.0 ng/mL (1–2.6 nmol/L) | Toxicity worse with ↓K⁺ |
| Gentamicin trough | < 1 mg/L | Nephro- and ototoxic |

## 1.7 Cerebrospinal fluid (CSF)

| Feature | Normal | Bacterial meningitis | Viral meningitis | TB meningitis |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Appearance | Clear | Turbid/cloudy | Clear | Clear/fibrin web |
| Opening pressure | 7–18 cmH₂O | High | Normal/↑ | High |
| White cells | < 5 /mm³ (lymphocytes) | ↑↑ neutrophils | ↑ lymphocytes | ↑ lymphocytes |
| Protein | 0.15–0.45 g/L | High | Normal/slightly ↑ | Very high |
| Glucose | 2.5–4.5 mmol/L (≈ 60% of blood) | Low | Normal | Low |

## 1.8 Haemodynamic values

| Parameter | Normal | Notes |
|---|---|---|
| Central venous pressure (CVP) | 2–8 mmHg (3–10 cmH₂O) | ↓ hypovolaemia; ↑ fluid overload, RV failure, tamponade, PE, high PEEP |
| Right atrial pressure | 2–6 mmHg | |
| Right ventricular pressure | 15–25 / 0–8 mmHg | |
| Pulmonary artery pressure | 15–25 / 8–15 mmHg (mean 10–20) | Pulmonary hypertension mean > 20–25 |
| Pulmonary artery wedge pressure (PAWP/PCWP) | 6–12 mmHg | Reflects left atrial / LV filling pressure; > 18 = pulmonary oedema risk |
| Cardiac output (CO) | 4–8 L/min | CO = HR × SV |
| Cardiac index (CI) | 2.5–4.0 L/min/m² | < 2.2 = cardiogenic shock |
| Stroke volume (SV) | 60–100 mL/beat | |
| Systemic vascular resistance (SVR) | 800–1200 dyn·s/cm⁵ | ↓ septic/anaphylactic/neurogenic shock; ↑ cardiogenic/hypovolaemic |
| Pulmonary vascular resistance (PVR) | < 250 dyn·s/cm⁵ | |
| Ejection fraction (EF) | 55–70% | Heart failure with reduced EF < 40% |
| Intracranial pressure (ICP) | 5–15 mmHg (adult) | Treat if > 20–22 mmHg |
| Cerebral perfusion pressure (CPP) | 60–80 mmHg | CPP = MAP − ICP; keep ≥ 60–70 |
| Intra-abdominal pressure | 0–5 mmHg (ICU 5–7) | Intra-abdominal hypertension ≥ 12; abdominal compartment syndrome > 20 with organ failure |
| Oxygen delivery (DO₂) | ~1000 mL/min (500–600 mL/min/m²) | |
| Oxygen consumption (VO₂) | ~250 mL/min | |
| Mixed venous saturation (SvO₂) | 60–75% | |

## 1.9 Respiratory and ventilator values

| Parameter | Normal / target |
|---|---|
| Tidal volume (spontaneous) | 5–7 mL/kg (~500 mL) |
| Ventilator tidal volume | 6–8 mL/kg predicted body weight (ARDS: 6 mL/kg; range 4–8) |
| Minute ventilation | 5–8 L/min |
| Vital capacity | 65–75 mL/kg (~4.8 L); < 10–15 mL/kg = intubation likely (e.g. Guillain–Barré, myasthenia) |
| Plateau pressure | ≤ 30 cmH₂O |
| Peak inspiratory pressure | usually < 35–40 cmH₂O |
| PEEP | physiological 3–5 cmH₂O; ARDS often 8–15+ |
| Driving pressure | ≤ 15 cmH₂O |
| Static compliance | 50–100 mL/cmH₂O |
| FiO₂ | room air 21%; aim lowest FiO₂ for SpO₂ 92–96% (≥ 0.6 for long risks O₂ toxicity) |
| I:E ratio | 1:2 (asthma/COPD 1:3–1:4) |
| EtCO₂ | 35–45 mmHg (usually 2–5 mmHg below PaCO₂) |
| Negative inspiratory force (NIF/MIP) | more negative than −20 to −30 cmH₂O for weaning |
| RSBI (f/Vt) | < 105 predicts weaning success |
| ETT cuff pressure | 20–30 cmH₂O (≈ 15–22 mmHg) |
| Peak expiratory flow | 400–600 L/min (adult; depends on age, height, sex) |
| FEV₁/FVC | > 70–80% (< 70% = obstruction) |

## 1.10 Neurological values

| Item | Normal |
|---|---|
| Glasgow Coma Scale | 15/15 (minimum 3). Mild 13–15, moderate 9–12, severe ≤ 8 → protect airway |
| Pupils | 2–5 mm, equal, react briskly (PERRLA). Unequal by > 1 mm = abnormal |
| ICP / CPP | ICP 5–15 mmHg; CPP 60–80 mmHg |
| CSF volume / production | ~150 mL; ~500 mL/day |
| Brain oxygen (PbtO₂) | > 20 mmHg |

## 1.11 Obstetric and neonatal values

| Item | Normal |
|---|---|
| Fetal heart rate | 110–160 beats/min |
| APGAR score (1 and 5 min) | 7–10 normal; 4–6 moderate depression; 0–3 severe |
| Neonatal blood glucose | ≥ 2.6 mmol/L (≥ 45 mg/dL) |
| Neonatal temperature | 36.5–37.5 °C |
| Neonatal jaundice | Physiological appears after 24 h, peaks day 3–5; jaundice in first 24 h is pathological |
| Blood loss at delivery | Vaginal ≤ 500 mL; caesarean ≤ 1000 mL. PPH = more than this |
| Pre-eclampsia | BP ≥ 140/90 after 20 weeks + proteinuria/organ involvement; severe ≥ 160/110 |
| Magnesium sulphate (therapeutic) | 2–3.5 mmol/L (4–7 mEq/L). Toxic signs: loss of knee jerk (> 3.5–5 mmol/L), RR < 12, urine < 30 mL/hr; respiratory arrest > 6 mmol/L. Antidote: 10% calcium gluconate 10 mL IV |
