## 2.1 How medical words are built

| Part | Meaning | Example |
|---|---|---|
| a-, an- | without, absence | apnoea (no breathing), anuria (no urine) |
| brady- | slow | bradycardia, bradypnoea |
| tachy- | fast | tachycardia, tachypnoea |
| dys- | difficult, abnormal | dyspnoea, dysphagia, dysrhythmia |
| hyper- | above, excessive | hyperkalaemia, hypertension |
| hypo- | below, deficient | hypoglycaemia, hypoxia |
| poly- | many, much | polyuria, polydipsia |
| olig- | few, scanty | oliguria |
| hem-, haem-, -aemia | blood | haemoptysis, anaemia, bacteraemia |
| -itis | inflammation | pancreatitis, meningitis |
| -ectomy | surgical removal | thyroidectomy, nephrectomy |
| -ostomy | surgical opening | tracheostomy, colostomy |
| -otomy | cutting into | thoracotomy, craniotomy |
| -plegia / -paresis | paralysis / weakness | hemiplegia, paraparesis |
| -pnoea | breathing | orthopnoea, dyspnoea |
| -uria | urine | haematuria, proteinuria |
| -algia | pain | neuralgia, myalgia |
| -emesis | vomiting | haematemesis, hyperemesis |
| -megaly | enlargement | cardiomegaly, hepatomegaly |
| -penia | deficiency | thrombocytopenia, neutropenia |
| -cyte | cell | erythrocyte, leucocyte |
| -oma | tumour/swelling | haematoma, adenoma |
| -rrhage / -rrhoea | burst forth / flow | haemorrhage, rhinorrhoea |
| cardi-, pulmo-, pneumo- | heart, lung, air/lung | cardiomyopathy, pneumothorax |
| nephr-, ren- | kidney | nephritis, renal |
| hepat- | liver | hepatitis |
| cyan- | blue | cyanosis |

## 2.2 A–Z glossary of key critical care terms

| Term | Meaning |
|---|---|
| Acidosis / acidaemia | Process causing ↑H⁺ / blood pH < 7.35 |
| Afterload | Resistance the ventricle pumps against (SVR for left ventricle) |
| Agonal breathing | Gasping, ineffective breathing of cardiac arrest — treat as NOT breathing |
| Alkalosis / alkalaemia | Process causing ↓H⁺ / blood pH > 7.45 |
| Anaphylaxis | Severe, life-threatening allergic reaction (airway, breathing, circulation problems) — IM adrenaline 0.5 mg |
| Anuria | Urine < 100 mL/24 h |
| Apnoea | Absence of breathing (≥ 20 s in adults; neonates ≥ 20 s or shorter with bradycardia/cyanosis) |
| Arrhythmia / dysrhythmia | Abnormal heart rhythm |
| Ascites | Fluid in the peritoneal cavity |
| Aspiration | Inhalation of gastric contents/foreign material into the lungs |
| Asystole | No electrical activity of the heart (flat line) — non-shockable |
| Atelectasis | Collapse of alveoli/part of lung |
| Auto-PEEP | Trapped air from incomplete exhalation (intrinsic PEEP) |
| Azotaemia | ↑ urea and creatinine in the blood |
| Barotrauma | Lung injury from high pressure (pneumothorax, surgical emphysema) |
| Bradycardia | Heart rate < 60/min |
| Bradypnoea | Respiratory rate < 12/min |
| Cardiac output | Volume pumped by the heart per minute (HR × SV) |
| Cardiac tamponade | Fluid/blood in the pericardium compressing the heart (Beck's triad) |
| Cheyne–Stokes respiration | Cycles of deepening then shallowing breaths with apnoea — brain damage, heart failure, dying |
| Coagulopathy | Impaired blood clotting |
| Compliance | Distensibility of the lungs (volume change per pressure change) |
| Contractility | Force of myocardial contraction independent of preload/afterload (inotropy) |
| Crepitations (crackles) | Fine/coarse sounds on inspiration — fluid in alveoli (oedema, pneumonia) |
| Cyanosis | Bluish skin/mucosa; central (lips, tongue) = hypoxaemia; appears when deoxy-Hb > 5 g/dL |
| Debridement | Removal of dead tissue |
| Decerebrate posturing | Arms and legs EXTEND to pain — brainstem (midbrain/pons) lesion, worse prognosis |
| Decorticate posturing | Arms FLEX to the chest, legs extend — cortical/upper brain lesion |
| Dehiscence | Wound edges separate |
| Delirium | Acute fluctuating confusion with inattention (assess with CAM-ICU) |
| Diaphoresis | Profuse sweating |
| DIC | Disseminated intravascular coagulation — widespread clotting then bleeding |
| Diplopia | Double vision |
| Diuresis | Increased urine output |
| Dysphagia / odynophagia | Difficulty swallowing / painful swallowing |
| Dyspnoea | Difficult or laboured breathing |
| Embolism | Blockage of a vessel by a travelling clot, fat, air or amniotic fluid |
| Emphysema (surgical) | Air under the skin (crackles to touch, "crepitus") |
| Epistaxis | Nosebleed |
| Evisceration | Abdominal organs protrude through a wound |
| Exudate | Protein-rich fluid (inflammation/infection) |
| Haematemesis | Vomiting blood |
| Haematochezia | Fresh red blood per rectum |
| Haemoptysis | Coughing up blood |
| Haemothorax | Blood in the pleural space |
| Hemiplegia / hemiparesis | Paralysis / weakness of one side of the body |
| Hypercapnia | PaCO₂ > 45 mmHg |
| Hypocapnia | PaCO₂ < 35 mmHg |
| Hypoxaemia | Low oxygen in the blood (PaO₂ < 80 mmHg) |
| Hypoxia | Low oxygen at tissue level |
| Hypovolaemia | Low circulating blood volume |
| Iatrogenic | Caused by medical treatment |
| Ileus | Bowel stops moving (paralytic) |
| Infarction | Tissue death from loss of blood supply |
| Inotrope | Drug that changes contractility (positive: dobutamine, adrenaline) |
| Ischaemia | Inadequate blood supply (reversible) |
| Kussmaul breathing | Deep, rapid breathing of metabolic acidosis (DKA) |
| Lethargy | Drowsy, sluggish, but rousable |
| Malaena | Black tarry stool — upper GI bleeding |
| Miosis / mydriasis | Pupil constriction / dilation |
| MODS | Multiple organ dysfunction syndrome |
| Nosocomial | Hospital-acquired (infection) |
| Nystagmus | Involuntary rhythmic eye movements |
| Obtunded | Reduced alertness, slow responses |
| Oedema | Excess fluid in tissues (pitting/non-pitting) |
| Oliguria | Urine < 0.5 mL/kg/hr or < 400 mL/day |
| Orthopnoea | Breathlessness lying flat (heart failure) |
| Paroxysmal nocturnal dyspnoea | Wakes at night breathless (left heart failure) |
| Pericardial rub | Scratching sound — pericarditis |
| Petechiae | Pinpoint bleeding spots (low platelets) |
| Pneumothorax | Air in the pleural space; tension = life-threatening shift |
| Polydipsia / polyuria | Excessive thirst / urine |
| Preload | Volume (stretch) in the ventricle at end of diastole (CVP/PAWP estimate) |
| Pulse deficit | Apical rate higher than radial rate (atrial fibrillation) |
| Pulsus paradoxus | SBP falls > 10 mmHg on inspiration (tamponade, severe asthma) |
| Pyrexia | Fever |
| Rhonchi | Low-pitched snoring sounds — secretions in large airways |
| Sepsis | Life-threatening organ dysfunction from a dysregulated response to infection |
| Shock | Inadequate tissue perfusion and oxygen delivery |
| SIRS | 2 or more: temp > 38 or < 36; HR > 90; RR > 20 or PaCO₂ < 32; WBC > 12 or < 4 |
| Status epilepticus | Seizure > 5 minutes, or repeated seizures without recovery |
| Stridor | High-pitched noise on inspiration — upper airway obstruction (emergency) |
| Stupor | Rousable only by vigorous/painful stimuli |
| Syncope | Fainting (transient loss of consciousness) |
| Tachycardia | Heart rate > 100/min |
| Tachypnoea | Respiratory rate > 20/min |
| Tenesmus | Constant urge to pass stool |
| Thrombosis | Clot formation inside a vessel |
| Transudate | Low-protein fluid (heart failure, low albumin) |
| Trismus | Lockjaw (tetanus) |
| Vasopressor | Drug that constricts vessels to raise BP (noradrenaline, vasopressin) |
| Volutrauma | Lung injury from over-stretching with large volumes |
| Wheeze | Musical expiratory sound — narrowed small airways (asthma, COPD) |

