($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$3. Formulas and Calculations$s$, false, 2, $s$3. Formulas and Calculations$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 3. Formulas and Calculations

> Golden rules: write the formula first, convert units BEFORE you calculate (kg not lb; mcg vs mg; hours vs minutes), estimate the answer in your head, then check it is sensible. 1 mg = 1000 mcg; 1 g = 1000 mg; 1 L = 1000 mL; 1 kg = 2.2 lb.

## 3.1 IV fluids and drip rates

| What | Formula | Worked example |
|---|---|---|
| Rate in mL/hr (pump) | Volume (mL) ÷ time (hours) | 1000 mL over 8 h = 125 mL/hr |
| Drops per minute (gravity) | (Volume mL × drop factor) ÷ time in minutes | 1000 mL × 20 ÷ (8 × 60) = 41.7 ≈ 42 drops/min |
| Shortcut from mL/hr | mL/hr × drop factor ÷ 60 | 125 × 15 ÷ 60 = 31 drops/min |
| Microdrip (60 drops/mL) | drops/min = mL/hr | 45 mL/hr = 45 drops/min |
| Time to finish | Volume ÷ rate | 500 mL at 125 mL/hr = 4 h |

- Drop factors: macrodrip/standard 10, 15 or 20 drops/mL; blood set 15–20 drops/mL; microdrip (paediatric burette) 60 drops/mL.
- Blood: each unit started within 30 minutes of leaving the fridge and completed within 4 hours.

## 3.2 Drug dosage

| What | Formula | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Tablets or volume to give | (Dose wanted ÷ dose in stock) × stock volume | Want 500 mg, have 250 mg/5 mL → 500/250 × 5 = 10 mL |
| Weight-based dose | Dose per kg × weight (kg) | Gentamicin 5 mg/kg × 60 kg = 300 mg |
| Divided daily dose | (mg/kg/day × kg) ÷ number of doses | 60 mg/kg/day × 10 kg ÷ 3 = 200 mg 8-hourly |
| Concentration | Amount of drug ÷ volume | 400 mg dopamine in 250 mL = 1.6 mg/mL = 1600 mcg/mL |
| Percentage solutions | % = grams per 100 mL | 10% dextrose = 10 g/100 mL; 50 mL of 50% = 25 g |
| Ratio solutions | 1:1000 = 1 g in 1000 mL = 1 mg/mL | Adrenaline 1:1000 = 1 mg/mL (IM); 1:10,000 = 0.1 mg/mL (IV arrest) |

## 3.3 Infusions (critical care drips)

| Order | Formula for mL/hr | Example |
|---|---|---|
| mcg/kg/min | (Dose × weight × 60) ÷ concentration (mcg/mL) | Dopamine 5 mcg/kg/min, 70 kg, 1600 mcg/mL → 5 × 70 × 60 ÷ 1600 = 13.1 mL/hr |
| mcg/min | (Dose × 60) ÷ concentration (mcg/mL) | Noradrenaline 4 mg/250 mL = 16 mcg/mL; 8 mcg/min → 8 × 60 ÷ 16 = 30 mL/hr |
| mg/hr | Dose ÷ concentration (mg/mL) | Amiodarone 900 mg/500 mL = 1.8 mg/mL; 1 mg/min = 60 mg/hr → 33.3 mL/hr |
| units/hr | Dose ÷ concentration (units/mL) | Heparin 25,000 units/500 mL = 50 units/mL; 1000 units/hr → 20 mL/hr |
| Reverse (what dose is running?) | Rate (mL/hr) × concentration ÷ (weight × 60) | 15 mL/hr × 1600 ÷ (80 × 60) = 5 mcg/kg/min |

- Insulin infusion: 50 units in 50 mL = 1 unit/mL, so units/hr = mL/hr. DKA fixed rate 0.1 unit/kg/hr.
- Potassium: never IV push. Peripheral line ≤ 10 mmol/hr (and ≤ 40 mmol/L); central line up to 20 mmol/hr with cardiac monitoring.

## 3.4 Burns

| What | Formula | Example |
|---|---|---|
| Parkland (first 24 h) | 4 mL × weight (kg) × %TBSA (partial + full thickness) | 70 kg, 40% → 4 × 70 × 40 = 11,200 mL Ringer's lactate |
| First 8 hours (from time of BURN) | Half of total | 5,600 mL in first 8 h (700 mL/hr) |
| Next 16 hours | Other half | 5,600 mL over 16 h (350 mL/hr) |
| Late arrival | Half ÷ hours left of the first 8 | Arrives 2 h after burn → 5,600 ÷ 6 = 933 mL/hr |
| Children | 3–4 mL/kg/%TBSA + maintenance fluid with dextrose | |
| Modified Brooke | 2 mL/kg/%TBSA | |
| Urine output target | Adult 0.5–1 mL/kg/hr; child 1 mL/kg/hr; electrical burns 1–1.5 mL/kg/hr (myoglobin) | Titrate fluid to urine output, not the formula |

- Rule of Nines (adult): head 9, each arm 9, front trunk 18, back trunk 18, each leg 18, perineum 1 = 100%. Patient's palm with fingers ≈ 1%.
- Do NOT count superficial (first-degree, red only) burns in %TBSA.

## 3.5 Paediatric fluids

| What | Formula |
|---|---|
| Maintenance (Holliday–Segar, per day) | 100 mL/kg for first 10 kg + 50 mL/kg for next 10 kg + 20 mL/kg for each kg above 20 |
| Maintenance (4-2-1, per hour) | 4 mL/kg/hr first 10 kg + 2 mL/kg/hr next 10 kg + 1 mL/kg/hr each kg above 20 |
| Example: 25 kg child | 1000 + 500 + 100 = 1600 mL/day = 40 + 20 + 5 = 65 mL/hr |
| Fluid bolus (shock) | 10–20 mL/kg isotonic crystalloid (10 mL/kg in neonates/heart disease), reassess after each |
| Deficit (dehydration) | % dehydration × weight (kg) × 10 = mL (e.g. 10% × 12 kg × 10 = 1200 mL) |
| Weight estimate (1–10 years) | (Age + 4) × 2 |
| Adrenaline in cardiac arrest (child) | 10 mcg/kg (0.1 mL/kg of 1:10,000) |
| Defibrillation (child) | 4 J/kg |
| Dextrose for hypoglycaemia (child) | 2–5 mL/kg of 10% dextrose |
| ETT size (child > 1 year) | Internal diameter = (age ÷ 4) + 4 (uncuffed); (age ÷ 4) + 3.5 (cuffed). Depth at lips = (age ÷ 2) + 12 cm |

## 3.6 Haemodynamics and oxygenation

| What | Formula | Normal |
|---|---|---|
| Mean arterial pressure | (SBP + 2 × DBP) ÷ 3, or DBP + ⅓ pulse pressure | 70–100 mmHg |
| Pulse pressure | SBP − DBP | 30–50 mmHg |
| Cerebral perfusion pressure | MAP − ICP | 60–80 mmHg |
| Cardiac output | Heart rate × stroke volume | 4–8 L/min |
| Cardiac index | CO ÷ body surface area | 2.5–4.0 L/min/m² |
| Systemic vascular resistance | (MAP − CVP) ÷ CO × 80 | 800–1200 dyn·s/cm⁵ |
| Coronary perfusion pressure | Aortic DBP − LV end-diastolic pressure | > 50–60 mmHg |
| Shock index | Heart rate ÷ systolic BP | 0.5–0.7; > 1 = significant shock |
| Oxygen content (CaO₂) | (1.34 × Hb × SaO₂) + (0.003 × PaO₂) | ~20 mL O₂/dL |
| Oxygen delivery (DO₂) | CO × CaO₂ × 10 | ~1000 mL/min |
| P/F ratio | PaO₂ ÷ FiO₂ (as a decimal) | > 400; ARDS ≤ 300 |
| A–a gradient (room air) | PAO₂ − PaO₂, where PAO₂ = FiO₂ × 713 − PaCO₂ ÷ 0.8 | < (age ÷ 4) + 4; raised in V/Q mismatch, shunt, diffusion problems |
| Oxygen cylinder duration | Cylinder content (L) ÷ flow (L/min) | e.g. 680 L ÷ 10 L/min = 68 min |

## 3.7 Acid–base

| What | Formula | Normal / use |
|---|---|---|
| Anion gap | Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻) | 8–12; high = MUDPILES acids |
| Winter's formula (expected PaCO₂ in metabolic acidosis) | (1.5 × HCO₃⁻) + 8 ± 2 | Actual higher → added respiratory acidosis; lower → added respiratory alkalosis |
| Metabolic alkalosis expected PaCO₂ | 0.7 × HCO₃⁻ + 21 ± 2 (≈ HCO₃⁻ + 15) | |
| Acute respiratory acidosis | HCO₃⁻ rises 1 for every 10 mmHg ↑ PaCO₂ | Chronic: rises 3.5 per 10 |
| Acute respiratory alkalosis | HCO₃⁻ falls 2 for every 10 mmHg ↓ PaCO₂ | Chronic: falls 5 per 10 |
| Corrected calcium | Total Ca (mmol/L) + 0.02 × (40 − albumin g/L) | |
| Corrected sodium in hyperglycaemia | Na⁺ + 1.6 × [(glucose mg/dL − 100) ÷ 100] (≈ Na⁺ + glucose mmol/L ÷ 3.5 above 5.5) | |
| Serum osmolality | 2 × Na⁺ + glucose + urea (all mmol/L) | 275–295 mOsm/kg |
| Bicarbonate deficit | 0.5 × weight × (24 − HCO₃⁻) | Bicarbonate rarely given except severe acidosis/hyperkalaemia/TCA poisoning |

## 3.8 Ventilator calculations

| What | Formula | Example / target |
|---|---|---|
| Predicted body weight (male) | 50 + 0.91 × (height cm − 152.4) | 175 cm → 70.6 kg |
| Predicted body weight (female) | 45.5 + 0.91 × (height cm − 152.4) | 165 cm → 57 kg |
| Tidal volume | 6–8 mL/kg PBW (ARDS 6) | 70 kg PBW × 6 = 420 mL |
| Minute ventilation | Tidal volume × respiratory rate | 0.5 L × 14 = 7 L/min |
| Alveolar ventilation | (Vt − dead space) × RR | Dead space ≈ 2 mL/kg (~150 mL) |
| New rate to change PaCO₂ | Current RR × current PaCO₂ ÷ desired PaCO₂ | RR 14 × PaCO₂ 60 ÷ 40 = 21 |
| Static compliance | Vt ÷ (Pplat − PEEP) | 450 ÷ (25 − 10) = 30 mL/cmH₂O (low) |
| Dynamic compliance | Vt ÷ (PIP − PEEP) | |
| Airway resistance | (PIP − Pplat) ÷ flow (L/s) | normal 5–10 cmH₂O/L/s intubated |
| Driving pressure | Pplat − PEEP | ≤ 15 cmH₂O |
| RSBI | RR ÷ Vt in litres | 25 ÷ 0.4 = 62.5 (< 105 favours weaning) |
| I:E ratio | Ti : (60/RR − Ti) | RR 15, Ti 1 s → 1:3 |

## 3.9 Other bedside calculations

| What | Formula |
|---|---|
| BMI | Weight (kg) ÷ height (m)². Normal 18.5–24.9; overweight 25–29.9; obese ≥ 30 |
| Body surface area (Mosteller) | √[height (cm) × weight (kg) ÷ 3600] |
| Ideal body weight (Devine) | Same as predicted body weight above |
| Fluid balance | Total intake − total output (+ insensible losses ~500–800 mL/day; add ~10% per °C of fever) |
| Urine output per kg | mL of urine ÷ hours ÷ kg |
| Energy needs (ICU) | 20–25 kcal/kg/day early, 25–30 in recovery; protein 1.2–2.0 g/kg/day |
| Calories | Carbohydrate 4 kcal/g (IV dextrose 3.4); protein 4 kcal/g; fat 9 kcal/g; alcohol 7 kcal/g; propofol 1.1 kcal/mL |
| Creatinine clearance (Cockcroft–Gault) | [(140 − age) × weight (kg)] ÷ (72 × serum creatinine mg/dL), × 0.85 if female |
| Heart rate from ECG | 300 ÷ large squares between R waves, or 1500 ÷ small squares; irregular: QRS in 6 s × 10 |
| GCS | Eyes (4) + Verbal (5) + Motor (6) = 3–15 |
| Blood volume | Adult ~70 mL/kg (male 75, female 65); child 80; neonate 85–90 mL/kg |
| Allowable blood loss | EBV × (starting Hct − target Hct) ÷ starting Hct |
