($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$4. Applied Anatomy and Physiology I: Cells, Fluids and Blood$s$, false, 4, $s$4. Applied Anatomy and Physiology I: Cells, Fluids and Blood$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 4. Applied Anatomy and Physiology I: Cells, Fluids and Blood

## 4.1 The cell and homeostasis

- **Homeostasis** = keeping the internal environment stable (temperature, pH, glucose, fluids). Mostly by **negative feedback** (a change triggers a response that reverses it, e.g. high glucose → insulin). **Positive feedback** amplifies (labour contractions with oxytocin, blood clotting).
- Cell membrane = phospholipid bilayer; controls entry by diffusion, osmosis, facilitated diffusion (no energy) and active transport (uses ATP, e.g. **Na⁺/K⁺ pump**: 3 Na⁺ out, 2 K⁺ in).
- **Mitochondria** make ATP by aerobic respiration (1 glucose → ~36–38 ATP). Without oxygen → anaerobic glycolysis → only 2 ATP + **lactic acid** (why shock causes lactic acidosis).
- Cell injury: reversible (swelling) → irreversible (necrosis). **Apoptosis** = programmed, tidy cell death.

## 4.2 Body fluids

| Compartment | Share of body weight | Adult 70 kg |
|---|---|---|
| Total body water | ~60% (infant 75–80%, elderly/female ~50%) | ~42 L |
| Intracellular fluid (ICF) | ⅔ of water (40% of weight) | ~28 L |
| Extracellular fluid (ECF) | ⅓ of water (20% of weight) | ~14 L |
| — Interstitial | ¾ of ECF | ~10.5 L |
| — Intravascular (plasma) | ¼ of ECF | ~3.5 L (blood volume ~5 L) |

- Main ECF ions: **sodium** and chloride. Main ICF ion: **potassium** (and phosphate).
- **Osmosis**: water moves to the side with more solute. **Osmolality** normal 275–295 mOsm/kg.
- **Starling forces**: hydrostatic pressure pushes fluid OUT of capillaries; oncotic pressure (albumin) pulls fluid IN. Oedema = ↑ hydrostatic (heart failure, fluid overload), ↓ oncotic (low albumin: liver disease, nephrotic syndrome, malnutrition), ↑ capillary permeability (sepsis, burns, ARDS) or blocked lymphatics.
- Daily water balance (adult): intake ~2.5 L (drink 1.5, food 0.8, metabolism 0.2); output ~2.5 L (urine 1.5, skin 0.5, lungs 0.4, faeces 0.1). **Insensible loss** (skin + lungs) ~800 mL/day; ↑ with fever, tachypnoea, burns.

### IV fluids

| Fluid | Type | Contents / use | Cautions |
|---|---|---|---|
| 0.9% sodium chloride (normal saline) | Isotonic crystalloid | Na⁺ 154, Cl⁻ 154 mmol/L; resuscitation, with blood | Large volumes → hyperchloraemic acidosis |
| Ringer's lactate / Hartmann's | Isotonic, balanced | Na⁺ 130, K⁺ 4, Ca²⁺, lactate (→ bicarbonate); burns, trauma, surgery | Avoid with blood (calcium); caution severe liver failure |
| 5% dextrose | Isotonic in bag, becomes hypotonic (free water) | Maintenance water; glucose 50 g/L | Never for resuscitation; hyponatraemia, raised ICP |
| Dextrose saline (4% + 0.18%) | Hypotonic | Paediatric/adult maintenance (with care) | Hyponatraemia |
| 0.45% saline | Hypotonic | Hypernatraemia, HHS (after initial saline) | Cerebral oedema if too fast |
| 3% saline | Hypertonic | Raised ICP, severe symptomatic hyponatraemia | Raise Na⁺ ≤ 8–10 mmol/L/24 h (osmotic demyelination) |
| 10%, 50% dextrose | Hypertonic | Hypoglycaemia (50% via large vein; children 10% 2–5 mL/kg) | Phlebitis, extravasation |
| Albumin 4.5% / 20% | Colloid | Hypoalbuminaemia, large paracentesis, burns after 24 h | Expensive |
| Gelatins (Gelofusine), starches | Synthetic colloids | Volume expansion | Starches cause kidney injury — avoid in sepsis |
| Blood products | — | See 4.4 | |

## 4.3 Blood

- Blood volume ~70 mL/kg (5 L in adults). Plasma 55%; cells 45% (**haematocrit**).
- **Red cells**: live ~120 days; made in bone marrow (needs iron, B12, folate, **erythropoietin from the kidney**); carry O₂ on **haemoglobin** (each gram carries 1.34 mL O₂).
- **White cells**: neutrophils (bacteria, first responders, 50–70%), lymphocytes (viruses, immunity, 20–40%), monocytes → macrophages, eosinophils (allergy, parasites), basophils (histamine).
- **Platelets** (thrombocytes): live ~7–10 days; form the platelet plug.

### Haemostasis (stopping bleeding)

1. **Vascular spasm** (vasoconstriction).
2. **Platelet plug** (platelets stick and aggregate; aspirin/clopidogrel block this).
3. **Coagulation cascade**: extrinsic pathway (tissue factor, factor VII — measured by **PT/INR**) and intrinsic pathway (factors XII, XI, IX, VIII — measured by **aPTT**) meet at the common pathway: factor X → prothrombin (II) → **thrombin** → fibrinogen (I) → **fibrin** clot. Calcium (factor IV) is needed throughout.
4. **Fibrinolysis**: plasminogen → plasmin dissolves the clot (alteplase/streptokinase speed this; tranexamic acid blocks it). Breakdown product = **D-dimer**.

- Vitamin K-dependent factors: **II, VII, IX, X** (and proteins C and S) — blocked by warfarin; made in the liver.
- **Heparin** activates antithrombin (monitor aPTT; antidote **protamine**). **Warfarin** (monitor INR; antidote **vitamin K**, prothrombin complex concentrate, FFP).

### Blood groups and transfusion

| Group | Antigen on cells | Antibodies in plasma | Can receive red cells from |
|---|---|---|---|
| A | A | anti-B | A, O |
| B | B | anti-A | B, O |
| AB | A and B | none | **all** (universal recipient) |
| O | none | anti-A and anti-B | O only (**universal donor** of red cells; O negative in emergencies) |

- Rhesus: Rh-negative mother with Rh-positive baby → give **anti-D** within 72 h to prevent haemolytic disease of the newborn.
- Universal plasma donor = AB.

| Product | Use | Key points |
|---|---|---|
| Packed red cells | Anaemia, bleeding | 1 unit raises Hb ~1 g/dL; store 2–6 °C; complete within 4 h |
| Platelets | < 10 × 10⁹/L, or bleeding/procedure with low platelets | Room temperature with agitation; never refrigerate |
| Fresh frozen plasma (FFP) | Clotting factor replacement (bleeding with high INR, DIC, massive transfusion) | Thaw before use; 10–15 mL/kg |
| Cryoprecipitate | Low fibrinogen (< 1.5 g/L), DIC | Fibrinogen, factor VIII, vWF |
| Whole blood | Massive haemorrhage where components unavailable | |

### Transfusion reactions

| Reaction | Timing | Signs | Action |
|---|---|---|---|
| Acute haemolytic (ABO incompatible) | Minutes (first 15 min) | Fever, rigors, loin/back pain, chest pain, hypotension, haemoglobinuria, DIC | **STOP transfusion**, keep IV open with saline, call doctor, send bag + bloods + urine, recheck identity |
| Febrile non-haemolytic | During/after | Fever, chills | Stop, check, antipyretic |
| Allergic / anaphylaxis | Minutes | Urticaria, itch → wheeze, hypotension | Stop; antihistamine; anaphylaxis → IM adrenaline |
| TACO (circulatory overload) | During/within 6 h | Dyspnoea, hypertension, crackles, ↑JVP | Stop/slow, sit up, oxygen, diuretic |
| TRALI (lung injury) | Within 6 h | Hypoxaemia, bilateral infiltrates, often hypotension, fever | Stop, oxygen/ventilation; no diuretic |
| Bacterial contamination | Minutes–hours | High fever, shock | Stop, cultures, antibiotics |
| Massive transfusion complications | — | Hypothermia, hypocalcaemia (citrate), hyperkalaemia, coagulopathy, acidosis | Warm blood, calcium, 1:1:1 ratio |

- Checks: two nurses at the bedside — patient ID band vs blood bag vs prescription; baseline vitals, then at 15 minutes, then per protocol; use a blood set with a filter; nothing else in the same line (only 0.9% saline).

## 4.4 Immunity and inflammation

- **Innate** (non-specific, immediate): skin, mucous membranes, neutrophils, macrophages, complement, fever, inflammation.
- **Adaptive** (specific, has memory): B lymphocytes → antibodies (humoral); T lymphocytes (cell-mediated; CD4 helper cells are destroyed by HIV).
- Antibodies: **IgG** (most common, crosses placenta), **IgM** (first produced, acute infection), **IgA** (secretions, breast milk), **IgE** (allergy, anaphylaxis), IgD.
- **Inflammation** signs: rubor (redness), calor (heat), tumor (swelling), dolor (pain), loss of function.
- Immunity types: active natural (infection), active artificial (vaccine), passive natural (placenta, breast milk), passive artificial (antitoxin, immunoglobulin, anti-D).
- Hypersensitivity: Type I immediate/IgE (anaphylaxis), Type II cytotoxic (transfusion reaction), Type III immune complex (glomerulonephritis), Type IV delayed/T-cell (Mantoux test, contact dermatitis).
