![The heart — chambers, valves and blood flow](keynote/heart.svg)

## 5.1 Structure of the heart

- Cone-shaped, size of a fist (~300 g), in the **mediastinum**, apex at the **5th left intercostal space, mid-clavicular line** (apex beat).
- Layers: **pericardium** (fibrous + serous, with 15–50 mL fluid), **myocardium** (muscle), **endocardium** (lines chambers and valves).
- **Atrioventricular valves**: tricuspid (right), mitral/bicuspid (left) — held by chordae tendineae and **papillary muscles** (rupture after MI → acute mitral regurgitation, pulmonary oedema, new murmur).
- **Semilunar valves**: pulmonary and aortic.
- **Heart sounds**: **S1 "lub"** = closure of mitral and tricuspid (start of systole); **S2 "dub"** = closure of aortic and pulmonary (start of diastole). **S3** = heart failure/fluid overload (normal in children/pregnancy); **S4** = stiff ventricle (hypertension, LVH).
- Auscultation points ("All Physicians Take Money"): **A**ortic 2nd right ICS; **P**ulmonary 2nd left ICS; **T**ricuspid 4th–5th left sternal edge; **M**itral 5th left ICS mid-clavicular line.

### Coronary circulation

| Artery | Supplies | MI territory / ECG leads |
|---|---|---|
| Right coronary artery (RCA) | Right atrium and ventricle, inferior wall, **SA node (~60%) and AV node (~90%)** | Inferior MI: II, III, aVF — watch for **bradycardia, heart block, RV infarction (avoid nitrates; give fluids)** |
| Left anterior descending (LAD) | Anterior wall, septum, apex | Anterior MI: V1–V4 — largest, "widow-maker" |
| Circumflex | Lateral and posterior wall | Lateral MI: I, aVL, V5–V6 |

- Coronary arteries fill during **diastole** (tachycardia shortens diastole → less coronary flow).

## 5.2 Conduction system and ECG

![Conduction system of the heart](keynote/conduction.svg)

| Pacemaker | Intrinsic rate |
|---|---|
| SA node | 60–100 /min |
| AV junction | 40–60 /min |
| Ventricles (Purkinje) | 20–40 /min |

![The normal ECG complex](keynote/ecg.svg)

- **12-lead ECG**: limb leads I, II, III, aVR, aVL, aVF; chest leads V1–V6. Chest lead placement: V1 4th ICS right sternal edge; V2 4th ICS left sternal edge; V4 5th ICS mid-clavicular; V3 between V2 and V4; V5 anterior axillary line; V6 mid-axillary line (same level as V4).
- Monitoring leads (3-lead): white/RA right shoulder, black/LA left shoulder, red/LL left lower chest ("white on right, smoke over fire").
- Lead II is best for seeing P waves and rhythm.

## 5.3 The cardiac cycle and cardiac output

- **Systole** = contraction (ejection); **diastole** = relaxation (filling, ~⅔ of the cycle at rest). Atrial contraction ("atrial kick") gives 20–30% of ventricular filling — lost in atrial fibrillation.
- **Cardiac output (CO) = heart rate × stroke volume** (70 × 70 mL ≈ 5 L/min).
- **Stroke volume** depends on:
  - **Preload** — filling/stretch at end of diastole (CVP, PAWP). **Frank–Starling law**: more stretch → stronger contraction (up to a limit).
  - **Afterload** — resistance to ejection (SVR, aortic stenosis, hypertension). ↓ afterload with vasodilators.
  - **Contractility** — force of contraction (↑ by sympathetic stimulation, inotropes; ↓ by hypoxia, acidosis, beta-blockers, MI).
- **Blood pressure = cardiac output × systemic vascular resistance**.
- **Ejection fraction** = SV ÷ end-diastolic volume, normal 55–70%.

## 5.4 Control of blood pressure

- **Baroreceptors** (carotid sinus, aortic arch) sense stretch → medulla adjusts heart rate and vessel tone within seconds (carotid sinus massage slows heart rate).
- **Autonomic nervous system**: sympathetic (noradrenaline: ↑HR, ↑contractility, vasoconstriction); parasympathetic (vagus, acetylcholine: ↓HR).
- **Hormones**: RAAS (diagram below), ADH (vasopressin), adrenaline, natriuretic peptides (ANP/BNP — released when atria/ventricles are stretched; cause diuresis and vasodilation).

![Renin–angiotensin–aldosterone system](keynote/raas.svg)

### Adrenergic receptors (important for inotropes)

| Receptor | Location | Effect when stimulated |
|---|---|---|
| Alpha-1 (α1) | Blood vessels | **Vasoconstriction** → ↑SVR, ↑BP |
| Beta-1 (β1) | Heart | **↑ Heart rate, ↑ contractility** ("1 heart") |
| Beta-2 (β2) | Lungs, vessels in muscle | **Bronchodilation**, vasodilation ("2 lungs") |
| Dopaminergic (D1) | Renal, mesenteric vessels | Vasodilation |

## 5.5 Blood vessels and circulation

- **Arteries** (thick muscular walls, high pressure) → arterioles (main site of resistance) → **capillaries** (exchange; one cell thick) → venules → **veins** (thin walls, valves, hold ~65–70% of blood volume = capacitance).
- **Systemic circulation**: left ventricle → aorta → body → vena cavae → right atrium.
- **Pulmonary circulation**: right ventricle → pulmonary arteries (deoxygenated) → lungs → pulmonary veins (oxygenated) → left atrium. (The pulmonary artery is the only artery carrying deoxygenated blood in adults.)
- **Fetal circulation**: three shunts — **ductus venosus** (bypasses liver), **foramen ovale** (RA → LA), **ductus arteriosus** (pulmonary artery → aorta). Close after birth; a patent ductus arteriosus gives a "machinery" murmur.
- Pulse points: temporal, carotid (use in arrest; adults), brachial (infants in arrest), radial, femoral, popliteal, posterior tibial, dorsalis pedis. A palpable radial pulse suggests SBP ≥ ~80 mmHg (rough guide only).

## 5.6 Key cardiovascular physiology for the ICU nurse

- **Myocardial oxygen demand** rises with HR, contractility and wall tension (afterload/preload) → tachycardia is dangerous in ischaemia.
- **Venous return** falls with positive-pressure ventilation and PEEP (↑ intrathoracic pressure) → hypotension after intubation, especially if hypovolaemic.
- **Hypokalaemia and hyperkalaemia, hypomagnesaemia, hypoxia and acidosis** all cause arrhythmias — check them in every arrhythmia.
- **Jugular venous pressure (JVP)**: normal ≤ 3–4 cm above the sternal angle at 45°; raised in right heart failure, fluid overload, tamponade, tension pneumothorax.
