($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$6. Applied Anatomy and Physiology III: The Respiratory System$s$, false, 6, $s$6. Applied Anatomy and Physiology III: The Respiratory System$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 6. Applied Anatomy and Physiology III: The Respiratory System

![Respiratory tree and gas exchange](keynote/airway.svg)

## 6.1 Structure

- **Upper airway**: nose (warms, filters, humidifies), pharynx, **larynx** (vocal cords; narrowest part of the adult airway is the glottis; in young children the **cricoid** ring). The **epiglottis** covers the larynx during swallowing.
- **Lower airway**: trachea (C-shaped cartilage rings, ~10–12 cm, divides at the **carina, T4–T5**), main bronchi, lobar and segmental bronchi, bronchioles, alveoli.
- **Right main bronchus** is wider, shorter and more vertical → inhaled objects and a too-deep endotracheal tube usually go right (absent breath sounds on the LEFT).
- Lungs: **right = 3 lobes**, **left = 2 lobes** (+ lingula; space for the heart).
- **Pleura**: visceral (on lung) and parietal (on chest wall) with a thin layer of fluid; negative intrapleural pressure keeps the lung expanded. Air → pneumothorax; blood → haemothorax; pus → empyema; fluid → effusion; lymph → chylothorax.
- **Muscles of breathing**: diaphragm (main; phrenic nerve **C3, C4, C5** — "C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive"), external intercostals. Accessory: sternocleidomastoid, scalenes, trapezius (in respiratory distress). Expiration is passive at rest; active with internal intercostals and abdominal muscles.
- **Surfactant** (type II pneumocytes) lowers surface tension so alveoli don't collapse; lacking in premature babies (< 34 weeks) → respiratory distress syndrome. Mother given steroids before preterm delivery to mature the lungs.

## 6.2 Mechanics of breathing

- Inspiration: diaphragm contracts and moves down, chest volume ↑, pressure in the lungs falls below atmospheric → air flows in (Boyle's law: pressure × volume is constant).
- **Compliance** = how easily the lungs stretch. ↓ compliance (stiff lungs): ARDS, pulmonary oedema, fibrosis, pneumonia, obesity, abdominal distension. ↑ compliance: emphysema (lungs inflate easily but don't recoil → air trapping).
- **Resistance** = narrowing of airways: asthma, COPD, secretions, bronchospasm, small/kinked tube.
- **Work of breathing** ↑ with ↓ compliance or ↑ resistance → fatigue → respiratory failure.

![Lung volumes and capacities](keynote/lung_volumes.svg)

| Volume / capacity | Definition | Adult value |
|---|---|---|
| Tidal volume (TV/Vt) | Air in and out in a normal breath | ~500 mL (6–7 mL/kg) |
| Inspiratory reserve volume (IRV) | Extra air that can be breathed in after a normal breath | ~3000 mL |
| Expiratory reserve volume (ERV) | Extra air that can be breathed out after a normal breath | ~1100 mL |
| Residual volume (RV) | Air left after maximal expiration (cannot be exhaled) | ~1200 mL |
| Vital capacity (VC) | IRV + TV + ERV | ~4800 mL |
| Functional residual capacity (FRC) | ERV + RV (air left after a normal breath) | ~2300 mL — PEEP increases FRC |
| Inspiratory capacity (IC) | TV + IRV | ~3500 mL |
| Total lung capacity (TLC) | VC + RV | ~6000 mL |

- **Dead space** = air that does not take part in gas exchange. Anatomical ~150 mL (2 mL/kg). Alveolar dead space ↑ in pulmonary embolism, low cardiac output.
- **Obstructive disease** (asthma, COPD): FEV₁/FVC < 70%, ↑RV, air trapping. **Restrictive disease** (fibrosis, obesity, neuromuscular disease, kyphoscoliosis): all volumes ↓, ratio normal or high.

## 6.3 Gas exchange and transport

- Gas moves by **diffusion** from high to low partial pressure (Fick's law: faster with larger area, thinner membrane, greater pressure difference). Slowed by pulmonary oedema, fibrosis; area ↓ in emphysema, pneumonectomy.
- Air: O₂ 21%, N₂ 78%, CO₂ 0.04%. Atmospheric pressure 760 mmHg; inspired PO₂ ≈ 150 mmHg; alveolar PAO₂ ≈ 100 mmHg; arterial PaO₂ 80–100; venous PvO₂ ≈ 40. PaCO₂ 40, PvCO₂ 46.
- **CO₂ diffuses ~20 times more easily than O₂**, so hypoxaemia appears before hypercapnia in diffusion problems.
- **Oxygen transport**: 98% bound to haemoglobin, 2% dissolved. **CO₂ transport**: ~70% as bicarbonate (carbonic anhydrase: CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻), ~20% on haemoglobin (carbamino), ~10% dissolved.
- **V/Q (ventilation/perfusion) matching**: normal ~0.8. **Shunt** (perfusion without ventilation: pneumonia, atelectasis, ARDS) → hypoxaemia that responds poorly to oxygen; treat with PEEP/recruitment. **Dead space** (ventilation without perfusion: PE).
- **Hypoxic pulmonary vasoconstriction**: lung vessels constrict where alveoli are poorly ventilated, diverting blood to better areas.

![Oxygen–haemoglobin dissociation curve](keynote/odc.svg)

- The **steep part** of the curve: a small fall in PaO₂ below 60 mmHg causes a large fall in saturation — why SpO₂ 90% (PaO₂ ≈ 60) is the danger point.
- **Carbon monoxide** binds haemoglobin ~240 times more strongly than oxygen and shifts the curve left; **pulse oximetry reads falsely normal** — use co-oximetry (COHb) and give 100% oxygen.
- **Anaemia**: SaO₂ may be normal but oxygen content is low.

## 6.4 Control of breathing

- **Respiratory centres** in the **medulla** (rhythm) and **pons** (pattern).
- **Central chemoreceptors** (medulla) respond to **CO₂/H⁺ in the CSF** — the main drive to breathe.
- **Peripheral chemoreceptors** (carotid and aortic bodies) respond mainly to **low PaO₂** (< 60 mmHg), also CO₂ and H⁺.
- **Chronic CO₂ retainers (some COPD patients)** rely more on the hypoxic drive and on V/Q effects — excessive oxygen can raise PaCO₂ (CO₂ narcosis). **Never withhold oxygen from a hypoxic patient**, but titrate to SpO₂ **88–92%** with a Venturi mask.
- Abnormal patterns: **Kussmaul** (deep, rapid — metabolic acidosis), **Cheyne–Stokes** (crescendo–decrescendo with apnoea — heart failure, brain injury), **Biot's/ataxic** (irregular with apnoea — medulla/pons damage), **apneustic** (prolonged inspiration — pons lesion), **central neurogenic hyperventilation** (midbrain/pons).

## 6.5 Respiratory failure

| Type | Definition | Causes |
|---|---|---|
| Type 1 (hypoxaemic) | PaO₂ < 60 mmHg (8 kPa) with normal or low PaCO₂ | Pneumonia, pulmonary oedema, ARDS, PE, asthma (early), fibrosis — V/Q mismatch/shunt |
| Type 2 (hypercapnic, ventilatory) | PaO₂ < 60 and **PaCO₂ > 45–50 mmHg** with acidaemia | Hypoventilation: COPD, exhausted asthmatic, opioids/sedatives, Guillain–Barré, myasthenia, high spinal injury, chest wall injury, obesity |

- Signs of increasing distress: tachypnoea (> 30), accessory muscle use, nasal flaring, tracheal tug, inability to speak in sentences, sweating, tachycardia, confusion/agitation → then **exhaustion**: falling respiratory rate, drowsiness, silent chest, bradycardia, cyanosis — **pre-arrest**.
- A **rising PaCO₂ in an asthmatic** is an ominous sign of tiring.
