($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$7. Applied Anatomy and Physiology IV: The Nervous System$s$, false, 7, $s$7. Applied Anatomy and Physiology IV: The Nervous System$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 7. Applied Anatomy and Physiology IV: The Nervous System

![The brain — regions and functions](keynote/brain.svg)

## 7.1 Organisation

- **Central nervous system (CNS)**: brain and spinal cord. **Peripheral nervous system**: 12 pairs of cranial nerves, 31 pairs of spinal nerves; **somatic** (voluntary) and **autonomic** (sympathetic "fight or flight", parasympathetic "rest and digest").
- **Neurone**: dendrites → cell body → axon (myelin speeds conduction; destroyed in multiple sclerosis; peripheral myelin attacked in Guillain–Barré).
- **Synapse**: neurotransmitters — acetylcholine (neuromuscular junction; blocked by curare-like drugs; broken down by acetylcholinesterase, which organophosphates and neostigmine inhibit), noradrenaline, dopamine (↓ in Parkinson's), serotonin, GABA (inhibitory; enhanced by benzodiazepines, propofol, barbiturates), glutamate (excitatory).
- **Meninges**: dura mater (outer), arachnoid, pia mater. **Epidural haematoma** — arterial (middle meningeal artery), lucid interval, rapid deterioration, lens-shaped on CT. **Subdural** — venous (bridging veins), elderly/alcoholics, slower, crescent-shaped. **Subarachnoid** — ruptured aneurysm, "worst headache of my life", neck stiffness.
- **CSF**: made by the **choroid plexus** (~500 mL/day; ~150 mL present), flows through the ventricles, absorbed by arachnoid villi. Blockage → hydrocephalus.
- **Blood supply**: internal carotids and vertebral arteries join at the **circle of Willis**. The brain receives ~15% of cardiac output and uses ~20% of the body's oxygen; it has no glucose/oxygen store — irreversible damage after ~4–6 minutes without blood flow. **Autoregulation** keeps cerebral blood flow constant between MAP ~60–150 mmHg (lost after brain injury). **CO₂ is a powerful cerebral vasodilator**: high PaCO₂ → ↑ cerebral blood flow → ↑ICP (keep PaCO₂ 35–40 in head injury; brief hyperventilation only for impending herniation).
- **Blood–brain barrier**: keeps many drugs/toxins out.

## 7.2 Intracranial pressure

![Intracranial pressure–volume curve](keynote/icp.svg)

- **Early signs of raised ICP**: headache, vomiting, restlessness/confusion, **falling GCS** (most sensitive sign), sluggish pupil.
- **Late signs**: fixed dilated pupil (same side as lesion — CN III compression), **Cushing's triad** (hypertension with widened pulse pressure, bradycardia, irregular breathing), posturing, coma.
- **Nursing measures to lower ICP**: head of bed **30°**, head and neck **neutral** (no tight ETT ties/collars obstructing jugular veins), avoid hip flexion > 90°, normothermia, treat pain/agitation (sedation), avoid hypoxia and hypercapnia, keep PaCO₂ 35–40, suction only when needed (pre-oxygenate, < 10–15 s), avoid coughing/straining (stool softeners), cluster care carefully, osmotherapy as prescribed (**mannitol** 0.25–1 g/kg — monitor serum osmolality < 320, urine output; or **3% saline**), seizure prevention, keep MAP for CPP 60–70.

## 7.3 Glasgow Coma Scale

| Eye opening (E) | Verbal response (V) | Best motor response (M) |
|---|---|---|
| 4 Spontaneous | 5 Orientated | 6 Obeys commands |
| 3 To voice | 4 Confused | 5 Localises to pain |
| 2 To pain | 3 Inappropriate words | 4 Withdraws from pain (normal flexion) |
| 1 None | 2 Incomprehensible sounds | 3 Abnormal flexion (decorticate) |
| | 1 None | 2 Extension (decerebrate) |
| | | 1 None |

- Total 3–15. Record as E_V_M_ (e.g. E2 V3 M5 = 10). Intubated: V = "T" (e.g. 7T). Use a central painful stimulus (trapezius squeeze, supraorbital pressure) for motor response.
- **GCS ≤ 8 → intubate ("8 = intubate")**; a fall of **2 or more** points = urgent review.
- **AVPU**: Alert, Voice, Pain, Unresponsive (P ≈ GCS 8).
- **Pupils**: size, equality and reaction. Unilateral fixed dilated = herniation on that side; bilateral pinpoint = opioids or **pontine** haemorrhage; bilateral fixed dilated = severe hypoxic injury, brainstem death, atropine/sympathomimetic drugs.

## 7.4 Cranial nerves

| No. | Nerve | Function | Quick test |
|---|---|---|---|
| I | Olfactory | Smell | Identify smells |
| II | Optic | Vision | Acuity, fields; afferent pupil reflex |
| III | Oculomotor | Most eye movements, **pupil constriction**, eyelid elevation | Pupil reaction; ptosis, "down and out" eye |
| IV | Trochlear | Eye down and in (superior oblique) | Look down and in (reading, stairs) |
| V | Trigeminal | Facial sensation, chewing; **corneal reflex (sensory)** | Corneal reflex, clench jaw |
| VI | Abducens | Eye outward (lateral rectus) | Look to the side |
| VII | Facial | Facial expression, taste anterior ⅔, eye closure; corneal reflex (motor) | Smile, close eyes (Bell's palsy) |
| VIII | Vestibulocochlear | Hearing, balance | Oculocephalic (doll's eyes), caloric test |
| IX | Glossopharyngeal | Swallowing, gag (sensory), taste posterior ⅓ | Gag reflex |
| X | Vagus | Gag (motor), swallowing, voice, **parasympathetic to heart/gut** | Gag, cough, uvula midline |
| XI | Accessory | Shoulder shrug, head turn | Shrug |
| XII | Hypoglossal | Tongue movement | Tongue protrudes towards a weak side |

- Mnemonic (names): "Oh Oh Oh To Touch And Feel Very Good Velvet, Ah Heaven". Function (S = sensory, M = motor, B = both): "Some Say Marry Money But My Brother Says Big Brains Matter More".
- **Brainstem reflexes** used in brain death testing: pupils (II/III), corneal (V/VII), oculocephalic and vestibulo-ocular (III/VI/VIII), gag (IX/X), cough (X), plus apnoea test.

## 7.5 Spinal cord

- Ends at **L1–L2** (conus medullaris), then cauda equina — lumbar puncture at **L3–L4 or L4–L5**.
- Ascending tracts (sensory): dorsal columns (fine touch, vibration, proprioception), spinothalamic (pain, temperature). Descending (motor): corticospinal tract (crosses in the medulla).
- **Upper motor neurone** lesion (brain/cord): spastic, hyperreflexia, upgoing plantar (Babinski +). **Lower motor neurone** (nerve root/peripheral nerve): flaccid, wasting, ↓ reflexes, fasciculations.

| Injury level | Function lost / kept |
|---|---|
| C1–C3 | No breathing — ventilator-dependent |
| C4 | Diaphragm weak/partial; may need ventilation; no arm function |
| C5 | Diaphragm intact; shoulder and elbow flexion (biceps) |
| C6 | Wrist extension |
| C7 | Elbow extension (triceps), some hand function |
| C8–T1 | Hand/finger function; intercostals lost |
| T6 and above | Risk of **autonomic dysreflexia**; neurogenic shock |
| T12–L1 | Legs paralysed (paraplegia), bowel/bladder affected |

- **Dermatomes to remember**: C6 thumb, C7 middle finger, C8 little finger, **T4 nipple**, T7 xiphisternum, **T10 umbilicus**, L1 inguinal ligament, L4 knee/medial leg, L5 great toe/dorsum foot, S1 little toe/lateral foot, S2–S4 perineum.
- **Spinal shock**: temporary loss of all reflexes below the injury (flaccid). **Neurogenic shock** (injury above T6): loss of sympathetic tone → **hypotension with bradycardia**, warm dry skin.
- **Autonomic dysreflexia** (above T6, after spinal shock resolves): trigger below the injury (full bladder — most common, blocked catheter, constipation, tight clothing, pressure sore) → severe hypertension, pounding headache, flushing/sweating above injury, **bradycardia**. Action: **sit the patient up**, find and remove the cause (check catheter/bladder first), loosen clothing, monitor BP every 2–5 min, antihypertensive if persists.

## 7.6 Autonomic nervous system

| | Sympathetic (fight or flight) | Parasympathetic (rest and digest) |
|---|---|---|
| Neurotransmitter at organ | Noradrenaline (sweat glands: acetylcholine) | Acetylcholine |
| Heart | ↑ rate and contractility | ↓ rate (vagus) |
| Lungs | Bronchodilation | Bronchoconstriction, ↑ secretions |
| Pupils | Dilate (mydriasis) | Constrict (miosis) |
| Gut | ↓ motility, sphincters close | ↑ motility, secretions |
| Bladder | Retention | Emptying |
| Glands | Sweating | Salivation, tears |
| Blood glucose | ↑ (glycogenolysis) | — |

- **Cholinergic excess** (organophosphates) = parasympathetic overdrive: **SLUDGE/DUMBELS** (Salivation, Lacrimation, Urination, Defecation, GI upset, Emesis; Bradycardia, Bronchorrhoea, Bronchospasm, Miosis) — treat with **atropine** (until chest is dry) and pralidoxime.
- **Anticholinergic** (atropine, antihistamines, tricyclics): "hot as a hare, dry as a bone, red as a beet, blind as a bat, mad as a hatter, full as a flask" — hyperthermia, dry skin, flushing, dilated pupils, delirium, urinary retention, tachycardia.
