## 8.1 The kidney

![The nephron](keynote/nephron.svg)

- Two kidneys, retroperitoneal at **T12–L3** (right slightly lower — liver above). Each has ~1 million **nephrons**. Receive ~20–25% of cardiac output (~1.2 L/min).
- **Functions** (mnemonic **A WET BED**): **A**cid–base balance, **W**ater balance, **E**lectrolytes, **T**oxin/waste removal (urea, creatinine, drugs), **B**lood pressure (renin), **E**rythropoietin (red cells), **D** vitamin activation (calcium/bones).
- **Glomerular filtration rate** ~125 mL/min (180 L/day filtered, 99% reabsorbed, 1–2 L urine). Autoregulated between MAP ~80–180 mmHg; below MAP ~65–70 filtration falls → oliguria.
- **ADH** (from posterior pituitary, made in hypothalamus) → water reabsorption in collecting ducts (concentrated urine). Lack → **diabetes insipidus** (large volumes of dilute urine, high Na⁺). Excess → **SIADH** (water retention, low Na⁺, concentrated urine).
- **Aldosterone** (adrenal cortex) → Na⁺ and water retained, K⁺ and H⁺ excreted.
- **Acid–base role**: reabsorbs filtered bicarbonate (PCT), excretes H⁺ (as ammonium and phosphate), makes new bicarbonate — slow compensation (2–5 days).

| AKI category | Cause | Clues |
|---|---|---|
| Pre-renal (most common in ICU) | ↓ perfusion: hypovolaemia, haemorrhage, shock, heart failure, sepsis | Urine Na⁺ < 20, concentrated urine (SG > 1.020), urea:creatinine ratio high; reversible with fluids/perfusion |
| Intrinsic (renal) | Acute tubular necrosis (prolonged ischaemia, nephrotoxins — aminoglycosides, contrast, NSAIDs, myoglobin from rhabdomyolysis), glomerulonephritis | Urine Na⁺ > 40, muddy brown casts, fixed SG ~1.010 |
| Post-renal | Obstruction: enlarged prostate, stones, tumour, **blocked catheter** | Distended bladder, hydronephrosis — check the catheter first! |

- AKI phases: **oliguric** (risk of fluid overload, hyperkalaemia, metabolic acidosis, uraemia) → **diuretic** (large urine output — risk of dehydration, ↓K⁺, ↓Na⁺) → **recovery**.
- **Indications for urgent dialysis (AEIOU)**: **A**cidosis (pH < 7.1–7.15), **E**lectrolytes (refractory K⁺ > 6.5), **I**ntoxication (lithium, methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylates, theophylline), **O**verload (pulmonary oedema not responding to diuretics), **U**raemia (encephalopathy, pericarditis, bleeding).

## 8.2 The endocrine system

| Gland | Hormone | Action | Too little / too much |
|---|---|---|---|
| Hypothalamus | Releasing hormones, ADH, oxytocin (stored in posterior pituitary) | Controls pituitary | — |
| Anterior pituitary | GH, ACTH, TSH, FSH/LH, prolactin | Controls other glands | Sheehan's syndrome (after PPH); acromegaly; prolactinoma |
| Posterior pituitary | ADH, oxytocin | Water retention; uterine contraction, milk let-down | DI / SIADH |
| Thyroid | T3, T4 (↑ metabolism), calcitonin (↓ Ca²⁺) | Metabolic rate, heat | Hypothyroid/myxoedema coma; hyperthyroid/**thyroid storm** |
| Parathyroid | PTH (↑ Ca²⁺) | Bone, kidney, gut | Accidental removal at thyroidectomy → **hypocalcaemia** (tingling, tetany, Chvostek, Trousseau) |
| Adrenal cortex | Cortisol (stress, glucose, anti-inflammatory), aldosterone (Na⁺/K⁺), androgens | | Addison's/**adrenal crisis** (↓BP, ↓Na⁺, ↑K⁺, ↓glucose); Cushing's; Conn's (↑BP, ↓K⁺, alkalosis) |
| Adrenal medulla | Adrenaline, noradrenaline | Fight or flight | Phaeochromocytoma (episodic hypertension) |
| Pancreas (islets of Langerhans) | **Insulin** (β cells, ↓ glucose), **glucagon** (α cells, ↑ glucose), somatostatin | Glucose control | Diabetes (DKA/HHS), insulinoma |

- **Insulin** moves glucose AND **potassium** into cells → insulin therapy causes **hypokalaemia** (check K⁺ before and during DKA treatment; used to treat hyperkalaemia with dextrose).
- **Stress response** in critical illness: ↑ cortisol, adrenaline, glucagon, ADH → hyperglycaemia, fluid retention, catabolism. Patients on long-term steroids need **stress-dose steroids** (abrupt stop → adrenal crisis).

## 8.3 The gastrointestinal tract

- Mouth (mechanical digestion; salivary amylase) → oesophagus (peristalsis; lower oesophageal sphincter) → stomach (HCl from parietal cells — also make **intrinsic factor** for B12; pepsin; holds ~1–1.5 L) → small intestine (duodenum receives bile and pancreatic juice; jejunum and **ileum absorb nutrients**; terminal ileum absorbs B12 and bile salts) → large intestine (water absorption, vitamin K made by bacteria) → rectum.
- **Pancreas (exocrine)**: amylase, lipase, trypsinogen and bicarbonate into the duodenum. In pancreatitis these enzymes digest the pancreas (autodigestion).
- **Upper GI bleed** = above the ligament of Treitz (oesophagus, stomach, duodenum): haematemesis, coffee-ground vomit, **malaena**; raised urea. **Lower GI bleed**: fresh red blood per rectum (haematochezia) — though a brisk upper bleed can also do this.
- **Splanchnic circulation** is shut down early in shock → gut ischaemia, translocation of bacteria, stress ulcers, ileus. Early enteral feeding protects the gut.

## 8.4 The liver

- Largest internal organ (~1.5 kg), right upper quadrant; dual blood supply: **hepatic artery** (25%, oxygen) and **portal vein** (75%, nutrients from the gut).
- **Functions**: makes **albumin** and **clotting factors** (II, VII, IX, X need vitamin K), glucose storage/release (glycogen), metabolises drugs and hormones, converts **ammonia → urea**, makes bile (bilirubin excretion), stores vitamins (A, D, B12) and iron, makes cholesterol.
- **Liver failure** → jaundice, coagulopathy (↑INR, bleeding), hypoglycaemia, hypoalbuminaemia (oedema, ascites), **hepatic encephalopathy** (↑ammonia: confusion, asterixis/flapping tremor; treat with **lactulose**, rifaximin, protein in moderation, avoid sedatives), **portal hypertension** (oesophageal varices, splenomegaly, caput medusae), hepatorenal syndrome, infection risk.
- **Jaundice**: pre-hepatic (haemolysis — unconjugated), hepatic (hepatitis, cirrhosis), post-hepatic/obstructive (gallstones, pancreatic cancer — conjugated; pale stool, dark urine, itch).

## 8.5 The skin

![Burn depth and skin layers](keynote/burn_depth.svg)

- Largest organ (~16% of body weight). **Epidermis** (no blood vessels; stratum basale regenerates; melanocytes), **dermis** (collagen, vessels, nerves, sweat and sebaceous glands, hair follicles), **subcutaneous tissue** (fat, insulation).
- **Functions**: protection/barrier (infection, fluid loss), **thermoregulation** (sweating, vasodilation/constriction), sensation, vitamin D synthesis, excretion, absorption.
- Burns destroy this barrier → massive fluid loss (capillary leak), infection, hypothermia, protein loss, pain.
- **Wound healing**: haemostasis → inflammation (0–4 days) → proliferation (granulation, 4–21 days) → maturation/remodelling (up to 2 years). Primary intention (clean edges sutured), secondary (open wound, granulates), tertiary/delayed primary (left open then closed). Needs oxygen, perfusion, protein, vitamin C, zinc; impaired by diabetes, steroids, smoking, infection, malnutrition.
- **Pressure injury stages**: 1 non-blanching redness (intact skin); 2 partial thickness (shallow ulcer/blister); 3 full thickness (fat visible); 4 full thickness with bone/tendon/muscle; unstageable (covered by slough/eschar); deep tissue injury (purple/maroon intact skin). **Braden scale** for risk (6–23; ≤ 18 at risk, ≤ 12 high risk). Turn 2-hourly, pressure-relieving mattress, nutrition, moisture control, devices (ETT, NG, masks, catheters) cause injuries too.

## 8.6 Musculoskeletal essentials

- **Compartment syndrome**: pressure in a closed muscle compartment (fracture, crush, tight cast, burns, reperfusion) → **6 Ps**: Pain out of proportion (especially on passive stretch — earliest), Pressure, Paraesthesia, Pallor, Paralysis, Pulselessness (late). Action: remove/loosen dressings and casts, keep limb at **heart level** (not elevated), urgent surgical review for **fasciotomy**.
- **Rhabdomyolysis**: muscle breakdown (crush, burns, seizures, prolonged lying, drugs) → **myoglobin** (dark "cola" urine), very high CK, hyperkalaemia → AKI. Treat with aggressive IV fluids aiming urine 200–300 mL/hr (adult).
- **Fat embolism** (long bone fractures, 24–72 h): respiratory distress, confusion, **petechial rash** (chest, axilla, conjunctiva).
