($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$9. Acid–Base Balance, pH and ABG Interpretation$s$, false, 9, $s$9. Acid–Base Balance, pH and ABG Interpretation$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 9. Acid–Base Balance, pH and ABG Interpretation

## 9.1 The basics

- **pH** measures hydrogen ion (H⁺) concentration: more H⁺ = lower pH = more acidic. Normal blood pH **7.35–7.45** (H⁺ ~40 nmol/L). Each 0.3 fall in pH doubles H⁺. Survival is unlikely below 6.8 or above 7.8.
- **Acids** give up H⁺; **bases** accept H⁺. The body produces **volatile acid** (CO₂ — removed by the lungs) and **fixed/metabolic acids** (lactic acid, ketoacids, sulphuric and phosphoric acid — removed by the kidneys).
- The key equation (carbonic anhydrase): **CO₂ + H₂O ⇌ H₂CO₃ ⇌ H⁺ + HCO₃⁻**. More CO₂ → more H⁺ → acidosis.
- Henderson–Hasselbalch idea: **pH depends on the RATIO of HCO₃⁻ (kidney) to PaCO₂ (lung)**. Normal ratio 20:1. Anything that keeps the ratio 20:1 keeps the pH normal — that is how compensation works.

## 9.2 Three lines of defence

| Defence | Speed | How |
|---|---|---|
| 1. Chemical buffers | Seconds | **Bicarbonate–carbonic acid** (main ECF buffer), **haemoglobin** (main RBC buffer), proteins, phosphate (main ICF/urine buffer) |
| 2. Lungs (respiratory) | Minutes to hours | Change rate/depth of breathing to blow off or retain CO₂ |
| 3. Kidneys (renal/metabolic) | Hours to **days (2–5)** — most powerful | Excrete H⁺, reabsorb/make HCO₃⁻ |

- The lungs compensate for **metabolic** problems; the kidneys compensate for **respiratory** problems. **Compensation never overshoots** — the pH does not cross to the other side of 7.40.

## 9.3 Normal values

| Value | Normal | Low | High |
|---|---|---|---|
| pH | 7.35–7.45 | Acidaemia | Alkalaemia |
| PaCO₂ (acid) | 35–45 mmHg | Respiratory alkalosis (hyperventilation) | Respiratory acidosis (hypoventilation) |
| HCO₃⁻ (base) | 22–26 mmol/L | Metabolic acidosis | Metabolic alkalosis |
| PaO₂ | 80–100 mmHg | Hypoxaemia | Hyperoxia |
| Base excess | −2 to +2 | Metabolic acidosis (base deficit) | Metabolic alkalosis |
| SaO₂ | 95–100% | | |

## 9.4 The 5-step method

![5 steps to interpret an ABG](keynote/abg_flow.svg)

**ROME**: **R**espiratory **O**pposite (pH and PaCO₂ move in opposite directions), **M**etabolic **E**qual (pH and HCO₃⁻ move in the same direction).

Another way — the **tic-tac-toe method**: write "ACID / NORMAL / BASE" as three columns. Put pH, PaCO₂ (high = acid) and HCO₃⁻ (high = base) in the right column. The value in the same column as the pH is the cause; the one in the opposite column is compensating.

## 9.5 The four primary disorders

| Disorder | pH | Primary change | Compensation | Common causes | Signs |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Respiratory acidosis** | ↓ | **PaCO₂ ↑** | Kidneys ↑ HCO₃⁻ | **Hypoventilation**: COPD, opioid/sedative overdose, head injury, exhausted asthma, Guillain–Barré, myasthenia, chest trauma, obesity, ventilator rate too low | Drowsiness, headache, confusion, flushed warm skin, tachycardia, **CO₂ narcosis**, coma |
| **Respiratory alkalosis** | ↑ | **PaCO₂ ↓** | Kidneys ↓ HCO₃⁻ | **Hyperventilation**: anxiety, pain, fever, hypoxia, PE, early sepsis, early salicylate poisoning, pregnancy, high altitude, liver failure, ventilator rate too high | Light-headed, **tingling/numbness around the mouth and fingers**, carpopedal spasm (↓ionised calcium), palpitations |
| **Metabolic acidosis** | ↓ | **HCO₃⁻ ↓** | Lungs ↓ PaCO₂ (**Kussmaul breathing**) | DKA, lactic acidosis (shock, sepsis, cardiac arrest, seizures), renal failure, diarrhoea, poisoning (methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylates, iron), starvation | Deep rapid breathing, headache, confusion, hypotension, arrhythmias, **hyperkalaemia** |
| **Metabolic alkalosis** | ↑ | **HCO₃⁻ ↑** | Lungs ↑ PaCO₂ (**slow shallow breathing**) | **Vomiting**, NG suction, diuretics, **hypokalaemia**, excess antacids/bicarbonate, massive transfusion (citrate), Cushing's/Conn's | Muscle weakness, cramps, tetany, confusion, arrhythmias (with ↓K⁺), hypoventilation |

### Degree of compensation

| State | pH | PaCO₂ and HCO₃⁻ |
|---|---|---|
| Uncompensated | Abnormal | One abnormal, the other **normal** |
| Partially compensated | Abnormal | **Both abnormal**, moving in the compensating direction |
| Fully compensated | **Normal (7.35–7.45)** | Both abnormal. Decide the primary disorder by which side of 7.40 the pH is on (7.35–7.39 = acidosis; 7.41–7.45 = alkalosis) |
| Mixed (combined) | Very abnormal | Both abnormal and **pushing the pH the same way** (e.g. PaCO₂ ↑ AND HCO₃⁻ ↓ → mixed acidosis in cardiac arrest) |

## 9.6 Worked examples

| ABG | Interpretation | Likely situation |
|---|---|---|
| pH 7.28, PaCO₂ 60, HCO₃⁻ 25 | Uncompensated (acute) respiratory acidosis | Opioid overdose, tiring asthmatic |
| pH 7.33, PaCO₂ 66, HCO₃⁻ 32 | Partially compensated respiratory acidosis | COPD exacerbation |
| pH 7.37, PaCO₂ 58, HCO₃⁻ 34 | Fully compensated respiratory acidosis | Stable chronic COPD |
| pH 7.52, PaCO₂ 28, HCO₃⁻ 24 | Uncompensated respiratory alkalosis | Anxiety/pain hyperventilation |
| pH 7.18, PaCO₂ 22, HCO₃⁻ 8 | Partially compensated metabolic acidosis | DKA with Kussmaul breathing |
| pH 7.36, PaCO₂ 28, HCO₃⁻ 16 | Fully compensated metabolic acidosis | Chronic kidney disease |
| pH 7.51, PaCO₂ 40, HCO₃⁻ 33 | Uncompensated metabolic alkalosis | Vomiting, NG suction |
| pH 7.48, PaCO₂ 50, HCO₃⁻ 36 | Partially compensated metabolic alkalosis | Prolonged vomiting, diuretics |
| pH 7.08, PaCO₂ 62, HCO₃⁻ 16 | Mixed respiratory and metabolic acidosis | Cardiac arrest, septic shock with exhaustion |
| pH 7.40, PaCO₂ 40, HCO₃⁻ 24, PaO₂ 55 | Normal acid–base, moderate hypoxaemia | Pneumonia, early PE |

## 9.7 Anion gap and metabolic acidosis

- **Anion gap = Na⁺ − (Cl⁻ + HCO₃⁻)**, normal **8–12** mmol/L. It shows unmeasured acids.
- **High anion gap** metabolic acidosis — **MUDPILES**: **M**ethanol, **U**raemia (renal failure), **D**KA (and alcoholic/starvation ketoacidosis), **P**araldehyde/paracetamol (massive), **I**ron/isoniazid, **L**actic acidosis, **E**thylene glycol, **S**alicylates. (Newer: GOLDMARK.)
- **Normal anion gap** (hyperchloraemic) — **HARDASS**: Hyperalimentation, Acetazolamide, Renal tubular acidosis, **Diarrhoea**, Addison's, pancreatic fistula, **Saline excess**.
- **Lactate**: > 2 mmol/L abnormal; ≥ 4 severe (septic shock with lactate > 2 despite fluids + vasopressors needed for MAP ≥ 65). Type A = hypoperfusion (shock); type B = metformin, liver failure, adrenaline, seizures.
- **Winter's formula**: expected PaCO₂ = (1.5 × HCO₃⁻) + 8 ± 2. If actual PaCO₂ is higher → added respiratory acidosis (patient tiring — prepare for ventilation); lower → added respiratory alkalosis.

## 9.8 Electrolytes and acid–base

- **Acidosis → hyperkalaemia** (H⁺ moves into cells, K⁺ moves out). Rule of thumb: K⁺ rises ~0.6 mmol/L for each 0.1 fall in pH. When the acidosis is corrected (e.g. insulin in DKA), K⁺ falls quickly — monitor and replace.
- **Alkalosis → hypokalaemia** and ↓ ionised calcium (tingling, tetany, arrhythmias).
- **Hypokalaemia causes metabolic alkalosis** (and keeps it going) — correct K⁺ and chloride (0.9% saline) in vomiting-related alkalosis.

## 9.9 Nursing management by disorder

- **Respiratory acidosis**: treat the cause; position upright, encourage deep breathing/coughing, physiotherapy, suction; bronchodilators; reverse opioids with **naloxone** / benzodiazepines with flumazenil (caution); controlled oxygen (COPD 88–92%); **NIV (BiPAP)** if pH < 7.35 and PaCO₂ > 45–50 in COPD; intubate if failing. On a ventilator: **increase minute ventilation** (rate first, or tidal volume) to blow off CO₂. Avoid sedatives.
- **Respiratory alkalosis**: treat the cause (pain relief, reduce anxiety — calm coaching, slow breathing, treat fever/hypoxia); on a ventilator **reduce rate/tidal volume** or sedate if the patient is over-breathing. Rebreathing into a bag is no longer recommended (risk of hypoxia).
- **Metabolic acidosis**: treat the cause — fluids/insulin for DKA, restore perfusion in shock (fluids, vasopressors, source control), dialysis for renal failure, antidotes for poisoning. Monitor K⁺ and ECG. **Sodium bicarbonate** only in specific cases (severe acidosis pH < 7.1 with hyperkalaemia, tricyclic overdose, some renal failure) — it can worsen intracellular acidosis and cause hypernatraemia, fluid overload, hypokalaemia.
- **Metabolic alkalosis**: stop the cause (antiemetics, review NG suction and diuretics), replace fluid with 0.9% saline and **potassium chloride**; acetazolamide in some cases; monitor for arrhythmias and hypoventilation.

> Exam tip: In every ABG question, first decide **acidosis or alkalosis from the pH**, then find **which value matches it** (PaCO₂ high = acid, HCO₃⁻ low = acid). The matching value is the primary problem. If the other value is also abnormal in the opposite direction, there is compensation. If the pH is normal but both values are abnormal, it is fully compensated.
