## 10.1 Oxygen therapy

![Oxygen delivery devices](keynote/oxygen.svg)

- Oxygen is a **drug** — needs a prescription with target saturation (except in emergencies).
- **Targets**: most acutely ill adults **94–98%**; patients at risk of hypercapnia (COPD, obesity hypoventilation, neuromuscular/chest wall disease) **88–92%**; after cardiac arrest/ROSC 94–98% (avoid hyperoxia); critically ill on ventilators often 92–96%.
- **Signs of hypoxia**: restlessness, anxiety, confusion (early); tachycardia, tachypnoea, hypertension (early) → cyanosis, bradycardia, hypotension, arrhythmias (late).
- **Hazards**: fire (no smoking, no oil/grease on fittings), oxygen toxicity (FiO₂ > 0.6 for > 24–48 h — absorption atelectasis, lung injury), retinopathy of prematurity (neonates), CO₂ retention in at-risk patients, drying of mucosa (humidify above 4 L/min or for long periods).
- **Pulse oximetry pitfalls**: falsely low with poor perfusion/cold peripheries, motion, nail polish, bright light; **falsely normal in carbon monoxide poisoning** and doesn't show PaCO₂ (a patient on high oxygen can have a normal SpO₂ while CO₂ rises).

## 10.2 Airway assessment and opening

- **Look, listen, feel**: see-saw (paradoxical) breathing, noisy breathing (snoring = tongue/partial obstruction; gurgling = fluid; **stridor = upper airway narrowing — emergency**; wheeze = lower airway), silence = complete obstruction.
- **Head tilt–chin lift** (no neck injury) or **jaw thrust** (suspected cervical spine injury).
- **Oropharyngeal (Guedel) airway**: size from the centre of the incisors to the angle of the jaw; only in unconscious patients without a gag reflex.
- **Nasopharyngeal airway**: tolerated by semi-conscious patients; size ≈ patient's little finger; **avoid in suspected base-of-skull fracture** (CSF from nose/ears, raccoon eyes, Battle's sign).
- **Suction** with a rigid Yankauer for the mouth.
- **Choking adult (conscious)**: encourage cough → 5 back blows → 5 abdominal thrusts (chest thrusts in pregnancy/obesity; infants: 5 back blows + 5 chest thrusts, no abdominal thrusts) → if unconscious start CPR.
- **Supraglottic airway** (i-gel, LMA): quick rescue airway; does not fully protect from aspiration.

## 10.3 Endotracheal intubation

- **Indications**: airway protection (GCS ≤ 8, loss of gag/cough, burns/inhalation injury, facial trauma, anaphylaxis), respiratory failure (hypoxaemia/hypercapnia not responding to NIV), need for ventilation (shock, cardiac arrest), procedures/anaesthesia, to allow suction.
- **Preparation (SOAP-ME)**: **S**uction, **O**xygen (pre-oxygenate 100% for 3–5 min), **A**irway equipment (laryngoscope blades checked, ETT sizes ± one size, stylet/bougie, syringe, tape/tie, Magill forceps, backup supraglottic airway and surgical airway kit), **P**ositioning ("sniffing position" — neck flexed, head extended; ramp in obesity) and **P**harmacology (induction, paralytic, vasopressor ready), **M**onitoring (ECG, BP, SpO₂, **waveform capnography**), **E**quipment for after (ventilator, sedation).
- **Rapid sequence induction (RSI)**: pre-oxygenate → induction agent (ketamine 1–2 mg/kg, propofol 1–2 mg/kg, etomidate 0.3 mg/kg, thiopentone 3–5 mg/kg) + neuromuscular blocker (**suxamethonium 1–1.5 mg/kg** — fast, short; avoid in hyperkalaemia, burns/crush > 24 h, spinal injury, neuromuscular disease, MH history; or **rocuronium 1–1.2 mg/kg** — reversed by **sugammadex**) → no bag ventilation unless desaturating → intubate.
- **Adult ETT size**: female 7.0–7.5 mm, male 8.0–8.5 mm. Depth at the teeth: female ~21 cm, male ~23 cm.
- **Confirm placement**: **waveform capnography (gold standard — sustained EtCO₂ trace)**, bilateral chest rise and equal breath sounds, no sounds over the epigastrium, misting, SpO₂, **chest X-ray** (tip 3–5 cm above the carina, about T2–T4). Record the depth (cm at teeth/lips).
- **Cuff pressure 20–30 cmH₂O** (check every shift with a manometer): too low → leak and silent aspiration (VAP); too high → tracheal ischaemia, stenosis, tracheo-oesophageal fistula.
- **Complications**: hypoxia, oesophageal intubation, **right main bronchus intubation** (left side silent), dental/airway trauma, aspiration, laryngospasm, bradycardia (vagal), **hypotension after intubation** (sedation + positive pressure reduces venous return — have fluids and vasopressor ready).

## 10.4 Care of the intubated patient

- Secure the tube (tape/holder), record the depth at the lips each shift, move tube side to side every 24 h (with a second nurse) to prevent pressure injury.
- **Humidification** (heat-moisture exchanger or heated humidifier) — the nose is bypassed; dry secretions block tubes.
- Mouth care with chlorhexidine (0.12–0.2%) / tooth brushing, head of bed 30–45° (VAP bundle).
- **Suctioning (closed or open)**: only when needed (secretions visible/audible, ↑ peak pressure, ↓ SpO₂, saw-tooth flow wave); **pre-oxygenate** (100% for 30–60 s); catheter no more than **half the internal diameter** of the tube; pressure **80–150 mmHg (adults)**; insert without suction until resistance, withdraw 1 cm, then suction while withdrawing; **no longer than 10–15 seconds**; maximum 2–3 passes; do not instil saline routinely. Watch for hypoxia, bradycardia (vagal), arrhythmias, ↑ICP, bronchospasm, trauma.
- **Unplanned extubation**: stay with the patient, call for help, open airway, bag-valve-mask with 100% O₂, assess — prevent with adequate securing, sedation/analgesia, explanation and, as a last resort, restraints per policy.

## 10.5 Tracheostomy

- **Indications**: prolonged ventilation (usually after 7–14 days), weaning, upper airway obstruction, airway protection, secretion management.
- **Advantages over ETT**: more comfortable, less sedation, easier mouth care and weaning, less dead space, may allow speaking (with deflated cuff and speaking valve).
- **Types**: cuffed/uncuffed, fenestrated, with inner cannula (clean/change to prevent blockage), percutaneous vs surgical.
- **Bedside emergency equipment**: spare tubes (same size and one size smaller), **tracheal dilators**, obturator, suction, oxygen, bag-valve-mask, syringe for the cuff.
- **Stoma care**: clean with saline at least daily, keep dry, keyhole dressing, ties allow 1–2 fingers.
- **Accidental decannulation**: a fresh tract (< 5–7 days) can close fast — call for help, oxygen to face and stoma, hold the stoma open with dilators/insert a smaller tube, or oxygenate from above; never force.
- **Blocked tube**: remove/clean inner cannula, suction, deflate cuff; if not resolving, remove tube and re-insert or ventilate via face/stoma.
- **Complications**: early — bleeding, pneumothorax, surgical emphysema, displacement, tube blockage; late — infection, tracheal stenosis, tracheo-oesophageal fistula, tracheo-innominate artery fistula (massive bleeding).
- **Decannulation**: cuff deflation trials → capping/speaking valve → removal, airtight dressing over stoma.

## 10.6 Chest drains (underwater seal)

- **Indications**: pneumothorax, haemothorax, empyema, large effusion, after cardiothoracic surgery. Inserted in the **"triangle of safety"** — 4th–5th intercostal space, mid-axillary line (just above the rib below, to avoid the neurovascular bundle).
- **Underwater seal**: tube 2–3 cm under water in the bottle; the bottle **always below chest level**.
- **Swinging (tidaling)** of fluid with breathing = tube is patent and in the pleural space (stops when the lung re-expands or the tube is blocked/kinked). **Bubbling** = air leak from the lung (expected with pneumothorax); continuous bubbling in the bottle with no improvement → check for leak in the system.
- **Do not clamp routinely** (risk of tension pneumothorax) — only briefly to change the bottle or locate a leak, or per order. If the tube comes out of the chest → cover with a sterile occlusive dressing taped on 3 sides (or sterile gauze), call for help. If disconnected → put the end in sterile water, reconnect.
- **Report**: drainage **> 1500 mL immediately or > 200 mL/hr for 2–4 hours** (massive haemothorax/ongoing bleeding → surgery); sudden stop of drainage with distress; surgical emphysema; signs of tension.
- Milking/stripping only per local policy (can create very negative pressure).
- **Removal**: at end of expiration or held inspiration (Valsalva), occlusive dressing, chest X-ray afterwards.
- **Tension pneumothorax**: severe distress, hypotension, distended neck veins, **tracheal deviation AWAY from the affected side** (late), absent breath sounds and hyper-resonance on the affected side, high ventilator pressures → **immediate needle decompression** (2nd ICS mid-clavicular line, or 4th–5th ICS anterior/mid-axillary line in adults) then chest drain. Do not wait for a chest X-ray.
