($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$11. Mechanical Ventilation — Everything You Need$s$, false, 11, $s$11. Mechanical Ventilation — Everything You Need$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 11. Mechanical Ventilation — Everything You Need

## 11.1 What a ventilator does

- Normal breathing uses **negative pressure** (chest expands, air is sucked in). A ventilator uses **positive pressure** — it pushes air in. Expiration is passive.
- Goals: improve **oxygenation** (FiO₂, PEEP, mean airway pressure) and **ventilation/CO₂ removal** (minute ventilation = rate × tidal volume), reduce work of breathing, protect the airway, allow sedation/paralysis.

| To improve | Change |
|---|---|
| **Oxygenation (PaO₂/SpO₂ low)** | ↑ **FiO₂**, ↑ **PEEP**, ↑ inspiratory time/I:E (mean airway pressure), recruitment, prone positioning, suction, treat cause |
| **CO₂ high (respiratory acidosis)** | ↑ **respiratory rate** or ↑ **tidal volume** (↑ minute ventilation); in obstruction allow more expiratory time |
| **CO₂ low (respiratory alkalosis)** | ↓ rate or ↓ tidal volume; treat pain/anxiety (patient over-triggering) |

## 11.2 Indications

- **Respiratory failure**: PaO₂ < 60 mmHg on FiO₂ > 0.5–0.6; PaCO₂ > 50 with pH < 7.25 (acute), RR > 35, vital capacity < 10–15 mL/kg, NIF weaker than −20 to −25 cmH₂O, exhaustion.
- **Airway protection**: GCS ≤ 8, loss of protective reflexes, upper airway obstruction/burns.
- **Circulatory**: shock, cardiac arrest, to reduce oxygen demand.
- **Other**: neuromuscular weakness (Guillain–Barré, myasthenic crisis, tetanus, high spinal injury), surgery/anaesthesia, controlled ventilation for raised ICP, flail chest.

## 11.3 Ventilator settings

| Setting | Meaning | Usual starting value |
|---|---|---|
| Mode | How breaths are delivered (see 11.4) | AC/VC or SIMV/PC per unit |
| FiO₂ | Fraction of inspired oxygen | 1.0 (100%) after intubation, then wean to lowest for SpO₂ 92–96% (≤ 0.6) |
| Tidal volume (Vt) | Volume per breath | **6–8 mL/kg predicted body weight** (ARDS 6 mL/kg) |
| Respiratory rate (f) | Mandatory breaths/min | 12–20 (higher in ARDS/acidosis; lower in asthma/COPD) |
| PEEP | Pressure kept in the lungs at end-expiration (keeps alveoli open, ↑ FRC, ↑ oxygenation) | 5 cmH₂O; higher in ARDS (8–15+) |
| Inspiratory pressure (PC) / pressure support (PS) | Pressure above PEEP | PC 10–20; PS 5–15 cmH₂O |
| Peak flow / inspiratory time | Speed of delivery / time for each breath | Flow 40–60 L/min; Ti ~1 s |
| I:E ratio | Inspiratory : expiratory time | **1:2** (COPD/asthma 1:3–1:4 to allow exhalation) |
| Trigger sensitivity | Effort needed to start a breath | Flow −1 to −3 L/min or pressure −1 to −2 cmH₂O |
| Alarms | High/low pressure, low volume, apnoea, high rate | High pressure ~10 cmH₂O above PIP |

## 11.4 Modes of ventilation

| Mode | How it works | Use / notes |
|---|---|---|
| **CMV (controlled mandatory)** | Set rate and volume/pressure; patient **cannot** trigger breaths | Paralysed/deeply sedated patients only; distressing if awake |
| **AC (assist-control)** | Set rate and Vt; **every breath — machine or patient-triggered — gets the full set breath** | Full support, rests muscles; risk of **hyperventilation/respiratory alkalosis and breath stacking (auto-PEEP)** if the patient breathes fast |
| **SIMV (synchronised intermittent mandatory)** | Set number of full breaths synchronised with the patient; **in between, the patient breathes spontaneously at their own Vt** (usually with pressure support) | Partial support; used for weaning; less alkalosis than AC |
| **Volume control (VC)** | Set **volume**; pressure varies with compliance/resistance | Guarantees minute ventilation; **watch peak/plateau pressures** |
| **Pressure control (PC)** | Set **pressure** and time; volume varies | Limits barotrauma; **watch exhaled tidal volume** (falls if lungs get stiffer or tube blocks) |
| **PRVC (pressure-regulated volume control)** | Targets a set volume using the lowest pressure | Combines the benefits of both |
| **PSV (pressure support)** | Patient triggers every breath and controls rate; ventilator adds set pressure to each breath | **Needs an intact respiratory drive** (no backup rate — set apnoea alarm/backup); weaning |
| **CPAP** | One continuous pressure throughout; patient does all breathing | Weaning, NIV for pulmonary oedema/sleep apnoea, neonates |
| **BiPAP / BiLevel (NIV)** | IPAP (inspiration) + EPAP (expiration = PEEP) by mask | COPD exacerbation (hypercapnia), cardiogenic pulmonary oedema, post-extubation |
| **APRV** | Long high pressure with brief releases | Rescue mode in severe ARDS |
| **HFOV** | Very small volumes at very high rates | Rescue in ARDS; neonates |

## 11.5 Monitoring the ventilated patient

![Ventilator pressure waveform](keynote/vent_pressure.svg)

- Every hour (and after every change): **mode and settings match the prescription**, FiO₂, set and **exhaled** tidal volume, rate (set and total), **PIP, plateau pressure, PEEP**, minute ventilation, alarm limits, SpO₂, **EtCO₂**, chest movement and breath sounds, **ETT depth and cuff pressure**, humidifier/HME, condensation in tubing (drain away from the patient), patient comfort and synchrony, sedation score (RASS) and pain score (CPOT).
- ABG 20–30 minutes after changes and as needed.
- **Bedside must always have**: a self-inflating **bag-valve-mask with oxygen and PEEP valve**, working suction, spare ETT/trach, airway equipment.

![Capnography waveforms](keynote/capnography.svg)

## 11.6 Alarms and troubleshooting

> First rule: **look at the patient, not the machine.** If the patient is in distress and you cannot quickly find the problem → **disconnect and hand-ventilate with 100% oxygen** with a bag, and call for help.

**DOPE** — causes of sudden deterioration in an intubated patient:

| Letter | Problem | Check / action |
|---|---|---|
| **D** — Displacement | Tube out, in the oesophagus or too deep (right bronchus) | Capnography trace, depth at lips, chest rise, breath sounds; re-intubate/reposition |
| **O** — Obstruction | Secretions, mucus plug, kinked or bitten tube, blood | Pass a suction catheter; bite block; if cannot pass → change tube |
| **P** — Pneumothorax | Especially tension (high pressures, falling BP, unequal air entry, tracheal shift) | Needle decompression, chest drain |
| **E** — Equipment failure | Ventilator, oxygen supply, circuit disconnection/leak | Hand-ventilate, check oxygen source and circuit |

(**DOTTS** for the response: **D**isconnect, **O**xygen 100% bag, **T**ube position/patency, **T**ension pneumothorax, **S**tack — release breath stacking.)

| Alarm | Common causes | Actions |
|---|---|---|
| **High pressure** | Coughing/"fighting" ventilator, biting tube, **secretions**, kinked tube, water in tubing, bronchospasm, pneumothorax, ↓ compliance (ARDS, pulmonary oedema), tube in right bronchus, abdominal distension | Assess patient; suction; bite block; straighten tubing/drain water; bronchodilator; sedate if dyssynchrony; check breath sounds; chest X-ray; DOPE |
| **Low pressure / low volume** | **Disconnection**, leak in circuit, **cuff leak/deflated cuff** (air heard from mouth), tube displaced/extubation, chest drain leak | Check all connections from patient to machine; check cuff pressure and tube depth; bag-ventilate if unresolved |
| **Apnoea** | Patient not breathing in a spontaneous mode (PSV/CPAP), over-sedation, disconnection | Stimulate, check sedation, switch to a mode with a backup rate |
| **High respiratory rate** | Pain, anxiety, hypoxia, fever, acidosis, auto-triggering from a leak/water | Assess and treat cause; check trigger |
| **High minute volume** | Tachypnoea, hyperventilation | As above |
| **Low minute volume** | Fatigue, sedation, leak | Assess; increase support |
| **Oxygen supply / power** | Gas or power failure | Hand-ventilate; connect to cylinder; ventilator battery |

- **Peak high, plateau normal** → airway **resistance** (secretions, bronchospasm, kinked/bitten tube). **Peak and plateau both high** → lung **compliance** problem (pneumothorax, ARDS, oedema, atelectasis, right bronchus intubation, abdominal compartment).
- **Patient–ventilator dyssynchrony** ("fighting the ventilator"): check for hypoxia, pain, anxiety, full bladder, wrong settings (flow too low, trigger too insensitive), auto-PEEP; adjust settings, analgesia then sedation.
- **Auto-PEEP / breath stacking** (asthma, COPD, high rate): air trapped because exhalation is incomplete → ↑ intrathoracic pressure → **hypotension**, barotrauma, difficult triggering. Detect with an expiratory hold or flow not returning to zero. Fix: ↓ rate, ↓ Vt, ↑ expiratory time (I:E 1:4), treat bronchospasm; in crisis **disconnect briefly** to let the trapped air out.

## 11.7 Lung-protective ventilation and ARDS

- **ARDS (Berlin definition)**: onset within 1 week of a known insult, **bilateral opacities** on chest X-ray not fully explained by effusion/collapse, not mainly due to heart failure/fluid overload, and **P/F ratio ≤ 300 with PEEP ≥ 5**: mild 201–300, moderate 101–200, severe ≤ 100. Causes: sepsis (most common), pneumonia, aspiration, trauma, massive transfusion (TRALI), pancreatitis, burns/smoke inhalation, drowning.
- **Lung-protective strategy**:
  - **Tidal volume 6 mL/kg predicted body weight** (4–8).
  - **Plateau pressure ≤ 30 cmH₂O**; driving pressure ≤ 15.
  - **Higher PEEP** for moderate–severe ARDS (PEEP/FiO₂ table).
  - **Permissive hypercapnia** — accept PaCO₂ rising with pH ≥ 7.20–7.25 (not in raised ICP).
  - Oxygen targets PaO₂ 55–80 mmHg / SpO₂ 88–95%.
  - **Prone positioning** 12–16 h/day for P/F < 150 (improves V/Q matching; watch ETT, lines, eyes, face/pressure areas, enteral feed).
  - Conservative fluids once shock resolved.
  - Short course of neuromuscular blockade (cisatracurium) in early severe ARDS.
  - ECMO in refractory cases.

## 11.8 Complications of mechanical ventilation

| Complication | Prevention / recognition |
|---|---|
| **Ventilator-associated pneumonia (VAP)** — pneumonia > 48 h after intubation | **VAP bundle**: head of bed **30–45°**, daily sedation hold and readiness-to-wean assessment, oral care with chlorhexidine/tooth brushing, **subglottic secretion drainage**, cuff pressure 20–30 cmH₂O, hand hygiene, closed suction, drain condensate away from patient, avoid unnecessary circuit changes, peptic ulcer and DVT prophylaxis, early enteral feeding. Signs: new infiltrate, fever, purulent secretions, ↑ WBC, worsening oxygenation |
| **Barotrauma / volutrauma** | Pneumothorax, pneumomediastinum, surgical emphysema — keep Pplat ≤ 30, Vt 6–8 mL/kg |
| **Haemodynamic** | ↓ venous return → hypotension (especially hypovolaemia, high PEEP) — fluids, vasopressors |
| **Oxygen toxicity** | Keep FiO₂ ≤ 0.6 when possible |
| **Atelectasis** | PEEP, turning, physiotherapy, suction |
| **Auto-PEEP** | See above |
| **Airway injury** | Cuff pressure, tube care, tracheal stenosis |
| **Stress ulcers / GI bleeding** | PPI or H₂ blocker in high-risk patients |
| **DVT/PE** | Heparin/LMWH, compression devices |
| **ICU-acquired weakness, pressure injury, delirium** | Early mobility, light sedation, ABCDEF bundle |
| **Fluid retention, ↑ICP, ileus, sinusitis, eye injury** | Monitor; eye care (lubricate, tape lids closed) in sedated patients |
| **Psychological** | Communication boards, explanation, family presence, sleep |

## 11.9 Sedation, analgesia and the ABCDEF bundle

- **Analgesia first** ("analgosedation") — fentanyl, morphine; pain tool **CPOT** (0–8; > 2 = pain) or BPS in non-verbal patients.
- **Sedation target RASS 0 to −2** (light) unless there is a reason for deep sedation (raised ICP, severe ARDS, paralysis, status epilepticus). Propofol and dexmedetomidine are preferred over benzodiazepine infusions (less delirium).

| RASS | Description |
|---|---|
| +4 | Combative |
| +3 | Very agitated (pulls tubes) |
| +2 | Agitated |
| +1 | Restless |
| 0 | Alert and calm |
| −1 | Drowsy (awakens to voice > 10 s) |
| −2 | Light sedation (awakens to voice < 10 s) |
| −3 | Moderate sedation (movement to voice, no eye contact) |
| −4 | Deep sedation (movement only to physical stimulus) |
| −5 | Unrousable |

- **Delirium**: assess with **CAM-ICU** (acute change/fluctuation + inattention + either disorganised thinking or altered level of consciousness). Hyperactive, hypoactive (most common, often missed) or mixed. Prevent: reorientation, clocks, glasses/hearing aids, day–night routine, mobility, family, avoid benzodiazepines, treat pain.
- **ABCDEF bundle**: **A**ssess, prevent and manage pain; **B**oth spontaneous awakening (SAT) and breathing trials (SBT); **C**hoice of analgesia and sedation; **D**elirium assess/prevent/manage; **E**arly mobility and exercise; **F**amily engagement.
- **Neuromuscular blockers** (cisatracurium, rocuronium, vecuronium): no sedative or analgesic effect — **always give adequate sedation and analgesia**; monitor with **train-of-four** (aim 1–2 twitches out of 4); eye care, pressure care, DVT prophylaxis.

## 11.10 Weaning and extubation

- **Daily readiness screen**: cause of respiratory failure improving; **FiO₂ ≤ 0.4–0.5 and PEEP ≤ 5–8** with PaO₂ ≥ 60 (P/F > 150–200); haemodynamically stable on no/low vasopressors; awake, able to follow commands (RASS 0/−1); able to cough and manage secretions; no new major problems; acceptable pH (≥ 7.30–7.35) and electrolytes; Hb adequate.
- **Weaning predictors**: **RSBI (RR ÷ Vt in L) < 105**; NIF more negative than −20 to −30 cmH₂O; vital capacity > 10–15 mL/kg; minute ventilation < 10 L/min.
- **Spontaneous breathing trial (SBT)**: 30–120 min on CPAP/low pressure support (5–7 cmH₂O) or T-piece.
- **SBT failure signs — stop and rest the patient**: RR > 35 for > 5 min, SpO₂ < 90%, HR > 140 or ↑/↓ > 20%, SBP > 180 or < 90, agitation, anxiety, sweating, accessory muscle use, paradoxical breathing, falling level of consciousness, arrhythmias.
- **Extubation**: explain; sit upright; pre-oxygenate; suction ETT and mouth; deflate cuff (a "cuff leak" suggests no major laryngeal oedema); remove at **peak inspiration** (or with a positive-pressure breath) and ask the patient to cough; apply oxygen (or HFNC/NIV in high-risk patients); encourage deep breathing and coughing; monitor closely for 24 h; nil by mouth until swallow assessed.
- **Post-extubation stridor** (laryngeal oedema): sit up, humidified oxygen, **nebulised adrenaline**, IV steroids; reintubate if failing. Steroids before extubation for high-risk patients.

## 11.11 Non-invasive ventilation (NIV)

- **Indications**: **COPD exacerbation with respiratory acidosis (pH < 7.35, PaCO₂ > 45–50)** — best evidence; **acute cardiogenic pulmonary oedema** (CPAP/BiPAP); obesity hypoventilation; immunocompromised patients; post-extubation in high-risk patients; palliative.
- **Contraindications**: respiratory or cardiac arrest, inability to protect the airway (GCS ≤ 8, vomiting, copious secretions), facial trauma/burns/surgery, upper GI surgery/bleeding, untreated pneumothorax, haemodynamic instability, agitated/uncooperative patient, bowel obstruction.
- **Typical start (BiPAP)**: IPAP 10–12 cmH₂O (increase to 15–20), EPAP 4–5 cmH₂O; FiO₂ for SpO₂ 88–92% in COPD. IPAP − EPAP = pressure support (blows off CO₂); EPAP improves oxygenation.
- **Nursing**: explain and coach, correct mask size and fit (allow a small leak, protect the nasal bridge with a dressing — pressure injury), upright position, watch for **gastric distension** (NG tube if needed), eye irritation, dry mouth (humidify), aspiration; ABG after 1–2 hours. **Failure signs** (worsening pH/PaCO₂, ↓ consciousness, exhaustion, instability) → intubate; do not delay.
- **High-flow nasal cannula (HFNC)**: up to 60 L/min heated humidified gas, FiO₂ 0.21–1.0; small PEEP effect, washes out dead space, comfortable; for type 1 failure and after extubation.
