($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$12. Haemodynamic Monitoring, Shock and Sepsis$s$, false, 12, $s$12. Haemodynamic Monitoring, Shock and Sepsis$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 12. Haemodynamic Monitoring, Shock and Sepsis

## 12.1 Invasive monitoring

### Arterial line

- Usually **radial artery** (do **Allen's test** first — checks ulnar collateral flow), also femoral, brachial.
- Gives beat-to-beat BP, MAP and access for ABGs. The waveform has a sharp upstroke and a **dicrotic notch** (aortic valve closure).
- **Transducer set-up**: pressure bag inflated to **300 mmHg** (flush ~3 mL/hr keeps the line patent), **level the transducer to the phlebostatic axis** (4th intercostal space, mid-axillary line = level of the right atrium) and **zero to atmosphere** at the start of each shift, after position changes and if readings are doubtful. Transducer too high → falsely LOW readings; too low → falsely HIGH.
- **Square-wave (fast flush) test** checks damping: **overdamped** (flattened waveform, no dicrotic notch — air bubbles, clot, kinks, loose connections, low pressure bag) → falsely low systolic; **underdamped** (spiky) → falsely high systolic.
- Complications: bleeding/disconnection (**never** inject drugs into an arterial line; luer-lock connections, visible site), thrombosis/ischaemia (check colour, warmth, capillary refill distal to the line), infection, air embolism.

### Central venous catheter (CVC) and CVP

- Sites: internal jugular, **subclavian** (lowest infection risk, higher pneumothorax risk), femoral (highest infection risk). Tip at the junction of the superior vena cava and right atrium — **confirm on chest X-ray** before use (and to exclude pneumothorax).
- Uses: vasopressors/inotropes and irritant drugs, CVP, ScvO₂, TPN, large-volume infusion, dialysis (special catheter).
- **CVP 2–8 mmHg** (3–10 cmH₂O); measure at **end-expiration**, patient supine (or same position each time), transducer at the phlebostatic axis. The **trend** matters more than one value.
  - Low CVP: hypovolaemia, vasodilation.
  - High CVP: fluid overload, right heart failure, **cardiac tamponade**, **tension pneumothorax**, pulmonary hypertension/PE, high PEEP, tricuspid regurgitation.
- Insertion/removal: **Trendelenburg (head down)** for jugular/subclavian lines (fills veins, prevents air embolism); remove with the patient flat/head down, ask them to hold their breath (Valsalva) or remove at end-expiration on a ventilator, firm pressure for 5 min, **occlusive dressing**.
- **Air embolism** (sudden breathlessness, chest pain, hypotension, churning "mill-wheel" murmur): clamp the line, **lie the patient on the LEFT side, head down** (Durant's manoeuvre), 100% oxygen, call for help.
- **CLABSI prevention bundle**: hand hygiene, maximal sterile barrier precautions at insertion, **2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol** skin prep (allow to dry), avoid femoral site, daily review and **prompt removal of unnecessary lines**, scrub the hub before every access, transparent dressing changed every 7 days (gauze every 2 days) or if soiled.

### Pulmonary artery (Swan–Ganz) catheter

- Measures right-sided pressures, **PAWP (wedge)** = estimate of left ventricular filling pressure, cardiac output (thermodilution), SvO₂.
- **Waveform changes during insertion**: right atrium → right ventricle (tall, ventricular ectopics/VT risk) → pulmonary artery (dicrotic notch) → wedge (balloon inflated).
- Safety: inflate the balloon with **≤ 1.5 mL of air** only to read the wedge, **for no more than 15 seconds (2–3 breaths)**, then **deflate passively**; never leave it inflated; a **persistently wedged trace with the balloon down = catheter has migrated** → risk of pulmonary infarction/artery rupture → pull back per protocol/notify. Never flush while wedged.

### Haemodynamic profiles in shock

| Shock type | CVP | PAWP | Cardiac output | SVR | Skin |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| Hypovolaemic | ↓ | ↓ | ↓ | ↑ | Cold, clammy |
| Cardiogenic | ↑ | ↑ | ↓↓ | ↑ | Cold, clammy, pulmonary oedema |
| Obstructive (tamponade, tension pneumothorax, massive PE) | ↑ | ↑ or normal (PE ↓) | ↓ | ↑ | Cold, distended neck veins |
| Distributive — septic (early/warm) | ↓/normal | ↓/normal | ↑ or normal | ↓↓ | Warm, flushed, bounding pulse (later cold) |
| Anaphylactic | ↓ | ↓ | ↓ | ↓↓ | Flushed, urticaria, angio-oedema, wheeze |
| Neurogenic | ↓ | ↓ | ↓ | ↓ | Warm, dry; **bradycardia** |

## 12.2 Shock

- **Shock** = inadequate tissue perfusion → cells switch to anaerobic metabolism → **lactic acidosis** → organ failure.
- **Stages**:
  1. **Initial** — no clinical signs; ↑ lactate.
  2. **Compensatory** — sympathetic + RAAS activation: **tachycardia (earliest sign in most)**, tachypnoea, narrowed pulse pressure, cool pale skin, ↓ urine output, thirst, anxiety; **BP maintained**.
  3. **Progressive** — compensation fails: hypotension (SBP < 90 / MAP < 65), confusion, oliguria, metabolic acidosis, mottled skin, slow capillary refill.
  4. **Refractory/irreversible** — multi-organ failure, unresponsive to treatment.
- **Haemorrhagic shock classes (ATLS)**:

| Class | Blood loss | Heart rate | BP | Pulse pressure | Resp. rate | Urine (mL/hr) | Mental state |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| I | < 15% (< 750 mL) | < 100 | Normal | Normal | 14–20 | > 30 | Slightly anxious |
| II | 15–30% (750–1500) | 100–120 | Normal | ↓ | 20–30 | 20–30 | Anxious |
| III | 30–40% (1500–2000) | 120–140 | ↓ | ↓ | 30–40 | 5–15 | Confused |
| IV | > 40% (> 2000) | > 140 | ↓↓ | ↓ | > 35 | Negligible | Lethargic |

- **General management**: ABCDE, high-flow oxygen, **two large-bore IV cannulas (14–16G)**, bloods (FBC, U&E, lactate, group and crossmatch, clotting, ABG, cultures), monitor (ECG, BP, SpO₂, urine catheter — hourly output, GCS, temperature), position flat/legs raised (passive leg raise as a fluid-responsiveness test), keep warm, treat the cause.
- **Hypovolaemic/haemorrhagic**: **stop the bleeding** (pressure, tourniquet, surgery), **blood products early** in a balanced ratio (1:1:1 red cells:plasma:platelets — massive transfusion protocol), **tranexamic acid within 3 hours** of injury, permissive hypotension (SBP ~80–90) until bleeding controlled (not in head injury), avoid the **lethal triad: hypothermia, acidosis, coagulopathy**.
- **Cardiogenic**: oxygen, careful/no fluid boluses (small if no pulmonary oedema), **inotropes (dobutamine)** ± noradrenaline, treat the cause (revascularisation for MI), diuretics for pulmonary oedema, IABP/mechanical support.
- **Obstructive**: relieve the obstruction — needle decompression/chest drain (tension pneumothorax), **pericardiocentesis** (tamponade), thrombolysis/embolectomy (massive PE).
- **Anaphylactic**: remove trigger, call for help, lie flat (sit up if breathing difficulty; left lateral if pregnant), **IM adrenaline 0.5 mg (0.5 mL of 1:1000)** into the anterolateral thigh, **repeat every 5 minutes** if needed; high-flow oxygen; IV fluid bolus 500–1000 mL; then antihistamine (chlorphenamine), steroid (hydrocortisone), salbutamol for wheeze; IV adrenaline infusion if refractory (experts). Child dose IM adrenaline: < 6 months 100–150 mcg, 6 months–6 years 150 mcg, 6–12 years 300 mcg, > 12 years 500 mcg.
- **Neurogenic**: fluids, **vasopressors** (noradrenaline), atropine for bradycardia, spinal immobilisation, keep warm.

## 12.3 Sepsis and septic shock

- **Sepsis (Sepsis-3)** = life-threatening **organ dysfunction** caused by a dysregulated host response to infection (SOFA score increase ≥ 2).
- **Septic shock** = sepsis needing **vasopressors to keep MAP ≥ 65 mmHg** AND **lactate > 2 mmol/L** despite adequate fluid resuscitation (mortality > 40%).
- **qSOFA** (bedside screen, 1 point each, ≥ 2 = high risk): **RR ≥ 22**, **altered mentation (GCS < 15)**, **SBP ≤ 100**.
- **SIRS** (older criteria): ≥ 2 of temperature > 38 or < 36 °C, HR > 90, RR > 20 or PaCO₂ < 32, WBC > 12 or < 4 × 10⁹/L.
- **Early signs** often subtle: confusion in the elderly, tachypnoea, fever or **hypothermia**, mottling, reduced urine output.

### Hour-1 bundle (Surviving Sepsis Campaign) — start immediately

1. **Measure lactate** (re-measure within 2–4 h if > 2).
2. **Blood cultures before antibiotics** (2 sets, different sites) — but do not delay antibiotics.
3. **Broad-spectrum antibiotics** within **1 hour** of recognition (each hour of delay ↑ mortality).
4. **30 mL/kg crystalloid** (balanced, e.g. Ringer's lactate) for hypotension or lactate ≥ 4 mmol/L, given within 3 hours, reassessing for fluid overload.
5. **Vasopressors** if hypotensive during/after fluids to keep **MAP ≥ 65 mmHg** — **noradrenaline first line** (can start peripherally via a large proximal vein while central access is obtained); add vasopressin; adrenaline; dobutamine if poor cardiac function.

- Also: **source control** (drain abscess, remove infected lines/catheters, surgery) within 6–12 h, oxygen, hourly urine output (≥ 0.5 mL/kg/hr), glucose 8–10 mmol/L (144–180 mg/dL), **hydrocortisone 200 mg/day** if vasopressor needs persist, DVT and stress-ulcer prophylaxis, nutrition, reassess fluid responsiveness (passive leg raise, capillary refill, lactate clearance).
- **Neutropenic sepsis** (neutrophils < 0.5–1.0 with fever ≥ 38): antibiotics **within 1 hour**, protective isolation.

## 12.4 Multiple organ dysfunction syndrome (MODS)

- Progressive failure of two or more organs after a major insult (sepsis, trauma, burns, pancreatitis). Order often: lungs (ARDS) → kidneys/liver → cardiovascular → brain → coagulation (DIC).
- **DIC**: widespread clotting uses up platelets and clotting factors → bleeding from multiple sites (IV sites, gums, wounds), petechiae, **↓ platelets, ↑ PT/INR and aPTT, ↓ fibrinogen, ↑ D-dimer**. Treat the cause; platelets, FFP, cryoprecipitate as needed; handle gently, avoid IM injections, minimise venepunctures.
