## 13.1 Basic and advanced life support (adult)

- **BLS sequence**: check **D**anger → **R**esponse (shout and shake shoulders) → **S**hout for help → open **A**irway → check **B**reathing (look, listen, feel, ≤ 10 s; agonal gasps = not breathing) → call **resuscitation team/emergency number** and get an **AED** → **C**ompressions: **30 compressions : 2 breaths**.
- **Good-quality CPR**: centre of the chest (lower half of the sternum), depth **5–6 cm**, rate **100–120/min**, allow full recoil, minimise interruptions (< 10 s), change compressors every 2 minutes, avoid excessive ventilation (each breath ~1 s, visible chest rise). With an advanced airway: continuous compressions and **10 breaths/min**.
- **Children**: 15:2 (two rescuers; 30:2 for a lone lay rescuer), depth ⅓ of the chest (~4 cm infant, ~5 cm child), give **5 rescue breaths first** (paediatric hypoxic arrest). Infant compressions: two fingers or two-thumb encircling.
- **Newborn**: 3 compressions : 1 breath (rate 120 events/min), start compressions if HR < 60 after effective ventilation.

### ALS algorithm

| Rhythm | Shockable? | Actions |
|---|---|---|
| **Ventricular fibrillation (VF) / pulseless VT** | **Yes** | Shock (biphasic **150–200 J**, then up to 360) → CPR 2 min → rhythm check. **Adrenaline 1 mg IV after the 3rd shock**, then every 3–5 min (every other cycle). **Amiodarone 300 mg IV after the 3rd shock**, then 150 mg after the 5th. |
| **Asystole / PEA** | **No** | CPR → **adrenaline 1 mg IV as soon as access is gained**, then every 3–5 min. Look for reversible causes. |

- **Reversible causes — 4 Hs and 4 Ts**: **H**ypoxia, **H**ypovolaemia, **H**ypo-/hyperkalaemia (and other metabolic: hypoglycaemia, hypocalcaemia, acidosis), **H**ypothermia/hyperthermia; **T**ension pneumothorax, **T**amponade (cardiac), **T**oxins, **T**hrombosis (coronary or pulmonary).
- **Waveform capnography in CPR**: confirms tube placement; EtCO₂ < 10 mmHg suggests poor compressions; a sudden rise to 35–40+ suggests **ROSC**.
- **After ROSC**: ABCDE, SpO₂ 94–98%, PaCO₂ normal (35–45), MAP ≥ 65 (often ≥ 80 for the brain), 12-lead ECG (PCI if STEMI), glucose 4–10 mmol/L, **temperature control (prevent fever > 37.7 °C for ≥ 72 h)**, treat seizures, look for the cause.
- **Paediatric doses**: adrenaline **10 mcg/kg** (0.1 mL/kg of 1:10,000), shock **4 J/kg**, amiodarone 5 mg/kg.

## 13.2 ECG rhythms to recognise

| Rhythm | Key features | Action |
|---|---|---|
| Sinus rhythm | Regular, P before every QRS, PR 0.12–0.20, rate 60–100 | — |
| Sinus tachycardia | Sinus, rate > 100 | Treat the cause (pain, fever, hypovolaemia, anxiety, hypoxia, sepsis) |
| Sinus bradycardia | Sinus, rate < 60 | Treat if symptomatic: **atropine 500 mcg IV**, repeat to max 3 mg; pacing; adrenaline/isoprenaline infusion |
| Atrial fibrillation (AF) | **Irregularly irregular**, no P waves, fibrillatory baseline | Unstable → synchronised cardioversion; stable → rate control (beta-blocker, diltiazem, digoxin) or rhythm control (amiodarone); **anticoagulate** (stroke risk — CHA₂DS₂-VASc) |
| Atrial flutter | **Saw-tooth flutter waves**, often 2:1 (ventricular rate ~150) | Like AF; very responsive to cardioversion |
| SVT | Regular, narrow, fast (150–250), P waves hidden | **Vagal manoeuvres** (modified Valsalva) → **adenosine 6 mg rapid IV push** + flush (then 12 mg, 12 mg) — warn of a brief "doom" feeling/asystole; unstable → cardioversion |
| Ventricular tachycardia (VT) | Regular, **broad QRS (> 0.12 s)**, rate > 100 | Pulseless → defibrillate (ALS). With pulse and unstable (shock, syncope, chest pain, heart failure) → **synchronised cardioversion**; stable → **amiodarone 300 mg over 10–20 min** |
| Torsades de pointes | Polymorphic VT, twisting axis; long QT | **Magnesium 2 g IV**; correct K⁺; stop QT-prolonging drugs; defibrillate if pulseless |
| Ventricular fibrillation | Chaotic, no recognisable QRS, no pulse | **Defibrillate immediately** + CPR |
| Asystole | Flat line (check leads, gain) | CPR, adrenaline |
| PEA | Organised electrical activity but **no pulse** | CPR, adrenaline, find 4Hs/4Ts |
| 1st-degree AV block | **PR > 0.20 s** constant, every P conducted | Monitor; usually benign |
| 2nd-degree Mobitz I (Wenckebach) | **PR gets longer and longer until a QRS is dropped** | Usually benign (inferior MI, drugs) |
| 2nd-degree Mobitz II | Constant PR with **sudden dropped QRS** | Risk of complete block → pacing |
| 3rd-degree (complete) heart block | **P waves and QRS unrelated** (AV dissociation), slow ventricular rate (20–40) | Atropine (often ineffective), **transcutaneous/transvenous pacing** |
| Premature ventricular complexes | Early wide bizarre QRS, compensatory pause | Check K⁺, Mg²⁺, hypoxia, ischaemia |

- **Synchronised cardioversion** (shock timed on the R wave — avoids R-on-T VF): sedate, sync button ON (check markers on each R wave), AF 120–200 J biphasic, flutter/SVT 70–150 J. **Defibrillation** (unsynchronised) for VF/pulseless VT.
- **Defibrillation safety**: oxygen away (1 m), no one touching the patient/bed ("stand clear, oxygen away, shocking"), pads not over a pacemaker/ECG electrodes/patches.
- **Electrolytes on ECG**: **hyperkalaemia** — tall peaked T waves → flat P, wide QRS → sine wave → VF/asystole. **Hypokalaemia** — flat T, **U waves**, ST depression, arrhythmias. **Hypocalcaemia** — long QT. **Hypercalcaemia** — short QT.

## 13.3 Acute coronary syndrome (ACS)

- Spectrum: **unstable angina** (no troponin rise), **NSTEMI** (troponin rise, no ST elevation), **STEMI** (ST elevation ≥ 1 mm in 2 contiguous limb leads or ≥ 2 mm in chest leads, or new LBBB).
- Presentation: central crushing chest pain > 15–20 min radiating to arm/jaw, sweating, nausea, breathlessness. **Atypical** (silent) in **diabetics, elderly, women** — breathlessness, fatigue, epigastric pain.
- **Immediate care**: 12-lead ECG within 10 minutes, monitor, IV access, troponin.
- **MONA** (use selectively):
  - **M**orphine for pain (with antiemetic).
  - **O**xygen **only if SpO₂ < 90–94%**.
  - **N**itrates (GTN sublingual 0.4 mg every 5 min × 3) — **avoid if SBP < 90, RV infarction, sildenafil use within 24–48 h**.
  - **A**spirin **300 mg chewed** (unless allergic).
- Add: second antiplatelet (clopidogrel/ticagrelor), anticoagulant (heparin/enoxaparin), beta-blocker (if no heart failure/shock), statin, ACE inhibitor.
- **STEMI reperfusion**: **primary PCI** within 90–120 min of first medical contact (door-to-balloon), or **thrombolysis** (streptokinase, alteplase, tenecteplase) within 12 h of onset if PCI not available (**door-to-needle ≤ 30 min**).
- **Thrombolysis contraindications**: previous haemorrhagic stroke, ischaemic stroke within 3–6 months, active bleeding, recent major surgery/trauma/head injury (3 weeks), aortic dissection, uncontrolled BP > 180/110, bleeding disorders. Watch for bleeding, reperfusion arrhythmias, hypotension, allergy (streptokinase).
- **Complications of MI**: arrhythmias (most common cause of early death — VF), heart failure/cardiogenic shock, papillary muscle rupture (acute mitral regurgitation), ventricular septal rupture, free-wall rupture → tamponade, pericarditis (and Dressler's syndrome weeks later), ventricular aneurysm, thromboembolism.

## 13.4 Heart failure and acute pulmonary oedema

- **Left-sided failure** → lungs: dyspnoea, **orthopnoea**, PND, crackles, pink frothy sputum, S3, hypoxaemia. **Right-sided failure** → body: ↑JVP, peripheral (pitting) oedema, hepatomegaly, ascites, weight gain.
- **Acute pulmonary oedema management**: **sit upright with legs dependent**, high-flow oxygen/**CPAP or BiPAP**, **IV furosemide** (40–80 mg; venodilation then diuresis — monitor urine output, K⁺), **GTN** (sublingual/IV infusion if SBP > 100–110 — reduces preload and afterload), morphine (cautiously), treat the cause (ischaemia, arrhythmia, hypertension), inotropes if cardiogenic shock. Fluid restriction, daily weight (1 kg ≈ 1 L), strict fluid balance.
- Chronic: ACE inhibitor/ARB/ARNI, beta-blocker, aldosterone antagonist (spironolactone — ↑K⁺), SGLT2 inhibitor, diuretics; low salt; teach to report **weight gain > 2 kg in 3 days**.

## 13.5 Other cardiac emergencies

- **Cardiac tamponade** — **Beck's triad**: hypotension, **↑JVP (distended neck veins)**, **muffled heart sounds**; also tachycardia, **pulsus paradoxus** (> 10 mmHg fall on inspiration), electrical alternans, low-voltage ECG. After cardiac surgery: sudden stop of chest drainage with falling BP and rising CVP. Treatment: **pericardiocentesis** or surgery; fluids to maintain preload.
- **Hypertensive emergency** (BP ≥ 180/120 with organ damage — encephalopathy, stroke, pulmonary oedema, AKI, eclampsia, dissection): IV labetalol, GTN, nicardipine, hydralazine; **lower MAP by no more than 20–25% in the first hour** (except aortic dissection — target SBP < 120 quickly).
- **Aortic dissection**: tearing chest pain to the back, **unequal arm BPs/pulses**, widened mediastinum. Control HR (< 60) and BP with beta-blocker first; surgery for type A.
- **Infective endocarditis**: fever + new murmur; Osler's nodes, Janeway lesions, splinter haemorrhages, Roth spots; IV drug users (tricuspid).

## 13.6 Pacemakers and IABP

- **Temporary pacing**: transcutaneous (pads; sedate/analgesia — painful) or transvenous. Check **capture** (pacing spike followed by a QRS **and a pulse**) and **sensing**.
  - **Failure to capture**: spikes with no QRS — lead displacement, low output, ↑ threshold (ischaemia, ↓K⁺) → increase mA, reposition patient, check connections.
  - **Failure to sense**: spikes in the wrong place/on T waves (R-on-T risk) → increase sensitivity.
  - **Failure to pace**: no spikes when needed — battery, connection, oversensing.
- **Pacemaker codes**: first letter = chamber paced, second = chamber sensed, third = response (e.g. VVI, DDD).
- **Intra-aortic balloon pump (IABP)**: balloon in the descending thoracic aorta (tip just below the left subclavian artery) **inflates in diastole** (at the dicrotic notch → ↑ coronary perfusion) and **deflates just before systole** (↓ afterload, ↓ myocardial O₂ demand). Used for cardiogenic shock, refractory angina, mechanical MI complications. Nursing: check **limb perfusion** distal to insertion (cold pulseless leg = limb ischaemia — urgent), **left radial pulse/urine output** (balloon too high blocks the subclavian; too low blocks renal arteries), keep the leg straight (head of bed ≤ 30°), anticoagulation and bleeding, platelets (thrombocytopenia), timing. Weaning: reduce the ratio 1:1 → 1:2 → 1:3.
