## 15.1 Burns

![Rule of Nines](keynote/rule_nines.svg)

- **Types**: thermal (flame, scald, contact), chemical (acid, **alkali — deeper, keeps burning**), electrical (small entry/exit wounds, big internal damage, arrhythmias, rhabdomyolysis), radiation, inhalation.
- **First aid**: stop the burning process; remove clothing/jewellery (not stuck material); **cool running water for 20 minutes** (within 3 h) — no ice; cover with cling film; keep the patient warm. Chemical burns: brush off powder, irrigate copiously.
- **Severity**: depth (see chapter 8 diagram), %TBSA, site (face, hands, feet, perineum, joints = special areas), inhalation injury, age, comorbidities. **Major burn** (adults): > 15–20% TBSA partial/full thickness (> 10% children) → IV fluid resuscitation and referral.

### Inhalation injury — airway first

- Suspect with: burns in an **enclosed space**, **facial/neck burns**, **singed nasal hairs/eyebrows**, **soot in mouth/nose or carbonaceous sputum**, **hoarse voice, stridor**, wheeze, altered consciousness.
- Airway oedema worsens over **24–48 h** → **early intubation** before swelling makes it impossible (use an uncut tube).
- **Carbon monoxide poisoning**: headache, nausea, confusion, "cherry-red" (late, unreliable); **SpO₂ falsely normal** — measure **COHb**; treat with **100% oxygen** via non-rebreather (half-life of COHb falls from ~4–6 h to ~1 h); hyperbaric oxygen in severe cases.
- **Cyanide** (burning plastics): unexplained severe lactic acidosis, collapse → hydroxocobalamin.

### Fluid resuscitation and monitoring

- **Parkland**: 4 mL × kg × %TBSA of **Ringer's lactate** in 24 h — **half in the first 8 h from the time of the burn**, half in the next 16 h. Children: add maintenance fluid with dextrose.
- The formula is only a start — **titrate to urine output**: adult **0.5–1 mL/kg/hr** (~30–50 mL/hr), child 1 mL/kg/hr, electrical burns with myoglobinuria **1–1.5 mL/kg/hr** (higher until urine clears).
- Watch for **over-resuscitation** ("fluid creep"): pulmonary oedema, abdominal and limb compartment syndromes.
- After 24 h capillaries begin to seal; colloid (albumin) may be added; fluid shifts back (diuresis) at 48–72 h.

### Other burns care

- **Circumferential full-thickness burns** of limbs/chest act like a tourniquet → compartment syndrome / restricted breathing → **escharotomy**.
- **Electrolytes**: early **hyperkalaemia** (cell destruction), later hypokalaemia; hyponatraemia; metabolic acidosis.
- **Infection** (leading cause of death after the first days): strict asepsis, dressings (silver sulfadiazine — not on the face/pregnancy/neonates; silver dressings), tetanus prophylaxis; no routine prophylactic systemic antibiotics.
- **Nutrition**: hypermetabolic state — **early enteral feeding** (within 24 h), high protein (1.5–2 g/kg/day) and calories; glutamine, vitamins, zinc.
- **Pain**: IV opioids (not IM — poor absorption in shock), premedicate before dressings.
- **Other**: stress ulcer prophylaxis (**Curling's ulcer**), hypothermia prevention (warm room), positioning to prevent contractures (position of comfort = position of contracture), physiotherapy, eye care, psychological support.
- **Phases**: emergent/resuscitative (0–48/72 h) → acute (wound healing, infection) → rehabilitation.

## 15.2 Trauma — primary survey (ATLS)

| Step | Assess | Act |
|---|---|---|
| **<C>** Catastrophic haemorrhage | Massive external bleeding | Direct pressure, **tourniquet**, haemostatic dressing |
| **A** — Airway with **cervical spine protection** | Speaking? Noises, obstruction, facial trauma, burns | Jaw thrust, suction, airway adjuncts, intubation; manual in-line stabilisation |
| **B** — Breathing | RR, SpO₂, chest movement, trachea, breath sounds, percussion | Oxygen; treat **tension pneumothorax** (decompress), **open pneumothorax** (3-sided dressing), massive haemothorax (chest drain), **flail chest** (analgesia, ventilation) |
| **C** — Circulation with haemorrhage control | HR, BP, capillary refill, skin, external bleeding; hidden bleeding in "**chest, abdomen, pelvis, long bones, floor**" | 2 large-bore cannulas, blood early (massive transfusion), **pelvic binder**, splint femur, tranexamic acid ≤ 3 h, warm fluids |
| **D** — Disability | GCS/AVPU, pupils, glucose, limb movement | Prevent secondary brain injury |
| **E** — Exposure / environment | Full undress, log-roll, look for injuries | **Prevent hypothermia** (warm blankets, fluids, room) |

- **Secondary survey** after stabilising: head-to-toe exam, **AMPLE** history (**A**llergies, **M**edications, **P**ast history/pregnancy, **L**ast meal, **E**vents).
- **Lethal triad**: hypothermia + acidosis + coagulopathy (add hypocalcaemia — "lethal diamond").
- **Flail chest**: ≥ 2 adjacent ribs fractured in ≥ 2 places → **paradoxical movement**; the underlying **pulmonary contusion** causes hypoxaemia; analgesia (epidural/nerve blocks), oxygen, ventilation if needed.
- **Pelvic fracture**: can hide 1.5–3 L of blood; **pelvic binder** at the level of the greater trochanters; no "springing" of the pelvis; **no urinary catheter if blood at the urethral meatus** (urethral injury).
- **Spinal injury**: immobilise (collar, blocks, log-roll with 4–5 people, head holder controls), watch neurogenic shock and breathing (C3–C5).
- **Abdominal trauma**: FAST ultrasound; seat-belt sign; Kehr's sign (left shoulder tip pain = splenic rupture).
- **Major incident triage** (sieve): walking → green (delayed/minor); not breathing after airway opened → dead/expectant; RR < 10 or > 29/30, or capillary refill > 2 s / HR > 120 → red (immediate); otherwise yellow (urgent). **START** triage uses RR > 30, perfusion (refill > 2 s / no radial pulse) and mental status (can't follow commands).

## 15.3 Poisoning — general approach

1. **ABCDE**, glucose, temperature, ECG (QRS/QT), ABG — **stabilise before identifying** the poison. Protect the airway if GCS ≤ 8.
2. **History**: what, how much, when, route, other substances (alcohol), intent (always assess suicide risk once stable), bring containers.
3. **Decontamination**: **activated charcoal 50 g** (1 g/kg) **within 1 hour** of ingestion for adsorbable drugs, only with a safe/protected airway. **Not** for corrosives, hydrocarbons/petrol/kerosene, alcohols, iron, lithium, heavy metals. **Do not induce vomiting.** Whole-bowel irrigation for iron, lithium, sustained-release drugs, drug packets.
4. **Enhanced elimination**: multiple-dose charcoal (carbamazepine, theophylline, phenobarbital, quinine, dapsone), urinary alkalinisation (salicylates), **haemodialysis** (lithium, methanol, ethylene glycol, salicylates, theophylline, metformin).
5. **Antidotes** (table).

### Toxidromes

| Toxidrome | Causes | Signs |
|---|---|---|
| **Opioid** | Morphine, heroin, tramadol, codeine | **Pinpoint pupils, respiratory depression (RR < 12), coma**, hypotension → **naloxone** |
| **Cholinergic** | **Organophosphate insecticides**, carbamates, nerve agents | **SLUDGE/DUMBELS**: salivation, lacrimation, urination, diarrhoea, vomiting, **bronchorrhoea/bronchospasm, bradycardia, miosis**, sweating, fasciculations, weakness → atropine + pralidoxime |
| **Anticholinergic** | Atropine, antihistamines, **tricyclic antidepressants**, datura | Hot, dry, flushed, **dilated pupils**, delirium, urinary retention, tachycardia, ↓ bowel sounds |
| **Sympathomimetic** | Cocaine, amphetamines, ecstasy | Agitation, **dilated pupils**, tachycardia, hypertension, hyperthermia, **sweating** (unlike anticholinergic), seizures → benzodiazepines |
| **Sedative–hypnotic** | Benzodiazepines, barbiturates, alcohol | CNS and respiratory depression, normal/small pupils, hypotension |
| **Serotonin syndrome** | SSRIs + tramadol/MAOIs/ecstasy | Agitation, hyperthermia, **clonus/hyperreflexia** (legs), diarrhoea, sweating |

### Antidotes

| Poison | Antidote / key treatment |
|---|---|
| **Paracetamol** | **N-acetylcysteine (NAC)** — most effective within 8 h; use the **level at ≥ 4 h** on the treatment nomogram (treat at once if late, staggered or unknown time); 21-hour infusion (3 bags; newer 12-h regimens). Anaphylactoid reaction (flushing, rash, wheeze) → stop/slow, antihistamine, restart. Watch ALT, INR, glucose (liver failure day 2–4) |
| **Opioids** | **Naloxone** 0.4 mg IV (titrate 0.1–0.4 mg; IM/IN also) — short half-life, **re-sedation** likely with long-acting opioids → infusion; may precipitate withdrawal |
| **Benzodiazepines** | **Flumazenil** — caution: seizures in chronic users/TCA co-ingestion; rarely used |
| **Organophosphates** | **Atropine** 1–2 mg IV, **double every 5 min until the chest is clear** (dry secretions, HR > 80, SBP > 80) — pupil size is not the target; **pralidoxime**; decontaminate skin (protect staff) |
| **Warfarin / rat poison (superwarfarins)** | **Vitamin K** (prolonged for superwarfarins), prothrombin complex concentrate, FFP |
| **Heparin** | **Protamine sulphate** |
| **Iron** | **Desferrioxamine** |
| **Methanol / ethylene glycol** | **Fomepizole** or ethanol; dialysis; folate (methanol). Methanol → **visual loss**; ethylene glycol → renal failure, calcium oxalate crystals |
| **Digoxin** | **Digoxin-specific antibody (Fab)**; correct K⁺ |
| **Beta-blockers** | **Glucagon**, high-dose insulin + glucose, atropine, pacing |
| **Calcium channel blockers** | **Calcium**, high-dose insulin euglycaemia therapy |
| **Tricyclic antidepressants** | **Sodium bicarbonate** (QRS > 100 ms, arrhythmias, seizures, hypotension) |
| **Cyanide** | **Hydroxocobalamin** |
| **Carbon monoxide** | **100% oxygen** (hyperbaric if severe) |
| **Lead and heavy metals** | Chelating agents (dimercaprol, EDTA, succimer) |
| **Local anaesthetic toxicity** | **20% lipid emulsion** (Intralipid) |
| **Malignant hyperthermia** | **Dantrolene** |
| **Magnesium sulphate** | **Calcium gluconate** |
| **Insulin / sulphonylurea** | Glucose (octreotide for sulphonylureas) |
| **Neuromuscular block (rocuronium)** | **Sugammadex**; (non-depolarising blockers generally) neostigmine + glycopyrrolate |
| **Snake bite** | Antivenom (if systemic envenomation), immobilise limb at heart level, no tourniquet/incision/sucking; 20-minute whole blood clotting test |

- **Salicylate (aspirin) poisoning**: tinnitus, vomiting, sweating, hyperventilation — **early respiratory alkalosis, then high-anion-gap metabolic acidosis**; urinary alkalinisation with bicarbonate; dialysis if severe.
- **Paraquat** (herbicide, common in Nigeria): mouth ulcers, then pulmonary fibrosis; **avoid high oxygen** (worsens lung damage) unless severely hypoxic; Fuller's earth/charcoal early.
- **Kerosene/petrol ingestion**: risk of **aspiration pneumonitis** — do not induce vomiting or give charcoal; observe breathing, chest X-ray.
- **Corrosives** (acids/alkalis): do not induce vomiting or neutralise; no charcoal; airway, analgesia, endoscopy.
- **Lithium toxicity**: coarse tremor, ataxia, confusion, seizures — stop lithium, fluids, **haemodialysis** if severe.
- After stabilisation: **psychiatric assessment and suicide risk assessment** before discharge for every deliberate self-poisoning.
