## 16.1 Principles

- **Pharmacokinetics** (what the body does to the drug): **A**bsorption, **D**istribution, **M**etabolism (mostly liver — cytochrome P450), **E**xcretion (mostly kidney). **Half-life** = time for the level to halve; steady state after ~4–5 half-lives. **First-pass effect**: oral drugs partly destroyed by the liver before reaching the circulation (e.g. GTN given sublingually).
- **Pharmacodynamics** (what the drug does to the body): **agonist** (activates receptor), **antagonist** (blocks), therapeutic index (narrow: digoxin, lithium, gentamicin, phenytoin, theophylline, warfarin — monitor levels).
- In critical illness: poor gut absorption, altered protein binding (low albumin), liver/kidney failure → accumulation, fluid shifts → adjust doses; prefer IV route.
- **Rights of medication administration**: right patient, drug, dose, route, time, documentation, reason, response, and the right to refuse. Double-check high-alert drugs (insulin, heparin, potassium, opioids, inotropes, chemotherapy, neuromuscular blockers).
- **Infusion safety**: dedicated lumen for vasopressors/inotropes (no boluses through it), label lines, **never interrupt vasopressor infusions** (have the next syringe ready — "double pumping"/piggy-back change), central line preferred for vasopressors (peripheral short term in a large vein with close checking).
- **Extravasation** of vasopressors (noradrenaline: pale, cold, swollen site): stop, aspirate, elevate, **phentolamine** infiltration per protocol, notify.

## 16.2 Vasopressors and inotropes

| Drug | Receptors | Main effect | Usual dose | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Noradrenaline (norepinephrine)** | α1 >> β1 | Vasoconstriction, ↑ MAP | 0.01–0.5 (up to 1) mcg/kg/min | **First-line in septic shock**; central line preferred; reflex bradycardia; limb/gut ischaemia at high doses |
| **Adrenaline (epinephrine)** | β1, β2, α1 (dose-dependent) | ↑ HR, ↑ contractility, vasoconstriction, bronchodilation | 0.01–0.5 mcg/kg/min; arrest 1 mg IV; anaphylaxis 0.5 mg IM | Tachyarrhythmias, ↑ lactate, hyperglycaemia |
| **Dopamine** | Low: dopaminergic; medium (5–10 mcg/kg/min): β1; high (> 10): α1 | Dose-related | 2–20 mcg/kg/min | More arrhythmias than noradrenaline; "renal dose" has no proven benefit |
| **Dobutamine** | **β1** (some β2) | **↑ contractility (inotrope)**, mild vasodilation | 2.5–20 mcg/kg/min | **Cardiogenic shock, low cardiac output**; may ↓ BP; tachycardia |
| **Vasopressin** | V1 receptors | Vasoconstriction | 0.03–0.04 units/min (fixed) | Added to noradrenaline in septic shock; digital/gut ischaemia |
| **Phenylephrine** | Pure α1 | Vasoconstriction | Bolus 50–100 mcg; infusion | Reflex bradycardia; anaesthesia-induced hypotension |
| **Milrinone** | PDE-3 inhibitor ("inodilator") | ↑ contractility + vasodilation | 0.375–0.75 mcg/kg/min | Hypotension; renal dosing; arrhythmias |
| **Isoprenaline** | β1, β2 | ↑ HR | 0.5–10 mcg/min | Bradycardia/heart block while awaiting pacing |

## 16.3 Cardiovascular drugs

| Drug | Use | Key nursing points |
|---|---|---|
| **Amiodarone** | VF/VT, AF | Arrest 300 mg IV; stable VT/AF 300 mg over 20–60 min then 900 mg/24 h; **central line** preferred (phlebitis), use 5% dextrose; hypotension, bradycardia, long QT; long-term: thyroid, lung fibrosis, liver, photosensitivity, corneal deposits |
| **Adenosine** | SVT | **6 mg rapid IV push + 20 mL flush** (large proximal vein), then 12 mg, 12 mg; brief asystole/flushing/chest tightness; avoid in asthma; half-life seconds |
| **Atropine** | Symptomatic bradycardia; organophosphates | 500 mcg IV every 3–5 min to max 3 mg (bradycardia); doses < 500 mcg may cause paradoxical bradycardia |
| **Digoxin** | AF rate control, heart failure | Check **apical pulse for 1 minute — withhold if < 60**; toxicity (↑ by **hypokalaemia**, renal failure): nausea, vomiting, **yellow-green vision**, arrhythmias → Digibind |
| **Beta-blockers** (metoprolol, propranolol, esmolol, labetalol) | Rate control, ACS, hypertension, thyroid storm | Bradycardia, hypotension, bronchospasm (avoid in asthma), mask hypoglycaemia |
| **GTN (nitroglycerin)** | Angina, ACS, pulmonary oedema, hypertension | Headache, hypotension; **avoid SBP < 90, RV infarction, phosphodiesterase inhibitors (sildenafil)**; glass bottle/non-PVC set for infusion |
| **Sodium nitroprusside** | Hypertensive emergency | Light-sensitive (cover); **cyanide toxicity** with prolonged use |
| **ACE inhibitors** (captopril, lisinopril) | Hypertension, heart failure, post-MI | Dry cough, **hyperkalaemia**, angio-oedema, first-dose hypotension, renal impairment |
| **Furosemide** (loop diuretic) | Fluid overload, pulmonary oedema | **Hypokalaemia**, hyponatraemia, dehydration, ototoxicity (fast IV), hypotension; give in the morning |
| **Spironolactone** | Heart failure, ascites | **Hyperkalaemia**, gynaecomastia |
| **Mannitol** (osmotic diuretic) | Raised ICP | 0.25–1 g/kg over 15–20 min; filter (crystals); monitor osmolality (< 320), urine output, electrolytes |

## 16.4 Anticoagulants, antiplatelets and thrombolytics

| Drug | Monitoring | Reversal / notes |
|---|---|---|
| **Unfractionated heparin** | **aPTT** (1.5–2.5 × normal), platelets (**HIT** — heparin-induced thrombocytopenia, platelets fall ~50% at 5–10 days) | **Protamine sulphate** (1 mg per 100 units); short half-life |
| **Low-molecular-weight heparin** (enoxaparin) | Anti-Xa (renal failure, pregnancy, obesity) | Subcutaneous; **don't expel the air bubble or rub the site**; partial protamine reversal |
| **Warfarin** | **INR** (target 2–3; mechanical valves 2.5–3.5) | **Vitamin K**, prothrombin complex concentrate, FFP; many interactions (antibiotics, amiodarone, alcohol); leafy greens affect INR |
| **DOACs** (rivaroxaban, apixaban, dabigatran) | Usually none | Idarucizumab (dabigatran), andexanet alfa |
| **Aspirin, clopidogrel, ticagrelor** | — | Antiplatelet; GI bleeding |
| **Thrombolytics** (streptokinase, alteplase, tenecteplase) | Bleeding, neuro obs | Contraindications in chapter 13; tranexamic acid/FFP/cryo for bleeding |
| **Tranexamic acid** | — | Antifibrinolytic: trauma (1 g over 10 min within 3 h, then 1 g over 8 h), PPH |

## 16.5 Sedation, analgesia and neuromuscular blockers

| Drug | Class / use | Key points |
|---|---|---|
| **Propofol** | Sedative-hypnotic (GABA) | Rapid on/off; **hypotension**, bradycardia; lipid (1.1 kcal/mL — count in nutrition), **hypertriglyceridaemia**, pancreatitis; **propofol infusion syndrome** (high dose/long — acidosis, rhabdomyolysis, arrhythmias); strict asepsis (change tubing/bottle every 6–12 h); no analgesia |
| **Midazolam** | Benzodiazepine | Accumulates (liver/renal failure, obesity, elderly) → prolonged sedation; **delirium**; respiratory depression; reversal flumazenil |
| **Lorazepam / diazepam** | Benzodiazepines | Seizures, alcohol withdrawal |
| **Dexmedetomidine** | α2 agonist | "Cooperative" sedation, **no respiratory depression**, less delirium; bradycardia, hypotension |
| **Ketamine** | NMDA antagonist, dissociative | Analgesia + sedation, **maintains BP and airway reflexes**, bronchodilator (asthma); emergence hallucinations, ↑ secretions |
| **Morphine** | Opioid | Analgesia; respiratory depression, hypotension (histamine), constipation, nausea, itch; **active metabolite accumulates in renal failure** |
| **Fentanyl** | Opioid (100× morphine) | Cardiostable, fast; chest wall rigidity with fast large doses; accumulates in long infusions |
| **Remifentanil** | Ultra-short opioid | No accumulation; hyperalgesia after stopping |
| **Paracetamol** | Analgesic/antipyretic | Max **4 g/day** (less in liver disease, low weight, alcohol misuse) |
| **NSAIDs** (ibuprofen, diclofenac) | Analgesia | **Avoid in AKI, GI bleeding, asthma (some), hypovolaemia** |
| **Suxamethonium** | **Depolarising** neuromuscular blocker | 1–1.5 mg/kg; onset 45–60 s, lasts 5–10 min; fasciculations, **hyperkalaemia** (avoid in burns/crush/spinal injury > 24–48 h, renal failure with ↑K⁺), **malignant hyperthermia**, bradycardia, prolonged in **pseudocholinesterase deficiency** ("suxamethonium apnoea") |
| **Rocuronium, vecuronium, atracurium, cisatracurium** | **Non-depolarising** blockers | Rocuronium reversed by **sugammadex**; others by **neostigmine** (+ glycopyrrolate/atropine); atracurium/cisatracurium broken down in plasma (**Hofmann elimination**) — good in liver/renal failure |

## 16.6 Respiratory drugs

- **Salbutamol** (β2 agonist): nebuliser 2.5–5 mg (back-to-back in severe asthma); tremor, tachycardia, **hypokalaemia**, lactic acidosis.
- **Ipratropium** (anticholinergic) 500 mcg nebulised — adds to salbutamol in severe asthma/COPD.
- **Steroids**: prednisolone 40–50 mg oral or hydrocortisone 100 mg IV (asthma, COPD).
- **Magnesium sulphate 2 g IV over 20 min** in severe/life-threatening asthma.
- **Aminophylline**: narrow therapeutic range (theophylline 10–20 mg/L); arrhythmias, seizures, vomiting.
- **Inhaled nitric oxide**: selective pulmonary vasodilator (rescue in ARDS, pulmonary hypertension).

## 16.7 Antimicrobials

- Take **cultures before the first dose** (without delaying antibiotics in sepsis). Give the first dose **on time**; check allergies; review at 48–72 h (de-escalate).
- **Aminoglycosides (gentamicin, amikacin)**: **nephrotoxic and ototoxic** — monitor trough levels, urine output, creatinine, hearing.
- **Vancomycin**: monitor trough levels; infuse slowly (≥ 60 min) — fast infusion causes **"red man syndrome"** (flushing, hypotension); nephrotoxic.
- **Metronidazole**: anaerobes; **no alcohol** (disulfiram-like reaction); metallic taste.
- **Penicillins/cephalosporins**: anaphylaxis risk (ask about allergy; ~1–2% cross-reactivity).
- **Carbapenems** (meropenem): reserved for resistant organisms.
- **Fluoroquinolones** (ciprofloxacin): tendon rupture, QT prolongation.
- **Antifungals** (fluconazole, amphotericin B — nephrotoxic, infusion reactions).
- Antimicrobial stewardship: right drug, dose, duration; prevents resistance (MRSA, ESBL, carbapenem-resistant organisms).

## 16.8 Other ICU drugs

| Drug | Use | Key points |
|---|---|---|
| **PPIs** (omeprazole, pantoprazole), H₂ blockers (ranitidine) | Stress ulcer prophylaxis, GI bleeding | Linked with C. difficile and pneumonia — use only when indicated |
| **Insulin (soluble/regular)** | Hyperglycaemia, DKA, hyperkalaemia | Only soluble insulin IV; check glucose hourly on infusion; ICU target 6–10 mmol/L; hypokalaemia |
| **Potassium chloride** | Hypokalaemia | **Never IV push**; diluted; via pump; ≤ 10 mmol/hr peripherally (≤ 20–40 mmol/hr centrally with ECG monitoring) |
| **Calcium gluconate / chloride** | Hyperkalaemia, hypocalcaemia, CCB/magnesium toxicity | Chloride is 3× stronger and very irritant — central line; not with bicarbonate (precipitates) |
| **Sodium bicarbonate 8.4%** | Severe acidosis with hyperkalaemia, TCA overdose | Hypernatraemia, extravasation injury, ↓ ionised Ca²⁺ |
| **Magnesium sulphate** | Torsades, asthma, pre-eclampsia/eclampsia, hypomagnesaemia | Monitor reflexes, RR, urine output (see chapter 18) |
| **Hydrocortisone** | Septic shock (200 mg/day), adrenal crisis (100 mg stat), anaphylaxis, asthma | Hyperglycaemia, infection risk |
| **Dexamethasone** | Cerebral oedema (tumour), meningitis, airway oedema, fetal lung maturity | |
| **Ondansetron / metoclopramide** | Nausea | Ondansetron — QT; metoclopramide — dystonia, not in bowel obstruction |
| **Lactulose** | Hepatic encephalopathy, constipation | Aim 2–3 soft stools/day |
| **Thiamine (vitamin B1)** | Alcohol misuse, malnutrition | **Give before glucose** to prevent **Wernicke's encephalopathy** (confusion, ataxia, ophthalmoplegia); refeeding syndrome |
| **Oxytocin, misoprostol, ergometrine, carboprost** | Postpartum haemorrhage | See chapter 18 |
