## 17.1 Pre-operative assessment

- **ASA physical status**: I healthy; II mild systemic disease (controlled hypertension, mild asthma); III severe systemic disease (poorly controlled diabetes, COPD, morbid obesity, stable angina); IV severe disease that is a constant threat to life (recent MI, sepsis, end-stage organ failure); V moribund, not expected to survive without surgery; VI brain-dead organ donor. "E" added for emergency.
- **Fasting**: solids/milk **6 h**, breast milk 4 h, **clear fluids 2 h** (1 h in some paediatric policies). Emergencies: aspiration risk → rapid sequence induction.
- **Airway assessment**: **Mallampati** class I–IV (I: soft palate, uvula, pillars visible; IV: only hard palate) — higher class = harder intubation; mouth opening (> 3 fingers), thyromental distance (> 6.5 cm, 3 fingers), neck movement, dentition, previous difficult airway. Difficult bag-mask ventilation predictors: obesity, beard, no teeth, snoring/sleep apnoea, age > 55.
- **Medications**: continue most (beta-blockers, antihypertensives except often ACE inhibitors/ARBs on the morning), adjust **insulin/oral hypoglycaemics** (omit metformin/SGLT2 inhibitors per policy), stop **anticoagulants/antiplatelets** as advised (warfarin ~5 days, clopidogrel 5–7 days), steroids → stress dose.
- **Consent**: informed, voluntary, by a competent adult; before premedication.
- **Checklist**: identity, site marking, allergies, fasting, jewellery/dentures/nail polish removed, pregnancy test (women of child-bearing age), investigations, blood group and save/crossmatch, consent; **WHO Surgical Safety Checklist** — sign in (before induction), time out (before incision), sign out (before leaving theatre).

## 17.2 Types of anaesthesia

- **General anaesthesia**: triad of **hypnosis (unconsciousness), analgesia, muscle relaxation**. Phases: induction (IV — propofol, thiopentone, ketamine, etomidate; or inhalational — sevoflurane in children) → maintenance (volatile: sevoflurane, isoflurane, desflurane; nitrous oxide; or TIVA with propofol) → emergence/reversal.
- **Guedel's stages** (ether): 1 analgesia → 2 excitement/delirium (risk of laryngospasm, vomiting) → 3 surgical anaesthesia → 4 overdose/medullary paralysis.
- **Regional anaesthesia**:
  - **Spinal** (subarachnoid; below L1–L2): fast dense block; **hypotension** (sympathetic block — fluids, vasopressor e.g. ephedrine/phenylephrine), bradycardia, high spinal (breathing difficulty), **post-dural puncture headache** (worse sitting up — fluids, caffeine, analgesia, blood patch), urinary retention.
  - **Epidural** (catheter, slower, used for labour and post-op analgesia): hypotension, catheter migration, epidural haematoma/abscess (back pain + new leg weakness — emergency), check sensory level, pressure areas.
  - Nerve blocks and local infiltration.
- **Local anaesthetic systemic toxicity (LAST)**: perioral numbness, tinnitus, metallic taste, confusion → seizures → arrhythmias/cardiac arrest. Stop injecting, call for help, airway/oxygen, benzodiazepine for seizures, **20% lipid emulsion** (1.5 mL/kg bolus then infusion), prolonged CPR. Maximum doses: **lidocaine 3 mg/kg (7 mg/kg with adrenaline)**; **bupivacaine 2 mg/kg** (most cardiotoxic). Never use adrenaline-containing local anaesthetic on fingers, toes, penis, ears, nose (end arteries — traditional teaching).
- **Conscious (procedural) sedation**: patient keeps protective reflexes and responds to commands; monitor SpO₂, RR, BP, capnography.

## 17.3 Anaesthetic emergencies

- **Malignant hyperthermia**: inherited (autosomal dominant); triggered by **volatile anaesthetics and suxamethonium**. Early signs: **rising EtCO₂ (earliest)**, tachycardia, masseter/jaw rigidity, generalised rigidity, then **hyperthermia** (late), metabolic and respiratory acidosis, **hyperkalaemia**, myoglobinuria, arrhythmias. Treatment: stop triggers, hyperventilate with 100% oxygen, **dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg IV** (repeat to 10 mg/kg; each vial mixed with 60 mL sterile water — needs several staff), active cooling, treat hyperkalaemia and arrhythmias, urine output ≥ 2 mL/kg/hr, ICU. Family testing.
- **Laryngospasm**: stridor/no air movement at light anaesthesia/extubation → remove stimulus, jaw thrust ("Larson's point"), **CPAP with 100% oxygen**, deepen anaesthesia (propofol), small dose of **suxamethonium** if persisting; watch for negative-pressure pulmonary oedema.
- **Bronchospasm**: wheeze, high airway pressures, shark-fin capnography — 100% oxygen, deepen volatile anaesthesia, salbutamol, adrenaline if severe.
- **Aspiration** (Mendelson's syndrome): head-down lateral position, **suction**, 100% oxygen, intubate; may progress to ARDS. Prevent with fasting, RSI with cricoid pressure, antacids/H₂ blockers in obstetrics.
- **Anaphylaxis** under anaesthesia (muscle relaxants, antibiotics, latex, chlorhexidine): hypotension, bronchospasm, rash — adrenaline (IV 50 mcg boluses by anaesthetist), fluids; send mast cell tryptase.
- **Failed intubation**: call for help, maintain oxygenation (bag-mask, supraglottic airway); "can't intubate, can't oxygenate" → **front-of-neck access (cricothyroidotomy)**.
- **Hypothermia**: shivering, ↑ oxygen consumption, coagulopathy, wound infection, delayed drug clearance — prevent with warming blankets, warmed fluids, room temperature.

## 17.4 Post-anaesthesia care unit (PACU / recovery)

- **Immediate priorities — airway, breathing, circulation**: lateral/recovery position until reflexes return, oxygen, monitor SpO₂, RR, HR, BP, temperature, consciousness, pain, nausea, wound/drains, urine output.
- **Discharge from recovery — modified Aldrete score** (activity, respiration, circulation, consciousness, oxygen saturation; 0–2 each, **≥ 9/10** to discharge).
- **Common problems**:
  - **Airway obstruction** (tongue — jaw thrust, airway adjunct).
  - **Hypoventilation/hypoxia** (residual opioids — naloxone; residual paralysis — reversal; atelectasis).
  - **Hypotension** (bleeding — check wounds/drains; spinal; drugs).
  - **PONV** (risk factors: female, non-smoker, history of PONV/motion sickness, post-op opioids — Apfel score) → antiemetics (ondansetron, dexamethasone, cyclizine).
  - **Pain** (multimodal analgesia: paracetamol, NSAIDs if safe, opioids, regional blocks; PCA — only the patient presses the button).
  - **Shivering**, emergence delirium (especially children, ketamine), urinary retention.
- **Post-op complications by time**:
  - **Day 0–1**: bleeding, hypotension, atelectasis (fever first 24–48 h), pain, nausea.
  - **Day 2–5**: pneumonia, urinary tract infection, wound infection (day 3–5), paralytic ileus, DVT.
  - **Day 5–10**: wound dehiscence, anastomotic leak, **PE** (sudden breathlessness, chest pain, tachycardia).
  - **Fever mnemonic ("5 Ws")**: **W**ind (lungs, day 1–2), **W**ater (urine, day 3–5), **W**ound (day 5–7), **W**alking (DVT, day 5+), **W**onder drugs (drug fever, any time).
- **Wound dehiscence/evisceration**: patient supine with knees flexed, cover with **sterile saline-soaked dressings**, do not push organs back, NBM, call the surgeon, monitor for shock.
- **DVT prevention**: early mobilisation, leg exercises, compression stockings/intermittent pneumatic compression, low-molecular-weight heparin, hydration.
- **Incentive spirometry**, deep breathing and coughing (splint the wound with a pillow) prevent atelectasis and pneumonia.
