## 18.1 Physiological changes in pregnancy (why pregnant patients are different)

- **Cardiovascular**: blood volume ↑ 40–50%, cardiac output ↑ 30–50%, HR ↑ 10–20/min, BP falls in the 2nd trimester, **physiological (dilutional) anaemia**, hypercoagulable (↑ DVT/PE risk).
- **Aortocaval compression** after ~20 weeks when supine → hypotension and fetal distress → **left lateral tilt (15–30°) or manual uterine displacement**.
- **Respiratory**: ↑ oxygen consumption, ↓ FRC → **rapid desaturation**, compensated **respiratory alkalosis** (normal PaCO₂ ~30–32 mmHg), airway oedema (difficult intubation — smaller ETT).
- **GI**: delayed gastric emptying, relaxed lower oesophageal sphincter → **aspiration risk** (rapid sequence induction).
- **Renal**: GFR ↑ 50% (lower normal creatinine).
- **Cardiac arrest in pregnancy**: CPR with manual left uterine displacement, hands slightly higher on the sternum; **perimortem caesarean (resuscitative hysterotomy) within 4–5 minutes** of arrest if ≥ 20 weeks and no ROSC.

## 18.2 Hypertensive disorders, pre-eclampsia and eclampsia

- **Pre-eclampsia**: BP ≥ 140/90 after 20 weeks with proteinuria or organ involvement (renal, liver, neurological, haematological, fetal growth restriction). **Severe**: BP ≥ 160/110, severe headache, visual disturbance, epigastric/RUQ pain, clonus, low platelets, raised liver enzymes, pulmonary oedema, oliguria.
- **HELLP syndrome**: **H**aemolysis, **E**levated **L**iver enzymes, **L**ow **P**latelets.
- **Eclampsia**: seizures in pre-eclampsia (can be postpartum, up to 48 h or more).
- **Management**:
  - **Antihypertensives**: labetalol (oral/IV), nifedipine (oral), hydralazine (IV); target < 150/100 (135/85 in some guidelines).
  - **Magnesium sulphate** to prevent/treat seizures — **Zuspan regimen**: 4 g IV over 5–15 min, then **1 g/hr** for 24 h. **Pritchard regimen** (common in Nigeria): 4 g IV + 10 g IM (5 g each buttock), then 5 g IM every 4 h.
  - **Magnesium monitoring** (hourly): **patellar reflex present, RR ≥ 12–16/min, urine output ≥ 25–30 mL/hr**, SpO₂, consciousness. **Toxicity**: loss of reflexes → respiratory depression → cardiac arrest — stop infusion, **10% calcium gluconate 10 mL IV over 10 min**.
  - Strict fluid balance (risk of pulmonary oedema — restrict ~80 mL/hr), delivery is the definitive treatment, steroids for fetal lungs if preterm.
- **Eclamptic seizure**: call for help, left lateral position, airway and oxygen, prevent injury, magnesium sulphate (not diazepam/phenytoin first line), control BP, plan delivery once stable.

## 18.3 Obstetric haemorrhage

- **Antepartum haemorrhage** (after 24 weeks):
  - **Placenta praevia** — painless bright-red bleeding, soft non-tender uterus; **no vaginal examination**.
  - **Placental abruption** — painful, tense "woody" uterus, may be concealed, fetal distress, DIC risk.
  - **Uterine rupture** — previous caesarean scar, sudden severe pain, bleeding, loss of contractions, fetal bradycardia, shock.
- **Postpartum haemorrhage (PPH)**: blood loss **≥ 500 mL after vaginal birth or ≥ 1000 mL after caesarean** (or any loss causing instability); primary within 24 h, secondary 24 h–12 weeks.
  - **Causes — 4 Ts**: **T**one (uterine atony — most common ~70%), **T**rauma (tears, rupture, inversion), **T**issue (retained placenta/clots), **T**hrombin (coagulopathy).
  - **Management**: call for help, ABCDE, two large-bore cannulas, crossmatch, **fundal massage (rub up a contraction)**, empty the bladder (catheter), **oxytocin** 5–10 units IV/IM then infusion, **ergometrine** 0.5 mg (avoid in hypertension/pre-eclampsia), **misoprostol** 800 mcg, **carboprost** 250 mcg IM every 15 min (avoid in asthma), **tranexamic acid 1 g IV within 3 h of birth**, bimanual compression, balloon tamponade, uterine artery ligation/B-Lynch suture/hysterectomy; blood products; monitor vitals, fundal height and urine output; prevent hypothermia.
- **Amniotic fluid embolism**: sudden collapse, hypoxia, hypotension, DIC during labour/delivery — supportive resuscitation.
- **Obstetric cholestasis, peripartum cardiomyopathy, sepsis** (major cause of maternal death — use the maternal early warning score).

## 18.4 Neonatal essentials

- **APGAR** at 1 and 5 minutes (each 0–2): **A**ppearance (colour), **P**ulse (> 100 = 2), **G**rimace (reflex irritability), **A**ctivity (tone), **R**espiration (good cry = 2). 7–10 normal, 4–6 moderate, 0–3 severe.
- **Newborn resuscitation**: dry and stimulate, keep warm (hat, plastic bag for preterm), open airway (neutral position), **5 inflation breaths** (30 cmH₂O term, 2–3 s each), reassess HR; if HR < 60 after effective ventilation → **chest compressions 3:1** (two-thumb encircling, lower third of the sternum); adrenaline 10–30 mcg/kg IV (umbilical venous catheter). Start resuscitation in **air** for term babies; target pre-ductal SpO₂ rising to ~90% by 10 min.
- **Thermoregulation**: neonates lose heat fast (large surface area, little fat) → **brown fat** metabolism → hypoglycaemia and acidosis; cold stress → keep 36.5–37.5 °C, skin-to-skin (kangaroo care).
- **Neonatal respiratory distress**: grunting, nasal flaring, recession, tachypnoea > 60, cyanosis. **Surfactant deficiency** in preterm babies → surfactant via ETT, CPAP. **Transient tachypnoea of the newborn** (caesarean births). **Meconium aspiration**.
- **Neonatal jaundice**: physiological appears after 24 h; **jaundice within 24 h is pathological** (haemolysis — ABO/Rh incompatibility, G6PD deficiency — common in Nigeria) → phototherapy (cover eyes and genitals, turn, fluids, temperature) or exchange transfusion; risk of **kernicterus**.
- **Neonatal hypoglycaemia** (< 2.6 mmol/L): jitteriness, poor feeding, apnoea, seizures — feed, 10% dextrose 2 mL/kg IV.
- **Neonatal sepsis**: temperature instability, poor feeding, lethargy, apnoea, jaundice — cultures and antibiotics (ampicillin + gentamicin).
- **Neonatal tetanus**: poor sucking on day 3–14 (unclean cord care), spasms, trismus — preventable by maternal tetanus immunisation and clean delivery.

## 18.5 Paediatric critical care

- **Children are not small adults**:
  - Airway: large head and occiput (neutral position in infants — towel under shoulders), large tongue, high anterior larynx, **narrowest point at the cricoid** (traditional), short trachea (ETT easily displaced), obligate nose breathers (infants).
  - Breathing: diaphragmatic breathing, compliant chest wall (recession), fewer alveoli, tire quickly.
  - Circulation: cardiac output depends on **heart rate** — **bradycardia is a pre-arrest sign** (usually from hypoxia); **hypotension is a LATE sign** (compensate until 25–30% volume loss) — watch HR, capillary refill, mental state, urine output.
  - Higher metabolic rate, glucose needs; larger surface area → heat loss; immature liver/kidneys (dose by weight).
- **Paediatric assessment triangle**: appearance (tone, interaction, consolability, look/gaze, speech/cry — "TICLS"), work of breathing, circulation to the skin.
- **Dehydration (WHO/IMCI)**:

| Degree | Signs | Treatment |
|---|---|---|
| No dehydration | Well, alert | Plan A: extra fluids, ORS, zinc at home |
| Some (5–10%) | Restless/irritable, **sunken eyes, drinks eagerly/thirsty, skin pinch goes back slowly** | Plan B: **ORS 75 mL/kg over 4 h** |
| Severe (> 10%) | Lethargic/unconscious, sunken eyes, **drinks poorly/unable**, skin pinch goes back very slowly (> 2 s) | Plan C: **IV Ringer's lactate 100 mL/kg** — infants (< 12 months) 30 mL/kg in 1 h then 70 mL/kg in 5 h; older children 30 mL/kg in 30 min then 70 mL/kg in 2½ h |

- **Severe acute malnutrition**: different rules — avoid rapid IV fluids (heart failure), use ReSoMal, treat hypoglycaemia, hypothermia, infection; F-75 then F-100 feeds; watch **refeeding syndrome** (↓ phosphate, K⁺, Mg²⁺).
- **Paediatric shock**: 10–20 mL/kg isotonic bolus (10 mL/kg in neonates, malnutrition, heart disease), reassess after each; inotropes (adrenaline) if fluid-refractory; IO access if IV fails within ~90 s.
- **Febrile convulsions** (6 months–5/6 years): with fever, usually viral; protect, recovery position, time it; benzodiazepine if > 5 min; treat fever for comfort (does not prevent recurrence); reassure parents.
- **Croup** (parainfluenza): barking cough, stridor — keep calm (don't examine the throat), dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg, nebulised adrenaline if severe. **Epiglottitis** (Hib): drooling, tripod position, toxic, muffled voice — **do not examine the throat or upset the child**; anaesthetist/ENT secure airway.
- **Bronchiolitis** (RSV, < 1 year): supportive — oxygen, suction, fluids/NG feeds, CPAP.
- **Severe malaria** (common in Nigeria): altered consciousness, convulsions, severe anaemia, respiratory distress (acidosis), hypoglycaemia, prostration, jaundice, haemoglobinuria — **IV/IM artesunate** (2.4 mg/kg at 0, 12, 24 h then daily; 3 mg/kg if < 20 kg), check glucose, treat seizures, transfuse if Hb < 5 g/dL (or < 7 with distress).
- **Sickle cell crisis**: vaso-occlusive pain (analgesia — opioids, hydration, oxygen if hypoxic, warmth), **acute chest syndrome** (chest pain, fever, new infiltrate, hypoxia — oxygen, antibiotics, transfusion), splenic sequestration (sudden anaemia, big spleen, shock), aplastic crisis (parvovirus), stroke; avoid cold, dehydration, hypoxia.
- **Paediatric surgical emergencies**: **pyloric stenosis** (3–6 weeks, projectile non-bilious vomiting, olive-shaped mass, **hypochloraemic hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis** — correct fluids/electrolytes before pyloromyotomy); **intussusception** (6–18 months, colicky pain with drawing up legs, **"redcurrant jelly" stools**, sausage-shaped mass); **tracheo-oesophageal fistula/oesophageal atresia** (frothing, choking/cyanosis with feeds — keep head up, continuous pouch suction (Replogle tube), NBM); **Hirschsprung's disease** (delayed meconium > 48 h); **gastroschisis/exomphalos** (cover bowel with cling film, keep warm, NG tube, fluids).
