## 19.1 Infection prevention and control (IPC)

- **Chain of infection**: infectious agent → reservoir → portal of exit → mode of transmission → portal of entry → susceptible host. Break any link.
- **Hand hygiene — WHO 5 moments**: (1) before touching a patient, (2) **before a clean/aseptic procedure**, (3) **after body fluid exposure risk**, (4) after touching a patient, (5) after touching patient surroundings. Alcohol rub 20–30 s; soap and water 40–60 s (visibly soiled hands, **C. difficile** and norovirus — alcohol does not kill spores).
- **Standard precautions** for everyone: hand hygiene, PPE by risk, respiratory hygiene, safe sharps (no recapping, sharps bin ¾ full), safe injection practice, environmental cleaning, waste segregation, linen handling, patient placement.
- **Transmission-based precautions**:

| Type | Examples | Precautions |
|---|---|---|
| **Contact** | MRSA, VRE, CRE, C. difficile, scabies, wound infections | Gloves and apron/gown, dedicated equipment, single room/cohort |
| **Droplet** (> 5 µm, ~1–2 m) | Influenza, meningococcal meningitis (first 24 h of antibiotics), pertussis, diphtheria, mumps, rubella | **Surgical mask** within 1–2 m, single room/cohort |
| **Airborne** (< 5 µm, travel far) | **TB**, measles, chickenpox, disseminated zoster, aerosol-generating procedures in COVID-19 | **N95/FFP2–3 respirator** (fit-tested), **negative-pressure room**, door closed |

- **PPE order** — donning: gown → mask/respirator → goggles/face shield → gloves. **Doffing** (most contaminated first): gloves → goggles → gown → mask (outside the room), hand hygiene between steps.
- **Lassa fever and viral haemorrhagic fevers** (endemic in Nigeria): strict isolation, full PPE, barrier nursing, safe waste/body handling, contact tracing; ribavirin early.
- **Device-associated infections and bundles**: VAP (chapter 11), **CLABSI** (chapter 12), **CAUTI** (aseptic insertion, closed drainage, bag below the bladder and off the floor, daily review/remove early, meatal hygiene), surgical site infection (pre-op shower, clip not shave, antibiotic prophylaxis within 60 min before incision, normothermia, glucose control).
- **Waste segregation (Nigeria/WHO colour code)**: black — general non-infectious; **yellow** — infectious/clinical; red — highly infectious/anatomical; **sharps — puncture-proof container**; brown — chemical/pharmaceutical.
- **Needlestick injury**: encourage bleeding, wash with soap and running water (don't scrub/suck), report immediately, risk assessment, **post-exposure prophylaxis for HIV within 72 h (ideally within 1–2 h)**, hepatitis B vaccine/immunoglobulin, baseline and follow-up testing.
- **Sterilisation** (kills all organisms including spores — autoclave **121 °C for 15–30 min** or 134 °C for 3–10 min, ethylene oxide, dry heat) vs **disinfection** (kills most organisms, not all spores — chlorine 0.5% for spills, glutaraldehyde, alcohol) vs **cleaning** (removes dirt — always first).

## 19.2 Nutrition in critical illness

- **Malnutrition** worsens healing, immunity, weaning and survival. Screen on admission (e.g. NUTRIC, MUST); weight, BMI, albumin (also falls with inflammation), intake.
- **Energy and protein**: 20–25 kcal/kg/day in the first days (avoid overfeeding), rising to 25–30; **protein 1.2–2.0 g/kg/day** (burns and trauma higher); fluid ~30 mL/kg/day.
- **Enteral first** ("if the gut works, use it") — start **within 24–48 h** once haemodynamically stable. Benefits: maintains gut barrier, fewer infections, cheaper.
- **NG tube placement check**: **aspirate pH ≤ 5.5** (pH paper) and/or **chest X-ray** (gold standard, especially in ventilated patients); measure NEX (nose–earlobe–xiphisternum); **never use the "whoosh" test** (auscultation) or water bubbling. Recheck before each use and after vomiting/coughing.
- **Enteral feeding care**: head of bed **30–45°** during and 30–60 min after feeds, check gastric residual volumes per local policy (usually hold if > 250–500 mL), flush tubes with 30 mL water before/after feeds and medications (don't mix medications with feed; give separately), watch for diarrhoea (consider C. difficile, osmotic medicines), aspiration, tube blockage, refeeding syndrome.
- **Parenteral nutrition (TPN)**: only when enteral is impossible/inadequate; **central line, dedicated lumen**, strict asepsis; change bag and giving set every 24 h; monitor **blood glucose** (hyperglycaemia), electrolytes, liver function, triglycerides, fluid balance; don't stop abruptly (rebound hypoglycaemia — use 10% dextrose); risks: line sepsis, hyperglycaemia, fatty liver.
- **Refeeding syndrome** (starved, alcoholic, anorexic, long NBM patients): start feeds slowly (10–20 kcal/kg/day), give **thiamine before feeding**, monitor and replace **phosphate (main problem), potassium, magnesium**; risk of heart failure, arrhythmias, respiratory failure, confusion.
- Special diets: renal (low K⁺, phosphate, salt, fluid; protein adjusted — higher on dialysis), liver (adequate protein, late-evening snack, low salt for ascites), COPD (avoid excess carbohydrate → ↑CO₂), diabetes (carbohydrate control), burns (high protein/calories).
- **Vitamins**: A (vision, epithelium), D (calcium, bone — rickets), E (antioxidant), **K (clotting)**, B1 thiamine (**Wernicke's**, beriberi), B3 niacin (pellagra: dermatitis, diarrhoea, dementia), B9 folate and B12 (megaloblastic anaemia; folate prevents neural tube defects), C (collagen, wound healing — scurvy). Minerals: iron (Hb), zinc (healing, immunity, diarrhoea treatment), iodine (thyroid).

## 19.3 Ethics in critical care

| Principle | Meaning | ICU example |
|---|---|---|
| **Autonomy** | Respect for the competent patient's own decisions | Informed consent; right to refuse treatment; advance directives |
| **Beneficence** | Do good | Act in the patient's best interest |
| **Non-maleficence** | Do no harm | Avoid harmful or futile treatment; safe practice |
| **Justice** | Fairness | Fair allocation of scarce ICU beds and resources |
| **Veracity** | Truthfulness | Honest communication with patient/family |
| **Fidelity** | Keeping promises, loyalty | Keeping commitments |
| **Confidentiality** | Protecting private information | Share only with those who need to know (or legal requirement) |
| **Accountability** | Answerable for one's actions | Documentation, scope of practice |
| **Advocacy** | Speaking up for the patient | Raising concerns for a patient who cannot speak |

- **Informed consent** needs: capacity (understand, retain, weigh, communicate), **adequate information** (nature, benefits, risks, alternatives), **voluntariness** (no coercion), documentation. In an emergency with an unconscious patient, treat under the **doctrine of necessity** (implied consent) in the patient's best interest. Minors (< 18 in Nigeria) — parent/guardian.
- **End-of-life care**: DNAR decisions documented by the senior doctor after discussion; withholding and withdrawing treatment are ethically equivalent; comfort care (analgesia, sedation, mouth care, family presence, spiritual/cultural needs); **double effect** — giving opioids to relieve suffering is ethical even if it may hasten death (intent is relief, not death).
- **Brain death (death by neurological criteria)**: known cause, coma, no confounders (sedatives, hypothermia, metabolic), absent brainstem reflexes and **positive apnoea test** (no breathing with PaCO₂ > 60 mmHg) — by two senior doctors; organ donation discussion.
- **Ethical dilemmas**: futility, resource allocation (triage), family disagreement, confidentiality vs safety, conscientious objection. Use ethics committees.

## 19.4 Legal aspects (Nigeria)

- **Nursing and Midwifery Council of Nigeria (NMCN)**: established by the **Nursing and Midwifery (Registration, etc.) Act (Cap N143, LFN 2004)**; regulates education, registration and licensing (renewable licence), sets standards, and enforces discipline (Nurses and Midwives Investigating Panel/Tribunal). Code of Professional Conduct.
- **Negligence** — four elements: **duty of care**, **breach** of that duty (falling below the standard of a reasonably competent nurse), **causation**, and **damage/harm**. **Malpractice** = professional negligence.
- **Assault** (threat of touch) and **battery** (touching without consent — e.g. treatment without consent); **false imprisonment** (unjustified restraint); **defamation** (libel written, slander spoken).
- **Vicarious liability**: the employer is liable for the acts of employees within the course of employment (the nurse remains personally accountable).
- **Documentation**: legal record — contemporaneous, accurate, legible, dated, timed and signed; no erasing (single line, initial); **"if it is not documented, it was not done"**; avoid abbreviations that cause errors.
- **Incident reporting**: report errors, near misses and adverse events promptly (just culture, not blame).
- **Restraints**: last resort, least restrictive, per policy with documentation and frequent review.
- **Delegation**: the nurse delegates tasks within the delegatee's competence and remains accountable.
- **Coroner's cases**: unexpected deaths, deaths within 24 h of admission/surgery, trauma, poisoning, suspicious deaths — do not remove lines/tubes before instruction.

## 19.5 Research methodology (key points)

- **Research process**: identify the problem → literature review → theoretical framework → objectives/hypotheses → design → population and sampling → data collection → analysis → interpretation and conclusion → dissemination.
- **Hypotheses**: **null (H₀)** — no relationship/difference; **alternative (H₁)**. **Type I error** = rejecting a true null hypothesis (false positive, α, usually 0.05); **Type II error** = accepting a false null (false negative, β). **Power** = 1 − β (aim ≥ 80%). **p < 0.05** = statistically significant.
- **Variables**: independent (cause/manipulated), dependent (effect/outcome), extraneous/confounding.
- **Designs**: **quantitative** — experimental (**RCT**: randomisation, control, manipulation — highest single-study evidence), quasi-experimental (no randomisation), descriptive, correlational, cross-sectional, cohort (prospective), case–control (retrospective). **Qualitative** — phenomenology (lived experience), grounded theory (develops theory), ethnography (culture), case study. **Mixed methods**.
- **Hierarchy of evidence**: systematic review/meta-analysis of RCTs → RCT → cohort → case–control → case series/reports → expert opinion.
- **Sampling**: probability (simple random, systematic, stratified, cluster — allow generalisation) vs non-probability (convenience, purposive, quota, snowball).
- **Measurement scales**: **nominal** (categories — sex, blood group), **ordinal** (ranked — pain mild/moderate/severe, Likert), **interval** (equal intervals, no true zero — temperature °C), **ratio** (true zero — weight, height, BP).
- **Statistics**: mean (average), median (middle), mode (most frequent), range, standard deviation; chi-square (categorical), t-test (2 means), ANOVA (> 2 means), Pearson/Spearman correlation.
- **Validity** (measures what it should — content, construct, criterion; internal and external validity) and **reliability** (consistency — test–retest, inter-rater, Cronbach's alpha ≥ 0.7).
- **Research ethics**: informed consent, voluntary participation and right to withdraw, confidentiality/anonymity, beneficence/no harm, justice, **ethical committee approval**; landmark codes: Nuremberg Code, Declaration of Helsinki, Belmont Report.
- **Evidence-based practice**: best research evidence + clinical expertise + patient values. **PICO** question: Population, Intervention, Comparison, Outcome.

## 19.6 Management and leadership

- **Management functions** (POSDCORB/Fayol): planning, organising, staffing, directing/leading, coordinating, reporting, budgeting, **controlling**. Planning always comes first.
- **Leadership styles**: **autocratic** (best in emergencies — cardiac arrest, mass casualty), **democratic/participative** (routine planning, morale), **laissez-faire** (experienced self-directed teams only), **transformational** (inspires vision and change), transactional (rewards/punishments), situational (style fits the situation and staff readiness).
- **Motivation theories**: Maslow's hierarchy (physiological → safety → love/belonging → esteem → self-actualisation — also used to prioritise care: physiological needs/ABC first), Herzberg's two-factor (hygiene factors prevent dissatisfaction; motivators satisfy), McGregor's Theory X/Y.
- **Delegation — five rights**: right task, right circumstance, right person, right direction/communication, right supervision/evaluation. Do not delegate assessment, evaluation or clinical judgement to unqualified staff.
- **Care delivery models**: primary nursing (one nurse accountable for a patient's whole stay — best continuity), team nursing, functional/task nursing, total patient care (common in ICU — 1:1 nurse-patient for ventilated patients; 1:2 for high dependency).
- **Communication — SBAR**: Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation (handover, calling a doctor).
- **Conflict resolution**: collaborating (win–win — best), compromising, accommodating, avoiding, competing.
- **Change theory (Lewin)**: unfreeze → change (move) → refreeze.
- **Quality improvement**: PDSA (Plan–Do–Study–Act), clinical audit, root cause analysis (after sentinel events), fishbone diagram, Donabedian model (structure–process–outcome), KPIs (VAP rate, CLABSI rate, pressure injuries, unplanned extubations, mortality).
- **Budgeting**: operating (daily running costs), capital (equipment > set value, e.g. ventilators), personnel (staff).
- **Staffing the ICU**: based on patient acuity/dependency, not just bed numbers; skill mix; continuing education and competency checks (e.g. a new nurse who can't operate a ventilator → supervised training and competency assessment, not punishment).

## 19.7 The nursing process

- **ADPIE**: **A**ssessment (collect subjective and objective data) → nursing **D**iagnosis (NANDA-I: problem related to cause as evidenced by signs — e.g. "Ineffective airway clearance related to thick secretions as evidenced by coarse crackles and SpO₂ 88%") → **P**lanning (prioritise; **SMART** goals — Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Realistic, Time-bound) → **I**mplementation (interventions with rationale) → **E**valuation (goal met/partly/not met → revise).
- **Prioritisation**: ABC (airway first), Maslow (physiological before psychological), actual before potential problems, acute before chronic, unstable before stable.
- **Data types**: subjective (what the patient says — "I have a headache 7/10") and objective (what you observe/measure — BP, wound, lab values). Primary source = the patient.
- **Nursing theorists** (common questions): **Florence Nightingale** (environment), **Virginia Henderson** (14 basic needs, independence), **Dorothea Orem** (self-care deficit — wholly compensatory, partly compensatory, supportive-educative; ICU patients often wholly compensatory), **Callista Roy** (adaptation — physiological, self-concept, role function, interdependence modes), **Hildegard Peplau** (interpersonal relations — orientation, identification, exploitation, resolution), **Jean Watson** (caring), **Imogene King** (goal attainment), **Betty Neuman** (systems model, stressors), **Patricia Benner** (novice to expert: novice → advanced beginner → competent → proficient → expert), **Madeleine Leininger** (transcultural nursing).
