## 20.1 Mnemonics

| Mnemonic | Stands for |
|---|---|
| **ROME** | Respiratory Opposite, Metabolic Equal (pH vs PaCO₂/HCO₃⁻) |
| **MUDPILES** | High anion gap acidosis: Methanol, Uraemia, DKA, Paracetamol/paraldehyde, Iron/isoniazid, Lactic acid, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates |
| **HARDASS** | Normal anion gap acidosis: Hyperalimentation, Acetazolamide, Renal tubular acidosis, Diarrhoea, Addison's, pancreatic fistula, Saline |
| **DOPE** | Ventilated patient deteriorates: Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment |
| **4 Hs and 4 Ts** | Reversible arrest causes: Hypoxia, Hypovolaemia, Hypo/hyperkalaemia (metabolic), Hypothermia; Tension pneumothorax, Tamponade, Toxins, Thrombosis |
| **MONA** | ACS: Morphine, Oxygen (if SpO₂ < 90–94%), Nitrates, Aspirin |
| **AEIOU** | Urgent dialysis: Acidosis, Electrolytes (K⁺), Intoxication, Overload, Uraemia |
| **A WET BED** | Kidney functions: Acid–base, Water, Electrolytes, Toxins, BP, Erythropoietin, vitamin D |
| **SLUDGE / DUMBELS** | Cholinergic (organophosphate) poisoning |
| **CADET face Right** | Right shift of the O₂ curve: CO₂, Acid, 2,3-DPG, Exercise, Temperature |
| **C3, 4, 5 keeps the diaphragm alive** | Phrenic nerve roots |
| **8 = intubate** | GCS ≤ 8 → protect the airway |
| **Beck's triad** | Tamponade: hypotension, ↑JVP, muffled heart sounds |
| **Cushing's triad** | Raised ICP: hypertension (wide pulse pressure), bradycardia, irregular breathing |
| **6 Ps** | Compartment syndrome/limb ischaemia: Pain, Pressure, Paraesthesia, Pallor, Paralysis, Pulselessness |
| **4 Ts (PPH)** | Tone, Trauma, Tissue, Thrombin |
| **I GET SMASHED** | Pancreatitis causes |
| **5 Ws** | Post-op fever: Wind, Water, Wound, Walking, Wonder drugs |
| **SBAR** | Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation |
| **ADPIE** | Nursing process: Assessment, Diagnosis, Planning, Implementation, Evaluation |
| **AMPLE** | Allergies, Medications, Past history/pregnancy, Last meal, Events |
| **SOAP-ME** | Intubation prep: Suction, Oxygen, Airway, Positioning/Pharmacology, Monitoring, Equipment |
| **All Physicians Take Money** | Heart auscultation: Aortic, Pulmonary, Tricuspid, Mitral |
| **White on right, smoke over fire** | 3-lead ECG: white RA, black LA (above), red LL |
| **"Stones, bones, groans, moans"** | Hypercalcaemia: renal stones, bone pain, abdominal pain, psychiatric/confusion |
| **"Hot as a hare, dry as a bone..."** | Anticholinergic toxidrome |
| **Rule of 15** | Hypoglycaemia: 15 g fast sugar, recheck in 15 minutes |

## 20.2 Top 50 facts to remember

1. Normal pH **7.35–7.45**; PaCO₂ **35–45**; HCO₃⁻ **22–26**; PaO₂ **80–100** mmHg.
2. Normal K⁺ **3.5–5.0**; Na⁺ **135–145**; Ca²⁺ **2.2–2.6** mmol/L; glucose **4–7.8** mmol/L.
3. **MAP ≥ 65** mmHg; **CPP = MAP − ICP**, keep 60–70; normal ICP **5–15** mmHg.
4. **CVP 2–8 mmHg**; **PAWP 6–12 mmHg**; **CI 2.5–4.0**; CO 4–8 L/min.
5. Urine output **≥ 0.5 mL/kg/hr** (child 1, infant 2).
6. **Parkland = 4 mL × kg × %TBSA**, half in the first **8 h from the burn**.
7. Rule of Nines: head 9, arm 9, front trunk 18, back trunk 18, leg 18, perineum 1.
8. Drip rate = **volume × drop factor ÷ minutes**.
9. mcg/kg/min → mL/hr = **dose × kg × 60 ÷ concentration (mcg/mL)**.
10. Ventilator Vt **6–8 mL/kg predicted body weight**; ARDS **6**; **Pplat ≤ 30**; driving pressure ≤ 15.
11. ARDS P/F: **mild ≤ 300, moderate ≤ 200, severe ≤ 100** (PEEP ≥ 5).
12. **RSBI < 105** predicts successful weaning.
13. **High-pressure alarm** = obstruction/secretions/biting/bronchospasm/pneumothorax; **low-pressure alarm** = leak/disconnection.
14. **Peak ↑ + plateau normal = resistance**; **both ↑ = compliance problem**.
15. Increase **FiO₂/PEEP for oxygenation**; increase **rate/Vt to remove CO₂**.
16. **VAP bundle**: HOB 30–45°, sedation hold, oral chlorhexidine, subglottic suction, cuff 20–30 cmH₂O, PUD/DVT prophylaxis.
17. Suction: pre-oxygenate, ≤ **10–15 s**, 80–150 mmHg, catheter ≤ half the tube diameter.
18. ETT confirmation gold standard = **waveform capnography**.
19. Right main bronchus is **wider, shorter, more vertical**.
20. **GCS 3–15**; ≤ 8 intubate; a drop of 2 = urgent.
21. **Cushing's triad** = late sign of raised ICP.
22. Raised ICP care: HOB 30°, neutral neck, normocapnia, avoid hypoxia, mannitol/3% saline.
23. Autonomic dysreflexia: **sit up, check the bladder** first.
24. Neurogenic shock = **hypotension + bradycardia**.
25. Septic shock: **lactate > 2 + vasopressors for MAP ≥ 65**; **noradrenaline first line**.
26. Sepsis hour-1: lactate, **cultures before antibiotics**, antibiotics within 1 h, **30 mL/kg** crystalloid, vasopressors.
27. qSOFA: RR ≥ 22, altered mentation, SBP ≤ 100.
28. Anaphylaxis: **IM adrenaline 0.5 mg (1:1000)** thigh, repeat every 5 min.
29. VF/pVT: shock → CPR → adrenaline 1 mg and amiodarone 300 mg **after the 3rd shock**.
30. Asystole/PEA: adrenaline 1 mg **as soon as possible**, every 3–5 min.
31. CPR: **100–120/min, 5–6 cm, 30:2**; child 15:2.
32. SVT: vagal manoeuvres → **adenosine 6 mg rapid push** → 12 mg → 12 mg.
33. Bradycardia: **atropine 500 mcg**, max 3 mg; pacing.
34. Hyperkalaemia: **calcium gluconate** (protect heart) → **insulin + glucose**, salbutamol → remove K⁺ (dialysis).
35. Hyperkalaemia ECG: **peaked T waves**; hypokalaemia: **U waves**.
36. Digoxin: withhold if apical pulse < 60; toxicity worse with ↓K⁺.
37. **DKA**: fluids first, check K⁺ before insulin, **0.1 unit/kg/hr**, add dextrose when glucose < 14.
38. Hypoglycaemia: oral glucose if awake; IV dextrose or IM glucagon if not; **thiamine before glucose** in alcoholics.
39. SIADH: low Na⁺, concentrated urine → fluid restrict. DI: high Na⁺, dilute urine → desmopressin.
40. Thyroidectomy: watch **airway (haematoma/oedema), hypocalcaemia, voice (recurrent laryngeal nerve)**.
41. Paracetamol overdose: **N-acetylcysteine**; level at 4 h.
42. Opioid overdose: **naloxone** (watch re-sedation). Organophosphate: **atropine until chest dry** + pralidoxime.
43. Magnesium toxicity: **loss of knee jerk** first → **calcium gluconate**.
44. PPH: **≥ 500 mL** vaginal / ≥ 1000 mL caesarean; **Tone** is the commonest cause → fundal massage + oxytocin.
45. Pre-eclampsia: **magnesium sulphate**; BP ≥ 160/110 = severe.
46. Children: **bradycardia and hypotension are late signs**; weight = (age + 4) × 2.
47. Tension pneumothorax: **needle decompression first**, X-ray later; trachea shifts away.
48. Chest drain: bottle **below chest level**, don't clamp routinely, report > 200 mL/hr for 2–4 h.
49. Negligence = **duty, breach, causation, damage**.
50. Hand hygiene **5 moments**; TB = **airborne** (N95, negative pressure); meningococcus = droplet.

## 20.3 Daily ICU nursing checklist (FAST HUGS BID)

| Letter | Check |
|---|---|
| **F**eeding | Enteral started? Target met? |
| **A**nalgesia | Pain controlled (CPOT/score)? |
| **S**edation | RASS target, daily sedation hold |
| **T**hromboprophylaxis | Heparin/LMWH, stockings, IPC |
| **H**ead of bed | 30–45° |
| **U**lcer prophylaxis | Stress ulcer prophylaxis if indicated |
| **G**lucose control | 6–10 mmol/L |
| **S**pontaneous breathing trial | Ready to wean? |
| **B**owel care | Bowels open? Laxatives? |
| **I**ndwelling catheters | Can lines/urinary catheter come out? |
| **D**e-escalation | Antibiotics reviewed? |

> Final tip: in the examination, read the last line of the question first ("the PRIORITY / FIRST / BEST action"), eliminate options that cause harm or delay life-saving care, and apply **ABC → safety → comfort**. Assess before you act, unless the patient is in immediate danger.
