($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$21. A–Z Abbreviations from the Day 1–15 Mocks$s$, false, 21, $s$21. A–Z Abbreviations from the Day 1–15 Mocks$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 21. A–Z Abbreviations from the Day 1–15 Mocks

This volume carries on from Book 1 (Chapters 1–20). Chapter 2 of Book 1 has the basic abbreviations. This chapter adds every **other** abbreviation that came up in the Day 1–15 mock exams, each with a one-line simple explanation.

> Tip: In the exam, if you know what the letters stand for, you can often work out the answer. Read each line as "letters → full words → what it means at the bedside".

## 21.1 A – C

| Abbreviation | Full meaning | Simple explanation |
|---|---|---|
| AAA | Abdominal aortic aneurysm | Ballooned aorta in the belly; rupture = sudden back pain + shock |
| ABCDEF bundle | Assess pain, Both SAT/SBT, Choice of sedation, Delirium, Early mobility, Family | ICU care bundle to reduce delirium and ventilator days |
| ACE (inhibitor) | Angiotensin-converting enzyme | "-pril" drugs; lower BP; side effects: dry cough, high K⁺, angioedema |
| ACV / AC | Assist-control ventilation | Every breath (machine or patient-started) gets the full set breath |
| ADPIE | Assess, Diagnose, Plan, Implement, Evaluate | The 5 steps of the nursing process in order |
| AEIOU | Acidosis, Electrolytes, Intoxication, Overload, Uraemia | Reasons to start urgent dialysis |
| AFE | Amniotic fluid embolism | Sudden collapse, hypoxia and bleeding (DIC) in labour |
| AFLP | Acute fatty liver of pregnancy | 3rd-trimester liver failure: low glucose, jaundice, DIC |
| AIN | Acute interstitial nephritis | Drug allergy in the kidney (rash, fever, eosinophils) |
| ALARA | As low as reasonably achievable | Radiation rule: least dose possible (time, distance, shielding) |
| ALT / AST | Alanine / aspartate aminotransferase | Liver enzymes; high = liver cell damage |
| AMPLE | Allergies, Medications, Past history, Last meal, Events | Quick history in trauma |
| ANOVA | Analysis of variance | Statistics test comparing the means of 3 or more groups |
| APTT | Activated partial thromboplastin time | Clotting test for unfractionated heparin (normal ~25–35 s) |
| ARDS | Acute respiratory distress syndrome | Leaky, stiff lungs; P/F ≤300 with bilateral shadows, not heart failure |
| ASA | American Society of Anesthesiologists (grade) | Fitness for anaesthesia, I (healthy) to VI (brain-dead donor) |
| ATLS | Advanced Trauma Life Support | Trauma system: primary survey ABCDE, then secondary survey |
| ATN | Acute tubular necrosis | Commonest ICU kidney injury (low BP, toxins); muddy brown casts |
| BCMA | Bar-code medication administration | Scanning the patient band and drug to stop drug errors |
| BIS | Bispectral index | Number (0–100) for depth of sedation; 40–60 = general anaesthesia |
| BNP | B-type natriuretic peptide | Hormone from a stretched heart; high in heart failure |
| BPS | Behavioural Pain Scale | Pain score for ventilated patients (face, upper limbs, ventilator), 3–12 |
| BTS | British Thoracic Society | Group that writes chest drain, oxygen and asthma guidelines |
| CABG | Coronary artery bypass graft | Heart bypass surgery |
| CAM-ICU | Confusion Assessment Method for the ICU | Delirium test: sudden change + inattention + (disorganised thinking or altered consciousness) |
| CaO₂ | Arterial oxygen content | (1.34 × Hb × SaO₂) + (0.003 × PaO₂); normal ~20 mL/dL |
| CAUTI | Catheter-associated urinary tract infection | Prevent: only insert if needed, closed system, remove early |
| CD4 | Cluster of differentiation 4 (T-cells) | HIV immune count; below 200 = AIDS, PCP risk |
| CDSS | Clinical decision support system | Computer that gives alerts and advice (e.g. drug interaction warning) |
| CINAHL | Cumulative Index to Nursing and Allied Health Literature | Nursing research database (like PubMed for nurses) |
| CLABSI / CRBSI | Central line-associated / catheter-related bloodstream infection | Infection from a central line; prevent with the insertion and care bundle |
| CMV | Controlled mandatory ventilation / cytomegalovirus | Ventilator mode where the machine does all breaths / a virus (risk in transplants) |
| COHb | Carboxyhaemoglobin | CO poisoning level; SpO₂ is FALSELY normal, so treat with 100% O₂ |
| CPP | Cerebral perfusion pressure | MAP − ICP; keep 60–70 mmHg |
| CPU | Central processing unit | The "brain" of a computer |
| CRASH | (trial) Corticosteroid Randomisation After Significant Head injury | Showed steroids do NOT help head injury (CRASH-2/3: tranexamic acid) |
| CRRT | Continuous renal replacement therapy | Slow 24-hour dialysis for patients with low BP |
| CTG | Cardiotocography | Monitor of fetal heart and contractions |
| CVVH / CVVHD / CVVHDF | Continuous veno-venous haemofiltration / haemodialysis / haemodiafiltration | Types of CRRT (convection / diffusion / both) |

## 21.2 D – I

| Abbreviation | Full meaning | Simple explanation |
|---|---|---|
| D5W / D10W | Dextrose 5% / 10% in water | Sugar drip; D10 for hypoglycaemia in babies (2 mL/kg) |
| DBP / SBP | Diastolic / systolic blood pressure | Bottom / top BP number |
| DMPA | Depot medroxyprogesterone acetate | 3-monthly contraceptive injection |
| DNR / DNACPR | Do not (attempt) resuscitate | No CPR if heart stops; all other care continues |
| DOPE | Displacement, Obstruction, Pneumothorax, Equipment | Causes of sudden desaturation in an intubated patient |
| 2,3-DPG | 2,3-diphosphoglycerate | Red-cell chemical; high → oxygen released more easily (right shift) |
| EBP | Evidence-based practice | Using best research + clinical skill + patient wishes together |
| EF | Ejection fraction | % of blood pumped out each beat; normal 55–70% |
| EHR / EMR | Electronic health / medical record | Computerised patient record |
| EPAP / IPAP | Expiratory / inspiratory positive airway pressure | BiPAP settings: EPAP = PEEP, IPAP = top pressure |
| ERCP | Endoscopic retrograde cholangiopancreatography | Camera into bile duct to remove stones |
| ESR | Erythrocyte sedimentation rate | Non-specific inflammation test |
| EtCO₂ | End-tidal carbon dioxide | CO₂ at end of breath; normal 35–45 mmHg; confirms ETT position |
| EVD | External ventricular drain | Tube in the brain ventricle to drain CSF and measure ICP |
| FALLS / FAST (stroke) | Face, Arm, Speech, Time | Stroke warning signs — act quickly |
| FAST (trauma) | Focused assessment with sonography in trauma | Bedside scan for blood in belly or around heart |
| FeNa | Fractional excretion of sodium | Below 1% = pre-renal (dry); above 2% = ATN |
| FEV₁ / FVC | Forced expiratory volume in 1 s / forced vital capacity | Ratio below 0.7 = obstructive disease (asthma, COPD) |
| FFP / FFP3 | Fresh frozen plasma / filtering face piece class 3 mask | Plasma with clotting factors / high-filtration respirator mask for airborne germs |
| FIFO | First in, first out | Stock rule: use the oldest stock first |
| FITT | Frequency, Intensity, Time, Type | Exercise prescription |
| FRC | Functional residual capacity | Air left in lungs after a normal breath out (~2.4 L); PEEP increases it |
| FTP | File transfer protocol | Way of moving files between computers |
| GABA | Gamma-aminobutyric acid | Calming brain chemical; benzodiazepines and propofol act here |
| GBS | Guillain–Barré syndrome / Group B streptococcus | Ascending paralysis / newborn infection |
| GRV | Gastric residual volume | Feed left in stomach; very large volumes (e.g. >500 mL) → review feeding |
| GTN | Glyceryl trinitrate | Nitrate for chest pain/high BP; side effects: headache, low BP |
| HALT | Hungry, Angry, Lonely, Tired | Self-check for staff stress and errors |
| HCl | Hydrochloric acid | Stomach acid; vomiting it out → metabolic alkalosis |
| HELLP | Haemolysis, Elevated Liver enzymes, Low Platelets | Severe form of pre-eclampsia; deliver the baby |
| HHS | Hyperosmolar hyperglycaemic state | Very high sugar (>33), very dry, no big ketones; type 2 diabetes |
| HIS | Hospital information system | Hospital-wide computer system |
| HIT | Heparin-induced thrombocytopenia | Platelets fall (>50%) 5–10 days after heparin; clots, not bleeding; stop ALL heparin |
| HL7 | Health Level Seven | Standard that lets health computer systems "talk" to each other |
| HME | Heat and moisture exchanger | "Artificial nose" filter on the ventilator circuit |
| HOCM | Hypertrophic obstructive cardiomyopathy | Thick heart muscle; sudden death in young athletes |
| HRS | Hepatorenal syndrome | Kidney failure caused by liver failure |
| HUS | Haemolytic uraemic syndrome | Anaemia + low platelets + kidney failure (often after E. coli diarrhoea) |
| IAH / IAP | Intra-abdominal hypertension / pressure | Bladder pressure ≥12 mmHg = IAH; ≥20 + organ failure = abdominal compartment syndrome |
| ICDSC | Intensive Care Delirium Screening Checklist | 8-item delirium score; 4 or more = delirium |
| ICS | Intercostal space | Space between ribs (e.g. "2nd ICS mid-clavicular line") |
| ICT | Information and communication technology | Computers, phones, networks used in health care |
| IgE / IgG / IgA / IgM | Immunoglobulins (antibodies) | IgE = allergy; IgG = crosses placenta, long memory; IgA = secretions, breast milk; IgM = first response |
| INR | International normalised ratio | Clotting test for warfarin; target usually 2–3 |
| ITP | Immune thrombocytopenic purpura | Body destroys its own platelets |
| IUGR | Intrauterine growth restriction | Baby smaller than expected in the womb |

## 21.3 L – R

| Abbreviation | Full meaning | Simple explanation |
|---|---|---|
| LAN / WAN | Local / wide area network | Computers linked in one building / over a large area |
| LDH | Lactate dehydrogenase | Rises with cell breakdown (haemolysis, HELLP, tumours) |
| LMWH | Low-molecular-weight heparin | Enoxaparin; subcutaneous; check anti-Xa, not APTT |
| LOCM | Low-osmolar contrast medium | X-ray dye that is safer for kidneys |
| LVEDV / LVEDP | Left ventricular end-diastolic volume / pressure | The "preload" of the left heart |
| MAC | Minimum alveolar concentration | Anaesthetic gas strength; 1 MAC stops movement in 50% of patients |
| MBO | Management by objectives | Manager and staff agree goals together and review them |
| MDRO | Multidrug-resistant organism | Germ resistant to many antibiotics (MRSA, VRE, CRE) |
| MEOWS | Modified Early Obstetric Warning Score | Early warning chart for pregnant/postnatal women |
| MH | Malignant hyperthermia | Reaction to suxamethonium/volatile gas: high EtCO₂, rigidity, fever → dantrolene |
| MRC (scale) | Medical Research Council muscle power scale | 0 (no movement) to 5 (normal power) |
| MRSA | Methicillin-resistant Staphylococcus aureus | Resistant germ; contact precautions; vancomycin |
| MUDPILES | Methanol, Uraemia, DKA, Paraldehyde/Propylene glycol, Iron/Isoniazid, Lactate, Ethylene glycol, Salicylates | Causes of HIGH anion gap metabolic acidosis |
| NAC | N-acetylcysteine | Antidote for paracetamol overdose |
| NANDA | North American Nursing Diagnosis Association | List of approved nursing diagnoses |
| NEWS2 | National Early Warning Score 2 | Vital-sign score; 5+ = urgent review, 7+ = emergency |
| NIC / NOC | Nursing Interventions / Outcomes Classification | Standard names for nursing actions / results |
| NKCC2 | Na-K-2Cl co-transporter | Pump in the loop of Henle blocked by furosemide |
| NMS | Neuroleptic malignant syndrome | Antipsychotic reaction: high fever, rigidity, high CK → dantrolene/bromocriptine |
| NYHA | New York Heart Association | Heart failure classes I (no symptoms) to IV (symptoms at rest) |
| ORS | Oral rehydration solution | Salt-sugar drink for diarrhoea |
| PbtO₂ | Brain tissue oxygen tension | Licox probe; normal 20–35 mmHg |
| PCA | Patient-controlled analgesia | Pump the patient presses for a dose of painkiller |
| PCC | Prothrombin complex concentrate | Fast reversal of warfarin (with vitamin K) |
| PCI | Percutaneous coronary intervention | Opening a blocked heart artery with a balloon/stent |
| PCP | Pneumocystis pneumonia | Lung infection when CD4 is below 200; co-trimoxazole |
| PCWP / PAWP | Pulmonary capillary / artery wedge pressure | Reflects left atrial pressure; normal 6–12 mmHg |
| PEP | Post-exposure prophylaxis | HIV drugs after a needlestick — start within 1–2 hours (max 72 h) |
| PES | Problem, Etiology, Signs/Symptoms | Format for writing a nursing diagnosis |
| PF4 | Platelet factor 4 | Antibodies against heparin–PF4 cause HIT |
| PICA | Posterior inferior cerebellar artery | Its stroke causes lateral medullary (Wallenberg) syndrome |
| PSE | Portosystemic encephalopathy | Hepatic encephalopathy (ammonia) — lactulose |
| PTH | Parathyroid hormone | Raises blood calcium |
| PTU | Propylthiouracil | Anti-thyroid drug for thyroid storm (also blocks T4 → T3) |
| RAM | Random access memory | Computer's short-term working memory (lost when switched off) |
| RCA | Root cause analysis / right coronary artery | Finding the system cause of an incident / artery for the inferior wall |
| RCT | Randomised controlled trial | Strongest single study for treatment questions |
| RSBI | Rapid shallow breathing index | Rate ÷ tidal volume (L); below 105 = likely to wean |
| RSI | Rapid sequence induction | Fast intubation for a "full stomach" (pre-oxygenate, drugs together, no bag-mask) |
| RTA | Renal tubular acidosis / road traffic accident | Normal anion gap acidosis / trauma |
| RUMBA | Relevant, Understandable, Measurable, Behavioural, Achievable | Rules for writing nursing goals/outcomes |

## 21.4 S – Z and symbols

| Abbreviation | Full meaning | Simple explanation |
|---|---|---|
| S1Q3T3 | Deep S in lead I, Q and inverted T in lead III | ECG pattern of pulmonary embolism (not always present) |
| SBAR | Situation, Background, Assessment, Recommendation | Safe way to hand over or call a doctor |
| ScvO₂ / SvO₂ | Central / mixed venous oxygen saturation | Oxygen left after tissues use it; ScvO₂ normal >70% |
| SGA | Supraglottic airway / small for gestational age | Laryngeal mask (i-gel, LMA) / baby below 10th centile |
| SGLT2 (inhibitor) | Sodium–glucose co-transporter 2 | "-gliflozin" drugs; can cause euglycaemic DKA |
| SJS / TEN | Stevens–Johnson syndrome / toxic epidermal necrolysis | Drug reaction with skin and mucosa peeling; <10% / >30% body surface; treat like a burn |
| SLUDGE | Salivation, Lacrimation, Urination, Defecation, GI upset, Emesis | Signs of organophosphate poisoning → atropine + pralidoxime |
| SMART | Specific, Measurable, Achievable, Realistic, Time-bound | How to write objectives |
| SOFA | Sequential Organ Failure Assessment | Organ-failure score; rise of 2+ with infection = sepsis |
| SSRI | Selective serotonin reuptake inhibitor | Antidepressant; overdose → serotonin syndrome |
| SWOT | Strengths, Weaknesses, Opportunities, Threats | Planning tool for a unit or project |
| TACO | Transfusion-associated circulatory overload | Too much blood too fast: high BP, high JVP, oedema → slow/stop, diuretic |
| TCA | Tricyclic antidepressant | Overdose: wide QRS, seizures, arrhythmia → sodium bicarbonate |
| TENS | Transcutaneous electrical nerve stimulation | Pain relief by skin electrodes |
| TMP | Transmembrane pressure | CRRT filter pressure; rising = filter clotting |
| TRALI | Transfusion-related acute lung injury | Breathing failure within 6 h of transfusion; low or normal BP; not fluid overload |
| TSH / TRH | Thyroid-stimulating / thyrotropin-releasing hormone | Pituitary / hypothalamus thyroid control hormones |
| TTN | Transient tachypnoea of the newborn | Fast breathing after C-section; settles in 24–72 h |
| TTP | Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura | Low platelets, haemolysis, fever, confusion, kidney failure → plasma exchange |
| TURP | Transurethral resection of prostate | Risk: "TURP syndrome" = low sodium from irrigation fluid |
| UWSD | Underwater seal drain | Chest drain bottle system |
| UVC | Umbilical venous catheter | Emergency IV access in a newborn |
| V4R | Right-sided chest lead V4 | Shows right ventricular infarct (in inferior MI) — avoid nitrates, give fluid |
| VAP | Ventilator-associated pneumonia | Pneumonia 48 h+ after intubation; prevent with the VAP bundle |
| VSD | Ventricular septal defect | Hole between ventricles; loud harsh murmur at left sternal edge |
| Vt / VT | Tidal volume / ventricular tachycardia | Breath size (6–8 mL/kg PBW) / fast wide-complex rhythm |
| % TBSA | Percentage of total body surface area | Burn size (rule of nines, palm = 1%) |

## 21.5 Exam word-traps

- **EXCEPT / NOT / LEAST** → you are looking for the ONE wrong option. Read each option and say "true or false" — the false one is your answer.
- **FIRST / PRIORITY / INITIAL** → think airway → breathing → circulation, and safety before treatment.
- **MOST / BEST** → more than one option may be true; choose the one that helps the patient most, or that a nurse can do right now.
- **ALWAYS / NEVER** in an option → usually wrong, unless it is a safety rule (e.g. "never clamp a bubbling chest drain").
- Numbers: learn the **normal range**; options just outside it are designed to catch you.
