($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$22. ICU Machines and Equipment — Line by Line$s$, false, 22, $s$22. ICU Machines and Equipment — Line by Line$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 22. ICU Machines and Equipment — Line by Line

Day 8 had a whole section on machines. This chapter takes each machine in turn: what it is, how it works in simple words, what to check, and what goes wrong.

## 22.1 The bedside monitor

- **What it shows:** ECG, heart rate, SpO₂, non-invasive BP (NIBP), respiratory rate, temperature, and if connected: arterial BP, CVP, EtCO₂, ICP.
- **ECG leads (3-lead):** RA = **R**ight **A**rm (white), LA = **L**eft **A**rm (black), LL = **L**eft **L**eg (red). Say "**white is right, smoke over fire**" (black above red).
- **5-lead:** adds RL (green, right leg) and V (brown, chest V1 position) → can monitor for ischaemia.
- **Alarms:** set them for each patient (e.g. HR 50–120), never switch them off. A silent alarm is a safety incident.
- **SpO₂ errors:** cold hands, shivering, nail polish, poor perfusion, carbon monoxide (falsely normal), methaemoglobin (reads ~85%), dark skin pigment may over-read slightly. Look at the **pleth wave** — a good wave means a true reading.
- **NIBP cuff:** width = 40% of arm circumference. Too small → falsely HIGH. Too big → falsely LOW.

## 22.2 Infusion pumps and syringe drivers

- **Volumetric pump** = gives mL/hour from a bag (fluids, feeds, big volumes).
- **Syringe pump (driver)** = pushes a syringe slowly; very accurate at low rates (inotropes, sedation, insulin, heparin).
- **Rule for vasoactive drugs:** own dedicated line/lumen, never flush it, never give boluses through it. Use the **"double pumping"** (piggy-back) method to change syringes, so the BP doesn't drop.
- **Siphoning** = syringe empties by gravity if it is placed higher than the patient and the clamp is broken → keep the pump level with the patient and use anti-siphon valves.
- **Occlusion alarm** = something is blocking flow: clamp closed, kinked tube, tissued cannula. Check from the patient back to the pump.
- **"Smart pumps"** have a drug library with dose limits (soft and hard limits) to stop 10× errors.
- Label every line with drug, concentration, date and time.

## 22.3 Defibrillator

![Defibrillator pad placement](keynote/defib.svg)

- **Defibrillation** = unsynchronised shock to stop VF/pulseless VT.
- **Cardioversion** = synchronised shock (**SYNC** on), timed on the R wave, for an unstable rhythm WITH a pulse. Sedate the patient first if awake.
- **Monophasic** = current goes one way (360 J). **Biphasic** = current goes there and back; needs less energy (120–200 J) and is more effective.
- **AED** (automated external defibrillator) reads the rhythm and tells you whether to shock — for anyone to use.
- Shock in **pulseless VT/VF only**. Never shock asystole (it does no good and stops CPR).

## 22.4 Suction machine

- Wall suction pressure for adults: **80–150 mmHg** (≈ 10–20 kPa). Children 80–100, neonates 60–80 mmHg.
- **Catheter size:** no more than **half** the ETT internal diameter (formula: (ETT size − 1) × 2 in French gauge, e.g. ETT 8 → 14 Fr).
- **Each pass ≤10–15 seconds**; suction only while **withdrawing**; pre-oxygenate with 100% O₂.
- **Closed (in-line) suction** keeps PEEP on and lowers infection risk in ventilated patients.
- Do **not** routinely put saline down the tube.
- Watch for: low SpO₂, slow heart rate (vagal), bleeding, raised ICP (head injury — keep it short).

## 22.5 Oxygen devices and humidification

| Device | Flow | FiO₂ (approx.) | Simple point |
|---|---|---|---|
| Nasal cannula | 1–4 L/min | 24–36% | Each 1 L adds ~4% |
| Simple face mask | 5–10 L/min | 40–60% | At least 5 L, so CO₂ is not breathed back |
| Venturi mask | Set by colour | 24, 28, 31, 35, 40, 60% | **Fixed, exact** FiO₂ — best for COPD |
| Non-rebreather (reservoir) mask | 15 L/min | 60–90% | Bag must stay inflated; for emergencies |
| High-flow nasal cannula (HFNC) | up to 60 L/min | 21–100% | Warmed + humidified; gives small PEEP and washes out CO₂ |

- **Humidification:** the nose normally warms and wets air. A tube bypasses the nose → thick secretions, blocked tubes. Use an **HME** (artificial nose) or a **heated humidifier** (never both together).
- **Oxygen cylinder colours** (Nigeria/ISO): oxygen = **white** shoulder (older black body, white top); nitrous oxide = blue; medical air = black/white; carbon dioxide = grey; Entonox = blue and white.
- **Oxygen is a drug**: prescribe a target (94–98%, or **88–92% in COPD with CO₂ retention**).
- Fire risk: no smoking, no oil/grease on valves.

## 22.6 The mechanical ventilator — parts

1. **Gas supply** — oxygen and air from the wall (or a compressor).
2. **Blender** — mixes them to the FiO₂ you set.
3. **Inspiratory limb** — tube that carries gas TO the patient (through the humidifier).
4. **Y-piece** — joins the two limbs at the patient's tube.
5. **Expiratory limb** — tube that carries breath back FROM the patient.
6. **Exhalation valve** — holds PEEP; opens to let breath out.
7. **Sensors** — measure pressure, flow and volume; drive the alarms.
8. **Bacterial/viral filters** — protect the machine and the room.

- **Before use:** do the pre-use check (leak test, compliance test), set alarms, keep a **bag-valve-mask and oxygen at every bedside**.
- **Power failure / machine failure:** disconnect and **hand-ventilate with the bag on 100% O₂**, call for help. "When in doubt, take it out (of the ventilator) and bag."

## 22.7 Chest drain system

![Chest drain three-chamber system](keynote/chest_drain.svg)

- **Swinging** (tidal movement) in the water-seal chamber = the drain is open and working.
- **Bubbling** in the water-seal = air is coming out of the chest (air leak). Expected with a pneumothorax; new heavy bubbling = leak in the tubing or the lung.
- **Never lift the bottle above the chest** — water can siphon back into the chest.
- **Clamp only** briefly to change the bottle, to find a leak, or when the doctor orders it before removal. **Never clamp a bubbling drain** (risk of tension pneumothorax).
- **Removal:** patient holds breath at the end of a deep breath **in** (or does Valsalva), pull out quickly, seal with an occlusive dressing, chest X-ray after.

## 22.8 CRRT machine (continuous dialysis)

![CRRT circuit](keynote/crrt.svg)

- Runs slowly for 24 hours, so blood pressure is kept stable. Used for unstable ICU patients with kidney failure.
- **Access line = red** (blood out of patient). **Return line = blue** (blood back).
- **Alarm checks:** access pressure very negative = catheter kinked or patient moved; return pressure high = clot or kink on the return side; **TMP rising = filter clotting**; air detector alarm = air in the return line (never bypass it).
- **Nursing:** hourly fluid balance, watch potassium, phosphate, magnesium and calcium, warm the patient, care of the dialysis catheter (strict asepsis).

## 22.9 Other ICU equipment you must know

- **Bag-valve-mask (Ambu bag):** use with oxygen at **15 L/min** and the reservoir bag. Adult bag ~1600 mL; give ~500–600 mL (just enough to see the chest rise) over 1 second.
- **Laryngoscope:** check the light works **before** intubation; Macintosh (curved) blade for adults, Miller (straight) for infants.
- **Capnograph (EtCO₂):** a square waveform proves the tube is in the trachea. In CPR, EtCO₂ above 10–20 mmHg = good compressions; a sudden rise = return of circulation (ROSC).
- **Warming devices:** forced-air blankets, warm IV fluids (for blood and big volumes) — prevent the lethal triad of hypothermia, acidosis and coagulopathy.
- **Pressure-relieving mattress** (alternating air) — reduces pressure ulcers, but you must still turn the patient.
- **Intermittent pneumatic compression (IPC) stockings** — prevent DVT when anticoagulants are not allowed.
- **Glucometer** — calibrate/quality-check daily; cleanse the finger with water (sugar on skin gives a false high).
- **Blood gas analyser** — send ABG on ice if it will be delayed more than 15–30 minutes; remove air bubbles (air bubbles make PaO₂ look falsely normal/high and PaCO₂ falsely low).

## 22.10 Equipment safety and maintenance

- **Planned preventive maintenance (PPM)** — servicing on a schedule, by biomedical engineering, with a sticker showing the next date.
- **Daily checks** — defibrillator, suction, emergency trolley, oxygen and bag at every bed. Record and sign.
- **Faulty equipment** — label "DO NOT USE", remove from service, report (incident form), never repair it yourself.
- **Electrical safety** — no extension cables on the floor, no wet hands, report frayed cables. Microshock risk with pacing wires — wear gloves.
- **Cleaning between patients** — follow the Spaulding classification (Chapter 31).
