($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$23. Haemodynamic Monitoring — Made Simple$s$, false, 23, $s$23. Haemodynamic Monitoring — Made Simple$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 23. Haemodynamic Monitoring — Made Simple

Days 9, 12 and 13 had many haemodynamic questions. "Haemo" means blood, "dynamic" means movement: this chapter is about measuring how well blood is moving.

## 23.1 Four words that explain everything

- **Preload** = how full the heart is before it squeezes (stretch). Measured by CVP (right) and PAWP (left). Too low → give fluid. Too high → diuretic.
- **Afterload** = how hard the heart must push to get blood out (resistance). Measured by SVR (body) and PVR (lungs). High → vasodilator. Low (sepsis) → vasopressor.
- **Contractility** = how strongly the heart squeezes. Improved by **inotropes** (dobutamine, adrenaline, milrinone).
- **Heart rate** = how often it squeezes. Cardiac output = **HR × stroke volume**.

> In simple words: the heart is a pump. Preload = water in the tank. Afterload = how narrow the pipe is. Contractility = how strong the pump is.

**Frank–Starling law:** the more the heart muscle is stretched (more filling), the harder it contracts — but only up to a point. An overstretched (failing) heart pumps less.

## 23.2 Arterial line

![Arterial line waveform and square-wave test](keynote/art_line.svg)

- **Why:** beat-by-beat BP (for inotropes, shock, unstable patients) and easy ABG sampling.
- **Usual site:** **radial artery**. Do the **modified Allen test** first (checks the ulnar artery can supply the hand).
- **Set-up:** pressure bag at **300 mmHg** with normal saline (gives ~3 mL/hour to keep it open), transducer, monitor.
- **Levelling:** transducer at the **phlebostatic axis** (4th intercostal space, mid-axillary line = level of right atrium). Too high → reading falsely LOW. Too low → reading falsely HIGH.
- **Zeroing:** open the transducer to air and press "zero" — at the start, every shift, and when readings look wrong.
- **Dangers:** bleeding if disconnected (check connections, keep visible), ischaemia of the hand, infection, accidental drug injection (label it **red "ARTERIAL — no drugs"**).
- **Swinging arterial trace** (BP goes up and down with breathing on the ventilator) = patient is probably **fluid-responsive** (hypovolaemic).

## 23.3 Central venous pressure (CVP)

![CVP waveform](keynote/cvp_wave.svg)

- **CVP** = pressure in the right atrium/superior vena cava. Shows right heart filling.
- **Line sites:** internal jugular, subclavian (lowest infection rate, risk of pneumothorax), femoral (highest infection rate).
- **Insertion:** patient head-down (Trendelenburg) for neck/chest lines so veins fill and **air embolism** is prevented. Chest X-ray after (tip position and pneumothorax).
- **Air embolism** (sudden breathlessness, low BP, "mill-wheel" murmur): clamp the line, put the patient **left side down, head down (Durant's position)**, 100% oxygen.
- **Removing a CVC:** patient flat or slightly head down, ask them to breathe out or hold their breath (Valsalva), pull, press for 5 min, airtight dressing.

## 23.4 Pulmonary artery (Swan–Ganz) catheter

![PA catheter pressures](keynote/pa_cath.svg)

- Measures pressures in the right heart and lungs, **PAWP** (the left heart's filling), cardiac output and SvO₂.
- **PAWP** rises in left ventricular failure and fluid overload; it falls in hypovolaemia.
- Uses less often today (more complications), but still examined.

## 23.5 The numbers (normal values)

| Measure | Normal | Simple meaning |
|---|---|---|
| MAP | 70–105 mmHg (target ≥65) | Average push of blood to the organs |
| CVP / RAP | 2–8 mmHg | Right heart filling |
| PA pressure | 15–25 / 8–15 mmHg (mean 10–20) | Pressure in lung arteries |
| PAWP | 6–12 mmHg | Left heart filling |
| Cardiac output (CO) | 4–8 L/min | Blood pumped each minute |
| Cardiac index (CI) | 2.5–4 L/min/m² | CO adjusted for body size (more exact) |
| Stroke volume (SV) | 60–100 mL | Blood per beat |
| SVR | 800–1200 dyn·s/cm⁵ | Resistance in body arteries |
| PVR | 100–250 dyn·s/cm⁵ | Resistance in lung arteries |
| SvO₂ / ScvO₂ | 60–80% / >70% | Oxygen left in returning blood |
| Lactate | <2 mmol/L | High = tissues short of oxygen |
| DO₂ | ~1000 mL/min | Oxygen delivered to tissues |

## 23.6 Reading the pattern (shock profiles)

![Shock profiles](keynote/shock_table.svg)

- **Low SvO₂** (below 60%) = tissues are taking extra oxygen because delivery is low: low CO, anaemia, low SpO₂, or high demand (fever, shivering, fitting).
- **High SvO₂** (above 80%) = tissues can't use oxygen: **sepsis** (shunting), cyanide poisoning, or wedged PA catheter.
- **Fluid responsiveness:** a patient is fluid-responsive if their stroke volume rises by **≥10–15%** after a fluid bolus or a **passive leg raise** (legs up 45°, trunk flat — acts like a 300 mL "self-transfusion" for 1 minute, and can be undone).
- **Fluid challenge:** 250–500 mL crystalloid over 10–15 minutes, then check BP, HR, CVP, urine and lungs.

## 23.7 Non-invasive and less-invasive monitoring

- **Pulse contour devices** (PiCCO, LiDCO, FloTrac): work out CO from the arterial waveform.
- **Echocardiography (bedside echo):** looks at heart filling, squeeze, fluid around the heart (tamponade).
- **Capillary refill time:** normal <2–3 seconds (press the fingertip for 5 s). Slow refill = poor perfusion.
- **Urine output:** ≥0.5 mL/kg/hour in adults (1 mL/kg/h in children) — "the kidney is the window of perfusion".
- **Mottling** of the knees and **cold peripheries** = poor perfusion; the mottling score is a strong warning sign.

## 23.8 Nursing care for any invasive line — checklist

1. Hand hygiene and aseptic non-touch technique (ANTT) every time you touch it.
2. Check the site every shift: redness, swelling, pus, bleeding (use a **VIP score** for cannulae).
3. Transparent dressing changed every 7 days (gauze: every 2 days) or when wet/loose.
4. Change the transducer set and flush every **96 hours** (or as per policy).
5. Keep a **closed system** — fewer breaks = fewer infections. Scrub the hub with 2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol before access.
6. Review every day: **is this line still needed?** Remove it as soon as possible.
7. Document: insertion date, site, length at skin, waveform and readings.
