($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$24. "Identify the Disease" — Clue to Diagnosis$s$, false, 24, $s$24. "Identify the Disease" — Clue to Diagnosis$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 24. "Identify the Disease" — Clue to Diagnosis

Day 8 asked you to name the disease from a short story. The trick is to spot the **key clue** (the classic sign). Learn the clue → diagnosis lines below.

## 24.1 Cardiovascular

| Key clue in the question | Diagnosis | First action |
|---|---|---|
| Crushing central chest pain >20 min, sweating, ST elevation | **STEMI** (heart attack) | MONA-style care: aspirin 300 mg, oxygen if SpO₂ <94%, GTN (if BP ok), call for PCI/thrombolysis |
| Tearing chest pain going to the back, BP different in each arm, wide mediastinum on X-ray | **Aortic dissection** | Lower BP (labetalol) to SBP 100–120, NO thrombolysis, surgery |
| Low BP + raised JVP + muffled heart sounds (**Beck's triad**), pulsus paradoxus | **Cardiac tamponade** | Pericardiocentesis |
| Pink frothy sputum, crackles both bases, orthopnoea | **Acute pulmonary oedema (left heart failure)** | Sit up, oxygen, GTN, furosemide, CPAP |
| Raised JVP, swollen ankles, enlarged liver | **Right heart failure** | Diuretics, treat the cause |
| Fever + new murmur + splinter haemorrhages, Janeway lesions, Osler's nodes | **Infective endocarditis** | 3 sets of blood cultures before antibiotics |
| Inferior MI (II, III, aVF) + low BP + clear lungs + raised JVP | **Right ventricular infarct** | Give FLUIDS, avoid nitrates and morphine; check V4R |
| Sharp chest pain better leaning forward, saddle ST elevation, rub | **Pericarditis** | NSAID/colchicine |
| Young athlete collapses on exercise, family history | **HOCM** | Avoid dehydration; beta-blocker |
| Sudden pain, pale, pulseless, cold leg (6 Ps) | **Acute limb ischaemia** | Heparin, vascular surgeon |
| Pulsatile belly mass, back pain, shock | **Ruptured AAA** | Permissive hypotension, theatre |
| ST elevation in many leads + low BP after chest trauma | **Cardiac contusion / tamponade** | Echo, monitor |

**The 6 Ps of ischaemia:** Pain, Pallor, Pulselessness, Paraesthesia, Paralysis, Perishingly cold.

## 24.2 Respiratory

| Key clue | Diagnosis | First action |
|---|---|---|
| Sudden breathlessness + chest pain after surgery/long travel, calf swelling, tachycardia | **Pulmonary embolism** | Oxygen, anticoagulate, CTPA; thrombolysis if shocked |
| Trauma, absent breath sounds, trachea pushed AWAY, low BP, raised JVP | **Tension pneumothorax** | Needle decompression NOW (2nd ICS mid-clavicular or 4th–5th ICS mid-axillary) |
| Stony dull percussion, reduced breath sounds, trachea away (big) | **Pleural effusion** | Chest drain/aspiration |
| Dull percussion, bronchial breathing, fever, rusty sputum | **Lobar pneumonia (consolidation)** | Oxygen, cultures, antibiotics within 1 hour if septic |
| Silent chest, can't talk, SpO₂ <92%, normal/rising PaCO₂ | **Life-threatening asthma** | O₂, nebulised salbutamol + ipratropium, IV hydrocortisone, IV magnesium 2 g |
| Barrel chest, pursed lips, CO₂ retention | **COPD** | Controlled O₂ 88–92%, nebulisers, NIV if pH <7.35 |
| Bilateral white-out after sepsis/trauma, P/F <300, normal PAWP | **ARDS** | Lung-protective ventilation 6 mL/kg, PEEP, prone if P/F <150 |
| Fever, night sweats, weight loss, haemoptysis | **Tuberculosis** | Airborne isolation (FFP3/N95), sputum AFB/GeneXpert |
| Crackling under the skin of neck/chest | **Surgical (subcutaneous) emphysema** | Check for pneumothorax / chest drain problem |
| Inspiratory stridor in a child, drooling, sitting forward | **Epiglottitis** | Do not examine the throat, keep calm, senior anaesthetist |
| Barking cough, stridor, mild fever in toddler | **Croup** | Dexamethasone; nebulised adrenaline if severe |
| Fat embolism: long-bone fracture + confusion + petechial rash + hypoxia (24–72 h) | **Fat embolism syndrome** | Oxygen, supportive, early fracture fixation |

## 24.3 Neurological

| Key clue | Diagnosis | First action |
|---|---|---|
| Worst headache of life, "thunderclap", neck stiffness | **Subarachnoid haemorrhage** | CT; nimodipine (prevents vasospasm) |
| Head injury, lucid interval, then drowsy, fixed dilated pupil same side | **Extradural (epidural) haematoma** | Urgent CT and surgery |
| Elderly/alcoholic, slow confusion over days–weeks after a minor fall | **Chronic subdural haematoma** | CT, burr holes |
| Fever, neck stiffness, photophobia, purpuric rash | **Meningococcal meningitis** | IV ceftriaxone at once (don't wait for LP), droplet isolation |
| Ascending weakness from legs, lost reflexes, after a gut/chest infection | **Guillain–Barré syndrome** | Monitor **FVC** (intubate if <15–20 mL/kg), IVIG or plasma exchange |
| Weakness worse at end of day, ptosis, double vision | **Myasthenia gravis** | Pyridostigmine; crisis → ventilate, IVIG/plasma exchange |
| High BP + slow pulse + irregular breathing | **Raised ICP (Cushing triad)** | Head up 30°, call neurosurgeon, mannitol |
| Spinal injury above T6, very high BP, slow pulse, sweating/flushing ABOVE injury | **Autonomic dysreflexia** | Sit up, find the trigger (**full bladder** most common, constipation), loosen clothes |
| Spinal injury, low BP + SLOW pulse, warm skin | **Neurogenic shock** | Fluids, vasopressor, atropine |
| Loss of motor below the injury but feeling of light touch kept | **Anterior cord syndrome** | — |
| Weak arms more than legs (older person, neck extension) | **Central cord syndrome** | — |
| Same-side weakness and opposite pain/temperature loss | **Brown-Séquard syndrome** (half cord) | — |
| Babinski sign (big toe goes UP on stroking the sole) in an adult | **Upper motor neurone lesion** (normal in babies under 1–2 years) | — |
| Sudden face/arm weakness, speech problem | **Stroke** | Time it; CT before any clot-buster; glucose check |
| Seizure lasting >5 minutes | **Status epilepticus** | Lorazepam/diazepam → phenytoin/levetiracetam → anaesthetise |

## 24.4 Abdomen, endocrine and others

| Key clue | Diagnosis |
|---|---|
| Epigastric pain to the back, raised amylase/lipase, Grey-Turner (flank) / Cullen (umbilicus) bruising | **Acute pancreatitis** |
| Pain starts at the umbilicus, moves to right iliac fossa; McBurney's point tenderness | **Appendicitis** |
| Rigid "board-like" abdomen, gas under diaphragm | **Perforated viscus (e.g. peptic ulcer)** |
| Fever + jaundice + RUQ pain (Charcot's triad) | **Ascending cholangitis** |
| Fruity breath, Kussmaul breathing, glucose >11, ketones | **DKA** |
| Very high glucose (>33 mmol/L), very dry, confused, no big ketones | **HHS** |
| Fever >40, fast AF, agitation, after surgery in a thyroid patient | **Thyroid storm** |
| Hypothermia, slow pulse, puffy face, coma | **Myxoedema coma** |
| Low BP not responding to fluids, low Na, high K, low glucose, pigmentation | **Addisonian (adrenal) crisis** → IV hydrocortisone 100 mg |
| Large dilute urine output (>3 L/day) after head injury, high Na | **Diabetes insipidus** → desmopressin |
| Low Na with concentrated urine, normal fluid status | **SIADH** → fluid restriction |
| Tense distended abdomen, rising airway pressure, low urine, bladder pressure ≥20 | **Abdominal compartment syndrome** |
| Confusion, flapping tremor (asterixis), liver disease | **Hepatic encephalopathy** → lactulose |
| Wide skin peeling + mouth/eye ulcers after a new drug | **SJS / TEN** |
| Fever, rigidity, high CK after antipsychotic | **Neuroleptic malignant syndrome** |
| High EtCO₂, rigidity, fast heart, high temp in theatre | **Malignant hyperthermia** → dantrolene 2.5 mg/kg |
| Agitation, clonus, high temp after SSRI/tramadol | **Serotonin syndrome** |
| Low Na and confusion after TURP | **TURP syndrome** |

## 24.5 How to answer "identify the disease" questions

1. Underline the **one sign that is unique** (Beck's triad, lucid interval, fruity breath).
2. Check the **setting** (after surgery → PE; after transfusion → TRALI/TACO; spinal injury → dysreflexia).
3. Look at the **numbers** (low pulse + low BP = neurogenic; high pulse + low BP = most other shocks).
4. Eliminate options that don't fit **all** the clues.
