## 27.1 Anaphylaxis — step by step

1. **Recognise:** sudden airway swelling/stridor, wheeze, low BP, and usually skin signs (hives, flushing). Trigger: drug (antibiotic, muscle relaxant), food, sting, contrast, latex.
2. **Call for help, stop the trigger** (stop the drip, remove the latex).
3. **Adrenaline IM 1:1000 (1 mg/mL)** into the **outer thigh:** adult **0.5 mg (0.5 mL)**; child 6–12 years 0.3 mg; child under 6 years 0.15 mg. **Repeat every 5 minutes** if no better.
4. **Lie flat, legs raised** (sit up if breathing is the main problem; pregnant — left side).
5. **High-flow oxygen 15 L/min**, IV access, **fluid bolus 500–1000 mL** crystalloid (child 10 mL/kg).
6. After stabilising: antihistamine (chlorphenamine) and hydrocortisone are **second-line** (they don't save life in the first minutes).
7. **Refractory** (no better after 2 IM doses): IV adrenaline infusion by an expert.
8. **Mast cell tryptase** blood test: as soon as possible, at 1–2 hours, and at 24 hours.
9. **Observe** for at least 6–12 hours (biphasic reaction). Document the allergy; teach the auto-injector (adrenaline pen).

## 27.2 Electrolyte emergencies — ECG and treatment

| Problem | Signs and ECG | Treatment |
|---|---|---|
| **Hyperkalaemia** (K >6.5 or ECG change) | **Tall tented T**, flat P, wide QRS → sine wave → VF | 1. **Calcium gluconate 10% 30 mL** (protects heart) 2. **Insulin 10 units + glucose 25 g** (shifts K in) 3. Salbutamol nebuliser 4. Remove K: dialysis, calcium resonium, furosemide |
| **Hypokalaemia** (K <3.5) | Weakness, cramps, **U waves**, flat T, ST depression, arrhythmias | KCl IV: max **10 mmol/hr** peripheral (up to 20–40 mmol/hr central, on monitor); never a bolus |
| **Hypercalcaemia** | "Bones, stones, groans, moans", short QT | IV saline, bisphosphonates, calcitonin |
| **Hypocalcaemia** | **Chvostek** (cheek tap → twitch), **Trousseau** (BP cuff → hand spasm), tetany, long QT | Calcium gluconate 10% IV |
| **Hypomagnesaemia** | Tremor, arrhythmias (**torsades de pointes**), low K that won't correct | **Magnesium sulphate 2 g IV** |
| **Hyponatraemia** (Na <135) | Confusion, fits when severe | Severe symptoms: **3% saline 150 mL bolus**. Correct slowly: **max 8–10 mmol/L in 24 h** (too fast → osmotic demyelination) |
| **Hypernatraemia** (Na >145) | Thirst, confusion, fits | Free water slowly (max ~10 mmol/L/day) |
| **Hypophosphataemia** | Weak muscles and diaphragm (failure to wean), confusion — **refeeding syndrome** | Phosphate replacement; feed slowly |

## 27.3 SIADH, diabetes insipidus and cerebral salt wasting

| | **SIADH** | **Diabetes insipidus (cranial)** | **Cerebral salt wasting** |
|---|---|---|---|
| Problem | Too much ADH → water kept | Too little ADH → water lost | Kidneys lose salt and water (after brain injury/SAH) |
| Urine | Small volume, **concentrated** | **Huge volume (>3 L/day or >200 mL/hr)**, dilute (SG <1.005) | Large volume, salty |
| Serum Na | **LOW** | **HIGH** | **LOW** |
| Fluid status | Normal / slightly overloaded | Dehydrated | **Dehydrated** |
| Treatment | **Fluid restriction**, (tolvaptan, 3% saline if severe) | **Desmopressin (DDAVP)**, replace fluid | **Salt and fluid replacement** (NOT restriction), fludrocortisone |

> Easy way: SIADH = "**S**oaking (too wet)". DI = "**D**ry and **I**ncreased urine". CSW = low sodium AND dry.

## 27.4 Diabetic emergencies

| | **DKA** | **HHS** | **Hypoglycaemia** |
|---|---|---|---|
| Who | Type 1 (also SGLT2 drugs — normal sugar DKA) | Type 2, elderly | Insulin/sulphonylurea users |
| Glucose | >11 mmol/L | Usually >30 mmol/L | <4 mmol/L |
| Ketones | >3 mmol/L | Low | — |
| pH / HCO₃⁻ | <7.3 / <15 | Normal-ish (>7.3) | — |
| Osmolality | Raised | **>320 mOsm/kg** | — |
| Key danger | Low K⁺ with insulin, cerebral oedema in children | Clots (DVT), very dry, fits | Brain damage, fits |
| Main treatment | **Fluids first** (0.9% saline 1 L in 1 h), **fixed-rate insulin 0.1 units/kg/hr**, add potassium, add 10% glucose when sugar <14 | Slow fluids (main treatment), low-dose insulin only when sugar stops falling | Awake: 15–20 g fast sugar. Unconscious: **IV 10% glucose 150–200 mL** (or 20% 75–100 mL), or **glucagon 1 mg IM** |

## 27.5 Thyroid and adrenal emergencies

- **Thyroid storm:** very high temperature, fast AF, agitation, diarrhoea, heart failure. Trigger: surgery, infection, stopping drugs. Treat: **propranolol** (heart), **PTU or carbimazole** (stop making hormone), **Lugol's iodine** given **at least 1 hour after** PTU (stops release), **hydrocortisone**, cooling (paracetamol, NOT aspirin — it frees thyroid hormone).
- **Myxoedema coma:** hypothermia, slow pulse, low Na, low glucose, CO₂ retention, coma. Treat: **IV levothyroxine/T3** slowly, **hydrocortisone first**, warm slowly, ventilate.
- **Adrenal (Addisonian) crisis:** shock that does not respond to fluids, vomiting, low Na, high K, low glucose. Treat: **hydrocortisone 100 mg IV at once**, saline, glucose. Patients on long-term steroids need extra ("stress dose") steroid when sick or having surgery.

## 27.6 Liver and gut emergencies

- **Upper GI bleed:** haematemesis (vomited blood, "coffee grounds") or **melaena** (black tarry stool). **Glasgow–Blatchford score** decides urgency. Two big cannulas, cross-match, transfuse to Hb 70–80 g/L, **PPI**, endoscopy within 24 h.
- **Variceal bleed** (liver disease): **terlipressin**, IV antibiotics, endoscopic banding; Sengstaken–Blakemore tube as a bridge (watch airway — keep scissors at the bedside).
- **Acute liver failure:** jaundice + encephalopathy + INR >1.5 in someone without previous liver disease. Paracetamol overdose = commonest cause → **N-acetylcysteine (NAC)**. Watch glucose (low), ICP (cerebral oedema), bleeding, kidneys.
- **Hepatic encephalopathy:** ammonia → confusion, asterixis. **Lactulose** (2–3 soft stools a day), rifaximin, treat the trigger (bleeding, infection, constipation, sedatives).
- **Acute pancreatitis:** severe epigastric pain to the back, amylase/lipase **>3× normal**. Fluids (goal-directed), pain relief, early enteral feeding, watch for low calcium, ARDS and shock.
- **Abdominal compartment syndrome:** measure **bladder pressure** (25 mL saline in the empty bladder, zero at the mid-axillary line at the iliac crest, read at end-expiration, supine). **Normal 5–7 mmHg**; ≥12 = IAH; **>20 + new organ failure = ACS** → decompress (NG, rectal tube, drain, laparotomy).
- **Bowel obstruction:** colicky pain, vomiting, distension, no flatus. "Drip and suck": IV fluids + NG tube on free drainage.

## 27.7 Urological emergencies

- **Acute urinary retention:** painful full bladder, can't pass urine → catheterise; record the residual volume. Watch for **post-obstructive diuresis** afterwards (very high urine output → replace the fluid).
- **TURP syndrome:** irrigation fluid (glycine/water) absorbed during prostate surgery → **low sodium**, confusion, fits, high then low BP, slow pulse. Stop surgery, furosemide, hypertonic saline if severe. Use saline irrigation with bipolar TURP.
- **Testicular torsion:** sudden severe testicular pain in a young male, high-riding testis → **surgery within 6 hours**.
- **Priapism** (erection >4 h, painful) — e.g. in sickle cell disease → aspiration by urologist.
- **Haematuria with clots** → 3-way catheter and continuous bladder irrigation; watch input/output so the bladder doesn't over-fill.
- **Rhabdomyolysis** (crush, prolonged lying, seizures): dark (cola) urine, very high **CK**, high K⁺ → aggressive fluids, urine output **200–300 mL/hr**, treat high K⁺.

## 27.8 Skin emergencies

- **SJS/TEN:** after a new drug (carbamazepine, phenytoin, allopurinol, sulphonamides, nevirapine). Fever, then painful red skin that peels; **positive Nikolsky sign** (skin slides off with gentle rubbing); mouth, eye and genital ulcers. **SJS <10% BSA, overlap 10–30%, TEN >30%.**
  - **Stop the drug**, manage like a burn (warm room, fluids, non-stick dressings, barrier nursing), eye care (ophthalmologist), mouth care, nutrition, pain relief.
- **Pressure ulcer stages:** 1 = red skin that doesn't blanch; 2 = partial thickness (blister/shallow); 3 = full thickness to fat; 4 = muscle/bone visible; plus unstageable and deep tissue injury. Use the **Braden scale** (≤18 = at risk; lower = higher risk).
- **Necrotising fasciitis:** pain out of proportion to what you see, rapid spread, crepitus, sepsis → urgent surgery and antibiotics.

## 27.9 Poisoning — antidotes to know

| Poison | Antidote / key treatment |
|---|---|
| Paracetamol | **N-acetylcysteine (NAC)** |
| Opioids (morphine, heroin, tramadol) | **Naloxone** 0.4 mg IV, repeat (short-acting — re-dose) |
| Benzodiazepines | Flumazenil (rarely — risk of fits) |
| Organophosphates (insecticides) | **Atropine** (until chest is dry) + **pralidoxime**; remove clothes, wash skin |
| Carbon monoxide | **100% oxygen** (hyperbaric if available) |
| Cyanide (house fires) | Hydroxocobalamin |
| Iron | Desferrioxamine |
| Warfarin | Vitamin K, PCC, FFP |
| Heparin | Protamine sulphate |
| Digoxin | Digoxin-specific antibody (Digibind) |
| Beta-blocker | Glucagon, high-dose insulin |
| Calcium channel blocker | Calcium, high-dose insulin |
| Tricyclic antidepressant | **Sodium bicarbonate** (wide QRS) |
| Methanol / ethylene glycol | Fomepizole or ethanol; dialysis |
| Local anaesthetic toxicity | **20% lipid emulsion** |
| Methaemoglobinaemia | Methylene blue |
| Snake bite | Antivenom (polyvalent); immobilise, no tourniquet/cutting |
| Kerosene / paraffin (children) | **Do NOT induce vomiting** (aspiration pneumonitis); oxygen, observe |

- **Activated charcoal** 50 g within 1 hour for many tablets — NOT for alcohols, iron, lithium, acids/alkalis, hydrocarbons, or if the airway is not protected.
- **Corrosives (acid/alkali):** no vomiting, no neutralising, no charcoal; airway check, endoscopy.
