## 29.1 Taking samples correctly

- **Order of draw** (stops additives contaminating the next tube): **blood culture → citrate (light blue, clotting) → plain/serum (red/gold) → heparin (green) → EDTA (purple, FBC) → fluoride (grey, glucose)**.
- **Light blue (citrate)** must be filled to the line (the ratio of blood to citrate matters).
- **Don't take blood from the arm with a drip** — it dilutes the sample (falsely low Hb, high glucose/K depending on the drip).
- **Haemolysed sample** (squeezing, small needle, shaking, delay) → **falsely high potassium**. Repeat it before treating.
- **Blood cultures:** before antibiotics, from **2 different sites**, clean the skin with 2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol and let it dry, fill aerobic bottle first (if using a needle), 8–10 mL per bottle in adults.
- **ABG:** heparinised syringe, no air bubbles, analyse within 10–15 min (or on ice), note FiO₂ and temperature.
- **Label at the bedside** in front of the patient — never pre-label tubes.

## 29.2 Full blood count — what each part means

| Test | Normal (adult) | Low means | High means |
|---|---|---|---|
| Hb | M 130–180, F 115–165 g/L | Anaemia (bleeding, haemolysis, poor production) | Dehydration, polycythaemia |
| WBC | 4–11 × 10⁹/L | Neutropenia (chemo, sepsis) → protective isolation | Infection, steroids, leukaemia |
| Neutrophils | 2–7.5 × 10⁹/L | <0.5 = severe neutropenia | Bacterial infection |
| Lymphocytes | 1.3–4 × 10⁹/L | HIV, steroids | Viral infection, CLL |
| Eosinophils | 0.04–0.4 | — | Allergy, parasites, drug reaction (AIN) |
| Platelets | 150–400 × 10⁹/L | Bleeding risk; <50 for surgery, <20 spontaneous bleeding risk, <10 very high risk | Inflammation, after splenectomy |
| MCV | 80–100 fL | **Microcytic**: iron deficiency, thalassaemia | **Macrocytic**: B12/folate deficiency, alcohol |
| Reticulocytes | 0.5–2.5% | Marrow not producing | Marrow working hard (bleeding, haemolysis) |

## 29.3 Clotting tests

| Test | Normal | Checks | Used to monitor |
|---|---|---|---|
| PT | 11–15 s | Extrinsic pathway (factor VII) | Warfarin, liver function |
| **INR** | 0.8–1.2 (target 2–3 on warfarin; 2.5–3.5 for mechanical valves) | PT made standard | **Warfarin** |
| **APTT** | 25–35 s | Intrinsic pathway | **Unfractionated heparin** (target 1.5–2.5 × normal) |
| Fibrinogen | 2–4 g/L | Clot "glue" | Low in DIC, massive bleeding → cryoprecipitate |
| D-dimer | <0.5 mg/L | Clot breakdown | Rule OUT PE/DVT if low; high in DIC |
| Anti-Xa | — | LMWH effect | Enoxaparin (kidney failure, obesity, pregnancy) |
| Bleeding time | 2–9 min | Platelet function | Aspirin, uraemia |

## 29.4 Bleeding and clotting disorders

- **DIC (disseminated intravascular coagulation):** clotting everywhere uses up the factors → bleeding everywhere (oozing from lines, gums). Causes: sepsis, trauma, AFE, placental abruption, HELLP. Labs: **low platelets, low fibrinogen, long PT/APTT, high D-dimer**. Treat the cause + replace (platelets, FFP, cryoprecipitate).
- **HIT:** platelets fall >50% **5–10 days** after starting heparin; causes **clots**, not bleeding. Stop **all** heparin (including flushes); use argatroban or fondaparinux. 4Ts score.
- **TTP:** Pentad — **fever, low platelets, haemolytic anaemia (schistocytes on film), neurological signs, kidney problems**. **Urgent plasma exchange**; do NOT give platelets.
- **ITP:** immune destruction of platelets; steroids, IVIG.
- **Haemophilia A** = factor VIII deficiency; **B** = factor IX. Long APTT, normal PT. No IM injections.
- **Sickle cell crisis:** painful vaso-occlusive crisis, acute chest syndrome (fever, chest pain, new X-ray shadow, hypoxia — the main cause of death), splenic sequestration (children: big spleen + falling Hb). Treat: **oxygen, warm IV fluids, strong analgesia within 30 min**, antibiotics, exchange transfusion for acute chest syndrome/stroke. Avoid cold, dehydration and hypoxia. Genotypes: **HbAA** normal, **HbAS** trait (carrier), **HbSS** disease, **HbSC** milder disease.

## 29.5 Blood products

| Product | Storage | Use | Key fact |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Packed red cells** | 2–6 °C, up to 35–42 days | Anaemia, bleeding | 1 unit raises Hb ~10 g/L; transfuse within **4 hours** of leaving storage |
| **Platelets** | **20–24 °C with constant agitation**, 5–7 days | Low platelets/bleeding | **Never refrigerate**; give quickly (~30 min); ABO-compatible preferred |
| **FFP** | −25 °C; thawed then used within 24 h (kept at 4 °C) | Clotting factor replacement (DIC, massive bleed, warfarin if no PCC) | 15 mL/kg; ABO-compatible |
| **Cryoprecipitate** | Frozen | **Fibrinogen** replacement (fibrinogen <1.5 g/L) | Also factor VIII and vWF |
| Albumin 4.5% / 20% | Room temperature | Volume, low albumin, paracentesis | Not a "blood" component (no cross-match) |
| PCC (Octaplex/Beriplex) | — | Fast warfarin reversal | With IV vitamin K 5–10 mg |

- **Blood giving set:** has a **170–200 micron filter**. Change it every 12 hours or after 2–4 units. Only **0.9% saline** in the same line (no dextrose — clumping/haemolysis; no Ringer's lactate — the calcium makes it clot).
- **Blood warmer** for rapid/massive transfusion or cold agglutinins (never a microwave or hot water).
- **Irradiated blood** for immunocompromised patients (stops graft-versus-host disease). **CMV-negative** for babies, pregnancy, transplants.
- **Jehovah's Witnesses:** respect informed refusal of blood (an adult with capacity). Use cell salvage if acceptable, erythropoietin, iron, tranexamic acid. For a child, the court can authorise treatment.

## 29.6 Blood groups and compatibility

![Blood group compatibility](keynote/blood_groups.svg)

- ABO antigens are on the red cells; the **antibodies** are in the plasma (group A has anti-B, group B has anti-A, group O has both, AB has none).
- **Rh(D):** Rh-negative mothers with an Rh-positive baby can make anti-D → **haemolytic disease of the newborn** in the next pregnancy → give **anti-D immunoglobulin** within 72 hours of a sensitising event.
- **Group and save** = group + antibody screen (no blood set aside). **Cross-match** = donor blood tested against the patient's blood and reserved.
- **Emergency:** **O-negative** red cells (O-positive acceptable for men and older women in a massive bleed) until group-specific is ready (about 10–20 min).

## 29.7 Transfusion reactions — know the difference

| Reaction | When | Signs | Action |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Acute haemolytic (ABO incompatible)** | **Minutes** — first 15 min | Fever, chills, **loin/back pain**, pain at the IV site, low BP, **red/dark urine**, DIC | **STOP**, keep IV open with new saline line, call doctor, resuscitate, return bag + giving set, bloods, urine. Usually a **wrong-patient error** |
| **Febrile non-haemolytic** | During or after | Temperature rise <2 °C, chills, stable otherwise | Slow/stop, paracetamol, check; may restart if mild |
| **Allergic (mild)** | Any time | Itch, hives only | Slow/pause, antihistamine, may continue |
| **Anaphylaxis** | Minutes | Wheeze, low BP, angioedema | Stop, **adrenaline IM 0.5 mg**; IgA-deficient patients at risk |
| **TACO** (circulatory overload) | Within 12 h | Breathless, **HIGH BP**, high JVP, crackles, positive fluid balance | Stop/slow, sit up, oxygen, **furosemide**. Prevent: one unit at a time in elderly/heart failure |
| **TRALI** (lung injury) | Within **6 h** | Breathless, hypoxia, **LOW or normal BP**, fever, bilateral infiltrates, normal JVP | Stop, oxygen/ventilation (supportive); **diuretics don't help**; report (donor antibodies) |
| **Bacterial contamination** | Minutes–hours | High fever, rigors, shock — commonest with platelets | Stop, blood cultures from patient and bag, antibiotics |
| **Delayed haemolytic** | 5–10 days | Falling Hb, jaundice, fever | Check antibody screen |
| **Transfusion-associated GVHD** | 1–6 weeks | Rash, diarrhoea, liver failure, pancytopenia — often fatal | Prevent with irradiated blood |

> One-line memory: **TACO = Too much (high BP, give diuretic). TRALI = Lungs injured (low BP, no diuretic).**

## 29.8 Biochemistry quick table

| Test | Normal | Key point |
|---|---|---|
| Urea | 2.5–7.8 mmol/L | High in kidney failure, dehydration, **GI bleed** (digested blood) |
| Creatinine | 60–110 µmol/L | Best simple kidney marker; KDIGO AKI = rise ≥26.5 µmol/L in 48 h or ≥1.5× baseline or urine <0.5 mL/kg/h for 6 h |
| CK | 25–200 U/L | Very high in rhabdomyolysis, NMS, MH |
| Troponin | <14 ng/L (assay-specific) | Rises 3–6 h after MI, stays up 7–14 days |
| CRP | <5 mg/L | Inflammation; rises in 6–8 h |
| Procalcitonin | <0.5 ng/mL | Higher in **bacterial** infection; helps stop antibiotics |
| Lactate | <2 mmol/L | ≥4 = severe hypoperfusion (sepsis bundle trigger) |
| Ammonia | <50 µmol/L | High in liver failure → encephalopathy |
| Serum osmolality | 275–295 mOsm/kg | = 2 × Na + urea + glucose |
| Albumin | 35–50 g/L | Low in critical illness, liver disease, malnutrition; corrects calcium |
