($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$30. Radiology and Imaging in the Critically Ill$s$, false, 30, $s$30. Radiology and Imaging in the Critically Ill$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 30. Radiology and Imaging in the Critically Ill

## 30.1 Types of imaging — which test for what

| Imaging | Uses | Simple point |
|---|---|---|
| **Plain X-ray** | Chest (tubes, lines, lungs), bones, abdomen | Quick, portable, low radiation |
| **CT (computed tomography)** | Head injury, stroke (bleed vs clot), PE (**CT pulmonary angiogram**), trauma, abdomen | Many X-rays → slices; more radiation; contrast may harm kidneys |
| **MRI (magnetic resonance)** | Spinal cord, brain detail, soft tissue | No radiation but a **very strong magnet** |
| **Ultrasound** | FAST, echo, DVT, lines, bladder volume, pleural fluid | No radiation, bedside, safe in pregnancy |
| **Fluoroscopy** | Moving X-ray (pacing wire, PICC, ERCP) | Higher radiation dose |
| **Nuclear scan (V/Q)** | PE when CT contrast can't be used | Radioactive tracer |
| **Angiography** | Blood vessels (coronary, bleeding vessel embolisation) | Contrast via an artery — check the puncture site and pulses afterwards |

- **CT head without contrast is the first test in stroke** — to rule out bleeding before giving a clot-buster.
- **CT pulmonary angiography** is the investigation of choice for PE.
- **Ultrasound** is first for gallstones, DVT and in pregnancy.

## 30.2 Reading a chest X-ray

![Chest X-ray: tubes and lines](keynote/cxr.svg)

**Before you read:** right patient, right date, **PA or AP**, rotation (clavicle heads equal), inspiration (6 anterior ribs above the diaphragm), penetration.

| Finding | What it looks like | Meaning |
|---|---|---|
| Pneumothorax | Black area with **no lung markings**, sharp lung edge; **deep sulcus sign** on a supine film | Air in pleural space |
| Tension pneumothorax | Above + **trachea and mediastinum pushed away**, flat diaphragm | Emergency — don't wait for the X-ray |
| Pleural effusion | White at the base with a **meniscus**, blunt costophrenic angle | Fluid in pleural space |
| Consolidation | Fluffy white patch, **air bronchograms** | Pneumonia |
| Collapse (atelectasis) | White, volume loss, trachea pulled **towards** it | Blocked bronchus (mucus plug) |
| Pulmonary oedema | **Bat-wing** shadows, Kerley B lines, upper-lobe diversion, big heart, effusions | Heart failure / fluid overload |
| ARDS | Bilateral patchy shadows, normal-size heart | Leaky lungs |
| Wide mediastinum (>8 cm) | Wide central shadow | **Aortic dissection/rupture** |
| Air under the diaphragm | Crescent of black under the diaphragm (erect film) | **Perforated bowel** |
| Cardiomegaly | Heart > half of chest width (on **PA** film) | Heart failure, effusion |

> Remember: **"Trachea pushed AWAY = tension or big effusion; trachea PULLED TOWARDS = collapse."**

## 30.3 Checking tubes and lines on X-ray

- **ETT:** tip 3–5 cm above the carina (about level of the clavicle heads, T2–T4). Too deep → **right main bronchus** (the right bronchus is shorter, wider and more vertical) → left lung collapses.
- **CVC (jugular/subclavian):** tip at the lower **SVC / cavo-atrial junction** (around the level of the carina). Check for pneumothorax after insertion.
- **NG tube:** follows the midline, **bisects the carina**, crosses the diaphragm, tip **10 cm below** it on the left side. Tube in a bronchus = remove at once.
- **Chest drain:** all side holes inside the chest.
- **PA catheter:** tip within 2 cm of the hilum (not out in the periphery).
- **IABP (intra-aortic balloon):** tip **2–3 cm below the top of the aortic arch** (at the level of the 2nd–3rd intercostal space / carina).
- **Pacing wire:** tip at the apex of the right ventricle.

## 30.4 Contrast safety

- **Contrast-associated kidney injury:** creatinine rises 48–72 hours after contrast. **Risk:** CKD, diabetes, dehydration, heart failure, metformin, NSAIDs, big contrast dose.
- **Prevent:** IV **hydration** with 0.9% saline before and after, use the smallest dose of **low-osmolar contrast (LOCM)**, avoid nephrotoxins, check creatinine at 48–72 hours. **Stop metformin** (risk of lactic acidosis) if kidney function is poor, and restart after 48 h if creatinine is stable.
- **Allergy:** ask about previous reactions. Iodine/shellfish allergy is **not** a reason to refuse contrast (myth), but a previous contrast reaction is. Have adrenaline ready.
- **Extravasation** (contrast leaks into tissue): stop, elevate the limb, cold compress, inform the doctor, mark and document.

## 30.5 MRI safety (exam favourite)

- The magnet is **always on**. **Any metal can fly** into the scanner (oxygen cylinders, scissors, drip stands) and kill.
- **Screening checklist** for everyone: pacemakers/ICDs (many are a contraindication unless "MRI-conditional"), cochlear implants, metal fragments in the eye (welders), aneurysm clips, some heart valves, pumps.
- **ICU patients:** need **MRI-compatible** ventilator, pumps and monitors; long lines through the wall; remove ECG electrodes with metal and ECG leads (burns).
- **Pregnancy:** MRI is generally safe after the first trimester; **gadolinium** contrast is avoided in pregnancy and in severe kidney failure (nephrogenic systemic fibrosis).

## 30.6 Radiation protection

- **ALARA** = As Low As Reasonably Achievable.
- The three rules: **Time** (shortest time near the source), **Distance** (stand at least **2 metres** away — doubling the distance gives a quarter of the dose, the **inverse square law**), **Shielding** (lead apron ≥0.25–0.5 mm lead, thyroid collar, lead screens).
- **Dosimeter (film badge/TLD)** measures staff radiation exposure.
- **Pregnant women:** ask about the last period before X-rays in women aged 12–55 ("10-day rule"/"28-day rule"); pregnant staff should not hold patients during X-rays.
- Never hold a patient during an X-ray unless essential — then wear lead and stay out of the main beam.
