($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$31. Reducing Hospital-Acquired Infection: Bundles, Sterilisation and Isolation$s$, false, 31, $s$31. Reducing Hospital-Acquired Infection: Bundles, Sterilisation and Isolation$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 31. Reducing Hospital-Acquired Infection: Bundles, Sterilisation and Isolation

Day 12 Section A was all about this. ICU patients have tubes in every opening and weak immunity — that's why ICU has the highest infection rate in the hospital.

## 31.1 The chain of infection (break any link)

1. **Infectious agent** (bacteria, virus, fungus) → kill it: cleaning, disinfection, sterilisation, antibiotics.
2. **Reservoir** (patients, staff, equipment, water) → clean environment, screen and treat carriers.
3. **Portal of exit** (wounds, secretions, blood) → cover wounds, cough etiquette.
4. **Mode of transmission** (contact, droplet, airborne, vector, common vehicle) → **hand hygiene** (the single most important measure), PPE, isolation.
5. **Portal of entry** (lines, catheters, ETT, surgical wounds) → asepsis, remove devices early.
6. **Susceptible host** (old, young, immunosuppressed, critically ill) → nutrition, vaccination, glucose control.

## 31.2 Hand hygiene

- **WHO 5 moments:** 1. Before touching a patient 2. **Before a clean/aseptic procedure** 3. After body fluid exposure risk 4. After touching a patient 5. After touching the patient's surroundings.
- **Alcohol hand rub** (20–30 seconds) for most moments — fastest and best.
- **Soap and water** (40–60 seconds) when hands are **visibly dirty**, and for **Clostridioides difficile** (spores) and **norovirus** — alcohol does not kill spores.
- **Bare below the elbows**, short nails, no artificial nails, no rings with stones.

## 31.3 Care bundles (a group of 3–6 evidence-based actions done together, every time)

### VAP (ventilator-associated pneumonia) bundle
- **Head of bed up 30–45°**.
- **Daily sedation hold** and assessment for weaning/extubation.
- **Oral care with chlorhexidine** (0.12–0.2%) / tooth brushing.
- **Subglottic secretion drainage** (ETT with a suction port above the cuff).
- **Cuff pressure 20–30 cmH₂O** (check every 4–8 hours).
- **Peptic ulcer prophylaxis** and **DVT prophylaxis** (part of many bundles).
- Avoid unnecessary circuit changes (only when soiled), closed suction.

### Central line (CLABSI) bundle
**At insertion:**
- Hand hygiene; **maximal sterile barrier** (cap, mask, sterile gown, gloves, full-body drape).
- **2% chlorhexidine in 70% alcohol** skin prep, allowed to dry.
- **Best site: subclavian**; avoid femoral in adults.
- Ultrasound guidance; checklist with the power to stop the procedure.

**Maintenance:**
- Daily review of need → remove ASAP.
- "Scrub the hub" 15 seconds before every access.
- Transparent dressing (change every 7 days, or sooner if loose/wet/soiled).
- Change giving sets: 96 hours (72–96) for clear fluids, **24 hours for TPN/lipids**, after each unit of blood (or 12 h).

### CAUTI bundle
- Catheterise only when needed (not for convenience/incontinence).
- Aseptic insertion; closed drainage system; never disconnect.
- **Bag below bladder level, off the floor**; empty with a clean container per patient.
- Daily review → remove early (**nurse-led removal protocols**).

### Surgical site infection (SSI) bundle
- No routine shaving (use clippers on the day of surgery), antibiotic prophylaxis **within 60 min before incision**, normothermia, glucose control, chlorhexidine skin prep.

### Sepsis "Hour-1" bundle (also an infection bundle)
1. Measure lactate. 2. **Blood cultures before antibiotics**. 3. **Broad-spectrum antibiotics**. 4. 30 mL/kg crystalloid for low BP or lactate ≥4. 5. Vasopressors if MAP <65 after fluids.

## 31.4 Standard and transmission-based precautions

- **Standard precautions** = for **every patient, every time**: hand hygiene, PPE when touching body fluids, safe sharps, cleaning, waste handling, cough etiquette.

| Precaution | Spread by | Examples | What you need |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Contact** | Touch (hands, equipment) | **MRSA**, VRE, C. difficile, scabies, diarrhoea | Single room, gloves + apron/gown, dedicated equipment |
| **Droplet** (>5 µm, fall within 1–2 m) | Coughs, sneezes | **Meningococcus**, influenza, mumps, pertussis, diphtheria | Surgical mask within 1–2 m, single room |
| **Airborne** (<5 µm, float in air) | Tiny particles | **TB, measles, chickenpox**, aerosol-generating procedures for COVID-19 | **Negative-pressure room**, **FFP3/N95 respirator** (fit-tested), door closed |
| **Protective (reverse) isolation** | — | Neutropenic patients, burns, transplant | Positive-pressure room, protect the patient from us |

- **Order to put on PPE (donning):** hand hygiene → gown → mask/respirator → goggles/face shield → gloves.
- **Order to take off (doffing):** **gloves first** → hand hygiene → goggles → gown → mask (last, outside the room) → hand hygiene.
- **Aerosol-generating procedures (AGPs):** intubation, extubation, open suction, NIV, high-flow O₂, bronchoscopy, CPR, tracheostomy care.

## 31.5 Cleaning, disinfection and sterilisation

![Spaulding classification](keynote/spaulding.svg)

- **Cleaning** = removes dirt and most germs (detergent and water). Always first.
- **Disinfection** = kills most germs but not all spores.
- **Sterilisation** = kills **all** microorganisms, **including spores**.
- **Antisepsis** = disinfection of **living tissue** (skin) — chlorhexidine, povidone-iodine, alcohol.

| Method | How | Used for |
|---|---|---|
| **Autoclave (steam under pressure)** | **121 °C for 15 min** or **134 °C for 3 min** | Most metal instruments, linen — commonest, cheapest |
| Dry heat (hot air oven) | 160 °C for 2 hours / 170 °C for 1 hour | Glassware, powders, oils |
| **Ethylene oxide gas** | Low temperature gas | Heat-sensitive items (plastics, electronics); needs aeration (toxic) |
| Hydrogen peroxide plasma | Low temperature | Heat-sensitive items, no toxic residue |
| Gamma radiation | Industrial | Single-use disposables (syringes, gloves) |
| Glutaraldehyde 2% (Cidex) | Soak 20 min = high-level disinfection; 10 h = sterilisation | Endoscopes (irritant — ventilation, PPE) |
| Chlorine (bleach) | 1,000 ppm (0.1%) general; **10,000 ppm (1%) for blood spills** | Surfaces, spills, C. diff areas |
| Alcohol 70% | Rub | Skin, small surfaces; not for spores |

- **Sterilisation indicators:** autoclave tape changes colour (shows the item was processed, not that it is sterile); **biological indicators** (spores of *Geobacillus stearothermophilus*) prove sterility; Bowie–Dick test checks air removal daily.
- **Single-use items:** never reprocess (a crossed-out number "2" on the pack means do not re-use).

## 31.6 Sharps, spills and waste

- **Never re-cap needles.** Sharps bin at the point of use; close when **three-quarters full**.
- **Needlestick injury:** let it bleed freely, wash with soap and running water (don't scrub or suck), report, risk assessment, **HIV PEP within 1–2 hours (no later than 72 hours)**, hepatitis B vaccine/immunoglobulin, baseline bloods, follow-up.
- **Blood spill:** gloves/apron, cover with chlorine granules/10,000 ppm, wait, wipe up, clean, dispose as clinical waste.
- **Waste colour codes (common in Nigeria):** **yellow** = infectious/clinical waste (incinerate); **black** = general/non-infectious waste; **red** = anatomical/highly infectious waste (some hospitals); **yellow sharps container** for sharps; **brown** = chemical/pharmaceutical. Follow your hospital's policy.

## 31.7 Antimicrobial stewardship and resistant organisms

- **Stewardship** = the right antibiotic, right dose, right time, right duration — and stop when not needed (review at 48–72 h, "start smart, then focus").
- **MRSA** → vancomycin/linezolid; contact precautions; decolonise (nasal mupirocin + chlorhexidine wash).
- **VRE**, **ESBL**, **CRE** (carbapenem-resistant) → contact precautions, cohorting.
- **C. difficile** (after antibiotics): smelly diarrhoea → isolate, **soap and water**, chlorine cleaning, oral vancomycin/fidaxomicin, stop the causing antibiotic.
- **Surveillance** = collecting infection data (rates per 1,000 device-days) and feeding it back to staff.
