($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$32. Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care — The Extra Topics$s$, false, 32, $s$32. Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care — The Extra Topics$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 32. Obstetric, Neonatal and Paediatric Critical Care — The Extra Topics

The O&G and paediatric sections came up every day (140 Obstetric & Neonatal questions in Days 10–15 alone). Book 1 Chapter 18 has the basics; this chapter adds the topics the mocks tested on top of that.

## 32.1 Obstetric emergencies — clue → action

| Emergency | Key clue | Immediate action |
|---|---|---|
| **Postpartum haemorrhage (PPH)** | ≥500 mL after vaginal birth (≥1000 mL = major), or any loss with shock | Call for help, **rub the uterus**, oxytocin, empty the bladder, 2 large cannulas, **tranexamic acid 1 g within 3 hours**, carboprost/misoprostol, theatre |
| **Eclampsia** | Fits in pre-eclampsia | Left lateral position, airway, **magnesium sulphate 4 g IV over 5–15 min, then 1 g/hour for 24 h**, control BP, deliver |
| **HELLP** | RUQ/epigastric pain, low platelets, high liver enzymes, haemolysis (high LDH) | Stabilise, MgSO₄, BP control, **deliver** |
| **Acute fatty liver of pregnancy (AFLP)** | Vomiting, jaundice, **low glucose**, high ammonia, DIC, 3rd trimester | Correct glucose and clotting, **deliver** |
| **Amniotic fluid embolism** | Sudden breathlessness, collapse, fits and bleeding (DIC) in labour or just after | CPR, oxygen, blood products, deliver |
| **Placental abruption** | **Painful** bleeding, hard "woody" tender uterus, fetal distress; bleeding may be hidden | Resuscitate, deliver, watch for DIC |
| **Placenta praevia** | **Painless** bright red bleeding | **No vaginal examination**, ultrasound, C-section |
| **Uterine rupture** | Scar, sudden pain, contractions stop, fetal parts easy to feel, shock | Laparotomy |
| **Cord prolapse** | Cord seen or felt after membranes rupture | **Push the presenting part up** (don't handle the cord), knee–chest or exaggerated Sims position, fill the bladder (500 mL), urgent delivery |
| **Shoulder dystocia** | "Turtle sign" — head comes out and pulls back | Call for help, **McRoberts** (thighs to the abdomen) + suprapubic pressure (HELPERR) |
| **Uterine inversion** | Shock out of proportion to bleeding, no uterus felt in the abdomen | Replace the uterus at once (Johnson manoeuvre), then oxytocin |
| **Peripartum cardiomyopathy** | Heart failure in the last month or up to 5 months after birth | Heart-failure treatment, bromocriptine (specialist) |

**PPH causes — the 4 Ts:** **T**one (soft uterus — 70%), **T**rauma (tears), **T**issue (retained placenta), **T**hrombin (clotting problems).

**Magnesium toxicity (check hourly):** **knee reflexes lost first** (at ~7–10 mmol/L), then **breathing slows** (RR <12), then cardiac arrest. Keep urine output **>30 mL/hour** (magnesium leaves through the kidneys). **Antidote: calcium gluconate 10%, 10 mL IV over 10 minutes.** Therapeutic level 2–4 mmol/L.

**MEOWS** — early warning chart for pregnant/postnatal women (pregnancy changes the normal values). Normal in pregnancy: HR up 10–20, BP lower in the 2nd trimester, RR slightly higher, **PaCO₂ lower (~30 mmHg)** — so a "normal" PaCO₂ of 40 in a pregnant woman is a warning sign.

**Maternal cardiac arrest:** manual left uterine displacement, early intubation (difficult airway), **perimortem C-section at 4 minutes** if no ROSC (delivery by 5 min) when the uterus is at or above the umbilicus (≥20 weeks).

## 32.2 Neonatal resuscitation

![Newborn life support](keynote/nls.svg)

- **APGAR** (at 1 and 5 minutes; 0–2 for each): **A**ppearance (colour), **P**ulse, **G**rimace (reflex irritability), **A**ctivity (tone), **R**espiration. **7–10 normal, 4–6 moderate, 0–3 severe.**
- **The most important action in neonatal resuscitation is ventilating the lungs** — most babies need breaths, not compressions.
- **Meconium:** a vigorous baby needs no suction; a floppy baby → look in the mouth, suction if obstructed, then breaths.
- **Umbilical venous catheter (UVC)** = fastest emergency IV access in a newborn.

## 32.3 Neonatal problems

| Condition | Who / clue | Care |
|---|---|---|
| **Respiratory distress syndrome (RDS)** | **Preterm**, no surfactant; grunting, recession, nasal flaring, ground-glass X-ray | **Antenatal steroids** (to mother), CPAP, **surfactant via the ETT** |
| **Transient tachypnoea of the newborn (TTN)** | Term, C-section, fast breathing; lung fluid not cleared | Oxygen/CPAP; settles in 24–72 h |
| **Meconium aspiration** | Post-term, fetal distress, green fluid | Ventilation, surfactant; risk of pulmonary hypertension |
| **Neonatal jaundice** | **Within 24 h = always abnormal** (haemolysis, ABO/Rh) | **Phototherapy** (eyes covered, turn, extra fluids); **exchange transfusion** if very high; kernicterus risk |
| **Hypoglycaemia** | Glucose <2.6 mmol/L; jittery, poor feeding, fits | Feed; **IV 10% dextrose 2 mL/kg** bolus then infusion |
| **Hypothermia** | Temp <36.5 °C | Skin-to-skin, cap, warm room, incubator. Babies lose heat by **radiation, convection, conduction, evaporation** |
| **Necrotising enterocolitis (NEC)** | Preterm, formula-fed; distended belly, bloody stool, bile vomit; **pneumatosis intestinalis** (gas in bowel wall) on X-ray | **Nil by mouth**, NG on free drainage, IV fluids, antibiotics, surgery if perforation |
| **Neonatal sepsis** | Temperature instability, poor feeding, apnoea | Cultures, **benzylpenicillin + gentamicin** within 1 hour |
| **Tracheo-oesophageal fistula / oesophageal atresia** | Frothy saliva, choking/cyanosis on feeding, NG tube **won't pass** | Nil by mouth, continuous suction of the upper pouch (Replogle tube), head up, surgery; look for **VACTERL** |
| **Congenital diaphragmatic hernia** | **Scaphoid (sunken) abdomen**, bowel sounds in chest, heart sounds shifted right | **No bag-mask ventilation** — intubate, NG tube to decompress |
| **Gastroschisis / exomphalos** | Bowel outside the abdomen (gastroschisis: no sac; exomphalos: covered by a sac) | Cover with **cling film/sterile bag**, keep warm, NG tube, fluids |
| **Persistent pulmonary hypertension (PPHN)** | Cyanosis, pre-ductal SpO₂ > post-ductal by >10% | Oxygen, ventilation, nitric oxide |

- **Neonatal values:** HR 110–160 (100–180 in resus), RR 40–60, temperature 36.5–37.5 °C, glucose ≥2.6 mmol/L, blood volume ~85 mL/kg.
- **Fluids (term):** day 1 ≈ 60 mL/kg/day, rising ~20–30 mL/kg/day to 150 mL/kg/day by day 5.
- **Vitamin K** at birth (prevents haemorrhagic disease of the newborn).

## 32.4 Paediatric emergencies

| Condition | Clue | Key care |
|---|---|---|
| **Croup** (laryngotracheobronchitis) | 6 months–3 years, **barking cough**, stridor, mild fever, worse at night | **Dexamethasone 0.15 mg/kg**, nebulised adrenaline if severe; keep calm |
| **Epiglottitis** | 2–6 years, toxic, **drooling**, tripod sitting, muffled voice, no cough | **Do not look in the throat or lay flat**, keep calm on parent's lap, senior anaesthetist/ENT, intubate in theatre; Hib vaccine prevents it |
| **Bronchiolitis** | Under 1 year (RSV), crackles and wheeze, poor feeding | Supportive: oxygen if SpO₂ <90–92%, nasal suction, NG/IV fluids, CPAP |
| **Foreign body aspiration** | Sudden choking, unilateral wheeze; **right** bronchus | Back blows (5) + chest thrusts (infant) / abdominal thrusts (child >1 year); bronchoscopy |
| **Intussusception** | 3 months–3 years, colicky pain with drawing up of legs, **redcurrant-jelly stool**, sausage-shaped mass | IV fluids, NG, air/contrast enema reduction, surgery |
| **Pyloric stenosis** | 2–8 weeks, **projectile non-bilious vomiting**, hungry after, olive-shaped mass | **Hypochloraemic, hypokalaemic metabolic alkalosis** → correct fluids FIRST, then pyloromyotomy |
| **Malrotation with volvulus** | **Bilious (green) vomiting** in a baby | **Surgical emergency** |
| **Hirschsprung's disease** | Delayed meconium (>48 h), distension | Rectal biopsy, surgery |
| **Dehydration / gastroenteritis** | Sunken eyes/fontanelle, dry mouth, poor skin turgor, lethargy | ORS (mild–moderate); IV **10 mL/kg** bolus for shock (20 mL/kg in some protocols), then replacement |
| **Sickle cell crisis in a child** | Pain, fever, big spleen, pallor | Pain relief within 30 min, fluids, oxygen, antibiotics, transfuse for sequestration |
| **Febrile convulsion** | 6 months–5 years, fever, short generalised fit | Safety, recovery position, treat fever; benzodiazepine if >5 min |
| **DKA in children** | Same as adults but **cerebral oedema risk** | **Slow fluids** (no rapid boluses unless shocked), **no insulin bolus** (0.05–0.1 units/kg/hr, start 1–2 h after fluids), watch for headache/slow pulse/confusion → mannitol/3% saline |
| **Severe malaria** | Fever, coma, fits, severe anaemia, acidosis | **IV artesunate**, check glucose, transfuse if Hb <5 g/dL (or <7 with distress) |
| **Kerosene ingestion** | Coughing, breathlessness | **Don't induce vomiting**, oxygen, observe for pneumonitis |

**Paediatric numbers to remember:**
- **Weight estimate (1–10 years):** (age + 4) × 2 kg.
- **ETT size (uncuffed):** age ÷ 4 + 4; cuffed: age ÷ 4 + 3.5. **Length (oral):** age ÷ 2 + 12 cm.
- **Fluid bolus:** 10 mL/kg (reassess, repeat up to 40–60 mL/kg) — 20 mL/kg in some protocols.
- **Maintenance fluid (Holliday–Segar, "4-2-1"):** 4 mL/kg/hr for the first 10 kg + 2 mL/kg/hr for the next 10 kg + 1 mL/kg/hr for each kg above 20.
- **Adrenaline in arrest:** 10 mcg/kg (0.1 mL/kg of 1:10,000). **Defibrillation:** 4 J/kg. **Glucose:** 2 mL/kg of 10% dextrose.
- **Paediatric CPR:** **15 compressions : 2 breaths** (2 rescuers); start with **5 rescue breaths**; compress one-third of chest depth; rate 100–120/min.
- **Paediatric GCS:** verbal score adapted for babies (coos/babbles = 5, cries to pain = 3).
- **Urine output:** ≥1 mL/kg/hr (infants 2 mL/kg/hr).

## 32.5 Paediatric surgical, respiratory and haematological points

- **Children compensate, then crash:** BP stays normal until ~30–40% blood loss. **Tachycardia and slow capillary refill are early signs; low BP is a late, pre-arrest sign.**
- **Bradycardia in a sick child = hypoxia until proved otherwise** → oxygenate/ventilate first.
- **Airway:** large tongue, big occiput (in infants, use a **neutral** head position with a towel under the shoulders), floppy epiglottis, narrowest at the **cricoid** (younger children).
- **Thalassaemia/sickle cell transfusion:** watch iron overload (desferrioxamine).
- **Haemophilia bleed** (joint/muscle) → give factor first, then investigate; no IM injections, no aspirin/NSAIDs.
- **Leukaemia in children:** fever + neutropenia = **neutropenic sepsis** → antibiotics within 1 hour; **tumour lysis syndrome** (high K, high PO₄, high urate, low Ca) → hyperhydration, rasburicase/allopurinol.
