($s$keynote_notes$s$, $s$33. ICU Management, Quality, Safety, Leadership, Theories, Teaching and Family Care$s$, false, 33, $s$33. ICU Management, Quality, Safety, Leadership, Theories, Teaching and Family Care$s$, null, null, null, $s$# 33. ICU Management, Quality, Safety, Leadership, Theories, Teaching and Family Care

The **Ethics, Law & Management** category had the most questions in Days 10–15 (180). This chapter and the next cover it.

## 33.1 The ICU unit — organisation

- **Levels of care:** **Level 0** = normal ward; **Level 1** = ward with extra support/outreach; **Level 2 (HDU)** = single organ support (e.g. NIV, one inotrope), nurse : patient **1 : 2**; **Level 3 (ICU)** = advanced respiratory support or 2+ organs, nurse : patient **1 : 1**.
- **Bed space:** about **20–25 m²** per bed in modern ICUs, with enough room around the bed for the team, equipment and X-ray. Each bed has oxygen, air and suction outlets (often 2–3 of each), many power sockets, and emergency power.
- **Isolation rooms:** at least 1 for every 6–10 beds (some negative pressure).
- **Location:** close to theatre, A&E, radiology and lifts.
- **Admission criteria:** reversible condition, needs organ support, likely to benefit. **Discharge** when organ support is no longer needed (step down to HDU/ward with a handover).
- **Outreach team / rapid response team:** ICU nurses/doctors who see deteriorating ward patients (triggered by NEWS2).

## 33.2 Management functions and principles

- **Henri Fayol's functions:** **Planning, Organising, Commanding (Directing), Coordinating, Controlling** (POCCC). Luther Gulick extended it: **POSDCORB** — Planning, Organising, Staffing, Directing, Coordinating, Reporting, Budgeting.
- **Planning** comes first; **controlling** = comparing results with standards and correcting.
- **Unity of command** = each staff member reports to one boss. **Span of control** = number of people one manager supervises.
- **Delegation:** the **5 rights** — right task, right circumstances, right person, right direction/communication, right supervision. You can delegate the task, but **not the accountability**.
- **Management by objectives (MBO)** (Peter Drucker): manager and staff set goals together, then review results.
- **Budget types:** **operating** (day-to-day: salaries, consumables), **capital** (big items: ventilators, building), **zero-based** (start from zero each year and justify everything).
- **Stock control:** **FIFO** (first in, first out); par levels; check expiry dates.

## 33.3 Leadership styles

| Style | What it is | Best used |
|---|---|---|
| **Autocratic** | Leader decides alone | **Emergencies** (cardiac arrest, mass casualty), new untrained staff |
| **Democratic (participative)** | Team shares in decisions | Planning, change, experienced teams |
| **Laissez-faire** | Leader leaves the team to decide | Highly skilled, motivated experts — risky in ICU |
| **Transformational** | Inspires a shared vision, motivates people to go beyond | Building a strong unit culture — the best long-term |
| **Transactional** | Rewards and punishments for tasks | Routine work |
| **Situational** (Hersey–Blanchard) | Changes style to fit the staff's readiness | Mixed teams |

## 33.4 Motivation and change theories

- **Maslow's hierarchy of needs** (bottom to top): **physiological → safety → love/belonging → esteem → self-actualisation**. Meet the lower needs first (in patients: **airway/breathing first**).
- **Herzberg's two-factor theory:** **hygiene factors** (salary, conditions, policies) stop dissatisfaction; **motivators** (achievement, recognition, responsibility, growth) create satisfaction.
- **McGregor's Theory X** (people are lazy, need control) vs **Theory Y** (people like work and responsibility).
- **Lewin's change theory:** **Unfreeze** (create the need for change) → **Change/Move** (introduce it) → **Refreeze** (make it the new normal).
- **Resistance to change** is normal: involve staff early, explain why, train, support.

## 33.5 Staffing, rostering and conflict

- **Staffing methods:** **patient acuity/dependency** systems (nurses based on how sick patients are) are better than fixed ratios.
- **Duty rota:** fair, planned in advance, skill mix on each shift (an experienced ICU nurse on every shift).
- **Conflict resolution styles (Thomas–Kilmann):** **collaborating** (win–win, best for important issues), compromising, competing, accommodating, avoiding.
- **Burnout:** emotional exhaustion, depersonalisation, low sense of achievement. Prevent: adequate staffing, breaks, debriefing, rotation, support, recognition. **Compassion fatigue** and **moral distress** are common in ICU.
- **Debriefing** after a cardiac arrest or a death — supports staff and improves practice.

## 33.6 Quality and patient safety

![PDSA cycle and quality improvement](keynote/pdsa.svg)

- **Donabedian model:** **Structure** (staff, equipment, building) → **Process** (what we do: hand hygiene rate, bundles) → **Outcome** (VAP rate, mortality, satisfaction).
- **Clinical audit cycle:** choose a topic → set a **standard** → collect data → **compare** with the standard → make changes → **re-audit** (close the loop).
- **Quality indicators for ICU:** VAP/CLABSI/CAUTI rates, pressure ulcers, unplanned extubation, re-admission within 48 h, mortality (SMR), medication errors, patient/family satisfaction.
- **Total quality management (TQM)** = everyone is responsible for quality, continuous improvement. **Six Sigma** = reduce variation. **Lean** = remove waste.
- **Swiss cheese model (James Reason):** an accident happens when the holes in several layers of defence line up — fix the system.
- **Root cause analysis (RCA):** after a serious incident; asks "**why?**" repeatedly (5 whys, fishbone diagram); **no blame**.
- **Incident reporting:** report all incidents and **near-misses** promptly and honestly (no-blame/just culture). Fill an incident form; it is **not** filed in the patient's notes.
- **Never events:** wrong-site surgery, wrong-route drug (e.g. IV for oral), retained swab, misplaced NG tube used for feeding.
- **Risk assessment:** identify hazard → who may be harmed → evaluate (likelihood × severity) → control → review. **Risk register** = list of a unit's risks.

## 33.7 Communication tools

- **SBAR:** **S**ituation (what is happening now), **B**ackground (history), **A**ssessment (what you think), **R**ecommendation (what you need).
- **Handover:** at the bedside where possible, structured, includes the plan, lines, drugs, risks, family issues.
- **Closed-loop communication:** say the order, repeat it back, confirm ("Adrenaline 1 mg given").
- **Read-back** for telephone orders and critical results.
- **WHO surgical safety checklist:** sign in (before anaesthesia), time out (before incision), sign out (before leaving theatre).

## 33.8 Nursing theories (who said what)

| Theorist | Theory | Key idea |
|---|---|---|
| **Florence Nightingale** | Environmental theory | Clean air, water, light, quiet, drainage → healing |
| **Virginia Henderson** | **14 basic needs** | Nurse helps the patient do what they would do for themselves if they had the strength ("breathe normally" is need 1) |
| **Dorothea Orem** | **Self-care deficit** | Wholly compensatory (ICU patient — nurse does everything), partly compensatory, supportive-educative |
| **Callista Roy** | **Adaptation model** | Person adapts to stimuli in 4 modes (physiological, self-concept, role, interdependence) |
| **Hildegard Peplau** | **Interpersonal relations** | Nurse–patient relationship: orientation → identification → exploitation → resolution |
| **Imogene King** | Goal attainment | Nurse and patient set goals together |
| **Betty Neuman** | Systems model | Lines of defence against stressors; primary/secondary/tertiary prevention |
| **Madeleine Leininger** | Transcultural nursing | Culturally congruent care |
| **Jean Watson** | Human caring | Caring as the core of nursing |
| **Patricia Benner** | **Novice to expert** | 5 stages: novice → advanced beginner → competent → proficient → expert |
| **Martha Rogers** | Unitary human beings | Person and environment are energy fields |
| **Nancy Roper** (Roper–Logan–Tierney) | **12 activities of living** | Maintaining a safe environment, breathing, eating, etc. |

## 33.9 Teaching and learning in the ICU

- **Bloom's taxonomy — 3 domains:** **cognitive** (knowledge), **affective** (attitudes, feelings), **psychomotor** (skills, e.g. suctioning).
- Cognitive levels (low → high): remember → understand → apply → analyse → evaluate → create.
- **Adult learning (andragogy, Malcolm Knowles):** adults are self-directed, use experience, want relevant, problem-centred learning.
- **Teaching methods:** demonstration and **return demonstration** (best for skills), simulation, case study, bedside teaching, e-learning.
- **Preceptor** = experienced nurse who supervises a new nurse for a set time; **mentor** = longer-term guide and supporter.
- **Patient/family teaching:** assess readiness and literacy, use simple words, teach-back method ("tell me in your own words").
- **Continuing professional development (CPD):** required to renew your licence (NMCN).

## 33.10 Family care and stress in critical care

- **Family needs in ICU (Molter's study):** top needs are **information/assurance** (honest, regular updates), **proximity** (being near the patient), comfort, support.
- **Open/flexible visiting** reduces family anxiety and patient delirium.
- **Family-centred care:** involve families in rounds and simple care (mouth care, massage) if they wish.
- **Breaking bad news — SPIKES:** **S**etting (private, sit down), **P**erception (what do they know?), **I**nvitation (how much do they want to know?), **K**nowledge (give it simply, warning shot, no jargon), **E**motions (respond with empathy, silence), **S**trategy and summary (next steps).
- **Post-intensive care syndrome (PICS):** after ICU, patients (and families — PICS-F) may have weakness, memory problems, anxiety, depression and **PTSD**. ICU diaries and follow-up clinics help.
- **Stress responses in patients:** noise, lights, pain, no sleep, fear, being unable to talk → delirium. Reduce noise (<45 dB in the day), dim lights at night, give communication boards/pen and paper to intubated patients.
- **Grief stages (Kübler-Ross):** **denial, anger, bargaining, depression, acceptance** (not always in order).
