Read this list on the morning of the exam.

## 38.1 Numbers

1. Normal pH 7.35–7.45; PaCO₂ 35–45 mmHg; HCO₃⁻ 22–26 mmol/L; PaO₂ 80–100 mmHg.
2. MAP target ≥65 mmHg; CPP = MAP − ICP, aim 60–70.
3. Normal ICP 5–15 mmHg; treat above 20–22.
4. CVP 2–8 mmHg; PAWP 6–12 mmHg; CO 4–8 L/min; CI 2.5–4.
5. Urine output ≥0.5 mL/kg/hr (adult), ≥1 mL/kg/hr (child).
6. Tidal volume 6–8 mL/kg predicted body weight (6 in ARDS); plateau ≤30 cmH₂O.
7. ETT cuff pressure 20–30 cmH₂O.
8. Suction: 80–150 mmHg adult; ≤10–15 s per pass.
9. RSBI <105 = likely to wean.
10. GCS ≤8 → intubate ("8 — intubate").
11. Potassium: max 10 mmol/hr peripherally.
12. Parkland: 4 mL × kg × %TBSA; half in the first 8 h from the time of the burn.
13. Paediatric fluid bolus 10–20 mL/kg; weight (age + 4) × 2.
14. Neonatal HR 110–160; RR 40–60; glucose ≥2.6 mmol/L.
15. MgSO₄ loading 4 g, maintenance 1–2 g/hr; antidote calcium gluconate.
16. Adrenaline: arrest 1 mg IV every 3–5 min; anaphylaxis 0.5 mg IM.
17. Amiodarone 300 mg after the 3rd shock, 150 mg after the 5th.
18. Paediatric shock 4 J/kg; CPR 15:2; neonatal 3:1.
19. Transfuse red cells within 4 hours; platelets at room temperature with agitation.
20. Autoclave 121 °C for 15 min or 134 °C for 3 min.

## 38.2 Signs and clues

21. Beck's triad (low BP, high JVP, muffled sounds) = tamponade.
22. Cushing triad (high BP, slow pulse, irregular breathing) = raised ICP (late).
23. Trachea pushed away + no breath sounds + shock = tension pneumothorax → needle now.
24. Low BP + slow pulse + warm skin after spinal injury = neurogenic shock.
25. Grey-Turner (flank) and Cullen (umbilicus) bruising = haemorrhagic pancreatitis.
26. Chvostek and Trousseau = low calcium.
27. Tall tented T waves = high potassium; U waves = low potassium.
28. Kussmaul breathing + fruity breath = DKA.
29. Lucid interval = extradural haematoma.
30. Ascending weakness + lost reflexes = Guillain–Barré; monitor FVC.
31. Redcurrant-jelly stool = intussusception; projectile non-bilious vomiting = pyloric stenosis.
32. Barking cough = croup; drooling + tripod = epiglottitis (don't examine the throat).
33. Painless bleeding = placenta praevia; painful + hard uterus = abruption.
34. Knee reflexes lost = magnesium toxicity (first sign).
35. High BP + high JVP after transfusion = TACO; low BP + lung injury = TRALI.

## 38.3 First actions and priorities

36. Every emergency: Airway → Breathing → Circulation → Disability → Exposure.
37. Ventilator alarm and you don't know why → disconnect and bag with 100% O₂ (DOPE).
38. Anaphylaxis → IM adrenaline first (antihistamines are second-line).
39. Hyperkalaemia with ECG change → calcium gluconate first (protects the heart).
40. Septic shock → cultures, antibiotics within 1 hour, 30 mL/kg fluid, noradrenaline.
41. Haemolytic transfusion reaction → STOP the transfusion, keep the line open with saline.
42. Air embolism → clamp, left side down + head down, 100% O₂.
43. Chest drain disconnected → put the end under sterile water; don't clamp.
44. Neonate not breathing → dry, warm, 5 inflation breaths (most need only breaths).
45. Autonomic dysreflexia → sit up, find the cause (check the bladder first).
46. Blocked tracheostomy → remove the inner tube first.
47. Cord prolapse → push the presenting part up, knee–chest position.
48. Needlestick → bleed, wash, report, PEP within 1–2 hours.
49. MRI → screen for metal every time.
50. NG tube → confirm position (pH ≤5.5 or X-ray) before the first feed.

## 38.4 Professional practice

51. Hand hygiene is the single most important way to prevent infection.
52. Airborne (TB, measles, chickenpox) → FFP3/N95 + negative-pressure room.
53. Negligence = duty, breach, causation, damage.
54. Valid consent = informed, voluntary, with capacity.
55. Documentation errors → single line, initial, date — never correction fluid.
56. Autocratic leadership is best in emergencies; transformational is best long-term.
57. Lewin: unfreeze → change → refreeze.
58. PES: Problem — related to Etiology — as evidenced by Signs.
59. Independent variable = the cause; dependent variable = the effect measured.
60. Ratio scale has a true zero (weight); ordinal has order without equal gaps (pain scale).

> Good luck. Read the question twice, look for the key word (FIRST, MOST, EXCEPT), choose the safest answer that helps the patient most right now.
